Oven Spring happens during baking, while gluten formation happens during kneading.', '["Sentence A is correct","Sentence B is correct","Both sentences are correct","Both sentences are incorrect"]'::jsonb, '["Food Service Systems"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-23T15:00:20.210Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-22T15:00:20.210Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.039Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-22T15:00:20.210Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1755152936920-24', NULL, 'anki-deck-1755151710403-1790432463944-n11t8', 'Sentence A. Margarine is a mixture of skim milk and vegetable fat.
Sentence B. Margarine contains hydrogenated fat.', 'Both sentences are correct', 'Margarine – a butter substitute made from vegetable oils and may contain butter, salt, artificial coloring, emulsifiers, and preservatives; made by emulsifying fat other than milk fat with cultured milk; contains eighty percent (80%) fat and sixteen percent (16%) water; usually fortified

Margarine is roughly 80% fat (from vegetable oils or animal fats) and about 17% to 18.5% liquid. Pasteurized skimmed milk, water, or soybean protein fluid makes up this liquid portion.

Traditional margarines also commonly contain hydrogenated or otherwise modified vegetable fats to obtain suitable texture and plasticity.

[Note: "made by emulsifying fat other than milk fat with cultured milk"  is a specific traditional or legal standard (resembling statutory definitions found in some food codes like the FDA/Codex Alimentarius for standard margarine blends), though today''s products can use various water-in-oil formulations without strictly requiring cultured milk.]', '["Sentence A is correct","Sentence B is correct","Both sentences are correct","Both sentences are incorrect"]'::jsonb, '["Food Service Systems"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-23T15:48:22.686Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-22T15:48:22.686Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.039Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-22T15:48:22.686Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1755152970919-25', NULL, 'anki-deck-1755151710403-1790432463944-n11t8', 'Sentence A. Chicken fat contains soft fats.
Sentence B. Chicken fat is more plastic than pork fat.', 'Sentence A is correct', '• Sentence A is CORRECT: Chicken fat contains a very high concentration of monounsaturated and polyunsaturated fatty acids. These are chemically classified as "soft fats" because they have low melting points, making chicken fat soft and oily at room temperature.

• Sentence B is INCORRECT: Because chicken fat turns into liquid oil so easily, it cannot retain its shape under manual pressure. Pork fat (lard) is actually more plastic than chicken fat. Lard forms a stable crystalline matrix at room temperature that allows it to be pliable, spreadable, and hold structural shape—which is the textbook definition of a plastic fat in food science.

Pork Fat (Lard): Higher saturated fat content (around 39–45%) gives it a firmer, more stable crystalline structure at room temperature, allowing it to hold a shape and perform well as a plastic shortening in baking and meat processing. 

Chicken Fat: Higher unsaturated fat content (around 65–68%) gives it a much lower melting point (melting around 23–27°C or lower) and causes it to liquefy rapidly. 

Because it turns to liquid oil so quickly, it has a narrower solid-to-liquid range and lower structural plasticity than pork fat.', '["Sentence A is correct","Sentence B is correct","Both sentences are correct","Both sentences are incorrect"]'::jsonb, '["Food Service Systems"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-23T16:46:08.825Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.3,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-22T16:46:08.825Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.039Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-22T16:46:08.825Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1755152997900-26', NULL, 'anki-deck-1755151710403-1790432463944-n11t8', 'Sentence A. Moist heat cooking is used in tough or less tender meat.
Sentence B. Water solubilizes collagen making meat tender.', 'Sentence A is correct', 'Moist-heat cooking is suitable for less tender cuts because prolonged heating in the presence of moisture converts collagen into gelatin, thereby improving tenderness.

Water alone is not enough to dissolve collagen. When meat reaches temperatures between 160°F to 205°F (71°C to 96°C) range, the tightly wound collagen proteins begin to uncoil and denature.

The presence of water is chemically required to break the molecular bonds of the uncoiled collagen. Through a process called partial hydrolysis, the water solubilizes the tough collagen and converts it into gelatin.', '["Sentence A is correct","Sentence B is correct","Both sentences are correct","Both sentences are incorrect"]'::jsonb, '["Food Service Systems"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-23T16:43:08.740Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.3,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-22T16:43:08.740Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.039Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-22T16:43:08.740Z"'::jsonb),
