('anki-card-1756357717281-0', NULL, 'anki-deck-1756357465257-1790432463920-rf85m', 'During prolonged starvation, which of the following substances can serve as an energy source? 
I. β-hydroxybutyric acid 
II. Glucose 
III. Acetoacetic acid', 'I and II only', 'During prolonged starvation, the body undergoes metabolic adaptations to preserve lean body mass while ensuring a continuous supply of energy to vital organs. Initially, glycogen stores are depleted within approximately 24 hours. Thereafter, gluconeogenesis becomes the major source of blood glucose, using amino acids, lactate, and glycerol as substrates.

Although glucose production decreases over time to conserve muscle protein, it remains essential because certain tissues cannot utilize fatty acids. The liver simultaneously increases ketogenesis, producing the ketone bodies beta-hydroxybutyrate and acetoacetate from acetyl-CoA generated by beta-oxidation of fatty acids.

These ketone bodies become major fuels for the brain after several days of starvation, reducing its dependence on glucose and sparing muscle protein.

Thus:
• β-Hydroxybutyrate is an important alternative fuel during starvation.
• Acetoacetate is another major ketone body used for energy.
• Glucose continues to be required by tissues such as red blood cells, the renal medulla, portions of the brain, and other glucose-dependent cells.

Therefore, all three substances serve as energy sources during starvation.', '["I only","I and II only","II and III only","All of the above"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-07T11:31:41.735Z","interval":3,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":2}'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:31:41.735Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.942Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:31:41.735Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756357800278-1', NULL, 'anki-deck-1756357465257-1790432463920-rf85m', '__ is a hormone that diminishes appetite and increase energy expenditure while __ increase appetite and decreases energy expenditure.', 'Leptin, neuropeptide Y', 'Leptin is a hormone secreted primarily by adipocytes. It acts on the hypothalamus to suppress food intake and increase energy expenditure by signaling adequate energy stores. 

Neuropeptide Y (NPY) is one of the most potent appetite-stimulating neurotransmitters in the hypothalamus. It increases food intake, promotes fat storage, and decreases energy expenditure, especially during fasting or caloric restriction. 

Leptin and NPY exert opposing effects: 
• High leptin suppresses NPY activity. 
• Low leptin (e.g., starvation) increases NPY secretion, stimulating hunger.', '["Neuropeptide Y, leptin","Leptin, neuropeptide Y","Cortisol, leptin","Leptin, glucagon"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-07T11:48:34.105Z","interval":3,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":2}'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:48:34.105Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.942Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:48:34.105Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756357827142-2', NULL, 'anki-deck-1756357465257-1790432463920-rf85m', 'Evaluate the following statements regarding amino acid metabolism.
I. Amino acids are deaminated first into ketoacid before being utilized for energy. 
II. Deamination primarily occurs in the kidneys.', 'Both statements are incorrect.', 'Statement 1 is imprecise. Most amino acids first undergo transamination, transferring their amino group to α-ketoglutarate to form glutamate. Glutamate is then oxidatively deaminated, producing ammonia and regenerating α-ketoglutarate. Thus, deamination is not the universal "first" step for amino acid catabolism. 

Statement 2 is also incorrect. The liver is the primary site of amino acid deamination and urea synthesis. While the kidneys participate in amino acid metabolism and ammonia production, they are not the principal site of deamination. Therefore, both statements are incorrect.', '["Only statement 1 is correct.","Only statement 2 is correct.","Both statements are correct.","Both statements are incorrect."]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 3, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-12T11:37:03.458Z","interval":8,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":3}'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:37:03.458Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.942Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:37:03.458Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756357854657-3', NULL, 'anki-deck-1756357465257-1790432463920-rf85m', 'The following are metabolic pathways of cholesterol EXCEPT:', 'Energy production', 'In humans, the core structure of cholesterol consists of a highly stable four-ring steroid nucleus (perhydrocyclopentanophenanthrene ring). Unlike fatty acids, carbohydrates, or proteins, the human body completely lacks the enzymes required to break down or cleave this robust ring structure to generate energy or ATP. Therefore, cholesterol provides 0 calories per gram and is never utilized as a metabolic fuel source.

• Secretion of bile and Excretion of feces: Because the body cannot oxidize or dismantle the steroid ring for energy, the only way it can physically eliminate cholesterol is through the liver. The liver converts cholesterol into bile acids and bile salts, which are secreted into the digestive tract to help emulsify dietary fats. While most bile is reabsorbed, a small portion escapes recycling and is unalterably eliminated from the body via fecal excretion.
• Hormone production: Cholesterol serves as the absolute, obligatory structural precursor for the synthesis of all steroid hormones in the body. Specialized endocrine tissues modify cholesterol to produce glucocorticoids (like cortisol), mineralocorticoids (like aldosterone), and sex hormones (like estrogen, progesterone, and testosterone).', '["Secretion of bile","Excretion of feces","Energy production","Hormone production"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-07T11:53:06.583Z","interval":3,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":2}'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:53:06.583Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.942Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:53:06.583Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756357882524-4', NULL, 'anki-deck-1756357465257-1790432463920-rf85m', 'This enzyme allows the brain and erythrocytes to utilize glucose even in state of low concentrations.', 'Hexokinase', 'Hexokinase catalyzes the first step of glycolysis by converting glucose to glucose-6-phosphate. It has a low Km (high affinity) for glucose, allowing tissues like the brain and red blood cells to continue utilizing glucose even when blood glucose levels are low. 

This is physiologically important because: 
• The brain relies heavily on glucose under normal conditions. 
• Red blood cells depend exclusively on glycolysis because they lack mitochondria. 

By contrast, the liver expresses glucokinase, which has a higher Km and functions primarily when glucose concentrations are elevated after meals.', '["Glucose-6-phosphate","Hexokinase","ATP","Fructose-6-phosphate"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-07T11:45:04.728Z","interval":3,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":2}'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:45:04.728Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.942Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:45:04.728Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756357909472-5', NULL, 'anki-deck-1756357465257-1790432463920-rf85m', 'NAD+ contains ___ while FAD contains ___.', 'Niacin, riboflavin', 'NAD⁺ (Nicotinamide Adenine Dinucleotide) and FAD (Flavin Adenine Dinucleotide) are two of the most important coenzymes involved in oxidation-reduction (redox) reactions and energy metabolism. 

NAD⁺ is synthesized from niacin (Vitamin B₃). During glycolysis, the TCA cycle, and β-oxidation, NAD⁺ accepts electrons and hydrogen ions to become NADH, which subsequently donates these electrons to the Electron Transport Chain (ETC) to generate ATP. 

FAD, on the other hand, is derived from riboflavin (Vitamin B₂). It accepts two hydrogen atoms to form FADH₂, another reduced coenzyme that delivers electrons to Complex II of the ETC. 

Although both NADH and FADH₂ ultimately produce ATP, NADH generates more ATP because it donates electrons earlier in the ETC.', '["Thiamine, riboflavin","Riboflavin, thiamine","Niacin, riboflavin","Riboflavin, niacin"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-07T11:44:46.913Z","interval":3,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":2}'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:44:46.913Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.942Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:44:46.913Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756357937304-6', NULL, 'anki-deck-1756357465257-1790432463920-rf85m', 'Phosphoglycerate kinase functions in carbohydrate metabolism to produce ATP via:', 'Substrate level phosphorylation', 'Phosphoglycerate kinase catalyzes the conversion of: 1,3-bisphosphoglycerate → 3-phosphoglycerate 

During this reaction, a phosphate group is directly transferred from the substrate to ADP, producing ATP. This direct transfer is called substrate-level phosphorylation. Unlike oxidative phosphorylation, this reaction: 
• does not require oxygen, 
• does not occur in mitochondria, 
• does not involve the electron transport chain. 

Another example of substrate-level phosphorylation occurs during the conversion of phosphoenolpyruvate (PEP) to pyruvate by pyruvate kinase.', '["Oxidative phosphorylation","Substrate level phosphorylation","Oxidative decarboxylation","Phosphorolysis"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-06T02:49:54.424Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.1,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-11-05T02:49:54.424Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.942Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-05T02:49:54.424Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756357964604-7', NULL, 'anki-deck-1756357465257-1790432463920-rf85m', 'Under the influence of sunlight, __ is synthesized in our skin from ___.', 'Vitamin D₃, 7-dehydrocholesterol', 'When your skin is exposed to the ultraviolet B (UVB) radiation in sunlight, it breaks down a specific form of cholesterol naturally present in your skin cells called 7-dehydrocholesterol.

This chemical reaction converts the cholesterol precursor into Vitamin D3 (cholecalciferol), which your liver and kidneys then convert into its active hormone form.', '["Vitamin D₃, 7-dehydrocholesterol","Calcium, vitamin D","Vitamin E, cholesterol","Vitamin B1, protein"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-07T11:31:29.077Z","interval":3,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":2}'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:31:29.077Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.942Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:31:29.077Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756357988685-8', NULL, 'anki-deck-1756357465257-1790432463920-rf85m', 'Which of the following is true for BMR?', 'Pregnancy increases BMR', 'Basal Metabolic Rate (BMR) represents the minimum amount of energy required to maintain vital physiologic functions while the body is at complete rest, in the post-absorptive state, and under thermoneutral conditions. 

Pregnancy increases BMR because of: 
• fetal growth, 
• placental metabolism, 
• expansion of maternal tissues, 
• increased cardiac and respiratory work, 
• elevated hormonal activity. 

BMR may increase by approximately 15–20% during pregnancy, particularly in the second and third trimesters.', '["Puberty decreases BMR","Fasting increases BMR","Pregnancy increases BMR","Females have higher BMR than males"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-07T11:43:54.778Z","interval":3,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":2}'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:43:54.778Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.942Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:43:54.778Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756358024433-9', NULL, 'anki-deck-1756357465257-1790432463920-rf85m', 'A mechanism by which Vitamin C acts as antioxidant to protect the body is by:
I. Reacts directly with peroxynitrite 
II. Reacts directly with hypochlorite-derived oxidants 
III. Scavenges reactive oxygen species such as superoxide and hydroxyl radicals', 'All of the above', 'Vitamin C (ascorbic acid) is a water-soluble antioxidant that protects cells from oxidative damage by donating electrons to neutralize reactive oxygen and nitrogen species. 

Its antioxidant actions include: 
• scavenging superoxide radicals, 
• scavenging hydroxyl radicals, 
• reacting with peroxynitrite, 
• neutralizing hypochlorite-derived oxidants generated during inflammatory responses, 
• regenerating oxidized vitamin E, thereby helping maintain membrane antioxidant defenses. 

These mechanisms contribute to the protection of lipids, proteins, DNA, and other cellular components from oxidative injury.', '["I only","II only","III only","All of the above"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-07T11:29:28.910Z","interval":3,"easeFactor":2.3,"repetitions":2}'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:29:28.910Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.942Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:29:28.910Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756358055766-10', NULL, 'anki-deck-1756357465257-1790432463920-rf85m', 'Which of the following are sources of acetyl-CoA?
I. β-oxidation of fatty acids 
II. Oxidative decarboxylation of pyruvate
III. Catabolism of ketogenic amino acids', 'All of the above', 'Acetyl-CoA is a central metabolic intermediate that links carbohydrate, lipid, and protein metabolism. It enters the tricarboxylic acid (TCA) cycle for ATP production or serves as a precursor for ketone bodies, cholesterol, and fatty acid synthesis. The major sources of acetyl-CoA include: 

I. β-Oxidation of fatty acids: Fatty acids undergo β-oxidation in the mitochondria, sequentially removing two-carbon fragments as acetyl-CoA. 
II. Oxidative decarboxylation of pyruvate: Pyruvate, the end product of glycolysis, is converted to acetyl-CoA by the pyruvate dehydrogenase complex before entering the TCA cycle. 
III. Ketogenic amino acids: The exclusively ketogenic amino acids leucine and lysine, as well as portions of isoleucine, phenylalanine, tryptophan, tyrosine, and threonine, are metabolized to acetyl-CoA or acetoacetate.', '["I and II","II and  III","I and III","All of the above"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-07T12:00:31.324Z","interval":3,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":2}'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T12:00:31.324Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.942Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T12:00:31.324Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756358078147-11', NULL, 'anki-deck-1756357465257-1790432463920-rf85m', 'Deficiency of this vitamin causes Wernicke-Korsakoff syndrome:', 'Vitamin B1', 'Wernicke–Korsakoff syndrome is a neurologic disorder resulting from severe thiamine deficiency. It is classically associated with chronic alcohol use disorder but may also occur in prolonged malnutrition, hyperemesis gravidarum, bariatric surgery, or conditions causing prolonged inadequate intake. 

The syndrome consists of two related conditions: 
Wernicke encephalopathy (acute): 
• Confusion 
• Ophthalmoplegia or nystagmus 
• Ataxia 

Korsakoff syndrome (chronic): 
• Severe memory impairment
• Confabulation 
• Irreversible cognitive deficits if untreated 

Thiamine functions as thiamine pyrophosphate (TPP), a coenzyme for pyruvate dehydrogenase, α-ketoglutarate dehydrogenase, and transketolase. Deficiency impairs cerebral energy metabolism, particularly in brain regions with high metabolic demands.', '["Vitamin A","Vitamin B1","Vitamin B3","Vitamin C"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-07T11:41:51.859Z","interval":3,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":2}'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:41:51.859Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.942Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:41:51.859Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756358125134-12', NULL, 'anki-deck-1756357465257-1790432463920-rf85m', 'Percent of iron left unabsorbed when iron-rich foods is taken with coffee or tea:', '50%', 'Tannins and polyphenols present in tea and coffee strongly chelate non-heme iron in the intestinal lumen, inhibiting iron absorption by approximately 50% to 60%.', '["30%","40%","50%","60%"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-07T11:55:58.902Z","interval":3,"easeFactor":2.1,"repetitions":2}'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:55:58.902Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.942Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:55:58.902Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756358145111-13', NULL, 'anki-deck-1756357465257-1790432463920-rf85m', 'This mineral potentiates the action of insulin, allowing glucose entry into cells, and facilitates insulin binding to cell surfaces.', 'Chromium', 'Chromium is an essential trace mineral involved in carbohydrate, lipid, and protein metabolism. It enhances the action of insulin by improving insulin receptor activity and intracellular signaling, thereby promoting glucose uptake by cells. Although chromium deficiency is uncommon, severe deficiency may lead to impaired glucose tolerance and insulin resistance, particularly in patients receiving long-term parenteral nutrition without adequate chromium supplementation.', '["Cobalt","Chromium","Molybdenum","Zinc"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-07T11:13:11.522Z","interval":3,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":2}'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:13:11.522Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.942Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:13:11.522Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756358183293-14', NULL, 'anki-deck-1756357465257-1790432463920-rf85m', 'Significant weight loss is defined as:
I. 5% over a month
II. >5% over 1 month
III. 7.5% over 3 months
IV. >7.5% over 3 months', 'I and III only', 'Under ASPEN / ESPEN clinical guidelines, Significant weight loss is defined as: 5% in 1 month, 7.5% in 3 months, or 10% in 6 months (Statements I and III). Severe weight loss exceeds these parameters.', '["I and III only","III and IV only","All of the above","None of the above"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-07T12:05:38.542Z","interval":3,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":2}'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T12:05:38.542Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.942Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T12:05:38.542Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756358220808-15', NULL, 'anki-deck-1756357465257-1790432463920-rf85m', 'All of the following are components of the body''s buffer systems that help prevent drastic changes in blood pH, EXCEPT:', 'Lipids', 'The human body maintains a tightly regulated blood pH of approximately 7.35–7.45 through several physiologic buffer systems that minimize sudden changes in hydrogen ion (H⁺) concentration. These buffers act immediately before respiratory and renal compensatory mechanisms take effect. 

The three major chemical buffer systems are: 
• Bicarbonate–carbonic acid buffer system – the principal extracellular buffer responsible for maintaining blood pH. 
• Protein buffer system – includes plasma proteins and intracellular proteins such as hemoglobin, which can bind or release hydrogen ions. 
• Phosphate buffer system – functions primarily within cells and in the renal tubules, where it helps regulate acid excretion. 

Lipids are not considered a physiologic buffer system. Although lipids have numerous biological functions—including energy storage, cell membrane structure, and signaling—they do not play a significant role in buffering hydrogen ions or maintaining acid-base balance.', '["Bicarbonate–carbonic acid buffer system","Protein buffer system","Lipids","Phosphate buffer system"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-07T11:35:02.002Z","interval":3,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":2}'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:35:02.002Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.942Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:35:02.002Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756358246606-16', NULL, 'anki-deck-1756357465257-1790432463920-rf85m', '____ stimulates sodium retention while ___ stimulates water retention.', 'Aldosterone, vasopressin', 'Aldosterone is a steroid hormone produced by the adrenal cortex. Its primary physiological role is to act on the kidneys (specifically the distal tubules and collecting ducts) to stimulate sodium retention (reabsorption). As it pulls sodium back into the blood, potassium is excreted.

Vasopressin, also known as Antidiuretic Hormone (ADH), is synthesized in the hypothalamus and released by the posterior pituitary. Its primary role is to insert aquaporins (water channels) into the collecting ducts of the kidneys to stimulate water retention (reabsorption) independently of sodium, helping to dilute blood osmolality.

• Renin is an enzyme (not a hormone) secreted by the kidneys that cleaves angiotensinogen into Angiotensin I. It triggers a cascade but does not directly retain sodium or water on its own.', '["Vasopressin, aldosterone","Aldosterone, vasopressin","Renin, aldosterone","Renin, angiotensin"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-07T12:02:44.145Z","interval":3,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":2}'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T12:02:44.145Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.942Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T12:02:44.145Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756358274204-17', NULL, 'anki-deck-1756357465257-1790432463920-rf85m', 'Consider the following statements about indoles:
Statement 1: Indoles are phytochemicals found in broccoli and other cruciferous vegetables that induce the production of detoxification enzymes, helping protect cells against carcinogens. 
Statement 2: Indoles may influence estrogen metabolism and modulate estrogen activity.', 'Both statements are correct.', '• Statement 1 is CORRECT: Indoles, such as indole-3-carbinol (I3C) and its metabolic derivative 3,3''-diindolylmethane (DIM), are potent sulfur-containing phytochemicals derived from the breakdown of glucosinolates found heavily in cruciferous vegetables like broccoli, cabbage, cauliflower, and Brussels sprouts. Biochemically, they stimulate and upregulate Phase I and Phase II biotransformation (detoxification) enzymes in the liver and gastrointestinal tract. This metabolic acceleration helps the body break down, neutralize, and safely eliminate carcinogens, xenobiotics, and cellular toxins before they can cause DNA damage.

• Statement 2 is CORRECT: Indoles are heavily researched for their distinct capacity to influence estrogen metabolism and modulate overall estrogen activity. They alter the pathways of hormone breakdown in the liver, effectively driving the conversion of strong, highly active estrogens into less potent, protective metabolites (favoring 2-hydroxyestrone over the more harmful 16-alpha-hydroxyestrone). By acting as natural estrogen modulators or blockers, dietary indoles provide systemic chemopreventive traits against hormone-dependent conditions, including breast and endometrial cancers.', '["Only statement 1 is correct.","Only statement 2 is correct.","Both statements are correct.","Both statements are incorrect."]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 3, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-12T12:03:47.963Z","interval":8,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":3}'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T12:03:47.963Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.942Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T12:03:47.963Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756358297222-18', NULL, 'anki-deck-1756357465257-1790432463920-rf85m', 'Phytochemicals found in whole grains that minds to minerals and prevents free radical formation.', 'Phytic acid', 'Phytic acid (also known as phytate) is a unique compound found in the bran and germ layer of whole grains, nuts, and legumes.

It is well-known for its ability to bind (chelate) to minerals like iron, zinc, calcium, and magnesium.

While this mineral-binding property often labels it as an "antinutrient" because it reduces mineral absorption, it also gives phytic acid a potent antioxidant function. By specifically binding to free iron, it suppresses iron-catalyzed oxidative reactions, thereby preventing the formation of harmful free radicals.', '["Phenols","Flavonoids","Phytic acid","Phytosterols"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-07T11:47:43.022Z","interval":3,"easeFactor":2.1,"repetitions":2}'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:47:43.022Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.942Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:47:43.022Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756358339102-19', NULL, 'anki-deck-1756357465257-1790432463920-rf85m', 'Vitamin losses increase due to the following conditions EXCEPT:', 'Cooking below the temperature of boiling water with excessive agitation', 'While boiling water (100°C) combined with excessive agitation drastically accelerates the leaching and thermal destruction of water-soluble vitamins (like Vitamin C and Thiamine), cooking below the boiling point (such as poaching or gentle simmering) exposes the food to less thermal stress. Even with agitation, keeping the temperature lower is a primary strategy used to preserve heat-sensitive nutrients compared to full boiling.

	• Increased exposure to high temperatures or sunshine: Heat and ultraviolet (UV) light rapidly degrade photosensitive and heat-labile vitamins (such as Riboflavin/B2 and Vitamin C).
	• Increased exposure to air: Oxygen triggers oxidative degradation, which quickly destroys atmospheric-sensitive vitamins like Vitamin C, A, and E.
	• Increased length of storage: Prolonged storage allows baseline chemical degradation and oxidation to occur over time, steadily reducing the total nutrient potency of the food.', '["Increased exposure to high temperatures or sunshine during storage","Increased length of storage","Increased exposure to air","Cooking below the temperature of boiling water with excessive agitation"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-07T11:42:34.346Z","interval":3,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":2}'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:42:34.346Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.942Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:42:34.346Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756358371332-20', NULL, 'anki-deck-1756357465257-1790432463920-rf85m', 'Which of the following is needed when the body experiences stress?', 'Growth hormone and insulin-like factors provision', 'Physiologic stress resulting from trauma, burns, major surgery, sepsis, severe infection, or critical illness activates a complex neuroendocrine response that promotes survival by mobilizing energy stores and maintaining vital organ function.

The stress response is characterized by increased secretion of several counter-regulatory hormones, including:
• Growth hormone (GH)
• Cortisol
• Catecholamines (epinephrine and norepinephrine)
• Glucagon

The growth hormone–insulin-like growth factor (GH–IGF) axis is also altered during stress. Although GH secretion generally increases, severe illness is often associated with GH resistance, resulting in reduced hepatic production of IGF-1 despite elevated GH concentrations.

These hormonal changes contribute to:
• Increased protein catabolism
• Increased gluconeogenesis
• Increased lipolysis
• Increased resting energy expenditure
• Mobilization of amino acids for immune function and tissue repair

Among the available options, Growth hormone and insulin-like factors is the best answer because it refers to the endocrine mediators involved in the physiologic stress response. It should be noted, however, that this question has a psychometric limitation. From a nutrition management perspective, the more appropriate answer would be adequate energy and increased protein intake, but this option is not provided.', '["Minimum calorie","Low protein","Fat-soluble vitamins and supplementation","Growth hormone and insulin-like factors provision"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-07T11:56:31.274Z","interval":3,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":2}'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:56:31.274Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.942Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:56:31.274Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756358394699-21', NULL, 'anki-deck-1756357465257-1790432463920-rf85m', 'Recommended high protein regimen for wound healing:', 'A and C', 'Adequate protein is essential for collagen synthesis, immune function, and tissue repair. Patients with severe wounds or extensive burns may require protein intakes as high as 2.0–3.0 g/kg/day, while protein may also account for approximately 20–25% of total energy intake depending on the clinical condition.', '["20-25% of TER","15-20% of TER","2.5-3 g/kg","A and C"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-07T11:55:45.404Z","interval":3,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":2}'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:55:45.404Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.942Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:55:45.404Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756358420564-22', NULL, 'anki-deck-1756357465257-1790432463920-rf85m', 'High cortisol stimulates:', 'Mobilization of free fatty acid', 'Cortisol is a major stress hormone that functions to provide energy substrates to the body during flight-or-fight situations or prolonged stress. It directly stimulates lipolysis—the breakdown of stored fats in adipose tissue—which results in the mobilization of free fatty acids into the bloodstream to be used as an alternative fuel source by peripheral tissues.

• Use of glucose except for the brain: Cortisol actually decreases or inhibits the peripheral utilization of glucose in tissues like muscle and fat. It does this precisely to save or spare glucose exclusively for the brain. Therefore, this statement is factually backwards; it decreases glucose use in peripheral tissues to safeguard it for the brain.
• Retention of water and sodium: Cortisol does exhibit weak mineralocorticoid activity at very high pathological levels (which can cause secondary sodium retention and water buildup), but its primary, direct metabolic action across basic physiology and nutrition textbooks is the mobilization of metabolic fuels, making free fatty acid mobilization the classical intended answer.', '["Mobilization of free fatty acid","Use of glucose except for the brain","Retention of water","Retention of sodium"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-22T14:50:07.305Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-21T14:50:07.305Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.942Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-21T14:50:07.305Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756358439765-23', NULL, 'anki-deck-1756357465257-1790432463920-rf85m', 'These nutrients are essential for wound healing EXCEPT:', 'Sodium', 'Arginine, glutamine, and vitamin C support collagen synthesis, immune function, and tissue repair. Sodium is important for fluid balance but is not considered a specific nutrient that enhances wound healing.', '["Glutamine","Arginine","Sodium","Vitamin C"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 3, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-12T11:51:18.118Z","interval":8,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":3}'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:51:18.118Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.942Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:51:18.118Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756358487962-24', NULL, 'anki-deck-1756357465257-1790432463920-rf85m', 'Which omega-3 fatty acid found in fish oil is primarily responsible for reducing the production of pro-inflammatory cytokines?', 'Eicosapentaenoic Acid (EPA)', 'EPA competes with arachidonic acid for cyclooxygenase and lipoxygenase enzymes, resulting in the production of less inflammatory eicosanoids. DHA also has anti-inflammatory effects but EPA is more directly associated with reducing inflammatory cytokine production.', '["DocosaHexaenoic Acid (DHA)","Eicosapentaenoic Acid (EPA)","Arachidonic Acid","All of the above"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-07T11:44:01.992Z","interval":3,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":2}'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:44:01.992Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.942Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:44:01.992Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756358507660-25', NULL, 'anki-deck-1756357465257-1790432463920-rf85m', 'Which mineral reduces intestinal absorption of fluoride by forming insoluble complexes?', 'Calcium', 'Calcium binds fluoride in the gastrointestinal tract, decreasing its absorption. This interaction is clinically relevant when fluoride supplements are prescribed.', '["Magnesium","Iron","Calcium","Manganese"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-07T11:32:55.010Z","interval":3,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":2}'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:32:55.010Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.942Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:32:55.010Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756358541523-26', NULL, 'anki-deck-1756357465257-1790432463920-rf85m', 'For cancer patients in chemotherapy treatment, the following medical nutrition therapy helps in the management of dysgeusia, EXCEPT:', 'Avoid drinking fluids with meals', 'Patients with dysgeusia benefit from good oral hygiene, mouth rinses, and sugar-free gum to stimulate saliva. Avoiding fluids during meals is generally recommended for early satiety or dumping syndrome, not for taste alterations.', '["Use of mouth rinse","Chewing sugar-free gums and mints","Avoid drinking fluids with meals","SFF"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-07T11:50:44.495Z","interval":3,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":2}'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:50:44.495Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.942Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:50:44.495Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756358564560-27', NULL, 'anki-deck-1756357465257-1790432463920-rf85m', 'Common side effect of gastric resection:', 'Dumping syndrome', 'Dumping syndrome is one of the most common complications following partial or total gastric resection, particularly after procedures such as Billroth I, Billroth II, Roux-en-Y reconstruction, and sleeve gastrectomy. It occurs because the stomach loses part of its reservoir function, allowing hyperosmolar food—especially those rich in simple carbohydrates—to empty rapidly into the small intestine. There are two recognized forms: 

• Early dumping syndrome (10–30 minutes after eating): Characterized by abdominal cramps, nausea, diarrhea, bloating, flushing, tachycardia, dizziness, and hypotension due to rapid fluid shifts from the bloodstream into the intestinal lumen. 
• Late dumping syndrome (1–3 hours after eating): Results from an exaggerated insulin response following rapid glucose absorption, leading to reactive hypoglycemia with symptoms such as sweating, weakness, tremors, hunger, and confusion. 

Medical nutrition therapy (MNT) is the primary treatment and includes: 
• Eating small, frequent meals 
• Limiting foods high in simple sugars 
• Increasing protein and moderate fat intake 
• Consuming soluble fiber when appropriate 
• Separating liquids from meals by about 30 minutes 
• Resting in a semi-recumbent position after meals in some patients 

Persistent symptoms that do not respond to dietary modification may require pharmacologic therapy (e.g., acarbose for late dumping syndrome or octreotide for severe cases).', '["Blind loop syndrome","Dumping syndrome","Dysphagia","Fistula formation"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-07T11:47:49.541Z","interval":3,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":2}'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:47:49.541Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.942Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:47:49.541Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756358586640-28', NULL, 'anki-deck-1756357465257-1790432463920-rf85m', 'Not allowed in tyramine-restricted diets:', 'Fava beans', 'Fava beans (broad beans) contain high amounts of L-dopa, which can metabolize into tyramine and other compounds that interact dangerously with Monoamine Oxidase Inhibitors (MAOIs). Additionally, the pods of broad beans are explicitly restricted on low-tyramine diets because they accumulate toxic levels of tyramine as they age.

When a patient taking an MAOI medication consumes high-tyramine foods, the tyramine cannot be safely broken down in the gut. It enters the bloodstream and causes a massive release of norepinephrine, leading to a life-threatening hypertensive crisis (a dangerous spike in blood pressure).

Other foods commonly restricted include aged cheeses, cured and fermented meats, yeast extracts, fermented soy products, sauerkraut, kimchi, certain alcoholic beverages (especially tap beers and some red wines), and improperly stored or spoiled protein-rich foods. Current recommendations emphasize that tyramine content varies with aging, fermentation, ripening, and storage conditions. Fresh foods are generally lower in tyramine than aged or fermented products.', '["Grapefruit juice","Fava beans","Simple sugars","Banana"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-07T11:37:45.816Z","interval":3,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":2}'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:37:45.816Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.942Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:37:45.816Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756358609457-29', NULL, 'anki-deck-1756357465257-1790432463920-rf85m', 'Phytochemicals found in berries, black tea, and citrus fruits that act as antioxidants and scavenge carcinogens.', 'Flavonoids', 'Flavonoids are a diverse class of polyphenolic plant compounds widely distributed in foods like berries, citrus fruits, and black or green tea.

They are heavily studied in nutrition and clinical biology for their powerful ability to act as direct antioxidants. They scavenge free radicals and carcinogens, alter cellular signaling pathways to trigger carcinogen excretion, and suppress abnormal cellular multiplication to reduce chronic disease risks.

• Phytic acid: Phytic acid is primarily found in the bran and germ layers of whole grains and legumes, where it acts as a mineral-chelating agent.
• Phenols: While flavonoids are structurally a subgroup of larger phenolic compounds, "Phenols" (misspelled as Phenlos in the option) is a broad umbrella category. Flavonoids is the more distinct, precise, and targeted textbook choice for this specific combination of food sources (berries, tea, and citrus).
• Phytosterols: These are plant-derived sterols structurally identical to cholesterol. Their primary therapeutic mechanism is blocking the absorption of dietary cholesterol in the small intestine, not acting as primary carcinogen scavengers.', '["Phenols","Flavonoids","Phytic acid","Phytosterols"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-07T11:31:14.158Z","interval":3,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":2}'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:31:14.158Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.942Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:31:14.158Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756358629971-30', NULL, 'anki-deck-1756357465257-1790432463920-rf85m', 'Galactosemia is an inherited disorder characterized by the inability to metabolize galactose due to deficiency of the enzyme galactose-1-phosphate uridylyltransferase (GALT). Which of the following is a characteristic manifestation of this condition?', 'Growth failure', 'Classic galactosemia is an autosomal recessive metabolic disorder caused by a deficiency in the GALT enzyme. This deficiency prevents the infant from safely breaking down galactose (a component of lactose found in breast milk and standard formulas).

Because the infant cannot convert galactose into glucose for energy, toxic metabolites like galactose-1-phosphate accumulate rapidly in tissues. This causes immediate feeding intolerance, severe vomiting, and systemic illness within days of birth. This inability to utilize nutrients manifests primarily and consistently as growth failure (failure to thrive).

Immediate elimination of lactose and galactose from the diet is essential. Starting this dietary intervention early is the cornerstone of treatment to reverse growth failure, resolve acute symptoms like jaundice, and prevent severe, life-threatening complications like E. coli sepsis.', '["Growth failure","Mental retardation","Apathy","Lack of pigmentation"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-07T11:42:01.952Z","interval":3,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":2}'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:42:01.952Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.942Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:42:01.952Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756358654438-31', NULL, 'anki-deck-1756357465257-1790432463920-rf85m', 'Which of the following are included in the nutrition management of peptic ulcer?', 'All of the above', 'The nutritional management of Peptic Ulcer Disease (PUD) heavily focuses on reducing foods and beverages that stimulate the overproduction of stomach acid, cause direct irritation to the gastric mucosa, or delay the healing process:

• Decrease consumption of coffee and caffeine: Both caffeinated and decaffeinated coffee trigger gastric acid secretion and can worsen upper gastrointestinal discomfort.
• Decrease consumption of spices: Irritating spices—especially black pepper, chili powder, and red pepper—can induce severe dyspemia (indigestion) and irritate the open lining of an existing gastric or duodenal ulcer.
• Decrease consumption of alcohol: Alcohol can directly damage the protective mucosal barrier of the stomach, exacerbate inflammation, and increase susceptibility to acid damage.', '["Decrease consumption of alcohol","Decrease consumption of spices","Decrease consumption of coffee and caffeine","All of the above"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-07T11:38:00.772Z","interval":3,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":2}'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:38:00.772Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.942Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:38:00.772Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756358681302-32', NULL, 'anki-deck-1756357465257-1790432463920-rf85m', 'A patient is receiving treatment for Crohn''s disease. Which food found on the patient''s food tray should the patient avoid?', 'Fresh salad', 'Crohn''s disease is a chronic inflammatory bowel disease (IBD) that can affect any part of the gastrointestinal tract. During an acute flare, patients often experience abdominal pain, diarrhea, weight loss, fatigue, and malabsorption. Nutrition therapy focuses on reducing gastrointestinal irritation, maintaining nutritional status, and preventing deficiencies. 

Fresh salad contains raw vegetables that are generally high in insoluble fiber, which can increase stool volume and intestinal motility. In patients with active Crohn''s disease—especially those with intestinal inflammation, strictures, or obstruction risk—raw vegetables may worsen abdominal pain, bloating, diarrhea, or cramping.', '["Fresh salad","White rice","Baked chicken","Cooked skinless apples"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-07T11:51:14.201Z","interval":3,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":2}'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:51:14.201Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.942Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:51:14.201Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756358702535-33', NULL, 'anki-deck-1756357465257-1790432463920-rf85m', 'Products that contain live microorganisms in sufficient numbers to alter the intestinal microbiota and promote microbial balance are known as:', 'Probiotics', 'Probiotics are live microorganisms that, when administered in adequate amounts, confer a health benefit on the host. Common probiotic organisms include species of Lactobacillus, Bifidobacterium, Saccharomyces boulardii, and certain strains of Bacillus.', '["Antibiotics","Probiotics","Fruits and vegetables","Digestive enzymes"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-07T11:51:28.670Z","interval":3,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":2}'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:51:28.670Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.942Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:51:28.670Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756358746082-34', NULL, 'anki-deck-1756357465257-1790432463920-rf85m', 'Which of the following is true about megaloblastic anemia?
I. It may be clinically manifested by neuropathy.
II. Vitamin E is required in its dietary management.', 'Only statement 1 is correct.', 'Megaloblastic anemia is primarily caused by a deficiency in Vitamin B12 (cobalamin) or Vitamin B9 (folate). When it stems from a Vitamin B12 deficiency, it uniquely presents with neurological complications, such as peripheral neuropathy (tingling, numbness, or a "pins and needles" sensation in the hands and feet), loss of vibratory sense, and gait imbalances. This occurs because Vitamin B12 is mandatory for maintaining the structural integrity of the myelin sheath that insulates nerves.

• Statement 2 is INCORRECT: Vitamin E plays no direct physiological role in the development or dietary management of megaloblastic anemia. Vitamin E deficiency causes hemolytic anemia (where fragile red blood cells prematurely rupture due to oxidative damage to their cell membranes), not megaloblastic anemia. The dietary management of megaloblastic anemia strictly requires therapeutic supplementation of Vitamin B12 or Folic Acid.', '["Only statement 1 is correct.","Only statement 2 is correct.","Both statements are correct.","Both statements are incorrect."]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-07T11:40:36.453Z","interval":3,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":2}'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:40:36.453Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.942Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:40:36.453Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756358773066-35', NULL, 'anki-deck-1756357465257-1790432463920-rf85m', 'Which of the following is/are nutritional effects of chemotherapy?
I. Dysgeusia
II. Diarrhea and constipation
III. Esophagitis with dysphagia', 'All of the above', 'Chemotherapy drugs target rapidly dividing cells throughout the body. Because the cells lining the entire gastrointestinal (GI) tract and the taste buds regenerate quickly, they are highly vulnerable to chemotherapy, leading to several significant nutritional side effects:

• Dysgeusia: Chemotherapy frequently damages the taste receptor cells in the mouth, causing an altered or metallic taste. This severely diminishes appetite and food enjoyment.
• Diarrhea and constipation: Chemotherapy can disrupt normal bowel motility and damage the intestinal mucosal lining, leading to severe diarrhea. Conversely, the use of anti-nausea medications (antiemetics) given alongside chemotherapy often causes severe constipation.
• Esophagitis with dysphagia: The treatment commonly induces mucositis (inflammation and ulceration of the mucous membranes) along the esophagus. This painful swelling (esophagitis) directly results in dysphagia (difficulty swallowing), making oral food intake very challenging.', '["I only","I and III only","All of the above","None of the above"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-07T11:55:22.407Z","interval":3,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":2}'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:55:22.407Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.942Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:55:22.407Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756358805597-36', NULL, 'anki-deck-1756357465257-1790432463920-rf85m', 'In copper-deficiency anemia, iron can not be released because:', 'Ceruloplasmin is lacking', 'Copper deficiency is an uncommon but recognized cause of anemia. Copper is an essential trace mineral required for the activity of several enzymes involved in iron metabolism, hematopoiesis, and cellular respiration. One of the most important copper-containing enzymes is ceruloplasmin, a ferroxidase that oxidizes ferrous iron (Fe²⁺) to ferric iron (Fe³⁺). This conversion is necessary for iron to bind to transferrin, the principal iron transport protein in plasma. 

When copper is deficient: 
• Ceruloplasmin activity decreases. 
• Iron cannot be efficiently mobilized from storage sites such as the liver and macrophages. 
• Iron transport to the bone marrow is impaired. 
• Hemoglobin synthesis becomes inadequate despite normal or even increased body iron stores. 

As a result, patients may develop microcytic, normocytic, or occasionally macrocytic anemia, often accompanied by neutropenia. Copper deficiency should be suspected in patients with unexplained anemia who have undergone bariatric surgery, have prolonged parenteral nutrition without adequate copper supplementation, malabsorptive disorders, or excessive zinc intake, which interferes with copper absorption.', '["Protein is lacking","Vitamin B6 is lacking","Clotting factors are lacking","Ceruloplasmin is lacking"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 3, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-12T11:37:41.035Z","interval":8,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":3}'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:37:41.035Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.942Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:37:41.035Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756358828080-37', NULL, 'anki-deck-1756357465257-1790432463920-rf85m', 'It describes the bone after the developmental period is completed and often used for bone monitoring in adults:', 'Bone mineral density', 'Bone mineral density (BMD) is the absolute "gold standard" metric used in clinical settings to monitor adult skeletal health over time. It measures the amount of mineral matter (like calcium) per square centimeter of bone tissue via diagnostic tools such as Dual-Energy X-ray Absorptiometry (DEXA scans).

While bones stop growing in length and reach their ultimate structural ceiling at the end of the growth period, the tracking of a patient''s current bone status throughout mature adulthood focuses heavily on their BMD values to screen for conditions like osteopenia and osteoporosis.

• Peak Bone Mass: Peak Bone Mass refers exclusively to the maximum amount of bone tissue an individual can physically accumulate by the end of their developmental phase (typically around ages 20 to 30). Because it describes a specific, static historical milestone rather than a dynamic continuous variable, it is not what is tracked during Routine adult bone monitoring.
• Bone mineral content (BMC) measures total mineral weight rather than concentration/density. In standard diagnostic jargon, Bone mass is a conceptual term often used interchangeably with BMD, but BMD is the official diagnostic metric quantified for patient monitoring.', '["Bone mineral content","Bone mineral density","Peak Bone Mass","Bone mass"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-07T11:41:39.631Z","interval":3,"easeFactor":2.3,"repetitions":2}'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:41:39.631Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.942Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:41:39.631Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756358851944-38', NULL, 'anki-deck-1756357465257-1790432463920-rf85m', 'The vertebrae and bones of the jaw are classified as ____ because of their shapes:', 'Irregular bones', 'Irregular bones are defined by their complex, highly specialized shapes that do not cleanly fit into standard geometric categories like long, short, or flat. They typically feature a mix of ridges, notches, and projections.

Each vertebra in the spine consists of a thick vertebral body, an arch, and multiple bony projections (such as the spinous and transverse processes) that serve as crucial muscle attachment points and shield the spinal cord.

The jawbones are part of the facial skeleton. The mandible (lower jaw) and maxilla (upper jaw) feature a highly irregular, irregular layout tailored to house tooth sockets, support facial muscles, and form parts of the nasal and oral cavities.

• Flat bones: These are thin, flattened, and usually slightly curved bones designed for extensive muscle attachment or organ protection (e.g., the ribs, sternum, and most cranial skull bones). While some older literatures loosely lump the flat portions of the jaw with flat bones, modern structural taxonomy officially classifies the facial bones—including the mandible—as irregular.
• Short bones: These are roughly cube-shaped, having an approximately equal length, width, and thickness to provide stability and support (e.g., the carpals in the wrist and tarsals in the ankle).
• Long bones: These are elongated, cylindrical shafts that are noticeably longer than they are wide, acting as levers to facilitate body movement (e.g., the femur, humerus, and phalanges).', '["Long bones","Short bones","Flat bones","Irregular bones"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-07T11:51:33.845Z","interval":3,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":2}'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:51:33.845Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.942Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:51:33.845Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756358883675-39', NULL, 'anki-deck-1756357465257-1790432463920-rf85m', 'Wilson’s disease is the inability of the body to metabolize _____, while hemochromatosis is a disorder in _____ metabolism.', 'Copper, iron', 'Wilson''s Disease (Copper Accumulation): This is an autosomal recessive genetic disorder caused by a mutation in the ATP7B gene. This mutation disrupts the liver''s ability to transport and excrete excess copper into bile. As a result, toxic levels of copper build up primarily in the liver, brain, and the corneas of the eyes (forming classic golden-brown Kayser-Fleischer rings).

Hemochromatosis (Iron Overload): This metabolic disorder is characterized by a failure in iron-sensing mechanisms (often due to mutations in the HFE gene), which leads to the uncontrolled, excessive absorption of dietary iron from the gastrointestinal tract. The excess iron gets deposited heavily into vital organs such as the liver, heart, and pancreas, which can cause cirrhosis, cardiac arrhythmias, and diabetes (traditionally termed "bronze diabetes").', '["Iron, copper","Calcium, iron","Copper, iron","Iron, calcium"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-07T11:45:17.656Z","interval":3,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":2}'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:45:17.656Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.942Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:45:17.656Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756358909108-40', NULL, 'anki-deck-1756357465257-1790432463920-rf85m', 'Dietary management for Parkinson’s Disease
I. Adjust food consistency to facilitate chewing and swallowing; ensure adequate fiber and fluid intake; limit dietary protein at breakfast and lunch in patients receiving levodopa therapy when indicated; avoid foods or supplements containing large amounts of L-dopa.
II. Adjust food consistency according to tolerance; provide nutrient-dense foods; and offer small, frequent meals.', 'All of the above', 'Both statements accurately outline essential, standard clinical protocols for managing a patient with Parkinson''s Disease (PD):

	• Dysphagia & Constipation: As PD progresses, motor degradation often leads to chewing and swallowing difficulties (dysphagia), requiring textured food or modified consistencies. Autonomic dysfunction also slows down the GI tract, making a high-fiber and high-fluid regimen critical to preventing chronic constipation.
	• The Protein-Levodopa Interaction: Levodopa (the primary medication for PD) utilizes the same active transport carriers across the blood-brain barrier as dietary amino acids. Limiting or shifting protein intake away from breakfast and lunch prevents dietary protein from competing with the medication, ensuring the drug maintains its clinical efficacy throughout the day.
	• Exogenous L-dopa: Avoiding foods/supplements with variable amounts of natural L-dopa (like fava beans) prevents unpredictable fluctuations or sudden toxicity.

	• Malnutrition & Early Satiety: Due to tremors, high metabolic demands, and slow chewing, PD patients are exceptionally prone to unintentional weight loss and muscle wasting. Offering small, frequent, and nutrient-dense meals tailored to their structural texture tolerance optimizes daily caloric intake without overwhelming the patient or causing early fatigue during meals.', '["I only","II only","None of the above","All of the above"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-07T11:52:54.232Z","interval":3,"easeFactor":2.1,"repetitions":2}'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:52:54.232Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.942Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:52:54.232Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756358929525-41', NULL, 'anki-deck-1756357465257-1790432463920-rf85m', 'It is also known as Lou Gehrig disease:', 'Amyotrophic lateral sclerosis', 'Amyotrophic lateral sclerosis (ALS), also known as Lou Gehrig''s disease, is a progressive neurodegenerative disorder characterized by the degeneration of upper and lower motor neurons. The disease leads to progressive muscle weakness, muscle wasting, spasticity, dysarthria, dysphagia, and eventually respiratory failure. Although cognition is preserved in many patients, some develop frontotemporal cognitive impairment or dementia.

From a nutrition perspective, ALS is associated with:
• Progressive dysphagia, increasing the risk of aspiration.
• Weight loss due to reduced oral intake and increased energy expenditure.
• Loss of muscle mass and protein-energy malnutrition.
• Increased risk of dehydration.

Medical nutrition therapy aims to:
• Maintain body weight and preserve lean body mass.
• Provide high-energy, high-protein meals when appropriate.
• Modify food texture and liquid consistency according to swallowing ability.
• Recommend oral nutrition supplements when intake is inadequate.
• Consider enteral nutrition (e.g., gastrostomy feeding) when oral intake becomes unsafe or insufficient.

Early nutrition intervention is associated with improved nutritional status and may contribute to a better quality of life.', '["Alzheimer''s disease","Amyotrophic lateral sclerosis","Cerebral palsy","Parkinson''s Disease"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 3, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-12T11:56:45.572Z","interval":8,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":3}'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:56:45.572Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.942Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:56:45.572Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756358957273-42', NULL, 'anki-deck-1756357465257-1790432463920-rf85m', 'The following are general dietary management for fevers and infections, EXCEPT:', 'Low fat', 'During fever and infection, the body enters a hypermetabolic and catabolic state, resulting in increased energy expenditure, accelerated protein breakdown, and increased fluid losses. Nutrition therapy aims to support immune function, preserve lean body mass, promote recovery, and prevent malnutrition. 

A low-fat diet is not routinely recommended for patients with fever or infection. Dietary fat is an important source of concentrated energy, particularly when appetite is poor. Fat restriction is indicated only in specific clinical conditions such as pancreatitis, fat malabsorption, or certain hepatobiliary disorders—not as part of the general management of fever and infection.', '["High Kcal","High Protein","High fluids","Low fat"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-07T11:55:52.122Z","interval":3,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":2}'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:55:52.122Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.942Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:55:52.122Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756358980389-43', NULL, 'anki-deck-1756357465257-1790432463920-rf85m', 'An immunologic disease of the joints and related structures', 'Rheumatoid Arthritis', 'Rheumatoid Arthritis (RA) is a chronic, systemic autoimmune (immunologic) disorder. In RA, the body''s immune system mistakenly attacks the synovium—the thin membrane that lines the joints. This leads to severe, painful inflammation, cartilage destruction, and bone erosion.

	• Osteoporosis: This is a metabolic bone disease characterized by a loss of bone density and increased fragility, typically caused by hormonal changes, aging, or nutritional deficiencies rather than an immune system attack.
	• Sjögren’s syndrome: While this is an immunologic/autoimmune disease, its primary targets are the moisture-producing glands (such as salivary and tear glands), leading to severe dry eyes and dry mouth. Although it can be associated with joint pain, the primary immunologic joint disease listed is RA.
	• Gout: This is a metabolic form of inflammatory arthritis caused by the accumulation and deposition of uric acid (monosodium urate) crystals in the joints, not an immunologic or autoimmune malfunction.', '["Rheumatoid Arthritis","Gout","Sjogren’s syndrome","Osteoporosis"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-22T13:46:29.867Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-21T13:46:29.867Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.942Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-21T13:46:29.867Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756358998839-44', NULL, 'anki-deck-1756357465257-1790432463920-rf85m', 'Which of the following is NOT a general dietary recommendation for patients with HIV/AIDS?', 'Fiber-restricted', 'Nutrition therapy is an essential component of the management of HIV/AIDS, helping maintain body weight, preserve lean body mass, support immune function, and improve quality of life. Energy and protein requirements are often increased because of chronic infection, inflammation, opportunistic infections, and the increased metabolic demands associated with HIV. 

General nutrition recommendations include: 
• High-calorie diet to prevent or manage unintended weight loss. 
• High-protein diet to preserve muscle mass and promote tissue repair. 
• Adequate intake of vitamins, minerals, and fluids. 
• Food safety practices to reduce the risk of foodborne illness. 
• Nutrition interventions tailored to treatment-related side effects such as nausea, diarrhea, or oral lesions.

Dietary fiber provides important benefits, including promoting bowel health, improving glycemic control, and supporting a healthy gut microbiota. Fiber restriction is reserved for specific clinical situations, such as severe diarrhea, intestinal obstruction, or acute gastrointestinal complications, and is not recommended as a standard dietary approach for all patients with HIV/AIDS.', '["High calorie","High protein","Fat-controlled","Fiber-restricted"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-07T11:36:50.055Z","interval":3,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":2}'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:36:50.055Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.942Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:36:50.055Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756359023804-45', NULL, 'anki-deck-1756357465257-1790432463920-rf85m', 'The following are dietary recommendations for Dengue fever, EXCEPT:', 'Fat-restricted', 'Nutrition management is an important component of supportive care for dengue fever, particularly because patients commonly experience fever, anorexia, nausea, vomiting, abdominal pain, fatigue, and dehydration. The goals of nutrition therapy are to maintain hydration, provide adequate energy and protein, and support recovery. 

General dietary recommendations include: 
• High-calorie diet to meet the increased energy demands associated with acute infection and fever. 
• High-protein diet to support tissue repair and preserve lean body mass, especially in patients with poor oral intake. 
• Adjusted food consistency according to tolerance, such as soft or liquid diets for patients with nausea, vomiting, sore throat, or poor appetite. 
• Adequate oral fluids (or intravenous fluids when indicated) to prevent or treat dehydration, which is a major concern in dengue. 

There is no routine recommendation to restrict dietary fat in patients with dengue fever. Fat restriction is reserved for specific gastrointestinal or hepatobiliary conditions and is not part of standard nutrition management for uncomplicated dengue infection.', '["High calorie","High protein","Adjusted food consistency depending on tolerance","Fat-restricted"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-07T11:31:21.080Z","interval":3,"easeFactor":2.3,"repetitions":2}'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:31:21.080Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.942Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:31:21.080Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756359051804-46', NULL, 'anki-deck-1756357465257-1790432463920-rf85m', 'Which of the following is NOT a source of data for nutrition assessment?', 'None of the above', 'All of the options listed are actually valid and standard sources of data used by dietitians and healthcare professionals when performing a thorough Nutrition Assessment:

• Family history: Part of the Client History (CH) domain. It provides vital background on hereditary risks for chronic metabolic conditions like diabetes, cardiovascular diseases, or inborn errors of metabolism.
• Medical record: This serves as a primary hub for objective data including patient demographics, medical history, clinical diagnoses, surgical histories, and Biochemical Data/Medical Tests (such as lab panels or imaging).
• Referral form: A referral from a physician or another healthcare provider frequently contains initial clinical data, anthropometric measurements, the explicit medical reason for nutrition intervention, and immediate dietary constraints.', '["Family history","Medical record","Referral form","None of the above"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-07T11:52:59.607Z","interval":3,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":2}'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:52:59.607Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.942Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:52:59.607Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756359083584-47', NULL, 'anki-deck-1756357465257-1790432463920-rf85m', 'Severe weight loss is defined as:
I. 5% over a month
II. >5% over 1 month
III. 7.5% over 3 months
IV. >7.5% over 3 months', 'II and IV only', 'Severe weight loss is diagnosed when unintentional weight loss exceeds significant thresholds: >5% in 1 month, >7.5% in 3 months, or >10% in 6 months (Statements II and IV).', '["I and III only","II and IV only","All of the above","None of the above"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-07T11:53:59.214Z","interval":3,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":2}'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:53:59.214Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.942Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:53:59.214Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756359114848-48', NULL, 'anki-deck-1756357465257-1790432463920-rf85m', 'Dietary management for children with severe acute malnutrition during nutritional rehabilitation:
I. Aim to provide 100–200 kcal/kg actual body weight per day, depending on the phase of treatment and clinical condition. 
II. Provide approximately 120–150 mL fluid/kg actual body weight per day, with adjustments based on hydration status and clinical judgment. 
III. Use food items that promote early satiety.', 'I and II only', 'Children with severe acute malnutrition (SAM) require carefully planned nutrition therapy that restores nutritional status while minimizing complications such as refeeding syndrome, heart failure, and electrolyte imbalance. Management follows a phased approach consisting of stabilization, transition, and rehabilitation. 

Statement I is correct. During the rehabilitation phase, children require high-energy diets to achieve catch-up growth. Energy requirements generally range from 100 to 200 kcal/kg/day, depending on the stage of treatment, clinical status, and feeding regimen. Therapeutic foods such as F-100 or Ready-to-Use Therapeutic Food (RUTF) are commonly used during rehabilitation. 

Statement II is correct. Fluid management is equally important because children with severe malnutrition have altered fluid and electrolyte balance. Total fluid intake commonly ranges from 120–150 mL/kg/day, but it should always be individualized according to hydration status, ongoing losses, cardiac function, and the phase of treatment. Excessive fluid administration should be avoided because malnourished children are susceptible to fluid overload. 

Statement III is incorrect. The goal of nutrition therapy is to maximize nutrient and energy intake, not to promote early satiety. Foods that cause early fullness may reduce overall caloric intake and delay nutritional recovery. Instead, children should receive energy-dense, nutrient-dense foods in frequent, small feedings that are well tolerated.', '["I only","II only","I and II only","All of the above"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 3, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-12T11:31:48.081Z","interval":8,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":3}'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:31:48.081Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.942Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:31:48.081Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756359141665-49', NULL, 'anki-deck-1756357465257-1790432463920-rf85m', 'The following are characteristics of ketogenic diet, EXCEPT:', 'Provides all the vitamins and minerals needed', 'A classic ketogenic diet is highly restrictive because it eliminates major food groups like most fruits, starchy vegetables, whole grains, and dairy products. Because of this extreme restriction, the diet is notoriously deficient in essential micronutrients (such as calcium, vitamin D, selenium, zinc, and water-soluble B vitamins). Patients on a clinical ketogenic diet must take targeted, sugar-free vitamin and mineral supplements to avoid severe deficiencies.', '["Planned diet provides adequate calories","Contains 1-2 grams of protein per kilogram body weight","Provides ketogenic ration of 3-4 grams fat for every 1 gram of carbohydrate and protein","Provides all the vitamins and minerals needed"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-07T11:39:17.852Z","interval":3,"easeFactor":2.3,"repetitions":2}'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:39:17.852Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.942Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:39:17.852Z"'::jsonb)
