('anki-card-1756359163332-50', NULL, 'anki-deck-1756357465257-1790432463920-rf85m', 'Dietary management for pancreatitis:', 'NPO, enzyme replacement, low fat, avoidance of stimulants', 'Pancreatitis is inflammation of the pancreas and may be classified as acute or chronic. Although both conditions require nutrition management, their dietary recommendations differ according to disease stage and severity. 

During acute pancreatitis, patients with moderate to severe disease may initially require bowel rest if oral feeding is not tolerated because of severe abdominal pain, nausea, vomiting, or ileus. However, current guidelines recommend early oral or enteral feeding once clinically tolerated, as prolonged fasting is no longer routinely advised. In chronic pancreatitis, nutrition therapy aims to prevent malnutrition, reduce pancreatic stimulation, and improve nutrient absorption.

• NPO (Nil Per Os / Nothing by Mouth): During acute episodes or severe flare-ups of pancreatitis, bowel rest (keeping the patient NPO) is primary to minimize pancreatic stimulation and reduce auto-digestion of the organ.
• Enzyme Replacement: For patients with exocrine pancreatic insufficiency (EPI) or chronic pancreatitis, taking Pancreatic Enzyme Replacement Therapy (PERT) with meals is necessary to facilitate proper macronutrient digestion and nutrient absorption.
• Low Fat: Fat is a potent stimulant of pancreatic enzyme secretion. Restricting fat intake (often between 30–50 grams per day) and spreading it across small, frequent meals helps alleviate abdominal pain and prevent fatty stools (steatorrhea).
• Avoidance of Stimulants: Patients must strictly avoid pancreatic irritants and stimulants, most notably alcohol and nicotine, as they directly exacerbate inflammation and disease progression.', '["High kcal, low protein, high carbohydrate, high fiber","NPO, enzyme replacement, low fat, avoidance of stimulants","High kcal, high protein, low fat","Liquid diet, iron supplementation, low fiber"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-07T11:44:39.032Z","interval":3,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":2}'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:44:39.032Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.942Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:44:39.032Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756359185779-51', NULL, 'anki-deck-1756357465257-1790432463920-rf85m', 'It refers to the accumulation of fluids in the abdominal cavity:', 'Ascites', 'Ascites specifically refers to the abnormal accumulation of excess fluid within the peritoneal (abdominal) cavity. It is most commonly driven by severe liver diseases like cirrhosis, where high blood pressure in the liver''s veins (portal hypertension) forces fluid to leak into the abdomen.

	• Esophageal varices: These are enlarged, abnormal veins in the lower part of the esophagus that develop due to obstructed blood flow through the liver. While they share a common cause with ascites (portal hypertension), they are blood vessels, not fluid accumulation.
	• Collateral formation: This is the biological process where the body grows alternative blood pathways (collateral vessels) to detour around a blocked or high-pressure vein.
	• Edema: This is a broad, generic medical term for swelling caused by fluid trapped in any body tissue. While ascites is technically a localized type of edema, the term edema on its own usually refers to generalized swelling in the limbs (like ankle or leg edema) rather than the abdominal space.', '["Esophageal varices","Ascites","Edema","Collateral formation"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-22T13:58:36.080Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-21T13:58:36.080Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.942Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-21T13:58:36.080Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756359204412-52', NULL, 'anki-deck-1756357465257-1790432463920-rf85m', 'Dietary recommendation for Biliary dyskinesia caused by increased contraction of the gallbladder:', 'Low fat', 'Biliary dyskinesia is a functional disorder of the gallbladder characterized by abnormal gallbladder motility, resulting in impaired bile flow and biliary-type pain in the absence of gallstones or structural abnormalities. 

In some patients, symptoms are associated with exaggerated gallbladder contraction, particularly after consuming fatty meals. Dietary fat stimulates the release of cholecystokinin (CCK) from the duodenum. CCK causes the gallbladder to contract and release bile into the small intestine. In patients with biliary dyskinesia, especially those whose symptoms are triggered by gallbladder contraction, high-fat meals can exacerbate pain and biliary colic. 

Therefore, a low-fat diet is commonly recommended to: 
• Reduce gallbladder stimulation 
• Minimize biliary pain after meals 
• Improve tolerance to oral intake 
• Decrease the frequency of symptomatic episodes 

Although a low-fat diet helps manage symptoms, it should still provide adequate essential fatty acids and fat-soluble vitamins, and prolonged severe fat restriction is generally not recommended unless clinically indicated.', '["Low fat","High fat","Low protein","High protein"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-06T02:50:15.579Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.3,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-11-05T02:50:15.579Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.942Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-05T02:50:15.579Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756359226144-53', NULL, 'anki-deck-1756357465257-1790432463920-rf85m', 'Protein recommendation for wound healing?', '20-25% of TER', 'Adequate protein is essential for collagen synthesis, immune function, and tissue repair. Patients with severe wounds or extensive burns may require protein intakes as high as 2.0–3.0 g/kg/day, while protein may also account for approximately 20–25% of total energy intake depending on the clinical condition.', '["20-25% of TER","1.5 – 2 g/kg","15-20% of TER","50 % of TER"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-06T02:47:56.974Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.3,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-11-05T02:47:56.974Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.942Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-05T02:47:56.974Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756359253793-54', NULL, 'anki-deck-1756357465257-1790432463920-rf85m', 'Dietary management for typhoid fever:
 I. High-calorie, high-protein, high-fluid, and low-fiber diet 
II. High-calorie, high-protein diet with sodium and fluid restriction, plus vitamin and mineral supplementation 
III. High-calorie, high-protein, fat-restricted diet', 'I only', 'Typhoid fever is a systemic bacterial infection caused by Salmonella enterica serovar Typhi. Patients commonly experience prolonged fever, anorexia, abdominal discomfort, diarrhea or constipation, and generalized weakness, all of which may contribute to poor nutritional intake and increased metabolic demands. 

Routine sodium and fluid restriction is not recommended in typhoid fever. Instead, maintaining adequate hydration is an important component of treatment. Fluid restriction would only be indicated in unrelated conditions such as heart failure or severe renal disease. 

Routine fat restriction is also not recommended for uncomplicated typhoid fever. Fat intake may be modified based on individual tolerance, but there is no evidence-based recommendation to prescribe a fat-restricted diet solely because of typhoid fever.', '["I only","II only","III only","All of the above"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-23T09:38:31.662Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.3,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-22T09:38:31.662Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.942Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-22T09:38:31.662Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756359272525-55', NULL, 'anki-deck-1756357465257-1790432463920-rf85m', 'Aggressive nutrition support is required for burned patients with:', 'Burns exceeding 20 % of the body surface area', 'Major burn injuries trigger one of the most intense hypermetabolic and hypercatabolic responses known in clinical medicine. This metabolic surge significantly accelerates protein breakdown and energy expenditure. Clinical guidelines (such as ESPEN and ASPEN) establish that burns exceeding 20% of the Total Body Surface Area (TBSA) represent critical, severe trauma that demands immediate, aggressive nutrition support (often via early enteral nutrition) to prevent severe muscle wasting, support wound healing, and minimize infection risks.

• 10% TBSA: Minor burns involving 10% or less of the body surface area generally do not induce a systemic, high-grade hypermetabolic crisis. Most of these patients can meet their elevated nutritional needs through a high-calorie, high-protein oral diet without requiring aggressive specialized nutritional support interventions (like tube feeding or parenteral nutrition).
• Diarrhea: While diarrhea is a clinical concern that causes fluid and electrolyte losses, it is a gastrointestinal symptom rather than an automatic primary indicator for aggressive nutrition support. In fact, if a burn patient develops severe diarrhea, enteral tube feeding rates might need to be adjusted or formulas changed rather than aggressively increased.', '["Burns exceeding 20 % of the body surface area","Burns with 10 % of the body surface area","Diarrhea","All of the above"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 3, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-12T11:45:10.222Z","interval":8,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":3}'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:45:10.222Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.942Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:45:10.222Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756359297941-56', NULL, 'anki-deck-1756357465257-1790432463920-rf85m', 'Drug that may cause nausea and vomiting', 'Digitalis', 'Nausea and vomiting are the classic, earliest gastrointestinal hallmarks of digitalis toxicity (e.g., from digoxin). Digitalis works by inhibiting the Na⁺/K⁺ ATPase pump to treat heart failure and arrhythmias, but it has a very narrow therapeutic window.

When digitalis levels become elevated in the blood, the drug directly stimulates the chemoreceptor trigger zone (CTZ) in the medulla oblongata of the brainstem, which triggers a powerful urge to vomit. It is also frequently accompanied by neurological symptoms like anorexia, confusion, and classic visual disturbances (such as a yellow-green halo or blurred vision).

	• Antacids: Are primarily used to neutralize gastric acid and help alleviate nausea associated with indigestion or GERD rather than causing it.
	• Laxatives: Typically cause abdominal cramping, bloating, and diarrhea rather than upper GI symptoms like vomiting.
	• Amphetamines: While stimulants can occasionally cause nausea due to sympathetic nervous system activation, it is not a hallmark warning sign of toxicity or a key diagnostic indicator in the way it is uniquely tied to digitalis management in medical examinations.', '["Antacid","Laxative","Digitalis","Amphetamines"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 3, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-12T11:35:24.590Z","interval":8,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":3}'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:35:24.590Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.942Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:35:24.590Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756359315840-57', NULL, 'anki-deck-1756357465257-1790432463920-rf85m', 'May decrease folate levels:', 'Aspirin', 'In the bloodstream, aspirin and its active metabolite (salicylic acid) bind heavily to plasma proteins. This presence competitively displaces folate from its primary transport proteins, leading to an increased clearance and higher urinary excretion of unbound folate.

Research indicates that aspirin down-regulates Dihydrofolate Reductase (DHFR), a critical enzyme required to convert dietary folate into its biologically active form. Additionally, salicylic acid acts as a low-affinity inhibitor of the Reduced Folate Carrier-1 (RFC-1), which actively transports active folates into tissues and across the blood-brain barrier.

Because of this dual-action interference—accelerating the excretion of circulating folate while simultaneously hindering its activation and cellular uptake—chronic or high-dose aspirin users face a documented clinical risk of impaired folate status and depletion.', '["Aspirin","Antacid","Laxative","Warfarin"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-06T02:46:56.153Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.1,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-11-05T02:46:56.153Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.942Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-05T02:46:56.153Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756359441517-58', NULL, 'anki-deck-1756357465257-1790432463920-rf85m', 'Nutrition intervention for patient with inborn error of metabolism if there is accumulation of precursor to toxic levels:', 'Restrict source of precursor', 'Inborn errors of metabolism (IEMs) are inherited genetic disorders caused by deficiencies or dysfunction of enzymes involved in metabolic pathways. When an enzyme is absent or has markedly reduced activity, the substrate (or metabolic precursor) cannot be adequately metabolized, resulting in its accumulation to toxic concentrations while the downstream product may become deficient. 

A cornerstone of nutrition management is restricting the dietary source of the accumulating precursor to prevent toxicity while still providing sufficient nutrients for normal growth and development. Examples include: 
• Phenylketonuria (PKU): Restrict phenylalanine 
• Galactosemia: Eliminate galactose and lactose 
• Maple syrup urine disease (MSUD): Restrict branched-chain amino acids 
• Hereditary fructose intolerance: Eliminate fructose, sucrose, and sorbitol 

Because many precursor nutrients are essential, dietary restriction should be carefully individualized and supervised by a metabolic dietitian to prevent deficiency while maintaining metabolic control.', '["Increase the amount of substrate","Avoid exposure","Restrict source of precursor","All of the above"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-07T12:02:58.600Z","interval":3,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":2}'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T12:02:58.600Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.942Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T12:02:58.600Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756359466117-59', NULL, 'anki-deck-1756357465257-1790432463920-rf85m', 'The following are characteristics of marasmus, EXCEPT:', 'Minimal problem in brain development', 'Marasmus is a severe form of protein-energy malnutrition (PEM) resulting primarily from chronic deficiency of both energy and protein. It is characterized by marked wasting of adipose tissue and skeletal muscle, leading to severe weight loss and failure to thrive. Typical clinical features include: 

• Severe wasting ("skin and bones" appearance) 
• Marked loss of subcutaneous fat 
• Muscle wasting 
• Growth retardation 
• Poor weight gain 
• Irritability or apathy 
• Increased susceptibility to infection

Among the choices, minimal problem in brain development is the exception because brain development is not minimally affected.', '["Low disease resistance","Occurs most commonly in children aged 6-18 months","Minimal problem in brain development","Results in little or no fat under the skin to insulate against cold"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-07T11:51:52.370Z","interval":3,"easeFactor":2.3,"repetitions":2}'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:51:52.370Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.942Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:51:52.370Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756359490165-60', NULL, 'anki-deck-1756357465257-1790432463920-rf85m', 'Which of the following is a correctly written Nutrition Diagnosis statement using the Nutrition Care Process (PES format)?
I. Inadequate energy intake related to chewing difficulty as evidenced by energy intake meeting only 55% of estimated requirements. 
II. Inadequate energy intake due to decreased appetite that leads to weight loss.', 'I only', 'Statement I correctly adheres to the standard Nutrition Care Process (NCP) PES format: Problem (Inadequate energy intake) related to Etiology (chewing difficulty) as evidenced by Signs/Symptoms (energy intake meeting only 55% of requirements). Statement II lacks standard diagnostic syntax.', '["I only","II only","Both I and II","None of the above"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-07T11:49:03.480Z","interval":3,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":2}'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:49:03.480Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.942Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:49:03.480Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756359511097-61', NULL, 'anki-deck-1756357465257-1790432463920-rf85m', 'A 32-year-old woman (Ms. BMA) wants to determine her target heart rate for moderate- to vigorous-intensity aerobic exercise. Based on the age-predicted maximum heart rate method, what is her target heart rate range?', '122-160 beats', 'A commonly used method for estimating maximum heart rate (HRmax) is: HRmax = 220 − age 

For a 32-year-old: 
• HRmax = 220 − 32 = 188 beats/min 

The recommended target heart rate (THR) for moderate- to vigorous-intensity exercise is approximately 65–85% of HRmax. 

Calculation: 
• Lower limit: 188 × 0.65 = 122 beats/min 
• Upper limit: 188 × 0.85 = 160 beats/min 

Therefore, the target heart rate range is: 122–160 beats/min. Although newer equations such as Tanaka''s formula (208 − 0.7 × age) may estimate HRmax more accurately in some populations, the 220 − age equation remains the formula most commonly used in basic exercise prescription and is frequently tested in licensure examinations.', '["122-160 beats","150-160 beats","138-200 beats","162-202 beats"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-07T11:40:20.786Z","interval":3,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":2}'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:40:20.786Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.942Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:40:20.786Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756359531580-62', NULL, 'anki-deck-1756357465257-1790432463920-rf85m', 'Osmolality of body fluids.', '290 mOsm/kg', 'Osmolality is the concentration of osmotically active particles per kilogram of solvent (water) and is expressed as milliosmoles per kilogram (mOsm/kg). It reflects the body''s water balance and is tightly regulated to maintain normal cellular function. 

The normal plasma osmolality is approximately: 
• 275–295 mOsm/kg, with an average value of 290 mOsm/kg', '["290 mOsm/kg","400 mOsm/kg","480 mOsm/kg","200 mOsm/kg"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-07T11:36:40.049Z","interval":3,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":2}'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:36:40.049Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.942Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:36:40.049Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756359553746-63', NULL, 'anki-deck-1756357465257-1790432463920-rf85m', 'Which of the following statement/s is/are true in describing carbohydrates in EN?', 'All of the above', 'Carbohydrates are the primary source of energy in most enteral nutrition (EN) formulas, typically providing 40–60% of total calories. The type of carbohydrate used depends on the formula''s intended purpose, desired osmolality, digestibility, and patient tolerance. Common carbohydrate sources include: 
• Corn syrup solids 
• Maltodextrin 
• Hydrolyzed cornstarch 
• Modified starches 

These ingredients are preferred because they: 
• Are easily digested and absorbed 
• Contribute relatively low osmolality compared with simple sugars 
• Provide a readily available energy source 
• Produce desirable physical properties in the formula 

Some oral nutritional supplements (ONS) and oral enteral formulas may also contain sucrose or other simple sugars to improve taste and increase patient acceptance. However, sucrose is generally used less frequently in tube-feeding formulas because complex carbohydrates are usually better tolerated and produce lower osmolality.', '["Usually corn syrup solids","Hydrolyed formulas from cornstarch or maltodextrin","Sucrose added in formulas meant for oral consumption","All of the above"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-07T12:01:09.454Z","interval":3,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":2}'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T12:01:09.454Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.942Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T12:01:09.454Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756359576028-64', NULL, 'anki-deck-1756357465257-1790432463920-rf85m', 'Not included in the list of complications to watch out in EN.', 'None of the above', 'In clinical dietetics and nutrition support, enteral nutrition (EN) complications are broadly classified into three main categories: mechanical, gastrointestinal (GI), and metabolic.

• Aspiration, tube displacement, or tube occlusion (Mechanical Complications): Aspiration of formula into the lungs is the most critical and life-threatening complication of EN, potentially leading to aspiration pneumonia. Accidental tube dislodgement/displacement and tube clogging (occlusion) from medication or viscous formulas are highly common mechanical issues that require routine tracking and preventative flushing protocols.

• Diarrhea or constipation (Gastrointestinal Complications): GI intolerance is exceptionally frequent during tube feeding. Diarrhea can stem from high osmolarity formulas, rapid administration rates, bacterial contamination, or medications (like sorbitol). Constipation can occur due to inadequate fluid or fiber delivery, or low patient mobility.

• Dehydration (Metabolic Complications): Dehydration is a recognized metabolic issue that can manifest if a patient is given high-protein or hyperosmolar enteral formulas without adequate free water flushes to compensate for the solute load, or if they are losing excessive fluid through unchecked diarrhea or vomiting.', '["Aspiration, tube displacement, or tube occlusion (clogging)","Diarrhea or constipation","Dehydration","None of the above"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-07T11:45:26.741Z","interval":3,"easeFactor":2.3,"repetitions":2}'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:45:26.741Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.942Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:45:26.741Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756359596927-65', NULL, 'anki-deck-1756357465257-1790432463920-rf85m', 'Which of the following foods are considered high biologic value?', 'Fish and chicken leg', 'High biological value (HBV) proteins, also known as complete proteins, contain all nine essential amino acids in sufficient amounts to support normal growth, tissue repair, enzyme and hormone synthesis, and maintenance of body tissues. They are also generally highly digestible and efficiently utilized by the body. 

Most animal-derived foods are considered HBV protein sources, including: 
• Fish 
• Chicken 
• Beef 
• Pork 
• Eggs 
• Milk and dairy products 

Since both fish and chicken provide complete, high-quality protein, this is the correct answer.', '["Tofu and chicken leg","Fish and chicken leg","Fish and beans","Chicken and cured ham"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-07T12:03:26.501Z","interval":3,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":2}'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T12:03:26.501Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.942Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T12:03:26.501Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756359620593-66', NULL, 'anki-deck-1756357465257-1790432463920-rf85m', 'For an adult patient with chronic kidney disease (CKD) and an estimated glomerular filtration rate (eGFR) of <25 mL/min/1.73 m² who is not receiving dialysis, what is the recommended dietary protein intake?', '0.6 to 0.7 g/kg/day', 'In patients with advanced chronic kidney disease (CKD) who are not on dialysis, dietary protein restriction is recommended to reduce the accumulation of nitrogenous waste products, decrease uremic symptoms, and potentially slow the progression of kidney disease. 

The 2020 KDOQI Clinical Practice Guideline for Nutrition in CKD recommends: 
• 0.55–0.60 g/kg ideal body weight/day for metabolically stable adults with CKD stages 3–5 who are not on dialysis. 
• A slightly higher intake (0.6–0.8 g/kg/day) may be appropriate in selected patients, particularly those with diabetes, older adults, or individuals at risk of protein-energy wasting. 

Therefore, among the choices provided, 0.6–0.7 g/kg/day is the best answer.', '["0.6 to 0.7 g/kg/day","0.28 g/kg/day with EAA supplementation","0 to 1.2 g/kg/day with dialysis","None of the above"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-07T11:43:19.064Z","interval":3,"easeFactor":2.1,"repetitions":2}'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:43:19.064Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.942Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:43:19.064Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756359638175-67', NULL, 'anki-deck-1756357465257-1790432463920-rf85m', 'Which of the following may suppress the appetite?', 'Amphetamines', 'Amphetamines are central nervous system (CNS) stimulants that increase the release and inhibit the reuptake of dopamine and norepinephrine in the brain. These neurotransmitters act on the hypothalamus, reducing hunger signals and increasing satiety. 

Because of this mechanism, amphetamines produce appetite suppression, which can lead to: 
• Reduced food intake 
• Weight loss 
• Poor nutritional status with prolonged use 
• Growth impairment in children receiving long-term stimulant therapy', '["Amphetamines","Phenobarbital","Digitalis","Methotrexate"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-07T11:52:31.106Z","interval":3,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":2}'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:52:31.106Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.942Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:52:31.106Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756359656640-68', NULL, 'anki-deck-1756357465257-1790432463920-rf85m', 'Compute for the GFR of a male patient, 65 years old, stands 5’5 and dry weight of 48 kg. Serum creatinine is 5.0 mg/dL.', '18.5 mL/min', 'Using the Cockcroft-Gault / CKD-EPI equation for renal function evaluation, a 65-year-old male with dry weight 48 kg and serum creatinine 5.0 mg/dL has an estimated GFR of approximately 18.5 mL/min, placing the patient in CKD Stage 4 (severely decreased).', '["18.5 mL/min","20.5 mL/min","16.5 mL/min","30.5 mL/min"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-07T12:02:16.073Z","interval":3,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":2}'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T12:02:16.073Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.942Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T12:02:16.073Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756359687641-69', NULL, 'anki-deck-1756357465257-1790432463920-rf85m', 'What is the protein requirement for a male, 65 years old, who stands 5’5" and has a dry weight of 48 kg? His serum creatinine is 5.0 mg/dL.', '0.28 g/kg/day with EAA supplementation', 'Step-by-Step Rationale

1. Assess Kidney Function (GFR):
Using the standard Cockcroft-Gault equation to estimate the Glomerular Filtration Rate (GFR) based on the client''s metrics:

GFR = [(140 - Age) x Weight (kg)] / [72 x Serum Creatinine]
GFR = [(140 - 65) x 48] / [72 x 5.0] = [75 x 48] / 360 = 10.0 mL/min

A GFR of 10.0 mL/min confirms the patient is in Stage 5 Chronic Kidney Disease (CKD) / End-Stage Renal Disease (ESRD) and is currently in the pre-dialysis phase.

2. Determine Protein Requirements:
For a patient with advanced renal failure who is not yet on active dialysis, minimizing nitrogenous waste build-up while preventing lean muscle wasting is critical. Clinical guidelines specify a Very Low-Protein Diet (VLPD) targeting 0.28 g/kg/day, which must be supplemented with Essential Amino Acids (EAA) or ketoacid analogs to fulfill basic physiological needs.', '["0.6 to 0.7 g/kg/day","0.28 g/kg/day with EAA supplementation","0 to 1.2 g/kg/day with dialysis","None of the above"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 3, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-11T11:45:48.398Z","interval":7,"easeFactor":2.3,"repetitions":3}'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:45:48.398Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.942Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:45:48.398Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756359703538-70', NULL, 'anki-deck-1756357465257-1790432463920-rf85m', 'Milk substitute given for patient with phenylketonuria', 'Phenex-1', 'Phenylketonuria (PKU) is an autosomal recessive inborn error of metabolism caused by deficiency of the enzyme phenylalanine hydroxylase (PAH). As a result, phenylalanine cannot be adequately converted to tyrosine, leading to the accumulation of phenylalanine in the blood and brain. 

Phenex-1 is one such formula designed specifically for infants with PKU, providing essential amino acids (except phenylalanine), vitamins, minerals, and energy to support normal growth and development.', '["Lonalac","Phenex-1","Soyalac","Probana"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-07T11:29:46.976Z","interval":3,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":2}'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:29:46.976Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.942Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:29:46.976Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756359729439-71', NULL, 'anki-deck-1756357465257-1790432463920-rf85m', 'Which of the following statement summarizes the NCP?', 'NCP is intended to establish a standardized process and provide consistency', 'The Nutrition Care Process (NCP) is a systematic, evidence-based framework developed by the Academy of Nutrition and Dietetics to guide registered nutritionist-dietitians in delivering high-quality, individualized nutrition care. 

The primary purpose of the NCP is to: 
• Establish a standardized approach to nutrition care 
• Improve consistency among practitioners 
• Enhance communication among healthcare professionals 
• Promote evidence-based decision-making 
• Improve patient outcomes', '["NCP is a systematic method intended to standardize nutrition care for clients","NCP is intended to establish a standardized process and provide consistency","NCP sequence is linear with a definitive start and stop not requiring reassessment","NCP consists of five steps, is non-linear and is considered continual and ongoing"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-07T11:56:40.688Z","interval":3,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":2}'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:56:40.688Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.942Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:56:40.688Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756359766585-72', NULL, 'anki-deck-1756357465257-1790432463920-rf85m', 'Which of the following is a correctly written PES (Problem–Etiology–Signs/Symptoms) statement for a nutrition diagnosis?', 'Inadequate energy intake related to decreased appetite secondary to chronic COPD as evidenced by consuming less than 75% of estimated energy needs for 1 month and unintentional weight loss of 6% in one month.', 'A properly structured PES statement connects the specific nutrition problem to an etiology using "related to" and substantiates it with objective clinical signs and symptoms using "as evidenced by".', '["Inadequate energy intake related to decreased appetite secondary to chronic COPD as evidenced by consuming less than 75% of estimated energy needs for 1 month and unintentional weight loss of 6% in one month.","Inadequate energy intake related to consuming less than 75% of estimated energy needs for 1 month and unintentional weight loss of 6% in one month as evidenced by decreased appetite secondary to chronic COPD.","Decreased appetite secondary to chronic COPD related to inadequate energy intake as evidenced by consuming less than 75% of estimated energy needs for 1 month and unintentional weight loss of 6% in one month.","Consuming less than 75% of estimated energy needs for 1 month and unintentional weight loss of 6% in one month."]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 3, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-12T11:42:15.804Z","interval":8,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":3}'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:42:15.804Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.942Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:42:15.804Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756359784085-73', NULL, 'anki-deck-1756357465257-1790432463920-rf85m', 'Water losses of _______ of total body weight can be fatal.', '9-12%', 'Water is the largest component of the human body, accounting for approximately 50–60% of body weight in adults, although this varies with age, sex, and body composition. 

Maintaining fluid balance is essential for normal physiologic functions, including: 
• Regulation of body temperature 
• Transport of nutrients and waste products 
• Maintenance of blood volume and blood pressure 
• Cellular metabolism 
• Electrolyte balance 

The severity of dehydration is often expressed as the percentage of body weight lost due to water deficit. Therefore, water losses of approximately 9–12% of body weight can become life-threatening and may be fatal if not rapidly corrected.', '["2-3%","3%","9-12%","5%"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-07T11:47:23.632Z","interval":3,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":2}'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:47:23.632Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.942Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:47:23.632Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756359819634-74', NULL, 'anki-deck-1756357465257-1790432463920-rf85m', 'Which is not true about enzymes?
I. Chemical substances from the endocrine glands that can stimulate reactions in the body
II. Primarily protein catalysts of biochemical reactions (with some RNA molecules acting as catalytic ribozymes)
III. Facilitate the making and breaking of chemical bonds during biochemical reactions
IV. Identified by the place they come from and the compounds they work on', 'I only', 'Enzymes are biological catalysts that accelerate biochemical reactions by lowering the activation energy required for a reaction to occur. They are essential for virtually all metabolic processes, including digestion, energy production, DNA synthesis, and cellular repair. Most enzymes are proteins, although a small number of RNA molecules called ribozymes also possess catalytic activity. 

Statement I is incorrect because it actually describes hormones, not enzymes. 

Hormones are: 
• Chemical messengers 
• Secreted by endocrine glands 
• Transported through the bloodstream 
• Responsible for regulating physiologic processes 

In contrast, enzymes: 
• Catalyze chemical reactions 
• Are not secreted primarily for signaling 
• Are generally specific to particular substrates 
• Remain unchanged after the reaction', '["I only","II only","III only","None of the above"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-07T11:36:36.617Z","interval":3,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":2}'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:36:36.617Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.942Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:36:36.617Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756359843048-75', NULL, 'anki-deck-1756357465257-1790432463920-rf85m', 'Easy to digest snack food – they are rich in CHO but generally low in protein, fiber, and many other nutrients found in a well-balanced diet.', 'Energy bars', 'In sports nutrition and dietetics review frameworks, conventional energy bars are primarily designed to deliver quick, easily accessible fuel to working muscles. They are heavily packed with simple and refined carbohydrates (CHO) to provide a rapid blood glucose spike before or during endurance performance.

Unlike meal replacements or protein bars, traditional energy bars are kept purposefully low in protein, dietary fiber, and healthy fats. Fiber and protein naturally slow down gastric emptying and delay digestion. By keeping these nutrients minimal, the bar functions as an easy-to-digest snack that leaves the stomach rapidly, providing fast energy without causing heavy gastrointestinal distress, cramping, or bloating during physical exertion.', '["Protein shakes","Whey protein","High-quality protein","Energy bars"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 3, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-12T11:35:07.565Z","interval":8,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":3}'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:35:07.565Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.942Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:35:07.565Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756359859217-76', NULL, 'anki-deck-1756357465257-1790432463920-rf85m', 'Designed to maximize post-workout refueling of muscle glycogen; promote greater recovery of muscle energy and muscle protein resynthesis than carbohydrate drinks.', 'Recovery drinks', 'Recovery drinks are specifically formulated with a specific carbohydrate-to-protein ratio (typically 3:1 or 4:1). The targeted dose of fast-acting carbohydrates quickly replenishes depleted muscle glycogen stores after intense exercise.

Research shows that adding protein to a post-workout carbohydrate drink stimulates greater glycogen resynthesis than carbohydrates alone, as the combination triggers a more robust insulin response (which drives glucose into muscle cells).

While traditional sports drinks only provide carbohydrates and electrolytes to maintain hydration and energy during exercise, recovery drinks deliver essential amino acids (from protein) to repair muscle tissue damage and kickstart protein synthesis after exercise.', '["Sports drinks","Recovery drinks","Protein shakes","Energy bars"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-07T11:52:00.938Z","interval":3,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":2}'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:52:00.938Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.942Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:52:00.938Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756359891081-77', NULL, 'anki-deck-1756357465257-1790432463920-rf85m', 'Which of the following is NOT a recommended dietary practice for marathon or other endurance athletes?', 'Consume a high-protein, low-carbohydrate diet.', 'Endurance events such as marathons rely heavily on carbohydrates as the primary fuel source, particularly during moderate- to high-intensity exercise. Adequate carbohydrate intake helps maintain blood glucose levels, replenish muscle glycogen stores, delay fatigue, and improve endurance performance. Consequently, dietary strategies that severely restrict carbohydrates are generally not recommended for marathon runners. Although protein is essential for muscle repair, recovery, and adaptation to training, consuming excessive protein at the expense of carbohydrates may impair glycogen storage and reduce exercise performance. 

Current sports nutrition guidelines recommend that endurance athletes consume sufficient carbohydrates based on their training load, moderate amounts of protein to support recovery, and healthy fats to meet energy needs and maintain overall health. For the NDLE, remember that nutrition recommendations for endurance athletes emphasize adequate carbohydrate availability, not high-protein or carbohydrate-restricted diets. While individualized nutrition plans are increasingly common, carbohydrates remain the cornerstone of endurance performance.', '["Consume a high-protein, low-carbohydrate diet.","High-protein diets with minimal carbohydrate intake are not recommended for endurance athletes.","Follow a balanced diet consisting of approximately 15–20% protein, 30% fat, and 50–55% carbohydrates.","Provide adequate carbohydrates to fuel training and long-distance running days."]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 3, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-11T11:55:15.177Z","interval":7,"easeFactor":2.3,"repetitions":3}'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:55:15.177Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.942Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:55:15.177Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756359912762-78', NULL, 'anki-deck-1756357465257-1790432463920-rf85m', 'Which of the following is true for nutritional management of esophageal obstruction?', 'Liquid diets', 'Esophageal obstruction is characterized by partial or complete blockage of the esophagus, leading to dysphagia (difficulty swallowing) and impaired passage of food from the mouth to the stomach. Common causes include esophageal cancer, benign strictures, esophageal webs, achalasia, or foreign body obstruction. The primary goal of nutrition therapy is to maintain adequate nutritional intake while minimizing swallowing difficulty and the risk of aspiration. 

Depending on the severity of the obstruction, patients may require a clear liquid diet, full liquid diet, or texture-modified diet. When oral intake becomes inadequate or unsafe, enteral nutrition via a feeding tube (e.g., gastrostomy or jejunostomy) may be necessary.', '["Liquid diets","Low fat diet","Low calorie","High protein"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-06T02:46:38.892Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.3,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-11-05T02:46:38.892Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.942Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-05T02:46:38.892Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756359933978-79', NULL, 'anki-deck-1756357465257-1790432463920-rf85m', 'Which of the following is TRUE on the effects of certain nutrients on drug efficacy?', 'All of the above', 'Food and nutrients can significantly influence the absorption, distribution, metabolism, and excretion (ADME) of medications, thereby affecting their efficacy and safety. These interactions may enhance or reduce the therapeutic effects of drugs and, in some cases, increase the risk of adverse reactions. Several mechanisms explain nutrient–drug interactions: 

• Alteration of gastric acidity. Certain foods and nutrients can modify gastric pH, influencing the dissolution and absorption of orally administered medications. Drugs that require an acidic environment, for example, may be less effectively absorbed when gastric pH is increased. 
• Changes in urine pH. Diets rich in certain foods can acidify or alkalinize the urine, affecting the renal excretion of weak acids and weak bases. Consequently, the duration and concentration of some drugs in the body may change. 
• Competition for absorption or transport sites. Some nutrients compete with drugs for intestinal transport mechanisms or form insoluble complexes that reduce drug absorption. A classic example is calcium binding with tetracycline or fluoroquinolone antibiotics, significantly decreasing their absorption. 

For the NDLE, nutrient–drug interactions are commonly tested because they directly influence nutrition care planning, patient counseling, and medication administration. Nutritionist-dietitians should recognize common interactions and provide appropriate dietary advice to optimize therapeutic outcomes.', '["Nutrients from food that change stomach acidity causes alteration in orally administered drugs","Nutrients may alter urine pH which leads to drug excretion","Nutrients may compete with the absorptive sites","All of the above"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-07T11:54:35.562Z","interval":3,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":2}'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:54:35.562Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.942Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:54:35.562Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756359950945-80', NULL, 'anki-deck-1756357465257-1790432463920-rf85m', 'How much ml per feeding will be given to a patient who is ordered to consume 1.8 liters of tube
feeding with 2:1 dilution every 6 hours?', '450 ml', '1.8 liters (1800 mL) administered every 6 hours corresponds to 4 feedings daily (24 hours / 6 hours = 4). 1800 mL divided by 4 feedings equals 450 mL per feeding.', '["625 ml","450 ml","750 ml","225 ml"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-07T11:46:52.215Z","interval":3,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":2}'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:46:52.215Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.942Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:46:52.215Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756359971776-81', NULL, 'anki-deck-1756357465257-1790432463920-rf85m', 'All of the following are true of the basal energy expenditure (BEE) except', 'Loss of muscle in old age which may lead to decreased BEE is inevitable', 'The decrease in muscle mass (sarcopenia) and the resulting drop in BEE during old age is not inevitable. Regular resistance training, proper physical activity, and a targeted protein intake can preserve, build, or slow down the loss of lean muscle mass as a person ages, maintaining a higher baseline metabolism.

• It is highest in the first years of life: Size-adjusted BEE peaks sharply during infancy (up to around 15 months of age), running roughly 50 percent higher than adult metabolic values due to rapid growth and development.
• Men have higher BEE than women: Lean body mass is a principal driver of metabolism. Because men generally possess a higher percentage of lean muscle mass and less body fat than women of an equal age and surface area, their baseline BEE is naturally higher.
• It is measured in the fasted state: True clinical measurement of BEE requires the subject to be at absolute rest in a comfortable room temperature after a 12-hour fast to eliminate the thermal effects of digestion.', '["It is highest in the first years of life","It is measured in the fasted state","Loss of muscle in old age which may lead to decreased BEE is inevitable","Men have higher BEE than women"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-07T11:34:55.032Z","interval":3,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":2}'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:34:55.032Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.942Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:34:55.032Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756359990260-82', NULL, 'anki-deck-1756357465257-1790432463920-rf85m', 'All of the following are scanning laboratory techniques used to measure body composition EXCEPT:', 'Bioelectrical Impedance Analysis (BIA)', 'Several imaging or scanning techniques are available to assess body composition by quantifying fat mass, lean body mass, bone mineral content, and, in some cases, regional fat distribution. Computed Tomography (CT), Dual-Energy X-ray Absorptiometry (DXA), and Magnetic Resonance Imaging (MRI) are considered scanning or imaging techniques because they generate anatomical images that can accurately distinguish between different body tissues. 

In contrast, Bioelectrical Impedance Analysis (BIA) is not a scanning technique. Instead, it estimates body composition by passing a low-level electrical current through the body and measuring electrical impedance. Since lean tissue contains more water and electrolytes than fat tissue, it conducts electricity more readily.', '["Bioelectrical Impedance Analysis (BIA)","Computed Tomography (CT)","Dual-Energy X-ray Absorptiometry (DXA)","Magnetic Resonance Imaging (MRI)"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-07T11:55:37.673Z","interval":3,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":2}'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:55:37.673Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.942Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:55:37.673Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756360033540-83', NULL, 'anki-deck-1756357465257-1790432463920-rf85m', 'A 55-year-old Filipino male laboratory technician diagnosed with Type 2 Diabetes Mellitus visited the Nutrition Clinic for counseling. His anthropometric measurements are as follows: height = 5''7" (170 cm), weight = 75 kg, and waist circumference = 36 inches. His laboratory results revealed the following: HbA1c = 9%; total cholesterol = 250 mg/dL; LDL cholesterol = 300 mg/dL; and uric acid = 9 mg/dL. He also reported joint pain, lower back pain, and swelling of the joints. Based on the WHO BMI classification, what is the patient''s nutritional status?', 'Overweight', 'Body Mass Index (BMI) is a simple anthropometric index used to classify the nutritional status of adults. It is calculated using the formula: BMI = Weight (kg) ÷ Height (m)² 

For this patient: 
• Height = 170 cm = 1.70 m 
• Weight = 75 kg 
BMI = 75 ÷ (1.70)² = 75 ÷ 2.89 = 25.95 kg/m² 

Using the World Health Organization (WHO) international BMI classification, a BMI of 25.95 kg/m² falls under Overweight (25.0–29.9 kg/m²).', '["Normal","Overweight","Obese","Morbidly obese"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-23T07:02:41.243Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-22T07:02:41.243Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.942Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-22T07:02:41.590Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756360059738-84', NULL, 'anki-deck-1756357465257-1790432463920-rf85m', 'The Registered Dietitian Nutritionist (RDN) is reviewing a patient''s understanding of and adherence to a prescribed consistent carbohydrate diet. Three months later, the patient''s hemoglobin A1c (HbA1c) is reassessed, and progress toward the target range is evaluated. This scenario describes which step of the Nutrition Care Process (NCP)?', 'Nutrition Monitoring and Evaluation', 'In this scenario, the RDN is reviewing the patient''s adherence to the prescribed nutrition intervention and reassessing HbA1c after three months to determine whether the nutrition goals have been achieved. These activities are characteristic of Nutrition Monitoring and Evaluation, which involves measuring and comparing outcomes against established nutrition goals and determining whether modifications to the nutrition care plan are necessary. 

Hemoglobin A1c is an important monitoring indicator because it reflects the patient''s average blood glucose over the previous 2–3 months. Therefore, reassessing HbA1c after three months is clinically appropriate to evaluate the effectiveness of a consistent carbohydrate meal plan and the patient''s adherence to nutrition recommendations.', '["Nutrition Assessment","Nutrition Diagnosis","Nutrition Intervention","Nutrition Monitoring and Evaluation"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 3, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-12T11:36:57.236Z","interval":8,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":3}'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:36:57.236Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.942Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:36:57.236Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756360079504-85', NULL, 'anki-deck-1756357465257-1790432463920-rf85m', 'A 55-year-old Filipino male laboratory technician diagnosed with Type 2 Diabetes Mellitus visited the Nutrition Clinic for counseling. His anthropometric measurements are as follows: height = 5''7" (170 cm), weight = 75 kg, and waist circumference = 36 inches. His laboratory results revealed the following: HbA1c = 9%; total cholesterol = 250 mg/dL; LDL cholesterol = 300 mg/dL; and uric acid = 9 mg/dL. He also reported joint pain, lower back pain, and swelling of the joints. The patient''s HbA1c result is indicative of ______ glucose control.', 'Poor', 'Hemoglobin A1c (HbA1c), also known as glycated hemoglobin, reflects the average blood glucose concentration over the previous 2–3 months, corresponding to the lifespan of red blood cells. It is one of the most important laboratory parameters used to assess long-term glycemic control in patients with diabetes mellitus. An HbA1c of 9% indicates poor glycemic control, suggesting that blood glucose levels have remained persistently elevated over the past several months. 

For most nonpregnant adults with diabetes, the American Diabetes Association (ADA) recommends an HbA1c target of <7%, although individualized goals may be appropriate depending on age, comorbidities, risk of hypoglycemia, and life expectancy.', '["Good","Fair","Moderate","Poor"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-07T11:52:15.199Z","interval":3,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":2}'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:52:15.199Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.942Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:52:15.199Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756360097120-86', NULL, 'anki-deck-1756357465257-1790432463920-rf85m', 'A 55-year-old Filipino male laboratory technician diagnosed with Type 2 Diabetes Mellitus visited the Nutrition Clinic for counseling. His anthropometric measurements are as follows: height = 5''7" (170 cm), weight = 75 kg, and waist circumference = 36 inches. His laboratory results revealed the following: HbA1c = 9%; total cholesterol = 250 mg/dL; LDL cholesterol = 300 mg/dL; and uric acid = 9 mg/dL. He also reported joint pain, lower back pain, and swelling of the joints. Aside from prescribing a consistent carbohydrate diet, the dietitian advised him to increase his dietary fiber intake. What is the recommended daily fiber intake for this patient?', '35 grams', 'Dietary fiber is an important component of Medical Nutrition Therapy (MNT) for individuals with Type 2 Diabetes Mellitus. Increasing fiber intake has been shown to improve glycemic control, promote satiety, reduce postprandial blood glucose excursions, improve bowel function, and contribute to lowering total and LDL cholesterol levels. These benefits are particularly important for this patient, who has poor glycemic control (HbA1c = 9%) and dyslipidemia. 

Current evidence recommends that adults with diabetes consume at least 14 g of dietary fiber per 1,000 kcal, which generally translates to approximately 25–38 g/day, depending on age, sex, and energy requirements. For adult men, a daily intake of about 30–38 g/day is generally recommended.', '["35 grams","45 grams","55 grams","65 grams"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-07T11:10:45.347Z","interval":3,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":2}'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:10:45.347Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.942Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:10:45.347Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756360130769-87', NULL, 'anki-deck-1756357465257-1790432463920-rf85m', 'A 32-year-old nurse was referred to the Nutrition Clinic for nutrition counseling. She is currently in her second trimester of pregnancy and was diagnosed with Gestational Diabetes Mellitus (GDM) based on elevated Oral Glucose Tolerance Test (OGTT) and Fasting Blood Glucose (FBG) results. Her medical history revealed a family history of diabetes mellitus (father with Type 2 Diabetes Mellitus), a pre-pregnancy BMI of 32 kg/m², and Asian ethnicity. Which of the following are the non-modifiable risk factors for GDM present in this patient? 
I. Age
II. Family history of diabetes mellitus
III. Pre-pregnancy BMI
IV. Race', 'I, II and IV only', 'Gestational Diabetes Mellitus (GDM) is glucose intolerance first recognized during pregnancy. Several maternal characteristics increase the risk of developing GDM and are classified as either modifiable or non-modifiable. In this patient: 
• Age (32 years) – Although she is younger than the commonly cited threshold of ≥35 years, maternal age itself is considered a non-modifiable risk factor because it cannot be changed. Increasing maternal age is associated with a higher risk of GDM. 
• Family history of diabetes mellitus – A first-degree relative with Type 2 Diabetes Mellitus significantly increases the risk because of genetic predisposition. This is a non-modifiable risk factor. 
• Race/Ethnicity (Asian descent) – Women of Asian ancestry have a higher prevalence of GDM than some other populations, making ethnicity another non-modifiable risk factor. 

• Pre-pregnancy BMI of 32 kg/m² – This represents obesity, which is a well-established risk factor for GDM. However, it is considered modifiable, as body weight can be improved before pregnancy through appropriate lifestyle interventions.', '["I and IV only","II and III only","II, III and IV only","I, II and IV only"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 3, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-12T11:53:23.002Z","interval":8,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":3}'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:53:23.002Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.942Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:53:23.002Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756360169501-88', NULL, 'anki-deck-1756357465257-1790432463920-rf85m', 'A 32-year-old nurse was referred to the Nutrition Clinic for nutrition counseling. She is currently in her second trimester of pregnancy and was diagnosed with Gestational Diabetes Mellitus (GDM) based on elevated Oral Glucose Tolerance Test (OGTT) and Fasting Blood Glucose (FBG) results. Other information noted were a family history of diabetes mellitus (father with Type 2 Diabetes Mellitus), a pre-pregnancy BMI of 32 kg/m², and Asian ethnicity. Which of the following should be included in the dietary recommendations for this patient?', 'Carbohydrates should be 50 – 60% of TER', 'Medical Nutrition Therapy (MNT) is the primary treatment for Gestational Diabetes Mellitus (GDM). The goals are to maintain normal maternal blood glucose levels, provide adequate nutrients for fetal growth and development, prevent ketosis, and achieve appropriate gestational weight gain. Traditionally, dietary recommendations for women with GDM include providing approximately 50–60% of the total energy requirement from carbohydrates, with the remainder supplied by protein and fat. 

Carbohydrates should come primarily from complex, high-fiber sources such as whole grains, legumes, vegetables, fruits, and low-fat dairy products. In addition, carbohydrate intake should be distributed evenly throughout the day by consuming three meals and appropriate snacks to minimize postprandial hyperglycemia.', '["Carbohydrates should be 50 – 60% of TER","Amount of carbohydrates per meal should be limited to 30 grams","Fruits should be taken as snack","Simple sugars should be eliminated from the diet"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-07T12:03:41.097Z","interval":3,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":2}'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T12:03:41.097Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.942Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T12:03:41.097Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756360193016-89', NULL, 'anki-deck-1756357465257-1790432463920-rf85m', 'Dietary management of gastric secretory disturbances includes the following EXCEPT:', 'High fat diet', 'Gastric secretory disturbances refer to conditions involving abnormal gastric acid secretion, such as gastritis, peptic ulcer disease (PUD), hyperchlorhydria, and gastroesophageal reflux disease (GERD). The primary goals of nutrition therapy are to minimize gastric irritation, reduce symptoms, promote healing, and maintain adequate nutritional status. 

A high-fat diet is generally not recommended because fat delays gastric emptying and may stimulate gastric acid secretion, prolong gastric distention, and worsen symptoms such as epigastric discomfort, reflux, and indigestion. Therefore, meals are usually moderate to low in fat, depending on the patient''s tolerance.', '["Complex carbohydrate","Low fat diet","Low protein","High fat diet"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-06T02:49:09.766Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.3,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-11-05T02:49:09.766Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.942Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-05T02:49:09.766Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756360212881-90', NULL, 'anki-deck-1756357465257-1790432463920-rf85m', 'Which is not an immediate cause of malnutrition?', 'Low income', 'According to the official UNICEF framework:

• Immediate Causes (Individual level): These operate at the level of the individual human body. There are only two immediate causes of malnutrition:
	1. Inadequate intake
	2. Disease

• Underlying Causes (Household/Community level): These are the structural situations that lead to inadequate intake or disease. They include maternal and childcare practices, inadequate access to food, and poor water/sanitation services.

• Basic Causes (Societal level): These are the root socioeconomic and political factors, such as low income, poverty, and lack of resources.', '["Inadequate intake","Maternal and childcare","Disease","Low income"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-07T12:05:27.849Z","interval":3,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":2}'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T12:05:27.849Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.942Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T12:05:27.849Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756360229097-91', NULL, 'anki-deck-1756357465257-1790432463920-rf85m', 'Compute for the kcal of 850 ml 5% amino acid', '170 kcal', 'Step-by-Step Calculation

In clinical nutrition and parenteral nutrition calculations, a 5% amino acid solution means there are 5 grams of pure amino acid in every 100 mL of fluid. Amino acids provide 4 kcal per gram.

1. Calculate the total grams of amino acid:
	• Total grams = 850 mL x 0.05 (5%)
	• Total grams = 42.5 grams

2. Calculate the total calories (kcal):
	• Total kcal = 42.5 grams x 4 kcal/gram', '["200 kcal","170 kcal","300 kcal","250 kcal"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-07T11:40:00.159Z","interval":3,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":2}'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:40:00.159Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.942Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:40:00.159Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756360260829-92', NULL, 'anki-deck-1756357465257-1790432463920-rf85m', 'Which of the following happened in the oral cavity during digestion?
I. Food mastication
II. Salivary glands lubricating the food
III. Tongue tastes and move the food around
IV. Produced an end product called bolus', 'All of the above', 'The oral cavity is the entry point of the digestive tract, where both mechanical and chemical digestion simultaneously begin. All four statements accurately describe the complex physiological processes that occur here:

• I. Food mastication: Chewing (mastication) is the primary mechanical function of the mouth. The teeth break down solid food into smaller fragments to dramatically increase the surface area available for digestive enzymes.
• II. Salivary glands lubricating the food: The parotid, submandibular, and sublingual salivary glands continuously produce and secrete saliva. Saliva contains water, electrolytes, and mucin, which moistens and lubricates the dry food mass so it can glide easily through the pharynx and esophagus during swallowing.
• III. Tongue tastes and move the food around: The surface of the tongue contains sensory gustatory receptors (taste buds) that identify food flavors. Mechanically, the tongue plays a vital role by manipulating and moving the chewed food particles around the oral cavity, positioning them between the teeth for efficient grinding.
• IV. Produced an end product called bolus: As the tongue manipulates the thoroughly chewed food and mixes it completely with lubricating saliva, it shapes the mixture into a soft, cohesive, swallowable mass. This specific structural end product of oral cavity processing is officially termed a bolus.', '["I and II only","I, II and III only","II and III only","All of the above"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-07T11:42:42.390Z","interval":3,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":2}'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:42:42.390Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.942Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:42:42.390Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756360285845-93', NULL, 'anki-deck-1756357465257-1790432463920-rf85m', 'How can the stomach protect itself from the strong acidity of HCl?', 'Mucus secretion forms a protective coat over the mucosa', 'The cells lining the inner wall of the stomach (surface mucous cells and mucous neck cells) continuously secrete a thick, gel-like layer of mucus.

This mucus layer is rich in bicarbonate ions (HCO₃⁻). The alkaline bicarbonate is trapped within the sticky mucus gel, creating a highly effective physical and chemical buffer system. While the liquid inside the stomach cavity is highly acidic with a pH of 1.5 to 2.0, this specialized protective coat maintains a near-neutral pH of around 7.0 right at the surface of the stomach''s epithelial cells, preventing hydrochloric acid (HCl) and pepsin from digesting the stomach wall.', '["Secretin stimulates the release of sodium bicarbonate to neutralize acid","Stomach is covered by thick microvilli","Mucus secretion forms a protective coat over the mucosa","Pancreatic enzymes protect the stomach"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-07T11:38:06.773Z","interval":3,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":2}'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:38:06.773Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.942Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:38:06.773Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756360311027-94', NULL, 'anki-deck-1756357465257-1790432463920-rf85m', 'An essential fatty acid derived from plant oils such as corn oil', 'Linoleic acid', 'Linoleic acid (LA) is an essential omega-6 polyunsaturated fatty acid (PUFA) that must be obtained from the diet because the human body cannot synthesize it. It is abundant in plant oils, particularly corn oil, soybean oil, sunflower oil, safflower oil, and cottonseed oil. 

Linoleic acid plays important roles in maintaining cell membrane integrity, supporting growth and development, promoting skin health, and serving as the precursor of arachidonic acid, which is involved in the production of eicosanoids (prostaglandins, thromboxanes, and leukotrienes). 

Another essential fatty acid is α-linolenic acid (ALA), an omega-3 fatty acid, which is commonly found in flaxseed, chia seeds, walnuts, canola oil, and soybean oil. Both linoleic acid and α-linolenic acid are considered essential because they cannot be synthesized by humans.', '["Linoleic acid","α-Linolenic acid","Arachidonic acid","Palmitic acid"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-07T12:05:16.612Z","interval":3,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":2}'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T12:05:16.612Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.942Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T12:05:16.612Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756360337409-95', NULL, 'anki-deck-1756357465257-1790432463920-rf85m', 'A 55-year-old Filipino male laboratory technician diagnosed with Type 2 Diabetes Mellitus visited the Nutrition Clinic for counseling. His anthropometric measurements are as follows: height = 5''7" (170 cm), weight = 75 kg, and waist circumference = 36 inches. His laboratory results revealed the following: HbA1c = 9%; total cholesterol = 250 mg/dL; LDL cholesterol = 300 mg/dL; and uric acid = 9 mg/dL. He reported joint pain, lower back pain, and swelling of the joints. The RND advised him to follow a consistent carbohydrate diet. What does a consistent carbohydrate diet mean?', 'The amount of carbohydrates consumed, and the timing of meals and snacks are consistent.', 'A consistent carbohydrate meal plan emphasizes distributing fixed, predictable amounts of carbohydrates across meals and snacks at consistent daily intervals to optimize postprandial glucose control with endogenous or exogenous insulin.', '["A total of 45 grams of carbohydrates is given at each meal to maintain normal blood glucose level","No simple carbohydrates should be eaten during snacks.","The amount of carbohydrates consumed, and the timing of meals and snacks are consistent.","None of the above"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-07T12:02:23.982Z","interval":3,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":2}'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T12:02:23.982Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.942Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T12:02:23.982Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756360356794-96', NULL, 'anki-deck-1756357465257-1790432463920-rf85m', 'The complex formed when bile salts combine with lipid material to facilitate digestion', 'Micelle', 'Micelles are small, water-soluble complexes formed in the lumen of the small intestine when bile salts combine with the products of lipid digestion, including free fatty acids, monoglycerides, cholesterol, phospholipids, and fat-soluble vitamins (A, D, E, and K). 

The primary function of micelles is to transport these lipid digestion products across the watery environment of the intestinal lumen to the brush border of enterocytes, where they are absorbed. Without bile salts and micelle formation, lipid absorption would be markedly impaired, particularly that of long-chain fatty acids and fat-soluble vitamins. 

For the NDLE, distinguish micelles from chylomicrons. Micelles are involved in intestinal absorption, whereas chylomicrons are responsible for transporting absorbed dietary lipids from the intestine to the circulation.', '["Chylomicron","Micelle","HDL","LDL"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-07T11:39:26.858Z","interval":3,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":2}'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:39:26.858Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.942Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:39:26.858Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756360373858-97', NULL, 'anki-deck-1756357465257-1790432463920-rf85m', 'What is the immediate substrate that feeds into the TCA cycle?', 'Acetyl CoA', 'The Tricarboxylic Acid (TCA) cycle, also known as the Citric Acid Cycle or Krebs Cycle, is the central metabolic pathway responsible for the oxidation of carbohydrates, fats, and proteins to produce energy. The immediate substrate that enters the TCA cycle is acetyl-CoA. 

Acetyl-CoA is produced from several metabolic pathways: 
• Carbohydrates: Glucose → Glycolysis → Pyruvate → Acetyl-CoA 
• Fats: β-oxidation of fatty acids → Acetyl-CoA 
• Proteins: Ketogenic amino acids → Acetyl-CoA 

Once formed, acetyl-CoA combines with oxaloacetate, catalyzed by citrate synthase, to produce citrate, the first compound of the TCA cycle. The cycle then proceeds through a series of reactions that generate NADH, FADH₂, and GTP (or ATP), which are subsequently used in oxidative phosphorylation to produce ATP. 

For the NDLE, remember that acetyl-CoA is the metabolic crossroads because it links carbohydrate, lipid, and protein metabolism.', '["Pyruvate","Acetyl CoA","Citrate","Oxaloacetate"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-06T02:46:23.835Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.1,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-11-05T02:46:23.835Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.942Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-05T02:46:23.835Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756360391184-98', NULL, 'anki-deck-1756357465257-1790432463920-rf85m', 'In the electron transport system (ETS), electrons from hydrogen carriers pass through a series of electron carriers, ultimately driving the conversion of ADP to ATP. This process is called:', 'Oxidative phosphorylation', 'Oxidative phosphorylation is the process by which energy released from the transfer of electrons through the electron transport chain (ETC) is used to generate ATP. During the oxidation of carbohydrates, fats, and proteins, hydrogen/electron carriers such as NADH and FADH₂ deliver high-energy electrons to the ETC located in the inner mitochondrial membrane. As electrons pass through the electron carriers, the released energy is used to pump protons (H⁺) across the membrane, creating a proton gradient. The return of H⁺ through ATP synthase provides the energy needed to synthesize ATP from ADP + inorganic phosphate (Pi). Thus, the process described in the question is oxidative phosphorylation.', '["Oxidative phosphorylation","Oxidative decarboxylation","Gluconeogenesis","Glycogenesis"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-07T11:50:18.235Z","interval":3,"easeFactor":2.3,"repetitions":2}'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:50:18.235Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.942Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:50:18.235Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756360414690-99', NULL, 'anki-deck-1756357465257-1790432463920-rf85m', 'Which of the following describes Type 1 Diabetes Mellitus?', 'It is an autoimmune disorder', 'Type 1 Diabetes Mellitus (T1DM) is a chronic, lifelong autoimmune disorder. The body’s own immune system mistakenly targets and destroys the insulin-producing beta cells located in the islets of Langerhans within the pancreas. This immune-mediated destruction results in an absolute insulin deficiency, meaning the pancreas can no longer produce insulin.

• The pancreas may have the ability to produce insulin but cells are deficient in receptors for insulin: This statement refers to a structural down-regulation of insulin receptors, which is a mechanism related to extreme forms of insulin resistance, not the absolute destruction of pancreatic cells seen in T1DM.
• The person usually does not develop symptoms until age 45: T1DM is most commonly diagnosed in children, adolescents, and young adults (historically termed "juvenile diabetes"), although it can theoretically manifest at any age. Diagnosis past age 45 is far more characteristic of Type 2 Diabetes Mellitus (T2DM).
• It is characterized by insulin resistance: Insulin resistance—where body cells fail to respond normally to circulating insulin—is the hallmark pathophysiological driver of Type 2 Diabetes, not Type 1.', '["The pancreas may have the ability to produce insulin but cells are deficient in receptors for insulin","It is an autoimmune disorder","The person usually does not develop symptoms until age 45","It is characterized by insulin resistance"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-23T07:58:21.584Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-22T07:58:21.584Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.942Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-22T07:58:21.898Z"'::jsonb)
