INSERT INTO public.flashcards (id, user_id, deck_id, front, back, rationale, options, tags, ndle_subject, difficulty, leitner_box, user_notes, sm2, last_reviewed_at, created_at, updated_at) VALUES
('anki-card-1756360436873-100', NULL, 'anki-deck-1756357465257-1790432463920-rf85m', 'The following are functions of sodium, except:', 'Participation in detoxification reactions', 'Sodium is the major extracellular cation and plays an important role in maintaining extracellular fluid volume and osmotic balance. It is also essential for nerve impulse transmission and muscle contraction through its role in generating membrane potentials. Sodium-dependent transporters in the intestinal epithelium also facilitate the absorption of glucose and other nutrients, such as certain amino acids. 

However, detoxification reactions are not a primary function of sodium. Detoxification involves processes primarily carried out by the liver, including enzymatic reactions such as oxidation, reduction, hydrolysis, and conjugation.', '["Maintenance of normal extracellular fluid balance","Participation in detoxification reactions","Nerve impulse transmission and muscular contraction","Absorption of glucose and transport of other nutrients"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 3, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-11T11:32:10.735Z","interval":7,"easeFactor":2.3,"repetitions":3}'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:32:10.735Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.255Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:32:10.735Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756360451789-101', NULL, 'anki-deck-1756357465257-1790432463920-rf85m', 'The RQ of glucose is:', '1', 'The respiratory quotient (RQ) is the ratio of the amount of carbon dioxide (CO₂) produced to oxygen (O₂) consumed during the oxidation of a nutrient. For carbohydrate, specifically glucose: C6​H12​O6 ​+ 6O2 ​→ 6CO2 ​+ 6H2​O Therefore: RQ = 6CO2 ​÷ 6O2 ​= 1.0 Thus, the RQ of glucose is 1.0.

RQ values differ according to the macronutrient being oxidized: 
• Carbohydrate = 1.0 
• Protein ≈ 0.8 
• Fat = 0.7 
• Mixed diet ≈ 0.8–0.9 

This concept is important in indirect calorimetry, which estimates energy expenditure by measuring oxygen consumption and carbon dioxide production.', '["1","0.9","0.8","0.7"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-07T11:35:40.780Z","interval":3,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":2}'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:35:40.780Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.255Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:35:40.780Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756360473855-102', NULL, 'anki-deck-1756357465257-1790432463920-rf85m', 'Fats and carbohydrates are called “protein sparing” due to which of the following factors?', 'Their presence reduces the need of utilizing proteins for energy', 'Fats and carbohydrates provide adequate energy substrates (ATP) for cellular metabolism, preventing amino acids from being deaminated and used for fuel, thereby sparing dietary and somatic proteins for tissue maintenance and repair.', '["They comprise the major portions of diets","The body does not need protein if fats and carbohydrates are present","Their presence reduces the need of utilizing proteins for energy","They prevent storage of proteins"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-07T11:35:45.277Z","interval":3,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":2}'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:35:45.277Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.255Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:35:45.277Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756360492070-103', NULL, 'anki-deck-1756357465257-1790432463920-rf85m', 'The primary risk factor for COPD is:', 'cigarette smoking', 'Cigarette smoking is the most important and common risk factor for COPD. Chronic exposure to tobacco smoke causes airway inflammation, destruction of lung tissue, and progressive, largely irreversible airflow limitation. Other inhaled pollutants can also contribute, particularly occupational exposure to dusts, fumes, and chemicals, but cigarette smoking remains the major preventable risk factor. 

Alpha-1 antitrypsin deficiency is an important genetic risk factor for COPD, particularly emphysema at a younger age, but it is relatively uncommon. Frequent respiratory infections may worsen respiratory health but are not the primary risk factor for developing COPD.', '["alpha-I antitrypsin deficiency","occupational exposure to dusts or chemicals","cigarette smoking","frequent respiratory infections"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 3, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-12T11:29:11.820Z","interval":8,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":3}'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:29:11.820Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.255Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:29:11.820Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756360507135-104', NULL, 'anki-deck-1756357465257-1790432463920-rf85m', 'The weight loss and wasting that often occur in COPD can be caused by:', 'all of the above', 'Weight loss and muscle wasting are common in advanced COPD and may result from several interacting factors. 
• Reduced food intake may occur because of dyspnea, fatigue, early satiety, or difficulty eating while breathing. 
• Increased metabolic rate can occur because the work of breathing is increased, raising energy expenditure. 
• Reduced exercise tolerance decreases physical activity and contributes to loss of muscle mass and strength. 

These factors can create a cycle of poor intake → weight loss → muscle loss → reduced respiratory muscle strength → greater difficulty breathing. Therefore, nutrition management aims to provide adequate energy and protein while avoiding overfeeding.', '["reduced food intake","increased metabolic rate","reduced exercise tolerance","all of the above"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-07T11:57:01.097Z","interval":3,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":2}'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:57:01.097Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.255Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:57:01.097Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756360533917-105', NULL, 'anki-deck-1756357465257-1790432463920-rf85m', 'Nutrition therapy for a person with respiratory failure includes:', 'careful attention to providing enough, but not too much energy', 'In patients with acute or chronic respiratory failure, providing too much total energy (overfeeding) is dangerous. Excess calories—especially from carbohydrates but also from fats and proteins—accelerate lipogenesis, which drives up carbon dioxide (CO₂) production. Since the patient''s compromised respiratory system cannot handle the increased respiratory workload needed to blow off this extra CO₂, overfeeding can trigger hypercapnia and make it extremely difficult to wean them off a mechanical ventilator.

At the same time, underfeeding must be strictly avoided. If a patient does not receive enough calories, their body will enter a catabolic state, breaking down skeletal muscle—including the diaphragm and intercostal respiratory muscles—which worsens muscle weakness and leads to respiratory muscle failure. Therefore, nutrition therapy must be balanced to provide enough energy to meet metabolic needs without exceeding them.', '["careful attention to providing enough, but not too much energy","a generous fluid intake to facilitate mucus clearance","a high fat intake to prevent weight loss and wasting","a high carbohydrate intake to increase carbon dioxide production"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-07T11:50:54.005Z","interval":3,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":2}'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:50:54.005Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.255Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:50:54.005Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756360561799-106', NULL, 'anki-deck-1756357465257-1790432463920-rf85m', 'A dialysis patient was prescribed 55 grams protein per day. The amount of HBV should be at least:', '33 grams', 'In chronic dialysis patients (hemodialysis or peritoneal dialysis), clinical practice guidelines recommend that at least 50% to 60% of total dietary protein be of High Biological Value (HBV). 60% of 55 g = 33 grams.', '["41 grams","33 grams","39 grams","66 grams"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-07T11:32:01.614Z","interval":3,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":2}'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:32:01.614Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.255Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:32:01.614Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756360579748-107', NULL, 'anki-deck-1756357465257-1790432463920-rf85m', 'CHO 900 kcal is ____% of TER 1500 kcal.', '60%', 'To determine the percentage of total energy provided by carbohydrate:
 % of TER = [(kcal from CHO) ÷ TER] * 100 = (900 kcal ÷ 1500) * 100 = 60% 

Therefore, carbohydrate provides 60% of the TER. This type of calculation is commonly used when evaluating whether a patient''s macronutrient distribution meets the prescribed nutrition plan.', '["40%","50%","60%","70%"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 3, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-12T11:29:50.630Z","interval":8,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":3}'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:29:50.630Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.255Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:29:50.630Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756360594665-108', NULL, 'anki-deck-1756357465257-1790432463920-rf85m', 'The normal protein requirement of a Filipino adult.', '1g/KDBW', 'For Philippine nutrition assessment and meal planning, the commonly used reference for the protein requirement of a healthy Filipino adult is approximately 1.0–1.1 g/kg body weight per day, depending on the reference and individual circumstances.

Protein needs are not identical for everyone. Requirements may increase during pregnancy, lactation, older age, intense physical activity, illness, trauma, and other conditions associated with increased protein needs. For clinical nutrition, the appropriate weight basis and protein target should be individualized.', '["1g/KDBW","5g/KDBW","2g/KDBW","3g/KDBW"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-07T12:00:03.536Z","interval":3,"easeFactor":2.3,"repetitions":2}'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T12:00:03.536Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.255Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T12:00:03.536Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756360613330-109', NULL, 'anki-deck-1756357465257-1790432463920-rf85m', 'Most of the body''s energy needs is provided by:', 'Glucose and fatty acids', 'Under normal resting and post-absorptive physiological states, cellular energy is predominantly generated through the aerobic oxidation of glucose and free fatty acids.', '["Fats and proteins","Vitamins and minerals","Glucose and fatty acids","Glycerol and amino acids"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-07T11:40:04.566Z","interval":3,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":2}'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:40:04.566Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.255Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:40:04.566Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756360633795-110', NULL, 'anki-deck-1756357465257-1790432463920-rf85m', 'A diet prescription of 1800 kcal with distribution of C 60% P 15% F 25% will provide:', 'C 270 g, P 70 g, F 50 g', 'To calculate the grams of each macronutrient: 

1. Carbohydrate 
Carbohydrate provides 4 kcal/g: 
CHO: 60% → 1,080 kcal ÷ 4 = 270 g 

2. Protein 
Protein provides 4 kcal/g:
Protein: 15% → 270 kcal ÷ 4 = 67.5 g → 70 g 
Using the rounding convention applied in this question, 67.5 g is rounded to 70 g. 

3. Fat 
Fat provides 9 kcal/g: 
Fat: 25% → 450 kcal ÷ 9 = 50 g 

Therefore, the prescribed diet provides approximately 270 g carbohydrate, 70 g protein, and 50 g fat.', '["C 270 g, P 70 g, F 50 g","C 275 g, P 67.5g, F 50 g","C 275 g, P 70 g, F 50 g","C 270 g, P 70 g, F 55 g"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-07T11:59:32.121Z","interval":3,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":2}'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:59:32.121Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.255Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:59:32.121Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756360649362-111', NULL, 'anki-deck-1756357465257-1790432463920-rf85m', 'What is the total protein requirement/day of a 7-year-old child using NPC method?', '44 grams/day', 'Using the NPC method, first determine the child''s desirable body weight (DBW): 

DBW = (age in years * 2) + 8 
For a 7-year-old: DBW = (7 * 2) + 8 = 22 kg 

Using the 2.0 g protein/kg DBW/day recommendation from the NDAP Diet Manual, 5th edition: CHON req’t = DBW * 2.0 g/kgDBW 

Therefore, the child''s total protein requirement is 44 g/day. This question is specifically testing the clinical computation convention used in the NDAP Diet Manual, rather than asking for the current general population protein RDA/RNI.', '["20 grams/day","24.2 grams/day","33 grams/day","44 grams/day"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-07T11:15:43.999Z","interval":3,"easeFactor":2.3,"repetitions":2}'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:15:43.999Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.255Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:15:43.999Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756360669027-112', NULL, 'anki-deck-1756357465257-1790432463920-rf85m', 'A BMI of 23 kg/m² according to the Asia-Pacific classification is considered:', 'Overweight', 'The Asia-Pacific BMI classification uses lower BMI cutoffs for overweight and obesity compared with the standard WHO classification because Asian populations may have a higher risk of type 2 diabetes and cardiovascular disease at lower BMI values. 

Under the Asia-Pacific classification: 
• <18.5 kg/m² → Underweight 
• 18.5–22.9 kg/m² → Normal 
• 23.0–24.9 kg/m² → Overweight 
• 25.0–29.9 kg/m² → Obese I 
• ≥30.0 kg/m² → Obese II 

Therefore, a BMI of 23 kg/m² is classified as overweight.', '["Normal","Overweight","Obese","None of the above"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 3, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-12T11:43:33.438Z","interval":8,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":3}'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:43:33.438Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.255Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:43:33.438Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756360689143-113', NULL, 'anki-deck-1756357465257-1790432463920-rf85m', 'A primary feature of emphysema is:', 'destruction of the walls separating the alveoli', 'Emphysema is a form of COPD characterized by abnormal and permanent enlargement of airspaces distal to the terminal bronchioles, accompanied by destruction of alveolar walls and loss of the normal elastic recoil of the lungs. 

Destruction of the alveolar walls reduces the surface area available for gas exchange and causes neighboring alveoli to merge into larger, less efficient air spaces. Loss of elastic recoil also contributes to air trapping and difficulty exhaling, particularly during forced expiration.

This distinguishes emphysema from chronic bronchitis, in which chronic airway inflammation and excessive mucus production are more prominent features.', '["obstruction within the bronchi","obstruction within the bronchioles","destruction of the walls separating the alveoli","excessive lung elasticity"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 3, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-12T11:38:22.927Z","interval":8,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":3}'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:38:22.927Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.255Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:38:22.927Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756360705175-114', NULL, 'anki-deck-1756357465257-1790432463920-rf85m', 'A physician ordered 1500 kcal tube feeding formula, 1.5:1 dilution every 4 hours. The volume per feeding is:', '167 mL/feed', 'First, determine the total volume of formula required: (1500 kcal ÷ 1.5 kcal/mL) = 1000 mL 

Since feeding is given every 4 hours, the patient receives: 24 ÷ 4 = 6 feedings / day 

Therefore: 1000 mL ÷ 6 = 166.7 mL / feed 
Rounded to the nearest whole number: 167 ml / feed', '["167 mL/feed","250 mL/feed","150 mL/feed","300 mL/feed"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 3, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-12T12:04:55.309Z","interval":8,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":3}'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T12:04:55.309Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.255Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T12:04:55.309Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756360720690-115', NULL, 'anki-deck-1756357465257-1790432463920-rf85m', 'How many mg of potassium is contained in 40 mEq?', '1560 mg K', 'For potassium (K⁺), the conversion between mEq and mg is: mg = mEq * (atomic weight ÷ valence) 

Potassium has an atomic weight of approximately 39 mg/mmol and a valence of +1. 

Therefore: 40 mEq * (39mg ÷ 1) = 1560 mg K 

Thus, 40 mEq of potassium = 1,560 mg K. This conversion is clinically important because electrolytes may be prescribed or reported in mEq, while nutrition labels, laboratory references, and calculations may use mg.', '["1500 mg K","1560 mg K","2000 mg K","1864 mg K"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-07T11:29:22.943Z","interval":3,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":2}'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:29:22.943Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.255Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:29:22.943Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756360739276-116', NULL, 'anki-deck-1756357465257-1790432463920-rf85m', 'What should be done when a known diabetic patient is undergoing hypoglycemia?', 'Give 1 tbsp sugar, then wait for 15 minutes', 'For a conscious patient with hypoglycemia who can safely swallow, the recommended immediate treatment is the 15-15 rule: provide approximately 15 g of fast-acting carbohydrate, then recheck blood glucose after 15 minutes. If blood glucose remains low, repeat the treatment. One tablespoon of table sugar provides approximately 12–15 g of carbohydrate, making it the best answer among the choices.', '["Give 1 tbsp sugar, then wait for 15 minutes","Give a cup of water to rehydrate","Inject an insulin short","Provide 1 pc candy"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-07T11:43:05.416Z","interval":3,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":2}'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:43:05.416Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.255Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:43:05.416Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756360756642-117', NULL, 'anki-deck-1756357465257-1790432463920-rf85m', 'Inherent Na in the diet is 950 mg. The Diet Rx states that your patient is allowed 90 mEq/day. How much is the salt is to be added to meet the Diet Rx?', '2.8 grams', 'Step 1: Convert the Diet Rx from mEq to milligrams (mg) of Sodium
	• Rule: 1 mEq of Sodium (Na) = 23 mg
	• Calculation: 90 mEq x 23 mg/mEq = 2,070 mg of total allowed Sodium

Step 2: Calculate the additional Sodium needed
	• Subtract the inherent sodium already in the food from the total allowed sodium.
	• Calculation: 2,070 mg (Total Allowed) - 950 mg (Inherent) = 1,120 mg of additional Sodium needed

Step 3: Convert the additional Sodium to grams of Table Salt (NaCl)
	• Rule: Table salt is 40% sodium (0.40). To find the amount of salt, divide the sodium milligrams by 0.40.
	• Calculation: 1,120 mg / 0.40 = 2,800 mg of salt
	• Convert to grams: 2,800 mg / 1,000 = 2.8 grams of salt', '["2.8 grams","2.0 grams","4.3 grams","5.0 grams"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 3, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-12T11:38:56.482Z","interval":8,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":3}'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:38:56.482Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.255Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:38:56.482Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756360787739-118', NULL, 'anki-deck-1756357465257-1790432463920-rf85m', 'Which of the following is a free food?', 'Tea', 'In clinical dietetics and food exchange systems, a free food is defined as any food or beverage that contains negligible amounts of macronutrients—specifically less than 20 calories and 5 grams or less of carbohydrates per serving.

Unsweetened black, green, or herbal tea is virtually calorie-free and carbohydrate-free. Because it does not cause any significant spike in blood glucose or daily caloric intake, it can be consumed freely by patients (such as those managing diabetes or weight) without needing to be calculated or swapped out in a meal plan.', '["Peanut brittle","Soft drinks","Tea","All of the above"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 3, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-12T11:47:53.976Z","interval":8,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":3}'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:47:53.976Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.255Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:47:53.976Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756360806038-119', NULL, 'anki-deck-1756357465257-1790432463920-rf85m', 'What is the BMI of a 5''4" individual with an actual weight of 60 kg?', '22.7  kg/m²', 'BMI is calculated using: Weight in kg ÷ Height in m² 

First, convert 5''4" to meters: 5’4” = 64 inches = 1.6256 meters 

Then calculate BMI: 60 kg ÷ (1.6256)² = 60 ÷ 2.6426 = ~22.7 kg/m²', '["22  kg/m²","22.7  kg/m²","23  kg/m²","25  kg/m²"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-07T12:02:01.381Z","interval":3,"easeFactor":2.3,"repetitions":2}'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T12:02:01.381Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.255Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T12:02:01.381Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756360824736-120', NULL, 'anki-deck-1756357465257-1790432463920-rf85m', 'Which of the following statements is FALSE?', 'None of the above', 'All three statements are considered true based on commonly used Philippine food-composition references for purine content. Purines are naturally occurring compounds found in many foods. They are metabolized to uric acid, making dietary purine intake particularly relevant in the nutritional management of gout and hyperuricemia. 

• Tamban (sardines) is a fish with relatively high purine content. 
• Dried beans also contain purines, but their purine content may be lower than that of certain high-purine fish such as sardines. 
• Chicken organ meats, including gizzard and intestine, contain substantial amounts of purines. 
• Pork liver is a high-purine organ meat and has a higher purine content than pork blood in the referenced food data.', '["Tamban has higher purine content as compared to dried beans.","Chicken gizzard has about the same purine content of that of the chicken intestine.","Pork liver has higher purine content as compared to pork blood","None of the above"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 3, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-12T11:42:52.625Z","interval":8,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":3}'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:42:52.625Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.255Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:42:52.625Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756360861451-121', NULL, 'anki-deck-1756357465257-1790432463920-rf85m', 'Which of the following statements is TRUE?', 'Evaporated milk has higher cholesterol content than lean fish', 'Evaporated milk is an animal-derived food and therefore contains cholesterol. Based on the food-composition values used in the original question, its cholesterol content is higher than that of lean meat when compared using the specified serving/reference amounts. The question is testing the ability to compare cholesterol content among commonly consumed foods, rather than simply identifying whether a food contains cholesterol. 

"Vegetables have a higher cholesterol content than fruits" is false because plant foods do not contain cholesterol. Vegetables and fruits naturally contain zero dietary cholesterol. 

"Shrimp has a higher cholesterol content than butter" should also not be selected based simply on the fact that shrimp is recognized as relatively high in cholesterol. Cholesterol comparisons must be made using the same reference/serving basis in the food-composition table.', '["Evaporated milk has higher cholesterol content than lean fish","Vegetable has higher cholesterol content as compared to fruits","Shrimp has higher cholesterol content than butter","None of the above"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-05T11:54:58.457Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.1,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:54:58.457Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.255Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:54:58.457Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756360878484-122', NULL, 'anki-deck-1756357465257-1790432463920-rf85m', 'Diet prescribed for patients with calcium oxalate stones', 'Acid-ash diet', 'Historically, an acid-ash diet was prescribed for calcium oxalate kidney stones. The traditional rationale was that acidification of the urine was thought to help maintain the solubility of calcium salts and reduce the formation of calcium-based stones. In contrast, an alkaline-ash diet was traditionally used for uric acid and cystine stones, for which increasing urinary pH promotes greater solubility.', '["Alkaline-ash diet","Acid-ash diet","High fiber diet","Mediterranean Diet"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-07T12:05:10.960Z","interval":3,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":2}'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T12:05:10.960Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.255Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T12:05:10.960Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756360894100-123', NULL, 'anki-deck-1756357465257-1790432463920-rf85m', 'A patient is prescribed a diet containing 1,500 mg of sodium. How many mEq of sodium are contained in the diet?', '65 mEq Na', 'To convert milligrams of sodium to milliequivalents (mEq), use: mEq = (mg * valence) ÷ atomic weight 

Sodium (Na⁺) has: 
• Atomic weight ≈ 23 
• Valence = 1 

Therefore: mEq Na = (1500 * 1) ÷ 23 = 65.2 mEq 

Thus, the calculated value is approximately 65 mEq.', '["55 mEq Na","60 mEq Na","65 mEq Na","70 mEq Na"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-23T06:53:41.619Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-22T06:53:41.619Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.255Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-22T06:53:41.619Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756360915749-124', NULL, 'anki-deck-1756357465257-1790432463920-rf85m', 'A low calcium diet contains no more than _____ mg calcium', '600 mg', 'In traditional therapeutic diet planning, a low-calcium diet is generally prescribed to provide no more than 600 mg calcium per day. This is a therapeutic restriction, not the recommended daily calcium intake for healthy individuals. 

Calcium is essential for bone and tooth mineralization, muscle contraction, nerve transmission, and blood clotting, so restricting calcium should be done only when clinically indicated. For NDLE purposes, when the question asks for the traditional definition of a low-calcium diet, remember ≤600 mg/day.', '["500 mg","600 mg","700 mg","800 mg"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-06T02:46:11.328Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.1,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-11-05T02:46:11.328Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.255Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-05T02:46:11.328Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756360933631-125', NULL, 'anki-deck-1756357465257-1790432463920-rf85m', 'Diet prescribed for patients with cystine stone', 'Alkaline-ash diet', 'An alkaline-ash diet is the traditional dietary prescription for patients with cystine stones. Cystinuria is an inherited disorder in which excessive amounts of cystine are excreted in the urine. Because cystine has poor solubility in acidic urine, it is more likely to precipitate and form stones. Increasing urinary pH makes cystine more soluble and therefore helps reduce cystine crystallization. This principle remains clinically relevant. 

Current management of cystinuria emphasizes high fluid intake to dilute urinary cystine and urinary alkalinization, commonly with potassium citrate, to maintain an appropriately alkaline urine. Dietary sodium restriction is also important because high sodium intake can increase urinary cystine excretion.', '["Alkaline-ash diet","Acid-ash diet","High fiber diet","Mediterranean Diet"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-22T14:05:52.860Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-21T14:05:52.860Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.255Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-21T14:05:52.860Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756360963266-126', NULL, 'anki-deck-1756357465257-1790432463920-rf85m', 'Which of the following statements is true?', 'Creatinine is directly affected by dietary protein intake', 'While serum creatinine is primarily a reflection of endogenous muscle mass, it can be significantly and directly affected by dietary protein intake, specifically through the consumption of cooked meat.

During the cooking process, the intense heat converts a portion of the creatine naturally present in animal muscle tissue into creatinine. When a patient consumes a cooked-meat meal, this pre-formed creatinine is rapidly absorbed across the gastrointestinal tract and enters the bloodstream.

This causes a temporary, artificial spike in serum creatinine levels independently of the patient''s actual kidney function. This dietary variable is clinically critical to recognize during nutrition and renal assessments, as a recent high-protein meat meal can skew laboratory results and lead to a falsely lower estimated Glomerular Filtration Rate (eGFR).', '["BUN can be used as the sole indicator of nutritional status","Cereals are acid-forming foods","Creatinine is directly affected by dietary protein intake","None of the above"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-07T12:04:01.032Z","interval":3,"easeFactor":2.3,"repetitions":2}'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T12:04:01.032Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.255Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T12:04:01.032Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756361001180-127', NULL, 'anki-deck-1756357465257-1790432463920-rf85m', 'Which of the following statements is true?', 'All of the above', 'All three statements describe principles used in therapeutic diet planning for renal conditions. 

Creatinine clearance is an indicator of renal filtration, and protein prescription in renal disease is adjusted according to the degree of renal impairment. As renal function declines, protein intake may need to be controlled to reduce nitrogenous waste production. Therefore, within the context of the diet-therapy principle tested here, better/higher creatinine clearance permits a less restricted protein intake, whereas greater renal impairment generally requires closer protein management. 

For urinary stone management, oatmeal can be included in an oxalate-controlled diet because it is not among the foods classically considered very high in oxalate. 

The statement regarding fats reflects the older diet-therapy teaching that high fat intake may promote urinary urate retention, particularly in the context of uric acid metabolism and stone formation.', '["The higher the creatinine clearance, the greater the level of protein allowed.","Oatmeal is allowed in oxalate-controlled diet","Fats favor the retention of urates","All of the above"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-06T02:50:21.251Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.1,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-11-05T02:50:21.251Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.255Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-05T02:50:21.251Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756361021277-128', NULL, 'anki-deck-1756357465257-1790432463920-rf85m', 'This diet is known as the cholesterol- and saturated fat restricted diet', 'TLC diet', 'TLC stands for Therapeutic Lifestyle Changes. It is a clinically designed medical nutrition therapy created by the National Institutes of Health (NIH) specifically to lower high blood cholesterol and reduce the risk of heart disease. Its primary guidelines mandate restricting saturated fat to less than 7% of total daily calories and limiting dietary cholesterol to less than 200 mg per day.

	• Atkin''s diet & Ketogenic Diet: These are low-carbohydrate, high-fat diets. They encourage high consumption of fats (including saturated fats) and proteins, making them the opposite of a fat-restricted eating plan.
	• DASH diet: While DASH (Dietary Approaches to Stop Hypertension) is also a heart-healthy diet low in saturated fat, its definitive, hallmark feature is sodium restriction aimed at lowering blood pressure. The TLC diet is the classic textbook answer explicitly labeled as the cholesterol- and saturated fat-restricted diet.', '["Ketogenic Diet","DASH diet","TLC diet","Atkin''s diet"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-07T11:47:58.751Z","interval":3,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":2}'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:47:58.751Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.255Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:47:58.751Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756361038961-129', NULL, 'anki-deck-1756357465257-1790432463920-rf85m', 'Which among the following has the highest sodium content?', 'Carrots', 'Among the choices, carrots have the highest sodium content based on the food-composition data underlying this question. Although all four are fresh vegetables and are generally considered low-sodium foods, their naturally occurring sodium concentrations are not identical. This is an important distinction in nutrition assessment and diet therapy: foods can be categorized as generally low in sodium while still having measurable differences in their sodium content. 

For NDLE purposes, this question is primarily testing knowledge of the relative sodium content of common vegetables, rather than asking which food is considered “high sodium” clinically. In a sodium-restricted diet, carrots, cabbage, sayote, and pake may all be used, with the major dietary sodium reduction usually coming from limiting salt, processed foods, canned products, seasoning mixes, sauces, and other sodium-containing condiments.', '["Carrots","Cabbage","Sayote","Pake"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-07T12:04:32.930Z","interval":3,"easeFactor":2.3,"repetitions":2}'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T12:04:32.930Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.255Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T12:04:32.930Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756361061342-130', NULL, 'anki-deck-1756357465257-1790432463920-rf85m', 'All the foods in the list are allowed among patients with celiac disease:', 'Sour cream, dried beans, millet, plain gelatin', 'Patients diagnosed with celiac disease must strictly follow a lifelong gluten-free diet to prevent an autoimmune reaction that damages the villi of the small intestine. This means avoiding the proteins found in wheat, barley, and rye.

• Sour cream: A plain dairy product that is naturally gluten-free.
• Dried beans: Legumes that contain no gluten proteins.
• Millet: A naturally gluten-free cereal grain that serves as a safe alternative to wheat.
• Plain gelatin: A pure animal collagen product containing no grain fillers or gluten.

Why the Other Options contain forbidden foods:
• Option A is incorrect because: Graham crackers are made from wheat flour, and Ovaltine contains malt extract derived from barley. Both will trigger a celiac flare-up. Vienna sausage also frequently uses wheat flour as a binder.
• Option B is incorrect because: Breaded fish is coated in wheat-based breadcrumbs. Additionally, textured vegetable protein (TVP) can sometimes be cross-contaminated or derived from wheat gluten.', '["Textured vegetable protein, plain yogurt, breaded fish, popcorn","Sour cream, dried beans, millet, plain gelatin","Tapioca starch, ovaltine, graham, Vienna sausage","None of the above"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-07T11:32:32.214Z","interval":3,"easeFactor":2.3,"repetitions":2}'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:32:32.214Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.255Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:32:32.214Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756361099908-131', NULL, 'anki-deck-1756357465257-1790432463920-rf85m', 'All the foods in the list are not allowed among patients with celiac disease:', 'Distilled white wine, vodka, vanilla ice cream in cone, crema de fruta', '• Vanilla ice cream in a cone: Regular ice cream cones are made from wheat flour, which contains gluten and is highly toxic to individuals with celiac disease.
• Crema de fruta: This traditional Filipino dessert is natively constructed using layers of sponge cake (made with standard wheat flour) or graham crackers, both of which contain gluten and are strictly forbidden.

Because a person with celiac disease cannot consume wheat-based cones or traditional sponge cake/graham desserts, they cannot safely eat everything presented in this selection.

• Other choices: These lists contain naturally gluten-free foods (like corn, maple syrup, wine, fresh vegetables, tofu, and lactose-free milk) which are generally allowed on a celiac diet. Therefore, they do not fulfill the criteria of a "not allowed" list.', '["Fresh vegetables washed in water with baking soda, margarine, tofu, Lactaid","Distilled white wine, vodka, vanilla ice cream in cone, crema de fruta","Corn and maple syrup, fruit ices, sorbet, wine","All of the above"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-23T09:35:01.934Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.3,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-22T09:35:01.934Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.255Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-22T09:35:01.934Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756361117906-132', NULL, 'anki-deck-1756357465257-1790432463920-rf85m', 'Statements describing the management of celiac disease:', 'The management of celiac disease is a life-long gluten-free diet.', 'The cornerstone of celiac disease management is a strict, lifelong gluten-free diet. Gluten exposure triggers an immune-mediated response that can cause intestinal mucosal injury even when the patient does not experience obvious gastrointestinal symptoms. Therefore, the absence of symptoms does not mean that celiac disease has disappeared or that gluten can safely be reintroduced. Strict adherence to a gluten-free diet promotes intestinal healing, improves nutritional status, and reduces the risk of complications associated with ongoing gluten exposure.', '["The management of celiac disease is a life-long gluten-free diet.","Small quantities of gluten may be allowed when celiac disease no longer manifests.","Enough scientific evidence exists for what constitutes a safe amount of gluten intake.","All of the above"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-07T11:36:24.209Z","interval":3,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":2}'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:36:24.209Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.255Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:36:24.209Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756361161771-133', NULL, 'anki-deck-1756357465257-1790432463920-rf85m', 'A low phosphorous diet contains less than ___ mg phosphorous', '1000 mg', 'For traditional renal diet-therapy teaching, a low-phosphorus diet is generally defined as providing less than 800–1,000 mg phosphorus per day, with the exact prescription individualized according to renal function, serum phosphorus, dietary protein requirements, and dialysis status. Therefore, among the choices given, less than 1,000 mg/day is the best answer.', '["1000 mg","1200 mg","1400 mg","1600 mg"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 3, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-11T11:43:09.166Z","interval":7,"easeFactor":2.3,"repetitions":3}'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:43:09.166Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.255Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:43:09.166Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756361194919-134', NULL, 'anki-deck-1756357465257-1790432463920-rf85m', 'Pregnancy-induced hypertension is not:', 'a complication of diabetes', 'Diabetes mellitus (both preexisting type 1/type 2 diabetes and gestational diabetes) is a major risk factor that predisposes a woman to developing pregnancy-induced hypertension (PIH). However, PIH itself is a specific vascular disorder that arises directly from placental dysfunction and endothelial stress, not a medical complication caused by diabetes.

	• Characterized by edema: PIH is classically identified in board exam frameworks by a hallmark triad of symptoms: hypertension, generalized or pitting edema, and proteinuria.
	• A complication of pregnancy: PIH is, by definition, a major medical complication that specifically occurs during pregnancy (typically developing after the 20th week of gestation).
	• Characterized by hypertension: Elevated blood pressure (systolic BP equal to or greater than 140 mmHg or diastolic BP equal to or greater than 90 mmHg) is the primary diagnostic feature of the condition.', '["a complication of diabetes","a complication of pregnancy","characterized by hypertension","characterized by edema"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-07T11:49:08.433Z","interval":3,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":2}'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:49:08.433Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.255Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:49:08.433Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756361212436-135', NULL, 'anki-deck-1756357465257-1790432463920-rf85m', 'The addition of 15 grams salt in the diet increases sodium content by:', '6 grams', 'Table salt is chemically known as sodium chloride (NaCl). By molecular weight, pure table salt is composed of approximately 40% sodium and 60% chloride.

To determine how much sodium is present in a given mass of table salt, you multiply the total mass by 40% (0.40):
15 grams of salt x 0.40 = 6 grams of sodium', '["3,000 mg","2 grams","6 grams","8 grams"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-07T11:35:36.596Z","interval":3,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":2}'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:35:36.596Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.255Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:35:36.596Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756361233728-136', NULL, 'anki-deck-1756357465257-1790432463920-rf85m', 'Azotemia is detected through the measurement of:', 'Blood urea nitrogen', 'Azotemia is a medical condition characterized by an abnormally high concentration of nitrogenous waste compounds (such as blood urea nitrogen (BUN) and creatinine) in the blood.

It occurs when the kidneys are damaged or poorly perfused, leaving them unable to filter and excrete these metabolic waste products efficiently. It is a hallmark diagnostic feature of acute kidney injury (AKI) and chronic kidney disease (CKD).

• Uric acid in urine: While uric acid is a nitrogenous waste, tracking its presence in urine does not define azotemia. Elevated blood uric acid is known as hyperuricemia, which is linked to gout or kidney stones.
• Blood glucose: This measures blood sugar levels and is used to monitor metabolism, insulin function, and conditions like diabetes mellitus.
• Serum albumin: This measures the primary protein in blood plasma to evaluate a patient''s liver function and overall visceral protein/nutritional status.', '["Blood urea nitrogen","Uric acid in urine","Serum albumin","Blood glucose"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-07T11:43:38.163Z","interval":3,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":2}'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:43:38.163Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.255Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:43:38.163Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756361258901-137', NULL, 'anki-deck-1756357465257-1790432463920-rf85m', 'Non-digestible food ingredients that encourage the growth of beneficial bacteria:', 'Prebiotic', 'Prebiotics are substrates that are selectively utilized by host microorganisms and provide a health benefit. Many are non-digestible carbohydrates, such as certain oligosaccharides and inulin-type fructans, that reach the colon and are fermented by beneficial microorganisms. This fermentation can produce short-chain fatty acids (SCFAs), which contribute to intestinal and metabolic health. Thus, the description in the question corresponds to a prebiotic, not a probiotic. 

For NDLE purposes, distinguish the three terms clearly: 

Prebiotics are substrates that beneficial microorganisms use; 
Probiotics are live microorganisms that provide a health benefit when administered in adequate amounts; and 
Microbiota refers to the community of microorganisms living in a particular environment, such as the gastrointestinal tract.', '["Probiotic","Prebiotic","Microbiota","None of the above"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-07T12:01:32.308Z","interval":3,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":2}'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T12:01:32.308Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.255Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T12:01:32.308Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756361280516-138', NULL, 'anki-deck-1756357465257-1790432463920-rf85m', 'Why is saturated fat called "saturated"?', 'The fatty acid carbon chains are saturated with hydrogen', 'Saturated fatty acids contain only single bonds between adjacent carbon atoms, meaning each carbon atom is fully bonded ("saturated") with hydrogen atoms.', '["The fat is saturated with water","The fatty acid carbon chains are saturated with hydrogen","The fatty acid chains can have more water added","They saturate the body fat when eaten"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 3, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-12T11:40:28.941Z","interval":8,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":3}'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:40:28.941Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.255Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:40:28.941Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756361313665-139', NULL, 'anki-deck-1756357465257-1790432463920-rf85m', 'Vitamin/s known to be at low levels in patients following gluten-free diets', 'All of the above', 'Traditional gluten-containing wheat flour products are heavily fortified and enriched by law with crucial micronutrients, including thiamine, riboflavin, niacin, iron, and folic acid (folate). In contrast, commercially available gluten-free substitute products are frequently manufactured using refined starches (such as tapioca, corn, or rice starch) that are not routinely fortified, leading to chronic dietary deficits.

• Folate (Vitamin B9): Folate deficiency is exceptionally common among individuals adhering to a gluten-free diet (GFD) due to the absence of enriched wheat products, coupled with a typically low intake of alternative whole grains.
• Vitamin B6 and Vitamin B12: Multiple nutritional monitoring studies and clinical reviews indicate that patients on a strict GFD for extended periods show suboptimal plasma concentrations of Vitamin B6 and Vitamin B12. In patients with celiac disease, initial B12 deficiency stems from mucosal malabsorption in the ileum, which can persist if the diet is structurally restrictive or lacking in variety.', '["Folate","Vit B6, Vit B12","Vit B12, folate","All of the above"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-07T11:37:11.480Z","interval":3,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":2}'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:37:11.480Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.255Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:37:11.480Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756361329748-140', NULL, 'anki-deck-1756357465257-1790432463920-rf85m', 'It is formed when two amino acids are bonded together:', 'Dipeptide', 'A dipeptide is formed when two amino acids are joined by a peptide bond. This occurs through a condensation (dehydration) reaction in which the carboxyl group of one amino acid reacts with the amino group of another, releasing one molecule of water. The resulting molecule contains two amino acid residues linked by one peptide bond. 

For NDLE purposes, remember the progression: 
2 amino acids = dipeptide; 3 amino acids = tripeptide; several amino acids = oligopeptide; long chains = polypeptide/protein. 

The number of peptide bonds is also important: two amino acids form one peptide bond, three amino acids form two peptide bonds, and so forth.', '["Alanine","Dissacharide","Protein","Dipeptide"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 3, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-12T11:40:25.120Z","interval":8,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":3}'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:40:25.120Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.255Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:40:25.120Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756361348931-141', NULL, 'anki-deck-1756357465257-1790432463920-rf85m', 'Kidneys control pH through the following processes except:', 'Formation of carbonic acid', 'In the context of the body''s acid-base regulatory systems, the respiratory system (lungs) is the primary organ responsible for manipulating carbonic acid levels. The lungs control pH by exhaling or retaining carbon dioxide (CO₂), which directly dictates the formation and breakdown of volatile carbonic acid (H₂CO₃) in the blood.

The kidneys, on the other hand, handle non-volatile (fixed) acids. Their primary toolset for pH control relies on the active biochemical processes listed in the other options:
• Resorption of sodium ions: In the renal tubules, sodium reabsorption is directly coupled to acid excretion via Na⁺/H⁺ antiporters. The kidney actively reabsorbs sodium back into the bloodstream in exchange for pumping hydrogen ions out.
• Synthesis of ammonia: The renal cells produce ammonia (NH₃) to bind with free hydrogen ions in the urine, trapping them safely as ammonium (NH₄⁺) for excretion.
• Secretion of hydrogen ions: The kidneys physically pump H⁺ ions out of the plasma into the tubular fluid to directly eliminate acid from the body.', '["Formation of carbonic acid","Secretion Of hydrogen ions","Synthesis of ammonia","Resorption of sodium ions"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 3, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-12T11:32:06.203Z","interval":8,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":3}'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:32:06.203Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.255Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:32:06.203Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756361368928-142', NULL, 'anki-deck-1756357465257-1790432463920-rf85m', 'The average respiratory quotient of a mixed diet:', '0.85', 'The respiratory quotient (RQ) is the ratio of carbon dioxide produced to oxygen consumed during metabolism: RQ = VCO₂ / VO₂. The RQ differs according to the macronutrient being oxidized. Carbohydrate has an RQ of 1.0, protein approximately 0.8, and fat approximately 0.7. Because a typical mixed diet supplies a combination of carbohydrate, protein, and fat, its average RQ is approximately 0.8–0.85, with 0.85 being the conventional value used in nutrition and indirect calorimetry questions.', '["0.65","0.85","0.75","0.95"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-07T11:47:00.683Z","interval":3,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":2}'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:47:00.683Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.255Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:47:00.683Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756361387878-143', NULL, 'anki-deck-1756357465257-1790432463920-rf85m', 'Factors that improve LDL to HDL ratio except:', 'Higher intakes of saturated fatty acids', 'Consuming higher amounts of saturated fatty acids raises both low-density lipoprotein (LDL) and high-density lipoprotein (HDL) cholesterol. However, it drives a much larger increase in LDL ("bad") cholesterol compared to HDL. This selectively worsens the overall LDL-to-HDL ratio, increasing cardiovascular risk.

• Moderate R-OH (Alcohol) Consumption: In medical terminology, "R-OH" stands for alcohol. Scientific literature consistently shows that light-to-moderate alcohol intake (such as one drink a day for women or two for men) can moderately elevate HDL cholesterol levels, which helps optimize the blood lipid ratio.
• Antioxidants: Dietary antioxidants (found in abundance in colorful fruits, vegetables, and whole grains) shield circulating LDL particles from undergoing oxidative modification. Because oxidized LDL is what triggers plaque accumulation in artery walls, antioxidants maintain healthy vascular transport and protect the functional efficiency of lipid handling.
• Weight Control: Dropping excess body weight and avoiding abdominal obesity are highly effective ways to manage dyslipidemia. Achieving a healthy weight directly reduces total triglycerides and lowers LDL cholesterol levels while boosting cardioprotective HDL.', '["Weight control","Antioxidants","Moderate R-OH consumption","Higher intakes of saturated fatty acids"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-07T11:42:47.012Z","interval":3,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":2}'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:42:47.012Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.255Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:42:47.012Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756361404260-144', NULL, 'anki-deck-1756357465257-1790432463920-rf85m', 'Sources of trans fatty acid except:', 'Sardines', 'Trans fatty acids can occur naturally in small amounts in milk and butter because ruminant animals produce naturally occurring trans fats through microbial fermentation in the rumen. Trans fats can also be present in commercial baked products such as cookies, particularly when partially hydrogenated oils are used, although their use has been substantially reduced or eliminated in many countries.

Sardines, on the other hand, are oily fish and are primarily valued as sources of omega-3 polyunsaturated fatty acids, particularly EPA and DHA. Although naturally occurring trans fatty acids can be detected in various foods in very small amounts, sardines are not considered a meaningful dietary source of trans fat.', '["Cookies","Milk","Butter","Sardines"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-07T11:56:18.085Z","interval":3,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":2}'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:56:18.085Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.255Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:56:18.085Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756361453258-145', NULL, 'anki-deck-1756357465257-1790432463920-rf85m', 'The main goal of the Krebs cycle is to produce H⁺ and electrons. These by-products are then transferred to the:', 'Electron Transport Chain', 'The Krebs cycle (citric acid cycle/TCA cycle) does not directly produce large amounts of ATP. Its major role is to oxidize acetyl-CoA and capture energy in the form of the reduced electron carriers NADH and FADH₂. These carriers transport high-energy electrons to the electron transport chain (ETC) located in the inner mitochondrial membrane. As electrons move through the ETC, energy is used to pump H⁺ across the inner mitochondrial membrane, creating a proton gradient that drives ATP synthesis through oxidative phosphorylation. 

For NDLE purposes, the wording “H⁺ and electrons” is a little imprecise. The Krebs cycle primarily produces NADH and FADH₂, which carry the high-energy electrons to the ETC. The Krebs cycle also contributes indirectly to proton generation through subsequent oxidative phosphorylation, but free H⁺ is not its principal product.', '["Embden-Meyerhoff Pathway","Electron Transport Chain","Citric Acid Cycle","Cori Cycle"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-05T11:38:51.985Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.3,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:38:51.985Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.255Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:38:51.985Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756361467974-146', NULL, 'anki-deck-1756357465257-1790432463920-rf85m', 'Allowed amount of purine in a purine-restricted diet:', '150 mg', 'A purine-restricted diet is designed to reduce dietary purine exposure in patients with gout, hyperuricemia, or uric acid–related conditions. Traditional diet manuals commonly identify foods containing 150 mg or more of purine per serving as foods to be restricted or avoided. Thus, 150 mg is an important threshold associated with purine-restricted diet teaching.', '["150 mg","250 mg","300 mg","0 mg"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-07T11:41:43.693Z","interval":3,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":2}'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:41:43.693Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.255Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:41:43.693Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756361482939-147', NULL, 'anki-deck-1756357465257-1790432463920-rf85m', 'Inactive precursors of enzymes:', 'Zymogens', 'A zymogen (also frequently referred to as a proenzyme) is a biologically inactive precursor of an enzyme. It requires an organic chemical alteration—most commonly a specific biochemical cleavage event where a piece of its blocking peptide chain is sliced off—to expose its active catalytic site and transform into a functional, active enzyme.

In human nutrition and digestion, zymogens are absolutely critical for structural self-preservation. For example, the pancreas synthesizes and secretes protein-digesting enzymes in their inactive zymogen forms (such as trypsinogen, chymotrypsinogen, and procarboxypeptidase). This ensures that these powerful proteolytic enzymes do not accidentally digest and destroy the cells of the pancreas or the pancreatic duct themselves. They are only activated once they safely reach the lumen of the small intestine.

• Hormones: Hormones are chemical messengers synthesized by endocrine glands that travel through the bloodstream to regulate distant target organs and physiological activities. They are not enzymes or enzyme precursors.
• Nucleic acid : These are large biopolymers (like DNA and RNA) responsible for carrying and translating genetic information within cells.
• Holoenzymes: A holoenzyme is a fully active, structurally complete enzyme that is combined with its necessary coenzyme, cofactor, or prosthetic group. It is the opposite of an inactive precursor.', '["Nucleic acid","Zymogens","Hormones","Holoenzymes"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-07T11:57:07.290Z","interval":3,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":2}'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:57:07.290Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.255Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:57:07.290Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756361500255-148', NULL, 'anki-deck-1756357465257-1790432463920-rf85m', 'Vitamin E maintains prothrombin level in the blood plasma. Vitamin K is an anticoagulant.', 'Both statements are incorrect.', 'Statement 1 is incorrect. Vitamin K, not vitamin E, is essential for the synthesis of several blood-clotting proteins, including prothrombin (factor II), in the liver. Vitamin K functions as a cofactor for the γ-carboxylation of specific glutamate residues on vitamin K–dependent clotting factors, allowing them to participate effectively in coagulation. Vitamin E, in contrast, is primarily recognized for its antioxidant function and does not maintain plasma prothrombin levels as its physiological role. 

Statement 2 is also incorrect. Vitamin K is not an anticoagulant; it supports normal blood coagulation. Vitamin K deficiency can impair clotting-factor activation and increase bleeding tendency. In fact, warfarin, an anticoagulant medication, works partly by inhibiting vitamin K recycling, thereby reducing the activity of vitamin K–dependent clotting factors.', '["Only statement 1 is correct.","Only statement 2 is correct.","Both statements are correct.","Both statements are incorrect."]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-07T11:53:42.352Z","interval":3,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":2}'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:53:42.352Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.255Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:53:42.352Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756361517538-149', NULL, 'anki-deck-1756357465257-1790432463920-rf85m', 'The enzyme pepsin is not denatured in the stomach despite the very acidic environment due to hydrochloric acid (HCl). How can this be?', 'Pepsin is secreted in its precursor form, pepsinogen', 'The stomach avoids auto-digestion and protects its own mucosal lining from premature proteolysis (protein breakdown) by not secreting active pepsin directly. Instead, gastric chief cells synthesize and secrete pepsinogen, which is an inactive precursor or zymogen.

Once pepsinogen enters the gastric lumen, the strongly acidic environment created by hydrochloric acid (HCl) causes the protein chain to unfold. This allows pepsinogen to cleave itself, initiating its conversion into the active, functional enzyme pepsin precisely where it is needed to digest dietary proteins.', '["Pepsin is the only protein enzyme that functions best in an acidic environment","Pepsin is able to resist denaturation by increasing the pH within the stomach, making it less acidic","Pepsin is also digested along with other proteins in the stomach","Pepsin is secreted in its precursor form, pepsinogen"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 3, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-12T11:49:13.269Z","interval":8,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":3}'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:49:13.269Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.255Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:49:13.269Z"'::jsonb)
