INSERT INTO public.flashcards (id, user_id, deck_id, front, back, rationale, options, tags, ndle_subject, difficulty, leitner_box, user_notes, sm2, last_reviewed_at, created_at, updated_at) VALUES
('anki-card-1756361536704-150', NULL, 'anki-deck-1756357465257-1790432463920-rf85m', 'Nutrients that leave the GIT via the lymphatic system:', 'Fats and fat-soluble vitamins', 'During digestion in the small intestine, long-chain fatty acids and fat-soluble vitamins (Vitamins A, D, E, and K) are packed into spherical lipid clusters called micelles. Once absorbed into the intestinal lining cells (enterocytes), they are re-assembled into even larger lipoprotein spheres called chylomicrogens. Because these structures are too bulky to fit through the tiny pores (fenestrations) of standard blood capillaries, they cannot enter the bloodstream directly.

Instead, these large lipid packages slip into lacteals—specialized, wide-pored lymphatic capillaries located at the center of each intestinal villus. The fluid (called chyle) travels slowly through the lymphatic system, completely bypassing the liver at first, before emptying directly into the main bloodstream via the thoracic duct near the heart.

• Water, Proteins, Minerals, and Water-Soluble Vitamins: These nutrients are water-soluble and split into small, single molecules (like glucose, amino acids, and free ions). Because they are small enough to pass easily through the pores of regular capillaries, they exit the GI tract straight into the portal blood circulation, traveling directly to the liver for immediate processing.', '["Proteins and minerals","Water and minerals","All vitamins and minerals","Fats and fat-soluble vitamins"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-07T11:42:57.189Z","interval":3,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":2}'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:42:57.189Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.255Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:42:57.189Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756361564987-151', NULL, 'anki-deck-1756357465257-1790432463920-rf85m', 'A Recommended Dietary Allowance (RDA) represents the:', 'Average amount of a nutrient considered adequate to meet the nutrient needs of all healthy people', 'The Recommended Dietary Allowance (RDA) is the average daily nutrient intake level that is sufficient to meet the nutrient requirements of nearly all (97–98%) healthy individuals in a particular age, sex, and life-stage group. It is primarily used as a goal for planning nutritionally adequate diets for individuals. Importantly, the RDA is not literally enough for 100% of people, because individual nutrient requirements vary. 

The RDA is derived from the Estimated Average Requirement (EAR). When the requirement distribution is approximately normal, the RDA is generally set at the EAR plus two standard deviations, thereby covering the requirements of approximately 97–98% of healthy individuals. In clinical and community nutrition practice, RDAs are useful for planning diets, while the EAR is generally more appropriate for assessing the adequacy of nutrient intakes in groups.', '["Highest amount of a nutrient that appears safe for most healthy people","Average amount of a nutrient that will maintain a specific biochemical or physiological function in half of the population","Average amount of a nutrient considered adequate to meet the nutrient needs of all healthy people","Lowest amount of a nutrient considered adequate to meet the nutrient needs of all healthy people"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-07T11:29:38.495Z","interval":3,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":2}'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:29:38.495Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.255Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:29:38.495Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756361621252-152', NULL, 'anki-deck-1756357465257-1790432463920-rf85m', 'When the body''s glycogen stores become depleted during fasting, the body will synthesize glucose primarily from:', 'Glucogenic amino acid', 'When the body''s liver glycogen stores are completely depleted during an extended fast (typically after 12 to 24 hours), blood glucose levels must be strictly maintained to supply glucose-dependent organs like the brain. The liver shifts completely to gluconeogenesis—the de novo creation of glucose from non-carbohydrate precursors.

Muscle tissue is broken down via proteolysis, releasing amino acids into the bloodstream. Glucogenic amino acids (such as alanine and glutamine) are transported to the liver, where their carbon skeletons are converted into pyruvate or TCA cycle intermediates, directly fueling the pathway to synthesize new glucose.

• Fatty acids: Even though adipose tissue releases abundant free fatty acids during fasting, the catabolism of even-chain fatty acids produces acetyl-CoA. In humans, acetyl-CoA cannot be converted into pyruvate because the pyruvate dehydrogenase reaction is irreversible, meaning fatty acids cannot be used to synthesize glucose. (Note: Only the tiny glycerol backbone of a triglyceride can enter gluconeogenesis, not the fatty acid chains themselves).
• Ketone bodies: Ketone bodies (such as acetoacetate and beta-hydroxybutyrate) are synthesized from fatty acid-derived acetyl-CoA in the liver. They serve as an alternative fuel source for the brain and heart during starvation to conserve protein, but they cannot be chemically reversed back into glucose.
• Cholesterol: Cholesterol is a structural component of cell membranes and a precursor for steroid hormones and bile acids, but it plays absolutely no role in energy production or glucose synthesis.', '["Fatty acid","Glucogenic amino acid","Ketone bodies","Cholesterol"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 3, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-11T11:29:08.275Z","interval":7,"easeFactor":2.3,"repetitions":3}'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:29:08.275Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.255Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:29:08.275Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756361640799-153', NULL, 'anki-deck-1756357465257-1790432463920-rf85m', 'Role of villi and microvilli in the small intestine is to:', 'Increase the surface area for nutrient absorption', 'The villi and microvilli are specialized structures lining the small intestine that greatly increase the surface area available for nutrient absorption. Villi are finger-like projections of the intestinal mucosa, while microvilli are microscopic projections of the apical membrane of enterocytes that form the brush border. Together, they maximize contact between digested nutrients and absorptive epithelial cells, making the small intestine highly efficient at nutrient absorption. 

The villi also contain blood capillaries and a central lymphatic vessel called a lacteal. Water-soluble nutrients such as glucose and amino acids generally enter the blood capillaries, whereas most dietary lipids are incorporated into chylomicrons and enter the lymphatic system through the lacteals. This structural organization is important in understanding why the small intestine is the principal site of nutrient absorption.', '["Produce all the enzymes needed for digestion","Slow down the movement of food","Increase the surface area for nutrient absorption","Secrete water in the chyme"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-07T11:50:27.362Z","interval":3,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":2}'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:50:27.362Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.255Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:50:27.362Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756361689247-154', NULL, 'anki-deck-1756357465257-1790432463920-rf85m', 'A reduced blood albumin level leads to:', 'Edema', 'Albumin is the primary protein found in blood plasma. Its main physiological job is to generate and maintain colloid osmotic pressure (also called oncotic pressure) inside the blood vessels. This pressure acts like a magnet that holds water inside the bloodstream.

When blood albumin levels drop significantly (hypoalbuminemia)—which is common in liver failure, kidney disease (nephrotic syndrome), or severe malnutrition—the oncotic pressure drops with it. Without enough albumin to hold the water inside, fluid leaks out of the capillaries and into the surrounding interstitial tissues.

This abnormal collection of fluid in the tissue spaces causes noticeable swelling, known clinically as edema.

• Alkalosis: Alkalosis refers to an abnormally high, basic blood pH (above 7.45). Albumin does act as a minor blood buffer, but a low level of albumin does not systematically push the entire body into a state of metabolic or respiratory alkalosis. Blood pH is primarily managed by your lungs (carbon dioxide) and kidneys (bicarbonate).
• Dehydration: Dehydration means a total body depletion of water and fluids, usually caused by poor intake, sweating, or vomiting. In hypoalbuminemia, the patient is not lacking water—they actually have plenty of fluid, but it is just trapped in the wrong place (leaking out into the tissues rather than staying inside the blood vessels).
• Acidosis: Acidosis refers to an abnormally low, acidic blood pH (below 7.35). Because albumin molecules carry a slight negative charge and normally bind to a tiny amount of hydrogen ions, a severe loss of albumin would theoretically cause a very minor drop in unmeasured anions, but it never causes clinical metabolic or respiratory acidosis.', '["Dehydration","Edema","Acidosis","Alkalosis"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 3, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-12T12:03:54.778Z","interval":8,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":3}'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T12:03:54.778Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.255Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T12:03:54.778Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756361707730-155', NULL, 'anki-deck-1756357465257-1790432463920-rf85m', 'Positive nitrogen balance can be observed during:', 'Pregnancy', 'Nitrogen balance reflects the relationship between nitrogen intake, primarily from dietary protein, and nitrogen losses, mainly through urinary nitrogen excretion. Positive nitrogen balance occurs when nitrogen intake exceeds nitrogen losses, indicating that the body is retaining protein and synthesizing new tissue. This occurs during periods of growth and tissue building, including pregnancy, childhood, adolescence, and recovery from severe nutritional depletion. 

During pregnancy, positive nitrogen balance supports the synthesis of maternal tissues and the growth of the fetus, placenta, and maternal blood volume and other tissues. In contrast, fever, surgery, and shock are generally associated with physiological stress that increases protein catabolism and nitrogen losses, resulting in negative nitrogen balance, particularly when the stress response is significant or prolonged.', '["Fever","Surgery","Shock","Pregnancy"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-07T11:54:16.228Z","interval":3,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":2}'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:54:16.228Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.255Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:54:16.228Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756361722945-156', NULL, 'anki-deck-1756357465257-1790432463920-rf85m', 'Vessels where the exchange of oxygen, nutrients and waste products take place.', 'Capillaries', 'Capillaries are the smallest and most numerous blood vessels in the human body, serving as the functional "transfer stations" connecting arterioles to venules.

Unlike thick, muscular arteries or larger veins, the walls of capillaries are only one cell layer thick, composed entirely of a single layer of endothelial cells. This extremely thin barrier allows oxygen, glucose, amino acids, and vital hormones to readily pass from the bloodstream out into the surrounding body tissues via passive diffusion.

At the exact same time, cellular metabolic waste products (like carbon dioxide and urea) pass in the opposite direction—from the tissue cells back into the capillary lumen—so they can be safely transported away for filtration and excretion.

• Veins: Veins have relatively thick walls with one-way valves designed exclusively to carry deoxygenated blood under low pressure back up toward the heart. Their thick structural layers prevent any direct transfer of oxygen or nutrients.
• Arteries: Arteries have highly elastic, thick, muscular walls built to withstand the high-pressure surges of oxygenated blood being pumped directly away from the heart. Their dense structure completely prevents the diffusion of gases or nutrients.', '["Veins","Arteries","Capillaries","Veins and capillaries"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-07T11:58:31.976Z","interval":3,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":2}'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:58:31.976Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.255Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:58:31.976Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756361741563-157', NULL, 'anki-deck-1756357465257-1790432463920-rf85m', 'The second stage of carbohydrate metabolism which takes place in the mitochondria and requires oxygen.', 'Oxidative decarboxylation', 'In nutritional biochemistry and board examination frameworks, aerobic carbohydrate catabolism is traditionally classified into sequential stages:
	1. Stage 1: Glycolysis (anaerobic process taking place entirely in the cytoplasm).
	2. Stage 2: Oxidative decarboxylation (the transition phase moving into the Krebs cycle/TCA cycle).
	3. Stage 3: Oxidative phosphorylation via the Electron Transport Chain (ETC).

Once glycolysis yields pyruvate in the cytosol, the pyruvate is actively transported into the mitochondria. Here, the pyruvate dehydrogenase complex carries out oxidative decarboxylation, converting pyruvate into Acetyl-CoA while stripping away carbon dioxide and generating NADH. Because this pathway feeds directly into the aerobic respiration chain, it strictly requires oxygen to proceed.

• Glycolysis: This is the first stage of carbohydrate breakdown. It takes place in the anaerobic environment of the fluid cytoplasm (cytosol), meaning it does not require oxygen or mitochondrial machinery to function.
• Hexose monophosphate shunt: Also known as the Pentose Phosphate Pathway (PPP), this is an alternative pathway parallel to glycolysis used to generate NADPH and ribose 5-phosphate. It does not serve as the primary second stage of energy-yielding glucose metabolism.
• Oxidative phosphorylation: This is the final (third) stage of cellular respiration where the vast majority of ATP is structurally synthesized by ATP synthase along the inner mitochondrial membrane.', '["Oxidative decarboxylation","Oxidative phosphorylation","Hexose manophasphate shunt","Glycolysis"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-07T11:14:29.324Z","interval":3,"easeFactor":2.3,"repetitions":2}'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:14:29.324Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.255Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:14:29.324Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756362183952-158', NULL, 'anki-deck-1756357465257-1790432463920-rf85m', 'End products of the anaerobic phase of glucose metabolism', 'Pyruvate and lactic acid', 'The anaerobic phase of glucose metabolism refers primarily to glycolysis, which occurs in the cytoplasm and does not require oxygen directly. Glycolysis converts one molecule of glucose into two molecules of pyruvate, producing a net gain of 2 ATP and 2 NADH. When oxygen availability is limited or when glycolytic flux exceeds mitochondrial oxidative capacity, pyruvate can be reduced to lactate by lactate dehydrogenase. This regenerates NAD⁺, allowing glycolysis to continue producing ATP.', '["Pyruvate and lactic acid","Acetyl CoA","Carbon dioxide and water","All of the above"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-07T11:53:53.405Z","interval":3,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":2}'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:53:53.405Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.255Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:53:53.405Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756362201000-159', NULL, 'anki-deck-1756357465257-1790432463920-rf85m', 'All contain carbohydrates except:', 'Lean meat', 'Carbohydrates are found in a wide range of foods, particularly plant foods and certain dairy products. Wheat bread contains starch, while ripe fruits contain naturally occurring sugars such as glucose and fructose. Pasteurized milk also contains carbohydrate in the form of lactose, the naturally occurring sugar in milk. Pasteurization does not remove or substantially eliminate the lactose in milk. 

Lean meat, in contrast, contains primarily protein and fat and provides essentially no carbohydrate. Although very small amounts of carbohydrate-related compounds may be present in animal tissues, meat is generally classified as a negligible-carbohydrate food for nutrition and dietary planning purposes.', '["Wheat bread","Ripe fruit","Lean meat","Pasteurized milk"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-07T11:42:08.273Z","interval":3,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":2}'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:42:08.273Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.255Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:42:08.273Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756362218484-160', NULL, 'anki-deck-1756357465257-1790432463920-rf85m', 'In the WHO-BMI cut off point, a BMI of 35.99 is classified as:', 'Obese Class II', 'According to the WHO International BMI Classification: Underweight <18.5, Normal 18.5–24.9, Overweight/Pre-obese 25.0–29.9, Obese Class I 30.0–34.9, Obese Class II 35.0–39.9, and Obese Class III ≥40.0 kg/m².', '["Overweight","Pre-obese","Obese Class I","Obese Class II"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-07T11:51:38.383Z","interval":3,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":2}'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:51:38.383Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.255Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:51:38.383Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756362240252-161', NULL, 'anki-deck-1756357465257-1790432463920-rf85m', 'Nutritional management when a patient experiences vomiting', 'Withhold food until vomiting subsides', 'During active vomiting, the immediate nutritional priority is to allow the gastrointestinal tract to rest while preventing dehydration. Food is generally withheld temporarily until vomiting subsides, after which oral intake can be gradually resumed as tolerated. Once the patient can tolerate fluids, small, frequent amounts of fluid are preferred rather than large volumes at one time. 

Oral rehydration solutions may be particularly useful when fluid and electrolyte losses are significant. After vomiting has stopped, food should be reintroduced gradually, usually beginning with small portions and advancing according to tolerance. Large amounts of fluid with meals can worsen fullness and nausea, while hot, strongly scented, greasy, or spicy foods may be poorly tolerated. Cold or room-temperature foods may sometimes be better tolerated because they have less odor.', '["Liberal fluid intake during meals","Withhold food until vomiting subsides","All of the above","Serve hot soup"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-07T11:45:54.802Z","interval":3,"easeFactor":2.3,"repetitions":2}'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:45:54.802Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.255Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:45:54.802Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756362268798-162', NULL, 'anki-deck-1756357465257-1790432463920-rf85m', 'Which of the following is/are not true about dietary management of diabetes mellitus?
I. The type of carbohydrate is more important than the total amount of carbohydrates
II. The total amount of carbohydrate consumed is more important than the source of carbohydrate
III. People with diabetes mellitus can eat candies and other sugary foods.', 'I only', 'Statement I is NOT true because, in diabetes management, the total amount of carbohydrate consumed is a major determinant of postprandial blood glucose, particularly when considering carbohydrate counting and matching carbohydrate intake with insulin or other glucose-lowering therapy. The type and quality of carbohydrate are also important because fiber content, degree of processing, and food composition can affect the glycemic response, but carbohydrate type is not generally considered more important than the total amount consumed. Thus, saying that type is more important than total amount is an inaccurate statement. 

Statement II is considered true in the context of the fundamental principle of carbohydrate management: the amount of carbohydrate consumed has a substantial and predictable effect on blood glucose. This does not mean that carbohydrate source is unimportant; whole grains, legumes, vegetables, fruits, and other minimally processed carbohydrate-rich foods are generally preferred over highly refined foods and foods high in added sugars because of their fiber and nutrient content. 

Statement III is also true because people with diabetes do not have to completely eliminate candies and other sugary foods. These can be incorporated occasionally into an individualized meal plan, provided that carbohydrate and overall energy intake are appropriately managed.', '["I only","III only","Both II & III","Both I & II"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-05T11:37:27.304Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.1,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:37:27.304Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.255Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:37:27.304Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756362310496-163', NULL, 'anki-deck-1756357465257-1790432463920-rf85m', 'Consistent carbohydrate diet means:', 'The amount of carbohydrate consumed and the timing of meals and snacks are consistent', 'A consistent carbohydrate diet is a meal-planning approach commonly used in the management of diabetes mellitus. Rather than eliminating carbohydrates, the goal is to maintain a relatively consistent amount of carbohydrate at meals and snacks from day to day, while keeping meal timing regular. This helps minimize fluctuations in blood glucose levels and improves the effectiveness of diabetes medications, particularly insulin and insulin secretagogues. The emphasis is on consistency, not on consuming a fixed amount of carbohydrates for every individual.', '["The type and amount of carbohydrate in the diet must be consistent as well as the timing of meals and snacks","The amount of carbohydrate consumed and the timing of meals and snacks are consistent","In case of hypoglycemia, 15 grams of carbohydrates are given every 15 minutes to achieve consistent blood sugar level","45 to 75 grams of carbohydrates are given at each meal to maintain normal blood sugar level"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-07T12:01:01.623Z","interval":3,"easeFactor":2.3,"repetitions":2}'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T12:01:01.623Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.255Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T12:01:01.623Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756362330163-164', NULL, 'anki-deck-1756357465257-1790432463920-rf85m', 'During glycolysis and TCA, glucose is first broken down into _____, then _____, and finally
_____for use in the body.', 'pyruvate, acetyl CoA, energy', 'During glucose oxidation, glycolysis first converts glucose into pyruvate in the cytoplasm. Each glucose molecule produces two molecules of pyruvate. 

Under aerobic conditions, pyruvate enters the mitochondria and undergoes oxidative decarboxylation, producing acetyl-CoA. Acetyl-CoA then enters the tricarboxylic acid (TCA) cycle, where its carbon atoms are progressively oxidized and energy is captured primarily in the form of NADH and FADH₂. The reduced electron carriers generated during glycolysis, pyruvate oxidation, and the TCA cycle subsequently donate electrons to the electron transport chain, driving oxidative phosphorylation and the production of ATP. 

Thus, the simplified sequence tested by this question is glucose → pyruvate → acetyl-CoA → energy (ATP).', '["pyruvate, acetyl CoA, energy","amino acids, acetyl CoA, energy","acetyl CoA, pyruvate, energy","glycerol, pyruvate, energy"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-07T11:43:29.289Z","interval":3,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":2}'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:43:29.289Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.255Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:43:29.289Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756362355761-165', NULL, 'anki-deck-1756357465257-1790432463920-rf85m', 'What is the fate of excess intake of dietary protein?', 'After absorption, the extra amino acids will be rapidly degraded', 'Unlike carbohydrates and fats, the body does not have a specialized storage depot for excess amino acids or dietary protein. After dietary protein is digested and absorbed, amino acids enter the body''s amino acid pool and are used for protein synthesis and other metabolic functions. When amino acid availability exceeds the body''s immediate needs for protein synthesis and other required compounds, the excess amino acids are deaminated. Their nitrogen is converted primarily to urea in the liver and excreted in the urine, while their carbon skeletons can be used for energy or converted into glucose, ketone bodies, or other metabolites. 

Therefore, consuming more protein than needed does not result in the body storing the extra protein as protein for later use. The body continually regulates amino acid metabolism according to physiological needs. Excess energy from protein can ultimately contribute to energy storage as body fat if overall energy intake exceeds expenditure, but this occurs after amino acid catabolism, not through storage of excess dietary protein as a protein reserve.', '["After absorption, the liver will store the extra amino acids","After absorption, the extra amino acids will be rapidly degraded","Digestion will be decreased by 30-60% resulting in less absorption","After absorption, extra proteins will be synthesized and stored for use when protein intake returns to normal"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-07T11:43:46.260Z","interval":3,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":2}'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:43:46.260Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.255Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:43:46.260Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756362376044-166', NULL, 'anki-deck-1756357465257-1790432463920-rf85m', 'The enzyme sucrase acts on sucrose to produce:', 'Glucose and fructose', 'Sucrose is a disaccharide composed of one molecule of glucose and one molecule of fructose. The brush-border enzyme sucrase hydrolyzes the glycosidic bond in sucrose, releasing glucose and fructose as the final monosaccharide products. These monosaccharides can then be absorbed by the small intestine through specific transport mechanisms. 

Sucrase is part of the intestinal sucrase-isomaltase complex located on the brush border of enterocytes. The resulting glucose and fructose are absorbed primarily in the small intestine and enter the portal circulation, with glucose and fructose using different transport proteins. This illustrates the importance of brush-border enzymes in completing carbohydrate digestion before absorption.', '["Glucose and galactose","Glucose","Glucose and fructose","Maltose"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-07T11:30:06.375Z","interval":3,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":2}'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:30:06.375Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.255Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:30:06.375Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756362393075-167', NULL, 'anki-deck-1756357465257-1790432463920-rf85m', 'Essential amino acids except:', 'Glutamine', 'Histidine, leucine, and lysine are essential amino acids, meaning they cannot be synthesized by the human body in sufficient amounts to meet physiological needs and therefore must be obtained from the diet. Histidine is particularly important for growth and tissue repair, leucine is a branched-chain amino acid that plays an important role in stimulating muscle protein synthesis, and lysine is involved in protein synthesis and several other metabolic functions. 

Glutamine is classified as a nonessential amino acid under normal physiological conditions because the body can synthesize it from glutamate and ammonia. However, during severe illness, trauma, burns, or other metabolic stress, glutamine requirements may increase substantially and endogenous synthesis may not meet demand. It is therefore often described as conditionally essential in certain clinical situations.', '["Histidine","Leucine","Glutamine","Lysine"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-07T11:54:44.754Z","interval":3,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":2}'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:54:44.754Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.255Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:54:44.754Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756362418324-168', NULL, 'anki-deck-1756357465257-1790432463920-rf85m', 'Electron-carrier molecules that are used in the electron-transport chain to generate additional ATP are:', 'NADH and FADH2', 'NADH and FADH₂ are reduced electron carriers generated during several stages of cellular respiration, including glycolysis, pyruvate oxidation, and the TCA cycle. They carry high-energy electrons to the electron transport chain (ETC) located in the inner mitochondrial membrane. As electrons move through the ETC, the released energy is used to pump protons across the membrane, creating a proton gradient that drives ATP synthesis through ATP synthase. 

NADH donates electrons to Complex I, whereas FADH₂ donates electrons through Complex II. Because FADH₂ enters the ETC downstream of Complex I, it generally results in the production of less ATP than NADH. Modern estimates are approximately 2.5 ATP per NADH and 1.5 ATP per FADH₂, although the exact ATP yield can vary depending on cellular conditions and the pathway used to transfer cytosolic reducing equivalents into the mitochondria.', '["Glucose and ketone bodies","Acetyl-CoA and amino acids","NADH and FADH2","Pyruvic acid and lactic acid"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-07T11:36:19.454Z","interval":3,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":2}'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:36:19.454Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.255Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:36:19.454Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756362436457-169', NULL, 'anki-deck-1756357465257-1790432463920-rf85m', 'An essential fatty acid derived from plant oils such as corn oil:', 'Linoleic acid', 'Linoleic acid (18:2 n-6) is an essential omega-6 polyunsaturated fatty acid abundant in vegetable seed oils (corn, sunflower, safflower) that cannot be synthesized de novo by humans.', '["Linoleic acid","Linolenic acid","Arachidonic acid","Palmitic acid"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-07T11:48:17.983Z","interval":3,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":2}'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:48:17.983Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.255Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:48:17.983Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756362452206-170', NULL, 'anki-deck-1756357465257-1790432463920-rf85m', 'A 20% intravenous fat emulsion provides approximately how many kilocalories per 100 mL?', '200', 'A 20% intravenous lipid emulsion (ILE) contains approximately 20 g of lipid per 100 mL and is conventionally estimated to provide 2 kcal/mL, or approximately 200 kcal per 100 mL. This is the value commonly used when calculating the energy contribution of IV lipid emulsions in parenteral nutrition. 

A 10% ILE provides approximately 1.1 kcal/mL, while a 20% ILE provides approximately 2 kcal/mL. The distinction is important because the caloric value assigned to IV lipid emulsions is not calculated simply by multiplying grams of lipid by 9 kcal/g, as would commonly be done for dietary fat. 

Commercial IV lipid emulsions contain triglycerides along with emulsifying agents and other components, and their labeled caloric contribution is based on the formulation. For PN calculations, therefore, use the manufacturer/standard clinical kcal/mL value for the specific lipid emulsion.', '["110","200","180","20"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-07T11:37:16.128Z","interval":3,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":2}'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:37:16.128Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.255Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:37:16.128Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756362467607-171', NULL, 'anki-deck-1756357465257-1790432463920-rf85m', 'Which of the following contain the least amount of dietary fiber?', 'Well-milled rice', 'Well-milled rice contains the least dietary fiber among the choices because the milling process removes the outer layers of the rice grain, including much of the bran and germ, where dietary fiber is concentrated. As a result, well-milled or highly polished white rice contains substantially less fiber than less-refined grains and many plant foods. This makes well-milled rice a low-fiber carbohydrate source. 

In comparison, sinigwelas (Java plum), yellow camote (sweet potato), and lakatan (banana) are plant foods that retain their cellular structures and therefore provide dietary fiber. Their exact fiber content varies with variety, maturity, preparation, and serving size, but they generally provide more dietary fiber than well-milled rice. For NDLE purposes, the key concept is that refining and milling grains removes fiber-rich outer layers.', '["Sinigwelas","Yellow camote","Lakatan","Well-milled rice"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-07T12:05:04.352Z","interval":3,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":2}'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T12:05:04.352Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.255Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T12:05:04.352Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756362489204-172', NULL, 'anki-deck-1756357465257-1790432463920-rf85m', 'The following inhibit the absorption of iron EXCEPT:', 'Fructose', 'Iron absorption is influenced by both the chemical form of iron and components of the meal. Nonheme iron, the major form found in plant foods and fortified foods, is particularly affected by dietary factors. 

Coffee and tea can substantially reduce nonheme iron absorption because they contain polyphenolic compounds that bind iron and reduce its bioavailability. Soy products may also reduce nonheme iron absorption because of compounds such as phytate, although the magnitude of the effect depends on the food and overall dietary pattern. 

Fructose, in contrast, does not inhibit iron absorption and may actually enhance nonheme iron absorption under certain conditions. It has been associated with improved iron absorption, although its effect is less prominent than that of vitamin C.', '["Fructose","Soy products (if major dietary protein is from soy)","Coffee","Tea"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-07T11:37:36.680Z","interval":3,"easeFactor":2.3,"repetitions":2}'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:37:36.680Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.255Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:37:36.680Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756362505171-173', NULL, 'anki-deck-1756357465257-1790432463920-rf85m', 'Which of the following meats has the lowest kilocalories from fat per serving?', 'Chicken leg', 'Among these choices, chicken leg generally provides the lowest amount of fat-derived energy per standard serving when compared with pork pata, chicken wings, and Vienna sausage. Chicken leg is a relatively nutrient-dense source of protein, although its fat content varies depending on whether the skin is consumed and the specific cut and preparation method. Removing the skin substantially reduces its fat and total energy content. 

Pork pata and chicken wings tend to contain more fat per edible serving, while Vienna sausage is a processed meat product that commonly contains a relatively high proportion of fat. For nutrition assessment and meal planning, it is important to consider the edible portion, preparation method, skin, and processing because these can substantially alter the fat and energy content of meat products.', '["Pork pata","Chicken leg","Chicken wings","Vienna sausage"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 3, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-12T12:05:33.124Z","interval":8,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":3}'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T12:05:33.124Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.255Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T12:05:33.124Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756362525687-174', NULL, 'anki-deck-1756357465257-1790432463920-rf85m', 'Sodium equivalent of the low sodium diet?', 'Less than 2g sodium/day', 'A low-sodium diet generally limits sodium intake to approximately 2,000 mg/day or less, particularly in clinical nutrition for conditions such as hypertension, heart failure, and certain renal or hepatic disorders. Sodium restriction reduces sodium and water retention and may help control blood pressure and fluid balance. Importantly, the exact sodium prescription should be individualized according to the patient''s diagnosis, clinical status, and current guidelines.', '["Less than 2g sodium/day","1 to 3g sodium/day","2g sodium/day","No added salt"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-22T14:47:13.431Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-21T14:47:13.431Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.255Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-22T10:26:40.397Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756362542518-175', NULL, 'anki-deck-1756357465257-1790432463920-rf85m', 'Using the Tannhauser’s method, a 5’6” person has an ideal body weight of:', '61 kg', 'Step-by-Step Calculation

Tannhauser''s Method (widely used in the Philippines to estimate Desirable Body Weight or DBW) utilizes the following formula:
DBW (kg) = (Height in cm - 100) - [10% x (Height in cm - 100)]

1. Convert height to centimeters:
• 5 feet 6 inches = 66 inches
• 66 inches x 2.54 cm/inch = 167.64 cm

2. Calculate initial DBW baseline:
• 167.64 - 100 = 67.64 kg

3. Deduct 10% for the Filipino/Asian frame adjustment:
• 10% of 67.64 = 6.764 kg
• 67.64 kg - 6.764 kg = 60.876 kg

Rounding 60.876 kg to the nearest whole number gives 61 kg.', '["58 kg","72 kg","61 kg","None of the above"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-22T14:43:18.419Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-21T14:43:18.419Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.255Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-21T14:43:18.602Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756362557616-176', NULL, 'anki-deck-1756357465257-1790432463920-rf85m', 'The desirable body weight of a 4-year-old girl is:', '16 kg', 'For children 1–6 years of age, a commonly taught formula for estimating expected or desirable body weight is Weight (kg) = (age in years × 2) + 8. 

For a 4-year-old child, the calculation is therefore (4 × 2) + 8 = 16 kg. Thus, the answer is 16 kg. This is a quick estimation formula commonly used in nutrition education and examination settings.', '["12 kg","14.5 kg","16 kg","20 kg"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-07T11:54:11.052Z","interval":3,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":2}'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:54:11.052Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.255Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:54:11.052Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756362581783-177', NULL, 'anki-deck-1756357465257-1790432463920-rf85m', 'A diabetic patient with HbA1c test result of 10% is indicative of _____ glucose control.', 'Poor', 'An HbA1c of 10% indicates poor glycemic control and reflects substantially elevated average blood glucose over approximately the preceding 2–3 months. HbA1c measures the percentage of hemoglobin that has glucose attached to it (glycated hemoglobin). The higher the average blood glucose concentration, the greater the proportion of glycated hemoglobin. An HbA1c of 10% corresponds to an estimated average glucose (eAG) of approximately 240 mg/dL (13.3 mmol/L). 

For most nonpregnant adults with diabetes, the commonly recommended treatment goal is an HbA1c of <7%, although targets should be individualized according to age, comorbidities, hypoglycemia risk, life expectancy, and patient preferences.', '["Good","Fair","Moderate","Poor"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-07T11:42:20.657Z","interval":3,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":2}'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:42:20.657Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.255Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:42:20.657Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756362596950-178', NULL, 'anki-deck-1756357465257-1790432463920-rf85m', 'A deficiency of UDP-glucose:galactose-1-phosphate uridylyltransferase (GALT) will lead to an accumulation of:', 'Galactose', 'UDP-glucose:galactose-1-phosphate uridylyltransferase (GALT) is a key enzyme in the Leloir pathway, which metabolizes galactose into glucose-derived metabolites. GALT normally converts galactose-1-phosphate + UDP-glucose → UDP-galactose + glucose-1-phosphate. 

When GALT is deficient, as occurs in classic galactosemia, galactose-1-phosphate accumulates in tissues, while galactose is also elevated in the blood. Some excess galactose is converted to galactitol, which contributes to complications such as cataracts.', '["Glucose","Galactose","Glycogen","UDP"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-07T11:36:06.678Z","interval":3,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":2}'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:36:06.678Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.255Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:36:06.678Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756362614717-179', NULL, 'anki-deck-1756357465257-1790432463920-rf85m', 'During aerobic respiration, the decomposition of one glucose molecule produces:', '38 molecules of ATP', 'Using the traditional biochemical calculation commonly taught in nutrition and NDLE review materials, the complete aerobic oxidation of one glucose molecule produces a maximum of 38 ATP. 

This calculation assigns 3 ATP per NADH and 2 ATP per FADH₂. Glycolysis produces 2 ATP and 2 NADH; pyruvate oxidation produces 2 NADH; and the TCA cycle produces 2 ATP (or GTP), 6 NADH, and 2 FADH₂. Altogether, this gives 38 ATP per glucose under the traditional accounting system.', '["40 molecules of ATP","46 molecules of ATP","38 molecules of ATP","48 molecules of ATP"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 3, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-12T11:45:41.540Z","interval":8,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":3}'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:45:41.540Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.255Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:45:41.540Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756362641915-180', NULL, 'anki-deck-1756357465257-1790432463920-rf85m', 'To increase appetite, which of the following is/are recommended?
I. Relaxed eating
II. Small frequent feedings
III. Eating favorite food', 'All of the above', 'All three strategies can help improve appetite and food intake, particularly in patients experiencing poor appetite. Relaxed eating can make meals more pleasant and reduce anxiety or stress associated with eating. Creating an appealing mealtime environment and avoiding pressure to eat may encourage intake. Small, frequent meals are useful because a large meal may be overwhelming for someone with a poor appetite; smaller portions are often better tolerated and can help increase total daily intake. Offering favorite or preferred foods is another practical strategy because food preference and palatability strongly influence willingness to eat. 

In clinical nutrition, these approaches are commonly incorporated into individualized nutrition care, especially when the immediate goal is to increase energy and nutrient intake. However, favorite foods should still be selected with consideration for the patient''s medical condition and dietary requirements.', '["II only","II and III only","All of the above","None of the above"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-07T12:02:50.904Z","interval":3,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":2}'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T12:02:50.904Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.255Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T12:02:50.904Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756362658815-181', NULL, 'anki-deck-1756357465257-1790432463920-rf85m', 'Generally, men have higher BMR/kg than women because:', 'they have more lean tissues/unit weight', 'Men generally possess a greater percentage of lean body mass (fat-free skeletal muscle and organ tissue) per unit of body weight compared to women, which is the primary determinant of higher Basal Metabolic Rate (BMR).', '["they are more active","they have more body fat","they have more lean tissues/unit weight","they are heavier"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-07T11:37:21.308Z","interval":3,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":2}'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:37:21.308Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.255Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:37:21.308Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756362674847-182', NULL, 'anki-deck-1756357465257-1790432463920-rf85m', 'A sensible weight reducing program includes:', 'diet, exercise and behavior modification', 'Comprehensive and evidence-based weight management requires a sustainable multidisciplinary approach incorporating modest caloric restriction (diet), regular physical activity (exercise), and cognitive behavioral therapy (behavior modification).', '["drugs to effect rapid weight loss","use of formula diets to ensure appropriate calorie intake","use of herbal preparations as an adjunct to diet","diet, exercise and behavior modification"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 3, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-12T11:48:40.476Z","interval":8,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":3}'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:48:40.476Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.255Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:48:40.476Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756362689728-183', NULL, 'anki-deck-1756357465257-1790432463920-rf85m', 'In conditions characterized by hypochlorhydria or achlorhydria, a suitable diet is one that is:', 'easy to digest or consists of predigested protein like protein hydrolysates', 'Hypochlorhydria refers to reduced gastric acid secretion, while achlorhydria refers to an absence or near-absence of hydrochloric acid (HCl) in the stomach. Gastric acid contributes to the denaturation of dietary proteins and facilitates the conversion of pepsinogen to pepsin, which begins protein digestion in the stomach. When gastric acid is markedly reduced or absent, protein digestion in the stomach may be impaired. 

Therefore, traditionally, a diet emphasizing easily digested foods and, when clinically indicated, predigested protein such as protein hydrolysates may be recommended. However, the important modern clinical point is that hypochlorhydria or achlorhydria does not automatically require a low-protein diet or routine use of protein hydrolysates. Protein digestion continues in the small intestine through pancreatic and brush-border enzymes. Management should focus on the underlying cause and the patient''s nutritional status.', '["low in fiber","low in protein","easy to digest or consists of predigested protein like protein hydrolysates","acid-ash"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 3, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-12T12:04:42.379Z","interval":8,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":3}'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T12:04:42.379Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.255Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T12:04:42.379Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756362706261-184', NULL, 'anki-deck-1756357465257-1790432463920-rf85m', 'Foot care is an important aspect of diabetes education of patients to avoid:', 'wounds on the feet which may become gangrenous', 'Proper diabetic foot care is essential because diabetes can cause peripheral neuropathy and peripheral arterial disease, both of which increase the risk of foot injury and delayed wound healing. Neuropathy may reduce protective sensation, so a patient may not notice a blister, cut, burn, pressure injury, or poorly fitting footwear. Reduced blood flow can further impair tissue healing and increase the risk of infection. 

Regular foot inspection, appropriate footwear, foot hygiene, and prompt attention to injuries help prevent minor problems from progressing to serious ulcers and, in severe cases, gangrene and amputation.', '["microvascular complications","edema of the lower extremities","numbness of the lower extremities","wounds on the feet which may become gangrenous"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 3, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-12T12:03:03.385Z","interval":8,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":3}'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T12:03:03.385Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.255Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T12:03:03.385Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756362719810-185', NULL, 'anki-deck-1756357465257-1790432463920-rf85m', 'If a person wants to improve his/her fiber intake, which of the following food would be best?', 'Saluyot', 'Saluyot (jute leaves) is the best choice among the options because leafy vegetables such as saluyot provide a relatively high amount of dietary fiber compared with the other vegetables listed. Saluyot is also a good source of several micronutrients and is commonly consumed as a vegetable in the Philippines. Increasing intake of fiber-rich vegetables can contribute to improved bowel function and overall diet quality. 

Radish, cucumber, and tomato also contain dietary fiber, but their fiber contribution per comparable edible portion is generally lower than that of saluyot. For practical nutrition counseling, however, the overall dietary pattern matters: a person should obtain fiber from a variety of vegetables, fruits, whole grains, legumes, nuts, and seeds, rather than relying on one food alone.', '["Radish","Saluyot","Cucumber","Tomato"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-07T11:51:44.740Z","interval":3,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":2}'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:51:44.740Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.255Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:51:44.740Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756362740493-186', NULL, 'anki-deck-1756357465257-1790432463920-rf85m', 'Some clinicians recommend egg whites as the sole source of protein for patients with renal failure because:', 'egg white protein has the highest biological value', 'Egg white is considered a high-quality protein because it contains all the essential amino acids in proportions that support human protein needs and has high protein quality. 

In patients with chronic kidney disease (CKD), when protein intake is restricted or carefully prescribed, choosing high-quality protein sources can help provide adequate essential amino acids while limiting the total amount of protein consumed. Egg white is also relatively low in phosphorus compared with the whole egg because most of the egg''s phosphorus is found in the yolk. 

However, egg whites should not routinely be considered the sole protein source for every patient with renal failure. Current CKD nutrition care emphasizes individualized protein and energy prescriptions according to CKD stage, dialysis status, nutritional status, and other clinical factors. Patients receiving dialysis generally have higher protein requirements than patients with non-dialysis CKD. The key concept tested here is high protein quality/biological value, not a therapeutic effect unique to egg white.', '["egg whites are the most acceptable protein source","they have a therapeutic effect","egg white protein has the highest biological value","other proteins are harmful"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 3, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-12T11:51:01.423Z","interval":8,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":3}'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:51:01.423Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.255Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:51:01.423Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756362754910-187', NULL, 'anki-deck-1756357465257-1790432463920-rf85m', 'Elimination diets are used in:', 'Allergy', 'Elimination diets systematically eliminate suspected offending foods/allergens from the diet to observe symptom resolution, serving as a primary diagnostic and therapeutic tool for food allergies and intolerances.', '["Cancer","Allergy","skin disease","gout"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 3, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-12T11:29:41.837Z","interval":8,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":3}'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:29:41.837Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.255Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:29:41.837Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756362772508-188', NULL, 'anki-deck-1756357465257-1790432463920-rf85m', 'A mechanical soft diet includes:', 'easy to chew foods, including foods in the regular diet', 'A mechanical soft diet consists of foods that are soft in texture and easy to chew and swallow, with modification of the texture achieved through methods such as chopping, grinding, mashing, or cooking foods until tender. The goal is to reduce the mechanical effort required to chew food while still allowing a broader variety of foods than a full-liquid or pureed diet. Depending on the patient''s needs, foods from a regular diet can be modified to achieve the appropriate texture. 

A mechanical soft diet is therefore primarily defined by texture and ease of chewing, not by whether foods are liquid or low in fiber. It may be prescribed for patients with difficulty chewing because of dental problems, oral surgery, weakness, or other conditions affecting chewing. The specific texture should always be matched to the patient''s swallowing ability because a mechanical soft diet is not automatically appropriate for someone with dysphagia.', '["soft foods","liquid foods","easy to chew foods, including foods in the regular diet","low fiber foods"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 3, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-12T12:01:37.979Z","interval":8,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":3}'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T12:01:37.979Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.255Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T12:01:37.979Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756362788975-189', NULL, 'anki-deck-1756357465257-1790432463920-rf85m', 'A morbidly obese patient underwent gastric bypass. What classical side effect can the patient experience as a result of the surgery?', 'Dumping syndrome', 'Dumping syndrome is a classic complication of gastric bypass surgery. Because the stomach''s normal reservoir function is altered and food can pass relatively rapidly into the small intestine, particularly after meals high in simple carbohydrates, patients may experience rapid gastric emptying. This can produce early symptoms such as abdominal cramping, bloating, nausea, diarrhea, dizziness, flushing, and tachycardia shortly after eating. The rapid delivery of carbohydrate can also cause a subsequent exaggerated insulin response. 

Dumping syndrome may also have a late phase, typically occurring 1–3 hours after eating, characterized by symptoms of hypoglycemia such as weakness, sweating, tremors, palpitations, and confusion. Dietary management commonly includes small, frequent meals; limiting rapidly absorbed carbohydrates; emphasizing protein and complex carbohydrates; and separating fluids from meals when appropriate. Although nutritional deficiencies and undernutrition can also occur after bariatric surgery, dumping syndrome is the classical side effect specifically associated with altered gastric anatomy and rapid gastric emptying.', '["Undernutrition","Gastrostomy","Dumping syndrome","GERD"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-07T12:01:26.523Z","interval":3,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":2}'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T12:01:26.523Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.255Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T12:01:26.523Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756362804907-190', NULL, 'anki-deck-1756357465257-1790432463920-rf85m', 'Lactose intolerance is due to:', 'lactase deficiency', 'Lactose intolerance occurs when there is insufficient lactase, the brush-border enzyme responsible for breaking down lactose into its absorbable monosaccharides, glucose and galactose. When lactose is not adequately digested in the small intestine, it reaches the colon, where gut bacteria ferment it and produce gases and other metabolites. The resulting osmotic effect and fermentation can cause bloating, abdominal pain, flatulence, and diarrhea after consuming lactose-containing foods.', '["inflammation of gastric mucosa","lactase deficiency","milk allergy","Hyperacidity"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-07T12:04:37.316Z","interval":3,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":2}'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T12:04:37.316Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.255Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T12:04:37.316Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756362823839-191', NULL, 'anki-deck-1756357465257-1790432463920-rf85m', 'Fats are generally restricted in diseases of the liver because:', 'All of the above', 'In chronic liver diseases, fat restriction is indicated because damaged hepatocytes exhibit impaired bile acid synthesis/secretion for micelle formation, and impaired beta-oxidation can exacerbate hepatic steatosis.', '["impaired production and transport of bile","fat metabolism may be impaired and result in fatty liver","All of the above","None of the above"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-07T11:31:36.220Z","interval":3,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":2}'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:31:36.220Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.255Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:31:36.220Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756362839988-192', NULL, 'anki-deck-1756357465257-1790432463920-rf85m', 'A dietary risk factor of coronary artery disease is high intake of:', 'saturated fat', 'Elevated dietary intake of saturated fatty acids and industrial trans-fats increases serum low-density lipoprotein cholesterol (LDL-C) and arterial inflammation, a major independent risk factor for coronary artery disease.', '["polyunsaturated fat","monounsaturated fat","saturated fat","cis-fatty acid"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-07T12:00:23.607Z","interval":3,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":2}'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T12:00:23.607Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.255Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T12:00:23.607Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756362854708-193', NULL, 'anki-deck-1756357465257-1790432463920-rf85m', 'Dietary fibers are useful in preventing hypercholesterolemia because they:', 'bind cholesterol containing bile acids which are eventually excreted', 'Certain soluble, viscous dietary fibers can help lower blood LDL cholesterol by increasing the excretion of bile acids and cholesterol in the feces. The liver must use circulating cholesterol to synthesize new bile acids, thereby increasing hepatic demand for cholesterol and contributing to lower LDL cholesterol concentrations. Examples of cholesterol-lowering soluble fibers include β-glucan from oats and barley, psyllium, and some fibers from legumes. The original wording is somewhat simplified because fiber does not literally “bind cholesterol-containing bile acids” in every case; increased bile acid excretion and fermentation-related mechanisms both contribute.', '["bind cholesterol containing bile acids which are eventually excreted","produce HDL cholesterol","are antioxidants","carriers of omega 3-fatty acids"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-07T11:32:45.133Z","interval":3,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":2}'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:32:45.133Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.255Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:32:45.133Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756362871587-194', NULL, 'anki-deck-1756357465257-1790432463920-rf85m', 'The conservative management of chronic kidney disease which is characterized by progressive and irreversible destruction of nephrons consists of:', 'diet and drugs', 'Conservative management of chronic kidney disease (CKD) aims to slow disease progression, manage complications, and maintain quality of life without renal replacement therapy. It includes appropriate dietary management and pharmacologic treatment, along with control of blood pressure, diabetes, cardiovascular risk factors, electrolyte and acid-base abnormalities, anemia, and other complications. Nutrition therapy may involve individualized adjustments to protein, sodium, potassium, phosphorus, and energy intake depending on the CKD stage and laboratory findings. 

Dialysis and kidney transplantation are forms of kidney replacement therapy, not conservative management. Importantly, conservative management is not simply “diet only”; medications and comprehensive medical management are integral.', '["diet only","diet and drugs","dialysis","kidney transplant"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-07T11:58:51.371Z","interval":3,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":2}'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:58:51.371Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.255Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:58:51.371Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756362885537-195', NULL, 'anki-deck-1756357465257-1790432463920-rf85m', 'Food to emphasize when it is necessary to increase potassium:', 'Fruits', 'Fruits are generally good sources of potassium and are commonly emphasized when increasing dietary potassium. Examples include banana, oranges, melon, papaya, avocado, and dried fruits. Potassium is an important intracellular electrolyte involved in nerve transmission, muscle contraction, and maintenance of normal cardiac function. However, potassium intake should be individualized because patients with chronic kidney disease, hyperkalemia, or certain medications may instead require potassium restriction. 

Starchy roots such as potatoes and sweet potatoes can also provide substantial potassium, so they are not inherently poor choices. However, among the options, fruits are the most appropriate general food group to emphasize when the goal is to increase potassium intake.', '["Fruits","Cream","starchy roots","cereals"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-07T11:58:43.252Z","interval":3,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":2}'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:58:43.252Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.255Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:58:43.252Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756362904486-196', NULL, 'anki-deck-1756357465257-1790432463920-rf85m', 'The C P F and kcal value of a breakfast containing 2 rice B exchanges, 2 medium fat exchanges, and 1 fruit exchange.', 'C 56g P 20 g F 12 g E 412 kcal', 'Using the Philippine Food Exchange List (FEL), here is the exact step-by-step breakdown of the computation.

1. Rice B (2 exchanges):
	• Carbohydrates: 2 x 23g = 46g
	• Protein: 2 x 2g = 4g
	• Fat: 0g
	• Calories: 2 x 100 kcal = 200 kcal

2. Medium-Fat Meat (2 exchanges):
	• Carbohydrates: 0g
	• Protein: 2 x 8g = 16g
	• Fat: 2 x 6g = 12g
	• Calories: 2 x 86 kcal = 172 kcal

3. Fruit (1 exchange):
	• Carbohydrates: 1 x 10g = 10g
	• Protein: 0g
	• Fat: 0g
	• Calories: 1 x 40 kcal = 40 kcal

Grand Totals:
	• Carbohydrates: 46g + 0g + 10g = 56 grams
	• Protein: 4g + 16g + 0g = 20 grams
	• Fat: 0g + 12g + 0g = 12 grams
	• Energy: 200 + 172 + 40 = 412 kcal

Verifying the Math with Atwater Multipliers:
• Carbohydrates: 56g x 4 kcal/g = 224 kcal
• Protein: 20g x 4 kcal/g = 80 kcal
• Fat: 12g x 9 kcal/g = 108 kcal
• Total Energy: 224 + 80 + 108 = 412 kcal', '["C 46 g P 16 g F 10g E 338 kcal","C 56 g P 16 g F 6 g E 342 kcal","C 46 g P 20 g F 10 g E 354 kcal","C 56g P 20 g F 12 g E 412 kcal"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 3, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-12T11:54:28.486Z","interval":8,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":3}'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:54:28.486Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.255Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:54:28.486Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756362921734-197', NULL, 'anki-deck-1756357465257-1790432463920-rf85m', 'All measures are allowed to increase the energy intake of renal patient except:', 'serving fruit cocktail with heavy syrup', 'When a renal patient requires increased energy intake, particularly when protein, potassium, phosphorus, or fluid may need to be controlled, energy can be increased by using calorie-dense foods that provide relatively little protein and minerals. Adding sugar, oil, and unsalted margarine can increase energy density without substantially increasing protein. 

In contrast, fruit cocktail with heavy syrup may be inappropriate depending on the patient''s potassium and fluid restrictions; canned fruits may also contribute substantial potassium, and the syrup contributes mainly sugar rather than being a particularly useful renal nutrition strategy.', '["using additional sugar in foods","preparing foods with oil","adding unsalted margarine","serving fruit cocktail with heavy syrup"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-07T11:14:04.202Z","interval":3,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":2}'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:14:04.202Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.255Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:14:04.202Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756362937619-198', NULL, 'anki-deck-1756357465257-1790432463920-rf85m', 'Which of the following are conditions that resulted from vitamin C deficiency?
I. Beriberi, confusion, depression, convulsions, scurvy 
II. Microcytic anemia, bleeding gums, loosened teeth, scurvy 
III. Rashes, hair loss, microcytic anemia, bleeding gums 
IV. Confusion, convulsions, loosened teeth, scurvy, hair loss', 'II only', 'Vitamin C deficiency causes scurvy, characterized by impaired collagen synthesis and connective-tissue abnormalities. Classic manifestations include bleeding or swollen gums, loose teeth, poor wound healing, petechiae/ecchymoses, and fatigue. Vitamin C also enhances non-heme iron absorption, so deficiency can contribute to anemia; however, the anemia associated with vitamin C deficiency is not necessarily microcytic, and may be related to impaired iron absorption and blood loss.

Among the choices, Statement II is the best match because it contains the classic manifestations of scurvy.', '["I only","II only","II and III only","I and IV only"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-06T02:47:38.241Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.3,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-11-05T02:47:38.241Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.255Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-05T02:47:38.241Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756362954916-199', NULL, 'anki-deck-1756357465257-1790432463920-rf85m', 'The immediate goal of dietary management in acute diarrhea is:', 'prevent dehydration', 'In acute diarrhea, the rapid and massive loss of water and critical electrolytes (like sodium, potassium, and bicarbonate) through the stool pose an immediate risk of hypovolemic shock and cardiovascular collapse, particularly in vulnerable populations.

While preventing nutritional deficiencies and restoring long-term nutritional status are essential milestones during the subsequent convalescent phase, they cannot be addressed if the patient is acutely unstable. Therefore, the absolute primary, immediate goal of clinical dietary management is maintaining fluid volume and preventing or correcting dehydration through the immediate administration of oral rehydration solutions (ORS).', '["restore nutritional status","prevent dehydration","prevent deficiency","withhold food"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-07T11:56:52.987Z","interval":3,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":2}'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:56:52.987Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.255Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:56:52.987Z"'::jsonb)
