('card-1792663303603-gy2x0', NULL, 'anki-deck-1756357465257-1790432463920-rf85m', 'Drinking coffee or tea together with an iron-rich meal primarily decreases the absorption of which type of iron?', 'Non-heme iron', 'Coffee and tea contain polyphenols and tannins that form insoluble complexes with non-heme iron, substantially reducing its intestinal absorption. The inhibitory effect is greatest when these beverages are consumed with or shortly after meals. Heme iron, derived from animal foods, is absorbed through a different mechanism and is much less affected by dietary inhibitors. Vitamin C can counteract this inhibition by reducing ferric iron (Fe³⁺) to the more absorbable ferrous form (Fe²⁺).', '["Non-heme iron","Heme iron","Ferritin-bound iron","Both heme and non-heme iron equally"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, 'Nutritional Biochemistry and Clinical Dietetics', 'medium', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-07T11:57:56.897Z","interval":3,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":2}'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:57:56.897Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-22T10:01:45.146Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:57:56.897Z"'::jsonb),
('card-1792663393777-ahua3', NULL, 'anki-deck-1756357465257-1790432463920-rf85m', 'Which lipoprotein is primarily responsible for transporting dietary triglycerides from the intestine to peripheral tissues?', 'Chylomicrons', 'Chylomicrons are the largest and least dense lipoproteins. They are synthesized in the intestinal mucosal cells after dietary fat absorption and transport exogenous triglycerides, cholesterol, fat-soluble vitamins, and phospholipids through the lymphatic system before entering the bloodstream. Lipoprotein lipase (LPL), activated by apolipoprotein C-II, hydrolyzes triglycerides in chylomicrons, releasing free fatty acids for uptake by adipose tissue and skeletal muscle. The remaining chylomicron remnants are taken up by the liver.', '["Chylomicrons","HDL","LDL","VLDL"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, 'Nutritional Biochemistry and Clinical Dietetics', 'medium', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-07T11:44:54.381Z","interval":3,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":2}'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:44:54.381Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-22T10:03:15.390Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:44:54.381Z"'::jsonb),
('card-1792663446718-613b8', NULL, 'anki-deck-1756357465257-1790432463920-rf85m', 'Which enzyme is responsible for hydrolyzing triglycerides in circulating chylomicrons and VLDL?', 'Lipoprotein lipase', 'Lipoprotein lipase (LPL) is an extracellular enzyme attached to the capillary endothelium of adipose tissue, skeletal muscle, and cardiac muscle. It hydrolyzes triglycerides contained in circulating chylomicrons and VLDL into free fatty acids and glycerol. 

The released fatty acids are either: 
• Stored as triglycerides in adipose tissue, or 
• Oxidized by muscle tissue for energy. 

LPL activity is stimulated by apolipoprotein C-II, making Apo C-II an essential cofactor for normal triglyceride metabolism.', '["Lipoprotein lipase","Hormone-sensitive lipase","Pancreatic lipase","Hepatic lipase"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, 'Nutritional Biochemistry and Clinical Dietetics', 'medium', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-07T11:29:59.665Z","interval":3,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":2}'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:29:59.665Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-22T10:04:08.299Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:29:59.665Z"'::jsonb),
('card-1792663543407-vbzop', NULL, 'anki-deck-1756357465257-1790432463920-rf85m', 'Which of the following hormones primarily stimulates glycogen breakdown in the liver during fasting?', 'Glucagon', 'Glucagon is a peptide hormone secreted by the pancreatic α-cells in response to declining blood glucose concentrations. Its primary role is to maintain euglycemia during the post-absorptive state by stimulating hepatic glucose production. 

The major metabolic actions of glucagon include: 
• Glycogenolysis – breakdown of liver glycogen into glucose.
• Gluconeogenesis – synthesis of glucose from non-carbohydrate precursors such as lactate, glycerol, and glucogenic amino acids. 
• Lipolysis – indirectly promotes mobilization of fatty acids from adipose tissue. 
• Ketogenesis – enhances ketone body formation during prolonged fasting. It is important to note that skeletal muscle lacks glucagon receptors. 

Therefore, glucagon promotes glycogenolysis primarily in the liver, whereas muscle glycogen breakdown is stimulated mainly by epinephrine and muscle contraction.', '["Glucagon","Insulin","Leptin","Aldosterone"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, 'Nutritional Biochemistry and Clinical Dietetics', 'medium', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-07T11:35:20.323Z","interval":3,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":2}'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:35:20.323Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-22T10:05:44.858Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:35:20.323Z"'::jsonb),
('card-1792663653577-6ss8f', NULL, 'anki-deck-1756357465257-1790432463920-rf85m', 'Which of the following best describes the primary function of bile salts in lipid digestion?', 'Emulsify dietary fats and facilitate micelle formation', 'Bile salts are amphipathic molecules synthesized from cholesterol in the liver and stored in the gallbladder. Upon ingestion of dietary fat, they are released into the duodenum where they perform two major functions: 

• Emulsification – breaking large fat globules into smaller droplets, thereby increasing the surface area available for pancreatic lipase. 
• Micelle formation – surrounding fatty acids, monoglycerides, cholesterol, and fat-soluble vitamins to facilitate their transport to the intestinal brush border for absorption. 

Bile salts themselves do not digest fats; rather, they enhance the efficiency of pancreatic lipase and promote lipid absorption.', '["Emulsify dietary fats and facilitate micelle formation","Hydrolyze triglycerides into fatty acids","Digest proteins in the stomach","Activate pancreatic amylase"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, 'Nutritional Biochemistry and Clinical Dietetics', 'medium', 3, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-12T11:58:07.704Z","interval":8,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":3}'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:58:07.704Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-22T10:07:35.029Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:58:07.704Z"'::jsonb),
('card-1792663850326-umk9r', NULL, 'anki-deck-1756357465257-1790432463920-rf85m', 'Which of the following describes the maximum amount of bone tissue attained after skeletal development is completed and serves as an important determinant of bone health in adulthood?', 'Peak Bone Mass', 'Peak bone mass (PBM) is the maximum amount of bone tissue accumulated by an individual at the end of skeletal maturation, usually achieved during the late teens to early 30s, depending on the skeletal site, sex, genetics, and lifestyle factors. It represents the highest level of bone strength attained before the gradual age-related decline in bone mass begins. 

Peak bone mass is influenced by several factors, including: 
• Genetics (the strongest determinant) 
• Adequate calcium and vitamin D intake 
• Regular weight-bearing and resistance exercise 
• Hormonal status (especially estrogen and testosterone) 
• Adequate protein intake 
• Avoidance of smoking and excessive alcohol consumption

A higher peak bone mass reduces the risk of osteopenia, osteoporosis, and fragility fractures later in life. Therefore, childhood, adolescence, and young adulthood are considered critical periods for optimizing bone health.', '["Peak Bone Mass","Bone mineral content","Bone mineral density","Bone mass"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, 'Nutritional Biochemistry and Clinical Dietetics', 'medium', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-07T11:50:35.494Z","interval":3,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":2}'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:50:35.494Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-22T10:10:52.015Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T11:50:35.494Z"'::jsonb),
('card-1792663993534-s8949', NULL, 'anki-deck-1756357465257-1790432463920-rf85m', 'Protein recommendation for adults with pressure injuries or significant wounds to promote wound healing:', '1.5 – 2 g/kg', 'Protein is one of the most important nutrients for wound healing, as it is required for collagen synthesis, fibroblast proliferation, angiogenesis, immune function, and tissue repair. Inadequate protein intake delays wound healing, impairs immune responses, and increases the risk of wound infection and chronic non-healing wounds. Patients with pressure injuries, surgical wounds, burns, traumatic injuries, or chronic ulcers generally have increased protein requirements because of the increased metabolic demands associated with tissue repair. 

Current clinical nutrition guidelines recommend approximately 1.25–1.5 g/kg/day of protein for most patients with pressure injuries and up to 1.5–2.0 g/kg/day for individuals with extensive wounds, burns, or severe catabolic conditions, provided renal function is adequate.', '["1.5 – 2 g/kg","20-25% of TER","15-20% of TER","50% of TER"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, 'Nutritional Biochemistry and Clinical Dietetics', 'medium', 3, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-11T12:03:21.354Z","interval":7,"easeFactor":2.3,"repetitions":3}'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T12:03:21.354Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-22T10:13:15.262Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T12:03:21.354Z"'::jsonb),
('card-1792664150515-tfyqi', NULL, 'anki-deck-1756357465257-1790432463920-rf85m', 'Which medication is most strongly associated with folate deficiency during long-term therapy?', 'Methotrexate', 'Methotrexate is an antimetabolite that competitively inhibits dihydrofolate reductase (DHFR), the enzyme responsible for converting dihydrofolate into tetrahydrofolate, the biologically active form of folate. This inhibition impairs the synthesis of purines and thymidylate, ultimately reducing DNA and RNA synthesis.

Because rapidly dividing cells rely heavily on folate-dependent DNA synthesis, long-term methotrexate therapy may lead to: 
• Folate deficiency 
• Megaloblastic anemia 
• Oral mucositis 
• Glossitis 
• Gastrointestinal disturbances
• Elevated homocysteine levels 

Methotrexate is widely used in the treatment of: 
• Rheumatoid arthritis 
• Psoriasis 
• Certain cancers 
• Other autoimmune diseases 

To reduce adverse effects while preserving therapeutic efficacy, patients receiving low-dose methotrexate for autoimmune diseases are commonly prescribed folic acid supplementation, usually on days when methotrexate is not taken or according to the physician''s protocol.', '["Methotrexate","Digoxin","Omeprazole","Metformin"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, 'Nutritional Biochemistry and Clinical Dietetics', 'medium', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-06T02:48:16.885Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.3,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-11-05T02:48:16.885Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-22T10:15:52.498Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-05T02:48:16.885Z"'::jsonb),
('card-1792664240726-6kbqv', NULL, 'anki-deck-1756357465257-1790432463920-rf85m', 'Which equation is currently recommended by KDIGO for estimating glomerular filtration rate (eGFR) in adults?', 'CKD-EPI 2021 equation', 'The Kidney Disease: Improving Global Outcomes (KDIGO) 2024 Clinical Practice Guideline recommends using the 2021 Chronic Kidney Disease Epidemiology Collaboration (CKD-EPI) creatinine equation as the preferred equation for estimating glomerular filtration rate (eGFR) in adults. 

The CKD-EPI 2021 equation: 
• Estimates kidney function using serum creatinine, age, and sex 
• Removes the race coefficient that was included in earlier equations 
• Provides more equitable assessment across different populations 
• Generally demonstrates better accuracy than the older MDRD equation, particularly at higher GFR values 

Estimated GFR is essential for: 
• Diagnosing chronic kidney disease (CKD) 
• Staging CKD 
• Monitoring disease progression 
• Guiding medication dosing 
• Planning nutrition therapy', '["CKD-EPI 2021 equation","Cockcroft–Gault equation","MDRD equation","Jelliffe equation"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, 'Nutritional Biochemistry and Clinical Dietetics', 'medium', 3, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-12T12:05:22.323Z","interval":8,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":3}'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T12:05:22.323Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-22T10:17:22.427Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-04T12:05:22.323Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758689696588-0', NULL, 'anki-deck-1758689429896-1790185671252', 'In collagen synthesis, ____ acts as a reducing agent to convert oxidized iron atom back to its reduced state. *', 'ascorbic acid', 'During collagen synthesis, two critical enzymes—prolyl hydroxylase and lysyl hydroxylase—are responsible for adding hydroxyl (-OH) groups to proline and lysine amino acids. This structural modification allows collagen strands to cross-link properly, forming a stable triple-helix network.

These hydroxylase enzymes require iron in its active, reduced ferrous state (Fe²⁺) to operate. During the chemical reaction, the iron atom naturally becomes oxidized into its inactive ferric state (Fe³⁺). Ascorbic acid (Vitamin C) acts as the essential electron donor or reducing agent, transferring electrons to convert the inactive ferric iron back into its active ferrous state. Without enough vitamin C, the iron stays oxidized, collagen remains under-hydroxylated, and connective tissues weaken (resulting in scurvy).', '["ascorbic acid","pantothenic acid","pyridoxine","folic acid"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-29T12:46:38.676Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.3,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-28T12:46:38.676Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-23T17:47:52.013Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-28T12:46:38.871Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758689735321-1', NULL, 'anki-deck-1758689429896-1790185671252', 'The average respiratory quotient of a mixed diet is: *', '0.85', '', '["0.75","0.95","0.85","0.65"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-01T12:38:30.683Z","interval":3,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":2}'::jsonb, '"2026-09-28T12:38:30.683Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-23T17:47:52.013Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-28T12:38:30.982Z"'::jsonb),
