('anki-card-1758690564366-43', NULL, 'anki-deck-1758689429896-1790185671252', 'Olestra is artificial fat that does not provide kilocalories but has the following effect on one’s nutrition: *', 'It prevents the absorption of fat-soluble vitamins.', 'Olestra is a non-absorbable fat substitute made of sucrose polyester. Because human digestive enzymes cannot break its large chemical structure down, it passes through the gastrointestinal tract entirely intact.

As it moves through the gut, olestra behaves like a physical liquid solvent. It dissolves and traps the fat-soluble vitamins (A, D, E, and K) along with carotenoids from other consumed foods. Because olestra cannot be absorbed, these vital nutrients are bound to it and carried straight out of the body, presenting a major nutritional deficit. This is why the FDA legally mandates that foods made with olestra must be fortified with vitamins A, D, E, and K to offset this specific malabsorption effect.

• It prevents the absorption of water-soluble vitamins: This is scientifically incorrect. Water-soluble vitamins (like Vitamin C and B-complex vitamins) dissolve in water, not lipids. Therefore, they are entirely unaffected by the presence of olestra and are absorbed normally by the body.
• It results in flatulence: While gastrointestinal symptoms like flatulence, cramping, or loose stools can occur when olestra is consumed in extremely massive, non-standard quantities, the primary, classic nutrition-board-tested hazard is its direct, chemical inhibition of fat-soluble vitamin uptake.', '["a & b only","It prevents the absorption of water-soluble vitamins.","It results in flatulence.","It prevents the absorption of fat-soluble vitamins."]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 5, '', '{"dueDate":"2028-04-21T13:49:06.229Z","interval":529,"easeFactor":2.1,"repetitions":9}'::jsonb, '"2026-11-09T13:49:06.229Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-23T17:47:52.013Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-09T13:49:06.229Z"'::jsonb)
