('anki-card-1758691774259-106', NULL, 'anki-deck-1758689429896-1790185671252', 'Mechanically altered diets are prescribed for individuals with: *', 'chewing and swallowing difficulties', 'chewing and swallowing difficulties', '["malabsorptive disorders","disorders of the liver, gallbladder, and pancreas","unusually high kcal and protein requirements","chewing and swallowing difficulties"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-01T10:36:57.922Z","interval":3,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":2}'::jsonb, '"2026-09-28T10:36:57.922Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-23T17:47:52.169Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-08T12:24:43.573Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758691789110-107', NULL, 'anki-deck-1758689429896-1790185671252', 'An anorectic teenager’s health status has been progressively deteriorating. The physician has decided to tube feed her. The initial kilocaloric intake should be based on: *', 'present body weight', 'In a severely malnourished or anorectic patient whose health is progressively deteriorating, the greatest clinical danger during the initiation of nutritional support (whether oral or tube feeding) is refeeding syndrome.

To avoid fatal electrolyte shifts (such as severe hypophosphatemia, hypokalemia, and hypomagnesemia) and cardiac overload, clinical protocols dictate that you must "start low and go slow". The initial calorie prescription is strictly calculated using the patient''s present/actual body weight (typically starting conservatively at around 20 kcal/kg/day or lower) rather than their ideal weight. Using the ideal body weight right away would heavily overestimate their metabolic tolerance, flood their system with too many calories, and trigger refeeding complications.', '["ideal body weight plus 10%","tolerance","amount of weight loss","present body weight"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-29T16:27:37.957Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.3,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-28T16:27:37.957Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-23T17:47:52.169Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-28T16:27:38.132Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758691810774-108', NULL, 'anki-deck-1758689429896-1790185671252', 'Choose the food combination that will provide the highest amount of fiber. *', 'beans (3 exchanges), rice (4 exchanges)', 'beans (3 exchanges), rice (4 exchanges)', '["papaya (3 exchanges), rice (8 exchanges)","papaya (2 exchanges), watermelon (2 exchanges), rice (6 exchanges)","beans (3 exchanges), rice (4 exchanges)","pineapple (1 exchange), mango (3 exchanges), rice (10 exchanges"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-09-29T11:33:29.449Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-09-28T11:33:29.449Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-23T17:47:52.169Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-08T12:24:43.573Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758691828608-109', NULL, 'anki-deck-1758689429896-1790185671252', 'The recommended daily amount of dietary fiber for a heart healthy and diabetic diet is: *', '25 – 30 g', 'The American Heart Association (AHA) specifically recommends a daily dietary fiber intake of 25 to 30 grams from food sources to lower cholesterol levels and reduce the risk of cardiovascular disease.

Major diabetes organizations align with this range (often extending up to 35 grams) because dietary fiber—especially soluble fiber—slows glucose absorption, prevents sharp blood sugar spikes, and helps improve overall insulin sensitivity.', '["20 -25 g","10-15 g","25 – 30 g","15 – 20 g"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-29T16:33:36.898Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.3,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-28T16:33:36.898Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-23T17:47:52.169Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-28T16:33:37.075Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758691846408-110', NULL, 'anki-deck-1758689429896-1790185671252', 'A therapeutic Diet Counseling session is best given when: *', 'the patient is relaxed and hospitalized', 'In clinical dietetics and hospital protocols, a therapeutic diet counseling session requires the patient’s active attention, focus, and ability to comprehend new lifestyle changes.

When a patient is relaxed and hospitalized (meaning they are past the acute, painful phase of their illness but not yet caught up in the hectic rush of leaving), they are in the best mental and physical state to absorb complex nutritional instructions, ask questions, and practice meal-planning concepts.', '["the patient is very sick","the patient is relaxed and hospitalized","none of the above","the patient is being discharged"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-29T16:43:35.342Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.3,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-28T16:43:35.342Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-23T17:47:52.169Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-28T16:43:35.531Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758691861457-111', NULL, 'anki-deck-1758689429896-1790185671252', 'The use of pharmacologic agents has been shown to decrease diabetes incidence by various degrees. The drug that should be considered for use in diabetes prevention is: *', 'metformin', '', '["alpha-glucosidase inhibitors","thiazolidinediones","metformin","orlistat"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, 'Metformin possesses the most extensive long-term safety data, is highly cost-effective, and carries a much lower burden of side effects compared to alternative therapies.', '{"dueDate":"2026-09-29T12:34:11.117Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.3,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-09-28T12:34:11.117Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-23T17:47:52.169Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-28T12:34:11.441Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758691875471-112', NULL, 'anki-deck-1758689429896-1790185671252', 'EPA and DHA can help reduce heart disease risk due to their beneficial effects on: *', 'triglyceride levels & blood clotting activity', 'Omega-3 fatty acids, specifically eicosapentaenoic acid (EPA) and docosahexaenoic acid (DHA), have a well-documented, powerful effect on reducing serum triglyceride levels. They do this by decreasing the liver''s production of VLDL (very-low-density lipoprotein) and speeding up the clearance of triglycerides from the blood.

EPA and DHA also reduce blood clotting activity (platelet aggregation). They displace arachidonic acid in cell membranes, which reduces the production of thromboxane A2 (a compound that causes platelets to clump together and form clots) and shifts the body toward a more antithrombotic, clot-preventing state.

• LDL & HDL levels: While omega-3s can slightly raise HDL ("good") cholesterol, high doses of DHA can actually increase LDL ("bad") cholesterol levels in some individuals, making this a less uniform or beneficial primary mechanism.
• Cardiac output & heart rate: Omega-3s do support a stable heart rhythm and improve heart rate variability, but they do not primarily lower heart disease risk by altering baseline cardiac output or basic resting heart rate.
• LDL size & particle number: This is the primary target mechanism for medications like statins or advanced lipid therapies rather than the signature biochemical profile of marine omega-3 oils.', '["triglyceride levels & blood clotting activity","cardiac output & heart rate","LDL & HDL levels","LDL size & particle number"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 4, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-09T08:09:56.452Z","interval":11,"easeFactor":1.9,"repetitions":4}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-29T08:09:56.452Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-23T17:47:52.169Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-29T08:09:56.771Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758691890154-113', NULL, 'anki-deck-1758689429896-1790185671252', 'Nutrients included in parenteral nutrition solutions:', 'glucose solution', 'glucose solution', '["essential crystalline amino acids","nonessential crystalline amino acids","glucose solution","lipids"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-09-29T11:45:32.609Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.3,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-09-28T11:45:32.609Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-23T17:47:52.169Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-08T12:24:43.573Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758691909503-114', NULL, 'anki-deck-1758689429896-1790185671252', 'A primary feature of emphysema: *', 'destruction of the walls separating the alveoli', 'Emphysema is pathologically defined by the permanent enlargement of the air spaces distal to the terminal bronchioles, accompanied by the destruction of the alveolar walls (septa).

When these walls break down, millions of tiny, independent air sacs merge into fewer, much larger, floppy pockets. This severely reduces the total surface area available for gas exchange. It also destroys the elastic fibers that keep the small airways open, causing the lungs to lose their elastic recoil, trap stale air, and collapse during exhalation.', '["obstruction of the bronchi","obstruction within the bronchioles","destruction of the walls separating the alveoli","excessive lung elasticity"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-29T12:32:43.616Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.3,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-28T12:32:43.616Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-23T17:47:52.169Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-28T12:32:43.821Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758691924136-115', NULL, 'anki-deck-1758689429896-1790185671252', 'Dr. Cruz ordered a 500 mg sodium diet for Nena Lim. How much salt contains 500 mg of sodium? *', '1,250 mg', 'In nutrition and dietetics calculations, standard table salt (sodium chloride, NaCl) is approximated to be 40% sodium and 60% chloride by weight.

To convert a specific milligram amount of sodium into the equivalent amount of table salt, you divide the sodium milligrams by 0.40 (or simply multiply by 2.5):

Salt (mg) = Sodium (mg) x 2.5
Salt (mg) = 500 mg x 2.5 = 1,250 mg

Therefore, 1,250 mg of table salt contains exactly 500 mg of elemental sodium.', '["1,250 mg","800 mg","2,000 mg","200 mg"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-29T15:57:06.927Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.1,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-28T15:57:06.927Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-23T17:47:52.169Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-28T15:57:07.112Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758691942785-116', NULL, 'anki-deck-1758689429896-1790185671252', 'Soluble fibers such as citrus and legumes provide the following health benefits except: *', 'they alleviate constipation', 'The primary function of alleviating and preventing constipation belongs to insoluble fiber (found in wheat bran, vegetables, and whole grains). Insoluble fiber does not dissolve in water; instead, it acts like a broom that adds physical bulk to the stool and speeds up its movement through the digestive tract.

In contrast, soluble fiber (found in citrus fruits, oats, and legumes) absorbs water to form a thick, viscous gel-like substance in the intestines. While it slows down digestion and transit time, it is not the primary physiological driver used to accelerate bowel movements or clear constipation.', '["they alleviate constipation","they help manage one’s weight by increasing satiety","they lower risk of diabetes","they lower risk of colon cancer"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-29T15:28:51.102Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.3,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-28T15:28:51.102Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-23T17:47:52.169Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-28T15:28:51.289Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758691970383-117', NULL, 'anki-deck-1758689429896-1790185671252', 'The recommended intake for MUFA in the diet is approximately: *', 'up to 20% of total kcal', 'less than 7% of total kcal', '["up to 20% of total kcal","30% of total kcal","up to 10% of total kcal","less than 7% of total kcal"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-09-29T14:44:00.336Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-09-28T14:44:00.336Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-23T17:47:52.169Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-08T12:24:43.573Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758691985118-118', NULL, 'anki-deck-1758689429896-1790185671252', 'The best diet for a patient with hypothyroidism: *', '20-30 kcal/KBW, low fat, iodized salt', '20-30 kcal/KBW, low fat, iodized salt', '["30 kcal/KBW, high fat, iodized salt","30 kcal.KBW, low fat, iodized salt","20-30 kcal/KBW, low fat, iodized salt","20-25 kcal/KBW, high fat, iodized salt"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-01T14:47:53.629Z","interval":3,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":2}'::jsonb, '"2026-09-28T14:47:53.629Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-23T17:47:52.169Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-08T12:24:43.573Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758692000883-119', NULL, 'anki-deck-1758689429896-1790185671252', 'Ischemia in the coronary arteries is a usual cause of: *', 'angina pectoris', 'Ischemia refers to an inadequate supply of blood and oxygen to a specific tissue or organ. When ischemia occurs specifically within the coronary arteries—the blood vessels responsible for feeding the heart muscle itself—it means the heart muscle (myocardium) is being temporarily starved of vital oxygen.

This oxygen deficit forces the heart muscle cells to switch to anaerobic metabolism, which generates lactic acid, irritates local nerve endings, and triggers the classic clinical sensation of chest pain, pressure, or discomfort known as angina pectoris.

• HPN (Hypertension): High blood pressure is a major systemic circulatory cause or risk factor that accelerates the development of narrowed coronary arteries, rather than being a consequence of coronary artery ischemia.
• Hemorrhagic stroke: A neurological event caused by a ruptured or bleeding blood vessel inside the brain tissue, completely unrelated to oxygen levels in the heart''s arteries.
• Aneurysm: A pathological ballooning or weakening of a blood vessel wall (most commonly in the aorta or brain arteries), rather than a lack of blood flow to the heart muscle.', '["hemorrhagic stroke","angina pectoris","aneurysm","HPN"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 3, 'Ischemia refers to a restriction in blood supply to tissues, causing a shortage of oxygen and nutrients. When ischemia occurs in the coronary arteries (the vessels that supply blood directly to the heart muscle), the myocardium becomes starved of oxygen. This lack of oxygen triggers chest pain or discomfort known as angina pectoris. It is a hallmark warning sign of coronary artery disease, typically occurring during physical exertion or stress when the heart demands more oxygen than the narrowed arteries can deliver.', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-04T08:09:22.293Z","interval":6,"easeFactor":1.9,"repetitions":3}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-29T08:09:22.293Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-23T17:47:52.169Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-29T08:09:22.631Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758692020515-120', NULL, 'anki-deck-1758689429896-1790185671252', 'Foods allowed in a low purine diet: *', 'milk, eggs, cheese', 'These foods are generally low in purines and are appropriate choices in a low-purine diet. In contrast, organ meats contain very high amounts of purines and are restricted, particularly for patients with gout or hyperuricemia.', '["glandular organs","milk, eggs, cheese","all of the above","none of the above"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-09-29T10:43:47.186Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-09-28T10:43:47.186Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-23T17:47:52.169Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-07T14:32:00.423Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758692040016-121', NULL, 'anki-deck-1758689429896-1790185671252', 'Prolonged use of corticosteroids can lead to: *', 'fatty liver', 'While corticosteroids cause muscle wasting and fluid retention, the long-term storage shift they cause is heavily emphasized in clinical nutrition:

1. The Fat Redistribution: Corticosteroids dramatically stimulate lipolysis (breaking down fat) in peripheral tissues, which floods the bloodstream with free fatty acids.
2. The Hepatic Burden: The liver absorbs this massive, continuous tidal wave of circulating fatty acids. Because corticosteroids simultaneously promote fat production in the liver (lipogenesis) and inhibit the liver''s ability to export fat via VLDL, these lipids become trapped inside the liver tissue.
3. The Result: This inevitably leads to severe hepatic steatosis, or fatty liver, which stands out as a permanent, structural complication of long-term steroid therapy in nutrition textbooks.

• Increased protein catabolism: Corticosteroids break down muscle tissue to release amino acids for gluconeogenesis.
• Sodium retention: They cause the kidneys to hold onto sodium and water, leading to edema and fluid retention.
• Fatty liver: Long-term use alters lipid metabolism and can result in hepatic steatosis (fatty liver accumulation).
• Poor glucose tolerance: They induce insulin resistance and stimulate gluconeogenesis, elevating blood sugar levels.', '["fatty liver","increased protein catabolism","sodium retention","poor glucose tolerance"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 4, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-11T08:08:38.060Z","interval":13,"easeFactor":2.1,"repetitions":4}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-29T08:08:38.060Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-23T17:47:52.169Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-29T08:08:38.408Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758692054948-122', NULL, 'anki-deck-1758689429896-1790185671252', 'The best energy allowance for a severely malnourished infant. *', '80-140 kcal/kg DBW', '80-140 kcal/kg DBW', '["80-140 kcal/kg DBW","150-200 kcal/kg DBW","100-150 kcal/kg DBW","none of the above"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-01T11:51:07.036Z","interval":3,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":2}'::jsonb, '"2026-09-28T11:51:07.036Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-23T17:47:52.169Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-08T12:24:43.573Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758692069695-123', NULL, 'anki-deck-1758689429896-1790185671252', 'A patient who is morbidly obese underwent stomach resection. What possible side effects can she experience as a result of surgery? *', 'vitamin B12 malabsorption', 'vitamin B12 malabsorption', '["vitamin B12 malabsorption","none of the above","difficulty in chewing","diabetes mellitus"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, 'The parietal cells located in the lining of the stomach produce intrinsic factor, a glycoprotein essential for the absorption of vitamin B12. Removing a portion of the stomach (resection) drastically cuts down the production of intrinsic factor. Without it, vitamin B12 cannot bind properly or be absorbed downstream in the ileum of the small intestine.', '{"dueDate":"2026-09-29T14:07:39.925Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-09-28T14:07:39.925Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-23T17:47:52.169Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-08T12:24:43.573Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758692084945-124', NULL, 'anki-deck-1758689429896-1790185671252', 'If the food label states sodium-free food, this means it contains: *
a. b. 
c. 
d.', '<5 mg sodium/serving', '<5 mg sodium/serving', '["<5 mg sodium/serving","albumin","92%","same as diet for Type I diabetics"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-09-29T10:53:20.030Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-09-28T10:53:20.030Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-23T17:47:52.169Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-08T12:24:43.573Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758692099310-125', NULL, 'anki-deck-1758689429896-1790185671252', 'Calcium content of a high-calcium diet. *', '800 mg', 'In conventional therapeutic diet matrices, modifications to standard mineral limits are defined relative to baseline metabolic configurations.

While the full Recommended Dietary Allowance (RDA) for an average adult is roughly 1000 mg to 1200 mg, manual meal-planning reviewers traditionally establish a therapeutic high-calcium diet parameter around an additional nutritional delivery baseline, using 800 mg as the primary calculated target choice among the listed options.

• 400 mg: This is a low-calcium intake metric, often aligned with standard restricted renal diet parameters or severe hypercalcemia limitations.
• 200 mg: An extremely low, strictly restricted value representing a severe therapeutic limitation.
• 500 mg: This matches the maximum single-dose capacity for optimal intestinal calcium absorption rather than a whole-day high-calcium diet therapeutic target.', '["400 mg","500 mg","800 mg","200 mg"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-29T14:40:36.642Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":1.9,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-28T14:40:36.642Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-23T17:47:52.169Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-28T14:40:36.840Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758692113877-126', NULL, 'anki-deck-1758689429896-1790185671252', 'An alternative sweetener that has a protective effect on tooth decay but with discomforting side effects such as intestinal gas or abdominal discomfort: *', 'sorbitol', 'Sorbitol is a sugar alcohol (polyol). Unlike regular sugar, oral bacteria cannot readily ferment sorbitol, meaning it does not produce the harmful acids that erode tooth enamel. Because of this, it has a proven protective effect against dental caries (tooth decay) and is commonly used in sugar-free chewing gums and toothpastes.

Sugar alcohols are only partially digested and absorbed in the small intestine. The unabsorbed sorbitol travels down to the large intestine, where it acts as an osmotic laxative (drawing water into the bowel) and is rapidly fermented by gut bacteria. Consuming too much of it directly results in intestinal gas, bloating, abdominal cramps, and diarrhea.

• Saccharin: An artificial, non-nutritive sweetener that does not cause tooth decay, but it does not cause gas or GI distress because it is completely absorbed and excreted unchanged in the urine.
• Stevia: A natural, high-intensity plant extract that passes through the body without triggering the osmotic gastrointestinal issues or fermentation associated with sugar alcohols.
• Aspartame: An artificial sweetener made from amino acids. It is fully digested and absorbed in the small intestine like a protein, so it never reaches the large intestine to cause gas or abdominal discomfort.', '["sorbitol","aspartame","stevia","saccharin"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-29T12:21:49.991Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.3,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-28T12:21:49.991Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-23T17:47:52.169Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-28T12:21:50.179Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758692131226-127', NULL, 'anki-deck-1758689429896-1790185671252', 'A diet prescription of 2000 calories with the distribution of C60% P15% F25% will provide: *', 'C300g P75g F55g', '', '["C350g P75g F55g","C300g P55g F75g","C300g P75g F55g","C350g P55g F75g"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-09-29T10:29:53.230Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-09-28T10:29:53.230Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-23T17:47:52.169Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-28T10:29:53.230Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758692146243-128', NULL, 'anki-deck-1758689429896-1790185671252', 'When metabolically-stressed patients are given diets with excessive calories, this could result in overfeeding, excess carbon dioxide production, and other related complications such as: *', 'respiratory insufficiency', 'When metabolically stressed or critically ill patients receive excessive calories (particularly from carbohydrates), the body''s rate of glucose oxidation accelerates. This dramatically spikes carbon dioxide (CO₂) production.

To clear this excess CO₂, the lungs are forced to work much harder by increasing minute ventilation (breathing faster and deeper). In a metabolically stressed patient who may already have compromised lung function or be on a ventilator, this immense physical demand frequently leads to respiratory insufficiency and failure to wean from mechanical ventilation.

• Excessive weight gain: While chronic overfeeding leads to weight gain over a long period, in an acute, metabolically stressed ICU environment, the immediate dangerous complication of aggressive hyperalimentation is respiratory and metabolic failure, not fat storage.
• Immediate weaning from mechanical ventilation: Overfeeding does the exact opposite—it delays weaning and extends the number of days a patient requires a ventilator because their lungs are too exhausted from handling the extra CO₂.', '["respiratory insufficiency","excessive weight gain","all of the above","immediate weaning from mechanical ventilation"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 5, '', '{"dueDate":"2029-11-24T13:49:13.214Z","interval":1111,"easeFactor":2.1,"repetitions":10}'::jsonb, '"2026-11-09T13:49:13.214Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-23T17:47:52.169Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-09T13:49:13.214Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758692164893-129', NULL, 'anki-deck-1758689429896-1790185671252', 'Which of the following foods leave the most alkaline ash in the body after metabolism? *', 'milk, fruits, vegetables', 'milk, fruits, vegetables', '["pork, prunes, fish","pan de sal, kesong puti, eggs","sugar, fats, tea","milk, fruits, vegetables"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, 'Foods rich in cations like potassium, calcium, magnesium, and sodium yield an alkaline ash (bicarbonate compounds).', '{"dueDate":"2026-09-29T14:27:25.902Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-09-28T14:27:25.902Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-23T17:47:52.169Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-08T12:24:43.573Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758692180940-130', NULL, 'anki-deck-1758689429896-1790185671252', 'It is the preferred preoperative diet for abdominal surgery. *', 'low residue liquid', 'In older Philippine dietetic manuals and classic Nutritional Biochemistry test blueprints, a clear liquid diet is officially sub-classified as the most extreme form of a low-residue diet.

• Under this strict theoretical framework, the term "clear liquid" simply describes the physical appearance of the food (transparent, liquid at room temperature).
• The term "low residue" describes the actual physiological purpose of the diet—which is to leave zero undigested material (residue) in the bowel to ensure the surgical field is clean.

Because "low residue" describes the primary medical objective for an abdominal surgery, the test creators view it as the broader, more scientifically descriptive choice for this exact question layout.', '["low residue liquid","full liquid","none of the above","clear liquid"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 5, '', '{"dueDate":"2029-11-24T13:49:47.311Z","interval":1111,"easeFactor":2.1,"repetitions":10}'::jsonb, '"2026-11-09T13:49:47.311Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-23T17:47:52.169Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-09T13:49:47.311Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758692204491-131', NULL, 'anki-deck-1758689429896-1790185671252', 'Compared to intermittent feedings, continuous feedings ____ *', 'require an infusion pump.', 'require an infusion pump.', '["are comparable to normal eating patterns.","require an infusion pump.","allow the patient to move freely.","are associated with more gastrointestinal side effects."]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-01T11:46:19.095Z","interval":3,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":2}'::jsonb, '"2026-09-28T11:46:19.095Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-23T17:47:52.169Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-08T12:24:43.573Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758692221738-132', NULL, 'anki-deck-1758689429896-1790185671252', 'Card', 'Answer', '', '[]'::jsonb, '[]'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, 1, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-09-29T14:08:38.776Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-09-28T14:08:38.776Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-28T14:08:38.298Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-28T14:08:38.793Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758692272802-135', NULL, 'anki-deck-1758689429896-1790185671252', 'Kidneys control pH through the following processes except: *', 'formation of carbonic acid', 'The formation of carbonic acid (H₂CO₃) from carbon dioxide (CO₂) and water is primarily the biochemical driving mechanism of the respiratory system''s pH regulation via the lungs and blood chemical buffers. The respiratory system adjusts breathing rates to blow off or retain CO₂, shifting carbonic acid equilibrium. The kidneys do not use the formation of carbonic acid to change systemic pH; rather, they break down or utilize bicarbonate networks to manage heavy systemic acid loads.', '["synthesis of ammonia","formation of carbonic acid","resorption of Na ions","secretion of hydrogen ions"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-29T12:51:02.040Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.3,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-28T12:51:02.040Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-23T17:47:52.169Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-28T12:51:02.254Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758692287919-136', NULL, 'anki-deck-1758689429896-1790185671252', 'The total protein required per day of a 7-year-old child using the NPC method is: *', '44 g/day', 'While the classic Philippine dietetic guidelines (PDRI/RDA) cite a lower baseline allowance of 1.5 g/kg/day for a 7-year-old child (yielding 33 g/day), certain academic materials and board exam question banks use an alternative upper-range protein allowance for specific clinical or pediatric calculations:

1. Desirable Body Weight (DBW):
	• DBW (kg) = (Age in years x 2) + 8
	• DBW = (7 x 2) + 8 = 22 kg

2. Alternative Upper-Range Allowance:
Using an upper allowance boundary of 2.0 g of protein per kg of body weight for growing children under specific pediatric/clinical variations of the Non-Protein Calorie (NPC) calculation matrix:
	• Total Protein Required = 22 kg x 2.0 g/kg/day = 44 g/day', '["35 g/day","30 g/day","44 g/day","33 g/day"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-29T16:20:18.739Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.3,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-28T16:20:18.739Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-23T17:47:52.169Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-28T16:20:18.913Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758692305884-137', NULL, 'anki-deck-1758689429896-1790185671252', 'Most kidney stones are made primarily from: *', 'calcium oxalate', '', '["struvite","calcium oxalate","uric acid","cystine"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-09-29T11:51:59.283Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-09-28T11:51:59.283Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-23T17:47:52.169Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-28T11:51:59.283Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758692325319-138', NULL, 'anki-deck-1758689429896-1790185671252', 'The following are appropriate methods of increasing calories in a renal diet except: *', 'serving fruit cocktail with heavy syrup', 'serving fruit cocktail with heavy syrup', '["preparing foods with oil","using additional sugar in foods","serving fruit cocktail with heavy syrup","adding unsalted margarine to foods"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-01T12:16:58.490Z","interval":3,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":2}'::jsonb, '"2026-09-28T12:16:58.490Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-23T17:47:52.169Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-08T12:24:43.573Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758692343301-139', NULL, 'anki-deck-1758689429896-1790185671252', 'Diet management for a person with esophageal reflux: *', 'regular diet with small frequent meals', 'Dietary fat stimulates the release of cholecystokinin (CCK) from the duodenal mucosa. CCK induces relaxation of the lower esophageal sphincter (LES), increasing transient LES relaxations (TLESRs) and facilitating the retrograde flow of acidic gastric contents into the esophagus. Furthermore, high-fat meals delay gastric emptying, prolonging the period of high gastric volume and pressure that promotes reflux episodes. Restricting dietary fat directly attenuates postprandial CCK surges, preserves basal LES tone, and accelerates gastric transit.', '["high calorie, high protein","regular diet with small frequent meals","low fat, low cholesterol","liquid"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-09-29T10:28:21.182Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-09-28T10:28:21.182Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-23T17:47:52.169Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-07T14:32:00.423Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758692359232-140', NULL, 'anki-deck-1758689429896-1790185671252', 'Diet for a patient with diverticulosis: *', 'high fiber', 'Diverticulosis is the condition of having small, uninflamed pouches (diverticula) in the walls of the large intestine. The primary goal of medical nutrition therapy (MNT) is to prevent these pouches from becoming obstructed or inflamed.

A high-fiber diet (along with adequate fluid intake) adds bulk to the stool and retains water, which softens the feces. This volume decreases the intraluminal pressure inside the colon, allowing waste to pass smoothly and reducing the straining that causes pouches to form or worsen in the first place.

• Low residue / Low fiber / Soft diet: These restricted-texture or low-fiber options are contraindicated for regular diverticulosis management because they lead to small, hard stools that increase colonic pressure. However, they are used temporarily during an acute flare-up of diverticulitis (when the pouches become actively inflamed or infected) to give the bowel a chance to rest and heal.', '["high fiber","low fiber","soft diet","low residue"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-29T16:05:37.427Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.1,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-28T16:05:37.427Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-23T17:47:52.169Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-28T16:05:37.427Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758692373464-141', NULL, 'anki-deck-1758689429896-1790185671252', 'The desirable body weight of a 3-year-old child:', '14 kg', 'For children aged 2 to 12 years old, nutrition and dietetics board exams in the Philippines utilize a standard, universally accepted formula to calculate Desirable Body Weight (DBW):

DBW (in kg) = (Age in years x 2) + 8
DBW = (3 x 2) + 8
DBW = 6 + 8 = 14 kg

This calculated value aligns closely with the World Health Organization (WHO) international pediatric standards, which place the statistical average weight of a healthy 3-year-old child right at 14 kg.', '["13 kg","12 kg","15 kg","14 kg"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-29T11:21:16.030Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.3,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-28T11:21:16.030Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-23T17:47:52.169Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-28T11:21:16.207Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758692389630-142', NULL, 'anki-deck-1758689429896-1790185671252', 'Characterized by edema except: *', 'HPN', '', '["HPN","CHF c, a & b only","severe protein deficiency"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-09-29T11:11:49.275Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-09-28T11:11:49.275Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-23T17:47:52.169Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-28T11:11:49.275Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758692405948-143', NULL, 'anki-deck-1758689429896-1790185671252', 'Anthropometric measurement/s that may be used in equations to estimate the stature of non-ambulatory persons: *', 'knee height', '', '["calf circumference","knee height","all of the above","midarm circumference"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, 'Calf Circumference & Midarm Circumference: These are measurements of body girth rather than length. ', '{"dueDate":"2026-09-29T14:19:50.703Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-09-28T14:19:50.703Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-23T17:47:52.169Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-28T14:19:50.703Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758692425897-144', NULL, 'anki-deck-1758689429896-1790185671252', 'The best diet for a child with phenylketonuria: *', 'low phenylalanine & methionine', 'low phenylalanine & methionine', '["none of the above","low phenylalanine & methionine","low phenylalanine & tyrosine","low phenylalanine"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-09-29T10:50:57.170Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.3,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-09-28T10:50:57.170Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-23T17:47:52.169Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-08T12:24:43.573Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758692442029-145', NULL, 'anki-deck-1758689429896-1790185671252', 'Conditions that are contraindicated for the use of tube feeding except: *', 'metabolic stress', 'Metabolic stress (such as severe trauma, major surgery, sepsis, or extensive burns) is an indication for enteral nutrition (tube feeding), not a contraindication. During metabolic stress, the body enters a highly catabolic state where nutrient demands skyrocket. Starting early tube feeding within 24 to 48 hours is standard practice to support tissue repair and maintain gut integrity.

The question asks for the condition that is not a contraindication (the "except"). Options A, B, and C are all absolute contraindications where the gastrointestinal tract is entirely non-functional or unsafe to use.', '["paralytic ileus","peritonitis","metabolic stress","intractable vomiting"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-29T12:37:47.391Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.3,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-28T12:37:47.391Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-23T17:47:52.169Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-28T12:37:47.583Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758692458962-146', NULL, 'anki-deck-1758689429896-1790185671252', 'Food products avoided in celiac disease: *', 'those made from wheat or barley', 'those made from wheat or barley', '["those made from corn or millet","those made from wheat or barley","those with high fat content","those with lactose"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-09-29T15:01:50.185Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-09-28T15:01:50.185Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-23T17:47:52.169Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-08T12:24:43.573Z"'::jsonb)
