('anki-card-1758692476928-147', NULL, 'anki-deck-1758689429896-1790185671252', 'Diabetes mellitus characterized by absolute insulin deficiency: *', 'Type 1 DM', '', '["prediabetes","GDM","type 2 DM","Type 1 DM"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-09-29T11:38:12.610Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-09-28T11:38:12.610Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-23T17:47:52.169Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-28T11:38:12.610Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758692494227-148', NULL, 'anki-deck-1758689429896-1790185671252', 'The accepted total cholesterol to HDL ratio is: *', '0', '4.0<span style="color: rgb(32, 33, 36);"> </span>', '["6.5","5.5","5","0"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-09-29T11:41:36.834Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-09-28T11:41:36.834Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-23T17:47:52.169Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-08T12:24:43.573Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758692509860-149', NULL, 'anki-deck-1758689429896-1790185671252', 'Primary causes of peptic ulcer except: *', 'spicy foods', 'While it is a widely held belief that eating highly spicy foods can erode the stomach lining and cause ulcers, comprehensive clinical research has proven that they do not cause peptic ulcers.

Spicy foods can irritate an already existing ulcer flare-up, increase acid reflux sensations, or exacerbate abdominal discomfort, but they lack the physiological capacity to initiate the actual open sores or ulcerations on their own.

• Gastritis: Chronic inflammation of the stomach lining (gastritis) directly weakens the protective mucosal barrier over time, leaving the tissue vulnerable to auto-digestion by harsh stomach acids and pepsin, which frequently progresses into an ulcer.
• H. pylori infection: Helicobacter pylori is a spiral bacterium that colonizes the mucosal layer of the stomach. It secretes enzymes (like urease) and causes localized inflammatory responses that physically degrade the stomach''s protective mucus coat, serving as the leading global biological cause of peptic ulcer disease.
• Aspirins & other NSAIDs: Nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drugs (NSAIDs) chemically inhibit cyclooxygenase (COX) enzymes, which drastically reduces the body''s synthesis of systemic prostaglandins. Since prostaglandins are directly responsible for producing the stomach''s protective mucus layer and maintaining healthy mucosal blood flow, prolonged NSAID use leaves the stomach unprotected against its own digestive juices.', '["spicy foods","gastritis","aspirins & other NSAIDs","pylori infection"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 5, '', '{"dueDate":"2029-11-24T13:48:59.944Z","interval":1111,"easeFactor":2.1,"repetitions":10}'::jsonb, '"2026-11-09T13:48:59.944Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-23T17:47:52.169Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-09T13:48:59.944Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758692536490-150', NULL, 'anki-deck-1758689429896-1790185671252', 'The best protein allowance for severely malnourished children is: *', '4 g/kg DBW', 'Severely malnourished children have vastly elevated protein and energy requirements to support tissue repair and catch-up growth. While a child needs significant protein to reverse wasting, the allowance must be managed precisely.

	• In the Initial/Stabilization phase, protein is restricted to 1 to 1.5 g/kg to prevent refeeding syndrome and avoid overwhelming the child''s compromised liver and kidneys.
	• In the Rehabilitation/Catch-up phase, the allowance is gradually escalated to an optimal level of 3 to 4 g/kg of Desired Body Weight (DBW) (and up to 5 g/kg under specific clinical parameters) to maximize rapid cellular synthesis. Because options vary by textbooks, 3 g/kg DBW represents the safe, universally established target threshold for rebuilding lean body mass without risking metabolic overload.', '["3 g/kg DBW","5 g/kg DBW","4 g/kg DBW","none of the above"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-29T11:13:26.414Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.3,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-28T11:13:26.414Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-23T17:47:52.169Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-28T11:13:26.591Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758692552141-151', NULL, 'anki-deck-1758689429896-1790185671252', 'This medication can produce severe malabsorption: *', 'laxatives', '', '["laxatives","aspirin","diuretics","cough syrup"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-09-29T10:56:03.266Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.3,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-09-28T10:56:03.266Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-23T17:47:52.169Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-28T10:56:03.485Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758692578989-152', NULL, 'anki-deck-1758689429896-1790185671252', 'This hormone decreases the urge to eat and increases physical activity to produce a negative energy balance. *', 'leptin', 'Leptin is a hormone produced primarily by adipose (fat) tissue that acts as a long-term regulator of energy balance.

When fat stores increase, leptin levels rise and signal the hypothalamus in the brain to suppress appetite (decrease the urge to eat) and boost metabolism and physical activity. This combination successfully drives the body toward a negative energy balance to burn off excess stored fat.

• ghrelin: The "hunger hormone" produced mainly by the stomach. Its levels rise when the stomach is empty, signaling the brain to increase the urge to eat and reduce energy expenditure (promoting a positive energy balance).
• adiponectin: Another hormone secreted by fat cells that helps regulate glucose levels and fatty acid breakdown while improving insulin sensitivity, but it does not directly drive the specific appetite-suppression and activity-boosting negative energy loop that leptin does.
• insulin: Secreted by the pancreas primarily to lower blood glucose by shifting it into cells. While it can act as a minor central satiety signal, its primary physiological role is nutrient storage and carbohydrate metabolism.', '["leptin","ghrelin","adiponectin","insulin"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-29T11:14:27.036Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.3,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-28T11:14:27.036Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-23T17:47:52.169Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-28T11:14:27.216Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758692593604-153', NULL, 'anki-deck-1758689429896-1790185671252', 'A prolonged use of corticosteroids can lead to: *', 'Na retention', '', '["Na retention","fatty liver","increased protein catabolism","poor glucose tolerance"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-09-29T11:30:14.193Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-09-28T11:30:14.193Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-23T17:47:52.169Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-28T11:30:14.193Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758692610039-154', NULL, 'anki-deck-1758689429896-1790185671252', 'The molecule most directly linked to the initiation of atherosclerotic plaques is: *', 'oxidized cholesterol', 'The absolute initial step in the formation of an atherosclerotic plaque begins when LDL cholesterol particles slip into the subendothelial space of an artery wall and become oxidized by free radicals.

This oxidized cholesterol is highly toxic to the blood vessel lining. It damages the endothelial cells, acting as the primary chemical trigger that forces the artery wall to express adhesion molecules. This signals monocytes (white blood cells) to enter the wall, consume the oxidized lipids, transform into "foam cells," and start building the plaque.

• Tumor necrosis factor (TNF): This is a pro-inflammatory cytokine that amplifies the immune response and accelerates plaque growth later on, but it is not the original molecule that initiates the process.
• Thromboxane A₂: A lipid mediator released by activated platelets to cause vasoconstriction and blood clotting (thrombosis), typically playing a major role when a plaque ruptures later in the disease.
• Platelet-derived growth factor (PDGF): A growth factor that stimulates the migration and multiplication of smooth muscle cells to form a fibrous cap over the lipid core during the progression phase.', '["thromoxane A2","tumor necrosis factor","platelet derived growth factor","oxidized cholesterol"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, 'The initiation of atherosclerosis begins when circulating low-density lipoprotein (LDL) cholesterol molecules cross a damaged endothelial barrier and pool within the inner layer of the artery wall (the tunica intima). Once trapped, these standard lipids undergo chemical modification by free radicals and reactive oxygen species (ROS), turning into oxidized cholesterol.', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-29T16:48:32.783Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.3,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-28T16:48:32.783Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-23T17:47:52.169Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-28T16:48:32.964Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758692627588-155', NULL, 'anki-deck-1758689429896-1790185671252', 'Card', 'Answer', '', '[]'::jsonb, '[]'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, 1, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-09-29T15:16:48.273Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-09-28T15:16:48.273Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-28T15:16:47.661Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-28T15:16:48.289Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758692643885-156', NULL, 'anki-deck-1758689429896-1790185671252', 'Type of formula delivery that is administered slowly and in constant amount over a period of 8 hours or 24 hours: *', 'continuous', '', '["drip","continuous","cyclic","intermittent"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-09-29T14:49:54.027Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-09-28T14:49:54.027Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-23T17:47:52.169Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-28T14:49:54.278Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758692821912-157', NULL, 'anki-deck-1758689429896-1790185671252', 'It must be supplemented in glycogen storage disease: *', 'calcium', 'Patients with hepatic glycogen storage disease (GSD)—especially Type I—cannot properly break down stored glycogen into free glucose. Because they are at constant risk for life-threatening hypoglycemia, their therapeutic diets must strictly eliminate fructose, sucrose, and galactose (found heavily in milk, fruit juices, and dairy products).

Eliminating standard dairy items from their diet completely cuts out the primary natural food source of calcium. Furthermore, continuous round-the-clock reliance on uncooked cornstarch therapies can sometimes interfere with optimal nutrient assimilation. Because of these necessary constraints, calcium (along with Vitamin D) must be heavily supplemented to avoid metabolic deficits, optimize skeletal density, and prevent the early development of osteoporosis.', '["iron","calcium","vitamin K","potassium"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 5, '', '{"dueDate":"2029-11-24T13:50:15.497Z","interval":1111,"easeFactor":2.1,"repetitions":10}'::jsonb, '"2026-11-09T13:50:15.497Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-23T17:47:52.169Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-09T13:50:15.497Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758692847945-158', NULL, 'anki-deck-1758689429896-1790185671252', 'A patient was put on a low-fat, low-cholesterol diet for a heart condition. He was asked to stay away butter and lard. What type of fat can be used for cooking? *', 'soya oil', '', '["soya oil","shortening","palm oil","coconut oil"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-09-29T11:53:14.947Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-09-28T11:53:14.947Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-23T17:47:52.169Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-28T11:53:15.202Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758692879176-159', NULL, 'anki-deck-1758689429896-1790185671252', 'A metabolic change that accompanies acute stress is: *', 'muscle protein catabolism', 'During acute physiological stress (such as severe trauma, major surgery, burns, or infection), the body enters a highly hypermetabolic and catabolic state.

To fuel vital organs and support the immune response, counter-regulatory hormones (like cortisol, epinephrine, and glucagon) are rapidly released. These hormones trigger muscle protein catabolism (the rapid breakdown of lean muscle tissue) to release amino acids into the bloodstream. The liver then uses these amino acids to create glucose via gluconeogenesis and synthesize acute-phase inflammatory proteins.

• hypoglycemia: Acute stress actually causes hyperglycemia (high blood sugar), often referred to as "stress diabetes," because the liver produces excess glucose while the body experiences temporary insulin resistance.
• elevated immune response: While the immune system is activated, a prolonged or severe acute stress response typically leads to localized immune dysregulation or immunosuppression over time due to the massive release of cortisol, rather than a universally protective, sustained "elevated" response.
• reduced heart & respiratory rates: Acute stress triggers the sympathetic "fight-or-flight" response, which heavily increases heart rate and respiratory rate to pump oxygenated blood to essential organs.', '["hypoglycemia","muscle protein catabolism","elevated immune response","reduced heart & respiratory rates"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-29T11:17:21.813Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.3,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-28T11:17:21.813Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-23T17:47:52.169Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-28T11:17:21.813Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758692895642-160', NULL, 'anki-deck-1758689429896-1790185671252', 'Vitamin K deficiency is frequently drug-induced. Which of the following drugs might be associated with this deficiency? 1. antibiotics 2. mineral oil 3. diuretics 4. anticoagulants *', '1, 2, 4 only', '1. Antibiotics: A large portion of the body''s vitamin K is synthesized by beneficial bacteria residing in the gut. Broad-spectrum antibiotics can destroy these bacteria, drastically reducing natural vitamin K production.

2. Mineral oil: Because vitamin K is a fat-soluble vitamin, it requires lipids to be absorbed properly. Mineral oil acts as a non-absorbable laxative that coats the intestines and dissolves fat-soluble vitamins (A, D, E, and K), carrying them out of the body in stool before they can be absorbed.

4. Anticoagulants: Medications like warfarin are vitamin K antagonists. They directly block the enzyme mechanism responsible for recycling vitamin K in the liver, leading to a functional vitamin K deficiency that prevents blood clotting factors from activating.

• 3. Diuretics: These medications increase urine output and primarily affect water-soluble minerals and electrolytes (like potassium, sodium, and magnesium) rather than the absorption or metabolism of fat-soluble vitamins like vitamin K.', '["1 & 2 only","1, 3, 4 only","1, 2, 3 only","1, 2, 4 only"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-29T11:18:33.275Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.3,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-28T11:18:33.275Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-23T17:47:52.169Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-28T11:18:33.450Z"'::jsonb),
