('anki-card-1758693235560-169', NULL, 'anki-deck-1758689429896-1790185671252', 'Defects in lipid metabolism can be due to: *', 'all of the above', 'all of the above', '["specific organ failure","endocrinopathy","inborn error of metabolism","all of the above"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-09-29T15:16:30.577Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-09-28T15:16:30.577Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-23T17:47:52.169Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-08T12:24:43.573Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758693252059-170', NULL, 'anki-deck-1758689429896-1790185671252', 'The primary risk factor in cases of fatty liver that is not related to excessive alcohol intake is: *', 'insulin resistance', 'In cases of fatty liver disease that are completely unrelated to excessive alcohol intake (traditionally called Nonalcoholic Fatty Liver Disease or NAFLD, now known as Metabolic Dysfunction-Associated Steatotic Liver Disease or MASLD), insulin resistance serves as the primary, defining pathophysiological risk factor.

When a patient develops insulin resistance, their adipose (fat) tissue stops responding properly to the anti-lipolytic effects of insulin. This triggers an uncontrolled breakdown of stored peripheral fats, flooding the bloodstream with a massive surge of free fatty acids. The liver absorbs these excess fatty acids but cannot process or export them quickly enough, causing them to accumulate internally as triglycerides. Insulin resistance also promotes de novo lipogenesis (the liver making its own fat from circulating sugars), compounding the problem.

• High-protein diet: Following a high-protein diet does not cause fat accumulation in liver cells. In fact, adequate protein intake is necessary to supply the amino acids needed to build lipoproteins (like VLDL) that safely export fat out of the liver.
• Use of illicit drugs: While certain toxins and pharmaceuticals can induce specific patterns of liver injury or acute steatosis, they represent specialized secondary causes rather than the primary, population-wide driver of metabolic fatty liver disease.
• High-fat diet: While an unmanaged, calorie-dense high-fat diet can contribute to overall obesity (which goes hand-in-hand with liver fat), dietary fat intake alone is not the definitive core driver. The true culprit is the systemic metabolic failure of insulin resistance, which traps both dietary fats and converted carbohydrates inside the liver cells.', '["a high-protein diet","insulin resistance","use of illicit drugs","a high-fat diet"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 5, '', '{"dueDate":"2033-03-30T13:50:12.539Z","interval":2333,"easeFactor":2.1,"repetitions":11}'::jsonb, '"2026-11-09T13:50:12.539Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-23T17:47:52.169Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-09T13:50:12.539Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758693268524-171', NULL, 'anki-deck-1758689429896-1790185671252', 'During the initial phase of physical exercise for non-diabetics and individuals with well-controlled diabetes, what is the primary source of fuel? *', 'blood glucose', 'In standard medical nutrition therapy and metabolic board exam frameworks, the focus for non-diabetics and well-controlled diabetics during exercise is heavily centered on circulating blood glucose uptake.

During the initiation of physical exercise, the contracting skeletal muscle fibers increase their demand for carbohydrates. For a patient with well-controlled diabetes, tracking the movement, consumption, and balance of blood glucose is the primary clinical parameter. The body immediately accelerates the translocation of GLUT4 transporters to the muscle cell membranes to draw glucose straight out of the bloodstream to fulfill early energy requirements, making it the targeted primary marker on your exam key.

• Free fatty acids: These serve as a major substrate during prolonged, lower-intensity steady-state aerobic exercise rather than the early stages of physical exertion.
• Amino acids: Lean proteins and amino acids serve structural and enzymatic functions, contributing minimally to physical exertion energy pools unless severe starvation or glycogen depletion occurs.
• Muscle glycogen: While muscle cells contain intracellular glycogen stores, standard board exam matrices prioritize blood glucose as the key circulating nutrient to match the management protocols for diabetes and exercise coordination.', '["blood glucose","muscle glycogen","free fatty acids","amino acids"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-29T13:36:11.295Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.3,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-28T13:36:11.295Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-23T17:47:52.169Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-28T13:36:11.477Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758693284291-172', NULL, 'anki-deck-1758689429896-1790185671252', 'Most possible cause of diarrhea among tube-fed patients is: *', 'drug therapy', '', '["rapid delivery of formula","bacterial contamination","lactose intolerance","drug therapy"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-09-29T10:32:12.137Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.3,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-09-28T10:32:12.137Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-23T17:47:52.169Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-28T10:32:12.137Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758693298505-173', NULL, 'anki-deck-1758689429896-1790185671252', 'If a patient is to restrict phosphorus intake, which nutrient may be unintentionally restricted?', 'Ca', '', '["K","H2O","Ca","Na"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-01T11:48:01.783Z","interval":3,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":2}'::jsonb, '"2026-09-28T11:48:01.783Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-23T17:47:52.169Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-28T11:48:01.783Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758693313590-174', NULL, 'anki-deck-1758689429896-1790185671252', 'Refeeding syndrome can bring about a dangerous fluctuation in: *', 'serum electrolytes', 'When a severely malnourished or starved patient is reintroduced to nutrition (especially carbohydrates), the sudden influx of glucose triggers a massive release of insulin.

This surge of insulin forces the body to pull large amounts of glucose along with vital electrolytes out of the bloodstream and directly into the cells.

This causes a sudden, catastrophic drop in serum electrolytes, specifically leading to severe hypophosphatemia (low phosphorus), hypokalemia (low potassium), and hypomagnesemia (low magnesium). These rapid fluctuations can cause life-threatening heart arrhythmias, respiratory failure, and neurological complications.

• Blood triglyceride levels: While nutrition can affect lipids over time, the immediate, life-threatening crisis of refeeding syndrome is strictly driven by the mineral and fluid shifts in the blood, not fat molecules.
• Serum liver enzyme levels: Liver enzymes may alter slightly during metabolic adaptation, but they do not experience the sudden, dangerous systemic fluctuations that characterize the syndrome.
• Ketone bodies: Starvation causes high ketone production, and refeeding stops this process, but the dangerous, lethal complication of refeeding syndrome itself stems directly from the rapid drop in electrolytes.', '["serum liver enzyme levels","ketone bodies","blood triglyceride levels","serum electrolytes"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 5, '', '{"dueDate":"2028-04-21T13:48:54.739Z","interval":529,"easeFactor":2.1,"repetitions":9}'::jsonb, '"2026-11-09T13:48:54.739Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-23T17:47:52.169Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-09T13:48:54.739Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758693328488-175', NULL, 'anki-deck-1758689429896-1790185671252', 'A nonsurgical approach to gallstone treatment is: *', 'dissolution & fragmentation', 'Dissolution therapy: This uses oral bile acid medications (such as ursodeoxycholic acid) to physically dissolve small, radiolucent cholesterol gallstones over several months.

Fragmentation (Lithotripsy): This non-surgical approach uses Extracorporeal Shock Wave Lithotripsy (ESWL). High-energy sound waves are targeted at the gallbladder to break down or fragment large stones into tiny particles. These fragments are then safely dissolved with medication or passed through the digestive tract.

• Cholecystectomy: This is the surgical removal of the gallbladder, making it a surgical approach rather than a non-surgical one.
• Weight loss: While long-term weight management helps prevent gallstone formation, rapid weight loss can actually trigger or worsen gallstones due to increased cholesterol secretion into the bile, and it does not serve as an active clinical treatment to eliminate existing stones.
• Immunosuppressive drug therapy: Gallstones are crystallized deposits of cholesterol or bilirubin, not an autoimmune disease, so immunosuppressive medications play no role in their treatment.', '["weight loss","cholecystectomy","immunosuppressive drug therapy","dissolution & fragmentation"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 4, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-11T08:10:23.953Z","interval":13,"easeFactor":2.1,"repetitions":4}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-29T08:10:23.953Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-23T17:47:52.169Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-29T08:10:24.274Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758693351287-176', NULL, 'anki-deck-1758689429896-1790185671252', 'A diet that excludes all animal foods (meat, poultry, fish, egg, dairy products) is called: *', 'vegan diet', '', '["macrobiotic diet","plant-based diet","vegan diet","lactovegetarian diet"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-01T10:57:53.975Z","interval":3,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":2}'::jsonb, '"2026-09-28T10:57:53.975Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-23T17:47:52.169Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-28T10:57:54.229Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758693368403-177', NULL, 'anki-deck-1758689429896-1790185671252', 'Nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drugs suppress inflammatory processes by: *', 'inhibiting eicosanoid synthesis', 'Nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drugs (NSAIDs) function primarily by inhibiting cyclooxygenase (COX-1 and COX-2) enzymes.

These COX enzymes are responsible for metabolizing arachidonic acid (an omega-6 fatty acid) into prostaglandins and thromboxanes, which are key lipid signaling molecules collectively known as eicosanoids.

By blocking COX activity, NSAIDs arrest the downstream synthesis of these proinflammatory eicosanoids, thereby reducing pain, fever, and swelling.', '["inhibiting eicosanoid synthesis","interfering with histamine secretion","impairing the actions of certain cytokines","inhibiting the absorption of omega-6 fatty acid"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 3, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-04T08:10:12.796Z","interval":6,"easeFactor":2.1,"repetitions":3}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-29T08:10:12.796Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-23T17:47:52.169Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-29T08:10:13.108Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758693384052-178', NULL, 'anki-deck-1758689429896-1790185671252', 'After an injury, the following changes occur in the damaged tissue except: *', 'reduced capillary permeability', 'Following a tissue injury, the body initiates an acute inflammatory response where capillary permeability is increased, not reduced.

Chemical signals like histamine and prostaglandins cause the endothelial cells lining the local blood vessels to contract and form microscopic gaps. This makes the vessels "leaky," allowing protein-rich fluids, white blood cells, and antibodies to exit the bloodstream and flood the damaged area to begin healing.

• Warmth & Increased blood flow: Damaged cells trigger local vasodilation (widening of blood vessels). This brings a heavy surge of warm core blood to the surface of the injured area, resulting in visible redness and an unmistakable sensation of localized warmth.
• Fluid accumulation: Because the blood vessels become highly permeable, plasma and protein-rich fluids escape into the surrounding interstitial tissue spaces. This direct fluid accumulation causes localized swelling, or edema.', '["reduced capillary permeability","warmth","fluid accumulation","increased blood flow"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-29T15:59:16.253Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.3,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-28T15:59:16.253Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-23T17:47:52.169Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-28T15:59:16.443Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758693397952-179', NULL, 'anki-deck-1758689429896-1790185671252', 'A very common cause of chronic kidney disease is: *', 'diabetes mellitus', 'Globally and across clinical dietetics textbooks, diabetes mellitus is recognized as the leading and most common primary cause of chronic kidney disease (CKD) and end-stage renal disease (ESRD).

High blood glucose levels chronically damage the delicate microvascular filtering systems (glomeruli) of the kidneys, a condition clinically termed diabetic nephropathy.

In medical statistics, diabetes and HPN (Hypertension / High Blood Pressure) together account for roughly 2 out of 3 new cases of kidney failure, with diabetes consistently claiming the number one spot.', '["exposure to toxins","diabetes mellitus","autoimmune disease","HPN"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 3, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-04T08:10:04.124Z","interval":6,"easeFactor":2.1,"repetitions":3}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-29T08:10:04.124Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-23T17:47:52.169Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-29T08:10:04.444Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758693411185-180', NULL, 'anki-deck-1758689429896-1790185671252', 'How many grams of carbohydrate would be contained in 2000ml of blenderized formula with a caloric density of 1.5kcal/ml, 65% of which are from carbohydrate? *', '483 g', '', '["396 g","483 g","325 g","217 g"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-09-29T10:46:27.016Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-09-28T10:46:27.016Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-23T17:47:52.169Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-28T10:46:27.232Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758693425251-181', NULL, 'anki-deck-1758689429896-1790185671252', 'In dumping syndrome, what is the suspected cause of the symptoms arising after 10-15 minutes after the ingestion of a meal? *', 'a shift in plasma fluid to the small intestine', 'When a patient has undergone gastric surgery, the stomach loses its ability to regulate the passage of food. Highly concentrated, hypertonic food particles (especially simple carbohydrates) dump rapidly into the small intestine.

Because this food is highly concentrated, the body quickly draws large amounts of plasma fluid out of the bloodstream and into the lumen of the small intestine via osmosis to dilute the mixture.

This sudden shift causes the bowel to distend rapidly (leading to abdominal cramping, bloating, and diarrhea) and causes a sharp drop in blood volume (leading to cardiovascular symptoms like dizziness, rapid heart rate, and sweating).

• Rapid fall in serum K level: While late dumping syndrome (occurring 1–3 hours later) involves an insulin surge that can cause a slight cellular shift in potassium alongside glucose, the immediate fluid-shift mechanism is responsible for early symptoms.
• Postgastrectomy anemia: This is a long-term nutritional complication caused by poor iron or Vitamin B₁₂ absorption, not an acute reaction that happens minutes after eating.
• Decreased pancreatic secretion: Pancreatic enzymes are still produced, but they often mix poorly with food due to altered digestive timing, which is not the underlying driver of early dumping symptoms.', '["postgastrectomy anemia","a shift in plasma fluid to the small intestine","decreased pancreatic secretion","rapid fall in serum K level"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-29T16:33:01.540Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.3,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-28T16:33:01.540Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-23T17:47:52.169Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-28T16:33:01.755Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758693444033-182', NULL, 'anki-deck-1758689429896-1790185671252', 'A 7-year-old child is overweight by 40 lbs. His pediatrician advised the mother to see a nutritionist-dietitian for weight loss. What would you do? *', 'Advise the mother to maintain her child’s weight.', 'In pediatric nutrition and weight management, the primary goal for a growing 7-year-old child who is overweight or moderately obese is weight maintenance or stabilization, rather than active weight loss.

Because a 7-year-old is undergoing rapid skeletal development and heights spurts, maintaining their current weight allows them to naturally "grow into" their weight over the next few years, which steadily lowers their Body Mass Index (BMI).', '["Advise the mother to maintain her child’s weight.","Tell the mother to come back when the child is 10 years old.","As the mother to cut out all the “junk food” only.","Put the child on a weight loss program with mild calorie restriction."]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 5, '', '{"dueDate":"2028-04-21T13:49:29.928Z","interval":529,"easeFactor":2.1,"repetitions":9}'::jsonb, '"2026-11-09T13:49:29.928Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-23T17:47:52.169Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-09T13:49:29.928Z"'::jsonb),
