('anki-card-1758693476915-184', NULL, 'anki-deck-1758689429896-1790185671252', 'An 18-year-old patient has non-insulin dependent diabetes mellitus of youth (NIDDMY). What diet is appropriate for his condition? *', 'same as diet for Type I diabetics', 'Same as diet for type II diabetic', '["same as diet for Type II diabetics","same as diet for Type I diabetics","low carbohydrate diet","none of the above"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-09-29T12:21:36.342Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.3,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-09-28T12:21:36.342Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-23T17:47:52.169Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-08T12:24:43.573Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758693491615-185', NULL, 'anki-deck-1758689429896-1790185671252', 'All measures are allowed to increase the energy intake of a renal patient except: *', 'serving fruit cocktail with heavy syrup', 'Preparing foods with oil', '["serving fruit cocktail with heavy syrup","using additional sugar in foods","preparing foods with oil","adding unsalted margarine"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-09-29T14:38:57.463Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-09-28T14:38:57.463Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-23T17:47:52.169Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-08T12:24:43.573Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758693506495-186', NULL, 'anki-deck-1758689429896-1790185671252', 'It is considered an advantage of peritoneal dialysis: *', 'liberalized diet', 'Because peritoneal dialysis (PD) is typically performed every day or continuously overnight, it mimics the natural function of the kidneys much more closely than hemodialysis.

As a result, waste products, fluid, sodium, and potassium do not build up as drastically between treatments. This grants the patient a much more flexible, less restricted, or liberalized diet and fluid intake plan compared to the strict dietary limits required for hemodialysis.', '["liberalized diet","potential risk of peritonitis","minimal protein loss","mobility"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-29T11:15:19.652Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.3,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-28T11:15:19.652Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-23T17:47:52.169Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-28T11:15:19.836Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758693522512-187', NULL, 'anki-deck-1758689429896-1790185671252', 'It is conducted to evaluate long-term glycemic control: *', 'measuring glycated hemoglobin', 'measuring glycated hemoglobin', '["self-monitoring of blood glucose","testing urinary protein levels","testing urinary ketone levels","measuring glycated hemoglobin"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-09-29T10:48:15.407Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-09-28T10:48:15.407Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-23T17:47:52.169Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-08T12:24:43.573Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758693542878-188', NULL, 'anki-deck-1758689429896-1790185671252', 'Card', 'Answer', '', '[]'::jsonb, '[]'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-29T15:51:17.146Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.3,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-28T15:51:17.146Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-28T14:49:10.924Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-28T15:51:17.341Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758693557143-189', NULL, 'anki-deck-1758689429896-1790185671252', 'Card', 'Answer', '', '[]'::jsonb, '[]'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-29T16:54:26.053Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.3,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-28T16:54:26.053Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-28T11:57:09.946Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-28T16:54:26.234Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758693571292-190', NULL, 'anki-deck-1758689429896-1790185671252', 'Triglyceride levels of patients with mild hypertriglyceridemia may be improved by: *', 'limiting their intake of refined carbohydrates', 'When a person consumes an excess of refined carbohydrates (such as white sugar, high-fructose corn syrup, white flour, and sugary beverages), the liver rapidly processes the surging influx of simple sugars.

Through a metabolic process called de novo lipogenesis, the liver converts these excess carbohydrates directly into fatty acids, which are then packaged into VLDL (very-low-density lipoprotein) particles as triglyceride levels. Limiting refined carbohydrates and replacing them with complex, high-fiber options is the most powerful and direct dietary intervention for dropping serum triglycerides back into a healthy range.', '["limiting their intake of refined carbohydrates","reducing their cholesterol intake","consuming moderate amounts of alcohol","reducing their sodium intake"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-29T14:57:21.505Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.1,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-28T14:57:21.505Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-23T17:47:52.169Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-28T14:57:21.702Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758693613457-192', NULL, 'anki-deck-1758689429896-1790185671252', 'Refer to the case below: A 35-year-old diabetic teacher went to the Nutrition Clinic for counseling. She is 5’4” tall andweighs 86 kg. She enrolled in a gymnasium and tried taking special slimming teas but she onlyexperienced hypoglycemia. She never lost a pound, instead gained weight. Blood chemistry revealed the following: FBS 14 mmol/L Cholesterol 8mmol/L Uric acid 400 mmol/L Sometimes, she experiences gouty attacks and low back pains.
How much overweight is she? *', '154%', 'not more than 5 exchanges', '["155%","154%","156%","153%"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-09-29T14:08:31.779Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-09-28T14:08:31.779Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-23T17:47:52.169Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-08T12:24:43.573Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758693637557-193', NULL, 'anki-deck-1758689429896-1790185671252', 'Refer to the case below: A 35-year-old diabetic teacher went to the Nutrition Clinic for counseling. She is 5’4” tall andweighs 86 kg. She enrolled in a gymnasium and tried taking special slimming teas but she onlyexperienced hypoglycemia. She never lost a pound, instead gained weight. Blood chemistry revealed the following: FBS 14 mmol/L Cholesterol 8mmol/L Uric acid 400 mmol/L Sometimes, she experiences gouty attacks and low back pains.
What would be the best protein requirement? *', '55 g', '1. Calculate the Desirable Body Weight (DBW):
	• Convert height to centimeters: 5 feet 4 inches = 64 inches.
	• 64 inches x 2.54 cm/inch = 162.56 cm
	• Use Tannhauser’s Method (the standard method utilized in Philippine board exams):
		• DBW (kg) = (Height in cm - 100) - 10%
		• Base weight = 162.56 - 100 = 62.56 kg
		• Deduct 10%: 62.56 - 6.25 = 56.31 kg
	• Rounding off the DBW gives approximately 55 kg.

2. Determine the Protein Requirement:
	• For an adult with diabetes and normal renal function, the standard nutritional care plan uses a baseline allowance of 1.0 gram of protein per kilogram of DBW per day.
	• Total Protein Required = 55 kg DBW x 1.0 g/kg = 55 grams

Because her blood work reveals elevated uric acid (400 mmol/L) and she experiences gouty attacks, keeping the protein requirement at a controlled, standard adult level of 1.0 g/kg (rather than high-protein or upper-range clinical levels) helps manage purine intake while protecting kidney health.', '["55 g","50 g","65 g","60 g"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, 'For a female who is 5''4" (162.5 cm), the estimated DBW using standard clinical formulas (like Tannhauser''s method) is approximately 55 to 56 kg. The baseline recommendation for a patient with controlled/early-stage conditions is 1.0 gram of protein per kilogram of DBW per day. The patient has an elevated uric acid level of 400 µmol/L and suffers from acute gouty attacks. Keeping protein at a conservative, adequate baseline of 55 g (roughly 15% of her target therapeutic energy requirement) prevents excessive purine load from animal proteins while providing enough essential amino acids to protect muscle mass during weight loss. ', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-29T16:24:31.803Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.3,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-28T16:24:31.803Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-28T11:40:39.332Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-28T16:24:31.978Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758693669971-194', NULL, 'anki-deck-1758689429896-1790185671252', 'Refer to the case below: A 35-year-old diabetic teacher went to the Nutrition Clinic for counseling. She is 5’4” tall andweighs 86 kg. She enrolled in a gymnasium and tried taking special slimming teas but she onlyexperienced hypoglycemia. She never lost a pound, instead gained weight. Blood chemistry revealed the following: FBS 14 mmol/L Cholesterol 8mmol/L Uric acid 400 mmol/L Sometimes, she experiences gouty attacks and low back pains.
What should be her daily fluid intake? *', '3 liters', 'The patient has a significantly elevated uric acid level (400 µmol/L) and is actively experiencing symptomatic gouty attacks.

In medical nutrition therapy (MNT) for hyperuricemia and gout, increasing fluid intake is a primary non-pharmacological strategy. Drinking 3 liters of water per day (approximately 12 to 14 cups) is the standard clinical recommendation to help dilute the concentration of uric acid in the blood and continuously flush out uric acid crystals through the kidneys.

The patient also complains of low back pains, which are classic clinical warnings of crystalluria or microscopic uric acid stone formation in the renal tract. Forcing a fluid target of 3 liters ensures high urine output, which reduces the saturation of urate in the kidneys and directly prevents the precipitation of painful uric acid calculi (stones).

• 4 liters / 5 liters: While staying well-hydrated is crucial, pushing a patient''s daily baseline up to 4 or 5 liters is excessively high for standard therapeutic maintenance and can unnecessary strain the kidneys or risk electrolyte imbalances like hyponatremia unless a severe acute toxic state is being aggressively managed in a hospital.
• 2 liters: This is the standard fluid recommendation (about 8 cups) for average, healthy adults. It is insufficient for a patient actively experiencing gouty attacks and potential renal stone pains, as it does not provide the extra volumetric flushing capacity required to lower high serum uric acid concentrations.', '["2 liters","4 liters","3 liters","5 liters"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 5, 'The patient has an elevated serum uric acid level of 400 µmol/L and suffers from painful gouty attacks. The frontline non-pharmacological treatment for hyperuricemia is to increase fluid intake to 3 liters per day (approx. 12 cups). Consuming 3 liters of fluid helps keep the kidneys well-perfused, dilutes the urine, and physically flushes out excess uric acid. This high volume prevents uric acid from crystallizing in the joints (which causes gout flares) and reduces the risk of forming uric acid kidney stones.', '{"dueDate":"2029-11-24T13:49:49.945Z","interval":1111,"easeFactor":2.1,"repetitions":10}'::jsonb, '"2026-11-09T13:49:49.945Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-23T17:47:52.169Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-09T13:49:49.945Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758693714719-196', NULL, 'anki-deck-1758689429896-1790185671252', 'Card', 'Answer', '', '[]'::jsonb, '[]'::jsonb, NULL, NULL, 1, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-09-29T14:14:44.444Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-09-28T14:14:44.444Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-28T14:14:43.958Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-28T14:14:44.463Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758693735169-197', NULL, 'anki-deck-1758689429896-1790185671252', 'Refer to the case below: A 35-year-old diabetic teacher went to the Nutrition Clinic for counseling. She is 5’4” tall andweighs 86 kg. She enrolled in a gymnasium and tried taking special slimming teas but she onlyexperienced hypoglycemia. She never lost a pound, instead gained weight. Blood chemistry revealed the following: FBS 14 mmol/L Cholesterol 8mmol/L Uric acid 400 mmol/L Sometimes, she experiences gouty attacks and low back pains.
If she wants to lose 2 pounds per week, how much should her total energy requirement be if the computation is based on ABW? *', '2000 kcal', 'The question explicitly specifies that the energy calculation must be based on her Actual Body Weight (ABW) of 86 kg. In standard clinical dietetics equations, an adult with an active daily schedule (such as a school teacher who stands and walks during lessons) combined with gym enrollment has an estimated maintenance energy factor of 35 kcal/kg of body weight:

Maintenance Calories = 86 kg x 35 kcal/kg = 3010 kcal/day

To achieve a target weight loss of 2 pounds per week, a person must establish a total weekly deficit of 7,000 calories. This breaks down to a mandatory deduction of exactly 1,000 kcal per day from their baseline maintenance requirement:

Target TER = 3010 kcal - 1000 kcal = 2010 kcal

Rounding this value to the nearest standard clinical prescription block yields exactly 2000 kcal.', '["2000 kcal","1900 kcal","1500 kcal","1000 kcal"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 5, '', '{"dueDate":"2028-04-21T13:48:45.514Z","interval":529,"easeFactor":2.1,"repetitions":9}'::jsonb, '"2026-11-09T13:48:45.514Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-23T17:47:52.169Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-09T13:48:45.514Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758693754151-198', NULL, 'anki-deck-1758689429896-1790185671252', 'Refer to the case below: A 35-year-old diabetic teacher went to the Nutrition Clinic for counseling. She is 5’4” tall andweighs 86 kg. She enrolled in a gymnasium and tried taking special slimming teas but she onlyexperienced hypoglycemia. She never lost a pound, instead gained weight. Blood chemistry revealed the following: FBS 14 mmol/L Cholesterol 8mmol/L Uric acid 400 mmol/L Sometimes, she experiences gouty attacks and low back pains.
What would be the best level of fat? *', '15 -20% of TER', 'This patient presents with multiple critical metabolic disorders—poorly controlled diabetes, obesity, hyperuricemia (gout), and severe hypercholesterolemia.

In standard board exam matrices, when a diabetic patient presents with elevated blood cholesterol levels, clinical guidelines prioritize managing cardiovascular risk. Restricting fat intake to a lower proportion of Total Energy Requirement (TER) assists in reducing saturated fat and dietary cholesterol. This therapeutic restriction helps address circulating blood lipids and manage obesity, while carbohydrate quality is managed separately to stabilize blood sugar.', '["25- 35% of TER","30-35% of TER","15 -20% of TER","20-25% of TER"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 5, '', '{"dueDate":"2033-03-30T13:50:18.373Z","interval":2333,"easeFactor":2.1,"repetitions":11}'::jsonb, '"2026-11-09T13:50:18.373Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-23T17:47:52.169Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-09T13:50:18.373Z"'::jsonb)
