('anki-card-1758693785316-199', NULL, 'anki-deck-1758689429896-1790185671252', 'Refer to the case below: A 35-year-old diabetic teacher went to the Nutrition Clinic for counseling. She is 5’4” tall andweighs 86 kg. She enrolled in a gymnasium and tried taking special slimming teas but she onlyexperienced hypoglycemia. She never lost a pound, instead gained weight. Blood chemistry revealed the following: FBS 14 mmol/L Cholesterol 8mmol/L Uric acid 400 mmol/L Sometimes, she experiences gouty attacks and low back pains.
Her body mass index is: *', '33', 'not more than 5 exchanges', '["34","35","33","32"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-09-29T15:16:23.965Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-09-28T15:16:23.965Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-23T17:47:52.169Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-08T12:24:43.573Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758693802965-200', NULL, 'anki-deck-1758689429896-1790185671252', 'Refer to the case below: A 35-year-old diabetic teacher went to the Nutrition Clinic for counseling. She is 5’4” tall andweighs 86 kg. She enrolled in a gymnasium and tried taking special slimming teas but she onlyexperienced hypoglycemia. She never lost a pound, instead gained weight. Blood chemistry revealed the following: FBS 14 mmol/L Cholesterol 8mmol/L Uric acid 400 mmol/L Sometimes, she experiences gouty attacks and low back pains.
How much is her total energy requirement based on DBW? *', '1950 kcal', '1. Re-establishing the Desirable Body Weight (DBW)

From our previous step, her height of 5''4" (162.56 cm) processed through Tannhauser''s Method yields:
• Base Weight: 162.56 - 100 = 62.56 kg
• Deduct 10% for female frame: 62.56 - 6.25 = 56.31 kg

2. Identifying the Activity Factor

• Her occupation as a teacher classifies her physical activity level as Light Activity.
• The standard calorie allowance factor for an individual with light activity is 35 kcal per kilogram of DBW per day.

3. Calculating the Total Energy Requirement (TER)

Multiplying her precise DBW by the light activity factor:
• TER = 56.31 kg × 35 kcal/kg = 1970.85 kcal

4. Applying Dietetic Rounding Rules

In Philippine dietetics, final caloric requirements are strictly rounded to the nearest 50 kcal block to simplify meal exchanges:
• Rounding 1970.85 kcal to the nearest 50 kcal block gives 1950 kcal', '["1950 kcal","1850 kcal","1900 kcal","2000 kcal"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 3, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-04T08:10:01.516Z","interval":6,"easeFactor":2.1,"repetitions":3}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-29T08:10:01.516Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-23T17:47:52.275Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-29T08:10:01.845Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1754741867292-0', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783608654256-1786809564961', 'The human milk has a high concentration of the following minerals EXCEPT:', 'Iron', 'Human breast milk naturally contains exceptionally low concentrations of iron (approximately 0.3 to 0.7 mg per liter in mature milk).

While the trace amount of iron present in breast milk is extremely well-absorbed by the infant''s gut due to high bioavailability (~20% to 50% absorption efficiency compared to only ~4% to 7% in commercial formulas), the absolute quantitative concentration of the mineral itself remains very poor. This is why healthy, full-term infants must rely heavily on their built-in liver iron stores during the first few months of life.

• Calcium and Phosphorus: These are vital macrominerals that are heavily present in human milk to construct the structural architecture of the infant''s rapidly growing skeletal system.
• Potassium: Potassium is one of the most prominent intracellular electrolytes found in high maternal concentrations within breast milk fluid to stabilize cellular membrane potentials and fluid homeostasis for the baby.', '["Calcium","Potassium","Iron","Phophorus"]'::jsonb, '["General Dietetics"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-01T14:27:32.579Z","interval":3,"easeFactor":2.35,"repetitions":2}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-29T14:27:32.579Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:28.332Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-29T14:27:32.579Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1754741963569-1', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783608654256-1786809564961', 'Development which starts in the central axis and progresses towards the extremities', 'Proximodistal', 'In human growth and development, the proximodistal pattern dictates that physical development and motor control progress from the center of the body outward toward the extremities (periphery). For example, a developing fetus grows its torso and shoulder buds before its arms, hands, and fingers. Similarly, an infant gains muscle control over their trunk and arms before they master the fine motor control of their fingers.

• Cephalocaudal: This describes development that moves from head-to-toe. A fetus''s head develops much faster than its lower body, and an infant learns to hold their head up before they learn to sit up or walk.
• Hypertrophy and Hyperplasia: These are specific cellular growth mechanisms (increase in cell size and cell number, respectively) rather than directional paths of physical development.', '["Cephalocaudal","Proximodistal","Hyperplasia","Hypertropy"]'::jsonb, '["General Dietetics"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-01T13:32:03.617Z","interval":3,"easeFactor":2.1,"repetitions":2}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-29T13:32:03.617Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:28.332Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-29T13:32:03.821Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1754742040498-2', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783608654256-1786809564961', 'Age period characterized by rapid differentiation and establishment of systems and orgarns:', 'Embryonic', 'The embryonic period spans from the third week through the eighth week of pregnancy. This critical phase is defined by organogenesis, which is the rapid differentiation of cells into distinct germ cell layers (ectoderm, mesoderm, and endoderm) and the foundation of all major body systems and rudimentary organs. By the end of the eighth week, the basic structural form of the human body and its systems are fully established.

The fetal periods (both early and late) span from the ninth week until birth. This phase is primarily characterized by the growth, refinement, and functional maturation of those already established systems rather than their initial structural establishment or differentiation.

The neonatal period refers to the first 28 days of life after birth, where the newborn adapts physiologically to extrauterine life.', '["Embryonic","Early fetal","Late fetal","Neonatal"]'::jsonb, '["General Dietetics"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-01T14:22:35.521Z","interval":3,"easeFactor":2.3,"repetitions":2}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-29T14:22:35.521Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:28.332Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-29T14:22:35.521Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1754742135578-3', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783608654256-1786809564961', 'A hormone secreted by the placenta that stimulates the secretion of estrogen and progresterone.', 'Chorionic gonadotropin', 'Secreted by the syncytiotrophoblast cells of the placenta immediately after implantation, hCG acts as a crucial chemical messenger during early pregnancy.

Its primary biological purpose is to travel to the ovaries and rescue the corpus luteum from breaking down. By binding to shared luteinizing hormone receptors, chorionic gonadotropin continuously stimulates the corpus luteum to secrete high levels of progesterone and estrogen. This preserves the lush endometrium and maintains the pregnancy until the placenta grows large enough to take over steroid production entirely.

• Placental lactogen: Also secreted by the placenta, this protein hormone primarily alters maternal carbohydrate and lipid metabolism to ensure a steady supply of glucose reaches the fetus. It also prepares the maternal breast tissue for future lactation, rather than stimulating steroid hormone production.
• Follicle-stimulating hormone (FSH) and Luteinizing hormone (LH): These are gonadotropins secreted by the anterior pituitary gland, not the placenta. Furthermore, maternal pituitary secretion of FSH and LH drops to near-zero during pregnancy due to the high negative feedback loops caused by circulating gestational steroids.', '["Luteinizing hormone","Chorionic gonadotropin","Placental lactogen","Follicle-stimulating hormone"]'::jsonb, '["General Dietetics"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 5, '', '{"dueDate":"2028-01-31T13:48:04.533Z","interval":448,"easeFactor":2.3,"repetitions":8}'::jsonb, '"2026-11-09T13:48:04.533Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:28.332Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-09T13:48:04.533Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1754742202441-4', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783608654256-1786809564961', 'Juan is a 6-month old infant. Using the established norms on mean ages, he is able to:', 'Crawl', '', '["Crawl","Walk with support","Grasp","Stand alone"]'::jsonb, '["General Dietetics"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-30T14:37:05.394Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-29T14:37:05.394Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:28.332Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-29T14:37:05.394Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1754742251189-5', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783608654256-1786809564961', 'A cell region acts as a selective barrier to the movement of substances into and out of the cell.', 'Plasma membrane', 'The plasma membrane (also called the cell membrane) is a dynamic lipid bilayer that serves as the boundary separating the intracellular environment from the extracellular space. Its primary physiological function is selective permeability, meaning it controls what enters and exits the cell. It allows essential nutrients and gases to enter while filtering out toxic materials and managing waste excretion.

• Nucleus: Acts as the control center of the cell and houses the genetic material (DNA).
• Ribosome: The site responsible for protein synthesis and translation.
• Cytoplasm: The gel-like internal region where cellular organelles are suspended and metabolic reactions take place.', '["Nucleus","Cytoplasm","Plasma membrane","Ribosome"]'::jsonb, '["General Dietetics"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-30T14:14:14.010Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-29T14:14:14.010Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:28.332Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-29T14:14:14.010Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1754742303784-6', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783608654256-1786809564961', 'A specialized organ formed during pregnancy which functions in removing fetal waste products', 'Placenta', 'The placenta is a highly specialized, temporary organ that forms inside the uterus exclusively during pregnancy. It establishes an interface between the maternal and fetal circulatory systems without letting their blood mix directly.

Deoxygenated blood carrying metabolic waste products from the fetus (such as carbon dioxide, urea, and creatinine) travels to the placenta. The placenta filters these waste materials out of the fetal bloodstream and transfers them into the mother''s blood, where her kidneys and lungs excrete them. Essentially, the placenta acts as the temporary lungs, liver, and kidneys for the baby until birth.

• Umbilical cord: This functions purely as a transport passageway or biological conduit. It contains blood vessels that physically carry waste to the placenta, but the cord itself does not process, filter, or remove the waste.
• Amniotic sac: This is the fluid-filled "bag of waters" that surrounds, cushions, and thermally protects the fetus, but it plays no role in chemical waste extraction.
• Uterus: This is the permanent muscular maternal organ that houses and protects the growing embryo and placenta, but it is not the actual specialized organ managing nutrient and waste exchange.', '["Amniotic sac","Placenta","Umbilical cord","Uterus"]'::jsonb, '["General Dietetics"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 3, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-05T15:11:05.179Z","interval":7,"easeFactor":2.3,"repetitions":3}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-29T15:11:05.179Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:28.332Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-29T15:11:05.388Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1754742369834-7', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783608654256-1786809564961', 'Normally, an infant is able to meet its iron needs for the first 3 months from:', 'its own liver stores of iron', 'During the third trimester of pregnancy, there is a massive transfer of iron from the mother across the placenta to the fetus. This allows a healthy, full-term newborn to be born with a rich endowment of iron, which is primarily accumulated and stored in the fetal liver as ferritin and hemosiderin.

During the first two months of life, a newborn''s hemoglobin concentration drops naturally because they are transitioning from the low-oxygen intrauterine environment to normal air breathing. As these extra fetal red blood cells break down, the iron inside them is recycled and pushed directly into the infant''s hepatic (liver) stores. These accumulated stores safely meet 100% of the infant''s growth demands for the first 3 to 6 months of life.

• The iron content in milk: Human breast milk contains exceptionally small, trace amounts of iron. While this minor amount is highly bioavailable, it is mathematically insufficient on its own to support rapid infant growth without the presence of the built-in hepatic reserves.
• Supplements: Healthy, full-term infants do not routinely require iron supplements during the first 3 months of life. Standard guidelines (like those from the American Academy of Pediatrics) only advise starting daily supplemental iron drops at 4 months of age for exclusively breastfed infants as their liver stores begin to deplete.
• Weaning food: Weaning foods (complementary solid foods) are strictly introduced at 6 months of age. An infant''s digestive and motor systems are completely unready for solid food within the first 3 months of life.', '["the iron content in milk","its own liver stores of iron","supplements","weaning food"]'::jsonb, '["General Dietetics"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-30T14:08:52.127Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-29T14:08:52.127Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:28.332Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-29T14:08:52.127Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1754742443213-8', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783608654256-1786809564961', 'What is the estimated weight of the amniotic fluid of a pregnant woman?', '2 lbs', 'When breaking down the component weight gain of a full-term pregnancy using clinical reference values (totaling 12.5 kg or ~27.5 lbs), the components weigh out as follows:

• Amniotic Fluid: ~1.8 lbs (0.80 kg) — which rounds to 2 lbs
• Baby (Fetus): ~7.5 lbs (3.40 kg) — note: this is likely where the answer key confused "7 lbs"
• Maternal Fat Stores: ~7.4 lbs (3.35 kg)
• Extracellular Extravascular Fluid: ~3.3 lbs (1.48 kg)
• Blood Volume: ~3.2 lbs (1.45 kg)
• Maternal Tissue: ~3.0 lbs (1.38 kg)
• Placenta: ~1.4 lbs (0.65 kg)', '["5 lbs","10.5 lbs","2 lbs","7 lbs"]'::jsonb, '["General Dietetics"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 5, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-12-25T15:11:07.567Z","interval":57,"easeFactor":2.1,"repetitions":6}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-29T15:11:07.567Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:28.332Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-29T15:11:07.778Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1754742529508-9', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783608654256-1786809564961', 'The following are dietary management for morning sickness during pregnancy EXCEPT:', 'Citrus juice, water, milk, coffee, or tea if experiencing nausea', 'When actively experiencing nausea, drinking standard beverages like citrus juices, plain water, milk, coffee, or tea is heavily discouraged because they can stimulate the stomach lining or trigger the gag reflex. Citrus juice is highly acidic, milk is heavy, and coffee/tea contain caffeine, which acts as a gastric irritant.

Clinical guidelines state that when a woman is actively nauseated, she should instead sip carbonated beverages (like ginger ale), suck on ice chips, or use clear broths. Furthermore, liquids should always be consumed between meals rather than with solid foods to prevent over-distending the stomach.', '["Frequent small meals","Dry toast or crackers","Citrus juice, water, milk, coffee, or tea if experiencing nausea","Avoidance of foods with offensive odors"]'::jsonb, '["General Dietetics"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-01T14:18:31.447Z","interval":3,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":2}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-29T14:18:31.447Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:28.332Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-29T14:18:31.447Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1754742827195-10', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783608654256-1786809564961', 'Possible consequences of iodine deficiency during pregnancy:', 'Cretinism', 'Cretinism (congenital iodine deficiency syndrome) is the most severe and clinically distinct manifestation of severe maternal iodine deficiency.

Because iodine is the foundational building block for thyroid hormones, a maternal deficiency causes a lack of thyroid hormone during critical stages of fetal development. This leads to irreversible damage to the central nervous system, characterized by severe intellectual disability, deaf-mutism, short stature (stunted growth), and neuromuscular spasticity.

While miscarriage, stillbirth, and low birth weight can be caused by many different nutritional deficiencies, infections, or obstetric complications, cretinism is specifically and uniquely tied to iodine deficiency. This makes it the absolute signature diagnostic answer for board exams.', '["Low birth weight","Stillbirth","Miscarriage","Cretinism"]'::jsonb, '["General Dietetics"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-30T13:47:43.802Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-29T13:47:43.802Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:28.332Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-29T13:47:43.802Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1754742882241-11', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783608654256-1786809564961', 'A characteristic of complementary foods wherein its nutritional value is comparable with breast milk', 'Adequate', 'The World Health Organization (WHO) outlines four essential characteristics for complementary feeding (often referred to as the T.A.S.A. guidelines). Each has a distinct definition:

• Adequate: This means the food is nutritionally balanced and sufficient. It provides the exact amount of energy, protein, macronutrients, and micronutrients necessary to bridge the gap and match or complement the high nutritional value of breast milk for a growing infant.
• Timely: This means the foods are introduced at the correct developmental age—specifically when the infant''s nutritional demands exceed what exclusive breast milk can provide (at 6 months of age).
• Safe: This means the foods are hygienically prepared, stored, and fed using clean hands and utensils to minimize the risk of contamination or diarrheal illness.
• Appropriate: This means the foods are given in a texture, consistency, and frequency that match the child''s developmental and motor abilities (e.g., transitioning from pureed to mashed to finger foods).', '["Timely","Adequate","Safe","Appropriate"]'::jsonb, '["General Dietetics"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-30T13:44:25.728Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-29T13:44:25.728Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:28.332Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-29T13:44:25.728Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1754742914457-12', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783608654256-1786809564961', 'The primary building substance of cell is:', 'Protein', 'Proteins are globally recognized as the primary structural and functional building blocks of all cells and tissues. They compose roughly 80% to 90% of the dry weight of most cells.

While water is the most abundant liquid environment inside a cell (making up its volume), proteins are the actual building substance that forms the structural cytoskeleton, ion channels, cell receptors, and catalytic enzymes necessary to assemble and maintain cell life.

• Fats: Primarily serve as long-term energy storage and form the lipid bilayer of cell membranes.
• Carbohydrates: Primarily serve as the immediate fuel source (glucose) to generate cellular energy (ATP).
• Water: Serves as the primary solvent or matrix inside the cell, but it does not act as a structural organic building compound.', '["Water","Protein","Fats","Carbohydrates"]'::jsonb, '["General Dietetics"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-30T13:55:42.755Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-29T13:55:42.755Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:28.332Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-29T13:55:42.755Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1754742972952-13', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783608654256-1786809564961', 'A drug that should be given to nursing with nursing mothers because of it may cause metabolic acidosis and can cause rash.', 'Aspirin', 'Aspirin (acetylsalicylic acid) is rapidly broken down into salicylic acid. When taken by a breastfeeding mother in high doses, significant amounts can pass into breast milk. Salicylates disrupt cellular metabolism by uncoupling oxidative phosphorylation. In young nursing infants with immature metabolic pathways, this accumulation directly leads to high anion gap metabolic acidosis.

In addition to metabolic imbalances, aspirin exposure through breast milk can trigger hypersensitivity reactions in the infant, causing a rash or skin abnormalities. It can also interfere with platelet aggregation, causing an increased risk of bruising or petechiae.

Beyond the immediate side effects of a rash and acidosis, maternal aspirin use is heavily discouraged because pediatric salicylate exposure is closely linked to Reye''s syndrome, a rare but life-threatening condition causing severe liver and brain swelling.

• Phenobarbital & Primidone: These are anticonvulsant medications. Their primary adverse effects on a nursing infant are severe drowsiness, sedation, poor feeding, and potential withdrawal symptoms upon weaning, rather than metabolic acidosis.
• Clemastine: This is an antihistamine. Its primary reported side effects in a breastfed baby include extreme drowsiness, irritability, and a high-pitched cry.', '["Aspirin","Clemastine","Phenobarital","Primidone"]'::jsonb, '["General Dietetics"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-01T13:36:00.321Z","interval":3,"easeFactor":2.3,"repetitions":2}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-29T13:36:00.321Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:28.332Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-29T13:36:00.321Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1754743026882-14', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783608654256-1786809564961', 'The following are hormones of lactation EXCEPT:', 'Insulin', 'Direct Lactation Hormones:
	• Prolactin: The absolute primary hormone responsible for the synthesis and production of milk within the mammary alveoli.
	• Oxytocin: The hormone responsible for the milk ejection (let-down) reflex, causing the myoepithelial cells surrounding the alveoli to contract.
	• Estrogen: Crucial during pregnancy for mammogenesis (the structural development and branching of the milk ducts) to prepare the breasts for lactation.

Insulin is a general metabolic hormone produced by the pancreas to regulate blood glucose homeostasis. While it exhibits minor cellular synergy to maintain healthy maternal tissue, it is an endocrine system baseline hormone rather than a primary, targeted hormone of lactation.', '["Prolactin","Estrogen","Oxytocin","Insulin"]'::jsonb, '["General Dietetics"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-30T13:40:50.235Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-29T13:40:50.235Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:28.332Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-29T13:40:50.436Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1754743063650-15', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783608654256-1786809564961', 'A premature infant is born fewer than ___ days of gestation.', '270', 'In many classic nutrition and nursing reviewers (such as local dietetics or NCM/obstetric notes), a premature infant is traditionally defined as one born fewer than 270 days of gestation (or weighing less than 2,500 grams).

Globally, the World Health Organization (WHO) and modern obstetrics define preterm birth strictly as a baby born before 37 completed weeks (which equals fewer than 259 days). However, among the standard multiple-choice options provided (270, 300, 170, 200), 270 days aligns with the classic threshold taught in older local curricula.', '["270","300","170","200"]'::jsonb, '["General Dietetics"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-30T14:17:48.436Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-29T14:17:48.436Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:28.332Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-29T14:17:48.436Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1754743169759-16', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783608654256-1786809564961', 'What is TRUE about the purpose of the menstrual cycle?', 'A and B are both TRUE', 'The overarching biological purpose of the entire menstrual cycle is to prepare the female reproductive system for a potential pregnancy. To achieve this goal, the cycle carries out two separate but deeply connected processes simultaneously:

• Statement A is TRUE: Driven by Follicle-Stimulating Hormone (FSH) and Luteinizing Hormone (LH), the ovarian cycle works to develop and release a single mature ovum (egg cell) from the ovaries during the ovulation phase.
• Statement D is TRUE: Driven by estrogen and progesterone, the uterine cycle concurrently works to prepare, thicken, and enrich the uterus lining (endometrium), creating a plush environment ready to sustain a fertilized egg if implantation takes place.', '["To develop and release a mature ovum","To prepare the uterus lining","A and B are both TRUE","A and B are both FALSE"]'::jsonb, '["General Dietetics"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-30T13:44:56.011Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-29T13:44:56.011Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:28.332Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-29T13:44:56.011Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1754743335200-17', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783608654256-1786809564961', 'What is the estimated weight of the placenta of a pregnant woman?', '2 lbs', 'At full term, a normal, healthy human placenta typically weighs around 500 to 650 grams (roughly 1.4 to 1.5 lbs, or up to ~2 lbs when grouped in standard prenatal weight-gain breakdowns).

When clinical references (such as the Mayo Clinic or standard obstetrics texts) break down the typical 25–35 lb pregnancy weight gain, the placenta is consistently categorized at 1.5 to 2 lbs.

When breaking down the component weight gain of a full-term pregnancy using clinical reference values (totaling 12.5 kg or ~27.5 lbs), the components weigh out as follows:

• Amniotic Fluid: ~1.8 lbs (0.80 kg) — which rounds to 2 lbs
• Baby (Fetus): ~7.5 lbs (3.40 kg) — note: this is likely where the answer key confused "7 lbs"
• Maternal Fat Stores: ~7.4 lbs (3.35 kg)
• Extracellular Extravascular Fluid: ~3.3 lbs (1.48 kg)
• Blood Volume: ~3.2 lbs (1.45 kg)
• Maternal Tissue: ~3.0 lbs (1.38 kg)
• Placenta: ~1.4 lbs (0.65 kg)', '["5 lbs","10.5 lbs","2 lbs","7 lbs"]'::jsonb, '["General Dietetics"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 5, '', '{"dueDate":"2028-01-31T13:48:06.936Z","interval":448,"easeFactor":2.3,"repetitions":8}'::jsonb, '"2026-11-09T13:48:06.936Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:28.332Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-09T13:48:06.936Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1754743554910-18', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783608654256-1786809564961', 'What is the process of milk synthesis and secretion of the mammary gland?', 'Lactogenesis', '• Lactogenesis: Defined exactly as the cellular process of milk synthesis and secretion by the epithelial cells of the mammary gland alveoli. It focuses on the biochemical mechanism inside the breast tissue where the components of milk are actively built and put together.
• Lactation: Defined more broadly as the entire period or time frame of milk production, or the overarching automatic physiological cycle extending from pregnancy through weaning.', '["Lactation","Lactogenesis","All of the above","None of the above"]'::jsonb, '["General Dietetics"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-30T14:05:21.013Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-29T14:05:21.013Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:28.332Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-29T14:05:21.013Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1754743639604-19', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783608654256-1786809564961', 'The mesoderm gives rise to:', 'Urogenital system', 'During early embryonic development, the mesoderm forms the intermediate layer sandwiched between the ectoderm and endoderm. It gives rise to structural, circulatory, and excretory systems.

The intermediate mesoderm specifically condenses into the urogenital ridges, which directly differentiate into the urinary system (kidneys and ducts) and the reproductive/genital system (gonads, internal sex organs, and uterus).

• Nervous system: Arises from the ectoderm (the outermost layer), specifically through the differentiation of the neuroectoderm into the neural tube and neural crest.
• Pancreas and Thyroid: Develop from the endoderm (the innermost layer), which buds outward to form the linings and organs of the digestive and respiratory tracts.', '["Nervous system","Urogenital system","Pancreas","Thyroid"]'::jsonb, '["General Dietetics"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 5, '', '{"dueDate":"2028-01-31T13:48:28.910Z","interval":448,"easeFactor":2.3,"repetitions":8}'::jsonb, '"2026-11-09T13:48:28.910Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:28.332Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-09T13:48:28.910Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1754743700850-20', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783608654256-1786809564961', 'Physiologic anemia of pregnancy is due to:', 'Increased blood volume', 'During pregnancy, a woman''s total blood volume expands dramatically to support the growing fetus. However, the plasma volume increases by roughly 50%, while the actual mass of red blood cells only increases by about 20% to 30%.

Because the liquid portion of the blood (plasma) expands much faster and more significantly than the red blood cells, it creates a dilutional effect. This lowers the overall concentration of hemoglobin and hematocrit, leading to what is safely classified as physiologic (or normal) anemia of pregnancy.

Note on other options: While iron deficiency and Vitamin B12 deficiency are common causes of pathological anemia during pregnancy, they do not cause the normal physiologic adaptation described above.', '["Iron deficiency","Vitamin B12 deficiency","Increased blood volume","Decreased blood volume"]'::jsonb, '["General Dietetics"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 5, '', '{"dueDate":"2033-03-30T13:48:33.214Z","interval":2333,"easeFactor":2.1,"repetitions":11}'::jsonb, '"2026-11-09T13:48:33.214Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:28.332Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-09T13:48:33.214Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1754743766762-21', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783608654256-1786809564961', 'What is the recommended ratio of whole milk and water when preparing milk for an infant below three months of age?', '1', 'For an infant below three months of age, the immature kidneys cannot handle the high solute load (protein and minerals) found in undiluted cow''s milk. To make it safer, traditional guidelines recommend mixing 1 part whole milk with 1 part water (a 1:1 ratio) to dilute the nutrient concentration.

Because the question asks for the numeric value of the ratio itself (whole milk to water), a 1:1 proportion reduces mathematically to 1.

For Your Review Notes:
• Below 3 months: 1:1 ratio (1 part milk to 1 part water) + sugar/carbohydrates to adjust calories.
• Over 3 months: The ratio traditionally shifts to 2:1 (2 parts milk to 1 part water) as the infant''s renal capacity matures.', '["1","2","3","4"]'::jsonb, '["General Dietetics"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 5, '', '{"dueDate":"2028-01-31T13:48:25.461Z","interval":448,"easeFactor":2.3,"repetitions":8}'::jsonb, '"2026-11-09T13:48:25.461Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:28.332Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-09T13:48:25.461Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1754743865760-22', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783608654256-1786809564961', 'Which is true about the amino acids in human milk?', 'Rich in taurine', 'Taurine is a sulfur-containing amino acid that is highly essential for infants to support bile acid conjugation, retinal development, and brain growth. It is famously recognized as the second most abundant free amino acid in human milk (surpassed only by glutamate).

• Human milk is uniquely characterized by being low in methionine and rich in cysteine. This provides a high cysteine-to-methionine ratio, which is ideal for newborns because their livers have a temporary deficiency in the enzyme cystathionase needed to convert methionine into cysteine. Option B actually describes the profile of cow''s milk.
• Human milk is physiologically designed to be low in phenylalanine and tyrosine. This low concentration safely protects the neonate’s immature metabolic capacity and prevents the harmful accumulation of these aromatic compounds in the blood. Cow''s milk and older infant formulas contain much higher levels of these two amino acids.', '["Rich in taurine","Higher in methionine and lower in cysteine","High in phenylalanine and tyrosine","All of the above"]'::jsonb, '["General Dietetics"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-01T13:43:36.758Z","interval":3,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":2}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-29T13:43:36.758Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:28.332Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-29T13:43:36.758Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1754743924189-23', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783608654256-1786809564961', 'An anti-infectious factor in human milk that protects the integrity of the GIT epithelium:', 'Prostaglandins', 'Prostaglandins (specifically PGE2 and PGI2) present in human milk exert a robust cytoprotective effect on the newborn''s gastrointestinal tract. They stimulate mucus and bicarbonate secretion, maintain mucosal blood flow, accelerate cellular repair, and prevent mucosal injury, thereby actively protecting the structural integrity of the gastrointestinal tract (GIT) epithelium against inflammatory and infectious damage.

Roles of the other anti-infectious factors in human milk:
• Bifidus factor: A carbohydrate compound that selectively promotes the growth of beneficial Lactobacillus bifidus in the infant’s gut. This creates an acidic environment that indirectly crowds out and prevents the colonization of pathogenic bacteria.
• Lactoferrin: An iron-binding glycoprotein that exerts its anti-infective effect by tightly sequestering free iron, making it completely unavailable to iron-dependent pathogenic bacteria (like E. coli).
• Lactoperoxidase: An enzyme that participates in the oxidative destruction of harmful bacteria by converting peroxide into antibacterial hypothiocyanite ions.', '["Bifidusfactor","Lactoperoxidase","Lactoferrin","Prostaglandins"]'::jsonb, '["General Dietetics"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-30T14:07:39.846Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-29T14:07:39.846Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:28.332Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-29T14:07:39.846Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1754743981089-24', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783608654256-1786809564961', 'Immunological factor in human milk that inhibits intracellular viral replication', 'Interferon', 'Interferon is a signaling protein (cytokine) found naturally in human breast milk. Its primary biological function is to trigger protective defenses within cells. When a virus attempts to attack, interferon signals neighboring cells to produce specific antiviral proteins that directly inhibit and block intracellular viral replication, limiting the spread of the virus within the infant''s host tissues.

• Macrophages: Large white blood cells that act locally in the infant''s gut to engulf and digest whole bacteria, cellular debris, and foreign particles through phagocytosis.
• Lactoferrin: An iron-binding glycoprotein that primarily prevents bacterial growth by starving iron-dependent pathogens (like E. coli). It also exerts surface-level antiviral effects by blocking viruses from physically sticking or adhering to the outside of host cell receptors, rather than working inside the cell.
• Lysozyme: An active enzyme that protects the infant by splitting open and lysing the cell walls of harmful Gram-positive bacteria.', '["Lactoferrin","Macrophages","Interferon","Lysozyme"]'::jsonb, '["General Dietetics"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 5, '', '{"dueDate":"2028-01-31T13:48:19.071Z","interval":448,"easeFactor":2.3,"repetitions":8}'::jsonb, '"2026-11-09T13:48:19.071Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:28.332Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-09T13:48:19.071Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1754744065633-25', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783608654256-1786809564961', 'The nutritional requirements are increased during pregnancy to:', 'A and B are both TRUE', 'Nutritional requirements increase during pregnancy to satisfy two primary physiological demands simultaneously:

• Statement A is TRUE: The maternal body must intentionally build nutritional and fat reserves. These physical stores are absolutely vital to sustain the intensive energy required during labor and delivery, and they serve as the foundational baseline required to successfully kickstart postpartum lactation.
• Statement D is TRUE: Additional nutrients are actively routed across the placenta to meet the direct structural needs of the growing fetus. Concurrently, extra nutrition is needed to fuel the expansion of critical maternal supporting systems, including placental growth, uterine expansion, breast tissue development, and a ~50% expansion in total plasma blood volume.', '["To meet the nutrient needs of the growing fetus, other maternal tissues","To build reserves in preparation of delivery and lactation","A and B are both TRUE","A and B are both FALSE"]'::jsonb, '["General Dietetics"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-30T13:39:58.541Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-29T13:39:58.541Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:28.332Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-29T13:39:58.541Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1754744373687-26', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783608654256-1786809564961', 'The calcium recommendation in the PDRI for a pregnant woman on her first month versus a six-month pregnant woman is:', 'the same', 'According to the Philippine Dietary Reference Intakes (PDRI), the baseline calcium requirement for a woman of childbearing age (e.g., 750–800 mg/day depending on age) does not change during the first two trimesters of pregnancy.

A woman in her first month is in her 1st trimester, and a woman in her sixth month is in her 2nd trimester. Because neither phase requires an increase, their daily recommended intakes are identical.

The PDRI explicitly applies the +50 mg/day calcium addition only during the 3rd trimester. This is when rapid fetal skeletal mineralization occurs, requiring extra maternal dietary support. In the earlier months (1st and 6th), the mother''s body naturally compensates by increasing its own intestinal calcium absorption efficiency, keeping the dietary recommendation unchanged.', '["the same","lower","higher","not comparable"]'::jsonb, '["General Dietetics"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 5, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-12-25T15:11:13.009Z","interval":57,"easeFactor":2.1,"repetitions":6}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-29T15:11:13.009Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:28.332Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-29T15:11:13.209Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1754744417414-27', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783608654256-1786809564961', 'What is the stomach capacity of an infant at birth?', '10 to 12ml', 'Anatomically, the newborn stomach is positioned horizontally with a high-riding, poorly developed fundus and a relaxed lower esophageal sphincter, resulting in a low baseline volume capacity of 10-12 mL (about 0.5 to 1 ounce). Colostrum volume produced during initial postpartum lactation matches this capacity perfectly, as concentrated nutrients meet metabolic demands without exceeding gastric distension limits or triggering excessive stretch receptor-mediated regurgitation.', '["5 to 10ml","10 to 12ml","20 to 25ml","25 to 30ml"]'::jsonb, '["General Dietetics"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-01T13:36:06.205Z","interval":3,"easeFactor":2.1,"repetitions":2}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-29T13:36:06.205Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:28.332Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-07T14:32:00.423Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1754744475599-28', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783608654256-1786809564961', 'How much weight gain will you recommend if a woman has a pre-pregnancy weight status of obese?', '6.8kg', 'According to the Institute of Medicine (IOM) global standards for gestational weight gain, recommendations are tailored specifically to a woman''s pre-pregnancy Body Mass Index (BMI):

• Obese Category (BMI ≥ 30.0): The official guideline recommends a total weight gain of 11 to 20 lbs, which mathematically converts to a range of 5.0 to 9.0 kg.
• Evaluating the Options: Among the given choices, 6.8 kg is the only value that falls squarely within the recommended 5.0–9.0 kg clinical safety window for a singleton pregnancy.

Recommended Gestational Weight Gain Guidelines

• Underweight (BMI < 18.5): 12.5 to 18.0 kg (28 to 40 lbs)
• Normal Weight (BMI 18.5 – 24.9): 11.5 to 16.0 kg (25 to 35 lbs)
• Overweight (BMI 25.0 – 29.9): 6.8 to 11.3 kg (15 to 25 lbs)
• Obese (BMI ≥ 30.0): 5.0 to 9.0 kg (11 to 20 lbs)', '["12.5kg","11.5kg","7.8kg","6.8kg"]'::jsonb, '["General Dietetics"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-30T13:39:26.984Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-29T13:39:26.984Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:28.332Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-29T13:39:26.984Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1754744541295-29', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783608654256-1786809564961', 'A condition in pregnancy formerly called toxemia', 'Pregnancy induced hypertension', 'Historically, healthcare providers used the term toxemia (or toxemia of pregnancy) because it was believed that the condition was caused by a mysterious "toxin" circulating in the maternal bloodstream.

Today, the medical community recognizes that this condition is rooted in a dysfunction of the placenta and blood vessels rather than a biological poison. As a result, the umbrella classification was updated to Pregnancy-Induced Hypertension (PIH), which encompasses a clinical spectrum including gestational hypertension, preeclampsia, and eclampsia.

• Gestational Diabetes: High blood glucose levels that develop for the first time during pregnancy.
• Hyperemesis Gravidarum: A severe, unrelenting form of nausea and vomiting during pregnancy that leads to dehydration and weight loss.
• Cachexia: A profound metabolic state of muscle wasting and severe weight loss typically associated with chronic illnesses like cancer.', '["Pregnancy induced hypertension","Gestational diabetes","Cachexia","Hyperemesis gravidarum"]'::jsonb, '["General Dietetics"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 5, '', '{"dueDate":"2028-01-31T13:47:58.399Z","interval":448,"easeFactor":2.3,"repetitions":8}'::jsonb, '"2026-11-09T13:47:58.399Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:28.332Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-09T13:47:58.399Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1754744633988-30', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783608654256-1786809564961', 'Which is a nutritional risk factor during pregnancy?', 'Weight gain of less than 1 kg per month after the 1st trimester', 'During the second and third trimesters, steady weight gain is critical because this is when the fetus grows rapidly and the mother expands her supportive tissues (blood volume, uterus, and fat stores).

Standard public health and maternal nutrition protocols (such as clinical criteria for identifying high-risk pregnancies) explicitly classify a weight gain of less than 1 kg (about 2.2 lbs) per month after the first trimester as a key indicator of inadequate gestational weight gain. This increases the statistical risk of intrauterine growth restriction (IUGR) and having a low birth weight infant.

• Hematocrit of greater than 35%: Normal hematocrit levels for pregnant women typically drop slightly due to hemodilution but a value above 35% indicates safe red blood cell concentrations (anemia is diagnosed when it drops below 32–33%). (Note: The unit in the question is a common exam typo; hematocrit is measured in percentages, not mg/dL).
• Weight gain of more than 85% standard weight: This option contains confusing terminology, but being at or above standard body weight thresholds is a baseline classification rather than an active gestational risk factor like low ongoing weight gain.
• Hemoglobin of greater than 12 g/dL: A hemoglobin level above 12 g/dL is completely normal and healthy. Maternal anemia is only diagnosed when hemoglobin falls below 11.0 g/dL in the first/third trimesters or below 10.5 g/dL in the second trimester.', '["Hemoglobin of greater than 12 g/dL","Hematocrit of greater than 35%","Weight gain of less than 1 kg per month after the 1st trimester","Weight gain of more than 85% standard weight"]'::jsonb, '["General Dietetics"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-30T14:24:57.622Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-29T14:24:57.622Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:28.332Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-29T14:24:57.822Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1754744770999-31', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783608654256-1786809564961', 'Babies with low birth weight:', 'Have high possibility of neonatal death compared with non-low birth weight infants', 'Low birth weight (LBW), defined as a birth weight under 2,500 grams (5.5 lbs), is globally recognized as one of the leading underlying contributors to neonatal and infant mortality. Because their vital organs (such as the lungs, immune system, and metabolic pathways) are vastly underdeveloped, LBW infants face a significantly higher mathematical risk of death in the first 28 days of life compared to normal-weight infants.

• The "Mature Kidneys" Trap: This correctly identifies that LBW babies have an increased susceptibility to infection and difficulty regulating body temperature (due to lack of insulating brown fat). However, it states they have mature kidneys. This is completely false. Low birth weight infants suffer from renal immaturity, featuring a lower glomerular filtration rate (GFR) that makes them highly susceptible to metabolic acidosis and fluid imbalances.', '["Have an increased susceptibility to infection, difficulty regulating body temperature and mature kidneys","Have high possibility of neonatal death compared with non-low birth weight infants","A and B are both TRUE","A and B are both FALSE"]'::jsonb, '["General Dietetics"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-01T14:15:29.617Z","interval":3,"easeFactor":2.3,"repetitions":2}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-29T14:15:29.617Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:28.332Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-29T14:15:29.617Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1754744835129-32', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783608654256-1786809564961', 'Effect of poor maternal diet, i.e. low in energy and protein, on human milk', 'Decreased milk volume', 'The human body prioritizes the survival of the infant by preserving the macronutrient quality (concentrations of protein, fat, and lactose) of breast milk even during maternal malnutrition. The body draws directly from maternal tissue and nutrient reserves to keep the milk''s nutrient composition remarkably stable.

While the quality is physiologically buffered, extreme or prolonged deficiency in maternal energy and protein intake triggers the body to save energy by reducing the total quantity (volume) of milk produced. Research shows that when a lactating mother''s intake falls below critical thresholds, it directly results in decreased milk volume rather than diluted milk quality.', '["Cassation of lactation","Reduced quality of milk","Decreased milk volume","Reduced quality and quantity"]'::jsonb, '["General Dietetics"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-01T13:38:00.709Z","interval":3,"easeFactor":2.3,"repetitions":2}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-29T13:38:00.709Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:28.332Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-29T13:38:00.709Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1754745091968-33', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783608654256-1786809564961', 'What is the dosage for vitamin A supplementation for pregnant women?', '10,000 IU', 'High doses of preformed Vitamin A (retinol) are highly teratogenic, meaning they can cross the placenta and cause severe congenital birth defects in the developing fetus. To balance maternal health with fetal safety, the World Health Organization (WHO) and the Philippine Department of Health (DOH) state that routine daily supplementation for pregnant women should never exceed 10,000 IU (or 25,000 IU if given as a weekly dose).

Under standard DOH guidelines for pregnant women in vitamin A-deficient areas, the official recommendation is 10,000 IU taken orally once a day, starting from the 4th month (2nd trimester) of pregnancy until delivery.

Safety Considerations by Population:

• High-dose capsules (such as 100,000 IU): These are strictly administered to specific infant populations rather than pregnant individuals.
• Massive-dose capsules (such as 200,000 IU): These are reserved for older children and postpartum or lactating mothers within a specific window after delivery.', '["10,000 IU","20,000 IU","100,000 IU","200,000 IU"]'::jsonb, '["General Dietetics"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 5, '', '{"dueDate":"2028-01-31T13:48:22.337Z","interval":448,"easeFactor":2.3,"repetitions":8}'::jsonb, '"2026-11-09T13:48:22.337Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:28.332Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-09T13:48:22.337Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1754745178979-34', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783608654256-1786809564961', 'Which of the following conditions lead to contraindication to breastfeeding?', 'Galactosemia', 'Galactosemia is a rare, inherited metabolic disorder where the infant lacks the necessary liver enzyme (galactose-1-phosphate uridyltransferase) to break down galactose. Because lactose (the main sugar in human breast milk) is broken down into glucose and galactose, consuming breast milk causes galactose to build up toxically in the baby''s body. This leads to severe complications like liver failure, blindness (cataracts), brain damage, and even death. Therefore, galactosemia is one of the very few absolute contraindications to breastfeeding; these infants must be fed a specialized, lactose-free formula (such as a soy-based formula).

• Multiple births: Having twins, triplets, or more is not a contraindication. The maternal body works on a supply-and-demand feedback system; the more infants suckle, the more milk the mammary glands produce to meet the demand.
• Diarrhea: If an infant has standard diarrhea, continuing breastfeeding is heavily encouraged. Breast milk is highly digestible, prevents dehydration, and delivers vital antibodies (like Secretory IgA) that help the infant''s gut fight off the underlying infection.
• Hemorrhagic disease of the newborn: This is a bleeding disorder caused by a temporary Vitamin K deficiency at birth. It is treated immediately with a routine Vitamin K intramuscular injection at delivery and does not prevent or require the stopping of breastfeeding.', '["Multiple births","Galactosemia","Hemorrhagic disease of the newborn","Diarrhea"]'::jsonb, '["General Dietetics"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-30T14:29:59.992Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-29T14:29:59.992Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:28.332Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-29T14:30:00.189Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1754745262474-35', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783608654256-1786809564961', 'The placenta is formed for different purposes EXCEPT:', 'For production of nutrients and vitamins', 'The placenta does not actually produce (manufacture) core nutrients, vitamins, or minerals. Instead, it relies on passive diffusion, active transport, and facilitated mechanisms to pull existing nutrients and vitamins directly from the mother''s bloodstream. The mother remains the sole manufacturer and source of these raw materials.

• To supply nutrients and oxygen: This is the paramount respiratory and nutritional lifeline of the placenta. It continuously channels oxygen and vital macronutrients (like glucose and amino acids) across its thin membrane into the fetal blood supply.
• For removing fetal waste products: The placenta functions effectively like the baby''s kidneys and lungs in utero. It filters out toxic byproducts of fetal metabolism—such as carbon dioxide, urea, uric acid, and creatinine—and pushes them into the maternal circulation to be excreted by the mother.
• For storage of nutrients and vitamins: The placenta actively metabolizes and stores specific reserves—including glycogen (stored glucose), iron, calcium, fats, and fat-soluble vitamins. It holds these in place to regulate their release so the fetus receives a steady, buffered stream of nourishment even if maternal intake fluctuates.', '["To supply the fetus with nutrients and oxygen","For production of nutrients and vitamins","For storage of nutrients and vitamins","For removing of fetal waste products"]'::jsonb, '["General Dietetics"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 5, '', '{"dueDate":"2028-01-31T13:48:14.727Z","interval":448,"easeFactor":2.3,"repetitions":8}'::jsonb, '"2026-11-09T13:48:14.727Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:28.332Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-09T13:48:14.727Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1754745326688-36', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783608654256-1786809564961', 'The lowest level of structural organization of the living human body', 'Chemical level', 'The structural organization of the human body progresses systematically from the simplest chemical building blocks up to the complete living being. The levels are arranged from lowest to highest complexity:

• Chemical level: The lowest and absolute simplest level. It is composed of atoms that combine to form molecules like water, DNA, and proteins.
• Cellular level: The level where molecules interact to form cells, which are the basic functional units of life.
• Tissue level: Groups of similar cells working together to perform a specialized shared function.
• Organ level: Structures made of two or more different tissue types joined together to perform specific tasks.
• System level: Consists of related organs that work together to accomplish a common purpose, like the digestive system.
• Organismal level: The highest level of organization, representing the living human body as a whole.

Because atoms and molecules form the foundation for all structures above it, the chemical level is always classified as the lowest tier.', '["Cellular level","Tissue level","Organ level","Chemical level"]'::jsonb, '["General Dietetics"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-30T14:40:43.635Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-29T14:40:43.635Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:28.332Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-29T14:40:43.831Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1754745375769-37', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783608654256-1786809564961', 'Cut-off weight for low birth weight:', '5.50 lbs or less', 'The World Health Organization (WHO) defines Low Birth Weight (LBW) as an absolute birth weight of less than 2,500 grams (up to and including 2,499 grams).

Converting 2,500 grams into the imperial system commonly used in board exams:
2,500 grams ÷ 453.59 = 5.511 lbs

Therefore, a threshold of 5.50 lbs represents the direct equivalent cutoff used to diagnose low birth weight in medical and public health data.

• Low Birth Weight (LBW): Less than 2,500 grams (~5.5 lbs)
• Very Low Birth Weight (VLBW): Less than 1,500 grams (~3.3 lbs)
• Extremely Low Birth Weight (ELBW): Less than 1,000 grams (~2.2 lbs)', '["5 lbs or less","5 kg or less","5.50 lbs or less","25 kg or less"]'::jsonb, '["General Dietetics"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-30T14:13:16.943Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-29T14:13:16.943Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:28.332Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-29T14:13:16.943Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1754745745303-38', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783608654256-1786809564961', 'Which of the following is a physiologic change which is common during pregnancy?', 'Decrease gastric acidity', 'While modern maternal clinical textbooks show that gastric pH can fluctuate or decrease (become more acidic) because the placenta releases the hormone gastrin, traditional local board exam reviewers and certain nursing test banks (like older HESI or maternal-child question matrices) rely on a different classic framework:

In this traditional testing framework, the massive surges of progesterone and estrogen during the first and second trimesters are taught to slow down general gastric acid secretion along with reducing smooth muscle tone.

This traditional framework asserts that a temporary reduction in gastric hydrochloric acid production occurs early in pregnancy. (Note: In these same test banks, the common symptom of heartburn is explained as a mechanical issue—the relaxation of the lower esophageal sphincter allowing even small amounts of stomach acid to travel upward—rather than a state of hyperacidity).

Fixed Rationale for Your Review Notes:
• Decrease gastric acidity: In localized test banks, pregnancy hormones are noted for temporarily suppressing gastric secretions and hydrochloric acid production, particularly during the first two trimesters.
• Increase calcium absorption: While physiologically true that calcium absorption increases significantly during pregnancy, if your specific board reviewer identifies Option B as the intended answer key, it means the test author is explicitly testing you on the hormone-driven reductions in gastric secretions.', '["Increase gastric tone and motility","Increase calcium absorption","Decrease metabolic rate","Decrease gastric acidity"]'::jsonb, '["General Dietetics"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 3, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-05T15:11:15.787Z","interval":7,"easeFactor":2.3,"repetitions":3}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-29T15:11:15.787Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:28.332Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-29T15:11:16.001Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1754745798767-39', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783608654256-1786809564961', 'Additional fluid intake per day recommended for lactation mothers', '750-1000ml', 'Standard textbooks and regional nutrition guidelines point out that a mother''s milk production typically reaches 750 to 1,000 ml per day during established, exclusive breastfeeding.

Since human breast milk is roughly 87% water, a mother producing 1,000 ml of milk is losing nearly 870 ml of pure water directly to her baby every single day. To completely balance this fluid loss and safeguard against dehydration, nutritional models advise expanding daily beverage consumption by an additional 750–1000 ml above basic baseline needs.

Breast milk is highly fluid-dense (~87% water). Because a fully lactating mother produces an average of 750–1000 ml of breast milk daily, guidelines suggest a direct, corresponding addition of 750–1000 ml of fluid per day to adequately preserve maternal hydration without compromising milk output volume.', '["100-250ml","250-500ml","500-750ml","750-1000ml"]'::jsonb, '["General Dietetics"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-01T14:38:25.206Z","interval":3,"easeFactor":2.3,"repetitions":2}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-29T14:38:25.206Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:28.332Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-29T14:38:25.206Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1754745988593-40', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783608654256-1786809564961', 'In the female hormonal system, the source of luteinizing hormone-releasing hormone or LHRH is:', 'Hypothalamus', 'Luteinizing hormone-releasing hormone (LHRH)—more commonly known as Gonadotropin-releasing hormone (GnRH)—is synthesized and released by neural cells located inside the hypothalamus.

The hypothalamus releases LHRH/GnRH in distinct pulses into the hypophyseal portal system. It travels directly down to the anterior pituitary gland, stimulating it to produce and secrete the actual gonadotropins: Luteinizing Hormone (LH) and Follicle-Stimulating Hormone (FSH).

• Anterior pituitary gland: Secretes LH and FSH, but does not produce the releasing hormone (LHRH).
• Ovary: Secretes estrogen and progesterone in response to LH and FSH stimulation.
• Posterior pituitary gland: Stores and releases oxytocin and antidiuretic hormone (ADH), which are produced by the hypothalamus.', '["Hypothalamus","Anterior pituitary gland","Posterior pituritary gland","Ovary"]'::jsonb, '["General Dietetics"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-30T13:35:55.089Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-29T13:35:55.089Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:28.332Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-29T13:35:55.089Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1754746091584-41', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783608654256-1786809564961', 'Which is true about the physical growth of an infant?', 'The infant''s length changes more slowly than the weight', 'During infancy, a baby undergoes a massive, rapid increase in physical mass compared to linear growth. An infant’s weight increases by roughly 200% in the first year (it triples from birth weight). In contrast, their linear length increases by about 50% over that same first year (gaining roughly 10 inches). Because the percentage and relative rate of height accumulation are much more gradual than the explosive accumulation of mass, it is clinically stated that infant length changes more slowly than weight.

• An infant''s birth weight typically doubles by 5 months of age (Gaber, 2019), not by two months. While it is true that birth weight triples by one year, the first half of that statement is physiologically incorrect for a standard, healthy tracking curve.', '["Infants birth weight doubles by about two months and triples by one year","The infant''s length changes more slowly than the weight","A and B are both TRUE","A and B are both FALSE"]'::jsonb, '["General Dietetics"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-30T14:42:31.453Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-29T14:42:31.453Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:28.332Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-29T14:42:31.453Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1754746310358-42', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783608654256-1786809564961', 'The ectoderm give rise to:', 'Nervous system', 'During early embryonic development, the ectoderm forms the outermost layer of the germ cells. It is responsible for giving rise to tissues that interact with the outside world. This includes the entire nervous system (brain, spinal cord, and peripheral nerves) as well as the epidermis of the skin, hair, nails, and the lens of the eye.

• Thyroid and Pancreas: These organs develop from the endoderm (the innermost germ layer), which forms the epithelial linings of the digestive tract, respiratory tract, and associated glands/organs.
• Urogenital system: Most components of the urinary and reproductive systems develop from the mesoderm (the middle germ layer), which also forms the bones, muscles, and circulatory system.', '["Nervous system","Urogenital system","Pancreas","Thyroid"]'::jsonb, '["General Dietetics"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-01T13:34:46.055Z","interval":3,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":2}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-29T13:34:46.055Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:28.332Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-29T13:34:46.055Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1754746373439-43', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783608654256-1786809564961', 'An infant is physiologically ready for solid foods when:', 'All of the above', 'An infant''s body undergoes several critical internal, physiological adaptations around 4 to 6 months of age that prepare the digestive tract to accept and process foods other than breast milk or formula:

• Enzymes for starch digestion are produced: At birth, an infant has very low levels of pancreatic amylase (the enzyme required to break down complex carbohydrates). By 4 to 6 months, production of these starch-digesting enzymes increases significantly, allowing the infant to digest cereals and mashed starches.
• Saliva is produced: Around the 4th month, saliva production increases dramatically (which is why babies drool so much at this age). Saliva contains salivary amylase, which lubricates food and begins the chemical breakdown of solids right in the mouth.
• Gastric acidity increases: An infant''s stomach secretions mature as they grow. The increase in gastric hydrochloric acid lowers the stomach''s pH, which is essential to activate protein-digesting enzymes (like pepsin) and create an acidic barrier that helps kill harmful bacteria introduced through solid foods.', '["Saliva is produced","Gastric acidity increases","Enzymes for starch digestion is produced","All of the above"]'::jsonb, '["General Dietetics"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-30T14:44:30.287Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-29T14:44:30.287Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:28.332Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-29T14:44:30.487Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1754746432086-44', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783608654256-1786809564961', 'It is a phase in cell growth characterized by cell proliferation', 'Hyperplasia', 'Hyperplasia (Cell Proliferation): This is defined as an increase in the absolute number of cells resulting from cell division and proliferation. During this growth phase, a mother cell divides into identical daughter cells (mitosis), expanding the body''s total cell count.

Hypertrophy (Cell Size): This refers to an increase in the physical size or volume of existing cells due to the accumulation of proteins, functional units, and cytoplasm, rather than cell multiplication.

• Cephalocaudal and Proximodistal: These describe the predictable directional paths or trends of physical growth and motor development (e.g., "head-to-toe" and "center-to-periphery"), not cellular mechanisms.', '["Cephalocaudal","Proximodistal","Hyperplasia","Hypertropy"]'::jsonb, '["General Dietetics"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-01T13:37:54.762Z","interval":3,"easeFactor":2.3,"repetitions":2}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-29T13:37:54.762Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:28.332Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-29T13:37:54.762Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1754746753503-45', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783608654256-1786809564961', 'Which of the following is NOT true about human milk composition?', 'The composition of human milk is dependent of the state of the mother''s nutrition', 'The human body prioritizes infant survival above all else. The primary macronutrient concentrations (total protein, lactose, calcium, and iron) in human breast milk remain remarkably stable and consistent, even if a mother is experiencing mild-to-moderate malnutrition. The body actively draws from maternal tissue and bone reserves to maintain this optimal combination for the baby.

Only the types of fatty acids (fat profile) and certain water-soluble vitamins (such as Vitamin C and B-vitamins) fluctuate directly based on what the mother eats. The overall core matrix and macronutrient composition remain largely independent of her nutritional state.', '["The composition of human milk is dependent of the state of the mother''s nutrition","Composition of human milk varies from one human to another","Human milk consist of a solution of protein, sugar, and salt","The human milk is a solution which variety of fatty compound are suspened"]'::jsonb, '["General Dietetics"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-30T14:43:42.980Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-29T14:43:42.980Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:28.332Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-29T14:43:42.980Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1754746934562-46', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783608654256-1786809564961', 'Decrease in the proportion of red blood cells relative to the plasma brought about by an increase in total plasma volume', 'Hemodilution', 'Hemodilution occurs when the volume of plasma (the liquid portion of the blood) increases at a faster rate than the actual production of red blood cells. Because there is more fluid stretching across the same relative mass of cells, the overall concentration of red blood cells, hemoglobin, and hematocrit decreases. This is the exact mechanism that causes physiologic anemia of pregnancy.

• Hemoconcentration: This is the exact opposite of hemodilution. It happens when plasma volume decreases (often due to severe dehydration), making the concentration of red blood cells appear falsely elevated.
• Hypochromic anemia: This refers to red blood cells that have less hemoglobin than normal, causing them to look pale under a microscope (typically seen in iron deficiency). It is an issue of cell quality, not plasma volume.
• Pernicious anemia: This is a specific type of macrocytic anemia caused by an autoimmune inability to absorb Vitamin B12 due to a lack of intrinsic factor, unrelated to plasma expansion.', '["Hypochromic anemia","Hemoconcentration","Hemodilution","Pernicious anemia"]'::jsonb, '["General Dietetics"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 5, '', '{"dueDate":"2086-01-03T13:48:11.147Z","interval":21605,"easeFactor":2.1,"repetitions":14}'::jsonb, '"2026-11-09T13:48:11.147Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:28.332Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-09T13:48:11.147Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1754746987676-47', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783608654256-1786809564961', 'Approximately how many IQ points are lost at birth among infants of iodine deficient mothers?', '13.5', 'Classic meta-analyses (such as the landmark review by Bleichrodt and Born) estimate that children and populations in severe-to-moderate iodine-deficient regions experience an average reduction of 13.5 intelligence quotient (IQ) points compared to iodine-sufficient populations.

Iodine is essential for thyroid hormone synthesis, which drives fetal brain and central nervous system development. Iodine deficiency is recognized globally as the leading cause of preventable brain damage and intellectual disability.', '["11.5","12","12.5","13.5"]'::jsonb, '["General Dietetics"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-01T14:44:33.137Z","interval":3,"easeFactor":2.3,"repetitions":2}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-29T14:44:33.137Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:28.332Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-29T14:44:33.332Z"'::jsonb)
