('anki-card-1754834438468-51', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783608697063-1790433139850-9sogp', 'The type of error when the null hypothesis is true, but the researcher concludes that it is false', 'Type 1 error', '', '["Type 1 error","Type 2 error","Probability error","Hypothesis error"]'::jsonb, '["General Dietetics"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'medium', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-08-23T20:00:00.000Z","interval":8,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":3}'::jsonb, NULL, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.117Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:12.431Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1754834591326-52', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783608697063-1790433139850-9sogp', 'Which of the following describes nutritional surveys?', 'A and B', 'Keeping watch over nutrition in order to make decisions that will lead to improvement in nutrition in population', '["Generate info on the prevalence and extent of nutrition related health problems","Results can be used to allocate resources to specific groups or to formulate policies to improve the overall nutrition of the population","Continuous or longitudinal monitoring of the nutritional status of a select group of people","A and B"]'::jsonb, '["General Dietetics"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'medium', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-08-22T20:00:00.000Z","interval":7,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":3}'::jsonb, NULL, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.117Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-08T12:24:43.573Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1754834876214-53', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783608697063-1790433139850-9sogp', 'Which of the following is false?', 'Study in the natural sciences often requires an interpretive epistemology and a contructionist ontology', 'In biostatistics and research methodology, statistical tests of significance can never provide absolute certainty or prove that an analysis or hypothesis is "correct" for sure.
• Hypothesis testing operates entirely on probability, measuring the strength of evidence against a null hypothesis.
• A statistically significant result (e.g., p < 0.05) simply means that the observed data or difference is highly unlikely to have occurred by pure random chance under the assumption that the null hypothesis is true. It always leaves open a margin of uncertainty.

• Option A is TRUE: Inferential statistics deal purely with mathematical probability, not certainty. There is always a non-zero risk that your conclusions are false positives or false negatives.
• Option B is TRUE: There is an inverse mathematical trade-off between the two types of errors. If a researcher strictly lowers the significance level (α, the probability of a Type I error) from 0.05 to 0.01, the standard for rejecting the null hypothesis becomes much harder to meet. This automatically increases the probability of committing a Type II error (β, missing a real effect).
• Option C is TRUE: Parametric tests (like the t-test or ANOVA) carry strict assumptions about data structure, requiring variables to be continuous and measured on an interval or ratio scale, alongside approximately normal distributions. Non-parametric tests are used for nominal or ordinal data.', '["Study in the natural sciences often requires an interpretive epistemology and a contructionist ontology","Study in the social sciences often requires an interpretive epistemology and a constructionist ontology","Study in the natural sciences often requires a positivistic epistemology and an objectivistic ontology","It is occasionally possible to combine these strategies by coding qualitative data quantitativey"]'::jsonb, '["General Dietetics"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 1, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-08-21T20:00:00.000Z","interval":6,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":3}'::jsonb, NULL, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.117Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-07T14:32:00.423Z"'::jsonb),
