('anki-card-1755046111415-0', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783608929173-1790433142619-teu0e', 'Which of the following is an objective of nutritional assessment?', 'A and B', '', '["Identify individuals who are malnourished","Provide data for designing a nutrition plan of care or nutrition programs that will prevent or minimize the development of malnutrition","Neither A nor B","A and B"]'::jsonb, '["General Dietetics"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-19T08:13:38.048Z","interval":3,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":3}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-16T08:13:38.048Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.958Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-16T08:13:38.375Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1755046180839-1', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783608929173-1790433142619-teu0e', 'Which of the following is the easiest and most popular method for obtaining information about dietary intake?', '24-hour recall', 'In clinical and community nutrition settings, the 24-hour dietary recall is recognized as the easiest and most popular method for collecting dietary intake data.

• Why it is the easiest: It carries a very low burden for the participant. It does not require literacy, advanced preparation, or active logging over several days like a food diary does.
• Why it is the most popular: It can be administered quickly by an interviewer (often taking only 15 to 20 minutes) using a backward-in-time structured recall approach. It provides a rapid snapshot of an individual''s immediate dietary pattern without altering their typical eating behavior.', '["Food frequency questionnaire","3-day food diary","24-hour recall","Direct observation"]'::jsonb, '["General Dietetics"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-19T09:06:52.148Z","interval":3,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":3}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-16T09:06:52.148Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.958Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-16T09:06:52.472Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1755046292035-2', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783608929173-1790433142619-teu0e', 'Which of the following is a common error that occurs with the 24-hour diet recall?', 'All of the above', 'All of the above', '["Snack items and the use of gravies, sauces, and condiments may be underreported","The individual of family member may not be able to recall the type or amount of food eaten","The intake within the last 24 hours may be atypical of usual intake","All of the above"]'::jsonb, '["General Dietetics"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-19T08:25:30.565Z","interval":3,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":3}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-16T08:25:30.565Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.958Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-08T12:24:43.573Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1755046358247-3', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783608929173-1790433142619-teu0e', 'Which of the following is a disadvantage of the food frequency questionnaire?', 'Noncompliance/Nonresponse', 'Noncompliance/Nonresponse', '["Noncompliance/Nonresponse","Atypical intake on the recording day","Conscious alteration of diet during the recording period","None of the above"]'::jsonb, '["General Dietetics"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 1, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-08-12T20:00:00.000Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":3}'::jsonb, NULL, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.958Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-08T12:24:43.573Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1755046416177-4', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783608929173-1790433142619-teu0e', 'Which is the most common site used for obtaining skinfold thickness measurement.', 'Tricep skinfold (TSF)', 'The triceps skinfold (TSF) is universally recognized as the most commonly used single-site skinfold measurement in clinical, nursing, and field research settings.

It is highly favored because it is the most easily accessible anatomical site, requires minimal subject repositioning, and possesses a vast wealth of reference data—such as the World Health Organization (WHO) growth standards—for rapid comparison.

While clinical models like the Durnin and Womersley system sum the triceps, biceps, subscapular, and suprailiac sites to track total body composition, the triceps remains the baseline index location used on its own to reliably evaluate upper-body fat reserves.', '["Biceps skinfold (BSF)","Suprailic skinfold","Subcapsular skinfold","Tricep skinfold (TSF)"]'::jsonb, '["General Dietetics"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-19T08:11:46.314Z","interval":3,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":3}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-16T08:11:46.314Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.958Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-16T08:11:46.604Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1755046454711-5', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783608929173-1790433142619-teu0e', 'What does the mid-upper arm muscle (MAMC) estimate?', 'A and B', 'Mid-upper arm muscle circumference (MAMC) is an anthropometric calculation derived mathematically by combining the Mid-Upper Arm Circumference (MUAC) and the Triceps Skinfold (TSF) thickness through the following formula:
MAMC = MUAC - (π * TSF)

It serves as a critical indicator in nutritional assessments because:
• Skeletal Muscle Reserves: Because the arm consists primarily of bone, muscle, and a surrounding layer of subcutaneous fat, subtracting the fat thickness (via the triceps skinfold) isolates the muscle component. This directly gauges the body''s skeletal muscle reserves.
• Lean Body Mass: In clinical malnutrition states (such as marasmus or chronic disease cachexia), the body breaks down its skeletal muscle tissue to use for energy. Therefore, measuring MAMC provides a reliable proxy estimate of a patient''s overall lean body mass.

Why "Fat reserve" is incorrect:
The Triceps Skinfold (TSF) thickness alone is what isolates and estimates subcutaneous fat reserves. MAMC specifically subtracts the fat to look only at muscle tissue.', '["The amount of lean body mass","Skeletal muscle reserves","Fat reserve","A and B"]'::jsonb, '["General Dietetics"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-17T17:12:55.828Z","interval":3,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":4}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-14T17:12:55.828Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.958Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-16T10:23:32.763Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1755046509573-6', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783608929173-1790433142619-teu0e', 'Which of the following dietary assessment methods provides more qualitative than quantitative information?', 'Food frequency', '', '["Food frequency","Diet history","Food record","All of the above"]'::jsonb, '["General Dietetics"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 1, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-08-12T20:00:00.000Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":3}'::jsonb, NULL, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.958Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:12.431Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1755046551289-7', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783608929173-1790433142619-teu0e', 'Food records are usually collected for what period of time?', '1-7 days', 'In standard nutritional assessment methodology, a formal, comprehensive food record (or weighed food diary) has historically been collected over a 7-day period. Tracking a full 7-day week accounts for day-to-day intake variations and ensures that both weekdays and weekend eating behaviors are fully represented.', '["1-7 days","1 day","3-4 days","2 weeks"]'::jsonb, '["General Dietetics"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-19T08:11:07.250Z","interval":3,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":3}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-16T08:11:07.250Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.958Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-16T09:33:38.537Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1755046604135-8', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783608929173-1790433142619-teu0e', 'In which method does the interviewer ask a person to report all food used by the household on an as-purchased basis?', 'Food account', '', '["Food account","Inventory","List recall","Food record"]'::jsonb, '["General Dietetics"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 1, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-08-12T20:00:00.000Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":2}'::jsonb, NULL, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.958Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:12.431Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1755046696749-9', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783608929173-1790433142619-teu0e', 'Which of the following statements are true regarding body measurements?', 'All of the above', 'All of the above', '["Infants and children under the age of 2 should be measure in the recumbent position","Knee height measurement is useful for people with spinal curvature","Head circumference is used as a measure of brain size in infant and young children","All of the above"]'::jsonb, '["General Dietetics"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-19T09:04:25.145Z","interval":3,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":4}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-16T09:04:25.145Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.958Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-08T12:24:43.573Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1755046751580-10', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783608929173-1790433142619-teu0e', 'What do waist-to-hip circumference ratios measure?', 'Intra-abdominal fat and subcutaneous fat', 'Waist-to-hip ratio (WHR) is an anthropometric index calculated by dividing your waist circumference by your hip circumference. It is primarily used to map out body fat distribution patterns (such as identifying "apple-shaped" vs. "pear-shaped" body types).

Because it gauges your midsection width, the ratio serves as an accessible proxy marker for tracking accumulated intra-abdominal fat (visceral fat that envelopes your internal organs) alongside abdominal subcutaneous fat (fat stored directly beneath the skin). An elevated ratio indicates central obesity, which heavily correlates with metabolic and cardiovascular diseases.', '["Fat-free mass","Intra-abdominal fat and subcutaneous fat","Protein-energy malnutrition","Skeletal mass"]'::jsonb, '["General Dietetics"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-19T08:28:25.922Z","interval":3,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":3}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-16T08:28:25.922Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.958Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-16T08:28:26.240Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1755046781362-11', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783608929173-1790433142619-teu0e', 'Taste acuity tests may be given to determine a deficiency of what element?', 'Copper', '', '["Copper","Iron","Selenium","Zinc"]'::jsonb, '["General Dietetics"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 1, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-08-12T20:00:00.000Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":3}'::jsonb, NULL, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.958Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:12.431Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1755046871555-12', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783608929173-1790433142619-teu0e', 'Which one of the following techniques can be used to measure visceral fat distribution?', 'Computed tomography', 'Computed tomography', '["Densitometry","Computed tomography","Air desplacement plethysmorgraphy","Bioelectric impedance analysis"]'::jsonb, '["General Dietetics"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 1, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-08-12T20:00:00.000Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":3}'::jsonb, NULL, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.958Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-08T12:24:43.573Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1755046923503-13', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783608929173-1790433142619-teu0e', 'Deficiency of which nutrient is associated with increased risk of infection in children, growth failure throughout childhood and delayed sexual maturity?', 'Zinc', 'Zinc deficiency is classically characterized by this specific triad of symptoms in children. Zinc is a critical trace mineral essential for cellular metabolism, DNA synthesis, and immune system function.
• Increased risk of infection: Zinc deficiency impairs cell-mediated immunity and mucosal barriers, making children highly susceptible to severe infections like diarrhea and pneumonia.
• Growth failure & Delayed sexual maturity: Zinc plays an indispensable structural role in growth hormone function and gonadotropin secretion. Severe deficiency directly arrests linear skeletal growth (causing stunting or growth failure) and leads to hypogonadism or delayed sexual development in adolescents.

• Calcium: Primary deficiencies lead to rickets in children and osteomalacia in adults, characterized by poor bone mineralization rather than delayed sexual maturity.
• Iodine: Deficiency causes goiter and hypothyroidism, and severe prenatal deficiency leads to cretinism (profound intellectual disability and physical stunting), but it is not typically grouped under this specific childhood triad.
• Riboflavin (Vitamin B2): Deficiency manifests as ariboflavinosis, which presents with cheilosis (cracked lips), angular stomatitis (cracks at the corners of the mouth), and glossitis (magenta tongue).', '["Iodine","Zinc","Riboflavin","Calcium"]'::jsonb, '["General Dietetics"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-19T08:00:07.006Z","interval":3,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":3}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-16T08:00:07.006Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.958Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-16T08:00:07.288Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1755046986669-14', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783608929173-1790433142619-teu0e', 'A requirement is the intake of a nutrient required by an individual to ___.', 'Prevent clinical signs of deficiency', 'Prevent clinical signs of deficiency', '["Prevent clinical signs of deficiency","Prevent subclinical signs of deficiency","Permit optimal function of biochemical pathways","None of the above"]'::jsonb, '["General Dietetics"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-19T08:19:37.362Z","interval":3,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":4}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-16T08:19:37.362Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.958Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-08T12:24:43.573Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1755047067496-15', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783608929173-1790433142619-teu0e', 'It is an objective, accurate, systematic analysis of a determinate body of empirical data, in order to discover recurring relationship among phenomena.', 'Research design', '', '["Science","Science method","Research","Research design"]'::jsonb, '["General Dietetics"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 1, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-08-12T20:00:00.000Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":3}'::jsonb, NULL, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.958Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:12.431Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1755047141778-16', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783608929173-1790433142619-teu0e', 'What type of study will you use if you want to conduct dose-response assays effect on individual cells?', 'In-vitro testing (human)', 'To find the right answer, you need to break down the two parts of the question:

1. "Individual Cells": Testing a substance on isolated cells or tissue cultures outside of a whole living organism is the definition of an in-vitro study (which literally translates from Latin as "in glass," referring to test tubes or petri dishes). Testing an entire living body would be an in-vivo study.
2. "Human" vs. "Animal": When a research scientist conducts basic laboratory dose-response assays on cell lines to eventually apply those findings to public health, nutrition, or medicine, using isolated human cells (such as human liver, muscle, or intestinal cell lines) is highly preferred. Human cell models provide more accurate information about human metabolic pathways and receptor behaviors than animal models can.

Quick Reference Map
• In-vitro: Performed outside a living organism using isolated cells, tissues, or organs in a controlled lab setup (e.g., test tubes, plates).
• In-vivo: Performed inside a whole, intact, living organism (such as feeding trials in living humans or lab animals).', '["In-vitro testing (human)","In-vivo studies (animals)","In-vivo studies (human)","In-vitro testing (animal)"]'::jsonb, '["General Dietetics"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-19T09:15:56.829Z","interval":3,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":4}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-16T09:15:56.829Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.958Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-16T09:15:57.148Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1755047189890-17', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783608929173-1790433142619-teu0e', 'Which of the following characterizes that the cause precedes the effect or outcome in a study?', 'Temporal relationship', 'Temporal relationship dictates that the exposure or proposed independent variable must occur first in time before the development of the disease or outcome. In the rules of causal inference (such as the Bradford Hill Criteria), this is the most fundamental and absolute requirement to prove that one variable causes another.

• Precision: Refers to the reproducibility and exactness of a specific measurement under unchanged conditions.
• Internal validity: The degree to which a study successfully rules out alternative explanations (confounders) for the results. While temporal precedence is a crucial requirement to establish internal validity for a causal claim, the specific concept of "time ordering" itself is defined as temporality.
• External validity: The extent to which the final results of a study can be generalized or applied to other settings, populations, or times.', '["Internal validity","External validity","Temporal relationship","Precision"]'::jsonb, '["General Dietetics"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-19T08:18:06.902Z","interval":3,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":4}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-16T08:18:06.902Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.958Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-16T08:18:07.207Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1755047253240-18', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783608929173-1790433142619-teu0e', 'An experiment in which groups of individuals rather than independent individuals are randomly allocated to intervention groups.', 'Cluster randomized trial', 'In epidemiology and research design, a cluster randomized trial (also known as a group-randomized trial) is specifically characterized by assigning intact groups of individuals—such as entire villages, schools, households, or clinics—to the intervention or control groups rather than randomizing each person independently.

• Randomized controlled trial (RCT): In its standard form, an RCT randomizes individual participants independently into different treatment arms.
• Cohort study: This is an observational study design where researchers track a group of individuals forward in time based on their exposure status; it does not involve random allocation.
• Case-control study: This is an observational retrospective study design where individuals are selected based on whether they have a specific outcome ("cases") or not ("controls") to investigate past exposures.', '["Cohort study","Case-control study","Randomized controlled trial","Cluster randomized trial"]'::jsonb, '["General Dietetics"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-19T08:58:44.029Z","interval":3,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":4}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-16T08:58:44.029Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.958Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-16T08:58:44.357Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1755047310569-19', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783608929173-1790433142619-teu0e', 'Which of the following is NOT considered as a systematic error?', 'Measurement error', 'Rheumatic heart disease', '["Selection bias","Recall bias from participants","Measurement error","Loss to follow-up"]'::jsonb, '["General Dietetics"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 1, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-08-12T20:00:00.000Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":3}'::jsonb, NULL, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.958Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-08T12:24:43.573Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1755047384682-20', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783608929173-1790433142619-teu0e', 'The process of this type of research is to describe, analyze and interpret the conditions that now exist.', 'Descriptive research', 'Descriptive research is explicitly designed to describe, analyze, and interpret a phenomenon, situation, or population as it currently exists. It focuses heavily on answering the what, where, when, and how questions without manipulating any of the research variables.

• Applied research focuses on finding practical solutions to an immediate problem using accepted theories.
• Experimental research relies on manipulating an independent variable to establish strict cause-and-effect relationships under controlled conditions.
• Historical research deals with uncovering, interpreting, and analyzing events that occurred in the past rather than conditions that now exist.', '["Historical research","Descriptive research","Experimental research","Applied research"]'::jsonb, '["General Dietetics"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-17T07:58:33.602Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-16T07:58:33.602Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.958Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-16T07:58:33.882Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1755047490294-21', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783608929173-1790433142619-teu0e', 'The study design which describes the experience of a single case or a group of patients with similar diagnosis based on sporadic discoveries of a unique, unusual or unexpected clinical observation. It provides first clues in recognition of disease and identifies risk factors or effects of exposure.', 'Case report', '1. "Describes the experience of a single case...": While a Case Series compiles multiple similar profiles together into a structured dataset, a Case Report is fundamentally defined as the baseline document describing the experience of a single case. In professional taxonomies, the parent definition of this type of design is titled under "Case Report" because it is the actual first line of evidence generated by an unexpected observation.

2. "Sporadic discoveries": A case series implies a systematic grouping or a consecutive collection of patients over time who meet specific criteria (e.g., tracking the next 5 patients who enter a clinic with a specific symptom). An unexpected, random, or sporadic clinical observation of a unique phenomenon is, by definition, published immediately as a singular Case Report to alert the medical community.

Summary
• Case Report: The baseline research design triggered by a sporadic, unusual, or unexpected clinical finding in an individual. It provides the very first clues that a new disease or exposure reaction exists.
• Case Series: The design used after multiple case reports accumulate, allowing researchers to aggregate a larger group of patients with that same diagnosis to look for statistical trends.', '["Case report","Case series","Case study","Ecological study"]'::jsonb, '["General Dietetics"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-17T09:17:26.732Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.3,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-16T09:17:26.732Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.958Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-16T09:17:27.040Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1755047594439-22', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783608929173-1790433142619-teu0e', 'What type of hypothesis is this?
Ho: The mean score of school children in the nutrition education in the control group is ≤75%.
Ha: The mean score of school children in the nutrition education in the control group is >75%.', 'Directional one-tailed hypothesis', 'To determine the type of hypothesis, you must always look closely at the mathematical sign used in the Alternative Hypothesis (Ha):
1. Directional: The alternative hypothesis explicitly states that the mean score is greater than (>) 75%. Because it specifies a particular direction of change (higher/improvement) rather than just any general difference, it is directional.
2. One-tailed: Because it only looks for an effect in one direction, the rejection region (critical region) is located entirely in a single extreme side—the right tail—of the sampling distribution curve.

Quick Reference Map
• Directional One-Tailed: Uses strictly > or < in Ha (e.g., higher, lower, increased, decreased).
• Non-Directional Two-Tailed: Uses strictly != or not equal to in Ha (e.g., different from, changed, not equal to).', '["Non-directional two-tailed hypothesis","Directional two-tailed hypothesis","Non-directional one-tailed hypothesis","Directional one-tailed hypothesis"]'::jsonb, '["General Dietetics"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-17T09:09:46.914Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-16T09:09:46.914Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.958Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-16T09:09:47.218Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1755047691868-23', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783608929173-1790433142619-teu0e', 'In the choice of research strategy, it answers "Does the data exist in a tangible or an intangible form?" and specifically understand how social factors interact.', 'Ontology-Constructionism', 'This question tests the foundational concepts of research philosophy (specifically within public health nutrition and community research modules):

1. Ontology: This branch of philosophy addresses the nature of reality and asks, "Does the data exist in a tangible or an intangible form?" It looks at whether social entities exist independently of our perceptions or if they are built up from our perceptions.
2. Constructionism (Subjectivism): This ontological stance holds that social phenomena and their meanings are continually being accomplished and revised by social actors. It specifically focuses on understanding how social factors interact to create meaning, as opposed to Objectivism, which views social reality as an external, concrete objective fact.

In contrast, Epistemology deals with how we acquire knowledge (e.g., through Positivism or Interpretivism), rather than whether reality itself is fundamentally tangible or intangible.', '["Epistemology-Positivism","Epistemology-Interpretivism","Ontology-Objectivism","Ontology-Constructionism"]'::jsonb, '["General Dietetics"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-17T09:29:42.974Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.3,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-16T09:29:42.974Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.958Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-16T09:29:43.300Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1755047750015-24', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783608929173-1790433142619-teu0e', 'A form of severe undernutrition that occurs after breastfeeding stops, and the child is weaned with a starchy diet.', 'Kwashiorkor', 'Kwashiorkor is a severe form of protein-energy malnutrition (PEM) that typically occurs during early childhood. The name stems from a Ga language word meaning "the sickness the old baby gets when the new baby comes."

It classically manifests when a child is abruptly weaned from protein-rich breast milk (often because a younger sibling is born) and transitioned onto an inadequate, starchy, protein-deficient diet (such as rice, maize, cassava, or porridge). While the child may receive enough calories from carbohydrates, the lack of protein causes fluid to leak into the tissues, leading to the condition''s signature edema (swollen belly and feet).', '["Marasmus","Kwashiorkor","Wasting","Stunting"]'::jsonb, '["General Dietetics"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-17T09:11:38.134Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-16T09:11:38.134Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.958Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-16T09:11:38.451Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1755047841910-25', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783608929173-1790433142619-teu0e', 'The law that mandate the establishment of the functional nutrition committee in every region, province, city, municipality, and baranagy in the country.', 'LOI 441', 'Letter of Instructions No. 441 (LOI 441), issued on July 31, 1976, directed the Department of Local Governments and Community Development (now the DILG) to establish functioning nutrition committees in every region, province, city, municipality, and barangay across the Philippines to properly execute the national nutrition plan at all subnational levels.

• PD 491 (Presidential Decree No. 491): Created the National Nutrition Council (NNC) in 1974 as the highest policy-making and coordinating body on nutrition in the country.
• AO No. 88 (Administrative Order No. 88): Deals with administrative government structural reorganizations unrelated to the foundational mandate of local nutrition committees.
• EO 285 (Executive Order No. 285): Reorganized the National Nutrition Council under the Department of Social Welfare and Development (and later amended by other EOs) rather than establishing the local committee mandates.', '["EO 285","LOI 441","PD 491","AO No. 88"]'::jsonb, '["General Dietetics"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-17T07:25:38.945Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-16T07:25:38.945Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.958Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-16T07:25:39.311Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1755047891909-26', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783608929173-1790433142619-teu0e', 'What is the stomach capacity of an infant at birth?', '10-12 ml', 'Anatomically, the newborn stomach is positioned horizontally with a high-riding, poorly developed fundus and a relaxed lower esophageal sphincter, resulting in a low baseline volume capacity of 10-12 mL (about 0.5 to 1 ounce). Colostrum volume produced during initial postpartum lactation matches this capacity perfectly, as concentrated nutrients meet metabolic demands without exceeding gastric distension limits or triggering excessive stretch receptor-mediated regurgitation.', '["5-10 ml","10-12 ml","20-25 ml","25-30 ml"]'::jsonb, '["General Dietetics"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-17T09:26:19.606Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.3,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-16T09:26:19.606Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.958Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-16T09:26:19.942Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1755047944990-27', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783608929173-1790433142619-teu0e', 'A wet nurse is:', 'A woman who breastfeeds another woman''s baby', 'A wet nurse is a woman employed or volunteered to breastfeed and care for another woman''s child, usually because the biological mother is unable, unavailable, or chooses not to breastfeed. This practice has been historically widespread and remains a traditional strategy in human infant feeding alternative networks.', '["Registered nurse","Registered midwife","A baby sitter","A woman who breastfeeds another woman''s baby"]'::jsonb, '["General Dietetics"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-17T09:14:34.637Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-16T09:14:34.637Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.958Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-16T09:14:34.968Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1755048013970-28', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783608929173-1790433142619-teu0e', 'It is the process of feeding a child who is not breastfed with a diet that provides all the nutrients the child needs until the child is fully fed on family food.', 'Replacement feeding', 'This is the exact, verbatim definition used by the World Health Organization (WHO) and global Infant and Young Child Feeding (IYCF) guidelines.

Replacement feeding is defined as the process of feeding a child who is not receiving any breast milk with a diet that provides all the nutrients the child needs until they can be fully transitioned onto regular family food. In public health settings (especially within maternal health and PMTCT/HIV programs), replacement feeding typically involves exclusive commercial infant formula for the first 6 months, followed by nutrient-dense solid foods paired with a suitable breast milk substitute from 6 months onward.', '["Complementary feeding","Cup feeding","Replacement feeding","Formula feeding"]'::jsonb, '["General Dietetics"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-17T09:12:19.407Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-16T09:12:19.407Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.958Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-16T09:12:19.717Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1755048061618-29', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783608929173-1790433142619-teu0e', 'A hormone that stimulates milk ejection and promotes uterine contraction.', 'Oxytocin', 'Oxytocin is a hormone produced in the hypothalamus and released by the posterior pituitary gland that plays two vital roles in maternal physiology:

1. Milk Ejection (The Let-Down Reflex): It causes the myoepithelial cells surrounding the alveoli of the mammary glands to contract, squeezing milk into the ducts so the baby can suckle.
2. Uterine Contraction: It stimulates the smooth muscles of the uterus to contract during labor (to deliver the baby) and postpartum (to minimize bleeding and help the uterus return to its pre-pregnancy size).

• Prolactin: This hormone is responsible for the actual production and synthesis of breast milk within the alveoli, but it does not eject it.
• Estrogen: This hormone stimulates the development of the mammary duct system during pregnancy but actually blocks the full effect of prolactin until after delivery.
• Growth hormone: This is involved in general tissue growth and metabolic functions, not specific lactational or reproductive reflexes.', '["Prolactin","Oxytocin","Estrogen","Growth hormone"]'::jsonb, '["General Dietetics"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-17T09:00:21.688Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-16T09:00:21.688Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.958Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-16T09:00:21.992Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1755048118831-30', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783608929173-1790433142619-teu0e', 'The estimated risk of HIV transimission from mother to child during breastfeeding is?', '5-20%', 'In the absence of any maternal or infant antiretroviral therapy (ART) interventions, the estimated risk of vertical HIV transmission specifically attributable to breastfeeding ranges between 5% and 20%.

The Overall Risk Breakdown (Without Intervention):

• Gestation and Labor/Delivery: 15% to 30% risk.
• Breastfeeding: Additional 5% to 20% risk (which heavily depends on the duration of breastfeeding, as prolonged mixed feeding over 2 years can double the acquisition risk closer to 20%).
• Total Combined Risk: 15% to 45%.', '["5-10%","10-15%","5-20%","15-25%"]'::jsonb, '["General Dietetics"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-17T08:27:22.984Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-16T08:27:22.984Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.958Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-16T08:27:23.310Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1755048155762-31', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783608929173-1790433142619-teu0e', 'The average daily milk consumption of infants for the first 6 months.', '750 ml', 'Clinical research and lactation guidelines indicate that exclusively breastfed or formula-fed infants consume an average of 25 ounces of milk per day between the ages of 1 and 6 months.

• When converted directly using standard measurements, 25 ounces equals 750 mL (25 x 30 mL/oz).
• Some pediatric and nutritional textbooks round this daily estimate to approximately 720 mL (calculated via alternative factors, such as roughly 24 oz x 30 mL).

Because normal daily intake typically ranges from 570 mL to 900 mL (with an overall average baseline of 739–750 mL), either C or B represents the clinically accepted daily average volume before the introduction of solid foods.', '["200 ml","250 ml","720 ml","750 ml"]'::jsonb, '["General Dietetics"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-17T09:19:11.660Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.3,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-16T09:19:11.660Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.958Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-16T09:19:11.979Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1755048188943-32', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783608929173-1790433142619-teu0e', 'It refers to the act of giving birth:', 'Parturition', 'Parturition is the specific clinical term for the act or process of giving birth to offspring.

• Puerperium: This is the postpartum period immediately following childbirth, lasting about 6 weeks, during which the mother''s reproductive organs return to their original non-pregnant condition.
• Parity: This refers to the number of pregnancies in which the fetus or fetuses have reached a viable gestational age (usually 20 to 24 weeks), regardless of whether the child is born alive or stillborn.
• Gestation: This is the period of development inside the womb from conception until birth (pregnancy).', '["Parturition","Puerperium","Gestation","Parity"]'::jsonb, '["General Dietetics"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-17T09:16:06.199Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.3,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-16T09:16:06.199Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.958Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-16T09:16:06.521Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1755048254475-33', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783608929173-1790433142619-teu0e', 'Approximately how many kilocalories does a mature milk contain?', '60-75 kcal/100 ml', 'On average, mature human breast milk contains about 65 to 70 kcal per 100 mL.

Standard pediatric and nutritional reference textbooks define the typical baseline range of energy yield for mature human milk as 60–75 kcal/100 mL (which perfectly corresponds to about 18–22 kcal per ounce).

This caloric density is primarily driven by its fat content (making up roughly 50% of the total energy) and carbohydrates/lactose (making up about 40%).', '["60-75 kcal/100 ml","80-90 kcal/100 ml","45-60 kcal/100 ml","90-105 kcal/100 ml"]'::jsonb, '["General Dietetics"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-17T07:26:26.028Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-16T07:26:26.028Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.958Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-16T07:26:26.334Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1755048286138-34', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783608929173-1790433142619-teu0e', 'What is the major maternal fuel source during pregnancy?', 'Fat', 'While the fetus relies almost exclusively on maternal glucose (carbohydrates) for its energy needs, the mother''s body undergoes a dramatic metabolic shift during pregnancy—particularly in the second half of gestation—to preserve that glucose for the rapidly growing baby.

• The Sparing Mechanism: Driven by placental hormones like Human Placental Lactogen (hPL), the mother develops a localized insulin resistance. This resistance decreases the mother''s own peripheral utilization of glucose.
• The Primary Fuel: To compensate and sustain her own metabolic needs, the mother accelerates lipolysis (the breakdown of fat stores). This allows her tissues to rely heavily on fat (free fatty acids and ketones) as her primary personal fuel source, effectively "sparing" the sugar for fetal growth.', '["Sugar","Protein","Glycogen","Fat"]'::jsonb, '["General Dietetics"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-17T08:26:19.776Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-16T08:26:19.776Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.958Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-16T08:26:20.103Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1755048313671-35', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783608929173-1790433142619-teu0e', 'Percent increase of total blood volume during pregnancy?', '33', 'While clinical textbooks often state a general maximum increase of "up to 50%" for maternal fluid expansion overall, precise metabolic and board-style questions evaluate the exact fractional mathematical relationship between the two main components of blood: Plasma and Red Blood Cells (RBCs).

1. Plasma Volume Increase: During pregnancy, blood plasma (the liquid portion) expands by approximately 45% to 50%.
2. Red Blood Cell Mass Increase: Red blood cells only expand by about 20% to 30% (if the mother has adequate iron stores).
3. The Total Net Blood Volume Increase: Because total blood volume is a weighted combination of these two components (Plasma + RBCs), the net average expansion across the entire duration of a typical healthy pregnancy equates to roughly one-third of the pre-pregnancy baseline, which mathematically maps directly to 33%.', '["50","33","18","25"]'::jsonb, '["General Dietetics"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-17T08:55:35.270Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-16T08:55:35.270Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.958Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-16T08:55:35.591Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1755048356535-36', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783608929173-1790433142619-teu0e', 'The goal of nutrition education:', 'To change behavior', 'While nutrition education naturally transfers information and increases awareness, global health frameworks (such as the FAO and USDA) explicitly state that the ultimate goal of nutrition education is to change behavior.

Programs that only focus on data transfer fail if they do not fix bad habits. Effective nutrition education is specifically designed to act as a motivational tool that translates knowledge into the continuous, voluntary adoption of healthy eating patterns and long-term dietary behavior changes.', '["To increase knowledge","To improve practices","To change behavior","To promote health"]'::jsonb, '["General Dietetics"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-17T08:25:22.722Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-16T08:25:22.722Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.958Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-16T08:25:23.028Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1755048409933-37', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783608929173-1790433142619-teu0e', 'An element of behavior change which pertains to the capacity of individual to perform a recommended response.', 'Self-efficacy', 'In health education and behavioral change models (such as the Health Belief Model and Social Cognitive Theory), self-efficacy is defined as an individual''s belief in their own capability to successfully execute a specific behavior or perform a recommended health response.

• Attitude: This refers to an individual''s positive or negative evaluations, feelings, or beliefs about a specific behavior, rather than their physical or mental capacity to perform it.
• Reactance: This is a psychological resistance or negative reaction that occurs when individuals feel their personal freedom or choices are being threatened or restricted.
• Cues to action: These are internal or external triggers (such as a doctor''s advice, a reminder app, or a family illness) that prompt a person to take action.', '["Attitude","Self-efficacy","Cues to action","Reactance"]'::jsonb, '["General Dietetics"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-17T08:50:30.232Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-16T08:50:30.232Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.958Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-16T08:50:30.232Z"'::jsonb)
