('anki-card-1754290845596-87', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783608953857-1790433145942-e1oub', 'An organized display of materials, specimens, models, or illustrations of an object or process.', 'Exhibit', 'Exhibit is the public display of materials for viewing and information.

• Campaign - coordinated, sustained effort to achieve a specific long-term goal.
• Promotion - activities intended to inform or persuade the audience to take action.
• Demonstration - showing how a task or skill is performed in a step-by-step manner.', '["Campaign","Promotion","Demonstration","Exhibit"]'::jsonb, '["General Dietetics"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-25T10:23:31.322Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-24T10:23:31.322Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.972Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-24T10:23:31.322Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1754291012756-88', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783608953857-1790433145942-e1oub', 'Why are theories important in nutrition education?', 'They are used to shape the nutrition education interventions and answer the "Why, What, and How"', 'The best answer is that theories are used to shape nutrition education interventions and answer the "why, what, and how" of behavior change, providing the conceptual foundation for designing effective programs. 

• The other choices each describe legitimate but narrower functions of theory (serving as the basis for behavior change, identifying target audiences, or identifying moderators of change), while the correct answer most comprehensively captures theory''s overall guiding role in shaping the full intervention design process.', '["They are the bases and center of behavior change","They are used to shape the nutrition education interventions and answer the \"Why, What, and How\"","They are useful in identifying target audience of nutrition education","They help nutrition educators identify and modify the specific set of moderators of behavior change"]'::jsonb, '["General Dietetics"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-25T15:32:22.529Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.3,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-24T15:32:22.529Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.972Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-24T15:32:22.737Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1754291121350-89', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783608953857-1790433145942-e1oub', 'A multi-level tool for promoting and sustaining risk-reducing behavior change in individuals and communities by distributing tailored specific messages thru variety of channels', 'Behavior Change Communication', '• Nutrition Education - narrower activity focused on knowledge/skill transfer
• Learning Strategies - specific pedagogical techniques
• Health Promotion (D) - broader umbrella concept encompassing BCC as one of its strategic tools', '["Nutrition Education","Behavior Change Communication","Learning Strategies","Health Promotion"]'::jsonb, '["General Dietetics"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-25T08:03:43.347Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-24T08:03:43.347Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.972Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-24T08:03:43.671Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1754291184031-90', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783608953857-1790433145942-e1oub', 'Which country made the first specific commitment on the UN Decade of Action on Nutrition?', 'Brazil', 'On May 22, 2017, Brazil became the first country to make SMART commitments as part of the United Nations (UN) Decade of Action on Nutrition 2016–2025. Ricardo Barros, Minister of Health of Brazil, made the announcement at the UN in Geneva, on the first day of the Seventieth World Health Assembly.

Brazil’s 3 commitments, to be achieved by 2019, are as follows:
• Stop the growth in the adult obesity rate (which currently stands at 20.8%)
• Reduce by at least 30% consumption of sugar-sweetened beverages among adults
• Increase by at least 17.8% the proportion of adults who regularly eat fruit and vegetables

Brazil outlined specific policy measures it will take to achieve these goals. These include fiscal measures (tax reductions, subsidies) to reduce the price of fresh foods, microcredit loans to family farmers, and cash transfers to poor families so that they can buy fresh produce.

See reference here: https://www.who.int/news/item/22-05-2017-brazil-first-country-to-make-specific-commitments-in-un-decade-of-action-on-nutrition', '["Norway","France","Ecuador","Brazil"]'::jsonb, '["General Dietetics"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-25T08:31:04.126Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-24T08:31:04.126Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.972Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-24T08:31:04.126Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1754291247828-91', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783608953857-1790433145942-e1oub', 'This sustainable development goal affirms to end hunger, achieve food security and improved nutrition, and promote sustainable agriculture.', '2', 'SDG 2: Zero Hunger aims to end hunger, achieve food security, and promote sustainable agriculture.

• SDG 1: No Poverty - End poverty in all its forms everywhere.
• SDG 3: Good Health and Well-being - Ensure healthy lives and promote well-being for all at all ages.
• SDG 4: Quality Education - Ensure inclusive and equitable quality education and promote lifelong learning opportunities for all.', '["1","2","3","4"]'::jsonb, '["General Dietetics"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-25T10:20:40.103Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-24T10:20:40.103Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.972Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-24T10:20:40.103Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1754291294124-92', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783608953857-1790433145942-e1oub', 'In what year was the first International Conference on Nutrition held?', '1992', 'In early 1989, at the fifteenth session of the Subcommittee on Nutrition of the United Nations Administrative Committee on Coordination (ACC/SCN), an international conference on nutrition was proposed. Subsequently the Executive Board, at its eighty-fifth session in January 1990, endorsed the proposal that WHO and FAO should jointly convene such a conference in close collaboration with other United Nations agencies and with concerned multilateral, bilateral and nongovernmental organizations.

The International Conference on Nutrition was held in Rome, from 5 to 11 December 1992. The Conference was not seen as an end in itself but rather as a step in the continuing process to strengthen commitment and the action necessary to prevent and alleviate nutrition problems. Enthusiasm has been generated in many countries for the implementation of plans which must now begin. Through increased awareness of the extent and seriousness of nutrition- and diet-related problems, the process leading to the Conference resulted in the public and private sectors'' increased willingness to work together to tackle them.', '["1992","1996","2002","2014"]'::jsonb, '["General Dietetics"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-25T10:43:58.441Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-24T10:43:58.441Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.972Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-24T10:43:58.629Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1754291358056-93', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783608953857-1790433145942-e1oub', 'The 2025 Global Nutrition Targets include reduction in childhood:', 'Wasting', 'Wasting is one of the six original WHO Global Nutrition Targets for 2025. The six original 2025 targets cover stunting, wasting, overweight, anemia, low birth weight, and exclusive breastfeeding.

• Target 1: 40% reduction in the number of children under five years of age who are stunted
• Target 2: 50% reduction in anaemia in women of reproductive age.
• Target 3: 30% reduction in low birth weight
• Target 6: Reduce and maintain wasting in children under five years of age to less than 5%.

Meanwhile, targets 4 and 5, which are close to being met in 2025, have been revised to raise ambition:
• Target 4: Childhood overweight: The ambition of the target has been strengthened from the original target of maintaining the 2012 prevalence to reducing overweight among children under five to below 5% by 2030.
• Target 5: Exclusive breastfeeding: To accelerate progress, the target has been increased from the original up to 50% of infants exclusively breastfed during the first six months of life by 2025 to up to 60% by 2030.

See reference here: https://www.who.int/teams/nutrition-and-food-safety/global-targets-2030', '["Anemia","Iodine Deficiency","Underweight","Wasting"]'::jsonb, '["General Dietetics"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-25T10:14:56.082Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-24T10:14:56.082Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.972Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-24T10:14:56.082Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1754291432368-94', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783608953857-1790433145942-e1oub', 'Which of the following conditions accounts for the most non-communicable disease worldwide?', 'Cardiovascular disease', 'According to official global statistics from the World Health Organization (WHO), Non-Communicable Diseases (NCDs) are responsible for approximately 74% of all deaths worldwide.

Among these conditions, cardiovascular diseases (CVDs) are the absolute leading cause of mortality and morbidity globally:
• Cardiovascular Diseases (CVDs): Account for the vast majority of NCD deaths, causing roughly 17.9 to 19 million deaths annually (primarily driven by ischemic heart disease and strokes).
• Cancers: Follow as the second largest group, accounting for around 9 to 10 million deaths each year.
• Chronic Respiratory Diseases: Account for approximately 3.9 to 4 million annual deaths.
• Diabetes: Accounts for roughly 1.6 to 2 million deaths annually.

Together, these four groups make up over 80% of all premature NCD-related mortality worldwide.', '["Breast cancer","Cardiovascular disease","Diabetes","Respiratory disease"]'::jsonb, '["General Dietetics"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-25T13:45:41.351Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-24T13:45:41.351Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.972Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-24T13:45:41.351Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1754291573529-95', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783608953857-1790433145942-e1oub', 'What is the WHO recommendation for physical activity among adolescents?', 'At least an average of 60 minutes per day of moderate to vigorous intensity across the week', 'It is recommended that children and adolescents should do at least an average of 60 minutes per day of moderate-to-vigorous-intensity, mostly aerobic, physical activity, across the week. 

Vigorous-intensity aerobic activities, as well as those that strengthen muscle and bone, should be incorporated at least 3 days a week.

Adults should do at least 150–300 minutes of moderate-intensity aerobic physical activity; or at least 75–150 minutes of vigorous-intensity aerobic physical activity; or an equivalent combination of moderate- and vigorous-intensity activity throughout the week, for substantial health benefits.

See reference here:

WHO guidelines on physical activity and sedentary behaviour (2020): https://iris.who.int/server/api/core/bitstreams/faa83413-d89e-4be9-bb01-b24671aef7ca/content', '["At least an average of 30 minutes per day of moderate to vigorous intensity across the week","At least an average of 60 minutes per day of moderate to vigorous intensity across the week","At least 75 minutes per day of moderate to vigorous intensity across the week","At least 150 minutes per day of moderate to vigorous intensity across the week"]'::jsonb, '["General Dietetics"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-25T15:33:47.543Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.3,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-24T15:33:47.543Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.972Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-24T15:33:47.744Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1754291660009-96', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783608953857-1790433145942-e1oub', 'The WHO recommends reducing the intake of free sugars to less than ___ in both adults and children', 'less than 10% of total energy intake', 'The World Health Organization’s new Guideline: Sugars intake for adults and
children recommends reduced intake of free sugars throughout the life course.
In both adults and children, the intake of free sugars should be reduced to less
than 10% of total energy intake. A further reduction to below 5% of total energy
intake would provide additional health benefits.

See reference here:

Information note about intake of sugars recommended
in the WHO guideline for adults and children (2015): https://iris.who.int/server/api/core/bitstreams/e14490d6-dba9-4994-aeef-0a2abb73d09c/content', '["less than 5% of total energy intake","less than 10% of total energy intake","less than 15% of total energy intake","less than 20% of total energy intake"]'::jsonb, '["General Dietetics"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-25T09:32:27.677Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-24T09:32:27.677Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.972Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-24T09:32:27.850Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1754291754705-97', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783608953857-1790433145942-e1oub', 'Delayed umbilical cord clamping not earlier than ___ is recommended for improved maternal and infant health and nutrition outcomes.', '1 minute after birth', 'In newly born term or preterm babies who do not require positive-pressure ventilation, the cord should not be clamped earlier than 1 min after birth (strong recommendation).

See references here:

WHO Guideline: delayed umbilical cord clamping for improved maternal and infant health and nutrition
Outcomes (2014): https://iris.who.int/server/api/core/bitstreams/cd57d2bf-b6be-4712-b23a-bad509587b7b/content

WHO Guidelines on Basic Newborn Resuscitation (2012): https://iris.who.int/server/api/core/bitstreams/e348f5c5-6841-409f-8b13-cf58e4da03e0/content', '["1 minute after birth","2 minutes after birth","3 minutes after birth","4 minutes after birth"]'::jsonb, '["General Dietetics"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-25T09:26:09.648Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-24T09:26:09.648Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.972Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-24T09:26:09.648Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1754291811918-98', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783608953857-1790433145942-e1oub', 'A high sodium consumption equivalent to __ grams salt/day contributes to high blood pressure.', '5', 'WHO recommends a reduction in sodium intake to reduce blood pressure and risk of cardiovascular disease, stroke and coronary heart disease in adults (strong recommendation). WHO recommends a reduction to <2 g/day sodium (5 g/day salt)
in adults (strong recommendation).

See reference here:
Guideline: Sodium intake for adults and children (2012): https://iris.who.int/server/api/core/bitstreams/d0f9feb5-ed78-44d1-9e06-533a93352012/content', '["2","5","5","6.5"]'::jsonb, '["General Dietetics"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-25T09:26:44.909Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-24T09:26:44.909Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.972Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-24T09:26:45.090Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1754291894665-99', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783608953857-1790433145942-e1oub', 'Newborns weighing 2000 g or less at birth should be provided with _____ Kangaroo mother care as possible.', 'Close to continuous', 'The WHO publication Kangaroo Mother Care: A Practical Guide recommends initiation of short KMC sessions when the baby starts to recover, even if the baby still requires medical treatment such as intravenous fluids or oxygen. 

It also recommends that continuous KMC should be initiated only after the baby is stable, meaning that the baby must be breathing spontaneously without additional oxygen. For the routine care of newborns weighing 2.0 kg or less at birth, the “WHO recommendations on interventions to improve preterm birth outcomes” recommend KMC, which should be initiated in health-care facilities as soon as the newborns are clinically stable. 

These babies should be provided with as close to continuous KMC as possible. Currently, there is no recommendation for KMC for unstable neonates weighing less than 2.0 kg at birth.

See references here:

Kangaroo mother care: Implementation strategy for scale-up adaptable to different country contexts (WHO, 2023): https://iris.who.int/server/api/core/bitstreams/71660bad-f029-4d9e-a2a4-63d8c59a8ce1/content

WHO Immediate KMC Study Group (2020). Impact of continuous Kangaroo Mother Care initiated immediately after birth (iKMC) on survival of newborns with birth weight between 1.0 to < 1.8 kg: study protocol for a randomized controlled trial. Trials, 21(1), 280. https://doi.org/10.1186/s13063-020-4101-1', '["Continuous","Close","Continuous to close","Close to continuous"]'::jsonb, '["General Dietetics"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-25T15:32:30.133Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.3,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-24T15:32:30.133Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.972Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-24T15:32:30.336Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1754291946981-100', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783608953857-1790433145942-e1oub', 'This pertains to a set of tools and procedures to classify the nature and severity of food insecurity.', 'IPC', 'IPC (Integrated Food Security Phase Classification) is a globally standardized system specifically designed to classify the nature and severity of food insecurity into distinct phases (from minimal to famine). 

• EWS refers to an Early Warning System, which focuses on early detection of deteriorating conditions. 
• OPT, Operation Timbang, is a child weighing activity, unrelated to food insecurity classification. 
• SFP, Supplementary Feeding Program, is an intervention, not a classification tool.', '["IPC","EWS","OPT","SFP"]'::jsonb, '["General Dietetics"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-25T15:34:18.075Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.3,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-24T15:34:18.075Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.310Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-24T15:34:18.279Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1754292037490-101', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783608953857-1790433145942-e1oub', 'This refers to a city or municipal-based nutrition surveillance system for detecting, at an early stage, an impending deterioration of food security situation based on agreed food and nutrition-related indicators and corresponding cut-offs or trigger levels.', 'EWS', 'The correct answer is EWS (Early Warning System), which is specifically designed at the LGU level to detect early signs of impending food security deterioration using agreed indicators and trigger levels. 

EWS and IPC work together in practice, but EWS is specifically the local, trigger-based detection mechanism.', '["IPC","EWS","OPT","SFP"]'::jsonb, '["General Dietetics"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-25T10:28:12.945Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-24T10:28:12.945Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.310Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-24T10:28:13.136Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1754292095672-102', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783608953857-1790433145942-e1oub', 'The Nutriskwela Community Radio Network partners named their stations with words starting with this letter.', 'K', 'The correct answer is the letter K, reflecting the “ka-“ branding convention used across Nutriskwela community radio stations established by the NNC.', '["C","K","N","M"]'::jsonb, '["General Dietetics"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-25T08:21:02.256Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-24T08:21:02.256Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.310Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-24T08:21:02.256Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1754292141054-103', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783608953857-1790433145942-e1oub', 'Promote Good Nutrition is a component of this program.', 'AHMP', 'The correct answer is AHMP (Anti-Hunger Mitigation Program), under which "Promote Good Nutrition" is listed as one of its core intervention components alongside food assistance and livelihood support strategies.', '["AHMP","IYCF","NGF","PSN"]'::jsonb, '["General Dietetics"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-25T07:42:07.810Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-24T07:42:07.810Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.310Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-24T07:42:07.810Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1754292209333-104', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783608953857-1790433145942-e1oub', '____ aims to improve the nutrition knowledge, attitudes, and practices of families to increase demand for adequate, nutritious, and safe food.', 'PGN', 'PGN (Promote Good Nutrition) is a key component of the Philippines'' Accelerated Hunger-Mitigation Program (AHMP). It is strategically designed to improve the nutrition-related knowledge, attitudes, and behaviors of families to cultivate a sustainable demand for adequate, healthy, and safe food.

• AHMP (Accelerated Hunger-Mitigation Program) is the overarching mother program addressing hunger and food security.
• IYCF (Infant and Young Child Feeding) represents the specific set of optimal guidelines and target behaviors (like exclusive breastfeeding and appropriate complementary feeding) that the PGN promotes to mothers and caregivers.', '["PGN","NGF","IYCF","AHMP"]'::jsonb, '["General Dietetics"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-25T07:43:18.771Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-24T07:43:18.771Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.310Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-24T07:43:18.771Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1754292404175-105', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783608953857-1790433145942-e1oub', 'What is stated in the 5th Nutritional Guidelines for Filipinos', 'Consume milk, milk product, and other calcium-rich foods, such as small fish and shellfish, every day for healthy bones and teeth', 'Formulated by the Food and Nutrition Research Institute (FNRI-DOST), the 10 messages (popularly popularized as the 10 Kumainments) are chronologically ordered as follows:

1. 1st Guideline: Eat a variety of foods everyday to get the nutrients needed by the body.
2. 2nd Guideline: Breastfeed infants exclusively from birth up to 6 months, then give appropriate complementary foods.
3. 3rd Guideline: Eat more vegetables and fruits every day.
4. 4th Guideline: Consume fish, lean meat, poultry, egg, dried beans or nuts daily.
5. 5th Guideline: Consume milk, milk products, and other calcium-rich foods.
6. 6th Guideline: Consume safe foods and water to prevent diarrhea.
7. 7th Guideline: Use iodized salt to prevent Iodine Deficiency Disorders.
8. 8th Guideline: Limit intake of salty, fried, fatty, and sugar-rich foods.
9. 9th Guideline: Attain normal body weight through proper diet and moderate physical activity.
10. 10th Guideline: Be physically active, make healthy food choices, manage stress, avoid alcoholic beverages, and do not smoke.', '["Consume milk, milk product, and other calcium-rich foods, such as small fish and shellfish, every day for healthy bones and teeth","Use iodized salt to prevent Iodine Deficiency Disorders","Eat more vegetables and fruits every day to get essential vitamins, minerals, and fiber for regulation of body processes.","Limit intake of salty, fried, fatty, and sugar-rich foods to prevent cardiovascular disease"]'::jsonb, '["General Dietetics"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-25T13:21:03.565Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-24T13:21:03.565Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.310Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-24T13:21:03.565Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1754292489190-106', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783608953857-1790433145942-e1oub', 'What is stated in the 7th Nutritional Guidelines for Filipinos', 'Use iodized salt to prevent Iodine Deficiency Disorders', 'Nutritional Guideline for Filipinos (NGF) Rule #7 specifically recommends: "Use iodized salt to prevent Iodine Deficiency Disorders (IDD)." Iodized salt is mandated under RA 8172 (ASIN Law) to eliminate goiter, cretinism, and mental impairment.', '["Consume milk, milk product, and other calcium-rich foods, such as small fish and shellfish, every day for healthy bones and teeth","Use iodized salt to prevent Iodine Deficiency Disorders","Eat more vegetables and fruits every day to get essential vitamins, minerals, and fiber for regulation of body processes.","Limit intake of salty, fried, fatty, and sugar-rich foods to prevent cardiovascular disease"]'::jsonb, '["General Dietetics"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-25T15:33:11.772Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.3,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-24T15:33:11.772Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.310Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-24T15:33:11.974Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1754292545086-107', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783608953857-1790433145942-e1oub', 'The current Nutritional Guildelines for Filipinos was approved and adopted in what year?', '2012', 'Official name: Nutritional guidelines for Filipinos (Filipino: Mga Gabay sa Wastong Nutrisyon Para sa Pilipino).

Publication year: The Philippines published its first dietary guidelines in 1990. They were revised in 2000 and again in 2012.

Process and stakeholders
The Food and Nutrition Research Institute – Department of Science and Technology (FNRI-DOST) initiated and chaired the inter-agency and multidisciplinary Technical Working Group on the 2012 guidelines.

The guidelines were approved by the National Nutrition Council. The communication and dissemination is spearheaded by the National Nutrition Council and endorsed by various government agencies.

See reference here: https://www.fao.org/nutrition/education/food-dietary-guidelines/regions/countries/philippines/en/', '["2000","2006","2012","2018"]'::jsonb, '["General Dietetics"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-25T10:00:41.892Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-24T10:00:41.892Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.310Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-24T10:00:41.892Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1754292589066-108', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783608953857-1790433145942-e1oub', 'Under the mandatory food fortification program, rice is fortified with', 'Iron', 'See RA 8976 (Philippine Food Fortification Act of 2000):

A. Vitamin A - oil
B. Iron – rice
C. Iodine - salt
D. Vitamin A and iron - wheat flour', '["Vit A","Iron","Iodine","Vit A and Iron"]'::jsonb, '["General Dietetics"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-25T09:48:41.498Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-24T09:48:41.498Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.310Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-24T09:48:41.498Z"'::jsonb),
