('anki-card-1755595866585-0', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783609168633-1790433136743-avakn', 'Clostridium Botulinum toxin can be completely destroyed at', 'boiling at 85C and higher', 'While the spores of Clostridium botulinum are notoriously tough and require temperatures above boiling under pressure (121°C or 250°F) to be destroyed, the actual botulinum neurotoxin itself is heat-sensitive (heat-labile).

According to guidelines from the World Health Organization (WHO), the pre-formed active toxin in food is completely inactivated and rendered harmless by boiling or thermal processing at internal temperatures of 85°C (185°F) or higher for at least 5 minutes.

• Very high pH / Very alkaline condition: Botulinum toxin is highly stable in acidic to neutral environments. While extreme, non-physiological alkaline environments can eventually destabilize proteins, chemical manipulation is not a standardized, safe, or reliable operational method used to neutralize the toxin in food systems.', '["very high pH","very alkaline condition","boiling at 85C and higher","none of the above"]'::jsonb, '["Food Service Systems"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'easy', 5, '', '{"dueDate":"2027-02-20T10:39:06.814Z","interval":115,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":5}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-28T10:39:06.814Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:28.857Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-28T10:39:07.141Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1755595942023-1', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783609168633-1790433136743-avakn', 'Used to limit the migration of moisture into the product', 'packaging', 'The primary purpose of food packaging (specifically moisture-barrier films, vacuum packs, and sealed containers) is to separate the food product from the surrounding environment.

It acts as a physical shield that directly limits or blocks the migration of moisture—preventing dry foods (like crackers or chips) from absorbing damp air and becoming soggy, and preventing moist foods (like meat or bread) from drying out or developing freezer burn.

• Drying: A preservation method that removes moisture already inside the food to lower water activity, but it does not stop external moisture from migrating back into the product afterward.
• Heating: Used to cook food, destroy micro-organisms (pasteurization/sterilization), or drive off water vapor, but it offers no physical barrier protection once the heat source is removed.
• Freezing: Turns liquid water inside the food into ice crystals to halt microbial growth. Without proper packaging, freezing actually accelerates moisture loss to the dry freezer air through sublimation (freezer burn).', '["heating","freezing","drying","packaging"]'::jsonb, '["Food Service Systems"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'easy', 5, '', '{"dueDate":"2027-02-18T10:17:45.908Z","interval":113,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":5}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-28T10:17:45.908Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:28.857Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-28T10:17:46.233Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1755595995569-2', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783609168633-1790433136743-avakn', 'Food additive used to prevent both enzymatic and non-enzymatic discoloration.', 'Sulfur dioxide', 'Sulfur dioxide (along with sulfites) is a uniquely versatile food additive used extensively as an anti-browning agent because it blocks both pathways of discoloration:
	• Enzymatic browning: It functions as a powerful enzyme inhibitor that permanently deactivates polyphenol oxidase (the enzyme responsible for turning cut apples, potatoes, and bananas brown).
	• Non-enzymatic browning: It acts as an oxygen scavenger and chemically reacts with carbonyl groups in reducing sugars, directly intercepting and halting both the Maillard reaction and caramelization pathways.

• Sodium nitrites: Primarily used in meat curing to stabilize the pink color of myoglobin and prevent the growth of Clostridium botulinum. It does not prevent standard carbohydrate or plant-based enzymatic discoloration.
• Benzoic acids: Synthetic chemical preservatives used primarily as antimicrobial agents to stop the growth of molds, yeasts, and bacteria in highly acidic foods like juices, carbonated beverages, and pickles.
• Potassium nitrates: Like sodium nitrites, these are used as curing salts for meat preservation and color fixing rather than broad spectrum anti-browning.', '["Potassium nitrates","Sulfur dioxide","Sodium nitrites","Benzoic acids"]'::jsonb, '["Food Service Systems"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'easy', 5, '', '{"dueDate":"2027-02-25T10:31:27.842Z","interval":120,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":5}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-28T10:31:27.842Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:28.857Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-28T10:31:28.159Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1755596035152-3', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783609168633-1790433136743-avakn', 'Which is a biological contaminant?', 'Molds', 'A biological contaminant refers to living organisms, or substances produced by living organisms, that can make food unsafe. Molds are microscopic fungi (living organisms) that can grow on food, produce dangerous mycotoxins, and cause foodborne illnesses or allergic reactions.

• Soil: A physical contaminant. It is an inanimate, foreign object or material that accidentally gets into food from the environment.
• Insecticides: A chemical contaminant. This category includes toxic chemical substances such as pest control products, cleaning agents, and sanitizers.
• Hair strand: A physical contaminant. It is a tangible, foreign object introduced during handling or preparation that poses a physical hazard or gross aesthetic issue.', '["Molds","Insecticides","Hair strand","Soil"]'::jsonb, '["Food Service Systems"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'easy', 5, '', '{"dueDate":"2027-02-28T10:05:25.998Z","interval":123,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":4}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-28T10:05:25.998Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:28.857Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-28T10:05:26.329Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1755596087631-4', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783609168633-1790433136743-avakn', 'Foodborne illness caused by a neurotoxin found in food.', 'Botulism', 'Botulism is caused by a highly potent nerve-attacking toxin (botulinum neurotoxin) produced by the spore-forming bacterium Clostridium botulinum.

Unlike standard foodborne pathogens that attack the gastrointestinal tract directly, this neurotoxin enters the bloodstream and binds to nerve endings, blocking the release of acetylcholine. This leads to specialized neurological symptoms such as double vision, drooping eyelids, difficulty swallowing, and progressive descending muscle paralysis. It is most commonly associated with improperly home-canned or fermented low-acid foods.

• Yersiniosis: An infection caused by Yersinia enterocolitica (often from undercooked pork) that triggers standard gastrointestinal distress and mimics appendicitis, rather than releasing a neurotoxin.
• Listeriosis: A serious infection caused by Listeria monocytogenes (often from deli meats or unpasteurized dairy). While it can cross into the nervous system causing meningitis in high-risk individuals, it is an invasive bacterial infection rather than an illness caused by pre-formed neurotoxins in food.
• Salmonellosis: A classic foodborne infection caused by Salmonella bacteria that results in localized intestinal inflammation (diarrhea, fever, and stomach cramps).', '["Salmonellosis","Listeriosis","Botulism","Yersiniosis"]'::jsonb, '["Food Service Systems"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'easy', 5, '', '{"dueDate":"2027-01-22T14:48:51.289Z","interval":85,"easeFactor":2.3,"repetitions":6}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-29T14:48:51.289Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:28.857Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-29T14:48:51.487Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1755596158079-5', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783609168633-1790433136743-avakn', 'A defect that confronts frozen foods characterized by dehydration of food.', 'freezer burn', 'Freezer burn is a food quality defect directly caused by the dehydration (moisture loss) and sublimation of ice crystals from the surface of frozen food into the surrounding dry freezer air.

It typically occurs when food is improperly wrapped or left in the freezer for too long. The affected areas look dry, discolored (grayish-brown on meats or white on poultry), and develop a tough, leathery texture due to the severe loss of moisture.

• Oxidative deterioration: This refers to chemical reactions between oxygen and the fats or pigments in food, leading to rancidity and off-flavors. While freezer burn often accelerates oxidation because the dried-out surface allows oxygen to penetrate deep into the food tissue, oxidation itself is a chemical reaction rather than a physical dehydration defect.
• Recrystallization: A physical process where small ice crystals inside frozen food melt and refreeze into larger ice crystals over time due to temperature fluctuations. This damages cell structures and causes drip loss when thawed, but it does not involve the total loss of moisture to the external air.
• Protein denaturation: A structural change where food proteins lose their natural shape and functionality during long-term frozen storage, making the meat tough or spongy. It is a biochemical degradation rather than a surface dehydration defect.', '["oxidative deterioration","freezer burn","recrystallization","protein denaturation"]'::jsonb, '["Food Service Systems"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'easy', 5, '', '{"dueDate":"2027-02-28T10:14:47.892Z","interval":123,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":5}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-28T10:14:47.892Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:28.857Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-28T10:14:48.229Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1755596198093-6', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783609168633-1790433136743-avakn', 'Group of microorganisms that can grow at very low temperatures.', 'psychrophiles', 'Psychrophiles (and cold-tolerant psychrotrophs) are specialized microorganisms that are uniquely adapted to survive and grow at very low temperatures, ranging from -15°C to 20°C (5°F to 68°F). They are responsible for the slow spoilage of refrigerated and frozen foods over time.

• Xerophiles: Microorganisms that grow best in environments with very low water activity or extremely dry conditions (such as molds on dried fruits or grains).
• Mesophiles: The largest group of bacteria, which thrive at moderate temperatures—typically between 20°C and 45°C (68°F to 113°F). Most common foodborne pathogens (like Salmonella and E. coli) are mesophiles.
• Thermophiles: Heat-loving microorganisms that grow best at elevated temperatures, typically between 45°C and 80°C (113°F to 176°F). These are responsible for flat sour spoilage in undercooled canned goods.', '["mesophiles","thermophiles","psychrophiles","xerophiles"]'::jsonb, '["Food Service Systems"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'easy', 5, '', '{"dueDate":"2027-02-08T10:37:40.525Z","interval":103,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":5}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-28T10:37:40.525Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:28.857Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-28T10:37:40.847Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1755596296691-7', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783609168633-1790433136743-avakn', 'Principle behind the use of low temperature as a method of food preservation.', 'Reduction of water activity and speedy removal of heat', 'When low temperature is used specifically as a long-term food preservation method (such as commercial freezing), the primary goal is the rapid removal of heat to solidify water into ice crystals.

Turning liquid water into ice directly reduces the water activity (aw) of the food. Because microorganisms require liquid water to metabolize nutrients, reproduce, and move, locking that water up in an unavailable crystalline form effectively halts their biological activity and preserves the product.', '["Reduction of water activity and speedy removal of heat","Killing the microbes by reducing oxygen and water activity","Killing the microbes by inactivating the enzymes","Protein denaturation and reduced oxygen availability"]'::jsonb, '["Food Service Systems"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'easy', 5, '', '{"dueDate":"2027-05-12T14:50:06.875Z","interval":195,"easeFactor":2.3,"repetitions":7}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-29T14:50:06.875Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:28.857Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-29T14:50:07.082Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1755596360803-8', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783609168633-1790433136743-avakn', 'Code of Sanitation of the Philippines', 'PD 856', 'Presidential Decree No. 856, signed in 1975, is officially known as the Code on Sanitation of the Philippines. It serves as the primary legal framework for public health and sanitation requirements in the country, dictating strict guidelines for food establishments, water supply, sewage disposal, and industrial hygiene.

• RA 3720: The Food, Drug, and Cosmetic Act (later amended by RA 9711 as the Food and Drug Administration Act of 2009), which ensures the safety and purity of foods, drugs, and cosmetics.
• RA 7394: The Consumer Act of the Philippines, which protects the interests of consumers, promotes their general welfare, and establishes standards of conduct for business and industry.
• PD 704: The Fisheries Decree of 1975, which revised and consolidated all laws affecting fishing and fisheries in the Philippines.', '["RA 7394","PD 704","RA 3720","PD 856"]'::jsonb, '["Food Service Systems"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'easy', 5, '', '{"dueDate":"2027-02-15T10:04:11.390Z","interval":110,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":4}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-28T10:04:11.390Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:28.857Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-28T10:04:11.713Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1755596378701-9', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783609168633-1790433136743-avakn', 'Consumer Act of the Philippines', 'RA 7394', 'Republic Act No. 7394, enacted in 1992, is officially known as the Consumer Act of the Philippines.

Its primary objective is to protect the interests of consumers, promote their general welfare, and establish high standards of conduct for business and industry. In foodservice and hospitality, it heavily regulates truth-in-menu laws, food product labeling, deceptive sales acts, and consumer product safety.

• RA 3720: The Food, Drug, and Cosmetic Act, which focuses on ensuring the pureness, safety, and proper labeling of foods, drugs, and cosmetics.
• PD 704: The Fisheries Decree of 1975, which governs the preservation, development, and utilization of fishery and aquatic resources.
• PD 856: The Code on Sanitation of the Philippines, which dictates structural public health and sanitary rules for food operations.', '["RA 7394","PD 704","RA 3720","PD 856"]'::jsonb, '["Food Service Systems"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'easy', 5, '', '{"dueDate":"2027-02-10T10:08:12.540Z","interval":105,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":4}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-28T10:08:12.540Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:28.857Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-28T10:08:12.857Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1755596448398-10', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783609168633-1790433136743-avakn', 'Deterioration of canned items due to undercooling that results to growth thermophilic microorganism.', 'Flat sours', '•Flat sour spoilage is caused by heat-loving, spore-forming bacteria (such as Bacillus stearothermophilus). If cans are undercooled after thermal processing or stored in warm temperatures, these thermophilic spores survive and germinate.

These microorganisms ferment the carbohydrates in the food and produce acid, but they do not produce gas. Because no gas is generated, the internal pressure remains unchanged, and the ends of the tin can stay completely flat and normal-looking from the outside. However, the food inside becomes sour and ruined.

• Flipper: A normal-looking can where one end will flip out or bulge when the side of the can is pressed, or when the can is struck against a hard surface.
• Hydrogen swell: A structural deformation caused by a chemical reaction between the acid in the food and the iron of the tinplate, producing hydrogen gas that bulges the can. It is not a thermophilic biological defect.
• Springer: A mild swell condition where one end of the can is permanently bulged outward, and pressing it down pops the opposite end out instead.', '["Flipper","Springer","Hydrogen swell","Flat sours"]'::jsonb, '["Food Service Systems"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'easy', 5, '', '{"dueDate":"2027-02-25T09:58:55.803Z","interval":120,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":8}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-28T09:58:55.803Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:28.857Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-28T09:58:55.803Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1755596499079-11', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783609168633-1790433136743-avakn', 'Microbes usually found in raw milk and undercooked chicken.', 'Campylobacter jejuni', 'Campylobacter jejuni is a microaerophilic bacterium that naturally resides in the intestinal tracts of warm-blooded animals, especially poultry.

In food safety, it is the classic pathogen most heavily associated with the consumption of undercooked chicken (due to contamination during processing) and raw, unpasteurized milk (from dairy cows shedding the bacteria). Consuming even a tiny dose can trigger severe gastroenteritis (campylobacteriosis) characterized by fever, abdominal cramps, and often bloody diarrhea.

• E. coli: While certain strains (like E. coli O157:H7) can contaminate raw milk and ground beef, its primary, textbook food vehicle is undercooked ground beef, contaminated fresh produce, or feces-contaminated water rather than being the definitive classic pair with poultry.
• Vibrio parahaemolyticus: This halophilic (salt-loving) bacterium is strictly associated with marine environments. Its primary food vehicles are raw or undercooked seafood and shellfish (like oysters, crabs, and shrimp), never poultry or raw land-mammal milk.', '["E. coli","Campylobacter jejuni","Vibrio parahaemolyticus","All of the above"]'::jsonb, '["Food Service Systems"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'easy', 5, '', '{"dueDate":"2027-02-15T10:20:33.260Z","interval":110,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":5}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-28T10:20:33.260Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:28.857Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-28T10:20:33.260Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1755596533029-12', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783609168633-1790433136743-avakn', 'Sugar concentrate made from crushed fruit pulp only is', 'Jam', 'By standard food processing and culinary definitions, jam is a sweet spread prepared by boiling crushed or pureed fruit pulp with sugar until it reaches a gelled consistency.

Because it incorporates the crushed pulp, it has a uniform but slightly textured consistency that holds its shape well when spread, though it is softer and less rigid than jelly.

• Preserve: Made using whole small fruits or uniform chunky pieces of fruit suspended in a clear, thick syrup, rather than using only fine pulp.
• Spread: A broad, generic umbrella term for anything you wipe on bread (which can include butter, margarine, or cream cheese); it is not a specific culinary definition for pure pulp concentrates.
• Jelly: Made strictly from clear fruit juice that has been filtered to remove all pulp and solid pieces, resulting in a firm, sparkling, translucent gel.', '["Jam","Jelly","Spread","Preserve"]'::jsonb, '["Food Service Systems"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'easy', 5, '', '{"dueDate":"2027-02-28T09:59:55.417Z","interval":123,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":5}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-28T09:59:55.417Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:28.857Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-28T09:59:55.751Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1755596572525-13', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783609168633-1790433136743-avakn', 'Which is a chemical contaminant?', 'Mercury', 'Mercury is a specific toxic heavy metal element. In food safety, it acts as a classic chemical contaminant (most notably in its organic form, methylmercury, which bioaccumulates in predatory seafood like shark, swordfish, and large tuna). Consuming it poses severe neurological health risks.

• Metals: A broad category that can represent a physical risk (metal shards from equipment) or a chemical risk (heavy metal pollution) depending on the form, making it less specific than Mercury.
• Glass: A physical contaminant. It is an inanimate, tangible object or structural fragment that accidentally enters food and poses an immediate danger of cuts, choking, or internal injury.
• Iodine 131: A radiological (radioactive) contaminant. While it has a chemical basis, food safety boards separate radioactive isotopes (like Cesium-137 and Iodine-131) into their own distinct class of contamination hazards.', '["Glass","Mercury","Iodine 131","Metals"]'::jsonb, '["Food Service Systems"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'easy', 5, '', '{"dueDate":"2027-02-13T10:29:54.582Z","interval":108,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":4}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-28T10:29:54.582Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:28.857Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-28T10:29:54.582Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1755596635874-14', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783609168633-1790433136743-avakn', 'This makes use of gases that liquesfy at very low temperature of about -60C wherein rate of freezing is ultra fast.', 'Cryogenic freezing', 'Cryogenic freezing utilizes cryogenic liquids or liquefied gases—most commonly liquid nitrogen (which operates at -196°C) or liquid carbon dioxide (which operates at -78°C)—sprayed or immersed directly onto food items.

Because these substances exist at ultra-low, freezing temperatures, the thermal heat transfer is immediate and direct. This yields an ultra-fast rate of freezing that prevents large ice crystals from forming, maximizing food texture, structure, and quality retention.

• Air freezing and Air blast freezing: These conventional methods rely on cold air circulation (with blast freezing blowing air mechanically at high velocities, usually around -30°C to -40°C). While quick, blast freezing uses circulating ambient air currents rather than direct exposure to liquefied gases to freeze materials.', '["Air blast freezing","Air freezing","Cryogenic freezing","None of the above"]'::jsonb, '["Food Service Systems"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'easy', 5, '', '{"dueDate":"2027-01-22T14:48:49.133Z","interval":85,"easeFactor":2.3,"repetitions":6}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-29T14:48:49.133Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:28.857Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-29T14:48:49.339Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1755596670087-15', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783609168633-1790433136743-avakn', 'Process of allowing meat to stand at low temperature for a few weeks is:', 'aging', 'Meat aging (or maturation) is the process of holding whole carcasses or primal cuts of meat at controlled, low refrigeration temperatures (typically 1°C to 4°C or 34°F to 40°F) for anywhere from a few weeks up to a month.

During this period, the meat''s natural proteolytic enzymes slowly break down the tough muscle fibers and connective tissues. This significantly improves the tenderness, juiciness, and depth of flavor.

• Curing: A preservation method that involves treating meat with salts, nitrites, and sugar to extend shelf life and fix its color.
• Preservation: A broad, general category of techniques (including freezing, canning, and drying) used to prevent spoilage, rather than a specific process aimed at weekly temperature holding for flavor/texture.
• Tenderization: While tenderization is the primary goal and structural outcome of aging, "tenderization" itself is an umbrella term that includes mechanical pounding, needle piercing, or using chemical/fruit enzymes (like papain). Aging is the precise name of the slow, cold-storage methodology described.', '["aging","curing","preservation","tenderization"]'::jsonb, '["Food Service Systems"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'easy', 5, '', '{"dueDate":"2027-02-23T09:58:09.305Z","interval":118,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":5}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-28T09:58:09.305Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:28.857Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-28T09:58:09.622Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1755596700986-16', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783609168633-1790433136743-avakn', 'Pasteurization is applied in processing foods that are generally', 'acidic', 'Pasteurization is a mild heat treatment (usually below 100°C) applied to foods to destroy pathogenic bacteria, yeasts, and molds.

In food technology, pasteurization is highly effective for acidic foods (such as fruit juices, vinegar, pickles, wine, and beer) because their low pH naturally prevents dangerous, heat-resistant spore-forming bacteria (like Clostridium botulinum) from growing. Therefore, mild pasteurization is all that is needed to clear away standard spoilage microbes and extend shelf life. Low-acid or basic foods usually require much harsher thermal processing (like full sterilization or autoclaving in canning) to be shelf-stable.', '["salty","basic","acidic","spicy"]'::jsonb, '["Food Service Systems"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'easy', 5, '', '{"dueDate":"2027-02-10T10:01:38.975Z","interval":105,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":5}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-28T10:01:38.975Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:28.857Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-28T10:01:39.295Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1755596760049-17', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783609168633-1790433136743-avakn', 'The amount of alcohol produced when making wines and beer depends primarily on', 'amount of fermentable carbohydrates', 'During fermentation, yeast (a microorganism) consumes fermentable carbohydrates (simple sugars like glucose, fructose, and maltose) and converts them into ethanol (alcohol) and carbon dioxide (CO₂).

The sugars serve as the fuel source for the yeast. The maximum potential alcohol content (the alcohol by volume or ABV) is directly dictated by how much sugar is available in the original fruit juice (must) or grain liquid (wort) for the yeast to eat. Once all the fermentable carbohydrates are consumed, alcohol production stops.', '["kind of microorganisms present","amount of fermentable carbohydrates","amount of oxygen present","amount of salt"]'::jsonb, '["Food Service Systems"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'easy', 5, '', '{"dueDate":"2027-02-10T10:16:36.618Z","interval":105,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":4}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-28T10:16:36.618Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:28.857Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-28T10:16:36.949Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1755596803814-18', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783609168633-1790433136743-avakn', 'Discoloration in canned products may be caused by:', 'All of the above', 'Discoloration in canned foods is a multi-faceted problem that can stem from biological, thermal, or chemical sources:

• Bacterial action: Certain anaerobic bacteria (like Desulfotomaculum nigrificans) produce hydrogen sulfide (H₂S) gas during spoilage. This gas dissolves into the food liquid and reacts chemically to form dark black precipitates (such as iron sulfide), severely blackening or discoloring the food and the inner lining of the tin.
• High temperature: Overheating during the thermal canning process or storing cans in a hot environment breaks down natural pigments (like chlorophyll or anthocyanins). Prolonged heat exposure can turn vibrant fruits or vegetables unappealingly brown, gray, or faded.
• Various metals: Chemical reactions occur if the food comes into direct contact with non-resistant metals during processing or inside a damaged tin container. For instance, trace amounts of iron or copper reacting with sulfur compounds in seafood or vegetables create dark metal sulfides, causing severe dark spotting or inky discoloration.', '["Bacterial action","Various metals","High temperature","All of the above"]'::jsonb, '["Food Service Systems"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'easy', 5, '', '{"dueDate":"2027-02-18T10:24:38.965Z","interval":113,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":4}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-28T10:24:38.965Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:28.857Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-28T10:24:39.291Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1755596859546-19', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783609168633-1790433136743-avakn', 'All are examples of conditions that promote microbial growth, EXCEPT:', 'low pH', 'A low pH means high acidity (such as lemon juice or vinegar). Most pathogenic bacteria grow best in a nearly neutral environment with a pH between 6.6 and 7.5. When the pH drops below 4.6, the acidic environment disrupts microbial cell membranes and alters internal proteins, inhibiting or completely stopping microbial growth rather than promoting it. This chemical barrier is why acidulation and pickling are highly effective methods for preserving food.

• Room temperature: Room temperature sits squarely in the culinary "Danger Zone" (41°F to 135°F), providing an ideal environment for mesophilic bacteria to thrive and multiply rapidly.
• Presence of oxygen: Most common foodborne pathogens are aerobic or facultative anaerobes, requiring or benefiting from the presence of oxygen to metabolize energy and grow.
• Availability of food/nutrients: Microorganisms need structural elements like proteins, sugars, nitrogen, and vitamins to sustain themselves, meaning nutrient-rich foods are highly susceptible to quick spoilage.', '["low pH","room temperature","presence of oxygen","availability of food/nutrients"]'::jsonb, '["Food Service Systems"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'easy', 5, '', '{"dueDate":"2029-08-24T14:48:42.477Z","interval":1030,"easeFactor":2.3,"repetitions":9}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-29T14:48:42.477Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:28.857Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-29T14:48:42.675Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1755596962374-20', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783609168633-1790433136743-avakn', 'Characterized by development of acidity in food without swelling cause by growth of thermophilic microorganisms as a result of lack of processing or cooking.', 'Flat sours', 'Flat sour spoilage is characterized by the development of lactic acid in canned food caused by heat-loving, spore-forming bacteria (such as Bacillus stearothermophilus).

When it occurs as a result of a lack of processing or undercooking, the thermal heat treatment fails to reach a high enough temperature to destroy these highly heat-resistant thermophilic spores.

Because these bacteria ferment the food carbohydrates into acid without generating gas, the internal pressure of the can remains unchanged. The ends of the tin can stay completely flat (non-swollen), hiding the spoilage until the consumer opens it and notices a sour taste and odor.

• Stack: Refers to "stack burning," which occurs when hot cans are piled or stacked together too closely after processing without proper cooling. The residual heat cooks the food inside the can, causing it to soften or discolor, but it is not a direct microbiological designation like flat sours.
• Non-swell: A descriptive state of a can rather than a specific microbiological spoilage term. While flat sours are non-swelled, "non-swell" isn''t the technical name of the reaction.
• Flipper: A normal-looking can where one end will flip out or bulge outward only when the side of the can is pressed or when the can is slammed against a hard surface.', '["Stack","Flat sours","Non-swell","Flipper"]'::jsonb, '["Food Service Systems"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'medium', 5, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-12-17T10:12:56.819Z","interval":50,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":4}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-28T10:12:56.819Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:28.857Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-28T10:12:57.140Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1755597010071-21', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783609168633-1790433136743-avakn', 'Temperature at which most of the pathogenic microbial activities are slowed down during refrigerator.', '10C', 'Pathogenic (disease-causing) bacteria are generally mesophiles, meaning they grow best at warm body or room temperatures. As the temperature drops toward 10C (50°F) and below, their metabolic processes and enzymatic activities slow down significantly.

Options B (15C), C (25C), and D (45C) all fall squarely inside the Temperature Danger Zone, where bacteria multiply rapidly. Out of all the options provided, 10C is the only temperature cold enough to actively chill food and retard harmful microbial activity. (Note: For true food safety holding standards, refrigerators should be kept even colder—at 4C / 40°F or lower—to halt growth completely).

• 15C: A cool room temperature where pathogens can still replicate easily.
• 25C: Standard comfortable room temperature, which serves as a highly ideal environment for rapid microbial growth.
• 45C: A warm thermal environment where certain heat-tolerant foodborne pathogens (like Clostridium perfringens) can multiply at their absolute fastest rates.', '["15C","45C","10C","25C"]'::jsonb, '["Food Service Systems"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'medium', 5, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-12-10T10:35:54.638Z","interval":43,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":4}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-28T10:35:54.638Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:28.857Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-28T10:35:54.936Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1755597067901-22', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783609168633-1790433136743-avakn', 'Genus with species capable of oxiding ethanol to acetic acid.', 'Acetobacter', 'Species within the genus Acetobacter (such as Acetobacter aceti) are collectively known as acetic acid bacteria. They are obligate aerobes capable of converting ethanol (alcohol) into acetic acid (vinegar) in the presence of oxygen. This biochemical reaction is the core process used globally to manufacture commercial vinegar.

• Escherichia: Best known for Escherichia coli (E. coli), which are coliform bacteria found in the intestines of warm-blooded organisms; they ferment sugars into mixed acids and gases, but do not oxidize ethanol to acetic acid.
• Saccharomyces: A genus of fungi that includes Saccharomyces cerevisiae (brewer''s or baker''s yeast). They perform alcoholic fermentation, converting sugars into ethanol and carbon dioxide (the exact opposite step of Acetobacter).
• Lactococcus: A genus of lactic acid bacteria (like Lactococcus lactis) used extensively in dairy fermentation to convert lactose into lactic acid, not ethanol into acetic acid.', '["Escherichia","Lactococcus","Acetobacter","Saccharomyces"]'::jsonb, '["Food Service Systems"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'medium', 5, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-12-05T10:02:27.471Z","interval":38,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":4}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-28T10:02:27.471Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:28.857Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-28T10:02:27.789Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1755597114583-23', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783609168633-1790433136743-avakn', 'Food microorganisms grow well at:', '4-60C', 'In food safety, the standard Temperature Danger Zone expressed in Celsius is 4°C to 60°C (which directly translates to the standard 40°F to 140°F range traditionally used by agencies like the USDA). Within this specific window, bacteria and other foodborne microorganisms encounter optimal conditions to thrive, multiply, and can double their population numbers in as little as 20 minutes.', '["4-60C","40-135C","0-40C","41-140C"]'::jsonb, '["Food Service Systems"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'medium', 5, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-12-17T10:36:41.977Z","interval":50,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":5}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-28T10:36:41.977Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:28.857Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-28T10:36:42.288Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1755597193612-24', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783609168633-1790433136743-avakn', 'Which of the following reduces the risk of foodborne illness cause by cross-contaminants?', 'Use clean utensils and color-coded chopping boards', 'Cross-contamination is the physical transfer of harmful bacteria or microorganisms from one food, piece of equipment, or surface to another.

Using distinct, color-coded chopping boards (such as red for raw meat, blue for seafood, and green for vegetables) paired with clean utensils creates a strict barrier between raw and ready-to-eat foods. This directly stops pathogens like Salmonella on raw chicken from traveling onto fresh, uncooked foods like salad lettuce.', '["Buy from trusted suppliers","Discard food stored beyond the expiration date","Use clean utensils and color-coded chopping boards","Require sick personnel to go on leave"]'::jsonb, '["Food Service Systems"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'medium', 5, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-12-15T10:07:20.994Z","interval":48,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":5}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-28T10:07:20.994Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:28.857Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-28T10:07:21.322Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756167925684-0', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783609175539-1790433140277-2y6kw', 'The transportation of prepared foods from production to the place of service.', 'Delivery', 'Food delivery is defined as the movement of food from production to service.', '["Delivery","Transfer","Dispatching","Assembling"]'::jsonb, '["Food Service Systems"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'easy', 5, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-20T20:00:00.000Z","interval":50,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":5}'::jsonb, NULL, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.104Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-14T16:37:08.404Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756167966710-1', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783609175539-1790433140277-2y6kw', 'Three work centers found on two opposite counters.', 'Galley', 'Two Wall or Galley - 1.2m space for drawers on opposite sides; sink and range are on the same side. It features two parallel counters facing each other. Places the three main work centers across from each other in a compact space.

• L-Shaped: Uses two adjacent perpendicular walls to form an "L" shape. Typically places two work centers on one wall and the third on the adjoining wall.
• U-Shaped: Features three continuous walls of cabinetry and counters forming a "U" shape. Distributes one work center per wall (e.g., sink on the back wall, stove and fridge on opposite side walls).
• Island: Features a standard perimeter layout (like an L-shape or single wall) with an independent, freestanding counter in the middle.', '["Galley","L-shaped","U-shaped","Island"]'::jsonb, '["Food Service Systems"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'easy', 5, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-14T20:00:00.000Z","interval":44,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":4}'::jsonb, NULL, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.104Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-14T16:39:20.415Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756168043907-2', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783609175539-1790433140277-2y6kw', 'Element of the foodservice system that refers to the resources needed to be able to achieve the objectives of the organization.', 'Input', 'The elements of the foodservice system
• Input- resources available to the system
• Operations - processing of the resources
• Output - the goal
• Control - component that regulates operations using a set of standards', '["Input","Control","Thruput","Feedback"]'::jsonb, '["Food Service Systems"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'easy', 5, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-19T20:00:00.000Z","interval":49,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":3}'::jsonb, NULL, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.104Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-14T16:34:54.305Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756168107172-3', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783609175539-1790433140277-2y6kw', 'Food is served and prepared under one roof. There is little time between food preparation and service.', 'Conventional', 'Conventional food system entails preparing and serving food in one roof thereby having little time between preparation and service.', '["Conventional","Commissary","Assembly","Ready prepared"]'::jsonb, '["Food Service Systems"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'easy', 5, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-18T20:00:00.000Z","interval":48,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":5}'::jsonb, NULL, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.104Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-14T16:35:12.303Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756168154885-4', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783609175539-1790433140277-2y6kw', 'Example of fixed or built-in equipment for delivery and service.', 'Dumbwaiter', 'A dumbwaiter is a small freight elevator or lift intended to carry food. Dumbwaiters found within modern structures, including both commercial, public and private buildings, are often connected between multiple floors.', '["Dumbwaiter","Portable totes","Pellet disc","Carts"]'::jsonb, '["Food Service Systems"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'easy', 5, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-18T20:00:00.000Z","interval":48,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":4}'::jsonb, NULL, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.104Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-14T16:37:29.071Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756168230048-5', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783609175539-1790433140277-2y6kw', 'In this style of tableservice, all food are prepared and portioned in the kitchen and attractively arranged on serving platters. Food is served to individual plates using cutleries.', 'Russian', 'Russian style of service entails fully preparing food in the kitchen and placed in platters for service to guests.', '["Russian","French","American","Blue plate"]'::jsonb, '["Food Service Systems"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'easy', 5, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-18T20:00:00.000Z","interval":48,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":4}'::jsonb, NULL, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.104Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-14T16:40:59.099Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756168292230-6', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783609175539-1790433140277-2y6kw', 'Type of menu commonly used in institution where the menu is repeated within a specified time span.', 'Cycle menu', 'Cycle or cyclical offers menu in rotation for a period of 6 to 30 days. Menu items are repeated at the end of a specified period. Most typically offered by non-commercial foodservices but may also be used by commercial buffet restaurants. It must be flexible to include leftovers or account for seasonal ingredients.', '["Cycle menu","Single-use menu","Selective menu","Static menu"]'::jsonb, '["Food Service Systems"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'easy', 5, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-19T20:00:00.000Z","interval":49,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":4}'::jsonb, NULL, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.104Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-14T16:39:33.469Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756168338778-7', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783609175539-1790433140277-2y6kw', 'What is the mark-up factor if the food cost is set at 33%?', '3', 'Mark up factor = 100/% food cost = 100/33 = 3', '["3","2","4","5"]'::jsonb, '["Food Service Systems"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'easy', 5, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-20T20:00:00.000Z","interval":50,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":3}'::jsonb, NULL, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.104Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-14T16:36:29.243Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756168391309-8', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783609175539-1790433140277-2y6kw', 'ISO standard of ensuring efficient, safe, and cleaner product and services:', '22000', 'The ISO 22000 is the comprehensive and internationally recognized standard for management systems in the food industry. With certification according to ISO 22000:2018 you guarantee the quality and safety of food along the entire production chain. From primary producers to transport companies, subcontractors, food manufacturers and retail.', '["22000","9000","1400","2001"]'::jsonb, '["Food Service Systems"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'easy', 5, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-17T20:00:00.000Z","interval":47,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":4}'::jsonb, NULL, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.104Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-14T16:35:36.255Z"'::jsonb)
