('anki-card-1758529952932-0', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783609212880-1790433142620-mxyd7', 'Which of the following is not a characteristic of a good cost control system.*', 'Designed to fit all types of food service operations', 'A good cost control system must be tailored specifically to the unique menu, scale, and operational requirements of each individual establishment. A generic system designed to fit all types of foodservice operations is ineffective because a hospital cafeteria, a fine-dining restaurant, and a fast-food chain require vastly different cost-control parameters.', '["Provides accurate, detailed, continuous data.","Designed to fit all types of food service operations","Simple to operate","Shows clear separation of duties"]'::jsonb, '["Food Service Systems"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 1, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-09-26T14:32:12.431Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":0}'::jsonb, NULL, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.930Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-09T02:50:34.742Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758529967998-1', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783609212880-1790433142620-mxyd7', 'Which statement is not true about food cost? *', 'It is not manageable', 'Food cost is the largest single variable cost element in foodservice and is subject to price fluctuations, but it is highly manageable through rigorous portion control, standard recipes, waste reduction, and careful purchasing. Claiming it is unmanageable is false.', '["It is the largest single cost element in foodservice","It is a variable cost","It is subject to price fluctuation","It is not manageable"]'::jsonb, '["Food Service Systems"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 1, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-09-26T14:32:12.431Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":0}'::jsonb, NULL, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.930Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-09T02:50:40.777Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758529980965-2', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783609212880-1790433142620-mxyd7', 'Which of the following activities is not directly related to food cost control? *', 'Improving service and satisfying customers', 'While customer satisfaction is vital for overall business success, improving service and satisfying customers is a quality and hospitality objective rather than an activity directly tied to food cost control (unlike waste reduction, yield tracking, and purchasing audits).', '["Generating management reports","Preventing wastes and inefficiencies","Improving service and satisfying customers","Reducing food related wastes & inefficiencies"]'::jsonb, '["Food Service Systems"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 1, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-09-26T14:32:12.431Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":0}'::jsonb, NULL, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.930Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-09T02:50:46.218Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758529995364-3', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783609212880-1790433142620-mxyd7', 'Which of the following information is not provided in a standard recipe?*', 'Food cost', 'A standardized recipe provides a list of ingredients, precise preparation procedures, recipe yield, and portion size. It does not provide food cost directly on the static recipe card because ingredient purchase prices fluctuate constantly; food cost must be calculated separately using current market prices.', '["List of ingredients","Food cost","Recipe Yield","Portion size"]'::jsonb, '["Food Service Systems"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 1, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-09-26T14:32:12.431Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":0}'::jsonb, NULL, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.930Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-09T02:50:55.059Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758530024246-4', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783609212880-1790433142620-mxyd7', 'Which statement is not true about popularity index?*', 'It indicates the need to increase portion size of items served', 'A popularity index (calculated as the number of specific menu items sold divided by total items sold) measures customer acceptance, preference, and sales trends over a given period to help forecast production volume. It tracks what items customers prefer, but it does not indicate or dictate changes to the portion size of items served, making this statement false and the correct exception.', '["It tells us how popular or acceptable a particular entrée is, at a particular period.","It guides us in projecting future sales for a particular entrée.","It signifies a trend in menu selection if the results are recurring.","It indicates the need to increase portion size of items served"]'::jsonb, '["Food Service Systems"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 1, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-09-26T14:32:12.431Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":0}'::jsonb, NULL, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.930Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-09T02:28:56.297Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758530050577-5', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783609212880-1790433142620-mxyd7', 'Which menu planning activity is not relevant to food cost control? *', 'It signifies a trend in menu selection if the results are recurring.', 'While observing recurring menu trends is useful for general operations, simply noting a recurring trend is not an active menu-planning activity specifically engineered for food cost control. In contrast, ensuring food quality standards, calculating popularity indexes for precise production forecasting, and maximizing seasonal produce directly control and optimize food costs.', '["Ensuring quality of food based on standards, from purchasing on to production.","Knowing the popularity index of menu items to guide in production planning.","It signifies a trend in menu selection if the results are recurring.","Maximizing use of fruits and vegetables while in season."]'::jsonb, '["Food Service Systems"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 1, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-09-26T14:32:12.431Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":0}'::jsonb, NULL, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.930Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-09T02:36:53.481Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758530070227-6', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783609212880-1790433142620-mxyd7', 'As Purchased weight of shrimps is 1 kilo and 250 grams. Edible portion is 850 rams. Yield percentage is*', '68%', 'To calculate the yield percentage:

As Purchased (AP) weight = 1,250 grams (1 kilo and 250 grams).

Edible Portion (EP) weight = 850 grams.

Yield Percentage = (EP / AP) x 100

Calculation = (850 / 1,250) x 100 = 68%.', '["68%","70%","82%","75%"]'::jsonb, '["Food Service Systems"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 1, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-09-26T14:32:12.431Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":0}'::jsonb, NULL, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.930Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-09T02:51:01.472Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758530085875-7', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783609212880-1790433142620-mxyd7', 'To prepare 50 portions of a standard recipe for 12 portions, how do I compute for scaling factor? *', 'Divide desired yield by the original yield', 'To compute the recipe scaling factor when adjusting quantities from an original yield to a desired yield, the universal formula is Desired Yield / Original Yield. For example, scaling a 12-portion recipe to 50 portions means dividing 50 by 12 to get the exact multiplication factor for every ingredient.', '["Divide desired yield by the original yield","Multiply desired yield by original yield.","Divide original yield by desired yield","Multiply original yield by desired yield"]'::jsonb, '["Food Service Systems"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 1, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-09-26T14:32:12.431Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":0}'::jsonb, NULL, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.930Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-09T02:29:02.111Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758530100642-8', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783609212880-1790433142620-mxyd7', 'A Chicken Sotanghon recipe yields 4 quarts and portion size is 1⁄2 cup. How many portions will this recipe make? *', '32 portions', 'To calculate the total number of portions:

Total volume = 4 quarts.

Since 1 quart = 4 cups, 4 quarts = 16 cups total.

Portion size = 1/2 cup per serving.

Total portions = 16 cups / (1/2 cup per serving) = 16 x 2 = 32 portions.', '["30 portions","26 portions","32 portions","16 portions"]'::jsonb, '["Food Service Systems"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 1, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-09-26T14:32:12.431Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":0}'::jsonb, NULL, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.930Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-09T02:37:04.224Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758530115760-9', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783609212880-1790433142620-mxyd7', 'Crispy, moist, uniformly cooked and flavorful fried chicken describe*', 'Quality standard', 'Describing a finished dish as crispy, moist, uniformly cooked, and flavorful evaluates its sensory attributes against an established quality standard.

Quantity standard: Refers to portion weights, counts, or batch yields.

Cost standard: Refers to food cost percentages and plate cost limits.

Service standard: Refers to hospitality, speed of delivery, and guest interaction protocols.', '["Quality standard","Quantity Standard","Cost standard","Service standard"]'::jsonb, '["Food Service Systems"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 1, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-09-26T14:32:12.431Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":0}'::jsonb, NULL, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.930Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-09T02:37:11.838Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758530132507-10', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783609212880-1790433142620-mxyd7', 'If an item is entrusted to me on aconsignment basis, it means*', 'I need to pay only what I am able to sell', 'Consignment is a commercial arrangement where a consignor (supplier or owner) entrusts goods to a consignee (seller or retail store) while retaining ownership of the merchandise. Under this arrangement, the consignee only pays the supplier for the items that are actually sold to customers, and unsold goods can typically be returned without financial penalty.', '["I have to pay for it in cash","I can pay for it on installment basis.","I need to pay only what I am able to sell","I can pay anytime but product is already considered sold"]'::jsonb, '["Food Service Systems"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 1, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-09-26T14:32:12.431Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":0}'::jsonb, NULL, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.930Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-09T02:43:24.350Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758530147725-11', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783609212880-1790433142620-mxyd7', 'Which of the following pricing methods is cost based? *', 'Food Cost%', 'The food cost percentage method is a direct cost-based pricing strategy. It calculates menu pricing using the raw ingredient cost divided by the desired target food cost percentage (e.g., Markup Factor = 100 / Target Food Cost %).

Traditional: Refers to arbitrary or legacy pricing methods rather than systematic cost calculations.

Competitive: A market-oriented pricing approach based on what rival restaurants charge, not internal costs.

Psychological: Pricing strategies structured around consumer perception (e.g., pricing items at P 199 instead of P 200).', '["Traditional","Competitive","Psychological","Food Cost%"]'::jsonb, '["Food Service Systems"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-10T13:06:13.145Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-09T13:06:13.145Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.930Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-09T02:44:20.815Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758530158873-12', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783609212880-1790433142620-mxyd7', 'Which of the following practices does not contribute to high food cost? *', 'use of requisition to withdraw stored goods', 'The use of requisitions to withdraw stored goods is an essential control mechanism and standard operating procedure in foodservice inventory management. It ensures that all stock movements out of the storeroom are authorized, tracked, and accounted for, thereby preventing loss and controlling food costs rather than contributing to high food costs.', '["overproduction and wastage","recipe costing once a year","food spoilage","use of requisition to withdraw stored goods"]'::jsonb, '["Food Service Systems"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 1, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-09-26T14:32:12.431Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":0}'::jsonb, NULL, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.930Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-09T02:44:32.307Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758530186887-13', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783609212880-1790433142620-mxyd7', 'What statement is NOT a reason for doing a physical inventory at the end of each trading period ? *', 'To clean the storeroom', 'A physical inventory count is conducted to determine the exact physical count and financial value of stock on hand, cross-check perpetual inventory records, check for slow-moving or obsolete items, and establish accurate Cost of Goods Sold calculations. While a storeroom may be tidied during an inventory count, cleaning the storeroom is a janitorial/maintenance task and is not the primary business reason for performing a formal physical inventory.', '["To determine items to be ordered","To determine value of inventory","To check on slow moving items","To clean the storeroom"]'::jsonb, '["Food Service Systems"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 1, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-09-26T14:32:12.431Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":0}'::jsonb, NULL, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.930Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-09T02:44:37.575Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758530207087-14', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783609212880-1790433142620-mxyd7', 'Which of the following information is not found in a guest check ?*', 'amount payable per guest', 'A standard guest check (server bill or receipt) displays the specific menu items ordered, their respective unit prices, the subtotal, taxes or service charges, and the total amount payable by the guest (or total table bill). It itemizes individual guests'' items if requested, but a general guest check typically shows the total table summary or individual charges without breaking down a separate isolated category labeled "amount payable per guest" distinct from the total bill. (Note: Let''s ensure your copy-paste formatting stays clean).', '["amount payable per guest","date of transaction","total amount payable by the guest","menu items ordered and respective prices"]'::jsonb, '["Food Service Systems"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 1, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-09-26T14:32:12.431Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":0}'::jsonb, NULL, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.930Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-09T02:44:45.734Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758530218803-15', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783609212880-1790433142620-mxyd7', 'Which of the following information is not provided in a budget? *', 'List of people assigned to implement budget', 'A budget is a financial and operational plan that provides forecasts of income and expenses, projected financial positions, and profit targets. However, the operational assignment of specific personnel to implement budget tasks is detailed in organizational staffing charts and operational workflows, not within the financial budget document itself.', '["Forecasts of income and expenses","Projected financial position","Profit targets","List of people assigned to implement budget"]'::jsonb, '["Food Service Systems"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 1, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-09-26T14:32:12.431Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":0}'::jsonb, NULL, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.930Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-09T02:44:50.974Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758530231652-16', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783609212880-1790433142620-mxyd7', 'This method of pricing is largely based on how much customers are willing to pay.*', 'What the market will bear', 'Pricing based on "what the market will bear" is a demand-oriented pricing strategy where menu prices are set primarily according to the maximum price customers are willing and able to pay based on perceived value, location, and dining atmosphere, regardless of strict cost calculations.

Food cost %: A cost-based pricing method using ingredient costs and target food cost percentages.

Competitive pricing: Based on matching or positioning prices relative to direct market competitors.

Volume pricing: Based on discounting quantities for bulk or large-scale purchases.', '["Food cost %","Competitive pricing","What the market will bear","Volume pricing"]'::jsonb, '["Food Service Systems"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 1, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-09-26T14:32:12.431Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":0}'::jsonb, NULL, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.930Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-09T02:45:09.739Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758530251834-17', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783609212880-1790433142620-mxyd7', 'This pricing method uses information on cost of ingredients and direct labor cost.*', 'Prime cost %', 'The prime cost percentage pricing method calculates menu prices by combining both the direct cost of ingredients (food cost) and the direct labor cost required to prepare the menu item, accounting for the two largest controllable expenses in foodservice operations.

Food cost %: Accounts strictly for raw ingredient costs without incorporating direct labor.

Minimum sale pricing: A formula used to calculate the absolute lowest price a menu item can be sold for without incurring a loss.

Menu pricing multiplier: A general markup factor derived from target food cost percentages.', '["Prime cost %","Food cost %","Minimum sale pricing","Menu pricing multiplier"]'::jsonb, '["Food Service Systems"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 1, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-09-26T14:32:12.431Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":0}'::jsonb, NULL, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.930Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-09T02:45:15.924Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758530266634-18', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783609212880-1790433142620-mxyd7', 'An arrangement between buyer and supplier, whereby items are purchased in the exact time when the items are to be used is called*', 'just in time purchasing', 'Just-in-Time (JIT) purchasing is an inventory management strategy where raw materials and food supplies are ordered and delivered exactly when they are needed for production, minimizing storage holding costs and spoilage.

Blanket purchase agreement: A standing purchase arrangement with a supplier for repetitive orders over a set period.

Prime vending: A vendor-partner agreement where an operation purchases most or all of its goods from a single primary supplier.

Futures and contract method: Locking in purchase prices and delivery commitments over extended future periods to hedge against market volatility.', '["just in time purchasing","blanket purchase agreement","prime vending","futures and contract method"]'::jsonb, '["Food Service Systems"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 1, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-09-26T14:32:12.431Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":0}'::jsonb, NULL, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.930Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-09T02:45:22.128Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758530285768-19', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783609212880-1790433142620-mxyd7', 'This type of inventory shows a running balance of each item stored, and is a continuous activity where the storekeeper keeps track of items delivered and items withdrawn.*', 'Perpetual', 'A perpetual inventory system maintains a continuous, running balance of every item in storage, where the storekeeper logs every delivery received and every requisition withdrawn in real-time.

Turnover: Measures how quickly inventory is sold and replaced over an accounting period.

Physical: An intermittent manual count of actual items on shelves.

On the spot: A colloquial term, not a formal inventory control system.', '["Perpetual","Turnover","On the spot","Physical"]'::jsonb, '["Food Service Systems"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 1, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-09-26T14:32:12.431Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":0}'::jsonb, NULL, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.930Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-09T02:45:37.327Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758530299532-20', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783609212880-1790433142620-mxyd7', 'The following factors affect a restaurant’s labor cost except*', 'Menu combination', 'A restaurant''s labor cost is directly driven by labor laws and union agreements (mandating wages, benefits, and overtime rules), the extensive use of convenience foods (which reduces prep labor hours), and daily food production volume (which dictates staffing levels needed). Menu combination affects food cost mix and popularity, but it does not directly dictate overall labor cost percentages compared to volume and operational prep complexity.', '["Labor laws and union agreements","Extensive use of convenience foods","Daily food production volume","Menu combination"]'::jsonb, '["Food Service Systems"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 1, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-09-26T14:32:12.431Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":0}'::jsonb, NULL, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.930Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-09T02:45:53.328Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758530312115-21', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783609212880-1790433142620-mxyd7', 'Compute for food cost percentage if sales is P 212,500 and food cost is P68,400.*', '32%', 'To compute the food cost percentage:

Formula = (Cost of Food / Total Sales) x 100

Given values: Food Cost = P 68,400; Sales = P 212,500.

Calculation = (68,400 / 212,500) x 100 = 32.18%, which rounds to 32%.', '["21%","27%","32%","41%"]'::jsonb, '["Food Service Systems"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 1, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-09-26T14:32:12.431Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":0}'::jsonb, NULL, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.930Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-09T02:45:58.843Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758530323999-22', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783609212880-1790433142620-mxyd7', 'Which practice helps control labor cost?*', 'Setting productivity standards', 'Labor cost is controlled by managing staff efficiency and output relative to hours worked. Setting productivity standards (such as meals produced per labor hour) directly establishes benchmarks to evaluate labor utilization, trim excess hours, and control overall labor costs.

Taking physical inventories: Controls food cost and stock levels, not labor cost.

Setting standard portion sizes: Controls food cost and ingredient usage.

Using standard recipes: Controls food cost consistency, yield, and purchasing waste.', '["Setting productivity standards","Taking physical inventories","Setting standard portion sizes","Using standard recipes"]'::jsonb, '["Food Service Systems"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 1, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-09-26T14:32:12.431Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":0}'::jsonb, NULL, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.930Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-09T02:29:15.108Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758530341247-23', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783609212880-1790433142620-mxyd7', 'Standard portion sizes helps managers control food _________.*', 'quantity', 'Standard portion sizes ensure that a fixed, predetermined amount of food is served on every plate, giving managers precise control over food quantity, portion cost, inventory depletion, and yield forecasting.

Quality: Regulated by standard recipes, ingredient specifications, and culinary training, not portion sizing alone.

Inventory records: Maintained through perpetual inventory logs and physical counts, though portion sizes help estimate usage.

Issuing: Managed via requisitions and stockroom sign-out sheets.', '["quality","quantity","inventory records","issuing"]'::jsonb, '["Food Service Systems"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 1, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-09-26T14:32:12.431Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":0}'::jsonb, NULL, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.930Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-09T02:29:21.822Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758530365596-24', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783609212880-1790433142620-mxyd7', 'A restaurant served 87 guests and made P 18, 705 sales. Average check per guest was______.*', 'P 215', 'In foodservice management and financial accounting, the average check (also termed cover average) measures the mean spending per customer. The standard mathematical formula is: Average Check = Total Sales Revenue / Total Number of Guests (Covers). Substituting the problem''s values: P 18,705 / 87 guests = P 215. This financial control metric allows managers to evaluate menu pricing efficiency, sales performance, and guest spending patterns.', '["P 250","P 215","P 205","P 217"]'::jsonb, '["Food Service Systems"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-30T08:57:11.842Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.3,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-29T08:57:11.842Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.930Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-29T08:57:11.842Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758530378596-25', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783609212880-1790433142620-mxyd7', 'To make a profit, total restaurant expenses must be less than _____ percent of its sales.*', '100%', 'To make a net profit, total restaurant expenses (food, labor, and overhead costs combined) must total less than 100% of total sales revenue so that a positive margin remains.', '["50%","75%","90%","100%"]'::jsonb, '["Food Service Systems"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 1, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-09-26T14:32:12.431Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":0}'::jsonb, NULL, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.930Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-09T02:36:00.468Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758530397279-26', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783609212880-1790433142620-mxyd7', 'If 43.5% of restaurant sales is needed to cover its variable costs, the remaining 56.5% is available to meet fixed costs and provide profits . The 56.5 % figure is called*', 'contribution margin', 'When variable costs are subtracted from sales revenue, the remaining percentage (in this case 56.5%) available to cover fixed costs and generate net profit is called the contribution margin (or contribution rate).', '["contribution rate","contribution margin","break-even point","variable rate"]'::jsonb, '["Food Service Systems"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 1, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-09-26T14:32:12.431Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":0}'::jsonb, NULL, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.930Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-09T02:36:07.254Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758530410295-27', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783609212880-1790433142620-mxyd7', 'Standard purchase specifications describes what kind of standard?*', 'Quality', 'Standard Purchase Specifications (SPS) outline the exact grade, size, brand, fat content, and overall quality standards required for food items purchased by an establishment.

Quantity: Managed through inventory par levels and requisition quantities.

Cost: Managed via budgets and competitive bidding, though quality specs indirectly impact pricing.', '["Quality","Quantity","Cost","Procedures"]'::jsonb, '["Food Service Systems"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 1, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-09-26T14:32:12.431Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":0}'::jsonb, NULL, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.930Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-09T02:36:21.251Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758530429927-28', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783609212880-1790433142620-mxyd7', 'A restaurant had 3 cases tomatoes (12 cans/case) at start of the month and used up 1 1/2 cases. How many cans must be purchased this month to bring the inventory back to the same starting level?*', '18', 'To solve this inventory replacement calculation:

Starting inventory = 3 cases x 12 cans/case = 36 cans.

Used amount = 1 1/2 cases = 1.5 x 12 = 18 cans used.

Remaining inventory = 36 - 18 = 18 cans left on hand.

To bring the inventory back to the starting level of 36 cans, the required purchase amount is: Starting level (36) - Current stock (18) = 18 cans to purchase.', '["12","18","24","36"]'::jsonb, '["Food Service Systems"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 1, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-09-26T14:32:12.431Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":0}'::jsonb, NULL, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.930Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-09T02:29:50.620Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758530451310-29', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783609212880-1790433142620-mxyd7', 'The normal quantity of a stored item required in a given operating period plus a safety factor for possible delay in delivery and for unexpected requirements, is called_______.*', 'par stock', 'Par stock is defined as the normal, predetermined quantity of a stored item required on hand to cover normal operating periods, plus an added safety buffer (safety stock) to account for potential delivery delays or unexpected demand spikes.

Reorder point: The specific inventory level trigger that signals when a purchase order must be placed.

Balance on hand: The actual physical count of stock currently sitting in the storeroom at any given moment.

Perpetual inventory: A running, continuous computerized or manual record of stock additions and issuances.', '["reorder point","balance on hand","par stock","perpetual inventory"]'::jsonb, '["Food Service Systems"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 1, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-09-26T14:32:12.431Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":0}'::jsonb, NULL, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.930Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-09T02:29:56.228Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758530466091-30', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783609212880-1790433142620-mxyd7', 'Centralized purchasing is most appropriate for which type of operation?*', 'Chain restaurant', 'Centralized purchasing (where a central headquarters or commissary handles buying, vendor negotiation, and distribution for multiple operational units) is most appropriate and cost-effective for chain restaurants and multi-unit franchises, leveraging bulk purchasing power and standardized specifications.

School cafeteria: Typically operates under single-site institutional procurement guidelines.

Food stall / Coffee shop: Independent single-unit operations where decentralized, local purchasing is more practical.', '["School cafeteria","Food stall","Coffee shop","Chain restaurant"]'::jsonb, '["Food Service Systems"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 1, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-09-26T14:32:12.431Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":0}'::jsonb, NULL, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.930Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-09T02:30:02.028Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758530480158-31', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783609212880-1790433142620-mxyd7', 'The most important responsibility of a Receiving Clerk is to document deliveries and*', 'check quality & quantity of deliveries', 'The most critical and primary responsibility of a Receiving Clerk upon the arrival of supplier shipments is to rigorously inspect and check the quality and quantity of deliveries against purchase orders and specifications before signing delivery receipts.', '["order items","pay suppliers","negotiate discounts","check quality & quantity of deliveries"]'::jsonb, '["Food Service Systems"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 1, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-09-26T14:32:12.431Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":0}'::jsonb, NULL, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.930Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-09T02:46:06.739Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758530497572-32', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783609212880-1790433142620-mxyd7', 'Upon receipt of goods, the Receiving Clerk (RC) should prepare and sign the _____*', 'invoice', 'Upon physical receipt and inspection of delivered goods on the loading dock, the Receiving Clerk verifies the items against the purchase order and signs the supplier''s invoice (or delivery receipt) to acknowledge receipt of the merchandise.

Specifications: Quality guidelines used before ordering, not signed upon delivery.

Purchase order: Generated by the buyer when placing the order, not signed by the receiver as proof of delivery.', '["invoice","specifications","purchase order","RC daily report"]'::jsonb, '["Food Service Systems"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 1, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-09-26T14:32:12.431Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":0}'::jsonb, NULL, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.930Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-09T02:33:42.621Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758530507939-33', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783609212880-1790433142620-mxyd7', 'Foods categorized as “directs” are *', 'charged to cost immediately upon delivery', 'Foods categorized as "directs" (such as fresh produce, delicate seafood, or bakery items delivered daily for immediate use without storeroom storage) are charged directly to food cost upon delivery rather than logged into perpetual inventory stock accounts.', '["charged to cost immediately upon delivery","verified against a purchase order","charged to cost when issued from inventory","verified against an invoice"]'::jsonb, '["Food Service Systems"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 1, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-09-26T14:32:12.431Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":0}'::jsonb, NULL, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.930Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-09T02:33:49.154Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758530520605-34', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783609212880-1790433142620-mxyd7', 'Which of the following is most likely to be “direct” ?*', 'Oysters', 'Oysters (along with fresh salad greens, delicate berries, and fresh daily bread) are highly perishable, short-shelf-life items classified as directs that go straight from the receiving dock into immediate kitchen preparation.

Canned vienna sausage / Bottled soda: Non-perishable staples stored safely in dry inventory as "stores."

Raw potatoes: While produce, hardy root vegetables are often stored in dry stock rooms, whereas ultra-perishable seafood like live oysters are classic directs.', '["Oysters","Canned vienna sausage","Raw potatoes","Bottled soda"]'::jsonb, '["Food Service Systems"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 1, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-09-26T14:32:12.431Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":0}'::jsonb, NULL, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.930Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-09T02:34:00.792Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758530533654-35', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783609212880-1790433142620-mxyd7', 'Which of the following is not a method of menu analysis?*', 'inventory of left overs', 'Menu analysis methods (such as popularity index, menu engineering, and meal count analyses) evaluate customer preferences, profitability, and sales volume. An inventory of leftovers is a kitchen waste tracking and storage check method, not a formal analytical tool for evaluating menu performance.

Meal Count: Tracks total portions served per meal period.

Menu engineering: Analyzes menu items based on their profitability and popularity matrix.

Popularity index: Measures the sales ratio of specific dishes relative to total entrees sold.', '["Meal Count","Menu engineering","Popularity index","inventory of left overs"]'::jsonb, '["Food Service Systems"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 1, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-09-26T14:32:12.431Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":0}'::jsonb, NULL, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.930Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-09T02:46:21.894Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758530547704-36', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783609212880-1790433142620-mxyd7', 'Which of the following best describes a standard recipe?*', 'Tried and tested formula with consistent results', 'A standardized recipe is a formally tested, evaluated, and proven recipe that consistently delivers the exact same yield, quality, and portion cost every single time it is prepared in an establishment.', '["Instructions for preparing a dish","Tried and tested formula with consistent results","Recipe with the same taste always","The same recipe any time, any where"]'::jsonb, '["Food Service Systems"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 1, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-09-26T14:32:12.431Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":0}'::jsonb, NULL, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.930Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-09T02:34:06.638Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758530562304-37', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783609212880-1790433142620-mxyd7', 'First-in-first- out (FIFO) is a method of*', 'rotating stock', 'First-In, First-Out (FIFO) is a fundamental inventory management principle used for rotating stock, ensuring that older items received or produced first are used before newly delivered supplies to prevent spoilage and waste.

Receiving deliveries: Involves inspecting and verifying incoming goods against purchase orders and invoices.

Ordering food: Managed through purchase specifications, par levels, and requisition schedules.

Maintaining storeroom security: Handled through locked doors, limited key access, and inventory auditing.', '["receiving deliveries","ordering food","rotating stock","maintaining storeroom security"]'::jsonb, '["Food Service Systems"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 1, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-09-26T14:32:12.431Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":0}'::jsonb, NULL, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.930Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-09T02:30:07.565Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758530575118-38', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783609212880-1790433142620-mxyd7', 'Which of the following documents is used when issuing inventory supplies?*', 'Requisition', 'A requisition (storeroom requisition slip) is the official internal document used by kitchen staff to request and record inventory supplies issued from the central storeroom to production areas.

Purchase order: Sent externally to a vendor to order new goods.

Purchase specifications: Quality and grade guidelines used when buying items.

Invoice: A bill issued by the supplier detailing delivered goods and costs.', '["Purchase order","Purchase specifications","Requisition","invoice"]'::jsonb, '["Food Service Systems"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 1, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-09-26T14:32:12.431Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":0}'::jsonb, NULL, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.930Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-09T02:30:12.908Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758530597370-39', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783609212880-1790433142620-mxyd7', 'If the portion size for Saba in Syrup is one piece whole banana, the portion is quantified by*', 'count', 'When an ingredient like a whole banana (Saba in Syrup) is portioned as single discrete units rather than by mass or volume, the portion is quantified by count (numerical unit measurement).

Size: Describes physical dimensions (e.g., small, medium, large), not an exact numerical portion count.

Weight: Measured in grams or pounds (e.g., 80g steak).

Invoice: A billing document, not a unit of measurement.', '["size","weight","count","invoice"]'::jsonb, '["Food Service Systems"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 1, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-09-26T14:32:12.431Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":0}'::jsonb, NULL, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.930Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-09T02:30:18.618Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758530612050-40', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783609212880-1790433142620-mxyd7', 'The portion cost of an 80 gram hamburger purchased at P300 per kilo of ground beef is _____*', 'P 24', 'To calculate the portion cost of the hamburger:

Purchase price of ground beef = P 300 per kilo (1,000 grams).

Cost per gram = P 300 / 1,000g = P 0.30 per gram.

Portion size = 80 grams.

Portion cost = 80g x P 0.30 = P 24.', '["P 24","P 12.50","P20","P30"]'::jsonb, '["Food Service Systems"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 1, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-09-26T14:32:12.431Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":0}'::jsonb, NULL, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.930Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-09T02:34:12.685Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758530625083-41', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783609212880-1790433142620-mxyd7', 'An 8 kilo ox tongue lost 30% of its weight after trimming. How much edible portion was left?*', '5.6 kilos', 'To calculate the edible portion (EP) remaining after trimming:

Original weight = 8 kilos.

Trim loss percentage = 30%.

Trim loss weight = 8 kilos x 0.30 = 2.4 kilos lost.

Edible portion left = Original weight (8) - Trim loss (2.4) = 5.6 kilos.', '["5.6 kilos","6.2 kilos","6.9 kilos","7.4 kilos"]'::jsonb, '["Food Service Systems"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 1, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-09-26T14:32:12.431Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":0}'::jsonb, NULL, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.930Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-09T02:34:20.775Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758530640816-42', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783609212880-1790433142620-mxyd7', 'When a chef tastes menu items and checks food plating before food is served to guests, he is ____*', 'checking if output meets food standards', 'When a chef tastes menu items and inspects food plating before it leaves the kitchen, they are actively checking if output meets food standards (quality control and assurance).

Training personnel: Mentoring staff, though tasting can be educational, the primary action is quality inspection.

Monitoring production performance: Broadly tracks kitchen speed and efficiency.

Giving feedback to employees: Communicating corrections, which follows inspection.', '["training personnel","monitoring production performance","checking if output meets food standards","giving feedback to employees"]'::jsonb, '["Food Service Systems"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 1, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-09-26T14:32:12.431Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":0}'::jsonb, NULL, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.930Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-09T02:46:35.526Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758530653632-43', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783609212880-1790433142620-mxyd7', 'A sales history is based on information taken from*', 'guest checks', 'A restaurant''s daily sales history (tracking exact items sold, guest counts, and revenue) is compiled directly from data captured on individual guest checks (or POS terminal transaction logs).

Perpetual inventories: Tracks stock levels and storeroom balances, not customer sales records.

Requisitions: Tracks internal stock issuances to kitchen production areas.

Production sheets: Outlines planned prep quantities, not historical sales logs.', '["perpetual inventories","requisitions","guest checks","production sheets"]'::jsonb, '["Food Service Systems"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 1, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-09-26T14:32:12.431Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":0}'::jsonb, NULL, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.930Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-09T02:46:40.533Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758530669065-44', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783609212880-1790433142620-mxyd7', 'If 40 portions of Lamb Steak were sold out of 118 different entrees, what is its popularity index?*', '34%', 'To calculate the popularity index of the Lamb Steak:

Formula = (Number of specific item sold / Total entrees sold) x 100

Given values: Lamb Steak sold = 40 portions; Total different entrees sold = 118.

Calculation = (40 / 118) x 100 = 33.89%, which rounds to 34%.', '["20%","34%","41%","52%"]'::jsonb, '["Food Service Systems"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 1, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-09-26T14:32:12.431Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":0}'::jsonb, NULL, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.930Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-09T02:46:45.632Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758530687497-45', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783609212880-1790433142620-mxyd7', 'Menu items to be prepared in one day are written on a*', 'production list', 'A production list (or prep sheet) outlines the specific menu items, batch quantities, and ingredients that must be prepared by the kitchen staff on a given day based on sales forecasts and par levels.

Bin card: A physical inventory card attached to storage shelves tracking item balances.

Sales history: Past sales records used for forecasting.

Requisition: A form used to withdraw items from storerooms.', '["production list","bin card","sales history","requisition"]'::jsonb, '["Food Service Systems"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 1, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-09-26T14:32:12.431Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":0}'::jsonb, NULL, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.930Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-09T02:46:52.051Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758530701581-46', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783609212880-1790433142620-mxyd7', 'A void sheet is used to record*', 'portions returned by customers', 'A void sheet (or void/return log) is an administrative control document used to record portions returned by customers due to errors, quality issues, or guest complaints, as well as cashier ring errors, allowing management to track waste and inventory variances.

Sales errors made by the cashier: Often tracked on correction or void slips, but returned food portions specifically populate void and waste logs.

Food items needed from inventory: Requested via storeroom requisitions.

Food items that are wasted: Logged in kitchen waste logs, while customer returns go on void/return sheets.', '["sales errors made by the cashier","portions returned by customers","food items needed from inventory","food items that are wasted"]'::jsonb, '["Food Service Systems"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 1, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-09-26T14:32:12.431Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":0}'::jsonb, NULL, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.930Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-09T02:46:57.256Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758530714029-47', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783609212880-1790433142620-mxyd7', 'When we do an actual count of all itemskept in the storeroom, we are doing a ____.*', 'physical inventory', 'When staff conduct a hands-on, physical count of all goods and items kept in the storeroom at the end of an accounting period, it is termed a physical inventory.

Inventory evaluation: The financial valuation process applied to inventory counts.

Inventory turnover: A financial ratio measuring how many times inventory is sold and replaced.

Perpetual inventory: A continuous running balance tracked via records.', '["physical inventory","inventory evaluation","inventory turnover","perpetual inventory"]'::jsonb, '["Food Service Systems"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 1, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-09-26T14:32:12.431Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":0}'::jsonb, NULL, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.930Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-09T02:47:04.301Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758530728345-48', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783609212880-1790433142620-mxyd7', 'To compute for cost of food sold, add purchases to opening inventory, and subtract ____.*', 'closing inventory', 'The standard Cost of Goods Sold (COGS) formula is: Beginning Inventory + Purchases - Closing Inventory = Cost of Food Sold. Subtracting the closing inventory value determines the exact net value of food consumed during that accounting period.

Stores / Directs / Requisitions: These are internal issue categories or inventory tracking partitions, not the deduction variable in the fundamental COGS equation.', '["closing inventory","stores","directs","requisitions"]'::jsonb, '["Food Service Systems"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 1, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-09-26T14:32:12.431Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":0}'::jsonb, NULL, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.930Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-09T02:30:25.793Z"'::jsonb)
