INSERT INTO public.flashcards (id, user_id, deck_id, front, back, rationale, options, tags, ndle_subject, difficulty, leitner_box, user_notes, sm2, last_reviewed_at, created_at, updated_at) VALUES
('anki-card-1758687183939-44', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783609236208-1790433142623-d6lsk', 'Which does not belong to the group? *', 'ribs', 'Ribs (pork spareribs or beef short ribs) are classified as tender cuts derived from muscles that get very little physical exercise (located along the upper rib cage). In contrast, the other options are classic tough cuts requiring long, slow cooking.

Pata: Pig''s trotters/legs, a heavily exercised cut rich in connective tissue and collagen requiring prolonged braising.

Brisket: Located in the heavily exercised breast/chest area of the animal, packed with tough connective tissue.

Hind shank: Derived from the lower leg of the animal, heavily exercised and full of tough tendons and collagen.', '["ribs","pata","brisket","hind shank"]'::jsonb, '["Food Service Systems"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 1, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-09-26T14:32:12.431Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":0}'::jsonb, NULL, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.929Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-08T14:01:03.922Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758687195189-45', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783609236208-1790433142623-d6lsk', 'The correct order of steps, from beginning to end, in preparing most types of candies: *', 'beating, heating, cooking, creating a syrup solution', 'Note: This specific question in local board exam pools contains a trick or flawed sequence wording in its key, but you must match the exact official answer. 

In confectionery production, the logical sequence starts with dissolving sugar in liquid (creating a syrup solution), followed by heating and boiling to the correct temperature stage (cooking), and finishing with cooling and agitation (beating) to control crystallization.', '["cooking, beating, heating, creating a syrup solution","creating a syrup solution, heating, cooking, beating","beating, heating, cooking, creating a syrup solution","creating a syrup solution, heating, beating, cooking"]'::jsonb, '["Food Service Systems"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-12T17:33:53.248Z","interval":3,"easeFactor":2.3,"repetitions":2}'::jsonb, '"2026-11-09T17:33:53.248Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.929Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-09T17:33:53.248Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758687208321-46', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783609236208-1790433142623-d6lsk', 'Which is expected to coagulate first? *', 'egg white', 'Egg white (albumen) consists primarily of globular proteins (such as ovalbumin) dissolved in water, which begin to denature and coagulate at a lower thermal threshold (starting around 60°C to 65°C / 140°F to 149°F) than egg yolks (which contain fats and lipoproteins that coagulate at 65°C to 70°C / 149°F to 158°F). Furthermore, adding sugar (as in a soufflé) or yolks raises the coagulation temperature, making pure egg white coagulate first.

Egg mixed with sugar as in soufflé: Sugar increases thermal stability and delays protein coagulation.

Whole egg: Contains yolk fats which elevate the overall coagulation range compared to pure white.

Egg yolk: Coagulates at a higher temperature range due to its high lipid and lipoprotein content.', '["egg mixed with sugar as in soufflé","whole egg","egg white","egg yolk"]'::jsonb, '["Food Service Systems"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 1, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-09-26T14:32:12.431Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":0}'::jsonb, NULL, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.929Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-08T14:28:07.392Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758687218004-47', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783609236208-1790433142623-d6lsk', 'If you see a cracked cake crust, this is most probably due to: *', 'all of the above', 'A cracked cake crust is a common baking defect that can be triggered by multiple compounding factors working together, including:

Using the wrong type of flour (such as high-protein bread flour instead of soft cake flour, creating excessive gluten structure).

Wrong method and duration of mixing (over-mixing develops too much gluten and incorporates excess air pockets).

Too hot oven (causes the outer crust to set and form prematurely while the interior batter is still expanding, bursting the hardened top surface).', '["use of wrong type of flour","wrong method and duration of mixing","too hot oven","all of the above"]'::jsonb, '["Food Service Systems"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 1, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-09-26T14:32:12.431Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":0}'::jsonb, NULL, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.929Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-08T14:28:15.354Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758687226619-48', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783609236208-1790433142623-d6lsk', 'The most stable of the plant pigments: *', 'carotenoid', 'In food science, carotenoids (the fat-soluble pigments responsible for the yellow, orange, and red colors in foods like carrots, squash, and tomatoes) are considered the most stable of the plant pigments. They are highly resistant to heat, acids, and alkalis during normal cooking and processing, retaining their bright color much better than other pigments.

• Anthocyanin: This water-soluble pigment (responsible for red, blue, and purple hues in berries and cabbage) is highly unstable. It is extremely sensitive to pH changes—turning red in acids and an unappetizing blue/green in alkalis—and easily leaches into cooking water.
• Astaxanthin: While technically a highly stable pigment belonging to the carotenoid family, it is primarily produced by microalgae and is overwhelmingly associated with marine life (giving salmon, shrimp, and flamingos their pink color) rather than being classified as a standard culinary "plant pigment" in foodservice.
• Chlorophyll: This green pigment is highly unstable. It is extremely sensitive to heat and acid, quickly degrading and converting into pheophytin, which gives overcooked green vegetables a drab, olive-green color.', '["carotenoid","anthocyanin","astaxanthin","chlorophyll"]'::jsonb, '["Food Service Systems"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-10T13:51:33.890Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.3,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-11-09T13:51:33.890Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.929Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-09T13:51:33.890Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758687233820-49', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783609236208-1790433142623-d6lsk', 'The number of pints in 1 gallon. *', '8 pints', 'Debate-free liquid volume conversion: 1 gallon equals 4 quarts, and since each quart contains 2 pints, multiplying 4 quarts by 2 gives exactly 8 pints per gallon.', '["9 pints","16 pints","6 ½ pints","8 pints"]'::jsonb, '["Food Service Systems"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 1, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-09-26T14:32:12.431Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":0}'::jsonb, NULL, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.929Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-08T14:28:22.229Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758687242985-50', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783609236208-1790433142623-d6lsk', 'A yeast bread that is poor in volume may be due to the following except: *', 'too little salt', 'In yeast bread production, salt plays a vital regulatory role beyond mere seasoning: it controls and slows down yeast fermentation activity. Too little salt causes the yeast to ferment and multiply too rapidly, exhausting its food supply prematurely and leading to a weak gas-retention structure that collapses or yields poor loaf volume.

Too much salt: Excessive salt dehydrates and inhibits yeast cells entirely, also causing poor volume.

Short proofing time: Prevents the dough from developing sufficient carbon dioxide gas before baking, resulting in a dense, low-volume loaf.

Too much kneading: Can over-stretch gluten strands or trap excessive heat, though moderate over-kneading is less common as a primary cause of total volume failure compared to salt imbalances.', '["too much salt","too little salt","short proofing time","too much kneading"]'::jsonb, '["Food Service Systems"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-10T17:21:31.651Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-11-09T17:21:31.651Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.929Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-09T17:21:31.651Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758687250168-51', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783609236208-1790433142623-d6lsk', 'The following changes occur when an egg ages except: *', 'acidity increases', 'When an egg ages, its acidity does not increase; it actually becomes significantly more alkaline. Fresh eggs contain dissolved carbon dioxide. Over time, this CO₂ escapes through the porous shell, which causes the egg''s pH to rise from a slightly neutral 7.6 to a highly alkaline 9.0 or 9.4.

• Air cell enlarges: As moisture and gases escape through the shell over time, the air cell at the blunt end of the egg grows larger.
• Water evaporates: The shell is porous, allowing internal water to slowly evaporate into the environment.
• Water enters into the yolk: Osmosis causes water to move from the thinning egg white (albumen) into the yolk. This stretches and weakens the vitelline membrane, causing older yolks to sit flat and break easily in the pan.', '["air cell enlarges","acidity increases","water evaporates","water enters into the yolk"]'::jsonb, '["Food Service Systems"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-10T17:34:25.786Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.3,"repetitions":0}'::jsonb, '"2026-11-09T17:34:25.786Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.929Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-09T17:34:25.786Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758687259803-52', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783609236208-1790433142623-d6lsk', 'Fudge is classified as a _____ type of candy. *', 'crystalline', 'In confectionery science, crystalline candies (such as fudge, fondant, divinity, and nougat) are characterized by their smooth, creamy texture. This texture is achieved by carefully cooling a sugar syrup and then beating it vigorously to force the sugar molecules to form millions of tiny, microscopic crystals.

• Non-crystalline: Also known as amorphous candies (like hard candies, lollipops, caramels, and brittles), these are cooked to higher temperatures and purposefully prevented from forming any crystals, resulting in a hard or chewy glass-like structure.
• Divinity: This is a specific example of a crystalline candy, not the broad category name itself.
• Hard: Hard candies are a subcategory of non-crystalline candies.', '["non-crystalline","crystalline","divinity","hard"]'::jsonb, '["Food Service Systems"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 1, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-09-26T14:32:12.431Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":0}'::jsonb, NULL, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.929Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-08T13:39:35.648Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758687275468-53', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783609236208-1790433142623-d6lsk', 'Cream of tartar in angel food cakes serves which of the following purposes? *', 'leavening agent, increases tenderness, and strengthens egg white foam', 'leavening agent, increases tenderness, and strengthens egg white fNote: In strict food science, cream of tartar (tartaric acid) is an acidifier, not a direct leavening agent itself. However, in the context of local board exam test banks, it is occasionally grouped with leavening because of its mechanical support role.

In foam cakes like angel food cake, the primary function of cream of tartar is to lower the pH of the egg whites. This acid strengthens and stabilizes the egg white foam, preventing it from collapsing in the oven. Because the trapped air in this foam acts as the sole physical leavening agent, stabilizing the foam directly supports leavening. Furthermore, the acid increases tenderness by preventing the egg proteins from coagulating too rigidly, and it whitens the cake crumb by preventing natural flour pigments (flavonoids) from yellowing.oam', '["leavening agent, strengthens egg white foam, and acts as a preservative","strengthens egg white foam, increases tenderness, and whitens color","increases tenderness, whitens color, and improves volume","leavening agent, increases tenderness, and strengthens egg white foam"]'::jsonb, '["Food Service Systems"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-10T17:38:28.040Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-11-09T17:38:28.040Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.929Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-09T17:38:28.040Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758687283967-54', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783609236208-1790433142623-d6lsk', 'What is the recommended frying temperature? *', '180°C', 'The standard, optimal temperature range for deep-frying most foods is between 350°F and 375°F. Converting this to Celsius yields a range of roughly 175°C to 190°C. Therefore, 180°C (approximately 356°F) is the perfect benchmark temperature to quickly seal the exterior of the food, preventing it from soaking up excess oil while allowing the interior to cook through.', '["220°F","180°F","180°C","220°C"]'::jsonb, '["Food Service Systems"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 1, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-09-26T14:32:12.431Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":0}'::jsonb, NULL, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.929Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-08T13:40:03.907Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758687292285-55', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783609236208-1790433142623-d6lsk', 'What is the safe temperature for cooking eggs? *', '71°C', 'According to standard ServSafe and USDA food safety guidelines, the safe internal cooking temperature for eggs being prepared for immediate service or hot holding is 160°F. Converted to Celsius, this is 71°C. Reaching this temperature ensures that pathogenic bacteria like Salmonella enteritidis, which can be present inside the egg, are destroyed.

• 71°F: This is room temperature (approx 21°C). Bacteria rapidly multiply at this temperature.
• 87°C and 78°C: These temperatures are unnecessarily high for standard egg safety and will result in severely overcooked, rubbery, or sulfurous eggs.', '["71°F","71°C","87°C","78°C"]'::jsonb, '["Food Service Systems"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 1, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-09-26T14:32:12.431Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":0}'::jsonb, NULL, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.929Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-08T13:40:42.653Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758687320048-56', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783609236208-1790433142623-d6lsk', 'The primary leavening agent used in making yeast breads: *', 'CO2', 'While yeast is the biological microorganism added to the dough, the actual physical substance (the agent) that gets trapped in the gluten network and causes the bread to expand and rise is carbon dioxide (CO₂) gas. The yeast digests the sugars in the dough through fermentation and excretes CO₂ and alcohol as byproducts.

• Yeast: Yeast is the producer of the leavening gas, not the physical gas itself.
• Steam: Steam is the primary leavening agent in high-hydration batters like cream puffs (choux pastry) or popovers, but not in standard yeast breads.
• Air: While air beaten into dough during mixing provides microscopic pockets for the CO₂ to expand into, the primary lifting force in yeast bread comes from the CO₂.', '["yeast","CO2","steam","air"]'::jsonb, '["Food Service Systems"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 1, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-09-26T14:32:12.431Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":0}'::jsonb, NULL, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.929Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-08T13:41:15.141Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758687329598-57', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783609236208-1790433142623-d6lsk', 'Which of these cakes should not be tested for doneness using a cake tester so as to preserve its delicate texture? *', 'sponge cake', 'Sponge cakes (along with angel food and chiffon cakes) are delicate foam cakes that rely almost entirely on an intricate matrix of whipped air bubbles for their structure. Piercing the center of a baking sponge cake with a wooden skewer or metal cake tester can puncture this fragile matrix, causing the delicate cell walls to collapse and the cake to sink in the middle. Instead, they should be tested by lightly pressing the center with a finger; if it springs back immediately, it is done.

Butter cake, fruit cake, and pound cake are all shortened cakes. They contain a high percentage of solid fat (butter/shortening) and have a very sturdy, dense flour crumb structure. They will not collapse when poked and are classically tested for doneness by inserting a toothpick or skewer to see if it comes out clean.', '["butter cake","fruit cake","sponge cake","pound cake"]'::jsonb, '["Food Service Systems"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 1, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-09-26T14:32:12.431Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":0}'::jsonb, NULL, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.929Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-08T13:41:34.948Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758687341880-58', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783609236208-1790433142623-d6lsk', 'The stage of beaten egg white that is characterized by white, dry flakes: *', 'brittle foam', 'When egg whites are over-whipped past the stiff peak stage, the protein film surrounding the trapped air cells stretches too far, ruptures, and loses its elasticity. Moisture escapes, causing the foam to collapse into rough, white, dry flakes that separate and clump together. This over-beaten state is useless for leavening as it cannot hold air or incorporate properly into batters.

• Slightly beaten stage: At this early stage, the foam is foamy, transparent, and holds large air bubbles with zero peaks, useful only for light thickening.
• Scrambled: This is a term used for cooked eggs, not a stage of beaten raw egg white foam.
• Stiff peak stage: At this ideal stage, the peaks stand straight up without falling over when the beaters are lifted, holding maximum air for volume without drying out.', '["slightly beaten stage","scrambled","brittle foam","stiff peak stage"]'::jsonb, '["Food Service Systems"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 1, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-09-26T14:32:12.431Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":0}'::jsonb, NULL, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.929Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-08T13:45:52.327Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758687350963-59', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783609236208-1790433142623-d6lsk', 'Which does not belong to the group? *', 'filled milk', 'Filled milk is a product where the natural butterfat of milk has been completely or partially extracted and replaced with vegetable oil (such as coconut or palm oil). It is categorized as a manufactured milk substitute or recombined dairy product.

• Buttermilk: This is a classic cultured dairy product made by fermenting liquid milk with lactic acid bacteria.
• Yogurt: This is a fermented dairy product produced by bacterial fermentation of milk using specific probiotic cultures.
• Sour cream: This is a traditional cultured cream product created by fermenting regular cream with lactic acid bacteria.', '["buttermilk","yogurt","filled milk","sour cream"]'::jsonb, '["Food Service Systems"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 1, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-09-26T14:32:12.431Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":0}'::jsonb, NULL, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.929Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-08T13:46:35.895Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758687368129-60', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783609236208-1790433142623-d6lsk', 'This alcoholic beverage is considered a hard drink: *', 'tequila', 'Tequila is classified as a distilled spirit, commonly referred to as a "hard drink" or hard liquor, produced by distilling fermented juices from the blue agave plant, resulting in a significantly higher alcohol-by-volume (ABV) percentage.

• Beer: This is a fermented malt beverage with a low ABV, classified as a brewed drink rather than a distilled spirit.
• Porter: This is a style of dark, fermented ale belonging to the beer family, not a hard drink.
• Herbal infusion: This is a non-alcoholic beverage made by steeping herbs or botanicals in hot water.', '["tequila","beer","porter","herbal infusion"]'::jsonb, '["Food Service Systems"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-10T17:33:26.864Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-11-09T17:33:26.864Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.929Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-09T17:33:26.864Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758687377479-61', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783609236208-1790433142623-d6lsk', 'A market form of milk that does not contain buttermilk: *', 'evaporated filled milk', 'Note: In locally circulated review materials, this question often contains a typographical error, using the word "buttermilk" when it actually means "butterfat" (milk fat). The rationale below addresses the core concept of the question.

In food standards and dairy manufacturing, filled milk refers to a milk product where the natural animal fat (butterfat) has been entirely extracted and replaced with vegetable oils (commonly coconut, palm, or corn oil). Because the original dairy fat is completely substituted, evaporated filled milk contains zero butterfat.

• Raw milk: This is unpasteurized, unhomogenized milk straight from the cow. It retains all of its natural components, including its full butterfat content.
• Recombined evaporated milk: This is created by taking skim milk powder and combining it back together with water and actual milk fat (butter oil) to recreate the composition of evaporated milk.
• Low fat milk: While the fat content is reduced, it still contains a distinct percentage (usually 1% to 2%) of natural dairy butterfat.', '["low fat milk","recombined evaporated milk","raw milk","evaporated filled milk"]'::jsonb, '["Food Service Systems"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-12T17:35:50.860Z","interval":3,"easeFactor":2.3,"repetitions":2}'::jsonb, '"2026-11-09T17:35:50.860Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.929Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-09T17:35:50.860Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758687387262-62', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783609236208-1790433142623-d6lsk', 'Fish cooks faster than beef, pork, and poultry because: *', 'There is much less collagen in fish than in beef, pork, and poultry.', 'Fish muscle fibers (myotomes) are very short and are held together by delicate, thin sheets of connective tissue (myocommata) containing significantly less total collagen and zero tough elastin compared to land mammals and poultry. This minimal, fragile connective tissue structure breaks down almost instantly upon heating, allowing fish to cook much faster than beef, pork, or poultry.', '["There is much less collagen in fish than in beef, pork, and poultry.","The protein structure of fish is less complex than in beef, pork, and poultry.","There is less fat in fish than in beef, pork, and poultry.","Fish has fewer bones in proportion to its flesh than in beef and pork."]'::jsonb, '["Food Service Systems"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-10T17:18:10.127Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-11-09T17:18:10.127Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.929Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-09T17:18:10.127Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758687397144-63', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783609236208-1790433142623-d6lsk', 'It does not belong to the group: *', 'peanut brittle', 'Peanut brittle is classified as a non-crystalline (amorphous/glassy) confection cooked to a high temperature and cooled rapidly without crystallization. In contrast, the other three options are classic examples of colloidal or whipped foam/emulsion systems containing trapped air or fat emulsions.

Cream puff batter: A high-moisture choux pastry dough relying on gelatinized starch and egg proteins to form an airy shell.

Mayonnaise: A stable oil-in-water culinary emulsion stabilized by egg yolks.

Ice cream: A complex frozen dairy emulsion and foam containing dispersed air cells, fat globules, and ice crystals.', '["cream puff batter","peanut brittle","mayonnaise","ice cream"]'::jsonb, '["Food Service Systems"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 1, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-09-26T14:32:12.431Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":0}'::jsonb, NULL, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.929Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-08T14:01:23.574Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758687403745-64', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783609236208-1790433142623-d6lsk', 'This product is not made from a dough: *', 'griddle cake', 'A griddle cake (such as a pancake or hotcake) is made from a thin, pourable batter, not a stiff, workable dough. A batter has a high liquid-to-flour ratio that allows it to flow freely or be poured onto a hot cooking surface.

Lasagna: Made from a stiff pasta dough rolled and cut into flat sheets before boiling.

Yeast bread: Made from a structured, kneaded yeast-leavened dough that can hold its shape.

Pie crust: Made from a cold-fat pastry dough that is rolled out and shaped into pie plates.', '["lasagna","griddle cake","yeast bread","pie crust"]'::jsonb, '["Food Service Systems"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 1, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-09-26T14:32:12.431Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":0}'::jsonb, NULL, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.929Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-08T14:01:41.947Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758687413328-65', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783609236208-1790433142623-d6lsk', 'Considered the mother of all shortened cakes: *', 'pound cake', 'The pound cake is historically considered the "mother of all shortened cakes" because its traditional formulation consisted of equal parts of four fundamental ingredients (one pound each of flour, butter, sugar, and eggs), laying the foundational ratio for modern butter and shortened cakes.

Butter cake: A direct evolutionary descendant of the pound cake, modified with chemical leaveners for a lighter crumb.

Yellow sponge cake: A foam-type cake relying on whipped whole eggs, belonging to an entirely different structural category than shortened cakes.

Baked Alaska: A composite dessert consisting of ice cream and cake encased in meringue, not a baseline cake type.', '["butter cake","yellow sponge cake","pound cake","baked Alaska"]'::jsonb, '["Food Service Systems"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 1, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-09-26T14:32:12.431Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":0}'::jsonb, NULL, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.929Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-08T14:01:47.569Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758687424993-66', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783609236208-1790433142623-d6lsk', 'It should not be used if the goal is to create a permanent emulsion: *', 'white pepper', 'White pepper is a spice ground from dried peppercorns and possesses no natural emulsifying properties. It will simply suspend temporarily before settling, making it useless for creating a stable food emulsion.

Protein: Proteins possess amphiphilic properties (both hydrophilic and hydrophobic regions), allowing them to coat oil droplets and act as powerful natural emulsifiers.

Lecithin: A potent natural phospholipid found abundantly in egg yolks and soybeans, serving as one of the premier food emulsifiers.

Egg: Egg yolks are packed with lipoproteins and lecithin, which are universally used to stabilize permanent emulsions like mayonnaise.', '["protein","lecithin","white pepper","egg"]'::jsonb, '["Food Service Systems"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-10T17:35:05.460Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.3,"repetitions":0}'::jsonb, '"2026-11-09T17:35:05.460Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.929Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-09T17:35:05.460Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758687433976-67', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783609236208-1790433142623-d6lsk', 'Fruit that is considered “simple” in nature: *', 'ponkan', 'In botanical fruit classification, a simple fruit develops from a single pistil or ovary of a single flower (which can be fleshy like a drupe, berry, or hesperidium, or dry). A ponkan (citrus mandarin/orange) is a hesperidium, which is classified botanically as a simple fleshy fruit arising from one flower.

Pineapple: This is a classic example of a multiple fruit, which develops from the tightly fused ovaries of an entire inflorescence (a cluster of many flowers merging together).

Strawberry: This is an aggregate fruit (and accessory fruit), where multiple ovaries from a single flower develop separately over a fleshy, enlarged receptacle.

Breadfruit: This is also a multiple fruit formed from the coalesced flowers of a dense inflorescence.', '["pineapple","ponkan","strawberry","breadfruit"]'::jsonb, '["Food Service Systems"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 1, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-09-26T14:32:12.431Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":0}'::jsonb, NULL, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.929Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-08T13:51:31.114Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758687442093-68', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783609236208-1790433142623-d6lsk', 'This cake depends on the stability of an egg white foam for structure and volume: *', 'angel food cake', 'Angel food cake is a classic foam cake that contains zero fat and chemical leaveners; its entire structure, volume, and rising power depend entirely on the mechanical stability and trapped air of whipped egg white foams.

Muffin: A shortened or quick bread product dependent on chemical leaveners (baking powder/soda) and gluten structure.

Delicate butter cake: Relies on creamed fat, flour, and chemical leaveners for its crumb structure.

Pound cake: Relies on creamed butter, sugar, and eggs with dense structural integrity rather than delicate egg white foam.', '["muffin","angel food cake","delicate butter cake","pound cake"]'::jsonb, '["Food Service Systems"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-10T17:30:11.431Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-11-09T17:30:11.431Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.929Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-09T17:30:11.431Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758687450061-69', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783609236208-1790433142623-d6lsk', 'The medium of heat transfer used in “sinigang na baboy”: *', 'water', 'In sinigang na baboy (a traditional Filipino sour broth soup), the primary medium of heat transfer is water (liquid conduction/convection). The heat energy is transferred directly from the boiling liquid medium to the pork cubes and vegetables cooking inside the pot.

Air: Air is the primary medium in baking, roasting, or grilling, not in submerged boiling soups.

Convection: While thermal currents circulate within the boiling water, the actual medium of heat transfer is the liquid water itself.

Conduction: Conduction refers to direct physical contact with solid surfaces (like the bottom of the pot), whereas the bulk cooking is achieved via the liquid medium.', '["air","convection","water","conduction"]'::jsonb, '["Food Service Systems"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-10T17:38:38.363Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.3,"repetitions":0}'::jsonb, '"2026-11-09T17:38:38.363Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.929Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-09T17:38:38.363Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758687457660-70', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783609236208-1790433142623-d6lsk', 'A dairy product not recommended to accompany coffee: *', 'light whipping cream', 'Light whipping cream contains roughly 30% to 35% milk fat, which is generally too low to trap air effectively for stable micro-foam creation when added to hot coffee. Furthermore, its lower fat threshold combined with high heat and coffee acidity causes it to easily feather, curdle, and separate into unsightly flakes rather than dissolving smoothly.

Table cream: Contains lower fat (~18-20%) and is commonly used as a light coffee creamer without heavy whipping expectations.

Half and half: A blend of equal parts milk and cream (~10-12% fat) designed specifically as a stable, light coffee additive.

Coffee cream: Formulated specifically for hot beverages, blending smoothly without curdling under standard coffee temperatures.', '["table cream","light whipping cream","half and half","coffee cream"]'::jsonb, '["Food Service Systems"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-10T17:38:48.910Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-11-09T17:38:48.910Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.929Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-09T17:38:48.910Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758687465442-71', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783609236208-1790433142623-d6lsk', 'Examples of ripened cheeses except: *', 'Edam (queso de bola)', 'Note: This is another classic flawed local board exam question where the key provided in the test bank is factually incorrect, but you must recognize how the board grades it. 

Botanically and technically, Edam (queso de bola) is a classic ripened (aged), semi-hard cheese cured over several months. (In contrast, cottage cheese and cream cheese are unripened fresh cheeses). Because exam banks occasionally misclassify Edam due to translation or categorization errors in local references, remember that Edam is aged/ripened, whereas cottage cheese, cream cheese, and fresh mozzarella are unripened.', '["Mozzarella","cottage cheese","Edam (queso de bola)","cream cheese"]'::jsonb, '["Food Service Systems"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 1, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-09-26T14:32:12.431Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":0}'::jsonb, NULL, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.929Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-08T14:02:25.438Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758687475209-72', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783609236208-1790433142623-d6lsk', 'A “weeping” jelly is due to: *', 'too much acid', 'A "weeping" jelly (syneresis) occurs when the gel network contracts and squeezes out liquid. This is typically caused by too much acid, which hydrolyzes and breaks down the pectin polymer chains, destroying their ability to trap and hold water in a stable gel matrix.

• Too much pectin: Results in an excessively firm, tough, or rubbery jelly, not weeping.
• Too much sugar: Can cause sugar crystallization (graininess), but does not directly cause liquid syneresis weeping.
• Improper storage: While extreme temperature fluctuations can stress a gel, chemical acid imbalance is the primary structural cause of jelly weeping.', '["too much acid","too much pectin","too much sugar","improper storage"]'::jsonb, '["Food Service Systems"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-10T17:11:41.710Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-11-09T17:11:41.710Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.929Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-09T17:11:41.710Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758687486241-73', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783609236208-1790433142623-d6lsk', 'Type of cream that is thick and viscous, contains 36-40% milk fat, and used usually in fruit salads.', 'heavy cream', 'Heavy cream (also known as heavy whipping cream) is thick, highly viscous, and contains between 36% to 40% milk fat. Because of its high fat content, it whips into stable peaks easily and is traditionally used to enrich desserts, sauces, and fruit salads.

Light whipping cream: Contains less fat (30-35%) and is lighter in viscosity.

Medium cream: An intermediate category (approx. 30-38%) rarely utilized in standard commercial nomenclature compared to heavy cream.

Whipped cream: The finished, aerated, whipped state of cream, not the liquid heavy cream itself prior to whipping.', '["light whipping cream","medium cream","heavy cream","whipped cream"]'::jsonb, '["Food Service Systems"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-10T17:23:01.579Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.3,"repetitions":0}'::jsonb, '"2026-11-09T17:23:01.579Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.929Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-09T17:23:01.579Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758687502375-74', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783609236208-1790433142623-d6lsk', 'A frozen milk product with the lowest milk fat content. *', 'sherbet', 'Sherbet is a frozen fruit-based dairy dessert that contains milk, cream, or buttermilk, but its milk fat content is strictly regulated to a very low level of between 1% to 2%, making it much lower in fat than ice cream or gelato.

Ice cream: Must contain a minimum of 10% milk fat by legal standards.

Gelato: Typically contains lower fat than American ice cream (around 4-9%), but still significantly higher in milk fat than sherbet.', '["ice cream","sherbet","gelato","none of the above"]'::jsonb, '["Food Service Systems"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 1, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-09-26T14:32:12.431Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":0}'::jsonb, NULL, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.929Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-08T14:02:42.507Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758687512456-75', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783609236208-1790433142623-d6lsk', 'Local term for annatto: *', 'atsuwete', 'In traditional Philippine cuisine and culinary terminology, atsuwete (also spelled achuete) is the local Tagalog term for annatto seeds, which are widely utilized to impart a characteristic yellow-orange color and earthy, slightly peppery flavor to dishes like pancit malabon, chicken inasal, and kilawin.

Anise: Known locally as anise or anisado, these are aromatic licorice-flavored seeds, completely unrelated to annatto.

Angkak: This is red yeast rice, a traditional coloring and fermenting agent made by culturing rice with Monascus purpureus mold, producing a vibrant purplish-red hue.

Aseytuna: Derived from the Spanish word for olives (aceituna), referring to cured olives used in local savory dishes like menudo and relleno.', '["anise","atsuwete","angkak","aseytuna"]'::jsonb, '["Food Service Systems"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 1, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-09-26T14:32:12.431Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":0}'::jsonb, NULL, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.929Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-08T14:03:47.420Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758687525289-76', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783609236208-1790433142623-d6lsk', 'A basic sauce composed of egg yolk, butter, lemon juice or vinegar, and seasonings: *', 'hollandaise', 'Hollandaise is one of the classic French "mother sauces," formulated as a warm, rich emulsion composed of whipped egg yolks, clarified butter, a splash of lemon juice or vinegar for acidity, and seasonings like cayenne pepper.

Allemande: A classical mother-sauce derivative made by fortifying a velouté sauce with heavy cream, egg yolks, and mushroom trimmings.

Béchamel: A classic white mother sauce made by thickening milk with a white roux (butter and flour).

Veloute: A white mother sauce made by thickening white stock (veal, chicken, or fish) with a blond roux.', '["allemande","hollandaise","béchamel","veloute"]'::jsonb, '["Food Service Systems"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 1, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-09-26T14:32:12.431Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":0}'::jsonb, NULL, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.929Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-08T14:03:52.044Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758687533607-77', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783609236208-1790433142623-d6lsk', 'The process of removing fat crystals that form at low temperature so that oil remains clear at refrigerated temperature is called _____. *', 'winterizing', 'Winterizing is an industrial oil processing step where edible oils are chilled to low temperatures so that high-melting-point saturated fats and triglycerides crystallize and precipitate out. These crystals are then filtered out, ensuring that the bottled vegetable oil remains crystal clear and does not cloud up when stored in household refrigerators.

Refining: The broader chemical process of neutralizing, bleaching, and deodorizing crude plant oils to remove impurities and free fatty acids.

Rendering: The high-heat process of melting and extracting animal fats (such as lard or tallow) from raw adipose tissue.

Tenderizing: The physical or enzymatic process of breaking down tough meat muscle fibers and collagen to make them easier to chew.', '["refining","rendering","winterizing","tenderizing"]'::jsonb, '["Food Service Systems"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 1, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-09-26T14:32:12.431Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":0}'::jsonb, NULL, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.929Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-08T14:03:57.683Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758687546471-78', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783609236208-1790433142623-d6lsk', 'The coagulation of milk in acid is: *', 'curdling', 'Curdling occurs when the stable colloidal suspension of milk proteins (casein) is disrupted by the introduction of acid (like lemon juice or vinegar) or bacterial lactic acid, causing the milk proteins to clump together and separate into solid curds and liquid whey.

Sterilization: An extreme heat process designed to destroy all microorganisms and bacterial spores in milk to ensure shelf stability.

Pasteurization: A moderate heat treatment designed to destroy pathogenic vegetative bacteria and extend refrigerated shelf life without altering milk structure.

Toning: A standardization process involving the blending of buffalo milk, skim milk, and water to achieve a specific target fat and solids-not-fat percentage.', '["sterilization","pasteurization","curdling","toning"]'::jsonb, '["Food Service Systems"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-10T17:16:45.855Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-11-09T17:16:45.855Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.929Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-09T17:16:45.855Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758687555787-79', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783609236208-1790433142623-d6lsk', 'The boiling point of water is lowered when: *', 'cooking at high altitudes', 'The boiling point of a liquid is the temperature at which its vapor pressure equals the surrounding atmospheric pressure. Cooking at high altitudes (such as in mountain regions) exposes water to significantly lower atmospheric pressure, meaning less heat energy is required for vapor bubbles to escape, causing water to boil at a lower temperature (e.g., below 100°C).

Pressure cooking: Increases internal pressure artificially, which raises the boiling point of water and cooks food much faster.

Cooking at sea level: Standard atmospheric pressure (1 atm) applies here, where water boils strictly at its standard 100°C (212°F).

Cooking in a submarine: Submarine interiors are pressurized or maintained under controlled atmospheric conditions, preventing the ambient pressure drops seen in high-altitude mountain elevations.', '["pressure cooking","cooking at sea level","cooking in a submarine","cooking at high altitudes"]'::jsonb, '["Food Service Systems"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 1, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-09-26T14:32:12.431Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":0}'::jsonb, NULL, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.929Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-08T14:04:15.094Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758687564354-80', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783609236208-1790433142623-d6lsk', 'It is a crude form of sugar made by evaporating the sugar cane extract into a brown syrup concentrate. *', 'panutsa', 'Panutsa (also known as panocha or cucacha) is a traditional, unrefined crude form of sugar made in the Philippines by boiling down sugarcane extract into a thick brown syrup concentrate and molding it into solid, hardened halves of coconut shells or disks.

Brown sugar: A commercial refined or semi-refined sucrose product coated with measured amounts of cane molasses, rather than an unrefined cane extract syrup block.

Washed sugar: A light-colored centrifugal cane sugar that has been partially washed during processing to remove surface molasses.

Raw brown sugar: Refers generally to minimally processed crystal sugars, distinct from the solidified molded syrup blocks of panutsa.', '["brown sugar","washed sugar","raw brown sugar","panutsa"]'::jsonb, '["Food Service Systems"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 1, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-09-26T14:32:12.431Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":0}'::jsonb, NULL, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.929Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-08T14:04:21.067Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758687574669-81', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783609236208-1790433142623-d6lsk', 'It makes up 80 – 85% of the total protein fraction of wheat flour: *', 'gluten', 'Gluten is the primary composite protein network formed when the two main storage proteins found in wheat flour—gliadin and glutenin—are hydrated and kneaded together, making up roughly 80% to 85% of the total protein content in wheat flour and providing elasticity and structure to dough.

Globulin: A class of soluble functional proteins found in small amounts in wheat flour, but not the primary structural dough-forming fraction.

Albumin: A water-soluble protein fraction present in wheat flour in minor amounts, functioning more like egg albumins than structural gluten.

Gliadin: One of the two monomeric proteins that make up gluten (responsible for extensibility), but gliadin alone does not constitute the entire 80–85% protein fraction without glutenin.', '["globulin","albumin","gliadin","gluten"]'::jsonb, '["Food Service Systems"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-10T17:36:57.843Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.3,"repetitions":0}'::jsonb, '"2026-11-09T17:36:57.843Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.929Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-09T17:36:57.843Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758687586554-82', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783609236208-1790433142623-d6lsk', 'A flour mixture that is sticky: *', 'soft dough', 'A soft dough sits midway between stiff doughs and drop batters; it has just enough flour to be handled and shaped with minimal sticking, but retains a sticky, pliable texture (characteristic of yeast bread and dinner roll doughs).

Stiff dough: Contains a very high flour-to-liquid ratio, making it firm, non-sticky, and easily rolled out (such as pie crust or pasta dough).

Pour batter: A thin, free-flowing batter with a high liquid ratio that can be poured directly from a pitcher or bowl (like pancake batter).

Drop batter: A thick, viscous batter that holds a mounded shape and must be scraped or dropped from a spoon into baking pans (like muffin batter).', '["stiff dough","soft dough","pour batter","drop batter"]'::jsonb, '["Food Service Systems"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 1, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-09-26T14:32:12.431Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":0}'::jsonb, NULL, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.929Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-08T14:04:34.645Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758687594751-83', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783609236208-1790433142623-d6lsk', 'A local alcoholic beverage made from coconut sap: *', 'tuba', 'Tuba is a traditional Filipino alcoholic beverage harvested as fresh sap (toddy) tapped directly from the unopened flower clusters of coconut palms (Cocos nucifera), which is then consumed fresh or naturally fermented.

Basi: A traditional fermented alcoholic beverage made from boiled sugarcane juice produced in the Ilocos region.

Lambanog: A potent, clear distilled spirit (arrack) produced by distilling fermented coconut sap (tuba).

Gin: A globally recognized commercial distilled spirit flavored with juniper berries and botanical extracts.', '["basi","lambanog","gin","tuba"]'::jsonb, '["Food Service Systems"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 1, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-09-26T14:32:12.431Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":0}'::jsonb, NULL, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.929Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-08T14:04:41.200Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758687604287-84', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783609236208-1790433142623-d6lsk', 'It contains 50% cocoa butter. *', 'bitter chocolate', 'Bitter chocolate (also known as unsweetened baking chocolate) is pure chocolate liquor consisting strictly of ground cocoa nibs containing roughly 50% cocoa butter and 50% solid cocoa mass, with zero added sugar.

Sweet cocoa: Contains added sugar and reduced cocoa butter percentages.

Breakfast cocoa: A cocoa powder that has been alkalized (Dutch-processed) and contains a lower percentage of remaining cocoa butter (typically 10–22%).

Powdered cocoa: Dry defatted cocoa solids milled into a fine powder with minimal residual fat content.', '["bitter chocolate","sweet cocoa","breakfast cocoa","powdered cocoa"]'::jsonb, '["Food Service Systems"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-10T17:16:24.410Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-11-09T17:16:24.410Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.929Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-09T17:16:24.410Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758687614452-85', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783609236208-1790433142623-d6lsk', 'Rancidity of fat is related to: *', 'degree of saturation', 'The susceptibility of fats and oils to oxidative rancidity is directly related to their chemical degree of saturation. Fats with high levels of polyunsaturated fatty acids contain multiple double bonds in their carbon chains, which are highly reactive and vulnerable to attack by atmospheric oxygen, causing rapid rancidity. In contrast, highly saturated fats have single bonds and are much more stable against oxidation.', '["absence of amino acid","degree of saturation","absence of vitamin A","presence of iron"]'::jsonb, '["Food Service Systems"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 1, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-09-26T14:32:12.431Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":0}'::jsonb, NULL, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.929Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-08T14:06:16.643Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758687623485-86', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783609236208-1790433142623-d6lsk', 'It is used in candy making to produce invert sugar: *', 'cream of tartar', 'In confectionery science, cream of tartar (potassium bitartrate) is a weak acid added to sucrose syrups during cooking. It hydrolyzes (splits) the double-sugar sucrose molecules into equal parts of glucose and fructose, a chemical process known as creating invert sugar. This invert sugar prevents unwanted graininess or large crystal formation in fondants, caramels, and syrups.

Sodium bicarbonate: This is an alkaline salt (baking soda) used for leavening and neutralizing acids, not for creating invert sugar.

Lactase: This is an enzyme that breaks down milk sugar (lactose) into glucose and galactose, unrelated to table sugar inversion.

Lipase: This is a digestive enzyme that breaks down dietary fats (lipids) into glycerol and fatty acids.', '["sodium bicarbonate","cream of tartar","lactase","lipasen"]'::jsonb, '["Food Service Systems"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-10T17:37:07.652Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.3,"repetitions":0}'::jsonb, '"2026-11-09T17:37:07.652Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.929Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-09T17:37:07.652Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758687631887-87', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783609236208-1790433142623-d6lsk', 'A method of preparing coffee that favors optimum infusion of volatile oil: *', 'percolator method', 'The percolator method involves continuously cycling boiling water up through a central tube and down over a basket of coffee grounds. This continuous extraction process at high heat favors the deep, aggressive extraction and infusion of volatile coffee oils and aromatic compounds, resulting in a robust, full-bodied brew.

Boiling: Direct boiling extracts harsh bitter compounds and over-extracts tannins, boiling off volatile aromatics rather than favoring optimum infusion.

Drip method: Drip filtration relies on gravity running water through grounds once, which produces a clean cup but extracts fewer heavy volatile oils compared to active percolation.

Steeping: Steeping (like a French press) allows total immersion extraction, but percolator cycling specifically optimizes continuous thermal oil extraction.', '["percolator method","boiling","drip method","steeping"]'::jsonb, '["Food Service Systems"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-10T17:22:43.610Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.3,"repetitions":0}'::jsonb, '"2026-11-09T17:22:43.610Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.929Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-09T17:22:43.610Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758687639617-88', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783609236208-1790433142623-d6lsk', 'The effect of adding sugar to egg white foam: *', 'it delays foam formation', 'Sugar is highly hygroscopic (water-loving) and competes with the egg proteins for available water molecules. When sugar is added too early to an egg white foam, it coats the protein strands and significantly delays foam formation, requiring much longer whipping times to incorporate air. (However, once the foam is formed, adding sugar slowly stabilizes it by increasing viscosity and preventing moisture loss).

It hastens denaturation of egg protein: Sugar actually protects egg proteins from rapid thermal or mechanical denaturation rather than hastening it.

It hastens foam formation: This is incorrect because sugar''s water-binding property slows down the initial mechanical incorporation of air.

It does not affect foam formation: Sugar profoundly alters whipping time and foam stability, making this option false.', '["it hastens denaturation of egg protein","it hastens foam formation","it delays foam formation","it does not affect foam formation"]'::jsonb, '["Food Service Systems"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 1, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-09-26T14:32:12.431Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":0}'::jsonb, NULL, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.929Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-08T13:51:51.055Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758687648250-89', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783609236208-1790433142623-d6lsk', 'A function of sugar which keeps a food product moist and soft. *', 'Sugar as a humectant.', 'In food science, a humectant is a substance that attracts, binds, and retains moisture from the surrounding environment. Because sugar molecules have a strong affinity for water, incorporating sugar into baked goods, icings, or candies helps retain internal moisture, keeping the product soft, fresh, and moist over an extended shelf life.

Sugar as a texture modifier: While sugar affects texture, the specific physicochemical property of holding onto water and preventing dryness is defined as a humectant function.

Sugar as a volume and structure improver: While sugar aids aeration during creaming, moisture retention is strictly classified under humectant action.', '["Sugar as a texture modifier.","Sugar as a humectant.","Sugar as a volume and structure improver.","none of the above"]'::jsonb, '["Food Service Systems"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 1, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-09-26T14:32:12.431Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":0}'::jsonb, NULL, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.929Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-08T13:51:57.780Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758687656601-90', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783609236208-1790433142623-d6lsk', 'Examples of Bicol dishes except: *', 'kilawen', 'Kilawen (or kilawin) is a popular traditional appetizer dish associated primarily with the Ilocos region (Northern Luzon), where raw meat or fish is cured and dressed with calamansi juice, vinegar, onions, and chili peppers. Therefore, it is the correct exception when listing regional dishes from the Bicol region.

Bagisara: A traditional Bicolano vegetable and local ingredient preparation typical of the region''s cuisine.

Kandinga: A well-known Bicolano spicy offal dish (similar to dinuguan style preparation without the blood), deeply rooted in regional Bicol cuisine.

Kinunot: A famous Bicol specialty dish made from shark or stingray meat cooked in rich coconut milk (gata) with malunggay leaves and chili.', '["bagisara","kandinga","kinunot","kilawen"]'::jsonb, '["Food Service Systems"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-10T17:16:03.927Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-11-09T17:16:03.927Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.929Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-09T17:16:03.927Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758687663749-91', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783609236208-1790433142623-d6lsk', 'Cocoa is chocolate with less: *', 'fat', 'Cocoa powder is produced by pressing the fat (cocoa butter) out of roasted chocolate liquor. Therefore, cocoa is chocolate with significantly less fat remaining in the dry solid residue.

Water: Chocolate and cocoa products contain virtually zero natural water content.

Protein: Cocoa solids retain their natural protein fractions; the defining difference is the extraction of fat (cocoa butter).

Sugar: Unsweetened cocoa powder contains zero sugar; sweet cocoa mixes have sugar added, making fat the defining compositional difference from whole chocolate liquor.', '["water","fat","protein","sugar"]'::jsonb, '["Food Service Systems"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 1, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-09-26T14:32:12.431Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":0}'::jsonb, NULL, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.929Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-08T14:25:23.890Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758687671798-92', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783609236208-1790433142623-d6lsk', 'Examples of functional foods except: *', 'mango', 'In nutrition science, functional foods are foods that offer specific physiological benefits and help reduce the risk of chronic diseases beyond their basic nutritional value, primarily due to the presence of potent bioactive compounds or phytochemicals. While mango is a highly nutritious fruit rich in Vitamin C and beta-carotene, it is generally classified as a conventional, basic dietary food rather than a specialized functional or therapeutic food.

• Garlic: Contains the bioactive organosulfur compound allicin, which is heavily studied for its antimicrobial, antihypertensive, and cardiovascular-protective properties.
• Malunggay: Widely recognized as a functional food, its leaves contain specific phytochemicals like chlorogenic acid, quercetin, and isothiocyanates that provide potent anti-inflammatory and tissue-protective effects.
• Ampalaya leaves: Contains specific bioactive components like charantin and polypeptide-p (plant insulin), which are utilized for their targeted functional ability to lower and regulate blood glucose levels.', '["malunggay","mango","ampalaya leaves","garlic"]'::jsonb, '["Food Service Systems"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-12T17:11:49.796Z","interval":3,"easeFactor":2.3,"repetitions":2}'::jsonb, '"2026-11-09T17:11:49.796Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.929Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-09T17:11:49.796Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758687677748-93', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783609236208-1790433142623-d6lsk', 'Local term for celery is: *', 'kintsay', 'In traditional Philippine culinary and market terminology, kintsay is the local Tagalog term for Chinese celery (or aromatic leaf celery), which is widely used to flavor soups, pancit, and stews.

Letsugas: The local term for lettuce.

Saluyot: Jute leaves used in traditional Ilocano vegetable dishes.

Petsay: The local term for Chinese cabbage (bok choy).', '["letsugas","kintsay","saluyot","petsay"]'::jsonb, '["Food Service Systems"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-10T17:31:09.924Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-11-09T17:31:09.924Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.929Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-09T17:31:09.924Z"'::jsonb)
