('anki-card-1757929858951-81', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783609425474-1790433136742-1d3n3', 'Food is Medicine', 'Korean', '', '["Korean","Indonesian","Japanese","Chinese"]'::jsonb, '["Food Service Systems"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-09T05:02:21.439Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.3,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-11-08T05:02:21.439Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.198Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-08T05:02:21.439Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1757929873799-82', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783609425474-1790433136742-1d3n3', 'Savory, hot, & Spicy', 'Indonesian', '', '["Korean","Indonesian","Japanese","Chinese"]'::jsonb, '["Food Service Systems"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-09T01:17:56.373Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-11-08T01:17:56.373Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.198Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-08T01:17:56.373Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1757929896831-83', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783609425474-1790433136742-1d3n3', 'Fresh herbs & spices including lemongrass & basil', 'Thai', 'Thai cuisine is world-renowned for its vibrant, highly aromatic flavor profiles built on a foundation of fresh herbs and spices rather than dried ones. Lemongrass and fresh basil (specifically Thai sweet basil and holy basil) are hallmark ingredients that give iconic dishes like Tom Yum soup, green curries, and Pad Kra Pao (basil stir-fry) their distinct, bright flavors.

While Indonesian (B) cuisine also uses lemongrass, it is better known for its complex, pounded wet spice pastes (bumbu) and sweet soy sauce (kecap manis). Japanese (C) relies on subtle umami flavors like dashi, soy sauce, and miso rather than strong fresh herbs. Korean (D) cuisine is heavily defined by fermented pastes (like gochujang and doenjang), garlic, sesame oil, and chili flakes (gochugaru).', '["Korean","Indonesian","Japanese","Thai"]'::jsonb, '["Food Service Systems"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-09T05:19:38.007Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.3,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-11-08T05:19:38.007Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.198Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-08T05:19:38.007Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1757929910600-84', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783609425474-1790433136742-1d3n3', 'Coconut and chilies are common ingredients', 'Bicol Region', 'In Philippine regional cuisine, the Bicol Region is famous for its signature flavor profile that heavily combines coconut milk (gata) and fiery chilies (sili). This distinct culinary pairing is the foundation of iconic Bicolano dishes such as Bicol Express (pork cooked in coconut milk and shrimp paste with abundant chilies) and Laing (taro leaves simmered in coconut milk and chilies).

• Southern Tagalog: While provinces in this region (like Quezon and Laguna) also prominently use coconut milk in their cooking, their dishes do not typically feature the intense, defining heat of chilies associated with Bicol.
• Central Luzon: Known as the culinary capital of the Philippines (particularly Pampanga), this region''s flavor profile leans toward savory, salty, and sweet, with a heavy emphasis on pork (e.g., sisig, tocino). Coconut milk and chilies are not their primary culinary markers.
• Ilocos Region: Ilocano cuisine is characterized by the use of fermented fish sauce/paste (bagoong isda), local vegetables (as seen in pinakbet), and deep-fried pork (bagnet). They rarely use coconut milk in their savory dishes.', '["Southern Tagalog","Central Luzon","Bicol Region","Ilocos Region"]'::jsonb, '["Food Service Systems"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-09T01:28:40.232Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-11-08T01:28:40.232Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.198Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-08T11:42:03.647Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1757929923414-85', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783609425474-1790433136742-1d3n3', 'Salty foods like patis and bagoong are common in family dining table because the people here believed that since they perspire a lot they need to replace the lost electrolytes.', 'Ilocos Region', 'In the study of Philippine regional food culture, the heavy reliance on salty condiments like bagoong isda (fermented fish paste) and patis (fish sauce) in the Ilocos Region is directly attributed to its geography and climate.

The Ilocos Region, located in northwestern Luzon, is characterized by a harsh, hot, and dry climate, coupled with rugged terrain that demands strenuous physical labor (primarily farming and fishing). Because the locals perspire heavily in this environment, consuming high-sodium foods like bagoong is a traditional, practical way to replenish lost salt and electrolytes. Additionally, salting and fermenting were historically essential preservation methods in this hot climate, embedding these extremely salty flavor profiles deep into Ilocano staples like pinakbet and dinengdeng.

• Southern Tagalog and Central Luzon: While both regions use patis and bagoong, their climates are generally more forgiving, and their culinary identities are not fundamentally defined by the need for extreme salt intake to offset harsh labor and heat in the same way Ilocano cuisine is.
• Bicol Region: As established previously, Bicolano cuisine is defined by the combination of coconut milk and chilies, not a primary reliance on heavily salted fermented fish for electrolyte replacement.', '["Southern Tagalog","Central Luzon","Bicol Region","Ilocos Region"]'::jsonb, '["Food Service Systems"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-09T01:14:10.269Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-11-08T01:14:10.269Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.198Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-08T11:43:55.806Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1757929940080-86', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783609425474-1790433136742-1d3n3', 'Dishes or delicacies made with banana is common in this region.', 'Southern Tagalog', 'The Southern Tagalog region is historically renowned for its vast agricultural plantations of coconuts and bananas (specifically the saba variety). Because of this abundant local supply, the region''s culinary tradition relies heavily on bananas to create a wide array of classic delicacies and everyday snacks. Favorites like nilupak na saging (pounded banana with grated coconut and sugar), minatamis na saging (sweetened bananas in syrup), turon, and banana cue are deeply rooted in this region''s food culture.

The Bicol Region (A) is best known for its heavy use of coconut milk (gata) and chili peppers (sili), alongside pili nut delicacies. The Ilocos Region (C) is famous for vegetable dishes flavored with bagoong (like pinakbet) and deep-fried pork (bagnet). Central Luzon (D) is recognized as the "Rice Granary of the Philippines," with Pampanga famed for savory meat dishes and Bulacan known for milk-based sweets (like pastillas), rather than banana-based delicacies.', '["Southern Tagalog","Central Luzon","Bicol Region","Ilocos Region"]'::jsonb, '["Food Service Systems"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-09T04:35:20.662Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.3,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-11-08T04:35:20.662Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.198Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-08T04:35:20.662Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1757929954779-87', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783609425474-1790433136742-1d3n3', 'Exotic dishes are found in some provinces of this region', 'Central Luzon', 'In Philippine culinary studies, Central Luzon—particularly the province of Pampanga, known as the Culinary Capital of the Philippines—is widely celebrated for its "exotic" and adventurous dishes. The province has a long history of utilizing unique local ingredients found in its rice fields.

Classic textbook examples of these exotic dishes include Kamaru (mole crickets, often cooked adobo-style or deep-fried) and Betute Tugak (farm frogs stuffed with minced pork and deep-fried).

• Southern Tagalog: Known for hearty meat soups (like Bulalo from Batangas) and fish preservation methods (sinaing na tulingan), rather than heavily featuring insects or amphibians.
• Bicol Region: Defined by its heavy use of coconut milk and chilies (Bicol Express, Laing).
• Ilocos Region: Characterized by salty, fermented flavors (bagoong), indigenous vegetables (pinakbet), and deep-fried pork (bagnet). While they do have unique dishes like jumping salad (live small shrimp), Central Luzon is the definitive textbook answer for exotic insect and amphibian delicacies.', '["Southern Tagalog","Central Luzon","Bicol Region","Ilocos Region"]'::jsonb, '["Food Service Systems"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-09T01:27:49.697Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-11-08T01:27:49.697Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.198Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-08T11:58:00.482Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1757929967928-88', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783609425474-1790433136742-1d3n3', 'Considered the rice bowl of the Philippines', 'Central Luzon', 'Central Luzon (Region III) contains the largest continuous plain in the country. Because of its vast, flat agricultural lands and massive rice production, it is universally recognized as the "Rice Bowl" or "Rice Granary of the Philippines."

Bicol Region (A) is famous for abaca, coconut, and pili nuts. Ilocos Region (B) is known for tobacco and garlic. Southern Tagalog (D) is a major producer of coconuts and various fruits, but does not match Central Luzon''s massive rice output.', '["Southern Tagalog","Central Luzon","Bicol Region","Ilocos Region"]'::jsonb, '["Food Service Systems"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-09T01:40:48.827Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.3,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-11-08T01:40:48.827Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.198Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-08T01:40:48.827Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1757929979928-89', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783609425474-1790433136742-1d3n3', 'Galangal, kafir, tamarind paste', 'Thai', 'Galangal (a citrusy/piney rhizome similar to ginger), Kaffir lime leaves (which provide a distinct aromatic citrus scent), and Tamarind paste (for sourness) are the foundational flavor trio of Thai cuisine. They form the signature flavor profile of famous Thai dishes like Tom Yum soup and various Thai curries.

Japanese (A) relies on soy sauce, dashi, mirin, and miso. French (B) relies on butter, wine, cream, and herbs like thyme or tarragon. Mediterranean (C) relies on olive oil, garlic, lemon, oregano, and basil.', '["French","Japanese","Thai","Mediterranean"]'::jsonb, '["Food Service Systems"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-09T05:28:29.749Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.1,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-11-08T05:28:29.749Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.198Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-08T05:28:29.749Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1757929993727-90', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783609425474-1790433136742-1d3n3', 'Olives, olive oil, tomatoes, feta cheese and spices', 'Mediterranean', 'The Mediterranean cuisine (specifically the food of Greece, Southern Italy, and the Levant) is defined by its heavy reliance on olive oil as the primary cooking fat, abundant use of fresh tomatoes, cured olives (like Kalamata), and regional dairy like sheep/goat''s milk feta cheese.

Thai (A) relies on coconut milk, fish sauce, lemongrass, and galangal. Japanese (C) relies on soy sauce, dashi, mirin, and miso. French (D) relies heavily on butter, heavy cream, wine, and aromatics like shallots and tarragon.', '["French","Japanese","Thai","Mediterranean"]'::jsonb, '["Food Service Systems"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-09T05:10:07.952Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.3,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-11-08T05:10:07.952Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.198Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-08T05:10:07.952Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1757930008710-91', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783609425474-1790433136742-1d3n3', 'Soy sauce, rice wine and rice vinegar', 'Japanese', 'In the study of world cuisines, the combination of soy sauce (shoyu), rice wine (sake and sweet mirin), and rice vinegar is the foundational flavor triad of Japanese cooking. These three ingredients are essential for creating the delicate, umami-rich, and perfectly balanced flavor profiles characteristic of Japanese dishes. For example, they serve as the base components for authentic teriyaki sauce, ponzu, and the seasoning for sushi rice.

• French: Traditional French cuisine relies heavily on grape wines, butter, cream, stocks, and wine vinegars (like white wine or champagne vinegar), not rice-based products or soy sauce.
• Thai: While Thai food occasionally incorporates soy sauce, its core defining flavor profile relies much more heavily on fish sauce (nam pla) for saltiness/umami, fresh lime juice or tamarind for acidity, and palm sugar for sweetness.
• Mediterranean: The Mediterranean flavor profile is fundamentally built around olive oil, lemon juice, garlic, and grape wines or grape-derived vinegars (such as balsamic or red wine vinegar).', '["French","Japanese","Thai","Mediterranean"]'::jsonb, '["Food Service Systems"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-09T01:05:15.682Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-11-08T01:05:15.682Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.198Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-08T11:45:23.698Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1757930028376-92', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783609425474-1790433136742-1d3n3', 'Fish sauce, lime, cilantro', 'Vietnamese', '', '["French","Japanese","Thai","Vietnamese"]'::jsonb, '["Food Service Systems"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-09T01:13:05.749Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-11-08T01:13:05.749Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.198Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-08T01:13:05.749Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1757930047007-93', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783609425474-1790433136742-1d3n3', 'Wine, cheese, butter', 'French', 'In classical culinary arts, the trio of wine, cheese, and butter is the fundamental hallmark of French cuisine.

French cooking techniques rely heavily on butter as the primary cooking fat and the essential base for classic sauces (such as building a roux or emulsifying a beurre blanc). Furthermore, France is globally renowned for its extensive viticulture and artisanal cheese production, both of which are deeply integrated into their daily meals, classic recipes (like Coq au Vin and Boeuf Bourguignon), and formal dining etiquette (which often includes a dedicated cheese course).

• Japanese and Thai: Traditional East and Southeast Asian cuisines historically do not feature dairy products like cheese and butter, relying instead on soy, fish sauce, and plant or seed oils.
• Mediterranean: While the Mediterranean diet prominently features wine and specific regional cheeses (like feta or pecorino), its defining, heart-healthy primary dietary fat is olive oil, not butter.', '["French","Japanese","Thai","Mediterranean"]'::jsonb, '["Food Service Systems"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-09T01:13:25.720Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-11-08T01:13:25.720Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.198Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-08T11:47:07.930Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1757930065108-94', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783609425474-1790433136742-1d3n3', 'Sauteed garlic, onions, tomatoes, fried fish, beaten egg', 'Cardillo', 'Cardillo (or Cardilyong Isda) is a traditional Filipino dish specifically defined by this exact combination of ingredients. It involves taking leftover or freshly fried fish (usually tilapia, dalag, or bangus) and simmering it in a savory base of sautéed garlic, onions, and tomatoes. The defining step of the dish is pouring beaten eggs into the simmering broth just before turning off the heat, which thickens the sauce and gives it a distinct, rich texture.

• Caldereta: A heavy meat stew (usually beef or goat) cooked in tomato sauce with liver spread, bell peppers, carrots, and potatoes.
• Pochero: A meat stew (beef, pork, or chicken) characterized by the inclusion of saba (plantain bananas), pechay, string beans, and sometimes chorizo, boiled in a savory broth.
• Menudo: A diced pork and pork liver stew cooked in tomato sauce with diced carrots, potatoes, and raisins.', '["Caldereta","Pochero","Menudo","Cardillo"]'::jsonb, '["Food Service Systems"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-09T01:06:48.270Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-11-08T01:06:48.270Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.198Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-08T11:49:57.282Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1757930078442-95', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783609425474-1790433136742-1d3n3', 'Beef, potatoes, carrots, bell peppers, liver spread, olives', 'Caldereta', 'In Philippine cuisine, liver spread is the defining culinary marker for Caldereta (Kaldereta).

Originally adapted from the Spanish beef stew, Caldereta is traditionally made with beef or goat (kambing). The liver spread is mashed into the tomato-based broth to act as both a thickener and a rich, savory flavor enhancer. The addition of olives, bell peppers, carrots, and potatoes completes this classic, hearty stew.

• Pochero: This is a clear broth-based stew (though sometimes using a light tomato base) characterized by the sweetness of saba (plantain bananas) and the inclusion of leafy greens like pechay or cabbage, garbanzos, and often chorizo. It does not use liver spread.
• Menudo: While Menudo also contains carrots, potatoes, and bell peppers in a tomato sauce, it is traditionally made with diced pork and small cubes of real pork liver (not liver spread), and it almost always includes raisins and sliced hotdogs.
• Cardillo: As covered previously, this is a dish of fried fish simmered with tomatoes and beaten eggs.', '["Caldereta","Pochero","Menudo","Cardillo"]'::jsonb, '["Food Service Systems"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-09T01:15:23.357Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-11-08T01:15:23.357Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.198Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-08T11:51:17.651Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1757930093074-96', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783609425474-1790433136742-1d3n3', 'Beef, carrots, potatoes, cabbage, garbanzos, kidney beans', 'Pochero', 'Pochero is a classic, hearty Filipino boiled stew (adapted from the Spanish cocido) that features beef (or pork), root crops like potatoes and carrots, leafy greens like cabbage or pechay, and legumes like garbanzos (chickpeas) and kidney beans. It is also famously known for including saba (plantain bananas).

Menudo (A) is a tomato-based stew traditionally made with diced pork and pork liver, not beef and cabbage. Cardillo (B) is a dish of fried fish simmered in a tomato and beaten egg sauce. Caldereta (C) is a heavy beef or goat stew cooked with tomato sauce and liver spread; while it has potatoes and carrots, it does not contain cabbage or kidney beans.', '["Caldereta","Pochero","Menudo","Cardillo"]'::jsonb, '["Food Service Systems"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-09T05:28:25.351Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.1,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-11-08T05:28:25.351Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.198Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-08T05:28:25.351Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1757930105589-97', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783609425474-1790433136742-1d3n3', 'Pork, carrots, potatoes, bell peppers, tomato sauce, green peas', 'Menudo', 'In Philippine culinary classification, Menudo is the classic tomato-based stew traditionally made with pork. The vegetables (carrots, potatoes, and bell peppers) are characteristically diced into small cubes to match the size of the meat. Green peas (along with raisins, sliced hotdogs, and cubed pork liver, though not explicitly listed in the prompt) are standard additions that complete the classic Menudo profile.

• Caldereta: As discussed previously, Caldereta is typically made with beef or goat (not pork) and is distinctly defined by the addition of liver spread to thicken the tomato sauce, often featuring olives and cheese.
• Pochero: This stew relies on the sweetness of saba (plantain bananas) and includes leafy greens (pechay/cabbage) and typically garbanzos (chickpeas) rather than green peas, usually served in a clear broth or very light tomato base.
• Cardillo: This is a dish consisting of fried fish simmered with tomatoes, onions, and beaten eggs, containing no pork, tomato sauce, or root vegetables.', '["Caldereta","Pochero","Menudo","Cardillo"]'::jsonb, '["Food Service Systems"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-09T01:23:35.250Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-11-08T01:23:35.250Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.198Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-08T11:52:21.913Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1757930123371-98', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783609425474-1790433136742-1d3n3', 'Sauteed garlic, onions, tomatoes, fried fish', 'Sarciado', 'Sarciado (specifically Sarciadong Isda) translates to "cooked with a thick sauce" (derived from the Spanish word salsa or the Tagalog sarsa). The dish is classically prepared by taking fried fish and topping it with a chunky, savory sauce made purely from sautéing garlic, onions, and an abundance of ripe tomatoes until they break down.

• Caldereta: A heavy beef or goat stew with liver spread and vegetables.
• Pochero: A meat stew featuring plantain bananas (saba) and leafy greens.
• Menudo: A diced pork stew with tomato sauce, carrots, and potatoes.', '["Caldereta","Pochero","Menudo","Sarciado"]'::jsonb, '["Food Service Systems"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-09T01:06:06.850Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-11-08T01:06:06.850Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.198Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-08T11:53:21.131Z"'::jsonb),
