('anki-card-1788370256316-52', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783610354046-1790433136740-nh68g', 'Which of the following is NOT a principle of individualized diet therapy?', 'The therapeutic diet should be identical for all patients with the same diagnosis.', 'commitment to excellence', '["The diet should meet the patient''s nutritional requirements.","The diet should consider the patient''s food preferences and cultural practices.","The therapeutic diet should be identical for all patients with the same diagnosis.","The diet should be modified according to the patient''s clinical condition."]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 1, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-09-02T20:00:00.000Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":2}'::jsonb, NULL, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.089Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-08T12:24:43.573Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1788370306504-53', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783610354046-1790433136740-nh68g', 'A controlled diet is best described as a diet modification in which:', 'A nutrient or dietary component is provided in an amount appropriate to the patient’s therapeutic needs', '', '["All nutrients are restricted.","The patient is allowed to eat they modified on their own.","A nutrient or dietary component is provided in an amount appropriate to the patient’s therapeutic needs","Only food texture is modified"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 1, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-09-02T20:00:00.000Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":2}'::jsonb, NULL, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.089Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:12.431Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1788370343591-54', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783610354046-1790433136740-nh68g', 'Which outcome measure would be most appropriate for monitoring the effectiveness of nutrition intervention for a patient with excessive energy intake?', 'Change in body weight or BMI over time', '', '["Serum albumin concentration","Hemoglobin concentration","Change in body weight or BMI over time","Serum bilirubin improvement"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 1, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-09-02T20:00:00.000Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":2}'::jsonb, NULL, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.089Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:12.431Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756781167524-0', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783610367197-1790433140273-gxvad', 'Classification obesity characterized by increase in the number of fat cells:', 'Hyperplastic', '• Hyperplastic Obesity: This is characterized by an increase in the total number of fat cells (adipocytes). It typically develops during childhood and adolescence when body growth occurs, making long-term weight management more challenging because the absolute number of cells does not decrease with weight loss.
• Hypertrophic Obesity: This is characterized by an increase in the size of the existing fat cells. This pattern is more common in adult-onset obesity, where the cells expand to store excess lipids.
• Android & Gynoid: These describe the anatomical distribution of fat rather than cellular characteristics. Android refers to "apple-shaped" abdominal fat distribution (common in males), while Gynoid refers to "pear-shaped" hip and thigh fat distribution (common in females).', '["Hyperplastic","Android","Hypertropic","Gynoid"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-07T08:07:45.065Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-06T08:07:45.065Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.019Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-06T08:07:45.274Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756781245630-1', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783610367197-1790433140273-gxvad', 'Dietary management for hepatitis:', 'High kcal, high protein, high carbohydrate, low fat', 'Medical nutrition therapy (MNT) for acute and chronic hepatitis focuses on supporting liver cell regeneration, preventing tissue catabolism, and easing digestive stress:
• High Kilocalorie (kcal) & High Carbohydrate: The liver''s ability to store glycogen and maintain blood glucose levels is impaired during inflammation. A high-calorie, carbohydrate-rich diet supplies the necessary energy, preserves muscle mass, and keeps glycogen stores replenished.
• High Protein: Protein provides the necessary amino acids needed to heal, repair, and regenerate damaged liver parenchyma. (Note: Protein is only restricted if the patient develops advanced hepatic encephalopathy where the liver can no longer clear ammonia).
• Low Fat: Because the inflamed liver struggles to produce and flow bile normally, fat digestion and metabolism are severely compromised. Restricting dietary fat minimizes symptoms like nausea, vomiting, abdominal pain, and steatorrhea (fatty stools).
• Why Fluids Are Not Restricted: Fluid restriction is not an intervention for hepatitis. It is typically reserved for downstream complications of advanced liver disease, such as ascites or severe fluid retention in end-stage cirrhosis.', '["High kcal, high protein, high carbohydrate, low fat","High kcal, low protein, high carbohydrate, low fat","High kcal, low protein, high carbohydrate, fluid restricted","High kcal, high protein, high carbohydrate, fluid restricted"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-07T09:01:30.154Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-06T09:01:30.154Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.019Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-06T09:01:30.154Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756781314144-2', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783610367197-1790433140273-gxvad', 'A broad term that includes drug-nutrient interactions and the effect of a medication on nutritional status:', 'Food-drug interaction', 'Food-drug interaction', '["Pharmaconutrition","Pharmacodynamics","Pharmacokinetics","Food-drug interaction"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-07T09:15:23.135Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-06T09:15:23.135Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.019Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-08T12:24:43.573Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756781411321-3', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783610367197-1790433140273-gxvad', 'Dietary recommendation for patient with liver cirrhosis:', 'High kcal, high protein, high carbohydrate, low fat', 'Medical nutrition therapy (MNT) for stable liver cirrhosis focus heavily on combatting severe tissue wasting (malnutrition) while protecting remaining hepatic function:

• High Kilocalorie (kcal) & High Carbohydrate: Patients with cirrhosis suffer from profound muscle wasting and rapid depletion of glycogen stores. A high-calorie, carbohydrate-rich diet prevents the breakdown of muscle tissue for energy.
• High Protein: Historically, protein was restricted out of fear it would cause hepatic encephalopathy. Modern clinical guidelines heavily contradict this; cirrhosis patients require an aggressive high-protein intake (typically 1.2–1.5 g/kg/day) to promote cellular repair, maintain lean body mass, and correct hypoalbuminemia. (Protein is only restricted or modified to branched-chain amino acids if the patient exhibits severe, refractory hepatic encephalopathy).
• Low Fat: Fat absorption is frequently compromised due to impaired bile synthesis or secretion. Restricting dietary fat minimizes unpleasant digestive symptoms like steatorrhea, abdominal bloating, and nausea.', '["High kcal, low protein, high carbohydrate, low fat","High kcal, high protein, high carbohydrate, low fat","High kcal, high protein, high carbohydrate, low fiber","High kcal, low protein, high carbohydrate, high fiber"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-07T09:31:09.134Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-06T09:31:09.134Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.019Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-06T09:31:09.134Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756781455168-4', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783610367197-1790433140273-gxvad', 'A classification of infection that last for weeks, months or years.', 'Chronic', '', '["Acute","Chronic","Recurrent","Intermittent"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-07T07:39:13.814Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-06T07:39:13.814Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.019Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-06T07:39:13.814Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756781615612-5', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783610367197-1790433140273-gxvad', 'Amount of calorie to reduce per day to effect weight loss of 1.5 lbs per week:', '750 kcal', '• 500 kcal: This deficit results in a weight loss of 1 lb per week.
• 1,000 kcal: This deficit results in a weight loss of 2 lbs per week.', '["750 kcal","500 kcal","1000 kcal","100 kcal"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-07T07:59:42.775Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-06T07:59:42.775Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.019Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-06T07:59:43.000Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756781666126-6', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783610367197-1790433140273-gxvad', 'Dietary management for fatty liver:', 'High protein, high kcal for underweight', 'Underweight patients with hepatic steatosis or steatohepatitis (such as alcoholic liver disease or advanced cirrhosis with severe muscle wasting) experience accelerated hepatic gluconeogenesis, glycogen depletion, and protein catabolism. A high-protein diet (1.2 to 1.5 g/kg/day) provides essential amino acids required for hepatic export protein synthesis (albumin, clotting factors), decreases skeletal muscle proteolysis, and prevents hepatic encephalopathy by maintaining visceral nitrogen balance. High kilocalories (35 to 40 kcal/kg/day) ensure positive energy balance, shifting metabolism away from endogenous fat mobilization and skeletal muscle breakdown while sparing dietary protein for tissue synthesis rather than oxidation.', '["High protein, high kcal for underweight","Low protein, low kcal for obese","High carbohydrates","IVF"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-07T09:31:36.441Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.3,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-06T09:31:36.441Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.019Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-06T09:31:36.441Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756781713239-7', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783610367197-1790433140273-gxvad', 'Alcohol induced cirrhosis:', 'Laennec''s cirrhosis', 'Laennec''s cirrhosis (historically named after the French physician René Laennec) is the specific medical term traditionally used to classify alcohol-induced liver cirrhosis. It develops over many years of chronic, heavy alcohol exposure, moving through phases of fatty liver infiltration (steatosis) and alcoholic hepatitis before replacing functional liver architecture with fine, uniform fibrous nodules and scar tissue.

	• Biliary cirrhosis stems from the destruction or long-term chronic obstruction of the bile ducts within the liver.
	• Obstructive cirrhosis is a mechanical description of biliary pathway blockages rather than a formal classification for alcohol abuse.
	• Cardiac cirrhosis is triggered by chronic, severe right-sided heart failure causing structural liver congestion.', '["Cardiac cirrhosis","Laennec''s cirrhosis","Biliary cirrhosis","Obstructive cirrhosis"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-07T09:14:14.473Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-06T09:14:14.473Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.019Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-06T09:14:14.687Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756781821922-8', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783610367197-1790433140273-gxvad', 'A food challenge in which neither the patient nor physician knows the identity of the test food component, which is usually disguised as a powder in a food known to be tolerated or in a gelatin capsule.', 'Double-blind, placebo-controlled food challenge (DBPCFC)', 'Double-blind, placebo-controlled food challenge (DBPCFC)', '["Double-blind, placebo-controlled food challenge (DBPCFC)","Single blind food challenge","Open food challenge","Food elimination"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-07T07:40:56.719Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-06T07:40:56.719Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.019Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-08T12:24:43.573Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756781869883-9', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783610367197-1790433140273-gxvad', 'The diameter of a feeding tube is measured in what units?', 'French unit', 'The external diameter of feeding tubes (as well as urinary catheters) is measured using the French scale (abbreviated as Fr or F).
• One French unit is equal to exactly 1/3 of a millimeter (0.33 mm). Therefore, a larger French number means a thicker tube (e.g., a 12 Fr tube has an outer diameter of 4 mm).', '["French unit","Diameter unit","Nasogastric tube","Tube circumference unit"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-07T09:32:22.031Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.3,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-06T09:32:22.031Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.019Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-06T09:32:22.031Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756781934881-10', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783610367197-1790433140273-gxvad', 'Reducing the kcalorie intake by 1000 per day will result to a weekly weight loss of:', '2 lbs', '', '["1 lb","2 lbs","5 lbs","7 lbs"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-09T08:32:10.022Z","interval":3,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":2}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-06T08:32:10.022Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.019Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-06T08:32:10.260Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756781996177-11', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783610367197-1790433140273-gxvad', 'A type of cirrhosis caused by obstruction of the bile ducts:', 'Biliary cirrhosis', 'Biliary cirrhosis develops when there is prolonged inflammation, scarring, or permanent mechanical obstruction within the bile duct system. When these ducts are blocked, bile cannot empty normally into the small intestine and instead backs up into the liver.

• Laennec''s cirrhosis: Also known as portal or alcoholic cirrhosis, this most common form of liver disease is explicitly caused by chronic, long-term alcohol abuse.
• Obstructive cirrhosis: While it sounds structurally logical, "obstructive" is a description of the mechanism rather than the formal, standard medical classification for this type of hepatic pathology.
• Cardiac cirrhosis: This rare form of liver scarring stems from severe, long-term right-sided heart failure that creates a chronic backpressure of venous blood (congestive hepatopathy) in the liver.', '["Cardiac cirrhosis","Laennec''s cirrhosis","Biliary cirrhosis","Obstructive cirrhosis"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-07T08:31:47.876Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-06T08:31:47.876Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.019Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-06T08:31:48.120Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756782081923-12', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783610367197-1790433140273-gxvad', 'The following are indications of parenteral nutrition, except:', 'Hemodynamically unstable patients', 'Hemodynamically unstable patients (those suffering from active shock, severe hypotension, or critical fluid/electrolyte imbalances) are a strict contraindication for parenteral nutrition, not an indication. Prior to initiating any form of nutritional support, the patient must be stabilized with fluid resuscitation and vasopressors. Attempting to infuse hyperosmolar PN into an unstable patient can worsen metabolic stress, increase the risk of severe refeeding complications, and destabilize hemodynamics further.', '["Severe diarrhea of  >500ml/day stool, or malabsorption","Hemodynamically unstable patients","Intractable vomiting","Small bowel or colon obstruction"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-07T08:20:47.724Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-06T08:20:47.724Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.019Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-06T08:20:47.941Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756782138754-13', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783610367197-1790433140273-gxvad', 'Also called portal systematic encephalopathy:', 'Hepatic coma', 'Portal-systemic encephalopathy (PSE) and hepatic encephalopathy are a spectrum of central nervous system dysfunctions caused by liver failure or shunting. Historically and in clinical question banks, hepatic coma (or coma hepaticum) is used interchangeably to describe its severe, final stage.', '["Obstructive cirrhosis","Portal hypertension","Hepatic coma","Pancreatitis"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-07T07:55:10.990Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-06T07:55:10.990Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.019Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-06T07:55:11.221Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756782176217-14', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783610367197-1790433140273-gxvad', 'Zinc is given to burned patients to:', 'Promote wound healing', 'Major burns cause extensive exudative loss of trace minerals through the open wounds. Zinc plays an indispensable role in every stage of tissue repair, including cellular proliferation, collagen synthesis, and re-epithelialization.', '["Prevent diarrhea","Promote wound healing","To supply oxidative enzymes","All of the above"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-07T08:16:31.514Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-06T08:16:31.514Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.019Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-06T08:16:31.752Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756782237664-15', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783610367197-1790433140273-gxvad', 'An eating disorder that is characterized by an individual that is extremely thin but still claims to "feel fat"?', 'Anorexia nervosa', 'Anorexia nervosa', '["Bulimia nervosa","Anorexia nervosa","Binging type","Restricting type"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-07T08:20:55.924Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-06T08:20:55.924Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.019Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-08T12:24:43.573Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756782298544-16', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783610367197-1790433140273-gxvad', 'Which of the following is TRUE about basal metabolic rate?', 'Fever increases BMR', 'When the body develops a fever, its internal temperature rises, which accelerates enzymatic and cellular activities to help fight off infection. As a general clinical rule, BMR increases by approximately 7% for every 1°F rise in body temperature.', '["Fever increases BMR","Pregnancy decreases BMR","Fasting increases BMR","Females have higher BMR than males"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-07T09:26:03.193Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-06T09:26:03.193Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.019Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-06T09:26:03.417Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756782377124-17', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783610367197-1790433140273-gxvad', 'Gastroesophageal reflux disease (GERD) is the constant regurgitation of acidic gastric contents into the lower part of the esophagus with symptoms including frequent and severe heartburn, dysphagia, and excessive belching.', 'True', 'Gastroesophageal reflux disease (GERD) occurs when the lower esophageal sphincter (LES) weakens or relaxes inappropriately. This structural failure allows acidic gastric juices, and sometimes bile, to flow backward (regurgitate) chronically into the esophagus.

Because the lining of the esophagus lacks the protective mucus barrier found in the stomach, this repeated exposure to harsh gastric acid causes intense irritation, inflammation, and injury. This pathophysiology directly manifests as the classic clinical triad:
• Severe, frequent heartburn (pyrosis)
• Dysphagia (difficulty swallowing due to inflammation or strictures)
• Excessive belching (eructation)', '["True","False","Maybe","Depends"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-07T08:55:32.966Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-06T08:55:32.966Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.019Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-06T08:55:33.192Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756782450322-18', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783610367197-1790433140273-gxvad', 'Compute for the osmolality of 210 ml of 5% amino acids.', '262 mOsm', '1. Find the total grams of amino acids: A 5% solution means there are 5 grams of solute in every 100 mL of fluid.
Total grams =\frac{5\text{\ g}}{100\text{\ mL}}\times 210\text{\ mL}=\mathbf{10.5}\text{\ grams\ of\ amino\ acids}\)
2. Apply the osmolarity factor for amino acids: In clinical nutrition and parenteral calculations, 1 gram of amino acids contributes approximately 10 mOsm to the solution.
3. Calculate the final osmolarity:
\(10.5\text{\ grams}\times 10\text{\ mOsm/g}=\mathbf{105}\text{\ mOsm}\)', '["262 mOsm","300 mOsm","290 mOsm","105 mOsm"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-07T08:19:24.613Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-06T08:19:24.613Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.019Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-06T08:19:24.613Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756782513801-19', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783610367197-1790433140273-gxvad', 'It is the disruption of the internal balance that lies beyond healthy and normal functioning:', 'Pathologic stress', '• Physiologic stress is a healthy, normal response of the body to daily changes or stressors (like exercise or cold weather) aimed at restoring homeostasis.
• When a stressor is too intense or prolonged, it pushes the body''s systems past their normal limits. This maladaptive state results in damage or a failure to maintain balance, turning into pathologic stress (which can eventually manifest as disease or tissue injury).', '["Stress","Physiologic stress","Pathologic stress","Hormonal stress"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-07T09:34:05.879Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.3,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-06T09:34:05.879Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.019Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-06T09:34:05.879Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756782543017-20', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783610367197-1790433140273-gxvad', 'May interact with Warfarin:', 'Vit K', '• Warfarin is an anticoagulant that works by blocking the enzyme responsible for recycling Vitamin K. Since Vitamin K is essential for the liver to synthesize blood-clotting factors (Factors II, VII, IX, and X), a sudden increase or decrease in dietary Vitamin K directly alters how well Warfarin works.
• Consuming high amounts of Vitamin K (like green leafy vegetables) will reverse or reduce the blood-thinning effect of Warfarin, increasing the risk of blood clots. Conversely, dropping Vitamin K intake can dangerousy increase the risk of bleeding.', '["Vit A","Vit D","Vit E","Vit K"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-07T09:32:47.914Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.3,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-06T09:32:47.914Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.019Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-06T09:32:47.914Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756782599114-21', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783610367197-1790433140273-gxvad', 'A classification of anemia to describe the hemoglobin content:', 'Hypochromic anemia', 'Anemias are classified morphologically using two main criteria: cell size and hemoglobin content (color).
• Hemoglobin Content (Color): The term chromic refers to color, which is determined by the concentration of hemoglobin inside the red blood cell (RBC).
	✓ Hypochromic means the cells have less hemoglobin than normal, making them look pale under a microscope.
	✓ Normochromic means the hemoglobin concentration is normal.
• Cell Size: The term cytic refers to the physical size of the RBC. Macrocytic, Normocytic, and Microcytic describe whether the cells are larger than normal, normal-sized, or smaller than normal. They do not classify hemoglobin content itself.', '["Macrocytic anemia","Normocytic anemia","Hypochromic anemia","Microcytic anemia"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-07T09:33:21.222Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.3,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-06T09:33:21.222Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.019Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-06T09:33:21.222Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756782632896-22', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783610367197-1790433140273-gxvad', 'All of the following increases BMR, EXCEPT:', 'Sleeping', 'Basal Metabolic Rate (BMR) represents the minimum energy required to keep vital functions (like breathing and circulation) active. During sleep, your muscle activity drops, your heart rate slows down, and your body enters its lowest metabolic state, causing your energy expenditure to drop below awake basal levels.', '["Thyroxine","Adrenaline","Stress hormone","Sleeping"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-07T08:23:43.455Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-06T08:23:43.455Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.019Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-06T08:23:43.676Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756782702775-23', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783610367197-1790433140273-gxvad', 'Important components of nutritional assessment include:
I. Gathering data
II. Comparing to relevant standards
III. Identifying possible problem area', 'All of the above', 'All of the above', '["I and II only","I and III only","II and III only","All of the above"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-07T09:14:37.623Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-06T09:14:37.623Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.019Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-08T12:24:43.573Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756782746857-24', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783610367197-1790433140273-gxvad', 'The following are the domain of nutrition intervention, EXCEPT:', 'None of the above', 'The 4 Official Domains of Nutrition Intervention:
• Food and/or Nutrient Delivery (ND): Customized food, snacks, enteral/parenteral nutrition, supplements, or feeding assistance.
• Nutrition Education (E): Formal process to instruct or train a patient in a nutrition-related skill or to provide knowledge.
• Nutrition Counseling (C): A supportive process to guide collaborative goal-setting and behavior change.
• Coordination of Nutrition Care (RC): Consulting with or referring to other healthcare providers, institutions, or agencies.', '["Food and nutrient delivery","Nutrition education","Coordination of nutrition care","None of the above"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-07T09:35:18.309Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.3,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-06T09:35:18.309Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.019Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-06T09:35:18.309Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756782799204-25', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783610367197-1790433140273-gxvad', '___ is the application of nutritional principles for enhacing athletic performance.', 'Sports nutrition', 'Sports nutrition', '["Sports science","Sports nutrition","Athletic nutrition","None of the above"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-07T07:50:50.062Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-06T07:50:50.062Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.019Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-08T12:24:43.573Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756782856569-26', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783610367197-1790433140273-gxvad', 'It is the gold standard in measuring energy requirements by measuring oxygen consumption and carbon dioxide production.', 'Indirect calorimetry', 'Indirect calorimetry', '["Bomb calorimeter","Respiration chamber","Indirect calorimetry","None of the above"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-07T07:45:12.237Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-06T07:45:12.237Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.019Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-08T12:24:43.573Z"'::jsonb)
