('anki-card-1756782917265-27', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783610367197-1790433140273-gxvad', 'These are the first line of immune system defense.', 'Skin and mucous membranes', 'The Three Lines of Defense
• First Line (Anatomical Barriers): Skin and mucous membranes form the primary physical and chemical shield of the innate immune system. They block pathogens mechanically from entering the body and secrete protective fluids (like sweat, tears, and gastric acid).
• Second Line (Nonspecific Internal Defenses): If pathogens breach the physical barriers, granulocytes and macrophages, along with inflammatory responses and fever, act as the internal backup to destroy the invaders non-specifically.
• Third Line (Adaptive Immunity): T lymphocytes and antibodies represent the highly targeted, specific adaptive immune system, which takes longer to activate but creates immunological memory.', '["Skin and mucous membranes","GIT secretions","T lymphocytes and antibodies","Granulocytes and macrophages"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-07T09:39:45.614Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.1,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-06T09:39:45.614Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.019Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-06T09:39:45.844Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756782979746-28', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783610367197-1790433140273-gxvad', 'Dextrose in PN provides this much calorie per gram.', '3 . 4 kcal/gram', 'In Parenteral Nutrition (PN), carbohydrates are delivered as dextrose monohydrate rather than pure anhydrous glucose. The attached water molecule adds weight but no calories, reducing its energy density from the standard dietary carbohydrate value of 4 kcal/g down to 3.4 kcal/gram.

Amino Acids (Protein)
• Energy Value: 4.0 kcal/gram
Intravenous Lipid Emulsions (IVLE / Fats)
• 10% Lipid Emulsion: Provides 1.1 kcal/mL
• 20% Lipid Emulsion: Provides 2.0 kcal/mL
• 30% Lipid Emulsion: Provides 3.0 kcal/mL (typically reserved for pharmacy compounding use only)', '["4 kcal/gram","2 kcal/gram","3 kcal/gram","3 . 4 kcal/gram"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-07T09:39:18.777Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.3,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-06T09:39:18.777Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.019Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-06T09:39:19.063Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756783040676-29', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783610367197-1790433140273-gxvad', 'Which of the following is common cause of peptic ulcer?', 'Both a and b', 'A peptic ulcer is an open sore or lesion that develops on the inner lining of the stomach (gastric ulcer) or the upper portion of the small intestine (duodenal ulcer). It occurs when the digestive tract''s protective mucous layer drops or when gastric acid production increases, allowing corrosive stomach juices to burn and erode the deeper underlying sub-mucosal tissue layers.

Together, H. pylori infection and NSAID use account for nearly all cases of peptic ulcer disease:
• H. pylori: This bacterium colonizes the protective mucous layer of the stomach and duodenum. It triggers chronic localized inflammation, weakening the lining and allowing corrosive stomach acid to eat away at the tissue.
• Long-term use of NSAIDs: Non-steroidal anti-inflammatory drugs (like ibuprofen, naproxen, and aspirin) block the COX-1 enzyme. Because this enzyme is responsible for producing the protective prostaglandins that maintain the stomach''s mucosal barrier, blocking it leaves the lining vulnerable to acid erosion.', '["H. pylori","Long term use of non-steroidal anti-inflammatory drugs","Both a and b","None of the above"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-07T08:15:16.530Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-06T08:15:16.530Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.019Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-06T08:15:16.530Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756783081174-30', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783610367197-1790433140273-gxvad', 'Which of the following is not a nutrient essential in blood formation?', 'Vit A', 'While Vitamin A supports immune health, cellular growth, and vision, it is not categorized as a primary, direct building block or regulator for hematopoiesis (blood cell production).

Why the Other Options are Essential
• Protein: Red blood cells are heavily made of proteins. Globin (the protein portion of hemoglobin) requires a constant supply of dietary amino acids to synthesize new red blood cells.
• Folic acid: This B-vitamin (Vitamin B9) is absolutely vital for DNA synthesis and normal cell division in the bone marrow. A deficiency directly causes macrocytic (megaloblastic) anemia.
• Vit C: Vitamin C acts as a critical enhancer for iron absorption in the intestines. Since iron is the absolute core component needed to make hemoglobin, Vitamin C plays an essential indirect role in healthy blood formation.', '["Vit A","Vit C","Protein","Folic acid"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-07T09:33:49.428Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.3,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-06T09:33:49.428Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.019Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-06T09:33:49.428Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756783206452-31', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783610367197-1790433140273-gxvad', 'Which of the following is/are true about evaluating nutrient intake of individuals using EAR and RDA?
I. If the intake is greater than RDA, there is a high level of confidence that intake is adequate
II. If the intake is between the EAR and the RDA, the intake probably needs to be improved', 'Both statement are correct', '• Statement I is correct: The Recommended Dietary Allowance (RDA) is calculated to meet the nutritional needs of nearly all (97% to 98%) healthy individuals in a specific life-stage and gender group. Therefore, if an individual''s chronic intake is monitored and found to be greater than the RDA, clinicians have a high level of confidence that their intake is entirely adequate.
• Statement II is correct: The Estimated Average Requirement (EAR) is the intake level estimated to meet the needs of only 50% of healthy individuals. If an individual''s intake falls between the EAR and the RDA, there is high statistical uncertainty regarding its adequacy, and the probability of deficiency is anywhere from 3% to 50%. Because it does not meet the safety margin of the individual''s RDA target, the intake probably needs to be improved or increased.', '["Only I is correct","Only II is correct","Both statement are correct","Both statement are incorrect"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-07T09:32:35.773Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.3,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-06T09:32:35.773Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.019Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-06T09:32:35.773Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756783255498-32', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783610367197-1790433140273-gxvad', 'Dietary recommendation for colostomy:', 'Low fiber', 'Immediately following surgery and during the initial recovery phase (typically the first 6 to 8 weeks), patients are placed on a strict low-fiber diet. This recommendation focuses on protecting the intestinal tract as it heals:

• Preventing Stoma Blockages: High-fiber foods (like whole grains, nuts, seeds, and raw fibrous vegetables) do not break down easily. They can form a physical mass that risks completely blocking the narrow stoma opening.
• Reducing GI Distress: A low-fiber, bland diet minimizes intestinal swelling, limits excessive stoma output, and controls gas or severe cramping while the bowel normalizes.', '["Low fiber","High fiber","Vit B12 supplementation","Increased carbohydrate"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-07T08:41:47.003Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-06T08:41:47.003Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.019Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-06T08:41:47.217Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756783288031-33', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783610367197-1790433140273-gxvad', 'Iatrogenic Malnutrition:', 'Physician-induced malnutrition', '', '["Physician-induced malnutrition","Cachexia","SAM","Anorexia"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-07T08:28:53.054Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-06T08:28:53.054Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.019Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-06T08:28:53.300Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756783368627-34', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783610367197-1790433140273-gxvad', 'Distinct parts of nutrition diagnostic statement:
I. problem
II. etiology
III. signs and symptoms
IV. Evidence', 'I, II, and III only', '', '["I and III only","I, II, and III only","I, II, and IV only","All of the above"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-07T08:19:40.177Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-06T08:19:40.177Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.019Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-06T08:19:40.429Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756783422741-35', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783610367197-1790433140273-gxvad', 'An adverse reaction to a food or food additive that does not involve the immune system.', 'Food intolerances', 'Food intolerances', '["Food allergy","Food intolerances","Antigen","Food sensitivity"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-07T08:16:50.831Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-06T08:16:50.831Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.019Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-08T12:24:43.573Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756783507571-36', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783610367197-1790433140273-gxvad', 'Weight loss target for obese adults:
I. 10% weight loss of total body weight over 6 months
II. Dietary changes include an energy deficit of 500-1000 kcal/day
III. TER of 2000 kcal per day', 'I and II only', 'Clinical guidelines for adult weight and obesity management establish specific, realistic milestones to ensure safety and long-term compliance:
• I. 10% weight loss of total body weight over 6 months: Major health organizations (such as the National Institutes of Health and the National Heart, Lung, and Blood Institute) define an initial target of reducing baseline body weight by 10% over a 6-month timeframe as the clinical standard. This pace is highly realistic and yields massive improvements in blood pressure, blood glucose, and lipid profiles.
• II. Dietary changes include an energy deficit of 500-1000 kcal/day: To achieve a steady, sustainable weight loss of 1 to 2 pounds per week, the standard prescription is to introduce a daily caloric deficit of 500 to 1,000 kcal below the patient''s Total Daily Energy Expenditure (TDEE).', '["I only","II only","I and II only","All of the above"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-07T09:08:47.984Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-06T09:08:47.984Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.019Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-06T09:08:48.228Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756783592031-37', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783610367197-1790433140273-gxvad', 'What is the total fluid volume for blenderized feeding of 1900 kcal, 1.5:1 dilution?', '1267 ml', 'Total volume (ml) = 1900 kcal/1.5/ml = 1267ml', '["1267 ml","1900 ml","950 ml","2850 ml"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-07T09:02:37.308Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-06T09:02:37.308Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.019Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-06T09:02:37.525Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756783686944-38', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783610367197-1790433140273-gxvad', 'Indication/s when nutritional support is generally required among patients.', 'All of the above', 'All of the above', '["10 days of prolonged partial starvation","Illness longer than 10 days","Significant/severe weight loss","All of the above"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-07T08:15:34.083Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-06T08:15:34.083Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.019Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-08T12:24:43.573Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756783737259-39', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783610367197-1790433140273-gxvad', 'Foods allowed in tyramine-restricted diet:', 'Some type of beans', 'A tyramine-restricted diet is commonly prescribed to individuals taking Monoamine Oxidase Inhibitors (MAOIs) to prevent a life-threatening hypertensive crisis. Tyramine builds up naturally in foods that are aged, fermented, cured, or smoked.

Some type of beans: While broad beans (like fava beans) contain high amounts of L-dopa/tyramine and must be strictly avoided, standard fresh or canned legumes (such as standard green beans, chickpeas, and lentils) are entirely safe and allowed on this diet.', '["Gouda cheese","Smoked ham","Some type of beans","A and B only"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-07T09:34:23.173Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.3,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-06T09:34:23.173Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.019Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-06T09:34:23.173Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756783783107-40', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783610367197-1790433140273-gxvad', 'Diseases within the small intestine generally result in malabsorption because of the impaired function of the organ.', 'True', 'The small intestine is the primary organ responsible for the chemical digestion and absorption of nutrients (such as carbohydrates, proteins, fats, vitamins, and minerals). It features specialized structural adaptations, namely villi and microvilli, which create a massive surface area for nutrient uptake.

When a disease affects the small intestine (such as Celiac disease, Crohn''s disease, or Tropical sprue), it damages this delicate mucosal lining, blunts the villi, and causes widespread inflammation. This impaired structural function directly prevents the organ from absorbing nutrients effectively, leading to clinical malabsorption, nutrient deficiencies, weight loss, and chronic diarrhea.', '["True","False","Maybe","Depends"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-07T08:50:20.443Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-06T08:50:20.443Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.019Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-06T08:50:20.672Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756783837417-41', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783610367197-1790433140273-gxvad', 'It is the phase of stress response characterized by failure of adaption leading to death.', 'Exhaustion phase', 'The Three Stages of Hans Selye''s General Adaptation Syndrome (GAS)
• Exhaustion Phase: This is the final stage of the stress response. If the stressor is severe, intense, or prolonged over a long period, the body''s energy reserves and compensatory resources become completely depleted. This failure of adaptation leads to immune suppression, systemic organ damage, and eventually death if left unchecked.

The Other Phases:
	• Alarm Reaction (Acute Phase): The initial "fight-or-flight" response where the sympathetic nervous system is triggered, releasing adrenaline and cortisol.
	• Resistance Phase (Adaptive Phase): The body attempts to adapt to the ongoing stressor, working to stabilize its physiological functions and return to a state of homeostasis.
	• Convalescence Phase: This refers to the recovery period after an illness or injury has passed, rather than a failure of stress management.', '["Acute phase","Adaptive phase","Convaslescence phase","Exhaustion phase"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-07T09:22:30.534Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-06T09:22:30.534Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.019Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-06T09:22:30.741Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756783888769-42', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783610367197-1790433140273-gxvad', 'The following are metabolic effects of fever, EXCEPT:', 'Decreases BMR', '', '["Increased tissue catabolism","Increased loss of body water","Increased electrolyte loss","Decreases BMR"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-07T09:26:31.985Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-06T09:26:31.985Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.019Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-06T09:26:32.218Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756783940432-43', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783610367197-1790433140273-gxvad', 'Which body component is directly proportional to basal metabolic rate?', 'Lean body mass', 'Lean body mass (LBM), which includes skeletal muscle, organs, and bone, consists of highly metabolic tissue. Every kilogram of LBM requires a significant amount of oxygen and energy just to maintain itself at rest. Because of this, it is the single most important body composition component directly proportional to your Basal Metabolic Rate (BMR)—as lean mass increases, BMR increases.', '["Body fat","Extracellular volume","Lean body mass","Total body mass"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-07T09:16:40.989Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-06T09:16:40.989Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.019Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-06T09:16:41.225Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756784012729-44', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783610367197-1790433140273-gxvad', 'Nutrition management for burns:
I. High calorie
II. High protein
III. Sodium controlled
IV. Fluid controlled', 'All of the above', '• I. High Calorie: Burn injuries trigger a massive hypermetabolic and catabolic state. Energy expenditure can double depending on the severity, requiring a high-calorie intake (predominantly from carbohydrates) to prevent significant weight and muscle tissue loss.
• II. High Protein: Large amounts of nitrogen and protein are lost through burn wounds. Aggressive high-protein delivery (often 1.5–2.0 g/kg/day or more) is essential to promote tissue repair, wound healing, and maintain lean mass.
• III. Sodium Controlled & IV. Fluid Controlled: While early-stage burn shock resuscitation demands large amounts of fluids and sodium, the downstream nutritional management phase shifts toward precision. In this phase, fluids and sodium are meticulously monitored, calculated, and controlled to prevent fluid overload, pulmonary edema, and severe electrolyte imbalances (like hypernatremia) while maintaining hemodynamic stability.', '["I and II only","I, II, III only","I, II, IV only","All of the above"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-07T09:39:40.272Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.1,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-06T09:39:40.272Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.019Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-06T09:39:40.501Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756784100025-45', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783610367197-1790433140273-gxvad', 'An anemia pertaining to anemia induced by easy damage of the RBCs?', 'Hemolytic anemia', 'Hemolytic anemia occurs when red blood cells (RBCs) are destroyed or broken down faster than the bone marrow can produce new ones. This premature destruction (hemolysis) can be caused by intrinsic defects inside the RBCs that make them weak, or external factors like autoimmune attacks, toxins, and infections that damage the cell membrane.

	• Microcytic anemia describes RBCs that are abnormally small in size, most commonly due to iron deficiency.
	• Hemorrhagic anemia is caused by excessive external or internal blood loss (bleeding), not cellular damage.
	• Pernicious anemia is a specific type of vitamin B12 deficiency anemia caused by a lack of intrinsic factor needed for absorption.', '["Microcytic anemia","Pernicious anemia","Hemorrhagic anemia","Hemolytic anemia"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-07T09:32:09.237Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.3,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-06T09:32:09.237Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.019Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-06T09:32:09.237Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756784157654-46', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783610367197-1790433140273-gxvad', 'Which of the following biochemical measures of nutritonal status is most likely associated with over nutrition?', 'Serum LDL', 'Serum LDL (low-density lipoprotein or "bad" cholesterol) reflects fat and lipid metabolism. Elevated levels are commonly driven by a chronic overnutrition of saturated fats, trans fats, and excess calories, making it a key biochemical indicator for overnutrition and cardiovascular risk.

The Other Options (Malnutrition/Under-nutrition):
	• Nitrogen balance (B) measures protein intake versus excretion and is typically used to monitor protein catabolism or adequacy.
	• Hemoglobin and hematocrit (C) screen for anemias caused by dietary deficiencies (like iron, folate, or B12).
	• Serum albumin (D) is a standard marker used to evaluate protein-calorie malnutrition or visceral protein depletion.', '["Hemoglobin and hematocrit","Nitrogen balance","Serum albumin","Serum LDL"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-07T07:52:35.653Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-06T07:52:35.653Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.019Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-06T07:52:35.846Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756784244635-47', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783610367197-1790433140273-gxvad', 'A term used to describe the surgical removal of the gallbladder, inflammation of the gallbladder.', 'Cholecystectomy, Cholecystitis', '• The Root: Cholecyst- refers to the gallbladder.
• Surgical Removal: The suffix -ectomy means surgical excision or removal. Therefore, cholecystectomy is the removal of the gallbladder.
• Inflammation: The suffix -itis means inflammation. Therefore, cholecystitis is inflammation of the gallbladder.

Why the Other Options Do Not Fit
• Hemorrhoidectomy is the removal of hemorrhoids; cholelithiasis is the presence of gallstones.
• Jejunoileostomy is a surgical opening between the jejunum and ileum; cholecystolithiasis specifically means stones inside the gallbladder.
• Colostomy is a surgical opening into the colon; choledocholithiasis means stones inside the common bile duct.', '["Colostomy, Choledocholithiasis","Jejunoileostomy, Cholecystolithiasis","Cholecystectomy, Cholecystitis","Hermorrhoidectomy,Cholelithiasis"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-07T08:27:18.374Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-06T08:27:18.374Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.019Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-06T08:27:18.603Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756784302449-48', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783610367197-1790433140273-gxvad', 'It refers to the provision of nutrients both orally and via tube directly into the GIT.', 'Enteral nutrition', 'Enteral nutrition is the broader term used when nutrients are delivered directly into the gastrointestinal tract (GIT). This includes both standard oral food intake (consuming regular foods or oral supplements) and tube feeding (delivering liquid formula via nasogastric, gastrostomy, or jejunostomy tubes).

	• Parenteral nutrition bypasses the GI tract entirely, delivering nutrients directly into a central or peripheral vein.
	• Nutrition intervention is a broad step in the Nutrition Care Process that encompasses any planned action to resolve a nutrition-related problem (e.g., diet education, counseling, or supplement provision).
	• Refeeding syndrome is a dangerous metabolic complication that can occur when restarting nutrition in severely malnourished individuals.', '["Nutrition intervention","Enteral nutrition","Parental nutrition","Refeeding syndrom"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-07T08:57:49.896Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-06T08:57:49.896Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.019Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-06T08:57:50.135Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756787234090-0', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783610375016-1790433139825-w0zcd', 'All of the following are attributes of functional foods, EXCEPT:', 'High calories', 'Functional foods are explicitly designed or naturally chosen to provide health benefits beyond basic nutrition (like raw caloric energy). They target specific physiological advantages (such as disease prevention) rather than loading the body with empty or high calories.', '["High fiber","Low cholesterol","Reduced caffeine","High calories"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-08T10:42:18.956Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.3,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-07T10:42:18.956Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.558Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-07T10:42:19.042Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756787280005-1', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783610375016-1790433139825-w0zcd', 'Phytochemicals that is found in turmeric that inhibits enzymes that activates carcinogens', 'Curcumin', '', '["Tannins","Flavonoids","Capsaicin","Curcumin"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-10T10:43:02.868Z","interval":3,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":2}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-07T10:43:02.868Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.558Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-07T10:43:02.954Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756787316135-2', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783610375016-1790433139825-w0zcd', '___ is a common feature in cancer patients.', 'Weight loss', 'Unintentional weight loss is one of the most prominent, universal, and classic hallmarks of cancer. It occurs in up to 60–80% of patients with advanced stages or specific tumor types (such as lung or upper gastrointestinal cancers). It is driven by cancer cachexia—a metabolic syndrome where tumor-induced inflammation breaks down muscle and fat tissue.', '["Taste/smell alteration","Dysphagia","Weight loss","Early satiety"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-10T10:51:39.524Z","interval":3,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":2}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-07T10:51:39.524Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.558Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-07T10:51:39.611Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756787361232-3', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783610375016-1790433139825-w0zcd', 'Dietary management for person with epilepsy:', 'Ketogenic diet', 'A ketogenic diet is a specialized, high-fat, very low-carbohydrate diet medically used to manage epilepsy, especially in patients with drug-resistant or refractory seizures. The severe reduction of carbohydrates forces the body to burn fat for fuel, generating ketone bodies that act as a stabilizing, anticonvulsant energy source for the brain.', '["Regular diet","High carohydrate","Low fat","Ketogenic diet"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-10T10:49:51.105Z","interval":3,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":2}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-07T10:49:51.105Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.558Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-07T10:49:51.158Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756787404314-4', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783610375016-1790433139825-w0zcd', 'Cushing''s disease is characterized by:', 'All of the above', 'Cushing''s disease is a specific type of Cushing''s syndrome caused by a benign tumor (adenoma) in the pituitary gland. This tumor secretes excessive amounts of Adrenocorticotropic Hormone (ACTH), which constantly overstimulates the adrenal glands to produce massive amounts of cortisol (the body''s primary stress hormone).

• Menstrual irregularities: Chronic high cortisol levels disrupt the normal pulsatile release of gonadotropin-releasing hormone (GnRH). This often leads to irregular periods or a complete absence of menstruation (amenorrhea) in women.
• Wasting: Cortisol is a highly catabolic hormone. Prolonged exposure causes muscle wasting and weakness, particularly visible in the thin arms and legs of patients (proximal muscle wasting).
• Low calcium levels: High levels of glucocorticoids significantly decrease intestinal absorption of calcium and increase its excretion through the kidneys. This contributes heavily to the bone loss, weakening, and osteoporosis seen in Cushing''s patients.', '["Low calcium levels","Wasting","Menstrual irregularities","All of the above"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-10T10:50:16.460Z","interval":3,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":2}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-07T10:50:16.460Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.558Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-07T10:50:16.546Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756787469329-5', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783610375016-1790433139825-w0zcd', 'The following diseases are included in the newborn screening panel EXCEPT:', 'Congenital hyperthyroidism', '• Newborn screening panels test for congenital hypothyroidism (an underactive thyroid gland that lacks sufficient hormone).
• Congenital adrenal hyperplasia (CAH) is a standard, universally included hormone disorder in newborn screening panels to prevent life-threatening adrenal crises.
• Maple syrup urine disease (MSUD) is a classic inborn error of amino acid metabolism that is globally included in primary newborn screening panels.', '["Maple syrup urine disease","Congenital hyperthyroidism","Congenital adrenal hyperplasia","None of the above"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-08T10:43:19.445Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.3,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-07T10:43:19.445Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.558Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-07T10:43:19.534Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756787505159-6', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783610375016-1790433139825-w0zcd', 'Phytochemicals found in hot peppers that modulates blood clotting.', 'Capsaicin', 'Capsaicin is the active, spicy chemical compound unique to chili and hot peppers. Beyond giving peppers their heat, capsaicin directly modulates blood clotting by acting as a natural blood thinner. It works by inhibiting platelet aggregation (preventing blood platelets from sticking together to form clots) and enhancing fibrinolytic activity (the body''s process of breaking down clots).

• Curcumin is the powerful anti-inflammatory phytochemical found in turmeric, not hot peppers.
•  Tannins are astringent polyphenols widespread in tea, coffee, and wine that bind to proteins and can inhibit iron absorption.
• Flavonoids are a very broad, massive class of plant pigments found in almost all fruits and vegetables.', '["Capsaicin","Tannins","Flavonoids","Curcumin"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-10T10:49:57.701Z","interval":3,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":2}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-07T10:49:57.701Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.558Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-07T10:49:57.785Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756787571157-7', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783610375016-1790433139825-w0zcd', 'What is the segment of gluten protein associated with an inappropriate immune response in Celiac disease?', 'Gliadin', 'Gluten is composed of two primary proteins: gliadin and glutenin. Gliadin is the specific, alcohol-soluble fraction that contains the toxic amino acid sequences (such as the 33-mer peptide) that the immune system mistakenly recognizes as a threat in celiac disease, triggering the destructive autoimmune response.

• Avenin is the specific storage protein found in oats. While some celiac patients are sensitive to it, it is distinct from gluten proteins.
• Zein is the primary storage protein found in corn (maize) and is entirely safe for individuals with celiac disease.
• Glutenin is the other half of the gluten protein pair responsible for dough elasticity. While it is part of gluten, it is not the primary initiator of the toxic autoimmune reaction compared to gliadin.', '["Glutenin","Gliadin","Zein","Avenin"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-10T10:50:46.182Z","interval":3,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":2}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-07T10:50:46.182Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.558Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-07T10:50:46.282Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756787614121-8', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783610375016-1790433139825-w0zcd', 'Dietary management for Wilson''s disease:', 'Copper-restricted diet', 'Wilson''s disease is a rare genetic disorder that prevents the body from properly excreting excess copper through the liver and bile. Because the body cannot get rid of it, copper builds up to toxic levels and deposits directly into vital organs—primarily the liver, brain, and the corneas of the eyes (causing characteristic copper-colored rings called Kayser-Fleischer rings). Left untreated, this toxic accumulation can lead to severe liver failure, neurological damage, and psychiatric symptoms.

The definitive dietary management for Wilson''s disease is a strict copper-restricted diet. Patients must strictly limit their intake of copper-rich foods—such as organ meats, shellfish, mushrooms, nuts, chocolate, and dried beans. This dietary restriction is used alongside medical therapies (like copper-chelating agents or zinc supplements) to prevent further organ toxicity.', '["Copper-restricted diet","Fe-restricted diet","High K diet","Gluten-restricted diet"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-10T10:45:12.002Z","interval":3,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":2}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-07T10:45:12.002Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.558Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-07T10:45:12.002Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756787681251-9', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783610375016-1790433139825-w0zcd', 'A gluten-free diet excludes:', 'Wheat, barley, rye, oats', 'A strict gluten-free diet permanently eliminates grains containing the protein gluten (and its toxic prolamin fractions: gliadin in wheat, secalin in rye, and hordein in barley). These cause severe small intestinal mucosal damage in individuals with celiac disease or gluten intolerance.

In classic nursing, nutrition, and board exam question banks (such as the Philippine Food Exchange List frameworks), oats are historically grouped alongside wheat, barley, and rye as prohibited foods. This is due to a high risk of cross-contamination during harvesting and processing, as well as the presence of avenin (a protein in oats similar to gluten that some sensitive individuals react to).', '["Wheat, corn, oats","Barley, soybeans, oats","Wheat, barley, rye, oats","Wheat, barley, rice, oats"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-10T10:45:54.639Z","interval":3,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":2}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-07T10:45:54.639Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.558Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-07T14:32:00.423Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756787722383-10', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783610375016-1790433139825-w0zcd', 'The following are hormones that regulate energy metabolism EXCEPT:', 'Calcitonin', 'Calcitonin is a hormone produced by the parafollicular cells (C-cells) of the thyroid gland. Its primary and specific function is to regulate calcium and phosphate balance in the body by lowering blood calcium levels (inhibiting bone-resorbing osteoclasts and promoting bone building). It does not play a direct role in macronutrient breakdown, cellular respiration, or overall energy metabolism.

• Thyroxine (T4) is the primary thyroid hormone responsible for establishing and regulating the body''s basal metabolic rate (BMR), dictating how fast cells utilize oxygen and burn energy.
• Norepinephrine acts as a stress hormone and neurotransmitter that activates the sympathetic nervous system. It rapidly mobilizes stored energy fuels by accelerating fat breakdown (lipolysis) and glycogen conversion into glucose for immediate energy use.
• Insulin is a major anabolic hormone produced by the pancreas. It heavily governs energy metabolism by directing cells to absorb glucose from the blood to either store it as glycogen or fat, or burn it for immediate energy.', '["Thyroxine","Norepinephrine","Calcitonin","Insulin"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-10T10:52:13.635Z","interval":3,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":2}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-07T10:52:13.635Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.558Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-07T10:52:13.722Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756787769214-11', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783610375016-1790433139825-w0zcd', 'Bone cells with an active role in releasing calcium into the blood.', 'Osteocytes', 'Osteocytes derive from osteoblasts that become trapped in the newly formed mineralized matrix. While historically viewed as passive bystanders, they form an extensive interconnected network via canaliculi that detects mechanical loading and orchestrates mineral homeostasis. Through the expression of parathyroid hormone (PTH) receptors, osteocytes respond to hypocalcemia by upregulating RANKL expression and actively participating in osteocytic osteolysis—a process where they secrete matrix-degrading enzymes (such as MMPs and cathepsins) to resorb surrounding perilacunar bone and mobilize calcium directly into the extracellular fluid and bloodstream.', '["Osteoclast","Osteocytes","Ostegenic cells","Osteoblasts"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-10T10:42:56.226Z","interval":3,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":2}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-07T10:42:56.226Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.558Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-07T10:42:56.313Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756787842411-12', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783610375016-1790433139825-w0zcd', 'Diet management for a child with Maple Syrup Urine Disease:', 'Low leucine, isoleucine, valine', 'Maple Syrup Urine Disease (MSUD) is an inherited metabolic disorder caused by a deficiency in the enzyme complex needed to break down protein building blocks. Specifically, the body cannot process the three branched-chain amino acids (BCAAs): leucine, isoleucine, and valine. Without proper breakdown, these amino acids and their toxic byproducts accumulate in the blood and urine, giving the urine a sweet odor reminiscent of maple syrup. If left unmanaged, the buildup becomes highly neurotoxic, causing brain damage, seizures, or coma.', '["Low isoleucine, valine, 100 mg b1 per day","Low leucine, isoleucine, valine","Low leucine, valine, protein","Low tyrosine, phenylalanine"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-10T10:51:53.997Z","interval":3,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":2}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-07T10:51:53.997Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.558Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-07T10:51:54.082Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756787907658-13', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783610375016-1790433139825-w0zcd', 'Nutrition recommendation for protein for patients with Chronic Renal failure:', '0.6 to 0.8 g/kg BW', 'For adults with advanced Chronic Renal Failure (CRF) who are stable and not yet on dialysis, medical guidelines (such as KDOQI clinical guidelines) recommend a low-protein diet to help manage the condition. Restricting dietary protein helps reduce the accumulation of toxic nitrogenous waste products (uremic toxins), minimizes kidney workload, and helps delay the progression toward end-stage renal disease.', '["Greater than or equal to 1.0 g/kg BW","0.6 to 0.8 g/kg BW","2 g/kg BW","0.8 to 1.0 g/kg BW"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-08T10:51:33.753Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.3,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-07T10:51:33.753Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.558Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-07T10:51:33.840Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756787957421-14', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783610375016-1790433139825-w0zcd', 'It is a syndrome characterized by weight loss, anorexia, asthenia, and anemia.', 'Cachexia', 'Cachexia (specifically cancer cachexia or wasting syndrome) is a complex metabolic syndrome defined precisely by involuntary weight loss, anorexia (loss of appetite), asthenia (severe physical weakness/loss of strength), and anemia. Unlike simple starvation, it is driven by systemic inflammation that actively breaks down skeletal muscle and fat tissue.', '["Malnutrition","Cachexia","Anorexia","Mucositis"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-10T10:50:21.978Z","interval":3,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":2}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-07T10:50:21.978Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.558Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-07T10:50:22.062Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756787992303-15', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783610375016-1790433139825-w0zcd', 'In bone formation, the production of parathyroid hormone is:', 'Decreased', 'During active bone formation, osteoblasts take calcium ions out of the blood to build and mineralize the new bone matrix. As a consequence, blood calcium levels drop. In response to this drop in serum calcium, the body acts to restore homeostasis by increasing parathyroid hormone production to trigger bone resorption. Conversely, when calcium is abundant and actively filling the bones, the production of parathyroid hormone is decreased (suppressed) via negative feedback to prevent further bone breakdown.

*An increase in parathyroid hormone production primarily drives bone resorption (breakdown) because it signals osteoclasts to dissolve bone and release calcium back into the blood.', '["Increased","Decreased","Uncertain","No effect"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-08T10:51:21.492Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.3,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-07T10:51:21.492Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.558Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-07T10:51:21.581Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756788030617-16', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783610375016-1790433139825-w0zcd', 'Dietary management for a person with Addison''s disease:', 'All of the above', 'Addison''s disease (primary adrenal insufficiency) occurs when the adrenal glands are damaged and cannot produce enough cortisol and aldosterone. Aldosterone is the hormone responsible for holding onto sodium and flushing out potassium. Without it, the body dangerously dumps sodium and retains potassium, while a lack of cortisol slows down metabolism and depletes energy reserves.

Because of this hormonal deficit, the dietary management must directly counteract these imbalances:

• High Na (Sodium): Due to the lack of aldosterone, the kidneys constantly excrete sodium into the urine, causing hyponatremia (low blood sodium). Patients need a high-sodium diet to replace these heavy losses and maintain stable blood pressure.
• Low K (Potassium): Without aldosterone to flush it out, potassium builds up dangerously in the blood (hyperkalemia). Patients must follow a low-potassium diet to prevent cardiac arrhythmias.
• High kcal (Calories): The lack of cortisol causes severe fatigue, muscle wasting, and frequent hypoglycemia (low blood sugar). A high-calorie, nutrient-dense diet with frequent small meals helps sustain energy levels and prevent dangerous blood sugar drops.', '["High Na","Low K","High kcal","All of the above"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-08T10:49:46.770Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.3,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-07T10:49:46.770Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.558Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-07T10:49:46.884Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756788086065-17', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783610375016-1790433139825-w0zcd', 'Bone cells responsible for the resorption of bone matrix and release of calcium and phosphate from the bone:', 'Osteoclasts', 'Osteoclasts are large, multinucleated cells that function as the "demolition crew" of the skeletal system. They secrete acids and specialized enzymes (like collagenase) to dissolve the mineralized bone matrix. This biological breakdown—called bone resorption—directly liberates and releases locked-up calcium and phosphate ions back into the bloodstream.', '["Osteogenic cells","Osteocytes","Osteoblast","Osteoclasts"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-10T10:52:32.185Z","interval":3,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":2}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-07T10:52:32.185Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.558Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-07T10:52:32.273Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756788135145-18', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783610375016-1790433139825-w0zcd', 'Energy requirement for patients with Chronic Renal Failure:', '35 kcal/kg BW', 'Traditional and classic clinical board guidelines (such as the standard National Kidney Foundation KDOQI Guidelines) heavily establish 35 kcal/kg/day as the baseline calorie target for adults with chronic renal failure. This high energy requirement is necessary to ensure the body does not break down its own muscle tissue for energy (preventing protein-energy wasting), allowing the strictly restricted dietary protein to be used efficiently for tissue repair.

• 30 kcal/kg BW: This is the adjusted baseline commonly allocated specifically for elderly patients (typically 60 years or older) with CRF because they have a lower metabolic rate and a more sedentary lifestyle.', '["25 kcal/kg BW","30 kcal/kg BW","35 kcal/kg BW","40 kcal/kg BW"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-08T10:51:00.966Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.3,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-07T10:51:00.966Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.558Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-07T10:51:01.056Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756788200827-19', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783610375016-1790433139825-w0zcd', 'To be avoided in the diet management of celiac disease:', 'all of the above', 'Gluten is a protein composite containing prolamins (gliadin in wheat, secalin in rye, hordein in barley, and avenin in some oats) that trigger an immune-mediated enteropathy in genetically susceptible individuals expressing HLA-DQ2 or HLA-DQ8 molecules. Gastric and pancreatic enzymes cannot fully break down proline- and glutamine-rich prolamin peptides, allowing them to cross the lamina propria where tissue transglutaminase (tTG) deamidates them, provoking a destructive CD4+ T-helper cell response, crypt hyperplasia, and villous atrophy in the small intestine. Furthermore, commercial soy sauce traditionally utilizes wheat as a primary fermentation ingredient, making it a major source of hidden gluten that must also be avoided unless explicitly labeled gluten-free.', '["rye, oats, wheat, barley","high fat, rye, oats, wheat, barley","soy sauce, rye, oats, wheat, barley","all of the above"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-08T10:46:26.691Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.3,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-07T10:46:26.691Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.558Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-07T10:46:26.691Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756788266106-20', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783610375016-1790433139825-w0zcd', 'The following are dietary management for Wernicke-Korsakoff syndrome, EXCEPT:', 'High energy and protein tube feeding', 'Wernicke-Korsakoff syndrome (WKS) is a severe neurological disorder caused by a profound deficiency of thiamine (Vitamin B1), most commonly seen in chronic alcohol use disorder. Thiamine is vital for glucose metabolism in the brain. Because body stores of thiamine are limited, a lack of this vitamin leads to cellular brain damage, manifesting as acute confusion, vision abnormalities, and severe memory loss.

• Why C is the EXCEPT option (Incorrect management): Initiating immediate high-energy, heavy carbohydrate tube feeding or glucose loads before correcting a thiamine deficiency is incredibly dangerous. Introducing massive calories/glucose rapidly consumes the body''s tiny remaining stores of thiamine to process those nutrients. This can trigger massive lactic acidosis and cause a sudden, catastrophic worsening of the neurological syndrome.', '["Thiamine supplementation","Adequare hydration","Avoidance of alcohol","High energy and protein tube feeding"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-08T10:50:06.182Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.3,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-07T10:50:06.182Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.558Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-07T10:50:06.266Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756788327186-21', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783610375016-1790433139825-w0zcd', 'Which of the following is/are carcinogens?
I. salt-cured processed foods
II. tobacco
III. coffee and tea', 'I and II only', '• Tobacco is a well-established Group 1 carcinogen (known to cause cancer in humans).
• Salt-cured processed foods, such as Chinese-style salted fish and processed meats, are also classified as Group 1 carcinogens by the World Health Organization (WHO) due to the presence of nitrates and other cancer-causing agents formed during processing.
x Coffee and tea are not classified as carcinogens. In fact, moderate consumption of coffee and tea is often linked to a reduced risk of certain types of cancer due to their high antioxidant content.', '["I only","I and II only","All of the above","None of the above"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-08T10:52:04.334Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.3,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-07T10:52:04.334Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.558Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-07T10:52:04.411Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756788377451-22', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783610375016-1790433139825-w0zcd', 'A high-fat, low-carbohydrate diet given given to patients to help control seizures.', 'Ketogenic diet', 'A ketogenic (keto) diet is a therapeutic, high-fat, adequate-protein, and very low-carbohydrate diet medically used to manage drug-resistant epilepsy, especially in children. By severely limiting carbohydrates, it forces the body to burn fat for fuel instead of glucose. This metabolic shift produces ketone bodies (acetoacetate and beta-hydroxybutyrate), which cross the blood-brain barrier and exert an anticonvulsant, seizure-stabilizing effect on the brain.', '["Ketogenic diet","Ketoacidosis","Ketoanalogues","Acid ash diet"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-10T10:51:27.029Z","interval":3,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":2}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-07T10:51:27.029Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.558Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-07T10:51:27.119Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756788418400-23', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783610375016-1790433139825-w0zcd', 'Dietary management for fasting hypoglycemia:', 'high carbohydrates', 'Fasting hypoglycemia happens when the body''s baseline sugar stores run dry during long stretches without food. In medical nutrition therapy, the ultimate defense against fasting crashes is a high-carbohydrate diet (typically accounting for 60% to 65% of total daily calories). This ensures liver glycogen stores are completely saturated and continuously available to buffer blood sugar levels during a fast.', '["complex carbohydrates","small frequent meals","high carbohydrates","fiber"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-10T10:42:32.688Z","interval":3,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":2}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-07T10:42:32.688Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.558Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-07T10:42:32.778Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756788502745-24', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783610375016-1790433139825-w0zcd', 'A neurodegenerative disease characterized by slow and decreased movements, muscular rigidity, resting tremor, posture instability, and decreased dopamine transmission to basal ganglia:', 'Parkinson''s disease', 'Parkinson''s disease is a neurodegenerative disorder caused by the loss of dopamine-producing neurons in the brain. This loss disrupts signals to the basal ganglia, which normally controls smooth, coordinated muscle function.

The classic four cardinal motor signs of this disease: bradykinesia (slow movements), muscular rigidity (stiffness), resting tremors, and postural instability (poor balance)—all directly caused by the drop in dopamine.

• Multiple sclerosis is an autoimmune attack on the myelin sheath of nerves, causing numbness, tingling, and vision issues, not a dopamine deficit.
• Cerebral palsy is a non-progressive movement disorder caused by brain damage that occurs around the time of birth.
• Epilepsy is characterized by sudden, abnormal bursts of electrical activity in the brain that trigger seizures.', '["Multiple sclerosis","Parkinson''s disease","Cerebral palsy","Epilepsy"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-10T10:44:51.553Z","interval":3,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":2}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-07T10:44:51.553Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.558Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-07T10:44:51.553Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756368874793-0', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783610383500-1790433139830-giedk', 'A complete or high-quality protein can be described as:', 'Containing essential amino acids in proper amounts and proportions', 'In nutrition and dietetics board exams, a complete or high-quality protein is defined by its ability to supply all nine essential amino acids that the human body cannot synthesize on its own.

Beyond just presence, these amino acids must be provided in proper amounts and biological proportions that match human tissue requirements. This allows the body to seamlessly synthesize its own structural and metabolic proteins without encountering a "limiting amino acid." Classic examples include animal sources (meat, fish, poultry, eggs, and dairy) as well as whole soy.', '["Providing some essential amino acids only","Combining essential and non-essential amino acids","Capable of continuous storage","Containing essential amino acids in proper amounts and proportions"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'easy', 5, '', '{"dueDate":"2842-05-18T13:49:44.533Z","interval":297863,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":13}'::jsonb, '"2026-11-09T13:49:44.533Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.744Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-09T13:49:44.533Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756368971497-1', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783610383500-1790433139830-giedk', 'It manufacturers enzymes to digest all energy yielding nutrients and release sodium bicarbonate to neutralize chyme that enters the small intestine.', 'Pancreas', 'GI hormones stimulate the pancreas to release sodium bicarbonate, an alkali which neutralizes the acidic chyme, to neutralize hydrochloric acid to pH 5-8.', '["Pancreas","Liver","Kidney","Gallbladder"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 3, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-31T08:07:22.254Z","interval":5,"easeFactor":2.35,"repetitions":4}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-26T08:07:22.254Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.744Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-26T08:07:22.349Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756369020058-2', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783610383500-1790433139830-giedk', 'Sugar alcohols are made up of:', 'Hydrogenated dextrins', 'Industrially, many bulk sugar alcohols (polyols) used in sugar-free products are produced via the hydrogenation (adding hydrogen molecules under pressure) of corn starch derivatives.

When corn starch is broken down, it creates a mixture of short-chain glucose polymers called dextrins. When these dextrins are completely hydrogenated, the aldehyde groups on the sugars are reduced to alcohol groups, forming a sweet mixture of sugar alcohols known collectively as hydrogenated dextrins or hydrogenated starch hydrolysates.', '["Glucose polymers","Hydrogenated dextrins","Galactose and glucose","Glucose and fructose"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'easy', 5, '', '{"dueDate":"2737-07-28T13:49:19.134Z","interval":259583,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":13}'::jsonb, '"2026-11-09T13:49:19.134Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.744Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-09T13:49:19.134Z"'::jsonb)
