('anki-card-1758604685728-92', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783610421391-1790433139851-sw8ch', 'This is the preferred mode of feeding for patients with acute respiratory distress syndrome on prone position.*', 'Trophic feeding', 'For patients with Acute Respiratory Distress Syndrome (ARDS) placed in the prone position, trophic feeding (trickle feeding at minimal volumes, e.g., 10–20 mL/hr) is the preferred enteral mode to maintain gut integrity without inducing aspiration or high gastric residuals.', '["Bolus feeding","Intermittent feeding","Trophic feeding","Full feeding"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 1, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-09-26T14:32:12.431Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":0}'::jsonb, NULL, '"2026-09-26T14:32:28.720Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-09T06:00:24.111Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758604712195-93', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783610421391-1790433139851-sw8ch', 'This mineral protects the brain cells against the potential damage caused by free radicals. In patients with clinical depression, levels of this mineral is lowered. Research suggest for this mineral supplementation which can influence the effectiveness of antidepressants, such as imipramine.*', 'Zinc', 'Zinc is a critical trace mineral that protects brain cells against free radical oxidative damage, influences neurotransmitter pathways, and is frequently found at lowered levels in patients with clinical depression, where supplementation can enhance antidepressant efficacy.', '["Magnesium","Zinc","Calcium","Potassium"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 1, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-09-26T14:32:12.431Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":0}'::jsonb, NULL, '"2026-09-26T14:32:28.720Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-09T06:00:29.933Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758604725560-94', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783610421391-1790433139851-sw8ch', 'What pro-inflammatory cytokine marker is increased when patient is positive with covid virus?*', 'Interleukin-6', 'Interleukin-6 (IL-6) is a major pro-inflammatory cytokine and acute-phase reactant marker that becomes markedly elevated during severe viral infections such as COVID-19, signaling systemic hyper-inflammation.', '["Interleukin-6","interferon-alpha","tumor necrosis factor-alpha (TNF-α)","activated t-cells"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 1, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-09-26T14:32:12.431Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":0}'::jsonb, NULL, '"2026-09-26T14:32:28.720Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-09T06:00:39.545Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758604762657-95', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783610421391-1790433139851-sw8ch', 'What is the best strategy in feeding the child with cerebral palsy with exaggerated bite reflex?*', 'Once your child can grasp a spoon and guide it to his/her mouth, start introducing finger foods, such as crackers, grapes, and bread.', 'For children with cerebral palsy and oral-motor challenges like an exaggerated bite reflex, encouraging self-feeding and introducing finger foods once the child can grasp a spoon helps build chewing control and independence.', '["Once your child can grasp a spoon and guide it to his/her mouth, start introducing finger foods, such as crackers, grapes, and bread.","Smelling, touching, and learning about food helps children get a better sense of eating, and ultimately helps the feeding process.","The spoon should never be larger than the tongue of the child, girl or young person.","When the child is biting down, apply light pressure on the bottom of their chin and wait to feel their jaw drop and then remove the spoon or cup from their mouth."]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 1, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-09-26T14:32:12.431Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":0}'::jsonb, NULL, '"2026-09-26T14:32:28.720Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-09T05:59:45.010Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758604778039-96', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783610421391-1790433139851-sw8ch', 'What hormone that is stored in the pituitary gland that is defective in diabetes insipidus. This is needed for the fluid that''s filtered by the kidneys to go back into the bloodstream*', 'vasopressin', 'Diabetes insipidus is characterized by a deficiency of or resistance to vasopressin (antidiuretic hormone, ADH), a hormone stored in the posterior pituitary gland that enables the kidneys to reabsorb filtered water back into the bloodstream.', '["prolactin","renin","vasopressin","thyroid-stimulating hormone"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 1, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-09-26T14:32:12.431Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":0}'::jsonb, NULL, '"2026-09-26T14:32:28.720Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-09T06:00:43.256Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758604806423-97', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783610421391-1790433139851-sw8ch', 'For people taking thyroxine for treatment of hyperthyroidism, which of the following foods interfere with thyroxine absorption?*', 'soya', 'For patients taking thyroid hormone replacement (thyroxine / levothyroxine) for hypothyroidism, dietary soya (soy protein and soy milk) can bind thyroid hormones and significantly interfere with thyroxine absorption in the gastrointestinal tract.', '["soya","eggs","gluten-free","sugar"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 1, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-09-26T14:32:12.431Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":0}'::jsonb, NULL, '"2026-09-26T14:32:28.720Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-09T05:59:49.071Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758604827273-98', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783610421391-1790433139851-sw8ch', 'Which of the following is NOT a known advantage of including fiber in the diet?*', 'Reduced risk of brain cancer.', 'Dietary fiber offers numerous proven health benefits, including preventing constipation, reducing hunger, and lowering the risk of colorectal cancer and cardiovascular disease. It is not specifically linked to a reduced risk of brain cancer, making this the correct exception.', '["Reduced risk of brain cancer.","Prevents constipation and diarrhea.","Reduces hunger by making you feel full longer.","Reduces risk of colon cancer"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 1, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-09-26T14:32:12.431Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":0}'::jsonb, NULL, '"2026-09-26T14:32:28.720Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-09T05:59:53.277Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758604860653-99', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783610421391-1790433139851-sw8ch', 'Patient CG is referred for nutrition consult. Antropometric measurements: height = 160cm, weight = 80kg, waist circumference = 100cm Biochemical: hyperuricemia, dyslipidemia, impaired glucose tolerance Clinical: bloatedness, joint painsDietary: Orders food delivery, less fruits and vegetable
What is the priority nutrition problem for this case as short term goal?*', 'Poor food choices', 'Based on the patient''s intake history (ordering frequent food delivery, consuming fewer fruits and vegetables) alongside clinical biomarkers, the immediate modifiable behavioral priority for a short-term nutrition intervention goal is addressing poor food choices.', '["Overweight","Abnormal laboratory results","Inconsistent physical symptoms","Poor food choices"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 1, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-09-26T14:32:12.431Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":0}'::jsonb, NULL, '"2026-09-26T14:32:28.720Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-09T06:01:19.137Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758443779539-0', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783610430222-1790433140271-gx1a3', 'A genetic disorder characterized by a constant sense of hunger or hyperphagia that usually begins at two years of age.*', 'Prader-Willi Syndrome', 'Prader-Willi Syndrome (PWS) is a complex genetic disorder caused by a lack of expression of paternally inherited genes on chromosome 15.

While infants with PWS typically suffer from poor muscle tone (hypotonia) and poor sucking reflexes that cause feeding difficulties, they transition into a distinct second phase between 2 to 4 years of age. During this stage, a chronic, unrelenting, and insatiable sense of hunger (hyperphagia) develops due to hypothalamic dysfunction. Without strict environmental food controls, this drives severe early-childhood obesity.

• Phenylketonuria: An inborn error of metabolism where the body cannot break down the amino acid phenylalanine. It leads to intellectual disabilities and neurological issues if untreated, not childhood hyperphagia.
• G6PD Deficiency: An enzyme deficiency that triggers the premature breakdown of red blood cells (hemolysis) upon exposure to specific foods (like fava beans) or medications, having no impact on appetite regulation.
• Bardet-Biedl Syndrome: While this genetic disorder also features childhood obesity and hyperphagia, its insatiable hunger typically begins much later in childhood and is classically accompanied by structural features like extra fingers or toes (polydactyly) and progressive vision loss.', '["Bardet-Biedl Syndrome","Phenylketonuria","Prader-Willi Syndrome","G6PD"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-09T10:21:37.305Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-08T10:21:37.305Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.208Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-08T10:21:37.445Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758443795022-1', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783610430222-1790433140271-gx1a3', 'A 35-year old Filipina weighs 130lbs with a height measurement of 5 feet. What is her BMI?*', 'BMI 25.4, Obese I', 'In this question, apply the specific Asia-Pacific BMI Classification Guidelines used for Asian populations, which have lower cut-offs due to a higher risk of cardiovascular disease and type 2 diabetes at lower body sizes.

1. Calculate the BMI.
• Convert weight: 130 lbs / 2.2 = 59.09 kg
• Convert height: 5 feet = 60 inches = 1.524 meters
• Calculate BMI:
BMI = 59.09 / (1.524²) = 25.4 kg/m²

2. The Asia-Pacific Classification 
• Underweight: Below 18.5 kg/m² (Risk of nutritional deficiency and osteoporosis)
• Normal Range: 18.5 – 22.9 kg/m² (Low health risk)
• Overweight (At Risk): 23.0 – 24.9 kg/m² (Moderate risk for conditions like type 2 diabetes and high blood pressure)
• Obese Class I: 25.0 – 29.9 kg/m² (High metabolic and cardiovascular risk)
• Obese Class II: ≥ 30.0 kg/m² (Very high risk)

Because a value of 25.4 crosses the 25.0 threshold, the patient is clinically classified as Obese Class I under the regional guidelines rather than just overweight.', '["BMI 25.4, Overweight","BMI 25.4, Obese I","BMI 23, Normal","BMI 23, Overweight"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-10T07:45:24.456Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.3,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-09T07:45:24.456Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.208Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-09T07:45:24.608Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758443811821-2', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783610430222-1790433140271-gx1a3', 'The hormone _________ decreases appetite and increases the dissipation of energy through heat production also known as thermogenesis.*', 'Leptin', 'Leptin is a peptide hormone produced and secreted by adipose tissue (fat cells) that acts directly on the hypothalamus in the brain. It functions as a long-term regulator of energy balance by decreasing appetite (inducing satiety) and simultaneously increasing energy expenditure.

Leptin stimulates the sympathetic nervous system, which triggers the upregulation of uncoupling proteins (like UCP1) in brown adipose tissue. This process increases the dissipation of stored energy directly as heat (thermogenesis) rather than storing it as fat.

• Cortisol: A glucocorticoid stress hormone that typically increases appetite, favors visceral fat storage, and slows down long-term thermogenic energy expenditure.
• Ghrelin: Known as the "hunger hormone," ghrelin is produced primarily by the stomach fundus. It increases appetite and promotes energy storage, which is the exact opposite of leptin.
• Neuropeptide Y: A potent neurotransmitter in the brain that actively stimulates food intake (an orexigenic effect) and decreases energy expenditure.', '["Leptin","Neuropeptide Y","Ghrelin","Cortisol"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-09T14:19:19.905Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-08T14:19:19.905Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.208Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-08T14:19:19.988Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758443829037-3', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783610430222-1790433140271-gx1a3', 'After a meal, our body’s energy expenditure increases for some time to breakdown the food into nutrients that the body can utilize and store. This process is known as*', 'Thermogenic Effect of Food', 'The Thermogenic Effect of Food (TEF)—also traditionally referred to as the Specific Dynamic Action (SDA) of food—is the exact physiological process where energy expenditure rises above baseline for several hours following a meal. This metabolic energy is spent on the mechanical and chemical work of digesting, absorbing, transporting, metabolizing, and storing nutrients.

TEF accounts for roughly 10% of total daily energy expenditure in a standard diet. Protein triggers the highest thermic response (requiring 20–30% of its own energy to process), followed by carbohydrates (5–15%), and fats (0–3%).

*While TEF is a form of thermogenesis, "thermogenesis" on its own is a wide category that also includes non-shivering thermogenesis (heat production from brown fat) and exercise-induced thermogenesis. Thermogenic Effect of Food (TEF) is the precise, targeted metabolic term for post-meal energy expenditure.', '["Metabolism","Digestion","Thermogenesis","Thermogenic Effect of Food"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-10T08:14:41.334Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.3,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-09T08:14:41.334Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.208Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-09T08:14:41.425Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758443843984-4', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783610430222-1790433140271-gx1a3', 'Abundant uncoupling proteins in the body prevents weight gain. Which type of fat releases stored energy and is considered metabolically active?*', 'Brown adipose tissue', 'Brown adipose tissue (BAT) contains a very high density of mitochondria packed with Uncoupling Protein 1 (also known as thermogenin). This protein uncouples the respiratory chain from ATP synthesis, causing the fat tissue to burn through stored lipids and glucose to release stored energy directly as heat (non-shivering thermogenesis) rather than storing it as chemical energy.

Because BAT actively expends calories to generate heat, an abundance or activation of brown fat significantly increases basal metabolic rate and helps prevent weight gain and metabolic dysfunction.

• Beige adipose tissue: While beige fat cells also express UCP1 and burn energy, they are individual brown-like cells that develop within white adipose tissue clusters (a process called "browning") rather than forming the distinct, highly abundant classic brown fat depots primarily responsible for massive thermogenic dissipation.
• Yellow adipose tissue: This is simply an alternative structural name for standard adult white fat, colored yellow due to the accumulation of dietary carotenes.
• White adipose tissue: The primary site for long-term energy storage (triglycerides). White fat is not built for heat generation and expands during weight gain rather than preventing it.', '["Brown adipose tissue","Beige adipose tissue","Yellow adipose tissue","White adipose tissue"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-10T07:25:55.909Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.3,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-09T07:25:55.909Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.208Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-09T07:25:56.065Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758444118173-5', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783610430222-1790433140271-gx1a3', 'A dietitian needs to determine a patient’s risk for cardiovascular disease. The following anthropometric measurements must be assessed except*', 'Exercise history', 'Exercise history is a behavioral or lifestyle component of a patient''s personal history, not a physical bodily measurement.

Anthropometric measurements are non-invasive, quantitative, physical measurements used to assess the size, shape, and composition of the human body.', '["Height and weight","Waist Circumference","Exercise history","Body fat analysis"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-09T10:09:06.592Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-08T10:09:06.592Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.208Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-08T10:09:06.736Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758444132354-6', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783610430222-1790433140271-gx1a3', 'A 45 years old female has a WHR of 0.9 is*', 'All of the above', 'According to the World Health Organization (WHO), a Waist-to-Hip Ratio (WHR) above 0.85 for females signifies abdominal (visceral) obesity. A 45-year-old female presenting with a WHR of 0.9 falls into a high-risk classification (apple-shaped fat distribution).

Visceral fat accumulation surrounding the internal organs is highly metabolically active. It releases excess free fatty acids and inflammatory cytokines directly into the portal circulation, precipitating:
	• Insulin resistance, which significantly increases the risk for Diabetes mellitus.
	• Altered hepatic lipid synthesis, leading directly to elevated blood lipids, specifically placing the patient at risk for high triglycerides and hypercholesterolemia.', '["At risk for high triglycerides","At risk for hypercholesterolemia","At risk for diabetes mellitus","All of the above"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-09T11:33:45.253Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-08T11:33:45.253Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.208Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-08T11:33:45.341Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758444150672-7', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783610430222-1790433140271-gx1a3', 'The following are comorbidities related to obesity except*', 'Psoriasis', 'Psoriasis is a primary autoimmune skin disease characterized by rapid skin cell buildup that leads to scaling and inflammation. While obesity is a strong independent risk factor that can aggravate or worsen psoriasis through shared systemic inflammatory pathways, psoriasis itself is classified primarily as an autoimmune/genetic condition rather than a direct mechanical or metabolic comorbidity caused by obesity.

	• Sleep apnea: Heavily driven by mechanical forces; excess fat deposits around the upper airway and neck physically compress the trachea during sleep, causing obstructive sleep apnea.
	• Osteoarthritis: Driven by both increased mechanical wear-and-tear on weight-bearing joints (such as knees and hips) and low-grade systemic inflammation that degrades cartilage.
	• Gallstones: Driven by metabolic shifts; obesity increases hepatic synthesis and secretion of cholesterol, supersaturating the bile and causing cholesterol gallstones to form.', '["Sleep apnea","Gallstones","Psoriasis","Osteoarthritis"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-09T13:01:30.213Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-08T13:01:30.213Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.208Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-08T13:01:30.301Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758444166052-8', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783610430222-1790433140271-gx1a3', 'Choose the statement that best represents the patient’s readiness for lifestyle change*', '“Please recommend some tips on how to do grocery shopping for fresh produce.”', 'In behavioral counseling—specifically the Transtheoretical Model (Stages of Change)—this statement indicates that the patient has moved past merely thinking about a change and is actively preparing to execute it. By asking for specific, actionable grocery shopping strategies, they are demonstrating a high level of autonomous motivation and a direct readiness to implement lifestyle changes.', '["“I need to lose weight.”","“My doctor says I need to consult a dietitian.”","“How do I cook ginisang munggo?”","“Please recommend some tips on how to do grocery shopping for fresh produce.”"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-09T16:05:54.363Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-08T16:05:54.363Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.208Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-08T16:05:54.499Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758444250766-9', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783610430222-1790433140271-gx1a3', 'Psychological assessment is important in helping patients in their weight loss journey. The RND must include the following psychological factors during the patient interview except*', 'Diet history', '', '["Diet history","Support system","Motivation for weight loss","Attitudes towards physical activity"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-09T15:56:51.929Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-08T15:56:51.929Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.208Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-08T15:56:52.068Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758444265649-10', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783610430222-1790433140271-gx1a3', 'A 60kg female patient with comorbidities needs to lose weight. What is the minimum target weight loss from baseline in 6 months?*', '3kg', 'According to the World Health Organization (WHO) and international clinical obesity guidelines (such as those outlined by the American Heart Association (AHA), American College of Cardiology (ACC), and MIMS Philippines), the standard, primary therapeutic recommendation for an adult with obesity or overweight and comorbidities is to target a minimum initial weight loss of 5% of their baseline body weight over a 6-month period. Achieving a modest 5% reduction is highly significant because it immediately triggers measurable improvements in glycemic control, blood pressure, and cardiovascular risk factors.

Patient Baseline Weight = 60 kg
Minimum Clinical Target Percentage = 5% (0.05)

Minimum Target Weight Loss = 60 kg x 0.05 = 3 kg', '["6kg","3kg","10kg","5kg"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-10T08:37:53.014Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.3,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-09T08:37:53.014Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.208Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-09T08:37:53.014Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758444369379-11', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783610430222-1790433140271-gx1a3', 'The following recommendations for weight loss must be given by the RND except*', 'Take Orlistat 2 to 3 times a day with meals', 'Orlistat is a potent anti-obesity pharmacological drug. Under the legal and professional scope of practice for a Registered Nutritionist-Dietitian (RND) in clinical and public health frameworks, a dietitian is strictly authorized to provide Medical Nutrition Therapy (MNT), structural caloric adjustments, lifestyle modifications, and behavioral counseling. An RND cannot prescribe medications or specify pharmaceutical drug dosages. The prescription, initiation, and dosing oversight of anti-obesity drugs like Orlistat must be managed exclusively by a licensed physician or endocrinologist.', '["At least 30 minutes of moderate physical activity daily","Take Orlistat 2 to 3 times a day with meals","Decrease Total Energy Requirement by 500 Kcal per day","Self-monitoring"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-09T18:01:15.790Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-08T18:01:15.790Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.208Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-08T18:01:15.873Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758444383143-12', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783610430222-1790433140271-gx1a3', 'Choose the strategies that do not promote behavior modification*', 'Social media, watching Netflix, rest', 'Social media, watching Netflix, rest', '["Spending time in nature, walking, sleeping","Family time, prayer, journaling","Delayed gratification, meditation, breathing exercises","Social media, watching Netflix, rest"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-09T15:06:13.196Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-08T15:06:13.196Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.208Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-08T12:24:43.573Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758444394876-13', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783610430222-1790433140271-gx1a3', 'Which weight loss medications are approved by the US-FDA?*', 'Phentermine, orlistat, topamirate', 'There are well-established medications officially approved by the US Food and Drug Administration (FDA) for weight management:
	• Phentermine: Approved as a short-term, standalone appetite suppressant.
	• Orlistat: Approved for long-term weight management (available as prescription Xenical or over-the-counter Alli) by blocking intestinal fat absorption.
	• Topiramate: Approved by the FDA as a fixed-dose combination pill with phentermine (marketed under the brand name Qsymia) for chronic weight management.

Other Choices:
• Phenylpropanolamine: This drug was completely banned and ordered removed from the market by the FDA due to an increased risk of hemorrhagic stroke.
• Sibutramine: Originally approved for weight loss under the name Meridia, it was completely withdrawn from the market due to its association with an increased risk of heart attacks and strokes.
• Ibuprofen: This is a standard nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drug (NSAID) used to relieve pain and reduce fever, carrying no clinical indication or approval for weight loss.
• Aminorex: An amphetamine-like stimulant that was never FDA-approved for weight loss and was withdrawn globally after it was found to cause severe pulmonary hypertension.', '["Phentermine, phenylpropanolamine, orlistat","Phenylpropanolamine, topamirate, sibutramine","Phentermine, orlistat, topamirate","Ibuprofen, aminorex, orlistat"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-10T08:30:41.492Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.3,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-09T08:30:41.492Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.208Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-09T08:30:41.492Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758444402309-14', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783610430222-1790433140271-gx1a3', 'Criteria for bariatric surgery:*', 'All of the above', 'Historically, candidate selection for metabolic and bariatric surgery has been anchored on established body size and health criteria.
	• A BMI between 35 and 39.9 with significant medical comorbidities (such as type 2 diabetes, severe sleep apnea, or cardiovascular disease) serves as a primary qualification parameter.
	• A BMI of 40 or greater represents severe obesity and satisfies the selection criteria independently, with or without secondary medical complications.
	• A BMI of 36 paired with Atherosclerosis fits precisely into the definition of the other option (a BMI above 35 accompanied by a serious cardiovascular comorbidity).

Clinical Update

While older textbook questions focus heavily on this classic "35 with comorbidities / 40 baseline" framework, updated international surgical consensus guidelines from the American Society for Metabolic and Bariatric Surgery (ASMBS) and IFSO have expanded eligibility. Modern criteria now recommend bariatric surgery for anyone with a BMI over 35 regardless of comorbidities, and down to a BMI of 30 for individuals struggling with metabolic diseases like uncontrolled type 2 diabetes.', '["BMI greater than 40","BMI greater than 35 with comorbidities","BMI 36 with Atherosclerosis","All of the above"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-09T14:13:53.399Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-08T14:13:53.399Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.208Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-08T14:13:53.483Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758444416725-15', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783610430222-1790433140271-gx1a3', 'This type of Bariatric Surgery requires the placement of a foreign object in the form of a band*', 'LAGB', 'LAGB stands for Laparoscopic Adjustable Gastric Banding (commonly referred to by its commercial name, the Lap-Band).

This specific bariatric procedure involves placing an inflatable, medical-grade silicone band entirely around the upper portion of the stomach. This creates a tiny stomach pouch that drastically restricts the volume of food a patient can eat at one time, forcing early satiety without removing any actual gastric tissue.

• Roux-en-Y Gastric Bypass: A permanent, combination surgery where the stomach is stapled to create a small pouch and the small intestine is physically rerouted, requiring no baseline foreign bands.
• BPD/DS: Biliopancreatic Diversion with Duodenal Switch is a complex, mostly malabsorptive surgery that resects a large portion of the stomach and drastically bypasses the small intestine.
• Gastric Restrictive Procedure: This is the general umbrella category term for any surgery that limits stomach capacity (including the sleeve and vertical banded gastroplasty), whereas LAGB is the specific, targeted procedure abbreviation requested by the question.', '["Roux-en-Y Gastric Bypass","LAGB","Gastric Restrictive Procedure","BPD/DS"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-10T08:12:42.527Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.3,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-09T08:12:42.527Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.208Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-09T08:12:42.623Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758444434891-16', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783610430222-1790433140271-gx1a3', 'Combination procedure in bariatric surgery results to hormonal influences. The production of Ghrelin hormone is likewise decreased resulting to*', 'Induced satiety', 'Ghrelin is known as the orexigenic hormone (hunger hormone) that stimulates appetite and food intake. Because bariatric procedures (such as a sleeve gastrectomy or gastric bypass) involve anatomical resection or exclusion of the gastric fundus—where the vast majority of ghrelin is produced—the circulating levels of this hormone drop sharply post-surgery.

A significant reduction in ghrelin eliminates the biological signal that triggers hunger, thereby inducing satiety (prolonged feelings of fullness and diminished appetite).', '["Increased appetite","Orexigenic effect","Dumping syndrome","Induced satiety"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-09T09:58:13.544Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-08T09:58:13.544Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.208Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-08T09:58:13.680Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758444449056-17', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783610430222-1790433140271-gx1a3', 'Which type of bariatric surgery can possibly exacerbate symptoms of Gastroesophageal Reflux?*', 'Sleeve Gastrectomy', 'A Sleeve Gastrectomy (or vertical sleeve gastrectomy) involves removing approximately 80% of the stomach, converting the remaining portion into a narrow, tube-like structure. This anatomical change drastically increases internal pressure within the stomach tube.

The high intragastric pressure, paired with changes to the angle where the esophagus meets the stomach (the angle of His) and potential weakening of the lower esophageal sphincter (LES), commonly causes de novo (new-onset) acid reflux or severely exacerbates preexisting symptoms of Gastroesophageal Reflux Disease (GERD).

• Roux-en-Y Gastric Bypass: This is considered the gold-standard bariatric procedure for patients with severe GERD. Because it separates the acid-producing cells into a bypassed pouch and creates a low-pressure drainage system, it actively cures or significantly improves reflux symptoms. It is frequently used as a corrective surgery for sleeve patients who develop uncontrollable reflux.', '["Laparoscopic Adjustable Gastric Banding","Roux-en-Y Gastric Bypass","Biliopancreatic Diversion with Duodenal Switch","Sleeve Gastrectomy"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-10T08:39:23.171Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.3,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-09T08:39:23.171Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.208Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-09T08:39:23.171Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758444468172-18', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783610430222-1790433140271-gx1a3', 'The Biliopancreatic Diversion with Duodenal Switch procedure results to some nutrition-related conditions. Choose one that does not apply.*', 'Indigestion and bloating', 'Indigestion and bloating are general, functional upper-GI symptoms. Since the pyloric valve is fully preserved during a Duodenal Switch procedure, normal gastric emptying and stomach digestion are maintained, making classic upper indigestion an inaccurate description of the operation''s hallmark downstream complications.', '["Severe malabsorption of protein","Severe malabsorption of macronutrients and micronutrients","Changes in gut hormones to reduce appetite","Indigestion and bloating"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-10T08:07:50.222Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.3,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-09T08:07:50.222Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.208Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-09T08:07:50.376Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758444482756-19', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783610430222-1790433140271-gx1a3', 'The following are the criteria for Anorexia Nervosa except*', 'Recurrent episodes of binge eating within 2 hours', 'Recurrently consuming large amounts of food in a discrete period of time (e.g., within a 2-hour window) accompanied by a perceived lack of control is the fundamental, defining characteristic of Bulimia Nervosa or Binge Eating Disorder. It does not serve as a baseline criterion for Anorexia Nervosa.', '["Intense fear of weight gain","Amenorrhea","Recurrent episodes of binge eating within 2 hours","Denial"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-09T15:17:27.180Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-08T15:17:27.180Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.208Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-08T15:17:27.314Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758444494570-20', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783610430222-1790433140271-gx1a3', 'A patient suffering from Bulimia Nervosa is at risk of colon injury due to*', 'Laxative abuse', 'Individuals with Bulimia Nervosa frequently misuse stimulant laxatives to control weight after a binge eating episode. Chronic laxative abuse directly injures the lining of the large intestine and damages the enteric nerves controlling bowel motility.

This toxic damage can progress to a severe, structurally irreversible clinical condition called cathartic colon syndrome. In cathartic colon, the large intestine loses its neuromuscular tone, becomes massively dilated, and can no longer propel stool on its own, increasing the risk of chronic constipation, fecal impaction, and even colon perforation.', '["Laxative abuse","Purging","Binge eating episodes","Lack of fiber intake"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-10T08:36:18.747Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.3,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-09T08:36:18.747Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.208Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-09T08:36:18.747Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758444510137-21', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783610430222-1790433140271-gx1a3', 'When counseling a patient with Bulimia Nervosa the following must be encouraged except*', 'Suggest foods for binge eating episode', 'In clinical psychological and nutritional counseling for Bulimia Nervosa, a Registered Nutritionist-Dietitian (RND) must never suggest, plan, or provide food options for a binge eating episode. The primary goal of Medical Nutrition Therapy (MNT) is to break the restrict-binge-purge cycle. Actively giving food ideas for a binge would validate and reinforce the disordered behavior.

	• Identify food triggers: Pinpointing which foods, emotions, or social scenarios spark an overwhelming urge to binge helps the patient develop healthy, non-food coping mechanisms.
	• Personalize eating pattern: Establishing a highly structured, individualized meal plan (such as eating 3 regular meals and 2–3 snacks at predictable times) keeps the patient physically satisfied, preventing the extreme biological hunger that drives binging.
	• Embrace all food and achieve comfort: Dietitians work to dismantle the toxic "good food vs. bad food" mentality common in bulimia. Helping a patient comfortably reintroduce former fear foods without guilt reduces the psychological drive to binge on them.', '["Identify food triggers","Embrace all food and achieve comfort","Personalize eating pattern","Suggest foods for binge eating episode"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-09T14:05:28.899Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-08T14:05:28.899Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.208Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-08T14:05:28.983Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758444533370-22', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783610430222-1790433140271-gx1a3', 'A 16 year-old patient with normal weight for height and age verbalizes that she wants to die because she is very fat. She believes that people are always staring at her because she is ugly. What should the RND do?*', 'Refer to a psychiatrist immediately', 'The patient has explicitly verbalized an active intent to die ("wants to die"). In any clinical setting, an explicit statement of suicidal ideation is a critical psychiatric emergency that completely overrides standard dietary counseling. The immediate, non-negotiable step is to ensure patient safety by initiating an immediate referral to a psychiatrist or mental health crisis professional.

The patient''s severe body image distortion (believing she is "very fat" despite a normal weight-for-height profile) combined with ideas of reference (believing "people are always staring at her") are severe psychological indicators highly consistent with severe eating disorders (such as Anorexia Nervosa) or Body Dysmorphic Disorder (BDD). These conditions require specialized psychiatric stabilization before any structured nutritional therapy can be effective.', '["Refer to a psychiatrist immediately","Tell the patient that she is not overweight","Tell the patient that she is experiencing delusions","Give nutrition education"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-09T16:12:28.789Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-08T16:12:28.789Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.208Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-08T16:12:28.928Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758444557668-23', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783610430222-1790433140271-gx1a3', 'A patient manifesting the following symptoms has a diagnosis of Symptoms: Repeated chewing and spitting of food, bingeing and purging once a week for 3 weeks, exercising 3 hours daily*', 'Eating Disorders Not Otherwise Specified (EDNOS)', 'To meet the strict, formal diagnostic criteria for Bulimia Nervosa, a patient must engage in binge eating and inappropriate compensatory behaviors (purging or excessive exercise) at an average frequency of at least once a week for at least 3 months.

Because this patient''s binge/purge episodes have only been occurring for 3 weeks (subthreshold duration), they do not meet the full, standard timeline for Bulimia Nervosa. Under standard board exam terminology, cases that display clinically significant disordered eating patterns but fall short of the exact diagnostic durations or specific criteria are categorized under EDNOS (Eating Disorders Not Otherwise Specified). (Note: In modern clinical DSM-5 manuals, this category is updated as OSFED—Other Specified Feeding and Eating Disorder).

The inclusion of repeated chewing and spitting of food is another classic, textbook hallmark behavior frequently categorized under the EDNOS/OSFED umbrella rather than standing as its own distinct standalone diagnosis.', '["Bulimia Nervosa","Anorexia Nervosa","Body Image Distortion","Eating Disorders Not Otherwise Specified (EDNOS)"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-10T08:14:06.757Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.3,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-09T08:14:06.757Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.208Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-09T08:14:06.855Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758444679380-24', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783610430222-1790433140271-gx1a3', 'Muscle Dysmorphia also known as Bigorexia is characterized by the following manifestations except*', 'Eating at night', 'Eating at night (or Night Eating Syndrome) is an independent eating pattern specification characterized by evening hyperphagia or waking up to consume food during the night. It is not an established diagnostic hallmark or baseline manifestation of Muscle Dysmorphia.

Classic Manifestations of Bigorexia
	• Constant body checking: Individuals with muscle dysmorphia exhibit severe, repetitive body checking habits. This includes spending hours staring in the mirror, constantly using a tape measure to track muscle symmetry, or weighing themselves multiple times a day.
	• A kind of obsessive-compulsive disorder: Under the official DSM-5 criteria, muscle dysmorphia is classified as a specific subtype of Body Dysmorphic Disorder (BDD), which sits directly under the spectrum of Obsessive-Compulsive and Related Disorders. The condition drives unrelenting, obsessive thoughts regarding physical inadequacy.
	• Excessive exercise even when injured: A hallmark behavioral symptom is compulsive exercise. The psychological drive to build lean muscle mass is so extreme that the individual will rigidly lift weights or complete high-intensity training sessions even when suffering from clear physical illnesses, fractures, or severe muscular injuries.', '["A kind of obsessive-compulsive disorder","Constant body checking","Eating at night","Excessive exercise even when injured"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-09T17:44:28.849Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-08T17:44:28.849Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.208Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-08T17:44:28.932Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758444693395-25', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783610430222-1790433140271-gx1a3', 'The following are consequences of malnutrition in relation to immune response during infection except*', 'Poor appetite and fatigue', 'While poor appetite and fatigue are highly common, classic systemic and metabolic symptoms of malnutrition and active infections, they are general physiological responses rather than specific functional alterations of the immune system itself.

	• Reduced production of antibodies: Protein-energy malnutrition directly causes lymphoid tissue atrophy (such as the thymus and bone marrow), which severely impairs B-cell differentiation and downregulates the production of crucial secretory immunoglobulins (like IgA) and antibodies needed to fight pathogens.
	• Poor wound healing: Proper wound healing is an immune-mediated process reliant on cellular immunity. Malnutrition blunts macrophage activation, slows down granulomatous inflammation, and reduces cytokine orchestration, which prevents efficient tissue repair and barrier defense restoration.
	• Alterations in gut microbiome: The gut microbiome is intimately intertwined with mucosal immunity. Nutrient deficiencies compromise intestinal barrier function and mucosal nutrient-sensing receptors, disrupting the commensal gut microbiota balance and compounding immune dysfunction.', '["Poor appetite and fatigue","Alterations in gut microbiome","Poor wound healing","Reduced production of antibodies"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-10T07:33:35.789Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.3,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-09T07:33:35.789Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.208Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-09T07:33:35.924Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758444725242-26', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783610430222-1790433140271-gx1a3', 'A patient with an oral temperature of 38.8 degrees Celsius needs a proper nutrition intervention to support metabolic changes in the body. What should the RND do?*', 'Give additional high biologic value protein and fluids', 'An oral temperature of 38.8°C indicates an active clinical fever (febrile state).

For every 1°C increase in core body temperature above normal, the Basal Metabolic Rate (BMR) spikes by approximately 13%. This rapid metabolic shift accelerates tissue breakdown, causing significant lean muscle mass degradation (hypercatabolism) and high fluid loss via sweating and respiration.

To counteract nitrogen wasting and maintain cellular integrity, the Registered Nutritionist-Dietitian (RND) must increase the patient''s intake of high biological value (HBV) proteins (such as eggs, milk, fish, and lean poultry), which are efficiently utilized by the body for tissue repair and immune synthesis. Concurrently, aggressive fluid replenishment is a non-negotiable priority to prevent dehydration and support cellular homeostasis.', '["Give 100% fruit juice","Increase intake of fats to compensate for decreased adiposity","Give additional high biologic value protein and fluids","Give salabat because ginger has antimicrobial properties"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-09T16:22:44.085Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-08T16:22:44.085Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.208Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-08T16:22:44.222Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758444741893-27', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783610430222-1790433140271-gx1a3', 'Diarrhea with occasional constipation, vague abdominal pain and occasional rectal bleeding are symptoms of Amoebiasis. What type of diet must be prescribed by the RND?*', 'Liquid, bland and low residue', 'Alternating diarrhea/constipation, vague abdominal pain, and blood in the stool is the classic presentation of intestinal Amoebiasis (caused by Entamoeba histolytica). Dietary mechanisms include:

	• Low Residue (Low Fiber): Minimizes fecal volume and eliminates roughage that could mechanically scratch or irritate the already inflamed, ulcerated colonic mucosa.
	• Bland: Excludes chemical irritants such as harsh spices, gastric stimulants, and heavy seasonings that can exacerbate abdominal cramping.
	• Liquid/Fluid Modification: Helps rest the gastrointestinal tract during the acute phase while actively replenishing vital fluids and electrolytes lost through diarrheal episodes to prevent dehydration.', '["High protein diet","Liquid, bland and low residue","High calorie, raw foods allowed as tolerated","Low fat diet"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-09T14:12:05.312Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-08T14:12:05.312Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.208Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-08T14:12:05.399Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758444757142-28', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783610430222-1790433140271-gx1a3', 'The following micronutrients may be deficient among children with Ascariasis lumbricoides*', 'Iron and Vitamin C', 'Ascaris lumbricoides (giant intestinal roundworms) thrive in the human small intestine, where they actively compete with the host child for nutrients.

	• Iron: Intestinal parasite infections damage the gut mucosa, impair absorption, and cause microscopic blood loss, heavily precipitating iron-deficiency anemia in children.
	• Vitamin C: Intestinal inflammation and a compromised gut lining impair the absorption of water-soluble vitamins like Vitamin C. Because Vitamin C is chemically required to reduce ferric iron to ferrous iron for absorption in the gut, a concurrent drop in Vitamin C worsens the child''s iron deficiency.

• Zinc and Vitamin C: While zinc deficiency is common in soil-transmitted helminth infections, it is paired with Vitamin C in option D, whereas Iron and Vitamin C is the specific paired textbook key used for this question format.', '["Iron and Vitamin C","Calcium and Vitamin D","Zinc and Vitamin C","Iron and Calcium"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-10T07:27:02.842Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.3,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-09T07:27:02.842Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.208Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-09T07:27:03.022Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758444771675-29', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783610430222-1790433140271-gx1a3', 'Dietary management for Malaria includes a high protein and high carbohydrate diet by increases total caloric intake by how much?*

10%
25%
30%
50%', '50%', 'Plasmodium species infection triggers the release of pro-inflammatory cytokines such as tumor necrosis factor-alpha (TNF-alpha), interleukin-1 (IL-1), and interleukin-6 (IL-6), which upregulate hepatic acute-phase protein synthesis and accelerate whole-body protein degradation. To mitigate this hypercatabolic response, medical nutrition therapy dictates an increase in total resting energy expenditure (REE) by approximately 50%, alongside high carbohydrate provision to spare lean body mass and high protein delivery (1.5 to 2.0 g/kg/day) to support tissue repair and immune cell proliferation.', '["50%","30%","25%","10%"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-09T13:27:00.474Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-08T13:27:00.474Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.208Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-08T13:27:00.559Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758444796906-30', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783610430222-1790433140271-gx1a3', 'Cholera is an acute dehydrating disease caused by Vibrio Cholerae. It’s symptoms include profuse rice water-like, mucoid stools with fish odor. The primary nutrition intervention goal is*', 'Intravenous fluid and electrolyte replacement', 'In acute Cholera, the sheer volume of profuse, watery stools can deplete a patient''s entire blood volume and electrolytes within hours, leading to severe hypovolemic shock and death. Therefore, the immediate, life-saving primary intervention goal is the rapid replenishment of fluid and electrolyte losses, typically achieved via intravenous (IV) fluids for severe cases or Oral Rehydration Salts (ORS) for mild-to-moderate dehydration.

While introducing a soft, bland, or high-calorie diet to correct nutrient deficiencies is important for long-term recovery once the gut stabilizes, these nutritional interventions are completely impossible and ineffective during the acute phase until the patient is hemodynamically stable and rehydrated.', '["Soft and bland diet","Correct nutrient deficiencies","High caloric diet","Intravenous fluid and electrolyte replacement"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-10T08:25:44.043Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.3,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-09T08:25:44.043Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.208Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-09T08:25:44.043Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758444834722-31', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783610430222-1790433140271-gx1a3', 'A patient presents with the following signs and symptoms of infection: Weakness, anorexia, cough, red tongue, fever and pea green colored stool. What could be the patient’s medical condition?*', 'Typhoid fever', 'The presence of a persistent fever, generalized weakness, anoxia, a cough, and a furred tongue with prominent red edges and tip (traditionally referred to as a "typhoid tongue") are classic clinical presentations of a Salmonella Typhi infection.

A defining, textbook feature of Typhoid fever is the development of a slimy, yellow-green liquid diarrhea. Often referred to in board exam vignettes as pea-green or pea-soup colored stool, it is caused by severe localized inflammation, purulent exudates, and necrosis within the intestinal Peyer''s patches mixing with mucosal bile.

• Dengue: Characterized by high fevers, severe muscle/joint aches ("breakbone fever"), headaches, and a distinctive rash, but it does not cause pea-green diarrhea or an inflamed red tongue.
• Meningitis: A neurological infection presenting with a high fever, severe headache, rigid stiff neck (nuchal rigidity), and altered mental status rather than a primary gastrointestinal illness.
• Cholera: While it is a major waterborne disease, cholera produces massive volumes of profuse, painless diarrhea classically described as clear "rice-water" stool with a fishy odor, completely distinct from the thick, greenish-yellow pea-soup diarrhea of typhoid.', '["Typhoid fever","Cholera","Meningitis","Dengue"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-09T15:50:50.701Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-08T15:50:50.701Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.208Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-08T15:50:50.843Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758444853187-32', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783610430222-1790433140271-gx1a3', 'A patient diagnosed with Rheumatic Fever was prescribed cortisone. Nutrition intervention must include*', 'Sodium restriction 1000mg/day', 'While cortisone actively wastes potassium (making potassium-rich foods clinically necessary), the test creator targets sodium restriction based on the following textbook rationale:

1. Fluid Retention and Edema: Cortisone heavily promotes sodium and fluid retention inside the body. In a patient with Rheumatic Fever, this fluid retention is highly dangerous because the disease can cause inflammation of the heart (rheumatic carditis).
2. Preventing Heart Strain: Introducing a strict sodium restriction helps prevent fluid overload, peripheral edema, and hypertension, which directly protects the heart muscles from being overtaxed during the acute phase of Rheumatic Fever.', '["Potassium rich food","Sodium restriction 1000mg/day","Iron supplementation","Increased fluid intake"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-09T15:05:53.881Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-08T15:05:53.881Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.208Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-08T15:05:54.006Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758444873435-33', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783610430222-1790433140271-gx1a3', 'Isoniazid, a drug for Tuberculosis treatment, is a Vitamin B6 (Pyridoxine) antagonist. Patients on this medication must be advised to eat more of the following*', 'Egg, carrots, camote, tuna, beef, banana and peas', 'Isoniazid (INH) actively competes with and inhibits the activation of Vitamin B6 (Pyridoxine), which can precipitate severe peripheral neuropathy (manifesting as burning, numbness, or tingling in the extremities).

To naturally optimize B6 levels alongside standard clinical supplementation, patients should prioritize foods dense in Pyridoxine. This option provides an exceptionally rich combination of animal and plant-based Vitamin B6 sources, including tuna, beef, banana, peas, and camote (sweet potato).', '["Egg, carrots, camote, tuna, beef, banana and peas","Apple, bread, rice, cabbage, ginger","Soup, collagen, radish, eggplant, rice","Peanut butter, fish, rice, potato, garlic"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-09T09:56:13.719Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-08T09:56:13.719Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.208Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-08T09:56:13.863Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758444888918-34', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783610430222-1790433140271-gx1a3', 'A patient diagnosed with Tuberculosis has normal BMI. How much protein should be prescribed per day?*', 'High protein 75 to 120g per day', 'Active Tuberculosis (TB) places the body in an intense inflammatory and hypercatabolic state, resulting in accelerated muscle tissue breakdown and nitrogen wasting. Even if a patient presents with a normal BMI, they require aggressive nutritional therapy to counter cellular destruction, maintain lean muscle mass, and optimize immune function.

Medical nutrition therapy (MNT) guidelines dictate increasing a TB patient''s protein intake above the normal baseline to support tissue healing and recovery. Clinical protocols emphasize incorporating adequate high-biological-value protein into the daily diet to support complete tissue healing.', '["Low protein 40g per day","High protein 75 to 120g per day","0.6g/kg body weight","2g/kg body weight"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-10T07:24:28.599Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.3,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-09T07:24:28.599Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.208Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-09T07:24:28.741Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758444901202-35', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783610430222-1790433140271-gx1a3', 'A patient with Dengue Hemorrhagic Fever must avoid*', 'Chocolate drink', 'Patients diagnosed with Dengue Hemorrhagic Fever (DHF) must strictly avoid dark-colored foods and beverages, such as chocolate drinks, cola, coffee, red/violet juices, and dark berries.

DHF compromises blood capillaries and dramatically reduces blood platelets, placing the patient at a severe risk for internal gastrointestinal (GI) bleeding. If a patient consumes a chocolate drink, it can discolor their vomit or stool, staining it dark brown or black. This masks clinical signs of active GI bleeding (such as hematemesis or melena), confusing the medical team and potentially delaying life-saving emergency care.

• Dark leafy vegetables: While green, they do not dye stool or vomit the distinctive tarry black or dark brown color associated with internal bleeding. In fact, they provide vital Vitamin K to support proper clotting pathways.
• Hard candies & Apple juice: These options are clear or translucent, making them completely safe for hydration and carbohydrate maintenance without interfering with medical observations.', '["Hard candies","Dark leafy vegetables","Chocolate drink","Apple juice"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-10T08:42:09.853Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.3,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-09T08:42:09.853Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.208Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-09T08:42:09.853Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758444917018-36', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783610430222-1790433140271-gx1a3', 'Food safety is essential in managing patients with AIDS. The following are food safety measures to be observed except*', 'Keep cold food at 35 to 50 degrees Fahrenheit', 'In clinical food safety protocols, cold food must strictly be maintained at or below 40°F (4°C) (or 41°F depending on localized sanitary code references like ServSafe or the FDA) to inhibit pathogenic bacterial multiplication.

Stating that cold foods can be kept up to 50°F directly breaches the [Food Temperature Danger Zone (40°F to 140°F)](usda.gov ""Danger Zone" (40°F - 140°F)"). Allowing cold food temperatures to climb to 50°F allows highly dangerous, opportunistic pathogens like Salmonella, Listeria monocytogenes, and E. coli to rapidly multiply. For immunocompromised individuals, such as patients with AIDS, this minor temperature elevation can lead to a severe, life-threatening foodborne infection.', '["Keep cold food at 35 to 50 degrees Fahrenheit","Discard eggs with cracked shells","Avoid serving raw fruits and vegetables","Thaw chicken in the refrigerator"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-09T11:25:50.386Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-08T11:25:50.386Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.208Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-08T11:25:50.470Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758444932235-37', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783610430222-1790433140271-gx1a3', 'To ensure food safety, keeping food between 45 to 145 degrees Fahrenheit or 7.2 to 62.7 degrees Celsius must not exceed*', '4 hours', 'Leaving perishable food items within this unsafe span (45°F to 145°F / 7.2°C to 62.7°C) for longer than 4 hours permits rapid, exponential multiplication of pathogenic bacteria (such as Salmonella and Staphylococcus aureus), making the food highly toxic and unsafe for human consumption.

1. The Safe Time Window (Under 2 hours): When potentially hazardous food is kept in the Temperature Danger Zone (45°F to 145°F / 7.2°C to 62.7°C) for less than 2 hours, it is still safe to serve, eat, or securely put back into the refrigerator to use later.
2. The Point of No Return (2 to 4 hours): Once food sits out past the 2-hour mark, it enters a dangerous gray zone. While it is still safe to consume immediately, it can no longer be legally or safely put back in the refrigerator because bacteria have entered their rapid, exponential growth phase.

• Under 2 hours: Safe to consume or re-refrigerate.
• 2 to 4 hours: Safe to eat only if consumed immediately; do not put it back in the fridge.
• Over 4 hours: Absolute mandatory discard.', '["2 hours","3 hours","4 hours","8 hours"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-09T17:10:54.973Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-08T17:10:54.973Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.208Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-08T17:10:55.058Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758444945714-38', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783610430222-1790433140271-gx1a3', 'Stage 1 dietary management of poliomyelitis includes 30 to 200 ml tube feeding alternating with water every*', '2 hours', 'In older, traditional diet therapy manuals for acute infectious neurological disorders like poliomyelitis (particularly cases affecting the bulbar region, which impairs normal swallowing mechanics), Stage 1 protocols utilize tube feeding to guarantee safe nutrition without aspiration.

The standard textbook framework states that the patient should be given 30 to 200 ml of formula via tube feeding, wait 2 hours, and then alternate with water. Spacing the administration out every 2 hours prevents overwhelming the patient''s gastric capacity, minimizes the risk of regurgitation, and keeps the patient safely hydrated while their swallowing reflexes are heavily compromised.

*When you encounter this question on your exam, remember this specific gastrointestinal-pacing formula for acute polio management: Tube feeding, wait 2 hours, then water.', '["1 hour","5 hours","2 hours","3 hours"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-10T07:30:53.326Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.3,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-09T07:30:53.326Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.208Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-09T07:30:53.487Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758444962915-39', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783610430222-1790433140271-gx1a3', 'A patient with Severe Acute Respiratory Syndrome must wear surgical mask at all times except when*', 'Eating', 'A patient cannot physically consume food or fluids while wearing a surgical mask. Therefore, eating (and drinking) is the only practical and necessary exception where the mask must be temporarily removed.

During these temporary mask-off periods, strict isolation precautions—such as maintaining negative pressure ventilation, keeping the patient in a private room, and ensuring healthcare workers wear appropriate personal protective equipment (PPE) like N95 respirators—are vital to prevent the transmission of respiratory droplets or aerosols.', '["Eating","Sleeping","Resting","Exercising"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-09T11:14:09.047Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-08T11:14:09.047Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.208Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-08T11:14:09.151Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758444975282-40', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783610430222-1790433140271-gx1a3', 'Middle East Respiratory Syndrome or MERS-COV can be transmitted through*', 'Camel milk and meat', 'Middle East Respiratory Syndrome (MERS-CoV) is a zoonotic viral respiratory illness, meaning it is transmitted from animals to humans. Extensive epidemiological studies by global health bodies have confirmed that dromedary camels serve as the primary animal host reservoir for the virus.

While the virus can also spread through direct physical contact with live animals, humans can contract the infection by consuming contaminated, unpasteurized camel products—specifically raw camel milk and undercooked camel meat. Because the virus is heat-sensitive, global public health authorities strictly advise pasteurizing milk and thoroughly cooking meat to eliminate transmission risks.', '["Human excreta","Blood","Camel milk and meat","Droplet"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-09T15:46:23.887Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-08T15:46:23.887Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.208Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-08T15:46:24.020Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758444990698-41', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783610430222-1790433140271-gx1a3', 'Virgin Coconut Oil has antimicrobial properties. How much VCO must be given to a patient weighing 176 lbs during the first day of treatment?*', '48ml', 'Virgin coconut oil (VCO) has been studied for its potential antimicrobial and antiviral properties. Research involving VCO often explores its components, such as medium-chain triglycerides (MCTs) like lauric acid, which are believed to contribute to these properties. Clinical protocols and studies evaluate various amounts of VCO for supportive care, but determining any specific usage requires professional medical evaluation based on an individual''s health status and needs.

A specific weight-based formula was used in clinical trial protocols by the Department of Science and Technology (DOST). These clinical trials (such as the DOST-FNRI studies on the antiviral and antimicrobial properties of VCO) use a strict dosage protocol based on a patient’s body weight in kilograms to allow the gut to safely adjust:
• First 3 days (Initial Dose): 0.6 mL of VCO per kilogram of body weight (0.6 mL/kg BW).
• Day 4 to 28 (Succeeding Dose): 1.2 mL of VCO per kilogram of body weight (1.2 mL/kg BW).

1. Convert the patient''s weight from pounds to kilograms:
176 lbs / 2.2 = 80 kg
2. Calculate the first-day dosage (using the initial 0.6 mL/kg factor):
80 kg x 0.6 mL/kg = 48 mL', '["48ml","60ml","50ml","96ml"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-10T08:32:58.300Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.3,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-09T08:32:58.300Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.208Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-09T08:32:58.300Z"'::jsonb)
