('anki-card-1758606953439-69', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783610451953-1790433140293-winqs', 'Microorganism that is used as basis to determine the thermal destruction of food spoilage microorganisms in low and acid canned foods is*', 'Clostridium botulinum', 'Clostridium botulinum spores serve as the primary target organism used to establish the thermal death time and retorting standards required for commercial canning of low-acid foods.', '["Saccharomyces cerevisiae","Staphylococcus aureus","Salmonella typhi","Clostridium botulinum"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-08T09:57:37.886Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-11-07T09:57:37.886Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.006Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-09T11:52:03.674Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758606967188-70', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783610451953-1790433140293-winqs', 'Which of the following microorganisms produce heat stable enterotoxin?*', 'Staphylococcus aureus', 'Staphylococcus aureus produces enterotoxins that resist high heat and can cause food poisoning even after the food is cooked.

• Aspergillus flavus produces aflatoxins (mycotoxins), not enterotoxins.
• Clostridium perfringens produces an enterotoxin in the gut during sporulation, but it is not typically classified as a pre-formed heat-stable food toxin in the same manner.
• Clostridium botulinum produces a neurotoxin that is heat-labile (destroyed by boiling).', '["Staphylococcus aureus","Aspergillus flavus","Clostridium perfringens","Clostridium botulinum"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-08T08:49:31.071Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-11-07T08:49:31.071Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.006Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-07T14:32:00.423Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758606978155-71', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783610451953-1790433140293-winqs', 'Which among the following is not a useful microorganism?*', 'Clostridium botulinum', 'Clostridium botulinum is a lethal pathogen producing botulinum neurotoxin, making it a dangerous biological agent with no useful commercial food application (unlike yeasts, molds, or acetic bacteria).', '["Clostridium botulinum","Acetobacter aceti","Saccharomyces cerevisiae","Aspergillus oryzae"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-08T10:04:04.757Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-11-07T10:04:04.757Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.006Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-09T11:52:11.278Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758606988321-72', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783610451953-1790433140293-winqs', 'The Father of Canning is *', 'Nicholas Appert', 'Nicholas Appert, a French chef and confectioner, is credited with developing the method of preserving food by sealing it in sterilized containers. His work in the late 18th and early 19th centuries led to the discovery that food could be preserved by heat treatment to kill bacteria and other microorganisms, thereby preventing spoilage.

• Louis Appert, often confused with Nicholas Appert, was actually his son who helped to commercialize and refine his father’s methods.
• Louis Pasteur, on the other hand, made significant contributions to microbiology and food preservation techniques, particularly pasteurization, which involves heating liquids to kill pathogens and extend shelf life. However, he is not directly associated with canning.', '["Nicholas Appert","Loius Appert","Loius Pasteur","Nicholas Pasteur"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-08T08:50:03.597Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-11-07T08:50:03.597Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.006Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-07T14:32:00.423Z"'::jsonb),
