('anki-card-1758607384435-94', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783610451953-1790433140293-winqs', 'Sentence A. Shelf life stable or intermediate (IM) moisture food has a moisture content of 15-50% and aw between 0.6-0.85.
Sentence B. Dried desiccated foods or low moisture (LM) food has a moisture content not higher than 25% and an aw between 0.0-0.60.*', 'Both sentences are correct.', 'Sentence A is correct: intermediate-moisture (IM) foods have 15–50% moisture and aw between 0.60–0.85. Sentence B is correct: low-moisture (LM) desiccated foods have moisture under 25% and aw between 0.0–0.60.', '["Sentence A is correct.","Sentence B is correct.","Both sentences are correct.","Both sentences are incorrect."]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-08T10:06:10.386Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-11-07T10:06:10.386Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.006Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-09T11:55:04.742Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758607400035-95', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783610451953-1790433140293-winqs', 'One of the following strain is not causative organism of bacterial food poisoning.*', 'Aspergillus flavus', 'Aspergillus flavus is a mycotoxin-producing mold (aflatoxin), not a bacterial food poisoning organism (making it the correct exception).', '["Clostridium welchi","Clostridium botulinum","Aspergillus flavus","Clostridium perfringes"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-08T09:15:57.038Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-11-07T09:15:57.038Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.006Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-09T11:53:59.829Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758607415535-96', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783610451953-1790433140293-winqs', 'Which of the following directly interacts with myoglobin in cured meats?*', 'NO2−', 'Nitrite (NO₂⁻) is the active curing agent that dissociates to form nitric oxide (NO), which interacts directly with meat myoglobin to form nitrosohemochrome, giving cured meats their characteristic pink color.', '["NO2−","NO3−","NO","NaNO3"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-08T14:10:18.428Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.3,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-11-07T14:10:18.428Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.006Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-09T11:55:27.077Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758607429168-97', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783610451953-1790433140293-winqs', 'Which of the following steps in rice wine fermentation supplies fermentable sugars converted by yeasts into sugars?*', 'Malting', 'Malting (or enzymatic starch conversion/hydrolysis via sprouted grains or koji inoculation like Aspergillus oryzae) supplies fermentable sugars for alcoholic beverage and rice wine fermentation.', '["Malting","Sprouting","Brewing","Addition of Aspergillus oryzae"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-08T14:12:11.320Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.3,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-11-07T14:12:11.320Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.006Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-09T11:55:32.072Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758607441915-98', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783610451953-1790433140293-winqs', 'A food additive that is commonly used to improve texture of preserved fruits and vegetables is*', 'Calcium chloride', 'Calcium chloride (CaCl₂) is added commonly in canned fruit and vegetable processing because calcium ions cross-link with cell-wall pectin to firm tissues and improve crispness.', '["Sodium chloride","Calcium chloride","Sodium carbobate","Potassium chloridde"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-08T14:11:07.862Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.3,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-11-07T14:11:07.862Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.006Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-09T11:56:12.492Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758607456483-99', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783610451953-1790433140293-winqs', 'Spoilage by thermophilic spore forming bacteria where no gas is formed but the canned product is sour or acidic.*', 'Flat sour', 'Flat sour spoilage is a thermophilic bacterial defect in canned goods where acid is produced without gas generation, leaving the can flat while souring the contents.', '["Hydrogen swell","Flat sour","Flipper","Springer"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-08T10:04:51.605Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-11-07T10:04:51.605Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.006Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-09T11:56:23.343Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758786507019-0', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783610488281-1790433142627-pf2m5', 'The most abundant element in the human body accounting for some 63% of body mass. *', 'oxygen', '', '["carbon","hydrogen","oxygen","nitrogen"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-01T07:05:10.885Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-09-30T07:05:10.885Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.989Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-30T07:05:10.885Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758786516134-1', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783610488281-1790433142627-pf2m5', 'Organelle in the cell where protein and lipid synthesis take place. *', 'endoplasmic reticulum', 'The endoplasmic reticulum (ER) is a continuous network of membranes divided into two functionally distinct areas that handle biosynthesis:
	• Rough ER: Studded with ribosomes, it is the primary site where protein synthesis and structural folding take place.
	• Smooth ER: Lacks ribosomes and contains specialized enzymes responsible for lipid synthesis (including phospholipids and cholesterol), steroid hormone production, and carbohydrate metabolism.

• Golgi apparatus: Acts as the cell''s shipping center. It does not synthesize proteins or lipids from scratch; instead, it modifies, sorts, and packages the proteins and lipids received from the ER.
• Nucleus: Serves as the control center containing the cell''s genetic blueprint (DNA). It is the site for DNA replication and transcription (RNA synthesis), not translation or lipid manufacturing.
• Mitochondria: Known as the powerhouse of the cell, its primary function is generating energy in the form of ATP via aerobic cellular respiration.', '["nucleus","endoplasmic reticulum","Golgi apparatus","mitochondria"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-22T12:54:35.038Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.3,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-21T12:54:35.038Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.989Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-21T12:54:35.213Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758786525133-2', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783610488281-1790433142627-pf2m5', 'Organelles that contain oxidases which help detoxify potentially harmful substances in the cell: *', 'peroxisomes', '', '["lysosomes","peroxisomes","mitochondria","Golgi apparatus"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-16T01:05:07.688Z","interval":3,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":2}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-13T01:05:07.688Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.989Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-13T01:05:07.763Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758786534435-3', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783610488281-1790433142627-pf2m5', 'The site of most ATP synthesis in cells. *', 'mitochondria', '', '["mitochondria","nucleus","Golgi apparatus","peroxisomes"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-03T05:31:21.600Z","interval":3,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":2}'::jsonb, '"2026-09-30T05:31:21.600Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.989Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-30T05:31:21.600Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758786544851-4', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783610488281-1790433142627-pf2m5', 'The form of vitamin that has no vitamin activity unless it is converted to its biologically active form in the body. *', 'provitamin', 'A provitamin is an inactive biochemical precursor that lacks direct vitamin activity when consumed. It requires specific chemical modification or enzymatic cleavage inside the human body to transform into its biologically active, functional form. A classic example is beta-carotene, a provitamin that the body cleaves into active Vitamin A (retinol).

	• Preformed vitamin: This describes a vitamin that is already in its fully active, biological state when ingested (such as retinol in animal products) and requires no internal conversion.
	• Vitamer: Vitamers are the multiple structural variations or chemical forms of a single vitamin that all possess similar vitamin activity (e.g., pyridoxine, pyridoxal, and pyridoxamine are all vitamers of Vitamin B₆).
	• Antivitamin: A chemical compound that actively blocks, inhibits, or destroys the biological action of a vitamin.', '["antivitamin","provitamin","vitamer","preformed vitamin"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-21T16:10:19.518Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-20T16:10:19.518Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.989Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-20T16:10:19.610Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758786559650-5', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783610488281-1790433142627-pf2m5', 'The following statements are true regarding polysaccharides except: *', 'Amylose is made up of continuous, branched chains.', 'Amylose is actually a linear, unbranched chain of glucose units linked together by a-1,4-glycosidic bonds. Because it is unbranched, it naturally twists into a helical/spiral shape.', '["They are polymers of glucose.","Amylose is made up of continuous, branched chains.","Amylopectin has α-1,6 bonds.","Glycogen is similar to amylopectin but is more highly branched"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-16T02:47:44.442Z","interval":3,"easeFactor":2.3,"repetitions":2}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-13T02:47:44.442Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.989Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-13T02:47:44.517Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758786567783-6', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783610488281-1790433142627-pf2m5', 'Glycolysis occurs in the: *', 'cytoplasm', 'Glycolysis is the universal metabolic pathway that breaks down one molecule of glucose into two molecules of pyruvate. It takes place entirely within the cytoplasm (specifically the cytosol) of the cell.

This location is specific because all ten of the soluble enzymes required to catalyze the step-by-step breakdown of glucose are located freely within the cytoplasmic fluid.

• Mitochondrial matrix: This is the internal fluid space of the mitochondria where the Krebs cycle (TCA cycle) and pyruvate oxidation happen.
• Mitochondrial membrane: The inner mitochondrial membrane houses the proteins for the electron transport chain and oxidative phosphorylation.', '["mitochondrial matrix","mitochondrial membrane","cytoplasm","none of the above"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-21T15:54:42.005Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-20T15:54:42.005Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.989Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-20T15:54:42.094Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758786575382-7', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783610488281-1790433142627-pf2m5', 'The hormone responsible for stimulating the uptake of glucose into the cells and its storage as glycogen in the liver and muscles: *', 'insulin', 'This anabolic hormone is secreted by the beta cells of the pancreas in response to high blood sugar levels (such as after eating). It lowers blood glucose by stimulating its uptake into cells (via GLUT-4 translocation) and promoting glycogenesis—the storage of excess glucose as glycogen in the liver and skeletal muscles.

• Glucagon: Produced by the alpha cells of the pancreas, glucagon has the exact opposite effect. It is released during fasting or low blood sugar states to trigger the liver to break glycogen back down into glucose (glycogenolysis).
• Epinephrine & Norepinephrine: Known as catecholamines, these stress hormones stimulate the breakdown of glycogen to rapidly elevate blood glucose levels for immediate energy during a fight-or-flight response.', '["insulin","glucagon","epinephrine","norepinephrine"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-22T12:57:19.064Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.3,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-21T12:57:19.064Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.989Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-21T12:57:19.240Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758786586715-8', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783610488281-1790433142627-pf2m5', 'The coenzyme needed to convert pyruvate to acetyl CoA: *', 'TPP', 'The conversion of pyruvate to acetyl-CoA is an oxidative decarboxylation reaction. Thiamine pyrophosphate, or TPP (derived from Vitamin B₁), serves as the coenzyme for E1 (pyruvate dehydrogenase), where it directly attacks and cleaves the carbonyl carbon of pyruvate, releasing carbon dioxide (decarboxylation). While NAD⁺ and FAD are also part of the 5-coenzyme complex (alongside Lipoic acid and CoA-SH), TPP is uniquely required as the initial binding and decarboxylating coenzyme.

• NAD: While NAD⁺ is required by the PDH complex (for the E3 subunit), its specific role is to act as the final electron acceptor to reset the system, producing NADH.
• FAD: FAD is an intermediate electron carrier within the E3 subunit of the complex that transfers its electrons to NAD⁺.
• PLP: Pyridoxal Phosphate (derived from Vitamin B₆) is not involved in this pathway at all. It is primarily the essential coenzyme for transamination and decarboxylation reactions in amino acid metabolism.', '["TPP","FAD","NAD","PLP"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-22T12:50:55.473Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.3,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-21T12:50:55.473Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.989Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-21T12:50:55.473Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758786707359-9', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783610488281-1790433142627-pf2m5', 'It is referred to as preformed vitamin A: *', 'retinol', 'This refers to the active, "ready-to-use" form of vitamin A that is found naturally in animal-derived foods (such as liver, eggs, and dairy). Retinol (along with its storage form, retinyl esters) is the specific compound widely designated as "preformed vitamin A" in nutritional science.

Once inside the body, retinol can be converted into retinal (essential for vision) and then further oxidized into retinoic acid (essential for gene regulation). While these are active retinoids, retinol is the primary preformed dietary source.

Meanwhile, Beta-carotene is a plant pigment classified as provitamin A. It is a precursor that the body must first chemically cleave and convert into active vitamin A before it can be used.', '["retinol","retinoic acid","beta-carotene","retinal"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, 'Preformed vitamin A refers to the active, biologically available forms of vitamin A found naturally in animal-derived foods (like liver, eggs, and dairy). In biochemistry and food labeling, retinol is the definitive reference molecule for preformed vitamin A. While its derivatives—like retinal and retinoic acid—are also metabolites of preformed vitamin A, retinol is the specific form absorbed from animal sources and stored directly by the liver.', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-11T13:38:33.995Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.3,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-10T13:38:33.995Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.989Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-10T13:38:34.067Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758786720660-10', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783610488281-1790433142627-pf2m5', 'The form of vitamin A needed in the synthesis of rhodopsin or the visual purple. *', 'retinal', 'Retinal (specifically in its 11-cis-retinal configuration) is the aldehyde form of vitamin A. It serves as the precise, light-absorbing chromophore that physically binds to the protein opsin to synthesize rhodopsin (visual purple) in the rod cells of the retina.', '["retinol","retinoic acid","beta-carotene","retinal"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-03T07:31:26.510Z","interval":3,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":2}'::jsonb, '"2026-09-30T07:31:26.510Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.989Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-30T07:31:26.510Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758786729259-11', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783610488281-1790433142627-pf2m5', 'The two simple sugars that make up maltose: *', '2 glucose', 'Also known as malt sugar, this disaccharide is formed by a covalent bond between two glucose molecules (specifically an α(1→4) glycosidic linkage). It is commonly produced during the digestion of starch by amylase.

• Glucose and Fructose: These two sugars combine to form sucrose (table sugar).
• Glucose and Galactose: These two sugars combine to form lactose (milk sugar).', '["2 glucose","2 fructose","glucose and fructose","glucose and galactose"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-22T12:02:19.628Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-21T12:02:19.628Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.989Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-21T12:02:19.948Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758786738109-12', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783610488281-1790433142627-pf2m5', 'The sugars composing the disaccharide sucrose are: *', 'glucose and fructose', 'glucose and fructose', '["fructose and galactose","2 glucose","glucose and fructose","2 fructose"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-03T04:55:03.116Z","interval":3,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":2}'::jsonb, '"2026-09-30T04:55:03.116Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.989Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-08T12:24:43.573Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758786752142-13', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783610488281-1790433142627-pf2m5', 'The principal carbohydrate stored in plants is: *', 'starch', 'Starch is a complex polysaccharide made of alpha-glucose monomers linked together. Plants produce excess glucose through photosynthesis and string thousands of these molecules together to form starch grains inside their cells. This serves as their primary, long-term chemical energy reserve for periods when sunlight is unavailable.

Plant starch is uniquely composed of a mixture of two distinct polymers: amylose (a linear, unbranched helical chain) and amylopectin (a highly branched chain).', '["cellulose","starch","glycogen","glucose"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-11T13:59:45.624Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.1,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-10T13:59:45.624Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.989Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-10T13:59:45.624Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758786794807-14', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783610488281-1790433142627-pf2m5', 'The protein found in human hair and fingernails as well as animal hooves, horns, and claws. *', 'keratin', 'Keratin is a tough, fibrous structural protein that forms the primary component of human hair, fingernails, and the outer layer of skin, as well as animal hooves, horns, claws, and feathers.

• Collagen is another structural protein, but it is found primarily in connective tissues like skin, bones, tendons, and cartilage.
• Cellulite is not a protein; it is a term used to describe the appearance of dimpled flesh caused by subcutaneous fat deposits.
• Glycogen is a polysaccharide (a complex carbohydrate), not a protein, used by animals to store glucose.', '["keratin","cellulite","collagen","glycogen"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, 'Keratin is a tough, fibrous structural protein that forms the primary chemical component of hair, fingernails, hooves, claws, and horns in animals. It protects epithelial cells from mechanical stress and damage. While it is also a structural protein, collagen is found primarily in connective tissues, skin dermis, tendons, ligaments, and cartilage. It provides tensile strength rather than forming rigid outer structures like nails or hooves.', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-16T02:59:28.372Z","interval":3,"easeFactor":2.3,"repetitions":2}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-13T02:59:28.372Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.989Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-13T02:59:28.447Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758786802137-15', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783610488281-1790433142627-pf2m5', 'This amino acid can form a special covalent bond called a disulfide bond. *', 'cysteine', 'Cysteine contains a highly reactive sulfhydryl group (-SH) (also known as a thiol group) on its side chain. When two cysteine residues come close to each other within a protein structure, their sulfhydryl groups can undergo an oxidation reaction. This reaction joins the two sulfur atoms together, forming a strong covalent disulfide bond (S-S), which plays a critical role in stabilizing a protein''s three-dimensional structure.

• Methionine: While methionine is the only other standard amino acid that contains sulfur, its sulfur atom is bound to a methyl group (a thioether group, -C-S-C-). Because it lacks a free, reactive -SH group, methionine cannot form disulfide bonds.
• Lysine and Tyrosine: Neither of these amino acids contains sulfur in their side chains. Lysine contains an amino group, and tyrosine contains a phenolic hydroxyl group.', '["tyrosine","methionine","cysteine","lysine"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-21T15:13:56.119Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-20T15:13:56.119Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.989Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-20T15:13:56.208Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758786810137-16', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783610488281-1790433142627-pf2m5', 'Which of the following is a branched-chain amino acid? *', 'isoleucine', 'There are exactly three branched-chain amino acids (BCAAs) among the standard amino acids: leucine, isoleucine, and valine. They are named for their unique, branching aliphatic hydrocarbon side-chain molecular structure.', '["alanine","lysine","isoleucine","glutamine"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-21T15:42:24.018Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-20T15:42:24.018Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.989Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-20T15:42:24.110Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758786819421-17', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783610488281-1790433142627-pf2m5', 'The building blocks of proteins are amino acids. What reaction allows several amino acids to join to form a protein? *', 'condensation reaction', 'condensation reaction', '["hydrolysis","condensation reaction","evaporation reaction","hydrogenation"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-01T06:09:58.933Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-09-30T06:09:58.933Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.989Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-08T12:24:43.573Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758786838387-18', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783610488281-1790433142627-pf2m5', 'The enzyme pepsin is not denatured in the stomach despite the very acidic environment due to the presence of hydrochloricacid. What could account for this? *', 'Pepsin is secreted in its precursor form, pepsinogen.', 'pepsin is secreted in its precursor form (pepsinogen)', '["Pepsin is the only protein-enzyme that functions best in an acidic environment.","Pepsin is able to resist denaturation by increasing the pH within the stomach, making it less acidic.","Pepsin is also digested along with other proteins in the stomach.","Pepsin is secreted in its precursor form, pepsinogen."]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-14T01:25:34.102Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-13T01:25:34.102Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.989Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-08T12:24:43.573Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758786847671-19', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783610488281-1790433142627-pf2m5', 'Ammonia is a nitrogenous waste product of protein metabolism that is harmful especially in high concentrations in the human body. Urea cycle is a means to detoxify and to prevent the accumulation of ammonia in the body by converting it to urea which is eventually excreted in the urine. How many moles of nitrogen enter the urea cycle in every turn? *', '2', 'A single molecule of urea has the chemical formula CO(NH₂)₂. As you can see from its structure, every single molecule of urea contains exactly two atoms of nitrogen. Therefore, for every complete turn of the urea cycle that creates one molecule of urea, exactly 2 moles of nitrogen must enter the cycle.

• Molar Mass: 60.06 g/mol
• Atom Count: A single molecule contains 1 Carbon, 1 Oxygen, 2 Nitrogens, and 4 Hydrogens (8 total atoms).', '["4","1","2","3"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-11T14:08:30.178Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.1,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-10T14:08:30.178Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.989Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-10T14:08:30.255Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758786856454-20', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783610488281-1790433142627-pf2m5', 'Ceruloplasmin is a type of: *', 'metalloprotein', 'Ceruloplasmin is a type of metalloprotein (specifically a copper-binding metalloprotein) and it is also a glycoprotein.', '["glycoprotein","phosphoprotein","metalloprotein","lipoprotein"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-11T13:25:14.339Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.3,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-10T13:25:14.339Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.989Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-10T13:25:14.409Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758786865434-21', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783610488281-1790433142627-pf2m5', 'Why is a saturated fat called “saturated”? *', 'The fatty acid carbon chain is saturated with hydrogen.', 'In a saturated fatty acid chain, there are only single bonds between the neighboring carbon atoms. Because carbon atoms must form four bonds, having only single bonds means each carbon atom can bind to the maximum possible number of hydrogen atoms.

In contrast, an unsaturated fat contains one or more carbon-to-carbon double bonds (C = C). These double bonds reduce the open spaces available for hydrogen, leaving the molecule "unsaturated".', '["The fat is saturated with water.","The fatty acid carbon chain is saturated with carbon.","The fatty acid carbon chain is saturated with hydrogen.","The fatty acid carbon chains can have more water added."]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-11T13:26:09.482Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.3,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-10T13:26:09.482Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.989Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-10T13:26:09.553Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758786887585-22', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783610488281-1790433142627-pf2m5', 'An example of a monounsaturated fatty acid is: *', 'oleic acid', '', '["linoleic acid","linolenic acid","palmitic acid","oleic acid"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '• Oleic acid (D) is a monounsaturated fatty acid (MUFA). It contains exactly one double bond in its hydrocarbon chain and is famously the primary component of olive oil.
• Linolenic acid (A) and Linoleic acid (C) are polyunsaturated fatty acids (PUFAs) because they contain multiple double bonds.
• Palmitic acid (B) is a saturated fatty acid with no double bonds.', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-16T01:04:57.366Z","interval":3,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":2}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-13T01:04:57.366Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.989Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-13T01:04:57.440Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758786921233-23', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783610488281-1790433142627-pf2m5', 'The fatty acid most commonly found in animal fats: *', 'stearic acid', '', '["linoleic acid","stearic acid","palmitic acid","myristic acid"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-02T13:35:24.818Z","interval":3,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":2}'::jsonb, '"2026-09-29T13:35:24.818Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.989Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-29T13:35:24.898Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758786937414-24', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783610488281-1790433142627-pf2m5', 'The rate-limiting step in glycolysis is: *', 'the phosphorylation of fructose 6-phosphate', 'The conversion of fructose 6-phosphate to fructose 1,6-bisphosphate is the key committed, irreversible, and rate-limiting step of glycolysis. This highly regulated step is catalyzed by the enzyme phosphofructokinase-1 (PFK-1). PFK-1 controls the overall speed of the pathway and is sensitive to the energy demands of the cell (inhibited by ATP and citrate, activated by AMP and fructose 2,6-bisphosphate).

• Isomerization of Glucose 6-phosphate: This is Step 2 of glycolysis catalyzed by phosphoglucose isomerase. It is a freely reversible reaction and is not a major regulatory or rate-limiting point.
• Breakdown of Glycogen: This process is called glycogenolysis and occurs before glycolysis can begin when using stored carbohydrates. Its rate-limiting enzyme is glycogen phosphorylase, which belongs to a separate metabolic pathway altogether.
• Phosphorylation of Glucose: This is Step 1 of glycolysis, catalyzed by hexokinase or glucokinase. While it is an irreversible, regulatory step that traps glucose inside the cell, it is not the primary rate-limiting step because glucose 6-phosphate can still leave the glycolytic pathway to enter other loops, such as the pentose phosphate pathway or glycogen synthesis.', '["the breakdown of glycogen","the phosphorylation of glucose","the isomerization of glucose 6-phosphate to fructose 6-phosphate","the phosphorylation of fructose 6-phosphate"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-22T12:51:11.873Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.3,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-21T12:51:11.873Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.989Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-21T12:51:11.873Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758786946947-25', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783610488281-1790433142627-pf2m5', 'During anaerobic glycolysis, there is a net production of lactate and ____. *', 'ATP', 'During anaerobic glycolysis, a single molecule of glucose is broken down in the cytosol without using oxygen. This pathway results in the net production of two distinct products:
1. Lactate (2 molecules)
2. ATP (a net yield of 2 molecules)

• NADH: While NADH is temporarily produced halfway through glycolysis, the cell immediately uses up that NADH to convert pyruvate into lactate. This means there is zero net production of NADH by the end of the anaerobic process.
• FADH₂: This electron carrier is only generated inside the mitochondria during the Krebs cycle (aerobic respiration), not during glycolysis.
• NAD⁺: Because NADH is continuously recycled back into NAD⁺ to keep glycolysis running, NAD⁺ is constantly consumed and regenerated, meaning it does not experience a net increase.', '["NAD+","NADH","ATP","FADH2"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-16T02:59:16.599Z","interval":3,"easeFactor":2.1,"repetitions":2}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-13T02:59:16.599Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.989Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-13T02:59:16.675Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758786957898-26', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783610488281-1790433142627-pf2m5', 'In the digestive tract, disaccharides are hydrolyzed to monosaccharides mainly by enzymes located in the: *', 'brush border of the small intestine', 'brush border of the small intestine.', '["gastric secretions","brush border of the small intestine","lumen of the small intestine","lumen of the large intestine"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 3, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-21T01:03:03.478Z","interval":8,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":3}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-13T01:03:03.478Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.989Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-08T12:24:43.573Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758786968964-27', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783610488281-1790433142627-pf2m5', 'The respiratory quotient (RQ) is defined as: *', '(liters CO2 produced) / (liters O2 consumed)', '(liters CO2 produced)/(liters O2 consumed).', '["(nitrogen intake) / (nitrogen excreted)","(caloric intake) / (fat free body weight)","(liters CO2 produced) / (liters O2 consumed)","(BMR + DIT + activity) / (fat free body weight)"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, 'Carbohydrates: \(\text{RQ} = 1.0\) (Because the amount of \[\text{O}_{2}\] required to burn a sugar equals the amount of \[\text{CO}_{2}\] produced).Proteins: \(\text{RQ} \approx 0.8\)Fats/Lipids: \(\text{RQ} \approx 0.7\) (Because fats are highly reduced molecules that require a much larger volume of oxygen to fully break down).Mixed Diet: A standard resting individual on a balanced diet usually has an average baseline \(\text{RQ} \approx 0.82\). ', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-01T07:10:25.560Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-09-30T07:10:25.560Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.989Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-08T12:24:43.573Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758786984347-28', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783610488281-1790433142627-pf2m5', 'In the small intestines: *', 'starch is digested by pancreatic amylase into maltose', 'While starch digestion begins in the mouth via salivary amylase, it is halted in the highly acidic stomach. Once the food enters the small intestine (specifically the duodenum), the pancreas secretes pancreatic amylase. This powerful enzyme breaks down remaining complex starches and polysaccharides into the disaccharide maltose and other short glucose polymers.', '["cellulose fiber is digested into monosaccharides","starch is digested by pancreatic amylase into maltose","fat is not hydrolyzed","protein is hydrolyzed by bile acids into amino acids"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-01T07:08:30.401Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.3,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-09-30T07:08:30.401Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.989Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-30T07:08:30.401Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758786991930-29', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783610488281-1790433142627-pf2m5', 'Pancreatic juice contains the following enzyme/s: *', 'trypsinogen', 'The pancreas synthesizes and secretes its protein-digesting enzymes in an inactive proenzyme (zymogen) form to prevent the enzymes from digesting the pancreas itself. Trypsinogen is secreted directly into the pancreatic juice. Once it arrives in the small intestine, it is activated into its working form, trypsin.

• Pepsinogen and pepsin are strictly found in gastric juice secreted by the stomach, not the pancreas.
• Enterokinase (also called enteropeptidase) is a brush-border enzyme produced by the mucosal lining of the small intestine. Its entire job is to sit in the intestine and activate the incoming trypsinogen.', '["trypsinogen","pepsinogen & pepsin","enterokinase","chymotrypsin"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-22T12:50:31.252Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.3,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-21T12:50:31.252Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.989Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-21T12:50:31.252Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758787002144-30', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783610488281-1790433142627-pf2m5', 'Digestion and absorption of carbohydrate involves all the following except: *', 'an acid stable amylase which acts in the stomach', 'The statement regarding ''an acid stable amylase which acts in the stomach'' is false, making it the correct choice for an ''EXCEPT'' question. Unlike lipid digestion—which utilizes acid-stable lingual and gastric lipases in the stomach—there is no gastric amylase secreted by humans, nor is salivary alpha-amylase acid-stable. Salivary amylase becomes inactivated when mixed with gastric juice at a pH below 4.0, halting enzymatic carbohydrate hydrolysis until pancreatic secretions neutralize the chyme in the small intestine.', '["salivary alpha amylase","disaccharidases in the intestines","an acid stable amylase which acts in the stomach","pancreatic alpha amylase"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-01T06:23:08.635Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.3,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-09-30T06:23:08.635Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.989Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-30T06:23:08.635Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758787027062-31', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783610488281-1790433142627-pf2m5', 'A cofactor that is a carrier of activated CO2 and participates in both gluconeogenesis and fatty acid synthesis is: *', 'biotin', 'Biotin (Vitamin B₇) functions as a specialized covalent cofactor for carboxylase enzymes, acting specifically as a carrier of activated carbon dioxide (CO₂).
It plays a vital dual role in both of the specified metabolic pathways:

• Gluconeogenesis: It serves as a cofactor for pyruvate carboxylase, which adds CO₂ to pyruvate to form oxaloacetate.
• Fatty Acid Synthesis: It serves as a cofactor for acetyl-CoA carboxylase, which adds CO₂ to acetyl-CoA to form malonyl-CoA (the rate-limiting step of fatty acid synthesis).

• Pantothenic acid (Vitamin B₅) is the precursor for Coenzyme A (CoA), which carries activated acyl groups, not CO₂.
• Pyridoxal phosphate (PLP / Vitamin B₆) participates primarily in amino acid metabolism (such as transamination and decarboxylation reactions).
• Flavin adenine dinucleotide (FAD / Vitamin B₂) functions as an electron carrier in oxidation-reduction (redox) reactions.', '["pyridoxal phosphate","biotin","flavin adenine dinucleotide","pantothenic acid"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, 'Biotin (Vitamin B7) serves as a prosthetic group covalently bound to carboxylase enzymes, specifically acting as a specialized carrier for activated carbon dioxide (CO2) groups.  It functions as a crucial cofactor for pyruvate carboxylase, the enzyme that converts pyruvate into oxaloacetate to kickstart the formation of glucose. It acts as a cofactor for acetyl-CoA carboxylase, the regulatory enzyme that converts acetyl-CoA into malonyl-CoA, a vital step in building fatty acids. ', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-11T14:10:04.830Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.1,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-10T14:10:04.830Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.989Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-10T14:10:04.914Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758787036945-32', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783610488281-1790433142627-pf2m5', 'This mineral works closely with insulin to regulate the release of energy from glucose: *', 'chromium', 'Chromium (specifically in its trivalent form, Cr³⁺) is an essential trace mineral that acts as an "insulin amplifier". It binds with other compounds in the body to form Glucose Tolerance Factor (GTF).

GTF stabilizes insulin and enhances its ability to dock onto insulin receptors on the surface of your cells. This binding clears glucose from the bloodstream and pulls it directly into the cells, where it can enter metabolic pathways (like glycolysis and the Krebs cycle) to be converted into usable cellular energy (ATP).

• Copper: Primarily required for iron metabolism, red blood cell synthesis, and cross-linking collagen.
• Iodine: Crucial for synthesizing thyroid hormones, which regulate overall baseline metabolic rate, but does not bind with insulin.
• Selenium: Functions as a vital component of antioxidant enzymes like glutathione peroxidase to neutralize free radicals.', '["iodine","selenium","copper","chromium"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-21T15:50:45.321Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-20T15:50:45.321Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.989Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-20T15:50:45.410Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758787045928-33', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783610488281-1790433142627-pf2m5', 'The B vitamin needed in the synthesis of niacin from tryptophan: *', 'pyridoxine', 'The body can manufacture niacin (Vitamin B₃) endogenously from the essential amino acid tryptophan.

Pyridoxine (Vitamin B₆) serves as an essential cofactor for the enzyme kynureninase. A deficiency in Vitamin B₆ halts this step, preventing tryptophan from converting into niacin and potentially leading to niacin-deficiency symptoms (like pellagra).

Riboflavin (Vitamin B₂) is also involved earlier in the pathway (for the enzyme kynurenine 3-monooxygenase), but kynureninase (B₆) is considered the most clinically significant bottleneck.', '["thiamin","pyridoxine","niacin","riboflavin"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, 'The liver can synthesize niacin (Vitamin B3) endogenously from the essential amino acid tryptophan through a complex multi-step metabolic route known as the kynurenine pathway. A critical rate-limiting enzyme in this conversion is kynureninase. This enzyme is strictly dependent on its active coenzyme cofactor, pyridoxal 5''-phosphate (PLP), which is the biologically active form of pyridoxine (Vitamin B6).', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-11T14:11:05.598Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.1,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-10T14:11:05.598Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.989Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-10T14:11:05.699Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758787268968-34', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783610488281-1790433142627-pf2m5', 'A patient with keratinized goosebump-like skin and a keratinized cornea (Bitot’s spots) has trouble seeing in the dark. The biochemical deficiency most likely related to loss of vision in the dark is in the: *', 'generation of all-trans retinal', 'The triad of keratinized "goosebump-like" skin (phrynoderma), keratinized conjunctival debris (Bitot''s spots), and difficulty seeing in dim light (nyctalopia/night blindness) represents a classic, textbook presentation of Vitamin A (retinol) deficiency.

Vitamin A is an essential precursor for the visual pigments in the retina. Inside the photoreceptor cells (rods), 11-cis retinal binds to the protein opsin to form rhodopsin.

When light hits the rod cells, 11-cis retinal absorbs the photon and undergoes a photochemical isomerization, converting into all-trans retinal. This precise shape change activates rhodopsin, triggering the nerve impulses sent to the brain that allow us to see in the dark. A chronic lack of vitamin A impairs the continuous recycling and generation of these retinal isomers, directly causing night blindness.

• gamma carboxylation: This describes the biochemical mechanism of Vitamin K, which activates clotting factors, not Vitamin A.
• alpha-tocopherol: This is the chemical name for Vitamin E. While it is a fat-soluble vitamin, a deficiency causes neurological issues and hemolytic anemia, not Bitot''s spots.
• Zinc absorption: While zinc deficiency can secondary impair vitamin A mobilization from the liver, the explicit biochemical failure directly responsible for the loss of night vision is the visual cycle''s failure to generate the retinal pigment.', '["absorption of zinc","absorption of alpha-tocopherol","gamma carboxylation of glutamate residues","generation of all-trans retinal"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-21T15:38:39.967Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-20T15:38:39.967Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.989Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-20T15:38:40.057Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758787283917-35', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783610488281-1790433142627-pf2m5', 'Your patient has a poorly healing wound, numerous recurring infections, dry scaly skin, and a complaint that food is tasteless. This patient most likely has a deficiency of: *', 'zinc', '', '["iron","phosphorus","zinc","copper"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-03T06:46:52.430Z","interval":3,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":2}'::jsonb, '"2026-09-30T06:46:52.430Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.989Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-30T06:46:52.430Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758787292732-36', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783610488281-1790433142627-pf2m5', 'Which of the following is not an essential amino acid? *', 'alanine', 'Alanine is a non-essential amino acid: The human body possesses the necessary enzymes to synthesize alanine internally (typically via the transamination of pyruvate). Because the body can produce it on its own, it does not strictly need to be obtained directly from your diet.

To instantly recall the 9 essential amino acids during a test, memorize the phrase "PVT TIM HALL":
• Phenylalanine
• Valine
• Threonine
• Tryptophan
• Isoleucine
• Methionine
• Histidine
• Arginine (often classified as semi-essential or required mostly for children)
• Leucine
• Lysine', '["alanine","tryptophan","lysine","phenylalanine"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-21T15:16:58.238Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-20T15:16:58.238Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.989Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-20T15:16:58.327Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758787310533-37', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783610488281-1790433142627-pf2m5', 'Laboratory tests for a 45-year-old male alcoholic patient show an accumulation of lactate and pyruvate in body fluids, suggesting a block in the conversion of pyruvate to acetyl CoA. He appears confused and shows a slight weakness of eye movement. This patient would most probably be treated with injections of: *', 'thiamin', 'The combination of chronic alcoholism, confusion, and weakness of eye movement (ophthalmoplegia) is a classic presentation of Wernicke-Korsakoff syndrome, a severe neurological complication caused by thiamin (Vitamin B1) deficiency.

Thiamin pyrophosphate (TPP) is an essential cofactor for the pyruvate dehydrogenase (PDH) complex, which converts pyruvate into acetyl CoA. When thiamin is deficient, this complex cannot function.

Because pyruvate cannot be converted into acetyl CoA, it shunts into alternative pathways. This leads to the accumulation of both pyruvate and lactate (via lactic acid fermentation) in body fluids.', '["thiamin","niacin","pantothenic acid","riboflavin"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-14T01:02:51.583Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-13T01:02:51.583Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.989Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-13T01:02:51.659Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758787319799-38', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783610488281-1790433142627-pf2m5', 'Tests for an elderly vegetarian patient indicate pernicious and megaloblastic anemia resulting from abnormally large “macrocytic” red blood cells that undergo rapid hemolysis. These results are most probably related to a vitamin deficiency in: *', 'folacin and cobalamin', 'Megaloblastic anemia (abnormally large, macrocytic red blood cells) is caused by a defect in DNA synthesis during red blood cell development. This specific type of anemia is caused by a deficiency in either folacin (folic acid/vitamin B9) or cobalamin (vitamin B12).

The patient is an elderly vegetarian. Vitamin B12 (cobalamin) is found almost exclusively in animal-derived foods. Because of this, long-term vegetarians are at an exceptionally high risk of developing a cobalamin deficiency.', '["ascorbic acid","riboflavin and niacin","folacin and cobalamin","pantothenic acid"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-16T01:24:14.354Z","interval":3,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":2}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-13T01:24:14.354Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.989Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-13T01:24:14.429Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758787333331-39', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783610488281-1790433142627-pf2m5', 'Urea synthesis takes place primarily in the: *', 'liver', '', '["skeletal muscle","liver","kidneys","small intestines"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, 'The liver makes urea, while the kidneys filter and excrete it.', '{"dueDate":"2026-09-30T07:04:12.523Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-09-29T07:04:12.523Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.989Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-29T07:04:12.523Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758787377796-40', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783610488281-1790433142627-pf2m5', 'Ketone bodies can be used as fuel by all the following tissues except: *', 'red blood cells', '', '["the brain","skeletal muscles","the heart","red blood cells"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-01T04:54:41.270Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-09-30T04:54:41.270Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.989Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-30T04:54:41.270Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758787392461-41', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783610488281-1790433142627-pf2m5', 'A key enzyme in carbohydrate metabolism found in the liver but not in muscles is: *', 'glucokinase', 'Glucokinase (also known as Hexokinase IV) is an irreversible enzyme found almost exclusively in the liver parenchymal cells and pancreatic beta cells. It is notably absent from skeletal muscle tissue.', '["phosphorylase","glucokinase","hexokinase","pyruvate dehydrogenase"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-11T13:24:40.566Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.3,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-10T13:24:40.566Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.989Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-10T13:24:40.566Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758787409895-42', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783610488281-1790433142627-pf2m5', 'When a muscle contracts aerobically, less lactic acid is formed than when it contracts anaerobically because under aerobic conditions ______: *', 'the lactic acid generated is rapidly converted back to glucose.', 'During physical exertion, any lactic acid (or more accurately, lactate) that is produced diffuses out of the skeletal muscle cells into the bloodstream and is transported to the liver. In the hepatic Cori cycle, hepatocytes take up this lactate, oxidize it back to pyruvate via lactate dehydrogenase, and subsequently convert it into glucose via gluconeogenesis, which is then released back into the bloodstream to support active muscle tissue.', '["muscles cannot convert glucose 6-phosphate to pyruvate.","most of the pyruvate generated is oxidized.","the lactic acid generated is rapidly converted back to glucose.","the major source of energy is the pentose pathway."]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-11T14:10:35.698Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.1,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-10T14:10:35.698Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.989Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-10T14:10:35.770Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758787419743-43', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783610488281-1790433142627-pf2m5', 'All of the following compounds can serve as the starting material for the synthesis of glucose via gluconeogenesis except: *', 'oleic acid', 'Oleic acid is a fatty acid (specifically a 18-carbon monounsaturated fatty acid). In humans and other mammals, even-chain fatty acids are broken down via beta-oxidation into acetyl-CoA.

Acetyl-CoA cannot be used to synthesize glucose because the reaction that converts pyruvate to acetyl-CoA (catalyzed by the pyruvate dehydrogenase complex) is completely irreversible. Additionally, for every two carbons that enter the citric acid cycle as acetyl-CoA, two carbons are lost as CO2, meaning there is no net synthesis of oxaloacetate to feed into gluconeogenesis.', '["alanine","oleic acid","aspartic acid","glycerol"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-22T12:51:43.044Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.1,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-21T12:51:43.044Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.989Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-21T12:51:43.044Z"'::jsonb)
