INSERT INTO public.flashcards (id, user_id, deck_id, front, back, rationale, options, tags, ndle_subject, difficulty, leitner_box, user_notes, sm2, last_reviewed_at, created_at, updated_at) VALUES
('anki-card-1758787985533-94', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783610488281-1790433142627-pf2m5', 'It is the largest single contributor to the body’s energy needs accounting for 60%-80% of its total need for calories. *', 'basal metabolism', 'Basal metabolism, or Basal Metabolic Rate (BMR), represents the baseline energy expended by the body at complete rest in a post-absorptive state. It is dedicated purely to maintaining life-sustaining vegetative functions—such as cellular respiration, heartbeat, brain activity, and body temperature regulation. In textbook nutrition frameworks, it serves as the largest single contributor to Total Energy Expenditure (TEE), typically accounting for 60% to 80% of daily calorie needs depending on an individual''s lean body mass.

• Thermic Effect of Food: This component represents the temporary increase in energy expenditure associated with digesting, absorbing, and storing nutrients from food. It accounts for only about 5% to 10% of total energy requirements.
• Adaptive Thermogenesis: This is the change in energy expenditure in response to environmental fluctuations (like extreme cold stress) or chronic overfeeding/underfeeding. It represents an auxiliary survival adjustment and constitutes a minor, highly variable percentage of daily needs.
• Physical Activity: This comprises the energy expended during voluntary muscle movement and exercise. While it is the most variable and changeable component of a person''s daily energy expenditure, it typically contributes only 15% to 30% of total calories in most normal-living adults.', '["physical activity","thermic effect of food","basal metabolism","adaptive thermogenesis"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-22T12:31:04.112Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-21T12:31:04.112Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.989Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-21T12:31:04.285Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758788003213-95', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783610488281-1790433142627-pf2m5', 'The Krebs cycle is oxygen-requiring and takes place in the: *', 'mitochondria', 'The Krebs cycle (also known as the citric acid cycle or TCA cycle) takes place exclusively within the matrix of the mitochondria. This is because all the specific enzymes, coenzymes, and transport proteins required for the cycle are housed inside the mitochondrial membranes.

Although the chemical steps of the Krebs cycle do not directly use oxygen gas (O₂), the cycle is considered aerobic (oxygen-requiring). It cannot run without oxygen because it relies on the electron transport chain (which requires oxygen as the final electron acceptor) to regenerate its essential starting coenzymes, NAD⁺ and FAD.', '["liver","mitochondria","muscle cells","nucleus"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-21T15:28:33.277Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-20T15:28:33.277Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.989Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-20T15:28:33.367Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758788015017-96', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783610488281-1790433142627-pf2m5', 'These conditions are anabolic processes indicating positive nitrogen balance except for: *', 'fever', '', '["pregnancy","convalescence","fever","growth"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-03T07:35:07.087Z","interval":3,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":2}'::jsonb, '"2026-09-30T07:35:07.087Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.989Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-30T07:35:07.087Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758788023532-97', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783610488281-1790433142627-pf2m5', 'Peak bone mass refers to the highest bone density achieved by an individual which usually occurs at the age of ____. *', '30', '• During childhood and adolescence, the body builds new bone faster than it breaks down old bone, causing bone mass to increase rapidly.
• Most people reach their peak bone mass—the maximum strength, density, and thickness their bones will ever achieve—by their late 20s or around age 30.
• After age 30, the remodeling balance shifts. The body begins to resorb (break down) bone slightly faster than it replaces it, leading to a gradual, lifelong loss of bone density.', '["20","30","40","50"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-11T13:42:07.334Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.3,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-10T13:42:07.334Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.989Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-10T13:42:07.407Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758788033199-98', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783610488281-1790433142627-pf2m5', 'Both soluble, more fermentable fiber and insoluble, less fermentable fiber have this common action in the body: *', 'they increase satiety or feeling of fullness', 'Both types of dietary fiber contribute to satiety (the feeling of being full after eating) through different physical mechanisms:
• Soluble Fiber: Absorbs water to form a thick, gel-like substance. This significantly slows down stomach emptying (gastric emptying), keeping you physically full for a longer duration.
• Insoluble Fiber: Does not dissolve in water. Instead, it acts as structural "roughage" that adds physical bulk and weight to the food matrix inside your digestive tract. This bulk physically stretches the stomach and intestinal walls, triggering nerve receptors that signal to your brain that you are full.', '["they lower blood cholesterol","they slow down the absorption of glucose","they speed up transit time through intestines","they increase satiety or feeling of fullness"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-11T14:10:25.077Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.1,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-10T14:10:25.077Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.989Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-10T14:10:25.154Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758788041865-99', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783610488281-1790433142627-pf2m5', 'The percentage of nitrogen absorbed from dietary protein by the body is known as: *', 'biological value', 'Biological Value (BV) measures protein quality by calculating the amount of nitrogen retained for tissue growth and maintenance divided by the total nitrogen absorbed from the diet. The formula is:

BV = (Nitrogen Retained / Nitrogen Absorbed) x 100

Because it evaluates the percentage of nitrogen utilized after it has already been successfully absorbed across the intestinal wall, it tracks how perfectly a protein''s amino acid profile matches human tissue needs.

	• Net Protein Utilization - NPU: This measures the percentage of nitrogen retained out of the total nitrogen ingested (intake) rather than absorbed. It combines both digestibility and biological value into one score.
	• Protein Efficiency Ratio - PER: This measures the weight gain of a growing subject per gram of protein consumed, typically tested on laboratory rats.
	• Amino Acid Score: This is a chemical score calculated in a lab by comparing a food’s essential amino acid levels against a standard reference protein profile, completely independent of biological absorption measurements.', '["net protein utilization","biological value","protein efficiency ratio","amino acid score"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-22T12:57:29.847Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.1,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-21T12:57:29.847Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.989Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-21T12:57:30.022Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758788051115-100', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783610488281-1790433142627-pf2m5', 'How many mEq are found in a phosphorus diet limited to 1,000 mg per day? *', '65', 'Phosphorus primarily circulates in extracellular and intracellular fluids as phosphate ions, predominantly a mixture of divalent ($HPO_4^{2-}$) and monovalent ($H_2PO_4^{-}$) species at physiological blood pH (7.4) in a roughly 4:1 ratio. For clinical nutrition and renal calculations involving elemental phosphorus (atomic weight approximately 30.97 g/mol), an effective valence of roughly 1.8 to 2 is applied depending on the exact ionic distribution, or more commonly in standard dietetic board calculations, the conversion utilizes the atomic weight and an assumed average valence or direct stoichiometric conversion factor where 1 mmol of phosphorus equals roughly 31 mg, and milliequivalents are derived via the formula: mEq = (mg / atomic weight) * valence. At physiological pH, calculating with an average valence of ~2.0 for total phosphate yields (1000 mg / 30.97 mg/mmol) * 2 = ~64.5 mEq, which rounds directly to 65 mEq.', '["14","28","32","65"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-11T14:08:47.920Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":1.9,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-10T14:08:47.920Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.338Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-10T14:08:48.000Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758788070231-101', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783610488281-1790433142627-pf2m5', 'How much sodium is 3 grams of salt? *', '1,200', 'Table salt (sodium chloride) consists of approximately 40% sodium and 60% chloride.

To find the amount of sodium in 3 grams of salt:
• Convert 3 grams of salt to milligrams: 3 grams = 3,000 milligrams.
• Calculate the sodium portion: 3,000 milligrams multiplied by 0.40 = 1,200 milligrams of sodium.', '["1,200","1,400","1,600","1,800"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-14T02:56:30.954Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-13T02:56:30.954Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.338Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-13T02:56:31.028Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758788079296-102', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783610488281-1790433142627-pf2m5', 'Using the quickest method for weight calculation, determine the weight for a small frame male at 5’10”. * hamwi method', '150', 'Step-by-Step Calculation:

1. Calculate Ideal Body Weight (IBW) for a medium frame:
The standard Hamwi method rule for males allocates 106 lbs for the first 5 feet (60 inches) of height, plus 6 lbs for every additional inch.
	• Height: 5''10" (which is 10 inches over 5 feet)
	• Base weight: 106 lbs
	• Additional inches: 10 inches × 6 lbs/inch = 60 lbs
	• Medium Frame Weight: 106 lbs + 60 lbs = 166 lbs

2. Adjust for a small frame size:
The Hamwi method dictates that you must subtract 10% from the medium frame total if the individual has a small frame.
	• 10% of 166 lbs = 16.6 lbs
	• Small Frame Weight: 166 lbs - 16.6 lbs = 149.4 lbs

When rounded to the nearest multiple of ten to match the multiple-choice selection, 149.4 lbs aligns perfectly with 150 lbs.', '["170","160","150","140"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-21T15:26:54.384Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-20T15:26:54.384Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.338Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-20T15:26:54.470Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758788088345-103', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783610488281-1790433142627-pf2m5', 'Maria consulted Ms. Uy, a registered ND for weight reduction. History of her weight gain is as follows: height = 5’5”; wrist circumference = 6”; Actual weight = 180 lbs.; and DBW = 125 lbs. What is Maria’s %DBW change? *', '44%', 'To find a person''s percentage over or under their DBW (the percentage weight change/deviation), you use this formula:
• % DBW Deviation = ((Actual Weight - DBW) ÷ DBW) × 100
• Calculation: ((180 lbs - 125 lbs) ÷ 125 lbs) × 100 = (55 ÷ 125) × 100 = 44%', '["20%","31%","38%","44%"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-16T02:46:45.023Z","interval":3,"easeFactor":2.1,"repetitions":2}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-13T02:46:45.023Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.338Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-13T02:46:45.095Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758788096694-104', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783610488281-1790433142627-pf2m5', 'How many grams of protein will be contained in 1.5 liters of blenderized feeding formula with a caloric density of 2 kcal/ml, 12% of which are from protein? *', '90 g', 'Step-by-Step Calculation:

1. Convert liters to milliliters:
1.5 liters = 1500 ml

2. Calculate the total calories in the formula:
Multiply the total volume by the caloric density.
1500 ml x 2 kcal/ml = 3000 kcal

3. Calculate the calories provided by protein:
Multiply the total calories by the protein percentage.
3000 kcal x 0.12 = 360 kcal from protein

4. Convert protein calories into grams:
Protein provides 4 calories per gram (4 kcal/g). Divide the protein calories by 4.
360 kcal / 4 kcal/g = 90 g', '["90 g","100 g","110 g","120 g"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-21T16:00:34.780Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-20T16:00:34.780Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.338Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-20T16:00:34.869Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758788112112-105', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783610488281-1790433142627-pf2m5', 'You are called to see this previously healthy person who is admitted for diagnostic procedures. You would initiate tube feedings because: *', 'she cannot swallow', 'she cannot swallow', '["skin tests are positive","she cannot swallow","creatine/height ratio is","triceps skinfold is 35 mm"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-16T00:59:15.647Z","interval":3,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":2}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-13T00:59:15.647Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.338Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-08T12:24:43.573Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758788121478-106', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783610488281-1790433142627-pf2m5', 'The most dangerous risk factor in the pathogenesis of cardiovascular disease is: *', 'hypertension', '', '["cigarette smoking","hypertension","hyperlipidemia","obesity"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-03T06:17:19.349Z","interval":3,"easeFactor":2.3,"repetitions":2}'::jsonb, '"2026-09-30T06:17:19.349Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.338Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-30T06:17:19.349Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758788132261-107', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783610488281-1790433142627-pf2m5', 'A 350-pound male is not seen while crossing the street and sustains bilateral femoral fractures. Later, he develops cellulites. You would: *', 'provide nutritional support to maintain body protein', 'During acute metabolic stress, the body aggressively breaks down skeletal muscle to mobilize amino acids for immune function and tissue healing. Providing adequate nutritional support with an emphasis on high protein content is critical to attenuate this muscle wasting, support the immune response to fight the cellulitis, and promote bone healing.

Even though the patient is severely obese at 350 pounds, a period of acute, multi-system trauma is not the time to induce a caloric deficit for the purpose of long-term fat loss. Restricting nutrients or feeding him strictly for weight loss would starve the wound healing process and drastically worsen net protein catabolism.', '["provide nutritional support to maintain body protein","calculate requirement based upon ideal body weight","provide hypocaloric feedings to facilitate weight loss","none of the above"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-11T14:00:44.680Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.3,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-10T14:00:44.680Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.338Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-10T14:00:44.680Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758788142744-108', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783610488281-1790433142627-pf2m5', 'In an end-stage uremic patient, which of the following dietary management would you recommend for the patient’s maintenance regimen? *', '40 grams protein diet', 'End-stage renal disease (ESRD) results in the accumulation of nitrogenous waste products (urea, creatinine, uric acid) due to failing glomerular filtration. While non-dialyzed uremic patients require strict low-protein diets (0.6 to 0.8 g/kg/day) to delay uremic symptoms, patients on maintenance dialysis require higher protein intakes to counteract intradialytic amino acid losses and persistent catabolism induced by the dialysis procedure itself. A tailored moderate protein level, such as 40 grams or higher depending on body weight (with at least 50% high biological value proteins containing essential amino acids), ensures positive nitrogen balance, prevents visceral protein depletion, and limits the accumulation of uremic toxins between treatments.', '["protein-free diet","20 grams protein diet","40 grams protein diet","amino acid supplementation for better protein control"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-22T12:54:02.545Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.3,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-21T12:54:02.545Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.338Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-21T12:54:02.718Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758788151059-109', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783610488281-1790433142627-pf2m5', 'A patient has adapted to starvation because: *', 'urinary N excretion is low', 'During the initial days of fasting, the body breaks down muscle tissue (protein catabolism) to extract amino acids for gluconeogenesis. This results in a high amount of nitrogen being excreted in the urine as urea.

As starvation becomes prolonged, the body undergoes a "protein-sparing" metabolic adaptation. The brain and other vital organs switch their primary fuel source from glucose to ketone bodies derived from fat stores.

Because the body no longer needs to aggressively break down muscle protein to make glucose, urinary nitrogen (N) excretion drops significantly, serving as the classic clinical marker that a patient has successfully adapted to starvation.', '["urinary N excretion is low","his weight loss is associated with sepsis","he has low blood ketones","temperature is 103F rectally"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-16T01:29:57.563Z","interval":3,"easeFactor":2.3,"repetitions":2}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-13T01:29:57.563Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.338Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-13T01:29:57.639Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758788160293-110', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783610488281-1790433142627-pf2m5', 'A non-insulin dependent diabetic who is hypertensive, obese, and non-ambulatory will benefit from which of the following recommendations? *', 'low calorie, no sugar, no fat, no salt', 'low calorie, no sugar, no fat, no salt', '["very low-calorie diet, low salt","very low fat, no salt, high fiber","total low calorie, low Na, high fiber","low calorie, no sugar, no fat, no salt"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-03T07:00:48.495Z","interval":3,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":2}'::jsonb, '"2026-09-30T07:00:48.495Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.338Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-08T12:24:43.573Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758788170544-111', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783610488281-1790433142627-pf2m5', 'A 45-year-old male with cancer of the upper GI tract is admitted for operation. Due to his 15% weight loss, your first recommendation would be to: *', 'feed him at least 10 days preoperatively', 'A 15% unintentional weight loss in a patient with upper GI tract cancer signifies severe malnutrition. Proceeding directly to major abdominal surgery in a severely malnourished patient drastically increases the risk of postoperative complications, including poor wound healing, anastomotic leaks, infections, and death.

Clinical guidelines dictate that patients with severe nutritional risk should receive 7 to 14 days of preoperative nutritional support (ideally via the enteral route, or parenteral if the gut is obstructed) to improve metabolic status and surgical outcomes, even if it means deliberately delaying the operation.', '["rush him to the OR","initiate TPN prior to surgery","advise surgeon to plan feeding jejunostomy at operation","feed him at least 10 days preoperatively"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-11T13:58:43.591Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.3,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-10T13:58:43.591Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.338Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-10T13:58:43.591Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758788179592-112', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783610488281-1790433142627-pf2m5', 'Which is not considered a complementary protein meal? *', 'bread with jam', 'The goal of complementary proteins is to pair foods containing incomplete proteins so that they compensate for each other''s lacking essential amino acids, ultimately providing a complete amino acid profile.

While bread (a grain) contains a small amount of incomplete protein, jam contains virtually zero protein (it is almost entirely simple sugars and carbohydrates). Because jam has no amino acids to contribute, it cannot complement the protein in the bread.', '["rice & monggo","macaroni with cheese","bread with jam","pizza with salami"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-22T12:06:37.699Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.3,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-21T12:06:37.699Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.338Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-21T12:06:38.016Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758788188142-113', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783610488281-1790433142627-pf2m5', 'Which has the highest osmolality? *', 'cola', '', '["sherbet","cola","milk","buko water"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, 'Osmolality measures the concentration of dissolved particles (solutes) per kilogram of fluid. The higher the amount of dissolved simple sugars and electrolytes in a liquid, the higher its osmolality. Standard carbonated colas possess a very high sugar content and high dissolved gas/electrolyte components, leading to a significantly high hypertonic osmolality ranging anywhere from 492 to 811 mOsm/kg.', '{"dueDate":"2026-09-30T07:08:39.340Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.3,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-09-29T07:08:39.340Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.338Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-29T07:08:39.416Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758788196391-114', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783610488281-1790433142627-pf2m5', 'Cirrhotic liver disease will promote a fluid and electrolyte imbalance because: *', 'Na is retained and holds fluid', 'Na is retained and holds fluid', '["excess fluids are lost in the bile","Na is retained and holds fluid","K is excreted in the urine","blood ammonia levels become elevated"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-01T05:09:59.638Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-09-30T05:09:59.638Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.338Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-08T12:24:43.573Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758788207125-115', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783610488281-1790433142627-pf2m5', 'Amino acid supplements taken in excess can lead to: *', 'loss of urinary Ca', 'When amino acid supplements or proteins are consumed in large excess, the body metabolizes them, breaking down sulfur-containing amino acids (like cysteine and methionine). This creates an acidic load (sulfate ions and hydrogen ions) in the bloodstream.

To neutralize and excrete this mild metabolic acidosis, the kidneys alter how they handle minerals. The higher acid load and increased glomerular filtration rate (GFR) directly impair the renal tubules from reabsorbing calcium, causing the body to spill out and lose high levels of calcium in the urine (hypercalciuria).', '["K retention","loss of urinary Ca","loss of Fe","vitamin deficiencies"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-21T15:53:27.653Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-20T15:53:27.653Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.338Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-20T15:53:27.749Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758788218074-116', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783610488281-1790433142627-pf2m5', 'An NPO diet is indicated prior to surgery to: *', 'prevent vomiting', '• The Risk of Anesthesia: General anesthesia relaxes the body''s protective airway reflexes, including the gag and cough reflexes, and relaxes the lower esophageal sphincter.
• Prevention of Aspiration: If a patient has food or fluid in their stomach during surgery, they are at a very high risk of vomiting or experiencing passive regurgitation while unconscious. Because their airway reflexes are suppressed, the acidic stomach contents can easily enter the lungs (pulmonary aspiration), leading to severe aspiration pneumonia, chemical pneumonitis, or airway obstruction. Going NPO (Nothing by Mouth) ensures the stomach is empty and safe prior to the procedure.', '["avoid dehydration","prevent vomiting","reduce bowel movement","none of the above"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-11T14:09:48.235Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.1,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-10T14:09:48.235Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.338Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-10T14:09:48.313Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758788228571-117', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783610488281-1790433142627-pf2m5', 'A patient who was morbidly obese underwent stomach resection. What possible side effect can she experience as a result of the surgery? *', 'vitamin B12 malabsorption', 'vitamin B12 malabsorption', '["difficulty in chewing","vitamin B12 malabsorption","diabetes mellitus","none of the above"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-01T05:03:06.619Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-09-30T05:03:06.619Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.338Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-08T12:24:43.573Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758788241723-118', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783610488281-1790433142627-pf2m5', 'A 15-year old insulin dependent diabetic girl is admitted in the hospital due to hyperglycemia. An appropriate nutrition education plan should begin with: *', 'a review of the relationship between blood glucose levels and intake of simple sugars', 'When a pediatric patient is acutely admitted for a crisis like hyperglycemia, nutrition education must begin with simple, high-yield, and immediate cause-and-effect relationships. Reviewing how simple sugars directly spike blood glucose levels provides the teen with immediate, understandable insight into her current clinical crisis.', '["a printed material that discusses the pathophysiology of diabetes","a detailed explanation of the exchange list and how to use it in meal planning","a review of the relationship between blood glucose levels and intake of simple sugars","a quiz to evaluate the patient’s knowledge of diet and diabetes"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-22T12:54:13.044Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.3,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-21T12:54:13.044Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.338Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-21T12:54:13.219Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758788250154-119', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783610488281-1790433142627-pf2m5', 'Foods which may be included in a full liquid diet: *', 'gelatin', 'all of the above', '["gelatin","soft cooked egg","all of the above","none of the above"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-01T05:21:45.944Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-09-30T05:21:45.944Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.338Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-08T12:24:43.573Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758788258339-120', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783610488281-1790433142627-pf2m5', 'The sodium output of the kidneys is controlled by: *', 'aldosterone', '', '["insulin","testosterone","aldosterone","ergosterone"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-09-29T15:52:04.733Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-09-28T15:52:04.733Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.338Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-28T15:52:04.733Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758788267005-121', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783610488281-1790433142627-pf2m5', 'How much carbohydrates would be contained in a 2000 ml tube feeding formula with a calorie density of 1.5 kcal/ml, 63% of which are from carbohydrates? *', '470g', 'First, calculate the total calories in the formula: 2000 ml multiplied by 1.5 kcal/ml equals 3000 total kcal.

Next, find the calories coming from carbohydrates: 3000 total kcal multiplied by 63 percent (0.63) equals 1890 kcal from carbohydrates.

Finally, convert the carbohydrate calories into grams (since 1 gram of carbohydrate provides 4 kcal): 1890 kcal divided by 4 kcal per gram equals 472.5 grams.', '["315g","470g","483g","396g"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-16T02:44:49.799Z","interval":3,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":2}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-13T02:44:49.799Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.338Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-13T02:44:49.873Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758788279690-122', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783610488281-1790433142627-pf2m5', 'A gradual transition from an IV feeding to an oral diet is primarily designed to: *', 'ensure that nutrient needs will continue to be met', 'The primary goal of a gradual transition (often called a "transitional feed") from intravenous (IV) nutrition to an oral diet is to ensure nutritional adequacy and patient safety. When a patient has been on IV nutrition, their gastrointestinal tract may have reduced capacity to absorb nutrients right away. 

By slowly tapering off the IV fluids while introducing oral foods, clinicians can track oral intake, verify that the gut is tolerating and absorbing the food properly, and ensure that the patient does not experience nutritional deficits during the switch.', '["improve the appetite","prevent hypoglycemia","ensure that nutrient needs will continue to be met","prevent apprehension about eating"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-16T02:46:29.133Z","interval":3,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":2}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-13T02:46:29.133Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.338Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-13T02:46:29.207Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758788287071-123', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783610488281-1790433142627-pf2m5', 'Preferred source of energy in COPD: *', 'fats', 'Patients with Chronic Obstructive Pulmonary Disease (COPD) struggle to exhale carbon dioxide (CO₂). Fats are the preferred energy source because they have a low Respiratory Quotient (around 0.7). This means that when the body metabolizes fats for energy, it produces less CO₂ per molecule of oxygen consumed compared to other macronutrients.

Meanwhile, Carbohydrates have an RQ of 1.0, meaning they produce the highest amount of CO₂ waste when broken down. This places an extra load on already compromised lungs, leading to respiratory distress.

A high-fat, low-carbohydrate diet helps reduce the overall burden on a COPD patient''s respiratory system while still providing adequate calories to fight tissue wasting.', '["protein","fats","carbohydrates","all of the above"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, 'The primary reason fats are the preferred energy source in Chronic Obstructive Pulmonary Disease (COPD) comes down to the Respiratory Quotient (the ratio of carbon dioxide produced to oxygen consumed by the body). Fats have a low RQ of approximately 0.7, meaning their metabolism generates significantly less carbon dioxide (CO2) compared to other nutrients. ', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-11T14:10:16.286Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.1,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-10T14:10:16.286Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.338Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-10T14:10:16.361Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758788297587-124', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783610488281-1790433142627-pf2m5', 'All of the following can be delivered intravenously except: *', 'hydrolyzed enteral formulas', 'Enteral formulas—including hydrolyzed or elemental formulas—are strictly designed to be processed through the gastrointestinal tract via oral ingestion or a feeding tube (such as an NG tube, G-tube, or J-tube).

Even though these formulas are "pre-digested" into smaller parts for patients with impaired gut absorption, they contain intact particles, fiber residues, and non-sterile compounds that are highly dangerous if introduced directly into the bloodstream. Delivering an enteral formula intravenously can cause catastrophic blood clots, septic shock, or fatal embolisms.', '["hydrolyzed enteral formulas","carbohydrate","amino acids","dextrose"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-11T14:01:32.456Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.3,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-10T14:01:32.456Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.338Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-10T14:01:32.532Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758788305903-125', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783610488281-1790433142627-pf2m5', 'A gluten-free diet excludes: *', 'wheat, barley, rye, and oats', 'A strict gluten-free diet permanently eliminates grains containing the protein gluten (and its toxic prolamin fractions: gliadin in wheat, secalin in rye, and hordein in barley). These cause severe small intestinal mucosal damage in individuals with celiac disease or gluten intolerance.

In classic nursing, nutrition, and board exam question banks (such as the Philippine Food Exchange List frameworks), oats are historically grouped alongside wheat, barley, and rye as prohibited foods. This is due to a high risk of cross-contamination during harvesting and processing, as well as the presence of avenin (a protein in oats similar to gluten that some sensitive individuals react to).', '["wheat, corn, and oats","barely, soybeans, oats, and corn","wheat, barley, rye, and oats","wheat, barley, rice, and oats"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-22T11:18:13.204Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-21T11:18:13.204Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.338Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-21T11:18:13.518Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758788322617-126', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783610488281-1790433142627-pf2m5', 'A soft diet for a patient with indigestion would include the following except: *', 'use foods that are chopped or pureed', 'A standard gastrointestinal soft diet (often called a bland or low-residue diet) is modified in chemical composition and digestibility rather than mechanical texture. It aims to ease abdominal distress, bloating, and irritation caused by indigestion (dyspepsia).

Mechanically altering foods by chopping, grinding, or pureeing is a requirement of a Mechanical Soft Diet or Pureed Diet, which is prescribed for patients with chewing or swallowing difficulties (such as poor dentition, dysphagia, or mouth surgery). A patient suffering from basic indigestion can chew normally; therefore, chopping or pureeing food is completely unnecessary.', '["limit highly seasoned foods","limit foods that produce gas","use foods that are chopped or pureed","limit high-fiber foods"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-22T12:38:51.312Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.3,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-21T12:38:51.312Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.338Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-21T12:38:51.491Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758788332085-127', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783610488281-1790433142627-pf2m5', 'Standard liquid diets provide all of the following except: *', 'a diet that is nutritionally complete', 'a diet that is nutritionally complete', '["a test of a person’s ability to tolerate oral foods","a diet that is nutritionally complete","a diet that can be eaten with minimal effort","a source of fluid and electrolytes"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-16T00:59:25.499Z","interval":3,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":2}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-13T00:59:25.499Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.338Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-08T12:24:43.573Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758788344702-128', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783610488281-1790433142627-pf2m5', 'Bland diets eliminate all of the following foods except: *', 'foods that delay gastric emptying', 'The core purpose of a bland diet is to reduce chemical and mechanical irritation to the gastrointestinal tract, decrease stomach acid secretion, and give the digestive system a rest.

Foods that delay gastric emptying (such as high-fat foods) are actually eliminated from a bland diet. High-fat items sit in the stomach longer, which stimulates prolonged gastric acid production and increases the risk of acid reflux. Therefore, a bland diet specifically incorporates easily digestible, low-fat carbohydrates and lean proteins that clear the stomach quickly rather than delaying emptying.', '["foods that cause indigestion","foods that irritate the lining of the esophagus and stomach","foods that significantly increase gastric acid production","foods that delay gastric emptying"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-22T12:50:04.597Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.3,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-21T12:50:04.597Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.338Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-21T12:50:04.597Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758788352733-129', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783610488281-1790433142627-pf2m5', 'Sudden tissue death caused by blockages of vessels that feed the heart muscle: *', 'heart attack', 'The literal medical definition of myocardial infarction is tissue death (infarction) of the heart muscle (myocardio) caused by a lack of oxygen (ischemia). This occurs abruptly when a coronary vessel—one of the arteries feeding oxygen-rich blood to the heart itself—becomes completely blocked by a blood clot or ruptured plaque.

• Stroke: This is sudden tissue damage or death, but it occurs in the brain, caused by a blockage or rupture of blood vessels feeding brain tissue.
• Transient Ischemic Attack (TIA): Often called a "mini-stroke," this involves a temporary blockage of blood vessels feeding the brain. Because the clot breaks apart quickly, it does not cause permanent tissue death.', '["stroke","transient ischemic attack","heart attack","none of the above"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-22T12:53:01.815Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.3,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-21T12:53:01.815Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.338Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-21T12:53:01.815Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758788360417-130', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783610488281-1790433142627-pf2m5', 'Using the NPC method, compute for the CHO and fat of a diet prescription with 2000kcal, P55g. *', 'C265g, F80g', 'Step-by-Step Breakdown using the Non-Protein Calorie (NPC) Method

To calculate the carbohydrate (CHO) and fat allocation for this diet prescription, we follow the standard percentage distribution of non-protein calories (using a standard 60% CHO and 40% Fat split for the remaining energy):

1. Calculate Protein Calories:
	• Protein allowance = 55g
	• Energy value of protein = 4 kcal/g
	• Protein Calories: 55g * 4 kcal = 220 kcal

2. Calculate Total Non-Protein Calories (NPC):
	• Total Energy Requirement (TER) = 2000 kcal
	• NPC = Total Calories - Protein Calories
	• NPC = 2000 kcal - 220 kcal = 1780 kcal

3. Compute Carbohydrate (CHO) Grams (60% of NPC):
	• CHO Calories = 1780 kcal * 0.60 = 1068 kcal
	• Energy value of carbohydrates = 4 kcal/g
	• CHO Grams = 1068 kcal / 4 = 267g
	• Rounding off to the nearest 5 grams = 265g

4. Compute Fat Grams (40% of NPC):
	• Fat Calories = 1780 kcal * 0.40 = 712 kcal
	• Energy value of fat = 9 kcal/g
	• Fat Grams = 712 kcal / 9 = 79.1g
	• Rounding off to the nearest 5 grams = 80g', '["C265g, F80g","C250g, F110g","C300g, F90g","C350g, F65g"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-22T11:59:28.891Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-21T11:59:28.891Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.338Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-21T11:59:29.202Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758788368667-131', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783610488281-1790433142627-pf2m5', 'Treatment for constipation: *', 'high fiber foods', '', '["high fiber foods","rest after meals","low fiber foods","limited fluids"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-21T15:44:39.172Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-20T15:44:39.172Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.338Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-20T15:44:39.261Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758788382553-132', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783610488281-1790433142627-pf2m5', 'Dr. Cruz ordered an additional 500mg Na in the diet of Maria Pecson. How much salt should be added to her food? *', '1250mg', 'Table salt (sodium chloride) is composed of approximately 40% sodium and 60% chloride.

To find the amount of salt needed to provide a specific amount of pure sodium, you divide the milligram amount of sodium by 0.40:

500 mg of sodium divided by 0.40 = 1250 mg of salt.', '["1500mg","500mg","1000mg","1250mg"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-16T02:43:07.498Z","interval":3,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":2}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-13T02:43:07.498Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.338Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-13T02:43:07.572Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758788394064-133', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783610488281-1790433142627-pf2m5', 'Characteristics of Type 1 diabetes: *', 'It is associated with ketosis and acidosis.', '• In Type 1 diabetes, the body''s immune system destroys pancreatic beta cells, leaving the body with an absolute lack of insulin.
• Because the body completely lacks the insulin required to pull glucose into cells for fuel, cells are starved for energy. The body compensates by rapidly breaking down fats into free fatty acids. A byproduct of this accelerated fat metabolism is the accumulation of acidic ketone bodies in the blood, which drops the blood''s pH and leads to diabetic ketoacidosis (DKA).', '["It is associated with ketosis and acidosis.","It is the predominant form of diabetes.","It frequently goes undiagnosed.","Insulin secretion is ineffective in preventing hyperglycemia."]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-01T07:03:28.114Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.3,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-09-30T07:03:28.114Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.338Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-30T07:03:28.114Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758788404230-134', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783610488281-1790433142627-pf2m5', 'A simple IV solution is most appropriate for: *', 'people who are well-nourished and are expected to eat in a few days', 'A simple IV solution (such as standard Normal Saline or D5W) contains only water, basic electrolytes, and sometimes a minimal amount of dextrose for basic cellular energy. It provides zero protein, zero fat, and very few calories.

Because it lacks any real nutritional substance, a simple IV is only meant for short-term hydration and electrolyte maintenance in patients who already have good nutritional reserves and will return to eating solid food very quickly.

Meanwhile, patients who are malnourished, have high metabolic nutrient demands, or cannot eat for prolonged periods require complex Parenteral Nutrition (TPN/PPN). TPN is an advanced, customized chemical cocktail delivering concentrated amino acids, lipids, vitamins, and minerals directly into the bloodstream to keep a starving patient alive.', '["people who are well-nourished and are expected to eat in a few days","people who have high nutrient needs","people who cannot eat for long periods of time","people who are malnourished"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '• Simple intravenous solutions (such as 0.9% Normal Saline or D5W) are designed primarily for short-term fluid maintenance, rehydration, and electrolyte balance.
• Nutritional Limitations: Simple IV crystalloid solutions do not provide adequate calories, amino acids, or complex nutrients. A person can safely rely on them for only a few days if they enter the hospital well-nourished, as the body can temporarily lean on its own nutritional reserves until oral intake resumes.', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-11T14:02:34.443Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.3,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-10T14:02:34.443Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.338Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-10T14:02:34.516Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758788413748-135', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783610488281-1790433142627-pf2m5', 'Diet therapy for a person with coronary heart failure may include: *', 'high calorie, high protein, low sodium diet', 'high calorie, high protein, low sodium diet', '["fat restrictions","fiber restrictions","potassium restriction","high calorie, high protein, low sodium diet"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-21T15:47:02.374Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-20T15:47:02.374Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.338Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-08T12:24:43.573Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758788421998-136', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783610488281-1790433142627-pf2m5', 'The best food to give when a patient has a blood sugar of 60mg. *', '1 tbsp sugar', 'A blood sugar level of 60 mg/dL indicates hypoglycemia (low blood sugar), which is defined as any value under 70 mg/dL. To properly treat this, clinical guidelines recommend the "Rule of 15": consume 15 grams of fast-acting, simple carbohydrates, wait 15 minutes, and recheck the blood sugar.

• 1/3 cup fruit juice: A 1/3 cup serving is only about 10 grams, which under-treats an adult.
• 5 glucose tablets: Standard glucose tablets usually contain 4 grams of carbohydrates each. Taking 5 tablets (20 grams) over-treats a mild low, which can cause a sharp rebound spike.
• 1 tsp honey: One teaspoon is only 5 grams.', '["1 tbsp sugar","1 tsp honey","1/3 cup fruit juice","5 glucose tablets"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-14T01:18:53.685Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-13T01:18:53.685Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.338Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-13T01:18:53.759Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758788434881-137', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783610488281-1790433142627-pf2m5', 'A renal diet intentionally restricts all of the following except: *', 'calcium', '', '["calcium","phosphorus","fluid","protein"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-16T01:28:55.992Z","interval":3,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":2}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-13T01:28:55.992Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.338Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-13T01:28:55.992Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758788442062-138', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783610488281-1790433142627-pf2m5', 'An adult with a usual body weight of 56kg will have fluid needs of about: *', '2000ml', 'Step-by-Step Calculation:

To calculate standard adult daily fluid maintenance needs, clinicians use the standard weight-based guideline of 35 ml per kg of body weight for healthy adults:

1. Multiply the individual''s usual body weight by the fluid factor:
56 kg x 35 ml/kg = 1960 ml

2. Match to the closest multiple choice selection:
1960 ml rounds directly up to 2000 ml.', '["2000ml","1500ml","2500ml","3000ml"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-22T12:53:27.675Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.3,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-21T12:53:27.675Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.338Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-21T12:53:27.675Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758788449130-139', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783610488281-1790433142627-pf2m5', 'How much protein is allowed for patients with gout? *', '1 g/kg BW', 'According to nutritional standards and clinical practice guides for hyperuricemia and gout patients, the recommended daily dietary protein allowance is 1 g per kilogram of body weight (1 g/kg BW).

While a high total volume of protein is not responsible for raising uric acid levels, the source of the protein matters. Gout patients are encouraged to meet their standard protein requirement (1 g/kg BW) by focusing on low-purine or plant-based protein options (such as low-fat dairy, eggs, tofu, and legumes) while strictly limiting high-purine animal proteins like organ meats, red meat, and seafood.', '["0.5 g/kg BW","0.8 g/kg BW","1 g/kg BW","5 g/kg BW"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-11T13:45:17.911Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.3,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-10T13:45:17.911Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.338Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-10T13:45:17.987Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758788457648-140', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783610488281-1790433142627-pf2m5', 'The best prescription for a female secretary with a height of 5 ft. 4 inches, weight of 70kg, and FBS of 7mmol/L: *', '1400 kcal/day', 'Step-by-Step Breakdown Using Standard Clinical Nutrition Formulas

To determine the best caloric prescription, we must calculate the patient''s Desirable Body Weight (DBW) and apply the correct activity factor adjusted for her clinical status (Overweight + Diabetic).

1. Find the Desirable Body Weight (DBW) using Tannhauser''s Method:
	• Convert height to centimeters: 5 ft. 4 inches = 64 inches * 2.54 = 162.56 cm
	• Deduct 100 from height in cm: 162.56 - 100 = 62.56 kg
	• Deduct 10% for the final DBW: 62.56 - 6.25 = 56.31 kg (rounded to 56 kg)

2. Evaluate her Clinical Status & Activity Level:
	• Current Weight: 70 kg (Since her DBW is 56 kg, she is significantly overweight).
	• FBS (Fasting Blood Sugar): 7.0 mmol/L (This meets the diagnostic threshold for Diabetes Mellitus).
	• Activity Level: As an office secretary, her physical activity is classified as Sedentary.

3. Apply the Weight Reduction/Diabetic Caloric Factor:
	• For a normal weight individual with a sedentary lifestyle, the baseline factor is 30 kcal/kg DBW.
	• However, because she is an overweight diabetic, a low-calorie weight reduction regimen is required to optimize insulin sensitivity. The textbook factor for weight reduction in sedentary individuals is 25 kcal/kg DBW.

4. Compute the Total Energy Requirement (TER):
	• TER = 56.31 kg (DBW) * 25 kcal/kg = 1407.75 kcal/day

Rounding to the nearest multiple of 100 as standard practice in dietetic prescriptions yields exactly 1400 kcal/day.', '["800 kcal/day","1000 kcal/day","1200 kcal/day","1400 kcal/day"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-22T12:52:47.960Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.3,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-21T12:52:47.960Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.338Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-21T12:52:47.960Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758788481962-141', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783610488281-1790433142627-pf2m5', 'Refer to the following information: A 25-year old female office clerk consulted at an out-patient clinic for dizziness and headache. She is 5’4” and weighs 70 kg. Her BP is 150/80 mmHg. This is her first consultation.
What is her DBW? *', '56.3 kg', 'Answer: <div>c. 1700 kcal</div>', '["56.3 kg","62.6 kg","163 lbs.","138 lbs."]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-01T06:19:51.687Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-09-30T06:19:51.687Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.338Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-08T12:24:43.573Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758788499561-142', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783610488281-1790433142627-pf2m5', 'Refer to the following information: A 25-year old female office clerk consulted at an out-patient clinic for dizziness and headache. She is 5’4” and weighs 70 kg. Her BP is 150/80 mmHg. This is her first consultation.
What is her TER per day? *', '1700 kcal', 'Answer: <div>c. 1700 kcal</div>', '["1400 kcal","1550 kcal","1700 kcal","2100 kcal"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-01T06:17:15.035Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-09-30T06:17:15.035Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.338Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-08T12:24:43.573Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758788515243-143', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783610488281-1790433142627-pf2m5', 'Refer to the following information: A 25-year old female office clerk consulted at an out-patient clinic for dizziness and headache. She is 5’4” and weighs 70 kg. Her BP is 150/80 mmHg. This is her first consultation.
Which of the following foods would you recommend? *', 'rice, pan de sal, camote tops, fish', 'The patient’s blood pressure is 150/80 mmHg, which classifies as Stage 2 Hypertension (specifically elevated systolic pressure). A primary nutritional intervention for managing high blood pressure is a low-sodium, heart-healthy diet rich in potassium and fiber (similar to the DASH diet approach).
	• Fresh Fish: Provides a lean, high-quality source of protein that is naturally low in sodium and rich in heart-healthy omega-3 fatty acids.
	• Camote Tops (Sweet Potato Leaves): A local powerhouse vegetable that is highly concentrated in potassium. Potassium actively works to lower blood pressure by helping the kidneys excrete excess sodium and relaxing blood vessel walls.
	• Rice & Pan de Sal: Serve as staple carbohydrates. While traditional pan de sal contains a small amount of salt, the meal as a whole is overwhelmingly comprised of fresh, unprocessed items.

The remaining options contain heavily processed, canned, or cured foods (ham, corned beef, pork and beans) that are loaded with hidden sodium, which can acutely worsen hypertension by causing fluid retention and increasing blood vessel pressure:', '["pan de sal, rice, cottage cheese, corned beef, ampalaya","rice, pan de sal, camote tops, fish","pork and beans, carrots, banana, rice","milk, ham, eggs, rice"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-22T11:11:10.701Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-21T11:11:10.701Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.338Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-21T11:11:11.007Z"'::jsonb)
