('anki-card-1758788522743-144', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783610488281-1790433142627-pf2m5', 'Principal measured index of renal failure. *', 'urea', '', '["urea","hemoglobin","glucose","amino acid"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-09-30T07:02:10.230Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-09-29T07:02:10.230Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.338Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-29T07:02:10.230Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758788532694-145', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783610488281-1790433142627-pf2m5', 'The primary purpose of nutrition screening is to: *', 'identify clients who need complete nutrition assessments', 'identify clients who need complete nutrition assessments', '["define nutritional status","identify clients who need complete nutrition assessments","determine nutrient needs","provide a basis for nutrition care plan"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-16T02:52:33.066Z","interval":3,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":2}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-13T02:52:33.066Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.338Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-08T12:24:43.573Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758788543791-146', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783610488281-1790433142627-pf2m5', 'The nutritionist-dietitian must closely monitor the client with nephrotic syndrome for deficiencies of which vitamin? *', 'D', 'Nephrotic syndrome is characterized by heavy proteinuria (>3.5 g/day), which leads to the urinary excretion of low-molecular-weight plasma proteins, including the 58-kDa vitamin D-binding protein (DBP). Because the majority of circulating 25-hydroxyvitamin D [25(OH)D] and 1,25-dihydroxyvitamin D [1,25(OH)2D] are bound to DBP, their continuous loss in the urine depletes total body stores. Furthermore, impaired renal parenchyma and chronic inflammation decrease the activity of 1alpha-hydroxylase, reducing the conversion of 25(OH)D to its active hormonal form, 1,25-dihydroxyvitamin D, thereby disrupting calcium homeostasis and secondary bone mineralization.', '["A","B₆","D","K"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-11T14:09:27.907Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.1,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-10T14:09:27.907Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.338Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-10T14:09:27.980Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758788551991-147', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783610488281-1790433142627-pf2m5', 'Aside from table salt, a low sodium diet limits the use of: *', 'processed foods', 'Aside from table salt, processed and ultra-processed foods are the single largest source of sodium in the modern diet. Manufacturers add massive amounts of sodium to packaged meals, canned soups, cured meats, and snacks for preservation and flavor enhancement. A low-sodium diet strictly mandates limiting these items.', '["fried foods","processed foods","dialyzed milk","all of the above"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-11T13:37:27.436Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.3,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-10T13:37:27.436Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.338Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-10T13:37:27.512Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758788559841-148', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783610488281-1790433142627-pf2m5', 'A ketogenic diet is characterized as: *', 'high fat, low CHO', 'A classic ketogenic diet is structured to shift the body''s primary metabolic fuel source from glucose to fat. To induce this state of ketosis, the diet must dramatically restrict carbohydrates (CHO) and replace those calories with dietary fat.

The standard ketogenic diet typically derives approximately 70% to 80% of daily calories from fat, 15% to 20% from protein, and only 5% to 10% from carbohydrates.', '["high fat, low CHO","low fat, high CHO","high protein, low CHO","high protein, low fat"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-21T15:31:26.182Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-20T15:31:26.182Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.338Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-20T15:31:26.272Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758788568541-149', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783610488281-1790433142627-pf2m5', 'Characterized by low grade fever, body tissue wasting, and dry cough. *', 'tuberculosis', 'This infectious disease is caused by the bacterium Mycobacterium tuberculosis. The classic symptoms of pulmonary TB include a lingering low-grade fever (often spiking in the afternoon or evening), dry cough (which may start dry before becoming productive or producing blood), and severe body tissue wasting (traditionally referred to as "consumption" due to profound, unintended weight loss and muscle atrophy).

	• COPD: Characterized primarily by a chronic productive cough (with mucus), wheezing, and progressive shortness of breath, rather than a presenting low-grade fever.
	• AIDS: While it causes wasting and fever, it is a complex systemic syndrome of immune failure that manifests through a wide variety of opportunistic infections rather than a localized dry cough complex.
	• Cholecystitis: This is an acute or chronic inflammation of the gallbladder that presents with sharp upper abdominal pain, nausea, and potentially a high fever, rather than chronic respiratory symptoms or progressive body wasting.', '["COPD","AIDS","cholecystitis","tuberculosis"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-11T13:39:31.454Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.3,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-10T13:39:31.454Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.338Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-10T13:39:31.526Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758788577374-150', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783610488281-1790433142627-pf2m5', 'A malabsorption problem brought about by shorter and less number of intestinal villi: *', 'non-tropical sprue', 'Non-tropical sprue (commonly known as celiac disease or gluten-sensitive enteropathy) is an autoimmune disorder triggered by ingesting gluten. The immune system mistakenly attacks the lining of the small intestine, leading to severe inflammation, crypt hyperplasia, and structural damage characterized by the shortening, flattening, and reduction in the number of intestinal villi (villous atrophy). Since villi are responsible for expanding the surface area of the gut, their damage drastically impairs nutrient absorption.', '["ulcerative colitis","tropical sprue","short gut syndrome","non-tropical sprue"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-22T12:22:50.163Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.3,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-21T12:22:50.163Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.338Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-21T12:22:50.340Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758788585823-151', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783610488281-1790433142627-pf2m5', 'Considered “neutral foods” when following either an alkaline ash or an acid ash diet: *', 'butter, candy, sugar', 'Foods that are primarily composed of pure fats, oils, or refined sugars leave behind no mineral residue that can alter urinary pH. Therefore, butter, cooking oils, margarine, plain candies, and table sugar are classified as strictly neutral. 

• Acid Ash Foods: Foods high in protein, phosphorus, and sulfur leave behind an acidic residue. This includes meat, fish, poultry, eggs, and whole grains.
• Alkaline Ash Foods: Foods rich in minerals like magnesium, potassium, and calcium leave behind an alkaline residue. This includes almost all fruits and vegetables (even acidic ones like lemons, because the organic citric acid is fully oxidized into water and CO₂, leaving alkaline minerals behind).', '["fruits and vegetables","butter, candy, sugar","whole grains and eggs","meat, fish, poultry"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-11T14:10:20.170Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.1,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-10T14:10:20.170Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.338Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-10T14:10:20.244Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758788596673-152', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783610488281-1790433142627-pf2m5', 'Desensitization is a treatment for: *', 'allergy', '', '["ulcerative colitis","psoriasis","acne","allergy"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-01T04:56:25.160Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-09-30T04:56:25.160Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.338Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-30T04:56:25.160Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758788606289-153', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783610488281-1790433142627-pf2m5', 'All measures are allowed to increase the energy intake of a renal patient except: *', 'serving fruit cocktail with heavy syrup', 'Preparing foods with oil', '["adding unsalted margarine","using additional sugar in foods","serving fruit cocktail with heavy syrup","preparing foods with oil"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-09-30T07:02:16.090Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-09-29T07:02:16.090Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.338Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-08T12:24:43.573Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758788616456-154', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783610488281-1790433142627-pf2m5', 'Severely malnourished children must be rehabilitated at the shortest time possible. Choose the diet for a malnourished child with diarrhea who has just been admitted in the hospital. *', 'regular foods plus a liquid preparation', 'In severe acute malnutrition (SAM) with diarrhea, mucosal atrophy, brush border enzyme depletion (lactase, sucrase), and impaired sodium-glucose cotransporter (SGLT-1) function demand cautious, low-osmolarity oral rehydration and specialized starter formulas (F-75 transitioning rapidly to F-100) combined with soft, regular foods to restore enterocyte integrity, provide adequate macronutrients for catch-up growth, and prevent villous atrophy without worsening osmotic diarrhea.', '["liquid diet high in calories and protein","BRAT","regular foods plus a liquid preparation","regular diet consisting of foods liked and disliked"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-22T12:51:04.544Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.3,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-21T12:51:04.544Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.338Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-21T12:51:04.544Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758788624621-155', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783610488281-1790433142627-pf2m5', 'Diet for calcium oxalate urinary calculi. *', 'alkaline ash diet', 'Calcium oxalate stones are the most common type of kidney stones. They tend to form more easily in acidic urine. An alkaline ash diet increases the pH of the urine (making it less acidic), which helps keep calcium oxalate and uric acid crystals dissolved so they can be passed safely. This diet focuses on eating foods like vegetables, fruits, and legumes, while reducing acid-forming foods like meats, cheeses, and grains.', '["acid ash diet","alkaline ash diet","neutral diet","low calcium diet"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-11T14:11:23.963Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":1.9,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-10T14:11:23.963Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.338Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-10T14:11:24.045Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758788632653-156', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783610488281-1790433142627-pf2m5', 'It is given when nutrient requirements are greatly increased and the GIT is not functional. *', 'TPN', 'When a patient''s gastrointestinal tract (GIT) is non-functional, blocked, or requires complete clinical rest, enteral options like a gastrostomy or a nasogastric tube (NGT) cannot be used safely. Bypassing the gut entirely becomes mandatory.

While basic "intravenous feeding" usually refers to peripheral lines giving short-term hydration or dilute nutrients (like PPN), TPN is a highly concentrated, hyperosmolar formula packed with complete macronutrients, vitamins, and minerals. Because it is designed to meet massive metabolic demands (such as in severe burns, sepsis, or major trauma), it must be safely administered through a large central vein.', '["NGT","intravenous feeding","gastrostomy","TPN"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-21T15:59:06.128Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-20T15:59:06.128Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.338Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-20T15:59:06.217Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758788641138-157', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783610488281-1790433142627-pf2m5', 'Method of feeding preferred when GIT is functional. *', 'oral', '', '["oral","gastrostomy","NGT","jejunostomy"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-09-29T16:00:49.737Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-09-28T16:00:49.737Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.338Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-28T16:00:49.737Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758788649670-158', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783610488281-1790433142627-pf2m5', 'The fat content of a 2,000 kcal diet providing 30% of calories from fat. *', '67g', 'Step-by-Step Calculation:

1. Find total calories from fat: Multiply the total calories by the fat percentage.
2,000 kcal x 0.30 = 600 kcal from fat

2. Convert calories to grams: Fat provides 9 calories per gram (9 kcal/g). Divide the fat calories by 9.
600 kcal / 9 kcal/g = 66.67g

When rounded to the nearest whole number, 66.67g gives you 67g.', '["60g","67g","77g","80g"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-21T15:19:44.862Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-20T15:19:44.862Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.338Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-20T15:19:44.952Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758788658988-159', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783610488281-1790433142627-pf2m5', 'Epidemiologic studies have shown that colon cancer is more frequent in populations consuming: *', 'a highly refined, low fiber diet', 'a highly refined, low fiber diet', '["a highly refined, low fiber diet","a high protein diet","a high cholesterol diet","a low calcium diet"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-01T06:46:47.901Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-09-30T06:46:47.901Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.338Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-08T12:24:43.573Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758788760531-160', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783610488281-1790433142627-pf2m5', 'Refer to the following information. Based on the FEL, the diet of a burn patient contains the following meal pattern for lunch: 90g fried chicken; 160g rice; 45g sauteed squash; 250g fresh milk; and 103g ripe mango.
How much is the CHO content of the meal? *', '71 g', 'According to the Philippine Food Exchange List (FEL), each food item is converted into exchange units to calculate its specific carbohydrate (CHO) content:

1. 160g Rice (2 Rice Exchanges):
• 1 exchange of cooked rice = 80g (contains 23g CHO)
• 160g = 2 exchanges
• CHO content: 2 x 23g = 46g

2. 250g Fresh Milk (1 Whole Milk Exchange):
• 1 exchange of whole milk = 250g (contains 12g CHO)
• CHO content: 1 x 12g = 12g

3. 103g Ripe Mango (1 Fruit Exchange):
• 1 exchange of fruit = approx. 100g (contains 10g CHO)
• CHO content: 1 x 10g = 10g

4. 45g Sauteed Squash (1 Vegetable B Exchange):
• 1 exchange of cooked Vegetable B = 45g (contains 3g CHO)
• CHO content: 1 x 3g = 3g (Note: The fat exchange added for sauteing oil contains 0g CHO).

5. 90g Fried Chicken (3 Medium-Fat Meat Exchanges):
• Meat exchanges contain 0g CHO.

Final CHO Summation:

• Rice: 46g
• Fresh Milk: 12g
• Ripe Mango: 10g
• Sauteed Squash: 3g
• Fried Chicken: 0g
• Total CHO Content: 46g + 12g + 10g + 3g = 71g', '["69 g","71 g","73 g","81 g"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-21T16:05:40.396Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-20T16:05:40.396Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.338Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-20T16:05:40.482Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758788776080-161', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783610488281-1790433142627-pf2m5', 'Refer to the following information. Based on the FEL, the diet of a burn patient contains the following meal pattern for lunch: 90g fried chicken; 160g rice; 45g sauteed squash; 250g fresh milk; and 103g ripe mango.
How much is the protein content of the meal? *', '37 g', '• 90g Fried Chicken: 90 grams divided by 30 grams per exchange equals 3 exchanges. 3 exchanges multiplied by 8 grams of protein equals 24 grams of protein.
• 160g Rice: 160 grams divided by 80 grams per exchange equals 2 exchanges. 2 exchanges multiplied by 2 grams of protein equals 4 grams of protein.
• 45g Sauteed Squash: 45 grams equals exactly 1 exchange of vegetable. 1 exchange multiplied by 1 gram of protein equals 1 gram of protein.
• 250g Fresh Milk: 250 grams equals 1 exchange of milk. 1 exchange multiplied by 8 grams of protein equals 8 grams of protein.
• 103g Ripe Mango: 1 exchange of fruit equals 0 grams of protein.

Total Calculation:
24 grams (Chicken) + 4 grams (Rice) + 1 gram (Squash) + 8 grams (Milk) + 0 grams (Mango) = 37 grams of protein', '["36 g","37 g","35 g","45 g"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-22T12:39:41.333Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.3,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-21T12:39:41.333Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.338Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-21T12:39:41.509Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758788797095-162', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783610488281-1790433142627-pf2m5', 'Refer to the following information. Based on the FEL, the diet of a burn patient contains the following meal pattern for lunch: 90g fried chicken; 160g rice; 45g sauteed squash; 250g fresh milk; and 103g ripe mango.
How much is the fat content of the meal? *', '33 g', 'According to the Philippine Food Exchange List (FEL), each food item is converted into exchange units to calculate its specific macronutrient content. Here is the breakdown for each item in the lunch pattern:

1. 160g Rice (2 Rice Exchanges):
• 1 exchange of cooked rice = 80g
• 160g = 2 exchanges
• Fat content = 0g

2. 250g Fresh Milk (1 Whole Milk Exchange):
• 1 exchange of whole milk = 250g
• Fat content = 10g

3. 103g Ripe Mango (1 Fruit Exchange):
• 1 exchange of ripe mango = 1 slice/portion (approx. 100g)
• Fat content = 0g

4. 45g Sauteed Squash (1 Vegetable Exchange + 1 Fat Exchange):
• 1 exchange of cooked squash = 45g (0g fat)
• Since it is sauteed, the FEL rules require adding 1 fat exchange for the cooking oil.
• 1 fat exchange = 5g

5. 90g Fried Chicken (3 Medium-Fat Meat Exchanges):
• 1 exchange of cooked meat = 30g
• 90g = 3 exchanges
• Chicken with skin or fried chicken is classified under Medium-Fat Meat, which contains 6g of fat per exchange.
• 3 exchanges x 6g = 18g

Final Fat Summation:

• Rice: 0g
• Fresh Milk: 10g
• Ripe Mango: 0g
• Sauteed Squash (Oil): 5g
• Fried Chicken: 18g
• Total Fat Content: 10g + 5g + 18g = 33g', '["27 g","30 g","33 g","35 g"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-21T15:35:50.422Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-20T15:35:50.422Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.338Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-20T15:35:50.511Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758788827111-163', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783610488281-1790433142627-pf2m5', 'Refer to the following information. Based on the FEL, the diet of a burn patient contains the following meal pattern for lunch: 90g fried chicken; 160g rice; 45g sauteed squash; 250g fresh milk; and 103g ripe mango.
How many kilocalories does the meal contain? *', '730 kcal', 'Step-by-Step Breakdown using the Food Exchange List (FEL)

To find the total energy value of this meal pattern, we break down each food item into its exact base exchanges and adjust for the preparation methods:

1. 160g Rice (Cooked Rice):
	• Exchange value: 1 exchange of cooked rice is equal to 80g (providing 100 kcal).
	• 160g / 80g = 2 Rice Exchanges.
	• Energy Contribution: 2 * 100 kcal = 200 kcal.

2. 250g Fresh Milk (Whole Milk):
	• Exchange value: 1 glass or 1 cup of whole fresh milk is equal to 250g (providing 170 kcal).
	• 250g / 250g = 1 Whole Milk Exchange.
	• Energy Contribution: 1 * 170 kcal = 170 kcal.

3. 103g Ripe Mango:
	• Exchange value: 1 exchange of ripe mango is equal to 103g gross weight (providing 40 kcal).
	• 103g / 103g = 1 Fruit Exchange.
	• Energy Contribution: 1 * 40 kcal = 40 kcal.

4. 45g Sauteed Squash (Vegetable B + Sauteing Fat):
	• Exchange value: 1 cooked vegetable exchange is equal to 1/2 cup or 45g (providing 16 kcal).
	• Preparation Modifier: Because the squash is sauteed, it adds 1 Fat Exchange (providing 45 kcal) for the cooking oil.
	• Energy Contribution: 16 kcal + 45 kcal = 61 kcal.

5. 90g Fried Chicken (Low-Fat Meat + Frying Fats):
	• Exchange value: Chicken is classified as a Low-Fat Meat, where 1 exchange equals 30g cooked meat (providing 41 kcal). Therefore, 90g / 30g = 3 Low-Fat Meat Exchanges.
	• Preparation Modifier: Because it is fried, each of the 3 meat exchanges absorbs approximately 1 Fat Exchange (3 * 45 kcal = 135 kcal).
	• Energy Contribution: (3 * 41 kcal) + 135 kcal = 258 kcal.

Total Energy Summation
Total Calorie Count = 200 kcal + 170 kcal + 40 kcal + 61 kcal + 258 kcal = 729 kcal

Rounding off to the nearest multiple of five as standard practice in FEL board examination questions, 729 kcal yields exactly 730 kcal.', '["650 kcal","700 kcal","730 kcal","775 kcal"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-22T11:17:02.909Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-21T11:17:02.909Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.338Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-21T11:17:03.223Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758788862311-164', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783610488281-1790433142627-pf2m5', 'It can bring about severe malabsorption: *', 'laxatives', 'Chronic overuse or abuse of laxatives directly induces severe malabsorption syndromes. Laxatives unnaturally accelerate intestinal motility (peristalsis) or alter osmotic pressure, which forces food contents to rush through the gastrointestinal tract. This rapid transit time denies the small and large intestines the necessary window to digest and absorb essential macronutrients, fat-soluble vitamins (like Vitamin D), and electrolytes. Over time, this leads to structural steatorrhea, mineral depletion, and severe undernutrition.

• Diuretics: While diuretics heavily interfere with fluid balance and can lead to severe potassium depletion or dehydration through increased urinary excretion, they impact renal function rather than causing global nutrient malabsorption in the gut.
• Paracetamol: This is a standard over-the-counter analgesic and antipyretic. While acute toxicity or overdose causes severe hepatotoxicity (liver damage), standard therapeutic use does not impair the mucosal lining of the intestines or produce malabsorption.
• Cough Syrup: Typically containing antitussives or expectorants, these formulations do not target or compromise the digestive mechanics or metabolic transport systems of the small intestine.', '["diuretics","laxatives","paracetamol","cough syrup"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-22T12:49:00.972Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-21T12:49:00.972Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.338Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-21T12:49:00.972Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758788870543-165', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783610488281-1790433142627-pf2m5', 'Cholesterol level in food considered safe for daily consumption. *', '300mg', 'For many years, traditional clinical nutrition guidelines and board manuals (such as the American Heart Association and national dietary guidelines) established a strict upper limit of 300 mg of dietary cholesterol per day for healthy adults to reduce the risk of cardiovascular disease.

While modern guidelines have shifted to focusing more broadly on limiting saturated fats, the classic dietary limit taught and tested in academic question banks for a "safe daily consumption level" remains 300 mg.', '["300mg","450mg","400mg","475mg"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-14T02:53:18.286Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-13T02:53:18.286Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.338Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-13T02:53:18.360Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758788878827-166', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783610488281-1790433142627-pf2m5', 'Diet management for a person with esophageal reflux. *', 'low fat, low cholesterol', 'Dietary fat stimulates the release of cholecystokinin (CCK) from the duodenal mucosa. CCK induces relaxation of the lower esophageal sphincter (LES), increasing transient LES relaxations (TLESRs) and facilitating the retrograde flow of acidic gastric contents into the esophagus. Furthermore, high-fat meals delay gastric emptying, prolonging the period of high gastric volume and pressure that promotes reflux episodes. Restricting dietary fat directly attenuates postprandial CCK surges, preserves basal LES tone, and accelerates gastric transit.', '["liquid diet","high calorie, high protein","regular diet with small frequent meals","low fat, low cholesterol"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-22T12:50:51.102Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.1,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-21T12:50:51.102Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.338Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-21T12:50:51.102Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758788886959-167', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783610488281-1790433142627-pf2m5', 'A specific indication of enteral nutrition: *', 'upper gastrointestinal bleed', 'upper gastrointestinal bleed', '["anorexia","inadequate intake","upper gastrointestinal bleed","intractable diarrhea"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-01T06:53:42.565Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-09-30T06:53:42.565Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.338Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-08T12:24:43.573Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758788896092-168', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783610488281-1790433142627-pf2m5', 'Malnutrition due to esophageal tumor is primarily related to: *', 'inadequate intake', 'The defining hallmark of an esophageal tumor is dysphagia (progressive difficulty swallowing) caused by the physical growth of the tumor narrowing the lumen of the esophagus.

While cancer cachexia can induce a catabolic state or alter systemic metabolism over time, the immediate and primary driver of severe protein-calorie malnutrition in these patients at the time of diagnosis is the simple, mechanical inability to physically swallow and consume enough food. This results in massive, structural inadequate food and fluid intake.', '["decreased absorption","inadequate intake","catabolic state","increased energy needs"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-11T14:10:12.680Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.1,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-10T14:10:12.680Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.338Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-10T14:10:12.754Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758788906159-169', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783610488281-1790433142627-pf2m5', 'Patients taking medicines with MAO inhibitors should be advised this diet: *', 'tyramine-restricted', 'Monoamine oxidase inhibitors (MAOIs) are a class of medications used primarily as antidepressants. They work by blocking the enzyme monoamine oxidase, which normally breaks down neurotransmitters as well as tyramine (an amino acid byproduct found in aged and fermented foods).

If a patient taking an MAOI consumes high-tyramine foods, the excess tyramine cannot be broken down. This causes a massive release of norepinephrine, leading to severe vasoconstriction and a dangerous blood pressure spike known as a hypertensive crisis. Therefore, these patients must strictly follow a tyramine-restricted diet.

Aged cheeses (like cheddar, blue, or swiss), cured or smoked meats (like salami and ham), soy sauce, tofu, fermented items (like sauerkraut), tap beer, and red wine.', '["protein-restricted","fat-restricted","tyramine-restricted","phenylalanine-restricted"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-22T12:06:20.710Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-21T12:06:20.710Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.338Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-21T12:06:21.022Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758788914657-170', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783610488281-1790433142627-pf2m5', 'Azotemia is detected through the measurement of: *', 'blood urea nitrogen', '• Azotemia is a medical condition characterized by abnormally high levels of nitrogen-containing compounds (such as urea and creatinine) in the blood.
• Detection: It is primarily detected through a Blood Urea Nitrogen (BUN) test or a serum creatinine test, both of which indicate how well the kidneys are filtering out waste products.', '["blood urea nitrogen","calcium in blood","uric acid in urine","sodium in the spinal fluid"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-03T07:08:58.820Z","interval":3,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":2}'::jsonb, '"2026-09-30T07:08:58.820Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.338Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-30T07:08:58.820Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758788927757-171', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783610488281-1790433142627-pf2m5', 'Exogenous sources of cholesterol include: *', 'dairy and glandular only', '• Exogenous cholesterol refers to cholesterol that comes from outside the body through the food we eat.
• The Golden Rule of Cholesterol: Cholesterol is only found in animal-based foods. Plants do not synthesize cholesterol.', '["dairy products","glandular organs","nuts","dairy and glandular only"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-03T06:56:47.109Z","interval":3,"easeFactor":2.3,"repetitions":2}'::jsonb, '"2026-09-30T06:56:47.109Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.338Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-30T06:56:47.109Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758788937423-172', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783610488281-1790433142627-pf2m5', 'A client has had a poor appetite for several days. Strategies that may be used to improve his food intake may include any of the following except: *', 'feed the client by tube', 'feed the client by tube', '["assist the client during meals","serve foods that are easy to eat","feed the client by tube","provide energy- and nutrient-dense foods"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-03T07:08:40.718Z","interval":3,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":2}'::jsonb, '"2026-09-30T07:08:40.718Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.338Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-08T12:24:43.573Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758788946590-173', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783610488281-1790433142627-pf2m5', 'Tube feedings can be discontinued gradually when: *', 'the client is able to eat foods or drink formula in sufficient amounts', 'Discontinuing a tube feeding (enteral nutrition) is a step-by-step process. Clinicians typically look for the patient to meet at least two-thirds (60% to 66%) of their daily caloric and fluid needs via oral intake (eating solid food or drinking nutrition shakes) before completely pulling or stopping the tube feeding.', '["the client is able to eat foods or drink formula in sufficient amounts","as soon as the medical condition resolves","planning for discharge begins","the client experiences hunger"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-21T15:39:24.298Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-20T15:39:24.298Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.338Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-20T15:39:24.387Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758788967688-174', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783610488281-1790433142627-pf2m5', 'A gradual transition from an IV feeding to an oral diet is primarily designed to?: *', 'ensure that nutrient needs will continue to be met', 'Transitional feeding is carefully structured to manage a patient''s progression from intravenous (parenteral) nutrition to eating by mouth. The foremost priority is ensuring that the patient doesn''t suffer from a drop in required calories, protein, fluids, or micronutrients while they gradually re-learn to chew, swallow, or digest regular food.', '["improve the appetite","prevent hypoglycemia","ensure that nutrient needs will continue to be met","prevent apprehension about eating"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-03T06:17:24.375Z","interval":3,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":2}'::jsonb, '"2026-09-30T06:17:24.375Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.338Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-30T06:17:24.375Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758788975988-175', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783610488281-1790433142627-pf2m5', 'The intravenous lipid emulsion provided two or three times a week in TPN formulas serve primarily as a source of: *', 'essential fatty acid', 'essential fatty acid', '["concentrated energy","essential fatty acid","fat-soluble vitamins","vitamin C"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-14T01:03:16.204Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-13T01:03:16.204Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.338Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-08T12:24:43.573Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758788985771-176', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783610488281-1790433142627-pf2m5', 'Wasting in people with COPD is associated with all of the following except: *', 'use of oxygen masks', 'diarrhea and malabsorption', '["anorexia and reduced food intake","medications used in treatment","use of oxygen masks","diarrhea and malabsorption"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-09-29T15:50:27.825Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-09-28T15:50:27.825Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.338Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-08T12:24:43.573Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758788996155-177', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783610488281-1790433142627-pf2m5', 'Clients undergoing colostomies and ileostomies may temporarily receive a low-fiber, bland diet to: *', 'help the stoma heal', 'After colostomy or ileostomy surgery, the bowel and the newly formed stoma are swollen, inflamed, and healing from surgical trauma. A low-fiber, bland diet minimizes irritation to the intestinal lining, reducing the workload on the gut so it can heal efficiently.

High-fiber foods leave a large amount of indigestible residue (bulk) in the digestive tract. Because the internal lumen of a new stoma is narrowed due to post-operative swelling, a high-fiber intake poses a strict risk of causing a painful mechanical bowel blockage.', '["increase intestinal motility","help the stoma heal","prevent malabsorption","prevent mineral deficiencies"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-21T15:29:56.909Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-20T15:29:56.909Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.338Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-20T15:29:56.997Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758789004420-178', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783610488281-1790433142627-pf2m5', 'Diet therapy for chronic pancreatitis may include all of the following except: *', 'a fat-restricted diet', 'International guidelines (such as ESPEN clinical guidelines) no longer recommend routine, strict dietary fat restriction for chronic pancreatitis. Because these patients suffer from severe malabsorption and weight loss, restricting fat overly constricts their caloric intake and worsens malnutrition. Instead, a normal, balanced physiological diet is prescribed in tandem with proper enzyme dosing. Fat restriction is only used as a temporary, short-term measure if painful fatty stools (steatorrhea) cannot be managed by medications.', '["a fat-restricted diet","enzyme replacements","small meals","a diet to control blood glucose"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-01T07:28:50.407Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-09-30T07:28:50.407Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.338Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-30T07:28:50.407Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758789013354-179', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783610488281-1790433142627-pf2m5', 'Nutrition problems associated with fat malabsorption syndromes include the following except: *', 'oxalate kidney stones', 'oxalate kidney stones', '["essential fatty acid deficiencies","severe weight loss and PEM","bone diseases","oxalate kidney stones"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-03T06:34:26.527Z","interval":3,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":2}'::jsonb, '"2026-09-30T06:34:26.527Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.338Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-08T12:24:43.573Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758789021870-180', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783610488281-1790433142627-pf2m5', 'The following may affect the nutrient needs of people with inflammatory bowel disease except: *', 'dumping syndrome', 'Dumping syndrome is a complication that occurs after gastric surgeries (like a partial or total gastrectomy) where food moves too rapidly from the stomach into the small intestine. It does not directly result from or impact the nutrient needs of Inflammatory Bowel Disease (IBD).

	• Medications: Common IBD drugs like corticosteroids lower calcium and vitamin D absorption, while others like sulfasalazine impair folate absorption, altering daily nutrient requirements.
	• Steatorrhea: Excessive fat in the stool (common in Crohn''s disease due to bile acid malabsorption) causes a severe loss of fat-soluble vitamins (A, D, E, and K) and calories, increasing nutritional requirements.
	• Fistulas: These abnormal tissue tunnels can leak digestive fluids, causing high-volume losses of protein, electrolytes, and fluids, which drastically spike a patient''s nutrient needs.', '["steatorrhea","medications","dumping syndrome","fistulas"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, 'Dumping Syndrome: This is a complication that occurs after gastric (stomach) surgeries—such as a gastric bypass or gastrectomy—where food moves too rapidly from the stomach into the small intestine. It is not an intrinsic feature or complication of Inflammatory Bowel Disease (IBD).', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-11T14:08:19.882Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.1,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-10T14:08:19.882Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.338Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-10T14:08:19.956Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758789038303-181', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783610488281-1790433142627-pf2m5', 'Ms. Padilla, 30 years old, has several family members who have had cancer. She asks the nurse how she can reduce her risk of cancer. What would you suggest? *', 'Reduce her use of smoked, salt-cured, and nitrate-cured foods.', 'Reduce her use of smoked, salt-cured, and nitrate-cured foods.', '["Limit her saturated fat intake to 30% .","Reduce her intake of sugars.","Reduce her consumption of unpeeled fruits and vegetables.","Reduce her use of smoked, salt-cured, and nitrate-cured foods."]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-01T05:10:50.558Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-09-30T05:10:50.558Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.338Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-08T12:24:43.573Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758789046035-182', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783610488281-1790433142627-pf2m5', 'Mr. Santos has lung cancer which has metastasized to his spine. Based on this information, it will be essential for the nutritionist-dietitian to monitor him for: *', 'hypercalcemia', 'When a primary tumor (such as lung cancer) metastasizes to the bone/spine, it initiates osteolytic bone destruction. The invading tumor cells stimulate specialized bone cells (osteoclasts) to break down the surrounding bone tissue.

As the bone structure is aggressively broken down and reabsorbed, massive amounts of calcium are dissolved and released directly into the bloodstream. This rapid influx overwhelms the kidneys'' ability to excrete it, leading to a dangerous oncologic emergency known as Hypercalcemia of Malignancy (HCM).

Monitoring calcium levels is critical because hypercalcemia can lead to severe confusion, muscle weakness, kidney stones, and lethal cardiac arrhythmias. While dietary calcium restriction is generally not the primary treatment for HCM (as the excess calcium is coming from the bones, not food), the dietitian must aggressively monitor hydration status, fluid balance, and electrolyte trends to support clinical management.', '["hypokalemia","hyponatremia","hypercalcemia","hyperchloremia"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-21T15:22:25.839Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-20T15:22:25.839Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.338Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-20T15:22:25.924Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758789056669-183', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783610488281-1790433142627-pf2m5', 'Diet order for irritable bowel syndrome: *', 'all of the above', 'Irritable Bowel Syndrome (IBS) is a functional gastrointestinal disorder where dietary management focuses on controlling symptoms like cramping, bloating, gas, diarrhea, and constipation. Standard clinical diet orders traditionally incorporate all three modifications:

• High-fiber: Gradually increasing dietary fiber (specifically soluble fiber like psyllium, oats, and flaxseeds) is a primary recommendation. It adds healthy bulk to stools to relieve constipation (IBS-C) while simultaneously helping to solidify loose stools in diarrhea-predominant IBS (IBS-D).
• Avoid highly seasoned foods: Spicy and heavily seasoned foods contain compounds (like capsaicin) that irritate the gut lining and accelerate GI transit. Eliminating them helps reduce abdominal pain, cramping, and sudden bowel urgency.
• Fat-restricted: High-fat, greasy, and deep-fried foods trigger an exaggerated gastrocolic reflex, which causes rapid, painful colonic contractions. Restricting dietary fat intake stabilizes gut motility and prevents immediate post-meal cramping and diarrhea.', '["avoid highly seasoned foods","high-fiber","fat-restricted","all of the above"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-11T14:08:59.614Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.1,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-10T14:08:59.614Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.338Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-10T14:08:59.690Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758789070217-184', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783610488281-1790433142627-pf2m5', 'A person who has done well for 3 years following a gastrectomy should be alert to signs of: *', 'vitamin B₁₂ deficiency', 'The human liver stores a substantial reserve of Vitamin B₁₂ (cobalamin), which typically lasts 3 to 5 years. Once these internal stores are fully exhausted, symptoms of deficiency begin to appear.

A gastrectomy removes the parietal cells of the stomach, which are responsible for secreting intrinsic factor. Intrinsic factor is a protein absolutely required for Vitamin B₁₂ absorption in the ileum. Without it, the body cannot absorb dietary Vitamin B₁₂, eventually causing macrocytic/pernicious anemia and neurological issues.', '["iron deficiency anemia","blind loop syndrome","vitamin B₁₂ deficiency","dumping syndrome"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-16T02:56:40.815Z","interval":3,"easeFactor":2.1,"repetitions":2}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-13T02:56:40.815Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.338Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-13T02:56:40.893Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758789079628-185', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783610488281-1790433142627-pf2m5', 'Dietary recommendation for clients with cystic fibrosis except: *', 'limit the use of table salt', 'Cystic fibrosis (CF) is caused by a genetic mutation that disrupts the CFTR protein, preventing cells from properly regulating chloride and sodium. As a result, individuals with CF lose massive amounts of salt through their sweat pores (often referred to as having "salty skin"). To prevent hyponatremia (dangerously low blood sodium), dehydration, and heat exhaustion, clients with CF are actively encouraged to liberalize their use of table salt and consume salty snacks. Limiting salt is contraindicated.

Historically, old test bank items sometimes listed fat restriction as an outdated management strategy because patients cannot digest fats well without enzymes. However, in modern clinical nutrition practice—combined with Pancreatic Enzyme Replacement Therapy (PERT)—a high-fat diet is heavily promoted alongside high calorie and high protein to help meet their high energy demands.', '["limit the use of table salt","high calorie","high protein","fat restrictions"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-22T12:49:29.663Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.3,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-21T12:49:29.663Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.338Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-21T12:49:29.663Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758789088184-186', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783610488281-1790433142627-pf2m5', 'Diet recommendations for clients suffering from severe stress: *', 'energy-modified, high protein diet', 'energy-modified, high protein diet', '["energy-modified, high protein diet","high calories, low protein","high carbohydrate, high fat","none of the above"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-03T06:44:43.075Z","interval":3,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":2}'::jsonb, '"2026-09-30T06:44:43.075Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.338Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-08T12:24:43.573Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758789097450-187', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783610488281-1790433142627-pf2m5', 'A type of COPD in which the lungs lose their elasticity and the client has difficulty breathing. *', 'emphysema', 'Emphysema is a major form of Chronic Obstructive Pulmonary Disease (COPD) characterized by the permanent destruction of the alveolar walls and pulmonary capillaries. This architectural destruction leads to a severe loss of lung elasticity. Without elastic recoil, the small airways collapse during exhalation, trapping air inside the lungs (hyperinflation) and making it deeply difficult for the patient to breathe.

• Bronchitis: Chronic bronchitis is characterized by chronic inflammation, swelling, and narrowing of the lining of the bronchial airways. Its primary presentation is a persistent, productive cough driven by severe mucus hypersecretion rather than a structural loss of lung tissue elasticity.', '["emphysema","bronchitis","both a & b","none of the above"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-22T12:50:10.757Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.3,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-21T12:50:10.757Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.338Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-21T12:50:10.757Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758789105216-188', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783610488281-1790433142627-pf2m5', 'The fluid needs of a child whose body weight is 14kg is approximately: *', '1200ml', '', '["800ml","1000ml","1200ml","1300ml"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-22T12:57:11.292Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.3,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-21T12:57:11.292Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.338Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-21T12:57:11.458Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758789112982-189', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783610488281-1790433142627-pf2m5', 'Risk assessment CHD includes measurement of blood lipids such as: *', 'all of the above', 'A comprehensive risk assessment for Coronary Heart Disease (CHD) relies on a full fasting lipid profile. Each of the lipid fractions listed in the other options provides distinct, critical diagnostic data regarding a patient''s cardiovascular health profile:

• Total Cholesterol (TC): Reflects the overall amount of cholesterol circulating in the bloodstream and is directly linked to blood vessel plaque buildup and disease risk.
• Triglycerides (TG): High levels of these blood fats serve as an independent risk factor for atherosclerosis and metabolic syndrome, often elevated by a high-fat diet, refined sugars, or alcohol.
• HDL-Cholesterol (HDL-C): Known as the "good cholesterol," it plays a protective role by removing excess cholesterol from peripheral tissues and transporting it back to the liver. High levels actively reduce CHD risk.
• LDL-Cholesterol (LDL-C): Designated as the "bad cholesterol" and the primary target of clinical lipid-lowering therapies, elevated circulating LDL leads directly to fatty accumulation and narrowing inside the coronary arteries.', '["total cholesterol, TG","LDL-cholesterol, total cholesterol","HDL cholesterol, TG","all of the above"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-22T12:38:32.627Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-21T12:38:32.627Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.338Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-21T12:38:32.797Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758789124116-190', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783610488281-1790433142627-pf2m5', 'The recommended intake for MUFA in the diet is approximately: *', 'less than 7% of total kcal', 'less than 7% of total kcal', '["30% of total kcal","less than 7% of total kcal","up to 10% of total kcal","up to 15% of total kcal"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-09-29T16:06:10.736Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-09-28T16:06:10.736Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.338Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-08T12:24:43.573Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758789131799-191', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783610488281-1790433142627-pf2m5', 'Flatulence, cramps, or diabetes may be some unpleasant side effects upon the initiation of this diet: *', 'high-fiber diet', 'When a person suddenly initiates a high-fiber diet, the digestive tract requires a transition period to adapt. The human body lacks the enzymes to digest dietary fiber, so it passes intact into the large intestine. There, resident gut bacteria ferment the fiber. A sudden influx of fiber causes rapid, aggressive bacterial fermentation, which produces large amounts of gas. This trapped gas directly results in flatulence, abdominal bloating, and painful cramps.

While fiber does not cause diabetes, a high-fiber diet is a primary clinical intervention utilized for the management of Diabetes Mellitus. Soluble fiber creates a gel-like substance in the gut that slows down the absorption of glucose, preventing rapid post-meal blood sugar spikes and improving insulin sensitivity.', '["fiber-restricted diet","high-fiber diet","soft diet","elemental diet"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-21T15:08:07.354Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-20T15:08:07.354Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.338Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-20T15:08:07.447Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758789140416-192', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783610488281-1790433142627-pf2m5', 'Chronic diarrhea may be accompanied by a number of nutritional deficiencies due to losses of: *', 'all of the above', 'Chronic diarrhea severely impairs the small and large intestines'' ability to absorb nutrients due to rapid transit time and mucosal damage. This leads to widespread malabsorption and excessive losses of:

• Na & K: Sodium and potassium are critical electrolytes lost in high volumes through watery stools, leading to dehydration and electrolyte imbalances.
• Vitamins & minerals: Fat-soluble vitamins (A, D, E, K), water-soluble vitamins (like B-complex), and minerals (such as iron, zinc, and magnesium) are rapidly depleted.
• Protein: Protein-losing enteropathy often occurs during chronic diarrhea, where plasma proteins leak directly into the gut lumen and are excreted, leading to tissue wasting and hypoalbuminemia.', '["Na & K","vitamins & minerals","protein","all of the above"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-11T14:08:52.910Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.1,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-10T14:08:52.910Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.338Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-10T14:08:52.985Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758789156863-193', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783610488281-1790433142627-pf2m5', 'This type of supplement has shown good results in reducing encephalopathy and in improving nitrogen and energy balance: *', 'branched-chain amino acids supplements', 'Patients with advanced liver disease or cirrhosis typically present with an altered amino acid profile. Their plasma levels of Branched-Chain Amino Acids (BCAAs: leucine, isoleucine, and valine) drop significantly, while levels of Aromatic Amino Acids (AAAs: phenylalanine, tyrosine, and tryptophan) rise. This imbalance allows excessive AAAs to cross the blood-brain barrier, altering neurotransmission and inducing hepatic encephalopathy.', '["vegetable ad casein-based protein supplements","aromatic amino acids supplements","branched-chain amino acids supplements","phenylalanine supplements"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-11T13:54:40.192Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.3,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-10T13:54:40.192Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.338Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-10T13:54:40.295Z"'::jsonb)
