('anki-card-1758789166179-194', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783610488281-1790433142627-pf2m5', 'A patient with gout and uricemia will benefit from which diet prescription? *', 'low purine, 1g protein/kg BW, calorie restricted', 'low purine, 1g protein/kg BW, calorie restricted', '["low purine, adequate protein, 3 liters water","low purine, 1.5 g protein/kg BW, low alcohol","low purine, high CHO, adequate protein, low fat","low purine, 1g protein/kg BW, calorie restricted"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-16T01:22:23.517Z","interval":3,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":2}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-13T01:22:23.517Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.338Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-08T12:24:43.573Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758789178614-195', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783610488281-1790433142627-pf2m5', 'Saturated fat level recommended for diabetic patients with elevated LDL cholesterol: *
a. b. 
c. 
d.', '<7% of total calories, 200 mg cholesterol/day', '<7% of total calories, 200 mg cholesterol/day', '["<7% of total calories, 200 mg cholesterol/day","150","mitochondria","25 kcal/KDBW"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-01T06:43:42.810Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-09-30T06:43:42.810Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.338Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-08T12:24:43.573Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758789188129-196', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783610488281-1790433142627-pf2m5', 'Which diet is best for a patient with hypothyroidism? *', '20-30 cal/KBW, low fat, iodized salt', '20-30 cal/KBW, low fat, iodized salt', '["30 cal/KBW, high fat, iodized salt","30 cal/KBW, low fat, iodized salt","20-25 cal/KBW, high fat, iodized salt","20-30 cal/KBW, low fat, iodized salt"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-01T05:13:15.347Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-09-30T05:13:15.347Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.338Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-08T12:24:43.573Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758789202878-197', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783610488281-1790433142627-pf2m5', 'The amount of kcal/KBW for bulimic patients on a weight maintenance diet: *', '25 kcal/KDBW', 'Research demonstrates that patients with bulimia nervosa who are in a weight-stable or weight-maintenance phase often have a significantly depressed metabolic rate. This is primarily caused by a history of chronic, severe energy restriction, chaotic eating cycles, and purging behaviors.

While a typical healthy, sedentary adult requires approximately 30 kcal/kg/day for weight maintenance, clinical evaluations show that weight-stable bulimic patients require fewer calories—typically averaging around 22 to 25 kcal per kilogram of body weight to maintain their weight.', '["20 kcal/KDBW","25 kcal/KDBW","35 kcal/KDBW","30 kcal/KDBW"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-21T15:30:12.320Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.3,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-20T15:30:12.320Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.338Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-20T15:30:12.408Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758789218745-198', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783610488281-1790433142627-pf2m5', 'A patient with a BMI of 42 will benefit from which diet prescription? *', '1200 kcal, 55% kcal from CHO, fat restricted', 'In traditional dietetic practice, a 1200 kcal diet is considered the gold-standard baseline weight-reduction diet for adults. It is widely recognized as the lowest safe calorie level that an individual can consume while still being able to meet all their essential vitamin and mineral requirements without mandatory medical supplementation.

"Fat Restricted" over Percentage Targets: Classic medical diet prescriptions for severe obesity prioritize explicit qualitative restrictions like "fat restricted" or "low fat" rather than precise percentage numbers. Because fat is the most calorie-dense macronutrient (9 kcal per gram), strictly restricting visible fats, fried foods, and high-fat items is the most direct clinical method used to ensure the patient adheres to a low-calorie limit.

Macronutrient Balance: Keeping carbohydrates at 55% ensures the patient receives enough complex carbohydrates to maintain energy levels and prevent ketosis, while the remaining calories are split between protein and minimal essential fats.', '["800 kcal, 55% kcal from CHO, 25% from fat","1000 kcal, 55% kcal from CHO, 12% from protein","1200 kcal, 55% kcal from CHO, fat restricted","1300 kcal, 55% kcal from CHO, 25% from fat"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-16T02:59:01.055Z","interval":3,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":2}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-13T02:59:01.055Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.338Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-13T02:59:01.130Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758789227159-199', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783610488281-1790433142627-pf2m5', 'A cholesterol level of 240 mg/dL needs treatment with: *', 'diet, exercise, & drugs', 'In adults, a total cholesterol level below 200 mg/dL is desirable. A level between 200 and 239 mg/dL is considered borderline high, and a level of 240 mg/dL or higher is officially classified as high.

Once total cholesterol hits the high threshold (240 mg/dL), guidelines recommend a multi-pronged medical intervention:
• Diet & Exercise: Initial and continuing therapeutic lifestyle changes—such as reducing saturated and trans fats, increasing dietary fiber, and initiating regular cardio—form the baseline of managing lipid profiles.
• Drugs: Because a level of 240 mg/dL significantly elevates the risk for coronary heart disease, stroke, and atherosclerosis, lifestyle changes alone are often insufficient. Pharmacological intervention (most commonly statins) is prescribed in tandem to inhibit hepatic cholesterol production and aggressively pull down circulating lipid levels.', '["diet","drugs","diet and drugs","diet, exercise, & drugs"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-11T13:54:24.889Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.3,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-10T13:54:24.889Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:30.338Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-10T13:54:24.968Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758967414963-0', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783610495718-1790433139833-fmjgg', 'The exchange of oxygen, nutrients, and waste products takes place in these vessels: *', 'capillaries', 'Capillaries are the microscopic, thin-walled blood vessels where the actual exchange of oxygen, nutrients, and metabolic waste products takes place between the circulating blood and surrounding tissue cells.

• Veins: Large capacitance vessels returning deoxygenated blood toward the heart.
• Arteries: Thick-walled high-pressure vessels transporting oxygenated blood away from the heart.', '["veins","capillaries","arteries","a & b only"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 1, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-09-26T14:32:12.431Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":0}'::jsonb, NULL, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.175Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-09T03:55:07.463Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758967424140-1', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783610495718-1790433139833-fmjgg', 'Site of most of the biochemical reactions in the cell: *', 'cytoplasm', 'The cytoplasm (specifically the cytosol and associated metabolic machinery) serves as the site for numerous foundational cellular biochemical reactions, including glycolysis, glycogen synthesis, and protein translation.

• Mitochondria: The specialized organelle responsible for oxidative phosphorylation, the Krebs cycle, and ATP generation.
• Nucleus: Houses genomic DNA and coordinates transcription and replication.
• Golgi apparatus: Modifies, sorts, and packages proteins for secretion.', '["mitochondria","cytoplasm","nucleus","golgi apparatus"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 1, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-09-26T14:32:12.431Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":0}'::jsonb, NULL, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.175Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-09T04:08:47.900Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758967433187-2', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783610495718-1790433139833-fmjgg', 'Absorption is the same as: *', 'assimilation', 'Assimilation refers to the biological process by which digested and absorbed nutrient molecules are incorporated into the tissues and cells of the body, making it synonymous with nutrient utilization and tissue incorporation.

• Digestion: The mechanical and chemical breakdown of food molecules in the gastrointestinal tract.
• Anabolism: The constructive phase of metabolism focused on building complex molecules from simpler precursors.
• Metabolism: The broader umbrella term encompassing all biochemical changes in the body.', '["digestion","anabolism","assimilation","metabolism"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 1, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-09-26T14:32:12.431Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":0}'::jsonb, NULL, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.175Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-09T04:08:54.783Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758967444937-3', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783610495718-1790433139833-fmjgg', 'It covers all the chemical changes that go on in the different body tissues, including both synthetic and degradative pathways. *', 'metabolism', 'Metabolism is the comprehensive sum of all chemical reactions occurring within living organisms, encompassing both anabolic (synthetic) and catabolic (degradative) pathways.

• Nutrition: The science and study of food nutrients and their physiological utilization.
• Physiology: The branch of biology dealing with the normal functions of living organisms and their parts.
• Absorption: The specific transport of nutrient molecules across the intestinal epithelium into blood or lymph.', '["nutrition","physiology","absorption","metabolism"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 1, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-09-26T14:32:12.431Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":0}'::jsonb, NULL, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.175Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-09T04:09:00.502Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758967456454-4', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783610495718-1790433139833-fmjgg', 'Nutrients which cannot be stored by the body for more than a few hours. *', 'amino acids', 'Unlike carbohydrates and fats, which are stored as glycogen or triglycerides, free amino acids absorbed from dietary proteins are not stored in a specialized reserve pool, leaving circulating pools turning over rapidly within hours.

• Calcium: Stored in substantial quantities within the mineralized bone matrix.
• Iron: Stored safely in the liver, spleen, and bone marrow bound to ferritin and hemosiderin.', '["amino acids","calcium","iron","none of the above"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 1, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-09-26T14:32:12.431Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":0}'::jsonb, NULL, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.175Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-09T04:09:07.027Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758967465869-5', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783610495718-1790433139833-fmjgg', 'The predominant proteoglycan in cartilage is: *', 'chondroitin sulfate', 'Chondroitin sulfate is a sulfated glycosaminoglycan and the predominant proteoglycan found in the extracellular matrix of articular cartilage, providing structural resistance to compression.

• Keratin sulfate: Found predominantly in the cornea, cartilage, and bone, but chondroitin sulfate is the major cartilage proteoglycan.
• Dermatan sulfate: Found mainly in skin, blood vessels, and heart valves.
• Heparin sulfate: An anticoagulant proteoglycan found on cell surfaces and in mast cells.', '["keratin sulfate","dermatan sulfate","chondroitin sulfate","heparin sulfate"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 1, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-09-26T14:32:12.431Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":0}'::jsonb, NULL, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.175Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-09T04:09:13.488Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758967485022-6', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783610495718-1790433139833-fmjgg', 'Which of the following tissues is glucose transport into the cell enhanced by insulin? *', 'adipose tissue', 'In insulin-sensitive tissues such as adipose tissue and skeletal muscle, insulin binding to its cell-surface receptor triggers the translocation of GLUT4 glucose transporters to the plasma membrane, significantly enhancing glucose uptake.

• Brain: Glucose uptake is insulin-independent and mediated primarily by GLUT1 and GLUT3 transporters.
• Nerve: Neural tissue glucose uptake is insulin-independent.
• Red blood cell: Erythrocytes utilize insulin-independent GLUT1 transporters for continuous glucose uptake.', '["brain","nerve","red blood cell","adipose tissue"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 1, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-09-26T14:32:12.431Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":0}'::jsonb, NULL, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.175Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-09T04:09:19.618Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758967494967-7', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783610495718-1790433139833-fmjgg', 'Which of the following statements about glucagon is incorrect? *', 'Glucagon and insulin are produced by β-cells of the islets of Langerhans in the pancreas.', 'This statement is incorrect, making it the right choice for the question. Insulin is synthesized and secreted exclusively by the beta (β) cells, whereas glucagon is synthesized and secreted by the alpha (α) cells of the pancreatic islets of Langerhans.

• High levels of blood glucose decrease the release of glucagon from the α-cells of the pancreas: True; hyperglycemia suppresses glucagon secretion.
• Glucagon levels increase after ingestion of a protein-rich meal: True; dietary amino acids stimulate both insulin and glucagon release.
• Glucagon increases intracellular cAMP in liver cells, causing glycogen breakdown: True; glucagon activates glycogenolysis via cAMP second-messenger cascades.', '["High levels of blood glucose decrease the release of glucagon from the α-cells of the pancreas.","Glucagon levels increase after ingestion of a protein-rich meal.","Glucagon increases the intracellular levels of cAMP in liver cells, causing an increase in glycogen breakdown.","Glucagon and insulin are produced by β-cells of the islets of Langerhans in the pancreas."]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 1, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-09-26T14:32:12.431Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":0}'::jsonb, NULL, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.175Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-09T04:09:54.395Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758967506669-8', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783610495718-1790433139833-fmjgg', 'Which of the following is the most important source of blood glucose during the last hours of a 48-hour fast? *', 'amino acids', 'During a prolonged 48-hour fast, liver glycogen stores are entirely depleted within the first 24 to 30 hours. Consequently, during the final hours of the fast, the primary and most important source of blood glucose is hepatic gluconeogenesis utilizing amino acids derived from muscle protein breakdown.

• Muscle glycogen: Trapped in muscle cells because muscle lacks glucose-6-phosphatase; cannot contribute directly to blood glucose.
• Fatty acids: Cannot be converted into net glucose in humans because acetyl-CoA cannot yield oxaloacetate.
• Liver glycogen: Completely exhausted during the early hours of a 48-hour fast.', '["muscle glycogen","fatty acids","liver glycogen","amino acids"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 1, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-09-26T14:32:12.431Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":0}'::jsonb, NULL, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.175Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-09T04:10:04.968Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758967515483-9', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783610495718-1790433139833-fmjgg', 'Glutathione functions in erythrocytes largely to: *', 'reduce oxidizing agents such as H₂O₂', 'Reduced glutathione (GSH) in red blood cells functions primarily as an essential antioxidant cofactor for glutathione peroxidase to reduce harmful oxidizing agents like hydrogen peroxide (H₂O₂), protecting red blood cell membranes from oxidative hemolysis.

• Reduce methemoglobin to hemoglobin: Primarily performed by the enzyme methemoglobin reductase (NADH-cytochrome b5 reductase), not glutathione directly.
• Produce NADH: Glycolytic pathways produce NADH, whereas glutathione is an antioxidant tripeptide.
• Reduce pyruvate to lactate: Catalyzed by lactate dehydrogenase during anaerobic glycolysis.', '["reduce methemoglobin to hemoglobin","produce NADH","reduce pyruvate to lactate","reduce oxidizing agents such as H₂O₂"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 1, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-09-26T14:32:12.431Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":0}'::jsonb, NULL, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.175Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-09T04:10:30.823Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758967528165-10', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783610495718-1790433139833-fmjgg', 'The synthesis of glucose from pyruvate by gluconeogenesis: *', 'requires biotin', 'The initial rate-limiting step of gluconeogenesis—converting pyruvate to oxaloacetate via pyruvate carboxylase—is a carboxylation reaction that strictly requires biotin as its essential coenzyme.

• Occurs exclusively in the cytoplasm: False; gluconeogenesis is split between the mitochondrial matrix and the cytoplasm.
• Is inhibited by elevated levels of glucagon: False; glucagon stimulates gluconeogenesis by promoting key enzyme gene expression and phosphorylation cascades.
• Involves lactate as an intermediate: Lactate is a gluconeogenic precursor, not an internal enzymatic intermediate of the pyruvate-to-glucose pathway.', '["occurs exclusively in the cytoplasm","is inhibited by elevated levels of glucagon","requires biotin","involves lactate as an intermediate"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 1, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-09-26T14:32:12.431Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":0}'::jsonb, NULL, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.175Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-09T04:10:42.229Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758967542498-11', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783610495718-1790433139833-fmjgg', 'It can be used as an indirect measure of BMR: *', 'thyroid function test', 'Because thyroid hormones (T3 and T4) are the primary master regulators of cellular oxidative metabolism and basal metabolic rate (BMR), a thyroid function test assessing circulating hormone levels can be used clinically as an indirect measure of BMR.

• Specific dynamic action: Refers to the thermic effect of food (dietary-induced thermogenesis), not basal metabolic rate.
• Direct calorimetry: Measures total heat production directly using a sealed chamber, which is a direct measurement rather than an indirect marker test.', '["specific dynamic action","thyroid function test","direct calorimetry","none of the above"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 1, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-09-26T14:32:12.431Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":0}'::jsonb, NULL, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.175Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-09T04:10:49.248Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758967554565-12', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783610495718-1790433139833-fmjgg', 'Which of the following does not depress calcium absorption? *', 'an acidic medium', 'Calcium absorption is enhanced by an acidic medium (such as gastric hydrochloric acid) because acidity keeps calcium soluble and ionized; therefore, an acidic medium promotes rather than depresses absorption.

• Presence of oxalates & phytates: Inhibit calcium absorption by forming insoluble mineral salts.
• Steatorrhea: Depresses calcium absorption because unabsorbed fatty acids bind calcium to form insoluble soaps.
• Vitamin C deficiency: Impairs collagen matrix formation and mineral solubility parameters.', '["presence of oxalates & phytates","steatorrhea","vitamin C deficiency","an acidic medium"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 1, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-09-26T14:32:12.431Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":0}'::jsonb, NULL, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.175Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-09T04:10:55.814Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758967570100-13', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783610495718-1790433139833-fmjgg', 'A hydrolytic reaction can: *', 'split a disaccharide to form two monosaccharides', 'A hydrolytic reaction (hydrolysis) utilizes a molecule of water to cleave and split a disaccharide bond to form two separate monosaccharide units.

• Bond two monosaccharides to form disaccharide: This describes a condensation or dehydration synthesis reaction, not hydrolysis.
• Form a molecule of water: Condensation reactions release water, whereas hydrolysis consumes water.', '["bond two monosaccharides to form disaccharide","form a molecule of water","split a disaccharide to form two monosaccharides"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 1, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-09-26T14:32:12.431Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":0}'::jsonb, NULL, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.175Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-09T04:11:02.834Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758967578999-14', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783610495718-1790433139833-fmjgg', 'The process of lipid absorption which involves the recycling of bile salts is also called: *', 'enterohepatic circulation', 'The continuous recycling pathway where bile salts are secreted into the small intestine, reabsorbed in the terminal ileum, and transported back via the portal vein to the liver for re-secretion is called the enterohepatic circulation.

• Cori cycle: The metabolic shuttle circulating lactate between muscle and liver.
• Krebs cycle: The mitochondrial central catabolic oxidative cycle.
• Lymphatic circulation: Transports chylomicrons, but bile salt recycling specifically defines enterohepatic circulation.', '["Cori cycle","Krebs cycle","lymphatic circulation","enterohepatic circulation"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 1, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-09-26T14:32:12.431Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":0}'::jsonb, NULL, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.175Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-09T04:11:07.871Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758967593230-15', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783610495718-1790433139833-fmjgg', 'A monounsaturated fatty acid that is non-hypercholesterolemic: *', 'oleic acid', 'Oleic acid is an 18-carbon monounsaturated fatty acid (omega-9) that is nutritionally non-hypercholesterolemic (it does not raise LDL cholesterol levels when replacing saturated fats in the diet).

• Linoleic acid: An essential polyunsaturated fatty acid (omega-6).
• Palmitic acid: A saturated fatty acid known to raise serum LDL cholesterol.
• Stearic acid: A saturated fatty acid, though relatively neutral, oleic acid is the quintessential monounsaturated example.', '["linoleic acid","palmitic acid","stearic acid","oleic acid"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 1, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-09-26T14:32:12.431Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":0}'::jsonb, NULL, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.175Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-09T04:11:15.534Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758967603514-16', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783610495718-1790433139833-fmjgg', 'The precursor of substances called eicosanoids: *', 'arachidonic acid', 'Arachidonic acid is a 20-carbon polyunsaturated omega-6 fatty acid embedded in cell membranes that serves as the primary biochemical precursor for synthesizing eicosanoids (prostaglandins, thromboxanes, and leukotrienes).

• Palmitic acid: A saturated fatty acid, not an eicosanoid precursor.
• Stearic acid: A long-chain saturated fatty acid.
• Oleic acid: A monounsaturated omega-9 fatty acid.', '["arachidonic acid","palmitic acid","stearic acid","oleic acid"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 1, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-09-26T14:32:12.431Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":0}'::jsonb, NULL, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.175Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-09T04:11:23.414Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758967622030-17', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783610495718-1790433139833-fmjgg', 'The breakdown of triglycerides into fatty acids and glycerol is: *', 'lipolysis', 'Lipolysis is the metabolic pathway and enzymatic breakdown process that hydrolyzes stored triglycerides into free fatty acids and glycerol.

• Glycolysis: The cytosolic breakdown of glucose into pyruvate.
• Lipogenesis: The anabolic synthesis of fatty acids and triglycerides from carbohydrate precursors.
• Beta oxidation: The mitochondrial degradation of fatty acids into acetyl-CoA.', '["glycolysis","lipogenesis","beta oxidation","lipolysis"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 1, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-09-26T14:32:12.431Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":0}'::jsonb, NULL, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.175Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-09T04:11:29.072Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758967630380-18', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783610495718-1790433139833-fmjgg', 'The condition for energy balance and weight maintenance is: *', 'RQ = 1', 'The Respiratory Quotient (RQ) is the ratio of carbon dioxide produced to oxygen consumed (VCO₂ / VO₂). When an individual is oxidizing pure carbohydrates under conditions of energy balance and weight maintenance, the RQ equals 1.0.

• RQ < 1: Indicates fat oxidation (approx. 0.7) or mixed/protein oxidation.
• RQ > 1: Indicates lipogenesis (net fat synthesis from excess carbohydrates).
• RQ = 0: Physiologically impossible during normal human gas exchange.', '["RQ = 1","RQ","RQ > 1","RQ = 0"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 1, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-09-26T14:32:12.431Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":0}'::jsonb, NULL, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.175Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-09T04:12:05.086Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758967639877-19', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783610495718-1790433139833-fmjgg', 'Glycogen stores in muscles can be increased during athletic competition by: *', 'glycogen loading', 'Glycogen loading (carbohydrate loading) is an athletic dietary strategy designed to maximize muscle glycogen storage capacity prior to prolonged endurance competitions by combining depletion workouts with high-carbohydrate intakes.

• Ingestion of low glycemic foods: Sustains gradual blood glucose release, but glycogen loading specifically maximizes storage volumes.
• Ingestion of fructose: Fructose replenishes hepatic glycogen more than direct skeletal muscle glycogen.
• A high protein intake: Supports muscle repair and anabolism, but does not maximize carbohydrate glycogen supercompensation.', '["ingestion of low glycemic foods","ingestion of fructose","glycogen loading","a high protein intake"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 1, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-09-26T14:32:12.431Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":0}'::jsonb, NULL, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.175Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-09T04:12:12.315Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758967651147-20', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783610495718-1790433139833-fmjgg', 'Sugar alcohols are made up of: *', 'hydrogenated dextrins', 'Sugar alcohols (polyols like sorbitol, mannitol, and xylitol) are commercially manufactured through the catalytic hydrogenation of sugars and carbohydrate dextrins.

• Glucose polymers: Refers to complex starches or maltodextrins, not sugar alcohols.
• Galactose & glucose: The constituent monosaccharides of the disaccharide lactose.
• Glucose & fructose: The constituent monosaccharides of sucrose.', '["glucose polymers","hydrogenated dextrins","galactose & glucose","glucose & fructose"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 1, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-09-26T14:32:12.431Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":0}'::jsonb, NULL, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.175Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-09T04:12:19.120Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758967659810-21', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783610495718-1790433139833-fmjgg', 'Which of the following minerals plays important roles in the metabolic pathways? *', 'magnesium, iron, copper', 'Magnesium, iron, and copper are essential metallic minerals that serve critical roles as enzymatic cofactors and redox carriers across core metabolic pathways (such as glycolysis, oxidative phosphorylation, and electron transport).

• Iodine, iron, copper: Iodine is specific to thyroid hormones rather than broad cellular metabolic pathways.
• Biotin, magnesium, copper: Biotin is a water-soluble vitamin, not a mineral.
• Zinc, iron, magnesium: Zinc is vital, but magnesium, iron, and copper form the classic trio highlighted in energy metabolism cofactors.', '["iodine, iron, copper","magnesium, iron, copper","biotin, magnesium, copper","zinc, iron, magnesium"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 1, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-09-26T14:32:12.431Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":0}'::jsonb, NULL, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.175Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-09T04:12:25.897Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758967668427-22', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783610495718-1790433139833-fmjgg', '____ is an amino acid in short supply and acts as a precursor for protein synthesis. *', 'limiting amino acid', 'A limiting amino acid is an essential amino acid present in a dietary protein in the lowest relative proportion compared to a reference protein pattern, thereby limiting the overall efficiency of protein synthesis.

• Branched chain amino acid: Refers to leucine, isoleucine, and valine, which feature branched aliphatic side chains.
• Ketogenic amino acid: Degrades into acetyl-CoA without yielding glucose precursors.
• Glucogenic amino acid: Yields carbon skeletons convertible to glucose.', '["branched chain amino acid","ketogenic amino acid","glucogenic amino acid","limiting amino acid"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 1, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-09-26T14:32:12.431Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":0}'::jsonb, NULL, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.175Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-09T04:12:32.863Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758967707707-23', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783610495718-1790433139833-fmjgg', '____ plays an important role in the metabolism of ammonia, in the formation of neurotransmitter gamma aminobutyric acid (GABA) in the nervous system, and is also a component of folate *', 'glutamic acid', 'Glutamic acid (glutamate) plays pivotal roles in transamination, ammonia detoxification by forming glutamine, serving as the immediate precursor for the inhibitory neurotransmitter GABA (gamma-aminobutyric acid), and acting structurally in folate coenzymes (polyglutamate tails).

• Phenylalanine: An essential aromatic amino acid.
• Glycine: The simplest non-essential amino acid, involved in heme and purine synthesis.
• Tryptophan: An essential aromatic amino acid serving as a serotonin and niacin precursor.', '["phenylalanine","glycine","glutamic acid","tryptophan"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 1, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-09-26T14:32:12.431Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":0}'::jsonb, NULL, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.175Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-09T04:12:39.368Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758967717875-24', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783610495718-1790433139833-fmjgg', 'The largest gland in the body; also called “warehouse and chemical manufacture” due to its involvement in more than 500 functions. *', 'liver', 'The liver is the largest internal glandular organ in the human body, performing over 500 vital biochemical functions that earn it the designation of the body''s primary chemical manufacturing and storage warehouse.

• Stomach: The digestive organ responsible for mechanical churning and gastric acid/pepsin secretion.
• Kidneys: Specialized excretory organs filtering blood and regulating fluid-electrolyte balance.
• Pancreas: An exocrine and endocrine gland secreting digestive enzymes and insulin/glucagon.', '["stomach","liver","kidneys","pancreas"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 1, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-09-26T14:32:12.431Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":0}'::jsonb, NULL, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.175Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-09T04:12:45.395Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758967729057-25', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783610495718-1790433139833-fmjgg', 'A neurotransmitter important in sleep and sensory perception that is synthesized from tryptophan. *', 'serotonin', 'Serotonin (5-hydroxytryptamine) is an important monoamine neurotransmitter synthesized via the hydroxylation and decarboxylation of the essential amino acid tryptophan, regulating sleep, mood, and sensory perception.

• Dopamine: A catecholamine neurotransmitter synthesized from tyrosine.
• Norepinephrine: A stress hormone and neurotransmitter derived from tyrosine and dopamine.', '["dopamine","serotonin","norepinephrine","none of the above"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 1, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-09-26T14:32:12.431Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":0}'::jsonb, NULL, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.175Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-09T04:12:52.517Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758967737207-26', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783610495718-1790433139833-fmjgg', 'Fortification of this vitamin may prove to be unsafe due to its ability to mask vitamin B12 deficiency symptoms when consumed in amounts more than 1 mg a day. *', 'folate', 'Excessive intake of supplemental folate (exceeding 1 mg/day) can correct the megaloblastic anemia caused by Vitamin B12 deficiency, while allowing the irreversible neurological damage of B12 deficiency to progress undetected, thereby masking the deficiency symptoms.

• Thiamin: Deficiency causes beriberi; fortification does not mask B12 neuropathy.
• Niacin: High doses cause vasodilation (niacin flush), but do not mask B12 deficiency.
• Riboflavin: Deficiency causes angular cheilosis and glossitis.', '["Thiamin","niacin","folate","riboflavin"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 1, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-09-26T14:32:12.431Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":0}'::jsonb, NULL, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.175Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-09T04:13:00.254Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758967750040-27', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783610495718-1790433139833-fmjgg', 'Urine formation consists of three basic processes: *', 'filtration, secretion, reabsorption', 'Urine formation by the nephron proceeds through three sequential physiological processes: glomerular filtration of blood plasma, tubular secretion of specific waste ions into the filtrate, and selective tubular reabsorption of water and vital solutes.

• Osmosis, diffusion, filtration: Describe biophysical transport mechanisms rather than the macro-processes of renal urine formation.
• Separation, secretion, osmosis: Incorrect terminology for renal clearance steps.
• Secretion, reabsorption, separation: Incorrect sequencing and non-standard terminology.', '["osmosis, diffusion, filtration","separation, secretion, osmosis","filtration, secretion, reabsorption","secretion, reabsorption, separation"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 1, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-09-26T14:32:12.431Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":0}'::jsonb, NULL, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.175Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-09T04:13:07.346Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758967766406-28', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783610495718-1790433139833-fmjgg', 'Functions of the kidneys except: *', 'secretion of adrenalin', 'Adrenaline (epinephrine) is synthesized and secreted by the adrenal medulla (the inner core of the adrenal glands), not the kidneys, making this the correct exception among renal functions.

• Excretion of metabolic wastes: A primary renal function clearing urea, creatinine, and uric acid.
• Maintenance of acid-base balance: Managed by renal hydrogen ion secretion and bicarbonate reabsorption.
• Regulation of calcium balance: Mediated by renal calcitriol activation and calcium reabsorption.', '["excretion of metabolic wastes","secretion of adrenalin","maintenance of acid-base balance","regulation of calcium balance"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 1, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-09-26T14:32:12.431Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":0}'::jsonb, NULL, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.175Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-09T04:13:12.711Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758967778604-29', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783610495718-1790433139833-fmjgg', 'Reabsorption of sodium in the distal and collecting tubules causes: *', 'expansion of blood volume', 'The active reabsorption of sodium ions in the renal distal and collecting tubules drives osmotic water reabsorption alongside it, leading to an expansion of extracellular fluid and blood volume.

• Secretion of parathyroid hormone (PTH): Regulated by serum calcium levels, not directly triggered by distal sodium reabsorption.
• Decreased water reabsorption: Sodium reabsorption increases water retention, not decreases it.
• Lowering of the glomerular filtration rate (GFR): GFR is regulated by afferent/efferent arteriolar vasomotor tone.', '["secretion of parathyroid hormone (PTH)","decreased water reabsorption","lowering of the glomerular filtration rate (GFR)","expansion of blood volume"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 1, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-09-26T14:32:12.431Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":0}'::jsonb, NULL, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.175Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-09T04:13:17.755Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758967787553-30', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783610495718-1790433139833-fmjgg', 'Kidneys control pH through the following processes except: *', 'formation of carbonic acid', 'The kidneys regulate systemic blood pH by excreting hydrogen ions, synthesizing and excreting ammonia, and resorbing bicarbonate ions. The reversible formation of carbonic acid occurs primarily in the bloodstream and red blood cells via carbonic anhydrase, making it the correct exception among renal pH control mechanisms.

• Secretion of hydrogen ions: A primary mechanism by which the kidneys acidify urine and raise blood pH.
• Synthesis of ammonia: Buffers urine by binding hydrogen ions to form ammonium (NH₄⁺).
• Resorption of sodium ions: Coupled with hydrogen ion secretion and bicarbonate recovery.', '["formation of carbonic acid","secretion of hydrogen ions","synthesis of ammonia","resorption of sodium ions"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 1, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-09-26T14:32:12.431Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":0}'::jsonb, NULL, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.175Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-09T04:13:47.863Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758967796171-31', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783610495718-1790433139833-fmjgg', 'A sulfur-containing amino acid which is a component of hair and nails. *', 'cystine', 'Cystine is a sulfur-containing amino acid formed by the oxidation coupling of two cysteine molecules, serving as a robust disulfide-bonded structural component found in high concentrations in fibrous proteins like hair, skin, and nails (keratin).

• Cysteine: The monomeric thiol-containing precursor to cystine.
• Methionine: An essential sulfur-containing amino acid, but cystine/cysteine is the primary structural keratin building block in hair and nails.', '["cysteine","methionine","cystine","none of the above"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 1, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-09-26T14:32:12.431Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":0}'::jsonb, NULL, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.175Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-09T04:13:56.798Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758967805369-32', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783610495718-1790433139833-fmjgg', 'Starch and starch degradation products that are not absorbed in the small intestine of healthy humans. *', 'resistant starch', 'Resistant starch encompasses all starches and starch degradation products that escape enzymatic digestion in the small intestine of healthy humans and pass into the colon for microbial fermentation.

• Dietary fiber: Plant structural cell wall components, whereas resistant starch is an unabsorbed functional starch fraction.
• Modified starch: Chemically or physically altered food starches designed for specific functional stability.', '["resistant starch","dietary fiber","modified starch","none of the above"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 1, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-09-26T14:32:12.431Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":0}'::jsonb, NULL, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.175Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-09T04:14:02.709Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758967813721-33', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783610495718-1790433139833-fmjgg', 'Sugars are absorbed in the microvilli of the small intestine through: *', 'active transport', 'Monosaccharides (such as glucose and galactose) are absorbed across the brush border microvilli of the small intestinal enterocytes via sodium-dependent carrier-mediated active transport (SGLT1).

• Osmosis: The passive net movement of water molecules across membranes.
• Diffusion: Movement down a concentration gradient without active transport proteins.
• Facilitated diffusion: Used by fructose (via GLUT5), but glucose/galactose rely heavily on active cotransport.', '["osmosis","active transport","diffusion","facilitated diffusion"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 1, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-09-26T14:32:12.431Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":0}'::jsonb, NULL, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.175Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-09T04:14:08.376Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758967823536-34', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783610495718-1790433139833-fmjgg', 'The principal source of energy for high-intensity activities is ____, while ____ is the main energy source for low-intensity activities. *', 'glucose; fat', 'During high-intensity, short-duration activities, the body relies primarily on carbohydrate (glucose and glycogen) for rapid ATP generation; during low-intensity, prolonged activities, fat becomes the predominant oxidative energy source.

• Protein; fat: Incorrect; protein is a minor fuel substrate during normal exercise.', '["fat; glucose","protein; fat","glucose; fat","protein; glucose"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 1, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-09-26T14:32:12.431Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":0}'::jsonb, NULL, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.175Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-09T04:14:19.859Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758967832903-35', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783610495718-1790433139833-fmjgg', 'These nutrients are important in building red blood cells that carry oxygen. 1. folate 2. copper 3. vitamin B12 4. zinc *', '1, 2, 3', 'Building healthy red blood cells capable of oxygen transport requires adequate iron alongside folate (1), copper (2) (essential for iron mobilization via ceruloplasmin), and vitamin B12 (3). Zinc (4) plays diverse immune and enzymatic roles, making 1, 2, and 3 the correct combination.', '["1, 2, 3","1, 2, 4","1, 3, 4","2, 3, 4"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 1, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-09-26T14:32:12.431Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":0}'::jsonb, NULL, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.175Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-09T04:14:29.043Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758967841935-36', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783610495718-1790433139833-fmjgg', 'These minerals regulate pH, water, and electrolyte balance. 1. sodium 2. calcium 3. potassium 4. chloride *', '1, 3, 4', 'Electrolytes responsible for regulating fluid distribution, osmotic pressure, and acid-base balance across body fluid compartments include sodium (1), potassium (3), and chlorides (4). Calcium (2) plays structural and neuromuscular roles, making 1, 3, and 4 the correct group.', '["1, 2, 3","2, 3, 4","1, 3, 4","1 & 4 only"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 1, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-09-26T14:32:12.431Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":0}'::jsonb, NULL, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.175Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-09T04:14:33.742Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758967850969-37', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783610495718-1790433139833-fmjgg', 'The largest single contributor to the body’s energy needs accounting for 60%-80% of the total need for calories. *', 'basal metabolism', 'Basal metabolism (Basal Metabolic Rate or BMR) accounts for the largest single share of total daily energy expenditure, consuming roughly 60% to 80% of total caloric needs to sustain vital organ function at rest.

• Physical activity: The most variable component, but typically accounts for a smaller percentage than basal metabolism depending on lifestyle.
• Thermic effect of food: Accounts for only about 10% of total daily energy expenditure.
• Adaptive thermogenesis: Refers to metabolic adjustments in response to temperature or starvation.', '["physical activity","thermic effect of food","basal metabolism","adaptive thermogenesis"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 1, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-09-26T14:32:12.431Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":0}'::jsonb, NULL, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.175Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-09T04:14:39.807Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758967859283-38', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783610495718-1790433139833-fmjgg', 'After the age of ____, bone resorption slowly begins to exceed bone formation. *', '30 years', 'Peak bone mass is achieved around young adulthood; after approximately 30 years of age, bone resorption naturally begins to gradually exceed bone formation, leading to a slow, progressive age-related decline in bone mineral density.', '["20 years","30 years","40 years","50 years"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 1, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-09-26T14:32:12.431Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":0}'::jsonb, NULL, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.175Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-09T04:14:48.178Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758967869049-39', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783610495718-1790433139833-fmjgg', 'The following are noncaloric sweeteners except for: *', 'xylitol or sorbitol', 'Sugar alcohols (polyols like xylitol or sorbitol) are nutritive sweeteners that provide calories (roughly 2.4 to 4 kcal/g), making them the correct exception among noncaloric sweeteners.

• Aspartame: A potent artificial noncaloric sweetener.
• Stevia: A natural plant-derived noncaloric sweetener.
• Sucralose or Splenda: A zero-calorie chlorinated sucrose derivative.', '["aspartame","xylitol or sorbitol","stevia","sucralose or Splenda"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 1, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-09-26T14:32:12.431Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":0}'::jsonb, NULL, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.175Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-09T04:14:55.263Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758967880000-40', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783610495718-1790433139833-fmjgg', 'The following are biological tests conducted to measure protein quality except: *', 'amino acid score', 'The amino acid score is a chemical calculation comparing the essential amino acid profile of a test protein against a reference protein standard, making it a chemical evaluation method rather than a biological animal feeding test.

• Net protein utilization: A biological animal test measuring nitrogen retention relative to intake.
• Protein efficiency ratio: A biological animal feeding test measuring weight gain relative to protein consumed.
• Net dietary protein calories percent: Evaluates protein quality and concentration biologically within total diets.', '["net protein utilization","protein efficiency ratio","amino acid score","net dietary protein calories percent"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 1, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-09-26T14:32:12.431Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":0}'::jsonb, NULL, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.175Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-09T04:15:01.238Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758967894565-41', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783610495718-1790433139833-fmjgg', 'Using the biological value (BV) of a protein food as basis, a food with a BV of _____ can support growth provided the individual also has an adequate supply of calories. *', 'more than 70', 'Proteins with a Biological Value (BV) of more than 70 are considered high-quality proteins capable of supporting normal human tissue growth and maintenance, provided overall caloric intake is adequate.', '["more than 50","more than 60","more than 70","none of the above"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 1, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-09-26T14:32:12.431Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":0}'::jsonb, NULL, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.175Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-09T04:15:07.124Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758967903264-42', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783610495718-1790433139833-fmjgg', 'The other names of vitamin B12 except: *', 'citrovorum factor', 'Vitamin B12 is known historically as the anti-pernicious anemia factor, animal protein factor, and erythrocyte maturation factor. The citrovorum factor is an old historical name for folinic acid (leucovorin, a reduced derivative of folic acid), making it the correct exception.

• Anti-pernicious anemia factor: A classical historical name for Vitamin B12.
• Animal protein factor: Early nutritional name given to Vitamin B12 before its chemical structure was isolated.
• Erythrocyte maturation factor: Reflects B12''s vital role in red blood cell maturation in bone marrow.', '["anti-pernicious anemia factor","animal protein factor","citrovorum factor","erythrocyte maturation factor"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 1, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-09-26T14:32:12.431Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":0}'::jsonb, NULL, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.175Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-09T04:15:13.091Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1758967917483-43', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783610495718-1790433139833-fmjgg', 'The main goal of the Krebs cycle is to produce H+ (electrons). These by-products are transferred by niacin and riboflavin to the: *', 'Electron transport chain', 'The primary goal of the Krebs (citric acid) cycle is to generate reduced coenzymes (NADH and FADH₂) carrying high-energy electrons, which are subsequently transferred to the electron transport chain in the mitochondrial inner membrane for oxidative phosphorylation and ATP synthesis.

• Embden-Meyerhoff pathway: Refers to cytosolic glycolysis, not the destination of Krebs cycle electrons.
• Citric acid cycle: The cycle itself generates the electrons, which are then exported out of the cycle.
• Cori cycle: The lactate-glucose shuttle loop between muscle and liver.', '["Embden-Meyerhoff pathway","Electron transport chain","Citric acid cycle","Cori cycle"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 1, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-09-26T14:32:12.431Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":0}'::jsonb, NULL, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.175Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-09T04:15:39.210Z"'::jsonb)
