Hypertensive Stage II: >160/>100', '["Normal: 80\n\nPrehypertensive: 120-139/80-89\n\nHypertensive Stage I: 140-159/90-99\n\nHypertensive Stage II: >160/>100","Congestive Heart Failure","Cerebrovascular accident","Gastrosucchorrhea"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-31T16:26:18.157Z","interval":3,"easeFactor":2.35,"repetitions":2}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-28T16:26:18.157Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.801Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-08T12:24:43.573Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756795259244-80', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783610598974-1790433142628-tj9iq', 'Prevents the tendency to form blood clots in patient with CAD, valve damage. Example is aspirin.', 'Anticoagulants', 'In generalized board exam questions, Anticoagulants (and antithrombotic families) are standard classifications for medications designed to prevent the tendency to form blood clots inside blood vessels and heart chambers.

They are heavily utilized in patients with coronary artery disease (CAD) to keep plaque rupture from causing a complete vascular blockage (heart attack), and in patients with valve damage or mechanical valves where turbulent blood flow puts them at extreme risk for clot formation.

Note on Pharmacology: While pharmacology textbooks distinguish between antiplatelet agents (like aspirin, which keeps platelets from clumping) and anticoagulants (like warfarin or heparin, which target coagulation cascade factors), standard board exam keys often use Anticoagulants as the macro-level matching category for clot-preventing medications when antiplatelets are not listed as a standalone choice.

• Digitalis: A cardiac glycoside that increases the force of heart contractions and slows heart rate; used primarily to treat heart failure and atrial fibrillation, not to directly prevent clotting.
• Beta-adrenergic (Beta-blockers): Medications that block epinephrine to lower heart rate and blood pressure, reducing the heart''s overall workload and oxygen demand.
• Calcium-channel blockers: Medications that relax and widen blood vessels by blocking calcium entry into muscle cells, which helps treat hypertension and stable angina rather than halting clot synthesis.', '["Anticoagulants","Beta-adrenergic","Calcium-channel blockers","Digitalis"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-31T13:11:50.468Z","interval":3,"easeFactor":2.35,"repetitions":2}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-28T13:11:50.468Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.801Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-28T13:11:50.664Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756795317108-81', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783610598974-1790433142628-tj9iq', 'Blockers blocks norepinephrine receptors in heart muscle and thus reduce the strength of the heart beat and may correct certain arrhythmias.', 'Beta-adrenergic', 'Beta-adrenergic blockers (commonly known as Beta-blockers, such as metoprolol or atenolol) work by directly binding to and blocking beta-1 adrenergic receptors on heart muscle cells.

By blocking the binding of stress hormones like norepinephrine and epinephrine, these medications decrease the force and strength of the heartbeat (negative inotropic effect) and lower the heart rate. This reduces the heart''s overall workload and oxygen demand while stabilizing electrical pathways to correct certain arrhythmias (irregular heartbeats).

• Anticoagulants: Medications used to prevent blood clot formation, typically by disrupting the coagulation cascade. They have no effect on norepinephrine receptors or heartbeat strength.
• Digitalis: A cardiac glycoside that does the opposite for strength—it increases the strength of the heartbeat (positive inotrope) while slowing the rate down, primarily used in heart failure management.
• Calcium-channel blockers: These medications block calcium ions from entering heart and blood vessel muscle cells. While they can treat arrhythmias and lower blood pressure, they target calcium channels rather than norepinephrine receptors.', '["Anticoagulants","Beta-adrenergic","Calcium-channel blockers","Digitalis"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 3, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-04T08:08:33.743Z","interval":6,"easeFactor":2.15,"repetitions":3}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-29T08:08:33.743Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.801Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-29T08:08:34.066Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756795367789-82', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783610598974-1790433142628-tj9iq', 'Refers to drugs that block the flow of calcium into cardiac muscle cells, thus reducing heart contraction. It may also lower BP.', 'Calcium-channel blockers', 'Calcium-channel blockers', '["Anticoagulants","Beta-adrenergic","Calcium-channel blockers","Digitalis"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-24T14:02:41.303Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-23T14:02:41.303Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.801Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-08T12:24:43.573Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756795462001-83', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783610598974-1790433142628-tj9iq', 'Drugs that slow the heart rate and increase strength of cardiac contraction; used in treatment of congestive heart failure (CHF) and arrhythmias. Digoxin is an example.', 'Digitalis', 'Digitalis works by reversibly inhibiting the sodium-potassium ATPase pump in myocardial cells. This inhibition directly causes:
	• Increased Contractile Strength (Positive Inotropy): It triggers a rise in intracellular calcium, which heavily strengthens the force of cardiac contractions, helping an enlarged, failing heart pump blood efficiently.
	• Slower Heart Rate (Negative Chronotropy): It increases vagal tone and slows electrical conduction through the atrioventricular (AV) node. This safely slows down a rapid heart rate, making it highly effective for treating chronic atrial fibrillation and congestive heart failure (CHF).

• Tissue plasminogen activator (tPA): A thrombolytic "clot-busting" agent used in emergencies to dissolve blood clots during an acute ischemic stroke or heart attack; it has no effect on heartbeat strength or rate.
• Nitroglycerin: A potent vasodilator used to relieve angina pectoris (chest pain) by widening the coronary arteries to improve blood flow, rather than changing the mechanical strength of myocardial contractions.
• Calcium-channel blockers: These medications relax blood vessels and lower blood pressure. While they can slow down the heart rate, they actually decrease the strength of heart contractions (negative inotrope), which is the exact opposite of what is described.', '["Calcium-channel blockers","Digitalis","Nitroglycerin","Tissue plasminogen activator"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 3, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-04T08:08:56.288Z","interval":6,"easeFactor":2.15,"repetitions":3}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-29T08:08:56.288Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.801Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-29T08:08:56.601Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756795513247-84', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783610598974-1790433142628-tj9iq', 'Refers to drug that dilate coronary blood vessels, thus increasing the flow of oxygenated blood to myocardium; used to relieve or prevent angina pectoris.', 'Nitroglycerin', 'Nitroglycerin is a potent vasodilator. Once administered, it is converted into nitric oxide, which relaxes the smooth muscle walls of blood vessels. This action dilates the coronary arteries, allowing a rapid surge of oxygenated blood to reach the oxygen-deprived heart muscle (myocardium).

By widening systemic veins and arteries, it decreases the workload on the heart and reduces preload, making it the classic, first-line drug of choice used to immediately relieve or prevent acute attacks of angina pectoris (chest pain).

• Calcium-channel blockers: While these medications also help manage angina and hypertension by relaxing blood vessels and lowering heart rate, they work by blocking calcium ions from entering muscle cells. They are typically used for long-term maintenance rather than serving as the definitive, rapid-relief anti-anginal agent.
• Tissue plasminogen activator (tPA): A powerful "clot-busting" thrombolytic drug used in emergencies to dissolve blood clots during an active ischemic stroke or myocardial infarction (heart attack). It does not function as a simple coronary vasodilator for angina.
• Digitalis (Digoxin): A cardiac glycoside used to treat heart failure and certain arrhythmias. It works by increasing the strength or force of heart contractions and slowing the heart rate, rather than directly dilating the coronary vessels to treat angina.', '["Calcium-channel blockers","Digitalis","Nitroglycerin","Tissue plasminogen activator"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 3, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-11-04T08:10:32.809Z","interval":6,"easeFactor":2.15,"repetitions":3}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-29T08:10:32.809Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.801Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-29T08:10:33.126Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756795550179-85', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783610598974-1790433142628-tj9iq', 'Streptokinase are drugs that are able to dissolve blood clots, which may be blocking coronary arteries.', 'Tissue plasminogen activator', 'The question refers to streptokinase, which belongs to a class of medications called thrombolytic agents or "clot busters".

Both streptokinase and tissue plasminogen activator (tPA) function by converting plasminogen into plasmin. Plasmin is the primary enzyme responsible for breaking down fibrin networks, effectively dissolving acute blood clots that block the coronary arteries during a myocardial infarction (heart attack).

• Digitalis: A cardiac glycoside that increases the force of heart contractions (positive inotrope) and slows the heart rate, primarily used for heart failure and atrial fibrillation.
• Nitroglycerin: A powerful vasodilator that relaxes and widens blood vessels to improve blood flow and relieve chest pain (angina), but it cannot dissolve an existing clot.
• Calcium-channel blockers: Anti-hypertensive and anti-anginal medications that relax blood vessels and lower the heart''s workload by disrupting calcium movement into muscle cells.', '["Calcium-channel blockers","Digitalis","Nitroglycerin","Tissue plasminogen activator"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-31T16:37:32.882Z","interval":3,"easeFactor":2.35,"repetitions":2}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-28T16:37:32.882Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.801Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-28T16:37:33.058Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756795676739-86', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783610598974-1790433142628-tj9iq', 'The elevation of plasma lipids, somtimes called hyperlipoproteinemia', 'Hyperlipidemia', 'Answer: Hyperlipedimia', '["Hyperlipidemia","Fatty liver","Hemorrhoids","Endocrine-related obesities"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-24T14:04:38.253Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-23T14:04:38.253Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.801Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-08T12:24:43.573Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756795807767-87', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783610598974-1790433142628-tj9iq', 'Refers to heart attack, death of a cardiac cell/tissue due to inadequate blood supply.', 'Myocardial Infarction', 'Myocardial Infarction', '["Myocardial Infarction","Congestive Heart Failure","Coronary Artery Disease"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-24T14:04:50.701Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-23T14:04:50.701Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.801Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-08T12:24:43.573Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756795863181-88', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783610598974-1790433142628-tj9iq', 'Refers to the failure of the left ventricle to pump effectively; characterized by dyspnea/orthopnea, pulmonary edema, and fatigability.', 'Congestive Heart Failure', 'Congestive Heart Failure (CHF) happens when the heart muscle—specifically the left ventricle—weakens or stiffens and fails to pump blood out to the body effectively.

Because the left ventricle cannot clear the blood it receives, fluid backs up into the lungs. This creates the classic clinical symptoms listed in the prompt:
	• Dyspnea/Orthopnea: Severe shortness of breath, especially when lying down flat (orthopnea), forcing patients to sleep propped up on pillows.
	• Pulmonary Edema: Fluid pooling directly inside the air sacs of the lungs.
	• Fatigability: Overwhelming fatigue because organs and working muscles are starved of oxygenated blood.

• Coronary Artery Disease: The narrowing or blockage of the arteries that supply blood directly to the heart muscle, usually caused by plaque buildup. While it is a leading cause of heart failure, it is the structural artery block rather than the pump failure itself.
• Myocardial Infarction: A heart attack. This is an acute, sudden event where blood flow to a section of the heart muscle is completely cut off, causing tissue death.
• Achlorhydria: A completely unrelated gastrointestinal condition characterized by the total absence of hydrochloric acid in the stomach juices.', '["Myocardial Infarction","Congestive Heart Failure","Coronary Artery Disease","Clinical option 4"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-31T15:48:35.440Z","interval":3,"easeFactor":2.35,"repetitions":2}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-28T15:48:35.440Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.801Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-28T15:48:35.638Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756795961443-89', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783610598974-1790433142628-tj9iq', 'The gradual thickening of the arteries of the walls due to the information of plaques.', 'Atherosclerosis', 'Atherosclerosis (derived from the Greek words athere meaning gruel or paste, and sclerosis meaning hardness) is the precise medical term for the gradual thickening, narrowing, and hardening of the arterial walls due to the formation of fibrofatty plaques (atheromas).

It begins when the inner lining of an artery (the endothelium) is damaged by factors like high blood pressure or smoking. This triggers a chronic inflammatory response where low-density lipoproteins (LDL) accumulate, become oxidized, and are swallowed by immune cells to form "foam cells." Over time, this mixture of cholesterol, cellular waste, and scar tissue calcifies into a rigid plaque that restricts blood flow.

• Hypertension: High blood pressure. While it causes chronic mechanical stress that damages vessel walls and drastically accelerates plaque formation, it is a hemodynamic measurement rather than the physical plaque architecture itself.
• Cerebrovascular accident (CVA): A stroke. This is a severe, acute clinical outcome or event that happens when a blood vessel supplying the brain either ruptures or becomes completely blocked by a piece of plaque that broke free.
• Coronary heart disease (CHD): A specific structural disease category that refers to the narrowing of the blood vessels feeding the heart muscle. While CHD is almost exclusively caused by atherosclerosis, atherosclerosis is the overarching universal process that can happen in arteries anywhere in the body (such as the brain, legs, or kidneys) [0.1].', '["Hypertension","Coronary heart disease","Atherosclerosis","Cerebrovascular accident"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-31T15:23:22.093Z","interval":3,"easeFactor":2.35,"repetitions":2}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-28T15:23:22.093Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.801Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-10-28T15:23:22.278Z"'::jsonb),
('anki-card-1756796000256-90', NULL, 'anki-deck-1783610598974-1790433142628-tj9iq', 'Refers to the disruption of blood supply to the brain due to the blockage or rupture of the blood vessels.', 'Cerebrovascular accident', 'Cerebrovascular accident', '["Hypertension","Coronary heart disease","Atherosclerosis","Cerebrovascular accident"]'::jsonb, '["Nutritional Biochemistry"]'::jsonb, NULL, 'hard', 2, '', '{"dueDate":"2026-10-24T14:05:23.569Z","interval":1,"easeFactor":2.5,"repetitions":1}'::jsonb, '"2026-10-23T14:05:23.569Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-09-26T14:32:29.801Z"'::jsonb, '"2026-11-08T12:24:43.573Z"'::jsonb),
