('mc:ceruloplasmin is a type of::::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'ceruloplasmin is a type of:', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Ceruloplasmin is a type of metalloprotein (specifically a copper-binding metalloprotein) and it is also a glycoprotein.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"metalloprotein\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Ceruloplasmin is a type of metalloprotein (specifically a copper-binding metalloprotein) and it is also a glycoprotein.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: glycoprotein, phosphoprotein, lipoprotein"}'::jsonb),
('mc:why is a saturated fat called “saturated”?:::the fatty acid carbon chain is saturated with hydrogen.', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'why is a saturated fat called “saturated”?', 'The fatty acid carbon chain is saturated with hydrogen.', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"In a saturated fatty acid chain, there are only single bonds between the neighboring carbon atoms. Because carbon atoms must form four bonds, having only single bonds means each carbon atom can bind to the maximum possible number of hydrogen atoms.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"The fatty acid carbon chain is saturated with hydrogen.\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"In a saturated fatty acid chain, there are only single bonds between the neighboring carbon atoms. Because carbon atoms must form four bonds, having only single bonds means each carbon atom can bind to the maximum possible number of hydrogen atoms.\n\nIn contrast, an unsaturated fat contains one or more carbon-to-carbon double bonds (C = C). These double bonds reduce the open spaces available for hydrogen, leaving the molecule \"unsaturated\".","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: The fat is saturated with water., The fatty acid carbon chain is saturated with carbon., The fatty acid carbon chains can have more water added."}'::jsonb),
('mc:why is a saturated fat called “saturated”?:::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'why is a saturated fat called “saturated”?', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"In a saturated fatty acid chain, there are only single bonds between the neighboring carbon atoms. Because carbon atoms must form four bonds, having only single bonds means each carbon atom can bind to the maximum possible number of hydrogen atoms.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"The fatty acid carbon chain is saturated with hydrogen.\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"In a saturated fatty acid chain, there are only single bonds between the neighboring carbon atoms. Because carbon atoms must form four bonds, having only single bonds means each carbon atom can bind to the maximum possible number of hydrogen atoms.\n\nIn contrast, an unsaturated fat contains one or more carbon-to-carbon double bonds (C = C). These double bonds reduce the open spaces available for hydrogen, leaving the molecule \"unsaturated\".","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: The fat is saturated with water., The fatty acid carbon chain is saturated with carbon., The fatty acid carbon chains can have more water added."}'::jsonb),
('mc:food service system utilized in most coffee shops like dunkin donuts::::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'food service system utilized in most coffee shops like dunkin donuts:', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Dunkin Donuts practices off premise food production and the food are usually just assembled, heated before service. This is a characteristic of assembly/serve foodserving system.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Assembly\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Dunkin Donuts practices off premise food production and the food are usually just assembled, heated before service. This is a characteristic of assembly/serve foodserving system.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Conventional, Commisary, Ready prepared"}'::jsonb),
('mc:digestion and absorption of carbohydrate involves all the following except::::an acid stable amylase which acts in the stomach', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'digestion and absorption of carbohydrate involves all the following except:', 'an acid stable amylase which acts in the stomach', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"The statement regarding ''an acid stable amylase which acts in the stomach'' is false, making it the correct choice for an ''EXCEPT'' question. Unlike lipid digestion—which utilizes acid-stable lingual and gastric lipases in the stomach—there is no gastric amylase secreted by humans, nor is salivary alpha-amylase acid-stable. Salivary amylase becomes inactivated when mixed with gastric juice at a pH below 4.0, halting enzymatic carbohydrate hydrolysis until pancreatic secretions neutralize the chyme in the small intestine.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"an acid stable amylase which acts in the stomach\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"The statement regarding ''an acid stable amylase which acts in the stomach'' is false, making it the correct choice for an ''EXCEPT'' question. Unlike lipid digestion—which utilizes acid-stable lingual and gastric lipases in the stomach—there is no gastric amylase secreted by humans, nor is salivary alpha-amylase acid-stable. Salivary amylase becomes inactivated when mixed with gastric juice at a pH below 4.0, halting enzymatic carbohydrate hydrolysis until pancreatic secretions neutralize the chyme in the small intestine.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: salivary alpha amylase, disaccharidases in the intestines, pancreatic alpha amylase"}'::jsonb),
('mc:digestion and absorption of carbohydrate involves all the following except::::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'digestion and absorption of carbohydrate involves all the following except:', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"The statement regarding ''an acid stable amylase which acts in the stomach'' is false, making it the correct choice for an ''EXCEPT'' question. Unlike lipid digestion—which utilizes acid-stable lingual and gastric lipases in the stomach—there is no gastric amylase secreted by humans, nor is salivary alpha-amylase acid-stable. Salivary amylase becomes inactivated when mixed with gastric juice at a pH below 4.0, halting enzymatic carbohydrate hydrolysis until pancreatic secretions neutralize the chyme in the small intestine.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"an acid stable amylase which acts in the stomach\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"The statement regarding ''an acid stable amylase which acts in the stomach'' is false, making it the correct choice for an ''EXCEPT'' question. Unlike lipid digestion—which utilizes acid-stable lingual and gastric lipases in the stomach—there is no gastric amylase secreted by humans, nor is salivary alpha-amylase acid-stable. Salivary amylase becomes inactivated when mixed with gastric juice at a pH below 4.0, halting enzymatic carbohydrate hydrolysis until pancreatic secretions neutralize the chyme in the small intestine.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: salivary alpha amylase, disaccharidases in the intestines, pancreatic alpha amylase"}'::jsonb),
('mc:this mineral works closely with insulin to regulate the release of energy from glucose::::chromium', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'this mineral works closely with insulin to regulate the release of energy from glucose:', 'chromium', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Chromium (specifically in its trivalent form, Cr³⁺) is an essential trace mineral that acts as an \"insulin amplifier\". It binds with other compounds in the body to form Glucose Tolerance Factor (GTF).","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"chromium\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Chromium (specifically in its trivalent form, Cr³⁺) is an essential trace mineral that acts as an \"insulin amplifier\". It binds with other compounds in the body to form Glucose Tolerance Factor (GTF).\n\nGTF stabilizes insulin and enhances its ability to dock onto insulin receptors on the surface of your cells. This binding clears glucose from the bloodstream and pulls it directly into the cells, where it can enter metabolic pathways (like glycolysis and the Krebs cycle) to be converted into usable cellular energy (ATP).\n\n• Copper: Primarily required for iron metabolism, red blood cell synthesis, and cross-linking collagen.\n• Iodine: Crucial for synthesizing thyroid hormones, which regulate overall baseline metabolic rate, but does not bind with insulin.\n• Selenium: Functions as a vital component of antioxidant enzymes like glutathione peroxidase to neutralize free radicals.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: iodine, selenium, copper"}'::jsonb),
('mc:all are feeding problems prevalent in adolescence except::::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'all are feeding problems prevalent in adolescence except:', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Gagging is a feeding problem prevalent among preschool children.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Gagging\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Gagging is a feeding problem prevalent among preschool children.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Snacking, Non-traditional eating patterns, Meal skipping"}'::jsonb),
('mc:all are dietary carbohydrates except::::glycogen', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'all are dietary carbohydrates except:', 'glycogen', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Dietary Fibers & Plant Carbs: Pectin, Starch, and Mucilages are carbohydrates synthesized by plants. Pectin and mucilages are classified as soluble dietary fibers, while starch is the primary digestible storage carbohydrate in plants.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"glycogen\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Dietary Fibers & Plant Carbs: Pectin, Starch, and Mucilages are carbohydrates synthesized by plants. Pectin and mucilages are classified as soluble dietary fibers, while starch is the primary digestible storage carbohydrate in plants.\n\nGlycogen is the complex storage carbohydrate synthesized and found inside animal tissue (specifically liver and muscle cells).","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: mucilages, starch, pectin"}'::jsonb),
('mc:this mineral works closely with insulin to regulate the release of energy from glucose::::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'this mineral works closely with insulin to regulate the release of energy from glucose:', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Chromium (specifically in its trivalent form, Cr³⁺) is an essential trace mineral that acts as an \"insulin amplifier\". It binds with other compounds in the body to form Glucose Tolerance Factor (GTF).","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"chromium\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Chromium (specifically in its trivalent form, Cr³⁺) is an essential trace mineral that acts as an \"insulin amplifier\". It binds with other compounds in the body to form Glucose Tolerance Factor (GTF).\n\nGTF stabilizes insulin and enhances its ability to dock onto insulin receptors on the surface of your cells. This binding clears glucose from the bloodstream and pulls it directly into the cells, where it can enter metabolic pathways (like glycolysis and the Krebs cycle) to be converted into usable cellular energy (ATP).\n\n• Copper: Primarily required for iron metabolism, red blood cell synthesis, and cross-linking collagen.\n• Iodine: Crucial for synthesizing thyroid hormones, which regulate overall baseline metabolic rate, but does not bind with insulin.\n• Selenium: Functions as a vital component of antioxidant enzymes like glutathione peroxidase to neutralize free radicals.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: iodine, selenium, copper"}'::jsonb),
('mc:the b vitamin needed in the synthesis of niacin from tryptophan::::pyridoxine', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'the b vitamin needed in the synthesis of niacin from tryptophan:', 'pyridoxine', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"The body can manufacture niacin (Vitamin B₃) endogenously from the essential amino acid tryptophan.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"pyridoxine\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"The body can manufacture niacin (Vitamin B₃) endogenously from the essential amino acid tryptophan.\n\nPyridoxine (Vitamin B₆) serves as an essential cofactor for the enzyme kynureninase. A deficiency in Vitamin B₆ halts this step, preventing tryptophan from converting into niacin and potentially leading to niacin-deficiency symptoms (like pellagra).\n\nRiboflavin (Vitamin B₂) is also involved earlier in the pathway (for the enzyme kynurenine 3-monooxygenase), but kynureninase (B₆) is considered the most clinically significant bottleneck.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: thiamin, niacin, riboflavin"}'::jsonb),
('mc:the b vitamin needed in the synthesis of niacin from tryptophan::::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'the b vitamin needed in the synthesis of niacin from tryptophan:', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"The body can manufacture niacin (Vitamin B₃) endogenously from the essential amino acid tryptophan.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"pyridoxine\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"The body can manufacture niacin (Vitamin B₃) endogenously from the essential amino acid tryptophan.\n\nPyridoxine (Vitamin B₆) serves as an essential cofactor for the enzyme kynureninase. A deficiency in Vitamin B₆ halts this step, preventing tryptophan from converting into niacin and potentially leading to niacin-deficiency symptoms (like pellagra).\n\nRiboflavin (Vitamin B₂) is also involved earlier in the pathway (for the enzyme kynurenine 3-monooxygenase), but kynureninase (B₆) is considered the most clinically significant bottleneck.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: thiamin, niacin, riboflavin"}'::jsonb),
('mc:which of the following is not an essential amino acid?:::alanine', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'which of the following is not an essential amino acid?', 'alanine', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Alanine is a non-essential amino acid: The human body possesses the necessary enzymes to synthesize alanine internally (typically via the transamination of pyruvate). Because the body can produce it on its own, it does not strictly need to be obtained directly from your diet.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"alanine\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Alanine is a non-essential amino acid: The human body possesses the necessary enzymes to synthesize alanine internally (typically via the transamination of pyruvate). Because the body can produce it on its own, it does not strictly need to be obtained directly from your diet.\n\nTo instantly recall the 9 essential amino acids during a test, memorize the phrase \"PVT TIM HALL\":\n• Phenylalanine\n• Valine\n• Threonine\n• Tryptophan\n• Isoleucine\n• Methionine\n• Histidine\n• Arginine (often classified as semi-essential or required mostly for children)\n• Leucine\n• Lysine","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: tryptophan, lysine, phenylalanine"}'::jsonb),
('mc:which of the following is not an essential amino acid?:::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'which of the following is not an essential amino acid?', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Alanine is a non-essential amino acid: The human body possesses the necessary enzymes to synthesize alanine internally (typically via the transamination of pyruvate). Because the body can produce it on its own, it does not strictly need to be obtained directly from your diet.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"alanine\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Alanine is a non-essential amino acid: The human body possesses the necessary enzymes to synthesize alanine internally (typically via the transamination of pyruvate). Because the body can produce it on its own, it does not strictly need to be obtained directly from your diet.\n\nTo instantly recall the 9 essential amino acids during a test, memorize the phrase \"PVT TIM HALL\":\n• Phenylalanine\n• Valine\n• Threonine\n• Tryptophan\n• Isoleucine\n• Methionine\n• Histidine\n• Arginine (often classified as semi-essential or required mostly for children)\n• Leucine\n• Lysine","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: tryptophan, lysine, phenylalanine"}'::jsonb),
('mc:laboratory tests for a 45-year-old male alcoholic patient show an accumulation of lactate and pyruvate in body fluids, suggesting a block in the conversion of pyruvate to acetyl coa. he appears confused and shows a slight weakness of eye movement. this patient would most probably be treated with injections of::::thiamin', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'laboratory tests for a 45-year-old male alcoholic patient show an accumulation of lactate and pyruvate in body fluids, suggesting a block in the conversion of pyruvate to acetyl coa. he appears confused and shows a slight weakness of eye movement. this patient would most probably be treated with injections of:', 'thiamin', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"The combination of chronic alcoholism, confusion, and weakness of eye movement (ophthalmoplegia) is a classic presentation of Wernicke-Korsakoff syndrome, a severe neurological complication caused by thiamin (Vitamin B1) deficiency.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"thiamin\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"The combination of chronic alcoholism, confusion, and weakness of eye movement (ophthalmoplegia) is a classic presentation of Wernicke-Korsakoff syndrome, a severe neurological complication caused by thiamin (Vitamin B1) deficiency.\n\nThiamin pyrophosphate (TPP) is an essential cofactor for the pyruvate dehydrogenase (PDH) complex, which converts pyruvate into acetyl CoA. When thiamin is deficient, this complex cannot function.\n\nBecause pyruvate cannot be converted into acetyl CoA, it shunts into alternative pathways. This leads to the accumulation of both pyruvate and lactate (via lactic acid fermentation) in body fluids.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: niacin, pantothenic acid, riboflavin"}'::jsonb),
('mc:laboratory tests for a 45-year-old male alcoholic patient show an accumulation of lactate and pyruvate in body fluids, suggesting a block in the conversion of pyruvate to acetyl coa. he appears confused and shows a slight weakness of eye movement. this patient would most probably be treated with injections of::::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'laboratory tests for a 45-year-old male alcoholic patient show an accumulation of lactate and pyruvate in body fluids, suggesting a block in the conversion of pyruvate to acetyl coa. he appears confused and shows a slight weakness of eye movement. this patient would most probably be treated with injections of:', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"The combination of chronic alcoholism, confusion, and weakness of eye movement (ophthalmoplegia) is a classic presentation of Wernicke-Korsakoff syndrome, a severe neurological complication caused by thiamin (Vitamin B1) deficiency.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"thiamin\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"The combination of chronic alcoholism, confusion, and weakness of eye movement (ophthalmoplegia) is a classic presentation of Wernicke-Korsakoff syndrome, a severe neurological complication caused by thiamin (Vitamin B1) deficiency.\n\nThiamin pyrophosphate (TPP) is an essential cofactor for the pyruvate dehydrogenase (PDH) complex, which converts pyruvate into acetyl CoA. When thiamin is deficient, this complex cannot function.\n\nBecause pyruvate cannot be converted into acetyl CoA, it shunts into alternative pathways. This leads to the accumulation of both pyruvate and lactate (via lactic acid fermentation) in body fluids.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: niacin, pantothenic acid, riboflavin"}'::jsonb),
('mc:a key enzyme in carbohydrate metabolism found in the liver but not in muscles is::::glucokinase', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'a key enzyme in carbohydrate metabolism found in the liver but not in muscles is:', 'glucokinase', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Glucokinase (also known as Hexokinase IV) is an irreversible enzyme found almost exclusively in the liver parenchymal cells and pancreatic beta cells. It is notably absent from skeletal muscle tissue.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"glucokinase\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Glucokinase (also known as Hexokinase IV) is an irreversible enzyme found almost exclusively in the liver parenchymal cells and pancreatic beta cells. It is notably absent from skeletal muscle tissue.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: phosphorylase, hexokinase, pyruvate dehydrogenase"}'::jsonb),
('mc:a key enzyme in carbohydrate metabolism found in the liver but not in muscles is::::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'a key enzyme in carbohydrate metabolism found in the liver but not in muscles is:', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Glucokinase (also known as Hexokinase IV) is an irreversible enzyme found almost exclusively in the liver parenchymal cells and pancreatic beta cells. It is notably absent from skeletal muscle tissue.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"glucokinase\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Glucokinase (also known as Hexokinase IV) is an irreversible enzyme found almost exclusively in the liver parenchymal cells and pancreatic beta cells. It is notably absent from skeletal muscle tissue.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: phosphorylase, hexokinase, pyruvate dehydrogenase"}'::jsonb),
('mc:when a muscle contracts aerobically, less lactic acid is formed than when it contracts anaerobically because under aerobic conditions ::::the lactic acid generated is rapidly converted back to glucose.', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'when a muscle contracts aerobically, less lactic acid is formed than when it contracts anaerobically because under aerobic conditions :', 'the lactic acid generated is rapidly converted back to glucose.', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"During physical exertion, any lactic acid (or more accurately, lactate) that is produced diffuses out of the skeletal muscle cells into the bloodstream and is transported to the liver. In the hepatic Cori cycle, hepatocytes take up this lactate, oxidize it back to pyruvate via lactate dehydrogenase, and subsequently convert it into glucose via gluconeogenesis, which is then released back into the bloodstream to support active muscle tissue.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"the lactic acid generated is rapidly converted back to glucose.\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"During physical exertion, any lactic acid (or more accurately, lactate) that is produced diffuses out of the skeletal muscle cells into the bloodstream and is transported to the liver. In the hepatic Cori cycle, hepatocytes take up this lactate, oxidize it back to pyruvate via lactate dehydrogenase, and subsequently convert it into glucose via gluconeogenesis, which is then released back into the bloodstream to support active muscle tissue.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: muscles cannot convert glucose 6-phosphate to pyruvate., most of the pyruvate generated is oxidized., the major source of energy is the pentose pathway."}'::jsonb),
('mc:in foodservice facilities and layout, supporting services include the following, except::::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'in foodservice facilities and layout, supporting services include the following, except:', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"It includes all plus an area for steam hose use to wash and sanitize carts, etc.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"None of the above\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"It includes all plus an area for steam hose use to wash and sanitize carts, etc.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Office, Janitor''s closet/storage, Trash and waste storage"}'::jsonb),
('mc:when a muscle contracts aerobically, less lactic acid is formed than when it contracts anaerobically because under aerobic conditions ::::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'when a muscle contracts aerobically, less lactic acid is formed than when it contracts anaerobically because under aerobic conditions :', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"During physical exertion, any lactic acid (or more accurately, lactate) that is produced diffuses out of the skeletal muscle cells into the bloodstream and is transported to the liver. In the hepatic Cori cycle, hepatocytes take up this lactate, oxidize it back to pyruvate via lactate dehydrogenase, and subsequently convert it into glucose via gluconeogenesis, which is then released back into the bloodstream to support active muscle tissue.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"the lactic acid generated is rapidly converted back to glucose.\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"During physical exertion, any lactic acid (or more accurately, lactate) that is produced diffuses out of the skeletal muscle cells into the bloodstream and is transported to the liver. In the hepatic Cori cycle, hepatocytes take up this lactate, oxidize it back to pyruvate via lactate dehydrogenase, and subsequently convert it into glucose via gluconeogenesis, which is then released back into the bloodstream to support active muscle tissue.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: muscles cannot convert glucose 6-phosphate to pyruvate., most of the pyruvate generated is oxidized., the major source of energy is the pentose pathway."}'::jsonb),
('mc:all of the following compounds can serve as the starting material for the synthesis of glucose via gluconeogenesis except::::oleic acid', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'all of the following compounds can serve as the starting material for the synthesis of glucose via gluconeogenesis except:', 'oleic acid', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Oleic acid is a fatty acid (specifically a 18-carbon monounsaturated fatty acid). In humans and other mammals, even-chain fatty acids are broken down via beta-oxidation into acetyl-CoA.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"oleic acid\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Oleic acid is a fatty acid (specifically a 18-carbon monounsaturated fatty acid). In humans and other mammals, even-chain fatty acids are broken down via beta-oxidation into acetyl-CoA.\n\nAcetyl-CoA cannot be used to synthesize glucose because the reaction that converts pyruvate to acetyl-CoA (catalyzed by the pyruvate dehydrogenase complex) is completely irreversible. Additionally, for every two carbons that enter the citric acid cycle as acetyl-CoA, two carbons are lost as CO2, meaning there is no net synthesis of oxaloacetate to feed into gluconeogenesis.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: alanine, aspartic acid, glycerol"}'::jsonb),
('mc:all of the following compounds can serve as the starting material for the synthesis of glucose via gluconeogenesis except::::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'all of the following compounds can serve as the starting material for the synthesis of glucose via gluconeogenesis except:', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Oleic acid is a fatty acid (specifically a 18-carbon monounsaturated fatty acid). In humans and other mammals, even-chain fatty acids are broken down via beta-oxidation into acetyl-CoA.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"oleic acid\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Oleic acid is a fatty acid (specifically a 18-carbon monounsaturated fatty acid). In humans and other mammals, even-chain fatty acids are broken down via beta-oxidation into acetyl-CoA.\n\nAcetyl-CoA cannot be used to synthesize glucose because the reaction that converts pyruvate to acetyl-CoA (catalyzed by the pyruvate dehydrogenase complex) is completely irreversible. Additionally, for every two carbons that enter the citric acid cycle as acetyl-CoA, two carbons are lost as CO2, meaning there is no net synthesis of oxaloacetate to feed into gluconeogenesis.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: alanine, aspartic acid, glycerol"}'::jsonb),
('mc:the conversion of 1 mol of pyruvate to 3 mol of co2 via pyruvate dehydrogenase and the citric acid cycle yields mol of nadh, mol of fadh2, and mol of atp or gtp.:::4; 1; 1', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'the conversion of 1 mol of pyruvate to 3 mol of co2 via pyruvate dehydrogenase and the citric acid cycle yields mol of nadh, mol of fadh2, and mol of atp or gtp.', '4; 1; 1', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"To calculate the total yield, we must look at two distinct cellular reactions: the Pyruvate Dehydrogenase (PDH) complex (the transition step) and the Citric Acid (Krebs) Cycle. 1. The Pyruvate Dehydrogenase (PDH) Complex (Transition Step): 1 Pyruvate (3 carbons) is converted into 1 Acetyl-CoA (2 carbons), releasing the first molecule of \\[\\text{CO}_{2}\\].Yield: 1 NADH 2. The Citric Acid Cycle: 1 Acetyl-CoA enters the cycle and is fully oxidized, releasing the remaining 2 molecules of \\[\\text{CO}_{2}\\].Yield: 3 NADH, 1 \\[\\text{FADH}_{2}\\], and 1 ATP (or GTP) via substrate-level phosphorylation. Total Cumulative Yield:Adding the yields of both steps together: NADH: \\(1\\text{ (from PDH)} + 3\\text{ (from Citric Acid Cycle)} =\\) 4 mol\\[\\text{FADH}_{2}\\]: \\(0\\text{ (from PDH)} + 1\\text{ (from Citric Acid Cycle)} =\\) 1 molATP/GTP: \\(0\\text{ (from PDH)} + 1\\text{ (from Citric Acid Cycle)} =\\) 1 mol","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"4; 1; 1\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"To calculate the total yield, we must look at two distinct cellular reactions: the Pyruvate Dehydrogenase (PDH) complex (the transition step) and the Citric Acid (Krebs) Cycle. 1. The Pyruvate Dehydrogenase (PDH) Complex (Transition Step): 1 Pyruvate (3 carbons) is converted into 1 Acetyl-CoA (2 carbons), releasing the first molecule of \\[\\text{CO}_{2}\\].Yield: 1 NADH 2. The Citric Acid Cycle: 1 Acetyl-CoA enters the cycle and is fully oxidized, releasing the remaining 2 molecules of \\[\\text{CO}_{2}\\].Yield: 3 NADH, 1 \\[\\text{FADH}_{2}\\], and 1 ATP (or GTP) via substrate-level phosphorylation. Total Cumulative Yield:Adding the yields of both steps together: NADH: \\(1\\text{ (from PDH)} + 3\\text{ (from Citric Acid Cycle)} =\\) 4 mol\\[\\text{FADH}_{2}\\]: \\(0\\text{ (from PDH)} + 1\\text{ (from Citric Acid Cycle)} =\\) 1 molATP/GTP: \\(0\\text{ (from PDH)} + 1\\text{ (from Citric Acid Cycle)} =\\) 1 mol","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: 3; 2; 0, 4; 2; 1, 3; 1; 1"}'::jsonb),
('mc:the conversion of 1 mol of pyruvate to 3 mol of co2 via pyruvate dehydrogenase and the citric acid cycle yields mol of nadh, mol of fadh2, and mol of atp or gtp.:::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'the conversion of 1 mol of pyruvate to 3 mol of co2 via pyruvate dehydrogenase and the citric acid cycle yields mol of nadh, mol of fadh2, and mol of atp or gtp.', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"To calculate the total yield, we must look at two distinct cellular reactions: the Pyruvate Dehydrogenase (PDH) complex (the transition step) and the Citric Acid (Krebs) Cycle. 1. The Pyruvate Dehydrogenase (PDH) Complex (Transition Step): 1 Pyruvate (3 carbons) is converted into 1 Acetyl-CoA (2 carbons), releasing the first molecule of \\[\\text{CO}_{2}\\].Yield: 1 NADH 2. The Citric Acid Cycle: 1 Acetyl-CoA enters the cycle and is fully oxidized, releasing the remaining 2 molecules of \\[\\text{CO}_{2}\\].Yield: 3 NADH, 1 \\[\\text{FADH}_{2}\\], and 1 ATP (or GTP) via substrate-level phosphorylation. Total Cumulative Yield:Adding the yields of both steps together: NADH: \\(1\\text{ (from PDH)} + 3\\text{ (from Citric Acid Cycle)} =\\) 4 mol\\[\\text{FADH}_{2}\\]: \\(0\\text{ (from PDH)} + 1\\text{ (from Citric Acid Cycle)} =\\) 1 molATP/GTP: \\(0\\text{ (from PDH)} + 1\\text{ (from Citric Acid Cycle)} =\\) 1 mol","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"4; 1; 1\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"To calculate the total yield, we must look at two distinct cellular reactions: the Pyruvate Dehydrogenase (PDH) complex (the transition step) and the Citric Acid (Krebs) Cycle. 1. The Pyruvate Dehydrogenase (PDH) Complex (Transition Step): 1 Pyruvate (3 carbons) is converted into 1 Acetyl-CoA (2 carbons), releasing the first molecule of \\[\\text{CO}_{2}\\].Yield: 1 NADH 2. The Citric Acid Cycle: 1 Acetyl-CoA enters the cycle and is fully oxidized, releasing the remaining 2 molecules of \\[\\text{CO}_{2}\\].Yield: 3 NADH, 1 \\[\\text{FADH}_{2}\\], and 1 ATP (or GTP) via substrate-level phosphorylation. Total Cumulative Yield:Adding the yields of both steps together: NADH: \\(1\\text{ (from PDH)} + 3\\text{ (from Citric Acid Cycle)} =\\) 4 mol\\[\\text{FADH}_{2}\\]: \\(0\\text{ (from PDH)} + 1\\text{ (from Citric Acid Cycle)} =\\) 1 molATP/GTP: \\(0\\text{ (from PDH)} + 1\\text{ (from Citric Acid Cycle)} =\\) 1 mol","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: 3; 2; 0, 4; 2; 1, 3; 1; 1"}'::jsonb),
('mc:the term “glycemic effect of food” includes the following meanings except::::how long blood glucose is elevated after a meal', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'the term “glycemic effect of food” includes the following meanings except:', 'how long blood glucose is elevated after a meal', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"The glycemic effect (often measured as the glycemic index or glycemic load) specifically evaluates the intensity, speed, and recovery of the blood sugar spike following food consumption.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"how long blood glucose is elevated after a meal\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"The glycemic effect (often measured as the glycemic index or glycemic load) specifically evaluates the intensity, speed, and recovery of the blood sugar spike following food consumption.\n\nWhile a glycemic curve tracks how high blood glucose rises, how fast it gets absorbed, and how rapidly the body secretes insulin to bring it back down to baseline, it does not look at prolonged, sustained elevation as a trait of the food itself. A long-lasting elevation of blood sugar is typically a reflection of a patient''s underlying pathology (such as insulin resistance or diabetes), rather than an intrinsic characteristic of the food''s glycemic effect.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: how quickly blood glucose returns to normal, how high blood glucose rises after a meal, how quickly glucose is absorbed after eating"}'::jsonb),
('mc:the term “glycemic effect of food” includes the following meanings except::::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'the term “glycemic effect of food” includes the following meanings except:', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"The glycemic effect (often measured as the glycemic index or glycemic load) specifically evaluates the intensity, speed, and recovery of the blood sugar spike following food consumption.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"how long blood glucose is elevated after a meal\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"The glycemic effect (often measured as the glycemic index or glycemic load) specifically evaluates the intensity, speed, and recovery of the blood sugar spike following food consumption.\n\nWhile a glycemic curve tracks how high blood glucose rises, how fast it gets absorbed, and how rapidly the body secretes insulin to bring it back down to baseline, it does not look at prolonged, sustained elevation as a trait of the food itself. A long-lasting elevation of blood sugar is typically a reflection of a patient''s underlying pathology (such as insulin resistance or diabetes), rather than an intrinsic characteristic of the food''s glycemic effect.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: how quickly blood glucose returns to normal, how high blood glucose rises after a meal, how quickly glucose is absorbed after eating"}'::jsonb),
('mc:lipoprotein made primarily by liver cells to transport lipids to various tissues in the body::::vldl', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'lipoprotein made primarily by liver cells to transport lipids to various tissues in the body:', 'VLDL', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"VLDL (Very Low-Density Lipoprotein) is synthesized and secreted primarily by liver cells. Its main job is to transport newly created endogenous triglycerides and cholesterol from the liver out to various peripheral tissues in the body.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"VLDL\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"VLDL (Very Low-Density Lipoprotein) is synthesized and secreted primarily by liver cells. Its main job is to transport newly created endogenous triglycerides and cholesterol from the liver out to various peripheral tissues in the body.\n\n\t• Chylomicrons: These are made by intestinal cells (enterocytes), not liver cells. They transport dietary (exogenous) lipids absorbed from food.\n\t• HDL (High-Density Lipoprotein): Its main job is reverse cholesterol transport, meaning it picks up excess cholesterol from various tissues and returns it back to the liver.\n\t• LDL (Low-Density Lipoprotein): While it does deliver cholesterol to various tissues, it is not made directly by liver cells. Instead, LDL is formed in the bloodstream as VLDL shrinks and loses its triglycerides.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: HDL, LDL, chylomicron"}'::jsonb),
('mc:lipoprotein made primarily by liver cells to transport lipids to various tissues in the body::::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'lipoprotein made primarily by liver cells to transport lipids to various tissues in the body:', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"VLDL (Very Low-Density Lipoprotein) is synthesized and secreted primarily by liver cells. Its main job is to transport newly created endogenous triglycerides and cholesterol from the liver out to various peripheral tissues in the body.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"VLDL\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"VLDL (Very Low-Density Lipoprotein) is synthesized and secreted primarily by liver cells. Its main job is to transport newly created endogenous triglycerides and cholesterol from the liver out to various peripheral tissues in the body.\n\n\t• Chylomicrons: These are made by intestinal cells (enterocytes), not liver cells. They transport dietary (exogenous) lipids absorbed from food.\n\t• HDL (High-Density Lipoprotein): Its main job is reverse cholesterol transport, meaning it picks up excess cholesterol from various tissues and returns it back to the liver.\n\t• LDL (Low-Density Lipoprotein): While it does deliver cholesterol to various tissues, it is not made directly by liver cells. Instead, LDL is formed in the bloodstream as VLDL shrinks and loses its triglycerides.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: HDL, LDL, chylomicron"}'::jsonb),
('mc:all of the following are functions of cobalamin except::::antioxidant', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'all of the following are functions of cobalamin except:', 'antioxidant', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Cobalamin (Vitamin B12) does not primarily function as a direct antioxidant in the body, unlike Vitamins C or E.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"antioxidant\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Cobalamin (Vitamin B12) does not primarily function as a direct antioxidant in the body, unlike Vitamins C or E.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: co-factors, healthy nerves (myelin sheath)"}'::jsonb),
('mc:iron absorption is increased by the following nutrients except::::zn', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'iron absorption is increased by the following nutrients except:', 'Zn', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Zinc acts as a powerful inhibitor of iron absorption, rather than an enhancer. High doses of supplemental zinc compete directly with iron for the same membrane transporter pathways—specifically the DMT1 (Divalent Metal Transporter 1) channel—in the brush border cells of the small intestine. Consuming a large amount of zinc blocks this pathway, significantly reducing the absorption rate of non-heme iron.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Zn\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Zinc acts as a powerful inhibitor of iron absorption, rather than an enhancer. High doses of supplemental zinc compete directly with iron for the same membrane transporter pathways—specifically the DMT1 (Divalent Metal Transporter 1) channel—in the brush border cells of the small intestine. Consuming a large amount of zinc blocks this pathway, significantly reducing the absorption rate of non-heme iron.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: vitamin C, Cu, vitamin A"}'::jsonb),
('mc:iron absorption is increased by the following nutrients except::::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'iron absorption is increased by the following nutrients except:', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Zinc acts as a powerful inhibitor of iron absorption, rather than an enhancer. High doses of supplemental zinc compete directly with iron for the same membrane transporter pathways—specifically the DMT1 (Divalent Metal Transporter 1) channel—in the brush border cells of the small intestine. Consuming a large amount of zinc blocks this pathway, significantly reducing the absorption rate of non-heme iron.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Zn\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Zinc acts as a powerful inhibitor of iron absorption, rather than an enhancer. High doses of supplemental zinc compete directly with iron for the same membrane transporter pathways—specifically the DMT1 (Divalent Metal Transporter 1) channel—in the brush border cells of the small intestine. Consuming a large amount of zinc blocks this pathway, significantly reducing the absorption rate of non-heme iron.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: vitamin C, Cu, vitamin A"}'::jsonb),
('mc:except for , these essential branched-chain amino acids are used by skeletal muscles for energy during prolonged endurance exercise::::tryptophan', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'except for , these essential branched-chain amino acids are used by skeletal muscles for energy during prolonged endurance exercise:', 'tryptophan', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"The three essential branched-chain amino acids are leucine, isoleucine, and valine. They are unique because unlike most other amino acids that are metabolized primarily in the liver, BCAAs skip the liver and are oxidized directly within skeletal muscle tissue to provide energy during prolonged endurance training.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"tryptophan\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"The three essential branched-chain amino acids are leucine, isoleucine, and valine. They are unique because unlike most other amino acids that are metabolized primarily in the liver, BCAAs skip the liver and are oxidized directly within skeletal muscle tissue to provide energy during prolonged endurance training.\n\nDuring prolonged endurance exercise, as muscle glycogen stores deplete, muscle cells increasingly burn leucine, isoleucine, and valine for fuel. This drops blood BCAA levels, allowing more tryptophan to cross the blood-brain barrier, which actually contributes to central nervous system fatigue.\n\nTryptophan is an aromatic amino acid, not a branched-chain amino acid. While it is essential, it is not metabolized directly by skeletal muscle for energy during exercise.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: isoleucine, leucine, valine"}'::jsonb),
('mc:except for , these essential branched-chain amino acids are used by skeletal muscles for energy during prolonged endurance exercise::::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'except for , these essential branched-chain amino acids are used by skeletal muscles for energy during prolonged endurance exercise:', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"The three essential branched-chain amino acids are leucine, isoleucine, and valine. They are unique because unlike most other amino acids that are metabolized primarily in the liver, BCAAs skip the liver and are oxidized directly within skeletal muscle tissue to provide energy during prolonged endurance training.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"tryptophan\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"The three essential branched-chain amino acids are leucine, isoleucine, and valine. They are unique because unlike most other amino acids that are metabolized primarily in the liver, BCAAs skip the liver and are oxidized directly within skeletal muscle tissue to provide energy during prolonged endurance training.\n\nDuring prolonged endurance exercise, as muscle glycogen stores deplete, muscle cells increasingly burn leucine, isoleucine, and valine for fuel. This drops blood BCAA levels, allowing more tryptophan to cross the blood-brain barrier, which actually contributes to central nervous system fatigue.\n\nTryptophan is an aromatic amino acid, not a branched-chain amino acid. While it is essential, it is not metabolized directly by skeletal muscle for energy during exercise.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: isoleucine, leucine, valine"}'::jsonb)
