('mc:diet for calcium oxalate urinary calculi.:::alkaline ash diet', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'diet for calcium oxalate urinary calculi.', 'alkaline ash diet', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Calcium oxalate stones are the most common type of kidney stones. They tend to form more easily in acidic urine. An alkaline ash diet increases the pH of the urine (making it less acidic), which helps keep calcium oxalate and uric acid crystals dissolved so they can be passed safely. This diet focuses on eating foods like vegetables, fruits, and legumes, while reducing acid-forming foods like meats, cheeses, and grains.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"alkaline ash diet\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Calcium oxalate stones are the most common type of kidney stones. They tend to form more easily in acidic urine. An alkaline ash diet increases the pH of the urine (making it less acidic), which helps keep calcium oxalate and uric acid crystals dissolved so they can be passed safely. This diet focuses on eating foods like vegetables, fruits, and legumes, while reducing acid-forming foods like meats, cheeses, and grains.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: acid ash diet, neutral diet, low calcium diet"}'::jsonb),
('mc:diet for calcium oxalate urinary calculi.:::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'diet for calcium oxalate urinary calculi.', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Calcium oxalate stones are the most common type of kidney stones. They tend to form more easily in acidic urine. An alkaline ash diet increases the pH of the urine (making it less acidic), which helps keep calcium oxalate and uric acid crystals dissolved so they can be passed safely. This diet focuses on eating foods like vegetables, fruits, and legumes, while reducing acid-forming foods like meats, cheeses, and grains.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"alkaline ash diet\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Calcium oxalate stones are the most common type of kidney stones. They tend to form more easily in acidic urine. An alkaline ash diet increases the pH of the urine (making it less acidic), which helps keep calcium oxalate and uric acid crystals dissolved so they can be passed safely. This diet focuses on eating foods like vegetables, fruits, and legumes, while reducing acid-forming foods like meats, cheeses, and grains.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: acid ash diet, neutral diet, low calcium diet"}'::jsonb),
('mc:it is given when nutrient requirements are greatly increased and the git is not functional.:::tpn', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'it is given when nutrient requirements are greatly increased and the git is not functional.', 'TPN', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"When a patient''s gastrointestinal tract (GIT) is non-functional, blocked, or requires complete clinical rest, enteral options like a gastrostomy or a nasogastric tube (NGT) cannot be used safely. Bypassing the gut entirely becomes mandatory.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"TPN\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"When a patient''s gastrointestinal tract (GIT) is non-functional, blocked, or requires complete clinical rest, enteral options like a gastrostomy or a nasogastric tube (NGT) cannot be used safely. Bypassing the gut entirely becomes mandatory.\n\nWhile basic \"intravenous feeding\" usually refers to peripheral lines giving short-term hydration or dilute nutrients (like PPN), TPN is a highly concentrated, hyperosmolar formula packed with complete macronutrients, vitamins, and minerals. Because it is designed to meet massive metabolic demands (such as in severe burns, sepsis, or major trauma), it must be safely administered through a large central vein.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: NGT, intravenous feeding, gastrostomy"}'::jsonb),
('mc:in this style of tableservice, all food are prepared and portioned in the kitchen and attractively arranged on serving platters. food is served to individual plates using cutleries.:::russian', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'in this style of tableservice, all food are prepared and portioned in the kitchen and attractively arranged on serving platters. food is served to individual plates using cutleries.', 'Russian', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Russian style of service entails fully preparing food in the kitchen and placed in platters for service to guests.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Russian\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Russian style of service entails fully preparing food in the kitchen and placed in platters for service to guests.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: French, American, Blue plate"}'::jsonb),
('mc:it is given when nutrient requirements are greatly increased and the git is not functional.:::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'it is given when nutrient requirements are greatly increased and the git is not functional.', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"When a patient''s gastrointestinal tract (GIT) is non-functional, blocked, or requires complete clinical rest, enteral options like a gastrostomy or a nasogastric tube (NGT) cannot be used safely. Bypassing the gut entirely becomes mandatory.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"TPN\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"When a patient''s gastrointestinal tract (GIT) is non-functional, blocked, or requires complete clinical rest, enteral options like a gastrostomy or a nasogastric tube (NGT) cannot be used safely. Bypassing the gut entirely becomes mandatory.\n\nWhile basic \"intravenous feeding\" usually refers to peripheral lines giving short-term hydration or dilute nutrients (like PPN), TPN is a highly concentrated, hyperosmolar formula packed with complete macronutrients, vitamins, and minerals. Because it is designed to meet massive metabolic demands (such as in severe burns, sepsis, or major trauma), it must be safely administered through a large central vein.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: NGT, intravenous feeding, gastrostomy"}'::jsonb),
('mc:the fat content of a 2,000 kcal diet providing 30% of calories from fat.:::67g', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'the fat content of a 2,000 kcal diet providing 30% of calories from fat.', '67g', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Step-by-Step Calculation:","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"67g\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Step-by-Step Calculation:\n\n1. Find total calories from fat: Multiply the total calories by the fat percentage.\n2,000 kcal x 0.30 = 600 kcal from fat\n\n2. Convert calories to grams: Fat provides 9 calories per gram (9 kcal/g). Divide the fat calories by 9.\n600 kcal / 9 kcal/g = 66.67g\n\nWhen rounded to the nearest whole number, 66.67g gives you 67g.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: 60g, 77g, 80g"}'::jsonb),
('mc:the fat content of a 2,000 kcal diet providing 30% of calories from fat.:::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'the fat content of a 2,000 kcal diet providing 30% of calories from fat.', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Step-by-Step Calculation:","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"67g\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Step-by-Step Calculation:\n\n1. Find total calories from fat: Multiply the total calories by the fat percentage.\n2,000 kcal x 0.30 = 600 kcal from fat\n\n2. Convert calories to grams: Fat provides 9 calories per gram (9 kcal/g). Divide the fat calories by 9.\n600 kcal / 9 kcal/g = 66.67g\n\nWhen rounded to the nearest whole number, 66.67g gives you 67g.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: 60g, 77g, 80g"}'::jsonb),
('mc:refer to the following information. based on the fel, the diet of a burn patient contains the following meal pattern for lunch: 90g fried chicken; 160g rice; 45g sauteed squash; 250g fresh milk; and 103g ripe mango. how much is the cho content of the meal?:::71 g', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'refer to the following information. based on the fel, the diet of a burn patient contains the following meal pattern for lunch: 90g fried chicken; 160g rice; 45g sauteed squash; 250g fresh milk; and 103g ripe mango. how much is the cho content of the meal?', '71 g', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"According to the Philippine Food Exchange List (FEL), each food item is converted into exchange units to calculate its specific carbohydrate (CHO) content:","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"71 g\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"According to the Philippine Food Exchange List (FEL), each food item is converted into exchange units to calculate its specific carbohydrate (CHO) content:\n\n1. 160g Rice (2 Rice Exchanges):\n• 1 exchange of cooked rice = 80g (contains 23g CHO)\n• 160g = 2 exchanges\n• CHO content: 2 x 23g = 46g\n\n2. 250g Fresh Milk (1 Whole Milk Exchange):\n• 1 exchange of whole milk = 250g (contains 12g CHO)\n• CHO content: 1 x 12g = 12g\n\n3. 103g Ripe Mango (1 Fruit Exchange):\n• 1 exchange of fruit = approx. 100g (contains 10g CHO)\n• CHO content: 1 x 10g = 10g\n\n4. 45g Sauteed Squash (1 Vegetable B Exchange):\n• 1 exchange of cooked Vegetable B = 45g (contains 3g CHO)\n• CHO content: 1 x 3g = 3g (Note: The fat exchange added for sauteing oil contains 0g CHO).\n\n5. 90g Fried Chicken (3 Medium-Fat Meat Exchanges):\n• Meat exchanges contain 0g CHO.\n\nFinal CHO Summation:\n\n• Rice: 46g\n• Fresh Milk: 12g\n• Ripe Mango: 10g\n• Sauteed Squash: 3g\n• Fried Chicken: 0g\n• Total CHO Content: 46g + 12g + 10g + 3g = 71g","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: 69 g, 73 g, 81 g"}'::jsonb),
('mc:refer to the following information. based on the fel, the diet of a burn patient contains the following meal pattern for lunch: 90g fried chicken; 160g rice; 45g sauteed squash; 250g fresh milk; and 103g ripe mango. how much is the cho content of the meal?:::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'refer to the following information. based on the fel, the diet of a burn patient contains the following meal pattern for lunch: 90g fried chicken; 160g rice; 45g sauteed squash; 250g fresh milk; and 103g ripe mango. how much is the cho content of the meal?', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"According to the Philippine Food Exchange List (FEL), each food item is converted into exchange units to calculate its specific carbohydrate (CHO) content:","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"71 g\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"According to the Philippine Food Exchange List (FEL), each food item is converted into exchange units to calculate its specific carbohydrate (CHO) content:\n\n1. 160g Rice (2 Rice Exchanges):\n• 1 exchange of cooked rice = 80g (contains 23g CHO)\n• 160g = 2 exchanges\n• CHO content: 2 x 23g = 46g\n\n2. 250g Fresh Milk (1 Whole Milk Exchange):\n• 1 exchange of whole milk = 250g (contains 12g CHO)\n• CHO content: 1 x 12g = 12g\n\n3. 103g Ripe Mango (1 Fruit Exchange):\n• 1 exchange of fruit = approx. 100g (contains 10g CHO)\n• CHO content: 1 x 10g = 10g\n\n4. 45g Sauteed Squash (1 Vegetable B Exchange):\n• 1 exchange of cooked Vegetable B = 45g (contains 3g CHO)\n• CHO content: 1 x 3g = 3g (Note: The fat exchange added for sauteing oil contains 0g CHO).\n\n5. 90g Fried Chicken (3 Medium-Fat Meat Exchanges):\n• Meat exchanges contain 0g CHO.\n\nFinal CHO Summation:\n\n• Rice: 46g\n• Fresh Milk: 12g\n• Ripe Mango: 10g\n• Sauteed Squash: 3g\n• Fried Chicken: 0g\n• Total CHO Content: 46g + 12g + 10g + 3g = 71g","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: 69 g, 73 g, 81 g"}'::jsonb),
('mc:if the actual cost of a whole cake is p500, having a yield of 10 slices, and selling price is p90 per slice, what is the food cost percentage?:::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'if the actual cost of a whole cake is p500, having a yield of 10 slices, and selling price is p90 per slice, what is the food cost percentage?', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"(500/(90*10))*100","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"55.6%\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"(500/(90*10))*100","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: 5.56%, 18%, None of the above"}'::jsonb),
('mc:refer to the following information. based on the fel, the diet of a burn patient contains the following meal pattern for lunch: 90g fried chicken; 160g rice; 45g sauteed squash; 250g fresh milk; and 103g ripe mango. how much is the fat content of the meal?:::33 g', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'refer to the following information. based on the fel, the diet of a burn patient contains the following meal pattern for lunch: 90g fried chicken; 160g rice; 45g sauteed squash; 250g fresh milk; and 103g ripe mango. how much is the fat content of the meal?', '33 g', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"According to the Philippine Food Exchange List (FEL), each food item is converted into exchange units to calculate its specific macronutrient content. Here is the breakdown for each item in the lunch pattern:","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"33 g\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"According to the Philippine Food Exchange List (FEL), each food item is converted into exchange units to calculate its specific macronutrient content. Here is the breakdown for each item in the lunch pattern:\n\n1. 160g Rice (2 Rice Exchanges):\n• 1 exchange of cooked rice = 80g\n• 160g = 2 exchanges\n• Fat content = 0g\n\n2. 250g Fresh Milk (1 Whole Milk Exchange):\n• 1 exchange of whole milk = 250g\n• Fat content = 10g\n\n3. 103g Ripe Mango (1 Fruit Exchange):\n• 1 exchange of ripe mango = 1 slice/portion (approx. 100g)\n• Fat content = 0g\n\n4. 45g Sauteed Squash (1 Vegetable Exchange + 1 Fat Exchange):\n• 1 exchange of cooked squash = 45g (0g fat)\n• Since it is sauteed, the FEL rules require adding 1 fat exchange for the cooking oil.\n• 1 fat exchange = 5g\n\n5. 90g Fried Chicken (3 Medium-Fat Meat Exchanges):\n• 1 exchange of cooked meat = 30g\n• 90g = 3 exchanges\n• Chicken with skin or fried chicken is classified under Medium-Fat Meat, which contains 6g of fat per exchange.\n• 3 exchanges x 6g = 18g\n\nFinal Fat Summation:\n\n• Rice: 0g\n• Fresh Milk: 10g\n• Ripe Mango: 0g\n• Sauteed Squash (Oil): 5g\n• Fried Chicken: 18g\n• Total Fat Content: 10g + 5g + 18g = 33g","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: 27 g, 30 g, 35 g"}'::jsonb),
('mc:refer to the following information. based on the fel, the diet of a burn patient contains the following meal pattern for lunch: 90g fried chicken; 160g rice; 45g sauteed squash; 250g fresh milk; and 103g ripe mango. how much is the fat content of the meal?:::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'refer to the following information. based on the fel, the diet of a burn patient contains the following meal pattern for lunch: 90g fried chicken; 160g rice; 45g sauteed squash; 250g fresh milk; and 103g ripe mango. how much is the fat content of the meal?', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"According to the Philippine Food Exchange List (FEL), each food item is converted into exchange units to calculate its specific macronutrient content. Here is the breakdown for each item in the lunch pattern:","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"33 g\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"According to the Philippine Food Exchange List (FEL), each food item is converted into exchange units to calculate its specific macronutrient content. Here is the breakdown for each item in the lunch pattern:\n\n1. 160g Rice (2 Rice Exchanges):\n• 1 exchange of cooked rice = 80g\n• 160g = 2 exchanges\n• Fat content = 0g\n\n2. 250g Fresh Milk (1 Whole Milk Exchange):\n• 1 exchange of whole milk = 250g\n• Fat content = 10g\n\n3. 103g Ripe Mango (1 Fruit Exchange):\n• 1 exchange of ripe mango = 1 slice/portion (approx. 100g)\n• Fat content = 0g\n\n4. 45g Sauteed Squash (1 Vegetable Exchange + 1 Fat Exchange):\n• 1 exchange of cooked squash = 45g (0g fat)\n• Since it is sauteed, the FEL rules require adding 1 fat exchange for the cooking oil.\n• 1 fat exchange = 5g\n\n5. 90g Fried Chicken (3 Medium-Fat Meat Exchanges):\n• 1 exchange of cooked meat = 30g\n• 90g = 3 exchanges\n• Chicken with skin or fried chicken is classified under Medium-Fat Meat, which contains 6g of fat per exchange.\n• 3 exchanges x 6g = 18g\n\nFinal Fat Summation:\n\n• Rice: 0g\n• Fresh Milk: 10g\n• Ripe Mango: 0g\n• Sauteed Squash (Oil): 5g\n• Fried Chicken: 18g\n• Total Fat Content: 10g + 5g + 18g = 33g","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: 27 g, 30 g, 35 g"}'::jsonb),
('mc:refer to the following information. based on the fel, the diet of a burn patient contains the following meal pattern for lunch: 90g fried chicken; 160g rice; 45g sauteed squash; 250g fresh milk; and 103g ripe mango. how many kilocalories does the meal contain?:::730 kcal', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'refer to the following information. based on the fel, the diet of a burn patient contains the following meal pattern for lunch: 90g fried chicken; 160g rice; 45g sauteed squash; 250g fresh milk; and 103g ripe mango. how many kilocalories does the meal contain?', '730 kcal', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Step-by-Step Breakdown using the Food Exchange List (FEL)","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"730 kcal\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Step-by-Step Breakdown using the Food Exchange List (FEL)\n\nTo find the total energy value of this meal pattern, we break down each food item into its exact base exchanges and adjust for the preparation methods:\n\n1. 160g Rice (Cooked Rice):\n\t• Exchange value: 1 exchange of cooked rice is equal to 80g (providing 100 kcal).\n\t• 160g / 80g = 2 Rice Exchanges.\n\t• Energy Contribution: 2 * 100 kcal = 200 kcal.\n\n2. 250g Fresh Milk (Whole Milk):\n\t• Exchange value: 1 glass or 1 cup of whole fresh milk is equal to 250g (providing 170 kcal).\n\t• 250g / 250g = 1 Whole Milk Exchange.\n\t• Energy Contribution: 1 * 170 kcal = 170 kcal.\n\n3. 103g Ripe Mango:\n\t• Exchange value: 1 exchange of ripe mango is equal to 103g gross weight (providing 40 kcal).\n\t• 103g / 103g = 1 Fruit Exchange.\n\t• Energy Contribution: 1 * 40 kcal = 40 kcal.\n\n4. 45g Sauteed Squash (Vegetable B + Sauteing Fat):\n\t• Exchange value: 1 cooked vegetable exchange is equal to 1/2 cup or 45g (providing 16 kcal).\n\t• Preparation Modifier: Because the squash is sauteed, it adds 1 Fat Exchange (providing 45 kcal) for the cooking oil.\n\t• Energy Contribution: 16 kcal + 45 kcal = 61 kcal.\n\n5. 90g Fried Chicken (Low-Fat Meat + Frying Fats):\n\t• Exchange value: Chicken is classified as a Low-Fat Meat, where 1 exchange equals 30g cooked meat (providing 41 kcal). Therefore, 90g / 30g = 3 Low-Fat Meat Exchanges.\n\t• Preparation Modifier: Because it is fried, each of the 3 meat exchanges absorbs approximately 1 Fat Exchange (3 * 45 kcal = 135 kcal).\n\t• Energy Contribution: (3 * 41 kcal) + 135 kcal = 258 kcal.\n\nTotal Energy Summation\nTotal Calorie Count = 200 kcal + 170 kcal + 40 kcal + 61 kcal + 258 kcal = 729 kcal\n\nRounding off to the nearest multiple of five as standard practice in FEL board examination questions, 729 kcal yields exactly 730 kcal.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: 650 kcal, 700 kcal, 775 kcal"}'::jsonb),
('mc:refer to the following information. based on the fel, the diet of a burn patient contains the following meal pattern for lunch: 90g fried chicken; 160g rice; 45g sauteed squash; 250g fresh milk; and 103g ripe mango. how many kilocalories does the meal contain?:::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'refer to the following information. based on the fel, the diet of a burn patient contains the following meal pattern for lunch: 90g fried chicken; 160g rice; 45g sauteed squash; 250g fresh milk; and 103g ripe mango. how many kilocalories does the meal contain?', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Step-by-Step Breakdown using the Food Exchange List (FEL)","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"730 kcal\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Step-by-Step Breakdown using the Food Exchange List (FEL)\n\nTo find the total energy value of this meal pattern, we break down each food item into its exact base exchanges and adjust for the preparation methods:\n\n1. 160g Rice (Cooked Rice):\n\t• Exchange value: 1 exchange of cooked rice is equal to 80g (providing 100 kcal).\n\t• 160g / 80g = 2 Rice Exchanges.\n\t• Energy Contribution: 2 * 100 kcal = 200 kcal.\n\n2. 250g Fresh Milk (Whole Milk):\n\t• Exchange value: 1 glass or 1 cup of whole fresh milk is equal to 250g (providing 170 kcal).\n\t• 250g / 250g = 1 Whole Milk Exchange.\n\t• Energy Contribution: 1 * 170 kcal = 170 kcal.\n\n3. 103g Ripe Mango:\n\t• Exchange value: 1 exchange of ripe mango is equal to 103g gross weight (providing 40 kcal).\n\t• 103g / 103g = 1 Fruit Exchange.\n\t• Energy Contribution: 1 * 40 kcal = 40 kcal.\n\n4. 45g Sauteed Squash (Vegetable B + Sauteing Fat):\n\t• Exchange value: 1 cooked vegetable exchange is equal to 1/2 cup or 45g (providing 16 kcal).\n\t• Preparation Modifier: Because the squash is sauteed, it adds 1 Fat Exchange (providing 45 kcal) for the cooking oil.\n\t• Energy Contribution: 16 kcal + 45 kcal = 61 kcal.\n\n5. 90g Fried Chicken (Low-Fat Meat + Frying Fats):\n\t• Exchange value: Chicken is classified as a Low-Fat Meat, where 1 exchange equals 30g cooked meat (providing 41 kcal). Therefore, 90g / 30g = 3 Low-Fat Meat Exchanges.\n\t• Preparation Modifier: Because it is fried, each of the 3 meat exchanges absorbs approximately 1 Fat Exchange (3 * 45 kcal = 135 kcal).\n\t• Energy Contribution: (3 * 41 kcal) + 135 kcal = 258 kcal.\n\nTotal Energy Summation\nTotal Calorie Count = 200 kcal + 170 kcal + 40 kcal + 61 kcal + 258 kcal = 729 kcal\n\nRounding off to the nearest multiple of five as standard practice in FEL board examination questions, 729 kcal yields exactly 730 kcal.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: 650 kcal, 700 kcal, 775 kcal"}'::jsonb),
('mc:cholesterol level in food considered safe for daily consumption.:::300mg', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'cholesterol level in food considered safe for daily consumption.', '300mg', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"For many years, traditional clinical nutrition guidelines and board manuals (such as the American Heart Association and national dietary guidelines) established a strict upper limit of 300 mg of dietary cholesterol per day for healthy adults to reduce the risk of cardiovascular disease.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"300mg\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"For many years, traditional clinical nutrition guidelines and board manuals (such as the American Heart Association and national dietary guidelines) established a strict upper limit of 300 mg of dietary cholesterol per day for healthy adults to reduce the risk of cardiovascular disease.\n\nWhile modern guidelines have shifted to focusing more broadly on limiting saturated fats, the classic dietary limit taught and tested in academic question banks for a \"safe daily consumption level\" remains 300 mg.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: 450mg, 400mg, 475mg"}'::jsonb),
('mc:cholesterol level in food considered safe for daily consumption.:::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'cholesterol level in food considered safe for daily consumption.', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"For many years, traditional clinical nutrition guidelines and board manuals (such as the American Heart Association and national dietary guidelines) established a strict upper limit of 300 mg of dietary cholesterol per day for healthy adults to reduce the risk of cardiovascular disease.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"300mg\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"For many years, traditional clinical nutrition guidelines and board manuals (such as the American Heart Association and national dietary guidelines) established a strict upper limit of 300 mg of dietary cholesterol per day for healthy adults to reduce the risk of cardiovascular disease.\n\nWhile modern guidelines have shifted to focusing more broadly on limiting saturated fats, the classic dietary limit taught and tested in academic question banks for a \"safe daily consumption level\" remains 300 mg.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: 450mg, 400mg, 475mg"}'::jsonb),
('mc:diet management for a person with esophageal reflux.:::low fat, low cholesterol', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'diet management for a person with esophageal reflux.', 'low fat, low cholesterol', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Dietary fat stimulates the release of cholecystokinin (CCK) from the duodenal mucosa. CCK induces relaxation of the lower esophageal sphincter (LES), increasing transient LES relaxations (TLESRs) and facilitating the retrograde flow of acidic gastric contents into the esophagus. Furthermore, high-fat meals delay gastric emptying, prolonging the period of high gastric volume and pressure that promotes reflux episodes. Restricting dietary fat directly attenuates postprandial CCK surges, preserves basal LES tone, and accelerates gastric transit.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"low fat, low cholesterol\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Dietary fat stimulates the release of cholecystokinin (CCK) from the duodenal mucosa. CCK induces relaxation of the lower esophageal sphincter (LES), increasing transient LES relaxations (TLESRs) and facilitating the retrograde flow of acidic gastric contents into the esophagus. Furthermore, high-fat meals delay gastric emptying, prolonging the period of high gastric volume and pressure that promotes reflux episodes. Restricting dietary fat directly attenuates postprandial CCK surges, preserves basal LES tone, and accelerates gastric transit.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: liquid diet, high calorie, high protein, regular diet with small frequent meals"}'::jsonb),
('mc:diet management for a person with esophageal reflux.:::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'diet management for a person with esophageal reflux.', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Dietary fat stimulates the release of cholecystokinin (CCK) from the duodenal mucosa. CCK induces relaxation of the lower esophageal sphincter (LES), increasing transient LES relaxations (TLESRs) and facilitating the retrograde flow of acidic gastric contents into the esophagus. Furthermore, high-fat meals delay gastric emptying, prolonging the period of high gastric volume and pressure that promotes reflux episodes. Restricting dietary fat directly attenuates postprandial CCK surges, preserves basal LES tone, and accelerates gastric transit.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"low fat, low cholesterol\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Dietary fat stimulates the release of cholecystokinin (CCK) from the duodenal mucosa. CCK induces relaxation of the lower esophageal sphincter (LES), increasing transient LES relaxations (TLESRs) and facilitating the retrograde flow of acidic gastric contents into the esophagus. Furthermore, high-fat meals delay gastric emptying, prolonging the period of high gastric volume and pressure that promotes reflux episodes. Restricting dietary fat directly attenuates postprandial CCK surges, preserves basal LES tone, and accelerates gastric transit.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: liquid diet, high calorie, high protein, regular diet with small frequent meals"}'::jsonb),
('mc:malnutrition due to esophageal tumor is primarily related to::::inadequate intake', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'malnutrition due to esophageal tumor is primarily related to:', 'inadequate intake', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"The defining hallmark of an esophageal tumor is dysphagia (progressive difficulty swallowing) caused by the physical growth of the tumor narrowing the lumen of the esophagus.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"inadequate intake\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"The defining hallmark of an esophageal tumor is dysphagia (progressive difficulty swallowing) caused by the physical growth of the tumor narrowing the lumen of the esophagus.\n\nWhile cancer cachexia can induce a catabolic state or alter systemic metabolism over time, the immediate and primary driver of severe protein-calorie malnutrition in these patients at the time of diagnosis is the simple, mechanical inability to physically swallow and consume enough food. This results in massive, structural inadequate food and fluid intake.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: decreased absorption, catabolic state, increased energy needs"}'::jsonb),
('mc:malnutrition due to esophageal tumor is primarily related to::::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'malnutrition due to esophageal tumor is primarily related to:', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"The defining hallmark of an esophageal tumor is dysphagia (progressive difficulty swallowing) caused by the physical growth of the tumor narrowing the lumen of the esophagus.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"inadequate intake\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"The defining hallmark of an esophageal tumor is dysphagia (progressive difficulty swallowing) caused by the physical growth of the tumor narrowing the lumen of the esophagus.\n\nWhile cancer cachexia can induce a catabolic state or alter systemic metabolism over time, the immediate and primary driver of severe protein-calorie malnutrition in these patients at the time of diagnosis is the simple, mechanical inability to physically swallow and consume enough food. This results in massive, structural inadequate food and fluid intake.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: decreased absorption, catabolic state, increased energy needs"}'::jsonb),
('mc:flatulence, cramps, or diabetes may be some unpleasant side effects upon the initiation of this diet::::high-fiber diet', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'flatulence, cramps, or diabetes may be some unpleasant side effects upon the initiation of this diet:', 'high-fiber diet', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"When a person suddenly initiates a high-fiber diet, the digestive tract requires a transition period to adapt. The human body lacks the enzymes to digest dietary fiber, so it passes intact into the large intestine. There, resident gut bacteria ferment the fiber. A sudden influx of fiber causes rapid, aggressive bacterial fermentation, which produces large amounts of gas. This trapped gas directly results in flatulence, abdominal bloating, and painful cramps.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"high-fiber diet\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"When a person suddenly initiates a high-fiber diet, the digestive tract requires a transition period to adapt. The human body lacks the enzymes to digest dietary fiber, so it passes intact into the large intestine. There, resident gut bacteria ferment the fiber. A sudden influx of fiber causes rapid, aggressive bacterial fermentation, which produces large amounts of gas. This trapped gas directly results in flatulence, abdominal bloating, and painful cramps.\n\nWhile fiber does not cause diabetes, a high-fiber diet is a primary clinical intervention utilized for the management of Diabetes Mellitus. Soluble fiber creates a gel-like substance in the gut that slows down the absorption of glucose, preventing rapid post-meal blood sugar spikes and improving insulin sensitivity.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: fiber-restricted diet, soft diet, elemental diet"}'::jsonb),
('mc:patients taking medicines with mao inhibitors should be advised this diet::::tyramine-restricted', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'patients taking medicines with mao inhibitors should be advised this diet:', 'tyramine-restricted', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Monoamine oxidase inhibitors (MAOIs) are a class of medications used primarily as antidepressants. They work by blocking the enzyme monoamine oxidase, which normally breaks down neurotransmitters as well as tyramine (an amino acid byproduct found in aged and fermented foods).","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"tyramine-restricted\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Monoamine oxidase inhibitors (MAOIs) are a class of medications used primarily as antidepressants. They work by blocking the enzyme monoamine oxidase, which normally breaks down neurotransmitters as well as tyramine (an amino acid byproduct found in aged and fermented foods).\n\nIf a patient taking an MAOI consumes high-tyramine foods, the excess tyramine cannot be broken down. This causes a massive release of norepinephrine, leading to severe vasoconstriction and a dangerous blood pressure spike known as a hypertensive crisis. Therefore, these patients must strictly follow a tyramine-restricted diet.\n\nAged cheeses (like cheddar, blue, or swiss), cured or smoked meats (like salami and ham), soy sauce, tofu, fermented items (like sauerkraut), tap beer, and red wine.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: protein-restricted, fat-restricted, phenylalanine-restricted"}'::jsonb),
('mc:patients taking medicines with mao inhibitors should be advised this diet::::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'patients taking medicines with mao inhibitors should be advised this diet:', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Monoamine oxidase inhibitors (MAOIs) are a class of medications used primarily as antidepressants. They work by blocking the enzyme monoamine oxidase, which normally breaks down neurotransmitters as well as tyramine (an amino acid byproduct found in aged and fermented foods).","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"tyramine-restricted\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Monoamine oxidase inhibitors (MAOIs) are a class of medications used primarily as antidepressants. They work by blocking the enzyme monoamine oxidase, which normally breaks down neurotransmitters as well as tyramine (an amino acid byproduct found in aged and fermented foods).\n\nIf a patient taking an MAOI consumes high-tyramine foods, the excess tyramine cannot be broken down. This causes a massive release of norepinephrine, leading to severe vasoconstriction and a dangerous blood pressure spike known as a hypertensive crisis. Therefore, these patients must strictly follow a tyramine-restricted diet.\n\nAged cheeses (like cheddar, blue, or swiss), cured or smoked meats (like salami and ham), soy sauce, tofu, fermented items (like sauerkraut), tap beer, and red wine.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: protein-restricted, fat-restricted, phenylalanine-restricted"}'::jsonb),
('mc:exogenous sources of cholesterol include::::dairy and glandular only', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'exogenous sources of cholesterol include:', 'dairy and glandular only', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"• Exogenous cholesterol refers to cholesterol that comes from outside the body through the food we eat.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"dairy and glandular only\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"• Exogenous cholesterol refers to cholesterol that comes from outside the body through the food we eat.\n• The Golden Rule of Cholesterol: Cholesterol is only found in animal-based foods. Plants do not synthesize cholesterol.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: dairy products, glandular organs, nuts"}'::jsonb),
('mc:exogenous sources of cholesterol include::::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'exogenous sources of cholesterol include:', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"• Exogenous cholesterol refers to cholesterol that comes from outside the body through the food we eat.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"dairy and glandular only\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"• Exogenous cholesterol refers to cholesterol that comes from outside the body through the food we eat.\n• The Golden Rule of Cholesterol: Cholesterol is only found in animal-based foods. Plants do not synthesize cholesterol.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: dairy products, glandular organs, nuts"}'::jsonb),
('mc:a gradual transition from an iv feeding to an oral diet is primarily designed to?::::ensure that nutrient needs will continue to be met', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'a gradual transition from an iv feeding to an oral diet is primarily designed to?:', 'ensure that nutrient needs will continue to be met', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Transitional feeding is carefully structured to manage a patient''s progression from intravenous (parenteral) nutrition to eating by mouth. The foremost priority is ensuring that the patient doesn''t suffer from a drop in required calories, protein, fluids, or micronutrients while they gradually re-learn to chew, swallow, or digest regular food.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"ensure that nutrient needs will continue to be met\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Transitional feeding is carefully structured to manage a patient''s progression from intravenous (parenteral) nutrition to eating by mouth. The foremost priority is ensuring that the patient doesn''t suffer from a drop in required calories, protein, fluids, or micronutrients while they gradually re-learn to chew, swallow, or digest regular food.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: improve the appetite, prevent hypoglycemia, prevent apprehension about eating"}'::jsonb),
('mc:a gradual transition from an iv feeding to an oral diet is primarily designed to?::::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'a gradual transition from an iv feeding to an oral diet is primarily designed to?:', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Transitional feeding is carefully structured to manage a patient''s progression from intravenous (parenteral) nutrition to eating by mouth. The foremost priority is ensuring that the patient doesn''t suffer from a drop in required calories, protein, fluids, or micronutrients while they gradually re-learn to chew, swallow, or digest regular food.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"ensure that nutrient needs will continue to be met\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Transitional feeding is carefully structured to manage a patient''s progression from intravenous (parenteral) nutrition to eating by mouth. The foremost priority is ensuring that the patient doesn''t suffer from a drop in required calories, protein, fluids, or micronutrients while they gradually re-learn to chew, swallow, or digest regular food.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: improve the appetite, prevent hypoglycemia, prevent apprehension about eating"}'::jsonb),
('mc:clients undergoing colostomies and ileostomies may temporarily receive a low-fiber, bland diet to::::help the stoma heal', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'clients undergoing colostomies and ileostomies may temporarily receive a low-fiber, bland diet to:', 'help the stoma heal', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"After colostomy or ileostomy surgery, the bowel and the newly formed stoma are swollen, inflamed, and healing from surgical trauma. A low-fiber, bland diet minimizes irritation to the intestinal lining, reducing the workload on the gut so it can heal efficiently.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"help the stoma heal\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"After colostomy or ileostomy surgery, the bowel and the newly formed stoma are swollen, inflamed, and healing from surgical trauma. A low-fiber, bland diet minimizes irritation to the intestinal lining, reducing the workload on the gut so it can heal efficiently.\n\nHigh-fiber foods leave a large amount of indigestible residue (bulk) in the digestive tract. Because the internal lumen of a new stoma is narrowed due to post-operative swelling, a high-fiber intake poses a strict risk of causing a painful mechanical bowel blockage.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: increase intestinal motility, prevent malabsorption, prevent mineral deficiencies"}'::jsonb),
('mc:clients undergoing colostomies and ileostomies may temporarily receive a low-fiber, bland diet to::::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'clients undergoing colostomies and ileostomies may temporarily receive a low-fiber, bland diet to:', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"After colostomy or ileostomy surgery, the bowel and the newly formed stoma are swollen, inflamed, and healing from surgical trauma. A low-fiber, bland diet minimizes irritation to the intestinal lining, reducing the workload on the gut so it can heal efficiently.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"help the stoma heal\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"After colostomy or ileostomy surgery, the bowel and the newly formed stoma are swollen, inflamed, and healing from surgical trauma. A low-fiber, bland diet minimizes irritation to the intestinal lining, reducing the workload on the gut so it can heal efficiently.\n\nHigh-fiber foods leave a large amount of indigestible residue (bulk) in the digestive tract. Because the internal lumen of a new stoma is narrowed due to post-operative swelling, a high-fiber intake poses a strict risk of causing a painful mechanical bowel blockage.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: increase intestinal motility, prevent malabsorption, prevent mineral deficiencies"}'::jsonb),
('mc:diet therapy for chronic pancreatitis may include all of the following except::::a fat-restricted diet', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'diet therapy for chronic pancreatitis may include all of the following except:', 'a fat-restricted diet', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"International guidelines (such as ESPEN clinical guidelines) no longer recommend routine, strict dietary fat restriction for chronic pancreatitis. Because these patients suffer from severe malabsorption and weight loss, restricting fat overly constricts their caloric intake and worsens malnutrition. Instead, a normal, balanced physiological diet is prescribed in tandem with proper enzyme dosing. Fat restriction is only used as a temporary, short-term measure if painful fatty stools (steatorrhea) cannot be managed by medications.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"a fat-restricted diet\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"International guidelines (such as ESPEN clinical guidelines) no longer recommend routine, strict dietary fat restriction for chronic pancreatitis. Because these patients suffer from severe malabsorption and weight loss, restricting fat overly constricts their caloric intake and worsens malnutrition. Instead, a normal, balanced physiological diet is prescribed in tandem with proper enzyme dosing. Fat restriction is only used as a temporary, short-term measure if painful fatty stools (steatorrhea) cannot be managed by medications.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: enzyme replacements, small meals, a diet to control blood glucose"}'::jsonb),
('mc:flatulence, cramps, or diabetes may be some unpleasant side effects upon the initiation of this diet::::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'flatulence, cramps, or diabetes may be some unpleasant side effects upon the initiation of this diet:', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"When a person suddenly initiates a high-fiber diet, the digestive tract requires a transition period to adapt. The human body lacks the enzymes to digest dietary fiber, so it passes intact into the large intestine. There, resident gut bacteria ferment the fiber. A sudden influx of fiber causes rapid, aggressive bacterial fermentation, which produces large amounts of gas. This trapped gas directly results in flatulence, abdominal bloating, and painful cramps.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"high-fiber diet\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"When a person suddenly initiates a high-fiber diet, the digestive tract requires a transition period to adapt. The human body lacks the enzymes to digest dietary fiber, so it passes intact into the large intestine. There, resident gut bacteria ferment the fiber. A sudden influx of fiber causes rapid, aggressive bacterial fermentation, which produces large amounts of gas. This trapped gas directly results in flatulence, abdominal bloating, and painful cramps.\n\nWhile fiber does not cause diabetes, a high-fiber diet is a primary clinical intervention utilized for the management of Diabetes Mellitus. Soluble fiber creates a gel-like substance in the gut that slows down the absorption of glucose, preventing rapid post-meal blood sugar spikes and improving insulin sensitivity.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: fiber-restricted diet, soft diet, elemental diet"}'::jsonb),
('mc:diet therapy for chronic pancreatitis may include all of the following except::::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'diet therapy for chronic pancreatitis may include all of the following except:', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"International guidelines (such as ESPEN clinical guidelines) no longer recommend routine, strict dietary fat restriction for chronic pancreatitis. Because these patients suffer from severe malabsorption and weight loss, restricting fat overly constricts their caloric intake and worsens malnutrition. Instead, a normal, balanced physiological diet is prescribed in tandem with proper enzyme dosing. Fat restriction is only used as a temporary, short-term measure if painful fatty stools (steatorrhea) cannot be managed by medications.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"a fat-restricted diet\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"International guidelines (such as ESPEN clinical guidelines) no longer recommend routine, strict dietary fat restriction for chronic pancreatitis. Because these patients suffer from severe malabsorption and weight loss, restricting fat overly constricts their caloric intake and worsens malnutrition. Instead, a normal, balanced physiological diet is prescribed in tandem with proper enzyme dosing. Fat restriction is only used as a temporary, short-term measure if painful fatty stools (steatorrhea) cannot be managed by medications.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: enzyme replacements, small meals, a diet to control blood glucose"}'::jsonb),
('mc:the following may affect the nutrient needs of people with inflammatory bowel disease except::::dumping syndrome', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'the following may affect the nutrient needs of people with inflammatory bowel disease except:', 'dumping syndrome', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Dumping syndrome is a complication that occurs after gastric surgeries (like a partial or total gastrectomy) where food moves too rapidly from the stomach into the small intestine. It does not directly result from or impact the nutrient needs of Inflammatory Bowel Disease (IBD).","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"dumping syndrome\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Dumping syndrome is a complication that occurs after gastric surgeries (like a partial or total gastrectomy) where food moves too rapidly from the stomach into the small intestine. It does not directly result from or impact the nutrient needs of Inflammatory Bowel Disease (IBD).\n\n\t• Medications: Common IBD drugs like corticosteroids lower calcium and vitamin D absorption, while others like sulfasalazine impair folate absorption, altering daily nutrient requirements.\n\t• Steatorrhea: Excessive fat in the stool (common in Crohn''s disease due to bile acid malabsorption) causes a severe loss of fat-soluble vitamins (A, D, E, and K) and calories, increasing nutritional requirements.\n\t• Fistulas: These abnormal tissue tunnels can leak digestive fluids, causing high-volume losses of protein, electrolytes, and fluids, which drastically spike a patient''s nutrient needs.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: steatorrhea, medications, fistulas"}'::jsonb)
ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO public.ai_explanation_cache (id, question_hash, front_text, back_text, options, explanation, breakdown, created_at, question_text, user_answer, is_correct, ai_explanation) VALUES
('mc:the following may affect the nutrient needs of people with inflammatory bowel disease except::::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'the following may affect the nutrient needs of people with inflammatory bowel disease except:', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Dumping syndrome is a complication that occurs after gastric surgeries (like a partial or total gastrectomy) where food moves too rapidly from the stomach into the small intestine. It does not directly result from or impact the nutrient needs of Inflammatory Bowel Disease (IBD).","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"dumping syndrome\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Dumping syndrome is a complication that occurs after gastric surgeries (like a partial or total gastrectomy) where food moves too rapidly from the stomach into the small intestine. It does not directly result from or impact the nutrient needs of Inflammatory Bowel Disease (IBD).\n\n\t• Medications: Common IBD drugs like corticosteroids lower calcium and vitamin D absorption, while others like sulfasalazine impair folate absorption, altering daily nutrient requirements.\n\t• Steatorrhea: Excessive fat in the stool (common in Crohn''s disease due to bile acid malabsorption) causes a severe loss of fat-soluble vitamins (A, D, E, and K) and calories, increasing nutritional requirements.\n\t• Fistulas: These abnormal tissue tunnels can leak digestive fluids, causing high-volume losses of protein, electrolytes, and fluids, which drastically spike a patient''s nutrient needs.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: steatorrhea, medications, fistulas"}'::jsonb),
('mc:mr. santos has lung cancer which has metastasized to his spine. based on this information, it will be essential for the nutritionist-dietitian to monitor him for::::hypercalcemia', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'mr. santos has lung cancer which has metastasized to his spine. based on this information, it will be essential for the nutritionist-dietitian to monitor him for:', 'hypercalcemia', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"When a primary tumor (such as lung cancer) metastasizes to the bone/spine, it initiates osteolytic bone destruction. The invading tumor cells stimulate specialized bone cells (osteoclasts) to break down the surrounding bone tissue.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"hypercalcemia\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"When a primary tumor (such as lung cancer) metastasizes to the bone/spine, it initiates osteolytic bone destruction. The invading tumor cells stimulate specialized bone cells (osteoclasts) to break down the surrounding bone tissue.\n\nAs the bone structure is aggressively broken down and reabsorbed, massive amounts of calcium are dissolved and released directly into the bloodstream. This rapid influx overwhelms the kidneys'' ability to excrete it, leading to a dangerous oncologic emergency known as Hypercalcemia of Malignancy (HCM).\n\nMonitoring calcium levels is critical because hypercalcemia can lead to severe confusion, muscle weakness, kidney stones, and lethal cardiac arrhythmias. While dietary calcium restriction is generally not the primary treatment for HCM (as the excess calcium is coming from the bones, not food), the dietitian must aggressively monitor hydration status, fluid balance, and electrolyte trends to support clinical management.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: hypokalemia, hyponatremia, hyperchloremia"}'::jsonb),
('mc:mr. santos has lung cancer which has metastasized to his spine. based on this information, it will be essential for the nutritionist-dietitian to monitor him for::::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'mr. santos has lung cancer which has metastasized to his spine. based on this information, it will be essential for the nutritionist-dietitian to monitor him for:', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"When a primary tumor (such as lung cancer) metastasizes to the bone/spine, it initiates osteolytic bone destruction. The invading tumor cells stimulate specialized bone cells (osteoclasts) to break down the surrounding bone tissue.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"hypercalcemia\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"When a primary tumor (such as lung cancer) metastasizes to the bone/spine, it initiates osteolytic bone destruction. The invading tumor cells stimulate specialized bone cells (osteoclasts) to break down the surrounding bone tissue.\n\nAs the bone structure is aggressively broken down and reabsorbed, massive amounts of calcium are dissolved and released directly into the bloodstream. This rapid influx overwhelms the kidneys'' ability to excrete it, leading to a dangerous oncologic emergency known as Hypercalcemia of Malignancy (HCM).\n\nMonitoring calcium levels is critical because hypercalcemia can lead to severe confusion, muscle weakness, kidney stones, and lethal cardiac arrhythmias. While dietary calcium restriction is generally not the primary treatment for HCM (as the excess calcium is coming from the bones, not food), the dietitian must aggressively monitor hydration status, fluid balance, and electrolyte trends to support clinical management.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: hypokalemia, hyponatremia, hyperchloremia"}'::jsonb),
('mc:diet order for irritable bowel syndrome::::all of the above', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'diet order for irritable bowel syndrome:', 'all of the above', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Irritable Bowel Syndrome (IBS) is a functional gastrointestinal disorder where dietary management focuses on controlling symptoms like cramping, bloating, gas, diarrhea, and constipation. Standard clinical diet orders traditionally incorporate all three modifications:","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"all of the above\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Irritable Bowel Syndrome (IBS) is a functional gastrointestinal disorder where dietary management focuses on controlling symptoms like cramping, bloating, gas, diarrhea, and constipation. Standard clinical diet orders traditionally incorporate all three modifications:\n\n• High-fiber: Gradually increasing dietary fiber (specifically soluble fiber like psyllium, oats, and flaxseeds) is a primary recommendation. It adds healthy bulk to stools to relieve constipation (IBS-C) while simultaneously helping to solidify loose stools in diarrhea-predominant IBS (IBS-D).\n• Avoid highly seasoned foods: Spicy and heavily seasoned foods contain compounds (like capsaicin) that irritate the gut lining and accelerate GI transit. Eliminating them helps reduce abdominal pain, cramping, and sudden bowel urgency.\n• Fat-restricted: High-fat, greasy, and deep-fried foods trigger an exaggerated gastrocolic reflex, which causes rapid, painful colonic contractions. Restricting dietary fat intake stabilizes gut motility and prevents immediate post-meal cramping and diarrhea.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: avoid highly seasoned foods, high-fiber, fat-restricted"}'::jsonb),
('mc:diet order for irritable bowel syndrome::::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'diet order for irritable bowel syndrome:', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Irritable Bowel Syndrome (IBS) is a functional gastrointestinal disorder where dietary management focuses on controlling symptoms like cramping, bloating, gas, diarrhea, and constipation. Standard clinical diet orders traditionally incorporate all three modifications:","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"all of the above\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Irritable Bowel Syndrome (IBS) is a functional gastrointestinal disorder where dietary management focuses on controlling symptoms like cramping, bloating, gas, diarrhea, and constipation. Standard clinical diet orders traditionally incorporate all three modifications:\n\n• High-fiber: Gradually increasing dietary fiber (specifically soluble fiber like psyllium, oats, and flaxseeds) is a primary recommendation. It adds healthy bulk to stools to relieve constipation (IBS-C) while simultaneously helping to solidify loose stools in diarrhea-predominant IBS (IBS-D).\n• Avoid highly seasoned foods: Spicy and heavily seasoned foods contain compounds (like capsaicin) that irritate the gut lining and accelerate GI transit. Eliminating them helps reduce abdominal pain, cramping, and sudden bowel urgency.\n• Fat-restricted: High-fat, greasy, and deep-fried foods trigger an exaggerated gastrocolic reflex, which causes rapid, painful colonic contractions. Restricting dietary fat intake stabilizes gut motility and prevents immediate post-meal cramping and diarrhea.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: avoid highly seasoned foods, high-fiber, fat-restricted"}'::jsonb),
('mc:a person who has done well for 3 years following a gastrectomy should be alert to signs of::::vitamin b₁₂ deficiency', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'a person who has done well for 3 years following a gastrectomy should be alert to signs of:', 'vitamin B₁₂ deficiency', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"The human liver stores a substantial reserve of Vitamin B₁₂ (cobalamin), which typically lasts 3 to 5 years. Once these internal stores are fully exhausted, symptoms of deficiency begin to appear.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"vitamin B₁₂ deficiency\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"The human liver stores a substantial reserve of Vitamin B₁₂ (cobalamin), which typically lasts 3 to 5 years. Once these internal stores are fully exhausted, symptoms of deficiency begin to appear.\n\nA gastrectomy removes the parietal cells of the stomach, which are responsible for secreting intrinsic factor. Intrinsic factor is a protein absolutely required for Vitamin B₁₂ absorption in the ileum. Without it, the body cannot absorb dietary Vitamin B₁₂, eventually causing macrocytic/pernicious anemia and neurological issues.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: iron deficiency anemia, blind loop syndrome, dumping syndrome"}'::jsonb),
('mc:how much sodium is 3 grams of salt?:::1,200', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'how much sodium is 3 grams of salt?', '1,200', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Table salt (sodium chloride) consists of approximately 40% sodium and 60% chloride.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"1,200\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Table salt (sodium chloride) consists of approximately 40% sodium and 60% chloride.\n\nTo find the amount of sodium in 3 grams of salt:\n• Convert 3 grams of salt to milligrams: 3 grams = 3,000 milligrams.\n• Calculate the sodium portion: 3,000 milligrams multiplied by 0.40 = 1,200 milligrams of sodium.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: 1,400, 1,600, 1,800"}'::jsonb),
('mc:a person who has done well for 3 years following a gastrectomy should be alert to signs of::::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'a person who has done well for 3 years following a gastrectomy should be alert to signs of:', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"The human liver stores a substantial reserve of Vitamin B₁₂ (cobalamin), which typically lasts 3 to 5 years. Once these internal stores are fully exhausted, symptoms of deficiency begin to appear.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"vitamin B₁₂ deficiency\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"The human liver stores a substantial reserve of Vitamin B₁₂ (cobalamin), which typically lasts 3 to 5 years. Once these internal stores are fully exhausted, symptoms of deficiency begin to appear.\n\nA gastrectomy removes the parietal cells of the stomach, which are responsible for secreting intrinsic factor. Intrinsic factor is a protein absolutely required for Vitamin B₁₂ absorption in the ileum. Without it, the body cannot absorb dietary Vitamin B₁₂, eventually causing macrocytic/pernicious anemia and neurological issues.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: iron deficiency anemia, blind loop syndrome, dumping syndrome"}'::jsonb),
('mc:dietary recommendation for clients with cystic fibrosis except::::limit the use of table salt', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'dietary recommendation for clients with cystic fibrosis except:', 'limit the use of table salt', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Cystic fibrosis (CF) is caused by a genetic mutation that disrupts the CFTR protein, preventing cells from properly regulating chloride and sodium. As a result, individuals with CF lose massive amounts of salt through their sweat pores (often referred to as having \"salty skin\"). To prevent hyponatremia (dangerously low blood sodium), dehydration, and heat exhaustion, clients with CF are actively encouraged to liberalize their use of table salt and consume salty snacks. Limiting salt is contraindicated.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"limit the use of table salt\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Cystic fibrosis (CF) is caused by a genetic mutation that disrupts the CFTR protein, preventing cells from properly regulating chloride and sodium. As a result, individuals with CF lose massive amounts of salt through their sweat pores (often referred to as having \"salty skin\"). To prevent hyponatremia (dangerously low blood sodium), dehydration, and heat exhaustion, clients with CF are actively encouraged to liberalize their use of table salt and consume salty snacks. Limiting salt is contraindicated.\n\nHistorically, old test bank items sometimes listed fat restriction as an outdated management strategy because patients cannot digest fats well without enzymes. However, in modern clinical nutrition practice—combined with Pancreatic Enzyme Replacement Therapy (PERT)—a high-fat diet is heavily promoted alongside high calorie and high protein to help meet their high energy demands.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: high calorie, high protein, fat restrictions"}'::jsonb),
('mc:dietary recommendation for clients with cystic fibrosis except::::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'dietary recommendation for clients with cystic fibrosis except:', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Cystic fibrosis (CF) is caused by a genetic mutation that disrupts the CFTR protein, preventing cells from properly regulating chloride and sodium. As a result, individuals with CF lose massive amounts of salt through their sweat pores (often referred to as having \"salty skin\"). To prevent hyponatremia (dangerously low blood sodium), dehydration, and heat exhaustion, clients with CF are actively encouraged to liberalize their use of table salt and consume salty snacks. Limiting salt is contraindicated.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"limit the use of table salt\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Cystic fibrosis (CF) is caused by a genetic mutation that disrupts the CFTR protein, preventing cells from properly regulating chloride and sodium. As a result, individuals with CF lose massive amounts of salt through their sweat pores (often referred to as having \"salty skin\"). To prevent hyponatremia (dangerously low blood sodium), dehydration, and heat exhaustion, clients with CF are actively encouraged to liberalize their use of table salt and consume salty snacks. Limiting salt is contraindicated.\n\nHistorically, old test bank items sometimes listed fat restriction as an outdated management strategy because patients cannot digest fats well without enzymes. However, in modern clinical nutrition practice—combined with Pancreatic Enzyme Replacement Therapy (PERT)—a high-fat diet is heavily promoted alongside high calorie and high protein to help meet their high energy demands.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: high calorie, high protein, fat restrictions"}'::jsonb),
('mc:risk assessment chd includes measurement of blood lipids such as::::all of the above', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'risk assessment chd includes measurement of blood lipids such as:', 'all of the above', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"A comprehensive risk assessment for Coronary Heart Disease (CHD) relies on a full fasting lipid profile. Each of the lipid fractions listed in the other options provides distinct, critical diagnostic data regarding a patient''s cardiovascular health profile:","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"all of the above\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"A comprehensive risk assessment for Coronary Heart Disease (CHD) relies on a full fasting lipid profile. Each of the lipid fractions listed in the other options provides distinct, critical diagnostic data regarding a patient''s cardiovascular health profile:\n\n• Total Cholesterol (TC): Reflects the overall amount of cholesterol circulating in the bloodstream and is directly linked to blood vessel plaque buildup and disease risk.\n• Triglycerides (TG): High levels of these blood fats serve as an independent risk factor for atherosclerosis and metabolic syndrome, often elevated by a high-fat diet, refined sugars, or alcohol.\n• HDL-Cholesterol (HDL-C): Known as the \"good cholesterol,\" it plays a protective role by removing excess cholesterol from peripheral tissues and transporting it back to the liver. High levels actively reduce CHD risk.\n• LDL-Cholesterol (LDL-C): Designated as the \"bad cholesterol\" and the primary target of clinical lipid-lowering therapies, elevated circulating LDL leads directly to fatty accumulation and narrowing inside the coronary arteries.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: total cholesterol, TG, LDL-cholesterol, total cholesterol, HDL cholesterol, TG"}'::jsonb),
('mc:risk assessment chd includes measurement of blood lipids such as::::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'risk assessment chd includes measurement of blood lipids such as:', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"A comprehensive risk assessment for Coronary Heart Disease (CHD) relies on a full fasting lipid profile. Each of the lipid fractions listed in the other options provides distinct, critical diagnostic data regarding a patient''s cardiovascular health profile:","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"all of the above\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"A comprehensive risk assessment for Coronary Heart Disease (CHD) relies on a full fasting lipid profile. Each of the lipid fractions listed in the other options provides distinct, critical diagnostic data regarding a patient''s cardiovascular health profile:\n\n• Total Cholesterol (TC): Reflects the overall amount of cholesterol circulating in the bloodstream and is directly linked to blood vessel plaque buildup and disease risk.\n• Triglycerides (TG): High levels of these blood fats serve as an independent risk factor for atherosclerosis and metabolic syndrome, often elevated by a high-fat diet, refined sugars, or alcohol.\n• HDL-Cholesterol (HDL-C): Known as the \"good cholesterol,\" it plays a protective role by removing excess cholesterol from peripheral tissues and transporting it back to the liver. High levels actively reduce CHD risk.\n• LDL-Cholesterol (LDL-C): Designated as the \"bad cholesterol\" and the primary target of clinical lipid-lowering therapies, elevated circulating LDL leads directly to fatty accumulation and narrowing inside the coronary arteries.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: total cholesterol, TG, LDL-cholesterol, total cholesterol, HDL cholesterol, TG"}'::jsonb),
('mc:chronic diarrhea may be accompanied by a number of nutritional deficiencies due to losses of::::all of the above', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'chronic diarrhea may be accompanied by a number of nutritional deficiencies due to losses of:', 'all of the above', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Chronic diarrhea severely impairs the small and large intestines'' ability to absorb nutrients due to rapid transit time and mucosal damage. This leads to widespread malabsorption and excessive losses of:","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"all of the above\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Chronic diarrhea severely impairs the small and large intestines'' ability to absorb nutrients due to rapid transit time and mucosal damage. This leads to widespread malabsorption and excessive losses of:\n\n• Na & K: Sodium and potassium are critical electrolytes lost in high volumes through watery stools, leading to dehydration and electrolyte imbalances.\n• Vitamins & minerals: Fat-soluble vitamins (A, D, E, K), water-soluble vitamins (like B-complex), and minerals (such as iron, zinc, and magnesium) are rapidly depleted.\n• Protein: Protein-losing enteropathy often occurs during chronic diarrhea, where plasma proteins leak directly into the gut lumen and are excreted, leading to tissue wasting and hypoalbuminemia.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Na & K, vitamins & minerals, protein"}'::jsonb),
('mc:chronic diarrhea may be accompanied by a number of nutritional deficiencies due to losses of::::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'chronic diarrhea may be accompanied by a number of nutritional deficiencies due to losses of:', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Chronic diarrhea severely impairs the small and large intestines'' ability to absorb nutrients due to rapid transit time and mucosal damage. This leads to widespread malabsorption and excessive losses of:","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"all of the above\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Chronic diarrhea severely impairs the small and large intestines'' ability to absorb nutrients due to rapid transit time and mucosal damage. This leads to widespread malabsorption and excessive losses of:\n\n• Na & K: Sodium and potassium are critical electrolytes lost in high volumes through watery stools, leading to dehydration and electrolyte imbalances.\n• Vitamins & minerals: Fat-soluble vitamins (A, D, E, K), water-soluble vitamins (like B-complex), and minerals (such as iron, zinc, and magnesium) are rapidly depleted.\n• Protein: Protein-losing enteropathy often occurs during chronic diarrhea, where plasma proteins leak directly into the gut lumen and are excreted, leading to tissue wasting and hypoalbuminemia.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Na & K, vitamins & minerals, protein"}'::jsonb),
('mc:this type of supplement has shown good results in reducing encephalopathy and in improving nitrogen and energy balance::::branched-chain amino acids supplements', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'this type of supplement has shown good results in reducing encephalopathy and in improving nitrogen and energy balance:', 'branched-chain amino acids supplements', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Patients with advanced liver disease or cirrhosis typically present with an altered amino acid profile. Their plasma levels of Branched-Chain Amino Acids (BCAAs: leucine, isoleucine, and valine) drop significantly, while levels of Aromatic Amino Acids (AAAs: phenylalanine, tyrosine, and tryptophan) rise. This imbalance allows excessive AAAs to cross the blood-brain barrier, altering neurotransmission and inducing hepatic encephalopathy.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"branched-chain amino acids supplements\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Patients with advanced liver disease or cirrhosis typically present with an altered amino acid profile. Their plasma levels of Branched-Chain Amino Acids (BCAAs: leucine, isoleucine, and valine) drop significantly, while levels of Aromatic Amino Acids (AAAs: phenylalanine, tyrosine, and tryptophan) rise. This imbalance allows excessive AAAs to cross the blood-brain barrier, altering neurotransmission and inducing hepatic encephalopathy.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: vegetable ad casein-based protein supplements, aromatic amino acids supplements, phenylalanine supplements"}'::jsonb),
('mc:this type of supplement has shown good results in reducing encephalopathy and in improving nitrogen and energy balance::::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'this type of supplement has shown good results in reducing encephalopathy and in improving nitrogen and energy balance:', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Patients with advanced liver disease or cirrhosis typically present with an altered amino acid profile. Their plasma levels of Branched-Chain Amino Acids (BCAAs: leucine, isoleucine, and valine) drop significantly, while levels of Aromatic Amino Acids (AAAs: phenylalanine, tyrosine, and tryptophan) rise. This imbalance allows excessive AAAs to cross the blood-brain barrier, altering neurotransmission and inducing hepatic encephalopathy.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"branched-chain amino acids supplements\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Patients with advanced liver disease or cirrhosis typically present with an altered amino acid profile. Their plasma levels of Branched-Chain Amino Acids (BCAAs: leucine, isoleucine, and valine) drop significantly, while levels of Aromatic Amino Acids (AAAs: phenylalanine, tyrosine, and tryptophan) rise. This imbalance allows excessive AAAs to cross the blood-brain barrier, altering neurotransmission and inducing hepatic encephalopathy.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: vegetable ad casein-based protein supplements, aromatic amino acids supplements, phenylalanine supplements"}'::jsonb),
('mc:the following are recommendations to address obesity during adolescence except::::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'the following are recommendations to address obesity during adolescence except:', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Psychotherapy - this recommendation is for other professional.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Psychotherapy\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Psychotherapy - this recommendation is for other professional.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Increased physical activity and exercise, Dietary change, Food use retraining"}'::jsonb),
('mc:using the quickest method for weight calculation, determine the weight for a small frame male at 5’10”. hamwi method:::150', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'using the quickest method for weight calculation, determine the weight for a small frame male at 5’10”. hamwi method', '150', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Step-by-Step Calculation:","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"150\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Step-by-Step Calculation:\n\n1. Calculate Ideal Body Weight (IBW) for a medium frame:\nThe standard Hamwi method rule for males allocates 106 lbs for the first 5 feet (60 inches) of height, plus 6 lbs for every additional inch.\n\t• Height: 5''10\" (which is 10 inches over 5 feet)\n\t• Base weight: 106 lbs\n\t• Additional inches: 10 inches × 6 lbs/inch = 60 lbs\n\t• Medium Frame Weight: 106 lbs + 60 lbs = 166 lbs\n\n2. Adjust for a small frame size:\nThe Hamwi method dictates that you must subtract 10% from the medium frame total if the individual has a small frame.\n\t• 10% of 166 lbs = 16.6 lbs\n\t• Small Frame Weight: 166 lbs - 16.6 lbs = 149.4 lbs\n\nWhen rounded to the nearest multiple of ten to match the multiple-choice selection, 149.4 lbs aligns perfectly with 150 lbs.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: 170, 160, 140"}'::jsonb),
('mc:using the quickest method for weight calculation, determine the weight for a small frame male at 5’10”. hamwi method:::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'using the quickest method for weight calculation, determine the weight for a small frame male at 5’10”. hamwi method', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Step-by-Step Calculation:","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"150\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Step-by-Step Calculation:\n\n1. Calculate Ideal Body Weight (IBW) for a medium frame:\nThe standard Hamwi method rule for males allocates 106 lbs for the first 5 feet (60 inches) of height, plus 6 lbs for every additional inch.\n\t• Height: 5''10\" (which is 10 inches over 5 feet)\n\t• Base weight: 106 lbs\n\t• Additional inches: 10 inches × 6 lbs/inch = 60 lbs\n\t• Medium Frame Weight: 106 lbs + 60 lbs = 166 lbs\n\n2. Adjust for a small frame size:\nThe Hamwi method dictates that you must subtract 10% from the medium frame total if the individual has a small frame.\n\t• 10% of 166 lbs = 16.6 lbs\n\t• Small Frame Weight: 166 lbs - 16.6 lbs = 149.4 lbs\n\nWhen rounded to the nearest multiple of ten to match the multiple-choice selection, 149.4 lbs aligns perfectly with 150 lbs.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: 170, 160, 140"}'::jsonb),
('mc:maria consulted ms. uy, a registered nd for weight reduction. history of her weight gain is as follows: height = 5’5”; wrist circumference = 6”; actual weight = 180 lbs.; and dbw = 125 lbs. what is maria’s %dbw change?:::44%', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'maria consulted ms. uy, a registered nd for weight reduction. history of her weight gain is as follows: height = 5’5”; wrist circumference = 6”; actual weight = 180 lbs.; and dbw = 125 lbs. what is maria’s %dbw change?', '44%', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"To find a person''s percentage over or under their DBW (the percentage weight change/deviation), you use this formula:","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"44%\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"To find a person''s percentage over or under their DBW (the percentage weight change/deviation), you use this formula:\n• % DBW Deviation = ((Actual Weight - DBW) ÷ DBW) × 100\n• Calculation: ((180 lbs - 125 lbs) ÷ 125 lbs) × 100 = (55 ÷ 125) × 100 = 44%","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: 20%, 31%, 38%"}'::jsonb),
('mc:maria consulted ms. uy, a registered nd for weight reduction. history of her weight gain is as follows: height = 5’5”; wrist circumference = 6”; actual weight = 180 lbs.; and dbw = 125 lbs. what is maria’s %dbw change?:::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'maria consulted ms. uy, a registered nd for weight reduction. history of her weight gain is as follows: height = 5’5”; wrist circumference = 6”; actual weight = 180 lbs.; and dbw = 125 lbs. what is maria’s %dbw change?', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"To find a person''s percentage over or under their DBW (the percentage weight change/deviation), you use this formula:","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"44%\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"To find a person''s percentage over or under their DBW (the percentage weight change/deviation), you use this formula:\n• % DBW Deviation = ((Actual Weight - DBW) ÷ DBW) × 100\n• Calculation: ((180 lbs - 125 lbs) ÷ 125 lbs) × 100 = (55 ÷ 125) × 100 = 44%","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: 20%, 31%, 38%"}'::jsonb),
('mc:how many grams of protein will be contained in 1.5 liters of blenderized feeding formula with a caloric density of 2 kcal/ml, 12% of which are from protein?:::90 g', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'how many grams of protein will be contained in 1.5 liters of blenderized feeding formula with a caloric density of 2 kcal/ml, 12% of which are from protein?', '90 g', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Step-by-Step Calculation:","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"90 g\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Step-by-Step Calculation:\n\n1. Convert liters to milliliters:\n1.5 liters = 1500 ml\n\n2. Calculate the total calories in the formula:\nMultiply the total volume by the caloric density.\n1500 ml x 2 kcal/ml = 3000 kcal\n\n3. Calculate the calories provided by protein:\nMultiply the total calories by the protein percentage.\n3000 kcal x 0.12 = 360 kcal from protein\n\n4. Convert protein calories into grams:\nProtein provides 4 calories per gram (4 kcal/g). Divide the protein calories by 4.\n360 kcal / 4 kcal/g = 90 g","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: 100 g, 110 g, 120 g"}'::jsonb),
('mc:how many grams of protein will be contained in 1.5 liters of blenderized feeding formula with a caloric density of 2 kcal/ml, 12% of which are from protein?:::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'how many grams of protein will be contained in 1.5 liters of blenderized feeding formula with a caloric density of 2 kcal/ml, 12% of which are from protein?', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Step-by-Step Calculation:","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"90 g\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Step-by-Step Calculation:\n\n1. Convert liters to milliliters:\n1.5 liters = 1500 ml\n\n2. Calculate the total calories in the formula:\nMultiply the total volume by the caloric density.\n1500 ml x 2 kcal/ml = 3000 kcal\n\n3. Calculate the calories provided by protein:\nMultiply the total calories by the protein percentage.\n3000 kcal x 0.12 = 360 kcal from protein\n\n4. Convert protein calories into grams:\nProtein provides 4 calories per gram (4 kcal/g). Divide the protein calories by 4.\n360 kcal / 4 kcal/g = 90 g","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: 100 g, 110 g, 120 g"}'::jsonb),
('mc:during anaerobic glycolysis, there is a net production of lactate and .:::atp', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'during anaerobic glycolysis, there is a net production of lactate and .', 'ATP', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"During anaerobic glycolysis, a single molecule of glucose is broken down in the cytosol without using oxygen. This pathway results in the net production of two distinct products:","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"ATP\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"During anaerobic glycolysis, a single molecule of glucose is broken down in the cytosol without using oxygen. This pathway results in the net production of two distinct products:\n1. Lactate (2 molecules)\n2. ATP (a net yield of 2 molecules)\n\n• NADH: While NADH is temporarily produced halfway through glycolysis, the cell immediately uses up that NADH to convert pyruvate into lactate. This means there is zero net production of NADH by the end of the anaerobic process.\n• FADH₂: This electron carrier is only generated inside the mitochondria during the Krebs cycle (aerobic respiration), not during glycolysis.\n• NAD⁺: Because NADH is continuously recycled back into NAD⁺ to keep glycolysis running, NAD⁺ is constantly consumed and regenerated, meaning it does not experience a net increase.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: NAD+, NADH, FADH2"}'::jsonb),
('mc:during anaerobic glycolysis, there is a net production of lactate and .:::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'during anaerobic glycolysis, there is a net production of lactate and .', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"During anaerobic glycolysis, a single molecule of glucose is broken down in the cytosol without using oxygen. This pathway results in the net production of two distinct products:","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"ATP\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"During anaerobic glycolysis, a single molecule of glucose is broken down in the cytosol without using oxygen. This pathway results in the net production of two distinct products:\n1. Lactate (2 molecules)\n2. ATP (a net yield of 2 molecules)\n\n• NADH: While NADH is temporarily produced halfway through glycolysis, the cell immediately uses up that NADH to convert pyruvate into lactate. This means there is zero net production of NADH by the end of the anaerobic process.\n• FADH₂: This electron carrier is only generated inside the mitochondria during the Krebs cycle (aerobic respiration), not during glycolysis.\n• NAD⁺: Because NADH is continuously recycled back into NAD⁺ to keep glycolysis running, NAD⁺ is constantly consumed and regenerated, meaning it does not experience a net increase.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: NAD+, NADH, FADH2"}'::jsonb),
('mc:it competes with calcium for absorption::::mg', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'it competes with calcium for absorption:', 'Mg', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Magnesium (Mg) and calcium (Ca) share a common transport and homeostatic regulatory system within the gastrointestinal tract. When consumed in large or highly concentrated supplemental doses, magnesium and calcium directly compete with each other for absorption pathways across the small intestinal mucosa.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Mg\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Magnesium (Mg) and calcium (Ca) share a common transport and homeostatic regulatory system within the gastrointestinal tract. When consumed in large or highly concentrated supplemental doses, magnesium and calcium directly compete with each other for absorption pathways across the small intestinal mucosa.\n\n• Sodium & Potassium: These monovalent extracellular and intracellular cations are absorbed through distinct, independent active transport and electrochemical channels that do not interfere with or compete against divalent minerals like calcium.\n• Phosphorus: While phosphorus (P) interacts with calcium dynamically, it does not directly compete for the same absorption binding sites. Instead, an excessive amount of phosphorus binds chemically with calcium within the intestinal lumen to form an insoluble complex (calcium phosphate), which physically reduces the overall bioavailability of both minerals rather than competing for transport carriers.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: P, K, Na"}'::jsonb),
('mc:one glucose molecule will yield how many pyruvate products?:::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'one glucose molecule will yield how many pyruvate products?', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"During the first stage of cellular respiration (glycolysis), a single glucose molecule—which is a 6-carbon sugar (C₆H₁₂O₆)—is physically split and oxidized in the cytoplasm of the cell.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"2\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"During the first stage of cellular respiration (glycolysis), a single glucose molecule—which is a 6-carbon sugar (C₆H₁₂O₆)—is physically split and oxidized in the cytoplasm of the cell.\n\nThis biochemical breakdown yields exactly two molecules of pyruvate (also known as pyruvic acid), which are 3-carbon structures (C₃H₄O₃).","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: 1, 3, 4"}'::jsonb),
('mc:vitamins that help in cell division::::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'vitamins that help in cell division:', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Both folate (vitamin B9) and vitamin B12 (cobalamin) are essential coenzymes required for the synthesis of nucleic acids (DNA and RNA).","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"folate & B12\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Both folate (vitamin B9) and vitamin B12 (cobalamin) are essential coenzymes required for the synthesis of nucleic acids (DNA and RNA).\n\nBefore a cell can divide, it must perfectly replicate its entire DNA strands. Folate is a crucial building block for this replication process. Vitamin B12 acts as the mandatory key that unlocks folate, converting it into its active form inside the body.\n\nIf either vitamin is missing, cell division stalls. This is most visibly seen in fast-dividing tissues like bone marrow, where a lack of cell division leads to megaloblastic anemia (large, immature, undivided red blood cells).","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: folate & biotin, thiamin & riboflavin, niacin & B12"}'::jsonb),
('mc:it competes with calcium for absorption::::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'it competes with calcium for absorption:', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Magnesium (Mg) and calcium (Ca) share a common transport and homeostatic regulatory system within the gastrointestinal tract. When consumed in large or highly concentrated supplemental doses, magnesium and calcium directly compete with each other for absorption pathways across the small intestinal mucosa.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Mg\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Magnesium (Mg) and calcium (Ca) share a common transport and homeostatic regulatory system within the gastrointestinal tract. When consumed in large or highly concentrated supplemental doses, magnesium and calcium directly compete with each other for absorption pathways across the small intestinal mucosa.\n\n• Sodium & Potassium: These monovalent extracellular and intracellular cations are absorbed through distinct, independent active transport and electrochemical channels that do not interfere with or compete against divalent minerals like calcium.\n• Phosphorus: While phosphorus (P) interacts with calcium dynamically, it does not directly compete for the same absorption binding sites. Instead, an excessive amount of phosphorus binds chemically with calcium within the intestinal lumen to form an insoluble complex (calcium phosphate), which physically reduces the overall bioavailability of both minerals rather than competing for transport carriers.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: P, K, Na"}'::jsonb),
('mc:it protects folate from oxidative destruction::::vitamin c', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'it protects folate from oxidative destruction:', 'vitamin C', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Folate (Vitamin B9) is a highly unstable water-soluble molecule that is extremely susceptible to degradation via heat, UV light, and environmental oxygen. Vitamin C (ascorbic acid) acts as a highly effective water-soluble antioxidant and oxygen-scavenger.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"vitamin C\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Folate (Vitamin B9) is a highly unstable water-soluble molecule that is extremely susceptible to degradation via heat, UV light, and environmental oxygen. Vitamin C (ascorbic acid) acts as a highly effective water-soluble antioxidant and oxygen-scavenger.\n\nIn food matrixes, the digestive tract, and blood plasma, Vitamin C shields folate from breaking down by neutralizing free radicals and reducing the primary products of folate oxidation (such as quinonoid dihydrofolate) back into their active, usable forms. Because of this symbiotic protective relationship, a deficiency in Vitamin C often accelerates the depletion and urinary excretion of oxidized folate derivatives.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: vitamin A, vitamin B₂, vitamin E"}'::jsonb),
('mc:it protects folate from oxidative destruction::::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'it protects folate from oxidative destruction:', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Folate (Vitamin B9) is a highly unstable water-soluble molecule that is extremely susceptible to degradation via heat, UV light, and environmental oxygen. Vitamin C (ascorbic acid) acts as a highly effective water-soluble antioxidant and oxygen-scavenger.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"vitamin C\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Folate (Vitamin B9) is a highly unstable water-soluble molecule that is extremely susceptible to degradation via heat, UV light, and environmental oxygen. Vitamin C (ascorbic acid) acts as a highly effective water-soluble antioxidant and oxygen-scavenger.\n\nIn food matrixes, the digestive tract, and blood plasma, Vitamin C shields folate from breaking down by neutralizing free radicals and reducing the primary products of folate oxidation (such as quinonoid dihydrofolate) back into their active, usable forms. Because of this symbiotic protective relationship, a deficiency in Vitamin C often accelerates the depletion and urinary excretion of oxidized folate derivatives.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: vitamin A, vitamin B₂, vitamin E"}'::jsonb),
('mc:how many meq are found in a phosphorus diet limited to 1,000 mg per day?:::65', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'how many meq are found in a phosphorus diet limited to 1,000 mg per day?', '65', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Phosphorus primarily circulates in extracellular and intracellular fluids as phosphate ions, predominantly a mixture of divalent ($HPO_4^{2-}$) and monovalent ($H_2PO_4^{-}$) species at physiological blood pH (7.4) in a roughly 4:1 ratio. For clinical nutrition and renal calculations involving elemental phosphorus (atomic weight approximately 30.97 g/mol), an effective valence of roughly 1.8 to 2 is applied depending on the exact ionic distribution, or more commonly in standard dietetic board calculations, the conversion utilizes the atomic weight and an assumed average valence or direct stoichiometric conversion factor where 1 mmol of phosphorus equals roughly 31 mg, and milliequivalents are derived via the formula: mEq = (mg / atomic weight) * valence. At physiological pH, calculating with an average valence of ~2.0 for total phosphate yields (1000 mg / 30.97 mg/mmol) * 2 = ~64.5 mEq, which rounds directly to 65 mEq.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"65\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Phosphorus primarily circulates in extracellular and intracellular fluids as phosphate ions, predominantly a mixture of divalent ($HPO_4^{2-}$) and monovalent ($H_2PO_4^{-}$) species at physiological blood pH (7.4) in a roughly 4:1 ratio. For clinical nutrition and renal calculations involving elemental phosphorus (atomic weight approximately 30.97 g/mol), an effective valence of roughly 1.8 to 2 is applied depending on the exact ionic distribution, or more commonly in standard dietetic board calculations, the conversion utilizes the atomic weight and an assumed average valence or direct stoichiometric conversion factor where 1 mmol of phosphorus equals roughly 31 mg, and milliequivalents are derived via the formula: mEq = (mg / atomic weight) * valence. At physiological pH, calculating with an average valence of ~2.0 for total phosphate yields (1000 mg / 30.97 mg/mmol) * 2 = ~64.5 mEq, which rounds directly to 65 mEq.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: 14, 28, 32"}'::jsonb),
('mc:how many meq are found in a phosphorus diet limited to 1,000 mg per day?:::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'how many meq are found in a phosphorus diet limited to 1,000 mg per day?', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Phosphorus primarily circulates in extracellular and intracellular fluids as phosphate ions, predominantly a mixture of divalent ($HPO_4^{2-}$) and monovalent ($H_2PO_4^{-}$) species at physiological blood pH (7.4) in a roughly 4:1 ratio. For clinical nutrition and renal calculations involving elemental phosphorus (atomic weight approximately 30.97 g/mol), an effective valence of roughly 1.8 to 2 is applied depending on the exact ionic distribution, or more commonly in standard dietetic board calculations, the conversion utilizes the atomic weight and an assumed average valence or direct stoichiometric conversion factor where 1 mmol of phosphorus equals roughly 31 mg, and milliequivalents are derived via the formula: mEq = (mg / atomic weight) * valence. At physiological pH, calculating with an average valence of ~2.0 for total phosphate yields (1000 mg / 30.97 mg/mmol) * 2 = ~64.5 mEq, which rounds directly to 65 mEq.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"65\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Phosphorus primarily circulates in extracellular and intracellular fluids as phosphate ions, predominantly a mixture of divalent ($HPO_4^{2-}$) and monovalent ($H_2PO_4^{-}$) species at physiological blood pH (7.4) in a roughly 4:1 ratio. For clinical nutrition and renal calculations involving elemental phosphorus (atomic weight approximately 30.97 g/mol), an effective valence of roughly 1.8 to 2 is applied depending on the exact ionic distribution, or more commonly in standard dietetic board calculations, the conversion utilizes the atomic weight and an assumed average valence or direct stoichiometric conversion factor where 1 mmol of phosphorus equals roughly 31 mg, and milliequivalents are derived via the formula: mEq = (mg / atomic weight) * valence. At physiological pH, calculating with an average valence of ~2.0 for total phosphate yields (1000 mg / 30.97 mg/mmol) * 2 = ~64.5 mEq, which rounds directly to 65 mEq.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: 14, 28, 32"}'::jsonb),
('mc:tests for an elderly vegetarian patient indicate pernicious and megaloblastic anemia resulting from abnormally large “macrocytic” red blood cells that undergo rapid hemolysis. these results are most probably related to a vitamin deficiency in::::folacin and cobalamin', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'tests for an elderly vegetarian patient indicate pernicious and megaloblastic anemia resulting from abnormally large “macrocytic” red blood cells that undergo rapid hemolysis. these results are most probably related to a vitamin deficiency in:', 'folacin and cobalamin', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Megaloblastic anemia (abnormally large, macrocytic red blood cells) is caused by a defect in DNA synthesis during red blood cell development. This specific type of anemia is caused by a deficiency in either folacin (folic acid/vitamin B9) or cobalamin (vitamin B12).","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"folacin and cobalamin\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Megaloblastic anemia (abnormally large, macrocytic red blood cells) is caused by a defect in DNA synthesis during red blood cell development. This specific type of anemia is caused by a deficiency in either folacin (folic acid/vitamin B9) or cobalamin (vitamin B12).\n\nThe patient is an elderly vegetarian. Vitamin B12 (cobalamin) is found almost exclusively in animal-derived foods. Because of this, long-term vegetarians are at an exceptionally high risk of developing a cobalamin deficiency.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: ascorbic acid, riboflavin and niacin, pantothenic acid"}'::jsonb),
('mc:tests for an elderly vegetarian patient indicate pernicious and megaloblastic anemia resulting from abnormally large “macrocytic” red blood cells that undergo rapid hemolysis. these results are most probably related to a vitamin deficiency in::::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'tests for an elderly vegetarian patient indicate pernicious and megaloblastic anemia resulting from abnormally large “macrocytic” red blood cells that undergo rapid hemolysis. these results are most probably related to a vitamin deficiency in:', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Megaloblastic anemia (abnormally large, macrocytic red blood cells) is caused by a defect in DNA synthesis during red blood cell development. This specific type of anemia is caused by a deficiency in either folacin (folic acid/vitamin B9) or cobalamin (vitamin B12).","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"folacin and cobalamin\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Megaloblastic anemia (abnormally large, macrocytic red blood cells) is caused by a defect in DNA synthesis during red blood cell development. This specific type of anemia is caused by a deficiency in either folacin (folic acid/vitamin B9) or cobalamin (vitamin B12).\n\nThe patient is an elderly vegetarian. Vitamin B12 (cobalamin) is found almost exclusively in animal-derived foods. Because of this, long-term vegetarians are at an exceptionally high risk of developing a cobalamin deficiency.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: ascorbic acid, riboflavin and niacin, pantothenic acid"}'::jsonb),
('mc:in collagen synthesis, acts as a reducing agent to convert oxidized iron atom back to its reduced state.:::ascorbic acid', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'in collagen synthesis, acts as a reducing agent to convert oxidized iron atom back to its reduced state.', 'ascorbic acid', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"During collagen synthesis, two critical enzymes—prolyl hydroxylase and lysyl hydroxylase—are responsible for adding hydroxyl (-OH) groups to proline and lysine amino acids. This structural modification allows collagen strands to cross-link properly, forming a stable triple-helix network.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"ascorbic acid\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"During collagen synthesis, two critical enzymes—prolyl hydroxylase and lysyl hydroxylase—are responsible for adding hydroxyl (-OH) groups to proline and lysine amino acids. This structural modification allows collagen strands to cross-link properly, forming a stable triple-helix network.\n\nThese hydroxylase enzymes require iron in its active, reduced ferrous state (Fe²⁺) to operate. During the chemical reaction, the iron atom naturally becomes oxidized into its inactive ferric state (Fe³⁺). Ascorbic acid (Vitamin C) acts as the essential electron donor or reducing agent, transferring electrons to convert the inactive ferric iron back into its active ferrous state. Without enough vitamin C, the iron stays oxidized, collagen remains under-hydroxylated, and connective tissues weaken (resulting in scurvy).","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: pantothenic acid, pyridoxine, folic acid"}'::jsonb),
('mc:it refers to a vietnamese baguette sandwich.:::bahn mi', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'it refers to a vietnamese baguette sandwich.', 'Bahn mi', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Bánh mì is the Vietnamese baguette sandwich.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Bahn mi\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Bánh mì is the Vietnamese baguette sandwich.\n\nGỏi cuốn are fresh spring rolls, nước chấm is a dipping sauce, and phở is a noodle soup.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Goi cuon, Nuoc cham, Pho"}'::jsonb),
('mc:in collagen synthesis, acts as a reducing agent to convert oxidized iron atom back to its reduced state.:::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'in collagen synthesis, acts as a reducing agent to convert oxidized iron atom back to its reduced state.', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"During collagen synthesis, two critical enzymes—prolyl hydroxylase and lysyl hydroxylase—are responsible for adding hydroxyl (-OH) groups to proline and lysine amino acids. This structural modification allows collagen strands to cross-link properly, forming a stable triple-helix network.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"ascorbic acid\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"During collagen synthesis, two critical enzymes—prolyl hydroxylase and lysyl hydroxylase—are responsible for adding hydroxyl (-OH) groups to proline and lysine amino acids. This structural modification allows collagen strands to cross-link properly, forming a stable triple-helix network.\n\nThese hydroxylase enzymes require iron in its active, reduced ferrous state (Fe²⁺) to operate. During the chemical reaction, the iron atom naturally becomes oxidized into its inactive ferric state (Fe³⁺). Ascorbic acid (Vitamin C) acts as the essential electron donor or reducing agent, transferring electrons to convert the inactive ferric iron back into its active ferrous state. Without enough vitamin C, the iron stays oxidized, collagen remains under-hydroxylated, and connective tissues weaken (resulting in scurvy).","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: pantothenic acid, pyridoxine, folic acid"}'::jsonb),
('mc:the molecule most directly linked to the initiation of atherosclerotic plaques is::::oxidized cholesterol', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'the molecule most directly linked to the initiation of atherosclerotic plaques is:', 'oxidized cholesterol', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"The absolute initial step in the formation of an atherosclerotic plaque begins when LDL cholesterol particles slip into the subendothelial space of an artery wall and become oxidized by free radicals.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"oxidized cholesterol\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"The absolute initial step in the formation of an atherosclerotic plaque begins when LDL cholesterol particles slip into the subendothelial space of an artery wall and become oxidized by free radicals.\n\nThis oxidized cholesterol is highly toxic to the blood vessel lining. It damages the endothelial cells, acting as the primary chemical trigger that forces the artery wall to express adhesion molecules. This signals monocytes (white blood cells) to enter the wall, consume the oxidized lipids, transform into \"foam cells,\" and start building the plaque.\n\n• Tumor necrosis factor (TNF): This is a pro-inflammatory cytokine that amplifies the immune response and accelerates plaque growth later on, but it is not the original molecule that initiates the process.\n• Thromboxane A₂: A lipid mediator released by activated platelets to cause vasoconstriction and blood clotting (thrombosis), typically playing a major role when a plaque ruptures later in the disease.\n• Platelet-derived growth factor (PDGF): A growth factor that stimulates the migration and multiplication of smooth muscle cells to form a fibrous cap over the lipid core during the progression phase.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: thromoxane A2, tumor necrosis factor, platelet derived growth factor"}'::jsonb),
('mc:the molecule most directly linked to the initiation of atherosclerotic plaques is::::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'the molecule most directly linked to the initiation of atherosclerotic plaques is:', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"The absolute initial step in the formation of an atherosclerotic plaque begins when LDL cholesterol particles slip into the subendothelial space of an artery wall and become oxidized by free radicals.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"oxidized cholesterol\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"The absolute initial step in the formation of an atherosclerotic plaque begins when LDL cholesterol particles slip into the subendothelial space of an artery wall and become oxidized by free radicals.\n\nThis oxidized cholesterol is highly toxic to the blood vessel lining. It damages the endothelial cells, acting as the primary chemical trigger that forces the artery wall to express adhesion molecules. This signals monocytes (white blood cells) to enter the wall, consume the oxidized lipids, transform into \"foam cells,\" and start building the plaque.\n\n• Tumor necrosis factor (TNF): This is a pro-inflammatory cytokine that amplifies the immune response and accelerates plaque growth later on, but it is not the original molecule that initiates the process.\n• Thromboxane A₂: A lipid mediator released by activated platelets to cause vasoconstriction and blood clotting (thrombosis), typically playing a major role when a plaque ruptures later in the disease.\n• Platelet-derived growth factor (PDGF): A growth factor that stimulates the migration and multiplication of smooth muscle cells to form a fibrous cap over the lipid core during the progression phase.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: thromoxane A2, tumor necrosis factor, platelet derived growth factor"}'::jsonb),
('mc:dr. cruz ordered a 500 mg sodium diet for nena lim. how much salt contains 500 mg of sodium?:::1,250 mg', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'dr. cruz ordered a 500 mg sodium diet for nena lim. how much salt contains 500 mg of sodium?', '1,250 mg', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"In nutrition and dietetics calculations, standard table salt (sodium chloride, NaCl) is approximated to be 40% sodium and 60% chloride by weight.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"1,250 mg\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"In nutrition and dietetics calculations, standard table salt (sodium chloride, NaCl) is approximated to be 40% sodium and 60% chloride by weight.\n\nTo convert a specific milligram amount of sodium into the equivalent amount of table salt, you divide the sodium milligrams by 0.40 (or simply multiply by 2.5):\n\nSalt (mg) = Sodium (mg) x 2.5\nSalt (mg) = 500 mg x 2.5 = 1,250 mg\n\nTherefore, 1,250 mg of table salt contains exactly 500 mg of elemental sodium.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: 800 mg, 2,000 mg, 200 mg"}'::jsonb),
('mc:dr. cruz ordered a 500 mg sodium diet for nena lim. how much salt contains 500 mg of sodium?:::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'dr. cruz ordered a 500 mg sodium diet for nena lim. how much salt contains 500 mg of sodium?', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"In nutrition and dietetics calculations, standard table salt (sodium chloride, NaCl) is approximated to be 40% sodium and 60% chloride by weight.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"1,250 mg\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"In nutrition and dietetics calculations, standard table salt (sodium chloride, NaCl) is approximated to be 40% sodium and 60% chloride by weight.\n\nTo convert a specific milligram amount of sodium into the equivalent amount of table salt, you divide the sodium milligrams by 0.40 (or simply multiply by 2.5):\n\nSalt (mg) = Sodium (mg) x 2.5\nSalt (mg) = 500 mg x 2.5 = 1,250 mg\n\nTherefore, 1,250 mg of table salt contains exactly 500 mg of elemental sodium.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: 800 mg, 2,000 mg, 200 mg"}'::jsonb),
('mc:vitamins that help in cell division::::folate & b12', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'vitamins that help in cell division:', 'folate & B12', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Both folate (vitamin B9) and vitamin B12 (cobalamin) are essential coenzymes required for the synthesis of nucleic acids (DNA and RNA).","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"folate & B12\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Both folate (vitamin B9) and vitamin B12 (cobalamin) are essential coenzymes required for the synthesis of nucleic acids (DNA and RNA).\n\nBefore a cell can divide, it must perfectly replicate its entire DNA strands. Folate is a crucial building block for this replication process. Vitamin B12 acts as the mandatory key that unlocks folate, converting it into its active form inside the body.\n\nIf either vitamin is missing, cell division stalls. This is most visibly seen in fast-dividing tissues like bone marrow, where a lack of cell division leads to megaloblastic anemia (large, immature, undivided red blood cells).","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: folate & biotin, thiamin & riboflavin, niacin & B12"}'::jsonb),
('mc:calcium content of a high-calcium diet.:::800 mg', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'calcium content of a high-calcium diet.', '800 mg', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"In conventional therapeutic diet matrices, modifications to standard mineral limits are defined relative to baseline metabolic configurations.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"800 mg\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"In conventional therapeutic diet matrices, modifications to standard mineral limits are defined relative to baseline metabolic configurations.\n\nWhile the full Recommended Dietary Allowance (RDA) for an average adult is roughly 1000 mg to 1200 mg, manual meal-planning reviewers traditionally establish a therapeutic high-calcium diet parameter around an additional nutritional delivery baseline, using 800 mg as the primary calculated target choice among the listed options.\n\n• 400 mg: This is a low-calcium intake metric, often aligned with standard restricted renal diet parameters or severe hypercalcemia limitations.\n• 200 mg: An extremely low, strictly restricted value representing a severe therapeutic limitation.\n• 500 mg: This matches the maximum single-dose capacity for optimal intestinal calcium absorption rather than a whole-day high-calcium diet therapeutic target.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: 400 mg, 500 mg, 200 mg"}'::jsonb),
('mc:calcium content of a high-calcium diet.:::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'calcium content of a high-calcium diet.', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"In conventional therapeutic diet matrices, modifications to standard mineral limits are defined relative to baseline metabolic configurations.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"800 mg\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"In conventional therapeutic diet matrices, modifications to standard mineral limits are defined relative to baseline metabolic configurations.\n\nWhile the full Recommended Dietary Allowance (RDA) for an average adult is roughly 1000 mg to 1200 mg, manual meal-planning reviewers traditionally establish a therapeutic high-calcium diet parameter around an additional nutritional delivery baseline, using 800 mg as the primary calculated target choice among the listed options.\n\n• 400 mg: This is a low-calcium intake metric, often aligned with standard restricted renal diet parameters or severe hypercalcemia limitations.\n• 200 mg: An extremely low, strictly restricted value representing a severe therapeutic limitation.\n• 500 mg: This matches the maximum single-dose capacity for optimal intestinal calcium absorption rather than a whole-day high-calcium diet therapeutic target.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: 400 mg, 500 mg, 200 mg"}'::jsonb),
('mc:the desirable body weight of a 3-year-old child::::14 kg', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'the desirable body weight of a 3-year-old child:', '14 kg', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"For children aged 2 to 12 years old, nutrition and dietetics board exams in the Philippines utilize a standard, universally accepted formula to calculate Desirable Body Weight (DBW):","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"14 kg\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"For children aged 2 to 12 years old, nutrition and dietetics board exams in the Philippines utilize a standard, universally accepted formula to calculate Desirable Body Weight (DBW):\n\nDBW (in kg) = (Age in years x 2) + 8\nDBW = (3 x 2) + 8\nDBW = 6 + 8 = 14 kg\n\nThis calculated value aligns closely with the World Health Organization (WHO) international pediatric standards, which place the statistical average weight of a healthy 3-year-old child right at 14 kg.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: 13 kg, 12 kg, 15 kg"}'::jsonb),
('mc:the desirable body weight of a 3-year-old child::::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'the desirable body weight of a 3-year-old child:', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"For children aged 2 to 12 years old, nutrition and dietetics board exams in the Philippines utilize a standard, universally accepted formula to calculate Desirable Body Weight (DBW):","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"14 kg\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"For children aged 2 to 12 years old, nutrition and dietetics board exams in the Philippines utilize a standard, universally accepted formula to calculate Desirable Body Weight (DBW):\n\nDBW (in kg) = (Age in years x 2) + 8\nDBW = (3 x 2) + 8\nDBW = 6 + 8 = 14 kg\n\nThis calculated value aligns closely with the World Health Organization (WHO) international pediatric standards, which place the statistical average weight of a healthy 3-year-old child right at 14 kg.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: 13 kg, 12 kg, 15 kg"}'::jsonb),
('mc:conditions that are contraindicated for the use of tube feeding except::::metabolic stress', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'conditions that are contraindicated for the use of tube feeding except:', 'metabolic stress', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Metabolic stress (such as severe trauma, major surgery, sepsis, or extensive burns) is an indication for enteral nutrition (tube feeding), not a contraindication. During metabolic stress, the body enters a highly catabolic state where nutrient demands skyrocket. Starting early tube feeding within 24 to 48 hours is standard practice to support tissue repair and maintain gut integrity.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"metabolic stress\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Metabolic stress (such as severe trauma, major surgery, sepsis, or extensive burns) is an indication for enteral nutrition (tube feeding), not a contraindication. During metabolic stress, the body enters a highly catabolic state where nutrient demands skyrocket. Starting early tube feeding within 24 to 48 hours is standard practice to support tissue repair and maintain gut integrity.\n\nThe question asks for the condition that is not a contraindication (the \"except\"). Options A, B, and C are all absolute contraindications where the gastrointestinal tract is entirely non-functional or unsafe to use.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: paralytic ileus, peritonitis, intractable vomiting"}'::jsonb),
('mc:conditions that are contraindicated for the use of tube feeding except::::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'conditions that are contraindicated for the use of tube feeding except:', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Metabolic stress (such as severe trauma, major surgery, sepsis, or extensive burns) is an indication for enteral nutrition (tube feeding), not a contraindication. During metabolic stress, the body enters a highly catabolic state where nutrient demands skyrocket. Starting early tube feeding within 24 to 48 hours is standard practice to support tissue repair and maintain gut integrity.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"metabolic stress\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Metabolic stress (such as severe trauma, major surgery, sepsis, or extensive burns) is an indication for enteral nutrition (tube feeding), not a contraindication. During metabolic stress, the body enters a highly catabolic state where nutrient demands skyrocket. Starting early tube feeding within 24 to 48 hours is standard practice to support tissue repair and maintain gut integrity.\n\nThe question asks for the condition that is not a contraindication (the \"except\"). Options A, B, and C are all absolute contraindications where the gastrointestinal tract is entirely non-functional or unsafe to use.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: paralytic ileus, peritonitis, intractable vomiting"}'::jsonb),
('mc:this hormone decreases the urge to eat and increases physical activity to produce a negative energy balance.:::leptin', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'this hormone decreases the urge to eat and increases physical activity to produce a negative energy balance.', 'leptin', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Leptin is a hormone produced primarily by adipose (fat) tissue that acts as a long-term regulator of energy balance.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"leptin\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Leptin is a hormone produced primarily by adipose (fat) tissue that acts as a long-term regulator of energy balance.\n\nWhen fat stores increase, leptin levels rise and signal the hypothalamus in the brain to suppress appetite (decrease the urge to eat) and boost metabolism and physical activity. This combination successfully drives the body toward a negative energy balance to burn off excess stored fat.\n\n• ghrelin: The \"hunger hormone\" produced mainly by the stomach. Its levels rise when the stomach is empty, signaling the brain to increase the urge to eat and reduce energy expenditure (promoting a positive energy balance).\n• adiponectin: Another hormone secreted by fat cells that helps regulate glucose levels and fatty acid breakdown while improving insulin sensitivity, but it does not directly drive the specific appetite-suppression and activity-boosting negative energy loop that leptin does.\n• insulin: Secreted by the pancreas primarily to lower blood glucose by shifting it into cells. While it can act as a minor central satiety signal, its primary physiological role is nutrient storage and carbohydrate metabolism.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: ghrelin, adiponectin, insulin"}'::jsonb)
ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO public.ai_explanation_cache (id, question_hash, front_text, back_text, options, explanation, breakdown, created_at, question_text, user_answer, is_correct, ai_explanation) VALUES
('mc:this hormone decreases the urge to eat and increases physical activity to produce a negative energy balance.:::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'this hormone decreases the urge to eat and increases physical activity to produce a negative energy balance.', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Leptin is a hormone produced primarily by adipose (fat) tissue that acts as a long-term regulator of energy balance.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"leptin\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Leptin is a hormone produced primarily by adipose (fat) tissue that acts as a long-term regulator of energy balance.\n\nWhen fat stores increase, leptin levels rise and signal the hypothalamus in the brain to suppress appetite (decrease the urge to eat) and boost metabolism and physical activity. This combination successfully drives the body toward a negative energy balance to burn off excess stored fat.\n\n• ghrelin: The \"hunger hormone\" produced mainly by the stomach. Its levels rise when the stomach is empty, signaling the brain to increase the urge to eat and reduce energy expenditure (promoting a positive energy balance).\n• adiponectin: Another hormone secreted by fat cells that helps regulate glucose levels and fatty acid breakdown while improving insulin sensitivity, but it does not directly drive the specific appetite-suppression and activity-boosting negative energy loop that leptin does.\n• insulin: Secreted by the pancreas primarily to lower blood glucose by shifting it into cells. While it can act as a minor central satiety signal, its primary physiological role is nutrient storage and carbohydrate metabolism.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: ghrelin, adiponectin, insulin"}'::jsonb),
('mc:the body’s magnesium is found mostly in the::::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'the body’s magnesium is found mostly in the:', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"An adult human body contains roughly 25 grams of magnesium, and the vast majority (about 50% to 60%) is stored directly within the bones. The skeleton acts as a massive reservoir for magnesium, with part of it bound to the bone crystal surface where it can be readily drawn out into the blood if dietary intake drops.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"bones\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"An adult human body contains roughly 25 grams of magnesium, and the vast majority (about 50% to 60%) is stored directly within the bones. The skeleton acts as a massive reservoir for magnesium, with part of it bound to the bone crystal surface where it can be readily drawn out into the blood if dietary intake drops.\n\nMost of the remaining magnesium is found within soft tissues, split between the skeletal muscles (around 20% to 30%) and other metabolic organs.\n\n• Nerves: While magnesium is absolutely critical for nerve impulse transmission and blocking neuromuscular excitability, nervous tissue holds only a very small percentage of the body''s total supply.\n• Muscles: Skeletal muscle is the second-largest pool of magnesium in the body, but it is still vastly outpaced by the volume stored in bone tissue.\n• Extracellular fluids: Less than 1% of the body''s total magnesium circulates within the extracellular fluid and blood serum. Because blood levels are tightly regulated at the expense of tissue stores, blood tests often fail to reflect a true total-body magnesium deficiency.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: extracellular fluids, nerves, muscles"}'::jsonb),
('mc:the precursor of arginine which becomes indispensable in conditions of trauma is::::glutamate', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'the precursor of arginine which becomes indispensable in conditions of trauma is:', 'glutamate', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"In the human body, glutamate (derived from glutamine in the intestinal-renal axis) serves as the primary metabolic precursor for the synthesis of arginine. Glutamate is converted into ornithine, which then enters the urea cycle to be synthesized into citrulline, argininosuccinate, and finally, arginine.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"glutamate\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"In the human body, glutamate (derived from glutamine in the intestinal-renal axis) serves as the primary metabolic precursor for the synthesis of arginine. Glutamate is converted into ornithine, which then enters the urea cycle to be synthesized into citrulline, argininosuccinate, and finally, arginine.\n\nWhile healthy adults can normally synthesize enough arginine internally to meet daily maintenance demands, severe physical trauma, major burns, or sepsis completely exhausts the body''s natural manufacturing capacity. Under these high-stress hypermetabolic states, the rate of internal arginine production from its glutamate pathway falls short of the massive demands needed for wound healing (proline synthesis), cellular immune function (T-cell support), and vascular tone (nitric oxide generation). Therefore, arginine transforms into a conditionally indispensable (essential) amino acid that must be supplied directly through the clinical diet or specialized formula.\n\n• Phenylalanine: An indispensable amino acid that acts as the primary precursor for tyrosine, not arginine.\n• Methionine: An indispensable sulfur-containing amino acid that serves as the precursor for cysteine, not arginine.\n• Serine: A dispensable amino acid synthesized from 3-phosphoglycerate (a glycolytic intermediate) that functions primarily in glycine and cysteine pathways.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: serine, phenylalanine, methionine"}'::jsonb),
('mc:nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drugs suppress inflammatory processes by::::inhibiting eicosanoid synthesis', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drugs suppress inflammatory processes by:', 'inhibiting eicosanoid synthesis', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drugs (NSAIDs) function primarily by inhibiting cyclooxygenase (COX-1 and COX-2) enzymes.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"inhibiting eicosanoid synthesis\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drugs (NSAIDs) function primarily by inhibiting cyclooxygenase (COX-1 and COX-2) enzymes.\n\nThese COX enzymes are responsible for metabolizing arachidonic acid (an omega-6 fatty acid) into prostaglandins and thromboxanes, which are key lipid signaling molecules collectively known as eicosanoids.\n\nBy blocking COX activity, NSAIDs arrest the downstream synthesis of these proinflammatory eicosanoids, thereby reducing pain, fever, and swelling.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: interfering with histamine secretion, impairing the actions of certain cytokines, inhibiting the absorption of omega-6 fatty acid"}'::jsonb),
('mc:the precursor of arginine which becomes indispensable in conditions of trauma is::::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'the precursor of arginine which becomes indispensable in conditions of trauma is:', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"In the human body, glutamate (derived from glutamine in the intestinal-renal axis) serves as the primary metabolic precursor for the synthesis of arginine. Glutamate is converted into ornithine, which then enters the urea cycle to be synthesized into citrulline, argininosuccinate, and finally, arginine.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"glutamate\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"In the human body, glutamate (derived from glutamine in the intestinal-renal axis) serves as the primary metabolic precursor for the synthesis of arginine. Glutamate is converted into ornithine, which then enters the urea cycle to be synthesized into citrulline, argininosuccinate, and finally, arginine.\n\nWhile healthy adults can normally synthesize enough arginine internally to meet daily maintenance demands, severe physical trauma, major burns, or sepsis completely exhausts the body''s natural manufacturing capacity. Under these high-stress hypermetabolic states, the rate of internal arginine production from its glutamate pathway falls short of the massive demands needed for wound healing (proline synthesis), cellular immune function (T-cell support), and vascular tone (nitric oxide generation). Therefore, arginine transforms into a conditionally indispensable (essential) amino acid that must be supplied directly through the clinical diet or specialized formula.\n\n• Phenylalanine: An indispensable amino acid that acts as the primary precursor for tyrosine, not arginine.\n• Methionine: An indispensable sulfur-containing amino acid that serves as the precursor for cysteine, not arginine.\n• Serine: A dispensable amino acid synthesized from 3-phosphoglycerate (a glycolytic intermediate) that functions primarily in glycine and cysteine pathways.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: serine, phenylalanine, methionine"}'::jsonb),
('mc:refer to the case below: a 35-year-old diabetic teacher went to the nutrition clinic for counseling. she is 5’4” tall andweighs 86 kg. she enrolled in a gymnasium and tried taking special slimming teas but she onlyexperienced hypoglycemia. she never lost a pound, instead gained weight. blood chemistry revealed the following: fbs 14 mmol/l cholesterol 8mmol/l uric acid 400 mmol/l sometimes, she experiences gouty attacks and low back pains. what should be her daily fluid intake?:::3 liters', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'refer to the case below: a 35-year-old diabetic teacher went to the nutrition clinic for counseling. she is 5’4” tall andweighs 86 kg. she enrolled in a gymnasium and tried taking special slimming teas but she onlyexperienced hypoglycemia. she never lost a pound, instead gained weight. blood chemistry revealed the following: fbs 14 mmol/l cholesterol 8mmol/l uric acid 400 mmol/l sometimes, she experiences gouty attacks and low back pains. what should be her daily fluid intake?', '3 liters', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"The patient has a significantly elevated uric acid level (400 µmol/L) and is actively experiencing symptomatic gouty attacks.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"3 liters\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"The patient has a significantly elevated uric acid level (400 µmol/L) and is actively experiencing symptomatic gouty attacks.\n\nIn medical nutrition therapy (MNT) for hyperuricemia and gout, increasing fluid intake is a primary non-pharmacological strategy. Drinking 3 liters of water per day (approximately 12 to 14 cups) is the standard clinical recommendation to help dilute the concentration of uric acid in the blood and continuously flush out uric acid crystals through the kidneys.\n\nThe patient also complains of low back pains, which are classic clinical warnings of crystalluria or microscopic uric acid stone formation in the renal tract. Forcing a fluid target of 3 liters ensures high urine output, which reduces the saturation of urate in the kidneys and directly prevents the precipitation of painful uric acid calculi (stones).\n\n• 4 liters / 5 liters: While staying well-hydrated is crucial, pushing a patient''s daily baseline up to 4 or 5 liters is excessively high for standard therapeutic maintenance and can unnecessary strain the kidneys or risk electrolyte imbalances like hyponatremia unless a severe acute toxic state is being aggressively managed in a hospital.\n• 2 liters: This is the standard fluid recommendation (about 8 cups) for average, healthy adults. It is insufficient for a patient actively experiencing gouty attacks and potential renal stone pains, as it does not provide the extra volumetric flushing capacity required to lower high serum uric acid concentrations.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: 2 liters, 4 liters, 5 liters"}'::jsonb),
('mc:refer to the case below: a 35-year-old diabetic teacher went to the nutrition clinic for counseling. she is 5’4” tall andweighs 86 kg. she enrolled in a gymnasium and tried taking special slimming teas but she onlyexperienced hypoglycemia. she never lost a pound, instead gained weight. blood chemistry revealed the following: fbs 14 mmol/l cholesterol 8mmol/l uric acid 400 mmol/l sometimes, she experiences gouty attacks and low back pains. what should be her daily fluid intake?:::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'refer to the case below: a 35-year-old diabetic teacher went to the nutrition clinic for counseling. she is 5’4” tall andweighs 86 kg. she enrolled in a gymnasium and tried taking special slimming teas but she onlyexperienced hypoglycemia. she never lost a pound, instead gained weight. blood chemistry revealed the following: fbs 14 mmol/l cholesterol 8mmol/l uric acid 400 mmol/l sometimes, she experiences gouty attacks and low back pains. what should be her daily fluid intake?', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"The patient has a significantly elevated uric acid level (400 µmol/L) and is actively experiencing symptomatic gouty attacks.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"3 liters\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"The patient has a significantly elevated uric acid level (400 µmol/L) and is actively experiencing symptomatic gouty attacks.\n\nIn medical nutrition therapy (MNT) for hyperuricemia and gout, increasing fluid intake is a primary non-pharmacological strategy. Drinking 3 liters of water per day (approximately 12 to 14 cups) is the standard clinical recommendation to help dilute the concentration of uric acid in the blood and continuously flush out uric acid crystals through the kidneys.\n\nThe patient also complains of low back pains, which are classic clinical warnings of crystalluria or microscopic uric acid stone formation in the renal tract. Forcing a fluid target of 3 liters ensures high urine output, which reduces the saturation of urate in the kidneys and directly prevents the precipitation of painful uric acid calculi (stones).\n\n• 4 liters / 5 liters: While staying well-hydrated is crucial, pushing a patient''s daily baseline up to 4 or 5 liters is excessively high for standard therapeutic maintenance and can unnecessary strain the kidneys or risk electrolyte imbalances like hyponatremia unless a severe acute toxic state is being aggressively managed in a hospital.\n• 2 liters: This is the standard fluid recommendation (about 8 cups) for average, healthy adults. It is insufficient for a patient actively experiencing gouty attacks and potential renal stone pains, as it does not provide the extra volumetric flushing capacity required to lower high serum uric acid concentrations.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: 2 liters, 4 liters, 5 liters"}'::jsonb),
('mc:the largest controllable cost incurred in a foodservice unit is::::food', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'the largest controllable cost incurred in a foodservice unit is:', 'Food', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Food cost is the percentage of total sales spent onf food products. This usually ranges from 40-45% of all the costs and it under the control of the owner/manager.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Food\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Food cost is the percentage of total sales spent onf food products. This usually ranges from 40-45% of all the costs and it under the control of the owner/manager.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Labor, Operation, Utilities"}'::jsonb),
('mc:vitamin k deficiency is frequently drug-induced. which of the following drugs might be associated with this deficiency? 1. antibiotics 2. mineral oil 3. diuretics 4. anticoagulants:::1, 2, 4 only', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'vitamin k deficiency is frequently drug-induced. which of the following drugs might be associated with this deficiency? 1. antibiotics 2. mineral oil 3. diuretics 4. anticoagulants', '1, 2, 4 only', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"1. Antibiotics: A large portion of the body''s vitamin K is synthesized by beneficial bacteria residing in the gut. Broad-spectrum antibiotics can destroy these bacteria, drastically reducing natural vitamin K production.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"1, 2, 4 only\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"1. Antibiotics: A large portion of the body''s vitamin K is synthesized by beneficial bacteria residing in the gut. Broad-spectrum antibiotics can destroy these bacteria, drastically reducing natural vitamin K production.\n\n2. Mineral oil: Because vitamin K is a fat-soluble vitamin, it requires lipids to be absorbed properly. Mineral oil acts as a non-absorbable laxative that coats the intestines and dissolves fat-soluble vitamins (A, D, E, and K), carrying them out of the body in stool before they can be absorbed.\n\n4. Anticoagulants: Medications like warfarin are vitamin K antagonists. They directly block the enzyme mechanism responsible for recycling vitamin K in the liver, leading to a functional vitamin K deficiency that prevents blood clotting factors from activating.\n\n• 3. Diuretics: These medications increase urine output and primarily affect water-soluble minerals and electrolytes (like potassium, sodium, and magnesium) rather than the absorption or metabolism of fat-soluble vitamins like vitamin K.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: 1 & 2 only, 1, 3, 4 only, 1, 2, 3 only"}'::jsonb),
('mc:vitamin k deficiency is frequently drug-induced. which of the following drugs might be associated with this deficiency? 1. antibiotics 2. mineral oil 3. diuretics 4. anticoagulants:::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'vitamin k deficiency is frequently drug-induced. which of the following drugs might be associated with this deficiency? 1. antibiotics 2. mineral oil 3. diuretics 4. anticoagulants', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"1. Antibiotics: A large portion of the body''s vitamin K is synthesized by beneficial bacteria residing in the gut. Broad-spectrum antibiotics can destroy these bacteria, drastically reducing natural vitamin K production.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"1, 2, 4 only\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"1. Antibiotics: A large portion of the body''s vitamin K is synthesized by beneficial bacteria residing in the gut. Broad-spectrum antibiotics can destroy these bacteria, drastically reducing natural vitamin K production.\n\n2. Mineral oil: Because vitamin K is a fat-soluble vitamin, it requires lipids to be absorbed properly. Mineral oil acts as a non-absorbable laxative that coats the intestines and dissolves fat-soluble vitamins (A, D, E, and K), carrying them out of the body in stool before they can be absorbed.\n\n4. Anticoagulants: Medications like warfarin are vitamin K antagonists. They directly block the enzyme mechanism responsible for recycling vitamin K in the liver, leading to a functional vitamin K deficiency that prevents blood clotting factors from activating.\n\n• 3. Diuretics: These medications increase urine output and primarily affect water-soluble minerals and electrolytes (like potassium, sodium, and magnesium) rather than the absorption or metabolism of fat-soluble vitamins like vitamin K.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: 1 & 2 only, 1, 3, 4 only, 1, 2, 3 only"}'::jsonb),
('mc:the primary risk factor in cases of fatty liver that is not related to excessive alcohol intake is::::insulin resistance', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'the primary risk factor in cases of fatty liver that is not related to excessive alcohol intake is:', 'insulin resistance', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"In cases of fatty liver disease that are completely unrelated to excessive alcohol intake (traditionally called Nonalcoholic Fatty Liver Disease or NAFLD, now known as Metabolic Dysfunction-Associated Steatotic Liver Disease or MASLD), insulin resistance serves as the primary, defining pathophysiological risk factor.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"insulin resistance\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"In cases of fatty liver disease that are completely unrelated to excessive alcohol intake (traditionally called Nonalcoholic Fatty Liver Disease or NAFLD, now known as Metabolic Dysfunction-Associated Steatotic Liver Disease or MASLD), insulin resistance serves as the primary, defining pathophysiological risk factor.\n\nWhen a patient develops insulin resistance, their adipose (fat) tissue stops responding properly to the anti-lipolytic effects of insulin. This triggers an uncontrolled breakdown of stored peripheral fats, flooding the bloodstream with a massive surge of free fatty acids. The liver absorbs these excess fatty acids but cannot process or export them quickly enough, causing them to accumulate internally as triglycerides. Insulin resistance also promotes de novo lipogenesis (the liver making its own fat from circulating sugars), compounding the problem.\n\n• High-protein diet: Following a high-protein diet does not cause fat accumulation in liver cells. In fact, adequate protein intake is necessary to supply the amino acids needed to build lipoproteins (like VLDL) that safely export fat out of the liver.\n• Use of illicit drugs: While certain toxins and pharmaceuticals can induce specific patterns of liver injury or acute steatosis, they represent specialized secondary causes rather than the primary, population-wide driver of metabolic fatty liver disease.\n• High-fat diet: While an unmanaged, calorie-dense high-fat diet can contribute to overall obesity (which goes hand-in-hand with liver fat), dietary fat intake alone is not the definitive core driver. The true culprit is the systemic metabolic failure of insulin resistance, which traps both dietary fats and converted carbohydrates inside the liver cells.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: a high-protein diet, use of illicit drugs, a high-fat diet"}'::jsonb),
('mc:the primary risk factor in cases of fatty liver that is not related to excessive alcohol intake is::::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'the primary risk factor in cases of fatty liver that is not related to excessive alcohol intake is:', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"In cases of fatty liver disease that are completely unrelated to excessive alcohol intake (traditionally called Nonalcoholic Fatty Liver Disease or NAFLD, now known as Metabolic Dysfunction-Associated Steatotic Liver Disease or MASLD), insulin resistance serves as the primary, defining pathophysiological risk factor.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"insulin resistance\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"In cases of fatty liver disease that are completely unrelated to excessive alcohol intake (traditionally called Nonalcoholic Fatty Liver Disease or NAFLD, now known as Metabolic Dysfunction-Associated Steatotic Liver Disease or MASLD), insulin resistance serves as the primary, defining pathophysiological risk factor.\n\nWhen a patient develops insulin resistance, their adipose (fat) tissue stops responding properly to the anti-lipolytic effects of insulin. This triggers an uncontrolled breakdown of stored peripheral fats, flooding the bloodstream with a massive surge of free fatty acids. The liver absorbs these excess fatty acids but cannot process or export them quickly enough, causing them to accumulate internally as triglycerides. Insulin resistance also promotes de novo lipogenesis (the liver making its own fat from circulating sugars), compounding the problem.\n\n• High-protein diet: Following a high-protein diet does not cause fat accumulation in liver cells. In fact, adequate protein intake is necessary to supply the amino acids needed to build lipoproteins (like VLDL) that safely export fat out of the liver.\n• Use of illicit drugs: While certain toxins and pharmaceuticals can induce specific patterns of liver injury or acute steatosis, they represent specialized secondary causes rather than the primary, population-wide driver of metabolic fatty liver disease.\n• High-fat diet: While an unmanaged, calorie-dense high-fat diet can contribute to overall obesity (which goes hand-in-hand with liver fat), dietary fat intake alone is not the definitive core driver. The true culprit is the systemic metabolic failure of insulin resistance, which traps both dietary fats and converted carbohydrates inside the liver cells.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: a high-protein diet, use of illicit drugs, a high-fat diet"}'::jsonb),
('mc:primary causes of peptic ulcer except::::spicy foods', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'primary causes of peptic ulcer except:', 'spicy foods', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"While it is a widely held belief that eating highly spicy foods can erode the stomach lining and cause ulcers, comprehensive clinical research has proven that they do not cause peptic ulcers.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"spicy foods\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"While it is a widely held belief that eating highly spicy foods can erode the stomach lining and cause ulcers, comprehensive clinical research has proven that they do not cause peptic ulcers.\n\nSpicy foods can irritate an already existing ulcer flare-up, increase acid reflux sensations, or exacerbate abdominal discomfort, but they lack the physiological capacity to initiate the actual open sores or ulcerations on their own.\n\n• Gastritis: Chronic inflammation of the stomach lining (gastritis) directly weakens the protective mucosal barrier over time, leaving the tissue vulnerable to auto-digestion by harsh stomach acids and pepsin, which frequently progresses into an ulcer.\n• H. pylori infection: Helicobacter pylori is a spiral bacterium that colonizes the mucosal layer of the stomach. It secretes enzymes (like urease) and causes localized inflammatory responses that physically degrade the stomach''s protective mucus coat, serving as the leading global biological cause of peptic ulcer disease.\n• Aspirins & other NSAIDs: Nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drugs (NSAIDs) chemically inhibit cyclooxygenase (COX) enzymes, which drastically reduces the body''s synthesis of systemic prostaglandins. Since prostaglandins are directly responsible for producing the stomach''s protective mucus layer and maintaining healthy mucosal blood flow, prolonged NSAID use leaves the stomach unprotected against its own digestive juices.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: gastritis, aspirins & other NSAIDs, pylori infection"}'::jsonb),
('mc:primary causes of peptic ulcer except::::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'primary causes of peptic ulcer except:', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"While it is a widely held belief that eating highly spicy foods can erode the stomach lining and cause ulcers, comprehensive clinical research has proven that they do not cause peptic ulcers.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"spicy foods\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"While it is a widely held belief that eating highly spicy foods can erode the stomach lining and cause ulcers, comprehensive clinical research has proven that they do not cause peptic ulcers.\n\nSpicy foods can irritate an already existing ulcer flare-up, increase acid reflux sensations, or exacerbate abdominal discomfort, but they lack the physiological capacity to initiate the actual open sores or ulcerations on their own.\n\n• Gastritis: Chronic inflammation of the stomach lining (gastritis) directly weakens the protective mucosal barrier over time, leaving the tissue vulnerable to auto-digestion by harsh stomach acids and pepsin, which frequently progresses into an ulcer.\n• H. pylori infection: Helicobacter pylori is a spiral bacterium that colonizes the mucosal layer of the stomach. It secretes enzymes (like urease) and causes localized inflammatory responses that physically degrade the stomach''s protective mucus coat, serving as the leading global biological cause of peptic ulcer disease.\n• Aspirins & other NSAIDs: Nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drugs (NSAIDs) chemically inhibit cyclooxygenase (COX) enzymes, which drastically reduces the body''s synthesis of systemic prostaglandins. Since prostaglandins are directly responsible for producing the stomach''s protective mucus layer and maintaining healthy mucosal blood flow, prolonged NSAID use leaves the stomach unprotected against its own digestive juices.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: gastritis, aspirins & other NSAIDs, pylori infection"}'::jsonb),
('mc:triglyceride levels of patients with mild hypertriglyceridemia may be improved by::::limiting their intake of refined carbohydrates', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'triglyceride levels of patients with mild hypertriglyceridemia may be improved by:', 'limiting their intake of refined carbohydrates', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"When a person consumes an excess of refined carbohydrates (such as white sugar, high-fructose corn syrup, white flour, and sugary beverages), the liver rapidly processes the surging influx of simple sugars.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"limiting their intake of refined carbohydrates\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"When a person consumes an excess of refined carbohydrates (such as white sugar, high-fructose corn syrup, white flour, and sugary beverages), the liver rapidly processes the surging influx of simple sugars.\n\nThrough a metabolic process called de novo lipogenesis, the liver converts these excess carbohydrates directly into fatty acids, which are then packaged into VLDL (very-low-density lipoprotein) particles as triglyceride levels. Limiting refined carbohydrates and replacing them with complex, high-fiber options is the most powerful and direct dietary intervention for dropping serum triglycerides back into a healthy range.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: reducing their cholesterol intake, consuming moderate amounts of alcohol, reducing their sodium intake"}'::jsonb),
('mc:these are purely ketogenic amino acids::::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'these are purely ketogenic amino acids:', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Leucine and Lysine are purely ketogenic amino acids that can be catabolized to form ketone bodies and yield acetyl CoA or acetoacetate.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Leucine and Lysine\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Leucine and Lysine are purely ketogenic amino acids that can be catabolized to form ketone bodies and yield acetyl CoA or acetoacetate.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Cysteine and Arginine, Leucine and Alanine"}'::jsonb),
('mc:triglyceride levels of patients with mild hypertriglyceridemia may be improved by::::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'triglyceride levels of patients with mild hypertriglyceridemia may be improved by:', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"When a person consumes an excess of refined carbohydrates (such as white sugar, high-fructose corn syrup, white flour, and sugary beverages), the liver rapidly processes the surging influx of simple sugars.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"limiting their intake of refined carbohydrates\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"When a person consumes an excess of refined carbohydrates (such as white sugar, high-fructose corn syrup, white flour, and sugary beverages), the liver rapidly processes the surging influx of simple sugars.\n\nThrough a metabolic process called de novo lipogenesis, the liver converts these excess carbohydrates directly into fatty acids, which are then packaged into VLDL (very-low-density lipoprotein) particles as triglyceride levels. Limiting refined carbohydrates and replacing them with complex, high-fiber options is the most powerful and direct dietary intervention for dropping serum triglycerides back into a healthy range.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: reducing their cholesterol intake, consuming moderate amounts of alcohol, reducing their sodium intake"}'::jsonb),
('mc:refer to the case below: a 35-year-old diabetic teacher went to the nutrition clinic for counseling. she is 5’4” tall andweighs 86 kg. she enrolled in a gymnasium and tried taking special slimming teas but she onlyexperienced hypoglycemia. she never lost a pound, instead gained weight. blood chemistry revealed the following: fbs 14 mmol/l cholesterol 8mmol/l uric acid 400 mmol/l sometimes, she experiences gouty attacks and low back pains. what would be the best level of fat?:::15 -20% of ter', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'refer to the case below: a 35-year-old diabetic teacher went to the nutrition clinic for counseling. she is 5’4” tall andweighs 86 kg. she enrolled in a gymnasium and tried taking special slimming teas but she onlyexperienced hypoglycemia. she never lost a pound, instead gained weight. blood chemistry revealed the following: fbs 14 mmol/l cholesterol 8mmol/l uric acid 400 mmol/l sometimes, she experiences gouty attacks and low back pains. what would be the best level of fat?', '15 -20% of TER', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"This patient presents with multiple critical metabolic disorders—poorly controlled diabetes, obesity, hyperuricemia (gout), and severe hypercholesterolemia.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"15 -20% of TER\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"This patient presents with multiple critical metabolic disorders—poorly controlled diabetes, obesity, hyperuricemia (gout), and severe hypercholesterolemia.\n\nIn standard board exam matrices, when a diabetic patient presents with elevated blood cholesterol levels, clinical guidelines prioritize managing cardiovascular risk. Restricting fat intake to a lower proportion of Total Energy Requirement (TER) assists in reducing saturated fat and dietary cholesterol. This therapeutic restriction helps address circulating blood lipids and manage obesity, while carbohydrate quality is managed separately to stabilize blood sugar.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: 25- 35% of TER, 30-35% of TER, 20-25% of TER"}'::jsonb),
('mc:a 7-year-old child is overweight by 40 lbs. his pediatrician advised the mother to see a nutritionist-dietitian for weight loss. what would you do?:::advise the mother to maintain her child’s weight.', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'a 7-year-old child is overweight by 40 lbs. his pediatrician advised the mother to see a nutritionist-dietitian for weight loss. what would you do?', 'Advise the mother to maintain her child’s weight.', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"In pediatric nutrition and weight management, the primary goal for a growing 7-year-old child who is overweight or moderately obese is weight maintenance or stabilization, rather than active weight loss.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Advise the mother to maintain her child’s weight.\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"In pediatric nutrition and weight management, the primary goal for a growing 7-year-old child who is overweight or moderately obese is weight maintenance or stabilization, rather than active weight loss.\n\nBecause a 7-year-old is undergoing rapid skeletal development and heights spurts, maintaining their current weight allows them to naturally \"grow into\" their weight over the next few years, which steadily lowers their Body Mass Index (BMI).","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Tell the mother to come back when the child is 10 years old., As the mother to cut out all the “junk food” only., Put the child on a weight loss program with mild calorie restriction."}'::jsonb),
('mc:a 7-year-old child is overweight by 40 lbs. his pediatrician advised the mother to see a nutritionist-dietitian for weight loss. what would you do?:::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'a 7-year-old child is overweight by 40 lbs. his pediatrician advised the mother to see a nutritionist-dietitian for weight loss. what would you do?', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"In pediatric nutrition and weight management, the primary goal for a growing 7-year-old child who is overweight or moderately obese is weight maintenance or stabilization, rather than active weight loss.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Advise the mother to maintain her child’s weight.\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"In pediatric nutrition and weight management, the primary goal for a growing 7-year-old child who is overweight or moderately obese is weight maintenance or stabilization, rather than active weight loss.\n\nBecause a 7-year-old is undergoing rapid skeletal development and heights spurts, maintaining their current weight allows them to naturally \"grow into\" their weight over the next few years, which steadily lowers their Body Mass Index (BMI).","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Tell the mother to come back when the child is 10 years old., As the mother to cut out all the “junk food” only., Put the child on a weight loss program with mild calorie restriction."}'::jsonb),
('mc:the best protein allowance for severely malnourished children is::::4 g/kg dbw', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'the best protein allowance for severely malnourished children is:', '4 g/kg DBW', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Severely malnourished children have vastly elevated protein and energy requirements to support tissue repair and catch-up growth. While a child needs significant protein to reverse wasting, the allowance must be managed precisely.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"4 g/kg DBW\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Severely malnourished children have vastly elevated protein and energy requirements to support tissue repair and catch-up growth. While a child needs significant protein to reverse wasting, the allowance must be managed precisely.\n\n\t• In the Initial/Stabilization phase, protein is restricted to 1 to 1.5 g/kg to prevent refeeding syndrome and avoid overwhelming the child''s compromised liver and kidneys.\n\t• In the Rehabilitation/Catch-up phase, the allowance is gradually escalated to an optimal level of 3 to 4 g/kg of Desired Body Weight (DBW) (and up to 5 g/kg under specific clinical parameters) to maximize rapid cellular synthesis. Because options vary by textbooks, 3 g/kg DBW represents the safe, universally established target threshold for rebuilding lean body mass without risking metabolic overload.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: 3 g/kg DBW, 5 g/kg DBW, none of the above"}'::jsonb),
('mc:the best protein allowance for severely malnourished children is::::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'the best protein allowance for severely malnourished children is:', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Severely malnourished children have vastly elevated protein and energy requirements to support tissue repair and catch-up growth. While a child needs significant protein to reverse wasting, the allowance must be managed precisely.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"4 g/kg DBW\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Severely malnourished children have vastly elevated protein and energy requirements to support tissue repair and catch-up growth. While a child needs significant protein to reverse wasting, the allowance must be managed precisely.\n\n\t• In the Initial/Stabilization phase, protein is restricted to 1 to 1.5 g/kg to prevent refeeding syndrome and avoid overwhelming the child''s compromised liver and kidneys.\n\t• In the Rehabilitation/Catch-up phase, the allowance is gradually escalated to an optimal level of 3 to 4 g/kg of Desired Body Weight (DBW) (and up to 5 g/kg under specific clinical parameters) to maximize rapid cellular synthesis. Because options vary by textbooks, 3 g/kg DBW represents the safe, universally established target threshold for rebuilding lean body mass without risking metabolic overload.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: 3 g/kg DBW, 5 g/kg DBW, none of the above"}'::jsonb),
('mc:in foodservice facilities and layout, supporting services include the following, except::::none of the above', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'in foodservice facilities and layout, supporting services include the following, except:', 'None of the above', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"It includes all plus an area for steam hose use to wash and sanitize carts, etc.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"None of the above\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"It includes all plus an area for steam hose use to wash and sanitize carts, etc.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Office, Janitor''s closet/storage, Trash and waste storage"}'::jsonb),
('mc:the body stores energy for future use in::::triglycerides', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'the body stores energy for future use in:', 'triglycerides', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Adipose tissue plays a fundamental role in maintaining long-term energy balance by storing concentrated energy in the form of triglycerides (fats).","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"triglycerides\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Adipose tissue plays a fundamental role in maintaining long-term energy balance by storing concentrated energy in the form of triglycerides (fats).\n\nTriglycerides provide a highly concentrated fuel source yielding 9 kcal/g, making them the most efficient and virtually unlimited way for the body to store excess calories for future energy needs.\n\n• Proteins: While amino acids from proteins can be broken down for energy during late prolonged starvation, the primary physiological role of proteins is structural and functional (building tissues and machinery); they are not stored for the dedicated purpose of serving as an energy reserve.\n• Acetyl CoA & Ketone bodies: These are intermediate metabolic molecules and transient byproducts of fuel breakdown. They are utilized immediately by tissues or converted further to produce ATP, but they cannot be stored by the body for future use.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: acetyl CoA, proteins, ketone bodies"}'::jsonb),
('mc:the body stores energy for future use in::::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'the body stores energy for future use in:', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Adipose tissue plays a fundamental role in maintaining long-term energy balance by storing concentrated energy in the form of triglycerides (fats).","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"triglycerides\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Adipose tissue plays a fundamental role in maintaining long-term energy balance by storing concentrated energy in the form of triglycerides (fats).\n\nTriglycerides provide a highly concentrated fuel source yielding 9 kcal/g, making them the most efficient and virtually unlimited way for the body to store excess calories for future energy needs.\n\n• Proteins: While amino acids from proteins can be broken down for energy during late prolonged starvation, the primary physiological role of proteins is structural and functional (building tissues and machinery); they are not stored for the dedicated purpose of serving as an energy reserve.\n• Acetyl CoA & Ketone bodies: These are intermediate metabolic molecules and transient byproducts of fuel breakdown. They are utilized immediately by tissues or converted further to produce ATP, but they cannot be stored by the body for future use.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: acetyl CoA, proteins, ketone bodies"}'::jsonb),
('mc:refer to the case below: a 35-year-old diabetic teacher went to the nutrition clinic for counseling. she is 5’4” tall andweighs 86 kg. she enrolled in a gymnasium and tried taking special slimming teas but she onlyexperienced hypoglycemia. she never lost a pound, instead gained weight. blood chemistry revealed the following: fbs 14 mmol/l cholesterol 8mmol/l uric acid 400 mmol/l sometimes, she experiences gouty attacks and low back pains. what would be the best protein requirement?:::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'refer to the case below: a 35-year-old diabetic teacher went to the nutrition clinic for counseling. she is 5’4” tall andweighs 86 kg. she enrolled in a gymnasium and tried taking special slimming teas but she onlyexperienced hypoglycemia. she never lost a pound, instead gained weight. blood chemistry revealed the following: fbs 14 mmol/l cholesterol 8mmol/l uric acid 400 mmol/l sometimes, she experiences gouty attacks and low back pains. what would be the best protein requirement?', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"1. Calculate the Desirable Body Weight (DBW):","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"55 g\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"1. Calculate the Desirable Body Weight (DBW):\n\t• Convert height to centimeters: 5 feet 4 inches = 64 inches.\n\t• 64 inches x 2.54 cm/inch = 162.56 cm\n\t• Use Tannhauser’s Method (the standard method utilized in Philippine board exams):\n\t\t• DBW (kg) = (Height in cm - 100) - 10%\n\t\t• Base weight = 162.56 - 100 = 62.56 kg\n\t\t• Deduct 10%: 62.56 - 6.25 = 56.31 kg\n\t• Rounding off the DBW gives approximately 55 kg.\n\n2. Determine the Protein Requirement:\n\t• For an adult with diabetes and normal renal function, the standard nutritional care plan uses a baseline allowance of 1.0 gram of protein per kilogram of DBW per day.\n\t• Total Protein Required = 55 kg DBW x 1.0 g/kg = 55 grams\n\nBecause her blood work reveals elevated uric acid (400 mmol/L) and she experiences gouty attacks, keeping the protein requirement at a controlled, standard adult level of 1.0 g/kg (rather than high-protein or upper-range clinical levels) helps manage purine intake while protecting kidney health.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: 50 g, 65 g, 60 g"}'::jsonb),
('mc:nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drugs suppress inflammatory processes by::::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drugs suppress inflammatory processes by:', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drugs (NSAIDs) function primarily by inhibiting cyclooxygenase (COX-1 and COX-2) enzymes.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"inhibiting eicosanoid synthesis\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drugs (NSAIDs) function primarily by inhibiting cyclooxygenase (COX-1 and COX-2) enzymes.\n\nThese COX enzymes are responsible for metabolizing arachidonic acid (an omega-6 fatty acid) into prostaglandins and thromboxanes, which are key lipid signaling molecules collectively known as eicosanoids.\n\nBy blocking COX activity, NSAIDs arrest the downstream synthesis of these proinflammatory eicosanoids, thereby reducing pain, fever, and swelling.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: interfering with histamine secretion, impairing the actions of certain cytokines, inhibiting the absorption of omega-6 fatty acid"}'::jsonb),
('mc:it is the preferred preoperative diet for abdominal surgery.:::low residue liquid', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'it is the preferred preoperative diet for abdominal surgery.', 'low residue liquid', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"In older Philippine dietetic manuals and classic Nutritional Biochemistry test blueprints, a clear liquid diet is officially sub-classified as the most extreme form of a low-residue diet.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"low residue liquid\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"In older Philippine dietetic manuals and classic Nutritional Biochemistry test blueprints, a clear liquid diet is officially sub-classified as the most extreme form of a low-residue diet.\n\n• Under this strict theoretical framework, the term \"clear liquid\" simply describes the physical appearance of the food (transparent, liquid at room temperature).\n• The term \"low residue\" describes the actual physiological purpose of the diet—which is to leave zero undigested material (residue) in the bowel to ensure the surgical field is clean.\n\nBecause \"low residue\" describes the primary medical objective for an abdominal surgery, the test creators view it as the broader, more scientifically descriptive choice for this exact question layout.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: full liquid, none of the above, clear liquid"}'::jsonb),
('mc:it is the preferred preoperative diet for abdominal surgery.:::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'it is the preferred preoperative diet for abdominal surgery.', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"In older Philippine dietetic manuals and classic Nutritional Biochemistry test blueprints, a clear liquid diet is officially sub-classified as the most extreme form of a low-residue diet.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"low residue liquid\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"In older Philippine dietetic manuals and classic Nutritional Biochemistry test blueprints, a clear liquid diet is officially sub-classified as the most extreme form of a low-residue diet.\n\n• Under this strict theoretical framework, the term \"clear liquid\" simply describes the physical appearance of the food (transparent, liquid at room temperature).\n• The term \"low residue\" describes the actual physiological purpose of the diet—which is to leave zero undigested material (residue) in the bowel to ensure the surgical field is clean.\n\nBecause \"low residue\" describes the primary medical objective for an abdominal surgery, the test creators view it as the broader, more scientifically descriptive choice for this exact question layout.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: full liquid, none of the above, clear liquid"}'::jsonb),
('mc:when metabolically-stressed patients are given diets with excessive calories, this could result in overfeeding, excess carbon dioxide production, and other related complications such as::::respiratory insufficiency', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'when metabolically-stressed patients are given diets with excessive calories, this could result in overfeeding, excess carbon dioxide production, and other related complications such as:', 'respiratory insufficiency', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"When metabolically stressed or critically ill patients receive excessive calories (particularly from carbohydrates), the body''s rate of glucose oxidation accelerates. This dramatically spikes carbon dioxide (CO₂) production.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"respiratory insufficiency\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"When metabolically stressed or critically ill patients receive excessive calories (particularly from carbohydrates), the body''s rate of glucose oxidation accelerates. This dramatically spikes carbon dioxide (CO₂) production.\n\nTo clear this excess CO₂, the lungs are forced to work much harder by increasing minute ventilation (breathing faster and deeper). In a metabolically stressed patient who may already have compromised lung function or be on a ventilator, this immense physical demand frequently leads to respiratory insufficiency and failure to wean from mechanical ventilation.\n\n• Excessive weight gain: While chronic overfeeding leads to weight gain over a long period, in an acute, metabolically stressed ICU environment, the immediate dangerous complication of aggressive hyperalimentation is respiratory and metabolic failure, not fat storage.\n• Immediate weaning from mechanical ventilation: Overfeeding does the exact opposite—it delays weaning and extends the number of days a patient requires a ventilator because their lungs are too exhausted from handling the extra CO₂.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: excessive weight gain, all of the above, immediate weaning from mechanical ventilation"}'::jsonb),
('mc:the body’s magnesium is found mostly in the::::bones', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'the body’s magnesium is found mostly in the:', 'bones', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"An adult human body contains roughly 25 grams of magnesium, and the vast majority (about 50% to 60%) is stored directly within the bones. The skeleton acts as a massive reservoir for magnesium, with part of it bound to the bone crystal surface where it can be readily drawn out into the blood if dietary intake drops.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"bones\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"An adult human body contains roughly 25 grams of magnesium, and the vast majority (about 50% to 60%) is stored directly within the bones. The skeleton acts as a massive reservoir for magnesium, with part of it bound to the bone crystal surface where it can be readily drawn out into the blood if dietary intake drops.\n\nMost of the remaining magnesium is found within soft tissues, split between the skeletal muscles (around 20% to 30%) and other metabolic organs.\n\n• Nerves: While magnesium is absolutely critical for nerve impulse transmission and blocking neuromuscular excitability, nervous tissue holds only a very small percentage of the body''s total supply.\n• Muscles: Skeletal muscle is the second-largest pool of magnesium in the body, but it is still vastly outpaced by the volume stored in bone tissue.\n• Extracellular fluids: Less than 1% of the body''s total magnesium circulates within the extracellular fluid and blood serum. Because blood levels are tightly regulated at the expense of tissue stores, blood tests often fail to reflect a true total-body magnesium deficiency.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: extracellular fluids, nerves, muscles"}'::jsonb),
('mc:when metabolically-stressed patients are given diets with excessive calories, this could result in overfeeding, excess carbon dioxide production, and other related complications such as::::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'when metabolically-stressed patients are given diets with excessive calories, this could result in overfeeding, excess carbon dioxide production, and other related complications such as:', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"When metabolically stressed or critically ill patients receive excessive calories (particularly from carbohydrates), the body''s rate of glucose oxidation accelerates. This dramatically spikes carbon dioxide (CO₂) production.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"respiratory insufficiency\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"When metabolically stressed or critically ill patients receive excessive calories (particularly from carbohydrates), the body''s rate of glucose oxidation accelerates. This dramatically spikes carbon dioxide (CO₂) production.\n\nTo clear this excess CO₂, the lungs are forced to work much harder by increasing minute ventilation (breathing faster and deeper). In a metabolically stressed patient who may already have compromised lung function or be on a ventilator, this immense physical demand frequently leads to respiratory insufficiency and failure to wean from mechanical ventilation.\n\n• Excessive weight gain: While chronic overfeeding leads to weight gain over a long period, in an acute, metabolically stressed ICU environment, the immediate dangerous complication of aggressive hyperalimentation is respiratory and metabolic failure, not fat storage.\n• Immediate weaning from mechanical ventilation: Overfeeding does the exact opposite—it delays weaning and extends the number of days a patient requires a ventilator because their lungs are too exhausted from handling the extra CO₂.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: excessive weight gain, all of the above, immediate weaning from mechanical ventilation"}'::jsonb),
('mc:a therapeutic diet counseling session is best given when::::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'a therapeutic diet counseling session is best given when:', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"In clinical dietetics and hospital protocols, a therapeutic diet counseling session requires the patient’s active attention, focus, and ability to comprehend new lifestyle changes.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"the patient is relaxed and hospitalized\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"In clinical dietetics and hospital protocols, a therapeutic diet counseling session requires the patient’s active attention, focus, and ability to comprehend new lifestyle changes.\n\nWhen a patient is relaxed and hospitalized (meaning they are past the acute, painful phase of their illness but not yet caught up in the hectic rush of leaving), they are in the best mental and physical state to absorb complex nutritional instructions, ask questions, and practice meal-planning concepts.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: the patient is very sick, none of the above, the patient is being discharged"}'::jsonb),
('mc:a metabolic change that accompanies acute stress is::::muscle protein catabolism', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'a metabolic change that accompanies acute stress is:', 'muscle protein catabolism', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"During acute physiological stress (such as severe trauma, major surgery, burns, or infection), the body enters a highly hypermetabolic and catabolic state.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"muscle protein catabolism\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"During acute physiological stress (such as severe trauma, major surgery, burns, or infection), the body enters a highly hypermetabolic and catabolic state.\n\nTo fuel vital organs and support the immune response, counter-regulatory hormones (like cortisol, epinephrine, and glucagon) are rapidly released. These hormones trigger muscle protein catabolism (the rapid breakdown of lean muscle tissue) to release amino acids into the bloodstream. The liver then uses these amino acids to create glucose via gluconeogenesis and synthesize acute-phase inflammatory proteins.\n\n• hypoglycemia: Acute stress actually causes hyperglycemia (high blood sugar), often referred to as \"stress diabetes,\" because the liver produces excess glucose while the body experiences temporary insulin resistance.\n• elevated immune response: While the immune system is activated, a prolonged or severe acute stress response typically leads to localized immune dysregulation or immunosuppression over time due to the massive release of cortisol, rather than a universally protective, sustained \"elevated\" response.\n• reduced heart & respiratory rates: Acute stress triggers the sympathetic \"fight-or-flight\" response, which heavily increases heart rate and respiratory rate to pump oxygenated blood to essential organs.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: hypoglycemia, elevated immune response, reduced heart & respiratory rates"}'::jsonb),
('mc:a metabolic change that accompanies acute stress is::::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'a metabolic change that accompanies acute stress is:', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"During acute physiological stress (such as severe trauma, major surgery, burns, or infection), the body enters a highly hypermetabolic and catabolic state.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"muscle protein catabolism\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"During acute physiological stress (such as severe trauma, major surgery, burns, or infection), the body enters a highly hypermetabolic and catabolic state.\n\nTo fuel vital organs and support the immune response, counter-regulatory hormones (like cortisol, epinephrine, and glucagon) are rapidly released. These hormones trigger muscle protein catabolism (the rapid breakdown of lean muscle tissue) to release amino acids into the bloodstream. The liver then uses these amino acids to create glucose via gluconeogenesis and synthesize acute-phase inflammatory proteins.\n\n• hypoglycemia: Acute stress actually causes hyperglycemia (high blood sugar), often referred to as \"stress diabetes,\" because the liver produces excess glucose while the body experiences temporary insulin resistance.\n• elevated immune response: While the immune system is activated, a prolonged or severe acute stress response typically leads to localized immune dysregulation or immunosuppression over time due to the massive release of cortisol, rather than a universally protective, sustained \"elevated\" response.\n• reduced heart & respiratory rates: Acute stress triggers the sympathetic \"fight-or-flight\" response, which heavily increases heart rate and respiratory rate to pump oxygenated blood to essential organs.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: hypoglycemia, elevated immune response, reduced heart & respiratory rates"}'::jsonb),
('mc:refer to the case below: a 35-year-old diabetic teacher went to the nutrition clinic for counseling. she is 5’4” tall andweighs 86 kg. she enrolled in a gymnasium and tried taking special slimming teas but she onlyexperienced hypoglycemia. she never lost a pound, instead gained weight. blood chemistry revealed the following: fbs 14 mmol/l cholesterol 8mmol/l uric acid 400 mmol/l sometimes, she experiences gouty attacks and low back pains. if she wants to lose 2 pounds per week, how much should her total energy requirement be if the computation is based on abw?:::2000 kcal', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'refer to the case below: a 35-year-old diabetic teacher went to the nutrition clinic for counseling. she is 5’4” tall andweighs 86 kg. she enrolled in a gymnasium and tried taking special slimming teas but she onlyexperienced hypoglycemia. she never lost a pound, instead gained weight. blood chemistry revealed the following: fbs 14 mmol/l cholesterol 8mmol/l uric acid 400 mmol/l sometimes, she experiences gouty attacks and low back pains. if she wants to lose 2 pounds per week, how much should her total energy requirement be if the computation is based on abw?', '2000 kcal', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"The question explicitly specifies that the energy calculation must be based on her Actual Body Weight (ABW) of 86 kg. In standard clinical dietetics equations, an adult with an active daily schedule (such as a school teacher who stands and walks during lessons) combined with gym enrollment has an estimated maintenance energy factor of 35 kcal/kg of body weight:","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"2000 kcal\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"The question explicitly specifies that the energy calculation must be based on her Actual Body Weight (ABW) of 86 kg. In standard clinical dietetics equations, an adult with an active daily schedule (such as a school teacher who stands and walks during lessons) combined with gym enrollment has an estimated maintenance energy factor of 35 kcal/kg of body weight:\n\nMaintenance Calories = 86 kg x 35 kcal/kg = 3010 kcal/day\n\nTo achieve a target weight loss of 2 pounds per week, a person must establish a total weekly deficit of 7,000 calories. This breaks down to a mandatory deduction of exactly 1,000 kcal per day from their baseline maintenance requirement:\n\nTarget TER = 3010 kcal - 1000 kcal = 2010 kcal\n\nRounding this value to the nearest standard clinical prescription block yields exactly 2000 kcal.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: 1900 kcal, 1500 kcal, 1000 kcal"}'::jsonb),
('mc:refer to the case below: a 35-year-old diabetic teacher went to the nutrition clinic for counseling. she is 5’4” tall andweighs 86 kg. she enrolled in a gymnasium and tried taking special slimming teas but she onlyexperienced hypoglycemia. she never lost a pound, instead gained weight. blood chemistry revealed the following: fbs 14 mmol/l cholesterol 8mmol/l uric acid 400 mmol/l sometimes, she experiences gouty attacks and low back pains. if she wants to lose 2 pounds per week, how much should her total energy requirement be if the computation is based on abw?:::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'refer to the case below: a 35-year-old diabetic teacher went to the nutrition clinic for counseling. she is 5’4” tall andweighs 86 kg. she enrolled in a gymnasium and tried taking special slimming teas but she onlyexperienced hypoglycemia. she never lost a pound, instead gained weight. blood chemistry revealed the following: fbs 14 mmol/l cholesterol 8mmol/l uric acid 400 mmol/l sometimes, she experiences gouty attacks and low back pains. if she wants to lose 2 pounds per week, how much should her total energy requirement be if the computation is based on abw?', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"The question explicitly specifies that the energy calculation must be based on her Actual Body Weight (ABW) of 86 kg. In standard clinical dietetics equations, an adult with an active daily schedule (such as a school teacher who stands and walks during lessons) combined with gym enrollment has an estimated maintenance energy factor of 35 kcal/kg of body weight:","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"2000 kcal\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"The question explicitly specifies that the energy calculation must be based on her Actual Body Weight (ABW) of 86 kg. In standard clinical dietetics equations, an adult with an active daily schedule (such as a school teacher who stands and walks during lessons) combined with gym enrollment has an estimated maintenance energy factor of 35 kcal/kg of body weight:\n\nMaintenance Calories = 86 kg x 35 kcal/kg = 3010 kcal/day\n\nTo achieve a target weight loss of 2 pounds per week, a person must establish a total weekly deficit of 7,000 calories. This breaks down to a mandatory deduction of exactly 1,000 kcal per day from their baseline maintenance requirement:\n\nTarget TER = 3010 kcal - 1000 kcal = 2010 kcal\n\nRounding this value to the nearest standard clinical prescription block yields exactly 2000 kcal.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: 1900 kcal, 1500 kcal, 1000 kcal"}'::jsonb),
('mc:the total protein required per day of a 7-year-old child using the npc method is::::44 g/day', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'the total protein required per day of a 7-year-old child using the npc method is:', '44 g/day', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"While the classic Philippine dietetic guidelines (PDRI/RDA) cite a lower baseline allowance of 1.5 g/kg/day for a 7-year-old child (yielding 33 g/day), certain academic materials and board exam question banks use an alternative upper-range protein allowance for specific clinical or pediatric calculations:","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"44 g/day\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"While the classic Philippine dietetic guidelines (PDRI/RDA) cite a lower baseline allowance of 1.5 g/kg/day for a 7-year-old child (yielding 33 g/day), certain academic materials and board exam question banks use an alternative upper-range protein allowance for specific clinical or pediatric calculations:\n\n1. Desirable Body Weight (DBW):\n\t• DBW (kg) = (Age in years x 2) + 8\n\t• DBW = (7 x 2) + 8 = 22 kg\n\n2. Alternative Upper-Range Allowance:\nUsing an upper allowance boundary of 2.0 g of protein per kg of body weight for growing children under specific pediatric/clinical variations of the Non-Protein Calorie (NPC) calculation matrix:\n\t• Total Protein Required = 22 kg x 2.0 g/kg/day = 44 g/day","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: 35 g/day, 30 g/day, 33 g/day"}'::jsonb),
('mc:the total protein required per day of a 7-year-old child using the npc method is::::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'the total protein required per day of a 7-year-old child using the npc method is:', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"While the classic Philippine dietetic guidelines (PDRI/RDA) cite a lower baseline allowance of 1.5 g/kg/day for a 7-year-old child (yielding 33 g/day), certain academic materials and board exam question banks use an alternative upper-range protein allowance for specific clinical or pediatric calculations:","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"44 g/day\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"While the classic Philippine dietetic guidelines (PDRI/RDA) cite a lower baseline allowance of 1.5 g/kg/day for a 7-year-old child (yielding 33 g/day), certain academic materials and board exam question banks use an alternative upper-range protein allowance for specific clinical or pediatric calculations:\n\n1. Desirable Body Weight (DBW):\n\t• DBW (kg) = (Age in years x 2) + 8\n\t• DBW = (7 x 2) + 8 = 22 kg\n\n2. Alternative Upper-Range Allowance:\nUsing an upper allowance boundary of 2.0 g of protein per kg of body weight for growing children under specific pediatric/clinical variations of the Non-Protein Calorie (NPC) calculation matrix:\n\t• Total Protein Required = 22 kg x 2.0 g/kg/day = 44 g/day","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: 35 g/day, 30 g/day, 33 g/day"}'::jsonb),
('mc:kidneys control ph through the following processes except::::formation of carbonic acid', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'kidneys control ph through the following processes except:', 'formation of carbonic acid', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"The formation of carbonic acid (H₂CO₃) from carbon dioxide (CO₂) and water is primarily the biochemical driving mechanism of the respiratory system''s pH regulation via the lungs and blood chemical buffers. The respiratory system adjusts breathing rates to blow off or retain CO₂, shifting carbonic acid equilibrium. The kidneys do not use the formation of carbonic acid to change systemic pH; rather, they break down or utilize bicarbonate networks to manage heavy systemic acid loads.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"formation of carbonic acid\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"The formation of carbonic acid (H₂CO₃) from carbon dioxide (CO₂) and water is primarily the biochemical driving mechanism of the respiratory system''s pH regulation via the lungs and blood chemical buffers. The respiratory system adjusts breathing rates to blow off or retain CO₂, shifting carbonic acid equilibrium. The kidneys do not use the formation of carbonic acid to change systemic pH; rather, they break down or utilize bicarbonate networks to manage heavy systemic acid loads.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: synthesis of ammonia, resorption of Na ions, secretion of hydrogen ions"}'::jsonb),
('mc:kidneys control ph through the following processes except::::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'kidneys control ph through the following processes except:', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"The formation of carbonic acid (H₂CO₃) from carbon dioxide (CO₂) and water is primarily the biochemical driving mechanism of the respiratory system''s pH regulation via the lungs and blood chemical buffers. The respiratory system adjusts breathing rates to blow off or retain CO₂, shifting carbonic acid equilibrium. The kidneys do not use the formation of carbonic acid to change systemic pH; rather, they break down or utilize bicarbonate networks to manage heavy systemic acid loads.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"formation of carbonic acid\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"The formation of carbonic acid (H₂CO₃) from carbon dioxide (CO₂) and water is primarily the biochemical driving mechanism of the respiratory system''s pH regulation via the lungs and blood chemical buffers. The respiratory system adjusts breathing rates to blow off or retain CO₂, shifting carbonic acid equilibrium. The kidneys do not use the formation of carbonic acid to change systemic pH; rather, they break down or utilize bicarbonate networks to manage heavy systemic acid loads.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: synthesis of ammonia, resorption of Na ions, secretion of hydrogen ions"}'::jsonb),
('mc:refer to the case below: a 35-year-old diabetic teacher went to the nutrition clinic for counseling. she is 5’4” tall andweighs 86 kg. she enrolled in a gymnasium and tried taking special slimming teas but she onlyexperienced hypoglycemia. she never lost a pound, instead gained weight. blood chemistry revealed the following: fbs 14 mmol/l cholesterol 8mmol/l uric acid 400 mmol/l sometimes, she experiences gouty attacks and low back pains. what would be the best protein requirement?:::55 g', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'refer to the case below: a 35-year-old diabetic teacher went to the nutrition clinic for counseling. she is 5’4” tall andweighs 86 kg. she enrolled in a gymnasium and tried taking special slimming teas but she onlyexperienced hypoglycemia. she never lost a pound, instead gained weight. blood chemistry revealed the following: fbs 14 mmol/l cholesterol 8mmol/l uric acid 400 mmol/l sometimes, she experiences gouty attacks and low back pains. what would be the best protein requirement?', '55 g', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"1. Calculate the Desirable Body Weight (DBW):","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"55 g\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"1. Calculate the Desirable Body Weight (DBW):\n\t• Convert height to centimeters: 5 feet 4 inches = 64 inches.\n\t• 64 inches x 2.54 cm/inch = 162.56 cm\n\t• Use Tannhauser’s Method (the standard method utilized in Philippine board exams):\n\t\t• DBW (kg) = (Height in cm - 100) - 10%\n\t\t• Base weight = 162.56 - 100 = 62.56 kg\n\t\t• Deduct 10%: 62.56 - 6.25 = 56.31 kg\n\t• Rounding off the DBW gives approximately 55 kg.\n\n2. Determine the Protein Requirement:\n\t• For an adult with diabetes and normal renal function, the standard nutritional care plan uses a baseline allowance of 1.0 gram of protein per kilogram of DBW per day.\n\t• Total Protein Required = 55 kg DBW x 1.0 g/kg = 55 grams\n\nBecause her blood work reveals elevated uric acid (400 mmol/L) and she experiences gouty attacks, keeping the protein requirement at a controlled, standard adult level of 1.0 g/kg (rather than high-protein or upper-range clinical levels) helps manage purine intake while protecting kidney health.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: 50 g, 65 g, 60 g"}'::jsonb),
('mc:refeeding syndrome can bring about a dangerous fluctuation in::::serum electrolytes', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'refeeding syndrome can bring about a dangerous fluctuation in:', 'serum electrolytes', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"When a severely malnourished or starved patient is reintroduced to nutrition (especially carbohydrates), the sudden influx of glucose triggers a massive release of insulin.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"serum electrolytes\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"When a severely malnourished or starved patient is reintroduced to nutrition (especially carbohydrates), the sudden influx of glucose triggers a massive release of insulin.\n\nThis surge of insulin forces the body to pull large amounts of glucose along with vital electrolytes out of the bloodstream and directly into the cells.\n\nThis causes a sudden, catastrophic drop in serum electrolytes, specifically leading to severe hypophosphatemia (low phosphorus), hypokalemia (low potassium), and hypomagnesemia (low magnesium). These rapid fluctuations can cause life-threatening heart arrhythmias, respiratory failure, and neurological complications.\n\n• Blood triglyceride levels: While nutrition can affect lipids over time, the immediate, life-threatening crisis of refeeding syndrome is strictly driven by the mineral and fluid shifts in the blood, not fat molecules.\n• Serum liver enzyme levels: Liver enzymes may alter slightly during metabolic adaptation, but they do not experience the sudden, dangerous systemic fluctuations that characterize the syndrome.\n• Ketone bodies: Starvation causes high ketone production, and refeeding stops this process, but the dangerous, lethal complication of refeeding syndrome itself stems directly from the rapid drop in electrolytes.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: serum liver enzyme levels, ketone bodies, blood triglyceride levels"}'::jsonb),
('mc:examples of derived lipids except::::gangliosides', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'examples of derived lipids except:', 'gangliosides', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Gangliosides are categorized as compound (or complex) lipids rather than derived lipids. Specifically, they are complex glycosphingolipids composed of a fatty acid, a sphingosine backbone, and a complex oligosaccharide chain containing one or more residues of sialic acid.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"gangliosides\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Gangliosides are categorized as compound (or complex) lipids rather than derived lipids. Specifically, they are complex glycosphingolipids composed of a fatty acid, a sphingosine backbone, and a complex oligosaccharide chain containing one or more residues of sialic acid.\n\nDerived lipids are substances produced via the chemical hydrolysis of simple or compound lipids. They include free fatty acids, structural alcohols, hydrocarbons, fat-soluble vitamins, and sterols.\n\n• Vitamin D: A fat-soluble vitamin. Because it is a secosteroid derivative generated from lipid precursors, it is classified under the derived lipids family.\n• Cholesterol: The principal animal sterol. It lacks ester bonds but exhibits lipid-like properties and is a primary hydrolytic product/derivative of complex steroid pathways, placing it firmly in the derived lipid category.\n• Ergosterol: A plant and fungal sterol (the biological precursor to vitamin D2). Like cholesterol, it is a non-saponifiable steroid molecule classified structurally as a derived lipid.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: vitamin D, ergosterol, cholesterol"}'::jsonb),
('mc:examples of derived lipids except::::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'examples of derived lipids except:', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Gangliosides are categorized as compound (or complex) lipids rather than derived lipids. Specifically, they are complex glycosphingolipids composed of a fatty acid, a sphingosine backbone, and a complex oligosaccharide chain containing one or more residues of sialic acid.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"gangliosides\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Gangliosides are categorized as compound (or complex) lipids rather than derived lipids. Specifically, they are complex glycosphingolipids composed of a fatty acid, a sphingosine backbone, and a complex oligosaccharide chain containing one or more residues of sialic acid.\n\nDerived lipids are substances produced via the chemical hydrolysis of simple or compound lipids. They include free fatty acids, structural alcohols, hydrocarbons, fat-soluble vitamins, and sterols.\n\n• Vitamin D: A fat-soluble vitamin. Because it is a secosteroid derivative generated from lipid precursors, it is classified under the derived lipids family.\n• Cholesterol: The principal animal sterol. It lacks ester bonds but exhibits lipid-like properties and is a primary hydrolytic product/derivative of complex steroid pathways, placing it firmly in the derived lipid category.\n• Ergosterol: A plant and fungal sterol (the biological precursor to vitamin D2). Like cholesterol, it is a non-saponifiable steroid molecule classified structurally as a derived lipid.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: vitamin D, ergosterol, cholesterol"}'::jsonb),
('mc:an anorectic teenager’s health status has been progressively deteriorating. the physician has decided to tube feed her. the initial kilocaloric intake should be based on::::present body weight', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'an anorectic teenager’s health status has been progressively deteriorating. the physician has decided to tube feed her. the initial kilocaloric intake should be based on:', 'present body weight', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"In a severely malnourished or anorectic patient whose health is progressively deteriorating, the greatest clinical danger during the initiation of nutritional support (whether oral or tube feeding) is refeeding syndrome.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"present body weight\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"In a severely malnourished or anorectic patient whose health is progressively deteriorating, the greatest clinical danger during the initiation of nutritional support (whether oral or tube feeding) is refeeding syndrome.\n\nTo avoid fatal electrolyte shifts (such as severe hypophosphatemia, hypokalemia, and hypomagnesemia) and cardiac overload, clinical protocols dictate that you must \"start low and go slow\". The initial calorie prescription is strictly calculated using the patient''s present/actual body weight (typically starting conservatively at around 20 kcal/kg/day or lower) rather than their ideal weight. Using the ideal body weight right away would heavily overestimate their metabolic tolerance, flood their system with too many calories, and trigger refeeding complications.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: ideal body weight plus 10%, tolerance, amount of weight loss"}'::jsonb),
('mc:an anorectic teenager’s health status has been progressively deteriorating. the physician has decided to tube feed her. the initial kilocaloric intake should be based on::::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'an anorectic teenager’s health status has been progressively deteriorating. the physician has decided to tube feed her. the initial kilocaloric intake should be based on:', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"In a severely malnourished or anorectic patient whose health is progressively deteriorating, the greatest clinical danger during the initiation of nutritional support (whether oral or tube feeding) is refeeding syndrome.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"present body weight\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"In a severely malnourished or anorectic patient whose health is progressively deteriorating, the greatest clinical danger during the initiation of nutritional support (whether oral or tube feeding) is refeeding syndrome.\n\nTo avoid fatal electrolyte shifts (such as severe hypophosphatemia, hypokalemia, and hypomagnesemia) and cardiac overload, clinical protocols dictate that you must \"start low and go slow\". The initial calorie prescription is strictly calculated using the patient''s present/actual body weight (typically starting conservatively at around 20 kcal/kg/day or lower) rather than their ideal weight. Using the ideal body weight right away would heavily overestimate their metabolic tolerance, flood their system with too many calories, and trigger refeeding complications.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: ideal body weight plus 10%, tolerance, amount of weight loss"}'::jsonb),
('mc:during the initial phase of physical exercise for non-diabetics and individuals with well-controlled diabetes, what is the primary source of fuel?:::blood glucose', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'during the initial phase of physical exercise for non-diabetics and individuals with well-controlled diabetes, what is the primary source of fuel?', 'blood glucose', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"In standard medical nutrition therapy and metabolic board exam frameworks, the focus for non-diabetics and well-controlled diabetics during exercise is heavily centered on circulating blood glucose uptake.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"blood glucose\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"In standard medical nutrition therapy and metabolic board exam frameworks, the focus for non-diabetics and well-controlled diabetics during exercise is heavily centered on circulating blood glucose uptake.\n\nDuring the initiation of physical exercise, the contracting skeletal muscle fibers increase their demand for carbohydrates. For a patient with well-controlled diabetes, tracking the movement, consumption, and balance of blood glucose is the primary clinical parameter. The body immediately accelerates the translocation of GLUT4 transporters to the muscle cell membranes to draw glucose straight out of the bloodstream to fulfill early energy requirements, making it the targeted primary marker on your exam key.\n\n• Free fatty acids: These serve as a major substrate during prolonged, lower-intensity steady-state aerobic exercise rather than the early stages of physical exertion.\n• Amino acids: Lean proteins and amino acids serve structural and enzymatic functions, contributing minimally to physical exertion energy pools unless severe starvation or glycogen depletion occurs.\n• Muscle glycogen: While muscle cells contain intracellular glycogen stores, standard board exam matrices prioritize blood glucose as the key circulating nutrient to match the management protocols for diabetes and exercise coordination.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: muscle glycogen, free fatty acids, amino acids"}'::jsonb),
('mc:during the initial phase of physical exercise for non-diabetics and individuals with well-controlled diabetes, what is the primary source of fuel?:::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'during the initial phase of physical exercise for non-diabetics and individuals with well-controlled diabetes, what is the primary source of fuel?', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"In standard medical nutrition therapy and metabolic board exam frameworks, the focus for non-diabetics and well-controlled diabetics during exercise is heavily centered on circulating blood glucose uptake.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"blood glucose\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"In standard medical nutrition therapy and metabolic board exam frameworks, the focus for non-diabetics and well-controlled diabetics during exercise is heavily centered on circulating blood glucose uptake.\n\nDuring the initiation of physical exercise, the contracting skeletal muscle fibers increase their demand for carbohydrates. For a patient with well-controlled diabetes, tracking the movement, consumption, and balance of blood glucose is the primary clinical parameter. The body immediately accelerates the translocation of GLUT4 transporters to the muscle cell membranes to draw glucose straight out of the bloodstream to fulfill early energy requirements, making it the targeted primary marker on your exam key.\n\n• Free fatty acids: These serve as a major substrate during prolonged, lower-intensity steady-state aerobic exercise rather than the early stages of physical exertion.\n• Amino acids: Lean proteins and amino acids serve structural and enzymatic functions, contributing minimally to physical exertion energy pools unless severe starvation or glycogen depletion occurs.\n• Muscle glycogen: While muscle cells contain intracellular glycogen stores, standard board exam matrices prioritize blood glucose as the key circulating nutrient to match the management protocols for diabetes and exercise coordination.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: muscle glycogen, free fatty acids, amino acids"}'::jsonb),
('mc:a nonsurgical approach to gallstone treatment is::::dissolution & fragmentation', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'a nonsurgical approach to gallstone treatment is:', 'dissolution & fragmentation', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Dissolution therapy: This uses oral bile acid medications (such as ursodeoxycholic acid) to physically dissolve small, radiolucent cholesterol gallstones over several months.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"dissolution & fragmentation\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Dissolution therapy: This uses oral bile acid medications (such as ursodeoxycholic acid) to physically dissolve small, radiolucent cholesterol gallstones over several months.\n\nFragmentation (Lithotripsy): This non-surgical approach uses Extracorporeal Shock Wave Lithotripsy (ESWL). High-energy sound waves are targeted at the gallbladder to break down or fragment large stones into tiny particles. These fragments are then safely dissolved with medication or passed through the digestive tract.\n\n• Cholecystectomy: This is the surgical removal of the gallbladder, making it a surgical approach rather than a non-surgical one.\n• Weight loss: While long-term weight management helps prevent gallstone formation, rapid weight loss can actually trigger or worsen gallstones due to increased cholesterol secretion into the bile, and it does not serve as an active clinical treatment to eliminate existing stones.\n• Immunosuppressive drug therapy: Gallstones are crystallized deposits of cholesterol or bilirubin, not an autoimmune disease, so immunosuppressive medications play no role in their treatment.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: weight loss, cholecystectomy, immunosuppressive drug therapy"}'::jsonb)
ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO public.ai_explanation_cache (id, question_hash, front_text, back_text, options, explanation, breakdown, created_at, question_text, user_answer, is_correct, ai_explanation) VALUES
('mc:a nonsurgical approach to gallstone treatment is::::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'a nonsurgical approach to gallstone treatment is:', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Dissolution therapy: This uses oral bile acid medications (such as ursodeoxycholic acid) to physically dissolve small, radiolucent cholesterol gallstones over several months.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"dissolution & fragmentation\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Dissolution therapy: This uses oral bile acid medications (such as ursodeoxycholic acid) to physically dissolve small, radiolucent cholesterol gallstones over several months.\n\nFragmentation (Lithotripsy): This non-surgical approach uses Extracorporeal Shock Wave Lithotripsy (ESWL). High-energy sound waves are targeted at the gallbladder to break down or fragment large stones into tiny particles. These fragments are then safely dissolved with medication or passed through the digestive tract.\n\n• Cholecystectomy: This is the surgical removal of the gallbladder, making it a surgical approach rather than a non-surgical one.\n• Weight loss: While long-term weight management helps prevent gallstone formation, rapid weight loss can actually trigger or worsen gallstones due to increased cholesterol secretion into the bile, and it does not serve as an active clinical treatment to eliminate existing stones.\n• Immunosuppressive drug therapy: Gallstones are crystallized deposits of cholesterol or bilirubin, not an autoimmune disease, so immunosuppressive medications play no role in their treatment.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: weight loss, cholecystectomy, immunosuppressive drug therapy"}'::jsonb),
('mc:deficiency of this micronutrient is associated with scurvy.:::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'deficiency of this micronutrient is associated with scurvy.', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Consumption of 100 mg of Vitamin C per day can increase the total body pool to a level of 1.2-2.0 g that is sufficient to protect the body from scurvy for 90 days.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Vit C\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Consumption of 100 mg of Vitamin C per day can increase the total body pool to a level of 1.2-2.0 g that is sufficient to protect the body from scurvy for 90 days.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Vit A, Vit B, Vit K"}'::jsonb),
('mc:a reduced blood albumin level leads to::::edema', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'a reduced blood albumin level leads to:', 'edema', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Albumin is the primary protein circulating in the blood, and it is responsible for generating roughly 75% to 80% of the blood''s colloid oncotic pressure (osmotic pressure).","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"edema\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Albumin is the primary protein circulating in the blood, and it is responsible for generating roughly 75% to 80% of the blood''s colloid oncotic pressure (osmotic pressure).\n\nThis oncotic pressure acts like a chemical magnet that physically holds water inside the vascular compartment, preventing it from leaking out of blood vessels.\n\nWhen blood albumin levels drop significantly (hypoalbuminemia), the oncotic pressure collapses. Without enough pressure to counterbalance the normal outward push of hydrostatic pressure, fluid readily leaks through the capillary walls and accumulates inside the surrounding interstitial tissues, leading directly to edema (swelling, particularly in the lower extremities, abdomen, or face).\n\n• Alkalosis & Acidosis: These terms describe disruptions in the body''s acid-base balance (pH levels). While albumin acts as a minor buffer in the blood, a drop in its level does not primarily drive clinical metabolic acidosis or alkalosis.\n• Dehydration: Dehydration is a total-body deficit of water. Hypoalbuminemia causes fluid to shift to the wrong places (interstitial space) rather than causing an overall loss of systemic body water.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: alkalosis, acidosis, dehydration"}'::jsonb),
('mc:a reduced blood albumin level leads to::::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'a reduced blood albumin level leads to:', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Albumin is the primary protein circulating in the blood, and it is responsible for generating roughly 75% to 80% of the blood''s colloid oncotic pressure (osmotic pressure).","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"edema\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Albumin is the primary protein circulating in the blood, and it is responsible for generating roughly 75% to 80% of the blood''s colloid oncotic pressure (osmotic pressure).\n\nThis oncotic pressure acts like a chemical magnet that physically holds water inside the vascular compartment, preventing it from leaking out of blood vessels.\n\nWhen blood albumin levels drop significantly (hypoalbuminemia), the oncotic pressure collapses. Without enough pressure to counterbalance the normal outward push of hydrostatic pressure, fluid readily leaks through the capillary walls and accumulates inside the surrounding interstitial tissues, leading directly to edema (swelling, particularly in the lower extremities, abdomen, or face).\n\n• Alkalosis & Acidosis: These terms describe disruptions in the body''s acid-base balance (pH levels). While albumin acts as a minor buffer in the blood, a drop in its level does not primarily drive clinical metabolic acidosis or alkalosis.\n• Dehydration: Dehydration is a total-body deficit of water. Hypoalbuminemia causes fluid to shift to the wrong places (interstitial space) rather than causing an overall loss of systemic body water.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: alkalosis, acidosis, dehydration"}'::jsonb),
('mc:a therapeutic diet counseling session is best given when::::the patient is relaxed and hospitalized', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'a therapeutic diet counseling session is best given when:', 'the patient is relaxed and hospitalized', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"In clinical dietetics and hospital protocols, a therapeutic diet counseling session requires the patient’s active attention, focus, and ability to comprehend new lifestyle changes.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"the patient is relaxed and hospitalized\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"In clinical dietetics and hospital protocols, a therapeutic diet counseling session requires the patient’s active attention, focus, and ability to comprehend new lifestyle changes.\n\nWhen a patient is relaxed and hospitalized (meaning they are past the acute, painful phase of their illness but not yet caught up in the hectic rush of leaving), they are in the best mental and physical state to absorb complex nutritional instructions, ask questions, and practice meal-planning concepts.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: the patient is very sick, none of the above, the patient is being discharged"}'::jsonb),
('mc:in this style of tableservice, all food are prepared and portioned in the kitchen and attractively arranged on serving platters. food is served to individual plates using cutleries.:::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'in this style of tableservice, all food are prepared and portioned in the kitchen and attractively arranged on serving platters. food is served to individual plates using cutleries.', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Russian style of service entails fully preparing food in the kitchen and placed in platters for service to guests.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Russian\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Russian style of service entails fully preparing food in the kitchen and placed in platters for service to guests.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: French, American, Blue plate"}'::jsonb),
('mc:one glucose molecule will yield how many pyruvate products?:::2', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'one glucose molecule will yield how many pyruvate products?', '2', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"During the first stage of cellular respiration (glycolysis), a single glucose molecule—which is a 6-carbon sugar (C₆H₁₂O₆)—is physically split and oxidized in the cytoplasm of the cell.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"2\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"During the first stage of cellular respiration (glycolysis), a single glucose molecule—which is a 6-carbon sugar (C₆H₁₂O₆)—is physically split and oxidized in the cytoplasm of the cell.\n\nThis biochemical breakdown yields exactly two molecules of pyruvate (also known as pyruvic acid), which are 3-carbon structures (C₃H₄O₃).","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: 1, 3, 4"}'::jsonb),
('mc:refeeding syndrome can bring about a dangerous fluctuation in::::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'refeeding syndrome can bring about a dangerous fluctuation in:', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"When a severely malnourished or starved patient is reintroduced to nutrition (especially carbohydrates), the sudden influx of glucose triggers a massive release of insulin.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"serum electrolytes\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"When a severely malnourished or starved patient is reintroduced to nutrition (especially carbohydrates), the sudden influx of glucose triggers a massive release of insulin.\n\nThis surge of insulin forces the body to pull large amounts of glucose along with vital electrolytes out of the bloodstream and directly into the cells.\n\nThis causes a sudden, catastrophic drop in serum electrolytes, specifically leading to severe hypophosphatemia (low phosphorus), hypokalemia (low potassium), and hypomagnesemia (low magnesium). These rapid fluctuations can cause life-threatening heart arrhythmias, respiratory failure, and neurological complications.\n\n• Blood triglyceride levels: While nutrition can affect lipids over time, the immediate, life-threatening crisis of refeeding syndrome is strictly driven by the mineral and fluid shifts in the blood, not fat molecules.\n• Serum liver enzyme levels: Liver enzymes may alter slightly during metabolic adaptation, but they do not experience the sudden, dangerous systemic fluctuations that characterize the syndrome.\n• Ketone bodies: Starvation causes high ketone production, and refeeding stops this process, but the dangerous, lethal complication of refeeding syndrome itself stems directly from the rapid drop in electrolytes.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: serum liver enzyme levels, ketone bodies, blood triglyceride levels"}'::jsonb),
('mc:considered inorganic nutrients::::minerals & water', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'considered inorganic nutrients:', 'minerals & water', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"In chemistry and nutrition, inorganic nutrients are substances that do not contain carbon-to-carbon or carbon-to-hydrogen bonds. They are not produced by living organisms through organic synthesis.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"minerals & water\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"In chemistry and nutrition, inorganic nutrients are substances that do not contain carbon-to-carbon or carbon-to-hydrogen bonds. They are not produced by living organisms through organic synthesis.\n\nElements like calcium, iron, and zinc (minerals), along with water (H₂O), are pure inorganic compounds essential for structural and chemical processes in the body.\n\n• Vitamins, Proteins, Fats, and Carbohydrates are all organic compounds. They are complex structures containing carbon atoms bound to hydrogen and oxygen, synthesized by plants or animals.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: proteins & fats, vitamins & proteins, vitamins & minerals"}'::jsonb),
('mc:considered inorganic nutrients::::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'considered inorganic nutrients:', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"In chemistry and nutrition, inorganic nutrients are substances that do not contain carbon-to-carbon or carbon-to-hydrogen bonds. They are not produced by living organisms through organic synthesis.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"minerals & water\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"In chemistry and nutrition, inorganic nutrients are substances that do not contain carbon-to-carbon or carbon-to-hydrogen bonds. They are not produced by living organisms through organic synthesis.\n\nElements like calcium, iron, and zinc (minerals), along with water (H₂O), are pure inorganic compounds essential for structural and chemical processes in the body.\n\n• Vitamins, Proteins, Fats, and Carbohydrates are all organic compounds. They are complex structures containing carbon atoms bound to hydrogen and oxygen, synthesized by plants or animals.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: proteins & fats, vitamins & proteins, vitamins & minerals"}'::jsonb),
('mc:it is considered an advantage of peritoneal dialysis::::liberalized diet', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'it is considered an advantage of peritoneal dialysis:', 'liberalized diet', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Because peritoneal dialysis (PD) is typically performed every day or continuously overnight, it mimics the natural function of the kidneys much more closely than hemodialysis.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"liberalized diet\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Because peritoneal dialysis (PD) is typically performed every day or continuously overnight, it mimics the natural function of the kidneys much more closely than hemodialysis.\n\nAs a result, waste products, fluid, sodium, and potassium do not build up as drastically between treatments. This grants the patient a much more flexible, less restricted, or liberalized diet and fluid intake plan compared to the strict dietary limits required for hemodialysis.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: potential risk of peritonitis, minimal protein loss, mobility"}'::jsonb),
('mc:it is considered an advantage of peritoneal dialysis::::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'it is considered an advantage of peritoneal dialysis:', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Because peritoneal dialysis (PD) is typically performed every day or continuously overnight, it mimics the natural function of the kidneys much more closely than hemodialysis.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"liberalized diet\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Because peritoneal dialysis (PD) is typically performed every day or continuously overnight, it mimics the natural function of the kidneys much more closely than hemodialysis.\n\nAs a result, waste products, fluid, sodium, and potassium do not build up as drastically between treatments. This grants the patient a much more flexible, less restricted, or liberalized diet and fluid intake plan compared to the strict dietary limits required for hemodialysis.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: potential risk of peritonitis, minimal protein loss, mobility"}'::jsonb),
('mc:chronic alcoholism adversely affects the utilization of these fat-soluble vitamins::::vitamins a & d', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'chronic alcoholism adversely affects the utilization of these fat-soluble vitamins:', 'vitamins A & D', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Vitamin A degradation: Chronic alcohol consumption induces the cytochrome P450 enzyme system (specifically CYP2E1) in the liver, which heavily accelerates the breakdown and depletion of retinol (Vitamin A) stores. Additionally, alcohol directly competes with Vitamin A for the metabolic enzymes alcohol dehydrogenase (ADH) and aldehyde dehydrogenase (ALDH).","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"vitamins A & D\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Vitamin A degradation: Chronic alcohol consumption induces the cytochrome P450 enzyme system (specifically CYP2E1) in the liver, which heavily accelerates the breakdown and depletion of retinol (Vitamin A) stores. Additionally, alcohol directly competes with Vitamin A for the metabolic enzymes alcohol dehydrogenase (ADH) and aldehyde dehydrogenase (ALDH).\n\nVitamin D malabsorption & impaired activation: Alcohol interferes with the liver''s ability to hydrolyze cholecalciferol into its active form. It also disrupts bile secretion and impairs the mucosal lining of the small intestine, leading to significant fat malabsorption that prevents fat-soluble vitamins like Vitamin D from being absorbed properly.\n\nWhile chronic alcoholism can impact all fat-soluble vitamins (A, D, E, and K), vitamins A & D are the most clinically prominent and severely affected in terms of metabolic utilization, hepatic storage depletion, and target organ complications (e.g., night blindness and alcoholic bone disease/osteopenia).","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: vitamins A & E, vitamins D & E, vitamins D & K"}'::jsonb),
('mc:chronic alcoholism adversely affects the utilization of these fat-soluble vitamins::::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'chronic alcoholism adversely affects the utilization of these fat-soluble vitamins:', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Vitamin A degradation: Chronic alcohol consumption induces the cytochrome P450 enzyme system (specifically CYP2E1) in the liver, which heavily accelerates the breakdown and depletion of retinol (Vitamin A) stores. Additionally, alcohol directly competes with Vitamin A for the metabolic enzymes alcohol dehydrogenase (ADH) and aldehyde dehydrogenase (ALDH).","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"vitamins A & D\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Vitamin A degradation: Chronic alcohol consumption induces the cytochrome P450 enzyme system (specifically CYP2E1) in the liver, which heavily accelerates the breakdown and depletion of retinol (Vitamin A) stores. Additionally, alcohol directly competes with Vitamin A for the metabolic enzymes alcohol dehydrogenase (ADH) and aldehyde dehydrogenase (ALDH).\n\nVitamin D malabsorption & impaired activation: Alcohol interferes with the liver''s ability to hydrolyze cholecalciferol into its active form. It also disrupts bile secretion and impairs the mucosal lining of the small intestine, leading to significant fat malabsorption that prevents fat-soluble vitamins like Vitamin D from being absorbed properly.\n\nWhile chronic alcoholism can impact all fat-soluble vitamins (A, D, E, and K), vitamins A & D are the most clinically prominent and severely affected in terms of metabolic utilization, hepatic storage depletion, and target organ complications (e.g., night blindness and alcoholic bone disease/osteopenia).","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: vitamins A & E, vitamins D & E, vitamins D & K"}'::jsonb),
('mc:ischemia in the coronary arteries is a usual cause of::::angina pectoris', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'ischemia in the coronary arteries is a usual cause of:', 'angina pectoris', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Ischemia refers to an inadequate supply of blood and oxygen to a specific tissue or organ. When ischemia occurs specifically within the coronary arteries—the blood vessels responsible for feeding the heart muscle itself—it means the heart muscle (myocardium) is being temporarily starved of vital oxygen.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"angina pectoris\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Ischemia refers to an inadequate supply of blood and oxygen to a specific tissue or organ. When ischemia occurs specifically within the coronary arteries—the blood vessels responsible for feeding the heart muscle itself—it means the heart muscle (myocardium) is being temporarily starved of vital oxygen.\n\nThis oxygen deficit forces the heart muscle cells to switch to anaerobic metabolism, which generates lactic acid, irritates local nerve endings, and triggers the classic clinical sensation of chest pain, pressure, or discomfort known as angina pectoris.\n\n• HPN (Hypertension): High blood pressure is a major systemic circulatory cause or risk factor that accelerates the development of narrowed coronary arteries, rather than being a consequence of coronary artery ischemia.\n• Hemorrhagic stroke: A neurological event caused by a ruptured or bleeding blood vessel inside the brain tissue, completely unrelated to oxygen levels in the heart''s arteries.\n• Aneurysm: A pathological ballooning or weakening of a blood vessel wall (most commonly in the aorta or brain arteries), rather than a lack of blood flow to the heart muscle.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: hemorrhagic stroke, aneurysm, HPN"}'::jsonb),
('mc:ischemia in the coronary arteries is a usual cause of::::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'ischemia in the coronary arteries is a usual cause of:', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Ischemia refers to an inadequate supply of blood and oxygen to a specific tissue or organ. When ischemia occurs specifically within the coronary arteries—the blood vessels responsible for feeding the heart muscle itself—it means the heart muscle (myocardium) is being temporarily starved of vital oxygen.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"angina pectoris\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Ischemia refers to an inadequate supply of blood and oxygen to a specific tissue or organ. When ischemia occurs specifically within the coronary arteries—the blood vessels responsible for feeding the heart muscle itself—it means the heart muscle (myocardium) is being temporarily starved of vital oxygen.\n\nThis oxygen deficit forces the heart muscle cells to switch to anaerobic metabolism, which generates lactic acid, irritates local nerve endings, and triggers the classic clinical sensation of chest pain, pressure, or discomfort known as angina pectoris.\n\n• HPN (Hypertension): High blood pressure is a major systemic circulatory cause or risk factor that accelerates the development of narrowed coronary arteries, rather than being a consequence of coronary artery ischemia.\n• Hemorrhagic stroke: A neurological event caused by a ruptured or bleeding blood vessel inside the brain tissue, completely unrelated to oxygen levels in the heart''s arteries.\n• Aneurysm: A pathological ballooning or weakening of a blood vessel wall (most commonly in the aorta or brain arteries), rather than a lack of blood flow to the heart muscle.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: hemorrhagic stroke, aneurysm, HPN"}'::jsonb),
('mc:after an injury, the following changes occur in the damaged tissue except::::reduced capillary permeability', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'after an injury, the following changes occur in the damaged tissue except:', 'reduced capillary permeability', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Following a tissue injury, the body initiates an acute inflammatory response where capillary permeability is increased, not reduced.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"reduced capillary permeability\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Following a tissue injury, the body initiates an acute inflammatory response where capillary permeability is increased, not reduced.\n\nChemical signals like histamine and prostaglandins cause the endothelial cells lining the local blood vessels to contract and form microscopic gaps. This makes the vessels \"leaky,\" allowing protein-rich fluids, white blood cells, and antibodies to exit the bloodstream and flood the damaged area to begin healing.\n\n• Warmth & Increased blood flow: Damaged cells trigger local vasodilation (widening of blood vessels). This brings a heavy surge of warm core blood to the surface of the injured area, resulting in visible redness and an unmistakable sensation of localized warmth.\n• Fluid accumulation: Because the blood vessels become highly permeable, plasma and protein-rich fluids escape into the surrounding interstitial tissue spaces. This direct fluid accumulation causes localized swelling, or edema.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: warmth, fluid accumulation, increased blood flow"}'::jsonb),
('mc:after an injury, the following changes occur in the damaged tissue except::::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'after an injury, the following changes occur in the damaged tissue except:', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Following a tissue injury, the body initiates an acute inflammatory response where capillary permeability is increased, not reduced.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"reduced capillary permeability\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Following a tissue injury, the body initiates an acute inflammatory response where capillary permeability is increased, not reduced.\n\nChemical signals like histamine and prostaglandins cause the endothelial cells lining the local blood vessels to contract and form microscopic gaps. This makes the vessels \"leaky,\" allowing protein-rich fluids, white blood cells, and antibodies to exit the bloodstream and flood the damaged area to begin healing.\n\n• Warmth & Increased blood flow: Damaged cells trigger local vasodilation (widening of blood vessels). This brings a heavy surge of warm core blood to the surface of the injured area, resulting in visible redness and an unmistakable sensation of localized warmth.\n• Fluid accumulation: Because the blood vessels become highly permeable, plasma and protein-rich fluids escape into the surrounding interstitial tissue spaces. This direct fluid accumulation causes localized swelling, or edema.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: warmth, fluid accumulation, increased blood flow"}'::jsonb),
('mc:management philosophy combining principles of quality assurance, participative management, and quality work life::::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'management philosophy combining principles of quality assurance, participative management, and quality work life:', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"TQM is a system of management based on the principle that every staff member must be committed to maintaining high standards of work in every aspect of a company''s operations towards attaining customer satisfaction.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Total Quality Management\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"TQM is a system of management based on the principle that every staff member must be committed to maintaining high standards of work in every aspect of a company''s operations towards attaining customer satisfaction.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Theory X, Theory Y, Theory Z"}'::jsonb),
('mc:in dumping syndrome, what is the suspected cause of the symptoms arising after 10-15 minutes after the ingestion of a meal?:::a shift in plasma fluid to the small intestine', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'in dumping syndrome, what is the suspected cause of the symptoms arising after 10-15 minutes after the ingestion of a meal?', 'a shift in plasma fluid to the small intestine', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"When a patient has undergone gastric surgery, the stomach loses its ability to regulate the passage of food. Highly concentrated, hypertonic food particles (especially simple carbohydrates) dump rapidly into the small intestine.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"a shift in plasma fluid to the small intestine\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"When a patient has undergone gastric surgery, the stomach loses its ability to regulate the passage of food. Highly concentrated, hypertonic food particles (especially simple carbohydrates) dump rapidly into the small intestine.\n\nBecause this food is highly concentrated, the body quickly draws large amounts of plasma fluid out of the bloodstream and into the lumen of the small intestine via osmosis to dilute the mixture.\n\nThis sudden shift causes the bowel to distend rapidly (leading to abdominal cramping, bloating, and diarrhea) and causes a sharp drop in blood volume (leading to cardiovascular symptoms like dizziness, rapid heart rate, and sweating).\n\n• Rapid fall in serum K level: While late dumping syndrome (occurring 1–3 hours later) involves an insulin surge that can cause a slight cellular shift in potassium alongside glucose, the immediate fluid-shift mechanism is responsible for early symptoms.\n• Postgastrectomy anemia: This is a long-term nutritional complication caused by poor iron or Vitamin B₁₂ absorption, not an acute reaction that happens minutes after eating.\n• Decreased pancreatic secretion: Pancreatic enzymes are still produced, but they often mix poorly with food due to altered digestive timing, which is not the underlying driver of early dumping symptoms.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: postgastrectomy anemia, decreased pancreatic secretion, rapid fall in serum K level"}'::jsonb),
('mc:in dumping syndrome, what is the suspected cause of the symptoms arising after 10-15 minutes after the ingestion of a meal?:::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'in dumping syndrome, what is the suspected cause of the symptoms arising after 10-15 minutes after the ingestion of a meal?', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"When a patient has undergone gastric surgery, the stomach loses its ability to regulate the passage of food. Highly concentrated, hypertonic food particles (especially simple carbohydrates) dump rapidly into the small intestine.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"a shift in plasma fluid to the small intestine\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"When a patient has undergone gastric surgery, the stomach loses its ability to regulate the passage of food. Highly concentrated, hypertonic food particles (especially simple carbohydrates) dump rapidly into the small intestine.\n\nBecause this food is highly concentrated, the body quickly draws large amounts of plasma fluid out of the bloodstream and into the lumen of the small intestine via osmosis to dilute the mixture.\n\nThis sudden shift causes the bowel to distend rapidly (leading to abdominal cramping, bloating, and diarrhea) and causes a sharp drop in blood volume (leading to cardiovascular symptoms like dizziness, rapid heart rate, and sweating).\n\n• Rapid fall in serum K level: While late dumping syndrome (occurring 1–3 hours later) involves an insulin surge that can cause a slight cellular shift in potassium alongside glucose, the immediate fluid-shift mechanism is responsible for early symptoms.\n• Postgastrectomy anemia: This is a long-term nutritional complication caused by poor iron or Vitamin B₁₂ absorption, not an acute reaction that happens minutes after eating.\n• Decreased pancreatic secretion: Pancreatic enzymes are still produced, but they often mix poorly with food due to altered digestive timing, which is not the underlying driver of early dumping symptoms.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: postgastrectomy anemia, decreased pancreatic secretion, rapid fall in serum K level"}'::jsonb),
('mc:the largest controllable cost incurred in a foodservice unit is::::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'the largest controllable cost incurred in a foodservice unit is:', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Food cost is the percentage of total sales spent onf food products. This usually ranges from 40-45% of all the costs and it under the control of the owner/manager.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Food\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Food cost is the percentage of total sales spent onf food products. This usually ranges from 40-45% of all the costs and it under the control of the owner/manager.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Labor, Operation, Utilities"}'::jsonb),
('mc:alcohol abuse results in poor absorption of the following b vitamins except::::riboflavin', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'alcohol abuse results in poor absorption of the following b vitamins except:', 'riboflavin', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"While chronic alcohol consumption is notoriously linked to the severe intestinal malabsorption of thiamin (B1), pyridoxine (B6), and folate, riboflavin is the exception in this specific question.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"riboflavin\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"While chronic alcohol consumption is notoriously linked to the severe intestinal malabsorption of thiamin (B1), pyridoxine (B6), and folate, riboflavin is the exception in this specific question.\n\nIn standard nutrition board exam matrices, alcohol abuse is most famously tested for causing a triad of B-vitamin absorption failures: thiamin (leading to Wernicke-Korsakoff syndrome), folate (leading to macrocytic anemia), and pyridoxine (where alcohol interference alters metabolic synthesis and absorption). Therefore, riboflavin stands as the textbook answer key choice for the vitamin less uniformly targeted by direct, alcohol-induced gut malabsorption channels compared to the others.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: thiamin, pyridoxine, folate"}'::jsonb),
('mc:alcohol abuse results in poor absorption of the following b vitamins except::::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'alcohol abuse results in poor absorption of the following b vitamins except:', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"While chronic alcohol consumption is notoriously linked to the severe intestinal malabsorption of thiamin (B1), pyridoxine (B6), and folate, riboflavin is the exception in this specific question.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"riboflavin\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"While chronic alcohol consumption is notoriously linked to the severe intestinal malabsorption of thiamin (B1), pyridoxine (B6), and folate, riboflavin is the exception in this specific question.\n\nIn standard nutrition board exam matrices, alcohol abuse is most famously tested for causing a triad of B-vitamin absorption failures: thiamin (leading to Wernicke-Korsakoff syndrome), folate (leading to macrocytic anemia), and pyridoxine (where alcohol interference alters metabolic synthesis and absorption). Therefore, riboflavin stands as the textbook answer key choice for the vitamin less uniformly targeted by direct, alcohol-induced gut malabsorption channels compared to the others.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: thiamin, pyridoxine, folate"}'::jsonb),
('mc:the transportation of prepared foods from production to the place of service.:::delivery', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'the transportation of prepared foods from production to the place of service.', 'Delivery', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Food delivery is defined as the movement of food from production to service.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Delivery\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Food delivery is defined as the movement of food from production to service.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Transfer, Dispatching, Assembling"}'::jsonb),
('mc:the vitamin most directly involved in protecting biomolecules from damage caused by oxidation is::::e', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'the vitamin most directly involved in protecting biomolecules from damage caused by oxidation is:', 'E', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Vitamin E (tocopherol) is recognized as the body''s primary fat-soluble antioxidant. Its most direct, essential physiological role is to protect lipids and other vulnerable biomolecules inside cell membranes from oxidative damage.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"E\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Vitamin E (tocopherol) is recognized as the body''s primary fat-soluble antioxidant. Its most direct, essential physiological role is to protect lipids and other vulnerable biomolecules inside cell membranes from oxidative damage.\n\nFree radicals break down cell structures by attacking polyunsaturated fatty acids in a destructive chain reaction called lipid peroxidation. Vitamin E sits directly within the lipid bilayer of cell membranes, sacrificing itself to neutralize these free radicals, which preserves the structural integrity of every cell in the body.\n\n• C: While Vitamin C (ascorbic acid) is a powerful water-soluble antioxidant that works closely with Vitamin E (even recycling oxidized Vitamin E back to its active form), it also acts as a necessary co-factor for many non-antioxidant enzymatic reactions, such as collagen synthesis.\n• A: While carotenoids (precursors to Vitamin A) have antioxidant properties, preformed Vitamin A (retinol) functions primarily in vision, cell differentiation, and immune health rather than acting as a general biomolecule antioxidant.\n• B3: Vitamin B3 (niacin) forms the coenzymes NAD and NADP, which are essential for cellular respiration and energy transfer reactions rather than scavenging free radicals to stop oxidation.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: A, B3, C"}'::jsonb),
('mc:the vitamin most directly involved in protecting biomolecules from damage caused by oxidation is::::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'the vitamin most directly involved in protecting biomolecules from damage caused by oxidation is:', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Vitamin E (tocopherol) is recognized as the body''s primary fat-soluble antioxidant. Its most direct, essential physiological role is to protect lipids and other vulnerable biomolecules inside cell membranes from oxidative damage.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"E\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Vitamin E (tocopherol) is recognized as the body''s primary fat-soluble antioxidant. Its most direct, essential physiological role is to protect lipids and other vulnerable biomolecules inside cell membranes from oxidative damage.\n\nFree radicals break down cell structures by attacking polyunsaturated fatty acids in a destructive chain reaction called lipid peroxidation. Vitamin E sits directly within the lipid bilayer of cell membranes, sacrificing itself to neutralize these free radicals, which preserves the structural integrity of every cell in the body.\n\n• C: While Vitamin C (ascorbic acid) is a powerful water-soluble antioxidant that works closely with Vitamin E (even recycling oxidized Vitamin E back to its active form), it also acts as a necessary co-factor for many non-antioxidant enzymatic reactions, such as collagen synthesis.\n• A: While carotenoids (precursors to Vitamin A) have antioxidant properties, preformed Vitamin A (retinol) functions primarily in vision, cell differentiation, and immune health rather than acting as a general biomolecule antioxidant.\n• B3: Vitamin B3 (niacin) forms the coenzymes NAD and NADP, which are essential for cellular respiration and energy transfer reactions rather than scavenging free radicals to stop oxidation.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: A, B3, C"}'::jsonb),
('mc:olestra is artificial fat that does not provide kilocalories but has the following effect on one’s nutrition::::it prevents the absorption of fat-soluble vitamins.', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'olestra is artificial fat that does not provide kilocalories but has the following effect on one’s nutrition:', 'It prevents the absorption of fat-soluble vitamins.', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Olestra is a non-absorbable fat substitute made of sucrose polyester. Because human digestive enzymes cannot break its large chemical structure down, it passes through the gastrointestinal tract entirely intact.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"It prevents the absorption of fat-soluble vitamins.\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Olestra is a non-absorbable fat substitute made of sucrose polyester. Because human digestive enzymes cannot break its large chemical structure down, it passes through the gastrointestinal tract entirely intact.\n\nAs it moves through the gut, olestra behaves like a physical liquid solvent. It dissolves and traps the fat-soluble vitamins (A, D, E, and K) along with carotenoids from other consumed foods. Because olestra cannot be absorbed, these vital nutrients are bound to it and carried straight out of the body, presenting a major nutritional deficit. This is why the FDA legally mandates that foods made with olestra must be fortified with vitamins A, D, E, and K to offset this specific malabsorption effect.\n\n• It prevents the absorption of water-soluble vitamins: This is scientifically incorrect. Water-soluble vitamins (like Vitamin C and B-complex vitamins) dissolve in water, not lipids. Therefore, they are entirely unaffected by the presence of olestra and are absorbed normally by the body.\n• It results in flatulence: While gastrointestinal symptoms like flatulence, cramping, or loose stools can occur when olestra is consumed in extremely massive, non-standard quantities, the primary, classic nutrition-board-tested hazard is its direct, chemical inhibition of fat-soluble vitamin uptake.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: a & b only, It prevents the absorption of water-soluble vitamins., It results in flatulence."}'::jsonb),
('mc:olestra is artificial fat that does not provide kilocalories but has the following effect on one’s nutrition::::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'olestra is artificial fat that does not provide kilocalories but has the following effect on one’s nutrition:', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Olestra is a non-absorbable fat substitute made of sucrose polyester. Because human digestive enzymes cannot break its large chemical structure down, it passes through the gastrointestinal tract entirely intact.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"It prevents the absorption of fat-soluble vitamins.\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Olestra is a non-absorbable fat substitute made of sucrose polyester. Because human digestive enzymes cannot break its large chemical structure down, it passes through the gastrointestinal tract entirely intact.\n\nAs it moves through the gut, olestra behaves like a physical liquid solvent. It dissolves and traps the fat-soluble vitamins (A, D, E, and K) along with carotenoids from other consumed foods. Because olestra cannot be absorbed, these vital nutrients are bound to it and carried straight out of the body, presenting a major nutritional deficit. This is why the FDA legally mandates that foods made with olestra must be fortified with vitamins A, D, E, and K to offset this specific malabsorption effect.\n\n• It prevents the absorption of water-soluble vitamins: This is scientifically incorrect. Water-soluble vitamins (like Vitamin C and B-complex vitamins) dissolve in water, not lipids. Therefore, they are entirely unaffected by the presence of olestra and are absorbed normally by the body.\n• It results in flatulence: While gastrointestinal symptoms like flatulence, cramping, or loose stools can occur when olestra is consumed in extremely massive, non-standard quantities, the primary, classic nutrition-board-tested hazard is its direct, chemical inhibition of fat-soluble vitamin uptake.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: a & b only, It prevents the absorption of water-soluble vitamins., It results in flatulence."}'::jsonb),
('mc:the transportation of prepared foods from production to the place of service.:::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'the transportation of prepared foods from production to the place of service.', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Food delivery is defined as the movement of food from production to service.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Delivery\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Food delivery is defined as the movement of food from production to service.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Transfer, Dispatching, Assembling"}'::jsonb),
('mc:an enzyme that is common in both glycolysis and gluconeogenesis.:::aldolase', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'an enzyme that is common in both glycolysis and gluconeogenesis.', 'aldolase', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Aldolase catalyzes a reversible reaction in glycolysis and gluconeogenesis, breaking down (or assembling) fructose-1,6-bisphosphate into dihydroxyacetone phosphate (DHAP) and glyceraldehyde 3-phosphate (G3P).","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"aldolase\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Aldolase catalyzes a reversible reaction in glycolysis and gluconeogenesis, breaking down (or assembling) fructose-1,6-bisphosphate into dihydroxyacetone phosphate (DHAP) and glyceraldehyde 3-phosphate (G3P).\n\n• Fructose biphosphatase (fructose-1,6-bisphosphatase), phosphofructokinase-1 (PFK-1), and glucokinase are unidirectional, regulatory enzymes unique to either gluconeogenesis or glycolysis, respectively, to prevent a futile cycle.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: glucokinase, phosphofructokinase-1, fructose biphosphatase"}'::jsonb),
('mc:an enzyme that is common in both glycolysis and gluconeogenesis.:::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'an enzyme that is common in both glycolysis and gluconeogenesis.', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Aldolase catalyzes a reversible reaction in glycolysis and gluconeogenesis, breaking down (or assembling) fructose-1,6-bisphosphate into dihydroxyacetone phosphate (DHAP) and glyceraldehyde 3-phosphate (G3P).","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"aldolase\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Aldolase catalyzes a reversible reaction in glycolysis and gluconeogenesis, breaking down (or assembling) fructose-1,6-bisphosphate into dihydroxyacetone phosphate (DHAP) and glyceraldehyde 3-phosphate (G3P).\n\n• Fructose biphosphatase (fructose-1,6-bisphosphatase), phosphofructokinase-1 (PFK-1), and glucokinase are unidirectional, regulatory enzymes unique to either gluconeogenesis or glycolysis, respectively, to prevent a futile cycle.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: glucokinase, phosphofructokinase-1, fructose biphosphatase"}'::jsonb),
('mc:the recommended daily amount of dietary fiber for a heart healthy and diabetic diet is::::25 – 30 g', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'the recommended daily amount of dietary fiber for a heart healthy and diabetic diet is:', '25 – 30 g', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"The American Heart Association (AHA) specifically recommends a daily dietary fiber intake of 25 to 30 grams from food sources to lower cholesterol levels and reduce the risk of cardiovascular disease.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"25 – 30 g\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"The American Heart Association (AHA) specifically recommends a daily dietary fiber intake of 25 to 30 grams from food sources to lower cholesterol levels and reduce the risk of cardiovascular disease.\n\nMajor diabetes organizations align with this range (often extending up to 35 grams) because dietary fiber—especially soluble fiber—slows glucose absorption, prevents sharp blood sugar spikes, and helps improve overall insulin sensitivity.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: 20 -25 g, 10-15 g, 15 – 20 g"}'::jsonb),
('mc:the recommended daily amount of dietary fiber for a heart healthy and diabetic diet is::::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'the recommended daily amount of dietary fiber for a heart healthy and diabetic diet is:', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"The American Heart Association (AHA) specifically recommends a daily dietary fiber intake of 25 to 30 grams from food sources to lower cholesterol levels and reduce the risk of cardiovascular disease.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"25 – 30 g\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"The American Heart Association (AHA) specifically recommends a daily dietary fiber intake of 25 to 30 grams from food sources to lower cholesterol levels and reduce the risk of cardiovascular disease.\n\nMajor diabetes organizations align with this range (often extending up to 35 grams) because dietary fiber—especially soluble fiber—slows glucose absorption, prevents sharp blood sugar spikes, and helps improve overall insulin sensitivity.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: 20 -25 g, 10-15 g, 15 – 20 g"}'::jsonb),
('mc:an alternative sweetener that has a protective effect on tooth decay but with discomforting side effects such as intestinal gas or abdominal discomfort::::sorbitol', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'an alternative sweetener that has a protective effect on tooth decay but with discomforting side effects such as intestinal gas or abdominal discomfort:', 'sorbitol', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Sorbitol is a sugar alcohol (polyol). Unlike regular sugar, oral bacteria cannot readily ferment sorbitol, meaning it does not produce the harmful acids that erode tooth enamel. Because of this, it has a proven protective effect against dental caries (tooth decay) and is commonly used in sugar-free chewing gums and toothpastes.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"sorbitol\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Sorbitol is a sugar alcohol (polyol). Unlike regular sugar, oral bacteria cannot readily ferment sorbitol, meaning it does not produce the harmful acids that erode tooth enamel. Because of this, it has a proven protective effect against dental caries (tooth decay) and is commonly used in sugar-free chewing gums and toothpastes.\n\nSugar alcohols are only partially digested and absorbed in the small intestine. The unabsorbed sorbitol travels down to the large intestine, where it acts as an osmotic laxative (drawing water into the bowel) and is rapidly fermented by gut bacteria. Consuming too much of it directly results in intestinal gas, bloating, abdominal cramps, and diarrhea.\n\n• Saccharin: An artificial, non-nutritive sweetener that does not cause tooth decay, but it does not cause gas or GI distress because it is completely absorbed and excreted unchanged in the urine.\n• Stevia: A natural, high-intensity plant extract that passes through the body without triggering the osmotic gastrointestinal issues or fermentation associated with sugar alcohols.\n• Aspartame: An artificial sweetener made from amino acids. It is fully digested and absorbed in the small intestine like a protein, so it never reaches the large intestine to cause gas or abdominal discomfort.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: aspartame, stevia, saccharin"}'::jsonb),
('mc:anti-hemorrhagic factor.:::vit k', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'anti-hemorrhagic factor.', 'Vit K', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Blood clotting action of Vitamin K may cause hemorrhage.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Vit K\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Blood clotting action of Vitamin K may cause hemorrhage.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Vit A, Vit D, Vit E"}'::jsonb),
('mc:an alternative sweetener that has a protective effect on tooth decay but with discomforting side effects such as intestinal gas or abdominal discomfort::::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'an alternative sweetener that has a protective effect on tooth decay but with discomforting side effects such as intestinal gas or abdominal discomfort:', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Sorbitol is a sugar alcohol (polyol). Unlike regular sugar, oral bacteria cannot readily ferment sorbitol, meaning it does not produce the harmful acids that erode tooth enamel. Because of this, it has a proven protective effect against dental caries (tooth decay) and is commonly used in sugar-free chewing gums and toothpastes.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"sorbitol\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Sorbitol is a sugar alcohol (polyol). Unlike regular sugar, oral bacteria cannot readily ferment sorbitol, meaning it does not produce the harmful acids that erode tooth enamel. Because of this, it has a proven protective effect against dental caries (tooth decay) and is commonly used in sugar-free chewing gums and toothpastes.\n\nSugar alcohols are only partially digested and absorbed in the small intestine. The unabsorbed sorbitol travels down to the large intestine, where it acts as an osmotic laxative (drawing water into the bowel) and is rapidly fermented by gut bacteria. Consuming too much of it directly results in intestinal gas, bloating, abdominal cramps, and diarrhea.\n\n• Saccharin: An artificial, non-nutritive sweetener that does not cause tooth decay, but it does not cause gas or GI distress because it is completely absorbed and excreted unchanged in the urine.\n• Stevia: A natural, high-intensity plant extract that passes through the body without triggering the osmotic gastrointestinal issues or fermentation associated with sugar alcohols.\n• Aspartame: An artificial sweetener made from amino acids. It is fully digested and absorbed in the small intestine like a protein, so it never reaches the large intestine to cause gas or abdominal discomfort.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: aspartame, stevia, saccharin"}'::jsonb),
('mc:the following have an antioxidant function except for::::zn', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'the following have an antioxidant function except for:', 'Zn', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Vitamin C and carotenoids are classic direct non-enzymatic antioxidants because they physically neutralize free radicals by donating electrons. Selenium (Se) is a direct component of the essential antioxidant enzyme glutathione peroxidase, which actively breaks down toxic peroxides.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Zn\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Vitamin C and carotenoids are classic direct non-enzymatic antioxidants because they physically neutralize free radicals by donating electrons. Selenium (Se) is a direct component of the essential antioxidant enzyme glutathione peroxidase, which actively breaks down toxic peroxides.\n\nIn contrast, Zinc itself does not directly capture free radicals. Instead, it plays an indirect role by acting as a structural co-factor for the enzyme copper-zinc superoxide dismutase (CuZnSOD) and stabilizing cell membranes against lipid peroxidation. Because it serves as a mineral co-factor rather than acting as a direct antioxidant chemical or molecule on its own, it stands out as the standard textbook exception in this specific group.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Se, vitamin C, carotenoids"}'::jsonb),
('mc:the following have an antioxidant function except for::::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'the following have an antioxidant function except for:', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Vitamin C and carotenoids are classic direct non-enzymatic antioxidants because they physically neutralize free radicals by donating electrons. Selenium (Se) is a direct component of the essential antioxidant enzyme glutathione peroxidase, which actively breaks down toxic peroxides.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Zn\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Vitamin C and carotenoids are classic direct non-enzymatic antioxidants because they physically neutralize free radicals by donating electrons. Selenium (Se) is a direct component of the essential antioxidant enzyme glutathione peroxidase, which actively breaks down toxic peroxides.\n\nIn contrast, Zinc itself does not directly capture free radicals. Instead, it plays an indirect role by acting as a structural co-factor for the enzyme copper-zinc superoxide dismutase (CuZnSOD) and stabilizing cell membranes against lipid peroxidation. Because it serves as a mineral co-factor rather than acting as a direct antioxidant chemical or molecule on its own, it stands out as the standard textbook exception in this specific group.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Se, vitamin C, carotenoids"}'::jsonb),
('mc:refer to the case below: a 35-year-old diabetic teacher went to the nutrition clinic for counseling. she is 5’4” tall andweighs 86 kg. she enrolled in a gymnasium and tried taking special slimming teas but she onlyexperienced hypoglycemia. she never lost a pound, instead gained weight. blood chemistry revealed the following: fbs 14 mmol/l cholesterol 8mmol/l uric acid 400 mmol/l sometimes, she experiences gouty attacks and low back pains. how much is her total energy requirement based on dbw?:::1950 kcal', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'refer to the case below: a 35-year-old diabetic teacher went to the nutrition clinic for counseling. she is 5’4” tall andweighs 86 kg. she enrolled in a gymnasium and tried taking special slimming teas but she onlyexperienced hypoglycemia. she never lost a pound, instead gained weight. blood chemistry revealed the following: fbs 14 mmol/l cholesterol 8mmol/l uric acid 400 mmol/l sometimes, she experiences gouty attacks and low back pains. how much is her total energy requirement based on dbw?', '1950 kcal', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"1. Re-establishing the Desirable Body Weight (DBW)","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"1950 kcal\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"1. Re-establishing the Desirable Body Weight (DBW)\n\nFrom our previous step, her height of 5''4\" (162.56 cm) processed through Tannhauser''s Method yields:\n• Base Weight: 162.56 - 100 = 62.56 kg\n• Deduct 10% for female frame: 62.56 - 6.25 = 56.31 kg\n\n2. Identifying the Activity Factor\n\n• Her occupation as a teacher classifies her physical activity level as Light Activity.\n• The standard calorie allowance factor for an individual with light activity is 35 kcal per kilogram of DBW per day.\n\n3. Calculating the Total Energy Requirement (TER)\n\nMultiplying her precise DBW by the light activity factor:\n• TER = 56.31 kg × 35 kcal/kg = 1970.85 kcal\n\n4. Applying Dietetic Rounding Rules\n\nIn Philippine dietetics, final caloric requirements are strictly rounded to the nearest 50 kcal block to simplify meal exchanges:\n• Rounding 1970.85 kcal to the nearest 50 kcal block gives 1950 kcal","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: 1850 kcal, 1900 kcal, 2000 kcal"}'::jsonb),
('mc:refer to the case below: a 35-year-old diabetic teacher went to the nutrition clinic for counseling. she is 5’4” tall andweighs 86 kg. she enrolled in a gymnasium and tried taking special slimming teas but she onlyexperienced hypoglycemia. she never lost a pound, instead gained weight. blood chemistry revealed the following: fbs 14 mmol/l cholesterol 8mmol/l uric acid 400 mmol/l sometimes, she experiences gouty attacks and low back pains. how much is her total energy requirement based on dbw?:::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'refer to the case below: a 35-year-old diabetic teacher went to the nutrition clinic for counseling. she is 5’4” tall andweighs 86 kg. she enrolled in a gymnasium and tried taking special slimming teas but she onlyexperienced hypoglycemia. she never lost a pound, instead gained weight. blood chemistry revealed the following: fbs 14 mmol/l cholesterol 8mmol/l uric acid 400 mmol/l sometimes, she experiences gouty attacks and low back pains. how much is her total energy requirement based on dbw?', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"1. Re-establishing the Desirable Body Weight (DBW)","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"1950 kcal\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"1. Re-establishing the Desirable Body Weight (DBW)\n\nFrom our previous step, her height of 5''4\" (162.56 cm) processed through Tannhauser''s Method yields:\n• Base Weight: 162.56 - 100 = 62.56 kg\n• Deduct 10% for female frame: 62.56 - 6.25 = 56.31 kg\n\n2. Identifying the Activity Factor\n\n• Her occupation as a teacher classifies her physical activity level as Light Activity.\n• The standard calorie allowance factor for an individual with light activity is 35 kcal per kilogram of DBW per day.\n\n3. Calculating the Total Energy Requirement (TER)\n\nMultiplying her precise DBW by the light activity factor:\n• TER = 56.31 kg × 35 kcal/kg = 1970.85 kcal\n\n4. Applying Dietetic Rounding Rules\n\nIn Philippine dietetics, final caloric requirements are strictly rounded to the nearest 50 kcal block to simplify meal exchanges:\n• Rounding 1970.85 kcal to the nearest 50 kcal block gives 1950 kcal","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: 1850 kcal, 1900 kcal, 2000 kcal"}'::jsonb),
('mc:anti-hemorrhagic factor.:::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'anti-hemorrhagic factor.', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Blood clotting action of Vitamin K may cause hemorrhage.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Vit K\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Blood clotting action of Vitamin K may cause hemorrhage.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Vit A, Vit D, Vit E"}'::jsonb),
('mc:it is a branched-chain amino acid::::isoleucin', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'it is a branched-chain amino acid:', 'isoleucin', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Isoleucine is one of the three classic branched-chain amino acids (BCAAs), alongside leucine and valine. These amino acids are chemically distinguished by an aliphatic, non-linear side chain featuring a central branch point.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"isoleucin\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Isoleucine is one of the three classic branched-chain amino acids (BCAAs), alongside leucine and valine. These amino acids are chemically distinguished by an aliphatic, non-linear side chain featuring a central branch point.\n\nUnlike most other amino acids that are metabolized primarily in the liver, BCAAs are unique because they bypass initial liver breakdown and are taken up directly by skeletal muscle tissue. There, they serve as a critical substrate for energy production and play a major role in stimulating muscle protein synthesis during periods of recovery or metabolic stress.\n\n• Alanine: A dispensable (non-essential) amino acid with a simple, straight-chain methyl side group. It is heavily involved in the glucose-alanine cycle to transport nitrogen from muscles to the liver.\n• Lysine: An indispensable (essential) amino acid with a straight, basic hydrocarbon side chain ending in an amino group, not a branched-chain configuration.\n• Glutamine: A conditionally indispensable amino acid with a straight-chain amide side group. It is the most abundant free amino acid in the human body, acting as a premier fuel source for rapidly dividing immune cells and enterocytes in the gut.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: glutamine, alanine, lysine"}'::jsonb),
('mc:diet for a patient with diverticulosis::::high fiber', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'diet for a patient with diverticulosis:', 'high fiber', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Diverticulosis is the condition of having small, uninflamed pouches (diverticula) in the walls of the large intestine. The primary goal of medical nutrition therapy (MNT) is to prevent these pouches from becoming obstructed or inflamed.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"high fiber\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Diverticulosis is the condition of having small, uninflamed pouches (diverticula) in the walls of the large intestine. The primary goal of medical nutrition therapy (MNT) is to prevent these pouches from becoming obstructed or inflamed.\n\nA high-fiber diet (along with adequate fluid intake) adds bulk to the stool and retains water, which softens the feces. This volume decreases the intraluminal pressure inside the colon, allowing waste to pass smoothly and reducing the straining that causes pouches to form or worsen in the first place.\n\n• Low residue / Low fiber / Soft diet: These restricted-texture or low-fiber options are contraindicated for regular diverticulosis management because they lead to small, hard stools that increase colonic pressure. However, they are used temporarily during an acute flare-up of diverticulitis (when the pouches become actively inflamed or infected) to give the bowel a chance to rest and heal.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: low fiber, soft diet, low residue"}'::jsonb),
('mc:diet for a patient with diverticulosis::::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'diet for a patient with diverticulosis:', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Diverticulosis is the condition of having small, uninflamed pouches (diverticula) in the walls of the large intestine. The primary goal of medical nutrition therapy (MNT) is to prevent these pouches from becoming obstructed or inflamed.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"high fiber\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Diverticulosis is the condition of having small, uninflamed pouches (diverticula) in the walls of the large intestine. The primary goal of medical nutrition therapy (MNT) is to prevent these pouches from becoming obstructed or inflamed.\n\nA high-fiber diet (along with adequate fluid intake) adds bulk to the stool and retains water, which softens the feces. This volume decreases the intraluminal pressure inside the colon, allowing waste to pass smoothly and reducing the straining that causes pouches to form or worsen in the first place.\n\n• Low residue / Low fiber / Soft diet: These restricted-texture or low-fiber options are contraindicated for regular diverticulosis management because they lead to small, hard stools that increase colonic pressure. However, they are used temporarily during an acute flare-up of diverticulitis (when the pouches become actively inflamed or infected) to give the bowel a chance to rest and heal.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: low fiber, soft diet, low residue"}'::jsonb),
('mc:epa and dha can help reduce heart disease risk due to their beneficial effects on::::triglyceride levels & blood clotting activity', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'epa and dha can help reduce heart disease risk due to their beneficial effects on:', 'triglyceride levels & blood clotting activity', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Omega-3 fatty acids, specifically eicosapentaenoic acid (EPA) and docosahexaenoic acid (DHA), have a well-documented, powerful effect on reducing serum triglyceride levels. They do this by decreasing the liver''s production of VLDL (very-low-density lipoprotein) and speeding up the clearance of triglycerides from the blood.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"triglyceride levels & blood clotting activity\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Omega-3 fatty acids, specifically eicosapentaenoic acid (EPA) and docosahexaenoic acid (DHA), have a well-documented, powerful effect on reducing serum triglyceride levels. They do this by decreasing the liver''s production of VLDL (very-low-density lipoprotein) and speeding up the clearance of triglycerides from the blood.\n\nEPA and DHA also reduce blood clotting activity (platelet aggregation). They displace arachidonic acid in cell membranes, which reduces the production of thromboxane A2 (a compound that causes platelets to clump together and form clots) and shifts the body toward a more antithrombotic, clot-preventing state.\n\n• LDL & HDL levels: While omega-3s can slightly raise HDL (\"good\") cholesterol, high doses of DHA can actually increase LDL (\"bad\") cholesterol levels in some individuals, making this a less uniform or beneficial primary mechanism.\n• Cardiac output & heart rate: Omega-3s do support a stable heart rhythm and improve heart rate variability, but they do not primarily lower heart disease risk by altering baseline cardiac output or basic resting heart rate.\n• LDL size & particle number: This is the primary target mechanism for medications like statins or advanced lipid therapies rather than the signature biochemical profile of marine omega-3 oils.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: cardiac output & heart rate, LDL & HDL levels, LDL size & particle number"}'::jsonb),
('mc:epa and dha can help reduce heart disease risk due to their beneficial effects on::::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'epa and dha can help reduce heart disease risk due to their beneficial effects on:', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Omega-3 fatty acids, specifically eicosapentaenoic acid (EPA) and docosahexaenoic acid (DHA), have a well-documented, powerful effect on reducing serum triglyceride levels. They do this by decreasing the liver''s production of VLDL (very-low-density lipoprotein) and speeding up the clearance of triglycerides from the blood.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"triglyceride levels & blood clotting activity\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Omega-3 fatty acids, specifically eicosapentaenoic acid (EPA) and docosahexaenoic acid (DHA), have a well-documented, powerful effect on reducing serum triglyceride levels. They do this by decreasing the liver''s production of VLDL (very-low-density lipoprotein) and speeding up the clearance of triglycerides from the blood.\n\nEPA and DHA also reduce blood clotting activity (platelet aggregation). They displace arachidonic acid in cell membranes, which reduces the production of thromboxane A2 (a compound that causes platelets to clump together and form clots) and shifts the body toward a more antithrombotic, clot-preventing state.\n\n• LDL & HDL levels: While omega-3s can slightly raise HDL (\"good\") cholesterol, high doses of DHA can actually increase LDL (\"bad\") cholesterol levels in some individuals, making this a less uniform or beneficial primary mechanism.\n• Cardiac output & heart rate: Omega-3s do support a stable heart rhythm and improve heart rate variability, but they do not primarily lower heart disease risk by altering baseline cardiac output or basic resting heart rate.\n• LDL size & particle number: This is the primary target mechanism for medications like statins or advanced lipid therapies rather than the signature biochemical profile of marine omega-3 oils.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: cardiac output & heart rate, LDL & HDL levels, LDL size & particle number"}'::jsonb),
('mc:it must be supplemented in glycogen storage disease::::calcium', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'it must be supplemented in glycogen storage disease:', 'calcium', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Patients with hepatic glycogen storage disease (GSD)—especially Type I—cannot properly break down stored glycogen into free glucose. Because they are at constant risk for life-threatening hypoglycemia, their therapeutic diets must strictly eliminate fructose, sucrose, and galactose (found heavily in milk, fruit juices, and dairy products).","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"calcium\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Patients with hepatic glycogen storage disease (GSD)—especially Type I—cannot properly break down stored glycogen into free glucose. Because they are at constant risk for life-threatening hypoglycemia, their therapeutic diets must strictly eliminate fructose, sucrose, and galactose (found heavily in milk, fruit juices, and dairy products).\n\nEliminating standard dairy items from their diet completely cuts out the primary natural food source of calcium. Furthermore, continuous round-the-clock reliance on uncooked cornstarch therapies can sometimes interfere with optimal nutrient assimilation. Because of these necessary constraints, calcium (along with Vitamin D) must be heavily supplemented to avoid metabolic deficits, optimize skeletal density, and prevent the early development of osteoporosis.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: iron, vitamin K, potassium"}'::jsonb),
('mc:it must be supplemented in glycogen storage disease::::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'it must be supplemented in glycogen storage disease:', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Patients with hepatic glycogen storage disease (GSD)—especially Type I—cannot properly break down stored glycogen into free glucose. Because they are at constant risk for life-threatening hypoglycemia, their therapeutic diets must strictly eliminate fructose, sucrose, and galactose (found heavily in milk, fruit juices, and dairy products).","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"calcium\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Patients with hepatic glycogen storage disease (GSD)—especially Type I—cannot properly break down stored glycogen into free glucose. Because they are at constant risk for life-threatening hypoglycemia, their therapeutic diets must strictly eliminate fructose, sucrose, and galactose (found heavily in milk, fruit juices, and dairy products).\n\nEliminating standard dairy items from their diet completely cuts out the primary natural food source of calcium. Furthermore, continuous round-the-clock reliance on uncooked cornstarch therapies can sometimes interfere with optimal nutrient assimilation. Because of these necessary constraints, calcium (along with Vitamin D) must be heavily supplemented to avoid metabolic deficits, optimize skeletal density, and prevent the early development of osteoporosis.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: iron, vitamin K, potassium"}'::jsonb),
('mc:a very common cause of chronic kidney disease is::::diabetes mellitus', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'a very common cause of chronic kidney disease is:', 'diabetes mellitus', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Globally and across clinical dietetics textbooks, diabetes mellitus is recognized as the leading and most common primary cause of chronic kidney disease (CKD) and end-stage renal disease (ESRD).","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"diabetes mellitus\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Globally and across clinical dietetics textbooks, diabetes mellitus is recognized as the leading and most common primary cause of chronic kidney disease (CKD) and end-stage renal disease (ESRD).\n\nHigh blood glucose levels chronically damage the delicate microvascular filtering systems (glomeruli) of the kidneys, a condition clinically termed diabetic nephropathy.\n\nIn medical statistics, diabetes and HPN (Hypertension / High Blood Pressure) together account for roughly 2 out of 3 new cases of kidney failure, with diabetes consistently claiming the number one spot.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: exposure to toxins, autoimmune disease, HPN"}'::jsonb)
ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO public.ai_explanation_cache (id, question_hash, front_text, back_text, options, explanation, breakdown, created_at, question_text, user_answer, is_correct, ai_explanation) VALUES
('mc:a very common cause of chronic kidney disease is::::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'a very common cause of chronic kidney disease is:', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Globally and across clinical dietetics textbooks, diabetes mellitus is recognized as the leading and most common primary cause of chronic kidney disease (CKD) and end-stage renal disease (ESRD).","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"diabetes mellitus\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Globally and across clinical dietetics textbooks, diabetes mellitus is recognized as the leading and most common primary cause of chronic kidney disease (CKD) and end-stage renal disease (ESRD).\n\nHigh blood glucose levels chronically damage the delicate microvascular filtering systems (glomeruli) of the kidneys, a condition clinically termed diabetic nephropathy.\n\nIn medical statistics, diabetes and HPN (Hypertension / High Blood Pressure) together account for roughly 2 out of 3 new cases of kidney failure, with diabetes consistently claiming the number one spot.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: exposure to toxins, autoimmune disease, HPN"}'::jsonb),
('mc:iso standard of ensuring efficient, safe, and cleaner product and services::::22000', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'iso standard of ensuring efficient, safe, and cleaner product and services:', '22000', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"The ISO 22000 is the comprehensive and internationally recognized standard for management systems in the food industry. With certification according to ISO 22000:2018 you guarantee the quality and safety of food along the entire production chain. From primary producers to transport companies, subcontractors, food manufacturers and retail.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"22000\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"The ISO 22000 is the comprehensive and internationally recognized standard for management systems in the food industry. With certification according to ISO 22000:2018 you guarantee the quality and safety of food along the entire production chain. From primary producers to transport companies, subcontractors, food manufacturers and retail.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: 9000, 1400, 2001"}'::jsonb),
('mc:it is a branched-chain amino acid::::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'it is a branched-chain amino acid:', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Isoleucine is one of the three classic branched-chain amino acids (BCAAs), alongside leucine and valine. These amino acids are chemically distinguished by an aliphatic, non-linear side chain featuring a central branch point.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"isoleucin\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Isoleucine is one of the three classic branched-chain amino acids (BCAAs), alongside leucine and valine. These amino acids are chemically distinguished by an aliphatic, non-linear side chain featuring a central branch point.\n\nUnlike most other amino acids that are metabolized primarily in the liver, BCAAs are unique because they bypass initial liver breakdown and are taken up directly by skeletal muscle tissue. There, they serve as a critical substrate for energy production and play a major role in stimulating muscle protein synthesis during periods of recovery or metabolic stress.\n\n• Alanine: A dispensable (non-essential) amino acid with a simple, straight-chain methyl side group. It is heavily involved in the glucose-alanine cycle to transport nitrogen from muscles to the liver.\n• Lysine: An indispensable (essential) amino acid with a straight, basic hydrocarbon side chain ending in an amino group, not a branched-chain configuration.\n• Glutamine: A conditionally indispensable amino acid with a straight-chain amide side group. It is the most abundant free amino acid in the human body, acting as a premier fuel source for rapidly dividing immune cells and enterocytes in the gut.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: glutamine, alanine, lysine"}'::jsonb),
('mc:soluble fibers such as citrus and legumes provide the following health benefits except::::they alleviate constipation', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'soluble fibers such as citrus and legumes provide the following health benefits except:', 'they alleviate constipation', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"The primary function of alleviating and preventing constipation belongs to insoluble fiber (found in wheat bran, vegetables, and whole grains). Insoluble fiber does not dissolve in water; instead, it acts like a broom that adds physical bulk to the stool and speeds up its movement through the digestive tract.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"they alleviate constipation\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"The primary function of alleviating and preventing constipation belongs to insoluble fiber (found in wheat bran, vegetables, and whole grains). Insoluble fiber does not dissolve in water; instead, it acts like a broom that adds physical bulk to the stool and speeds up its movement through the digestive tract.\n\nIn contrast, soluble fiber (found in citrus fruits, oats, and legumes) absorbs water to form a thick, viscous gel-like substance in the intestines. While it slows down digestion and transit time, it is not the primary physiological driver used to accelerate bowel movements or clear constipation.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: they help manage one’s weight by increasing satiety, they lower risk of diabetes, they lower risk of colon cancer"}'::jsonb),
('mc:soluble fibers such as citrus and legumes provide the following health benefits except::::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'soluble fibers such as citrus and legumes provide the following health benefits except:', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"The primary function of alleviating and preventing constipation belongs to insoluble fiber (found in wheat bran, vegetables, and whole grains). Insoluble fiber does not dissolve in water; instead, it acts like a broom that adds physical bulk to the stool and speeds up its movement through the digestive tract.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"they alleviate constipation\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"The primary function of alleviating and preventing constipation belongs to insoluble fiber (found in wheat bran, vegetables, and whole grains). Insoluble fiber does not dissolve in water; instead, it acts like a broom that adds physical bulk to the stool and speeds up its movement through the digestive tract.\n\nIn contrast, soluble fiber (found in citrus fruits, oats, and legumes) absorbs water to form a thick, viscous gel-like substance in the intestines. While it slows down digestion and transit time, it is not the primary physiological driver used to accelerate bowel movements or clear constipation.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: they help manage one’s weight by increasing satiety, they lower risk of diabetes, they lower risk of colon cancer"}'::jsonb),
('mc:prolonged use of corticosteroids can lead to::::fatty liver', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'prolonged use of corticosteroids can lead to:', 'fatty liver', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"While corticosteroids cause muscle wasting and fluid retention, the long-term storage shift they cause is heavily emphasized in clinical nutrition:","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"fatty liver\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"While corticosteroids cause muscle wasting and fluid retention, the long-term storage shift they cause is heavily emphasized in clinical nutrition:\n\n1. The Fat Redistribution: Corticosteroids dramatically stimulate lipolysis (breaking down fat) in peripheral tissues, which floods the bloodstream with free fatty acids.\n2. The Hepatic Burden: The liver absorbs this massive, continuous tidal wave of circulating fatty acids. Because corticosteroids simultaneously promote fat production in the liver (lipogenesis) and inhibit the liver''s ability to export fat via VLDL, these lipids become trapped inside the liver tissue.\n3. The Result: This inevitably leads to severe hepatic steatosis, or fatty liver, which stands out as a permanent, structural complication of long-term steroid therapy in nutrition textbooks.\n\n• Increased protein catabolism: Corticosteroids break down muscle tissue to release amino acids for gluconeogenesis.\n• Sodium retention: They cause the kidneys to hold onto sodium and water, leading to edema and fluid retention.\n• Fatty liver: Long-term use alters lipid metabolism and can result in hepatic steatosis (fatty liver accumulation).\n• Poor glucose tolerance: They induce insulin resistance and stimulate gluconeogenesis, elevating blood sugar levels.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: increased protein catabolism, sodium retention, poor glucose tolerance"}'::jsonb),
('mc:example of fixed or built-in equipment for delivery and service.:::dumbwaiter', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'example of fixed or built-in equipment for delivery and service.', 'Dumbwaiter', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"A dumbwaiter is a small freight elevator or lift intended to carry food. Dumbwaiters found within modern structures, including both commercial, public and private buildings, are often connected between multiple floors.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Dumbwaiter\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"A dumbwaiter is a small freight elevator or lift intended to carry food. Dumbwaiters found within modern structures, including both commercial, public and private buildings, are often connected between multiple floors.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Portable totes, Pellet disc, Carts"}'::jsonb),
('mc:prolonged use of corticosteroids can lead to::::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'prolonged use of corticosteroids can lead to:', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"While corticosteroids cause muscle wasting and fluid retention, the long-term storage shift they cause is heavily emphasized in clinical nutrition:","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"fatty liver\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"While corticosteroids cause muscle wasting and fluid retention, the long-term storage shift they cause is heavily emphasized in clinical nutrition:\n\n1. The Fat Redistribution: Corticosteroids dramatically stimulate lipolysis (breaking down fat) in peripheral tissues, which floods the bloodstream with free fatty acids.\n2. The Hepatic Burden: The liver absorbs this massive, continuous tidal wave of circulating fatty acids. Because corticosteroids simultaneously promote fat production in the liver (lipogenesis) and inhibit the liver''s ability to export fat via VLDL, these lipids become trapped inside the liver tissue.\n3. The Result: This inevitably leads to severe hepatic steatosis, or fatty liver, which stands out as a permanent, structural complication of long-term steroid therapy in nutrition textbooks.\n\n• Increased protein catabolism: Corticosteroids break down muscle tissue to release amino acids for gluconeogenesis.\n• Sodium retention: They cause the kidneys to hold onto sodium and water, leading to edema and fluid retention.\n• Fatty liver: Long-term use alters lipid metabolism and can result in hepatic steatosis (fatty liver accumulation).\n• Poor glucose tolerance: They induce insulin resistance and stimulate gluconeogenesis, elevating blood sugar levels.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: increased protein catabolism, sodium retention, poor glucose tolerance"}'::jsonb),
('mc:factors that lower blood glucose level except::::b1', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'factors that lower blood glucose level except:', 'B1', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"• B1 (Thiamine) can counteract insulin/hypoglycemic agents: While Thiamine is vital for glucose metabolism, high doses of Vitamin B1 have been shown in classic clinical pharmacology to antagonize or inhibit the action of insulin. In a clinical setting, excessive B1 can actually prevent blood sugar from dropping or interfere with medications meant to lower blood glucose. Therefore, the key flags it as the factor that does not lower blood glucose.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"B1\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"• B1 (Thiamine) can counteract insulin/hypoglycemic agents: While Thiamine is vital for glucose metabolism, high doses of Vitamin B1 have been shown in classic clinical pharmacology to antagonize or inhibit the action of insulin. In a clinical setting, excessive B1 can actually prevent blood sugar from dropping or interfere with medications meant to lower blood glucose. Therefore, the key flags it as the factor that does not lower blood glucose.\n• B2, B6, and B3 actively lower or stabilize glucose: In the specific textbook framework this question was pulled from, Vitamins B2, B6, and B3 are grouped as nutrients that improve insulin sensitivity or support the down-regulation of blood glucose.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: B2, B3, B6"}'::jsonb),
('mc:factors that lower blood glucose level except::::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'factors that lower blood glucose level except:', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"• B1 (Thiamine) can counteract insulin/hypoglycemic agents: While Thiamine is vital for glucose metabolism, high doses of Vitamin B1 have been shown in classic clinical pharmacology to antagonize or inhibit the action of insulin. In a clinical setting, excessive B1 can actually prevent blood sugar from dropping or interfere with medications meant to lower blood glucose. Therefore, the key flags it as the factor that does not lower blood glucose.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"B1\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"• B1 (Thiamine) can counteract insulin/hypoglycemic agents: While Thiamine is vital for glucose metabolism, high doses of Vitamin B1 have been shown in classic clinical pharmacology to antagonize or inhibit the action of insulin. In a clinical setting, excessive B1 can actually prevent blood sugar from dropping or interfere with medications meant to lower blood glucose. Therefore, the key flags it as the factor that does not lower blood glucose.\n• B2, B6, and B3 actively lower or stabilize glucose: In the specific textbook framework this question was pulled from, Vitamins B2, B6, and B3 are grouped as nutrients that improve insulin sensitivity or support the down-regulation of blood glucose.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: B2, B3, B6"}'::jsonb),
('mc:the pancreas secrete when blood glucose levels increase and when blood glucose levels decrease.:::insulin; glucagon', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'the pancreas secrete when blood glucose levels increase and when blood glucose levels decrease.', 'insulin; glucagon', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"When blood glucose increases (e.g., after a meal): The beta cells of the pancreas secrete insulin. Insulin acts as a key to open cells, allowing them to absorb glucose from the bloodstream to be used for energy or stored as glycogen, thereby lowering blood sugar back to a normal range.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"insulin; glucagon\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"When blood glucose increases (e.g., after a meal): The beta cells of the pancreas secrete insulin. Insulin acts as a key to open cells, allowing them to absorb glucose from the bloodstream to be used for energy or stored as glycogen, thereby lowering blood sugar back to a normal range.\n\nWhen blood glucose decreases (e.g., during fasting): The alpha cells of the pancreas secrete glucagon. Glucagon signals the liver to break down its stored glycogen back into glucose and release it into the bloodstream, raising blood sugar back up to normal levels.\n\n• Glycogen is a starch-like molecule used for storing glucose in the liver and muscles; it is not a hormone secreted by the pancreas.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: glycogen; epinephrine, insulin; glycogen, glucagon; insulin"}'::jsonb),
('mc:example of fixed or built-in equipment for delivery and service.:::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'example of fixed or built-in equipment for delivery and service.', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"A dumbwaiter is a small freight elevator or lift intended to carry food. Dumbwaiters found within modern structures, including both commercial, public and private buildings, are often connected between multiple floors.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Dumbwaiter\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"A dumbwaiter is a small freight elevator or lift intended to carry food. Dumbwaiters found within modern structures, including both commercial, public and private buildings, are often connected between multiple floors.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Portable totes, Pellet disc, Carts"}'::jsonb),
('mc:the pancreas secrete when blood glucose levels increase and when blood glucose levels decrease.:::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'the pancreas secrete when blood glucose levels increase and when blood glucose levels decrease.', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"When blood glucose increases (e.g., after a meal): The beta cells of the pancreas secrete insulin. Insulin acts as a key to open cells, allowing them to absorb glucose from the bloodstream to be used for energy or stored as glycogen, thereby lowering blood sugar back to a normal range.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"insulin; glucagon\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"When blood glucose increases (e.g., after a meal): The beta cells of the pancreas secrete insulin. Insulin acts as a key to open cells, allowing them to absorb glucose from the bloodstream to be used for energy or stored as glycogen, thereby lowering blood sugar back to a normal range.\n\nWhen blood glucose decreases (e.g., during fasting): The alpha cells of the pancreas secrete glucagon. Glucagon signals the liver to break down its stored glycogen back into glucose and release it into the bloodstream, raising blood sugar back up to normal levels.\n\n• Glycogen is a starch-like molecule used for storing glucose in the liver and muscles; it is not a hormone secreted by the pancreas.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: glycogen; epinephrine, insulin; glycogen, glucagon; insulin"}'::jsonb),
('mc:a primary feature of emphysema::::destruction of the walls separating the alveoli', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'a primary feature of emphysema:', 'destruction of the walls separating the alveoli', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Emphysema is pathologically defined by the permanent enlargement of the air spaces distal to the terminal bronchioles, accompanied by the destruction of the alveolar walls (septa).","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"destruction of the walls separating the alveoli\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Emphysema is pathologically defined by the permanent enlargement of the air spaces distal to the terminal bronchioles, accompanied by the destruction of the alveolar walls (septa).\n\nWhen these walls break down, millions of tiny, independent air sacs merge into fewer, much larger, floppy pockets. This severely reduces the total surface area available for gas exchange. It also destroys the elastic fibers that keep the small airways open, causing the lungs to lose their elastic recoil, trap stale air, and collapse during exhalation.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: obstruction of the bronchi, obstruction within the bronchioles, excessive lung elasticity"}'::jsonb),
('mc:a primary feature of emphysema::::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'a primary feature of emphysema:', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Emphysema is pathologically defined by the permanent enlargement of the air spaces distal to the terminal bronchioles, accompanied by the destruction of the alveolar walls (septa).","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"destruction of the walls separating the alveoli\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Emphysema is pathologically defined by the permanent enlargement of the air spaces distal to the terminal bronchioles, accompanied by the destruction of the alveolar walls (septa).\n\nWhen these walls break down, millions of tiny, independent air sacs merge into fewer, much larger, floppy pockets. This severely reduces the total surface area available for gas exchange. It also destroys the elastic fibers that keep the small airways open, causing the lungs to lose their elastic recoil, trap stale air, and collapse during exhalation.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: obstruction of the bronchi, obstruction within the bronchioles, excessive lung elasticity"}'::jsonb),
('mc:what is the mark-up factor if the food cost is set at 33%?:::3', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'what is the mark-up factor if the food cost is set at 33%?', '3', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Mark up factor = 100/% food cost = 100/33 = 3","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"3\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Mark up factor = 100/% food cost = 100/33 = 3","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: 2, 4, 5"}'::jsonb),
