('mc:type of menu commonly used in institution where the menu is repeated within a specified time span.:::cycle menu', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'type of menu commonly used in institution where the menu is repeated within a specified time span.', 'Cycle menu', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Cycle or cyclical offers menu in rotation for a period of 6 to 30 days. Menu items are repeated at the end of a specified period. Most typically offered by non-commercial foodservices but may also be used by commercial buffet restaurants. It must be flexible to include leftovers or account for seasonal ingredients.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Cycle menu\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Cycle or cyclical offers menu in rotation for a period of 6 to 30 days. Menu items are repeated at the end of a specified period. Most typically offered by non-commercial foodservices but may also be used by commercial buffet restaurants. It must be flexible to include leftovers or account for seasonal ingredients.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Single-use menu, Selective menu, Static menu"}'::jsonb),
('mc:type of menu commonly used in institution where the menu is repeated within a specified time span.:::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'type of menu commonly used in institution where the menu is repeated within a specified time span.', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Cycle or cyclical offers menu in rotation for a period of 6 to 30 days. Menu items are repeated at the end of a specified period. Most typically offered by non-commercial foodservices but may also be used by commercial buffet restaurants. It must be flexible to include leftovers or account for seasonal ingredients.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Cycle menu\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Cycle or cyclical offers menu in rotation for a period of 6 to 30 days. Menu items are repeated at the end of a specified period. Most typically offered by non-commercial foodservices but may also be used by commercial buffet restaurants. It must be flexible to include leftovers or account for seasonal ingredients.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Single-use menu, Selective menu, Static menu"}'::jsonb),
('mc:food service system utilized in most coffee shops like dunkin donuts::::assembly', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'food service system utilized in most coffee shops like dunkin donuts:', 'Assembly', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Dunkin Donuts practices off premise food production and the food are usually just assembled, heated before service. This is a characteristic of assembly/serve foodserving system.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Assembly\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Dunkin Donuts practices off premise food production and the food are usually just assembled, heated before service. This is a characteristic of assembly/serve foodserving system.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Conventional, Commisary, Ready prepared"}'::jsonb),
('mc:an inventory system when an item is allowed to deplete to safety level before new order is submitted to bring up level up to maximum.:::minimax', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'an inventory system when an item is allowed to deplete to safety level before new order is submitted to bring up level up to maximum.', 'Minimax', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Minimax system means that an item is allowed to deplete to safety level before new order is submitted to bring up level up to maximum.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Minimax\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Minimax system means that an item is allowed to deplete to safety level before new order is submitted to bring up level up to maximum.\n\nIn foodservice inventory management, the Minimax system establishes two primary stock triggers for each item: a minimum safety level (the floor) and a maximum capacity level (the ceiling). Stock is deliberately allowed to deplete until it hits that lower safety margin. Once it reaches that precise reorder point, a fresh order is generated to purchase exactly enough supply to restore the inventory back up to its defined maximum limit.\n\n• Partially blind method: This is a technique used during physical inventory receiving where the clerk is given an invoice or PO showing the item names but with some quantities blocked out, forcing them to do a blind, independent count.\n• Economic order quantity (EOQ): This is a mathematical formula used to determine the absolute most cost-effective amount of inventory to order by perfectly balancing holding costs against ordering costs. It focuses on cost optimization rather than a strict minimum-to-maximum safety cutoff loop.\n• Par stock: Unlike the fluctuating buffer of the minimax system, par stock requires a set, fixed total quantity of an item to be physically present on the shelves at any given time. Every single time a replenishment order is placed, a purchase is made to bring the stock right back up to that static \"par\" number.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Par stock, Partially blind method, Economic order quantity"}'::jsonb),
('mc:an inventory system when an item is allowed to deplete to safety level before new order is submitted to bring up level up to maximum.:::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'an inventory system when an item is allowed to deplete to safety level before new order is submitted to bring up level up to maximum.', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Minimax system means that an item is allowed to deplete to safety level before new order is submitted to bring up level up to maximum.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Minimax\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Minimax system means that an item is allowed to deplete to safety level before new order is submitted to bring up level up to maximum.\n\nIn foodservice inventory management, the Minimax system establishes two primary stock triggers for each item: a minimum safety level (the floor) and a maximum capacity level (the ceiling). Stock is deliberately allowed to deplete until it hits that lower safety margin. Once it reaches that precise reorder point, a fresh order is generated to purchase exactly enough supply to restore the inventory back up to its defined maximum limit.\n\n• Partially blind method: This is a technique used during physical inventory receiving where the clerk is given an invoice or PO showing the item names but with some quantities blocked out, forcing them to do a blind, independent count.\n• Economic order quantity (EOQ): This is a mathematical formula used to determine the absolute most cost-effective amount of inventory to order by perfectly balancing holding costs against ordering costs. It focuses on cost optimization rather than a strict minimum-to-maximum safety cutoff loop.\n• Par stock: Unlike the fluctuating buffer of the minimax system, par stock requires a set, fixed total quantity of an item to be physically present on the shelves at any given time. Every single time a replenishment order is placed, a purchase is made to bring the stock right back up to that static \"par\" number.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Par stock, Partially blind method, Economic order quantity"}'::jsonb),
('mc:it refers to all conditions and measures necessary to ensure safety and suitability of food at all stages of the food chain.:::food hygiene', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'it refers to all conditions and measures necessary to ensure safety and suitability of food at all stages of the food chain.', 'Food hygiene', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Food hygiene comprises conditions and measures necessary for the production, processing, storage, and distribution of food designed to ensure a safe, sound, and wholesome product fit for human consumption.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Food hygiene\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Food hygiene comprises conditions and measures necessary for the production, processing, storage, and distribution of food designed to ensure a safe, sound, and wholesome product fit for human consumption.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Food safety, Food sanitation, HACCP"}'::jsonb),
