('mc:according to the labor code of the philippines, service charge is to be distributed as follows::::85% for all covered employees; 15% to management', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'according to the labor code of the philippines, service charge is to be distributed as follows:', '85% for all covered employees; 15% to management', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Service charges and pooled tips – eighty-five percent (85%) equally shared by the employees, fifteen percent (15%) retained by the management.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"85% for all covered employees; 15% to management\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Service charges and pooled tips – eighty-five percent (85%) equally shared by the employees, fifteen percent (15%) retained by the management.\n\nAlthough recent:\nUnder Article 96 of the Labor Code of the Philippines (as amended by Republic Act No. 11360 and DOLE Department Order No. 242, Series of 2024), service charges collected by hotels, restaurants, and similar establishments are distributed as follows:\n• 100% Full Distribution: All collected service charges must be distributed completely and equally among covered employees. Management can no longer retain a percentage.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: 90% for all covered employees; 10% to management, Equal distribution among all employees including rank-and-file and management, 10% for all rank-and-file employees; 90% to management"}'::jsonb),
('mc:according to the labor code of the philippines, service charge is to be distributed as follows::::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'according to the labor code of the philippines, service charge is to be distributed as follows:', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Service charges and pooled tips – eighty-five percent (85%) equally shared by the employees, fifteen percent (15%) retained by the management.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"85% for all covered employees; 15% to management\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Service charges and pooled tips – eighty-five percent (85%) equally shared by the employees, fifteen percent (15%) retained by the management.\n\nAlthough recent:\nUnder Article 96 of the Labor Code of the Philippines (as amended by Republic Act No. 11360 and DOLE Department Order No. 242, Series of 2024), service charges collected by hotels, restaurants, and similar establishments are distributed as follows:\n• 100% Full Distribution: All collected service charges must be distributed completely and equally among covered employees. Management can no longer retain a percentage.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: 90% for all covered employees; 10% to management, Equal distribution among all employees including rank-and-file and management, 10% for all rank-and-file employees; 90% to management"}'::jsonb),
('mc:this refers to the amount of product that should be on hand at any given time.:::par stock', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'this refers to the amount of product that should be on hand at any given time.', 'Par stock', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Par Stock – amount of product that should be on hand between one delivery and the next. This represents the ideal or standard operating quantity of an item that must be physically physically present in stock at any given time to fulfill normal customer demand between delivery cycles. When stock levels drop below this baseline figure, it automatically triggers a reorder to bring the inventory back up to par.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Par stock\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Par Stock – amount of product that should be on hand between one delivery and the next. This represents the ideal or standard operating quantity of an item that must be physically physically present in stock at any given time to fulfill normal customer demand between delivery cycles. When stock levels drop below this baseline figure, it automatically triggers a reorder to bring the inventory back up to par.\n\n• Minimum stock: This is the absolute safety threshold or \"buffer\" limit below which inventory should never fall to avoid severe stockouts or operational emergencies.\n• Ending stock: The actual physical quantity of product remaining on hand at the exact conclusion of an accounting or inventory reporting period.\n• Perpetual inventory: A continuous tracking method or accounting ledger system that automatically registers adjustments to stock balances in real time as items are bought or sold.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Perpetual inventory, Minimum stock, Ending stock"}'::jsonb),
('mc:this refers to the amount of product that should be on hand at any given time.:::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'this refers to the amount of product that should be on hand at any given time.', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Par Stock – amount of product that should be on hand between one delivery and the next. This represents the ideal or standard operating quantity of an item that must be physically physically present in stock at any given time to fulfill normal customer demand between delivery cycles. When stock levels drop below this baseline figure, it automatically triggers a reorder to bring the inventory back up to par.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Par stock\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Par Stock – amount of product that should be on hand between one delivery and the next. This represents the ideal or standard operating quantity of an item that must be physically physically present in stock at any given time to fulfill normal customer demand between delivery cycles. When stock levels drop below this baseline figure, it automatically triggers a reorder to bring the inventory back up to par.\n\n• Minimum stock: This is the absolute safety threshold or \"buffer\" limit below which inventory should never fall to avoid severe stockouts or operational emergencies.\n• Ending stock: The actual physical quantity of product remaining on hand at the exact conclusion of an accounting or inventory reporting period.\n• Perpetual inventory: A continuous tracking method or accounting ledger system that automatically registers adjustments to stock balances in real time as items are bought or sold.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Perpetual inventory, Minimum stock, Ending stock"}'::jsonb),
