('mc:chemical precursors of vitamins that are converted within the body to active vitamins.:::provitamins', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'chemical precursors of vitamins that are converted within the body to active vitamins.', 'Provitamins', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Provitamins are inactive substances that the body can convert into active vitamins through normal metabolic processes. A classic example is beta-carotene, which the human body converts into active Vitamin A in the intestinal wall.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Provitamins\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Provitamins are inactive substances that the body can convert into active vitamins through normal metabolic processes. A classic example is beta-carotene, which the human body converts into active Vitamin A in the intestinal wall.\n\n• Tetrahydrofolate is already an active, coenzyme form of Vitamin B9 (folate), rather than a precursor.\n• Anti-vitamins are chemical compounds that inhibit the absorption or metabolic actions of vitamins.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Anti-vitamins, Previtamins, Tertrahydrofolate"}'::jsonb),
('mc:chemical precursors of vitamins that are converted within the body to active vitamins.:::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'chemical precursors of vitamins that are converted within the body to active vitamins.', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Provitamins are inactive substances that the body can convert into active vitamins through normal metabolic processes. A classic example is beta-carotene, which the human body converts into active Vitamin A in the intestinal wall.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Provitamins\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Provitamins are inactive substances that the body can convert into active vitamins through normal metabolic processes. A classic example is beta-carotene, which the human body converts into active Vitamin A in the intestinal wall.\n\n• Tetrahydrofolate is already an active, coenzyme form of Vitamin B9 (folate), rather than a precursor.\n• Anti-vitamins are chemical compounds that inhibit the absorption or metabolic actions of vitamins.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Anti-vitamins, Previtamins, Tertrahydrofolate"}'::jsonb),
('mc:the energy-releasing process by which large molecules are broken down to form smaller molecules is::::catabolism', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'the energy-releasing process by which large molecules are broken down to form smaller molecules is:', 'Catabolism', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Catabolism is the branch of metabolism specifically responsible for breaking down complex, large molecules (such as carbohydrates, proteins, and lipids) into smaller, simpler units. This degradation process releases energy, which cells capture and store primarily in the form of ATP.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Catabolism\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Catabolism is the branch of metabolism specifically responsible for breaking down complex, large molecules (such as carbohydrates, proteins, and lipids) into smaller, simpler units. This degradation process releases energy, which cells capture and store primarily in the form of ATP.\n\n• Anabolism is the exact opposite; it uses energy to build large molecules from smaller ones.\n• Metabolism is the overarching term that combines both catabolism and anabolism.\n• Glycolysis is a specific metabolic pathway that breaks down glucose, meaning it is an example of a catabolic process rather than the general definition.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Anabolism, Metabolism, Glycolysis"}'::jsonb),
('mc:the energy-releasing process by which large molecules are broken down to form smaller molecules is::::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'the energy-releasing process by which large molecules are broken down to form smaller molecules is:', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Catabolism is the branch of metabolism specifically responsible for breaking down complex, large molecules (such as carbohydrates, proteins, and lipids) into smaller, simpler units. This degradation process releases energy, which cells capture and store primarily in the form of ATP.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Catabolism\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Catabolism is the branch of metabolism specifically responsible for breaking down complex, large molecules (such as carbohydrates, proteins, and lipids) into smaller, simpler units. This degradation process releases energy, which cells capture and store primarily in the form of ATP.\n\n• Anabolism is the exact opposite; it uses energy to build large molecules from smaller ones.\n• Metabolism is the overarching term that combines both catabolism and anabolism.\n• Glycolysis is a specific metabolic pathway that breaks down glucose, meaning it is an example of a catabolic process rather than the general definition.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Anabolism, Metabolism, Glycolysis"}'::jsonb),
('mc:which of the following is not considered a micromineral?:::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'which of the following is not considered a micromineral?', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Chlorine is a macronutrient mineral present in amounts greater than 5g.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Chlorine\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Chlorine is a macronutrient mineral present in amounts greater than 5g.\n\nChlorine is classified as a macromineral (major mineral). Macrominerals are required by the body in large quantities (greater than 100 mg per day), alongside calcium, sodium, and potassium. Therefore, it is not a micromineral.\n\n• Copper, Zinc, and Iron: Both of these are classic examples of essential microminerals (trace minerals) required by the body in very small quantities.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Iron, Copper, Zinc"}'::jsonb),
('mc:a 6-carbon vitamin compound closely related to the monosaccharide glucose.:::vit b6', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'a 6-carbon vitamin compound closely related to the monosaccharide glucose.', 'Vit B6', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Vitamin B6 (specifically its vitamer Pyridoxine) is a 6-carbon compound (chemical formula: C₈H₁₁NO₃) containing a pyridine ring with six ring carbons. It has key structural similarities and functional relationships with glucose metabolism (e.g., serving as an essential cofactor for glycogen phosphorylase to break down glycogen into glucose units).","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Vit B6\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Vitamin B6 (specifically its vitamer Pyridoxine) is a 6-carbon compound (chemical formula: C₈H₁₁NO₃) containing a pyridine ring with six ring carbons. It has key structural similarities and functional relationships with glucose metabolism (e.g., serving as an essential cofactor for glycogen phosphorylase to break down glycogen into glucose units).\n\n• Note on Vitamin C: While Vitamin C (Ascorbic acid) is the classic 6-carbon vitamin structurally derived directly from and most closely resembling the monosaccharide glucose (C₆H₈O₆), it is not listed among the given options. Among the choices provided, Vitamin B6 matches the 6-carbon molecular framework constraint.\n• Vitamin B12 is an extremely large, complex organometallic compound containing dozens of carbons and a cobalt core.\n• Vitamins D₂ and D₃ are large Secosteroid structural variants containing over 27 carbons.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Vit B12, Vit D2, Vit D3"}'::jsonb),
('mc:a 6-carbon vitamin compound closely related to the monosaccharide glucose.:::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'a 6-carbon vitamin compound closely related to the monosaccharide glucose.', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Vitamin B6 (specifically its vitamer Pyridoxine) is a 6-carbon compound (chemical formula: C₈H₁₁NO₃) containing a pyridine ring with six ring carbons. It has key structural similarities and functional relationships with glucose metabolism (e.g., serving as an essential cofactor for glycogen phosphorylase to break down glycogen into glucose units).","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Vit B6\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Vitamin B6 (specifically its vitamer Pyridoxine) is a 6-carbon compound (chemical formula: C₈H₁₁NO₃) containing a pyridine ring with six ring carbons. It has key structural similarities and functional relationships with glucose metabolism (e.g., serving as an essential cofactor for glycogen phosphorylase to break down glycogen into glucose units).\n\n• Note on Vitamin C: While Vitamin C (Ascorbic acid) is the classic 6-carbon vitamin structurally derived directly from and most closely resembling the monosaccharide glucose (C₆H₈O₆), it is not listed among the given options. Among the choices provided, Vitamin B6 matches the 6-carbon molecular framework constraint.\n• Vitamin B12 is an extremely large, complex organometallic compound containing dozens of carbons and a cobalt core.\n• Vitamins D₂ and D₃ are large Secosteroid structural variants containing over 27 carbons.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Vit B12, Vit D2, Vit D3"}'::jsonb)
ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;
