('mc:a b-vitamin in pyruvate dehydrogenase, and enzyme that converts pyruvate to acetyl co-a.:::b1', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'a b-vitamin in pyruvate dehydrogenase, and enzyme that converts pyruvate to acetyl co-a.', 'B1', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Vitamin B1 or thiamin is a coenzyme of pyruvate dehydrogenase complex that catalyze the conversion of pyruvate to acetyl-coA.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"B1\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Vitamin B1 or thiamin is a coenzyme of pyruvate dehydrogenase complex that catalyze the conversion of pyruvate to acetyl-coA.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: B5, B2, B3"}'::jsonb),
('mc:a b-vitamin in pyruvate dehydrogenase, and enzyme that converts pyruvate to acetyl co-a.:::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'a b-vitamin in pyruvate dehydrogenase, and enzyme that converts pyruvate to acetyl co-a.', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Vitamin B1 or thiamin is a coenzyme of pyruvate dehydrogenase complex that catalyze the conversion of pyruvate to acetyl-coA.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"B1\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Vitamin B1 or thiamin is a coenzyme of pyruvate dehydrogenase complex that catalyze the conversion of pyruvate to acetyl-coA.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: B5, B2, B3"}'::jsonb),
('mc:which of the following is not considered a micromineral?:::chlorine', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'which of the following is not considered a micromineral?', 'Chlorine', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Chlorine is a macronutrient mineral present in amounts greater than 5g.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Chlorine\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Chlorine is a macronutrient mineral present in amounts greater than 5g.\n\nChlorine is classified as a macromineral (major mineral). Macrominerals are required by the body in large quantities (greater than 100 mg per day), alongside calcium, sodium, and potassium. Therefore, it is not a micromineral.\n\n• Copper, Zinc, and Iron: Both of these are classic examples of essential microminerals (trace minerals) required by the body in very small quantities.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Iron, Copper, Zinc"}'::jsonb),
('mc:helps maintain normal blood sugar level.:::chromium', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'helps maintain normal blood sugar level.', 'Chromium', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Chromium is a trace mineral that helps the body use insulin properly. It makes insulin more effective at moving sugar (glucose) from your blood into your cells for energy.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Chromium\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Chromium is a trace mineral that helps the body use insulin properly. It makes insulin more effective at moving sugar (glucose) from your blood into your cells for energy.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Iron, Selenium, Copper"}'::jsonb),
('mc:helps maintain normal blood sugar level.:::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'helps maintain normal blood sugar level.', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Chromium is a trace mineral that helps the body use insulin properly. It makes insulin more effective at moving sugar (glucose) from your blood into your cells for energy.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Chromium\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Chromium is a trace mineral that helps the body use insulin properly. It makes insulin more effective at moving sugar (glucose) from your blood into your cells for energy.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Iron, Selenium, Copper"}'::jsonb),
('mc:this micronutrient is deficient for those with rachitic rosary.:::vit d', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'this micronutrient is deficient for those with rachitic rosary.', 'Vit D', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Rachitic rosary is the swelling or beading at the costochondral junctions where the ribs meet the cartilage, looking like a string of beads on the chest. It is a classic physical sign of Rickets, caused by a lack of Vitamin D, which stops proper bone hardening.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Vit D\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Rachitic rosary is the swelling or beading at the costochondral junctions where the ribs meet the cartilage, looking like a string of beads on the chest. It is a classic physical sign of Rickets, caused by a lack of Vitamin D, which stops proper bone hardening.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Calcium, Phosphorus"}'::jsonb),
('mc:this micronutrient is deficient for those with rachitic rosary.:::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'this micronutrient is deficient for those with rachitic rosary.', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Rachitic rosary is the swelling or beading at the costochondral junctions where the ribs meet the cartilage, looking like a string of beads on the chest. It is a classic physical sign of Rickets, caused by a lack of Vitamin D, which stops proper bone hardening.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Vit D\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Rachitic rosary is the swelling or beading at the costochondral junctions where the ribs meet the cartilage, looking like a string of beads on the chest. It is a classic physical sign of Rickets, caused by a lack of Vitamin D, which stops proper bone hardening.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Calcium, Phosphorus"}'::jsonb),
('mc:its anti-vitamin is isonicotinic acid hydrazide used in the treatment of tuberculosis.:::vit b6', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'its anti-vitamin is isonicotinic acid hydrazide used in the treatment of tuberculosis.', 'Vit B6', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Isoniazid inhibits pyridoxine phosphokinase, which is responsible for maintaining the active form of vitamin B6. Isonicotinic acid hydrazide is known to increase vitamin B6 requirements.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Vit B6\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Isoniazid inhibits pyridoxine phosphokinase, which is responsible for maintaining the active form of vitamin B6. Isonicotinic acid hydrazide is known to increase vitamin B6 requirements.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Vit B1, Vit B12"}'::jsonb),
('mc:its anti-vitamin is isonicotinic acid hydrazide used in the treatment of tuberculosis.:::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'its anti-vitamin is isonicotinic acid hydrazide used in the treatment of tuberculosis.', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Isoniazid inhibits pyridoxine phosphokinase, which is responsible for maintaining the active form of vitamin B6. Isonicotinic acid hydrazide is known to increase vitamin B6 requirements.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Vit B6\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Isoniazid inhibits pyridoxine phosphokinase, which is responsible for maintaining the active form of vitamin B6. Isonicotinic acid hydrazide is known to increase vitamin B6 requirements.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Vit B1, Vit B12"}'::jsonb),
('mc:its coenzymes fad and fmn participate in oxidation-reduction reactions due to their ability to accept and transfer h atoms.:::riboflavin', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'its coenzymes fad and fmn participate in oxidation-reduction reactions due to their ability to accept and transfer h atoms.', 'Riboflavin', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Riboflavin (Vitamin B2) is the precursor for FAD (Flavin Adenine Dinucleotide) and FMN (Flavin Mononucleotide).","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Riboflavin\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Riboflavin (Vitamin B2) is the precursor for FAD (Flavin Adenine Dinucleotide) and FMN (Flavin Mononucleotide).\n\nRiboflavin, the primary redox active component of flavin, is involved in many redox processes in biogeochemical systems. The flavin ring in these coenzymes can reversibly accept and transfer two hydrogen atoms (electrons and protons), switching between oxidized and reduced states (e.g., FAD  FADH₂).","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Thiamin, Niacin"}'::jsonb),
('mc:its coenzymes fad and fmn participate in oxidation-reduction reactions due to their ability to accept and transfer h atoms.:::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'its coenzymes fad and fmn participate in oxidation-reduction reactions due to their ability to accept and transfer h atoms.', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Riboflavin (Vitamin B2) is the precursor for FAD (Flavin Adenine Dinucleotide) and FMN (Flavin Mononucleotide).","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Riboflavin\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Riboflavin (Vitamin B2) is the precursor for FAD (Flavin Adenine Dinucleotide) and FMN (Flavin Mononucleotide).\n\nRiboflavin, the primary redox active component of flavin, is involved in many redox processes in biogeochemical systems. The flavin ring in these coenzymes can reversibly accept and transfer two hydrogen atoms (electrons and protons), switching between oxidized and reduced states (e.g., FAD  FADH₂).","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Thiamin, Niacin"}'::jsonb),
('mc:anti-beriberi factor.:::thiamin', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'anti-beriberi factor.', 'Thiamin', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Beri-beri is the result of thiamin deficiency. Thiamin (Vitamin B1) is historically designated as the anti-beriberi factor because its clinical discovery directly stemmed from searching for a cure for beriberi.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Thiamin\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Beri-beri is the result of thiamin deficiency. Thiamin (Vitamin B1) is historically designated as the anti-beriberi factor because its clinical discovery directly stemmed from searching for a cure for beriberi.\n\nBeriberi is a severe deficiency disease caused by a lack of Thiamin, presenting as either dry beriberi (neurological damage, muscle wasting) or wet beriberi (cardiovascular failure, edema).\n\n• Niacin (B3) is the anti-pellagra factor, while Riboflavin (B2) deficiency causes ariboflavinosis (cheilosis, glossitis).","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Riboflavin, B4"}'::jsonb),
('mc:anti-beriberi factor.:::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'anti-beriberi factor.', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Beri-beri is the result of thiamin deficiency. Thiamin (Vitamin B1) is historically designated as the anti-beriberi factor because its clinical discovery directly stemmed from searching for a cure for beriberi.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Thiamin\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Beri-beri is the result of thiamin deficiency. Thiamin (Vitamin B1) is historically designated as the anti-beriberi factor because its clinical discovery directly stemmed from searching for a cure for beriberi.\n\nBeriberi is a severe deficiency disease caused by a lack of Thiamin, presenting as either dry beriberi (neurological damage, muscle wasting) or wet beriberi (cardiovascular failure, edema).\n\n• Niacin (B3) is the anti-pellagra factor, while Riboflavin (B2) deficiency causes ariboflavinosis (cheilosis, glossitis).","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Riboflavin, B4"}'::jsonb),
('mc:the following are functions of sodium, except::::participates in detoxification reactions', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'the following are functions of sodium, except:', 'Participates in detoxification reactions', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Sodium also maintains the normal pH value of extracellular fluids but does not participate in detoxification reactions.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Participates in detoxification reactions\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Sodium also maintains the normal pH value of extracellular fluids but does not participate in detoxification reactions.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Maintenance of normal extracellular fluid balance., Absorption of glucose and in the transport of other nutrients"}'::jsonb),
('mc:the following are functions of sodium, except::::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'the following are functions of sodium, except:', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Sodium also maintains the normal pH value of extracellular fluids but does not participate in detoxification reactions.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Participates in detoxification reactions\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Sodium also maintains the normal pH value of extracellular fluids but does not participate in detoxification reactions.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Maintenance of normal extracellular fluid balance., Absorption of glucose and in the transport of other nutrients"}'::jsonb),
('mc:a vitamin synthesized in the liver from the amino acid tryptophan.:::niacin', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'a vitamin synthesized in the liver from the amino acid tryptophan.', 'Niacin', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Also known as vitamin B3, niacin is unique among water-soluble vitamins because the body does not rely solely on dietary intake. The liver can directly synthesize niacin from the essential amino acid tryptophan via the kynurenine pathway. 1 mg of niacin can be synthesized from the ingestion of 60 mg of tryptophan.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Niacin\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Also known as vitamin B3, niacin is unique among water-soluble vitamins because the body does not rely solely on dietary intake. The liver can directly synthesize niacin from the essential amino acid tryptophan via the kynurenine pathway. 1 mg of niacin can be synthesized from the ingestion of 60 mg of tryptophan.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Thiamin, Riboflavin"}'::jsonb),
('mc:a vitamin synthesized in the liver from the amino acid tryptophan.:::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'a vitamin synthesized in the liver from the amino acid tryptophan.', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Also known as vitamin B3, niacin is unique among water-soluble vitamins because the body does not rely solely on dietary intake. The liver can directly synthesize niacin from the essential amino acid tryptophan via the kynurenine pathway. 1 mg of niacin can be synthesized from the ingestion of 60 mg of tryptophan.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Niacin\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Also known as vitamin B3, niacin is unique among water-soluble vitamins because the body does not rely solely on dietary intake. The liver can directly synthesize niacin from the essential amino acid tryptophan via the kynurenine pathway. 1 mg of niacin can be synthesized from the ingestion of 60 mg of tryptophan.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Thiamin, Riboflavin"}'::jsonb),
('mc:deficiency of this micronutrient is associated with bleeding associated with delayed blood clotting.:::vitamin k', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'deficiency of this micronutrient is associated with bleeding associated with delayed blood clotting.', 'Vitamin K', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Vitamin K is well-known as the \"clotting vitamin\". It functions as an essential cofactor for the enzyme that activates crucial blood coagulation factors (Factors II, VII, IX, and X) in the liver.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Vitamin K\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Vitamin K is well-known as the \"clotting vitamin\". It functions as an essential cofactor for the enzyme that activates crucial blood coagulation factors (Factors II, VII, IX, and X) in the liver.\n\nA deficiency in Vitamin K means these clotting factors cannot be properly synthesized, directly causing delayed blood clotting, prolonged prothrombin time, and an increased risk of bruising or bleeding (hemorrhage).\n\n• While a Vitamin C deficiency (scurvy) can cause fragile blood vessels and bleeding gums, it does not directly impair the chemical coagulation cascade like Vitamin K does.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Iron, Vit C, Choline"}'::jsonb),
('mc:deficiency of this micronutrient is associated with bleeding associated with delayed blood clotting.:::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'deficiency of this micronutrient is associated with bleeding associated with delayed blood clotting.', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Vitamin K is well-known as the \"clotting vitamin\". It functions as an essential cofactor for the enzyme that activates crucial blood coagulation factors (Factors II, VII, IX, and X) in the liver.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Vitamin K\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Vitamin K is well-known as the \"clotting vitamin\". It functions as an essential cofactor for the enzyme that activates crucial blood coagulation factors (Factors II, VII, IX, and X) in the liver.\n\nA deficiency in Vitamin K means these clotting factors cannot be properly synthesized, directly causing delayed blood clotting, prolonged prothrombin time, and an increased risk of bruising or bleeding (hemorrhage).\n\n• While a Vitamin C deficiency (scurvy) can cause fragile blood vessels and bleeding gums, it does not directly impair the chemical coagulation cascade like Vitamin K does.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Iron, Vit C, Choline"}'::jsonb),
('mc:it is considered both a vitamin and a hormone.:::vit d', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'it is considered both a vitamin and a hormone.', 'Vit D', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"As a vitamin: You can get it from food or supplements because your body needs it to stay healthy.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Vit D\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"As a vitamin: You can get it from food or supplements because your body needs it to stay healthy.\n\nAs a hormone: Your body can also make it in your skin when the sun shines on it. It travels through your blood to tell other parts of your body, like your bones and intestines, to absorb calcium.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Vit A, Vit E"}'::jsonb),
