('mc:it is considered antioxidative mineral.:::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'it is considered antioxidative mineral.', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Selenium is a cofactor of the antioxidant exam glutathione peroxidase, making it an antioxidant mineral.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Selenium\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Selenium is a cofactor of the antioxidant exam glutathione peroxidase, making it an antioxidant mineral.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Iron, Zinc"}'::jsonb),
('mc:all are sulfur-containing vitamin except::::b5', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'all are sulfur-containing vitamin except:', 'B5', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Among the choices provided, Vitamin B5 (Pantothenic acid) is the only one whose chemical structure does not inherently contain a sulfur atom.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"B5\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Among the choices provided, Vitamin B5 (Pantothenic acid) is the only one whose chemical structure does not inherently contain a sulfur atom.\n\n• Vitamin B1 (Thiamine): Contains a thiazole ring, which explicitly features a sulfur atom joined with nitrogen.\n• Vitamin B7 (Biotin): Features a tetrahydrothiophene ring system, which structurally contains a sulfur atom.\n• Vitamin B5 (Pantothenic acid): Formed from the combination of pantoic acid and beta-alanine. It contains carbon, hydrogen, nitrogen, and oxygen, but no sulfur. (Note: While it binds to Coenzyme A—which has a sulfur-containing thiol group—to function in the body, the vitamin molecule itself does not contain sulfur).","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: B1, B7, None of the above"}'::jsonb),
('mc:all are sulfur-containing vitamin except::::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'all are sulfur-containing vitamin except:', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Among the choices provided, Vitamin B5 (Pantothenic acid) is the only one whose chemical structure does not inherently contain a sulfur atom.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"B5\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Among the choices provided, Vitamin B5 (Pantothenic acid) is the only one whose chemical structure does not inherently contain a sulfur atom.\n\n• Vitamin B1 (Thiamine): Contains a thiazole ring, which explicitly features a sulfur atom joined with nitrogen.\n• Vitamin B7 (Biotin): Features a tetrahydrothiophene ring system, which structurally contains a sulfur atom.\n• Vitamin B5 (Pantothenic acid): Formed from the combination of pantoic acid and beta-alanine. It contains carbon, hydrogen, nitrogen, and oxygen, but no sulfur. (Note: While it binds to Coenzyme A—which has a sulfur-containing thiol group—to function in the body, the vitamin molecule itself does not contain sulfur).","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: B1, B7, None of the above"}'::jsonb)
