('mc:nutrient that promotes calcium-binding protein activity in the intestinal tract.:::vit d', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'nutrient that promotes calcium-binding protein activity in the intestinal tract.', 'Vit D', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Vitamin D facilitates calcium absorption.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Vit D\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Vitamin D facilitates calcium absorption.\n\nVitamin D (specifically its active form, calcitriol) acts as a hormone that upregulates the transcription and expression of calbindin, a crucial calcium-binding protein located in the intestinal epithelial cells. This protein physically binds and facilitates the active transport and absorption of calcium across the intestinal tract.\n\n• Vitamins A, E, and K do not play a direct role in inducing intestinal calcium-binding protein synthesis or managing active dietary calcium absorption.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Vit A, Vit K, Vit E"}'::jsonb),
