 support during the critical First 1,000 Days of child development.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Batangas, Batanes, Benguet"}'::jsonb),
('mc:who is the chair of provincial nutrition committee?:::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'who is the chair of provincial nutrition committee?', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"At every level of the local government unit (LGU) framework in the Philippines, the Local Chief Executive serves as the default chairperson of the local nutrition committee:","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Governor\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"At every level of the local government unit (LGU) framework in the Philippines, the Local Chief Executive serves as the default chairperson of the local nutrition committee:\n\n• Provincial Level: Chaired by the Governor.\n• City / Municipal Level: Chaired by the Mayor.\n• Barangay Level: Chaired by the Barangay Captain (Punong Barangay).\n\nThe Provincial Nutrition Action Officer (PNAO) operates under the leadership of the Governor to handle the day-to-day operations, implementation, and coordination of the province''s nutrition programs.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Provincial Nutrition Action Officer, Barangay Captain, Mayor"}'::jsonb),
('mc:the first philippine food and nutrition plan was called ?:::philippine nutrition program', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'the first philippine food and nutrition plan was called ?', 'Philippine Nutrition Program', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"In 1971, President Ferdinand Marcos Sr. issued Executive Order No. 285, authorizing the National Food and Agriculture Council (NFAC) to coordinate all nutrition activities.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Philippine Nutrition Program\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"In 1971, President Ferdinand Marcos Sr. issued Executive Order No. 285, authorizing the National Food and Agriculture Council (NFAC) to coordinate all nutrition activities.\n\nThis integrated nationwide operation was officially known as the Philippine Food and Nutrition Program (PFNP) or simply the Philippine Nutrition Program (PNP).\n\nIt served as the structural blueprint for national intervention before the creation of the National Nutrition Council (NNC) in 1974 and the later evolution of the framework into what we now call the Philippine Plan of Action for Nutrition (PPAN).\n\n• Philippine Food and Nutrition Program (PFNP, 1971–1974): Before the formal creation of the council, the National Food and Agriculture Council (NFAC) coordinated the first four-year Philippine Food and Nutrition Program under Executive Order No. 285.\n• Philippine Nutrition Program (PNP, 1974–1983): In 1974, Presidential Decree No. 491 established the National Nutrition Council (NNC), formally consolidating national nutrition activities into the Philippine Nutrition Program (PNP). It covered iterations such as PNP 1974–1977 and PNP 1978–1982.\n• Food and Nutrition Plan / Philippine Food and Nutrition Plan (FNP / PFNP, 1984–1992): In the mid-1980s, the blueprint returned to a linked agriculture-and-nutrition scope, adopted as the Food and Nutrition Plan (1984–1987) and later updated under the post-1986 reorganized NNC (via Executive Order No. 234) as the Philippine Food and Nutrition Plan (1987–1992).\n• Philippine Plan of Action for Nutrition (PPAN, 1993–Present): Following the global 1992 International Conference on Nutrition (ICN) in Rome, the Philippines aligned with international frameworks by adopting the title Philippine Plan of Action for Nutrition. \n\nWhile sometimes referenced alongside the medium-term planning cycles (e.g., MTPFNP/MTPPAN), the core official name of the plan series became PPAN, which has continued across successive medium-term cycles:\n\t• PPAN 1993–1998\n\t• PPAN 1999–2004\n\t• PPAN 2005–2010\n\t• PPAN 2011–2016\n\t• PPAN 2017–2022\n\t• PPAN 2023–2028","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Food and Nutrition Plan, Philippine Food and Nutrition Plan, Philippine Plan of Action for Nutrition"}'::jsonb),
('mc:the first philippine food and nutrition plan was called ?:::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'the first philippine food and nutrition plan was called ?', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"In 1971, President Ferdinand Marcos Sr. issued Executive Order No. 285, authorizing the National Food and Agriculture Council (NFAC) to coordinate all nutrition activities.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Philippine Nutrition Program\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"In 1971, President Ferdinand Marcos Sr. issued Executive Order No. 285, authorizing the National Food and Agriculture Council (NFAC) to coordinate all nutrition activities.\n\nThis integrated nationwide operation was officially known as the Philippine Food and Nutrition Program (PFNP) or simply the Philippine Nutrition Program (PNP).\n\nIt served as the structural blueprint for national intervention before the creation of the National Nutrition Council (NNC) in 1974 and the later evolution of the framework into what we now call the Philippine Plan of Action for Nutrition (PPAN).\n\n• Philippine Food and Nutrition Program (PFNP, 1971–1974): Before the formal creation of the council, the National Food and Agriculture Council (NFAC) coordinated the first four-year Philippine Food and Nutrition Program under Executive Order No. 285.\n• Philippine Nutrition Program (PNP, 1974–1983): In 1974, Presidential Decree No. 491 established the National Nutrition Council (NNC), formally consolidating national nutrition activities into the Philippine Nutrition Program (PNP). It covered iterations such as PNP 1974–1977 and PNP 1978–1982.\n• Food and Nutrition Plan / Philippine Food and Nutrition Plan (FNP / PFNP, 1984–1992): In the mid-1980s, the blueprint returned to a linked agriculture-and-nutrition scope, adopted as the Food and Nutrition Plan (1984–1987) and later updated under the post-1986 reorganized NNC (via Executive Order No. 234) as the Philippine Food and Nutrition Plan (1987–1992).\n• Philippine Plan of Action for Nutrition (PPAN, 1993–Present): Following the global 1992 International Conference on Nutrition (ICN) in Rome, the Philippines aligned with international frameworks by adopting the title Philippine Plan of Action for Nutrition. \n\nWhile sometimes referenced alongside the medium-term planning cycles (e.g., MTPFNP/MTPPAN), the core official name of the plan series became PPAN, which has continued across successive medium-term cycles:\n\t• PPAN 1993–1998\n\t• PPAN 1999–2004\n\t• PPAN 2005–2010\n\t• PPAN 2011–2016\n\t• PPAN 2017–2022\n\t• PPAN 2023–2028","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Food and Nutrition Plan, Philippine Food and Nutrition Plan, Philippine Plan of Action for Nutrition"}'::jsonb),
('mc:which law targets addressing specific micronutrient deficiencies by mandatory fortification of staple food items such as rice with iron, wheat flour with vit a and iron, refined sugar with vit a and cooking oil with vit a and voluntary fortification of other food items?:::ra 8976', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'which law targets addressing specific micronutrient deficiencies by mandatory fortification of staple food items such as rice with iron, wheat flour with vit a and iron, refined sugar with vit a and cooking oil with vit a and voluntary fortification of other food items?', 'RA 8976', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Republic Act 8976 (The Philippine Food Fortification Act of 2000) mandates the fortification of staple foods to address widespread micronutrient deficiencies (specifically Vitamin A, Iron, and Iodine). It explicitly requires the mandatory fortification of:","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"RA 8976\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Republic Act 8976 (The Philippine Food Fortification Act of 2000) mandates the fortification of staple foods to address widespread micronutrient deficiencies (specifically Vitamin A, Iron, and Iodine). It explicitly requires the mandatory fortification of:\n\t• Rice with Iron\n\t• Wheat flour with Vitamin A and Iron\n\t• Refined sugar with Vitamin A\n\t• Cooking oil with Vitamin A\n\nIt also covers the voluntary fortification of processed food items through the National Nutrition Council (NNC)''s \"Sangkap Pinoy\" seal program.\n\n• RA 8172: The ASIN Law (Act for Salt Iodization Nationwide), which mandates the iodization of all food-grade salt.\n• RA 10028: The Expanded Breastfeeding Promotion Act of 2009.\n• RA 7600: The Rooming-In and Breastfeeding Act of 1992.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: RA 7600, RA 8172, RA 10028"}'::jsonb),
('mc:is a cluster of programs that is comprehensive, long-range in time frame, and includes defined goals, strategies, and guidelines for implementation.:::plan', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'is a cluster of programs that is comprehensive, long-range in time frame, and includes defined goals, strategies, and guidelines for implementation.', 'Plan', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"According to the official Nutrition Program Management and community planning taxonomy, these structural terms are arranged in a specific hierarchy:","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Plan\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"According to the official Nutrition Program Management and community planning taxonomy, these structural terms are arranged in a specific hierarchy:\n\n1. Plan: This is the highest level of the hierarchy. It refers to a comprehensive, long-range cluster of programs that contains overarching defined goals, strategies, and broad guidelines for implementation. A prime example is the Philippine Plan of Action for Nutrition (PPAN).\n2. Program: This sits below a plan and is a set of interrelated projects that coordinates personnel, facilities, money, equipment, and supplies to accomplish a specific operational objective.\n3. Project: This is a specific, time-bound set of tasks or activities undertaken to achieve a given objective according to a defined budget and timetable.\n4. Policy: This serves as the broad, overarching legal or administrative instrument—the system of principles or rules—that guides subsequent development plans.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Policy, Program, Project"}'::jsonb),
('mc:is a cluster of programs that is comprehensive, long-range in time frame, and includes defined goals, strategies, and guidelines for implementation.:::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'is a cluster of programs that is comprehensive, long-range in time frame, and includes defined goals, strategies, and guidelines for implementation.', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"According to the official Nutrition Program Management and community planning taxonomy, these structural terms are arranged in a specific hierarchy:","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Plan\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"According to the official Nutrition Program Management and community planning taxonomy, these structural terms are arranged in a specific hierarchy:\n\n1. Plan: This is the highest level of the hierarchy. It refers to a comprehensive, long-range cluster of programs that contains overarching defined goals, strategies, and broad guidelines for implementation. A prime example is the Philippine Plan of Action for Nutrition (PPAN).\n2. Program: This sits below a plan and is a set of interrelated projects that coordinates personnel, facilities, money, equipment, and supplies to accomplish a specific operational objective.\n3. Project: This is a specific, time-bound set of tasks or activities undertaken to achieve a given objective according to a defined budget and timetable.\n4. Policy: This serves as the broad, overarching legal or administrative instrument—the system of principles or rules—that guides subsequent development plans.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Policy, Program, Project"}'::jsonb),
('mc:is a specific and time-bound set of tasks or activities undertaken to achieve a given objective according to a defined budget and timeable.:::project', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'is a specific and time-bound set of tasks or activities undertaken to achieve a given objective according to a defined budget and timeable.', 'Project', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"By standard administrative and project management definitions, a project is a temporary, specific, and time-bound endeavor. It possesses a clearly defined start and end date, sequential activities, and a dedicated budget allocated to produce a unique product, service, or result that fulfills a specific objective.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Project\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"By standard administrative and project management definitions, a project is a temporary, specific, and time-bound endeavor. It possesses a clearly defined start and end date, sequential activities, and a dedicated budget allocated to produce a unique product, service, or result that fulfills a specific objective.\n\n• Policy: A standing operational plan that serves as a broad, ongoing guideline for decision-making and employee behavior rather than a temporary set of tasks.\n• Plan: The overarching blueprint or management process of setting broad organizational goals and mapping out how to achieve them.\n• Program: A long-term, continuous framework that aggregates multiple individual projects under a shared theme or broader social goal, usually lacking a singular, short-term definitive timeline.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Policy, Plan, Program"}'::jsonb),
('mc:is a specific and time-bound set of tasks or activities undertaken to achieve a given objective according to a defined budget and timeable.:::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'is a specific and time-bound set of tasks or activities undertaken to achieve a given objective according to a defined budget and timeable.', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"By standard administrative and project management definitions, a project is a temporary, specific, and time-bound endeavor. It possesses a clearly defined start and end date, sequential activities, and a dedicated budget allocated to produce a unique product, service, or result that fulfills a specific objective.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Project\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"By standard administrative and project management definitions, a project is a temporary, specific, and time-bound endeavor. It possesses a clearly defined start and end date, sequential activities, and a dedicated budget allocated to produce a unique product, service, or result that fulfills a specific objective.\n\n• Policy: A standing operational plan that serves as a broad, ongoing guideline for decision-making and employee behavior rather than a temporary set of tasks.\n• Plan: The overarching blueprint or management process of setting broad organizational goals and mapping out how to achieve them.\n• Program: A long-term, continuous framework that aggregates multiple individual projects under a shared theme or broader social goal, usually lacking a singular, short-term definitive timeline.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Policy, Plan, Program"}'::jsonb),
('mc:these are programs that address the immediate determinants of fetal and child nutrition and development.:::nutrition-specific', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'these are programs that address the immediate determinants of fetal and child nutrition and development.', 'Nutrition-specific', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"According to global health frameworks (such as The Lancet Maternal and Child Nutrition Series) and Philippine law (such as Republic Act 11148), interventions are categorized based on what causes of malnutrition they address:","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Nutrition-specific\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"According to global health frameworks (such as The Lancet Maternal and Child Nutrition Series) and Philippine law (such as Republic Act 11148), interventions are categorized based on what causes of malnutrition they address:\n\n• Nutrition-specific programs directly address the immediate determinants of fetal and child nutrition and development. These include factors like adequate food and nutrient intake, infant feeding and caregiving practices, and reducing the burden of infectious diseases. Examples include micronutrient supplementation, exclusive breastfeeding promotion, and the treatment of severe acute malnutrition.\n• Nutrition-sensitive programs address the underlying determinants of malnutrition. These involve systemic sectors like agriculture, food security, social protection, clean water and sanitation (WASH), and female education.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Nutrition-related, Nutrition-sensitive, Nutrition-oriented"}'::jsonb),
('mc:these are programs that address the immediate determinants of fetal and child nutrition and development.:::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'these are programs that address the immediate determinants of fetal and child nutrition and development.', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"According to global health frameworks (such as The Lancet Maternal and Child Nutrition Series) and Philippine law (such as Republic Act 11148), interventions are categorized based on what causes of malnutrition they address:","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Nutrition-specific\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"According to global health frameworks (such as The Lancet Maternal and Child Nutrition Series) and Philippine law (such as Republic Act 11148), interventions are categorized based on what causes of malnutrition they address:\n\n• Nutrition-specific programs directly address the immediate determinants of fetal and child nutrition and development. These include factors like adequate food and nutrient intake, infant feeding and caregiving practices, and reducing the burden of infectious diseases. Examples include micronutrient supplementation, exclusive breastfeeding promotion, and the treatment of severe acute malnutrition.\n• Nutrition-sensitive programs address the underlying determinants of malnutrition. These involve systemic sectors like agriculture, food security, social protection, clean water and sanitation (WASH), and female education.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Nutrition-related, Nutrition-sensitive, Nutrition-oriented"}'::jsonb),
('mc:is the country''s highest policy-making and coordinating body on nutrition.:::nnc', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'is the country''s highest policy-making and coordinating body on nutrition.', 'NNC', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"The National Nutrition Council (NNC) is officially recognized by law as the Philippines'' highest policy-making and coordinating body on nutrition.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"NNC\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"The National Nutrition Council (NNC) is officially recognized by law as the Philippines'' highest policy-making and coordinating body on nutrition.\n\nIt was originally created under Presidential Decree No. 491 (The Nutrition Act of the Philippines) in 1974 and is an attached agency of the Department of Health (DOH). The NNC is responsible for orchestrating multi-sectoral strategies like the Philippine Plan of Action for Nutrition (PPAN) to eliminate hunger and malnutrition nationwide.\n\n• NEDA (National Economic and Development Authority): The country''s premier social and economic development planning and policy coordinating body.\n• NFP (Nutrition Foundation of the Philippines): A non-government, non-profit organization dedicated to promoting nutritional wellness.\n• NCP (Nutrition Center of the Philippines): A private foundation that develops programmatic interventions and specialized nutritional products.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: NCP, NFP, NEDA"}'::jsonb),
('mc:is the country''s highest policy-making and coordinating body on nutrition.:::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'is the country''s highest policy-making and coordinating body on nutrition.', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"The National Nutrition Council (NNC) is officially recognized by law as the Philippines'' highest policy-making and coordinating body on nutrition.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"NNC\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"The National Nutrition Council (NNC) is officially recognized by law as the Philippines'' highest policy-making and coordinating body on nutrition.\n\nIt was originally created under Presidential Decree No. 491 (The Nutrition Act of the Philippines) in 1974 and is an attached agency of the Department of Health (DOH). The NNC is responsible for orchestrating multi-sectoral strategies like the Philippine Plan of Action for Nutrition (PPAN) to eliminate hunger and malnutrition nationwide.\n\n• NEDA (National Economic and Development Authority): The country''s premier social and economic development planning and policy coordinating body.\n• NFP (Nutrition Foundation of the Philippines): A non-government, non-profit organization dedicated to promoting nutritional wellness.\n• NCP (Nutrition Center of the Philippines): A private foundation that develops programmatic interventions and specialized nutritional products.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: NCP, NFP, NEDA"}'::jsonb),
