Promotion Act of 2009.\n• RA 7600: The Rooming-In and Breastfeeding Act of 1992.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: RA 7600, RA 8172, RA 10028"}'::jsonb),
('mc:which of the following enhances the absorption of zinc?:::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'which of the following enhances the absorption of zinc?', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Glutamate and other amino acids can enhance zinc absorption by forming soluble complexes with zinc and facilitating its uptake in the small intestine. In contrast, substances such as phytate strongly inhibit zinc absorption by binding zinc and forming poorly absorbed complexes.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Glutamate\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Glutamate and other amino acids can enhance zinc absorption by forming soluble complexes with zinc and facilitating its uptake in the small intestine. In contrast, substances such as phytate strongly inhibit zinc absorption by binding zinc and forming poorly absorbed complexes.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Tryptophan, Acetyl CoA, Thiamin"}'::jsonb),
('mc:to prepare a potential bns for deployment in his/her barangay, a bns should undergo a training and days of practicum:::20 days', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'to prepare a potential bns for deployment in his/her barangay, a bns should undergo a training and days of practicum', '20 days', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"According to the National Nutrition Council (NNC) implementing guidelines under Presidential Decree No. 1569, a potential Barangay Nutrition Scholar (BNS) must successfully complete a two-part basic training program before full field deployment:","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"20 days\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"According to the National Nutrition Council (NNC) implementing guidelines under Presidential Decree No. 1569, a potential Barangay Nutrition Scholar (BNS) must successfully complete a two-part basic training program before full field deployment:\n\n1. Didactic Phase: A classroom-based instruction course (traditionally structured as a 5-day to 10-day basic course) covering core technical inputs on malnutrition, health, the First 1000 Days, and data tools.\n2. Practicum Phase: A mandatory 20-day hands-on community practicum.\n\nDuring the 20 days of field practice, the scholar learns to master community nutrition management tasks under direct supervision. This includes operating anthropometric equipment to weigh and measure preschoolers (Operation Timbang Plus), conducting localized family profiling interviews, building a community nutrition spot map, and formulating a customized Barangay Nutrition Action Plan (BNAP).","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: 5 days, 10 days, 15 days"}'::jsonb),
('mc:to prepare a potential bns for deployment in his/her barangay, a bns should undergo a training and days of practicum:::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'to prepare a potential bns for deployment in his/her barangay, a bns should undergo a training and days of practicum', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"According to the National Nutrition Council (NNC) implementing guidelines under Presidential Decree No. 1569, a potential Barangay Nutrition Scholar (BNS) must successfully complete a two-part basic training program before full field deployment:","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"20 days\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"According to the National Nutrition Council (NNC) implementing guidelines under Presidential Decree No. 1569, a potential Barangay Nutrition Scholar (BNS) must successfully complete a two-part basic training program before full field deployment:\n\n1. Didactic Phase: A classroom-based instruction course (traditionally structured as a 5-day to 10-day basic course) covering core technical inputs on malnutrition, health, the First 1000 Days, and data tools.\n2. Practicum Phase: A mandatory 20-day hands-on community practicum.\n\nDuring the 20 days of field practice, the scholar learns to master community nutrition management tasks under direct supervision. This includes operating anthropometric equipment to weigh and measure preschoolers (Operation Timbang Plus), conducting localized family profiling interviews, building a community nutrition spot map, and formulating a customized Barangay Nutrition Action Plan (BNAP).","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: 5 days, 10 days, 15 days"}'::jsonb),
('mc:compute the calories provided by a 2-l dialysate solution containing 2.5% dextrose.:::170 kcal', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'compute the calories provided by a 2-l dialysate solution containing 2.5% dextrose.', '170 kcal', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"A 2.5% dextrose solution contains 2.5 g of dextrose per 100 mL.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"170 kcal\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"A 2.5% dextrose solution contains 2.5 g of dextrose per 100 mL.\n\nFor 2 L (2,000 mL):\n2.5 g/100 mL × 2,000 mL = 50 g dextrose\n\nDextrose provides approximately 3.4 kcal/g\n50 g × 3.4 kcal/g = 170 kcal\n\nTherefore, the dialysate provides approximately 170 kcal.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: 108 kcal, 148 kcal, 200 kcal"}'::jsonb),
('mc:in the bns program guidelines, the direct trainer-supervisor of the bns is::::district/city nutrition program coordinator', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'in the bns program guidelines, the direct trainer-supervisor of the bns is:', 'District/City Nutrition Program Coordinator', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"According to the official guidelines issued by the National Nutrition Council (NNC), the District/City Nutrition Program Coordinator (D/CNPC) serves as the direct trainer-supervisor of the Barangay Nutrition Scholar (BNS).","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"District/City Nutrition Program Coordinator\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"According to the official guidelines issued by the National Nutrition Council (NNC), the District/City Nutrition Program Coordinator (D/CNPC) serves as the direct trainer-supervisor of the Barangay Nutrition Scholar (BNS).\n\n• The D/CNPC is explicitly tasked with organizing, conducting, and supervising both the core didactic training and the subsequent 20-day local community practicum for new scholars.\n• Guidelines specify that only in the structural or operational absence of an assigned D/CNPC will the local City/Municipal Nutrition Action Officer (NAO) step in to assume these training and supervision responsibilities.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: City/Municipal Nutrition Action Officer, Provincial Nutrition Action Officer, Regional Nutrition Program Coordinator"}'::jsonb),
('mc:in the bns program guidelines, the direct trainer-supervisor of the bns is::::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'in the bns program guidelines, the direct trainer-supervisor of the bns is:', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"According to the official guidelines issued by the National Nutrition Council (NNC), the District/City Nutrition Program Coordinator (D/CNPC) serves as the direct trainer-supervisor of the Barangay Nutrition Scholar (BNS).","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"District/City Nutrition Program Coordinator\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"According to the official guidelines issued by the National Nutrition Council (NNC), the District/City Nutrition Program Coordinator (D/CNPC) serves as the direct trainer-supervisor of the Barangay Nutrition Scholar (BNS).\n\n• The D/CNPC is explicitly tasked with organizing, conducting, and supervising both the core didactic training and the subsequent 20-day local community practicum for new scholars.\n• Guidelines specify that only in the structural or operational absence of an assigned D/CNPC will the local City/Municipal Nutrition Action Officer (NAO) step in to assume these training and supervision responsibilities.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: City/Municipal Nutrition Action Officer, Provincial Nutrition Action Officer, Regional Nutrition Program Coordinator"}'::jsonb),
('mc:the basic tasks of bns are the following except::::leads in the preparation of the local nutrition action plan', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'the basic tasks of bns are the following except:', 'Leads in the preparation of the local nutrition action plan', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"According to the official National Nutrition Council (NNC) guidelines and the Barangay Nutrition Scholar (BNS) Handbook, the basic tasks of a BNS are clearly codified as:","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Leads in the preparation of the local nutrition action plan\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"According to the official National Nutrition Council (NNC) guidelines and the Barangay Nutrition Scholar (BNS) Handbook, the basic tasks of a BNS are clearly codified as:\n\n1. Caring for the malnourished (identifying and tracking them via Operation Timbang Plus)\n2. Mobilizing the community\n3. Linkage-building\n4. Keeping records (maintaining barangay nutrition profiles and family sheets)\n\nThe BNS does not lead the preparation of the Local Nutrition Action Plan (LNAP). Leadership and authorship of the plan fall under the mandate of the Local Nutrition Committee (LNC) and its Chairperson (the Punong Barangay / Barangay Captain). The role of the BNS is strictly to assist and provide data to the committee during the formulation of the Barangay Nutrition Action Plan (BNAP).","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Caring for the malnourished, Mobilizing the community, Keeping records"}'::jsonb),
('mc:the basic tasks of bns are the following except::::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'the basic tasks of bns are the following except:', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"According to the official National Nutrition Council (NNC) guidelines and the Barangay Nutrition Scholar (BNS) Handbook, the basic tasks of a BNS are clearly codified as:","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Leads in the preparation of the local nutrition action plan\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"According to the official National Nutrition Council (NNC) guidelines and the Barangay Nutrition Scholar (BNS) Handbook, the basic tasks of a BNS are clearly codified as:\n\n1. Caring for the malnourished (identifying and tracking them via Operation Timbang Plus)\n2. Mobilizing the community\n3. Linkage-building\n4. Keeping records (maintaining barangay nutrition profiles and family sheets)\n\nThe BNS does not lead the preparation of the Local Nutrition Action Plan (LNAP). Leadership and authorship of the plan fall under the mandate of the Local Nutrition Committee (LNC) and its Chairperson (the Punong Barangay / Barangay Captain). The role of the BNS is strictly to assist and provide data to the committee during the formulation of the Barangay Nutrition Action Plan (BNAP).","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Caring for the malnourished, Mobilizing the community, Keeping records"}'::jsonb),
('mc:in conducting mental feeding to daycare students, which nutrition education material is best to use?:::cartoons', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'in conducting mental feeding to daycare students, which nutrition education material is best to use?', 'Cartoons', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Cartoons are colorful and engaging, appropriate for children.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Cartoons\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Cartoons are colorful and engaging, appropriate for children.\n\n• Diorama - formal\n• Comics - individual\n• Caricature - satirical or comic effect.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Diorama, Comics, Caricature"}'::jsonb),
('mc:a suitable indicator for the evaluation of child feeding programs is change in::::child''s weight for length/height', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'a suitable indicator for the evaluation of child feeding programs is change in:', 'Child''s weight for length/height', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Supplementary feeding programs for infants and young children primarily target the eradication of wasting (acute malnutrition) and the restoration of normal body mass. The most sensitive, immediate, and reliable indicator to evaluate whether a short-term feeding program is successfully working is the change in a child''s weight-for-length (for infants under 2 years) or weight-for-height (for older children).","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Child''s weight for length/height\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Supplementary feeding programs for infants and young children primarily target the eradication of wasting (acute malnutrition) and the restoration of normal body mass. The most sensitive, immediate, and reliable indicator to evaluate whether a short-term feeding program is successfully working is the change in a child''s weight-for-length (for infants under 2 years) or weight-for-height (for older children).","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Child''s height, Child''s improved feeding practices, Decreased incidence of diarrhea among children"}'::jsonb),
('mc:a suitable indicator for the evaluation of child feeding programs is change in::::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'a suitable indicator for the evaluation of child feeding programs is change in:', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Supplementary feeding programs for infants and young children primarily target the eradication of wasting (acute malnutrition) and the restoration of normal body mass. The most sensitive, immediate, and reliable indicator to evaluate whether a short-term feeding program is successfully working is the change in a child''s weight-for-length (for infants under 2 years) or weight-for-height (for older children).","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Child''s weight for length/height\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Supplementary feeding programs for infants and young children primarily target the eradication of wasting (acute malnutrition) and the restoration of normal body mass. The most sensitive, immediate, and reliable indicator to evaluate whether a short-term feeding program is successfully working is the change in a child''s weight-for-length (for infants under 2 years) or weight-for-height (for older children).","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Child''s height, Child''s improved feeding practices, Decreased incidence of diarrhea among children"}'::jsonb),
('mc:a patient on tube feeding has a diet rx of 2000 calories, 2:1 dilution; how much is the total fluid.:::1000 ml', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'a patient on tube feeding has a diet rx of 2000 calories, 2:1 dilution; how much is the total fluid.', '1000 ml', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"In the traditional tube-feeding prescription, a 2:1 dilution means 2 kcal for every 1 mL of formula. ","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"1000 ml\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"In the traditional tube-feeding prescription, a 2:1 dilution means 2 kcal for every 1 mL of formula. \n\nTherefore: 2,000 kcal ÷ 2 kcal/mL = 1,000 mL/day\n\nThus, the total volume of tube-feeding formula prescribed is 1,000 mL/day.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: 1500 ml, 1250 ml, 2250 ml"}'::jsonb),
('mc:a point person in nutrition who is a regular employee of and under the plantilla of nnc.:::rnpc', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'a point person in nutrition who is a regular employee of and under the plantilla of nnc.', 'RNPC', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"The Regional Nutrition Program Coordinator (RNPC) is a permanent, regular civil service worker who occupies a formal plantilla item directly under the budget and structural organization of the National Nutrition Council (NNC). They serve as the head of the NNC Regional Secretariat, managing regional policy execution, monitoring local implementations of the PPAN, and coordinating across subnational administrative levels.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"RNPC\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"The Regional Nutrition Program Coordinator (RNPC) is a permanent, regular civil service worker who occupies a formal plantilla item directly under the budget and structural organization of the National Nutrition Council (NNC). They serve as the head of the NNC Regional Secretariat, managing regional policy execution, monitoring local implementations of the PPAN, and coordinating across subnational administrative levels.\n\n• CNAO / CNPC (City Nutrition Action Officer / City Nutrition Program Coordinator) & DNPC (District Nutrition Program Coordinator): These positions operate at the Local Government Unit (LGU) level. They are typically funded by the local city, municipality, or province budgets rather than holding a national NNC secretariat plantilla item, or they are designated from other existing local health department roles.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: CNPC, DNPC, CNAO"}'::jsonb)
ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO public.ai_explanation_cache (id, question_hash, front_text, back_text, options, explanation, breakdown, created_at, question_text, user_answer, is_correct, ai_explanation) VALUES
('mc:the national dietary supplement program targets the following age groups except::::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'the national dietary supplement program targets the following age groups except:', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"The National Dietary Supplementation Program (NDSP)—including its banner strategy Tutok Kainan—deliberately excludes infants 0 to 5 months old from receiving solid food or dietary supplements. According to global and local healthcare standards, infants in this age bracket must practice strict exclusive breastfeeding; introducing any form of dietary supplementation or complementary food would disrupt this and increase the risk of infection.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Infants 0-5 months\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"The National Dietary Supplementation Program (NDSP)—including its banner strategy Tutok Kainan—deliberately excludes infants 0 to 5 months old from receiving solid food or dietary supplements. According to global and local healthcare standards, infants in this age bracket must practice strict exclusive breastfeeding; introducing any form of dietary supplementation or complementary food would disrupt this and increase the risk of infection.\n\nTargeted Vulnerable Groups Under the NDSP:\n• Pregnant women (especially those classified as nutritionally at-risk, provided food support for 90 days).\n• Young children 6 to 59 months old (broken down into infants/toddlers aged 6–23 months for complementary feeding, and children aged 24–59 months).\n• School children (specifically severely wasted or wasted kindergarten to Grade 6 students enrolled in public schools).","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Young children 6-59 months old, School children, Pregnant women"}'::jsonb),
('mc:a point person in nutrition who is a regular employee of and under the plantilla of nnc.:::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'a point person in nutrition who is a regular employee of and under the plantilla of nnc.', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"The Regional Nutrition Program Coordinator (RNPC) is a permanent, regular civil service worker who occupies a formal plantilla item directly under the budget and structural organization of the National Nutrition Council (NNC). They serve as the head of the NNC Regional Secretariat, managing regional policy execution, monitoring local implementations of the PPAN, and coordinating across subnational administrative levels.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"RNPC\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"The Regional Nutrition Program Coordinator (RNPC) is a permanent, regular civil service worker who occupies a formal plantilla item directly under the budget and structural organization of the National Nutrition Council (NNC). They serve as the head of the NNC Regional Secretariat, managing regional policy execution, monitoring local implementations of the PPAN, and coordinating across subnational administrative levels.\n\n• CNAO / CNPC (City Nutrition Action Officer / City Nutrition Program Coordinator) & DNPC (District Nutrition Program Coordinator): These positions operate at the Local Government Unit (LGU) level. They are typically funded by the local city, municipality, or province budgets rather than holding a national NNC secretariat plantilla item, or they are designated from other existing local health department roles.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: CNPC, DNPC, CNAO"}'::jsonb),
('mc:the philippine plan of action for nutrition is integrated and/or consistent with the following except::::philippine environmental plan', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'the philippine plan of action for nutrition is integrated and/or consistent with the following except:', 'Philippine Environmental Plan', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"The Philippine Plan of Action for Nutrition (PPAN) serves as the country’s comprehensive multi-sectoral framework designed to combat all forms of malnutrition. It is systematically aligned with, and anchored into, the following major national development strategies:","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Philippine Environmental Plan\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"The Philippine Plan of Action for Nutrition (PPAN) serves as the country’s comprehensive multi-sectoral framework designed to combat all forms of malnutrition. It is systematically aligned with, and anchored into, the following major national development strategies:\n\n• Philippine Development Plan (PDP): It is an integral sub-component of the national Philippine Development Plan framework, directly contributing to social transformation, human development, and poverty reduction goals.\n• Health for All Agenda of the DOH: It directly supports the Department of Health’s principal universal healthcare blueprints (such as the National Objectives for Health and its respective action agendas).\n• Ambisyon Natin 2040: It explicitly serves as an operational mechanism to meet the long-term vision of Ambisyon Natin 2040 by ensuring a healthy, well-nourished, and highly productive citizenry.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Philippine Development Plan, Health for All Agenda of DOH, Ambisyon Natin 2040"}'::jsonb),
('mc:the philippine plan of action for nutrition is integrated and/or consistent with the following except::::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'the philippine plan of action for nutrition is integrated and/or consistent with the following except:', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"The Philippine Plan of Action for Nutrition (PPAN) serves as the country’s comprehensive multi-sectoral framework designed to combat all forms of malnutrition. It is systematically aligned with, and anchored into, the following major national development strategies:","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Philippine Environmental Plan\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"The Philippine Plan of Action for Nutrition (PPAN) serves as the country’s comprehensive multi-sectoral framework designed to combat all forms of malnutrition. It is systematically aligned with, and anchored into, the following major national development strategies:\n\n• Philippine Development Plan (PDP): It is an integral sub-component of the national Philippine Development Plan framework, directly contributing to social transformation, human development, and poverty reduction goals.\n• Health for All Agenda of the DOH: It directly supports the Department of Health’s principal universal healthcare blueprints (such as the National Objectives for Health and its respective action agendas).\n• Ambisyon Natin 2040: It explicitly serves as an operational mechanism to meet the long-term vision of Ambisyon Natin 2040 by ensuring a healthy, well-nourished, and highly productive citizenry.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Philippine Development Plan, Health for All Agenda of DOH, Ambisyon Natin 2040"}'::jsonb),
