('mc:it refers to concrete and specific statement of desired achievement. it is more focused and specific time dimension, client population and end results.:::objectives', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'it refers to concrete and specific statement of desired achievement. it is more focused and specific time dimension, client population and end results.', 'Objectives', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"In program planning and strategic management, objectives are defined as concrete, precise, and short-term statements of desired achievement. Unlike broad statements, an objective is specifically written to outline:","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Objectives\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"In program planning and strategic management, objectives are defined as concrete, precise, and short-term statements of desired achievement. Unlike broad statements, an objective is specifically written to outline:\n• A defined client population (who will benefit)\n• A strict time dimension (when it will be achieved)\n• Explicit, measurable end results (what the exact target is)\n\n• Goals: These are broad, long-range, and general statements of an overall vision (e.g., \"To reduce the prevalence of malnutrition in the region\"), but they lack the operational and highly focused parameters of an objective.\n• Mission: This explains the primary motivation, fundamental beliefs, and the overall reason for the organization''s existence.\n• Vision: This is an aspirational, long-term description of a desired future state.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Vision, Mission, Goals"}'::jsonb),
('mc:it refers to concrete and specific statement of desired achievement. it is more focused and specific time dimension, client population and end results.:::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'it refers to concrete and specific statement of desired achievement. it is more focused and specific time dimension, client population and end results.', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"In program planning and strategic management, objectives are defined as concrete, precise, and short-term statements of desired achievement. Unlike broad statements, an objective is specifically written to outline:","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Objectives\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"In program planning and strategic management, objectives are defined as concrete, precise, and short-term statements of desired achievement. Unlike broad statements, an objective is specifically written to outline:\n• A defined client population (who will benefit)\n• A strict time dimension (when it will be achieved)\n• Explicit, measurable end results (what the exact target is)\n\n• Goals: These are broad, long-range, and general statements of an overall vision (e.g., \"To reduce the prevalence of malnutrition in the region\"), but they lack the operational and highly focused parameters of an objective.\n• Mission: This explains the primary motivation, fundamental beliefs, and the overall reason for the organization''s existence.\n• Vision: This is an aspirational, long-term description of a desired future state.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Vision, Mission, Goals"}'::jsonb),
('mc:among the criteria in selecting nutrition intervention, the following must not be missed::::relevance, feasibiltiy, effectiveness', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'among the criteria in selecting nutrition intervention, the following must not be missed:', 'Relevance, feasibiltiy, effectiveness', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"In classical program management and nutrition planning frameworks (such as those taught by FAO and the National Nutrition Council), interventions must pass three fundamental, non-negotiable filters during the screening phase:","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Relevance, feasibiltiy, effectiveness\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"In classical program management and nutrition planning frameworks (such as those taught by FAO and the National Nutrition Council), interventions must pass three fundamental, non-negotiable filters during the screening phase:\n\n1. Relevance (Appropriateness): Does the intervention directly solve the specific, prioritized nutrition problem of that population? (e.g., if the problem is Vitamin A deficiency, the intervention must be Vitamin A supplementation or food fortification, not general sanitation).\n2. Feasibility (Practicability): Can it actually be done given the available budget, technical expertise, administrative infrastructure, and time constraints?\n3. Effectiveness (Impact): Is there proven scientific or empirical evidence that this specific strategy will successfully lower malnutrition rates or improve the target nutritional indicators?","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Relevance, feasibility, ease in targeting and evaluation, Relevance, feasibility, cost-effectiveness, Relevance, feasibility, political support, willingness of beneficiaries to participate"}'::jsonb),
('mc:among the criteria in selecting nutrition intervention, the following must not be missed::::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'among the criteria in selecting nutrition intervention, the following must not be missed:', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"In classical program management and nutrition planning frameworks (such as those taught by FAO and the National Nutrition Council), interventions must pass three fundamental, non-negotiable filters during the screening phase:","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Relevance, feasibiltiy, effectiveness\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"In classical program management and nutrition planning frameworks (such as those taught by FAO and the National Nutrition Council), interventions must pass three fundamental, non-negotiable filters during the screening phase:\n\n1. Relevance (Appropriateness): Does the intervention directly solve the specific, prioritized nutrition problem of that population? (e.g., if the problem is Vitamin A deficiency, the intervention must be Vitamin A supplementation or food fortification, not general sanitation).\n2. Feasibility (Practicability): Can it actually be done given the available budget, technical expertise, administrative infrastructure, and time constraints?\n3. Effectiveness (Impact): Is there proven scientific or empirical evidence that this specific strategy will successfully lower malnutrition rates or improve the target nutritional indicators?","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Relevance, feasibility, ease in targeting and evaluation, Relevance, feasibility, cost-effectiveness, Relevance, feasibility, political support, willingness of beneficiaries to participate"}'::jsonb),
('mc:pre-program evaluation carried out prior to implementation but after program formulation. it is necessary to reassess development needs and project feasibility.:::ex-ante evaluation', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'pre-program evaluation carried out prior to implementation but after program formulation. it is necessary to reassess development needs and project feasibility.', 'Ex-ante evaluation', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Ex-ante evaluation (literally meaning \"before the event\" in Latin) is a pre-program evaluation conducted after the program has been formulated but before actual implementation begins. Its primary purpose is to critically re-assess the localized development needs and rigorously audit the overall project feasibility and design viability before resources are officially spent.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Ex-ante evaluation\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Ex-ante evaluation (literally meaning \"before the event\" in Latin) is a pre-program evaluation conducted after the program has been formulated but before actual implementation begins. Its primary purpose is to critically re-assess the localized development needs and rigorously audit the overall project feasibility and design viability before resources are officially spent.\n\n• Program implementation review / Mid-term evaluation: These are ongoing, formative evaluations conducted during the execution phase of the project to check progress and implement corrective adjustments.\n• Ex-post evaluation: This is an summative evaluation conducted at the very end or after the completion of the implementation stage to assess the total impact and outcomes.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Ex-post evaluation, Mid-term evaluation, Program implementation review"}'::jsonb),
('mc:pre-program evaluation carried out prior to implementation but after program formulation. it is necessary to reassess development needs and project feasibility.:::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'pre-program evaluation carried out prior to implementation but after program formulation. it is necessary to reassess development needs and project feasibility.', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Ex-ante evaluation (literally meaning \"before the event\" in Latin) is a pre-program evaluation conducted after the program has been formulated but before actual implementation begins. Its primary purpose is to critically re-assess the localized development needs and rigorously audit the overall project feasibility and design viability before resources are officially spent.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Ex-ante evaluation\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Ex-ante evaluation (literally meaning \"before the event\" in Latin) is a pre-program evaluation conducted after the program has been formulated but before actual implementation begins. Its primary purpose is to critically re-assess the localized development needs and rigorously audit the overall project feasibility and design viability before resources are officially spent.\n\n• Program implementation review / Mid-term evaluation: These are ongoing, formative evaluations conducted during the execution phase of the project to check progress and implement corrective adjustments.\n• Ex-post evaluation: This is an summative evaluation conducted at the very end or after the completion of the implementation stage to assess the total impact and outcomes.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Ex-post evaluation, Mid-term evaluation, Program implementation review"}'::jsonb),
('mc:food availability at the national level is not enough to provide food security for all households. moreover, food security at the national and household level does not automatically result to good nutritional status of an individual.:::both statements are true', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'food availability at the national level is not enough to provide food security for all households. moreover, food security at the national and household level does not automatically result to good nutritional status of an individual.', 'Both statements are true', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Statement 1 is true: Food availability at the national level (e.g., high national agricultural yields or food imports) speaks only to supply. It does not ensure food security for all households because of barriers to food access (such as low purchasing power, poverty, or poor market distribution systems).","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Both statements are true\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Statement 1 is true: Food availability at the national level (e.g., high national agricultural yields or food imports) speaks only to supply. It does not ensure food security for all households because of barriers to food access (such as low purchasing power, poverty, or poor market distribution systems).\n\nStatement 2 is true: Even if a household is fully food secure and has plenty of food on the table, it does not guarantee that every individual in that house will achieve an optimal nutritional status. This is because individual nutritional status depends heavily on food utilization (how the body absorbs nutrients, which can be ruined by infections or illness), intra-household food distribution (e.g., gender or age disparities in who eats first), and caregiving practices.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Only the first statement is true, Only the second statement is true, Both statements are false"}'::jsonb),
('mc:it is the focus of the sustainable development goal #2:::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'it is the focus of the sustainable development goal #2', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"The United Nations Sustainable Development Goals (SDGs) are a blueprint of 17 global goals to build a better future. SDG #2 focuses directly on ending hunger, achieving food security, improving nutrition, and promoting sustainable agriculture.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Hunger and malnutrition\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"The United Nations Sustainable Development Goals (SDGs) are a blueprint of 17 global goals to build a better future. SDG #2 focuses directly on ending hunger, achieving food security, improving nutrition, and promoting sustainable agriculture.\n\n• Health: This is SDG #3 (Good Health and Well-being).\n• Access to clean water and sanitation: This is SDG #6 (Clean Water and Sanitation).\n• Responsible production and consumption: This is SDG #12 (Responsible Consumption and Production).","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Health, Access to clean water and sanitation, Responsible production and consumption"}'::jsonb),
('mc:food availability at the national level is not enough to provide food security for all households. moreover, food security at the national and household level does not automatically result to good nutritional status of an individual.:::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'food availability at the national level is not enough to provide food security for all households. moreover, food security at the national and household level does not automatically result to good nutritional status of an individual.', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Statement 1 is true: Food availability at the national level (e.g., high national agricultural yields or food imports) speaks only to supply. It does not ensure food security for all households because of barriers to food access (such as low purchasing power, poverty, or poor market distribution systems).","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Both statements are true\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Statement 1 is true: Food availability at the national level (e.g., high national agricultural yields or food imports) speaks only to supply. It does not ensure food security for all households because of barriers to food access (such as low purchasing power, poverty, or poor market distribution systems).\n\nStatement 2 is true: Even if a household is fully food secure and has plenty of food on the table, it does not guarantee that every individual in that house will achieve an optimal nutritional status. This is because individual nutritional status depends heavily on food utilization (how the body absorbs nutrients, which can be ruined by infections or illness), intra-household food distribution (e.g., gender or age disparities in who eats first), and caregiving practices.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Only the first statement is true, Only the second statement is true, Both statements are false"}'::jsonb),
('mc:understanding the ecology of food and nutrition helps in the identification of the root causes of malnutrition problem. it can also help identifying appropriate solutions.:::both statements are true', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'understanding the ecology of food and nutrition helps in the identification of the root causes of malnutrition problem. it can also help identifying appropriate solutions.', 'Both statements are true', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"The first statement is true: The \"ecology of food and nutrition\" refers to the interaction between humans and the environmental, socio-economic, cultural, and political factors that dictate food availability, access, and biological utilization. Understanding this web helps investigators trace how systemic basic causes (like climate or policy) trickle down to underlying causes (like poor sanitation) and immediate causes (like inadequate dietary intake).","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Both statements are true\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"The first statement is true: The \"ecology of food and nutrition\" refers to the interaction between humans and the environmental, socio-economic, cultural, and political factors that dictate food availability, access, and biological utilization. Understanding this web helps investigators trace how systemic basic causes (like climate or policy) trickle down to underlying causes (like poor sanitation) and immediate causes (like inadequate dietary intake).\n\nThe second statement is true: By mapping out these exact ecological pathways, program planners can easily identify where a system is failing and design appropriate, targeted solutions—such as pairing nutrition-specific medical interventions with long-term, nutrition-sensitive agricultural or water security reforms.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Only the first statement is true, Only the second statement is true, Both statements are false"}'::jsonb),
('mc:understanding the ecology of food and nutrition helps in the identification of the root causes of malnutrition problem. it can also help identifying appropriate solutions.:::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'understanding the ecology of food and nutrition helps in the identification of the root causes of malnutrition problem. it can also help identifying appropriate solutions.', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"The first statement is true: The \"ecology of food and nutrition\" refers to the interaction between humans and the environmental, socio-economic, cultural, and political factors that dictate food availability, access, and biological utilization. Understanding this web helps investigators trace how systemic basic causes (like climate or policy) trickle down to underlying causes (like poor sanitation) and immediate causes (like inadequate dietary intake).","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Both statements are true\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"The first statement is true: The \"ecology of food and nutrition\" refers to the interaction between humans and the environmental, socio-economic, cultural, and political factors that dictate food availability, access, and biological utilization. Understanding this web helps investigators trace how systemic basic causes (like climate or policy) trickle down to underlying causes (like poor sanitation) and immediate causes (like inadequate dietary intake).\n\nThe second statement is true: By mapping out these exact ecological pathways, program planners can easily identify where a system is failing and design appropriate, targeted solutions—such as pairing nutrition-specific medical interventions with long-term, nutrition-sensitive agricultural or water security reforms.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Only the first statement is true, Only the second statement is true, Both statements are false"}'::jsonb),
('mc:it is a condition in which people do not get enough food to provide nutrients for the body.:::hunger', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'it is a condition in which people do not get enough food to provide nutrients for the body.', 'Hunger', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"In public health and food security frameworks, hunger is defined as an individual-level physiological condition where a person does not have access to enough food to provide the essential nutrients and dietary energy their body needs to function normally.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Hunger\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"In public health and food security frameworks, hunger is defined as an individual-level physiological condition where a person does not have access to enough food to provide the essential nutrients and dietary energy their body needs to function normally.\n\n• Food and nutrition security: This is a comprehensive, positive state where all people at all times have physical, social, and economic access to sufficient, safe, and nutritious food to meet their dietary needs for an active and healthy life.\n• Malaise: This is a general, non-specific medical term describing a vague feeling of bodily discomfort, illness, uneasiness, or lack of energy.\n• Food security: This focuses primarily on the broad availability, access, and stability of food supplies at the household or community level, rather than the specific physical condition of lacking food nutrients.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Food and nutrition security, Food security, Malaise"}'::jsonb),
('mc:under the mandatory food fortification program, cooking oil is fortified with::::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'under the mandatory food fortification program, cooking oil is fortified with:', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"See RA 8976 (Philippine Food Fortification Act of 2000):","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Vit A\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"See RA 8976 (Philippine Food Fortification Act of 2000):\n\nA. Vitamin A – oil, sugar\nB. Iron – rice\nC. Iodine - salt\nD. Vitamin A and iron - wheat flour","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Iron, Iodine, Vit A and Iron"}'::jsonb),
('mc:it is a condition in which people do not get enough food to provide nutrients for the body.:::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'it is a condition in which people do not get enough food to provide nutrients for the body.', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"In public health and food security frameworks, hunger is defined as an individual-level physiological condition where a person does not have access to enough food to provide the essential nutrients and dietary energy their body needs to function normally.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Hunger\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"In public health and food security frameworks, hunger is defined as an individual-level physiological condition where a person does not have access to enough food to provide the essential nutrients and dietary energy their body needs to function normally.\n\n• Food and nutrition security: This is a comprehensive, positive state where all people at all times have physical, social, and economic access to sufficient, safe, and nutritious food to meet their dietary needs for an active and healthy life.\n• Malaise: This is a general, non-specific medical term describing a vague feeling of bodily discomfort, illness, uneasiness, or lack of energy.\n• Food security: This focuses primarily on the broad availability, access, and stability of food supplies at the household or community level, rather than the specific physical condition of lacking food nutrients.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Food and nutrition security, Food security, Malaise"}'::jsonb),
('mc:all of the following are possible causes of transitory food insecurity except::::lack of food storage', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'all of the following are possible causes of transitory food insecurity except:', 'Lack of food storage', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Transitory food insecurity is defined as a short-term, temporary, or sudden drop in the ability to produce or access enough food. It is typically triggered by unpredictable external shocks.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Lack of food storage\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Transitory food insecurity is defined as a short-term, temporary, or sudden drop in the ability to produce or access enough food. It is typically triggered by unpredictable external shocks.\n\nDroughts and floods are classic examples of acute environmental shocks that cause immediate, temporary food shortages. The lean season represents a cyclical, temporary phase between planting and harvesting where food supplies naturally drop for a limited period.\n\nA lack of food storage, on the other hand, is a structural and systemic infrastructural deficiency. Because it is a permanent condition deeply embedded in a region''s agricultural infrastructure, it serves as a core driver of chronic food insecurity rather than a temporary or passing shock.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Lean season, Drought, Floods"}'::jsonb),
('mc:all of the following are possible causes of transitory food insecurity except::::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'all of the following are possible causes of transitory food insecurity except:', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Transitory food insecurity is defined as a short-term, temporary, or sudden drop in the ability to produce or access enough food. It is typically triggered by unpredictable external shocks.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Lack of food storage\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Transitory food insecurity is defined as a short-term, temporary, or sudden drop in the ability to produce or access enough food. It is typically triggered by unpredictable external shocks.\n\nDroughts and floods are classic examples of acute environmental shocks that cause immediate, temporary food shortages. The lean season represents a cyclical, temporary phase between planting and harvesting where food supplies naturally drop for a limited period.\n\nA lack of food storage, on the other hand, is a structural and systemic infrastructural deficiency. Because it is a permanent condition deeply embedded in a region''s agricultural infrastructure, it serves as a core driver of chronic food insecurity rather than a temporary or passing shock.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Lean season, Drought, Floods"}'::jsonb),
('mc:it is the focus of the sustainable development goal #2:::hunger and malnutrition', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'it is the focus of the sustainable development goal #2', 'Hunger and malnutrition', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"The United Nations Sustainable Development Goals (SDGs) are a blueprint of 17 global goals to build a better future. SDG #2 focuses directly on ending hunger, achieving food security, improving nutrition, and promoting sustainable agriculture.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Hunger and malnutrition\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"The United Nations Sustainable Development Goals (SDGs) are a blueprint of 17 global goals to build a better future. SDG #2 focuses directly on ending hunger, achieving food security, improving nutrition, and promoting sustainable agriculture.\n\n• Health: This is SDG #3 (Good Health and Well-being).\n• Access to clean water and sanitation: This is SDG #6 (Clean Water and Sanitation).\n• Responsible production and consumption: This is SDG #12 (Responsible Consumption and Production).","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Health, Access to clean water and sanitation, Responsible production and consumption"}'::jsonb),
('mc:a patient on tube feeding has a diet rx of 2000 calories, 2:1 dilution; how much is the total fluid.:::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'a patient on tube feeding has a diet rx of 2000 calories, 2:1 dilution; how much is the total fluid.', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"In the traditional tube-feeding prescription, a 2:1 dilution means 2 kcal for every 1 mL of formula. ","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"1000 ml\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"In the traditional tube-feeding prescription, a 2:1 dilution means 2 kcal for every 1 mL of formula. \n\nTherefore: 2,000 kcal ÷ 2 kcal/mL = 1,000 mL/day\n\nThus, the total volume of tube-feeding formula prescribed is 1,000 mL/day.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: 1500 ml, 1250 ml, 2250 ml"}'::jsonb),
('mc:which of the following provides information on annual food production, distribution of food within the country, and imports and exports?:::food balance sheets', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'which of the following provides information on annual food production, distribution of food within the country, and imports and exports?', 'Food balance sheets', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"A Food Balance Sheet (FBS) is a comprehensive macro-level statistical report compiled annually (often by national agencies like the Philippine Statistics Authority and globally by the Food and Agriculture Organization) to track a country''s total food supply and utilization over a specific timeframe.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Food balance sheets\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"A Food Balance Sheet (FBS) is a comprehensive macro-level statistical report compiled annually (often by national agencies like the Philippine Statistics Authority and globally by the Food and Agriculture Organization) to track a country''s total food supply and utilization over a specific timeframe.\n\nIt specifically synthesizes three critical dimensions of data:\n1. Annual Food Production: The total quantity of food commodities cultivated domestically.\n2. Imports and Exports: The flow of food moving into and out of the country''s borders to calculate net available supply.\n3. Distribution/Utilization: How the total available food is split within the country across human consumption, livestock feed, seed use, manufacturing, and structural waste.\n\n• Commercial market databases: These track private consumer buying trends, brand performance, and retail sales, rather than integrated national agricultural and border trade balances.\n• Food records: This is an individual-level dietary assessment method where a single person logs their personal daily intake, which tells you nothing about nationwide trade or agricultural production.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Food records, Commercial market databases, None of the above"}'::jsonb),
('mc:which of the following provides information on annual food production, distribution of food within the country, and imports and exports?:::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'which of the following provides information on annual food production, distribution of food within the country, and imports and exports?', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"A Food Balance Sheet (FBS) is a comprehensive macro-level statistical report compiled annually (often by national agencies like the Philippine Statistics Authority and globally by the Food and Agriculture Organization) to track a country''s total food supply and utilization over a specific timeframe.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Food balance sheets\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"A Food Balance Sheet (FBS) is a comprehensive macro-level statistical report compiled annually (often by national agencies like the Philippine Statistics Authority and globally by the Food and Agriculture Organization) to track a country''s total food supply and utilization over a specific timeframe.\n\nIt specifically synthesizes three critical dimensions of data:\n1. Annual Food Production: The total quantity of food commodities cultivated domestically.\n2. Imports and Exports: The flow of food moving into and out of the country''s borders to calculate net available supply.\n3. Distribution/Utilization: How the total available food is split within the country across human consumption, livestock feed, seed use, manufacturing, and structural waste.\n\n• Commercial market databases: These track private consumer buying trends, brand performance, and retail sales, rather than integrated national agricultural and border trade balances.\n• Food records: This is an individual-level dietary assessment method where a single person logs their personal daily intake, which tells you nothing about nationwide trade or agricultural production.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Food records, Commercial market databases, None of the above"}'::jsonb),
('mc:based on who cut-off points, a bmi of 27 is considered to be which of the following?:::overweight', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'based on who cut-off points, a bmi of 27 is considered to be which of the following?', 'Overweight', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"WHO Standard Adult BMI Reference Ranges","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Overweight\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"WHO Standard Adult BMI Reference Ranges\n\nThe international classification breaks down weight status according to these specific metric boundaries:\n• Underweight: BMI less than 18.5\n• Normal weight: BMI of 18.5 to 24.9\n• Overweight: BMI of 25.0 to 29.9 (A BMI of 27 falls directly in this bracket)\n• Obese: BMI of 30.0 or higher","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Underweight, Normal weight, Obese"}'::jsonb),
('mc:based on who cut-off points, a bmi of 27 is considered to be which of the following?:::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'based on who cut-off points, a bmi of 27 is considered to be which of the following?', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"WHO Standard Adult BMI Reference Ranges","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Overweight\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"WHO Standard Adult BMI Reference Ranges\n\nThe international classification breaks down weight status according to these specific metric boundaries:\n• Underweight: BMI less than 18.5\n• Normal weight: BMI of 18.5 to 24.9\n• Overweight: BMI of 25.0 to 29.9 (A BMI of 27 falls directly in this bracket)\n• Obese: BMI of 30.0 or higher","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Underweight, Normal weight, Obese"}'::jsonb),
('mc:abdominal obesity in females is defined as a waist–hip ratio greater than::::0.85', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'abdominal obesity in females is defined as a waist–hip ratio greater than:', '0.85', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"According to the World Health Organization (WHO) diagnostic guidelines, abdominal obesity (also known as central or android obesity) is measured using specific cutoff thresholds for the waist–hip ratio (WHR):","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"0.85\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"According to the World Health Organization (WHO) diagnostic guidelines, abdominal obesity (also known as central or android obesity) is measured using specific cutoff thresholds for the waist–hip ratio (WHR):\n\n• Females: Abdominal obesity is defined as a waist–hip ratio greater than 0.85.\n• Males: Abdominal obesity is defined as a waist–hip ratio greater than 0.90.\n\nAny values exceeding these points indicate a high concentration of visceral fat around the abdominal organs, which is heavily associated with an elevated risk of developing type 2 diabetes, hypertension, and cardiovascular diseases.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: 0.70, 0.95, 1"}'::jsonb),
('mc:abdominal obesity in females is defined as a waist–hip ratio greater than::::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'abdominal obesity in females is defined as a waist–hip ratio greater than:', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"According to the World Health Organization (WHO) diagnostic guidelines, abdominal obesity (also known as central or android obesity) is measured using specific cutoff thresholds for the waist–hip ratio (WHR):","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"0.85\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"According to the World Health Organization (WHO) diagnostic guidelines, abdominal obesity (also known as central or android obesity) is measured using specific cutoff thresholds for the waist–hip ratio (WHR):\n\n• Females: Abdominal obesity is defined as a waist–hip ratio greater than 0.85.\n• Males: Abdominal obesity is defined as a waist–hip ratio greater than 0.90.\n\nAny values exceeding these points indicate a high concentration of visceral fat around the abdominal organs, which is heavily associated with an elevated risk of developing type 2 diabetes, hypertension, and cardiovascular diseases.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: 0.70, 0.95, 1"}'::jsonb),
('mc:which dietary assessment method determines nutrient intake by asking how often certain foods are consumed?:::food frequency', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'which dietary assessment method determines nutrient intake by asking how often certain foods are consumed?', 'Food frequency', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"By definition, a Food Frequency Questionnaire (FFQ) asks a participant to report how often (the rate or frequency, such as daily, weekly, or monthly) they consume specific items from a pre-defined checklist of foods over a designated timeframe.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Food frequency\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"By definition, a Food Frequency Questionnaire (FFQ) asks a participant to report how often (the rate or frequency, such as daily, weekly, or monthly) they consume specific items from a pre-defined checklist of foods over a designated timeframe.\n\n• Diet history: This is an in-depth retrospective interview method that focuses on mapping out overall long-term habitual eating patterns, meal frequencies, cooking behaviors, and general food habits, rather than solely ticking off the frequencies of a structured food item checklist.\n• 24-hour recall: This method asks the participant to remember and describe all food and beverages consumed over the exact, immediate 24 hours prior to the interview. It looks at a single-day snapshot of consumption rather than the general frequency of specific items over time.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Diet history, 24-hour recall, None of the above"}'::jsonb),
('mc:which dietary assessment method determines nutrient intake by asking how often certain foods are consumed?:::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'which dietary assessment method determines nutrient intake by asking how often certain foods are consumed?', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"By definition, a Food Frequency Questionnaire (FFQ) asks a participant to report how often (the rate or frequency, such as daily, weekly, or monthly) they consume specific items from a pre-defined checklist of foods over a designated timeframe.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Food frequency\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"By definition, a Food Frequency Questionnaire (FFQ) asks a participant to report how often (the rate or frequency, such as daily, weekly, or monthly) they consume specific items from a pre-defined checklist of foods over a designated timeframe.\n\n• Diet history: This is an in-depth retrospective interview method that focuses on mapping out overall long-term habitual eating patterns, meal frequencies, cooking behaviors, and general food habits, rather than solely ticking off the frequencies of a structured food item checklist.\n• 24-hour recall: This method asks the participant to remember and describe all food and beverages consumed over the exact, immediate 24 hours prior to the interview. It looks at a single-day snapshot of consumption rather than the general frequency of specific items over time.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Diet history, 24-hour recall, None of the above"}'::jsonb),
('mc:which is true about the physical growth of an infant?:::the infant’s length changes more slowly than the weight', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'which is true about the physical growth of an infant?', 'The infant’s length changes more slowly than the weight', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"An infant''s birth weight typically doubles by about 4 to 6 months, not two months. It does triple by one year, but the first half of the statement is biologically inaccurate for normal infant development.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"The infant’s length changes more slowly than the weight\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"An infant''s birth weight typically doubles by about 4 to 6 months, not two months. It does triple by one year, but the first half of the statement is biologically inaccurate for normal infant development.\n\nWeight changes much faster and more dynamically than height (linear growth). During the first year, an infant''s weight triples (a 200% increase), whereas their length increases by roughly 50% (from an average of 20 inches at birth to about 30 inches at one year). Linear growth is a more gradual, steady biological process compared to rapid mass accumulation.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Infants’ birth weight doubles by about two months and triples by one year, A and B are both TRUE, A and B are both FALSE"}'::jsonb),
('mc:a major component of gastric juice::::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'a major component of gastric juice:', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Hydrochloric acid secretion is responsible for the strong acidic condition of the stomach. With such an acidic environment, ingested proteins are denaturized thus, facilitating their digestion.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"HCI\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Hydrochloric acid secretion is responsible for the strong acidic condition of the stomach. With such an acidic environment, ingested proteins are denaturized thus, facilitating their digestion.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Amylase, Hormones"}'::jsonb),
('mc:which is true about the physical growth of an infant?:::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'which is true about the physical growth of an infant?', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"An infant''s birth weight typically doubles by about 4 to 6 months, not two months. It does triple by one year, but the first half of the statement is biologically inaccurate for normal infant development.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"The infant’s length changes more slowly than the weight\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"An infant''s birth weight typically doubles by about 4 to 6 months, not two months. It does triple by one year, but the first half of the statement is biologically inaccurate for normal infant development.\n\nWeight changes much faster and more dynamically than height (linear growth). During the first year, an infant''s weight triples (a 200% increase), whereas their length increases by roughly 50% (from an average of 20 inches at birth to about 30 inches at one year). Linear growth is a more gradual, steady biological process compared to rapid mass accumulation.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Infants’ birth weight doubles by about two months and triples by one year, A and B are both TRUE, A and B are both FALSE"}'::jsonb),
('mc:an infant is physiologically ready for solid foods when::::all of the above', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'an infant is physiologically ready for solid foods when:', 'All of the above', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"An infant becomes developmentally and physiologically ready to transition to solid foods (complementary feeding) around 6 months of age, marked by several key internal changes:","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"All of the above\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"An infant becomes developmentally and physiologically ready to transition to solid foods (complementary feeding) around 6 months of age, marked by several key internal changes:\n• Saliva is produced: Around 3 to 6 months, an infant’s salivary glands mature, significantly increasing saliva production. Saliva contains lubricants and enzymes that help moisten and break down solid textures.\n\n• Gastric acidity increases: A newborn''s stomach has a relatively high pH (less acidic). As the infant approaches 6 months, gastric acid production increases, lowering the stomach pH to adult-like levels. This is necessary to properly digest complex proteins from solid foods and kill harmful foodborne pathogens.\n• Enzymes for starch digestion are produced: During early infancy, levels of pancreatic amylase (the enzyme needed to digest complex starches) are very low. By 6 months of age, the secretion of these digestive enzymes matures significantly, allowing the infant''s GI tract to safely process and absorb starches from rice, cereals, and mashed root crops.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Saliva is produced, Gastric acidity increases, Enzymes for starch digestion is produced"}'::jsonb),
('mc:an infant is physiologically ready for solid foods when::::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'an infant is physiologically ready for solid foods when:', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"An infant becomes developmentally and physiologically ready to transition to solid foods (complementary feeding) around 6 months of age, marked by several key internal changes:","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"All of the above\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"An infant becomes developmentally and physiologically ready to transition to solid foods (complementary feeding) around 6 months of age, marked by several key internal changes:\n• Saliva is produced: Around 3 to 6 months, an infant’s salivary glands mature, significantly increasing saliva production. Saliva contains lubricants and enzymes that help moisten and break down solid textures.\n\n• Gastric acidity increases: A newborn''s stomach has a relatively high pH (less acidic). As the infant approaches 6 months, gastric acid production increases, lowering the stomach pH to adult-like levels. This is necessary to properly digest complex proteins from solid foods and kill harmful foodborne pathogens.\n• Enzymes for starch digestion are produced: During early infancy, levels of pancreatic amylase (the enzyme needed to digest complex starches) are very low. By 6 months of age, the secretion of these digestive enzymes matures significantly, allowing the infant''s GI tract to safely process and absorb starches from rice, cereals, and mashed root crops.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Saliva is produced, Gastric acidity increases, Enzymes for starch digestion is produced"}'::jsonb),
('mc:which is true about the basal metabolic of the elderly?:::a and b are both false', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'which is true about the basal metabolic of the elderly?', 'A and B are both FALSE', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"The basal metabolic rate (BMR) does decline with age, but it happens because of a decrease in the functional capacity of cells and a prominent loss of functioning lean muscle tissue (sarcopenia). It does not decline because cell capacity increases.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"A and B are both FALSE\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"The basal metabolic rate (BMR) does decline with age, but it happens because of a decrease in the functional capacity of cells and a prominent loss of functioning lean muscle tissue (sarcopenia). It does not decline because cell capacity increases.\n\nThe decline in baseline metabolic needs is typically accompanied by an increase in body fat (adipose tissue) and a redistribution of fat toward the abdomen, rather than a decrease in body fat.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: It declines with age because of the increase in the functional capacity of cells, The declined metabolic needs is accompanied by a decrease in body fat, A and B are both TRUE"}'::jsonb),
('mc:which is true about the basal metabolic of the elderly?:::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'which is true about the basal metabolic of the elderly?', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"The basal metabolic rate (BMR) does decline with age, but it happens because of a decrease in the functional capacity of cells and a prominent loss of functioning lean muscle tissue (sarcopenia). It does not decline because cell capacity increases.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"A and B are both FALSE\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"The basal metabolic rate (BMR) does decline with age, but it happens because of a decrease in the functional capacity of cells and a prominent loss of functioning lean muscle tissue (sarcopenia). It does not decline because cell capacity increases.\n\nThe decline in baseline metabolic needs is typically accompanied by an increase in body fat (adipose tissue) and a redistribution of fat toward the abdomen, rather than a decrease in body fat.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: It declines with age because of the increase in the functional capacity of cells, The declined metabolic needs is accompanied by a decrease in body fat, A and B are both TRUE"}'::jsonb)
