('mc:all of the following are possible nutritional effects of intestinal resection among cancer patients except::::diabetes mellitus', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'all of the following are possible nutritional effects of intestinal resection among cancer patients except:', 'Diabetes mellitus', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Intestinal resection can lead to several gastrointestinal and nutritional complications depending on the site and extent of resection. These include diarrhea from reduced absorptive capacity, hyperoxaluria particularly after significant ileal resection, and blind loop syndrome when abnormal intestinal anatomy or stasis promotes bacterial overgrowth. Diabetes mellitus, however, is not a typical direct nutritional consequence of intestinal resection.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Diabetes mellitus\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Intestinal resection can lead to several gastrointestinal and nutritional complications depending on the site and extent of resection. These include diarrhea from reduced absorptive capacity, hyperoxaluria particularly after significant ileal resection, and blind loop syndrome when abnormal intestinal anatomy or stasis promotes bacterial overgrowth. Diabetes mellitus, however, is not a typical direct nutritional consequence of intestinal resection.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Blind loop syndrome, Hyperoxaluria, Diarrhea"}'::jsonb),
('mc:all of the following are possible nutritional effects of intestinal resection among cancer patients except::::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'all of the following are possible nutritional effects of intestinal resection among cancer patients except:', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Intestinal resection can lead to several gastrointestinal and nutritional complications depending on the site and extent of resection. These include diarrhea from reduced absorptive capacity, hyperoxaluria particularly after significant ileal resection, and blind loop syndrome when abnormal intestinal anatomy or stasis promotes bacterial overgrowth. Diabetes mellitus, however, is not a typical direct nutritional consequence of intestinal resection.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Diabetes mellitus\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Intestinal resection can lead to several gastrointestinal and nutritional complications depending on the site and extent of resection. These include diarrhea from reduced absorptive capacity, hyperoxaluria particularly after significant ileal resection, and blind loop syndrome when abnormal intestinal anatomy or stasis promotes bacterial overgrowth. Diabetes mellitus, however, is not a typical direct nutritional consequence of intestinal resection.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Blind loop syndrome, Hyperoxaluria, Diarrhea"}'::jsonb),
('mc:hypoparathyroidism can lead to chvostek''s and trousseau''s syndrome. hyperparathyroidism, on the other hand, can cause osteopenia and osteoporosis.:::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'hypoparathyroidism can lead to chvostek''s and trousseau''s syndrome. hyperparathyroidism, on the other hand, can cause osteopenia and osteoporosis.', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Hypoparathyroidism causes decreased parathyroid hormone (PTH), leading to hypocalcemia, which increases neuromuscular excitability and may produce Chvostek’s and Trousseau’s syndrome. In contrast, prolonged hyperparathyroidism increases PTH-mediated bone resorption, which can reduce bone mineral density and contribute to osteopenia and osteoporosis.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"both statements are correct\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Hypoparathyroidism causes decreased parathyroid hormone (PTH), leading to hypocalcemia, which increases neuromuscular excitability and may produce Chvostek’s and Trousseau’s syndrome. In contrast, prolonged hyperparathyroidism increases PTH-mediated bone resorption, which can reduce bone mineral density and contribute to osteopenia and osteoporosis.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: only statement 1 is correct, only statement 2 is correct, both statements are incorrect"}'::jsonb),
('mc:a general principle in the prevention and management of an inborn error of metabolism if there is accumulation of precursor to toxic levels::::restrict source of precursor', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'a general principle in the prevention and management of an inborn error of metabolism if there is accumulation of precursor to toxic levels:', 'Restrict source of precursor', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"When an inborn error of metabolism causes a precursor to accumulate to toxic concentrations because it cannot be adequately metabolized, a key treatment principle is to restrict the dietary source of that precursor. This reduces substrate entering the defective metabolic pathway and helps prevent further accumulation and toxicity. The specific dietary restriction depends on the metabolic disorder—for example, phenylalanine restriction in phenylketonuria (PKU).","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Restrict source of precursor\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"When an inborn error of metabolism causes a precursor to accumulate to toxic concentrations because it cannot be adequately metabolized, a key treatment principle is to restrict the dietary source of that precursor. This reduces substrate entering the defective metabolic pathway and helps prevent further accumulation and toxicity. The specific dietary restriction depends on the metabolic disorder—for example, phenylalanine restriction in phenylketonuria (PKU).","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Provide replacement, Give increased quantities of the vitamin involved, Avoid exposure"}'::jsonb),
('mc:a general principle in the prevention and management of an inborn error of metabolism if there is accumulation of precursor to toxic levels::::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'a general principle in the prevention and management of an inborn error of metabolism if there is accumulation of precursor to toxic levels:', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"When an inborn error of metabolism causes a precursor to accumulate to toxic concentrations because it cannot be adequately metabolized, a key treatment principle is to restrict the dietary source of that precursor. This reduces substrate entering the defective metabolic pathway and helps prevent further accumulation and toxicity. The specific dietary restriction depends on the metabolic disorder—for example, phenylalanine restriction in phenylketonuria (PKU).","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Restrict source of precursor\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"When an inborn error of metabolism causes a precursor to accumulate to toxic concentrations because it cannot be adequately metabolized, a key treatment principle is to restrict the dietary source of that precursor. This reduces substrate entering the defective metabolic pathway and helps prevent further accumulation and toxicity. The specific dietary restriction depends on the metabolic disorder—for example, phenylalanine restriction in phenylketonuria (PKU).","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Provide replacement, Give increased quantities of the vitamin involved, Avoid exposure"}'::jsonb),
('mc:how much energy can a 1 liter of 5% dextrose solution provide?:::170 kcal', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'how much energy can a 1 liter of 5% dextrose solution provide?', '170 kcal', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"A 5% dextrose solution (D5W) contains 5 g dextrose per 100 mL, or 50 g/L. Dextrose provides approximately 3.4 kcal/g when given intravenously.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"170 kcal\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"A 5% dextrose solution (D5W) contains 5 g dextrose per 100 mL, or 50 g/L. Dextrose provides approximately 3.4 kcal/g when given intravenously.\n\nTherefore: 50 g × 3.4 kcal/g = 170 kcal\n\nThus, 1 L of 5% dextrose provides approximately 170 kcal","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: 100 kcal, 150 kcal, 200 kcal"}'::jsonb),
('mc:how much energy can a 1 liter of 5% dextrose solution provide?:::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'how much energy can a 1 liter of 5% dextrose solution provide?', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"A 5% dextrose solution (D5W) contains 5 g dextrose per 100 mL, or 50 g/L. Dextrose provides approximately 3.4 kcal/g when given intravenously.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"170 kcal\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"A 5% dextrose solution (D5W) contains 5 g dextrose per 100 mL, or 50 g/L. Dextrose provides approximately 3.4 kcal/g when given intravenously.\n\nTherefore: 50 g × 3.4 kcal/g = 170 kcal\n\nThus, 1 L of 5% dextrose provides approximately 170 kcal","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: 100 kcal, 150 kcal, 200 kcal"}'::jsonb),
('mc:hypoparathyroidism can lead to chvostek''s and trousseau''s syndrome. hyperparathyroidism, on the other hand, can cause osteopenia and osteoporosis.:::both statements are correct', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'hypoparathyroidism can lead to chvostek''s and trousseau''s syndrome. hyperparathyroidism, on the other hand, can cause osteopenia and osteoporosis.', 'both statements are correct', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Hypoparathyroidism causes decreased parathyroid hormone (PTH), leading to hypocalcemia, which increases neuromuscular excitability and may produce Chvostek’s and Trousseau’s syndrome. In contrast, prolonged hyperparathyroidism increases PTH-mediated bone resorption, which can reduce bone mineral density and contribute to osteopenia and osteoporosis.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"both statements are correct\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Hypoparathyroidism causes decreased parathyroid hormone (PTH), leading to hypocalcemia, which increases neuromuscular excitability and may produce Chvostek’s and Trousseau’s syndrome. In contrast, prolonged hyperparathyroidism increases PTH-mediated bone resorption, which can reduce bone mineral density and contribute to osteopenia and osteoporosis.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: only statement 1 is correct, only statement 2 is correct, both statements are incorrect"}'::jsonb),
('mc:which of the following are disease of the large intestines?:::all of the above', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'which of the following are disease of the large intestines?', 'All of the above', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Diverticular disease, irritable bowel syndrome (IBS), and constipation are all disorders involving the large intestine. Diverticular disease involves the formation and/or inflammation of diverticula, typically in the colon. IBS is a disorder of gut-brain interaction characterized by abdominal pain associated with altered bowel habits, while constipation involves infrequent or difficult bowel movements and commonly reflects impaired colonic transit or evacuation.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"All of the above\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Diverticular disease, irritable bowel syndrome (IBS), and constipation are all disorders involving the large intestine. Diverticular disease involves the formation and/or inflammation of diverticula, typically in the colon. IBS is a disorder of gut-brain interaction characterized by abdominal pain associated with altered bowel habits, while constipation involves infrequent or difficult bowel movements and commonly reflects impaired colonic transit or evacuation.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Diverticular disease, Irritable bowel syndrome, Constipation"}'::jsonb),
('mc:which of the following are disease of the large intestines?:::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'which of the following are disease of the large intestines?', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Diverticular disease, irritable bowel syndrome (IBS), and constipation are all disorders involving the large intestine. Diverticular disease involves the formation and/or inflammation of diverticula, typically in the colon. IBS is a disorder of gut-brain interaction characterized by abdominal pain associated with altered bowel habits, while constipation involves infrequent or difficult bowel movements and commonly reflects impaired colonic transit or evacuation.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"All of the above\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Diverticular disease, irritable bowel syndrome (IBS), and constipation are all disorders involving the large intestine. Diverticular disease involves the formation and/or inflammation of diverticula, typically in the colon. IBS is a disorder of gut-brain interaction characterized by abdominal pain associated with altered bowel habits, while constipation involves infrequent or difficult bowel movements and commonly reflects impaired colonic transit or evacuation.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Diverticular disease, Irritable bowel syndrome, Constipation"}'::jsonb),
('mc:which of the following are included in the nutrition care guidelines for reducing gastroesophageal reflux disease?:::all of the above', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'which of the following are included in the nutrition care guidelines for reducing gastroesophageal reflux disease?', 'All of the above', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Nutrition management of GERD focuses on reducing factors that can worsen reflux symptoms. Large and high-fat meals can delay gastric emptying and may promote reflux. Alcoholic beverages may aggravate symptoms in some individuals, while acidic or highly spiced foods may worsen discomfort, particularly when esophageal irritation is present. Dietary triggers are individualized, so foods that consistently provoke symptoms should be identified and limited.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"All of the above\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Nutrition management of GERD focuses on reducing factors that can worsen reflux symptoms. Large and high-fat meals can delay gastric emptying and may promote reflux. Alcoholic beverages may aggravate symptoms in some individuals, while acidic or highly spiced foods may worsen discomfort, particularly when esophageal irritation is present. Dietary triggers are individualized, so foods that consistently provoke symptoms should be identified and limited.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Avoid large, high fat meals, Avoid acidic and highly spiced foods when inflammation exists, Avoid alcoholic beverages"}'::jsonb),
('mc:what is the best nutrition diagnosis?:::impaired nutrient utilization related to liver dysfunction as evidenced by steatorrhea', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'what is the best nutrition diagnosis?', 'Impaired nutrient utilization related to liver dysfunction as evidenced by steatorrhea', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Cirrhosis affects nutrient metabolism/absorption.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Impaired nutrient utilization related to liver dysfunction as evidenced by steatorrhea\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Cirrhosis affects nutrient metabolism/absorption.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Inadequate energy intake related to poor appetite as evidenced by weight loss, Inadequate oral intake related to ascites as evidenced by early satiety, Option D"}'::jsonb),
