('mc:views malnutrition as a disease which has to be cured by therapeutic approaches:::public health', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'views malnutrition as a disease which has to be cured by therapeutic approaches', 'Public Health', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"• Social Welfare - malnutrition as deprivation of social safety net","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Public Health\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"• Social Welfare - malnutrition as deprivation of social safety net\n• Agriculture - malnutrition due to limited food production\n• Education - malnutrition is due to poor nutrition knowledge and skills","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Social Welfare, Agriculture, Education"}'::jsonb),
('mc:which is the most important component of the medical nutrition theraphy for patients with celiac disease?:::all of the above', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'which is the most important component of the medical nutrition theraphy for patients with celiac disease?', 'All of the above', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"The cornerstone of medical nutrition therapy for celiac disease is a strict, lifelong gluten-free diet. This requires avoiding gluten-containing grains such as wheat, rye, and barley, carefully checking food labels for gluten-containing ingredients or contamination, and using naturally gluten-free alternatives such as rice, corn, and potatoes. Therefore, all three measures are important components of implementing a gluten-free diet.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"All of the above\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"The cornerstone of medical nutrition therapy for celiac disease is a strict, lifelong gluten-free diet. This requires avoiding gluten-containing grains such as wheat, rye, and barley, carefully checking food labels for gluten-containing ingredients or contamination, and using naturally gluten-free alternatives such as rice, corn, and potatoes. Therefore, all three measures are important components of implementing a gluten-free diet.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Omit sources of gluten as wheat, rye and barley, Reading labels to check if the product contains gluten, Substitute wheat flour with potato, rice, corn"}'::jsonb),
('mc:which is the most important component of the medical nutrition theraphy for patients with celiac disease?:::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'which is the most important component of the medical nutrition theraphy for patients with celiac disease?', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"The cornerstone of medical nutrition therapy for celiac disease is a strict, lifelong gluten-free diet. This requires avoiding gluten-containing grains such as wheat, rye, and barley, carefully checking food labels for gluten-containing ingredients or contamination, and using naturally gluten-free alternatives such as rice, corn, and potatoes. Therefore, all three measures are important components of implementing a gluten-free diet.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"All of the above\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"The cornerstone of medical nutrition therapy for celiac disease is a strict, lifelong gluten-free diet. This requires avoiding gluten-containing grains such as wheat, rye, and barley, carefully checking food labels for gluten-containing ingredients or contamination, and using naturally gluten-free alternatives such as rice, corn, and potatoes. Therefore, all three measures are important components of implementing a gluten-free diet.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Omit sources of gluten as wheat, rye and barley, Reading labels to check if the product contains gluten, Substitute wheat flour with potato, rice, corn"}'::jsonb),
('mc:childhood diarrhea plays a significant role in malnutrition. infections within the intestine can lead to loss of nutrients, damage to the gut mucosa, and impaired nutrient absorption. the who and unicef guidelines for managing diarrheal diseases in children include which of the following?:::all of the above', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'childhood diarrhea plays a significant role in malnutrition. infections within the intestine can lead to loss of nutrients, damage to the gut mucosa, and impaired nutrient absorption. the who and unicef guidelines for managing diarrheal diseases in children include which of the following?', 'All of the above', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Management of acute diarrhea in children emphasizes preventing and treating dehydration with oral rehydration solution (ORS), continuing breastfeeding and age-appropriate feeding to prevent deterioration of nutritional status, and zinc supplementation to reduce the duration and severity of diarrhea and the risk of subsequent episodes. Therefore, all three are key components of recommended management.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"All of the above\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Management of acute diarrhea in children emphasizes preventing and treating dehydration with oral rehydration solution (ORS), continuing breastfeeding and age-appropriate feeding to prevent deterioration of nutritional status, and zinc supplementation to reduce the duration and severity of diarrhea and the risk of subsequent episodes. Therefore, all three are key components of recommended management.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Fluid replacement, Continued feeding, Zinc supplementation"}'::jsonb),
('mc:childhood diarrhea plays a significant role in malnutrition. infections within the intestine can lead to loss of nutrients, damage to the gut mucosa, and impaired nutrient absorption. the who and unicef guidelines for managing diarrheal diseases in children include which of the following?:::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'childhood diarrhea plays a significant role in malnutrition. infections within the intestine can lead to loss of nutrients, damage to the gut mucosa, and impaired nutrient absorption. the who and unicef guidelines for managing diarrheal diseases in children include which of the following?', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Management of acute diarrhea in children emphasizes preventing and treating dehydration with oral rehydration solution (ORS), continuing breastfeeding and age-appropriate feeding to prevent deterioration of nutritional status, and zinc supplementation to reduce the duration and severity of diarrhea and the risk of subsequent episodes. Therefore, all three are key components of recommended management.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"All of the above\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Management of acute diarrhea in children emphasizes preventing and treating dehydration with oral rehydration solution (ORS), continuing breastfeeding and age-appropriate feeding to prevent deterioration of nutritional status, and zinc supplementation to reduce the duration and severity of diarrhea and the risk of subsequent episodes. Therefore, all three are key components of recommended management.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Fluid replacement, Continued feeding, Zinc supplementation"}'::jsonb),
('mc:which of the following is true about anemia? i. characterized by reduction in the size and quantity of red blood cells ii. result to decreased capacity of the blood to carry oxygen:::both statement are correct', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'which of the following is true about anemia? i. characterized by reduction in the size and quantity of red blood cells ii. result to decreased capacity of the blood to carry oxygen', 'both statement are correct', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Anemia is defined by a reduction in hemoglobin concentration, hematocrit, or red blood cell mass below the normal range, resulting in reduced oxygen-carrying capacity of the blood. ","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"both statement are correct\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Anemia is defined by a reduction in hemoglobin concentration, hematocrit, or red blood cell mass below the normal range, resulting in reduced oxygen-carrying capacity of the blood. \n\nStatement I is incorrect because anemia does not necessarily involve a reduction in RBC size. RBC size may be normal (normocytic), small (microcytic), or large (macrocytic), depending on the underlying cause.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: only statement 1 is correct, only statement 2 is correct, both statement are incorrect"}'::jsonb),
('mc:which of the following is true about anemia? i. characterized by reduction in the size and quantity of red blood cells ii. result to decreased capacity of the blood to carry oxygen:::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'which of the following is true about anemia? i. characterized by reduction in the size and quantity of red blood cells ii. result to decreased capacity of the blood to carry oxygen', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Anemia is defined by a reduction in hemoglobin concentration, hematocrit, or red blood cell mass below the normal range, resulting in reduced oxygen-carrying capacity of the blood. ","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"both statement are correct\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Anemia is defined by a reduction in hemoglobin concentration, hematocrit, or red blood cell mass below the normal range, resulting in reduced oxygen-carrying capacity of the blood. \n\nStatement I is incorrect because anemia does not necessarily involve a reduction in RBC size. RBC size may be normal (normocytic), small (microcytic), or large (macrocytic), depending on the underlying cause.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: only statement 1 is correct, only statement 2 is correct, both statement are incorrect"}'::jsonb),
('mc:which of the following proteins have an important role in the immune response?:::globulins', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'which of the following proteins have an important role in the immune response?', 'Globulins', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Globulins, particularly gamma globulins (immunoglobulins/antibodies), play an essential role in the immune response. Antibodies recognize and bind specific foreign antigens and help neutralize pathogens or facilitate their elimination. Other globulin proteins also include components involved in immune and inflammatory processes.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Globulins\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Globulins, particularly gamma globulins (immunoglobulins/antibodies), play an essential role in the immune response. Antibodies recognize and bind specific foreign antigens and help neutralize pathogens or facilitate their elimination. Other globulin proteins also include components involved in immune and inflammatory processes.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Fibrinogen, Lipoproteins, Albumin"}'::jsonb),
('mc:which of the following proteins have an important role in the immune response?:::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'which of the following proteins have an important role in the immune response?', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Globulins, particularly gamma globulins (immunoglobulins/antibodies), play an essential role in the immune response. Antibodies recognize and bind specific foreign antigens and help neutralize pathogens or facilitate their elimination. Other globulin proteins also include components involved in immune and inflammatory processes.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Globulins\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Globulins, particularly gamma globulins (immunoglobulins/antibodies), play an essential role in the immune response. Antibodies recognize and bind specific foreign antigens and help neutralize pathogens or facilitate their elimination. Other globulin proteins also include components involved in immune and inflammatory processes.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Fibrinogen, Lipoproteins, Albumin"}'::jsonb),
('mc:anemia that is induced by poor blood clotting::::hemorrhagic anemia', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'anemia that is induced by poor blood clotting:', 'Hemorrhagic anemia', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Hemorrhagic anemia results from excessive blood loss, which can occur when blood clotting is impaired. Poor clotting allows bleeding to continue or become excessive, leading to loss of red blood cells and hemoglobin. Causes may include coagulation disorders, severe vitamin K deficiency, anticoagulant therapy, or other conditions that impair hemostasis. However, technically, poor clotting is a cause of blood loss rather than the defining cause of hemorrhagic anemia.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Hemorrhagic anemia\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Hemorrhagic anemia results from excessive blood loss, which can occur when blood clotting is impaired. Poor clotting allows bleeding to continue or become excessive, leading to loss of red blood cells and hemoglobin. Causes may include coagulation disorders, severe vitamin K deficiency, anticoagulant therapy, or other conditions that impair hemostasis. However, technically, poor clotting is a cause of blood loss rather than the defining cause of hemorrhagic anemia.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Microcytic anemia, Pernicious anemia, Hemolytic anemia"}'::jsonb)
