('mc:dietary management for myasthenia gravis: i. adjusted food consistency depending on tolerance; nutrient-dense; small frequent feeding ii. avoid foods which contain i-dopa iii. limit dietary protein at breakfast and lunch iv. low glycemic index treatment:::i only', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'dietary management for myasthenia gravis: i. adjusted food consistency depending on tolerance; nutrient-dense; small frequent feeding ii. avoid foods which contain i-dopa iii. limit dietary protein at breakfast and lunch iv. low glycemic index treatment', 'I only', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"The major nutrition concerns in myasthenia gravis (MG) are fatigue and weakness of the muscles involved in chewing and swallowing, which can increase the risk of inadequate intake and aspiration. Appropriate management includes modifying food texture/consistency according to swallowing ability, choosing nutrient-dense foods, and using small, frequent meals to reduce fatigue during eating. Avoidance of L-dopa, protein restriction at specific meals, and low-glycemic-index treatment are not standard dietary therapies for MG.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"I only\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"The major nutrition concerns in myasthenia gravis (MG) are fatigue and weakness of the muscles involved in chewing and swallowing, which can increase the risk of inadequate intake and aspiration. Appropriate management includes modifying food texture/consistency according to swallowing ability, choosing nutrient-dense foods, and using small, frequent meals to reduce fatigue during eating. Avoidance of L-dopa, protein restriction at specific meals, and low-glycemic-index treatment are not standard dietary therapies for MG.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: I and II only, I, II, III only, All of the above"}'::jsonb),
('mc:dietary recommendation for rheumatic fever: i. high kcal, high protein, high fluids, low fiber ii.high kcal, high protein, na and fluid controlled, vitamin and mineral supplements iii. high kcal, high protein, fat-restricted:::ii only', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'dietary recommendation for rheumatic fever: i. high kcal, high protein, high fluids, low fiber ii.high kcal, high protein, na and fluid controlled, vitamin and mineral supplements iii. high kcal, high protein, fat-restricted', 'II only', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"During acute rheumatic fever, nutrition should support growth, tissue repair, and recovery, so adequate energy and high-quality protein are important. When there is carditis or heart failure, sodium and fluid may need to be restricted to help control edema and fluid overload. Adequate vitamins and minerals should also be provided. There is no routine indication for a low-fiber diet or for fat restriction in rheumatic fever itself.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"II only\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"During acute rheumatic fever, nutrition should support growth, tissue repair, and recovery, so adequate energy and high-quality protein are important. When there is carditis or heart failure, sodium and fluid may need to be restricted to help control edema and fluid overload. Adequate vitamins and minerals should also be provided. There is no routine indication for a low-fiber diet or for fat restriction in rheumatic fever itself.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: I only, III only, All of the above"}'::jsonb),
('mc:all of these are essential amino acids for adults except: i. arginine ii. glutamine iii. lysine iv. leucine:::i and ii', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'all of these are essential amino acids for adults except: i. arginine ii. glutamine iii. lysine iv. leucine', 'I and II', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"🟢 Arginine (I) is conditionally essential. Adults can make enough of it on their own, though growing children need it in their diet.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"I and II\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"🟢 Arginine (I) is conditionally essential. Adults can make enough of it on their own, though growing children need it in their diet.\n🟢 Glutamine (II) is a non-essential amino acid. Your body creates it in sufficient amounts under normal conditions.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: II only, III only, None of the above"}'::jsonb),
('mc:dietary recommendation for rheumatic fever: i. high kcal, high protein, high fluids, low fiber ii.high kcal, high protein, na and fluid controlled, vitamin and mineral supplements iii. high kcal, high protein, fat-restricted:::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'dietary recommendation for rheumatic fever: i. high kcal, high protein, high fluids, low fiber ii.high kcal, high protein, na and fluid controlled, vitamin and mineral supplements iii. high kcal, high protein, fat-restricted', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"During acute rheumatic fever, nutrition should support growth, tissue repair, and recovery, so adequate energy and high-quality protein are important. When there is carditis or heart failure, sodium and fluid may need to be restricted to help control edema and fluid overload. Adequate vitamins and minerals should also be provided. There is no routine indication for a low-fiber diet or for fat restriction in rheumatic fever itself.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"II only\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"During acute rheumatic fever, nutrition should support growth, tissue repair, and recovery, so adequate energy and high-quality protein are important. When there is carditis or heart failure, sodium and fluid may need to be restricted to help control edema and fluid overload. Adequate vitamins and minerals should also be provided. There is no routine indication for a low-fiber diet or for fat restriction in rheumatic fever itself.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: I only, III only, All of the above"}'::jsonb),
('mc:an 84 year-old client complains of chronic constipation. to improve bowel function, what could be a possible intervention?:::increasing fiber intake', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'an 84 year-old client complains of chronic constipation. to improve bowel function, what could be a possible intervention?', 'increasing fiber intake', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Increasing dietary fiber, together with adequate fluid intake and appropriate physical activity, is a first-line approach for managing constipation in older adults when there is no contraindication. Fiber increases stool bulk and can improve bowel regularity. Fiber should be increased gradually, with adequate fluid intake, to minimize bloating and discomfort. For an older adult with chronic constipation, the underlying cause and medications should also be assessed.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"increasing fiber intake\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Increasing dietary fiber, together with adequate fluid intake and appropriate physical activity, is a first-line approach for managing constipation in older adults when there is no contraindication. Fiber increases stool bulk and can improve bowel regularity. Fiber should be increased gradually, with adequate fluid intake, to minimize bloating and discomfort. For an older adult with chronic constipation, the underlying cause and medications should also be assessed.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: avoidance of binding foods such as cheese and chocolate, monitoring a balance between activity and rest, daily use of laxatives"}'::jsonb),
('mc:the difference between nitrogen intake and nitrogen excretion::::nitrogen balance', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'the difference between nitrogen intake and nitrogen excretion:', 'Nitrogen balance', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"At nitrogen equilibrium: N intake = N output; at positive nitrogen balance: N intake > N output; at negative nitrogen balance: N output > N intake.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Nitrogen balance\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"At nitrogen equilibrium: N intake = N output; at positive nitrogen balance: N intake > N output; at negative nitrogen balance: N output > N intake.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Protein digestibility, Amino acid score, Biologic value"}'::jsonb),
