('mc:what is the initial therapy for kwashiorkor?:::fluid balance restoration', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'what is the initial therapy for kwashiorkor?', 'fluid balance restoration', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"In severe acute malnutrition such as kwashiorkor, the initial priority is stabilization, particularly correction of life-threatening problems such as hypoglycemia, hypothermia, dehydration, and electrolyte imbalance. Rehydration must be done cautiously, because children with kwashiorkor may have edema and impaired cardiac/renal function, making them vulnerable to fluid overload. Energy and protein rehabilitation are important, but aggressive feeding or protein replacement should not be the first step during the initial stabilization phase.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"fluid balance restoration\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"In severe acute malnutrition such as kwashiorkor, the initial priority is stabilization, particularly correction of life-threatening problems such as hypoglycemia, hypothermia, dehydration, and electrolyte imbalance. Rehydration must be done cautiously, because children with kwashiorkor may have edema and impaired cardiac/renal function, making them vulnerable to fluid overload. Energy and protein rehabilitation are important, but aggressive feeding or protein replacement should not be the first step during the initial stabilization phase.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: fat replacement, energy replacement, protein replacement"}'::jsonb),
('mc:what is the initial therapy for kwashiorkor?:::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'what is the initial therapy for kwashiorkor?', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"In severe acute malnutrition such as kwashiorkor, the initial priority is stabilization, particularly correction of life-threatening problems such as hypoglycemia, hypothermia, dehydration, and electrolyte imbalance. Rehydration must be done cautiously, because children with kwashiorkor may have edema and impaired cardiac/renal function, making them vulnerable to fluid overload. Energy and protein rehabilitation are important, but aggressive feeding or protein replacement should not be the first step during the initial stabilization phase.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"fluid balance restoration\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"In severe acute malnutrition such as kwashiorkor, the initial priority is stabilization, particularly correction of life-threatening problems such as hypoglycemia, hypothermia, dehydration, and electrolyte imbalance. Rehydration must be done cautiously, because children with kwashiorkor may have edema and impaired cardiac/renal function, making them vulnerable to fluid overload. Energy and protein rehabilitation are important, but aggressive feeding or protein replacement should not be the first step during the initial stabilization phase.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: fat replacement, energy replacement, protein replacement"}'::jsonb),
('mc:the following are dietary management for anorexia nervosa, except::::use of food items that promote early satiety', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'the following are dietary management for anorexia nervosa, except:', 'use of food items that promote early satiety', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Nutrition therapy for anorexia nervosa aims to restore weight and nutritional status while minimizing factors that interfere with adequate intake. Foods that promote early satiety—such as bulky, high-fiber, or large-volume foods—are generally avoided because they can make it difficult for the patient to consume sufficient energy. Instead, small, frequent, energy-dense meals are useful. Cold or room-temperature foods may be better tolerated in some patients, and limiting caffeine can help reduce appetite suppression and excessive stimulation.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"use of food items that promote early satiety\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Nutrition therapy for anorexia nervosa aims to restore weight and nutritional status while minimizing factors that interfere with adequate intake. Foods that promote early satiety—such as bulky, high-fiber, or large-volume foods—are generally avoided because they can make it difficult for the patient to consume sufficient energy. Instead, small, frequent, energy-dense meals are useful. Cold or room-temperature foods may be better tolerated in some patients, and limiting caffeine can help reduce appetite suppression and excessive stimulation.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: small frequent feeding, give cold or room temperature foods, less caffeine"}'::jsonb),
('mc:the following are dietary management for anorexia nervosa, except::::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'the following are dietary management for anorexia nervosa, except:', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Nutrition therapy for anorexia nervosa aims to restore weight and nutritional status while minimizing factors that interfere with adequate intake. Foods that promote early satiety—such as bulky, high-fiber, or large-volume foods—are generally avoided because they can make it difficult for the patient to consume sufficient energy. Instead, small, frequent, energy-dense meals are useful. Cold or room-temperature foods may be better tolerated in some patients, and limiting caffeine can help reduce appetite suppression and excessive stimulation.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"use of food items that promote early satiety\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Nutrition therapy for anorexia nervosa aims to restore weight and nutritional status while minimizing factors that interfere with adequate intake. Foods that promote early satiety—such as bulky, high-fiber, or large-volume foods—are generally avoided because they can make it difficult for the patient to consume sufficient energy. Instead, small, frequent, energy-dense meals are useful. Cold or room-temperature foods may be better tolerated in some patients, and limiting caffeine can help reduce appetite suppression and excessive stimulation.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: small frequent feeding, give cold or room temperature foods, less caffeine"}'::jsonb),
('mc:which of the following is not recommended for patients with gastroesophageal reflux disease (gerd)?:::increase the interval between meals by skipping meals', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'which of the following is not recommended for patients with gastroesophageal reflux disease (gerd)?', 'Increase the interval between meals by skipping meals', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"GERD management emphasizes meal size, meal timing, body position, and individualized trigger-food avoidance rather than deliberately skipping meals. Large meals, especially near bedtime, can worsen reflux, while remaining upright after eating can reduce symptoms. Patients are encouraged to identify foods and beverages that reliably trigger their symptoms rather than unnecessarily eliminating foods. Skipping meals or intentionally prolonging the interval between meals is not a standard GERD intervention.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Increase the interval between meals by skipping meals\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"GERD management emphasizes meal size, meal timing, body position, and individualized trigger-food avoidance rather than deliberately skipping meals. Large meals, especially near bedtime, can worsen reflux, while remaining upright after eating can reduce symptoms. Patients are encouraged to identify foods and beverages that reliably trigger their symptoms rather than unnecessarily eliminating foods. Skipping meals or intentionally prolonging the interval between meals is not a standard GERD intervention.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Avoid large meals, particularly close to bedtime, Remain upright for several hours after meals, Identify and limit foods and beverages that consistently trigger symptoms"}'::jsonb),
('mc:views malnutrition as a disease which has to be cured by therapeutic approaches:::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'views malnutrition as a disease which has to be cured by therapeutic approaches', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"• Social Welfare - malnutrition as deprivation of social safety net","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Public Health\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"• Social Welfare - malnutrition as deprivation of social safety net\n• Agriculture - malnutrition due to limited food production\n• Education - malnutrition is due to poor nutrition knowledge and skills","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Social Welfare, Agriculture, Education"}'::jsonb),
('mc:which of the following is not recommended for patients with gastroesophageal reflux disease (gerd)?:::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'which of the following is not recommended for patients with gastroesophageal reflux disease (gerd)?', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"GERD management emphasizes meal size, meal timing, body position, and individualized trigger-food avoidance rather than deliberately skipping meals. Large meals, especially near bedtime, can worsen reflux, while remaining upright after eating can reduce symptoms. Patients are encouraged to identify foods and beverages that reliably trigger their symptoms rather than unnecessarily eliminating foods. Skipping meals or intentionally prolonging the interval between meals is not a standard GERD intervention.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Increase the interval between meals by skipping meals\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"GERD management emphasizes meal size, meal timing, body position, and individualized trigger-food avoidance rather than deliberately skipping meals. Large meals, especially near bedtime, can worsen reflux, while remaining upright after eating can reduce symptoms. Patients are encouraged to identify foods and beverages that reliably trigger their symptoms rather than unnecessarily eliminating foods. Skipping meals or intentionally prolonging the interval between meals is not a standard GERD intervention.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Avoid large meals, particularly close to bedtime, Remain upright for several hours after meals, Identify and limit foods and beverages that consistently trigger symptoms"}'::jsonb),
('mc:how much sodium does a 1 tsp of salt contain?:::2000 mg', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'how much sodium does a 1 tsp of salt contain?', '2000 mg', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"One teaspoon of table salt (sodium chloride) contains approximately 2,300 mg of sodium. However, nutrition teaching materials commonly use the approximation of 2,000 mg sodium per teaspoon of salt, which is the value this question is evidently targeting. Since the exact sodium content varies somewhat with the type and packing of salt, 2,000 mg is an approximation rather than an exact chemical value.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"2000 mg\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"One teaspoon of table salt (sodium chloride) contains approximately 2,300 mg of sodium. However, nutrition teaching materials commonly use the approximation of 2,000 mg sodium per teaspoon of salt, which is the value this question is evidently targeting. Since the exact sodium content varies somewhat with the type and packing of salt, 2,000 mg is an approximation rather than an exact chemical value.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: 2500 mg, 3000 mg, none of the above"}'::jsonb),
('mc:how much sodium does a 1 tsp of salt contain?:::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'how much sodium does a 1 tsp of salt contain?', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"One teaspoon of table salt (sodium chloride) contains approximately 2,300 mg of sodium. However, nutrition teaching materials commonly use the approximation of 2,000 mg sodium per teaspoon of salt, which is the value this question is evidently targeting. Since the exact sodium content varies somewhat with the type and packing of salt, 2,000 mg is an approximation rather than an exact chemical value.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"2000 mg\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"One teaspoon of table salt (sodium chloride) contains approximately 2,300 mg of sodium. However, nutrition teaching materials commonly use the approximation of 2,000 mg sodium per teaspoon of salt, which is the value this question is evidently targeting. Since the exact sodium content varies somewhat with the type and packing of salt, 2,000 mg is an approximation rather than an exact chemical value.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: 2500 mg, 3000 mg, none of the above"}'::jsonb),
('mc:dietary management for hepatic coma::::high kcal, high protein', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'dietary management for hepatic coma:', 'high kcal, high protein', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Current clinical consensus (from guidelines like AASLD, EASL, ESPEN, and ISHEN) rejects routine protein restriction in hepatic encephalopathy and hepatic coma because protein restriction worsens muscle wasting and catabolism without improving mental status. Patients generally require high caloric intake (about 30–40 kcal/kg/day) and adequate-to-high protein intake (about 1.2–1.5 g/kg/day), often favoring vegetable- or dairy-based proteins or branched-chain amino acids (BCAAs).","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"high kcal, high protein\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Current clinical consensus (from guidelines like AASLD, EASL, ESPEN, and ISHEN) rejects routine protein restriction in hepatic encephalopathy and hepatic coma because protein restriction worsens muscle wasting and catabolism without improving mental status. Patients generally require high caloric intake (about 30–40 kcal/kg/day) and adequate-to-high protein intake (about 1.2–1.5 g/kg/day), often favoring vegetable- or dairy-based proteins or branched-chain amino acids (BCAAs).","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: high kcal, zero to low protein, high kcal, adequate protein, adequate kcal and protein"}'::jsonb),
('mc:during nutrition assessment, you observe muscle wasting, temporal wasting, and poor skin turgor. these findings fall under which category?:::nutrition-focused physical findings', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'during nutrition assessment, you observe muscle wasting, temporal wasting, and poor skin turgor. these findings fall under which category?', 'Nutrition-focused physical findings', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"A nutrition-focused physical exam identifies physical signs of malnutrition such as muscle loss, fat depletion, and micronutrient deficiencies.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Nutrition-focused physical findings\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"A nutrition-focused physical exam identifies physical signs of malnutrition such as muscle loss, fat depletion, and micronutrient deficiencies.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Food and nutrition-related history, Biochemical data, Anthropometrics"}'::jsonb),
('mc:dietary management for hepatic coma::::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'dietary management for hepatic coma:', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Current clinical consensus (from guidelines like AASLD, EASL, ESPEN, and ISHEN) rejects routine protein restriction in hepatic encephalopathy and hepatic coma because protein restriction worsens muscle wasting and catabolism without improving mental status. Patients generally require high caloric intake (about 30–40 kcal/kg/day) and adequate-to-high protein intake (about 1.2–1.5 g/kg/day), often favoring vegetable- or dairy-based proteins or branched-chain amino acids (BCAAs).","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"high kcal, high protein\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Current clinical consensus (from guidelines like AASLD, EASL, ESPEN, and ISHEN) rejects routine protein restriction in hepatic encephalopathy and hepatic coma because protein restriction worsens muscle wasting and catabolism without improving mental status. Patients generally require high caloric intake (about 30–40 kcal/kg/day) and adequate-to-high protein intake (about 1.2–1.5 g/kg/day), often favoring vegetable- or dairy-based proteins or branched-chain amino acids (BCAAs).","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: high kcal, zero to low protein, high kcal, adequate protein, adequate kcal and protein"}'::jsonb),
('mc:what vitamin should be provided for the dietary management for sideroblastic anemia that is characterized by pale rbc but with normal hemoglobin levels?:::vit b6', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'what vitamin should be provided for the dietary management for sideroblastic anemia that is characterized by pale rbc but with normal hemoglobin levels?', 'Vit B6', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Vitamin B6 (pyridoxine) is an important cofactor in heme synthesis, particularly for the enzyme ALA synthase. Sideroblastic anemia results from impaired heme synthesis, causing iron to accumulate in erythroblast mitochondria and producing characteristic ring sideroblasts in the bone marrow. ","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Vit B6\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Vitamin B6 (pyridoxine) is an important cofactor in heme synthesis, particularly for the enzyme ALA synthase. Sideroblastic anemia results from impaired heme synthesis, causing iron to accumulate in erythroblast mitochondria and producing characteristic ring sideroblasts in the bone marrow. \n\nSome forms, particularly pyridoxine-responsive sideroblastic anemia, can respond to vitamin B6 supplementation. However, sideroblastic anemia has multiple causes, so B6 therapy is not appropriate for every case.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Vit B12, Vit K, Vit D"}'::jsonb),
('mc:what vitamin should be provided for the dietary management for sideroblastic anemia that is characterized by pale rbc but with normal hemoglobin levels?:::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'what vitamin should be provided for the dietary management for sideroblastic anemia that is characterized by pale rbc but with normal hemoglobin levels?', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Vitamin B6 (pyridoxine) is an important cofactor in heme synthesis, particularly for the enzyme ALA synthase. Sideroblastic anemia results from impaired heme synthesis, causing iron to accumulate in erythroblast mitochondria and producing characteristic ring sideroblasts in the bone marrow. ","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Vit B6\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Vitamin B6 (pyridoxine) is an important cofactor in heme synthesis, particularly for the enzyme ALA synthase. Sideroblastic anemia results from impaired heme synthesis, causing iron to accumulate in erythroblast mitochondria and producing characteristic ring sideroblasts in the bone marrow. \n\nSome forms, particularly pyridoxine-responsive sideroblastic anemia, can respond to vitamin B6 supplementation. However, sideroblastic anemia has multiple causes, so B6 therapy is not appropriate for every case.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Vit B12, Vit K, Vit D"}'::jsonb),
('mc:the following are dietary management measures for a patient with an ileostomy, except::::high fiber', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'the following are dietary management measures for a patient with an ileostomy, except:', 'high fiber', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"After an ileostomy, particularly during the initial postoperative period, a low-residue/low-fiber diet is commonly recommended to reduce stool volume and decrease the risk of obstruction. Adequate fluid and electrolyte intake is also important because ileostomy output can result in substantial fluid and sodium losses. Vitamin B12 supplementation may be necessary when the terminal ileum has been resected because this is the primary site of vitamin B12–intrinsic factor absorption. A low-fat diet may also be useful when fat malabsorption is present.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"high fiber\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"After an ileostomy, particularly during the initial postoperative period, a low-residue/low-fiber diet is commonly recommended to reduce stool volume and decrease the risk of obstruction. Adequate fluid and electrolyte intake is also important because ileostomy output can result in substantial fluid and sodium losses. Vitamin B12 supplementation may be necessary when the terminal ileum has been resected because this is the primary site of vitamin B12–intrinsic factor absorption. A low-fat diet may also be useful when fat malabsorption is present.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: vit B12 supplementation, low residue, low fat"}'::jsonb),
('mc:all of these are essential amino acids for adults except: i. arginine ii. glutamine iii. lysine iv. leucine:::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'all of these are essential amino acids for adults except: i. arginine ii. glutamine iii. lysine iv. leucine', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"🟢 Arginine (I) is conditionally essential. Adults can make enough of it on their own, though growing children need it in their diet.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"I and II\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"🟢 Arginine (I) is conditionally essential. Adults can make enough of it on their own, though growing children need it in their diet.\n🟢 Glutamine (II) is a non-essential amino acid. Your body creates it in sufficient amounts under normal conditions.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: II only, III only, None of the above"}'::jsonb),
('mc:the following are dietary management measures for a patient with an ileostomy, except::::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'the following are dietary management measures for a patient with an ileostomy, except:', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"After an ileostomy, particularly during the initial postoperative period, a low-residue/low-fiber diet is commonly recommended to reduce stool volume and decrease the risk of obstruction. Adequate fluid and electrolyte intake is also important because ileostomy output can result in substantial fluid and sodium losses. Vitamin B12 supplementation may be necessary when the terminal ileum has been resected because this is the primary site of vitamin B12–intrinsic factor absorption. A low-fat diet may also be useful when fat malabsorption is present.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"high fiber\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"After an ileostomy, particularly during the initial postoperative period, a low-residue/low-fiber diet is commonly recommended to reduce stool volume and decrease the risk of obstruction. Adequate fluid and electrolyte intake is also important because ileostomy output can result in substantial fluid and sodium losses. Vitamin B12 supplementation may be necessary when the terminal ileum has been resected because this is the primary site of vitamin B12–intrinsic factor absorption. A low-fat diet may also be useful when fat malabsorption is present.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: vit B12 supplementation, low residue, low fat"}'::jsonb),
('mc:foods to be restricted in dumping syndrome::::simple carbohydrates', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'foods to be restricted in dumping syndrome:', 'simple carbohydrates', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"In dumping syndrome, especially early dumping, rapid delivery of hyperosmolar food from the stomach into the small intestine can cause gastrointestinal and vasomotor symptoms.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"simple carbohydrates\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"In dumping syndrome, especially early dumping, rapid delivery of hyperosmolar food from the stomach into the small intestine can cause gastrointestinal and vasomotor symptoms.\n\nSimple carbohydrates and concentrated sugars should be limited because they rapidly increase intestinal osmotic load and glucose absorption, potentially worsening symptoms.\n\nMeals are generally made small and frequent, with adequate protein and moderate fat, and fluids are often consumed between rather than with meals.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: complex carbohydrates, protein foods, fat sources"}'::jsonb),
('mc:the science that deals with the progressive movement and alteration of chemical substances within the body::::pharmacokinetics', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'the science that deals with the progressive movement and alteration of chemical substances within the body:', 'Pharmacokinetics', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Pharmacokinetics describes what the body does to a drug, including its absorption, distribution, metabolism, and excretion (ADME). It examines how a drug moves through the body and how its concentration changes over time. ","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Pharmacokinetics\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Pharmacokinetics describes what the body does to a drug, including its absorption, distribution, metabolism, and excretion (ADME). It examines how a drug moves through the body and how its concentration changes over time. \n\nPharmacodynamics describes what the drug does to the body, including its mechanisms of action and effects on physiological processes. While Pharmacogenomics studies how an individual''s genetic variation influences drug response, including efficacy and adverse effects.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Pharmaconutrition, Pharmacodynamics, Pharmacogenomics"}'::jsonb),
('mc:the science that deals with the progressive movement and alteration of chemical substances within the body::::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'the science that deals with the progressive movement and alteration of chemical substances within the body:', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Pharmacokinetics describes what the body does to a drug, including its absorption, distribution, metabolism, and excretion (ADME). It examines how a drug moves through the body and how its concentration changes over time. ","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Pharmacokinetics\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Pharmacokinetics describes what the body does to a drug, including its absorption, distribution, metabolism, and excretion (ADME). It examines how a drug moves through the body and how its concentration changes over time. \n\nPharmacodynamics describes what the drug does to the body, including its mechanisms of action and effects on physiological processes. While Pharmacogenomics studies how an individual''s genetic variation influences drug response, including efficacy and adverse effects.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Pharmaconutrition, Pharmacodynamics, Pharmacogenomics"}'::jsonb),
('mc:may interact with phenobarbital through competitive inhibition::::folate', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'may interact with phenobarbital through competitive inhibition:', 'Folate', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Phenobarbital and other anticonvulsants can interfere with folate metabolism and utilization, and long-term anticonvulsant therapy is associated with reduced folate status. Folate supplementation may therefore be needed in patients receiving prolonged therapy. The interaction is commonly described in nutrition/drug-interaction references as an effect of anticonvulsants on folate metabolism, although the exact pharmacokinetic mechanism is more complex than simple competitive inhibition.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Folate\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Phenobarbital and other anticonvulsants can interfere with folate metabolism and utilization, and long-term anticonvulsant therapy is associated with reduced folate status. Folate supplementation may therefore be needed in patients receiving prolonged therapy. The interaction is commonly described in nutrition/drug-interaction references as an effect of anticonvulsants on folate metabolism, although the exact pharmacokinetic mechanism is more complex than simple competitive inhibition.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Vit K, B vitamins, Vit C"}'::jsonb),
