('mc:may interact with phenobarbital through competitive inhibition::::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'may interact with phenobarbital through competitive inhibition:', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Phenobarbital and other anticonvulsants can interfere with folate metabolism and utilization, and long-term anticonvulsant therapy is associated with reduced folate status. Folate supplementation may therefore be needed in patients receiving prolonged therapy. The interaction is commonly described in nutrition/drug-interaction references as an effect of anticonvulsants on folate metabolism, although the exact pharmacokinetic mechanism is more complex than simple competitive inhibition.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Folate\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Phenobarbital and other anticonvulsants can interfere with folate metabolism and utilization, and long-term anticonvulsant therapy is associated with reduced folate status. Folate supplementation may therefore be needed in patients receiving prolonged therapy. The interaction is commonly described in nutrition/drug-interaction references as an effect of anticonvulsants on folate metabolism, although the exact pharmacokinetic mechanism is more complex than simple competitive inhibition.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Vit K, B vitamins, Vit C"}'::jsonb),
('mc:foods to be restricted in dumping syndrome::::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'foods to be restricted in dumping syndrome:', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"In dumping syndrome, especially early dumping, rapid delivery of hyperosmolar food from the stomach into the small intestine can cause gastrointestinal and vasomotor symptoms.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"simple carbohydrates\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"In dumping syndrome, especially early dumping, rapid delivery of hyperosmolar food from the stomach into the small intestine can cause gastrointestinal and vasomotor symptoms.\n\nSimple carbohydrates and concentrated sugars should be limited because they rapidly increase intestinal osmotic load and glucose absorption, potentially worsening symptoms.\n\nMeals are generally made small and frequent, with adequate protein and moderate fat, and fluids are often consumed between rather than with meals.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: complex carbohydrates, protein foods, fat sources"}'::jsonb),
('mc:the following food are allowed for patient with celiac disease, except::::textured vegetable protein', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'the following food are allowed for patient with celiac disease, except:', 'textured vegetable protein', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Patients with celiac disease must avoid gluten-containing ingredients and products with significant risk of gluten contamination. Plain yogurt, plain popcorn, and eggs are naturally gluten-free. Textured vegetable protein (TVP) is typically made from soy and is naturally gluten-free, but commercial products may contain wheat-derived ingredients or be produced in facilities where cross-contact with gluten can occur. Therefore, patients with celiac disease should check the label and choose a product specifically identified as gluten-free when appropriate.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"textured vegetable protein\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Patients with celiac disease must avoid gluten-containing ingredients and products with significant risk of gluten contamination. Plain yogurt, plain popcorn, and eggs are naturally gluten-free. Textured vegetable protein (TVP) is typically made from soy and is naturally gluten-free, but commercial products may contain wheat-derived ingredients or be produced in facilities where cross-contact with gluten can occur. Therefore, patients with celiac disease should check the label and choose a product specifically identified as gluten-free when appropriate.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: yogurt, popcorn, egg"}'::jsonb),
('mc:which of the following is the primary purpose of nutrition screening?:::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'which of the following is the primary purpose of nutrition screening?', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Nutrition screening is a rapid process used to identify individuals who are at risk of malnutrition or who may benefit from further nutrition assessment. It is not intended to diagnose or fully characterize nutritional status. Those identified as being at risk are then referred for a comprehensive nutrition assessment, which provides the information needed for nutrition diagnosis and development of an appropriate nutrition intervention and care plan.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"identify clients who need complete nutrition assessment\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Nutrition screening is a rapid process used to identify individuals who are at risk of malnutrition or who may benefit from further nutrition assessment. It is not intended to diagnose or fully characterize nutritional status. Those identified as being at risk are then referred for a comprehensive nutrition assessment, which provides the information needed for nutrition diagnosis and development of an appropriate nutrition intervention and care plan.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: define nutritional status, determine nutrient needs, provide a basis for nutrition care plan"}'::jsonb),
('mc:the following food are allowed for patient with celiac disease, except::::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'the following food are allowed for patient with celiac disease, except:', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Patients with celiac disease must avoid gluten-containing ingredients and products with significant risk of gluten contamination. Plain yogurt, plain popcorn, and eggs are naturally gluten-free. Textured vegetable protein (TVP) is typically made from soy and is naturally gluten-free, but commercial products may contain wheat-derived ingredients or be produced in facilities where cross-contact with gluten can occur. Therefore, patients with celiac disease should check the label and choose a product specifically identified as gluten-free when appropriate.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"textured vegetable protein\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Patients with celiac disease must avoid gluten-containing ingredients and products with significant risk of gluten contamination. Plain yogurt, plain popcorn, and eggs are naturally gluten-free. Textured vegetable protein (TVP) is typically made from soy and is naturally gluten-free, but commercial products may contain wheat-derived ingredients or be produced in facilities where cross-contact with gluten can occur. Therefore, patients with celiac disease should check the label and choose a product specifically identified as gluten-free when appropriate.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: yogurt, popcorn, egg"}'::jsonb),
('mc:the difference between nitrogen intake and nitrogen excretion::::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'the difference between nitrogen intake and nitrogen excretion:', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"At nitrogen equilibrium: N intake = N output; at positive nitrogen balance: N intake > N output; at negative nitrogen balance: N output > N intake.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Nitrogen balance\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"At nitrogen equilibrium: N intake = N output; at positive nitrogen balance: N intake > N output; at negative nitrogen balance: N output > N intake.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Protein digestibility, Amino acid score, Biologic value"}'::jsonb),
('mc:the following are management of hyperparathyroidism except::::high calcium', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'the following are management of hyperparathyroidism except:', 'high calcium', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"In hyperparathyroidism, excessive calcium intake is generally not recommended because elevated parathyroid hormone (PTH) increases bone resorption and can contribute to hypercalcemia, particularly in primary hyperparathyroidism. ","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"high calcium\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"In hyperparathyroidism, excessive calcium intake is generally not recommended because elevated parathyroid hormone (PTH) increases bone resorption and can contribute to hypercalcemia, particularly in primary hyperparathyroidism. \n\nManagement focuses on addressing the underlying cause and maintaining appropriate calcium and phosphorus balance. In the traditional nutrition-management context, phosphate restriction/binders, adequate fluid intake, and an acid-ash diet may be included, particularly in renal/secondary hyperparathyroidism.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: provide phosphate binders, high fluid, acid ash diet"}'::jsonb),
('mc:the following are management of hyperparathyroidism except::::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'the following are management of hyperparathyroidism except:', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"In hyperparathyroidism, excessive calcium intake is generally not recommended because elevated parathyroid hormone (PTH) increases bone resorption and can contribute to hypercalcemia, particularly in primary hyperparathyroidism. ","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"high calcium\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"In hyperparathyroidism, excessive calcium intake is generally not recommended because elevated parathyroid hormone (PTH) increases bone resorption and can contribute to hypercalcemia, particularly in primary hyperparathyroidism. \n\nManagement focuses on addressing the underlying cause and maintaining appropriate calcium and phosphorus balance. In the traditional nutrition-management context, phosphate restriction/binders, adequate fluid intake, and an acid-ash diet may be included, particularly in renal/secondary hyperparathyroidism.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: provide phosphate binders, high fluid, acid ash diet"}'::jsonb),
('mc:which of the following is the primary purpose of nutrition screening?:::identify clients who need complete nutrition assessment', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'which of the following is the primary purpose of nutrition screening?', 'identify clients who need complete nutrition assessment', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Nutrition screening is a rapid process used to identify individuals who are at risk of malnutrition or who may benefit from further nutrition assessment. It is not intended to diagnose or fully characterize nutritional status. Those identified as being at risk are then referred for a comprehensive nutrition assessment, which provides the information needed for nutrition diagnosis and development of an appropriate nutrition intervention and care plan.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"identify clients who need complete nutrition assessment\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Nutrition screening is a rapid process used to identify individuals who are at risk of malnutrition or who may benefit from further nutrition assessment. It is not intended to diagnose or fully characterize nutritional status. Those identified as being at risk are then referred for a comprehensive nutrition assessment, which provides the information needed for nutrition diagnosis and development of an appropriate nutrition intervention and care plan.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: define nutritional status, determine nutrient needs, provide a basis for nutrition care plan"}'::jsonb)
