('mc:which of the following goals below is considered a smart goal?:::client to consume 1 oral nutrition supplement per day, 6 of 7 days per week for 1 month', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'which of the following goals below is considered a smart goal?', 'Client to consume 1 oral nutrition supplement per day, 6 of 7 days per week for 1 month', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"A SMART goal should be Specific, Measurable, Achievable, Relevant, and Time-bound.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Client to consume 1 oral nutrition supplement per day, 6 of 7 days per week for 1 month\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"A SMART goal should be Specific, Measurable, Achievable, Relevant, and Time-bound.\n\n• Specific: 1 oral nutrition supplement\n• Measurable: 1 supplement/day, on 6 of 7 days\n• Achievable: A concrete, realistic behavior target that can be monitored\n• Relevant: Directly addresses increasing oral nutritional intake\n• Time-bound: Within a defined period of 1 month","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: client to consume 1 oral nutrition supplement for 1 month, client to consume oral nutrition supplements 6 of 7 days per week, Client to consume oral nutrition supplements approximately half of the week"}'::jsonb),
('mc:which of the following goals below is considered a smart goal?:::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'which of the following goals below is considered a smart goal?', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"A SMART goal should be Specific, Measurable, Achievable, Relevant, and Time-bound.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Client to consume 1 oral nutrition supplement per day, 6 of 7 days per week for 1 month\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"A SMART goal should be Specific, Measurable, Achievable, Relevant, and Time-bound.\n\n• Specific: 1 oral nutrition supplement\n• Measurable: 1 supplement/day, on 6 of 7 days\n• Achievable: A concrete, realistic behavior target that can be monitored\n• Relevant: Directly addresses increasing oral nutritional intake\n• Time-bound: Within a defined period of 1 month","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: client to consume 1 oral nutrition supplement for 1 month, client to consume oral nutrition supplements 6 of 7 days per week, Client to consume oral nutrition supplements approximately half of the week"}'::jsonb),
('mc:in planning energy and nutrient requirements of athletes, the following factors are considered except::::none of the above', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'in planning energy and nutrient requirements of athletes, the following factors are considered except:', 'none of the above', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"All three factors—type of activity, duration, and intensity—are important when estimating an athlete''s energy and nutrient requirements.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"none of the above\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"All three factors—type of activity, duration, and intensity—are important when estimating an athlete''s energy and nutrient requirements.\n\n• Type of activity determines the predominant energy systems and muscle groups involved. Endurance, strength/power, and intermittent sports have different nutritional demands.\n• Duration affects total energy expenditure and the amount of carbohydrate, fluid, and electrolytes that may be required.\n• Intensity strongly influences energy expenditure and substrate utilization. Higher-intensity exercise generally increases the reliance on carbohydrate as a fuel source.\n\nOther important considerations include body size/composition, training volume, frequency, age, sex, environmental conditions, and the athlete''s goals.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: type of activity, duration, intensity"}'::jsonb),
('mc:in planning energy and nutrient requirements of athletes, the following factors are considered except::::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'in planning energy and nutrient requirements of athletes, the following factors are considered except:', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"All three factors—type of activity, duration, and intensity—are important when estimating an athlete''s energy and nutrient requirements.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"none of the above\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"All three factors—type of activity, duration, and intensity—are important when estimating an athlete''s energy and nutrient requirements.\n\n• Type of activity determines the predominant energy systems and muscle groups involved. Endurance, strength/power, and intermittent sports have different nutritional demands.\n• Duration affects total energy expenditure and the amount of carbohydrate, fluid, and electrolytes that may be required.\n• Intensity strongly influences energy expenditure and substrate utilization. Higher-intensity exercise generally increases the reliance on carbohydrate as a fuel source.\n\nOther important considerations include body size/composition, training volume, frequency, age, sex, environmental conditions, and the athlete''s goals.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: type of activity, duration, intensity"}'::jsonb),
('mc:the following statements regarding carbohydrates are true except::::carbohydrate loading is beneficial for athletes doing all type of exercise, both long-term and short term activities', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'the following statements regarding carbohydrates are true except:', 'carbohydrate loading is beneficial for athletes doing all type of exercise, both long-term and short term activities', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"It is a strategy specifically designed to maximize muscle glycogen storage for prolonged, endurance-based activities lasting longer than 90 minutes (such as marathons, triathlons, or long-distance cycling). It provides little to no performance benefit for short-term, high-intensity exercises (like sprinting, powerlifting, or short swimming events) because those activities do not exhaust the body’s baseline glycogen reserves.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"carbohydrate loading is beneficial for athletes doing all type of exercise, both long-term and short term activities\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"It is a strategy specifically designed to maximize muscle glycogen storage for prolonged, endurance-based activities lasting longer than 90 minutes (such as marathons, triathlons, or long-distance cycling). It provides little to no performance benefit for short-term, high-intensity exercises (like sprinting, powerlifting, or short swimming events) because those activities do not exhaust the body’s baseline glycogen reserves.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: carbohydrates is metabolized up to 3 times faster than fat which allows for faster muscle contractions, complex carbohydrates provide energy at a slow and gradual pace, simple carbohydrates cause a rapid drop in blood sugar"}'::jsonb),
('mc:the following statements regarding carbohydrates are true except::::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'the following statements regarding carbohydrates are true except:', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"It is a strategy specifically designed to maximize muscle glycogen storage for prolonged, endurance-based activities lasting longer than 90 minutes (such as marathons, triathlons, or long-distance cycling). It provides little to no performance benefit for short-term, high-intensity exercises (like sprinting, powerlifting, or short swimming events) because those activities do not exhaust the body’s baseline glycogen reserves.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"carbohydrate loading is beneficial for athletes doing all type of exercise, both long-term and short term activities\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"It is a strategy specifically designed to maximize muscle glycogen storage for prolonged, endurance-based activities lasting longer than 90 minutes (such as marathons, triathlons, or long-distance cycling). It provides little to no performance benefit for short-term, high-intensity exercises (like sprinting, powerlifting, or short swimming events) because those activities do not exhaust the body’s baseline glycogen reserves.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: carbohydrates is metabolized up to 3 times faster than fat which allows for faster muscle contractions, complex carbohydrates provide energy at a slow and gradual pace, simple carbohydrates cause a rapid drop in blood sugar"}'::jsonb),
('mc:which of the following best describes nutrition assessment according to the academy of nutrition and dietetics?:::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'which of the following best describes nutrition assessment according to the academy of nutrition and dietetics?', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Nutrition Assessment is the systematic process of obtaining and interpreting data to identify nutrition problems, forming the basis for the nutrition diagnosis.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Collecting, analyzing, and interpreting data to determine a nutrition-related problem\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Nutrition Assessment is the systematic process of obtaining and interpreting data to identify nutrition problems, forming the basis for the nutrition diagnosis.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Option B, Planning and documenting diet prescriptions for the patient, Providing individualized counseling sessions"}'::jsonb),
('mc:how many grams of addition protein daily is needed for muscle building?:::6-7 grams', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'how many grams of addition protein daily is needed for muscle building?', '6-7 grams', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Skeletal muscle hypertrophy is driven by a positive net protein balance, calculated as muscle protein synthesis (MPS) minus muscle protein breakdown (MPB). Achieving a net positive balance requires an elevation in intracellular amino acid availability—specifically essential amino acids (EAAs) like leucine, which activate the mechanistic target of rapamycin complex 1 (mTORC1) via the Sestrin2-GATOR2 pathway. When calculating the incremental dietary protein necessary to construct new contractile proteins (actin and myosin) alongside structural collagen matrices, clinical estimates establish that accumulating structural muscle tissue demands approximately 6 to 7 grams of net daily protein intake above maintenance requirements during active resistance training protocols.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"6-7 grams\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Skeletal muscle hypertrophy is driven by a positive net protein balance, calculated as muscle protein synthesis (MPS) minus muscle protein breakdown (MPB). Achieving a net positive balance requires an elevation in intracellular amino acid availability—specifically essential amino acids (EAAs) like leucine, which activate the mechanistic target of rapamycin complex 1 (mTORC1) via the Sestrin2-GATOR2 pathway. When calculating the incremental dietary protein necessary to construct new contractile proteins (actin and myosin) alongside structural collagen matrices, clinical estimates establish that accumulating structural muscle tissue demands approximately 6 to 7 grams of net daily protein intake above maintenance requirements during active resistance training protocols.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: 3 grams, 0.8g/kg BW, None of the above"}'::jsonb),
('mc:how many grams of addition protein daily is needed for muscle building?:::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'how many grams of addition protein daily is needed for muscle building?', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Skeletal muscle hypertrophy is driven by a positive net protein balance, calculated as muscle protein synthesis (MPS) minus muscle protein breakdown (MPB). Achieving a net positive balance requires an elevation in intracellular amino acid availability—specifically essential amino acids (EAAs) like leucine, which activate the mechanistic target of rapamycin complex 1 (mTORC1) via the Sestrin2-GATOR2 pathway. When calculating the incremental dietary protein necessary to construct new contractile proteins (actin and myosin) alongside structural collagen matrices, clinical estimates establish that accumulating structural muscle tissue demands approximately 6 to 7 grams of net daily protein intake above maintenance requirements during active resistance training protocols.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"6-7 grams\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Skeletal muscle hypertrophy is driven by a positive net protein balance, calculated as muscle protein synthesis (MPS) minus muscle protein breakdown (MPB). Achieving a net positive balance requires an elevation in intracellular amino acid availability—specifically essential amino acids (EAAs) like leucine, which activate the mechanistic target of rapamycin complex 1 (mTORC1) via the Sestrin2-GATOR2 pathway. When calculating the incremental dietary protein necessary to construct new contractile proteins (actin and myosin) alongside structural collagen matrices, clinical estimates establish that accumulating structural muscle tissue demands approximately 6 to 7 grams of net daily protein intake above maintenance requirements during active resistance training protocols.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: 3 grams, 0.8g/kg BW, None of the above"}'::jsonb),
('mc:this nutrient is supplemented with the idea of helping to build muscle and promote growth. humans synthesize adequate amounts of this nutrient; its functions are with the central nervous system and cell structure.:::choline', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'this nutrient is supplemented with the idea of helping to build muscle and promote growth. humans synthesize adequate amounts of this nutrient; its functions are with the central nervous system and cell structure.', 'choline', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Choline is the best answer. Although choline is not technically classified as a vitamin, it is an essential nutrient because endogenous synthesis is not always sufficient to meet physiological needs.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"choline\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Choline is the best answer. Although choline is not technically classified as a vitamin, it is an essential nutrient because endogenous synthesis is not always sufficient to meet physiological needs.\n\nCholine has several important functions, including:\n• Cell membrane structure: Choline is a component of phosphatidylcholine and sphingomyelin, important phospholipids in cell membranes.\n• Neurotransmission: Choline is the precursor of acetylcholine, an important neurotransmitter involved in memory, muscle control, and other nervous-system functions.\n• Lipid transport and metabolism: Choline is required for the synthesis of phosphatidylcholine and lipoproteins and helps prevent abnormal lipid accumulation in the liver.\n• Methyl-group metabolism: Choline can be oxidized to betaine, which participates in methyl-group transfer.\n\nCholine has historically been marketed or supplemented in sports settings with claims related to muscle performance, growth, or fat metabolism, but these claims should not be confused with established physiological functions. Evidence does not demonstrate that routine choline supplementation produces significant muscle-building effects in healthy individuals consuming adequate choline.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: chromium, riboflavin, pyridoxine"}'::jsonb),
('mc:this nutrient is supplemented with the idea of helping to build muscle and promote growth. humans synthesize adequate amounts of this nutrient; its functions are with the central nervous system and cell structure.:::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'this nutrient is supplemented with the idea of helping to build muscle and promote growth. humans synthesize adequate amounts of this nutrient; its functions are with the central nervous system and cell structure.', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Choline is the best answer. Although choline is not technically classified as a vitamin, it is an essential nutrient because endogenous synthesis is not always sufficient to meet physiological needs.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"choline\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Choline is the best answer. Although choline is not technically classified as a vitamin, it is an essential nutrient because endogenous synthesis is not always sufficient to meet physiological needs.\n\nCholine has several important functions, including:\n• Cell membrane structure: Choline is a component of phosphatidylcholine and sphingomyelin, important phospholipids in cell membranes.\n• Neurotransmission: Choline is the precursor of acetylcholine, an important neurotransmitter involved in memory, muscle control, and other nervous-system functions.\n• Lipid transport and metabolism: Choline is required for the synthesis of phosphatidylcholine and lipoproteins and helps prevent abnormal lipid accumulation in the liver.\n• Methyl-group metabolism: Choline can be oxidized to betaine, which participates in methyl-group transfer.\n\nCholine has historically been marketed or supplemented in sports settings with claims related to muscle performance, growth, or fat metabolism, but these claims should not be confused with established physiological functions. Evidence does not demonstrate that routine choline supplementation produces significant muscle-building effects in healthy individuals consuming adequate choline.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: chromium, riboflavin, pyridoxine"}'::jsonb),
('mc:the following are type of motility disease except::::achylia gastrica', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'the following are type of motility disease except:', 'achylia gastrica', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Achylia gastrica is not primarily a motility disorder. It refers to marked or complete absence of gastric hydrochloric acid and pepsin secretion, usually associated with gastric gland atrophy or other disorders affecting gastric secretory function.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"achylia gastrica\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Achylia gastrica is not primarily a motility disorder. It refers to marked or complete absence of gastric hydrochloric acid and pepsin secretion, usually associated with gastric gland atrophy or other disorders affecting gastric secretory function.\n\nIn contrast, gastric atony and gastrectasia are associated with impaired gastric motility and gastric emptying. Dyspepsia is primarily a symptom syndrome involving upper gastrointestinal discomfort and can be associated with abnormalities of gastric motility, accommodation, or sensation.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: dyspepsia, gastric atony, gastrectasia"}'::jsonb),
('mc:the following statements are true about food intolerances, except::::it causes anaphylactic life-threatening immune reaction', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'the following statements are true about food intolerances, except:', 'it causes anaphylactic life-threatening immune reaction', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"The key distinction between food allergy and food intolerance is the underlying mechanism.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"it causes anaphylactic life-threatening immune reaction\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"The key distinction between food allergy and food intolerance is the underlying mechanism.\n\nFood allergy involves an immune-mediated response to a food protein. An IgE-mediated food allergy can cause anaphylaxis, which is a potentially life-threatening systemic reaction.\n\nFood intolerance, on the other hand, generally refers to adverse reactions to food that do not involve an immune-mediated mechanism. Examples include lactose intolerance due to lactase deficiency and certain reactions resulting from pharmacologic substances in foods or metabolic disorders.\n\nTherefore, food intolerance does not cause anaphylaxis through an immune-mediated food-allergic reaction.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: food intolerances are more common than true food allergy, PKU and G6PD are examples of food intolerances caused by inborn errors of metabolism, none of the above"}'::jsonb),
('mc:the following statements are true about food intolerances, except::::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'the following statements are true about food intolerances, except:', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"The key distinction between food allergy and food intolerance is the underlying mechanism.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"it causes anaphylactic life-threatening immune reaction\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"The key distinction between food allergy and food intolerance is the underlying mechanism.\n\nFood allergy involves an immune-mediated response to a food protein. An IgE-mediated food allergy can cause anaphylaxis, which is a potentially life-threatening systemic reaction.\n\nFood intolerance, on the other hand, generally refers to adverse reactions to food that do not involve an immune-mediated mechanism. Examples include lactose intolerance due to lactase deficiency and certain reactions resulting from pharmacologic substances in foods or metabolic disorders.\n\nTherefore, food intolerance does not cause anaphylaxis through an immune-mediated food-allergic reaction.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: food intolerances are more common than true food allergy, PKU and G6PD are examples of food intolerances caused by inborn errors of metabolism, none of the above"}'::jsonb),
('mc:the point at which it is impossible to keep moving; corresponds to the time when the reserves of the glycogen in the muscles are depleted::::hitting the wall', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'the point at which it is impossible to keep moving; corresponds to the time when the reserves of the glycogen in the muscles are depleted:', 'hitting the wall', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"“Hitting the wall” is a term commonly used in endurance sports to describe a sudden and profound feeling of fatigue during prolonged exercise, traditionally associated with substantial depletion of muscle glycogen stores. The athlete may experience extreme fatigue, weakness, difficulty maintaining pace, and a strong perception that continuing the exercise is very difficult. It is better understood as a state of severe fatigue associated with low carbohydrate availability, along with other physiological and psychological factors.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"hitting the wall\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"“Hitting the wall” is a term commonly used in endurance sports to describe a sudden and profound feeling of fatigue during prolonged exercise, traditionally associated with substantial depletion of muscle glycogen stores. The athlete may experience extreme fatigue, weakness, difficulty maintaining pace, and a strong perception that continuing the exercise is very difficult. It is better understood as a state of severe fatigue associated with low carbohydrate availability, along with other physiological and psychological factors.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: carbohydrate loading, muscle fatigue, muscle cramps"}'::jsonb),
('mc:the point at which it is impossible to keep moving; corresponds to the time when the reserves of the glycogen in the muscles are depleted::::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'the point at which it is impossible to keep moving; corresponds to the time when the reserves of the glycogen in the muscles are depleted:', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"“Hitting the wall” is a term commonly used in endurance sports to describe a sudden and profound feeling of fatigue during prolonged exercise, traditionally associated with substantial depletion of muscle glycogen stores. The athlete may experience extreme fatigue, weakness, difficulty maintaining pace, and a strong perception that continuing the exercise is very difficult. It is better understood as a state of severe fatigue associated with low carbohydrate availability, along with other physiological and psychological factors.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"hitting the wall\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"“Hitting the wall” is a term commonly used in endurance sports to describe a sudden and profound feeling of fatigue during prolonged exercise, traditionally associated with substantial depletion of muscle glycogen stores. The athlete may experience extreme fatigue, weakness, difficulty maintaining pace, and a strong perception that continuing the exercise is very difficult. It is better understood as a state of severe fatigue associated with low carbohydrate availability, along with other physiological and psychological factors.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: carbohydrate loading, muscle fatigue, muscle cramps"}'::jsonb),
('mc:the following are type of motility disease except::::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'the following are type of motility disease except:', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Achylia gastrica is not primarily a motility disorder. It refers to marked or complete absence of gastric hydrochloric acid and pepsin secretion, usually associated with gastric gland atrophy or other disorders affecting gastric secretory function.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"achylia gastrica\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Achylia gastrica is not primarily a motility disorder. It refers to marked or complete absence of gastric hydrochloric acid and pepsin secretion, usually associated with gastric gland atrophy or other disorders affecting gastric secretory function.\n\nIn contrast, gastric atony and gastrectasia are associated with impaired gastric motility and gastric emptying. Dyspepsia is primarily a symptom syndrome involving upper gastrointestinal discomfort and can be associated with abnormalities of gastric motility, accommodation, or sensation.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: dyspepsia, gastric atony, gastrectasia"}'::jsonb),
('mc:a disease characterized by atrophy of the intestines due to lack of peptidase to digest the gliadin factors of gluten.:::gluten-sensitive enteropathy', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'a disease characterized by atrophy of the intestines due to lack of peptidase to digest the gliadin factors of gluten.', 'Gluten-sensitive enteropathy', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Gluten-sensitive enteropathy (celiac disease) is an immune-mediated disorder triggered by exposure to gluten in genetically susceptible individuals. Gluten contains gliadin, which contributes to the immune response that damages the small-intestinal mucosa.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Gluten-sensitive enteropathy\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Gluten-sensitive enteropathy (celiac disease) is an immune-mediated disorder triggered by exposure to gluten in genetically susceptible individuals. Gluten contains gliadin, which contributes to the immune response that damages the small-intestinal mucosa.\n\nThe resulting mucosal injury can produce villous atrophy, crypt hyperplasia, and impaired absorptive capacity, leading to malabsorption of nutrients. The disease involves an inappropriate immune response to gluten peptides, particularly in individuals carrying HLA-DQ2 or HLA-DQ8. \n\nTissue transglutaminase modifies gluten peptides, enhancing their presentation to T cells and triggering intestinal inflammation.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Enteritis, Tropical sprue, Lymphagiectasis"}'::jsonb),
('mc:"unintentional weight loss related to hypermetabolism secondary to cancer as evidenced by 10% weight loss in 6 months". which diagnosis domain is this?:::clinical', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, '"unintentional weight loss related to hypermetabolism secondary to cancer as evidenced by 10% weight loss in 6 months". which diagnosis domain is this?', 'Clinical', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Clinical domain covers weight changes, biochemical values, physical findings influenced by disease or condition.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Clinical\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Clinical domain covers weight changes, biochemical values, physical findings influenced by disease or condition.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Intake, None of the above, Behavioral-Environmental"}'::jsonb),
('mc:a disease characterized by atrophy of the intestines due to lack of peptidase to digest the gliadin factors of gluten.:::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'a disease characterized by atrophy of the intestines due to lack of peptidase to digest the gliadin factors of gluten.', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Gluten-sensitive enteropathy (celiac disease) is an immune-mediated disorder triggered by exposure to gluten in genetically susceptible individuals. Gluten contains gliadin, which contributes to the immune response that damages the small-intestinal mucosa.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Gluten-sensitive enteropathy\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Gluten-sensitive enteropathy (celiac disease) is an immune-mediated disorder triggered by exposure to gluten in genetically susceptible individuals. Gluten contains gliadin, which contributes to the immune response that damages the small-intestinal mucosa.\n\nThe resulting mucosal injury can produce villous atrophy, crypt hyperplasia, and impaired absorptive capacity, leading to malabsorption of nutrients. The disease involves an inappropriate immune response to gluten peptides, particularly in individuals carrying HLA-DQ2 or HLA-DQ8. \n\nTissue transglutaminase modifies gluten peptides, enhancing their presentation to T cells and triggering intestinal inflammation.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Enteritis, Tropical sprue, Lymphagiectasis"}'::jsonb),
('mc:which of the following is true for nutritional management of hemorrhoids?:::high fiber', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'which of the following is true for nutritional management of hemorrhoids?', 'high fiber', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"The primary nutrition goal in hemorrhoids is to prevent constipation and straining during defecation. Increasing dietary fiber helps by increasing stool bulk and, particularly when adequate fluid is consumed, improving stool consistency and promoting easier bowel movements.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"high fiber\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"The primary nutrition goal in hemorrhoids is to prevent constipation and straining during defecation. Increasing dietary fiber helps by increasing stool bulk and, particularly when adequate fluid is consumed, improving stool consistency and promoting easier bowel movements.\n\nCurrent evidence supports increasing dietary fiber intake as an effective conservative intervention for hemorrhoids. Fiber supplementation has been shown to reduce persistent symptoms and bleeding.\n\nFor adults, a practical target is generally around 25–30 g of fiber per day, with gradual increases and adequate fluid intake to minimize bloating or discomfort.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: high calorie, low protein, low residue"}'::jsonb),
('mc:which of the following is true for nutritional management of hemorrhoids?:::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'which of the following is true for nutritional management of hemorrhoids?', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"The primary nutrition goal in hemorrhoids is to prevent constipation and straining during defecation. Increasing dietary fiber helps by increasing stool bulk and, particularly when adequate fluid is consumed, improving stool consistency and promoting easier bowel movements.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"high fiber\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"The primary nutrition goal in hemorrhoids is to prevent constipation and straining during defecation. Increasing dietary fiber helps by increasing stool bulk and, particularly when adequate fluid is consumed, improving stool consistency and promoting easier bowel movements.\n\nCurrent evidence supports increasing dietary fiber intake as an effective conservative intervention for hemorrhoids. Fiber supplementation has been shown to reduce persistent symptoms and bleeding.\n\nFor adults, a practical target is generally around 25–30 g of fiber per day, with gradual increases and adequate fluid intake to minimize bloating or discomfort.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: high calorie, low protein, low residue"}'::jsonb),
('mc:the nutrition prescription is included in which step of the ncp?:::nutrition intervention', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'the nutrition prescription is included in which step of the ncp?', 'Nutrition intervention', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"The nutrition prescription is part of the Nutrition Intervention step of the Nutrition Care Process. It translates the nutrition diagnosis and assessment findings into a specific plan for addressing the identified nutrition problem.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Nutrition intervention\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"The nutrition prescription is part of the Nutrition Intervention step of the Nutrition Care Process. It translates the nutrition diagnosis and assessment findings into a specific plan for addressing the identified nutrition problem.\n\nThe nutrition prescription describes the recommended amount and/or type of food, nutrients, or nutrition-related intervention appropriate for the patient. It may include parameters such as energy, protein, fluid, carbohydrate, fat, sodium, or other nutrient requirements, depending on the patient''s condition and nutrition diagnosis. The prescription is developed during intervention, after the nutrition problem has been identified through assessment and diagnosis.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Nutrition assessment, Nutrition diagnosis, Nutrition monitoring and evaluation"}'::jsonb),
('mc:"unintentional weight loss related to hypermetabolism secondary to cancer as evidenced by 10% weight loss in 6 months". which diagnosis domain is this?:::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, '"unintentional weight loss related to hypermetabolism secondary to cancer as evidenced by 10% weight loss in 6 months". which diagnosis domain is this?', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Clinical domain covers weight changes, biochemical values, physical findings influenced by disease or condition.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Clinical\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Clinical domain covers weight changes, biochemical values, physical findings influenced by disease or condition.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Intake, None of the above, Behavioral-Environmental"}'::jsonb),
('mc:the nutrition prescription is included in which step of the ncp?:::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'the nutrition prescription is included in which step of the ncp?', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"The nutrition prescription is part of the Nutrition Intervention step of the Nutrition Care Process. It translates the nutrition diagnosis and assessment findings into a specific plan for addressing the identified nutrition problem.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Nutrition intervention\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"The nutrition prescription is part of the Nutrition Intervention step of the Nutrition Care Process. It translates the nutrition diagnosis and assessment findings into a specific plan for addressing the identified nutrition problem.\n\nThe nutrition prescription describes the recommended amount and/or type of food, nutrients, or nutrition-related intervention appropriate for the patient. It may include parameters such as energy, protein, fluid, carbohydrate, fat, sodium, or other nutrient requirements, depending on the patient''s condition and nutrition diagnosis. The prescription is developed during intervention, after the nutrition problem has been identified through assessment and diagnosis.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Nutrition assessment, Nutrition diagnosis, Nutrition monitoring and evaluation"}'::jsonb)
