('mc:which of the following is a nutrition diagnosis domain in ncpt?:::clinical, behavioral-environmental, intake', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'which of the following is a nutrition diagnosis domain in ncpt?', 'Clinical, Behavioral-Environmental, Intake', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Nutrition diagnoses fall into three domains: Intake (NI), Clinical (NC), and Behavioral-Environmental (NB).","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Clinical, Behavioral-Environmental, Intake\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Nutrition diagnoses fall into three domains: Intake (NI), Clinical (NC), and Behavioral-Environmental (NB).","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Food/Nutrition-Related History, Anthropometric Measurements, Nutrition-Focused Physical Findings, Assessment, Diagnosis, Intervention, Anthropometrics, Biochemical, Clinical"}'::jsonb),
('mc:an eating disorder characterized by recurrent episodes of eating excessively followed by compensatory purging, such as self-induced vomiting, is::::bulimia nervosa', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'an eating disorder characterized by recurrent episodes of eating excessively followed by compensatory purging, such as self-induced vomiting, is:', 'Bulimia nervosa', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Bulimia nervosa is characterized by recurrent binge-eating episodes accompanied by inappropriate compensatory behaviors, such as self-induced vomiting, misuse of laxatives or diuretics, fasting, or excessive exercise. Unlike anorexia nervosa, body weight in individuals with bulimia nervosa is often within or above the normal range, although this is not required for diagnosis.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Bulimia nervosa\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Bulimia nervosa is characterized by recurrent binge-eating episodes accompanied by inappropriate compensatory behaviors, such as self-induced vomiting, misuse of laxatives or diuretics, fasting, or excessive exercise. Unlike anorexia nervosa, body weight in individuals with bulimia nervosa is often within or above the normal range, although this is not required for diagnosis.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Anorexia nervosa, Binge-eating type, Restricting type"}'::jsonb),
