('mc:which of the following protein intakes is generally appropriate for a resistance-trained individual seeking to support muscle hypertrophy?:::approximately 1.4–2.0 g/kg bw/day', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'which of the following protein intakes is generally appropriate for a resistance-trained individual seeking to support muscle hypertrophy?', 'Approximately 1.4–2.0 g/kg BW/day', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"For individuals who participate in resistance training and are seeking to increase or maintain muscle mass, protein requirements are generally higher than the RDA for sedentary adults. The International Society of Sports Nutrition (ISSN) recommends approximately 1.4–2.0 g protein/kg body weight/day for most exercising individuals.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Approximately 1.4–2.0 g/kg BW/day\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"For individuals who participate in resistance training and are seeking to increase or maintain muscle mass, protein requirements are generally higher than the RDA for sedentary adults. The International Society of Sports Nutrition (ISSN) recommends approximately 1.4–2.0 g protein/kg body weight/day for most exercising individuals.\n\nProtein intake should also be distributed throughout the day rather than consumed in one large dose. Adequate total energy intake and resistance training are equally important because protein alone cannot maximize muscle hypertrophy.\n\nResearch also suggests that the increase in muscle-building benefit tends to plateau around ~1.6 g/kg/day for many resistance-trained individuals, although some individuals may benefit from intakes toward the upper end of the range depending on training volume, energy restriction, age, and other factors.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Approximately 0.6–0.8 g/kg BW/day, Approximately 3.0–4.0 g/kg BW/day, Protein intake does not need to increase with resistance training"}'::jsonb),
('mc:which of the following protein intakes is generally appropriate for a resistance-trained individual seeking to support muscle hypertrophy?:::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'which of the following protein intakes is generally appropriate for a resistance-trained individual seeking to support muscle hypertrophy?', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"For individuals who participate in resistance training and are seeking to increase or maintain muscle mass, protein requirements are generally higher than the RDA for sedentary adults. The International Society of Sports Nutrition (ISSN) recommends approximately 1.4–2.0 g protein/kg body weight/day for most exercising individuals.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Approximately 1.4–2.0 g/kg BW/day\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"For individuals who participate in resistance training and are seeking to increase or maintain muscle mass, protein requirements are generally higher than the RDA for sedentary adults. The International Society of Sports Nutrition (ISSN) recommends approximately 1.4–2.0 g protein/kg body weight/day for most exercising individuals.\n\nProtein intake should also be distributed throughout the day rather than consumed in one large dose. Adequate total energy intake and resistance training are equally important because protein alone cannot maximize muscle hypertrophy.\n\nResearch also suggests that the increase in muscle-building benefit tends to plateau around ~1.6 g/kg/day for many resistance-trained individuals, although some individuals may benefit from intakes toward the upper end of the range depending on training volume, energy restriction, age, and other factors.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Approximately 0.6–0.8 g/kg BW/day, Approximately 3.0–4.0 g/kg BW/day, Protein intake does not need to increase with resistance training"}'::jsonb),
