('mc:in patients with cirrhosis, which dietary strategy is most appropriate to help prevent protein-energy malnutrition?:::adequate energy and protein intake with small, frequent meals', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'in patients with cirrhosis, which dietary strategy is most appropriate to help prevent protein-energy malnutrition?', 'Adequate energy and protein intake with small, frequent meals', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Patients with cirrhosis are at high risk of protein-energy malnutrition and muscle wasting. Providing adequate energy and protein helps maintain lean body mass and prevent catabolism. Small, frequent meals and a late-evening snack can help reduce prolonged fasting and provide a more continuous supply of energy, particularly because glycogen stores may be reduced in advanced liver disease.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Adequate energy and protein intake with small, frequent meals\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Patients with cirrhosis are at high risk of protein-energy malnutrition and muscle wasting. Providing adequate energy and protein helps maintain lean body mass and prevent catabolism. Small, frequent meals and a late-evening snack can help reduce prolonged fasting and provide a more continuous supply of energy, particularly because glycogen stores may be reduced in advanced liver disease.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Prolonged fasting between meals, Routine severe restriction of dietary protein, Elimination of carbohydrate-rich foods"}'::jsonb)
