('mc:which of the following best describes nutrition assessment according to the academy of nutrition and dietetics?:::collecting, analyzing, and interpreting data to determine a nutrition-related problem', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'which of the following best describes nutrition assessment according to the academy of nutrition and dietetics?', 'Collecting, analyzing, and interpreting data to determine a nutrition-related problem', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Nutrition Assessment is the systematic process of obtaining and interpreting data to identify nutrition problems, forming the basis for the nutrition diagnosis.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Collecting, analyzing, and interpreting data to determine a nutrition-related problem\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Nutrition Assessment is the systematic process of obtaining and interpreting data to identify nutrition problems, forming the basis for the nutrition diagnosis.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Option B, Planning and documenting diet prescriptions for the patient, Providing individualized counseling sessions"}'::jsonb),
('mc:consumption of this food increases the risk for liver cancer:::moldy peanuts', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'consumption of this food increases the risk for liver cancer', 'Moldy peanuts', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Poorly stored grains and legumes—most notably peanuts—are highly susceptible to contamination by molds like Aspergillus flavus and Aspergillus parasiticus. These molds produce a potent, heat-stable mycotoxin known as aflatoxin (specifically Aflatoxin B1).","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Moldy peanuts\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Poorly stored grains and legumes—most notably peanuts—are highly susceptible to contamination by molds like Aspergillus flavus and Aspergillus parasiticus. These molds produce a potent, heat-stable mycotoxin known as aflatoxin (specifically Aflatoxin B1).\n\nAflatoxin is classified as a definitive Group 1 human carcinogen. Once ingested, the liver processes this toxin, creating a highly reactive intermediate metabolite that directly binds to DNA, inducing specific mutations in the p53 tumor suppressor gene. This DNA damage impairs the body''s natural defense against cellular mutations, directly triggering the development of hepatocellular carcinoma (liver cancer).","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Kimchi, Alcohol, Pork"}'::jsonb),
('mc:consumption of this food increases the risk for liver cancer:::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'consumption of this food increases the risk for liver cancer', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Poorly stored grains and legumes—most notably peanuts—are highly susceptible to contamination by molds like Aspergillus flavus and Aspergillus parasiticus. These molds produce a potent, heat-stable mycotoxin known as aflatoxin (specifically Aflatoxin B1).","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Moldy peanuts\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Poorly stored grains and legumes—most notably peanuts—are highly susceptible to contamination by molds like Aspergillus flavus and Aspergillus parasiticus. These molds produce a potent, heat-stable mycotoxin known as aflatoxin (specifically Aflatoxin B1).\n\nAflatoxin is classified as a definitive Group 1 human carcinogen. Once ingested, the liver processes this toxin, creating a highly reactive intermediate metabolite that directly binds to DNA, inducing specific mutations in the p53 tumor suppressor gene. This DNA damage impairs the body''s natural defense against cellular mutations, directly triggering the development of hepatocellular carcinoma (liver cancer).","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Kimchi, Alcohol, Pork"}'::jsonb),
('mc:the following are components of physical fitness except:::resilience', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'the following are components of physical fitness except', 'Resilience', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Resilience is a psychological and mental attribute that refers to a person''s ability to adapt, cope, and mentally bounce back from stress, adversity, or trauma. While it is highly beneficial for mental health, it is not classified as a physical component of fitness in physical education and sports science curriculum frameworks.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Resilience\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Resilience is a psychological and mental attribute that refers to a person''s ability to adapt, cope, and mentally bounce back from stress, adversity, or trauma. While it is highly beneficial for mental health, it is not classified as a physical component of fitness in physical education and sports science curriculum frameworks.\n\nThe Standard Components of Physical Fitness\n\nSkill-Related Fitness Components\nThese components focus on sports performance, motor skills, and technical precision:\n• Coordination: The ability to use different body parts together smoothly and efficiently (e.g., hand-eye coordination).\n• Speed: The ability to move your body or parts of your body quickly over a short distance.\n• Balance: The ability to maintain stability and control your body''s center of mass over a base of support.\n• Other components: Agility, Power, and Reaction Time\n\nHealth-Related Fitness Components\nThese focus on general physiological functioning, disease prevention, and daily bodily efficiency:\n• Cardiovascular Endurance\n• Muscular Strength\n• Muscular Endurance\n• Flexibility\n• Body Composition","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Balance, Coordination, Speed"}'::jsonb),
('mc:the following are components of physical fitness except:::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'the following are components of physical fitness except', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Resilience is a psychological and mental attribute that refers to a person''s ability to adapt, cope, and mentally bounce back from stress, adversity, or trauma. While it is highly beneficial for mental health, it is not classified as a physical component of fitness in physical education and sports science curriculum frameworks.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Resilience\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Resilience is a psychological and mental attribute that refers to a person''s ability to adapt, cope, and mentally bounce back from stress, adversity, or trauma. While it is highly beneficial for mental health, it is not classified as a physical component of fitness in physical education and sports science curriculum frameworks.\n\nThe Standard Components of Physical Fitness\n\nSkill-Related Fitness Components\nThese components focus on sports performance, motor skills, and technical precision:\n• Coordination: The ability to use different body parts together smoothly and efficiently (e.g., hand-eye coordination).\n• Speed: The ability to move your body or parts of your body quickly over a short distance.\n• Balance: The ability to maintain stability and control your body''s center of mass over a base of support.\n• Other components: Agility, Power, and Reaction Time\n\nHealth-Related Fitness Components\nThese focus on general physiological functioning, disease prevention, and daily bodily efficiency:\n• Cardiovascular Endurance\n• Muscular Strength\n• Muscular Endurance\n• Flexibility\n• Body Composition","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Balance, Coordination, Speed"}'::jsonb),
('mc:the muscle action that requires the use of energy in the form of atp:::relaxation', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'the muscle action that requires the use of energy in the form of atp', 'Relaxation', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"During muscle contraction, myosin heads bind to actin and execute the power stroke via the utilization of ATP, but the detachment of the cross-bridge and the sliding motion itself are driven by stored energy release. Conversely, muscle relaxation cannot occur spontaneously; it requires the active transport of Ca2+ back into the sarcoplasmic reticulum via the Sarco/Endoplasmic Reticulum Calcium ATPase (SERCA) pump, which directly consumes one molecule of ATP per two calcium ions pumped. Additionally, ATP binding to the myosin head is strictly required to break the rigor complex and allow the muscle to relax.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Relaxation\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"During muscle contraction, myosin heads bind to actin and execute the power stroke via the utilization of ATP, but the detachment of the cross-bridge and the sliding motion itself are driven by stored energy release. Conversely, muscle relaxation cannot occur spontaneously; it requires the active transport of Ca2+ back into the sarcoplasmic reticulum via the Sarco/Endoplasmic Reticulum Calcium ATPase (SERCA) pump, which directly consumes one molecule of ATP per two calcium ions pumped. Additionally, ATP binding to the myosin head is strictly required to break the rigor complex and allow the muscle to relax.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Contraction, Flexing, Moving"}'::jsonb),
('mc:the muscle action that requires the use of energy in the form of atp:::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'the muscle action that requires the use of energy in the form of atp', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"During muscle contraction, myosin heads bind to actin and execute the power stroke via the utilization of ATP, but the detachment of the cross-bridge and the sliding motion itself are driven by stored energy release. Conversely, muscle relaxation cannot occur spontaneously; it requires the active transport of Ca2+ back into the sarcoplasmic reticulum via the Sarco/Endoplasmic Reticulum Calcium ATPase (SERCA) pump, which directly consumes one molecule of ATP per two calcium ions pumped. Additionally, ATP binding to the myosin head is strictly required to break the rigor complex and allow the muscle to relax.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Relaxation\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"During muscle contraction, myosin heads bind to actin and execute the power stroke via the utilization of ATP, but the detachment of the cross-bridge and the sliding motion itself are driven by stored energy release. Conversely, muscle relaxation cannot occur spontaneously; it requires the active transport of Ca2+ back into the sarcoplasmic reticulum via the Sarco/Endoplasmic Reticulum Calcium ATPase (SERCA) pump, which directly consumes one molecule of ATP per two calcium ions pumped. Additionally, ATP binding to the myosin head is strictly required to break the rigor complex and allow the muscle to relax.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Contraction, Flexing, Moving"}'::jsonb),
('mc:a dietitian needs to determine a patient’s risk for cardiovascular disease. the following anthropometric measurements must be assessed except:::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'a dietitian needs to determine a patient’s risk for cardiovascular disease. the following anthropometric measurements must be assessed except', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Exercise history is a behavioral or lifestyle component of a patient''s personal history, not a physical bodily measurement.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Exercise history\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Exercise history is a behavioral or lifestyle component of a patient''s personal history, not a physical bodily measurement.\n\nAnthropometric measurements are non-invasive, quantitative, physical measurements used to assess the size, shape, and composition of the human body.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Height and weight, Waist Circumference, Body fat analysis"}'::jsonb),
('mc:the type of muscle that compromises 40% of body weight and is involved in heat production during exercise and motion.:::skeletal muscle', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'the type of muscle that compromises 40% of body weight and is involved in heat production during exercise and motion.', 'Skeletal muscle', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"In standard anatomy and physiology textbooks, skeletal muscle is the most abundant tissue type in the human body, accounting for approximately 40% of total body weight in a healthy adult.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Skeletal muscle\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"In standard anatomy and physiology textbooks, skeletal muscle is the most abundant tissue type in the human body, accounting for approximately 40% of total body weight in a healthy adult.\n\nSkeletal muscle cells are heavily packed with mitochondria. During physical exercise and regular movement, these muscles rapidly break down adenosine triphosphate (ATP) to drive contractions. This metabolic process generates an immense amount of thermal energy, serving as the body''s primary mechanism for heat production to help maintain homeostatic core temperature.\n\n• Smooth muscle fiber: Found within the walls of hollow internal organs (like blood vessels and the gastrointestinal tract). Smooth muscle contracts involuntarily and accounts for only a tiny fraction of overall body weight.\n• Cardiac muscle fiber: Highly specialized, involuntary striated muscle tissue found exclusively in the walls of the heart. It does not make up a massive 40% share of body weight.\n• Muscle mass: This is a broad, non-specific macroscopic term measuring total muscular composition rather than defining the specific type of muscle tissue requested by the question.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Smooth muscle fiber, Cardiac muscle fiber, Muscle mass"}'::jsonb),
('mc:the type of muscle that compromises 40% of body weight and is involved in heat production during exercise and motion.:::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'the type of muscle that compromises 40% of body weight and is involved in heat production during exercise and motion.', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"In standard anatomy and physiology textbooks, skeletal muscle is the most abundant tissue type in the human body, accounting for approximately 40% of total body weight in a healthy adult.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Skeletal muscle\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"In standard anatomy and physiology textbooks, skeletal muscle is the most abundant tissue type in the human body, accounting for approximately 40% of total body weight in a healthy adult.\n\nSkeletal muscle cells are heavily packed with mitochondria. During physical exercise and regular movement, these muscles rapidly break down adenosine triphosphate (ATP) to drive contractions. This metabolic process generates an immense amount of thermal energy, serving as the body''s primary mechanism for heat production to help maintain homeostatic core temperature.\n\n• Smooth muscle fiber: Found within the walls of hollow internal organs (like blood vessels and the gastrointestinal tract). Smooth muscle contracts involuntarily and accounts for only a tiny fraction of overall body weight.\n• Cardiac muscle fiber: Highly specialized, involuntary striated muscle tissue found exclusively in the walls of the heart. It does not make up a massive 40% share of body weight.\n• Muscle mass: This is a broad, non-specific macroscopic term measuring total muscular composition rather than defining the specific type of muscle tissue requested by the question.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Smooth muscle fiber, Cardiac muscle fiber, Muscle mass"}'::jsonb),
('mc:a 60kg female patient with comorbidities needs to lose weight. what is the minimum target weight loss from baseline in 6 months?:::3kg', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'a 60kg female patient with comorbidities needs to lose weight. what is the minimum target weight loss from baseline in 6 months?', '3kg', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"According to the World Health Organization (WHO) and international clinical obesity guidelines (such as those outlined by the American Heart Association (AHA), American College of Cardiology (ACC), and MIMS Philippines), the standard, primary therapeutic recommendation for an adult with obesity or overweight and comorbidities is to target a minimum initial weight loss of 5% of their baseline body weight over a 6-month period. Achieving a modest 5% reduction is highly significant because it immediately triggers measurable improvements in glycemic control, blood pressure, and cardiovascular risk factors.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"3kg\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"According to the World Health Organization (WHO) and international clinical obesity guidelines (such as those outlined by the American Heart Association (AHA), American College of Cardiology (ACC), and MIMS Philippines), the standard, primary therapeutic recommendation for an adult with obesity or overweight and comorbidities is to target a minimum initial weight loss of 5% of their baseline body weight over a 6-month period. Achieving a modest 5% reduction is highly significant because it immediately triggers measurable improvements in glycemic control, blood pressure, and cardiovascular risk factors.\n\nPatient Baseline Weight = 60 kg\nMinimum Clinical Target Percentage = 5% (0.05)\n\nMinimum Target Weight Loss = 60 kg x 0.05 = 3 kg","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: 6kg, 10kg, 5kg"}'::jsonb),
('mc:this type of exercise does not require oxygen.:::anaerobic exercise', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'this type of exercise does not require oxygen.', 'Anaerobic exercise', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Anaerobic exercise literally means exercise \"without oxygen.\" During these short, high-intensity bursts of physical activity, the muscles demand energy much faster than the body can transport oxygen to them.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Anaerobic exercise\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Anaerobic exercise literally means exercise \"without oxygen.\" During these short, high-intensity bursts of physical activity, the muscles demand energy much faster than the body can transport oxygen to them.\n\nTo keep up with this extreme demand, the muscle cells rely entirely on immediate energy pathways that do not require oxygen, specifically the phosphagen (ATP-CP) system and anaerobic glycolysis (which breaks down glycogen and produces lactic acid). Examples include heavy weightlifting, sprinting, and high-intensity interval training (HIIT).\n\n• Cardio / Aerobic exercise: \"Aerobic\" literally means \"with oxygen.\" Cardio or aerobic activities (like jogging, cycling, or swimming) are sustained at a lower intensity, allowing the heart and lungs to continuously supply enough oxygen to the muscles to generate energy efficiently.\n• Moderate intensity exercise: Activities performed at a moderate intensity rely primarily on aerobic pathways because the cardiovascular system can successfully keep up with the body''s steady oxygen demands.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Aerobic exercise, Cardio exercise, Moderate intensity exercise"}'::jsonb),
('mc:this type of exercise does not require oxygen.:::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'this type of exercise does not require oxygen.', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Anaerobic exercise literally means exercise \"without oxygen.\" During these short, high-intensity bursts of physical activity, the muscles demand energy much faster than the body can transport oxygen to them.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Anaerobic exercise\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Anaerobic exercise literally means exercise \"without oxygen.\" During these short, high-intensity bursts of physical activity, the muscles demand energy much faster than the body can transport oxygen to them.\n\nTo keep up with this extreme demand, the muscle cells rely entirely on immediate energy pathways that do not require oxygen, specifically the phosphagen (ATP-CP) system and anaerobic glycolysis (which breaks down glycogen and produces lactic acid). Examples include heavy weightlifting, sprinting, and high-intensity interval training (HIIT).\n\n• Cardio / Aerobic exercise: \"Aerobic\" literally means \"with oxygen.\" Cardio or aerobic activities (like jogging, cycling, or swimming) are sustained at a lower intensity, allowing the heart and lungs to continuously supply enough oxygen to the muscles to generate energy efficiently.\n• Moderate intensity exercise: Activities performed at a moderate intensity rely primarily on aerobic pathways because the cardiovascular system can successfully keep up with the body''s steady oxygen demands.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Aerobic exercise, Cardio exercise, Moderate intensity exercise"}'::jsonb),
('mc:a 35-year old filipina weighs 130lbs with a height measurement of 5 feet. what is her bmi?:::bmi 25.4, obese i', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'a 35-year old filipina weighs 130lbs with a height measurement of 5 feet. what is her bmi?', 'BMI 25.4, Obese I', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"In this question, apply the specific Asia-Pacific BMI Classification Guidelines used for Asian populations, which have lower cut-offs due to a higher risk of cardiovascular disease and type 2 diabetes at lower body sizes.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"BMI 25.4, Obese I\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"In this question, apply the specific Asia-Pacific BMI Classification Guidelines used for Asian populations, which have lower cut-offs due to a higher risk of cardiovascular disease and type 2 diabetes at lower body sizes.\n\n1. Calculate the BMI.\n• Convert weight: 130 lbs / 2.2 = 59.09 kg\n• Convert height: 5 feet = 60 inches = 1.524 meters\n• Calculate BMI:\nBMI = 59.09 / (1.524²) = 25.4 kg/m²\n\n2. The Asia-Pacific Classification \n• Underweight: Below 18.5 kg/m² (Risk of nutritional deficiency and osteoporosis)\n• Normal Range: 18.5 – 22.9 kg/m² (Low health risk)\n• Overweight (At Risk): 23.0 – 24.9 kg/m² (Moderate risk for conditions like type 2 diabetes and high blood pressure)\n• Obese Class I: 25.0 – 29.9 kg/m² (High metabolic and cardiovascular risk)\n• Obese Class II: ≥ 30.0 kg/m² (Very high risk)\n\nBecause a value of 25.4 crosses the 25.0 threshold, the patient is clinically classified as Obese Class I under the regional guidelines rather than just overweight.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: BMI 25.4, Overweight, BMI 23, Normal, BMI 23, Overweight"}'::jsonb),
('mc:a 35-year old filipina weighs 130lbs with a height measurement of 5 feet. what is her bmi?:::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'a 35-year old filipina weighs 130lbs with a height measurement of 5 feet. what is her bmi?', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"In this question, apply the specific Asia-Pacific BMI Classification Guidelines used for Asian populations, which have lower cut-offs due to a higher risk of cardiovascular disease and type 2 diabetes at lower body sizes.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"BMI 25.4, Obese I\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"In this question, apply the specific Asia-Pacific BMI Classification Guidelines used for Asian populations, which have lower cut-offs due to a higher risk of cardiovascular disease and type 2 diabetes at lower body sizes.\n\n1. Calculate the BMI.\n• Convert weight: 130 lbs / 2.2 = 59.09 kg\n• Convert height: 5 feet = 60 inches = 1.524 meters\n• Calculate BMI:\nBMI = 59.09 / (1.524²) = 25.4 kg/m²\n\n2. The Asia-Pacific Classification \n• Underweight: Below 18.5 kg/m² (Risk of nutritional deficiency and osteoporosis)\n• Normal Range: 18.5 – 22.9 kg/m² (Low health risk)\n• Overweight (At Risk): 23.0 – 24.9 kg/m² (Moderate risk for conditions like type 2 diabetes and high blood pressure)\n• Obese Class I: 25.0 – 29.9 kg/m² (High metabolic and cardiovascular risk)\n• Obese Class II: ≥ 30.0 kg/m² (Very high risk)\n\nBecause a value of 25.4 crosses the 25.0 threshold, the patient is clinically classified as Obese Class I under the regional guidelines rather than just overweight.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: BMI 25.4, Overweight, BMI 23, Normal, BMI 23, Overweight"}'::jsonb),
('mc:the hormone decreases appetite and increases the dissipation of energy through heat production also known as thermogenesis.:::leptin', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'the hormone decreases appetite and increases the dissipation of energy through heat production also known as thermogenesis.', 'Leptin', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Leptin is a peptide hormone produced and secreted by adipose tissue (fat cells) that acts directly on the hypothalamus in the brain. It functions as a long-term regulator of energy balance by decreasing appetite (inducing satiety) and simultaneously increasing energy expenditure.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Leptin\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Leptin is a peptide hormone produced and secreted by adipose tissue (fat cells) that acts directly on the hypothalamus in the brain. It functions as a long-term regulator of energy balance by decreasing appetite (inducing satiety) and simultaneously increasing energy expenditure.\n\nLeptin stimulates the sympathetic nervous system, which triggers the upregulation of uncoupling proteins (like UCP1) in brown adipose tissue. This process increases the dissipation of stored energy directly as heat (thermogenesis) rather than storing it as fat.\n\n• Cortisol: A glucocorticoid stress hormone that typically increases appetite, favors visceral fat storage, and slows down long-term thermogenic energy expenditure.\n• Ghrelin: Known as the \"hunger hormone,\" ghrelin is produced primarily by the stomach fundus. It increases appetite and promotes energy storage, which is the exact opposite of leptin.\n• Neuropeptide Y: A potent neurotransmitter in the brain that actively stimulates food intake (an orexigenic effect) and decreases energy expenditure.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Neuropeptide Y, Ghrelin, Cortisol"}'::jsonb),
('mc:the hormone decreases appetite and increases the dissipation of energy through heat production also known as thermogenesis.:::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'the hormone decreases appetite and increases the dissipation of energy through heat production also known as thermogenesis.', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Leptin is a peptide hormone produced and secreted by adipose tissue (fat cells) that acts directly on the hypothalamus in the brain. It functions as a long-term regulator of energy balance by decreasing appetite (inducing satiety) and simultaneously increasing energy expenditure.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Leptin\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Leptin is a peptide hormone produced and secreted by adipose tissue (fat cells) that acts directly on the hypothalamus in the brain. It functions as a long-term regulator of energy balance by decreasing appetite (inducing satiety) and simultaneously increasing energy expenditure.\n\nLeptin stimulates the sympathetic nervous system, which triggers the upregulation of uncoupling proteins (like UCP1) in brown adipose tissue. This process increases the dissipation of stored energy directly as heat (thermogenesis) rather than storing it as fat.\n\n• Cortisol: A glucocorticoid stress hormone that typically increases appetite, favors visceral fat storage, and slows down long-term thermogenic energy expenditure.\n• Ghrelin: Known as the \"hunger hormone,\" ghrelin is produced primarily by the stomach fundus. It increases appetite and promotes energy storage, which is the exact opposite of leptin.\n• Neuropeptide Y: A potent neurotransmitter in the brain that actively stimulates food intake (an orexigenic effect) and decreases energy expenditure.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Neuropeptide Y, Ghrelin, Cortisol"}'::jsonb),
('mc:the following are the criteria for anorexia nervosa except:::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'the following are the criteria for anorexia nervosa except', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Recurrently consuming large amounts of food in a discrete period of time (e.g., within a 2-hour window) accompanied by a perceived lack of control is the fundamental, defining characteristic of Bulimia Nervosa or Binge Eating Disorder. It does not serve as a baseline criterion for Anorexia Nervosa.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Recurrent episodes of binge eating within 2 hours\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Recurrently consuming large amounts of food in a discrete period of time (e.g., within a 2-hour window) accompanied by a perceived lack of control is the fundamental, defining characteristic of Bulimia Nervosa or Binge Eating Disorder. It does not serve as a baseline criterion for Anorexia Nervosa.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Intense fear of weight gain, Amenorrhea, Denial"}'::jsonb),
('mc:after a meal, our body’s energy expenditure increases for some time to breakdown the food into nutrients that the body can utilize and store. this process is known as:::thermogenic effect of food', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'after a meal, our body’s energy expenditure increases for some time to breakdown the food into nutrients that the body can utilize and store. this process is known as', 'Thermogenic Effect of Food', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"The Thermogenic Effect of Food (TEF)—also traditionally referred to as the Specific Dynamic Action (SDA) of food—is the exact physiological process where energy expenditure rises above baseline for several hours following a meal. This metabolic energy is spent on the mechanical and chemical work of digesting, absorbing, transporting, metabolizing, and storing nutrients.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Thermogenic Effect of Food\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"The Thermogenic Effect of Food (TEF)—also traditionally referred to as the Specific Dynamic Action (SDA) of food—is the exact physiological process where energy expenditure rises above baseline for several hours following a meal. This metabolic energy is spent on the mechanical and chemical work of digesting, absorbing, transporting, metabolizing, and storing nutrients.\n\nTEF accounts for roughly 10% of total daily energy expenditure in a standard diet. Protein triggers the highest thermic response (requiring 20–30% of its own energy to process), followed by carbohydrates (5–15%), and fats (0–3%).\n\n*While TEF is a form of thermogenesis, \"thermogenesis\" on its own is a wide category that also includes non-shivering thermogenesis (heat production from brown fat) and exercise-induced thermogenesis. Thermogenic Effect of Food (TEF) is the precise, targeted metabolic term for post-meal energy expenditure.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Metabolism, Digestion, Thermogenesis"}'::jsonb),
('mc:after a meal, our body’s energy expenditure increases for some time to breakdown the food into nutrients that the body can utilize and store. this process is known as:::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'after a meal, our body’s energy expenditure increases for some time to breakdown the food into nutrients that the body can utilize and store. this process is known as', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"The Thermogenic Effect of Food (TEF)—also traditionally referred to as the Specific Dynamic Action (SDA) of food—is the exact physiological process where energy expenditure rises above baseline for several hours following a meal. This metabolic energy is spent on the mechanical and chemical work of digesting, absorbing, transporting, metabolizing, and storing nutrients.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Thermogenic Effect of Food\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"The Thermogenic Effect of Food (TEF)—also traditionally referred to as the Specific Dynamic Action (SDA) of food—is the exact physiological process where energy expenditure rises above baseline for several hours following a meal. This metabolic energy is spent on the mechanical and chemical work of digesting, absorbing, transporting, metabolizing, and storing nutrients.\n\nTEF accounts for roughly 10% of total daily energy expenditure in a standard diet. Protein triggers the highest thermic response (requiring 20–30% of its own energy to process), followed by carbohydrates (5–15%), and fats (0–3%).\n\n*While TEF is a form of thermogenesis, \"thermogenesis\" on its own is a wide category that also includes non-shivering thermogenesis (heat production from brown fat) and exercise-induced thermogenesis. Thermogenic Effect of Food (TEF) is the precise, targeted metabolic term for post-meal energy expenditure.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Metabolism, Digestion, Thermogenesis"}'::jsonb),
('mc:abundant uncoupling proteins in the body prevents weight gain. which type of fat releases stored energy and is considered metabolically active?:::brown adipose tissue', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'abundant uncoupling proteins in the body prevents weight gain. which type of fat releases stored energy and is considered metabolically active?', 'Brown adipose tissue', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Brown adipose tissue (BAT) contains a very high density of mitochondria packed with Uncoupling Protein 1 (also known as thermogenin). This protein uncouples the respiratory chain from ATP synthesis, causing the fat tissue to burn through stored lipids and glucose to release stored energy directly as heat (non-shivering thermogenesis) rather than storing it as chemical energy.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Brown adipose tissue\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Brown adipose tissue (BAT) contains a very high density of mitochondria packed with Uncoupling Protein 1 (also known as thermogenin). This protein uncouples the respiratory chain from ATP synthesis, causing the fat tissue to burn through stored lipids and glucose to release stored energy directly as heat (non-shivering thermogenesis) rather than storing it as chemical energy.\n\nBecause BAT actively expends calories to generate heat, an abundance or activation of brown fat significantly increases basal metabolic rate and helps prevent weight gain and metabolic dysfunction.\n\n• Beige adipose tissue: While beige fat cells also express UCP1 and burn energy, they are individual brown-like cells that develop within white adipose tissue clusters (a process called \"browning\") rather than forming the distinct, highly abundant classic brown fat depots primarily responsible for massive thermogenic dissipation.\n• Yellow adipose tissue: This is simply an alternative structural name for standard adult white fat, colored yellow due to the accumulation of dietary carotenes.\n• White adipose tissue: The primary site for long-term energy storage (triglycerides). White fat is not built for heat generation and expands during weight gain rather than preventing it.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Beige adipose tissue, Yellow adipose tissue, White adipose tissue"}'::jsonb),
('mc:abundant uncoupling proteins in the body prevents weight gain. which type of fat releases stored energy and is considered metabolically active?:::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'abundant uncoupling proteins in the body prevents weight gain. which type of fat releases stored energy and is considered metabolically active?', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Brown adipose tissue (BAT) contains a very high density of mitochondria packed with Uncoupling Protein 1 (also known as thermogenin). This protein uncouples the respiratory chain from ATP synthesis, causing the fat tissue to burn through stored lipids and glucose to release stored energy directly as heat (non-shivering thermogenesis) rather than storing it as chemical energy.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Brown adipose tissue\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Brown adipose tissue (BAT) contains a very high density of mitochondria packed with Uncoupling Protein 1 (also known as thermogenin). This protein uncouples the respiratory chain from ATP synthesis, causing the fat tissue to burn through stored lipids and glucose to release stored energy directly as heat (non-shivering thermogenesis) rather than storing it as chemical energy.\n\nBecause BAT actively expends calories to generate heat, an abundance or activation of brown fat significantly increases basal metabolic rate and helps prevent weight gain and metabolic dysfunction.\n\n• Beige adipose tissue: While beige fat cells also express UCP1 and burn energy, they are individual brown-like cells that develop within white adipose tissue clusters (a process called \"browning\") rather than forming the distinct, highly abundant classic brown fat depots primarily responsible for massive thermogenic dissipation.\n• Yellow adipose tissue: This is simply an alternative structural name for standard adult white fat, colored yellow due to the accumulation of dietary carotenes.\n• White adipose tissue: The primary site for long-term energy storage (triglycerides). White fat is not built for heat generation and expands during weight gain rather than preventing it.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Beige adipose tissue, Yellow adipose tissue, White adipose tissue"}'::jsonb),
('mc:a dietitian needs to determine a patient’s risk for cardiovascular disease. the following anthropometric measurements must be assessed except:::exercise history', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'a dietitian needs to determine a patient’s risk for cardiovascular disease. the following anthropometric measurements must be assessed except', 'Exercise history', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Exercise history is a behavioral or lifestyle component of a patient''s personal history, not a physical bodily measurement.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Exercise history\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Exercise history is a behavioral or lifestyle component of a patient''s personal history, not a physical bodily measurement.\n\nAnthropometric measurements are non-invasive, quantitative, physical measurements used to assess the size, shape, and composition of the human body.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Height and weight, Waist Circumference, Body fat analysis"}'::jsonb),
('mc:a 45 years old female has a whr of 0.9 is:::all of the above', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'a 45 years old female has a whr of 0.9 is', 'All of the above', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"According to the World Health Organization (WHO), a Waist-to-Hip Ratio (WHR) above 0.85 for females signifies abdominal (visceral) obesity. A 45-year-old female presenting with a WHR of 0.9 falls into a high-risk classification (apple-shaped fat distribution).","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"All of the above\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"According to the World Health Organization (WHO), a Waist-to-Hip Ratio (WHR) above 0.85 for females signifies abdominal (visceral) obesity. A 45-year-old female presenting with a WHR of 0.9 falls into a high-risk classification (apple-shaped fat distribution).\n\nVisceral fat accumulation surrounding the internal organs is highly metabolically active. It releases excess free fatty acids and inflammatory cytokines directly into the portal circulation, precipitating:\n\t• Insulin resistance, which significantly increases the risk for Diabetes mellitus.\n\t• Altered hepatic lipid synthesis, leading directly to elevated blood lipids, specifically placing the patient at risk for high triglycerides and hypercholesterolemia.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: At risk for high triglycerides, At risk for hypercholesterolemia, At risk for diabetes mellitus"}'::jsonb),
('mc:a 60kg female patient with comorbidities needs to lose weight. what is the minimum target weight loss from baseline in 6 months?:::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'a 60kg female patient with comorbidities needs to lose weight. what is the minimum target weight loss from baseline in 6 months?', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"According to the World Health Organization (WHO) and international clinical obesity guidelines (such as those outlined by the American Heart Association (AHA), American College of Cardiology (ACC), and MIMS Philippines), the standard, primary therapeutic recommendation for an adult with obesity or overweight and comorbidities is to target a minimum initial weight loss of 5% of their baseline body weight over a 6-month period. Achieving a modest 5% reduction is highly significant because it immediately triggers measurable improvements in glycemic control, blood pressure, and cardiovascular risk factors.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"3kg\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"According to the World Health Organization (WHO) and international clinical obesity guidelines (such as those outlined by the American Heart Association (AHA), American College of Cardiology (ACC), and MIMS Philippines), the standard, primary therapeutic recommendation for an adult with obesity or overweight and comorbidities is to target a minimum initial weight loss of 5% of their baseline body weight over a 6-month period. Achieving a modest 5% reduction is highly significant because it immediately triggers measurable improvements in glycemic control, blood pressure, and cardiovascular risk factors.\n\nPatient Baseline Weight = 60 kg\nMinimum Clinical Target Percentage = 5% (0.05)\n\nMinimum Target Weight Loss = 60 kg x 0.05 = 3 kg","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: 6kg, 10kg, 5kg"}'::jsonb),
('mc:a 45 years old female has a whr of 0.9 is:::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'a 45 years old female has a whr of 0.9 is', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"According to the World Health Organization (WHO), a Waist-to-Hip Ratio (WHR) above 0.85 for females signifies abdominal (visceral) obesity. A 45-year-old female presenting with a WHR of 0.9 falls into a high-risk classification (apple-shaped fat distribution).","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"All of the above\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"According to the World Health Organization (WHO), a Waist-to-Hip Ratio (WHR) above 0.85 for females signifies abdominal (visceral) obesity. A 45-year-old female presenting with a WHR of 0.9 falls into a high-risk classification (apple-shaped fat distribution).\n\nVisceral fat accumulation surrounding the internal organs is highly metabolically active. It releases excess free fatty acids and inflammatory cytokines directly into the portal circulation, precipitating:\n\t• Insulin resistance, which significantly increases the risk for Diabetes mellitus.\n\t• Altered hepatic lipid synthesis, leading directly to elevated blood lipids, specifically placing the patient at risk for high triglycerides and hypercholesterolemia.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: At risk for high triglycerides, At risk for hypercholesterolemia, At risk for diabetes mellitus"}'::jsonb),
('mc:the following are comorbidities related to obesity except:::psoriasis', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'the following are comorbidities related to obesity except', 'Psoriasis', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Psoriasis is a primary autoimmune skin disease characterized by rapid skin cell buildup that leads to scaling and inflammation. While obesity is a strong independent risk factor that can aggravate or worsen psoriasis through shared systemic inflammatory pathways, psoriasis itself is classified primarily as an autoimmune/genetic condition rather than a direct mechanical or metabolic comorbidity caused by obesity.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Psoriasis\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Psoriasis is a primary autoimmune skin disease characterized by rapid skin cell buildup that leads to scaling and inflammation. While obesity is a strong independent risk factor that can aggravate or worsen psoriasis through shared systemic inflammatory pathways, psoriasis itself is classified primarily as an autoimmune/genetic condition rather than a direct mechanical or metabolic comorbidity caused by obesity.\n\n\t• Sleep apnea: Heavily driven by mechanical forces; excess fat deposits around the upper airway and neck physically compress the trachea during sleep, causing obstructive sleep apnea.\n\t• Osteoarthritis: Driven by both increased mechanical wear-and-tear on weight-bearing joints (such as knees and hips) and low-grade systemic inflammation that degrades cartilage.\n\t• Gallstones: Driven by metabolic shifts; obesity increases hepatic synthesis and secretion of cholesterol, supersaturating the bile and causing cholesterol gallstones to form.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Sleep apnea, Gallstones, Osteoarthritis"}'::jsonb),
('mc:the following are comorbidities related to obesity except:::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'the following are comorbidities related to obesity except', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Psoriasis is a primary autoimmune skin disease characterized by rapid skin cell buildup that leads to scaling and inflammation. While obesity is a strong independent risk factor that can aggravate or worsen psoriasis through shared systemic inflammatory pathways, psoriasis itself is classified primarily as an autoimmune/genetic condition rather than a direct mechanical or metabolic comorbidity caused by obesity.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Psoriasis\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Psoriasis is a primary autoimmune skin disease characterized by rapid skin cell buildup that leads to scaling and inflammation. While obesity is a strong independent risk factor that can aggravate or worsen psoriasis through shared systemic inflammatory pathways, psoriasis itself is classified primarily as an autoimmune/genetic condition rather than a direct mechanical or metabolic comorbidity caused by obesity.\n\n\t• Sleep apnea: Heavily driven by mechanical forces; excess fat deposits around the upper airway and neck physically compress the trachea during sleep, causing obstructive sleep apnea.\n\t• Osteoarthritis: Driven by both increased mechanical wear-and-tear on weight-bearing joints (such as knees and hips) and low-grade systemic inflammation that degrades cartilage.\n\t• Gallstones: Driven by metabolic shifts; obesity increases hepatic synthesis and secretion of cholesterol, supersaturating the bile and causing cholesterol gallstones to form.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Sleep apnea, Gallstones, Osteoarthritis"}'::jsonb),
('mc:choose the statement that best represents the patient’s readiness for lifestyle change:::“please recommend some tips on how to do grocery shopping for fresh produce.”', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'choose the statement that best represents the patient’s readiness for lifestyle change', '“Please recommend some tips on how to do grocery shopping for fresh produce.”', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"In behavioral counseling—specifically the Transtheoretical Model (Stages of Change)—this statement indicates that the patient has moved past merely thinking about a change and is actively preparing to execute it. By asking for specific, actionable grocery shopping strategies, they are demonstrating a high level of autonomous motivation and a direct readiness to implement lifestyle changes.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"“Please recommend some tips on how to do grocery shopping for fresh produce.”\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"In behavioral counseling—specifically the Transtheoretical Model (Stages of Change)—this statement indicates that the patient has moved past merely thinking about a change and is actively preparing to execute it. By asking for specific, actionable grocery shopping strategies, they are demonstrating a high level of autonomous motivation and a direct readiness to implement lifestyle changes.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: “I need to lose weight.”, “My doctor says I need to consult a dietitian.”, “How do I cook ginisang munggo?”"}'::jsonb),
