('mc:the following recommendations for weight loss must be given by the rnd except:::take orlistat 2 to 3 times a day with meals', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'the following recommendations for weight loss must be given by the rnd except', 'Take Orlistat 2 to 3 times a day with meals', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Orlistat is a potent anti-obesity pharmacological drug. Under the legal and professional scope of practice for a Registered Nutritionist-Dietitian (RND) in clinical and public health frameworks, a dietitian is strictly authorized to provide Medical Nutrition Therapy (MNT), structural caloric adjustments, lifestyle modifications, and behavioral counseling. An RND cannot prescribe medications or specify pharmaceutical drug dosages. The prescription, initiation, and dosing oversight of anti-obesity drugs like Orlistat must be managed exclusively by a licensed physician or endocrinologist.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Take Orlistat 2 to 3 times a day with meals\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Orlistat is a potent anti-obesity pharmacological drug. Under the legal and professional scope of practice for a Registered Nutritionist-Dietitian (RND) in clinical and public health frameworks, a dietitian is strictly authorized to provide Medical Nutrition Therapy (MNT), structural caloric adjustments, lifestyle modifications, and behavioral counseling. An RND cannot prescribe medications or specify pharmaceutical drug dosages. The prescription, initiation, and dosing oversight of anti-obesity drugs like Orlistat must be managed exclusively by a licensed physician or endocrinologist.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: At least 30 minutes of moderate physical activity daily, Decrease Total Energy Requirement by 500 Kcal per day, Self-monitoring"}'::jsonb),
('mc:the following recommendations for weight loss must be given by the rnd except:::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'the following recommendations for weight loss must be given by the rnd except', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Orlistat is a potent anti-obesity pharmacological drug. Under the legal and professional scope of practice for a Registered Nutritionist-Dietitian (RND) in clinical and public health frameworks, a dietitian is strictly authorized to provide Medical Nutrition Therapy (MNT), structural caloric adjustments, lifestyle modifications, and behavioral counseling. An RND cannot prescribe medications or specify pharmaceutical drug dosages. The prescription, initiation, and dosing oversight of anti-obesity drugs like Orlistat must be managed exclusively by a licensed physician or endocrinologist.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Take Orlistat 2 to 3 times a day with meals\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Orlistat is a potent anti-obesity pharmacological drug. Under the legal and professional scope of practice for a Registered Nutritionist-Dietitian (RND) in clinical and public health frameworks, a dietitian is strictly authorized to provide Medical Nutrition Therapy (MNT), structural caloric adjustments, lifestyle modifications, and behavioral counseling. An RND cannot prescribe medications or specify pharmaceutical drug dosages. The prescription, initiation, and dosing oversight of anti-obesity drugs like Orlistat must be managed exclusively by a licensed physician or endocrinologist.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: At least 30 minutes of moderate physical activity daily, Decrease Total Energy Requirement by 500 Kcal per day, Self-monitoring"}'::jsonb),
('mc:subs are::::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'subs are:', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Subs are sandwiches also known as hoagies or heroes, depending on regional terminology. Therefore all the listed descriptions apply.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"all of the above\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Subs are sandwiches also known as hoagies or heroes, depending on regional terminology. Therefore all the listed descriptions apply.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Hoagies, Heros, Sandwiches"}'::jsonb),
('mc:which weight loss medications are approved by the us-fda?:::phentermine, orlistat, topamirate', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'which weight loss medications are approved by the us-fda?', 'Phentermine, orlistat, topamirate', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"There are well-established medications officially approved by the US Food and Drug Administration (FDA) for weight management:","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Phentermine, orlistat, topamirate\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"There are well-established medications officially approved by the US Food and Drug Administration (FDA) for weight management:\n\t• Phentermine: Approved as a short-term, standalone appetite suppressant.\n\t• Orlistat: Approved for long-term weight management (available as prescription Xenical or over-the-counter Alli) by blocking intestinal fat absorption.\n\t• Topiramate: Approved by the FDA as a fixed-dose combination pill with phentermine (marketed under the brand name Qsymia) for chronic weight management.\n\nOther Choices:\n• Phenylpropanolamine: This drug was completely banned and ordered removed from the market by the FDA due to an increased risk of hemorrhagic stroke.\n• Sibutramine: Originally approved for weight loss under the name Meridia, it was completely withdrawn from the market due to its association with an increased risk of heart attacks and strokes.\n• Ibuprofen: This is a standard nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drug (NSAID) used to relieve pain and reduce fever, carrying no clinical indication or approval for weight loss.\n• Aminorex: An amphetamine-like stimulant that was never FDA-approved for weight loss and was withdrawn globally after it was found to cause severe pulmonary hypertension.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Phentermine, phenylpropanolamine, orlistat, Phenylpropanolamine, topamirate, sibutramine, Ibuprofen, aminorex, orlistat"}'::jsonb),
('mc:which weight loss medications are approved by the us-fda?:::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'which weight loss medications are approved by the us-fda?', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"There are well-established medications officially approved by the US Food and Drug Administration (FDA) for weight management:","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Phentermine, orlistat, topamirate\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"There are well-established medications officially approved by the US Food and Drug Administration (FDA) for weight management:\n\t• Phentermine: Approved as a short-term, standalone appetite suppressant.\n\t• Orlistat: Approved for long-term weight management (available as prescription Xenical or over-the-counter Alli) by blocking intestinal fat absorption.\n\t• Topiramate: Approved by the FDA as a fixed-dose combination pill with phentermine (marketed under the brand name Qsymia) for chronic weight management.\n\nOther Choices:\n• Phenylpropanolamine: This drug was completely banned and ordered removed from the market by the FDA due to an increased risk of hemorrhagic stroke.\n• Sibutramine: Originally approved for weight loss under the name Meridia, it was completely withdrawn from the market due to its association with an increased risk of heart attacks and strokes.\n• Ibuprofen: This is a standard nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drug (NSAID) used to relieve pain and reduce fever, carrying no clinical indication or approval for weight loss.\n• Aminorex: An amphetamine-like stimulant that was never FDA-approved for weight loss and was withdrawn globally after it was found to cause severe pulmonary hypertension.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Phentermine, phenylpropanolamine, orlistat, Phenylpropanolamine, topamirate, sibutramine, Ibuprofen, aminorex, orlistat"}'::jsonb),
('mc:the one animal polysaccharide that is not present in foods but is generally stored in the liver and muscle::::glycogen', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'the one animal polysaccharide that is not present in foods but is generally stored in the liver and muscle:', 'Glycogen', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Glycogen is the storage form of carbohydrates in animals.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Glycogen\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Glycogen is the storage form of carbohydrates in animals.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Lignin, Polymer, Lecithin"}'::jsonb),
('mc:criteria for bariatric surgery::::all of the above', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'criteria for bariatric surgery:', 'All of the above', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Historically, candidate selection for metabolic and bariatric surgery has been anchored on established body size and health criteria.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"All of the above\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Historically, candidate selection for metabolic and bariatric surgery has been anchored on established body size and health criteria.\n\t• A BMI between 35 and 39.9 with significant medical comorbidities (such as type 2 diabetes, severe sleep apnea, or cardiovascular disease) serves as a primary qualification parameter.\n\t• A BMI of 40 or greater represents severe obesity and satisfies the selection criteria independently, with or without secondary medical complications.\n\t• A BMI of 36 paired with Atherosclerosis fits precisely into the definition of the other option (a BMI above 35 accompanied by a serious cardiovascular comorbidity).\n\nClinical Update\n\nWhile older textbook questions focus heavily on this classic \"35 with comorbidities / 40 baseline\" framework, updated international surgical consensus guidelines from the American Society for Metabolic and Bariatric Surgery (ASMBS) and IFSO have expanded eligibility. Modern criteria now recommend bariatric surgery for anyone with a BMI over 35 regardless of comorbidities, and down to a BMI of 30 for individuals struggling with metabolic diseases like uncontrolled type 2 diabetes.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: BMI greater than 40, BMI greater than 35 with comorbidities, BMI 36 with Atherosclerosis"}'::jsonb),
('mc:criteria for bariatric surgery::::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'criteria for bariatric surgery:', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Historically, candidate selection for metabolic and bariatric surgery has been anchored on established body size and health criteria.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"All of the above\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Historically, candidate selection for metabolic and bariatric surgery has been anchored on established body size and health criteria.\n\t• A BMI between 35 and 39.9 with significant medical comorbidities (such as type 2 diabetes, severe sleep apnea, or cardiovascular disease) serves as a primary qualification parameter.\n\t• A BMI of 40 or greater represents severe obesity and satisfies the selection criteria independently, with or without secondary medical complications.\n\t• A BMI of 36 paired with Atherosclerosis fits precisely into the definition of the other option (a BMI above 35 accompanied by a serious cardiovascular comorbidity).\n\nClinical Update\n\nWhile older textbook questions focus heavily on this classic \"35 with comorbidities / 40 baseline\" framework, updated international surgical consensus guidelines from the American Society for Metabolic and Bariatric Surgery (ASMBS) and IFSO have expanded eligibility. Modern criteria now recommend bariatric surgery for anyone with a BMI over 35 regardless of comorbidities, and down to a BMI of 30 for individuals struggling with metabolic diseases like uncontrolled type 2 diabetes.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: BMI greater than 40, BMI greater than 35 with comorbidities, BMI 36 with Atherosclerosis"}'::jsonb),
('mc:this type of bariatric surgery requires the placement of a foreign object in the form of a band:::lagb', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'this type of bariatric surgery requires the placement of a foreign object in the form of a band', 'LAGB', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"LAGB stands for Laparoscopic Adjustable Gastric Banding (commonly referred to by its commercial name, the Lap-Band).","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"LAGB\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"LAGB stands for Laparoscopic Adjustable Gastric Banding (commonly referred to by its commercial name, the Lap-Band).\n\nThis specific bariatric procedure involves placing an inflatable, medical-grade silicone band entirely around the upper portion of the stomach. This creates a tiny stomach pouch that drastically restricts the volume of food a patient can eat at one time, forcing early satiety without removing any actual gastric tissue.\n\n• Roux-en-Y Gastric Bypass: A permanent, combination surgery where the stomach is stapled to create a small pouch and the small intestine is physically rerouted, requiring no baseline foreign bands.\n• BPD/DS: Biliopancreatic Diversion with Duodenal Switch is a complex, mostly malabsorptive surgery that resects a large portion of the stomach and drastically bypasses the small intestine.\n• Gastric Restrictive Procedure: This is the general umbrella category term for any surgery that limits stomach capacity (including the sleeve and vertical banded gastroplasty), whereas LAGB is the specific, targeted procedure abbreviation requested by the question.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Roux-en-Y Gastric Bypass, Gastric Restrictive Procedure, BPD/DS"}'::jsonb),
('mc:this type of bariatric surgery requires the placement of a foreign object in the form of a band:::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'this type of bariatric surgery requires the placement of a foreign object in the form of a band', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"LAGB stands for Laparoscopic Adjustable Gastric Banding (commonly referred to by its commercial name, the Lap-Band).","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"LAGB\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"LAGB stands for Laparoscopic Adjustable Gastric Banding (commonly referred to by its commercial name, the Lap-Band).\n\nThis specific bariatric procedure involves placing an inflatable, medical-grade silicone band entirely around the upper portion of the stomach. This creates a tiny stomach pouch that drastically restricts the volume of food a patient can eat at one time, forcing early satiety without removing any actual gastric tissue.\n\n• Roux-en-Y Gastric Bypass: A permanent, combination surgery where the stomach is stapled to create a small pouch and the small intestine is physically rerouted, requiring no baseline foreign bands.\n• BPD/DS: Biliopancreatic Diversion with Duodenal Switch is a complex, mostly malabsorptive surgery that resects a large portion of the stomach and drastically bypasses the small intestine.\n• Gastric Restrictive Procedure: This is the general umbrella category term for any surgery that limits stomach capacity (including the sleeve and vertical banded gastroplasty), whereas LAGB is the specific, targeted procedure abbreviation requested by the question.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Roux-en-Y Gastric Bypass, Gastric Restrictive Procedure, BPD/DS"}'::jsonb),
('mc:combination procedure in bariatric surgery results to hormonal influences. the production of ghrelin hormone is likewise decreased resulting to:::induced satiety', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'combination procedure in bariatric surgery results to hormonal influences. the production of ghrelin hormone is likewise decreased resulting to', 'Induced satiety', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Ghrelin is known as the orexigenic hormone (hunger hormone) that stimulates appetite and food intake. Because bariatric procedures (such as a sleeve gastrectomy or gastric bypass) involve anatomical resection or exclusion of the gastric fundus—where the vast majority of ghrelin is produced—the circulating levels of this hormone drop sharply post-surgery.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Induced satiety\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Ghrelin is known as the orexigenic hormone (hunger hormone) that stimulates appetite and food intake. Because bariatric procedures (such as a sleeve gastrectomy or gastric bypass) involve anatomical resection or exclusion of the gastric fundus—where the vast majority of ghrelin is produced—the circulating levels of this hormone drop sharply post-surgery.\n\nA significant reduction in ghrelin eliminates the biological signal that triggers hunger, thereby inducing satiety (prolonged feelings of fullness and diminished appetite).","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Increased appetite, Orexigenic effect, Dumping syndrome"}'::jsonb),
('mc:combination procedure in bariatric surgery results to hormonal influences. the production of ghrelin hormone is likewise decreased resulting to:::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'combination procedure in bariatric surgery results to hormonal influences. the production of ghrelin hormone is likewise decreased resulting to', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Ghrelin is known as the orexigenic hormone (hunger hormone) that stimulates appetite and food intake. Because bariatric procedures (such as a sleeve gastrectomy or gastric bypass) involve anatomical resection or exclusion of the gastric fundus—where the vast majority of ghrelin is produced—the circulating levels of this hormone drop sharply post-surgery.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Induced satiety\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Ghrelin is known as the orexigenic hormone (hunger hormone) that stimulates appetite and food intake. Because bariatric procedures (such as a sleeve gastrectomy or gastric bypass) involve anatomical resection or exclusion of the gastric fundus—where the vast majority of ghrelin is produced—the circulating levels of this hormone drop sharply post-surgery.\n\nA significant reduction in ghrelin eliminates the biological signal that triggers hunger, thereby inducing satiety (prolonged feelings of fullness and diminished appetite).","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Increased appetite, Orexigenic effect, Dumping syndrome"}'::jsonb),
('mc:which type of bariatric surgery can possibly exacerbate symptoms of gastroesophageal reflux?:::sleeve gastrectomy', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'which type of bariatric surgery can possibly exacerbate symptoms of gastroesophageal reflux?', 'Sleeve Gastrectomy', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"A Sleeve Gastrectomy (or vertical sleeve gastrectomy) involves removing approximately 80% of the stomach, converting the remaining portion into a narrow, tube-like structure. This anatomical change drastically increases internal pressure within the stomach tube.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Sleeve Gastrectomy\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"A Sleeve Gastrectomy (or vertical sleeve gastrectomy) involves removing approximately 80% of the stomach, converting the remaining portion into a narrow, tube-like structure. This anatomical change drastically increases internal pressure within the stomach tube.\n\nThe high intragastric pressure, paired with changes to the angle where the esophagus meets the stomach (the angle of His) and potential weakening of the lower esophageal sphincter (LES), commonly causes de novo (new-onset) acid reflux or severely exacerbates preexisting symptoms of Gastroesophageal Reflux Disease (GERD).\n\n• Roux-en-Y Gastric Bypass: This is considered the gold-standard bariatric procedure for patients with severe GERD. Because it separates the acid-producing cells into a bypassed pouch and creates a low-pressure drainage system, it actively cures or significantly improves reflux symptoms. It is frequently used as a corrective surgery for sleeve patients who develop uncontrollable reflux.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Laparoscopic Adjustable Gastric Banding, Roux-en-Y Gastric Bypass, Biliopancreatic Diversion with Duodenal Switch"}'::jsonb),
('mc:which type of bariatric surgery can possibly exacerbate symptoms of gastroesophageal reflux?:::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'which type of bariatric surgery can possibly exacerbate symptoms of gastroesophageal reflux?', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"A Sleeve Gastrectomy (or vertical sleeve gastrectomy) involves removing approximately 80% of the stomach, converting the remaining portion into a narrow, tube-like structure. This anatomical change drastically increases internal pressure within the stomach tube.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Sleeve Gastrectomy\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"A Sleeve Gastrectomy (or vertical sleeve gastrectomy) involves removing approximately 80% of the stomach, converting the remaining portion into a narrow, tube-like structure. This anatomical change drastically increases internal pressure within the stomach tube.\n\nThe high intragastric pressure, paired with changes to the angle where the esophagus meets the stomach (the angle of His) and potential weakening of the lower esophageal sphincter (LES), commonly causes de novo (new-onset) acid reflux or severely exacerbates preexisting symptoms of Gastroesophageal Reflux Disease (GERD).\n\n• Roux-en-Y Gastric Bypass: This is considered the gold-standard bariatric procedure for patients with severe GERD. Because it separates the acid-producing cells into a bypassed pouch and creates a low-pressure drainage system, it actively cures or significantly improves reflux symptoms. It is frequently used as a corrective surgery for sleeve patients who develop uncontrollable reflux.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Laparoscopic Adjustable Gastric Banding, Roux-en-Y Gastric Bypass, Biliopancreatic Diversion with Duodenal Switch"}'::jsonb),
('mc:the normal range of body temperature is:::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'the normal range of body temperature is', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"While 37°C (98.6°F) is universally recognized as the standard average human body temperature, normal physiological baselines safely fluctuate within a broader dynamic range.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"36.1 to 37.2 degrees Celsius\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"While 37°C (98.6°F) is universally recognized as the standard average human body temperature, normal physiological baselines safely fluctuate within a broader dynamic range.\n\nThe medically accepted range of 97°F to 99°F directly converts to 36.1°C to 37.2°C. Temperatures within this bracket are considered afebrile (normal and without fever).","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: 35 to 37 degrees Celsius, 35.5 to 37.4 degrees Celsius, 36 to 36.8 degrees Celsius"}'::jsonb)
ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO public.ai_explanation_cache (id, question_hash, front_text, back_text, options, explanation, breakdown, created_at, question_text, user_answer, is_correct, ai_explanation) VALUES
('mc:the biliopancreatic diversion with duodenal switch procedure results to some nutrition-related conditions. choose one that does not apply.:::indigestion and bloating', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'the biliopancreatic diversion with duodenal switch procedure results to some nutrition-related conditions. choose one that does not apply.', 'Indigestion and bloating', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Indigestion and bloating are general, functional upper-GI symptoms. Since the pyloric valve is fully preserved during a Duodenal Switch procedure, normal gastric emptying and stomach digestion are maintained, making classic upper indigestion an inaccurate description of the operation''s hallmark downstream complications.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Indigestion and bloating\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Indigestion and bloating are general, functional upper-GI symptoms. Since the pyloric valve is fully preserved during a Duodenal Switch procedure, normal gastric emptying and stomach digestion are maintained, making classic upper indigestion an inaccurate description of the operation''s hallmark downstream complications.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Severe malabsorption of protein, Severe malabsorption of macronutrients and micronutrients, Changes in gut hormones to reduce appetite"}'::jsonb),
('mc:the biliopancreatic diversion with duodenal switch procedure results to some nutrition-related conditions. choose one that does not apply.:::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'the biliopancreatic diversion with duodenal switch procedure results to some nutrition-related conditions. choose one that does not apply.', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Indigestion and bloating are general, functional upper-GI symptoms. Since the pyloric valve is fully preserved during a Duodenal Switch procedure, normal gastric emptying and stomach digestion are maintained, making classic upper indigestion an inaccurate description of the operation''s hallmark downstream complications.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Indigestion and bloating\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Indigestion and bloating are general, functional upper-GI symptoms. Since the pyloric valve is fully preserved during a Duodenal Switch procedure, normal gastric emptying and stomach digestion are maintained, making classic upper indigestion an inaccurate description of the operation''s hallmark downstream complications.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Severe malabsorption of protein, Severe malabsorption of macronutrients and micronutrients, Changes in gut hormones to reduce appetite"}'::jsonb),
('mc:the following are the criteria for anorexia nervosa except:::recurrent episodes of binge eating within 2 hours', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'the following are the criteria for anorexia nervosa except', 'Recurrent episodes of binge eating within 2 hours', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Recurrently consuming large amounts of food in a discrete period of time (e.g., within a 2-hour window) accompanied by a perceived lack of control is the fundamental, defining characteristic of Bulimia Nervosa or Binge Eating Disorder. It does not serve as a baseline criterion for Anorexia Nervosa.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Recurrent episodes of binge eating within 2 hours\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Recurrently consuming large amounts of food in a discrete period of time (e.g., within a 2-hour window) accompanied by a perceived lack of control is the fundamental, defining characteristic of Bulimia Nervosa or Binge Eating Disorder. It does not serve as a baseline criterion for Anorexia Nervosa.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Intense fear of weight gain, Amenorrhea, Denial"}'::jsonb),
('mc:a patient suffering from bulimia nervosa is at risk of colon injury due to:::laxative abuse', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'a patient suffering from bulimia nervosa is at risk of colon injury due to', 'Laxative abuse', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Individuals with Bulimia Nervosa frequently misuse stimulant laxatives to control weight after a binge eating episode. Chronic laxative abuse directly injures the lining of the large intestine and damages the enteric nerves controlling bowel motility.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Laxative abuse\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Individuals with Bulimia Nervosa frequently misuse stimulant laxatives to control weight after a binge eating episode. Chronic laxative abuse directly injures the lining of the large intestine and damages the enteric nerves controlling bowel motility.\n\nThis toxic damage can progress to a severe, structurally irreversible clinical condition called cathartic colon syndrome. In cathartic colon, the large intestine loses its neuromuscular tone, becomes massively dilated, and can no longer propel stool on its own, increasing the risk of chronic constipation, fecal impaction, and even colon perforation.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Purging, Binge eating episodes, Lack of fiber intake"}'::jsonb),
('mc:a patient suffering from bulimia nervosa is at risk of colon injury due to:::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'a patient suffering from bulimia nervosa is at risk of colon injury due to', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Individuals with Bulimia Nervosa frequently misuse stimulant laxatives to control weight after a binge eating episode. Chronic laxative abuse directly injures the lining of the large intestine and damages the enteric nerves controlling bowel motility.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Laxative abuse\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Individuals with Bulimia Nervosa frequently misuse stimulant laxatives to control weight after a binge eating episode. Chronic laxative abuse directly injures the lining of the large intestine and damages the enteric nerves controlling bowel motility.\n\nThis toxic damage can progress to a severe, structurally irreversible clinical condition called cathartic colon syndrome. In cathartic colon, the large intestine loses its neuromuscular tone, becomes massively dilated, and can no longer propel stool on its own, increasing the risk of chronic constipation, fecal impaction, and even colon perforation.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Purging, Binge eating episodes, Lack of fiber intake"}'::jsonb),
('mc:when counseling a patient with bulimia nervosa the following must be encouraged except:::suggest foods for binge eating episode', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'when counseling a patient with bulimia nervosa the following must be encouraged except', 'Suggest foods for binge eating episode', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"In clinical psychological and nutritional counseling for Bulimia Nervosa, a Registered Nutritionist-Dietitian (RND) must never suggest, plan, or provide food options for a binge eating episode. The primary goal of Medical Nutrition Therapy (MNT) is to break the restrict-binge-purge cycle. Actively giving food ideas for a binge would validate and reinforce the disordered behavior.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Suggest foods for binge eating episode\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"In clinical psychological and nutritional counseling for Bulimia Nervosa, a Registered Nutritionist-Dietitian (RND) must never suggest, plan, or provide food options for a binge eating episode. The primary goal of Medical Nutrition Therapy (MNT) is to break the restrict-binge-purge cycle. Actively giving food ideas for a binge would validate and reinforce the disordered behavior.\n\n\t• Identify food triggers: Pinpointing which foods, emotions, or social scenarios spark an overwhelming urge to binge helps the patient develop healthy, non-food coping mechanisms.\n\t• Personalize eating pattern: Establishing a highly structured, individualized meal plan (such as eating 3 regular meals and 2–3 snacks at predictable times) keeps the patient physically satisfied, preventing the extreme biological hunger that drives binging.\n\t• Embrace all food and achieve comfort: Dietitians work to dismantle the toxic \"good food vs. bad food\" mentality common in bulimia. Helping a patient comfortably reintroduce former fear foods without guilt reduces the psychological drive to binge on them.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Identify food triggers, Embrace all food and achieve comfort, Personalize eating pattern"}'::jsonb),
('mc:when counseling a patient with bulimia nervosa the following must be encouraged except:::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'when counseling a patient with bulimia nervosa the following must be encouraged except', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"In clinical psychological and nutritional counseling for Bulimia Nervosa, a Registered Nutritionist-Dietitian (RND) must never suggest, plan, or provide food options for a binge eating episode. The primary goal of Medical Nutrition Therapy (MNT) is to break the restrict-binge-purge cycle. Actively giving food ideas for a binge would validate and reinforce the disordered behavior.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Suggest foods for binge eating episode\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"In clinical psychological and nutritional counseling for Bulimia Nervosa, a Registered Nutritionist-Dietitian (RND) must never suggest, plan, or provide food options for a binge eating episode. The primary goal of Medical Nutrition Therapy (MNT) is to break the restrict-binge-purge cycle. Actively giving food ideas for a binge would validate and reinforce the disordered behavior.\n\n\t• Identify food triggers: Pinpointing which foods, emotions, or social scenarios spark an overwhelming urge to binge helps the patient develop healthy, non-food coping mechanisms.\n\t• Personalize eating pattern: Establishing a highly structured, individualized meal plan (such as eating 3 regular meals and 2–3 snacks at predictable times) keeps the patient physically satisfied, preventing the extreme biological hunger that drives binging.\n\t• Embrace all food and achieve comfort: Dietitians work to dismantle the toxic \"good food vs. bad food\" mentality common in bulimia. Helping a patient comfortably reintroduce former fear foods without guilt reduces the psychological drive to binge on them.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Identify food triggers, Embrace all food and achieve comfort, Personalize eating pattern"}'::jsonb),
('mc:to ensure food safety, keeping food between 45 to 145 degrees fahrenheit or 7.2 to 62.7 degrees celsius must not exceed:::4 hours', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'to ensure food safety, keeping food between 45 to 145 degrees fahrenheit or 7.2 to 62.7 degrees celsius must not exceed', '4 hours', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Leaving perishable food items within this unsafe span (45°F to 145°F / 7.2°C to 62.7°C) for longer than 4 hours permits rapid, exponential multiplication of pathogenic bacteria (such as Salmonella and Staphylococcus aureus), making the food highly toxic and unsafe for human consumption.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"4 hours\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Leaving perishable food items within this unsafe span (45°F to 145°F / 7.2°C to 62.7°C) for longer than 4 hours permits rapid, exponential multiplication of pathogenic bacteria (such as Salmonella and Staphylococcus aureus), making the food highly toxic and unsafe for human consumption.\n\n1. The Safe Time Window (Under 2 hours): When potentially hazardous food is kept in the Temperature Danger Zone (45°F to 145°F / 7.2°C to 62.7°C) for less than 2 hours, it is still safe to serve, eat, or securely put back into the refrigerator to use later.\n2. The Point of No Return (2 to 4 hours): Once food sits out past the 2-hour mark, it enters a dangerous gray zone. While it is still safe to consume immediately, it can no longer be legally or safely put back in the refrigerator because bacteria have entered their rapid, exponential growth phase.\n\n• Under 2 hours: Safe to consume or re-refrigerate.\n• 2 to 4 hours: Safe to eat only if consumed immediately; do not put it back in the fridge.\n• Over 4 hours: Absolute mandatory discard.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: 2 hours, 3 hours, 8 hours"}'::jsonb),
('mc:a 16 year-old patient with normal weight for height and age verbalizes that she wants to die because she is very fat. she believes that people are always staring at her because she is ugly. what should the rnd do?:::refer to a psychiatrist immediately', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'a 16 year-old patient with normal weight for height and age verbalizes that she wants to die because she is very fat. she believes that people are always staring at her because she is ugly. what should the rnd do?', 'Refer to a psychiatrist immediately', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"The patient has explicitly verbalized an active intent to die (\"wants to die\"). In any clinical setting, an explicit statement of suicidal ideation is a critical psychiatric emergency that completely overrides standard dietary counseling. The immediate, non-negotiable step is to ensure patient safety by initiating an immediate referral to a psychiatrist or mental health crisis professional.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Refer to a psychiatrist immediately\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"The patient has explicitly verbalized an active intent to die (\"wants to die\"). In any clinical setting, an explicit statement of suicidal ideation is a critical psychiatric emergency that completely overrides standard dietary counseling. The immediate, non-negotiable step is to ensure patient safety by initiating an immediate referral to a psychiatrist or mental health crisis professional.\n\nThe patient''s severe body image distortion (believing she is \"very fat\" despite a normal weight-for-height profile) combined with ideas of reference (believing \"people are always staring at her\") are severe psychological indicators highly consistent with severe eating disorders (such as Anorexia Nervosa) or Body Dysmorphic Disorder (BDD). These conditions require specialized psychiatric stabilization before any structured nutritional therapy can be effective.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Tell the patient that she is not overweight, Tell the patient that she is experiencing delusions, Give nutrition education"}'::jsonb),
('mc:to ensure food safety, keeping food between 45 to 145 degrees fahrenheit or 7.2 to 62.7 degrees celsius must not exceed:::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'to ensure food safety, keeping food between 45 to 145 degrees fahrenheit or 7.2 to 62.7 degrees celsius must not exceed', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Leaving perishable food items within this unsafe span (45°F to 145°F / 7.2°C to 62.7°C) for longer than 4 hours permits rapid, exponential multiplication of pathogenic bacteria (such as Salmonella and Staphylococcus aureus), making the food highly toxic and unsafe for human consumption.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"4 hours\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Leaving perishable food items within this unsafe span (45°F to 145°F / 7.2°C to 62.7°C) for longer than 4 hours permits rapid, exponential multiplication of pathogenic bacteria (such as Salmonella and Staphylococcus aureus), making the food highly toxic and unsafe for human consumption.\n\n1. The Safe Time Window (Under 2 hours): When potentially hazardous food is kept in the Temperature Danger Zone (45°F to 145°F / 7.2°C to 62.7°C) for less than 2 hours, it is still safe to serve, eat, or securely put back into the refrigerator to use later.\n2. The Point of No Return (2 to 4 hours): Once food sits out past the 2-hour mark, it enters a dangerous gray zone. While it is still safe to consume immediately, it can no longer be legally or safely put back in the refrigerator because bacteria have entered their rapid, exponential growth phase.\n\n• Under 2 hours: Safe to consume or re-refrigerate.\n• 2 to 4 hours: Safe to eat only if consumed immediately; do not put it back in the fridge.\n• Over 4 hours: Absolute mandatory discard.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: 2 hours, 3 hours, 8 hours"}'::jsonb),
('mc:a 16 year-old patient with normal weight for height and age verbalizes that she wants to die because she is very fat. she believes that people are always staring at her because she is ugly. what should the rnd do?:::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'a 16 year-old patient with normal weight for height and age verbalizes that she wants to die because she is very fat. she believes that people are always staring at her because she is ugly. what should the rnd do?', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"The patient has explicitly verbalized an active intent to die (\"wants to die\"). In any clinical setting, an explicit statement of suicidal ideation is a critical psychiatric emergency that completely overrides standard dietary counseling. The immediate, non-negotiable step is to ensure patient safety by initiating an immediate referral to a psychiatrist or mental health crisis professional.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Refer to a psychiatrist immediately\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"The patient has explicitly verbalized an active intent to die (\"wants to die\"). In any clinical setting, an explicit statement of suicidal ideation is a critical psychiatric emergency that completely overrides standard dietary counseling. The immediate, non-negotiable step is to ensure patient safety by initiating an immediate referral to a psychiatrist or mental health crisis professional.\n\nThe patient''s severe body image distortion (believing she is \"very fat\" despite a normal weight-for-height profile) combined with ideas of reference (believing \"people are always staring at her\") are severe psychological indicators highly consistent with severe eating disorders (such as Anorexia Nervosa) or Body Dysmorphic Disorder (BDD). These conditions require specialized psychiatric stabilization before any structured nutritional therapy can be effective.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Tell the patient that she is not overweight, Tell the patient that she is experiencing delusions, Give nutrition education"}'::jsonb),
('mc:a patient manifesting the following symptoms has a diagnosis of symptoms: repeated chewing and spitting of food, bingeing and purging once a week for 3 weeks, exercising 3 hours daily:::eating disorders not otherwise specified (ednos)', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'a patient manifesting the following symptoms has a diagnosis of symptoms: repeated chewing and spitting of food, bingeing and purging once a week for 3 weeks, exercising 3 hours daily', 'Eating Disorders Not Otherwise Specified (EDNOS)', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"To meet the strict, formal diagnostic criteria for Bulimia Nervosa, a patient must engage in binge eating and inappropriate compensatory behaviors (purging or excessive exercise) at an average frequency of at least once a week for at least 3 months.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Eating Disorders Not Otherwise Specified (EDNOS)\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"To meet the strict, formal diagnostic criteria for Bulimia Nervosa, a patient must engage in binge eating and inappropriate compensatory behaviors (purging or excessive exercise) at an average frequency of at least once a week for at least 3 months.\n\nBecause this patient''s binge/purge episodes have only been occurring for 3 weeks (subthreshold duration), they do not meet the full, standard timeline for Bulimia Nervosa. Under standard board exam terminology, cases that display clinically significant disordered eating patterns but fall short of the exact diagnostic durations or specific criteria are categorized under EDNOS (Eating Disorders Not Otherwise Specified). (Note: In modern clinical DSM-5 manuals, this category is updated as OSFED—Other Specified Feeding and Eating Disorder).\n\nThe inclusion of repeated chewing and spitting of food is another classic, textbook hallmark behavior frequently categorized under the EDNOS/OSFED umbrella rather than standing as its own distinct standalone diagnosis.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Bulimia Nervosa, Anorexia Nervosa, Body Image Distortion"}'::jsonb),
('mc:a patient manifesting the following symptoms has a diagnosis of symptoms: repeated chewing and spitting of food, bingeing and purging once a week for 3 weeks, exercising 3 hours daily:::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'a patient manifesting the following symptoms has a diagnosis of symptoms: repeated chewing and spitting of food, bingeing and purging once a week for 3 weeks, exercising 3 hours daily', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"To meet the strict, formal diagnostic criteria for Bulimia Nervosa, a patient must engage in binge eating and inappropriate compensatory behaviors (purging or excessive exercise) at an average frequency of at least once a week for at least 3 months.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Eating Disorders Not Otherwise Specified (EDNOS)\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"To meet the strict, formal diagnostic criteria for Bulimia Nervosa, a patient must engage in binge eating and inappropriate compensatory behaviors (purging or excessive exercise) at an average frequency of at least once a week for at least 3 months.\n\nBecause this patient''s binge/purge episodes have only been occurring for 3 weeks (subthreshold duration), they do not meet the full, standard timeline for Bulimia Nervosa. Under standard board exam terminology, cases that display clinically significant disordered eating patterns but fall short of the exact diagnostic durations or specific criteria are categorized under EDNOS (Eating Disorders Not Otherwise Specified). (Note: In modern clinical DSM-5 manuals, this category is updated as OSFED—Other Specified Feeding and Eating Disorder).\n\nThe inclusion of repeated chewing and spitting of food is another classic, textbook hallmark behavior frequently categorized under the EDNOS/OSFED umbrella rather than standing as its own distinct standalone diagnosis.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Bulimia Nervosa, Anorexia Nervosa, Body Image Distortion"}'::jsonb),
('mc:the wavelike action causing relaxation and contraction of the muscles of the gi tract.:::peristalsis', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'the wavelike action causing relaxation and contraction of the muscles of the gi tract.', 'Peristalsis', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"🔴 Digestion: The process of breaking down food into small nutrients.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Peristalsis\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"🔴 Digestion: The process of breaking down food into small nutrients.\n🔴 Segmentation: Back-and-forth mixing of food in the intestine.\n🔴 Excretion: The removal of waste products from the body.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Digestion, Segmentation, Excretion"}'::jsonb),
('mc:muscle dysmorphia also known as bigorexia is characterized by the following manifestations except:::eating at night', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'muscle dysmorphia also known as bigorexia is characterized by the following manifestations except', 'Eating at night', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Eating at night (or Night Eating Syndrome) is an independent eating pattern specification characterized by evening hyperphagia or waking up to consume food during the night. It is not an established diagnostic hallmark or baseline manifestation of Muscle Dysmorphia.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Eating at night\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Eating at night (or Night Eating Syndrome) is an independent eating pattern specification characterized by evening hyperphagia or waking up to consume food during the night. It is not an established diagnostic hallmark or baseline manifestation of Muscle Dysmorphia.\n\nClassic Manifestations of Bigorexia\n\t• Constant body checking: Individuals with muscle dysmorphia exhibit severe, repetitive body checking habits. This includes spending hours staring in the mirror, constantly using a tape measure to track muscle symmetry, or weighing themselves multiple times a day.\n\t• A kind of obsessive-compulsive disorder: Under the official DSM-5 criteria, muscle dysmorphia is classified as a specific subtype of Body Dysmorphic Disorder (BDD), which sits directly under the spectrum of Obsessive-Compulsive and Related Disorders. The condition drives unrelenting, obsessive thoughts regarding physical inadequacy.\n\t• Excessive exercise even when injured: A hallmark behavioral symptom is compulsive exercise. The psychological drive to build lean muscle mass is so extreme that the individual will rigidly lift weights or complete high-intensity training sessions even when suffering from clear physical illnesses, fractures, or severe muscular injuries.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: A kind of obsessive-compulsive disorder, Constant body checking, Excessive exercise even when injured"}'::jsonb),
('mc:muscle dysmorphia also known as bigorexia is characterized by the following manifestations except:::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'muscle dysmorphia also known as bigorexia is characterized by the following manifestations except', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Eating at night (or Night Eating Syndrome) is an independent eating pattern specification characterized by evening hyperphagia or waking up to consume food during the night. It is not an established diagnostic hallmark or baseline manifestation of Muscle Dysmorphia.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Eating at night\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Eating at night (or Night Eating Syndrome) is an independent eating pattern specification characterized by evening hyperphagia or waking up to consume food during the night. It is not an established diagnostic hallmark or baseline manifestation of Muscle Dysmorphia.\n\nClassic Manifestations of Bigorexia\n\t• Constant body checking: Individuals with muscle dysmorphia exhibit severe, repetitive body checking habits. This includes spending hours staring in the mirror, constantly using a tape measure to track muscle symmetry, or weighing themselves multiple times a day.\n\t• A kind of obsessive-compulsive disorder: Under the official DSM-5 criteria, muscle dysmorphia is classified as a specific subtype of Body Dysmorphic Disorder (BDD), which sits directly under the spectrum of Obsessive-Compulsive and Related Disorders. The condition drives unrelenting, obsessive thoughts regarding physical inadequacy.\n\t• Excessive exercise even when injured: A hallmark behavioral symptom is compulsive exercise. The psychological drive to build lean muscle mass is so extreme that the individual will rigidly lift weights or complete high-intensity training sessions even when suffering from clear physical illnesses, fractures, or severe muscular injuries.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: A kind of obsessive-compulsive disorder, Constant body checking, Excessive exercise even when injured"}'::jsonb),
('mc:the following are consequences of malnutrition in relation to immune response during infection except:::poor appetite and fatigue', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'the following are consequences of malnutrition in relation to immune response during infection except', 'Poor appetite and fatigue', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"While poor appetite and fatigue are highly common, classic systemic and metabolic symptoms of malnutrition and active infections, they are general physiological responses rather than specific functional alterations of the immune system itself.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Poor appetite and fatigue\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"While poor appetite and fatigue are highly common, classic systemic and metabolic symptoms of malnutrition and active infections, they are general physiological responses rather than specific functional alterations of the immune system itself.\n\n\t• Reduced production of antibodies: Protein-energy malnutrition directly causes lymphoid tissue atrophy (such as the thymus and bone marrow), which severely impairs B-cell differentiation and downregulates the production of crucial secretory immunoglobulins (like IgA) and antibodies needed to fight pathogens.\n\t• Poor wound healing: Proper wound healing is an immune-mediated process reliant on cellular immunity. Malnutrition blunts macrophage activation, slows down granulomatous inflammation, and reduces cytokine orchestration, which prevents efficient tissue repair and barrier defense restoration.\n\t• Alterations in gut microbiome: The gut microbiome is intimately intertwined with mucosal immunity. Nutrient deficiencies compromise intestinal barrier function and mucosal nutrient-sensing receptors, disrupting the commensal gut microbiota balance and compounding immune dysfunction.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Alterations in gut microbiome, Poor wound healing, Reduced production of antibodies"}'::jsonb),
('mc:the following are consequences of malnutrition in relation to immune response during infection except:::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'the following are consequences of malnutrition in relation to immune response during infection except', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"While poor appetite and fatigue are highly common, classic systemic and metabolic symptoms of malnutrition and active infections, they are general physiological responses rather than specific functional alterations of the immune system itself.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Poor appetite and fatigue\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"While poor appetite and fatigue are highly common, classic systemic and metabolic symptoms of malnutrition and active infections, they are general physiological responses rather than specific functional alterations of the immune system itself.\n\n\t• Reduced production of antibodies: Protein-energy malnutrition directly causes lymphoid tissue atrophy (such as the thymus and bone marrow), which severely impairs B-cell differentiation and downregulates the production of crucial secretory immunoglobulins (like IgA) and antibodies needed to fight pathogens.\n\t• Poor wound healing: Proper wound healing is an immune-mediated process reliant on cellular immunity. Malnutrition blunts macrophage activation, slows down granulomatous inflammation, and reduces cytokine orchestration, which prevents efficient tissue repair and barrier defense restoration.\n\t• Alterations in gut microbiome: The gut microbiome is intimately intertwined with mucosal immunity. Nutrient deficiencies compromise intestinal barrier function and mucosal nutrient-sensing receptors, disrupting the commensal gut microbiota balance and compounding immune dysfunction.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Alterations in gut microbiome, Poor wound healing, Reduced production of antibodies"}'::jsonb),
('mc:a patient with an oral temperature of 38.8 degrees celsius needs a proper nutrition intervention to support metabolic changes in the body. what should the rnd do?:::give additional high biologic value protein and fluids', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'a patient with an oral temperature of 38.8 degrees celsius needs a proper nutrition intervention to support metabolic changes in the body. what should the rnd do?', 'Give additional high biologic value protein and fluids', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"An oral temperature of 38.8°C indicates an active clinical fever (febrile state).","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Give additional high biologic value protein and fluids\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"An oral temperature of 38.8°C indicates an active clinical fever (febrile state).\n\nFor every 1°C increase in core body temperature above normal, the Basal Metabolic Rate (BMR) spikes by approximately 13%. This rapid metabolic shift accelerates tissue breakdown, causing significant lean muscle mass degradation (hypercatabolism) and high fluid loss via sweating and respiration.\n\nTo counteract nitrogen wasting and maintain cellular integrity, the Registered Nutritionist-Dietitian (RND) must increase the patient''s intake of high biological value (HBV) proteins (such as eggs, milk, fish, and lean poultry), which are efficiently utilized by the body for tissue repair and immune synthesis. Concurrently, aggressive fluid replenishment is a non-negotiable priority to prevent dehydration and support cellular homeostasis.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Give 100% fruit juice, Increase intake of fats to compensate for decreased adiposity, Give salabat because ginger has antimicrobial properties"}'::jsonb),
('mc:the wavelike action causing relaxation and contraction of the muscles of the gi tract.:::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'the wavelike action causing relaxation and contraction of the muscles of the gi tract.', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"🔴 Digestion: The process of breaking down food into small nutrients.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Peristalsis\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"🔴 Digestion: The process of breaking down food into small nutrients.\n🔴 Segmentation: Back-and-forth mixing of food in the intestine.\n🔴 Excretion: The removal of waste products from the body.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Digestion, Segmentation, Excretion"}'::jsonb),
('mc:bf is a 61-year-old female diagnosed with cancer of the stomach and has been bedridden for the past one month; height 5’6’’, weight: 50 kg, with a history of 10% weight loss for the past 3 months, has been consuming mostly liquid and semi foods amounting to 700 calories per day. the attending physician decided to give her parenteral nutrition prior to a contemplated procedure. which of the following tna (total nutrient admixture) is best for this patient?:::nutriflex 1000 in 1250 ml', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'bf is a 61-year-old female diagnosed with cancer of the stomach and has been bedridden for the past one month; height 5’6’’, weight: 50 kg, with a history of 10% weight loss for the past 3 months, has been consuming mostly liquid and semi foods amounting to 700 calories per day. the attending physician decided to give her parenteral nutrition prior to a contemplated procedure. which of the following tna (total nutrient admixture) is best for this patient?', 'Nutriflex 1000 in 1250 ml', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Patient BF is severely malnourished, has lost 10% of her body weight over 3 months, and has been surviving on a starvation-level intake of only 700 Calories per day. When a patient is chronically starved, starting Parenteral Nutrition at a full calorie load (like 1,400 or 1,500 Calories) can trigger fatal electrolyte shifts (hypophosphatemia, hypokalemia, and hypomagnesemia).","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Nutriflex 1000 in 1250 ml\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Patient BF is severely malnourished, has lost 10% of her body weight over 3 months, and has been surviving on a starvation-level intake of only 700 Calories per day. When a patient is chronically starved, starting Parenteral Nutrition at a full calorie load (like 1,400 or 1,500 Calories) can trigger fatal electrolyte shifts (hypophosphatemia, hypokalemia, and hypomagnesemia).\n\nStandard safety guidelines dictate that for severely malnourished or starved patients, clinicians must \"start low and go slow.\" Nutritional support should be initiated at a conservative baseline of 15 to 20 kcal/kg of body weight. For a 50 kg patient, this equals an initial daily energy target of roughly 750 to 1,000 Calories.\n\nNutriflex 1000 in 1250 ml delivers exactly 1,000 Calories in a safe, controlled volume. This makes it the ideal, safe starter formula to initiate her clinical feeding therapy without triggering refeeding syndrome.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Nutriflex 1400 in 1875 ml, Nutriflex 1500 in 1250 ml., Nutriflex 1900 in 2500 ml"}'::jsonb),
('mc:a patient with an oral temperature of 38.8 degrees celsius needs a proper nutrition intervention to support metabolic changes in the body. what should the rnd do?:::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'a patient with an oral temperature of 38.8 degrees celsius needs a proper nutrition intervention to support metabolic changes in the body. what should the rnd do?', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"An oral temperature of 38.8°C indicates an active clinical fever (febrile state).","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Give additional high biologic value protein and fluids\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"An oral temperature of 38.8°C indicates an active clinical fever (febrile state).\n\nFor every 1°C increase in core body temperature above normal, the Basal Metabolic Rate (BMR) spikes by approximately 13%. This rapid metabolic shift accelerates tissue breakdown, causing significant lean muscle mass degradation (hypercatabolism) and high fluid loss via sweating and respiration.\n\nTo counteract nitrogen wasting and maintain cellular integrity, the Registered Nutritionist-Dietitian (RND) must increase the patient''s intake of high biological value (HBV) proteins (such as eggs, milk, fish, and lean poultry), which are efficiently utilized by the body for tissue repair and immune synthesis. Concurrently, aggressive fluid replenishment is a non-negotiable priority to prevent dehydration and support cellular homeostasis.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Give 100% fruit juice, Increase intake of fats to compensate for decreased adiposity, Give salabat because ginger has antimicrobial properties"}'::jsonb),
('mc:diarrhea with occasional constipation, vague abdominal pain and occasional rectal bleeding are symptoms of amoebiasis. what type of diet must be prescribed by the rnd?:::liquid, bland and low residue', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'diarrhea with occasional constipation, vague abdominal pain and occasional rectal bleeding are symptoms of amoebiasis. what type of diet must be prescribed by the rnd?', 'Liquid, bland and low residue', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Alternating diarrhea/constipation, vague abdominal pain, and blood in the stool is the classic presentation of intestinal Amoebiasis (caused by Entamoeba histolytica). Dietary mechanisms include:","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Liquid, bland and low residue\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Alternating diarrhea/constipation, vague abdominal pain, and blood in the stool is the classic presentation of intestinal Amoebiasis (caused by Entamoeba histolytica). Dietary mechanisms include:\n\n\t• Low Residue (Low Fiber): Minimizes fecal volume and eliminates roughage that could mechanically scratch or irritate the already inflamed, ulcerated colonic mucosa.\n\t• Bland: Excludes chemical irritants such as harsh spices, gastric stimulants, and heavy seasonings that can exacerbate abdominal cramping.\n\t• Liquid/Fluid Modification: Helps rest the gastrointestinal tract during the acute phase while actively replenishing vital fluids and electrolytes lost through diarrheal episodes to prevent dehydration.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: High protein diet, High calorie, raw foods allowed as tolerated, Low fat diet"}'::jsonb),
('mc:diarrhea with occasional constipation, vague abdominal pain and occasional rectal bleeding are symptoms of amoebiasis. what type of diet must be prescribed by the rnd?:::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'diarrhea with occasional constipation, vague abdominal pain and occasional rectal bleeding are symptoms of amoebiasis. what type of diet must be prescribed by the rnd?', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Alternating diarrhea/constipation, vague abdominal pain, and blood in the stool is the classic presentation of intestinal Amoebiasis (caused by Entamoeba histolytica). Dietary mechanisms include:","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Liquid, bland and low residue\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Alternating diarrhea/constipation, vague abdominal pain, and blood in the stool is the classic presentation of intestinal Amoebiasis (caused by Entamoeba histolytica). Dietary mechanisms include:\n\n\t• Low Residue (Low Fiber): Minimizes fecal volume and eliminates roughage that could mechanically scratch or irritate the already inflamed, ulcerated colonic mucosa.\n\t• Bland: Excludes chemical irritants such as harsh spices, gastric stimulants, and heavy seasonings that can exacerbate abdominal cramping.\n\t• Liquid/Fluid Modification: Helps rest the gastrointestinal tract during the acute phase while actively replenishing vital fluids and electrolytes lost through diarrheal episodes to prevent dehydration.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: High protein diet, High calorie, raw foods allowed as tolerated, Low fat diet"}'::jsonb),
('mc:the following micronutrients may be deficient among children with ascariasis lumbricoides:::iron and vitamin c', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'the following micronutrients may be deficient among children with ascariasis lumbricoides', 'Iron and Vitamin C', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Ascaris lumbricoides (giant intestinal roundworms) thrive in the human small intestine, where they actively compete with the host child for nutrients.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Iron and Vitamin C\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Ascaris lumbricoides (giant intestinal roundworms) thrive in the human small intestine, where they actively compete with the host child for nutrients.\n\n\t• Iron: Intestinal parasite infections damage the gut mucosa, impair absorption, and cause microscopic blood loss, heavily precipitating iron-deficiency anemia in children.\n\t• Vitamin C: Intestinal inflammation and a compromised gut lining impair the absorption of water-soluble vitamins like Vitamin C. Because Vitamin C is chemically required to reduce ferric iron to ferrous iron for absorption in the gut, a concurrent drop in Vitamin C worsens the child''s iron deficiency.\n\n• Zinc and Vitamin C: While zinc deficiency is common in soil-transmitted helminth infections, it is paired with Vitamin C in option D, whereas Iron and Vitamin C is the specific paired textbook key used for this question format.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Calcium and Vitamin D, Zinc and Vitamin C, Iron and Calcium"}'::jsonb),
('mc:the following micronutrients may be deficient among children with ascariasis lumbricoides:::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'the following micronutrients may be deficient among children with ascariasis lumbricoides', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Ascaris lumbricoides (giant intestinal roundworms) thrive in the human small intestine, where they actively compete with the host child for nutrients.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Iron and Vitamin C\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Ascaris lumbricoides (giant intestinal roundworms) thrive in the human small intestine, where they actively compete with the host child for nutrients.\n\n\t• Iron: Intestinal parasite infections damage the gut mucosa, impair absorption, and cause microscopic blood loss, heavily precipitating iron-deficiency anemia in children.\n\t• Vitamin C: Intestinal inflammation and a compromised gut lining impair the absorption of water-soluble vitamins like Vitamin C. Because Vitamin C is chemically required to reduce ferric iron to ferrous iron for absorption in the gut, a concurrent drop in Vitamin C worsens the child''s iron deficiency.\n\n• Zinc and Vitamin C: While zinc deficiency is common in soil-transmitted helminth infections, it is paired with Vitamin C in option D, whereas Iron and Vitamin C is the specific paired textbook key used for this question format.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Calcium and Vitamin D, Zinc and Vitamin C, Iron and Calcium"}'::jsonb),
('mc:dietary management for malaria includes a high protein and high carbohydrate diet by increases total caloric intake by how much? 10% 25% 30% 50%:::50%', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'dietary management for malaria includes a high protein and high carbohydrate diet by increases total caloric intake by how much? 10% 25% 30% 50%', '50%', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Plasmodium species infection triggers the release of pro-inflammatory cytokines such as tumor necrosis factor-alpha (TNF-alpha), interleukin-1 (IL-1), and interleukin-6 (IL-6), which upregulate hepatic acute-phase protein synthesis and accelerate whole-body protein degradation. To mitigate this hypercatabolic response, medical nutrition therapy dictates an increase in total resting energy expenditure (REE) by approximately 50%, alongside high carbohydrate provision to spare lean body mass and high protein delivery (1.5 to 2.0 g/kg/day) to support tissue repair and immune cell proliferation.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"50%\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Plasmodium species infection triggers the release of pro-inflammatory cytokines such as tumor necrosis factor-alpha (TNF-alpha), interleukin-1 (IL-1), and interleukin-6 (IL-6), which upregulate hepatic acute-phase protein synthesis and accelerate whole-body protein degradation. To mitigate this hypercatabolic response, medical nutrition therapy dictates an increase in total resting energy expenditure (REE) by approximately 50%, alongside high carbohydrate provision to spare lean body mass and high protein delivery (1.5 to 2.0 g/kg/day) to support tissue repair and immune cell proliferation.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: 30%, 25%, 10%"}'::jsonb),
('mc:dietary management for malaria includes a high protein and high carbohydrate diet by increases total caloric intake by how much? 10% 25% 30% 50%:::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'dietary management for malaria includes a high protein and high carbohydrate diet by increases total caloric intake by how much? 10% 25% 30% 50%', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Plasmodium species infection triggers the release of pro-inflammatory cytokines such as tumor necrosis factor-alpha (TNF-alpha), interleukin-1 (IL-1), and interleukin-6 (IL-6), which upregulate hepatic acute-phase protein synthesis and accelerate whole-body protein degradation. To mitigate this hypercatabolic response, medical nutrition therapy dictates an increase in total resting energy expenditure (REE) by approximately 50%, alongside high carbohydrate provision to spare lean body mass and high protein delivery (1.5 to 2.0 g/kg/day) to support tissue repair and immune cell proliferation.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"50%\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Plasmodium species infection triggers the release of pro-inflammatory cytokines such as tumor necrosis factor-alpha (TNF-alpha), interleukin-1 (IL-1), and interleukin-6 (IL-6), which upregulate hepatic acute-phase protein synthesis and accelerate whole-body protein degradation. To mitigate this hypercatabolic response, medical nutrition therapy dictates an increase in total resting energy expenditure (REE) by approximately 50%, alongside high carbohydrate provision to spare lean body mass and high protein delivery (1.5 to 2.0 g/kg/day) to support tissue repair and immune cell proliferation.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: 30%, 25%, 10%"}'::jsonb),
('mc:cholera is an acute dehydrating disease caused by vibrio cholerae. it’s symptoms include profuse rice water-like, mucoid stools with fish odor. the primary nutrition intervention goal is:::intravenous fluid and electrolyte replacement', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'cholera is an acute dehydrating disease caused by vibrio cholerae. it’s symptoms include profuse rice water-like, mucoid stools with fish odor. the primary nutrition intervention goal is', 'Intravenous fluid and electrolyte replacement', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"In acute Cholera, the sheer volume of profuse, watery stools can deplete a patient''s entire blood volume and electrolytes within hours, leading to severe hypovolemic shock and death. Therefore, the immediate, life-saving primary intervention goal is the rapid replenishment of fluid and electrolyte losses, typically achieved via intravenous (IV) fluids for severe cases or Oral Rehydration Salts (ORS) for mild-to-moderate dehydration.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Intravenous fluid and electrolyte replacement\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"In acute Cholera, the sheer volume of profuse, watery stools can deplete a patient''s entire blood volume and electrolytes within hours, leading to severe hypovolemic shock and death. Therefore, the immediate, life-saving primary intervention goal is the rapid replenishment of fluid and electrolyte losses, typically achieved via intravenous (IV) fluids for severe cases or Oral Rehydration Salts (ORS) for mild-to-moderate dehydration.\n\nWhile introducing a soft, bland, or high-calorie diet to correct nutrient deficiencies is important for long-term recovery once the gut stabilizes, these nutritional interventions are completely impossible and ineffective during the acute phase until the patient is hemodynamically stable and rehydrated.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Soft and bland diet, Correct nutrient deficiencies, High caloric diet"}'::jsonb),
('mc:cholera is an acute dehydrating disease caused by vibrio cholerae. it’s symptoms include profuse rice water-like, mucoid stools with fish odor. the primary nutrition intervention goal is:::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'cholera is an acute dehydrating disease caused by vibrio cholerae. it’s symptoms include profuse rice water-like, mucoid stools with fish odor. the primary nutrition intervention goal is', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"In acute Cholera, the sheer volume of profuse, watery stools can deplete a patient''s entire blood volume and electrolytes within hours, leading to severe hypovolemic shock and death. Therefore, the immediate, life-saving primary intervention goal is the rapid replenishment of fluid and electrolyte losses, typically achieved via intravenous (IV) fluids for severe cases or Oral Rehydration Salts (ORS) for mild-to-moderate dehydration.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Intravenous fluid and electrolyte replacement\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"In acute Cholera, the sheer volume of profuse, watery stools can deplete a patient''s entire blood volume and electrolytes within hours, leading to severe hypovolemic shock and death. Therefore, the immediate, life-saving primary intervention goal is the rapid replenishment of fluid and electrolyte losses, typically achieved via intravenous (IV) fluids for severe cases or Oral Rehydration Salts (ORS) for mild-to-moderate dehydration.\n\nWhile introducing a soft, bland, or high-calorie diet to correct nutrient deficiencies is important for long-term recovery once the gut stabilizes, these nutritional interventions are completely impossible and ineffective during the acute phase until the patient is hemodynamically stable and rehydrated.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Soft and bland diet, Correct nutrient deficiencies, High caloric diet"}'::jsonb),
('mc:a patient presents with the following signs and symptoms of infection: weakness, anorexia, cough, red tongue, fever and pea green colored stool. what could be the patient’s medical condition?:::typhoid fever', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'a patient presents with the following signs and symptoms of infection: weakness, anorexia, cough, red tongue, fever and pea green colored stool. what could be the patient’s medical condition?', 'Typhoid fever', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"The presence of a persistent fever, generalized weakness, anoxia, a cough, and a furred tongue with prominent red edges and tip (traditionally referred to as a \"typhoid tongue\") are classic clinical presentations of a Salmonella Typhi infection.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Typhoid fever\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"The presence of a persistent fever, generalized weakness, anoxia, a cough, and a furred tongue with prominent red edges and tip (traditionally referred to as a \"typhoid tongue\") are classic clinical presentations of a Salmonella Typhi infection.\n\nA defining, textbook feature of Typhoid fever is the development of a slimy, yellow-green liquid diarrhea. Often referred to in board exam vignettes as pea-green or pea-soup colored stool, it is caused by severe localized inflammation, purulent exudates, and necrosis within the intestinal Peyer''s patches mixing with mucosal bile.\n\n• Dengue: Characterized by high fevers, severe muscle/joint aches (\"breakbone fever\"), headaches, and a distinctive rash, but it does not cause pea-green diarrhea or an inflamed red tongue.\n• Meningitis: A neurological infection presenting with a high fever, severe headache, rigid stiff neck (nuchal rigidity), and altered mental status rather than a primary gastrointestinal illness.\n• Cholera: While it is a major waterborne disease, cholera produces massive volumes of profuse, painless diarrhea classically described as clear \"rice-water\" stool with a fishy odor, completely distinct from the thick, greenish-yellow pea-soup diarrhea of typhoid.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Cholera, Meningitis, Dengue"}'::jsonb),
('mc:a patient presents with the following signs and symptoms of infection: weakness, anorexia, cough, red tongue, fever and pea green colored stool. what could be the patient’s medical condition?:::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'a patient presents with the following signs and symptoms of infection: weakness, anorexia, cough, red tongue, fever and pea green colored stool. what could be the patient’s medical condition?', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"The presence of a persistent fever, generalized weakness, anoxia, a cough, and a furred tongue with prominent red edges and tip (traditionally referred to as a \"typhoid tongue\") are classic clinical presentations of a Salmonella Typhi infection.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Typhoid fever\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"The presence of a persistent fever, generalized weakness, anoxia, a cough, and a furred tongue with prominent red edges and tip (traditionally referred to as a \"typhoid tongue\") are classic clinical presentations of a Salmonella Typhi infection.\n\nA defining, textbook feature of Typhoid fever is the development of a slimy, yellow-green liquid diarrhea. Often referred to in board exam vignettes as pea-green or pea-soup colored stool, it is caused by severe localized inflammation, purulent exudates, and necrosis within the intestinal Peyer''s patches mixing with mucosal bile.\n\n• Dengue: Characterized by high fevers, severe muscle/joint aches (\"breakbone fever\"), headaches, and a distinctive rash, but it does not cause pea-green diarrhea or an inflamed red tongue.\n• Meningitis: A neurological infection presenting with a high fever, severe headache, rigid stiff neck (nuchal rigidity), and altered mental status rather than a primary gastrointestinal illness.\n• Cholera: While it is a major waterborne disease, cholera produces massive volumes of profuse, painless diarrhea classically described as clear \"rice-water\" stool with a fishy odor, completely distinct from the thick, greenish-yellow pea-soup diarrhea of typhoid.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Cholera, Meningitis, Dengue"}'::jsonb),
('mc:a patient diagnosed with rheumatic fever was prescribed cortisone. nutrition intervention must include:::sodium restriction 1000mg/day', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'a patient diagnosed with rheumatic fever was prescribed cortisone. nutrition intervention must include', 'Sodium restriction 1000mg/day', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"While cortisone actively wastes potassium (making potassium-rich foods clinically necessary), the test creator targets sodium restriction based on the following textbook rationale:","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Sodium restriction 1000mg/day\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"While cortisone actively wastes potassium (making potassium-rich foods clinically necessary), the test creator targets sodium restriction based on the following textbook rationale:\n\n1. Fluid Retention and Edema: Cortisone heavily promotes sodium and fluid retention inside the body. In a patient with Rheumatic Fever, this fluid retention is highly dangerous because the disease can cause inflammation of the heart (rheumatic carditis).\n2. Preventing Heart Strain: Introducing a strict sodium restriction helps prevent fluid overload, peripheral edema, and hypertension, which directly protects the heart muscles from being overtaxed during the acute phase of Rheumatic Fever.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Potassium rich food, Iron supplementation, Increased fluid intake"}'::jsonb),
('mc:a patient diagnosed with rheumatic fever was prescribed cortisone. nutrition intervention must include:::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'a patient diagnosed with rheumatic fever was prescribed cortisone. nutrition intervention must include', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"While cortisone actively wastes potassium (making potassium-rich foods clinically necessary), the test creator targets sodium restriction based on the following textbook rationale:","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Sodium restriction 1000mg/day\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"While cortisone actively wastes potassium (making potassium-rich foods clinically necessary), the test creator targets sodium restriction based on the following textbook rationale:\n\n1. Fluid Retention and Edema: Cortisone heavily promotes sodium and fluid retention inside the body. In a patient with Rheumatic Fever, this fluid retention is highly dangerous because the disease can cause inflammation of the heart (rheumatic carditis).\n2. Preventing Heart Strain: Introducing a strict sodium restriction helps prevent fluid overload, peripheral edema, and hypertension, which directly protects the heart muscles from being overtaxed during the acute phase of Rheumatic Fever.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Potassium rich food, Iron supplementation, Increased fluid intake"}'::jsonb),
('mc:isoniazid, a drug for tuberculosis treatment, is a vitamin b6 (pyridoxine) antagonist. patients on this medication must be advised to eat more of the following:::egg, carrots, camote, tuna, beef, banana and peas', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'isoniazid, a drug for tuberculosis treatment, is a vitamin b6 (pyridoxine) antagonist. patients on this medication must be advised to eat more of the following', 'Egg, carrots, camote, tuna, beef, banana and peas', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Isoniazid (INH) actively competes with and inhibits the activation of Vitamin B6 (Pyridoxine), which can precipitate severe peripheral neuropathy (manifesting as burning, numbness, or tingling in the extremities).","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Egg, carrots, camote, tuna, beef, banana and peas\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Isoniazid (INH) actively competes with and inhibits the activation of Vitamin B6 (Pyridoxine), which can precipitate severe peripheral neuropathy (manifesting as burning, numbness, or tingling in the extremities).\n\nTo naturally optimize B6 levels alongside standard clinical supplementation, patients should prioritize foods dense in Pyridoxine. This option provides an exceptionally rich combination of animal and plant-based Vitamin B6 sources, including tuna, beef, banana, peas, and camote (sweet potato).","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Apple, bread, rice, cabbage, ginger, Soup, collagen, radish, eggplant, rice, Peanut butter, fish, rice, potato, garlic"}'::jsonb),
('mc:isoniazid, a drug for tuberculosis treatment, is a vitamin b6 (pyridoxine) antagonist. patients on this medication must be advised to eat more of the following:::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'isoniazid, a drug for tuberculosis treatment, is a vitamin b6 (pyridoxine) antagonist. patients on this medication must be advised to eat more of the following', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Isoniazid (INH) actively competes with and inhibits the activation of Vitamin B6 (Pyridoxine), which can precipitate severe peripheral neuropathy (manifesting as burning, numbness, or tingling in the extremities).","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Egg, carrots, camote, tuna, beef, banana and peas\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Isoniazid (INH) actively competes with and inhibits the activation of Vitamin B6 (Pyridoxine), which can precipitate severe peripheral neuropathy (manifesting as burning, numbness, or tingling in the extremities).\n\nTo naturally optimize B6 levels alongside standard clinical supplementation, patients should prioritize foods dense in Pyridoxine. This option provides an exceptionally rich combination of animal and plant-based Vitamin B6 sources, including tuna, beef, banana, peas, and camote (sweet potato).","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Apple, bread, rice, cabbage, ginger, Soup, collagen, radish, eggplant, rice, Peanut butter, fish, rice, potato, garlic"}'::jsonb),
('mc:a patient diagnosed with tuberculosis has normal bmi. how much protein should be prescribed per day?:::high protein 75 to 120g per day', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'a patient diagnosed with tuberculosis has normal bmi. how much protein should be prescribed per day?', 'High protein 75 to 120g per day', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Active Tuberculosis (TB) places the body in an intense inflammatory and hypercatabolic state, resulting in accelerated muscle tissue breakdown and nitrogen wasting. Even if a patient presents with a normal BMI, they require aggressive nutritional therapy to counter cellular destruction, maintain lean muscle mass, and optimize immune function.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"High protein 75 to 120g per day\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Active Tuberculosis (TB) places the body in an intense inflammatory and hypercatabolic state, resulting in accelerated muscle tissue breakdown and nitrogen wasting. Even if a patient presents with a normal BMI, they require aggressive nutritional therapy to counter cellular destruction, maintain lean muscle mass, and optimize immune function.\n\nMedical nutrition therapy (MNT) guidelines dictate increasing a TB patient''s protein intake above the normal baseline to support tissue healing and recovery. Clinical protocols emphasize incorporating adequate high-biological-value protein into the daily diet to support complete tissue healing.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Low protein 40g per day, 0.6g/kg body weight, 2g/kg body weight"}'::jsonb),
('mc:a patient diagnosed with tuberculosis has normal bmi. how much protein should be prescribed per day?:::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'a patient diagnosed with tuberculosis has normal bmi. how much protein should be prescribed per day?', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Active Tuberculosis (TB) places the body in an intense inflammatory and hypercatabolic state, resulting in accelerated muscle tissue breakdown and nitrogen wasting. Even if a patient presents with a normal BMI, they require aggressive nutritional therapy to counter cellular destruction, maintain lean muscle mass, and optimize immune function.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"High protein 75 to 120g per day\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Active Tuberculosis (TB) places the body in an intense inflammatory and hypercatabolic state, resulting in accelerated muscle tissue breakdown and nitrogen wasting. Even if a patient presents with a normal BMI, they require aggressive nutritional therapy to counter cellular destruction, maintain lean muscle mass, and optimize immune function.\n\nMedical nutrition therapy (MNT) guidelines dictate increasing a TB patient''s protein intake above the normal baseline to support tissue healing and recovery. Clinical protocols emphasize incorporating adequate high-biological-value protein into the daily diet to support complete tissue healing.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Low protein 40g per day, 0.6g/kg body weight, 2g/kg body weight"}'::jsonb),
('mc:a patient with dengue hemorrhagic fever must avoid:::chocolate drink', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'a patient with dengue hemorrhagic fever must avoid', 'Chocolate drink', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Patients diagnosed with Dengue Hemorrhagic Fever (DHF) must strictly avoid dark-colored foods and beverages, such as chocolate drinks, cola, coffee, red/violet juices, and dark berries.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Chocolate drink\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Patients diagnosed with Dengue Hemorrhagic Fever (DHF) must strictly avoid dark-colored foods and beverages, such as chocolate drinks, cola, coffee, red/violet juices, and dark berries.\n\nDHF compromises blood capillaries and dramatically reduces blood platelets, placing the patient at a severe risk for internal gastrointestinal (GI) bleeding. If a patient consumes a chocolate drink, it can discolor their vomit or stool, staining it dark brown or black. This masks clinical signs of active GI bleeding (such as hematemesis or melena), confusing the medical team and potentially delaying life-saving emergency care.\n\n• Dark leafy vegetables: While green, they do not dye stool or vomit the distinctive tarry black or dark brown color associated with internal bleeding. In fact, they provide vital Vitamin K to support proper clotting pathways.\n• Hard candies & Apple juice: These options are clear or translucent, making them completely safe for hydration and carbohydrate maintenance without interfering with medical observations.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Hard candies, Dark leafy vegetables, Apple juice"}'::jsonb),
('mc:a patient with dengue hemorrhagic fever must avoid:::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'a patient with dengue hemorrhagic fever must avoid', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Patients diagnosed with Dengue Hemorrhagic Fever (DHF) must strictly avoid dark-colored foods and beverages, such as chocolate drinks, cola, coffee, red/violet juices, and dark berries.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Chocolate drink\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Patients diagnosed with Dengue Hemorrhagic Fever (DHF) must strictly avoid dark-colored foods and beverages, such as chocolate drinks, cola, coffee, red/violet juices, and dark berries.\n\nDHF compromises blood capillaries and dramatically reduces blood platelets, placing the patient at a severe risk for internal gastrointestinal (GI) bleeding. If a patient consumes a chocolate drink, it can discolor their vomit or stool, staining it dark brown or black. This masks clinical signs of active GI bleeding (such as hematemesis or melena), confusing the medical team and potentially delaying life-saving emergency care.\n\n• Dark leafy vegetables: While green, they do not dye stool or vomit the distinctive tarry black or dark brown color associated with internal bleeding. In fact, they provide vital Vitamin K to support proper clotting pathways.\n• Hard candies & Apple juice: These options are clear or translucent, making them completely safe for hydration and carbohydrate maintenance without interfering with medical observations.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Hard candies, Dark leafy vegetables, Apple juice"}'::jsonb),
('mc:food safety is essential in managing patients with aids. the following are food safety measures to be observed except:::keep cold food at 35 to 50 degrees fahrenheit', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'food safety is essential in managing patients with aids. the following are food safety measures to be observed except', 'Keep cold food at 35 to 50 degrees Fahrenheit', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"In clinical food safety protocols, cold food must strictly be maintained at or below 40°F (4°C) (or 41°F depending on localized sanitary code references like ServSafe or the FDA) to inhibit pathogenic bacterial multiplication.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Keep cold food at 35 to 50 degrees Fahrenheit\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"In clinical food safety protocols, cold food must strictly be maintained at or below 40°F (4°C) (or 41°F depending on localized sanitary code references like ServSafe or the FDA) to inhibit pathogenic bacterial multiplication.\n\nStating that cold foods can be kept up to 50°F directly breaches the [Food Temperature Danger Zone (40°F to 140°F)](usda.gov \"\"Danger Zone\" (40°F - 140°F)\"). Allowing cold food temperatures to climb to 50°F allows highly dangerous, opportunistic pathogens like Salmonella, Listeria monocytogenes, and E. coli to rapidly multiply. For immunocompromised individuals, such as patients with AIDS, this minor temperature elevation can lead to a severe, life-threatening foodborne infection.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Discard eggs with cracked shells, Avoid serving raw fruits and vegetables, Thaw chicken in the refrigerator"}'::jsonb),
('mc:food safety is essential in managing patients with aids. the following are food safety measures to be observed except:::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'food safety is essential in managing patients with aids. the following are food safety measures to be observed except', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"In clinical food safety protocols, cold food must strictly be maintained at or below 40°F (4°C) (or 41°F depending on localized sanitary code references like ServSafe or the FDA) to inhibit pathogenic bacterial multiplication.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Keep cold food at 35 to 50 degrees Fahrenheit\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"In clinical food safety protocols, cold food must strictly be maintained at or below 40°F (4°C) (or 41°F depending on localized sanitary code references like ServSafe or the FDA) to inhibit pathogenic bacterial multiplication.\n\nStating that cold foods can be kept up to 50°F directly breaches the [Food Temperature Danger Zone (40°F to 140°F)](usda.gov \"\"Danger Zone\" (40°F - 140°F)\"). Allowing cold food temperatures to climb to 50°F allows highly dangerous, opportunistic pathogens like Salmonella, Listeria monocytogenes, and E. coli to rapidly multiply. For immunocompromised individuals, such as patients with AIDS, this minor temperature elevation can lead to a severe, life-threatening foodborne infection.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Discard eggs with cracked shells, Avoid serving raw fruits and vegetables, Thaw chicken in the refrigerator"}'::jsonb),
('mc:stage 1 dietary management of poliomyelitis includes 30 to 200 ml tube feeding alternating with water every:::2 hours', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'stage 1 dietary management of poliomyelitis includes 30 to 200 ml tube feeding alternating with water every', '2 hours', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"In older, traditional diet therapy manuals for acute infectious neurological disorders like poliomyelitis (particularly cases affecting the bulbar region, which impairs normal swallowing mechanics), Stage 1 protocols utilize tube feeding to guarantee safe nutrition without aspiration.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"2 hours\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"In older, traditional diet therapy manuals for acute infectious neurological disorders like poliomyelitis (particularly cases affecting the bulbar region, which impairs normal swallowing mechanics), Stage 1 protocols utilize tube feeding to guarantee safe nutrition without aspiration.\n\nThe standard textbook framework states that the patient should be given 30 to 200 ml of formula via tube feeding, wait 2 hours, and then alternate with water. Spacing the administration out every 2 hours prevents overwhelming the patient''s gastric capacity, minimizes the risk of regurgitation, and keeps the patient safely hydrated while their swallowing reflexes are heavily compromised.\n\n*When you encounter this question on your exam, remember this specific gastrointestinal-pacing formula for acute polio management: Tube feeding, wait 2 hours, then water.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: 1 hour, 5 hours, 3 hours"}'::jsonb),
('mc:stage 1 dietary management of poliomyelitis includes 30 to 200 ml tube feeding alternating with water every:::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'stage 1 dietary management of poliomyelitis includes 30 to 200 ml tube feeding alternating with water every', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"In older, traditional diet therapy manuals for acute infectious neurological disorders like poliomyelitis (particularly cases affecting the bulbar region, which impairs normal swallowing mechanics), Stage 1 protocols utilize tube feeding to guarantee safe nutrition without aspiration.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"2 hours\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"In older, traditional diet therapy manuals for acute infectious neurological disorders like poliomyelitis (particularly cases affecting the bulbar region, which impairs normal swallowing mechanics), Stage 1 protocols utilize tube feeding to guarantee safe nutrition without aspiration.\n\nThe standard textbook framework states that the patient should be given 30 to 200 ml of formula via tube feeding, wait 2 hours, and then alternate with water. Spacing the administration out every 2 hours prevents overwhelming the patient''s gastric capacity, minimizes the risk of regurgitation, and keeps the patient safely hydrated while their swallowing reflexes are heavily compromised.\n\n*When you encounter this question on your exam, remember this specific gastrointestinal-pacing formula for acute polio management: Tube feeding, wait 2 hours, then water.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: 1 hour, 5 hours, 3 hours"}'::jsonb),
('mc:a patient with severe acute respiratory syndrome must wear surgical mask at all times except when:::eating', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'a patient with severe acute respiratory syndrome must wear surgical mask at all times except when', 'Eating', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"A patient cannot physically consume food or fluids while wearing a surgical mask. Therefore, eating (and drinking) is the only practical and necessary exception where the mask must be temporarily removed.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Eating\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"A patient cannot physically consume food or fluids while wearing a surgical mask. Therefore, eating (and drinking) is the only practical and necessary exception where the mask must be temporarily removed.\n\nDuring these temporary mask-off periods, strict isolation precautions—such as maintaining negative pressure ventilation, keeping the patient in a private room, and ensuring healthcare workers wear appropriate personal protective equipment (PPE) like N95 respirators—are vital to prevent the transmission of respiratory droplets or aerosols.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Sleeping, Resting, Exercising"}'::jsonb),
('mc:the one animal polysaccharide that is not present in foods but is generally stored in the liver and muscle::::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'the one animal polysaccharide that is not present in foods but is generally stored in the liver and muscle:', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Glycogen is the storage form of carbohydrates in animals.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Glycogen\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Glycogen is the storage form of carbohydrates in animals.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Lignin, Polymer, Lecithin"}'::jsonb),
('mc:a patient with severe acute respiratory syndrome must wear surgical mask at all times except when:::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'a patient with severe acute respiratory syndrome must wear surgical mask at all times except when', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"A patient cannot physically consume food or fluids while wearing a surgical mask. Therefore, eating (and drinking) is the only practical and necessary exception where the mask must be temporarily removed.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Eating\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"A patient cannot physically consume food or fluids while wearing a surgical mask. Therefore, eating (and drinking) is the only practical and necessary exception where the mask must be temporarily removed.\n\nDuring these temporary mask-off periods, strict isolation precautions—such as maintaining negative pressure ventilation, keeping the patient in a private room, and ensuring healthcare workers wear appropriate personal protective equipment (PPE) like N95 respirators—are vital to prevent the transmission of respiratory droplets or aerosols.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Sleeping, Resting, Exercising"}'::jsonb),
('mc:middle east respiratory syndrome or mers-cov can be transmitted through:::camel milk and meat', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'middle east respiratory syndrome or mers-cov can be transmitted through', 'Camel milk and meat', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Middle East Respiratory Syndrome (MERS-CoV) is a zoonotic viral respiratory illness, meaning it is transmitted from animals to humans. Extensive epidemiological studies by global health bodies have confirmed that dromedary camels serve as the primary animal host reservoir for the virus.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Camel milk and meat\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Middle East Respiratory Syndrome (MERS-CoV) is a zoonotic viral respiratory illness, meaning it is transmitted from animals to humans. Extensive epidemiological studies by global health bodies have confirmed that dromedary camels serve as the primary animal host reservoir for the virus.\n\nWhile the virus can also spread through direct physical contact with live animals, humans can contract the infection by consuming contaminated, unpasteurized camel products—specifically raw camel milk and undercooked camel meat. Because the virus is heat-sensitive, global public health authorities strictly advise pasteurizing milk and thoroughly cooking meat to eliminate transmission risks.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Human excreta, Blood, Droplet"}'::jsonb),
('mc:middle east respiratory syndrome or mers-cov can be transmitted through:::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'middle east respiratory syndrome or mers-cov can be transmitted through', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Middle East Respiratory Syndrome (MERS-CoV) is a zoonotic viral respiratory illness, meaning it is transmitted from animals to humans. Extensive epidemiological studies by global health bodies have confirmed that dromedary camels serve as the primary animal host reservoir for the virus.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Camel milk and meat\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Middle East Respiratory Syndrome (MERS-CoV) is a zoonotic viral respiratory illness, meaning it is transmitted from animals to humans. Extensive epidemiological studies by global health bodies have confirmed that dromedary camels serve as the primary animal host reservoir for the virus.\n\nWhile the virus can also spread through direct physical contact with live animals, humans can contract the infection by consuming contaminated, unpasteurized camel products—specifically raw camel milk and undercooked camel meat. Because the virus is heat-sensitive, global public health authorities strictly advise pasteurizing milk and thoroughly cooking meat to eliminate transmission risks.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Human excreta, Blood, Droplet"}'::jsonb),
('mc:virgin coconut oil has antimicrobial properties. how much vco must be given to a patient weighing 176 lbs during the first day of treatment?:::48ml', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'virgin coconut oil has antimicrobial properties. how much vco must be given to a patient weighing 176 lbs during the first day of treatment?', '48ml', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Virgin coconut oil (VCO) has been studied for its potential antimicrobial and antiviral properties. Research involving VCO often explores its components, such as medium-chain triglycerides (MCTs) like lauric acid, which are believed to contribute to these properties. Clinical protocols and studies evaluate various amounts of VCO for supportive care, but determining any specific usage requires professional medical evaluation based on an individual''s health status and needs.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"48ml\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Virgin coconut oil (VCO) has been studied for its potential antimicrobial and antiviral properties. Research involving VCO often explores its components, such as medium-chain triglycerides (MCTs) like lauric acid, which are believed to contribute to these properties. Clinical protocols and studies evaluate various amounts of VCO for supportive care, but determining any specific usage requires professional medical evaluation based on an individual''s health status and needs.\n\nA specific weight-based formula was used in clinical trial protocols by the Department of Science and Technology (DOST). These clinical trials (such as the DOST-FNRI studies on the antiviral and antimicrobial properties of VCO) use a strict dosage protocol based on a patient’s body weight in kilograms to allow the gut to safely adjust:\n• First 3 days (Initial Dose): 0.6 mL of VCO per kilogram of body weight (0.6 mL/kg BW).\n• Day 4 to 28 (Succeeding Dose): 1.2 mL of VCO per kilogram of body weight (1.2 mL/kg BW).\n\n1. Convert the patient''s weight from pounds to kilograms:\n176 lbs / 2.2 = 80 kg\n2. Calculate the first-day dosage (using the initial 0.6 mL/kg factor):\n80 kg x 0.6 mL/kg = 48 mL","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: 60ml, 50ml, 96ml"}'::jsonb)
ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO public.ai_explanation_cache (id, question_hash, front_text, back_text, options, explanation, breakdown, created_at, question_text, user_answer, is_correct, ai_explanation) VALUES
('mc:hiv virus may be transmitted from mother to fetus through pregnancy. the virus is also found in breastmilk in low concentrations. the rnd must:::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'hiv virus may be transmitted from mother to fetus through pregnancy. the virus is also found in breastmilk in low concentrations. the rnd must', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Exclusive breastfeeding for the first 6 months of life, combined with maternal antiretroviral therapy (ART) that achieves viral suppression, significantly reduces postnatal mother-to-child transmission (MTCT) of HIV while drastically lowering infant mortality from infectious diseases compared to replacement feeding. Breast milk provides optimal immunological factors (such as secretory IgA, lactoferrin, and leukocytes) and complete nutrition, which protect against gastrointestinal and respiratory infections that are fatal in non-breastfed infants in high-mortality contexts.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Encourage breastfeeding exclusively\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Exclusive breastfeeding for the first 6 months of life, combined with maternal antiretroviral therapy (ART) that achieves viral suppression, significantly reduces postnatal mother-to-child transmission (MTCT) of HIV while drastically lowering infant mortality from infectious diseases compared to replacement feeding. Breast milk provides optimal immunological factors (such as secretory IgA, lactoferrin, and leukocytes) and complete nutrition, which protect against gastrointestinal and respiratory infections that are fatal in non-breastfed infants in high-mortality contexts.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Not recommend breastfeeding, Recommend infant formula, Recommend plant based milk"}'::jsonb),
('mc:the normal range of body temperature is:::36.1 to 37.2 degrees celsius', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'the normal range of body temperature is', '36.1 to 37.2 degrees Celsius', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"While 37°C (98.6°F) is universally recognized as the standard average human body temperature, normal physiological baselines safely fluctuate within a broader dynamic range.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"36.1 to 37.2 degrees Celsius\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"While 37°C (98.6°F) is universally recognized as the standard average human body temperature, normal physiological baselines safely fluctuate within a broader dynamic range.\n\nThe medically accepted range of 97°F to 99°F directly converts to 36.1°C to 37.2°C. Temperatures within this bracket are considered afebrile (normal and without fever).","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: 35 to 37 degrees Celsius, 35.5 to 37.4 degrees Celsius, 36 to 36.8 degrees Celsius"}'::jsonb),
('mc:virgin coconut oil has antimicrobial properties. how much vco must be given to a patient weighing 176 lbs during the first day of treatment?:::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'virgin coconut oil has antimicrobial properties. how much vco must be given to a patient weighing 176 lbs during the first day of treatment?', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Virgin coconut oil (VCO) has been studied for its potential antimicrobial and antiviral properties. Research involving VCO often explores its components, such as medium-chain triglycerides (MCTs) like lauric acid, which are believed to contribute to these properties. Clinical protocols and studies evaluate various amounts of VCO for supportive care, but determining any specific usage requires professional medical evaluation based on an individual''s health status and needs.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"48ml\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Virgin coconut oil (VCO) has been studied for its potential antimicrobial and antiviral properties. Research involving VCO often explores its components, such as medium-chain triglycerides (MCTs) like lauric acid, which are believed to contribute to these properties. Clinical protocols and studies evaluate various amounts of VCO for supportive care, but determining any specific usage requires professional medical evaluation based on an individual''s health status and needs.\n\nA specific weight-based formula was used in clinical trial protocols by the Department of Science and Technology (DOST). These clinical trials (such as the DOST-FNRI studies on the antiviral and antimicrobial properties of VCO) use a strict dosage protocol based on a patient’s body weight in kilograms to allow the gut to safely adjust:\n• First 3 days (Initial Dose): 0.6 mL of VCO per kilogram of body weight (0.6 mL/kg BW).\n• Day 4 to 28 (Succeeding Dose): 1.2 mL of VCO per kilogram of body weight (1.2 mL/kg BW).\n\n1. Convert the patient''s weight from pounds to kilograms:\n176 lbs / 2.2 = 80 kg\n2. Calculate the first-day dosage (using the initial 0.6 mL/kg factor):\n80 kg x 0.6 mL/kg = 48 mL","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: 60ml, 50ml, 96ml"}'::jsonb),
('mc:a food and water borne disease that affects the liver resulting to jaundice, severe anorexia, nausea, vomiting and abdominal tenderness:::hepatitis a', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'a food and water borne disease that affects the liver resulting to jaundice, severe anorexia, nausea, vomiting and abdominal tenderness', 'Hepatitis A', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Hepatitis A is a highly contagious liver infection caused by the Hepatitis A virus (HAV). It is classically defined as a food- and water-borne disease because it spreads primarily via the fecal-oral route. Transmission happens when a person ingests food or water contaminated with the feces of an infected individual (often due to poor hand hygiene or inadequate sewage treatment).","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Hepatitis A\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Hepatitis A is a highly contagious liver infection caused by the Hepatitis A virus (HAV). It is classically defined as a food- and water-borne disease because it spreads primarily via the fecal-oral route. Transmission happens when a person ingests food or water contaminated with the feces of an infected individual (often due to poor hand hygiene or inadequate sewage treatment).\n\n The virus multiplies inside the liver cells, sparking acute inflammation. This structural irritation produces a distinct, textbook cluster of gastrointestinal and hepatic symptoms:\n\t• Jaundice: Yellowing of the skin and the whites of the eyes caused by a buildup of bilirubin in the blood as the inflamed liver struggles to process it.\n\t• Severe Anorexia: A profound, sudden loss of appetite.\n\t• Nausea, Vomiting, and Abdominal Tenderness: Driven by an enlarged, inflamed liver (hepatomegaly) pressing against surrounding tissues in the right upper abdominal quadrant.\n\n• Hepatitis B & Hepatitis C: These viral infections are blood-borne and sexually transmitted pathogens. They spread via contact with infected bodily fluids, blood transfusions, or shared needles, rather than through contaminated food or drinking water.\n• Cirrhosis: This is a chronic, late-stage pathological condition characterized by irreversible scarring of the liver tissue. It is the long-term result of prolonged liver disease (like chronic alcoholism or chronic Hep B/C), not an acute foodborne illness.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Hepatitis C, Hepatitis B, Cirrhosis"}'::jsonb),
('mc:a food and water borne disease that affects the liver resulting to jaundice, severe anorexia, nausea, vomiting and abdominal tenderness:::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'a food and water borne disease that affects the liver resulting to jaundice, severe anorexia, nausea, vomiting and abdominal tenderness', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Hepatitis A is a highly contagious liver infection caused by the Hepatitis A virus (HAV). It is classically defined as a food- and water-borne disease because it spreads primarily via the fecal-oral route. Transmission happens when a person ingests food or water contaminated with the feces of an infected individual (often due to poor hand hygiene or inadequate sewage treatment).","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Hepatitis A\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Hepatitis A is a highly contagious liver infection caused by the Hepatitis A virus (HAV). It is classically defined as a food- and water-borne disease because it spreads primarily via the fecal-oral route. Transmission happens when a person ingests food or water contaminated with the feces of an infected individual (often due to poor hand hygiene or inadequate sewage treatment).\n\n The virus multiplies inside the liver cells, sparking acute inflammation. This structural irritation produces a distinct, textbook cluster of gastrointestinal and hepatic symptoms:\n\t• Jaundice: Yellowing of the skin and the whites of the eyes caused by a buildup of bilirubin in the blood as the inflamed liver struggles to process it.\n\t• Severe Anorexia: A profound, sudden loss of appetite.\n\t• Nausea, Vomiting, and Abdominal Tenderness: Driven by an enlarged, inflamed liver (hepatomegaly) pressing against surrounding tissues in the right upper abdominal quadrant.\n\n• Hepatitis B & Hepatitis C: These viral infections are blood-borne and sexually transmitted pathogens. They spread via contact with infected bodily fluids, blood transfusions, or shared needles, rather than through contaminated food or drinking water.\n• Cirrhosis: This is a chronic, late-stage pathological condition characterized by irreversible scarring of the liver tissue. It is the long-term result of prolonged liver disease (like chronic alcoholism or chronic Hep B/C), not an acute foodborne illness.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Hepatitis C, Hepatitis B, Cirrhosis"}'::jsonb),
('mc:hiv virus may be transmitted from mother to fetus through pregnancy. the virus is also found in breastmilk in low concentrations. the rnd must:::encourage breastfeeding exclusively', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'hiv virus may be transmitted from mother to fetus through pregnancy. the virus is also found in breastmilk in low concentrations. the rnd must', 'Encourage breastfeeding exclusively', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Exclusive breastfeeding for the first 6 months of life, combined with maternal antiretroviral therapy (ART) that achieves viral suppression, significantly reduces postnatal mother-to-child transmission (MTCT) of HIV while drastically lowering infant mortality from infectious diseases compared to replacement feeding. Breast milk provides optimal immunological factors (such as secretory IgA, lactoferrin, and leukocytes) and complete nutrition, which protect against gastrointestinal and respiratory infections that are fatal in non-breastfed infants in high-mortality contexts.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Encourage breastfeeding exclusively\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Exclusive breastfeeding for the first 6 months of life, combined with maternal antiretroviral therapy (ART) that achieves viral suppression, significantly reduces postnatal mother-to-child transmission (MTCT) of HIV while drastically lowering infant mortality from infectious diseases compared to replacement feeding. Breast milk provides optimal immunological factors (such as secretory IgA, lactoferrin, and leukocytes) and complete nutrition, which protect against gastrointestinal and respiratory infections that are fatal in non-breastfed infants in high-mortality contexts.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Not recommend breastfeeding, Recommend infant formula, Recommend plant based milk"}'::jsonb),
('mc:metabolic changes occur during surgery. the following nutrition related implications are true except:::sodium loss', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'metabolic changes occur during surgery. the following nutrition related implications are true except', 'Sodium loss', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Surgical stress activates the sympathetic nervous system and the renin-angiotensin-aldosterone system (RAAS), alongside non-osmotic release of antidiuretic hormone (ADH) from the posterior pituitary. Elevated circulating aldosterone acts on the distal convoluted tubules and cortical collecting ducts of the nephrons to increase sodium and water reabsorption while excreting potassium and hydrogen ions, resulting in sodium retention rather than sodium loss.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Sodium loss\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Surgical stress activates the sympathetic nervous system and the renin-angiotensin-aldosterone system (RAAS), alongside non-osmotic release of antidiuretic hormone (ADH) from the posterior pituitary. Elevated circulating aldosterone acts on the distal convoluted tubules and cortical collecting ducts of the nephrons to increase sodium and water reabsorption while excreting potassium and hydrogen ions, resulting in sodium retention rather than sodium loss.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Increased demands for Vitamin C, Increased protein requirements, Calcium loss"}'::jsonb),
('mc:metabolic changes occur during surgery. the following nutrition related implications are true except:::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'metabolic changes occur during surgery. the following nutrition related implications are true except', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Surgical stress activates the sympathetic nervous system and the renin-angiotensin-aldosterone system (RAAS), alongside non-osmotic release of antidiuretic hormone (ADH) from the posterior pituitary. Elevated circulating aldosterone acts on the distal convoluted tubules and cortical collecting ducts of the nephrons to increase sodium and water reabsorption while excreting potassium and hydrogen ions, resulting in sodium retention rather than sodium loss.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Sodium loss\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Surgical stress activates the sympathetic nervous system and the renin-angiotensin-aldosterone system (RAAS), alongside non-osmotic release of antidiuretic hormone (ADH) from the posterior pituitary. Elevated circulating aldosterone acts on the distal convoluted tubules and cortical collecting ducts of the nephrons to increase sodium and water reabsorption while excreting potassium and hydrogen ions, resulting in sodium retention rather than sodium loss.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Increased demands for Vitamin C, Increased protein requirements, Calcium loss"}'::jsonb),
('mc:prior to surgery, the patient must be put on nil per os or npo for how many hours?:::8 hours', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'prior to surgery, the patient must be put on nil per os or npo for how many hours?', '8 hours', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"In general medical and nursing board exam questions, the standard textbook baseline for a complete nil per os (NPO) order prior to elective surgery involving general anesthesia is 8 hours (often clinically phrased as \"NPO after midnight\" for a morning procedure).","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"8 hours\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"In general medical and nursing board exam questions, the standard textbook baseline for a complete nil per os (NPO) order prior to elective surgery involving general anesthesia is 8 hours (often clinically phrased as \"NPO after midnight\" for a morning procedure).\n\nThis fasting period guarantees the stomach is completely empty before anesthesia induction. This drastically minimizes the life-threatening risk of pulmonary aspiration (inhaling stomach content or vomitus into the lungs) and severe post-operative nausea.\n\nModern Clinical Guidelines (ASA)\nWhile standard multiple-choice questions typically group full NPO status into a general 8-hour window, modern American Society of Anesthesiologists (ASA) guidelines utilize a tiered approach:\n• 8 Hours: Required for heavy, fried, or fatty foods and meats (which delay gastric emptying).\n• 6 Hours: Required for light meals (e.g., toast) or non-human milk.\n• 2 Hours: Minimum restriction for clear liquids (such as water or pulp-free juices).","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: 12 hours, 16 hours, 24 hours"}'::jsonb),
('mc:prior to surgery, the patient must be put on nil per os or npo for how many hours?:::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'prior to surgery, the patient must be put on nil per os or npo for how many hours?', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"In general medical and nursing board exam questions, the standard textbook baseline for a complete nil per os (NPO) order prior to elective surgery involving general anesthesia is 8 hours (often clinically phrased as \"NPO after midnight\" for a morning procedure).","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"8 hours\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"In general medical and nursing board exam questions, the standard textbook baseline for a complete nil per os (NPO) order prior to elective surgery involving general anesthesia is 8 hours (often clinically phrased as \"NPO after midnight\" for a morning procedure).\n\nThis fasting period guarantees the stomach is completely empty before anesthesia induction. This drastically minimizes the life-threatening risk of pulmonary aspiration (inhaling stomach content or vomitus into the lungs) and severe post-operative nausea.\n\nModern Clinical Guidelines (ASA)\nWhile standard multiple-choice questions typically group full NPO status into a general 8-hour window, modern American Society of Anesthesiologists (ASA) guidelines utilize a tiered approach:\n• 8 Hours: Required for heavy, fried, or fatty foods and meats (which delay gastric emptying).\n• 6 Hours: Required for light meals (e.g., toast) or non-human milk.\n• 2 Hours: Minimum restriction for clear liquids (such as water or pulp-free juices).","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: 12 hours, 16 hours, 24 hours"}'::jsonb),
('mc:post surgery, the patient must be put on npo for 24 to 48 hours to prevent what?:::aspiration', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'post surgery, the patient must be put on npo for 24 to 48 hours to prevent what?', 'Aspiration', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"General anesthesia, opioid analgesics, and surgical stress profoundly suppress gastrointestinal motility, lower lower esophageal sphincter (LES) tone, and dull protective airway reflexes (such as the gag and cough reflexes). If oral intake is permitted while gastroparesis and neuromuscular depression persist, gastric contents, hydrochloric acid, and digestive enzymes can easily reflux into the pharynx and enter the respiratory tract, leading to aspiration pneumonitis (Mendelson''s syndrome) or bacterial pneumonia characterized by severe hypoxemia, acute lung injury, and inflammatory destruction of alveolar-capillary membranes.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Aspiration\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"General anesthesia, opioid analgesics, and surgical stress profoundly suppress gastrointestinal motility, lower lower esophageal sphincter (LES) tone, and dull protective airway reflexes (such as the gag and cough reflexes). If oral intake is permitted while gastroparesis and neuromuscular depression persist, gastric contents, hydrochloric acid, and digestive enzymes can easily reflux into the pharynx and enter the respiratory tract, leading to aspiration pneumonitis (Mendelson''s syndrome) or bacterial pneumonia characterized by severe hypoxemia, acute lung injury, and inflammatory destruction of alveolar-capillary membranes.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Diarrhea, Choking, GI distress"}'::jsonb),
('mc:post surgery, the patient must be put on npo for 24 to 48 hours to prevent what?:::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'post surgery, the patient must be put on npo for 24 to 48 hours to prevent what?', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"General anesthesia, opioid analgesics, and surgical stress profoundly suppress gastrointestinal motility, lower lower esophageal sphincter (LES) tone, and dull protective airway reflexes (such as the gag and cough reflexes). If oral intake is permitted while gastroparesis and neuromuscular depression persist, gastric contents, hydrochloric acid, and digestive enzymes can easily reflux into the pharynx and enter the respiratory tract, leading to aspiration pneumonitis (Mendelson''s syndrome) or bacterial pneumonia characterized by severe hypoxemia, acute lung injury, and inflammatory destruction of alveolar-capillary membranes.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Aspiration\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"General anesthesia, opioid analgesics, and surgical stress profoundly suppress gastrointestinal motility, lower lower esophageal sphincter (LES) tone, and dull protective airway reflexes (such as the gag and cough reflexes). If oral intake is permitted while gastroparesis and neuromuscular depression persist, gastric contents, hydrochloric acid, and digestive enzymes can easily reflux into the pharynx and enter the respiratory tract, leading to aspiration pneumonitis (Mendelson''s syndrome) or bacterial pneumonia characterized by severe hypoxemia, acute lung injury, and inflammatory destruction of alveolar-capillary membranes.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Diarrhea, Choking, GI distress"}'::jsonb),
('mc:what type of food must be served on the first day post tonsillectomy?:::very cold and mild flavored', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'what type of food must be served on the first day post tonsillectomy?', 'Very cold and mild flavored', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"A tonsillectomy leaves open, highly raw, and sensitive surgical wounds in the back of the throat. Introducing very cold foods and fluids (such as ice chips, non-red popsicles, and plain ice cream) induces immediate localized vasoconstriction (narrowing of the blood vessels). This reduces swelling, minimizes the risk of post-operative bleeding, and numbs raw nerve endings to alleviate acute throat pain.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Very cold and mild flavored\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"A tonsillectomy leaves open, highly raw, and sensitive surgical wounds in the back of the throat. Introducing very cold foods and fluids (such as ice chips, non-red popsicles, and plain ice cream) induces immediate localized vasoconstriction (narrowing of the blood vessels). This reduces swelling, minimizes the risk of post-operative bleeding, and numbs raw nerve endings to alleviate acute throat pain.\n\nThe food must be mildly flavored. Highly seasoned, spicy, salty, or acidic foods (such as citrus or tomato-based items) will cause intense chemical burning and painful irritation at the healing surgical site.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Warm and bland, Room temperature, Chilled food"}'::jsonb),
('mc:what type of food must be served on the first day post tonsillectomy?:::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'what type of food must be served on the first day post tonsillectomy?', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"A tonsillectomy leaves open, highly raw, and sensitive surgical wounds in the back of the throat. Introducing very cold foods and fluids (such as ice chips, non-red popsicles, and plain ice cream) induces immediate localized vasoconstriction (narrowing of the blood vessels). This reduces swelling, minimizes the risk of post-operative bleeding, and numbs raw nerve endings to alleviate acute throat pain.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Very cold and mild flavored\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"A tonsillectomy leaves open, highly raw, and sensitive surgical wounds in the back of the throat. Introducing very cold foods and fluids (such as ice chips, non-red popsicles, and plain ice cream) induces immediate localized vasoconstriction (narrowing of the blood vessels). This reduces swelling, minimizes the risk of post-operative bleeding, and numbs raw nerve endings to alleviate acute throat pain.\n\nThe food must be mildly flavored. Highly seasoned, spicy, salty, or acidic foods (such as citrus or tomato-based items) will cause intense chemical burning and painful irritation at the healing surgical site.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Warm and bland, Room temperature, Chilled food"}'::jsonb),
('mc:protein splitting enzyme secreted by the gastric glands and the stomach.:::pepsin', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'protein splitting enzyme secreted by the gastric glands and the stomach.', 'Pepsin', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Pepsin is the main protein-digesting enzyme in the stomach. It is secreted by the gastric glands in an inactive form called pepsinogen. Pepsin cleaves proteins to smaller polypeptides and some free amino acids.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Pepsin\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Pepsin is the main protein-digesting enzyme in the stomach. It is secreted by the gastric glands in an inactive form called pepsinogen. Pepsin cleaves proteins to smaller polypeptides and some free amino acids.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Rennin, Gastric lipase, Trypsin"}'::jsonb),
('mc:gastric surgeries result to the following nutrition related conditions except:::decreased peristalsis', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'gastric surgeries result to the following nutrition related conditions except', 'Decreased peristalsis', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Decreased peristalsis (or general sluggish movement of the intestines) is not a primary, direct nutrition-related condition caused by gastric surgeries. Peristalsis is a neuromuscular function of the intestinal tract. While systemic opioid medications or immediate post-operative anesthesia can temporarily slow it down, gastric resections themselves do not inherently induce chronic, baseline decreased peristalsis as a hallmark nutritional consequence.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Decreased peristalsis\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Decreased peristalsis (or general sluggish movement of the intestines) is not a primary, direct nutrition-related condition caused by gastric surgeries. Peristalsis is a neuromuscular function of the intestinal tract. While systemic opioid medications or immediate post-operative anesthesia can temporarily slow it down, gastric resections themselves do not inherently induce chronic, baseline decreased peristalsis as a hallmark nutritional consequence.\n\n\t• Decreased production of pepsin and HCl: Removing or bypassing large portions of the stomach (such as the fundus and body) drastically reduces the number of parietal and chief cells, cutting down the overall secretion of hydrochloric acid (HCl) and pepsinogen.\n\t• Macrocytic anemia: A lack of stomach tissue leads to a severe shortage of Intrinsic Factor, which is mandatory for absorbing Vitamin B12 in the terminal ileum. Long-term Vitamin B12 deficiency directly causes pernicious or macrocytic anemia.\n\t• Impaired fat utilization: The loss of stomach churning capacity, combined with a significant reduction in gastric lipase and altered structural transit, prevents the proper emulsification and breakdown of dietary fats, leading to lipid malabsorption.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Decreased production of pepsin and HCL, Impaired fat utilization, Macrocytic anemia"}'::jsonb),
('mc:gastric surgeries result to the following nutrition related conditions except:::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'gastric surgeries result to the following nutrition related conditions except', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Decreased peristalsis (or general sluggish movement of the intestines) is not a primary, direct nutrition-related condition caused by gastric surgeries. Peristalsis is a neuromuscular function of the intestinal tract. While systemic opioid medications or immediate post-operative anesthesia can temporarily slow it down, gastric resections themselves do not inherently induce chronic, baseline decreased peristalsis as a hallmark nutritional consequence.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Decreased peristalsis\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Decreased peristalsis (or general sluggish movement of the intestines) is not a primary, direct nutrition-related condition caused by gastric surgeries. Peristalsis is a neuromuscular function of the intestinal tract. While systemic opioid medications or immediate post-operative anesthesia can temporarily slow it down, gastric resections themselves do not inherently induce chronic, baseline decreased peristalsis as a hallmark nutritional consequence.\n\n\t• Decreased production of pepsin and HCl: Removing or bypassing large portions of the stomach (such as the fundus and body) drastically reduces the number of parietal and chief cells, cutting down the overall secretion of hydrochloric acid (HCl) and pepsinogen.\n\t• Macrocytic anemia: A lack of stomach tissue leads to a severe shortage of Intrinsic Factor, which is mandatory for absorbing Vitamin B12 in the terminal ileum. Long-term Vitamin B12 deficiency directly causes pernicious or macrocytic anemia.\n\t• Impaired fat utilization: The loss of stomach churning capacity, combined with a significant reduction in gastric lipase and altered structural transit, prevents the proper emulsification and breakdown of dietary fats, leading to lipid malabsorption.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Decreased production of pepsin and HCL, Impaired fat utilization, Macrocytic anemia"}'::jsonb),
('mc:after 7 days of gastric surgery, the patient can be given the following except:::high carbohydrate sources', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'after 7 days of gastric surgery, the patient can be given the following except', 'High carbohydrate sources', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Gastric surgery, commonly known as bariatric surgery, is a medical procedure designed to help individuals with severe obesity lose weight and improve related health conditions like type 2 diabetes, high blood pressure, and heart disease.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"High carbohydrate sources\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Gastric surgery, commonly known as bariatric surgery, is a medical procedure designed to help individuals with severe obesity lose weight and improve related health conditions like type 2 diabetes, high blood pressure, and heart disease.\n\nExactly one week (7 days) post-gastric surgery, a patient is transitioning into a smooth, full-liquid or pureed diet phase. Concentrated high carbohydrate sources—such as refined starches, sugary drinks, sweets, and juices—must be strictly avoided.\n\nIntroducing a high-carbohydrate load into a structurally altered or reduced stomach causes rapid gastric emptying into the small intestine. This hyperosmolar food mass pulls fluid into the bowel lumen, triggering acute dumping syndrome (characterized by severe abdominal cramps, nausea, rapid heart rate, sweating, and explosive diarrhea).","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Pureed vegetables, Soft protein sources, High protein and high fat diet"}'::jsonb),
('mc:which of the followng is not a substrate used to assess zinc status?:::nails', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'which of the followng is not a substrate used to assess zinc status?', 'Nails', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Nails are not a standard, validated substrate used in clinical or population-level zinc status assessment. ","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Nails\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Nails are not a standard, validated substrate used in clinical or population-level zinc status assessment. \n\nHair, saliva, and blood/plasma are all recognized substrates that have been used, with varying degrees of reliability, to assess zinc status in research and clinical settings.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Hair, Saliva, Blood"}'::jsonb),
('mc:what must an rnd recommend to prevent rapid dumping of food into jejunum?:::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'what must an rnd recommend to prevent rapid dumping of food into jejunum?', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"To prevent the rapid dumping of food contents from the stomach pouch straight into the jejunum, patients are advised to eat small, frequent meals throughout the day. This prevents overwhelming the stomach''s limited volume.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Small frequent feedings in supine position\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"To prevent the rapid dumping of food contents from the stomach pouch straight into the jejunum, patients are advised to eat small, frequent meals throughout the day. This prevents overwhelming the stomach''s limited volume.\n\nReclining or lying down in a supine position (flat on the back) or semi-recumbent position for 20 to 30 minutes immediately after eating uses gravity to actively slow down gastric emptying. This delays the rapid transit of food into the small intestine, providing immediate relief from both early and late dumping syndrome symptoms.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Simple carbohydrate sources, High fiber, low residue diet, 3 meals a day"}'::jsonb),
('mc:after 7 days of gastric surgery, the patient can be given the following except:::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'after 7 days of gastric surgery, the patient can be given the following except', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Gastric surgery, commonly known as bariatric surgery, is a medical procedure designed to help individuals with severe obesity lose weight and improve related health conditions like type 2 diabetes, high blood pressure, and heart disease.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"High carbohydrate sources\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Gastric surgery, commonly known as bariatric surgery, is a medical procedure designed to help individuals with severe obesity lose weight and improve related health conditions like type 2 diabetes, high blood pressure, and heart disease.\n\nExactly one week (7 days) post-gastric surgery, a patient is transitioning into a smooth, full-liquid or pureed diet phase. Concentrated high carbohydrate sources—such as refined starches, sugary drinks, sweets, and juices—must be strictly avoided.\n\nIntroducing a high-carbohydrate load into a structurally altered or reduced stomach causes rapid gastric emptying into the small intestine. This hyperosmolar food mass pulls fluid into the bowel lumen, triggering acute dumping syndrome (characterized by severe abdominal cramps, nausea, rapid heart rate, sweating, and explosive diarrhea).","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Pureed vegetables, Soft protein sources, High protein and high fat diet"}'::jsonb),
('mc:the patient is complaining of nausea, cramping, sweating, palpitations 10 minutes after eating. what is she experiencing?:::early dumping syndrome', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'the patient is complaining of nausea, cramping, sweating, palpitations 10 minutes after eating. what is she experiencing?', 'Early dumping syndrome', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"The symptoms of nausea, abdominal cramping, sweating, and heart palpitations occurring rapidly—within 10 to 30 minutes after eating—are the textbook diagnostic presentation of Early Dumping Syndrome.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Early dumping syndrome\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"The symptoms of nausea, abdominal cramping, sweating, and heart palpitations occurring rapidly—within 10 to 30 minutes after eating—are the textbook diagnostic presentation of Early Dumping Syndrome.\n\nThis condition occurs frequently in patients who have undergone gastric or bariatric surgeries. Because the stomach''s storage capacity or pyloric valve function is altered, a large mass of poorly digested, hyperosmolar food drops rapidly into the small intestine. This hyperosmolar mass causes a massive, sudden shift of fluid out of the bloodstream and into the intestinal lumen, leading to acute intestinal distension (causing cramps and nausea) and systemic volume depletion (causing sweating, dizziness, and palpitations).\n\n• Intermediate dumping syndrome: While some textbook sources split dumping into early and late, others refer to an intermediate phase occurring 30 minutes to 1 hour post-meal, primarily characterized by bloating and gas rather than this immediate cardiovascular and gastrointestinal surge.\n• Late Dumping Syndrome: Occurs much later—1 to 3 hours after eating—and is driven by a completely different mechanism: reactive hypoglycemia caused by a rapid spike and subsequent overshoot of insulin production.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Indigestion, Bloating, Intermediate dumping syndrome"}'::jsonb),
('mc:the patient is complaining of nausea, cramping, sweating, palpitations 10 minutes after eating. what is she experiencing?:::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'the patient is complaining of nausea, cramping, sweating, palpitations 10 minutes after eating. what is she experiencing?', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"The symptoms of nausea, abdominal cramping, sweating, and heart palpitations occurring rapidly—within 10 to 30 minutes after eating—are the textbook diagnostic presentation of Early Dumping Syndrome.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Early dumping syndrome\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"The symptoms of nausea, abdominal cramping, sweating, and heart palpitations occurring rapidly—within 10 to 30 minutes after eating—are the textbook diagnostic presentation of Early Dumping Syndrome.\n\nThis condition occurs frequently in patients who have undergone gastric or bariatric surgeries. Because the stomach''s storage capacity or pyloric valve function is altered, a large mass of poorly digested, hyperosmolar food drops rapidly into the small intestine. This hyperosmolar mass causes a massive, sudden shift of fluid out of the bloodstream and into the intestinal lumen, leading to acute intestinal distension (causing cramps and nausea) and systemic volume depletion (causing sweating, dizziness, and palpitations).\n\n• Intermediate dumping syndrome: While some textbook sources split dumping into early and late, others refer to an intermediate phase occurring 30 minutes to 1 hour post-meal, primarily characterized by bloating and gas rather than this immediate cardiovascular and gastrointestinal surge.\n• Late Dumping Syndrome: Occurs much later—1 to 3 hours after eating—and is driven by a completely different mechanism: reactive hypoglycemia caused by a rapid spike and subsequent overshoot of insulin production.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Indigestion, Bloating, Intermediate dumping syndrome"}'::jsonb),
('mc:what must an rnd recommend to prevent rapid dumping of food into jejunum?:::small frequent feedings in supine position', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'what must an rnd recommend to prevent rapid dumping of food into jejunum?', 'Small frequent feedings in supine position', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"To prevent the rapid dumping of food contents from the stomach pouch straight into the jejunum, patients are advised to eat small, frequent meals throughout the day. This prevents overwhelming the stomach''s limited volume.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Small frequent feedings in supine position\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"To prevent the rapid dumping of food contents from the stomach pouch straight into the jejunum, patients are advised to eat small, frequent meals throughout the day. This prevents overwhelming the stomach''s limited volume.\n\nReclining or lying down in a supine position (flat on the back) or semi-recumbent position for 20 to 30 minutes immediately after eating uses gravity to actively slow down gastric emptying. This delays the rapid transit of food into the small intestine, providing immediate relief from both early and late dumping syndrome symptoms.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Simple carbohydrate sources, High fiber, low residue diet, 3 meals a day"}'::jsonb),
('mc:as essential fatty acid derived from plant oils such as corn oil::::linoleic acid', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'as essential fatty acid derived from plant oils such as corn oil:', 'Linoleic acid', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Corn oil is an excellent source of essential fatty acids, which typically exceed 60% of its total composition, with the main contribution of linoleic acid (C18:2).","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Linoleic acid\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Corn oil is an excellent source of essential fatty acids, which typically exceed 60% of its total composition, with the main contribution of linoleic acid (C18:2).","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Palmitic acid, Linolenic acid, Arachidonic acid"}'::jsonb),
('mc:a patient who underwent ileostomy must be monitored for signs and symptoms of nutrient deficiencies for the following micronutrients except:::vitamin b6', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'a patient who underwent ileostomy must be monitored for signs and symptoms of nutrient deficiencies for the following micronutrients except', 'Vitamin B6', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Vitamin B6 (pyridoxine) is a water-soluble vitamin primarily absorbed in the jejunum (the middle section of the small intestine). Because an ileostomy redirects or resects parts of the ileum (the final section of the small intestine) and bypasses the large intestine, the jejunum is left completely intact. This allows Vitamin B6 to be absorbed normally, making routine monitoring for a B6 deficiency unnecessary.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Vitamin B6\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Vitamin B6 (pyridoxine) is a water-soluble vitamin primarily absorbed in the jejunum (the middle section of the small intestine). Because an ileostomy redirects or resects parts of the ileum (the final section of the small intestine) and bypasses the large intestine, the jejunum is left completely intact. This allows Vitamin B6 to be absorbed normally, making routine monitoring for a B6 deficiency unnecessary.\n\n\t• Vitamin B12: The terminal ileum is the only site in the entire gastrointestinal tract capable of absorbing Vitamin B12 (via the Intrinsic Factor complex). An ileostomy directly disrupts this area, placing the patient at an incredibly high risk for Vitamin B12 malabsorption and subsequent macrocytic/pernicious anemia.\n\t• Vitamins A & E: The ileum plays a critical role in the reabsorption of bile salts (enterohepatic circulation). When the ileum is bypassed or resected, the depletion of bile salts leads to the malabsorption of fats and fat-soluble vitamins (A, D, E, and K).","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Vitamin B12, Vitamin A, Vitamin E"}'::jsonb),
('mc:a patient who underwent ileostomy must be monitored for signs and symptoms of nutrient deficiencies for the following micronutrients except:::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'a patient who underwent ileostomy must be monitored for signs and symptoms of nutrient deficiencies for the following micronutrients except', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Vitamin B6 (pyridoxine) is a water-soluble vitamin primarily absorbed in the jejunum (the middle section of the small intestine). Because an ileostomy redirects or resects parts of the ileum (the final section of the small intestine) and bypasses the large intestine, the jejunum is left completely intact. This allows Vitamin B6 to be absorbed normally, making routine monitoring for a B6 deficiency unnecessary.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Vitamin B6\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Vitamin B6 (pyridoxine) is a water-soluble vitamin primarily absorbed in the jejunum (the middle section of the small intestine). Because an ileostomy redirects or resects parts of the ileum (the final section of the small intestine) and bypasses the large intestine, the jejunum is left completely intact. This allows Vitamin B6 to be absorbed normally, making routine monitoring for a B6 deficiency unnecessary.\n\n\t• Vitamin B12: The terminal ileum is the only site in the entire gastrointestinal tract capable of absorbing Vitamin B12 (via the Intrinsic Factor complex). An ileostomy directly disrupts this area, placing the patient at an incredibly high risk for Vitamin B12 malabsorption and subsequent macrocytic/pernicious anemia.\n\t• Vitamins A & E: The ileum plays a critical role in the reabsorption of bile salts (enterohepatic circulation). When the ileum is bypassed or resected, the depletion of bile salts leads to the malabsorption of fats and fat-soluble vitamins (A, D, E, and K).","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Vitamin B12, Vitamin A, Vitamin E"}'::jsonb),
('mc:the following are gas forming foods that must be avoided by patients with colostomy except:::squash', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'the following are gas forming foods that must be avoided by patients with colostomy except', 'Squash', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Squash (along with other well-cooked, skinless, non-cruciferous vegetables like spinach) is generally considered easy to digest and gentle on the digestive system. It does not naturally ferment aggressively or produce large amounts of gas.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Squash\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Squash (along with other well-cooked, skinless, non-cruciferous vegetables like spinach) is generally considered easy to digest and gentle on the digestive system. It does not naturally ferment aggressively or produce large amounts of gas.\n\n\t• Onion and garlic: These contain volatile sulfur compounds and fermentable carbohydrates (fructans) that cause high amounts of gas and strong stool odors.\n\t• Dairy: Milk and dairy products containing lactose are highly gas-producing, especially if a patient has any degree of baseline lactose intolerance.\n\t• Chewing gum: Chewing gum introduces a non-dietary source of gas by causing patients to continuously swallow excess air (aerophagia), leading to immediate flatus accumulation in the colostomy bag. Additionally, many chewing gums use artificial sweeteners (sugar alcohols), which further ferment into gas in the bowel.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Onion and garlic, Chewing gum, Dairy"}'::jsonb),
('mc:a patient has a high risk of developing oxalate stones. the rnd must recommend intake of high oxalate foods to be avoided. choose the food that is not high in oxalate content.:::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'a patient has a high risk of developing oxalate stones. the rnd must recommend intake of high oxalate foods to be avoided. choose the food that is not high in oxalate content.', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Bananas are naturally very low in oxalate content (typically containing less than 1–2 mg of oxalate per serving). Because they are completely safe to consume, they are highly recommended as a staple fruit choice for patients managing or preventing calcium oxalate kidney stones.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Banana\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Bananas are naturally very low in oxalate content (typically containing less than 1–2 mg of oxalate per serving). Because they are completely safe to consume, they are highly recommended as a staple fruit choice for patients managing or preventing calcium oxalate kidney stones.\n\n• Tofu: Made directly from soybeans, tofu and other soy products are notoriously high in oxalates. A standard serving of tofu can easily introduce an unsafe concentration of oxalates into the diet.\n• Chocolate: Cocoa powder and chocolate products contain very high levels of oxalic acid. Even small servings can significantly spike urinary oxalate levels.\n• Kangkong: Also known as water spinach, this dark green leafy vegetable is rich in oxalates, mirroring the high-oxalate profiles of traditional spinach and beet greens which are known to fuel calcium oxalate stone formation.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Tofu, Chocolate, Kangkong"}'::jsonb),
('mc:protein splitting enzyme secreted by the gastric glands and the stomach.:::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'protein splitting enzyme secreted by the gastric glands and the stomach.', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Pepsin is the main protein-digesting enzyme in the stomach. It is secreted by the gastric glands in an inactive form called pepsinogen. Pepsin cleaves proteins to smaller polypeptides and some free amino acids.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Pepsin\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Pepsin is the main protein-digesting enzyme in the stomach. It is secreted by the gastric glands in an inactive form called pepsinogen. Pepsin cleaves proteins to smaller polypeptides and some free amino acids.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Rennin, Gastric lipase, Trypsin"}'::jsonb),
('mc:the following are gas forming foods that must be avoided by patients with colostomy except:::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'the following are gas forming foods that must be avoided by patients with colostomy except', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Squash (along with other well-cooked, skinless, non-cruciferous vegetables like spinach) is generally considered easy to digest and gentle on the digestive system. It does not naturally ferment aggressively or produce large amounts of gas.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Squash\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Squash (along with other well-cooked, skinless, non-cruciferous vegetables like spinach) is generally considered easy to digest and gentle on the digestive system. It does not naturally ferment aggressively or produce large amounts of gas.\n\n\t• Onion and garlic: These contain volatile sulfur compounds and fermentable carbohydrates (fructans) that cause high amounts of gas and strong stool odors.\n\t• Dairy: Milk and dairy products containing lactose are highly gas-producing, especially if a patient has any degree of baseline lactose intolerance.\n\t• Chewing gum: Chewing gum introduces a non-dietary source of gas by causing patients to continuously swallow excess air (aerophagia), leading to immediate flatus accumulation in the colostomy bag. Additionally, many chewing gums use artificial sweeteners (sugar alcohols), which further ferment into gas in the bowel.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Onion and garlic, Chewing gum, Dairy"}'::jsonb),
('mc:surgery of the intestines results to changes in:::production of waste products, absorption of nutrients, and motility', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'surgery of the intestines results to changes in', 'Production of waste products, absorption of nutrients, and motility', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"• Absorption of nutrients: The small and large intestines are the primary sites for macro- and micronutrient, fluid, and electrolyte absorption. Resecting or altering these segments directly reduces the functional absorptive surface area.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Production of waste products, absorption of nutrients, and motility\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"• Absorption of nutrients: The small and large intestines are the primary sites for macro- and micronutrient, fluid, and electrolyte absorption. Resecting or altering these segments directly reduces the functional absorptive surface area.\n\t• Motility: Intestinal surgery alters the neuromuscular tissue, disrupting normal peristalsis and transit time.\n\t• Production of waste products: Because nutrient digestion and fluid reabsorption are disrupted, the consistency, volume, and composition of the fecal waste change dramatically (often resulting in loose stools or stomal output).","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Satiation, hormone level, absorption of nutrients, and motility, Appetite, weight changes, absorption of nutrients, and motility, Electrolyte balance, bowel movement, peristalsis"}'::jsonb),
