options: Protein and BCAA, Carbohydrate only, Carbohydrate and protein"}'::jsonb),
('mc:the following are adverse effects of protein overload except:::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'the following are adverse effects of protein overload except', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Fatigue is generally not considered a direct physiological adverse effect of acute protein overload. While low carbohydrate intake (often a byproduct of extreme high-protein dieting) can lead to lethargy or sluggishness from low glycogen stores, the structural overload of protein metabolism itself doesn''t characteristically induce fatigue as a primary metabolic side effect.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Fatigue\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Fatigue is generally not considered a direct physiological adverse effect of acute protein overload. While low carbohydrate intake (often a byproduct of extreme high-protein dieting) can lead to lethargy or sluggishness from low glycogen stores, the structural overload of protein metabolism itself doesn''t characteristically induce fatigue as a primary metabolic side effect.\n\n• Kidney and liver damage: Processing high loads of protein forces the liver to convert toxic byproducts (like ammonia) into urea, while forcing the kidneys to filter out this massive increase in nitrogenous waste. Over time, this significantly elevates the workload and can precipitate or accelerate kidney and liver strain, particularly in individuals with underlying or pre-existing renal compromise.\n• Calcium deficiency: High-protein intake (specifically from animal sources rich in sulfur-containing amino acids) generates a high dietary acid load in body fluids. To buffer this systemic acidity, the body resorbs bone minerals, causing an influx of calcium loss through the urine (hypercalciuria), which can compromise long-term bone density if calcium intake is inadequate.\n• Dehydration: The elimination of excess blood urea nitrogen requires a substantial amount of fluid to flush the waste out through the urinary tract. This constant osmotic diuresis rapidly depletes body fluids, leading to subclinical or clinical dehydration if the individual does not aggressively increase their water intake to compensate.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Kidney and liver damage, Dehydration, Calcium deficiency"}'::jsonb),
('mc:what type of fluid must be avoided 1 hour before a competition?:::cold milk', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'what type of fluid must be avoided 1 hour before a competition?', 'Cold milk', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Fluids high in protein and saturated fats—such as milk—take a significantly longer time to digest and exit the stomach. Consuming milk just one hour before a competition slows gastric emptying and heavily increases the risk of gastrointestinal (GI) distress, abdominal cramps, bloating, and nausea during intense physical activity.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Cold milk\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Fluids high in protein and saturated fats—such as milk—take a significantly longer time to digest and exit the stomach. Consuming milk just one hour before a competition slows gastric emptying and heavily increases the risk of gastrointestinal (GI) distress, abdominal cramps, bloating, and nausea during intense physical activity.\n\nIn the hour directly leading up to an athletic event, fluid intake should prioritize rapid gastric clearance and hydration.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Cold water, 100% fruit juice, Electrolyte drink"}'::jsonb),
('mc:what type of fluid must be avoided 1 hour before a competition?:::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'what type of fluid must be avoided 1 hour before a competition?', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Fluids high in protein and saturated fats—such as milk—take a significantly longer time to digest and exit the stomach. Consuming milk just one hour before a competition slows gastric emptying and heavily increases the risk of gastrointestinal (GI) distress, abdominal cramps, bloating, and nausea during intense physical activity.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Cold milk\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Fluids high in protein and saturated fats—such as milk—take a significantly longer time to digest and exit the stomach. Consuming milk just one hour before a competition slows gastric emptying and heavily increases the risk of gastrointestinal (GI) distress, abdominal cramps, bloating, and nausea during intense physical activity.\n\nIn the hour directly leading up to an athletic event, fluid intake should prioritize rapid gastric clearance and hydration.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Cold water, 100% fruit juice, Electrolyte drink"}'::jsonb),
('mc:dietary management for person with epilepsy::::ketogenic diet', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'dietary management for person with epilepsy:', 'Ketogenic diet', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"A ketogenic diet is a specialized, high-fat, very low-carbohydrate diet medically used to manage epilepsy, especially in patients with drug-resistant or refractory seizures. The severe reduction of carbohydrates forces the body to burn fat for fuel, generating ketone bodies that act as a stabilizing, anticonvulsant energy source for the brain.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Ketogenic diet\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"A ketogenic diet is a specialized, high-fat, very low-carbohydrate diet medically used to manage epilepsy, especially in patients with drug-resistant or refractory seizures. The severe reduction of carbohydrates forces the body to burn fat for fuel, generating ketone bodies that act as a stabilizing, anticonvulsant energy source for the brain.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Regular diet, High carohydrate, Low fat"}'::jsonb),
('mc:dietary management for person with epilepsy::::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'dietary management for person with epilepsy:', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"A ketogenic diet is a specialized, high-fat, very low-carbohydrate diet medically used to manage epilepsy, especially in patients with drug-resistant or refractory seizures. The severe reduction of carbohydrates forces the body to burn fat for fuel, generating ketone bodies that act as a stabilizing, anticonvulsant energy source for the brain.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Ketogenic diet\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"A ketogenic diet is a specialized, high-fat, very low-carbohydrate diet medically used to manage epilepsy, especially in patients with drug-resistant or refractory seizures. The severe reduction of carbohydrates forces the body to burn fat for fuel, generating ketone bodies that act as a stabilizing, anticonvulsant energy source for the brain.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Regular diet, High carohydrate, Low fat"}'::jsonb),
('mc:which supplement may delay mental fatigue among athletes?:::branched chain amino acid', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'which supplement may delay mental fatigue among athletes?', 'Branched Chain Amino Acid', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"During prolonged or high-intensity endurance exercise, free fatty acid levels in the blood rise, causing a surge in free tryptophan. Tryptophan crosses the blood-brain barrier and converts into serotonin—the neurotransmitter responsible for inducing drowsiness, muscle relaxation, and mental/central fatigue.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Branched Chain Amino Acid\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"During prolonged or high-intensity endurance exercise, free fatty acid levels in the blood rise, causing a surge in free tryptophan. Tryptophan crosses the blood-brain barrier and converts into serotonin—the neurotransmitter responsible for inducing drowsiness, muscle relaxation, and mental/central fatigue.\n\nBranched-Chain Amino Acids (BCAAs)—which include leucine, isoleucine, and valine—share the exact same transport carrier into the brain as tryptophan. By increasing BCAA concentrations in the bloodstream, these amino acids outcompete and block tryptophan from entering the brain. This effectively limits serotonin production, allowing athletes to maintain mental focus, perceive less exertion, and delay the onset of mental exhaustion during heavy training sessions.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Glutamine, Creatine Monohydrate, Probiotics"}'::jsonb),
('mc:which supplement may delay mental fatigue among athletes?:::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'which supplement may delay mental fatigue among athletes?', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"During prolonged or high-intensity endurance exercise, free fatty acid levels in the blood rise, causing a surge in free tryptophan. Tryptophan crosses the blood-brain barrier and converts into serotonin—the neurotransmitter responsible for inducing drowsiness, muscle relaxation, and mental/central fatigue.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Branched Chain Amino Acid\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"During prolonged or high-intensity endurance exercise, free fatty acid levels in the blood rise, causing a surge in free tryptophan. Tryptophan crosses the blood-brain barrier and converts into serotonin—the neurotransmitter responsible for inducing drowsiness, muscle relaxation, and mental/central fatigue.\n\nBranched-Chain Amino Acids (BCAAs)—which include leucine, isoleucine, and valine—share the exact same transport carrier into the brain as tryptophan. By increasing BCAA concentrations in the bloodstream, these amino acids outcompete and block tryptophan from entering the brain. This effectively limits serotonin production, allowing athletes to maintain mental focus, perceive less exertion, and delay the onset of mental exhaustion during heavy training sessions.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Glutamine, Creatine Monohydrate, Probiotics"}'::jsonb),
