('mc:following six months of nutrition education, caregivers demonstrated improved knowledge but household dietary diversity showed minimal improvement. which explanation is most appropriate?:::knowledge alone is insufficient; economic, environmental, cultural, and household factors also influence food choices.', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'following six months of nutrition education, caregivers demonstrated improved knowledge but household dietary diversity showed minimal improvement. which explanation is most appropriate?', 'Knowledge alone is insufficient; economic, environmental, cultural, and household factors also influence food choices.', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"In public health and community nutrition, a critical distinction is always made between outputs (gaining knowledge) and outcomes (actual behavioral change). A caregiver can perfectly understand the value of a diverse diet, but if the household faces economic barriers (poverty), environmental constraints (lack of market access to fresh produce), or deep-seated cultural preferences, they will not be able to translate that knowledge into practice.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Knowledge alone is insufficient; economic, environmental, cultural, and household factors also influence food choices.\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"In public health and community nutrition, a critical distinction is always made between outputs (gaining knowledge) and outcomes (actual behavioral change). A caregiver can perfectly understand the value of a diverse diet, but if the household faces economic barriers (poverty), environmental constraints (lack of market access to fresh produce), or deep-seated cultural preferences, they will not be able to translate that knowledge into practice.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Nutrition education should be discontinued., Participants should be required to attend more lectures., The educational materials should contain more technical information."}'::jsonb),
