('mc:which activity best demonstrates continuous quality improvement in a community nutrition program?:::regularly monitoring program indicators, identifying gaps, implementing corrective actions, and reassessing performance', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'which activity best demonstrates continuous quality improvement in a community nutrition program?', 'Regularly monitoring program indicators, identifying gaps, implementing corrective actions, and reassessing performance', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Continuous Quality Improvement (CQI) is an ongoing, systematic process. It relies on data collection, analyzing processes, testing changes, and evaluating outcomes to make a service better. Option C directly outlines this full, cyclical loop.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Regularly monitoring program indicators, identifying gaps, implementing corrective actions, and reassessing performance\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Continuous Quality Improvement (CQI) is an ongoing, systematic process. It relies on data collection, analyzing processes, testing changes, and evaluating outcomes to make a service better. Option C directly outlines this full, cyclical loop.\n\nOther options describe simple administrative, process-oriented tasks or resource acquisitions. While useful, they only represent single outputs or compliance measures; they do not inherently drive a continuous loop of evaluation and performance optimization.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Preparing accomplishment reports on time, Increasing the number of nutrition meetings conducted, Purchasing additional anthropometric equipment annually"}'::jsonb),
('mc:which activity best demonstrates continuous quality improvement in a community nutrition program?:::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'which activity best demonstrates continuous quality improvement in a community nutrition program?', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Continuous Quality Improvement (CQI) is an ongoing, systematic process. It relies on data collection, analyzing processes, testing changes, and evaluating outcomes to make a service better. Option C directly outlines this full, cyclical loop.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Regularly monitoring program indicators, identifying gaps, implementing corrective actions, and reassessing performance\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Continuous Quality Improvement (CQI) is an ongoing, systematic process. It relies on data collection, analyzing processes, testing changes, and evaluating outcomes to make a service better. Option C directly outlines this full, cyclical loop.\n\nOther options describe simple administrative, process-oriented tasks or resource acquisitions. While useful, they only represent single outputs or compliance measures; they do not inherently drive a continuous loop of evaluation and performance optimization.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Preparing accomplishment reports on time, Increasing the number of nutrition meetings conducted, Purchasing additional anthropometric equipment annually"}'::jsonb),
('mc:an evaluation showed that mothers understand the importance of exclusive breastfeeding but discontinue breastfeeding early because they return to work soon after giving birth. which intervention is most appropriate?:::address workplace support, family support, and breastfeeding counseling alongside education.', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'an evaluation showed that mothers understand the importance of exclusive breastfeeding but discontinue breastfeeding early because they return to work soon after giving birth. which intervention is most appropriate?', 'Address workplace support, family support, and breastfeeding counseling alongside education.', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"The evaluation explicitly shows that the problem is not a lack of knowledge (the mothers already understand the importance). The actual barrier is structural and environmental (returning to work). Therefore, providing more education or simply testing them again will completely fail to solve the problem.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Address workplace support, family support, and breastfeeding counseling alongside education.\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"The evaluation explicitly shows that the problem is not a lack of knowledge (the mothers already understand the importance). The actual barrier is structural and environmental (returning to work). Therefore, providing more education or simply testing them again will completely fail to solve the problem.\n\nTo support working mothers, a successful public health program must move beyond individual education and address the environment. This includes advocating for workplace support (such as lactation breaks and expression rooms), family backing, and practical counseling to manage milk storage while working.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Require mothers to attend more seminars., Distribute additional pamphlets., Increase post-test scores as the primary objective."}'::jsonb),
