('mc:which of the followng is not a substrate used to assess zinc status?:::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'which of the followng is not a substrate used to assess zinc status?', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Nails are not a standard, validated substrate used in clinical or population-level zinc status assessment. ","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Nails\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Nails are not a standard, validated substrate used in clinical or population-level zinc status assessment. \n\nHair, saliva, and blood/plasma are all recognized substrates that have been used, with varying degrees of reliability, to assess zinc status in research and clinical settings.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Hair, Saliva, Blood"}'::jsonb),
('mc:cushing''s disease is characterized by::::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'cushing''s disease is characterized by:', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Cushing''s disease is a specific type of Cushing''s syndrome caused by a benign tumor (adenoma) in the pituitary gland. This tumor secretes excessive amounts of Adrenocorticotropic Hormone (ACTH), which constantly overstimulates the adrenal glands to produce massive amounts of cortisol (the body''s primary stress hormone).","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"All of the above\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Cushing''s disease is a specific type of Cushing''s syndrome caused by a benign tumor (adenoma) in the pituitary gland. This tumor secretes excessive amounts of Adrenocorticotropic Hormone (ACTH), which constantly overstimulates the adrenal glands to produce massive amounts of cortisol (the body''s primary stress hormone).\n\n• Menstrual irregularities: Chronic high cortisol levels disrupt the normal pulsatile release of gonadotropin-releasing hormone (GnRH). This often leads to irregular periods or a complete absence of menstruation (amenorrhea) in women.\n• Wasting: Cortisol is a highly catabolic hormone. Prolonged exposure causes muscle wasting and weakness, particularly visible in the thin arms and legs of patients (proximal muscle wasting).\n• Low calcium levels: High levels of glucocorticoids significantly decrease intestinal absorption of calcium and increase its excretion through the kidneys. This contributes heavily to the bone loss, weakening, and osteoporosis seen in Cushing''s patients.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Low calcium levels, Wasting, Menstrual irregularities"}'::jsonb),
('mc:the following diseases are included in the newborn screening panel except::::congenital hyperthyroidism', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'the following diseases are included in the newborn screening panel except:', 'Congenital hyperthyroidism', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"• Newborn screening panels test for congenital hypothyroidism (an underactive thyroid gland that lacks sufficient hormone).","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Congenital hyperthyroidism\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"• Newborn screening panels test for congenital hypothyroidism (an underactive thyroid gland that lacks sufficient hormone).\n• Congenital adrenal hyperplasia (CAH) is a standard, universally included hormone disorder in newborn screening panels to prevent life-threatening adrenal crises.\n• Maple syrup urine disease (MSUD) is a classic inborn error of amino acid metabolism that is globally included in primary newborn screening panels.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Maple syrup urine disease, Congenital adrenal hyperplasia, None of the above"}'::jsonb),
('mc:the following diseases are included in the newborn screening panel except::::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'the following diseases are included in the newborn screening panel except:', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"• Newborn screening panels test for congenital hypothyroidism (an underactive thyroid gland that lacks sufficient hormone).","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Congenital hyperthyroidism\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"• Newborn screening panels test for congenital hypothyroidism (an underactive thyroid gland that lacks sufficient hormone).\n• Congenital adrenal hyperplasia (CAH) is a standard, universally included hormone disorder in newborn screening panels to prevent life-threatening adrenal crises.\n• Maple syrup urine disease (MSUD) is a classic inborn error of amino acid metabolism that is globally included in primary newborn screening panels.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Maple syrup urine disease, Congenital adrenal hyperplasia, None of the above"}'::jsonb),
('mc:phytochemicals found in hot peppers that modulates blood clotting.:::capsaicin', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'phytochemicals found in hot peppers that modulates blood clotting.', 'Capsaicin', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Capsaicin is the active, spicy chemical compound unique to chili and hot peppers. Beyond giving peppers their heat, capsaicin directly modulates blood clotting by acting as a natural blood thinner. It works by inhibiting platelet aggregation (preventing blood platelets from sticking together to form clots) and enhancing fibrinolytic activity (the body''s process of breaking down clots).","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Capsaicin\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Capsaicin is the active, spicy chemical compound unique to chili and hot peppers. Beyond giving peppers their heat, capsaicin directly modulates blood clotting by acting as a natural blood thinner. It works by inhibiting platelet aggregation (preventing blood platelets from sticking together to form clots) and enhancing fibrinolytic activity (the body''s process of breaking down clots).\n\n• Curcumin is the powerful anti-inflammatory phytochemical found in turmeric, not hot peppers.\n•  Tannins are astringent polyphenols widespread in tea, coffee, and wine that bind to proteins and can inhibit iron absorption.\n• Flavonoids are a very broad, massive class of plant pigments found in almost all fruits and vegetables.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Tannins, Flavonoids, Curcumin"}'::jsonb),
('mc:dietary management for wilson''s disease::::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'dietary management for wilson''s disease:', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Wilson''s disease is a rare genetic disorder that prevents the body from properly excreting excess copper through the liver and bile. Because the body cannot get rid of it, copper builds up to toxic levels and deposits directly into vital organs—primarily the liver, brain, and the corneas of the eyes (causing characteristic copper-colored rings called Kayser-Fleischer rings). Left untreated, this toxic accumulation can lead to severe liver failure, neurological damage, and psychiatric symptoms.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Copper-restricted diet\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Wilson''s disease is a rare genetic disorder that prevents the body from properly excreting excess copper through the liver and bile. Because the body cannot get rid of it, copper builds up to toxic levels and deposits directly into vital organs—primarily the liver, brain, and the corneas of the eyes (causing characteristic copper-colored rings called Kayser-Fleischer rings). Left untreated, this toxic accumulation can lead to severe liver failure, neurological damage, and psychiatric symptoms.\n\nThe definitive dietary management for Wilson''s disease is a strict copper-restricted diet. Patients must strictly limit their intake of copper-rich foods—such as organ meats, shellfish, mushrooms, nuts, chocolate, and dried beans. This dietary restriction is used alongside medical therapies (like copper-chelating agents or zinc supplements) to prevent further organ toxicity.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Fe-restricted diet, High K diet, Gluten-restricted diet"}'::jsonb),
('mc:phytochemicals found in hot peppers that modulates blood clotting.:::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'phytochemicals found in hot peppers that modulates blood clotting.', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Capsaicin is the active, spicy chemical compound unique to chili and hot peppers. Beyond giving peppers their heat, capsaicin directly modulates blood clotting by acting as a natural blood thinner. It works by inhibiting platelet aggregation (preventing blood platelets from sticking together to form clots) and enhancing fibrinolytic activity (the body''s process of breaking down clots).","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Capsaicin\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Capsaicin is the active, spicy chemical compound unique to chili and hot peppers. Beyond giving peppers their heat, capsaicin directly modulates blood clotting by acting as a natural blood thinner. It works by inhibiting platelet aggregation (preventing blood platelets from sticking together to form clots) and enhancing fibrinolytic activity (the body''s process of breaking down clots).\n\n• Curcumin is the powerful anti-inflammatory phytochemical found in turmeric, not hot peppers.\n•  Tannins are astringent polyphenols widespread in tea, coffee, and wine that bind to proteins and can inhibit iron absorption.\n• Flavonoids are a very broad, massive class of plant pigments found in almost all fruits and vegetables.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Tannins, Flavonoids, Curcumin"}'::jsonb),
('mc:what is the segment of gluten protein associated with an inappropriate immune response in celiac disease?:::gliadin', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'what is the segment of gluten protein associated with an inappropriate immune response in celiac disease?', 'Gliadin', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Gluten is composed of two primary proteins: gliadin and glutenin. Gliadin is the specific, alcohol-soluble fraction that contains the toxic amino acid sequences (such as the 33-mer peptide) that the immune system mistakenly recognizes as a threat in celiac disease, triggering the destructive autoimmune response.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Gliadin\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Gluten is composed of two primary proteins: gliadin and glutenin. Gliadin is the specific, alcohol-soluble fraction that contains the toxic amino acid sequences (such as the 33-mer peptide) that the immune system mistakenly recognizes as a threat in celiac disease, triggering the destructive autoimmune response.\n\n• Avenin is the specific storage protein found in oats. While some celiac patients are sensitive to it, it is distinct from gluten proteins.\n• Zein is the primary storage protein found in corn (maize) and is entirely safe for individuals with celiac disease.\n• Glutenin is the other half of the gluten protein pair responsible for dough elasticity. While it is part of gluten, it is not the primary initiator of the toxic autoimmune reaction compared to gliadin.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Glutenin, Zein, Avenin"}'::jsonb),
('mc:what is the segment of gluten protein associated with an inappropriate immune response in celiac disease?:::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'what is the segment of gluten protein associated with an inappropriate immune response in celiac disease?', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Gluten is composed of two primary proteins: gliadin and glutenin. Gliadin is the specific, alcohol-soluble fraction that contains the toxic amino acid sequences (such as the 33-mer peptide) that the immune system mistakenly recognizes as a threat in celiac disease, triggering the destructive autoimmune response.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Gliadin\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Gluten is composed of two primary proteins: gliadin and glutenin. Gliadin is the specific, alcohol-soluble fraction that contains the toxic amino acid sequences (such as the 33-mer peptide) that the immune system mistakenly recognizes as a threat in celiac disease, triggering the destructive autoimmune response.\n\n• Avenin is the specific storage protein found in oats. While some celiac patients are sensitive to it, it is distinct from gluten proteins.\n• Zein is the primary storage protein found in corn (maize) and is entirely safe for individuals with celiac disease.\n• Glutenin is the other half of the gluten protein pair responsible for dough elasticity. While it is part of gluten, it is not the primary initiator of the toxic autoimmune reaction compared to gliadin.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Glutenin, Zein, Avenin"}'::jsonb),
('mc:dietary management for wilson''s disease::::copper-restricted diet', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'dietary management for wilson''s disease:', 'Copper-restricted diet', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Wilson''s disease is a rare genetic disorder that prevents the body from properly excreting excess copper through the liver and bile. Because the body cannot get rid of it, copper builds up to toxic levels and deposits directly into vital organs—primarily the liver, brain, and the corneas of the eyes (causing characteristic copper-colored rings called Kayser-Fleischer rings). Left untreated, this toxic accumulation can lead to severe liver failure, neurological damage, and psychiatric symptoms.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Copper-restricted diet\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Wilson''s disease is a rare genetic disorder that prevents the body from properly excreting excess copper through the liver and bile. Because the body cannot get rid of it, copper builds up to toxic levels and deposits directly into vital organs—primarily the liver, brain, and the corneas of the eyes (causing characteristic copper-colored rings called Kayser-Fleischer rings). Left untreated, this toxic accumulation can lead to severe liver failure, neurological damage, and psychiatric symptoms.\n\nThe definitive dietary management for Wilson''s disease is a strict copper-restricted diet. Patients must strictly limit their intake of copper-rich foods—such as organ meats, shellfish, mushrooms, nuts, chocolate, and dried beans. This dietary restriction is used alongside medical therapies (like copper-chelating agents or zinc supplements) to prevent further organ toxicity.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Fe-restricted diet, High K diet, Gluten-restricted diet"}'::jsonb)
