('mc:which of the following is not one of the interlocking and interrelated roles of managers?:::organizational', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'which of the following is not one of the interlocking and interrelated roles of managers?', 'Organizational', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Henry Mintzberg, a management theorist, categorized managerial roles into three main categories:","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Organizational\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Henry Mintzberg, a management theorist, categorized managerial roles into three main categories:\n\n• Interpersonal roles: Roles that involve interactions with other people, including figurehead, leader, and liaison roles.\n• Informational roles: Roles that involve receiving, collecting, and disseminating information, including monitor, disseminator, and spokesperson roles.\n• Decisional roles: Roles that involve making decisions to solve problems or take advantage of opportunities, including entrepreneur, disturbance handler, resource allocator, and negotiator roles.\n\n“Organizational” does not fit into any of these categories directly as described by Mintzberg. Therefore, Organizational is not one of the interlocking and interrelated roles of managers according to this framework.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Informational, Interpersonal, Decisional"}'::jsonb),
('mc:which of the following is not one of the interlocking and interrelated roles of managers?:::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'which of the following is not one of the interlocking and interrelated roles of managers?', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Henry Mintzberg, a management theorist, categorized managerial roles into three main categories:","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Organizational\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Henry Mintzberg, a management theorist, categorized managerial roles into three main categories:\n\n• Interpersonal roles: Roles that involve interactions with other people, including figurehead, leader, and liaison roles.\n• Informational roles: Roles that involve receiving, collecting, and disseminating information, including monitor, disseminator, and spokesperson roles.\n• Decisional roles: Roles that involve making decisions to solve problems or take advantage of opportunities, including entrepreneur, disturbance handler, resource allocator, and negotiator roles.\n\n“Organizational” does not fit into any of these categories directly as described by Mintzberg. Therefore, Organizational is not one of the interlocking and interrelated roles of managers according to this framework.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Informational, Interpersonal, Decisional"}'::jsonb),
('mc:a device for collecting heat, smoke, steam, and grease vapors.:::hood', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'a device for collecting heat, smoke, steam, and grease vapors.', 'hood', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"A hood is the main device positioned over cooking equipment to capture and remove heat, smoke, steam, and grease vapors.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"hood\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"A hood is the main device positioned over cooking equipment to capture and remove heat, smoke, steam, and grease vapors.\n\n• Exhaust: Refers to the system that removes air, heat, and odors from a space, often connected to the hood in commercial kitchens.\n• Grease trap: A device used in plumbing systems to intercept grease and solids before they enter wastewater disposal systems.\n• Salamander: A type of broiler typically used in commercial kitchens to apply intense heat from above for browning or finishing dishes.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: exhaust, grease trap, salamander"}'::jsonb),
