('mc:the following are equipment used in sensory evaluation of food, except::::none of the above', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'the following are equipment used in sensory evaluation of food, except:', 'none of the above', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"• Viscosimeter: Measures the viscosity or thickness of liquids, which can be relevant in sensory evaluation, especially for texture assessment.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"none of the above\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"• Viscosimeter: Measures the viscosity or thickness of liquids, which can be relevant in sensory evaluation, especially for texture assessment.\n• Refractometer: Measures the refractive index of liquids, which is useful for assessing sugar content (Brix) in beverages and fruits, influencing taste perception.\n• Salometer: Measures the salinity or salt concentration in a solution, which can affect taste perception in salty foods.\n\nBased on these descriptions, all three instruments (viscosimeter, refractometer, and salometer) are potentially relevant in sensory evaluation depending on the specific attributes being assessed.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is none of the above because all of the listed instruments can be used in sensory evaluation of food, depending on the specific needs of the evaluation.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: viscosimeter, refractometer, salometer"}'::jsonb),
('mc:the following are equipment used in sensory evaluation of food, except::::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'the following are equipment used in sensory evaluation of food, except:', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"• Viscosimeter: Measures the viscosity or thickness of liquids, which can be relevant in sensory evaluation, especially for texture assessment.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"none of the above\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"• Viscosimeter: Measures the viscosity or thickness of liquids, which can be relevant in sensory evaluation, especially for texture assessment.\n• Refractometer: Measures the refractive index of liquids, which is useful for assessing sugar content (Brix) in beverages and fruits, influencing taste perception.\n• Salometer: Measures the salinity or salt concentration in a solution, which can affect taste perception in salty foods.\n\nBased on these descriptions, all three instruments (viscosimeter, refractometer, and salometer) are potentially relevant in sensory evaluation depending on the specific attributes being assessed.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is none of the above because all of the listed instruments can be used in sensory evaluation of food, depending on the specific needs of the evaluation.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: viscosimeter, refractometer, salometer"}'::jsonb),
('mc:ropiness refers particularly to spoilage of bread characterized by::::formation of viscous, sticky material like slime', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'ropiness refers particularly to spoilage of bread characterized by:', 'formation of viscous, sticky material like slime', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Ropiness refers particularly to spoilage of bread characterized by the formation of a viscous, sticky material like slime. This condition occurs due to microbial activity, typically from certain bacteria, Bacillus subtilis, that produce polysaccharides or exopolysaccharides that give the bread a slimy or rope-like texture. It is considered a form of spoilage that renders the bread unappealing and potentially unsafe to consume.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"formation of viscous, sticky material like slime\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Ropiness refers particularly to spoilage of bread characterized by the formation of a viscous, sticky material like slime. This condition occurs due to microbial activity, typically from certain bacteria, Bacillus subtilis, that produce polysaccharides or exopolysaccharides that give the bread a slimy or rope-like texture. It is considered a form of spoilage that renders the bread unappealing and potentially unsafe to consume.\n\n• Retrogradation: This is the process where starch molecules, particularly amylose and amylopectin, re-associate and crystallize after gelatinization, leading to the firming of bread over time. It’s one of the main causes of bread staling, making the bread crumb harder and less palatable.\n• Staling: This refers to the overall process of bread becoming stale, which includes retrogradation, moisture loss, and changes in texture. Staling makes bread crumb dry, crumbly, and less flavorful.\n• Drying: This occurs as bread loses moisture over time, contributing to the staling process. The bread becomes harder and less soft, affecting its texture and palatability.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: drying, staling, retrogradation"}'::jsonb),
('mc:ropiness refers particularly to spoilage of bread characterized by::::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'ropiness refers particularly to spoilage of bread characterized by:', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Ropiness refers particularly to spoilage of bread characterized by the formation of a viscous, sticky material like slime. This condition occurs due to microbial activity, typically from certain bacteria, Bacillus subtilis, that produce polysaccharides or exopolysaccharides that give the bread a slimy or rope-like texture. It is considered a form of spoilage that renders the bread unappealing and potentially unsafe to consume.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"formation of viscous, sticky material like slime\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Ropiness refers particularly to spoilage of bread characterized by the formation of a viscous, sticky material like slime. This condition occurs due to microbial activity, typically from certain bacteria, Bacillus subtilis, that produce polysaccharides or exopolysaccharides that give the bread a slimy or rope-like texture. It is considered a form of spoilage that renders the bread unappealing and potentially unsafe to consume.\n\n• Retrogradation: This is the process where starch molecules, particularly amylose and amylopectin, re-associate and crystallize after gelatinization, leading to the firming of bread over time. It’s one of the main causes of bread staling, making the bread crumb harder and less palatable.\n• Staling: This refers to the overall process of bread becoming stale, which includes retrogradation, moisture loss, and changes in texture. Staling makes bread crumb dry, crumbly, and less flavorful.\n• Drying: This occurs as bread loses moisture over time, contributing to the staling process. The bread becomes harder and less soft, affecting its texture and palatability.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: drying, staling, retrogradation"}'::jsonb),
('mc:burong talangka is an example of::::fermented product', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'burong talangka is an example of:', 'Fermented product', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Burong Talangka is a Filipino delicacy made from fermented rice and field crabs (talangka). The fermentation process involves the natural breakdown of carbohydrates by microorganisms (often lactic acid bacteria) present in the rice, which produces acids that help preserve and flavor the crab meat. This fermentation process gives Burong Talangka its distinctive tangy and salty flavor.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Fermented product\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Burong Talangka is a Filipino delicacy made from fermented rice and field crabs (talangka). The fermentation process involves the natural breakdown of carbohydrates by microorganisms (often lactic acid bacteria) present in the rice, which produces acids that help preserve and flavor the crab meat. This fermentation process gives Burong Talangka its distinctive tangy and salty flavor.\n\nWhile pickled and salted products also undergo preservation processes, Burong Talangka specifically relies on fermentation to achieve its characteristic taste and texture. Therefore, the correct answer is fermented product.\n\n• Pickling vs. Fermentation: Pickling typically involves immersing food in an acidic solution (like vinegar) to preserve it, which is different from the natural fermentation process that occurs in Burong Talangka. While pickling can also involve brine (saltwater), it does not involve the same microbial activity that defines fermentation.\n• Salted Product: While salt is a key ingredient in Burong Talangka, the product is not merely salted; the primary preservation and flavor development come from fermentation, not just the salting process.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Pickled product, Salted product, All of the above"}'::jsonb),
('mc:burong talangka is an example of::::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'burong talangka is an example of:', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Burong Talangka is a Filipino delicacy made from fermented rice and field crabs (talangka). The fermentation process involves the natural breakdown of carbohydrates by microorganisms (often lactic acid bacteria) present in the rice, which produces acids that help preserve and flavor the crab meat. This fermentation process gives Burong Talangka its distinctive tangy and salty flavor.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Fermented product\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Burong Talangka is a Filipino delicacy made from fermented rice and field crabs (talangka). The fermentation process involves the natural breakdown of carbohydrates by microorganisms (often lactic acid bacteria) present in the rice, which produces acids that help preserve and flavor the crab meat. This fermentation process gives Burong Talangka its distinctive tangy and salty flavor.\n\nWhile pickled and salted products also undergo preservation processes, Burong Talangka specifically relies on fermentation to achieve its characteristic taste and texture. Therefore, the correct answer is fermented product.\n\n• Pickling vs. Fermentation: Pickling typically involves immersing food in an acidic solution (like vinegar) to preserve it, which is different from the natural fermentation process that occurs in Burong Talangka. While pickling can also involve brine (saltwater), it does not involve the same microbial activity that defines fermentation.\n• Salted Product: While salt is a key ingredient in Burong Talangka, the product is not merely salted; the primary preservation and flavor development come from fermentation, not just the salting process.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Pickled product, Salted product, All of the above"}'::jsonb),
('mc:addition of salt or sugar to a liquid will affect its boiling point by::::increasing boiling point', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'addition of salt or sugar to a liquid will affect its boiling point by:', 'increasing boiling point', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"When a non-volatile solute (such as salt or sugar) is dissolved in a solvent (such as water), it disrupts the formation of solvent molecules at the surface, which are responsible for vapor pressure and boiling.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"increasing boiling point\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"When a non-volatile solute (such as salt or sugar) is dissolved in a solvent (such as water), it disrupts the formation of solvent molecules at the surface, which are responsible for vapor pressure and boiling.\n\nThe presence of the solute lowers the vapor pressure of the solvent. According to Raoult’s Law, the boiling point of a solution is higher than that of the pure solvent, depending on the concentration of the solute.\n\nTherefore, adding salt or sugar to a liquid (such as water) will increase its boiling point compared to pure water.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: decreasing boiling pint, increase and then decrease boiling point, no effect"}'::jsonb),
('mc:addition of salt or sugar to a liquid will affect its boiling point by::::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'addition of salt or sugar to a liquid will affect its boiling point by:', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"When a non-volatile solute (such as salt or sugar) is dissolved in a solvent (such as water), it disrupts the formation of solvent molecules at the surface, which are responsible for vapor pressure and boiling.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"increasing boiling point\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"When a non-volatile solute (such as salt or sugar) is dissolved in a solvent (such as water), it disrupts the formation of solvent molecules at the surface, which are responsible for vapor pressure and boiling.\n\nThe presence of the solute lowers the vapor pressure of the solvent. According to Raoult’s Law, the boiling point of a solution is higher than that of the pure solvent, depending on the concentration of the solute.\n\nTherefore, adding salt or sugar to a liquid (such as water) will increase its boiling point compared to pure water.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: decreasing boiling pint, increase and then decrease boiling point, no effect"}'::jsonb),
('mc:produced by fermentation involving lactic acid bacteria.:::yogurt', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'produced by fermentation involving lactic acid bacteria.', 'yogurt', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Yogurt is made by fermenting milk with lactic acid bacteria, such as Lactobacillus bulgaricus and Streptococcus thermophilus, which convert lactose (milk sugar) into lactic acid, giving yogurt its tangy flavor.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"yogurt\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Yogurt is made by fermenting milk with lactic acid bacteria, such as Lactobacillus bulgaricus and Streptococcus thermophilus, which convert lactose (milk sugar) into lactic acid, giving yogurt its tangy flavor.\n\n• Vinegar: Produced by the fermentation of ethanol (alcohol) into acetic acid by acetic acid bacteria, not lactic acid bacteria.\n• Coffee: Not produced by lactic acid fermentation. While some lactic acid bacteria may be involved during the fermentation process of coffee beans, the final product is not a result of lactic acid fermentation.\n• Beer: Produced by the fermentation of sugars by yeast (Saccharomyces cerevisiae) into alcohol and carbon dioxide. Lactic acid bacteria are not typically involved in the main fermentation process, although they can be involved in sour beers.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: vinegar, coffee, beer"}'::jsonb),
('mc:produced by fermentation involving lactic acid bacteria.:::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'produced by fermentation involving lactic acid bacteria.', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Yogurt is made by fermenting milk with lactic acid bacteria, such as Lactobacillus bulgaricus and Streptococcus thermophilus, which convert lactose (milk sugar) into lactic acid, giving yogurt its tangy flavor.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"yogurt\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Yogurt is made by fermenting milk with lactic acid bacteria, such as Lactobacillus bulgaricus and Streptococcus thermophilus, which convert lactose (milk sugar) into lactic acid, giving yogurt its tangy flavor.\n\n• Vinegar: Produced by the fermentation of ethanol (alcohol) into acetic acid by acetic acid bacteria, not lactic acid bacteria.\n• Coffee: Not produced by lactic acid fermentation. While some lactic acid bacteria may be involved during the fermentation process of coffee beans, the final product is not a result of lactic acid fermentation.\n• Beer: Produced by the fermentation of sugars by yeast (Saccharomyces cerevisiae) into alcohol and carbon dioxide. Lactic acid bacteria are not typically involved in the main fermentation process, although they can be involved in sour beers.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: vinegar, coffee, beer"}'::jsonb),
('mc:the following wine producing countries are considered to be "old world" wine regions except::::argentina', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'the following wine producing countries are considered to be "old world" wine regions except:', 'Argentina', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"The term “Old World” in wine terminology generally refers to wine-producing regions that have a long history of winemaking tradition and are located in Europe. The countries typically included in the Old World wine regions are:","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Argentina\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"The term “Old World” in wine terminology generally refers to wine-producing regions that have a long history of winemaking tradition and are located in Europe. The countries typically included in the Old World wine regions are:\n• France\n• Italy\n• Germany\nThese countries have deep-rooted histories in winemaking and are known for their traditional wine styles and techniques.\n\nArgentina, on the other hand, is considered a “New World” wine region. The New World refers to wine-producing regions outside of Europe, which have developed their wine industries more recently and often have different winemaking styles and practices compared to the Old World.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: France, Italy, Germany"}'::jsonb),
('mc:the following wine producing countries are considered to be "old world" wine regions except::::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'the following wine producing countries are considered to be "old world" wine regions except:', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"The term “Old World” in wine terminology generally refers to wine-producing regions that have a long history of winemaking tradition and are located in Europe. The countries typically included in the Old World wine regions are:","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Argentina\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"The term “Old World” in wine terminology generally refers to wine-producing regions that have a long history of winemaking tradition and are located in Europe. The countries typically included in the Old World wine regions are:\n• France\n• Italy\n• Germany\nThese countries have deep-rooted histories in winemaking and are known for their traditional wine styles and techniques.\n\nArgentina, on the other hand, is considered a “New World” wine region. The New World refers to wine-producing regions outside of Europe, which have developed their wine industries more recently and often have different winemaking styles and practices compared to the Old World.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: France, Italy, Germany"}'::jsonb),
('mc:callos is usually made from::::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'callos is usually made from:', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Callos is a classic Spanish-influenced Filipino stew whose primary and defining ingredient is beef tripe (ox tripe). The dish features tenderized tripe, often slow-cooked or pressure-cooked along with ox feet, chorizo de Bilbao, garbanzo beans, and bell peppers in a rich, savory, paprika-infused tomato sauce.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Tripe\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Callos is a classic Spanish-influenced Filipino stew whose primary and defining ingredient is beef tripe (ox tripe). The dish features tenderized tripe, often slow-cooked or pressure-cooked along with ox feet, chorizo de Bilbao, garbanzo beans, and bell peppers in a rich, savory, paprika-infused tomato sauce.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Liempo, Tenderloin, Brisket"}'::jsonb),
('mc:an act to strengthen the food safery regulatory system in the country::::ra 10611', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'an act to strengthen the food safery regulatory system in the country:', 'RA 10611', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"RA 10611, also known as the “Food Safety Act of 2013,” aims to protect public health and ensure food safety by providing the legal framework for the regulation, inspection, and monitoring of food products in the Philippines. It consolidates and harmonizes food safety standards and practices, covering the entire food chain from production to consumption.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"RA 10611\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"RA 10611, also known as the “Food Safety Act of 2013,” aims to protect public health and ensure food safety by providing the legal framework for the regulation, inspection, and monitoring of food products in the Philippines. It consolidates and harmonizes food safety standards and practices, covering the entire food chain from production to consumption.\n\n• PD 856: Presidential Decree No. 856, also known as the “Code on Sanitation of the Philippines,” which covers general sanitation practices including those related to food. \n• EO 51: Executive Order No. 51, which regulates the retail price of certain basic necessities and prime commodities.\n• RA 3720: Republic Act No. 3720, also known as the “Food, Drugs, Devices and Cosmetics Act,” which regulates food, drugs, devices, and cosmetics but has been superseded by RA 9711 and RA 10611 in terms of food safety provisions.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: PD 856, EO 51, RA 3720"}'::jsonb),
('mc:an act to strengthen the food safery regulatory system in the country::::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'an act to strengthen the food safery regulatory system in the country:', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"RA 10611, also known as the “Food Safety Act of 2013,” aims to protect public health and ensure food safety by providing the legal framework for the regulation, inspection, and monitoring of food products in the Philippines. It consolidates and harmonizes food safety standards and practices, covering the entire food chain from production to consumption.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"RA 10611\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"RA 10611, also known as the “Food Safety Act of 2013,” aims to protect public health and ensure food safety by providing the legal framework for the regulation, inspection, and monitoring of food products in the Philippines. It consolidates and harmonizes food safety standards and practices, covering the entire food chain from production to consumption.\n\n• PD 856: Presidential Decree No. 856, also known as the “Code on Sanitation of the Philippines,” which covers general sanitation practices including those related to food. \n• EO 51: Executive Order No. 51, which regulates the retail price of certain basic necessities and prime commodities.\n• RA 3720: Republic Act No. 3720, also known as the “Food, Drugs, Devices and Cosmetics Act,” which regulates food, drugs, devices, and cosmetics but has been superseded by RA 9711 and RA 10611 in terms of food safety provisions.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: PD 856, EO 51, RA 3720"}'::jsonb),
('mc:the flour with the highest protein content.:::bread flour', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'the flour with the highest protein content.', 'bread flour', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Bread flour is high in protein, usually around 12-14%. The higher protein content contributes to gluten formation, which gives bread its structure and elasticity.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"bread flour\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Bread flour is high in protein, usually around 12-14%. The higher protein content contributes to gluten formation, which gives bread its structure and elasticity.\n\n• Cake flour: Cake flour has the lowest protein content, around 7-9%. It is finely milled and has a lower protein content to produce tender cakes with a finer crumb.\n• Pastry flour: Pastry flour has a moderate protein content, typically around 9-10%. It strikes a balance between bread flour and cake flour, suitable for making pastries and some breads that require a tender texture.\n• All-purpose flour: All-purpose flour is a blend of hard and soft wheat flours, usually containing around 10-12% protein. It can be used for a variety of baking purposes, though it is not as high in protein as bread flour.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: pastry flour, cake flour, all purpose flour"}'::jsonb),
('mc:the flour with the highest protein content.:::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'the flour with the highest protein content.', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Bread flour is high in protein, usually around 12-14%. The higher protein content contributes to gluten formation, which gives bread its structure and elasticity.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"bread flour\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Bread flour is high in protein, usually around 12-14%. The higher protein content contributes to gluten formation, which gives bread its structure and elasticity.\n\n• Cake flour: Cake flour has the lowest protein content, around 7-9%. It is finely milled and has a lower protein content to produce tender cakes with a finer crumb.\n• Pastry flour: Pastry flour has a moderate protein content, typically around 9-10%. It strikes a balance between bread flour and cake flour, suitable for making pastries and some breads that require a tender texture.\n• All-purpose flour: All-purpose flour is a blend of hard and soft wheat flours, usually containing around 10-12% protein. It can be used for a variety of baking purposes, though it is not as high in protein as bread flour.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: pastry flour, cake flour, all purpose flour"}'::jsonb),
('mc:hygroscopicity is property associated with::::sugar', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'hygroscopicity is property associated with:', 'sugar', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Hygroscopicity refers to the ability of a substance to absorb moisture from the air. Sugars, such as sucrose, glucose, and fructose, are known to be hygroscopic, meaning they readily attract and hold water molecules from their surroundings. This property can affect the texture and stability of foods, especially in baked goods and confections.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"sugar\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Hygroscopicity refers to the ability of a substance to absorb moisture from the air. Sugars, such as sucrose, glucose, and fructose, are known to be hygroscopic, meaning they readily attract and hold water molecules from their surroundings. This property can affect the texture and stability of foods, especially in baked goods and confections.\n\nWater itself is not hygroscopic since it is the substance that other materials absorb. Oils are generally non-hygroscopic.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: water, oils, none of the above"}'::jsonb),
('mc:hygroscopicity is property associated with::::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'hygroscopicity is property associated with:', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Hygroscopicity refers to the ability of a substance to absorb moisture from the air. Sugars, such as sucrose, glucose, and fructose, are known to be hygroscopic, meaning they readily attract and hold water molecules from their surroundings. This property can affect the texture and stability of foods, especially in baked goods and confections.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"sugar\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Hygroscopicity refers to the ability of a substance to absorb moisture from the air. Sugars, such as sucrose, glucose, and fructose, are known to be hygroscopic, meaning they readily attract and hold water molecules from their surroundings. This property can affect the texture and stability of foods, especially in baked goods and confections.\n\nWater itself is not hygroscopic since it is the substance that other materials absorb. Oils are generally non-hygroscopic.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: water, oils, none of the above"}'::jsonb),
('mc:coriander is also::::all of the above', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'coriander is also:', 'all of the above', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"In culinary terms, “coriander” refers to the seeds of the coriander plant, which are used as a spice. On the other hand, “cilantro” refers to the leaves and stems of the same plant, which are used as an herb in cooking. Both coriander (seeds) and cilantro (leaves/stems) come from the Coriandrum sativum plant.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"all of the above\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"In culinary terms, “coriander” refers to the seeds of the coriander plant, which are used as a spice. On the other hand, “cilantro” refers to the leaves and stems of the same plant, which are used as an herb in cooking. Both coriander (seeds) and cilantro (leaves/stems) come from the Coriandrum sativum plant.\n\nWansoy is the Filipino term for cilantro, specifically referring to the leaves and stems of the coriander plant.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is all of the above: coriander, cilantro, and wansoy all refer to different parts of the same plant, Coriandrum sativum.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: cilantro, wansoy, none of the above"}'::jsonb),
('mc:coriander is also::::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'coriander is also:', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"In culinary terms, “coriander” refers to the seeds of the coriander plant, which are used as a spice. On the other hand, “cilantro” refers to the leaves and stems of the same plant, which are used as an herb in cooking. Both coriander (seeds) and cilantro (leaves/stems) come from the Coriandrum sativum plant.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"all of the above\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"In culinary terms, “coriander” refers to the seeds of the coriander plant, which are used as a spice. On the other hand, “cilantro” refers to the leaves and stems of the same plant, which are used as an herb in cooking. Both coriander (seeds) and cilantro (leaves/stems) come from the Coriandrum sativum plant.\n\nWansoy is the Filipino term for cilantro, specifically referring to the leaves and stems of the coriander plant.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is all of the above: coriander, cilantro, and wansoy all refer to different parts of the same plant, Coriandrum sativum.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: cilantro, wansoy, none of the above"}'::jsonb),
('mc:callos is usually made from::::tripe', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'callos is usually made from:', 'Tripe', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Callos is a classic Spanish-influenced Filipino stew whose primary and defining ingredient is beef tripe (ox tripe). The dish features tenderized tripe, often slow-cooked or pressure-cooked along with ox feet, chorizo de Bilbao, garbanzo beans, and bell peppers in a rich, savory, paprika-infused tomato sauce.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Tripe\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Callos is a classic Spanish-influenced Filipino stew whose primary and defining ingredient is beef tripe (ox tripe). The dish features tenderized tripe, often slow-cooked or pressure-cooked along with ox feet, chorizo de Bilbao, garbanzo beans, and bell peppers in a rich, savory, paprika-infused tomato sauce.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Liempo, Tenderloin, Brisket"}'::jsonb),
('mc:a dark ale with heavy foam, bitter flavor and lighter body.:::porter ale', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'a dark ale with heavy foam, bitter flavor and lighter body.', 'Porter ale', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Stout is a type of dark beer known for its rich, roasted malt flavors, which can include notes of coffee, chocolate, and caramel. It often has a creamy, thick foam due to the use of nitrogen or high carbonation levels. Stouts can vary in bitterness, but many varieties have a notable bitterness from roasted malts and hops.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Porter ale\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Stout is a type of dark beer known for its rich, roasted malt flavors, which can include notes of coffee, chocolate, and caramel. It often has a creamy, thick foam due to the use of nitrogen or high carbonation levels. Stouts can vary in bitterness, but many varieties have a notable bitterness from roasted malts and hops.\n\nA Porter is also a dark beer similar to Stout but typically lighter in body and with a slightly different malt profile. Both Stouts and Porters can exhibit similar characteristics, but Stouts are generally associated with a heavier body and more pronounced bitterness.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Stout ale, Lager beer, Bock beer"}'::jsonb),
('mc:a dark ale with heavy foam, bitter flavor and lighter body.:::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'a dark ale with heavy foam, bitter flavor and lighter body.', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Stout is a type of dark beer known for its rich, roasted malt flavors, which can include notes of coffee, chocolate, and caramel. It often has a creamy, thick foam due to the use of nitrogen or high carbonation levels. Stouts can vary in bitterness, but many varieties have a notable bitterness from roasted malts and hops.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Porter ale\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Stout is a type of dark beer known for its rich, roasted malt flavors, which can include notes of coffee, chocolate, and caramel. It often has a creamy, thick foam due to the use of nitrogen or high carbonation levels. Stouts can vary in bitterness, but many varieties have a notable bitterness from roasted malts and hops.\n\nA Porter is also a dark beer similar to Stout but typically lighter in body and with a slightly different malt profile. Both Stouts and Porters can exhibit similar characteristics, but Stouts are generally associated with a heavier body and more pronounced bitterness.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Stout ale, Lager beer, Bock beer"}'::jsonb),
('mc:example of fixed cost::::salaries of the managers', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'example of fixed cost:', 'salaries of the managers', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"A fixed cost is a cost that remains constant regardless of the level of production or sales. Salaries of the managers are typically a fixed cost because managers’ salaries are usually set amounts that do not vary based on the number of customers served or meals prepared.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"salaries of the managers\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"A fixed cost is a cost that remains constant regardless of the level of production or sales. Salaries of the managers are typically a fixed cost because managers’ salaries are usually set amounts that do not vary based on the number of customers served or meals prepared.\n\n• Wages of waiters and service personnel: This is generally a variable cost because it can fluctuate with the level of service provided, such as the number of customers served or hours worked.\n• Wages of kitchen and dining staff: Similar to waiters and service personnel, wages for kitchen and dining staff can vary based on the level of activity in the restaurant.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer among the options provided is salaries of the managers. Fixed costs remain constant over a specific period regardless of production or sales levels.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: wages of waiters and service personnel, wages of kitchen and dining staff, all of the above"}'::jsonb),
('mc:example of fixed cost::::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'example of fixed cost:', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"A fixed cost is a cost that remains constant regardless of the level of production or sales. Salaries of the managers are typically a fixed cost because managers’ salaries are usually set amounts that do not vary based on the number of customers served or meals prepared.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"salaries of the managers\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"A fixed cost is a cost that remains constant regardless of the level of production or sales. Salaries of the managers are typically a fixed cost because managers’ salaries are usually set amounts that do not vary based on the number of customers served or meals prepared.\n\n• Wages of waiters and service personnel: This is generally a variable cost because it can fluctuate with the level of service provided, such as the number of customers served or hours worked.\n• Wages of kitchen and dining staff: Similar to waiters and service personnel, wages for kitchen and dining staff can vary based on the level of activity in the restaurant.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer among the options provided is salaries of the managers. Fixed costs remain constant over a specific period regardless of production or sales levels.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: wages of waiters and service personnel, wages of kitchen and dining staff, all of the above"}'::jsonb),
('mc:zinc is given to burned patients to::::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'zinc is given to burned patients to:', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Major burns cause extensive exudative loss of trace minerals through the open wounds. Zinc plays an indispensable role in every stage of tissue repair, including cellular proliferation, collagen synthesis, and re-epithelialization.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Promote wound healing\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Major burns cause extensive exudative loss of trace minerals through the open wounds. Zinc plays an indispensable role in every stage of tissue repair, including cellular proliferation, collagen synthesis, and re-epithelialization.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Prevent diarrhea, To supply oxidative enzymes, All of the above"}'::jsonb),
('mc:black discoloration in canned products may be caused by::::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'black discoloration in canned products may be caused by:', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"• Bacillus nigrificans: This bacterium can produce black pigment and cause discoloration in canned foods.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"All of the above\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"• Bacillus nigrificans: This bacterium can produce black pigment and cause discoloration in canned foods.\n• Iron sulfide formation: Reaction between iron in the can and sulfur compounds in the food can lead to black or dark discoloration.\n• Hard water containing minerals: The minerals in hard water can interact with the food and contribute to color changes, including black discoloration.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Bacillus nigrificans, Iron sulfide formation, Hard water containing minerals"}'::jsonb),
('mc:sugar dissolved in water to form a syrup is a::::true solution', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'sugar dissolved in water to form a syrup is a:', 'true solution', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"In a true solution, the solute (sugar) is completely dissolved and evenly distributed in the solvent (water) at a molecular level. The sugar molecules become dispersed throughout the water, forming a homogeneous mixture where the sugar molecules are not visible to the naked eye and do not settle out over time.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"true solution\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"In a true solution, the solute (sugar) is completely dissolved and evenly distributed in the solvent (water) at a molecular level. The sugar molecules become dispersed throughout the water, forming a homogeneous mixture where the sugar molecules are not visible to the naked eye and do not settle out over time.\n\n• Mixture: A combination of substances that are physically combined but not chemically bonded.\n• Suspension: A mixture in which particles of a substance are dispersed throughout a liquid but are large enough to settle out over time. This does not describe sugar dissolved in water.\n• Colloidal dispersion: A mixture in which particles of one substance are dispersed throughout another substance at the molecular or microscopic level. Sugar dissolved in water does not form a colloidal dispersion because the sugar molecules are dissolved at the molecular level.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: mixture, suspension, colloidal dispersion"}'::jsonb),
('mc:sugar dissolved in water to form a syrup is a::::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'sugar dissolved in water to form a syrup is a:', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"In a true solution, the solute (sugar) is completely dissolved and evenly distributed in the solvent (water) at a molecular level. The sugar molecules become dispersed throughout the water, forming a homogeneous mixture where the sugar molecules are not visible to the naked eye and do not settle out over time.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"true solution\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"In a true solution, the solute (sugar) is completely dissolved and evenly distributed in the solvent (water) at a molecular level. The sugar molecules become dispersed throughout the water, forming a homogeneous mixture where the sugar molecules are not visible to the naked eye and do not settle out over time.\n\n• Mixture: A combination of substances that are physically combined but not chemically bonded.\n• Suspension: A mixture in which particles of a substance are dispersed throughout a liquid but are large enough to settle out over time. This does not describe sugar dissolved in water.\n• Colloidal dispersion: A mixture in which particles of one substance are dispersed throughout another substance at the molecular or microscopic level. Sugar dissolved in water does not form a colloidal dispersion because the sugar molecules are dissolved at the molecular level.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: mixture, suspension, colloidal dispersion"}'::jsonb),
('mc:the flavor and aroma of fruits and vegetables are due to::::volatile acids', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'the flavor and aroma of fruits and vegetables are due to:', 'volatile acids', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Volatile compounds are organic molecules that can evaporate at room temperature, contributing to the characteristic aroma and flavor of fruits and vegetables.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"volatile acids\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Volatile compounds are organic molecules that can evaporate at room temperature, contributing to the characteristic aroma and flavor of fruits and vegetables.\n\nWhile pigments, organic acids, and sulfur compounds can also influence the taste and aroma of fruits and vegetables to some extent, volatile compounds play a central role in determining their characteristic flavors and aromas.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: pigments, organic acids, sulfur compounds"}'::jsonb),
('mc:the flavor and aroma of fruits and vegetables are due to::::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'the flavor and aroma of fruits and vegetables are due to:', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Volatile compounds are organic molecules that can evaporate at room temperature, contributing to the characteristic aroma and flavor of fruits and vegetables.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"volatile acids\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Volatile compounds are organic molecules that can evaporate at room temperature, contributing to the characteristic aroma and flavor of fruits and vegetables.\n\nWhile pigments, organic acids, and sulfur compounds can also influence the taste and aroma of fruits and vegetables to some extent, volatile compounds play a central role in determining their characteristic flavors and aromas.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: pigments, organic acids, sulfur compounds"}'::jsonb),
('mc:a bacterium that can cause food poisoning through the production of toxins in improperly stored cooked foods, particularly rice and starchy foods.:::bacillus cereus', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'a bacterium that can cause food poisoning through the production of toxins in improperly stored cooked foods, particularly rice and starchy foods.', 'bacillus cereus', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Bacillus cereus is a spore-forming bacterium famously associated with \"fried rice syndrome.\" Its heat-resistant spores can survive the initial cooking process of rice or other starchy foods.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"bacillus cereus\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Bacillus cereus is a spore-forming bacterium famously associated with \"fried rice syndrome.\" Its heat-resistant spores can survive the initial cooking process of rice or other starchy foods.\n\nIf cooked food is left at room temperature (the temperature danger zone) or improperly stored, these spores germinate, multiply, and produce dangerous toxins.\n\nThe emetic (vomiting) toxin produced by Bacillus cereus is highly heat-stable, meaning that even reheating the food thoroughly will not destroy the toxin once it has formed.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: campylobacter jejuni, e. coli, trichinella spiralis"}'::jsonb)
