('mc:type of organization applicable to a growing organization, with diversified responsibilities, in need of coordinators and specialists to assist.:::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'type of organization applicable to a growing organization, with diversified responsibilities, in need of coordinators and specialists to assist.', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Line and staff type of organization combines elements of both line and staff functions. In addition to the direct line of authority (line functions), it includes specialized support and advisory roles (staff functions) such as specialists, coordinators, and support staff to assist the line managers.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"line and staff type\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Line and staff type of organization combines elements of both line and staff functions. In addition to the direct line of authority (line functions), it includes specialized support and advisory roles (staff functions) such as specialists, coordinators, and support staff to assist the line managers.\n\n• Line type: In a line organization, authority flows in a direct line from top to bottom, with each employee reporting to one supervisor. It is suitable for small organizations with simple structures and clear chains of command.\n• Functional type: This refers to organizations where employees are grouped by their specialty areas or functions (e.g., marketing, finance, operations). It is common in larger organizations where efficiency and specialization are key.\n• Line and functional type: This is not a standard organizational type. It may refer to a hybrid structure that combines line functions with functional departments.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: line type, functional type, line and functional type"}'::jsonb),
('mc:skill that is most essential for top level management::::conceptual', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'skill that is most essential for top level management:', 'conceptual', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Conceptual skill involves the ability to think strategically, understand complex situations, and develop innovative solutions. Top-level managers need strong conceptual skills to envision the organization’s future direction, set goals, and formulate strategic plans.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"conceptual\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Conceptual skill involves the ability to think strategically, understand complex situations, and develop innovative solutions. Top-level managers need strong conceptual skills to envision the organization’s future direction, set goals, and formulate strategic plans.\n\n• Technical skill: Refers to specific knowledge and expertise in a particular field or industry. While technical skills are important for lower levels of management, they become less critical at the top level where managers focus more on overseeing and directing rather than performing technical tasks.\n• Human skill: Also known as interpersonal or people skills, human skills involve the ability to work effectively with others, motivate and lead teams, and build relationships. These skills are important at all levels of management but are particularly crucial for middle managers who directly supervise employees.\n• Administrative skill: Involves the ability to manage tasks, processes, and resources efficiently. It includes planning, organizing, coordinating, and controlling activities within the organization. Administrative skills are essential for all levels of management but are typically more operational in nature compared to conceptual skills.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: technical, human, administrative"}'::jsonb),
