are essential for all levels of management but are typically more operational in nature compared to conceptual skills.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: technical, human, administrative"}'::jsonb),
('mc:inversion of sucrose can be facilitated by adding::::acid', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'inversion of sucrose can be facilitated by adding:', 'acid', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"The inversion of sucrose (the process where sucrose is hydrolyzed into its component sugars glucose and fructose) can be facilitated by adding acid.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"acid\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"The inversion of sucrose (the process where sucrose is hydrolyzed into its component sugars glucose and fructose) can be facilitated by adding acid.\n\nSucrose is a disaccharide composed of glucose and fructose. In the presence of an acid (such as lemon juice or cream of tartar), the acid catalyzes the hydrolysis of sucrose into its monosaccharide components, glucose, and fructose. This process is commonly used in cooking and baking to make syrups, candies, and other confections where the breakdown of sucrose into its sweeter-tasting components (glucose and fructose) enhances sweetness and texture.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: fat, table sugar, honey"}'::jsonb),
('mc:inversion of sucrose can be facilitated by adding::::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'inversion of sucrose can be facilitated by adding:', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"The inversion of sucrose (the process where sucrose is hydrolyzed into its component sugars glucose and fructose) can be facilitated by adding acid.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"acid\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"The inversion of sucrose (the process where sucrose is hydrolyzed into its component sugars glucose and fructose) can be facilitated by adding acid.\n\nSucrose is a disaccharide composed of glucose and fructose. In the presence of an acid (such as lemon juice or cream of tartar), the acid catalyzes the hydrolysis of sucrose into its monosaccharide components, glucose, and fructose. This process is commonly used in cooking and baking to make syrups, candies, and other confections where the breakdown of sucrose into its sweeter-tasting components (glucose and fructose) enhances sweetness and texture.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: fat, table sugar, honey"}'::jsonb),
('mc:drip is a method of making::::coffee', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'drip is a method of making:', 'coffee', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"In coffee brewing, the drip method, also known as drip coffee or filter coffee, involves pouring hot water over ground coffee beans contained in a filter. The water drips through the coffee grounds and filter into a pot or carafe below, extracting flavor and oils from the coffee grounds as it passes through. This method is popular for its simplicity and ability to produce a clean, flavorful cup of coffee.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"coffee\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"In coffee brewing, the drip method, also known as drip coffee or filter coffee, involves pouring hot water over ground coffee beans contained in a filter. The water drips through the coffee grounds and filter into a pot or carafe below, extracting flavor and oils from the coffee grounds as it passes through. This method is popular for its simplicity and ability to produce a clean, flavorful cup of coffee.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: tea, chocolate, breakfast certial"}'::jsonb),
