('mc:the fizz or bubbles in sparkling water is attributed to::::carbon dioxide', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'the fizz or bubbles in sparkling water is attributed to:', 'carbon dioxide', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Carbon dioxide (CO2) is dissolved under pressure in sparkling water. When the pressure is released, such as when the bottle is opened or the water is poured, the dissolved CO2 escapes as bubbles, creating the characteristic fizziness or effervescence in the water.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"carbon dioxide\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Carbon dioxide (CO2) is dissolved under pressure in sparkling water. When the pressure is released, such as when the bottle is opened or the water is poured, the dissolved CO2 escapes as bubbles, creating the characteristic fizziness or effervescence in the water.\n\n• Carbon carbonate: This is not a correct term; carbonates are compounds involving carbon and oxygen, but not directly related to the fizz in sparkling water.\n• Oxygen bromide: These are two separate chemical elements and do not contribute to the fizz in sparkling water.\n• Galactose: A type of sugar found in dairy products and some plants, not related to the fizz in sparkling water.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: carbon carbonate, oxygen bromide, galactose"}'::jsonb),
('mc:the fizz or bubbles in sparkling water is attributed to::::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'the fizz or bubbles in sparkling water is attributed to:', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Carbon dioxide (CO2) is dissolved under pressure in sparkling water. When the pressure is released, such as when the bottle is opened or the water is poured, the dissolved CO2 escapes as bubbles, creating the characteristic fizziness or effervescence in the water.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"carbon dioxide\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Carbon dioxide (CO2) is dissolved under pressure in sparkling water. When the pressure is released, such as when the bottle is opened or the water is poured, the dissolved CO2 escapes as bubbles, creating the characteristic fizziness or effervescence in the water.\n\n• Carbon carbonate: This is not a correct term; carbonates are compounds involving carbon and oxygen, but not directly related to the fizz in sparkling water.\n• Oxygen bromide: These are two separate chemical elements and do not contribute to the fizz in sparkling water.\n• Galactose: A type of sugar found in dairy products and some plants, not related to the fizz in sparkling water.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: carbon carbonate, oxygen bromide, galactose"}'::jsonb),
