('mc:a visayan soup dish made with pork or chicken, vegetables like gabi, corn, and green leafy vegetables, seasoned with ginger and other spices. it is comforting and mildly flavored.:::linat-an', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'a visayan soup dish made with pork or chicken, vegetables like gabi, corn, and green leafy vegetables, seasoned with ginger and other spices. it is comforting and mildly flavored.', 'Linat-an', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"• Tiula Itum: A traditional Tausug dish from the Sulu Archipelago in the southern Philippines. Its distinctive black color comes from the use of burned or charred coconut meat, which is combined with spices such as ginger and turmeric to create a flavorful and aromatic soup.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Linat-an\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"• Tiula Itum: A traditional Tausug dish from the Sulu Archipelago in the southern Philippines. Its distinctive black color comes from the use of burned or charred coconut meat, which is combined with spices such as ginger and turmeric to create a flavorful and aromatic soup.\n• Batchoy: A hearty noodle soup originating from Iloilo, Batchoy is made with a flavorful pork and beef broth, round noodles, and topped with pork offal, crushed pork cracklings, garlic, and an egg. It’s savory, rich, and often served hot.\n• Piarun: Also known as “Piyanggang,” this is a dish from the Tausug people of Mindanao, made with chicken or fish marinated in spices and burnt coconut, then grilled or stewed. It has a rich, smoky flavor with a complex blend of spices.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Tiula Itum, Batchoy, Piarun"}'::jsonb),
('mc:in a rural community, a nutritionist identifies a high prevalence of iron-deficiency anemia among women of childbearing age. the community has a strong agricultural tradition and relies heavily on local produce of whole grains and legumes, but the soil lacks bioavailable iron. a pilot program introduces a new iron-fortified flour, but a year later, the anemia rates have not significantly improved. applying the principles of community nutrition, what is the most plausible reason for the program''s failure?:::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'in a rural community, a nutritionist identifies a high prevalence of iron-deficiency anemia among women of childbearing age. the community has a strong agricultural tradition and relies heavily on local produce of whole grains and legumes, but the soil lacks bioavailable iron. a pilot program introduces a new iron-fortified flour, but a year later, the anemia rates have not significantly improved. applying the principles of community nutrition, what is the most plausible reason for the program''s failure?', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"The correct answer is that the intervention failed to account for food-food interactions, since the community''s diet is high in phytates (found abundantly in whole grains and legumes), which are well-known potent inhibitors of non-heme iron absorption, potentially neutralizing much of the benefit of the iron fortification.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"The intervention failed to account for potential food-food interactions, as the community''s diet is high in phytates, which inhibit iron absorption.\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"The correct answer is that the intervention failed to account for food-food interactions, since the community''s diet is high in phytates (found abundantly in whole grains and legumes), which are well-known potent inhibitors of non-heme iron absorption, potentially neutralizing much of the benefit of the iron fortification.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: The program did not employ a randomized controlled trial, making it impossible to accurately measure its impact on the target population., The nutritionist did not adequately engage local political leaders, resulting in a lack of policy support for the program''s long-term sustainability., The intervention was not tailored to the individual health needs of each participant, failing to address unique comorbidities that might interfere with iron absorption."}'::jsonb),
