('mc:also known as piyanggang, this is a dish from tausug people of mindanao, made with chicken or fish marinated in spices and burnt coconut, then grilled or stewed.:::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'also known as piyanggang, this is a dish from tausug people of mindanao, made with chicken or fish marinated in spices and burnt coconut, then grilled or stewed.', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"• Tiula Itum: A traditional Tausug dish from the Sulu Archipelago in the southern Philippines. Its distinctive black color comes from the use of burned or charred coconut meat, which is combined with spices such as ginger and turmeric to create a flavorful and aromatic soup.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Piarun\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"• Tiula Itum: A traditional Tausug dish from the Sulu Archipelago in the southern Philippines. Its distinctive black color comes from the use of burned or charred coconut meat, which is combined with spices such as ginger and turmeric to create a flavorful and aromatic soup.\n\n• Linat-an: A Visayan soup dish made with pork or chicken, vegetables like gabi (taro), corn, and green leafy vegetables, seasoned with ginger and other spices. It is comforting and mildly flavored.\n\n• Batchoy: A hearty noodle soup originating from Iloilo, Batchoy is made with a flavorful pork and beef broth, round noodles, and topped with pork offal, crushed pork cracklings, garlic, and an egg. It’s savory, rich, and often served hot.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Linat-an, Tiula Itum, Batchoy"}'::jsonb),
('mc:known as the most heat resistant spore-former found in food.:::clostridium botulinum', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'known as the most heat resistant spore-former found in food.', 'Clostridium botulinum', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Clostridium botulinum is a bacterium that forms highly resistant spores capable of surviving harsh conditions, including heat. These spores can withstand temperatures higher than those that typically kill other bacteria. This resilience is a concern in food safety because if foods contaminated with Clostridium botulinum spores are improperly prepared or stored, conditions may allow the spores to germinate and produce botulinum toxin, which can cause severe foodborne illness (botulism) if ingested.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Clostridium botulinum\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Clostridium botulinum is a bacterium that forms highly resistant spores capable of surviving harsh conditions, including heat. These spores can withstand temperatures higher than those that typically kill other bacteria. This resilience is a concern in food safety because if foods contaminated with Clostridium botulinum spores are improperly prepared or stored, conditions may allow the spores to germinate and produce botulinum toxin, which can cause severe foodborne illness (botulism) if ingested.\n\nIn canned foods, if the canning process does not achieve high enough temperatures or if there is a failure to seal the cans properly, C. botulinum spores can survive. Under these conditions, the spores can germinate and produce the toxin, which can cause botulism, a rare but serious illness characterized by symptoms like muscle weakness, difficulty swallowing, and respiratory issues. Proper canning techniques, including adequate heating and ensuring an airtight seal, are critical to preventing botulism.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Bacillus cereus, Mycobacterium tuberculosis, Salmonella typhimurium"}'::jsonb),
('mc:known as the most heat resistant spore-former found in food.:::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'known as the most heat resistant spore-former found in food.', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Clostridium botulinum is a bacterium that forms highly resistant spores capable of surviving harsh conditions, including heat. These spores can withstand temperatures higher than those that typically kill other bacteria. This resilience is a concern in food safety because if foods contaminated with Clostridium botulinum spores are improperly prepared or stored, conditions may allow the spores to germinate and produce botulinum toxin, which can cause severe foodborne illness (botulism) if ingested.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Clostridium botulinum\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Clostridium botulinum is a bacterium that forms highly resistant spores capable of surviving harsh conditions, including heat. These spores can withstand temperatures higher than those that typically kill other bacteria. This resilience is a concern in food safety because if foods contaminated with Clostridium botulinum spores are improperly prepared or stored, conditions may allow the spores to germinate and produce botulinum toxin, which can cause severe foodborne illness (botulism) if ingested.\n\nIn canned foods, if the canning process does not achieve high enough temperatures or if there is a failure to seal the cans properly, C. botulinum spores can survive. Under these conditions, the spores can germinate and produce the toxin, which can cause botulism, a rare but serious illness characterized by symptoms like muscle weakness, difficulty swallowing, and respiratory issues. Proper canning techniques, including adequate heating and ensuring an airtight seal, are critical to preventing botulism.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Bacillus cereus, Mycobacterium tuberculosis, Salmonella typhimurium"}'::jsonb),
('mc:black discoloration in canned products may be caused by::::all of the above', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'black discoloration in canned products may be caused by:', 'All of the above', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"• Bacillus nigrificans: This bacterium can produce black pigment and cause discoloration in canned foods.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"All of the above\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"• Bacillus nigrificans: This bacterium can produce black pigment and cause discoloration in canned foods.\n• Iron sulfide formation: Reaction between iron in the can and sulfur compounds in the food can lead to black or dark discoloration.\n• Hard water containing minerals: The minerals in hard water can interact with the food and contribute to color changes, including black discoloration.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Bacillus nigrificans, Iron sulfide formation, Hard water containing minerals"}'::jsonb),
('mc:basis of pasteurization time and temperature.:::destruction of coxiella burnetii', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'basis of pasteurization time and temperature.', 'Destruction of Coxiella burnetii', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Pasteurization is primarily designed to ensure the destruction of Coxiella burnetii, the most heat-resistant pathogen found in milk. This bacterium is more resistant to heat compared to other pathogens like Escherichia coli and Salmonella, making it a key target for pasteurization processes. Effective pasteurization parameters are established to ensure the safety of milk and dairy products by targeting this particularly resilient microorganism.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Destruction of Coxiella burnetii\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Pasteurization is primarily designed to ensure the destruction of Coxiella burnetii, the most heat-resistant pathogen found in milk. This bacterium is more resistant to heat compared to other pathogens like Escherichia coli and Salmonella, making it a key target for pasteurization processes. Effective pasteurization parameters are established to ensure the safety of milk and dairy products by targeting this particularly resilient microorganism.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Destruction of Clostridium botulinum, Destruction of E.coli O157:H7, Destruction of Serratia marcescens"}'::jsonb),
('mc:basis of pasteurization time and temperature.:::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'basis of pasteurization time and temperature.', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Pasteurization is primarily designed to ensure the destruction of Coxiella burnetii, the most heat-resistant pathogen found in milk. This bacterium is more resistant to heat compared to other pathogens like Escherichia coli and Salmonella, making it a key target for pasteurization processes. Effective pasteurization parameters are established to ensure the safety of milk and dairy products by targeting this particularly resilient microorganism.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Destruction of Coxiella burnetii\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Pasteurization is primarily designed to ensure the destruction of Coxiella burnetii, the most heat-resistant pathogen found in milk. This bacterium is more resistant to heat compared to other pathogens like Escherichia coli and Salmonella, making it a key target for pasteurization processes. Effective pasteurization parameters are established to ensure the safety of milk and dairy products by targeting this particularly resilient microorganism.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Destruction of Clostridium botulinum, Destruction of E.coli O157:H7, Destruction of Serratia marcescens"}'::jsonb),
('mc:it is a psychrotrophic foodborne pathogen.:::listeria monocytogenes', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'it is a psychrotrophic foodborne pathogen.', 'Listeria monocytogenes', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Listeria monocytogenes is a bacterium that can grow and thrive at refrigeration temperatures (psychrotrophic conditions), making it a significant concern in food safety. It can cause listeriosis, a serious illness primarily affecting pregnant women, newborns, elderly individuals, and those with weakened immune systems. Listeria monocytogenes is often associated with ready-to-eat foods such as deli meats, soft cheeses, and smoked seafood.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Listeria monocytogenes\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Listeria monocytogenes is a bacterium that can grow and thrive at refrigeration temperatures (psychrotrophic conditions), making it a significant concern in food safety. It can cause listeriosis, a serious illness primarily affecting pregnant women, newborns, elderly individuals, and those with weakened immune systems. Listeria monocytogenes is often associated with ready-to-eat foods such as deli meats, soft cheeses, and smoked seafood.\n\nIt can cause listeriosis, a serious illness primarily affecting pregnant women, newborns, elderly individuals, and those with weakened immune systems. Listeria monocytogenes is often associated with ready-to-eat foods such as deli meats, soft cheeses, and smoked seafood.\n\n• Clostridium botulinum: An anaerobic bacterium that grows in low-oxygen environments, such as improperly canned foods. It is not typically psychrotrophic.\n• Micrococcus luteus: A bacterium commonly found in soil, dust, and on skin. It is not known for psychrotrophic growth and is generally not associated with foodborne illness.\n• Salmonella typhimurium: A pathogenic bacterium that thrives at warmer temperatures and is commonly associated with foodborne illness. It is not psychrotrophic.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Clostridium botulinum, Micrococcus luteus, Salmonella typhimurium"}'::jsonb),
('mc:it is a psychrotrophic foodborne pathogen.:::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'it is a psychrotrophic foodborne pathogen.', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Listeria monocytogenes is a bacterium that can grow and thrive at refrigeration temperatures (psychrotrophic conditions), making it a significant concern in food safety. It can cause listeriosis, a serious illness primarily affecting pregnant women, newborns, elderly individuals, and those with weakened immune systems. Listeria monocytogenes is often associated with ready-to-eat foods such as deli meats, soft cheeses, and smoked seafood.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Listeria monocytogenes\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Listeria monocytogenes is a bacterium that can grow and thrive at refrigeration temperatures (psychrotrophic conditions), making it a significant concern in food safety. It can cause listeriosis, a serious illness primarily affecting pregnant women, newborns, elderly individuals, and those with weakened immune systems. Listeria monocytogenes is often associated with ready-to-eat foods such as deli meats, soft cheeses, and smoked seafood.\n\nIt can cause listeriosis, a serious illness primarily affecting pregnant women, newborns, elderly individuals, and those with weakened immune systems. Listeria monocytogenes is often associated with ready-to-eat foods such as deli meats, soft cheeses, and smoked seafood.\n\n• Clostridium botulinum: An anaerobic bacterium that grows in low-oxygen environments, such as improperly canned foods. It is not typically psychrotrophic.\n• Micrococcus luteus: A bacterium commonly found in soil, dust, and on skin. It is not known for psychrotrophic growth and is generally not associated with foodborne illness.\n• Salmonella typhimurium: A pathogenic bacterium that thrives at warmer temperatures and is commonly associated with foodborne illness. It is not psychrotrophic.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Clostridium botulinum, Micrococcus luteus, Salmonella typhimurium"}'::jsonb),
('mc:the ph of chili garlic is 6.0, therefore it is considered as::::low acid food', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'the ph of chili garlic is 6.0, therefore it is considered as:', 'Low acid food', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"According to food safety and regulatory guidelines (such as the FDA), foods are classified based on a critical pH threshold of 4.6:","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Low acid food\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"According to food safety and regulatory guidelines (such as the FDA), foods are classified based on a critical pH threshold of 4.6:\n\n• Low-acid food: Any food with a finished equilibrium pH greater than 4.6 and a water activity greater than 0.85. Because the chili garlic has a pH of 6.0 (which is above 4.6), it falls squarely into this category.\n• Acid food: Foods that naturally have a pH of 4.6 or below.\n• Acidified food: Low-acid foods to which acid(s) or acid food(s) are manually added to deliberately reduce the final equilibrium pH to 4.6 or below.\n\nBecause unacidified low-acid environments with a pH of 6.0 can support the growth of dangerous spores like Clostridium botulinum (botulism), commercial or shelf-stable chili garlic typically undergoes acidification to bring its pH down safely.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Acid food, Acidified food, High acid food"}'::jsonb),
('mc:the ph of chili garlic is 6.0, therefore it is considered as::::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'the ph of chili garlic is 6.0, therefore it is considered as:', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"According to food safety and regulatory guidelines (such as the FDA), foods are classified based on a critical pH threshold of 4.6:","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Low acid food\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"According to food safety and regulatory guidelines (such as the FDA), foods are classified based on a critical pH threshold of 4.6:\n\n• Low-acid food: Any food with a finished equilibrium pH greater than 4.6 and a water activity greater than 0.85. Because the chili garlic has a pH of 6.0 (which is above 4.6), it falls squarely into this category.\n• Acid food: Foods that naturally have a pH of 4.6 or below.\n• Acidified food: Low-acid foods to which acid(s) or acid food(s) are manually added to deliberately reduce the final equilibrium pH to 4.6 or below.\n\nBecause unacidified low-acid environments with a pH of 6.0 can support the growth of dangerous spores like Clostridium botulinum (botulism), commercial or shelf-stable chili garlic typically undergoes acidification to bring its pH down safely.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Acid food, Acidified food, High acid food"}'::jsonb),
('mc:it refers to the behaviors that can lead to good health and improve cleanliness.:::hygiene', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'it refers to the behaviors that can lead to good health and improve cleanliness.', 'hygiene', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Hygiene encompasses practices and behaviors that promote health and cleanliness, such as handwashing, personal grooming, maintaining clean environments, and following proper food handling practices. It is essential for preventing the spread of diseases and maintaining overall well-being.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"hygiene\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Hygiene encompasses practices and behaviors that promote health and cleanliness, such as handwashing, personal grooming, maintaining clean environments, and following proper food handling practices. It is essential for preventing the spread of diseases and maintaining overall well-being.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: sanitation, cleaning, disinfection"}'::jsonb),
('mc:it refers to the behaviors that can lead to good health and improve cleanliness.:::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'it refers to the behaviors that can lead to good health and improve cleanliness.', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Hygiene encompasses practices and behaviors that promote health and cleanliness, such as handwashing, personal grooming, maintaining clean environments, and following proper food handling practices. It is essential for preventing the spread of diseases and maintaining overall well-being.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"hygiene\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Hygiene encompasses practices and behaviors that promote health and cleanliness, such as handwashing, personal grooming, maintaining clean environments, and following proper food handling practices. It is essential for preventing the spread of diseases and maintaining overall well-being.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: sanitation, cleaning, disinfection"}'::jsonb),
('mc:among the advantages of this food service system are the savings from large volume purchasing, reduced labor duplications, and effective quality control from having only one kitchen that deliver to satellite units.:::commissary system', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'among the advantages of this food service system are the savings from large volume purchasing, reduced labor duplications, and effective quality control from having only one kitchen that deliver to satellite units.', 'Commissary system', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"In a Commissary system, food production is centralized in one main kitchen (commissary) where bulk food preparation and cooking take place. The prepared food is then distributed to satellite units or service points where it is served to customers. This centralized approach allows for efficient purchasing in bulk, reduces duplication of labor (since not every satellite unit needs its own full kitchen), and enables better quality control because all food production is managed centrally.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Commissary system\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"In a Commissary system, food production is centralized in one main kitchen (commissary) where bulk food preparation and cooking take place. The prepared food is then distributed to satellite units or service points where it is served to customers. This centralized approach allows for efficient purchasing in bulk, reduces duplication of labor (since not every satellite unit needs its own full kitchen), and enables better quality control because all food production is managed centrally.\n\n• Conventional System:\nDescription: In the conventional food service system, food is prepared, cooked, and served at the same location. It involves on-site preparation and serving, with all food being made fresh daily.\nAdvantages: Flexibility in menu changes, control over the preparation process, and immediate serving of freshly cooked food.\n\n• Ready Prepared System:\nDescription: Also known as the cook-chill or cook-freeze system, this involves preparing and cooking food in advance, then chilling or freezing it for later reheating and serving.\nAdvantages: Efficiency in labor and time, reduced peak-hour pressures, and extended service hours with consistent food quality.\n\n• Assembly/Serve System:\nDescription: This system involves assembling pre-prepared food items into final meals at the service site. The food is usually purchased fully prepared and only needs to be heated or assembled before serving.\nAdvantages: Minimal on-site labor required, consistency in food quality, and reduced need for complex food preparation equipment.\nEach system has its own set of advantages tailored to different operational needs and goals.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Conventional system, Ready Prepared system, Assembly/Serve system"}'::jsonb),
('mc:an inventory system where stocks are maintained at a certain level each time an order regardless of the amount on hand and the of order.:::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'an inventory system where stocks are maintained at a certain level each time an order regardless of the amount on hand and the of order.', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"In a par stock system, inventory is maintained at a predetermined level (par stock) regardless of current inventory levels. When the stock reaches or falls below this set level, an order is placed to replenish it back to the par level. This system focuses on keeping inventory consistently at the desired level to ensure smooth operations.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Par stock system\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"In a par stock system, inventory is maintained at a predetermined level (par stock) regardless of current inventory levels. When the stock reaches or falls below this set level, an order is placed to replenish it back to the par level. This system focuses on keeping inventory consistently at the desired level to ensure smooth operations.\n\nIn a Par stock system:\n\nA predetermined minimum quantity (the par level) of each item is maintained in stock. Whenever the inventory level drops to the par level due to sales or usage, a replenishment order is placed. The order quantity is typically fixed and does not depend on the current inventory level.\n\nThis system helps in maintaining consistent stock levels and ensures that inventory is replenished promptly when it reaches the par level.\n\nFIFO (First-In, First-Out):\nDescription: An inventory management method where the oldest stock (first-in) is sold or used first before newer stock (last-in). This approach helps prevent inventory from becoming obsolete and is often used for perishable goods.\n\nBuffer System:\nDescription: Also known as a safety stock system, this approach involves maintaining an additional quantity of stock (buffer or safety stock) to protect against variations in demand or supply delays. It ensures that there is always a reserve to cover unexpected changes.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: FIFO, Min-Max system, Buffer system"}'::jsonb),
('mc:among the advantages of this food service system are the savings from large volume purchasing, reduced labor duplications, and effective quality control from having only one kitchen that deliver to satellite units.:::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'among the advantages of this food service system are the savings from large volume purchasing, reduced labor duplications, and effective quality control from having only one kitchen that deliver to satellite units.', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"In a Commissary system, food production is centralized in one main kitchen (commissary) where bulk food preparation and cooking take place. The prepared food is then distributed to satellite units or service points where it is served to customers. This centralized approach allows for efficient purchasing in bulk, reduces duplication of labor (since not every satellite unit needs its own full kitchen), and enables better quality control because all food production is managed centrally.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Commissary system\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"In a Commissary system, food production is centralized in one main kitchen (commissary) where bulk food preparation and cooking take place. The prepared food is then distributed to satellite units or service points where it is served to customers. This centralized approach allows for efficient purchasing in bulk, reduces duplication of labor (since not every satellite unit needs its own full kitchen), and enables better quality control because all food production is managed centrally.\n\n• Conventional System:\nDescription: In the conventional food service system, food is prepared, cooked, and served at the same location. It involves on-site preparation and serving, with all food being made fresh daily.\nAdvantages: Flexibility in menu changes, control over the preparation process, and immediate serving of freshly cooked food.\n\n• Ready Prepared System:\nDescription: Also known as the cook-chill or cook-freeze system, this involves preparing and cooking food in advance, then chilling or freezing it for later reheating and serving.\nAdvantages: Efficiency in labor and time, reduced peak-hour pressures, and extended service hours with consistent food quality.\n\n• Assembly/Serve System:\nDescription: This system involves assembling pre-prepared food items into final meals at the service site. The food is usually purchased fully prepared and only needs to be heated or assembled before serving.\nAdvantages: Minimal on-site labor required, consistency in food quality, and reduced need for complex food preparation equipment.\nEach system has its own set of advantages tailored to different operational needs and goals.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Conventional system, Ready Prepared system, Assembly/Serve system"}'::jsonb),
('mc:this leadership theory sets an assumption that employees have an inherent dislike of work and will avoid it if possible.:::theory x', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'this leadership theory sets an assumption that employees have an inherent dislike of work and will avoid it if possible.', 'Theory X', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Theory X is part of Douglas McGregor’s Theory of Motivation and Management, which suggests that employees are inherently lazy and dislike work. According to Theory X, employees need to be closely supervised and controlled to ensure they work effectively.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Theory X\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Theory X is part of Douglas McGregor’s Theory of Motivation and Management, which suggests that employees are inherently lazy and dislike work. According to Theory X, employees need to be closely supervised and controlled to ensure they work effectively.\n\n• Scientific Approach: This approach, developed by Frederick Taylor, focuses on improving work efficiency through scientific methods, such as time studies and work specialization. It emphasizes optimizing tasks and productivity rather than assumptions about employee motivation.\n• Human Relations Approach: This approach highlights the importance of employee morale and interpersonal relationships in improving productivity. It emerged from studies such as the Hawthorne Studies and emphasizes the psychological and social aspects of work.\n• Theory Y: Proposed by Douglas McGregor, Theory Y is the counterpart to Theory X. It assumes that employees are self-motivated, enjoy work, and seek responsibility. This theory suggests that workers are motivated by intrinsic factors and can be creative and productive if given the right environment.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Scientific approach, Human relations approach, Theory Y"}'::jsonb),
