('mc:when purchasing canned goods, a bulging end is an indication that the product may::::cause botulism', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'when purchasing canned goods, a bulging end is an indication that the product may:', 'Cause botulism', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Bulging cans can be a sign of botulism, a serious and potentially deadly illness caused by the toxin produced by Clostridium botulinum bacteria. These bacteria can thrive in anaerobic (low-oxygen) conditions, such as those found inside improperly sealed or damaged cans. Consuming food contaminated with botulinum toxin can lead to severe illness.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Cause botulism\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Bulging cans can be a sign of botulism, a serious and potentially deadly illness caused by the toxin produced by Clostridium botulinum bacteria. These bacteria can thrive in anaerobic (low-oxygen) conditions, such as those found inside improperly sealed or damaged cans. Consuming food contaminated with botulinum toxin can lead to severe illness.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Be ok to purchase, Be lower in weight than normal, May have parasitic infestation"}'::jsonb),
('mc:when purchasing canned goods, a bulging end is an indication that the product may::::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'when purchasing canned goods, a bulging end is an indication that the product may:', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Bulging cans can be a sign of botulism, a serious and potentially deadly illness caused by the toxin produced by Clostridium botulinum bacteria. These bacteria can thrive in anaerobic (low-oxygen) conditions, such as those found inside improperly sealed or damaged cans. Consuming food contaminated with botulinum toxin can lead to severe illness.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Cause botulism\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Bulging cans can be a sign of botulism, a serious and potentially deadly illness caused by the toxin produced by Clostridium botulinum bacteria. These bacteria can thrive in anaerobic (low-oxygen) conditions, such as those found inside improperly sealed or damaged cans. Consuming food contaminated with botulinum toxin can lead to severe illness.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Be ok to purchase, Be lower in weight than normal, May have parasitic infestation"}'::jsonb),
('mc:it is an example of curing salt::::prague powder', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'it is an example of curing salt:', 'Prague powder', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Prague powder, also known as pink curing salt, is a specific type of curing salt used in preserving meats such as bacon, ham, and sausages. It contains sodium nitrite, which helps prevent bacterial growth and gives cured meats their characteristic pink color and flavor.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Prague powder\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Prague powder, also known as pink curing salt, is a specific type of curing salt used in preserving meats such as bacon, ham, and sausages. It contains sodium nitrite, which helps prevent bacterial growth and gives cured meats their characteristic pink color and flavor.\n\n• Rub: A mixture of spices, herbs, and sometimes salt that is applied to the surface of meat to add flavor. It is not a curing salt but is used primarily for seasoning.\n• Kosher salt: A coarse-grained salt commonly used in cooking and for koshering meat. It is not typically used for curing but is favored for its clean flavor and ability to draw out moisture from foods.\n• Fleur de sel: A type of sea salt harvested from the surface of salt ponds. It is prized for its delicate flavor and texture and is often used as a finishing salt, but it is not used for curing.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: rub, Kosher salt, leur de sel"}'::jsonb),
('mc:it is an example of curing salt::::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'it is an example of curing salt:', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Prague powder, also known as pink curing salt, is a specific type of curing salt used in preserving meats such as bacon, ham, and sausages. It contains sodium nitrite, which helps prevent bacterial growth and gives cured meats their characteristic pink color and flavor.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Prague powder\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Prague powder, also known as pink curing salt, is a specific type of curing salt used in preserving meats such as bacon, ham, and sausages. It contains sodium nitrite, which helps prevent bacterial growth and gives cured meats their characteristic pink color and flavor.\n\n• Rub: A mixture of spices, herbs, and sometimes salt that is applied to the surface of meat to add flavor. It is not a curing salt but is used primarily for seasoning.\n• Kosher salt: A coarse-grained salt commonly used in cooking and for koshering meat. It is not typically used for curing but is favored for its clean flavor and ability to draw out moisture from foods.\n• Fleur de sel: A type of sea salt harvested from the surface of salt ponds. It is prized for its delicate flavor and texture and is often used as a finishing salt, but it is not used for curing.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: rub, Kosher salt, leur de sel"}'::jsonb),
('mc:gas that cause the fruit to ripen::::ethylene', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'gas that cause the fruit to ripen:', 'ethylene', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Ethylene is a natural plant hormone that plays a key role in the ripening process of fruits. It is produced by fruits themselves as they ripen and can also be released by other fruits nearby, leading to a chain reaction of ripening in a group of fruits.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"ethylene\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Ethylene is a natural plant hormone that plays a key role in the ripening process of fruits. It is produced by fruits themselves as they ripen and can also be released by other fruits nearby, leading to a chain reaction of ripening in a group of fruits.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: carbon monoxide, propane, nitrogen"}'::jsonb),
('mc:ingredients in chocolate milk that comes from seaweed.:::carrageenan', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'ingredients in chocolate milk that comes from seaweed.', 'carrageenan', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Carrageenan is a natural food additive derived from various species of red seaweed. It is commonly used in the food industry as a thickening and stabilizing agent.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"carrageenan\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Carrageenan is a natural food additive derived from various species of red seaweed. It is commonly used in the food industry as a thickening and stabilizing agent.\n\nIn chocolate milk, carrageenan helps to prevent separation of the milk and cocoa particles, providing a smooth and creamy texture.\n\n• Glucose is a simple sugar that is often used as a sweetener in various food products. It is not derived from seaweed.\n• Sucrose is a common sugar, made up of glucose and fructose, typically derived from sugar cane or sugar beets. It is also not derived from seaweed.\n• Cocoa is the main ingredient in chocolate, derived from cocoa beans. It provides the chocolate flavor in chocolate milk but does not come from seaweed.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: glucose, sucrose, cocoa"}'::jsonb),
('mc:ingredients in chocolate milk that comes from seaweed.:::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'ingredients in chocolate milk that comes from seaweed.', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Carrageenan is a natural food additive derived from various species of red seaweed. It is commonly used in the food industry as a thickening and stabilizing agent.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"carrageenan\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Carrageenan is a natural food additive derived from various species of red seaweed. It is commonly used in the food industry as a thickening and stabilizing agent.\n\nIn chocolate milk, carrageenan helps to prevent separation of the milk and cocoa particles, providing a smooth and creamy texture.\n\n• Glucose is a simple sugar that is often used as a sweetener in various food products. It is not derived from seaweed.\n• Sucrose is a common sugar, made up of glucose and fructose, typically derived from sugar cane or sugar beets. It is also not derived from seaweed.\n• Cocoa is the main ingredient in chocolate, derived from cocoa beans. It provides the chocolate flavor in chocolate milk but does not come from seaweed.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: glucose, sucrose, cocoa"}'::jsonb),
('mc:the process by which a liquid is separated from a gel; commonly observed during cooling.:::syneresis', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'the process by which a liquid is separated from a gel; commonly observed during cooling.', 'syneresis', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Syneresis occurs when a gel contracts or shrinks, causing the expulsion of liquid from the gel network. This separation of liquid from the gel can be seen as small droplets or pools of liquid forming on the surface of the gel or around its edges.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"syneresis\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Syneresis occurs when a gel contracts or shrinks, causing the expulsion of liquid from the gel network. This separation of liquid from the gel can be seen as small droplets or pools of liquid forming on the surface of the gel or around its edges.\n\n• Gelation is the process of forming a gel from a liquid, usually by cooling or adding a gelling agent. This process is the opposite of syneresis, as it involves the formation of a gel rather than the separation of liquid from it.\n• Hydrolysis is a chemical process in which a molecule is broken down into smaller components by the addition of water. This term is more related to the breakdown of substances than to the separation of liquid from a gel.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: gelation, hydrolysis, gelatinization"}'::jsonb),
('mc:the process by which a liquid is separated from a gel; commonly observed during cooling.:::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'the process by which a liquid is separated from a gel; commonly observed during cooling.', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Syneresis occurs when a gel contracts or shrinks, causing the expulsion of liquid from the gel network. This separation of liquid from the gel can be seen as small droplets or pools of liquid forming on the surface of the gel or around its edges.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"syneresis\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Syneresis occurs when a gel contracts or shrinks, causing the expulsion of liquid from the gel network. This separation of liquid from the gel can be seen as small droplets or pools of liquid forming on the surface of the gel or around its edges.\n\n• Gelation is the process of forming a gel from a liquid, usually by cooling or adding a gelling agent. This process is the opposite of syneresis, as it involves the formation of a gel rather than the separation of liquid from it.\n• Hydrolysis is a chemical process in which a molecule is broken down into smaller components by the addition of water. This term is more related to the breakdown of substances than to the separation of liquid from a gel.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: gelation, hydrolysis, gelatinization"}'::jsonb)
