('mc:a foodservice operation orders 40 kg of ground beef. the specification requires 80% lean meat, delivery at 5°c or below, and packaging free from tears or leakage. the supplier delivers 40 kg of meat at 10°c, but several packages are leaking. which purchasing principle is most directly violated?:::right quality', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'a foodservice operation orders 40 kg of ground beef. the specification requires 80% lean meat, delivery at 5°c or below, and packaging free from tears or leakage. the supplier delivers 40 kg of meat at 10°c, but several packages are leaking. which purchasing principle is most directly violated?', 'Right quality', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Quantity is correct, but temperature and packaging condition fail to meet the established quality specifications.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Right quality\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Quantity is correct, but temperature and packaging condition fail to meet the established quality specifications.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Right quantity, Right price"}'::jsonb),
('mc:a foodservice operation orders 40 kg of ground beef. the specification requires 80% lean meat, delivery at 5°c or below, and packaging free from tears or leakage. the supplier delivers 40 kg of meat at 10°c, but several packages are leaking. which purchasing principle is most directly violated?:::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'a foodservice operation orders 40 kg of ground beef. the specification requires 80% lean meat, delivery at 5°c or below, and packaging free from tears or leakage. the supplier delivers 40 kg of meat at 10°c, but several packages are leaking. which purchasing principle is most directly violated?', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Quantity is correct, but temperature and packaging condition fail to meet the established quality specifications.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Right quality\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Quantity is correct, but temperature and packaging condition fail to meet the established quality specifications.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Right quantity, Right price"}'::jsonb),
('mc:a long-term care facility develops an eight-week menu. during lunch, residents receive the same entrée, vegetable, dessert, and beverage selected by the facility, with no choices offered within any menu category. however, the menu changes from week 1 to week 8 and then repeats. which classification best describes the menu?:::non-selective cycle menu', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'a long-term care facility develops an eight-week menu. during lunch, residents receive the same entrée, vegetable, dessert, and beverage selected by the facility, with no choices offered within any menu category. however, the menu changes from week 1 to week 8 and then repeats. which classification best describes the menu?', 'Non-selective cycle menu', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"The menu is non-selective because residents have no choices, and it is a cycle menu because the planned menu repeats after a specified period.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Non-selective cycle menu\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"The menu is non-selective because residents have no choices, and it is a cycle menu because the planned menu repeats after a specified period.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Selective cycle menu, Limited selective menu"}'::jsonb),
('mc:a long-term care facility develops an eight-week menu. during lunch, residents receive the same entrée, vegetable, dessert, and beverage selected by the facility, with no choices offered within any menu category. however, the menu changes from week 1 to week 8 and then repeats. which classification best describes the menu?:::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'a long-term care facility develops an eight-week menu. during lunch, residents receive the same entrée, vegetable, dessert, and beverage selected by the facility, with no choices offered within any menu category. however, the menu changes from week 1 to week 8 and then repeats. which classification best describes the menu?', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"The menu is non-selective because residents have no choices, and it is a cycle menu because the planned menu repeats after a specified period.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Non-selective cycle menu\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"The menu is non-selective because residents have no choices, and it is a cycle menu because the planned menu repeats after a specified period.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Selective cycle menu, Limited selective menu"}'::jsonb),
('mc:a receiver is given a purchase order that does not contain the quantities ordered. the receiver independently counts, weighs, and inspects every delivered item before comparing the results with the supplier’s invoice and purchase records. which receiving method is being used?:::blind-check receiving', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'a receiver is given a purchase order that does not contain the quantities ordered. the receiver independently counts, weighs, and inspects every delivered item before comparing the results with the supplier’s invoice and purchase records. which receiving method is being used?', 'Blind-check receiving', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"In blind receiving, the receiver does not initially see the quantities ordered and must independently determine the quantity and condition of the products before comparison with purchasing documents.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Blind-check receiving\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"In blind receiving, the receiver does not initially see the quantities ordered and must independently determine the quantity and condition of the products before comparison with purchasing documents.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Open-check receiving, Invoice receiving"}'::jsonb),
('mc:a receiver is given a purchase order that does not contain the quantities ordered. the receiver independently counts, weighs, and inspects every delivered item before comparing the results with the supplier’s invoice and purchase records. which receiving method is being used?:::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'a receiver is given a purchase order that does not contain the quantities ordered. the receiver independently counts, weighs, and inspects every delivered item before comparing the results with the supplier’s invoice and purchase records. which receiving method is being used?', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"In blind receiving, the receiver does not initially see the quantities ordered and must independently determine the quantity and condition of the products before comparison with purchasing documents.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Blind-check receiving\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"In blind receiving, the receiver does not initially see the quantities ordered and must independently determine the quantity and condition of the products before comparison with purchasing documents.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Open-check receiving, Invoice receiving"}'::jsonb),
('mc:a foodservice operation uses the following inventory data: • beginning inventory = ₱25,000 • purchases = ₱95,000 • ending inventory = ₱20,000 • spoilage = ₱4,000 if spoilage is recorded as food used rather than inventory remaining, what is the adjusted food cost including spoilage?:::₱104,000', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'a foodservice operation uses the following inventory data: • beginning inventory = ₱25,000 • purchases = ₱95,000 • ending inventory = ₱20,000 • spoilage = ₱4,000 if spoilage is recorded as food used rather than inventory remaining, what is the adjusted food cost including spoilage?', '₱104,000', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Cost of food used:","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"₱104,000\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Cost of food used:\n\n₱25,000 + ₱95,000 − ₱20,000 = ₱100,000\n\nAdding spoilage: ₱100,000 + ₱4,000 = ₱104,000 (Spoilage can significantly affect food cost)","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: ₱96,000, ₱100,000, ₱90,000"}'::jsonb),
('mc:a foodservice operation uses the following inventory data: • beginning inventory = ₱25,000 • purchases = ₱95,000 • ending inventory = ₱20,000 • spoilage = ₱4,000 if spoilage is recorded as food used rather than inventory remaining, what is the adjusted food cost including spoilage?:::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'a foodservice operation uses the following inventory data: • beginning inventory = ₱25,000 • purchases = ₱95,000 • ending inventory = ₱20,000 • spoilage = ₱4,000 if spoilage is recorded as food used rather than inventory remaining, what is the adjusted food cost including spoilage?', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Cost of food used:","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"₱104,000\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Cost of food used:\n\n₱25,000 + ₱95,000 − ₱20,000 = ₱100,000\n\nAdding spoilage: ₱100,000 + ₱4,000 = ₱104,000 (Spoilage can significantly affect food cost)","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: ₱96,000, ₱100,000, ₱90,000"}'::jsonb),
('mc:a hospital prepares large batches of entrées in a centralized production area. the food is cooked, portioned, rapidly chilled, stored under refrigeration for several days, and reheated shortly before being served to patients. which foodservice system is best described by this operation?:::ready-prepared system', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'a hospital prepares large batches of entrées in a centralized production area. the food is cooked, portioned, rapidly chilled, stored under refrigeration for several days, and reheated shortly before being served to patients. which foodservice system is best described by this operation?', 'Ready-prepared system', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"In a ready-prepared system, food is prepared in advance, then chilled or frozen for storage and subsequently reheated and served. The key distinguishing feature is the time separation between production and service.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Ready-prepared system\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"In a ready-prepared system, food is prepared in advance, then chilled or frozen for storage and subsequently reheated and served. The key distinguishing feature is the time separation between production and service.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Conventional system, Assembly-serve system, Commissary system"}'::jsonb),
('mc:a hospital prepares large batches of entrées in a centralized production area. the food is cooked, portioned, rapidly chilled, stored under refrigeration for several days, and reheated shortly before being served to patients. which foodservice system is best described by this operation?:::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'a hospital prepares large batches of entrées in a centralized production area. the food is cooked, portioned, rapidly chilled, stored under refrigeration for several days, and reheated shortly before being served to patients. which foodservice system is best described by this operation?', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"In a ready-prepared system, food is prepared in advance, then chilled or frozen for storage and subsequently reheated and served. The key distinguishing feature is the time separation between production and service.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Ready-prepared system\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"In a ready-prepared system, food is prepared in advance, then chilled or frozen for storage and subsequently reheated and served. The key distinguishing feature is the time separation between production and service.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Conventional system, Assembly-serve system, Commissary system"}'::jsonb),
('mc:a hospital is designing a menu for patients requiring therapeutic diets. the menu committee wants to ensure that meals are nutritionally appropriate while also considering food preferences, cultural acceptability, cost, and production capabilities.:::integrate nutritional, operational, economic, and patient-related considerations.', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'a hospital is designing a menu for patients requiring therapeutic diets. the menu committee wants to ensure that meals are nutritionally appropriate while also considering food preferences, cultural acceptability, cost, and production capabilities.', 'Integrate nutritional, operational, economic, and patient-related considerations.', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Effective institutional menu planning requires balancing nutritional adequacy, acceptability, cost, availability, equipment, labor, and therapeutic requirements.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Integrate nutritional, operational, economic, and patient-related considerations.\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Effective institutional menu planning requires balancing nutritional adequacy, acceptability, cost, availability, equipment, labor, and therapeutic requirements.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Design the menu based primarily on ingredient cost., Use the same menu for all patients to simplify production., None of the above"}'::jsonb),
('mc:a hospital is designing a menu for patients requiring therapeutic diets. the menu committee wants to ensure that meals are nutritionally appropriate while also considering food preferences, cultural acceptability, cost, and production capabilities.:::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'a hospital is designing a menu for patients requiring therapeutic diets. the menu committee wants to ensure that meals are nutritionally appropriate while also considering food preferences, cultural acceptability, cost, and production capabilities.', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Effective institutional menu planning requires balancing nutritional adequacy, acceptability, cost, availability, equipment, labor, and therapeutic requirements.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Integrate nutritional, operational, economic, and patient-related considerations.\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Effective institutional menu planning requires balancing nutritional adequacy, acceptability, cost, availability, equipment, labor, and therapeutic requirements.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Design the menu based primarily on ingredient cost., Use the same menu for all patients to simplify production., None of the above"}'::jsonb),
('mc:a procedure when the entire colon is bypassed, and opening is created from the ileum that is attached directly to the anus.:::ileorectal anastomosis', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'a procedure when the entire colon is bypassed, and opening is created from the ileum that is attached directly to the anus.', 'Ileorectal anastomosis', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"An anastomosis is a surgical term that means connecting or joining two distinct structures inside the body.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Ileorectal anastomosis\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"An anastomosis is a surgical term that means connecting or joining two distinct structures inside the body.\n\nWhen the entire colon is removed or bypassed, the small intestine (specifically the ileum) must be carefully brought down and surgically sewn directly to the remaining rectum or anus to allow waste to leave the body naturally without needing a permanent external pouch or stoma bag.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Tonsillectomy, Cholecystectomy, Hemorrhoidectomy"}'::jsonb),
('mc:a foodservice manager discovers that one of the following foods was left unrefrigerated for several hours. which food has the highest risk for bacterial growth, assuming all were initially free of pathogens?:::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'a foodservice manager discovers that one of the following foods was left unrefrigerated for several hours. which food has the highest risk for bacterial growth, assuming all were initially free of pathogens?', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Cooked chicken provides several favorable conditions described by FAT TOM: food, available moisture, nutrients, and a favorable temperature/pH environment. The other foods have characteristics such as low moisture or high salt that inhibit microbial growth.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Cooked chicken breast\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Cooked chicken provides several favorable conditions described by FAT TOM: food, available moisture, nutrients, and a favorable temperature/pH environment. The other foods have characteristics such as low moisture or high salt that inhibit microbial growth.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Peanut butter, Salted dried fish, Pepper"}'::jsonb),
('mc:a delivery arrives at a foodservice operation. the invoice indicates 20 cases of milk, but the receiver counts only 18 cases. two cases are also found to have damaged packaging. what should the receiver do first?:::document the quantity and condition discrepancy and follow the operation''s receiving procedure.', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'a delivery arrives at a foodservice operation. the invoice indicates 20 cases of milk, but the receiver counts only 18 cases. two cases are also found to have damaged packaging. what should the receiver do first?', 'Document the quantity and condition discrepancy and follow the operation''s receiving procedure.', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Receiving requires verification of quantity, quality, and condition against purchasing documents. Discrepancies should be documented and handled according to established procedures.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Document the quantity and condition discrepancy and follow the operation''s receiving procedure.\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Receiving requires verification of quantity, quality, and condition against purchasing documents. Discrepancies should be documented and handled according to established procedures.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Accept all 20 cases and report the discrepancy at the end of the day., Accept the shipment because the supplier''s invoice is the official record."}'::jsonb),
('mc:a delivery arrives at a foodservice operation. the invoice indicates 20 cases of milk, but the receiver counts only 18 cases. two cases are also found to have damaged packaging. what should the receiver do first?:::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'a delivery arrives at a foodservice operation. the invoice indicates 20 cases of milk, but the receiver counts only 18 cases. two cases are also found to have damaged packaging. what should the receiver do first?', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Receiving requires verification of quantity, quality, and condition against purchasing documents. Discrepancies should be documented and handled according to established procedures.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Document the quantity and condition discrepancy and follow the operation''s receiving procedure.\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Receiving requires verification of quantity, quality, and condition against purchasing documents. Discrepancies should be documented and handled according to established procedures.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Accept all 20 cases and report the discrepancy at the end of the day., Accept the shipment because the supplier''s invoice is the official record."}'::jsonb),
('mc:during monitoring, a foodservice employee discovers that a batch of cooked ground beef has reached only 65°c, below the established critical limit. what is the most appropriate immediate response?:::increase the cooking process until the established critical limit is achieved.', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'during monitoring, a foodservice employee discovers that a batch of cooked ground beef has reached only 65°c, below the established critical limit. what is the most appropriate immediate response?', 'Increase the cooking process until the established critical limit is achieved.', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"A deviation from a critical limit requires corrective action. If the food can still safely undergo the control step, it should be processed until the critical limit is achieved.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Increase the cooking process until the established critical limit is achieved.\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"A deviation from a critical limit requires corrective action. If the food can still safely undergo the control step, it should be processed until the critical limit is achieved.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Serve the food because it has already been cooked., Place the food in refrigeration and serve it tomorrow."}'::jsonb),
