('mc:what study design will you use to determine the relation of wealth status (rich, middle class) and fruit intake (in grams) per day.:::cross-sectional', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'what study design will you use to determine the relation of wealth status (rich, middle class) and fruit intake (in grams) per day.', 'Cross-sectional', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Cross-sectional is the most applicable for determining the current, simple relationship between an exposure (wealth status) and outcome (fruit intake).","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Cross-sectional\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Cross-sectional is the most applicable for determining the current, simple relationship between an exposure (wealth status) and outcome (fruit intake).\n\n• Cohort - aim to determine the cause-and-effect relationship between an exposure and the development of a disease/outcome over a long period of time\n• Case series - aims to provide a detailed description of a small number of patients (case series) or a single patient (case report) with an unusual or novel condition\n• Case-control - aims to determine the past exposures that led to a rare disease/outcome","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Cohort, Case series, Case control"}'::jsonb),
