('mc:refers to a phenomenon which is thought to occur when people observed during a research study temporarily change their behavior or performance:::hawthorne', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'refers to a phenomenon which is thought to occur when people observed during a research study temporarily change their behavior or performance', 'Hawthorne', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Hawthorne effect occurs when participants change their behavior simply because they know they are being observed or studied.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Hawthorne\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Hawthorne effect occurs when participants change their behavior simply because they know they are being observed or studied.\n\n• Ecological fallacy - Mistakenly applying conclusions drawn from group-level data (e.g., states, countries) to individuals within those groups.\n• Systematic error - Consistent, non-random error that always biases measurements in the same direction (e.g., a poorly calibrated scale).\n• Placebo effect - A positive or therapeutic effect that results from a participant''s expectation or belief in the treatment, rather than the treatment''s actual physiological mechanism.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Ecological facally, Systematic error, Placebo effect"}'::jsonb),
('mc:refers to a phenomenon which is thought to occur when people observed during a research study temporarily change their behavior or performance:::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'refers to a phenomenon which is thought to occur when people observed during a research study temporarily change their behavior or performance', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Hawthorne effect occurs when participants change their behavior simply because they know they are being observed or studied.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Hawthorne\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Hawthorne effect occurs when participants change their behavior simply because they know they are being observed or studied.\n\n• Ecological fallacy - Mistakenly applying conclusions drawn from group-level data (e.g., states, countries) to individuals within those groups.\n• Systematic error - Consistent, non-random error that always biases measurements in the same direction (e.g., a poorly calibrated scale).\n• Placebo effect - A positive or therapeutic effect that results from a participant''s expectation or belief in the treatment, rather than the treatment''s actual physiological mechanism.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Ecological facally, Systematic error, Placebo effect"}'::jsonb),
('mc:this means making up data, experiments, or other significant information in proposing, conducting, or reporting research:::fabrication', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'this means making up data, experiments, or other significant information in proposing, conducting, or reporting research', 'Fabrication', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Fabrication is the act of making up data or results entirely and recording or reporting them as true.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Fabrication\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Fabrication is the act of making up data or results entirely and recording or reporting them as true.\n\n• Falsification involves manipulating research materials, equipment, or processes, or changing/omitting data so that the research record does not accurately represent the actual work.\n• Plagiarism is using another person''s ideas, processes, results, or words without giving them proper credit.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Falsification, Plagiarism, Intentional misconduct"}'::jsonb),
('mc:this means making up data, experiments, or other significant information in proposing, conducting, or reporting research:::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'this means making up data, experiments, or other significant information in proposing, conducting, or reporting research', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Fabrication is the act of making up data or results entirely and recording or reporting them as true.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Fabrication\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Fabrication is the act of making up data or results entirely and recording or reporting them as true.\n\n• Falsification involves manipulating research materials, equipment, or processes, or changing/omitting data so that the research record does not accurately represent the actual work.\n• Plagiarism is using another person''s ideas, processes, results, or words without giving them proper credit.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Falsification, Plagiarism, Intentional misconduct"}'::jsonb),
('mc:which of the following is not a nutritional exposure?:::iq score', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'which of the following is not a nutritional exposure?', 'IQ score', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"IQ score functions as an outcome (dependent variable) not an exposure (independent variable/risk factor) in most nutrition research contexts. Physical activity, coffee intake, and reading food labels are all behaviors or exposures that can be investigated as potential predictors of a nutrition-related outcome.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"IQ score\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"IQ score functions as an outcome (dependent variable) not an exposure (independent variable/risk factor) in most nutrition research contexts. Physical activity, coffee intake, and reading food labels are all behaviors or exposures that can be investigated as potential predictors of a nutrition-related outcome.\n\nExposure is the cause or predictor (the independent variable or risk factor), which is the factor being investigated for its effect. Outcome is the effect or result (the dependent variable), which is the disease, event, or condition being studied.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Physical activity, Coffee intake, Reading food labels"}'::jsonb),
('mc:which of the following is not a nutritional exposure?:::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'which of the following is not a nutritional exposure?', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"IQ score functions as an outcome (dependent variable) not an exposure (independent variable/risk factor) in most nutrition research contexts. Physical activity, coffee intake, and reading food labels are all behaviors or exposures that can be investigated as potential predictors of a nutrition-related outcome.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"IQ score\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"IQ score functions as an outcome (dependent variable) not an exposure (independent variable/risk factor) in most nutrition research contexts. Physical activity, coffee intake, and reading food labels are all behaviors or exposures that can be investigated as potential predictors of a nutrition-related outcome.\n\nExposure is the cause or predictor (the independent variable or risk factor), which is the factor being investigated for its effect. Outcome is the effect or result (the dependent variable), which is the disease, event, or condition being studied.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Physical activity, Coffee intake, Reading food labels"}'::jsonb),
('mc:interpret the results of correlation between abdominal circumstances and hdl cholesterol level: r = 0.16, p = 0.002.:::there is no correlation between abdominal circumference and hdl cholesterol level, and it is unlikely that our result is caused by play of chance', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'interpret the results of correlation between abdominal circumstances and hdl cholesterol level: r = 0.16, p = 0.002.', 'There is no correlation between abdominal circumference and HDL cholesterol level, and it is unlikely that our result is caused by play of chance', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"The correct interpretation is that there is essentially no meaningful correlation between abdominal circumference and HDL cholesterol, and the small effect that was found is unlikely to be due to chance.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"There is no correlation between abdominal circumference and HDL cholesterol level, and it is unlikely that our result is caused by play of chance\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"The correct interpretation is that there is essentially no meaningful correlation between abdominal circumference and HDL cholesterol, and the small effect that was found is unlikely to be due to chance.\n\nr = 0.16 (Weak Positive Correlation). The value is close to zero, meaning the linear association is minor. The positive sign means that as abdominal circumference increases, HDL cholesterol tends to slightly increase as well.\n\np= 0.002 (Statistically Significant). Since p<0.05 (the common threshold), we reject the null hypothesis. The observed correlation of 0.16 is highly unlikely to have occurred by random chance.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: There is a significant correlation between abdominal circumference and HDL cholesterol level, There is no correlation between abdominal circumference and HDL cholesterol level, and it is likely that our result is caused by play of chance, Abdominal circumference measurement may predict HDL cholesterol level"}'::jsonb),
('mc:interpret the results of correlation between abdominal circumstances and hdl cholesterol level: r = 0.16, p = 0.002.:::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'interpret the results of correlation between abdominal circumstances and hdl cholesterol level: r = 0.16, p = 0.002.', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"The correct interpretation is that there is essentially no meaningful correlation between abdominal circumference and HDL cholesterol, and the small effect that was found is unlikely to be due to chance.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"There is no correlation between abdominal circumference and HDL cholesterol level, and it is unlikely that our result is caused by play of chance\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"The correct interpretation is that there is essentially no meaningful correlation between abdominal circumference and HDL cholesterol, and the small effect that was found is unlikely to be due to chance.\n\nr = 0.16 (Weak Positive Correlation). The value is close to zero, meaning the linear association is minor. The positive sign means that as abdominal circumference increases, HDL cholesterol tends to slightly increase as well.\n\np= 0.002 (Statistically Significant). Since p<0.05 (the common threshold), we reject the null hypothesis. The observed correlation of 0.16 is highly unlikely to have occurred by random chance.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: There is a significant correlation between abdominal circumference and HDL cholesterol level, There is no correlation between abdominal circumference and HDL cholesterol level, and it is likely that our result is caused by play of chance, Abdominal circumference measurement may predict HDL cholesterol level"}'::jsonb),
('mc:which of the following is false?:::the tests of significance enable the researcher to know for sure that the researcher''s analysis correct', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'which of the following is false?', 'The tests of significance enable the researcher to know for sure that the researcher''s analysis correct', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"In biostatistics and research methodology, statistical tests of significance can never provide absolute certainty or prove that an analysis or hypothesis is \"correct\" for sure.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"The tests of significance enable the researcher to know for sure that the researcher''s analysis correct\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"In biostatistics and research methodology, statistical tests of significance can never provide absolute certainty or prove that an analysis or hypothesis is \"correct\" for sure.\n• Hypothesis testing operates entirely on probability, measuring the strength of evidence against a null hypothesis.\n• A statistically significant result (e.g., p < 0.05) simply means that the observed data or difference is highly unlikely to have occurred by pure random chance under the assumption that the null hypothesis is true. It always leaves open a margin of uncertainty.\n\n• Option A is TRUE: Inferential statistics deal purely with mathematical probability, not certainty. There is always a non-zero risk that your conclusions are false positives or false negatives.\n• Option B is TRUE: There is an inverse mathematical trade-off between the two types of errors. If a researcher strictly lowers the significance level (α, the probability of a Type I error) from 0.05 to 0.01, the standard for rejecting the null hypothesis becomes much harder to meet. This automatically increases the probability of committing a Type II error (β, missing a real effect).\n• Option C is TRUE: Parametric tests (like the t-test or ANOVA) carry strict assumptions about data structure, requiring variables to be continuous and measured on an interval or ratio scale, alongside approximately normal distributions. Non-parametric tests are used for nominal or ordinal data.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: The tests of significance deal with probability not certainty, Generally speaking, a parametic test of significance should be used when the data represent interval or ratio scale, As a researcher decreases the chances of committing a Type I error, the researcher increases the probability of committing a Type II error"}'::jsonb),
