('mc:a cell region acts as a selective barrier to the movement of substances into and out of the cell.:::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'a cell region acts as a selective barrier to the movement of substances into and out of the cell.', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Plasma membrane - outer boundary; controls what enters and exits the cell","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Plasma membrane\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Plasma membrane - outer boundary; controls what enters and exits the cell\n\n• Nucleus - control center; stores DNA/Genetic Instructions.\n• Cytoplasm - jelly-like fluid; holds organelles; site of metabolism\n• Ribosome - protein factory; synthesizes proteins from mRNA","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Nucleus, Cytoplasm, Ribosome"}'::jsonb),
('mc:the primary building substance of cell is::::protein', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'the primary building substance of cell is:', 'Protein', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Protein - structure & function: acts as enzymes to catalyze reactions, builds structural components, and performs transport/movement.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Protein\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Protein - structure & function: acts as enzymes to catalyze reactions, builds structural components, and performs transport/movement.\n\n• Water: for solvent & medium: provides the environment for all chemical reactions (cytoplasm) and acts as a transport mechanism.\n• Fats – for boundary & energy storage: primary component of the cell membrane (boundary) and long-term energy reserve.\n• Carbohydrates – for immediate energy & recognition: the cell''s primary and immediate energy source, also used for cell-to-cell communication.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Water, Fat, Carbohydrates"}'::jsonb),
('mc:the lowest level of structural organization of the living human body:::chemical', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'the lowest level of structural organization of the living human body', 'Chemical', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"To understand the structural hierarchy of the human body, scientists view it in six increasing levels of complexity, from smallest to largest:","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Chemical\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"To understand the structural hierarchy of the human body, scientists view it in six increasing levels of complexity, from smallest to largest:\n\n1. Chemical Level (Lowest): This includes atoms (such as carbon, hydrogen, and oxygen) and molecules (like water, DNA, and glucose) that combine to form the basic building blocks of life.\n2. Cellular Level: Molecules combine to form cells, which are the basic structural and functional units of an organism.\n3. Tissue Level: Groups of similar cells join together to perform a highly specific function (e.g., epithelial, connective, muscular, or nervous tissue).\n4. Organ Level: Different types of tissues join together to form recognizable structures with specific shapes and functions (e.g., the stomach, heart, or liver).\n5. System Level: Related organs connect to form an organ system with a common function (e.g., the digestive or cardiovascular system).\n6. Organismal Level (Highest): All structural parts of the human body working together to create a single, living individual.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Cellular, Tissue, Organ"}'::jsonb),
('mc:the lowest level of structural organization of the living human body:::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'the lowest level of structural organization of the living human body', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"To understand the structural hierarchy of the human body, scientists view it in six increasing levels of complexity, from smallest to largest:","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Chemical\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"To understand the structural hierarchy of the human body, scientists view it in six increasing levels of complexity, from smallest to largest:\n\n1. Chemical Level (Lowest): This includes atoms (such as carbon, hydrogen, and oxygen) and molecules (like water, DNA, and glucose) that combine to form the basic building blocks of life.\n2. Cellular Level: Molecules combine to form cells, which are the basic structural and functional units of an organism.\n3. Tissue Level: Groups of similar cells join together to perform a highly specific function (e.g., epithelial, connective, muscular, or nervous tissue).\n4. Organ Level: Different types of tissues join together to form recognizable structures with specific shapes and functions (e.g., the stomach, heart, or liver).\n5. System Level: Related organs connect to form an organ system with a common function (e.g., the digestive or cardiovascular system).\n6. Organismal Level (Highest): All structural parts of the human body working together to create a single, living individual.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Cellular, Tissue, Organ"}'::jsonb),
('mc:a cell region acts as a selective barrier to the movement of substances into and out of the cell.:::plasma membrane', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'a cell region acts as a selective barrier to the movement of substances into and out of the cell.', 'Plasma membrane', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Plasma membrane - outer boundary; controls what enters and exits the cell","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Plasma membrane\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Plasma membrane - outer boundary; controls what enters and exits the cell\n\n• Nucleus - control center; stores DNA/Genetic Instructions.\n• Cytoplasm - jelly-like fluid; holds organelles; site of metabolism\n• Ribosome - protein factory; synthesizes proteins from mRNA","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Nucleus, Cytoplasm, Ribosome"}'::jsonb),
('mc:age period characterized by rapid differentiation and establishment of system and organs::::embryonic', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'age period characterized by rapid differentiation and establishment of system and organs:', 'Embryonic', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"The correct answer is embryonic period during which the basic body systems and organs rapidly differentiate and form (roughly weeks 3-8 of gestation). ","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Embryonic\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"The correct answer is embryonic period during which the basic body systems and organs rapidly differentiate and form (roughly weeks 3-8 of gestation). \n\nThe early fetal period and late fetal period are characterized more by growth and maturation of already-formed structures rather than initial organ formation. \n\nThe neonatal period refers to the first 28 days after birth, well after organogenesis is complete.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Early fetal, Late fetal, Neonatal"}'::jsonb),
