('mc:the process of physical development from child to adult and refers to the maturation of the body.:::puberty', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'the process of physical development from child to adult and refers to the maturation of the body.', 'Puberty', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Puberty is the biological process during which the body physically matures into an adult capable of reproduction, involving hormonal, physical, and sexual maturation. ","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Puberty\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Puberty is the biological process during which the body physically matures into an adult capable of reproduction, involving hormonal, physical, and sexual maturation. \n\n• Adolescence is the broader psychosocial and developmental life stage encompassing puberty but also including cognitive, emotional, and social development beyond just physical maturation. \n• Pubescence is a less standard/precise term sometimes used interchangeably but not the preferred technical term. \n• Maturity is too general and does not specifically denote the physical maturation process described.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Adolescence, Pubescence, Maturity"}'::jsonb),
('mc:psychological eating disorder that is also known as "george and purge":::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'psychological eating disorder that is also known as "george and purge"', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Bulimia nervosa - cycles of binging (overeating) followed by compensatory behaviors (e.g., purging, excessive exercise).","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Bulimia nervosa\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Bulimia nervosa - cycles of binging (overeating) followed by compensatory behaviors (e.g., purging, excessive exercise).\n\n• Anorexia nervosa - restriction leading to dangerously low body weight due to fear of fatness.\n• Binge eating - recurring episodes of uncontrolled eating (binging) without compensatory behaviors.\n• Pica - persistent consumption of non-food items (e.g., dirt, paint, paper).","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Anorexia nervosa, Binge eating, Pica"}'::jsonb),
('mc:the process of physical development from child to adult and refers to the maturation of the body.:::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'the process of physical development from child to adult and refers to the maturation of the body.', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Puberty is the biological process during which the body physically matures into an adult capable of reproduction, involving hormonal, physical, and sexual maturation. ","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Puberty\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Puberty is the biological process during which the body physically matures into an adult capable of reproduction, involving hormonal, physical, and sexual maturation. \n\n• Adolescence is the broader psychosocial and developmental life stage encompassing puberty but also including cognitive, emotional, and social development beyond just physical maturation. \n• Pubescence is a less standard/precise term sometimes used interchangeably but not the preferred technical term. \n• Maturity is too general and does not specifically denote the physical maturation process described.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Adolescence, Pubescence, Maturity"}'::jsonb),
('mc:which is not true about brain development during school age?:::rapid growth of brain and head', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'which is not true about brain development during school age?', 'Rapid growth of brain and head', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"The correct answer is rapid growth of the brain and head, because by school age brain growth has actually slowed considerably and is nearing completion, having occurred most rapidly during infancy and early childhood. ","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Rapid growth of brain and head\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"The correct answer is rapid growth of the brain and head, because by school age brain growth has actually slowed considerably and is nearing completion, having occurred most rapidly during infancy and early childhood. \n\nSlowing down of brain/head growth and brain growth being essentially complete by school age are both accurate descriptions of this developmental stage.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Slowing down of growth of brain and head, Brain growth is essential complete during school age, All of the above"}'::jsonb),
('mc:which is not true about brain development during school age?:::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'which is not true about brain development during school age?', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"The correct answer is rapid growth of the brain and head, because by school age brain growth has actually slowed considerably and is nearing completion, having occurred most rapidly during infancy and early childhood. ","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Rapid growth of brain and head\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"The correct answer is rapid growth of the brain and head, because by school age brain growth has actually slowed considerably and is nearing completion, having occurred most rapidly during infancy and early childhood. \n\nSlowing down of brain/head growth and brain growth being essentially complete by school age are both accurate descriptions of this developmental stage.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Slowing down of growth of brain and head, Brain growth is essential complete during school age, All of the above"}'::jsonb),
('mc:all are feeding problems prevalent in preschool-age except::::meal skipping', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'all are feeding problems prevalent in preschool-age except:', 'Meal skipping', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Meal skipping is more common among school children and adolescents.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Meal skipping\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Meal skipping is more common among school children and adolescents.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Food jags, Dawdling, Gagging"}'::jsonb),
