('mc:myers-briggs type indicator is an example of .:::learning styles', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'myers-briggs type indicator is an example of .', 'Learning styles', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"While the Myers-Briggs Type Indicator (MBTI) is fundamentally a psychological tool designed to map personality profiles, it is heavily utilized in public health education, academic counseling, and workforce training to classify distinct learning styles.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Learning styles\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"While the Myers-Briggs Type Indicator (MBTI) is fundamentally a psychological tool designed to map personality profiles, it is heavily utilized in public health education, academic counseling, and workforce training to classify distinct learning styles.\n\nThe four primary dichotomies directly correlate with how individuals absorb, process, and retain new information:\n• Extraversion (E) vs. Introversion (I): Dictates a preference for active, group-oriented learning versus reflective, independent study.\n• Sensing (S) vs. Intuition (N): Reflects a preference for concrete facts and sequential learning versus abstract theories and imaginative concepts.\n• Thinking (T) vs. Feeling (F): Influences whether a learner evaluates information using logical, objective analysis or personal, value-driven judgments.\n• Judging (J) vs. Perceiving (P): Governs structural preferences, distinguishing those who thrive on rigid timelines and planned tasks from those who prefer spontaneous, open-ended environments.\n\n• Behavior change theories - Transtheoretical Model (Stages of Change)\n• Learning theories - Social Learning Theory (Bandura)\n• Element of learning - Reinforcement","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Behavior change theories, Learning theories, Element of learning"}'::jsonb),
('mc:myers-briggs type indicator is an example of .:::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'myers-briggs type indicator is an example of .', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"While the Myers-Briggs Type Indicator (MBTI) is fundamentally a psychological tool designed to map personality profiles, it is heavily utilized in public health education, academic counseling, and workforce training to classify distinct learning styles.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Learning styles\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"While the Myers-Briggs Type Indicator (MBTI) is fundamentally a psychological tool designed to map personality profiles, it is heavily utilized in public health education, academic counseling, and workforce training to classify distinct learning styles.\n\nThe four primary dichotomies directly correlate with how individuals absorb, process, and retain new information:\n• Extraversion (E) vs. Introversion (I): Dictates a preference for active, group-oriented learning versus reflective, independent study.\n• Sensing (S) vs. Intuition (N): Reflects a preference for concrete facts and sequential learning versus abstract theories and imaginative concepts.\n• Thinking (T) vs. Feeling (F): Influences whether a learner evaluates information using logical, objective analysis or personal, value-driven judgments.\n• Judging (J) vs. Perceiving (P): Governs structural preferences, distinguishing those who thrive on rigid timelines and planned tasks from those who prefer spontaneous, open-ended environments.\n\n• Behavior change theories - Transtheoretical Model (Stages of Change)\n• Learning theories - Social Learning Theory (Bandura)\n• Element of learning - Reinforcement","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Behavior change theories, Learning theories, Element of learning"}'::jsonb),
