('mc:it describes the adequacy with which any measurement or index reflects the nutritional parameter of interest::::validity', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'it describes the adequacy with which any measurement or index reflects the nutritional parameter of interest:', 'Validity', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Validity - measuring what you intended to measure (truthfulness)","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Validity\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Validity - measuring what you intended to measure (truthfulness)\n\n• Accuracy - how close a measurement is to the true or accepted value (correctness)\n• Specificity - a test''s ability to correctly identify negative cases (truly non-diseased or non-affected).\n• Precision - how close repeated measurements are to each other (consistency/reproducibility)","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Accuracy, Specificity, Precision"}'::jsonb),
('mc:it describes the adequacy with which any measurement or index reflects the nutritional parameter of interest::::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'it describes the adequacy with which any measurement or index reflects the nutritional parameter of interest:', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Validity - measuring what you intended to measure (truthfulness)","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Validity\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Validity - measuring what you intended to measure (truthfulness)\n\n• Accuracy - how close a measurement is to the true or accepted value (correctness)\n• Specificity - a test''s ability to correctly identify negative cases (truly non-diseased or non-affected).\n• Precision - how close repeated measurements are to each other (consistency/reproducibility)","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Accuracy, Specificity, Precision"}'::jsonb),
('mc:which of the following methods can be used to estimate height?:::arm span', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'which of the following methods can be used to estimate height?', 'Arm span', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"When a patient cannot stand straight due to physical disabilities, bedridden status, or severe skeletal deformities (such as kyphosis or scoliosis), indirect anthropometric measurements are used to estimate stature:","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Arm span\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"When a patient cannot stand straight due to physical disabilities, bedridden status, or severe skeletal deformities (such as kyphosis or scoliosis), indirect anthropometric measurements are used to estimate stature:\n\n There is a strong biological correlation between an individual''s arm span and their actual height. In a healthy adult, the distance from the tip of the middle finger of one hand to the tip of the middle finger of the other hand (when arms are extended fully at a 90-degree angle from the torso) roughly equals their max height.\n\nOther common clinical measurements used in height-estimation equations include knee height and demispan (half of the arm span).\n\n• Skinfold thickness - fat deposits\n• MUAC - muscle mass\n• Chest circumference - chest size","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Skinfold thickness, MUAC, Chest circumference"}'::jsonb),
('mc:which of the following methods can be used to estimate height?:::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'which of the following methods can be used to estimate height?', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"When a patient cannot stand straight due to physical disabilities, bedridden status, or severe skeletal deformities (such as kyphosis or scoliosis), indirect anthropometric measurements are used to estimate stature:","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Arm span\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"When a patient cannot stand straight due to physical disabilities, bedridden status, or severe skeletal deformities (such as kyphosis or scoliosis), indirect anthropometric measurements are used to estimate stature:\n\n There is a strong biological correlation between an individual''s arm span and their actual height. In a healthy adult, the distance from the tip of the middle finger of one hand to the tip of the middle finger of the other hand (when arms are extended fully at a 90-degree angle from the torso) roughly equals their max height.\n\nOther common clinical measurements used in height-estimation equations include knee height and demispan (half of the arm span).\n\n• Skinfold thickness - fat deposits\n• MUAC - muscle mass\n• Chest circumference - chest size","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Skinfold thickness, MUAC, Chest circumference"}'::jsonb),
('mc:which of the following is a dietary assessment method which uses specific food item list to record intakes over a given period?:::food frequency', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'which of the following is a dietary assessment method which uses specific food item list to record intakes over a given period?', 'Food frequency', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Food frequency questionnaire (FFQ), which is defined precisely by its use of a structured, predetermined list of food items that respondents report consuming over a specified recall period (e.g., past month or year). ","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Food frequency\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Food frequency questionnaire (FFQ), which is defined precisely by its use of a structured, predetermined list of food items that respondents report consuming over a specified recall period (e.g., past month or year). \n\n• The 24-hour food recall instead captures all foods consumed in the preceding 24 hours without being restricted to a predetermined food list. \n• Diet records involve prospectively recording all foods eaten in real time, also without a fixed food item list. \n• Food weighing involves precisely weighing all food consumed.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: 24-hour food recall, Diet record, Food weighing"}'::jsonb),
('mc:which of the following is a dietary assessment method which uses specific food item list to record intakes over a given period?:::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'which of the following is a dietary assessment method which uses specific food item list to record intakes over a given period?', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Food frequency questionnaire (FFQ), which is defined precisely by its use of a structured, predetermined list of food items that respondents report consuming over a specified recall period (e.g., past month or year). ","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Food frequency\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Food frequency questionnaire (FFQ), which is defined precisely by its use of a structured, predetermined list of food items that respondents report consuming over a specified recall period (e.g., past month or year). \n\n• The 24-hour food recall instead captures all foods consumed in the preceding 24 hours without being restricted to a predetermined food list. \n• Diet records involve prospectively recording all foods eaten in real time, also without a fixed food item list. \n• Food weighing involves precisely weighing all food consumed.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: 24-hour food recall, Diet record, Food weighing"}'::jsonb),
('mc:a condition when folate and vitamin b12 are deficient:::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'a condition when folate and vitamin b12 are deficient', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Megaloblastic anemia - RBCs are large and immature due to impaired DNA synthesis; large (macrocytic) RBCs; caused by folate or vitamin B12 deficiency.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Megaloblastic anemia\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Megaloblastic anemia - RBCs are large and immature due to impaired DNA synthesis; large (macrocytic) RBCs; caused by folate or vitamin B12 deficiency.\n\n• Microcytic anemia - RBCs are abnormally small.\n• Pernicious anemia - megaloblastic anemia caused specifically by lack of intrinsic factor (for B12 absorption).\n• Hypochromic anemia - RBCs are pale due to insufficient hemoglobin.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Microcytic anemia, Pernicious anemia, Hypochromic anemia"}'::jsonb),
('mc:which of the following describes nutrition surveillance?:::keeping watch over nutrition in order to make decisions that will lead to improvement in nutrition in population', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'which of the following describes nutrition surveillance?', 'Keeping watch over nutrition in order to make decisions that will lead to improvement in nutrition in population', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"This responds to the main goal of nutrition surveillance is to continuously monitor a population''s nutritional status so leaders can make informed decisions and policies that improve health and fix nutrition problems.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Keeping watch over nutrition in order to make decisions that will lead to improvement in nutrition in population\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"This responds to the main goal of nutrition surveillance is to continuously monitor a population''s nutritional status so leaders can make informed decisions and policies that improve health and fix nutrition problems.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Provides information about dietary intake patterns, specific foods consumed and estimated nutrient intake, Mortality and morbidity data to identify groups at high risk and indicate the extent of risk of the community, All of the above"}'::jsonb)
