('mc:it is a condition when thiamin is deficient:::beri-beri', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'it is a condition when thiamin is deficient', 'Beri-beri', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Beri-beri is a classic, well-established clinical deficiency disease resulting from inadequate thiamine (vitamin B1) intake, presenting with neurological (dry beri-beri) or cardiovascular (wet beri-beri) manifestations. ","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Beri-beri\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Beri-beri is a classic, well-established clinical deficiency disease resulting from inadequate thiamine (vitamin B1) intake, presenting with neurological (dry beri-beri) or cardiovascular (wet beri-beri) manifestations. \n\n• Pellagra - niacin (vitamin B3) deficiency \n• Ariboflavinosis - riboflavin (vitamin B2) deficiency \n• Tiqui-tiqui is actually a traditional Filipino rice bran extract historically used as a treatment/cure for beri-beri","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Pellagra, Ariboflavinosis, Tiqui-tiqui"}'::jsonb),
('mc:it is a condition when thiamin is deficient:::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'it is a condition when thiamin is deficient', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Beri-beri is a classic, well-established clinical deficiency disease resulting from inadequate thiamine (vitamin B1) intake, presenting with neurological (dry beri-beri) or cardiovascular (wet beri-beri) manifestations. ","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Beri-beri\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Beri-beri is a classic, well-established clinical deficiency disease resulting from inadequate thiamine (vitamin B1) intake, presenting with neurological (dry beri-beri) or cardiovascular (wet beri-beri) manifestations. \n\n• Pellagra - niacin (vitamin B3) deficiency \n• Ariboflavinosis - riboflavin (vitamin B2) deficiency \n• Tiqui-tiqui is actually a traditional Filipino rice bran extract historically used as a treatment/cure for beri-beri","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Pellagra, Ariboflavinosis, Tiqui-tiqui"}'::jsonb),
('mc:multiple replicates of 24-hour food recall is used to measure nutrient intake to meet level of study objectives:::2nd', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'multiple replicates of 24-hour food recall is used to measure nutrient intake to meet level of study objectives', '2nd', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Second-level study objectives (often referred to as secondary or specific objectives) break down primary research goal into actionable, measurable steps. First-level (primary) objective states WHAT you want to achieve overall, second-level objectives explain HOW you will achieve it by focusing on specific variables, steps, or populations.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"2nd\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Second-level study objectives (often referred to as secondary or specific objectives) break down primary research goal into actionable, measurable steps. First-level (primary) objective states WHAT you want to achieve overall, second-level objectives explain HOW you will achieve it by focusing on specific variables, steps, or populations.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: 1st, 3rd, 4th"}'::jsonb),
('mc:multiple replicates of 24-hour food recall is used to measure nutrient intake to meet level of study objectives:::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'multiple replicates of 24-hour food recall is used to measure nutrient intake to meet level of study objectives', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Second-level study objectives (often referred to as secondary or specific objectives) break down primary research goal into actionable, measurable steps. First-level (primary) objective states WHAT you want to achieve overall, second-level objectives explain HOW you will achieve it by focusing on specific variables, steps, or populations.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"2nd\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Second-level study objectives (often referred to as secondary or specific objectives) break down primary research goal into actionable, measurable steps. First-level (primary) objective states WHAT you want to achieve overall, second-level objectives explain HOW you will achieve it by focusing on specific variables, steps, or populations.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: 1st, 3rd, 4th"}'::jsonb),
('mc:moderate idd in the population is indicated by median uie level of::::20-49 µg/l', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'moderate idd in the population is indicated by median uie level of:', '20-49 µg/L', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"The severity of Iodine Deficiency Disorders (IDD) in a population is classified based on the Median Urinary Iodine Excretion (UIE) or Concentration (UIC) levels:","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"20-49 µg/L\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"The severity of Iodine Deficiency Disorders (IDD) in a population is classified based on the Median Urinary Iodine Excretion (UIE) or Concentration (UIC) levels:\n• Severe Iodine Deficiency: Median UIE of <20 µg/L\n• Moderate Iodine Deficiency: Median UIE of 20–49 µg/L\n• Mild Iodine Deficiency: Median UIE of 50–99 µg/L\n• Adequate Iodine Nutrition: Median UIE of 100–199 µg/L (Target public health range for school-age children)","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: <20 µg/L, 50-99 µg/L, >100 µg/L"}'::jsonb),
('mc:moderate idd in the population is indicated by median uie level of::::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'moderate idd in the population is indicated by median uie level of:', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"The severity of Iodine Deficiency Disorders (IDD) in a population is classified based on the Median Urinary Iodine Excretion (UIE) or Concentration (UIC) levels:","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"20-49 µg/L\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"The severity of Iodine Deficiency Disorders (IDD) in a population is classified based on the Median Urinary Iodine Excretion (UIE) or Concentration (UIC) levels:\n• Severe Iodine Deficiency: Median UIE of <20 µg/L\n• Moderate Iodine Deficiency: Median UIE of 20–49 µg/L\n• Mild Iodine Deficiency: Median UIE of 50–99 µg/L\n• Adequate Iodine Nutrition: Median UIE of 100–199 µg/L (Target public health range for school-age children)","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: <20 µg/L, 50-99 µg/L, >100 µg/L"}'::jsonb),
('mc:the plasma retinol level of flora is 25 µg/dl. how do you interpret this value?:::flora has an acceptable plasma retinol level', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'the plasma retinol level of flora is 25 µg/dl. how do you interpret this value?', 'Flora has an acceptable plasma retinol level', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"In public health and clinical nutrition, the World Health Organization (WHO) and the National Institutes of Health (NIH) establish specific reference thresholds for circulating plasma or serum retinol to evaluate Vitamin A status:","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Flora has an acceptable plasma retinol level\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"In public health and clinical nutrition, the World Health Organization (WHO) and the National Institutes of Health (NIH) establish specific reference thresholds for circulating plasma or serum retinol to evaluate Vitamin A status:\n\n• Deficient / Severe Deficiency: Less than 10 µg/dL (< 0.35 µmol/L).\n• Low / Subclinical Deficiency: 10 to 20 µg/dL (0.35 to 0.70 µmol/L).\n• Acceptable / Adequate: More than 20 µg/dL (≥ 0.70 µmol/L).\n\nFlora''s level is 25 µg/dL, which places her comfortably above the 20 µg/dL cutoff, classifying her Vitamin A status as acceptable or adequate.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Flora has a deficient plasma retinol level, Flora has a low plasma retinol level, Flora has high plasma retinal level"}'::jsonb),
('mc:the plasma retinol level of flora is 25 µg/dl. how do you interpret this value?:::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'the plasma retinol level of flora is 25 µg/dl. how do you interpret this value?', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"In public health and clinical nutrition, the World Health Organization (WHO) and the National Institutes of Health (NIH) establish specific reference thresholds for circulating plasma or serum retinol to evaluate Vitamin A status:","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Flora has an acceptable plasma retinol level\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"In public health and clinical nutrition, the World Health Organization (WHO) and the National Institutes of Health (NIH) establish specific reference thresholds for circulating plasma or serum retinol to evaluate Vitamin A status:\n\n• Deficient / Severe Deficiency: Less than 10 µg/dL (< 0.35 µmol/L).\n• Low / Subclinical Deficiency: 10 to 20 µg/dL (0.35 to 0.70 µmol/L).\n• Acceptable / Adequate: More than 20 µg/dL (≥ 0.70 µmol/L).\n\nFlora''s level is 25 µg/dL, which places her comfortably above the 20 µg/dL cutoff, classifying her Vitamin A status as acceptable or adequate.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Flora has a deficient plasma retinol level, Flora has a low plasma retinol level, Flora has high plasma retinal level"}'::jsonb),
('mc:using the who standard, what is the classification of goiter if the thyroid gland is enlarged and easily visible from a distance?:::grade 2', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'using the who standard, what is the classification of goiter if the thyroid gland is enlarged and easily visible from a distance?', 'Grade 2', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"In 1994, the WHO, UNICEF, and the International Council for Control of Iodine Deficiency Disorders simplified the scale down to the standard 3-tier system:","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Grade 2\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"In 1994, the WHO, UNICEF, and the International Council for Control of Iodine Deficiency Disorders simplified the scale down to the standard 3-tier system:\n\n• Grade 0: No goiter (neither palpable nor visible).\n• Grade 1: A mass that is palpable but not visible when the neck is in a normal position.\n• Grade 2: A swelling that is clearly visible when the neck is in a normal position and is consistent with an enlarged thyroid upon palpation.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Grade 0, Grade 1, Grade 3"}'::jsonb),
('mc:using the who standard, what is the classification of goiter if the thyroid gland is enlarged and easily visible from a distance?:::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'using the who standard, what is the classification of goiter if the thyroid gland is enlarged and easily visible from a distance?', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"In 1994, the WHO, UNICEF, and the International Council for Control of Iodine Deficiency Disorders simplified the scale down to the standard 3-tier system:","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Grade 2\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"In 1994, the WHO, UNICEF, and the International Council for Control of Iodine Deficiency Disorders simplified the scale down to the standard 3-tier system:\n\n• Grade 0: No goiter (neither palpable nor visible).\n• Grade 1: A mass that is palpable but not visible when the neck is in a normal position.\n• Grade 2: A swelling that is clearly visible when the neck is in a normal position and is consistent with an enlarged thyroid upon palpation.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Grade 0, Grade 1, Grade 3"}'::jsonb),
('mc:keshan disease is a deficiency in?:::selenium', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'keshan disease is a deficiency in?', 'Selenium', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"The correct answer is selenium, a trace mineral deficiency specifically linked to Keshan disease, an endemic cardiomyopathy first identified in selenium-poor regions of China.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Selenium\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"The correct answer is selenium, a trace mineral deficiency specifically linked to Keshan disease, an endemic cardiomyopathy first identified in selenium-poor regions of China.\n\nKeshan disease is an endemic, potentially fatal form of dilated cardiomyopathy (weakening and enlargement of the heart muscle).\n\n It is directly linked to severe dietary selenium deficiency, typically occurring in rural regions where the soil lacks selenium, leaving local agricultural crops deplete of the mineral. It was first documented in Keshan County, China.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Chromium, Zinc, Folate"}'::jsonb),
('mc:keshan disease is a deficiency in?:::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'keshan disease is a deficiency in?', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"The correct answer is selenium, a trace mineral deficiency specifically linked to Keshan disease, an endemic cardiomyopathy first identified in selenium-poor regions of China.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Selenium\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"The correct answer is selenium, a trace mineral deficiency specifically linked to Keshan disease, an endemic cardiomyopathy first identified in selenium-poor regions of China.\n\nKeshan disease is an endemic, potentially fatal form of dilated cardiomyopathy (weakening and enlargement of the heart muscle).\n\n It is directly linked to severe dietary selenium deficiency, typically occurring in rural regions where the soil lacks selenium, leaving local agricultural crops deplete of the mineral. It was first documented in Keshan County, China.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Chromium, Zinc, Folate"}'::jsonb),
('mc:a condition characterized as having sand paper feel of the skin::::follicular hyperkeratosis', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'a condition characterized as having sand paper feel of the skin:', 'Follicular hyperkeratosis', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"In clinical nutrition and dermatology, follicular hyperkeratosis (also known as phrynoderma or \"toad skin\") is classic textbook-defined by a rough, sandpaper-like texture on the skin.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Follicular hyperkeratosis\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"In clinical nutrition and dermatology, follicular hyperkeratosis (also known as phrynoderma or \"toad skin\") is classic textbook-defined by a rough, sandpaper-like texture on the skin.\n\nThis condition occurs when the body overproduces keratin (a structural protein), which forms hard, scaly plugs that block the openings of hair follicles. This creates tiny, elevated, rough bumps typically found on the back of the upper arms, thighs, and buttocks.\n\nIn a board exam context, follicular hyperkeratosis is highly pathognomonic for Vitamin A deficiency or a chronic lack of essential fatty acids (like Omega-3).\n\n• Pellagrous dermatitis - symmetric, dark, scaling rash on sun-exposed skin (the \"casal''s necklace\").\n• Petechiae - tiny, non-blanching red or purple dots on the skin due to bleeding under the skin.\n• Xerosis - generalized, abnormal dryness of the skin (or mucous membranes).","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Pellagrous dermatitis, Petechiae, Xerosis"}'::jsonb),
('mc:a condition characterized as having sand paper feel of the skin::::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'a condition characterized as having sand paper feel of the skin:', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"In clinical nutrition and dermatology, follicular hyperkeratosis (also known as phrynoderma or \"toad skin\") is classic textbook-defined by a rough, sandpaper-like texture on the skin.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Follicular hyperkeratosis\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"In clinical nutrition and dermatology, follicular hyperkeratosis (also known as phrynoderma or \"toad skin\") is classic textbook-defined by a rough, sandpaper-like texture on the skin.\n\nThis condition occurs when the body overproduces keratin (a structural protein), which forms hard, scaly plugs that block the openings of hair follicles. This creates tiny, elevated, rough bumps typically found on the back of the upper arms, thighs, and buttocks.\n\nIn a board exam context, follicular hyperkeratosis is highly pathognomonic for Vitamin A deficiency or a chronic lack of essential fatty acids (like Omega-3).\n\n• Pellagrous dermatitis - symmetric, dark, scaling rash on sun-exposed skin (the \"casal''s necklace\").\n• Petechiae - tiny, non-blanching red or purple dots on the skin due to bleeding under the skin.\n• Xerosis - generalized, abnormal dryness of the skin (or mucous membranes).","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Pellagrous dermatitis, Petechiae, Xerosis"}'::jsonb),
('mc:a condition characterized by vertical fissuring in the lips::::cheilosis', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'a condition characterized by vertical fissuring in the lips:', 'Cheilosis', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Cheilosis is the vertical cracking/fissuring at the lips, sometimes extending to angular fissures at the mouth corners (angular stomatitis).","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Cheilosis\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Cheilosis is the vertical cracking/fissuring at the lips, sometimes extending to angular fissures at the mouth corners (angular stomatitis).\n\n• Fluorosis - affects tooth enamel (mottling) from excess fluoride\n• Glossitis - inflammation of the tongue\n• Dermatitis - general skin inflammation","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Fluorosis, Glossitis, Dermatitis"}'::jsonb),
('mc:a condition characterized by vertical fissuring in the lips::::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'a condition characterized by vertical fissuring in the lips:', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Cheilosis is the vertical cracking/fissuring at the lips, sometimes extending to angular fissures at the mouth corners (angular stomatitis).","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Cheilosis\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Cheilosis is the vertical cracking/fissuring at the lips, sometimes extending to angular fissures at the mouth corners (angular stomatitis).\n\n• Fluorosis - affects tooth enamel (mottling) from excess fluoride\n• Glossitis - inflammation of the tongue\n• Dermatitis - general skin inflammation","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Fluorosis, Glossitis, Dermatitis"}'::jsonb),
('mc:schilling test is used to assess the status of this vitamin?:::vit b12', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'schilling test is used to assess the status of this vitamin?', 'Vit B12', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"The Schilling test is a classic diagnostic procedure specifically designed to assess the body''s ability to absorb vitamin B12, including distinguishing whether malabsorption is due to intrinsic factor deficiency (pernicious anemia) or another cause.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Vit B12\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"The Schilling test is a classic diagnostic procedure specifically designed to assess the body''s ability to absorb vitamin B12, including distinguishing whether malabsorption is due to intrinsic factor deficiency (pernicious anemia) or another cause.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Vit B6, Vit B2, Vit B1"}'::jsonb),
('mc:schilling test is used to assess the status of this vitamin?:::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'schilling test is used to assess the status of this vitamin?', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"The Schilling test is a classic diagnostic procedure specifically designed to assess the body''s ability to absorb vitamin B12, including distinguishing whether malabsorption is due to intrinsic factor deficiency (pernicious anemia) or another cause.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Vit B12\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"The Schilling test is a classic diagnostic procedure specifically designed to assess the body''s ability to absorb vitamin B12, including distinguishing whether malabsorption is due to intrinsic factor deficiency (pernicious anemia) or another cause.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Vit B6, Vit B2, Vit B1"}'::jsonb),
('mc:a condition when folate and vitamin b12 are deficient:::megaloblastic anemia', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'a condition when folate and vitamin b12 are deficient', 'Megaloblastic anemia', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Megaloblastic anemia - RBCs are large and immature due to impaired DNA synthesis; large (macrocytic) RBCs; caused by folate or vitamin B12 deficiency.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Megaloblastic anemia\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Megaloblastic anemia - RBCs are large and immature due to impaired DNA synthesis; large (macrocytic) RBCs; caused by folate or vitamin B12 deficiency.\n\n• Microcytic anemia - RBCs are abnormally small.\n• Pernicious anemia - megaloblastic anemia caused specifically by lack of intrinsic factor (for B12 absorption).\n• Hypochromic anemia - RBCs are pale due to insufficient hemoglobin.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Microcytic anemia, Pernicious anemia, Hypochromic anemia"}'::jsonb),
('mc:which among these food items is a good source of preformed vitamin a?:::egg yolk', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'which among these food items is a good source of preformed vitamin a?', 'Egg yolk', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Egg yolk contains preformed vitamin A (retinol) directly, as animal-source foods provide vitamin A in its ready-to-use, preformed (retinol) state. ","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Egg yolk\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Egg yolk contains preformed vitamin A (retinol) directly, as animal-source foods provide vitamin A in its ready-to-use, preformed (retinol) state. \n\nGrains, peanuts, and corn oil are all plant-based foods that, if they contain any vitamin A activity at all, would provide it as provitamin A carotenoids (like beta-carotene) rather than preformed vitamin A, which the body must convert before use.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Grains, Peanuts, Corn oil"}'::jsonb),
('mc:which among these food items is a good source of preformed vitamin a?:::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'which among these food items is a good source of preformed vitamin a?', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Egg yolk contains preformed vitamin A (retinol) directly, as animal-source foods provide vitamin A in its ready-to-use, preformed (retinol) state. ","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Egg yolk\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Egg yolk contains preformed vitamin A (retinol) directly, as animal-source foods provide vitamin A in its ready-to-use, preformed (retinol) state. \n\nGrains, peanuts, and corn oil are all plant-based foods that, if they contain any vitamin A activity at all, would provide it as provitamin A carotenoids (like beta-carotene) rather than preformed vitamin A, which the body must convert before use.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Grains, Peanuts, Corn oil"}'::jsonb),
('mc:laxatives can affect nutrient absorption through:::altering motility', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'laxatives can affect nutrient absorption through', 'altering motility', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Laxatives work primarily by speeding up gastrointestinal transit time, which reduces the contact time between food/nutrients and the absorptive intestinal surface, thereby impairing nutrient absorption.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"altering motility\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Laxatives work primarily by speeding up gastrointestinal transit time, which reduces the contact time between food/nutrients and the absorptive intestinal surface, thereby impairing nutrient absorption.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: binding to nutrients, irritating the GIT, interfering with taste"}'::jsonb),
