('mc:an essential trace element for both humans and animals.:::molybdenum', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'an essential trace element for both humans and animals.', 'Molybdenum', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Molybdenum is a recognized essential trace element required by both humans and animals for enzymatic cofactor functions (such as in xanthine oxidase). ","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Molybdenum\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Molybdenum is a recognized essential trace element required by both humans and animals for enzymatic cofactor functions (such as in xanthine oxidase). \n\nBromine, lithium, and vanadium are elements that have been studied for potential biological roles but are not established as essential trace elements with confirmed dietary requirement status in the same way molybdenum is officially recognized.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Bromine, Lithium, Vanadium"}'::jsonb),
