('mc:it is a condition when vit b3 is deficient:::pellagra', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'it is a condition when vit b3 is deficient', 'Pellagra', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Pellagra is a niacin deficiency, classically presenting with the \"three Ds\": dermatitis, diarrhea, and dementia (and if untreated, a fourth D - death).","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Pellagra\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Pellagra is a niacin deficiency, classically presenting with the \"three Ds\": dermatitis, diarrhea, and dementia (and if untreated, a fourth D - death).\n\n• Ariboflavinosis – riboflavin deficiency\n• Beri-beri – thiamin deficiency\n• Tiqui-tiqui - extract to cure beri-beri","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Ariboflavinosis, Beri-beri, Tiqui-Tiqui"}'::jsonb),
('mc:it is a condition when vit b3 is deficient:::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'it is a condition when vit b3 is deficient', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Pellagra is a niacin deficiency, classically presenting with the \"three Ds\": dermatitis, diarrhea, and dementia (and if untreated, a fourth D - death).","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Pellagra\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Pellagra is a niacin deficiency, classically presenting with the \"three Ds\": dermatitis, diarrhea, and dementia (and if untreated, a fourth D - death).\n\n• Ariboflavinosis – riboflavin deficiency\n• Beri-beri – thiamin deficiency\n• Tiqui-tiqui - extract to cure beri-beri","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Ariboflavinosis, Beri-beri, Tiqui-Tiqui"}'::jsonb),
('mc:which of the following is a functional test used to assess vitamin a?:::dark adaptation test', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'which of the following is a functional test used to assess vitamin a?', 'Dark adaptation test', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"In nutritional assessment, diagnostic tools are broadly split into biochemical (static) tests and functional tests:","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Dark adaptation test\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"In nutritional assessment, diagnostic tools are broadly split into biochemical (static) tests and functional tests:\n\nA functional test measures the actual biological or physiological consequence of a nutrient deficiency on body function. Vitamin A is vital for the formation of rhodopsin (visual purple) in the rod cells of the retina, which allows eyes to see in dim light. The dark adaptation test evaluates the functional impairment of night vision caused by early-stage Vitamin A deficiency.\n\n\t• Test for serum albumin: A biochemical test primarily used as a general indicator of visceral protein status or systemic inflammation rather than a vitamin check.\n\t• Immunological test: Assesses immune system components (like T-cell counts or antibody levels). While Vitamin A impacts immunity, it is not a direct diagnostic test for it.\n\t• Test for urinary creatinine: Used alongside height measurements to calculate the creatinine-height index (CHI), which estimates skeletal muscle mass or evaluates kidney function.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Immunological test, Test for serum albumin, Test for urinary creatinine"}'::jsonb),
('mc:which of the following is a functional test used to assess vitamin a?:::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'which of the following is a functional test used to assess vitamin a?', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"In nutritional assessment, diagnostic tools are broadly split into biochemical (static) tests and functional tests:","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Dark adaptation test\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"In nutritional assessment, diagnostic tools are broadly split into biochemical (static) tests and functional tests:\n\nA functional test measures the actual biological or physiological consequence of a nutrient deficiency on body function. Vitamin A is vital for the formation of rhodopsin (visual purple) in the rod cells of the retina, which allows eyes to see in dim light. The dark adaptation test evaluates the functional impairment of night vision caused by early-stage Vitamin A deficiency.\n\n\t• Test for serum albumin: A biochemical test primarily used as a general indicator of visceral protein status or systemic inflammation rather than a vitamin check.\n\t• Immunological test: Assesses immune system components (like T-cell counts or antibody levels). While Vitamin A impacts immunity, it is not a direct diagnostic test for it.\n\t• Test for urinary creatinine: Used alongside height measurements to calculate the creatinine-height index (CHI), which estimates skeletal muscle mass or evaluates kidney function.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Immunological test, Test for serum albumin, Test for urinary creatinine"}'::jsonb),
('mc:breastmilk can be used for biochemical assessment of ?:::vit a', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'breastmilk can be used for biochemical assessment of ?', 'Vit A', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"In public health and community nutrition assessments, breast milk retinol concentration is a well-established, non-invasive biomarker used to evaluate the Vitamin A status of both lactating mothers and their breastfed infants.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Vit A\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"In public health and community nutrition assessments, breast milk retinol concentration is a well-established, non-invasive biomarker used to evaluate the Vitamin A status of both lactating mothers and their breastfed infants.\n\n• Breast milk retinol levels are highly sensitive to maternal Vitamin A intake and liver reserves.\n• The World Health Organization (WHO) recognizes breast milk testing as a valuable epidemiological tool. A high prevalence of low breast milk retinol concentrations (e.g., < 1.05 µmol/L or < 30 µg/dL) in a community indicates a subclinical Vitamin A deficiency problem requiring public health interventions like maternal postpartum supplementation or food fortification.\n\n• Vitamin K & Vitamin D: Human breast milk is inherently and universally low in both Vitamin K and Vitamin D, regardless of maternal nutritional status. Because their baseline levels in milk are naturally close to zero, breast milk is not used as a reliable biochemical index to assess systemic status for these vitamins.\n• Vitamin E: While present in breast milk (especially colostrum), serum alpha-tocopherol blood tests remain the primary clinical diagnostic tool for Vitamin E, rather than breast milk analysis.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Vit K, Vit E, Vit D"}'::jsonb),
('mc:breastmilk can be used for biochemical assessment of ?:::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'breastmilk can be used for biochemical assessment of ?', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"In public health and community nutrition assessments, breast milk retinol concentration is a well-established, non-invasive biomarker used to evaluate the Vitamin A status of both lactating mothers and their breastfed infants.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Vit A\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"In public health and community nutrition assessments, breast milk retinol concentration is a well-established, non-invasive biomarker used to evaluate the Vitamin A status of both lactating mothers and their breastfed infants.\n\n• Breast milk retinol levels are highly sensitive to maternal Vitamin A intake and liver reserves.\n• The World Health Organization (WHO) recognizes breast milk testing as a valuable epidemiological tool. A high prevalence of low breast milk retinol concentrations (e.g., < 1.05 µmol/L or < 30 µg/dL) in a community indicates a subclinical Vitamin A deficiency problem requiring public health interventions like maternal postpartum supplementation or food fortification.\n\n• Vitamin K & Vitamin D: Human breast milk is inherently and universally low in both Vitamin K and Vitamin D, regardless of maternal nutritional status. Because their baseline levels in milk are naturally close to zero, breast milk is not used as a reliable biochemical index to assess systemic status for these vitamins.\n• Vitamin E: While present in breast milk (especially colostrum), serum alpha-tocopherol blood tests remain the primary clinical diagnostic tool for Vitamin E, rather than breast milk analysis.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Vit K, Vit E, Vit D"}'::jsonb),
('mc:what is the biochemical criterion included in the who classification scheme for xerophthalmia?:::plasma vitamin a level', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'what is the biochemical criterion included in the who classification scheme for xerophthalmia?', 'Plasma Vitamin A level', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Plasma vitamin A level is the standard biochemical marker used alongside clinical/ocular signs (like night blindness, Bitot''s spots, corneal xerosis) in the WHO classification of xerophthalmia severity.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Plasma Vitamin A level\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Plasma vitamin A level is the standard biochemical marker used alongside clinical/ocular signs (like night blindness, Bitot''s spots, corneal xerosis) in the WHO classification of xerophthalmia severity.\n\n• Skinfold thickness – measures body fat\n• Sensitivity test for Vitamin A deficiency - vague, non-standard term rather than a recognized biochemical criterion\n• Urinary Vitamin A concentration - not the standard biochemical measure used for vitamin A status, since vitamin A is fat-soluble and stored/circulated differently than water-soluble vitamins excreted in urine.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Skinfold thickness, Sensitivity test for Vitamin A deficiency, Urinary Vitamin A concentration"}'::jsonb),
('mc:what is the biochemical criterion included in the who classification scheme for xerophthalmia?:::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'what is the biochemical criterion included in the who classification scheme for xerophthalmia?', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Plasma vitamin A level is the standard biochemical marker used alongside clinical/ocular signs (like night blindness, Bitot''s spots, corneal xerosis) in the WHO classification of xerophthalmia severity.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Plasma Vitamin A level\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Plasma vitamin A level is the standard biochemical marker used alongside clinical/ocular signs (like night blindness, Bitot''s spots, corneal xerosis) in the WHO classification of xerophthalmia severity.\n\n• Skinfold thickness – measures body fat\n• Sensitivity test for Vitamin A deficiency - vague, non-standard term rather than a recognized biochemical criterion\n• Urinary Vitamin A concentration - not the standard biochemical measure used for vitamin A status, since vitamin A is fat-soluble and stored/circulated differently than water-soluble vitamins excreted in urine.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Skinfold thickness, Sensitivity test for Vitamin A deficiency, Urinary Vitamin A concentration"}'::jsonb),
('mc:irma weighs 56 kg and stands 5 feet. what is her body mass index?:::24.11 kg/m²', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'irma weighs 56 kg and stands 5 feet. what is her body mass index?', '24.11 kg/m²', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Body Mass Index (BMI) is calculated using the formula:","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"24.11 kg/m²\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Body Mass Index (BMI) is calculated using the formula:\nBMI = Weight (kg) / [Height (m)]²\n\n1. Convert height from feet to meters:\n\t• 5 feet = 60 inches\n\t• 60 inches × 0.0254 meters/inch = 1.524 meters\n\n2. Square the height in meters:\n\t• 1.524 × 1.524 = 2.3226 m²\n\n3. Divide weight by squared height:\n\t• 56 kg / 2.3226 m² = 24.11\n\nAccording to both WHO and Asia-Pacific standards, a BMI of 24.11 places Irma in the \"Normal\" weight category.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: 36.75 kg/m², 23.33 kg/m², 36.14 kg/m²"}'::jsonb),
('mc:irma weighs 56 kg and stands 5 feet. what is her body mass index?:::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'irma weighs 56 kg and stands 5 feet. what is her body mass index?', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Body Mass Index (BMI) is calculated using the formula:","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"24.11 kg/m²\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Body Mass Index (BMI) is calculated using the formula:\nBMI = Weight (kg) / [Height (m)]²\n\n1. Convert height from feet to meters:\n\t• 5 feet = 60 inches\n\t• 60 inches × 0.0254 meters/inch = 1.524 meters\n\n2. Square the height in meters:\n\t• 1.524 × 1.524 = 2.3226 m²\n\n3. Divide weight by squared height:\n\t• 56 kg / 2.3226 m² = 24.11\n\nAccording to both WHO and Asia-Pacific standards, a BMI of 24.11 places Irma in the \"Normal\" weight category.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: 36.75 kg/m², 23.33 kg/m², 36.14 kg/m²"}'::jsonb),
('mc:the muac of ivanah kawali indicates 11.5 cm. what is her nutritional status?:::moderate acute malnutrition', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'the muac of ivanah kawali indicates 11.5 cm. what is her nutritional status?', 'Moderate acute malnutrition', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"The correct answer is Moderate Acute Malnutrition (MAM), since a MUAC value in the range of approximately 11.5-12.4 cm in children under five is classified by WHO as indicating moderate acute malnutrition. ","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Moderate acute malnutrition\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"The correct answer is Moderate Acute Malnutrition (MAM), since a MUAC value in the range of approximately 11.5-12.4 cm in children under five is classified by WHO as indicating moderate acute malnutrition. \n\nMUAC is a simple, field-friendly screening tool widely used in community and emergency nutrition programs to rapidly identify acute malnutrition in young children.\n\n• Severe Acute Malnutrition (SAM): Diagnosed only when the measurement drops strictly below 11.5 cm (<115 mm), or if bilateral pitting edema is present.\n• Normal Nutritional Status: Indicated by a MUAC of 12.5 cm or higher (≥125 mm).","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Normal, Stunting, Severe Acute malnutrition"}'::jsonb),
('mc:the muac of ivanah kawali indicates 11.5 cm. what is her nutritional status?:::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'the muac of ivanah kawali indicates 11.5 cm. what is her nutritional status?', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"The correct answer is Moderate Acute Malnutrition (MAM), since a MUAC value in the range of approximately 11.5-12.4 cm in children under five is classified by WHO as indicating moderate acute malnutrition. ","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Moderate acute malnutrition\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"The correct answer is Moderate Acute Malnutrition (MAM), since a MUAC value in the range of approximately 11.5-12.4 cm in children under five is classified by WHO as indicating moderate acute malnutrition. \n\nMUAC is a simple, field-friendly screening tool widely used in community and emergency nutrition programs to rapidly identify acute malnutrition in young children.\n\n• Severe Acute Malnutrition (SAM): Diagnosed only when the measurement drops strictly below 11.5 cm (<115 mm), or if bilateral pitting edema is present.\n• Normal Nutritional Status: Indicated by a MUAC of 12.5 cm or higher (≥125 mm).","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Normal, Stunting, Severe Acute malnutrition"}'::jsonb),
('mc:according to who, if the prevalence rate of anemia in a population is 20% it can be classified as?:::moderate', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'according to who, if the prevalence rate of anemia in a population is 20% it can be classified as?', 'Moderate', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"The World Health Organization (WHO) classifies the severity of anemia as a public health problem based on its prevalence rate within a given population:","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Moderate\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"The World Health Organization (WHO) classifies the severity of anemia as a public health problem based on its prevalence rate within a given population:\n\n• Normal (No problem): Prevalence of 4.9% or lower\n• Mild Public Health Problem: Prevalence of 5.0% to 19.9%\n• Moderate Public Health Problem: Prevalence of 20.0% to 39.9% — Since the population prevalence is exactly 20%, it marks the lower boundary of a moderate public health crisis, requiring immediate targeted community interventions (such as massive iron-folic acid supplementation or food fortification programs).\n• Severe Public Health Problem: Prevalence of 40.0% or higher","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: High, Mild, Low"}'::jsonb),
('mc:according to who, if the prevalence rate of anemia in a population is 20% it can be classified as?:::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'according to who, if the prevalence rate of anemia in a population is 20% it can be classified as?', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"The World Health Organization (WHO) classifies the severity of anemia as a public health problem based on its prevalence rate within a given population:","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Moderate\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"The World Health Organization (WHO) classifies the severity of anemia as a public health problem based on its prevalence rate within a given population:\n\n• Normal (No problem): Prevalence of 4.9% or lower\n• Mild Public Health Problem: Prevalence of 5.0% to 19.9%\n• Moderate Public Health Problem: Prevalence of 20.0% to 39.9% — Since the population prevalence is exactly 20%, it marks the lower boundary of a moderate public health crisis, requiring immediate targeted community interventions (such as massive iron-folic acid supplementation or food fortification programs).\n• Severe Public Health Problem: Prevalence of 40.0% or higher","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: High, Mild, Low"}'::jsonb),
('mc:a deficiency in can be seen in the nails:::selenium', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'a deficiency in can be seen in the nails', 'Selenium', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Selenium, whose deficiency can manifest with changes visible in the nails (among other signs), consistent with selenium''s role in numerous enzymatic and antioxidant functions. ","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Selenium\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Selenium, whose deficiency can manifest with changes visible in the nails (among other signs), consistent with selenium''s role in numerous enzymatic and antioxidant functions. \n\n• Iodine deficiency instead manifests primarily as goiter and thyroid-related signs. \n• Thiamin deficiency manifests as beri-beri-related neurological/cardiovascular signs. \n• Copper deficiency, while it can affect hair (as in Menkes disease), is not primarily associated with nail changes in the same way selenium is.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Iodine, Thiamin, Copper"}'::jsonb),
('mc:a deficiency in can be seen in the nails:::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'a deficiency in can be seen in the nails', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Selenium, whose deficiency can manifest with changes visible in the nails (among other signs), consistent with selenium''s role in numerous enzymatic and antioxidant functions. ","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Selenium\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Selenium, whose deficiency can manifest with changes visible in the nails (among other signs), consistent with selenium''s role in numerous enzymatic and antioxidant functions. \n\n• Iodine deficiency instead manifests primarily as goiter and thyroid-related signs. \n• Thiamin deficiency manifests as beri-beri-related neurological/cardiovascular signs. \n• Copper deficiency, while it can affect hair (as in Menkes disease), is not primarily associated with nail changes in the same way selenium is.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Iodine, Thiamin, Copper"}'::jsonb),
('mc:ms. santos is conducting a research which aims to measure the mean nutrient intake of school children. what dietary assessment method will you recommend?:::24-hour food recall', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'ms. santos is conducting a research which aims to measure the mean nutrient intake of school children. what dietary assessment method will you recommend?', '24-hour food recall', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"24-hour food recall is well-suited and commonly used for estimating the mean (average) nutrient intake of a group or population, since individual day-to-day variation tends to average out across a sufficiently large sample even with a single recall per person. ","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"24-hour food recall\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"24-hour food recall is well-suited and commonly used for estimating the mean (average) nutrient intake of a group or population, since individual day-to-day variation tends to average out across a sufficiently large sample even with a single recall per person. \n\n• Food frequency questionnaires are better suited for ranking individuals by relative intake or assessing habitual/long-term dietary patterns rather than precise mean nutrient quantities. \n• Diet records and food weighing are more resource-intensive, detailed methods typically reserved for smaller studies or when precise individual intake data is needed.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Food frequency, Diet record, Food weighing"}'::jsonb),
('mc:ms. santos is conducting a research which aims to measure the mean nutrient intake of school children. what dietary assessment method will you recommend?:::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'ms. santos is conducting a research which aims to measure the mean nutrient intake of school children. what dietary assessment method will you recommend?', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"24-hour food recall is well-suited and commonly used for estimating the mean (average) nutrient intake of a group or population, since individual day-to-day variation tends to average out across a sufficiently large sample even with a single recall per person. ","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"24-hour food recall\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"24-hour food recall is well-suited and commonly used for estimating the mean (average) nutrient intake of a group or population, since individual day-to-day variation tends to average out across a sufficiently large sample even with a single recall per person. \n\n• Food frequency questionnaires are better suited for ranking individuals by relative intake or assessing habitual/long-term dietary patterns rather than precise mean nutrient quantities. \n• Diet records and food weighing are more resource-intensive, detailed methods typically reserved for smaller studies or when precise individual intake data is needed.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Food frequency, Diet record, Food weighing"}'::jsonb)
