('mc:which of the following is the primary goal of a nutrition surveillance system?:::to inform public health policies and interventions related to nutrition', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'which of the following is the primary goal of a nutrition surveillance system?', 'To inform public health policies and interventions related to nutrition', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"The correct answer is to inform public health policies and interventions related to nutrition, which is the defining purpose of surveillance: continuous data collection specifically intended to guide decision-making.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"To inform public health policies and interventions related to nutrition\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"The correct answer is to inform public health policies and interventions related to nutrition, which is the defining purpose of surveillance: continuous data collection specifically intended to guide decision-making.\n\n• To diagnose individual nutritional deficiencies - surveillance systems track population trends, not individual diagnoses\n• To monitor trends in food prices - only one possible component, not the overall goal\n• To assess the nutritional status of individuals in a clinical setting - surveillance is population-based, not conducted in individual clinical settings.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: To diagnose individual nutritional deficiencies, To monitor trends in food prices, To assess the nutritional status of individuals in a clinical setting"}'::jsonb),
