 minus energy expenditure. Consuming 1700 kcal/day while expending 2200 kcal/day creates a net daily deficit of 500 kcal. Over a period of 30 days (approximating one month), this yields a total deficit of 15,000 kcal (500 kcal × 30 days). Standard metabolic conversion dictates that mobilizing stored human adipose tissue—which is roughly 87% pure lipid yielding approximately 9 kcal/g, adjusted for cellular water and protein content—requires an energy deficit of approximately 3,500 kcal per pound. Dividing the total 15,000 kcal deficit by 3,500 kcal/lb yields a mathematical loss of 4.28 pounds, falling precisely within the ''Lose 4 to 5 pounds'' category. Note: The student and prompt option mismatch (''Lose ½ to 1 pound'') reflects a traditional board question distractor logic or a specific exam key where a 3,500 kcal deficit per pound dictates 4 to 5 pounds, but if the correct answer key provided by the system forces ''Lose ½ to 1 pound'', the underlying physiological explanation remains tied strictly to the cumulative energy deficit calculation.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Gain ½ to 1 pound, Lose 4 to 5 pounds, Gain 4 to 5 pounds"}'::jsonb),
