('mc:let us trace the digestion of a peanut butter and banana sandwich on whole-wheat sesame bread through the gastrointestinal (gi) tract. in the mouth, there is chewing and swallowing, but little digestion takes place. which nutrients are involved in this process?:::carbohydrates and fiber', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'let us trace the digestion of a peanut butter and banana sandwich on whole-wheat sesame bread through the gastrointestinal (gi) tract. in the mouth, there is chewing and swallowing, but little digestion takes place. which nutrients are involved in this process?', 'Carbohydrates and fiber', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Salivary alpha-amylase is secreted by the parotid and submandibular glands, targeting internal alpha-1,4-glycosidic bonds of starches (found in the whole-wheat bread and banana) to produce dextrins and maltose. Dietary fiber (complex plant polysaccharides such as cellulose, hemicellulose, and pectin found in whole grains, sesame seeds, and banana) is resistant to human endogenous hydrolytic enzymes; thus, fiber undergoes mechanical size reduction via chewing but experiences zero enzymatic degradation in the mouth or stomach.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Carbohydrates and fiber\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Salivary alpha-amylase is secreted by the parotid and submandibular glands, targeting internal alpha-1,4-glycosidic bonds of starches (found in the whole-wheat bread and banana) to produce dextrins and maltose. Dietary fiber (complex plant polysaccharides such as cellulose, hemicellulose, and pectin found in whole grains, sesame seeds, and banana) is resistant to human endogenous hydrolytic enzymes; thus, fiber undergoes mechanical size reduction via chewing but experiences zero enzymatic degradation in the mouth or stomach.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Carbohydrates and protein, Carbohydrates and fats, Carbohydrates and vitamins"}'::jsonb),
('mc:let us trace the digestion of a peanut butter and banana sandwich on whole-wheat sesame bread through the gastrointestinal (gi) tract. in the mouth, there is chewing and swallowing, but little digestion takes place. which nutrients are involved in this process?:::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'let us trace the digestion of a peanut butter and banana sandwich on whole-wheat sesame bread through the gastrointestinal (gi) tract. in the mouth, there is chewing and swallowing, but little digestion takes place. which nutrients are involved in this process?', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Salivary alpha-amylase is secreted by the parotid and submandibular glands, targeting internal alpha-1,4-glycosidic bonds of starches (found in the whole-wheat bread and banana) to produce dextrins and maltose. Dietary fiber (complex plant polysaccharides such as cellulose, hemicellulose, and pectin found in whole grains, sesame seeds, and banana) is resistant to human endogenous hydrolytic enzymes; thus, fiber undergoes mechanical size reduction via chewing but experiences zero enzymatic degradation in the mouth or stomach.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Carbohydrates and fiber\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Salivary alpha-amylase is secreted by the parotid and submandibular glands, targeting internal alpha-1,4-glycosidic bonds of starches (found in the whole-wheat bread and banana) to produce dextrins and maltose. Dietary fiber (complex plant polysaccharides such as cellulose, hemicellulose, and pectin found in whole grains, sesame seeds, and banana) is resistant to human endogenous hydrolytic enzymes; thus, fiber undergoes mechanical size reduction via chewing but experiences zero enzymatic degradation in the mouth or stomach.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Carbohydrates and protein, Carbohydrates and fats, Carbohydrates and vitamins"}'::jsonb),
('mc:related to the digestion of a peanut butter and banana sandwich, the next process is in the stomach (collecting and churning with some digestion). which nutrients are involved in this process?:::carbohydrates, protein and fats', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'related to the digestion of a peanut butter and banana sandwich, the next process is in the stomach (collecting and churning with some digestion). which nutrients are involved in this process?', 'Carbohydrates, protein and fats', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"This continues the step-by-step digestion trace of the peanut butter, banana, and whole-wheat bread sandwich based on Whitney and Rolfes'' Understanding Nutrition. Inside the stomach, the main actions are mechanical churning and gastric acid secretion, which actively process all three macronutrients:","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Carbohydrates, protein and fats\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"This continues the step-by-step digestion trace of the peanut butter, banana, and whole-wheat bread sandwich based on Whitney and Rolfes'' Understanding Nutrition. Inside the stomach, the main actions are mechanical churning and gastric acid secretion, which actively process all three macronutrients:\n\n• Protein: Stomach acid (hydrochloric acid) denatures and uncoils the protein strands from the bread and peanut butter. Then, the gastric enzyme pepsin initiates chemical digestion by clipping these large protein strands into smaller peptide pieces.\n• Fats: Gastric juice contains gastric lipase (working alongside the lingual lipase swallowed from the mouth), which begins targeting and breaking down some of the surface fats found in the peanut butter.\n• Carbohydrates: Although the extreme acidity of the stomach temporarily pauses the action of salivary amylase (which digests starches), the simple fruit sugars and starches from the banana and bread remain a heavy part of the resulting fluid mixture. The stomach continues to physically churn them into a uniform liquid mass known as chyme before passing it into the small intestine.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Carbohydrates, Amino Acids, fats, Sugars, starch and fats, Sugars, starch, vitamins and minerals"}'::jsonb),
('mc:an alkaline ingredient used as a leavening.:::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'an alkaline ingredient used as a leavening.', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Baking soda is sodium bicarbonate, an alkaline leavening ingredient that releases carbon dioxide when it reacts with an acid and moisture.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Baking soda\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Baking soda is sodium bicarbonate, an alkaline leavening ingredient that releases carbon dioxide when it reacts with an acid and moisture.\n\nCream of tartar and vinegar are acidic, while fruit juice may be acidic but is not the standard alkaline leavener.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Cream of tartar, Vinegar, Fruit juice"}'::jsonb),
('mc:related to the digestion of a peanut butter and banana sandwich, the next process is in the stomach (collecting and churning with some digestion). which nutrients are involved in this process?:::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'related to the digestion of a peanut butter and banana sandwich, the next process is in the stomach (collecting and churning with some digestion). which nutrients are involved in this process?', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"This continues the step-by-step digestion trace of the peanut butter, banana, and whole-wheat bread sandwich based on Whitney and Rolfes'' Understanding Nutrition. Inside the stomach, the main actions are mechanical churning and gastric acid secretion, which actively process all three macronutrients:","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Carbohydrates, protein and fats\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"This continues the step-by-step digestion trace of the peanut butter, banana, and whole-wheat bread sandwich based on Whitney and Rolfes'' Understanding Nutrition. Inside the stomach, the main actions are mechanical churning and gastric acid secretion, which actively process all three macronutrients:\n\n• Protein: Stomach acid (hydrochloric acid) denatures and uncoils the protein strands from the bread and peanut butter. Then, the gastric enzyme pepsin initiates chemical digestion by clipping these large protein strands into smaller peptide pieces.\n• Fats: Gastric juice contains gastric lipase (working alongside the lingual lipase swallowed from the mouth), which begins targeting and breaking down some of the surface fats found in the peanut butter.\n• Carbohydrates: Although the extreme acidity of the stomach temporarily pauses the action of salivary amylase (which digests starches), the simple fruit sugars and starches from the banana and bread remain a heavy part of the resulting fluid mixture. The stomach continues to physically churn them into a uniform liquid mass known as chyme before passing it into the small intestine.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Carbohydrates, Amino Acids, fats, Sugars, starch and fats, Sugars, starch, vitamins and minerals"}'::jsonb),
('mc:from the stomach, the digestion of peanut butter and banana sandwich will now proceed to the small intestines, the following nutrients that are involved in this process are::::sugar, starch, protein, fats', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'from the stomach, the digestion of peanut butter and banana sandwich will now proceed to the small intestines, the following nutrients that are involved in this process are:', 'Sugar, Starch, Protein, Fats', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Upon arrival in the small intestine, the blended sandwich mass (chyme) triggers the pancreas and the walls of the small intestine to secrete digestive juices and enzymes. Unlike earlier stages where only specific items are targeted, the small intestine is the primary destination where all major macronutrients are simultaneously broken down and chemically processed for absorption:","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Sugar, Starch, Protein, Fats\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Upon arrival in the small intestine, the blended sandwich mass (chyme) triggers the pancreas and the walls of the small intestine to secrete digestive juices and enzymes. Unlike earlier stages where only specific items are targeted, the small intestine is the primary destination where all major macronutrients are simultaneously broken down and chemically processed for absorption:\n\n• Sugar (from the banana): Traverses the intestinal cells almost immediately on contact.\n• Starch (from the bread): Pancreatic amylase continues breaking down the remaining complex carbohydrates into simple sugars.\n• Protein (from the peanut butter and bread): Pancreatic proteases (like trypsin and chymotrypsin) break the partially uncoiled proteins into absorbable amino acids.\n• Fats (from the peanut butter): Gallbladder bile emulsifies the fats, allowing pancreatic lipase to digest them into fatty acids and glycerol.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Sugars from Banana, Starch, fats, vitamins and\nminerals, Sugars from Banana, Starch, fats, vitamins and minerals, Sugar, Starch, Fats"}'::jsonb),
('mc:from the stomach, the digestion of peanut butter and banana sandwich will now proceed to the small intestines, the following nutrients that are involved in this process are::::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'from the stomach, the digestion of peanut butter and banana sandwich will now proceed to the small intestines, the following nutrients that are involved in this process are:', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Upon arrival in the small intestine, the blended sandwich mass (chyme) triggers the pancreas and the walls of the small intestine to secrete digestive juices and enzymes. Unlike earlier stages where only specific items are targeted, the small intestine is the primary destination where all major macronutrients are simultaneously broken down and chemically processed for absorption:","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Sugar, Starch, Protein, Fats\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Upon arrival in the small intestine, the blended sandwich mass (chyme) triggers the pancreas and the walls of the small intestine to secrete digestive juices and enzymes. Unlike earlier stages where only specific items are targeted, the small intestine is the primary destination where all major macronutrients are simultaneously broken down and chemically processed for absorption:\n\n• Sugar (from the banana): Traverses the intestinal cells almost immediately on contact.\n• Starch (from the bread): Pancreatic amylase continues breaking down the remaining complex carbohydrates into simple sugars.\n• Protein (from the peanut butter and bread): Pancreatic proteases (like trypsin and chymotrypsin) break the partially uncoiled proteins into absorbable amino acids.\n• Fats (from the peanut butter): Gallbladder bile emulsifies the fats, allowing pancreatic lipase to digest them into fatty acids and glycerol.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Sugars from Banana, Starch, fats, vitamins and\nminerals, Sugars from Banana, Starch, fats, vitamins and minerals, Sugar, Starch, Fats"}'::jsonb),
('mc:finally, in the large intestine, the remains of the peanut butter and banana sandwich are processed for reabsorption and elimination. the following substances are absorbed at this stage, except::::insoluble fiber', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'finally, in the large intestine, the remains of the peanut butter and banana sandwich are processed for reabsorption and elimination. the following substances are absorbed at this stage, except:', 'Insoluble fiber', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"As the remains of the sandwich enter the large intestine, the body absorbs fluids (water) and some minerals (like sodium and potassium) back into the bloodstream. Additionally, the healthy bacteria living in your colon ferment and break down the soluble fibers from the banana and bread into short-chain fatty acids, which the body can partially absorb for energy.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Insoluble fiber\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"As the remains of the sandwich enter the large intestine, the body absorbs fluids (water) and some minerals (like sodium and potassium) back into the bloodstream. Additionally, the healthy bacteria living in your colon ferment and break down the soluble fibers from the banana and bread into short-chain fatty acids, which the body can partially absorb for energy.\n\nInsoluble fiber (found in the wheat bran, seeds, and nuts) cannot be dissolved in water or significantly broken down by intestinal bacteria. It passes through the large intestine completely intact, acting as roughage to bulk up the stool before being eliminated entirely as feces.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Fluids, Some minerals, Soluble fibers"}'::jsonb),
('mc:all newly hired employees should be given::::orientation', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'all newly hired employees should be given:', 'Orientation', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Orientation introduces newly hired employees to the organization, policies, workplace, and basic expectations before or at the start of employment.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Orientation\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Orientation introduces newly hired employees to the organization, policies, workplace, and basic expectations before or at the start of employment.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Pre-service training, IQ test, Medical check-up"}'::jsonb),
('mc:finally, in the large intestine, the remains of the peanut butter and banana sandwich are processed for reabsorption and elimination. the following substances are absorbed at this stage, except::::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'finally, in the large intestine, the remains of the peanut butter and banana sandwich are processed for reabsorption and elimination. the following substances are absorbed at this stage, except:', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"As the remains of the sandwich enter the large intestine, the body absorbs fluids (water) and some minerals (like sodium and potassium) back into the bloodstream. Additionally, the healthy bacteria living in your colon ferment and break down the soluble fibers from the banana and bread into short-chain fatty acids, which the body can partially absorb for energy.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Insoluble fiber\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"As the remains of the sandwich enter the large intestine, the body absorbs fluids (water) and some minerals (like sodium and potassium) back into the bloodstream. Additionally, the healthy bacteria living in your colon ferment and break down the soluble fibers from the banana and bread into short-chain fatty acids, which the body can partially absorb for energy.\n\nInsoluble fiber (found in the wheat bran, seeds, and nuts) cannot be dissolved in water or significantly broken down by intestinal bacteria. It passes through the large intestine completely intact, acting as roughage to bulk up the stool before being eliminated entirely as feces.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Fluids, Some minerals, Soluble fibers"}'::jsonb),
('mc:treatment of lactose intolerance requires limiting milk intake. the following nutrients must be included in the diet except:::zinc', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'treatment of lactose intolerance requires limiting milk intake. the following nutrients must be included in the diet except', 'Zinc', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"When a patient with lactose intolerance limits their dairy and milk intake, they drastically reduce their consumption of primary micronutrients naturally packed into milk fluid.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Zinc\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"When a patient with lactose intolerance limits their dairy and milk intake, they drastically reduce their consumption of primary micronutrients naturally packed into milk fluid.\n\nWhile milk does contain a small amount of zinc, zinc is highly abundant across multiple non-dairy food groups, including red meat, poultry, seafood (like shellfish and oysters), legumes, seeds, and fortified grains. Restricting dairy does not place a patient at a high clinical risk for zinc deficiency.\n\n\t• Calcium and Vitamin D: Milk is the primary source of bioavailable calcium and fortified Vitamin D in the human diet. Restricting milk directly exposes patients to bone-wasting deficiencies, requiring alternative replacement.\n\t• Riboflavin: Also known as Vitamin B2, riboflavin is heavily concentrated in fluid milk. Dairy products account for over a quarter of total daily riboflavin intake, making its targeted replacement essential when limiting milk.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Riboflavin, Vitamin D, Calcium"}'::jsonb),
('mc:weeping out of liquid from gelatinized starch during prolonged storage:::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'weeping out of liquid from gelatinized starch during prolonged storage', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Syneresis is the weeping or separation of liquid from a starch or gel network during storage. ","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"syneresis\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Syneresis is the weeping or separation of liquid from a starch or gel network during storage. \n\nRetrogradation is a broader starch reassociation process that can contribute to syneresis.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: retrogradation, gelatinization, gelation"}'::jsonb),
('mc:treatment of lactose intolerance requires limiting milk intake. the following nutrients must be included in the diet except:::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'treatment of lactose intolerance requires limiting milk intake. the following nutrients must be included in the diet except', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"When a patient with lactose intolerance limits their dairy and milk intake, they drastically reduce their consumption of primary micronutrients naturally packed into milk fluid.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Zinc\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"When a patient with lactose intolerance limits their dairy and milk intake, they drastically reduce their consumption of primary micronutrients naturally packed into milk fluid.\n\nWhile milk does contain a small amount of zinc, zinc is highly abundant across multiple non-dairy food groups, including red meat, poultry, seafood (like shellfish and oysters), legumes, seeds, and fortified grains. Restricting dairy does not place a patient at a high clinical risk for zinc deficiency.\n\n\t• Calcium and Vitamin D: Milk is the primary source of bioavailable calcium and fortified Vitamin D in the human diet. Restricting milk directly exposes patients to bone-wasting deficiencies, requiring alternative replacement.\n\t• Riboflavin: Also known as Vitamin B2, riboflavin is heavily concentrated in fluid milk. Dairy products account for over a quarter of total daily riboflavin intake, making its targeted replacement essential when limiting milk.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Riboflavin, Vitamin D, Calcium"}'::jsonb),
('mc:prebiotics can stimulate the growth or activity of resident bacteria within the large intestine. prebiotics can be found in the following foods except:::miso', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'prebiotics can stimulate the growth or activity of resident bacteria within the large intestine. prebiotics can be found in the following foods except', 'Miso', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Miso is a traditional Japanese seasoning produced by fermenting soybeans with salt and koji. Because it is a fermented food, miso naturally contains live, active bacterial cultures, which means it is classified as a Probiotic rather than a prebiotic.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Miso\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Miso is a traditional Japanese seasoning produced by fermenting soybeans with salt and koji. Because it is a fermented food, miso naturally contains live, active bacterial cultures, which means it is classified as a Probiotic rather than a prebiotic.\n\nPrebiotics are non-digestible, fiber-rich plant carbohydrates or polyphenols that act as structural \"food\" to nourish your resident gut bacteria.\n\t• Onions and garlic, along with Asparagus and Japanese artichoke, are exceptionally rich in inulin and fructooligosaccharides (FOS), which are the most clinically recognized prebiotic fibers.\n\t• Cocoa contains a high concentration of non-absorbable polyphenols (flavonoids), which are officially classified as non-carbohydrate prebiotic candidates because they stimulate the selective growth of beneficial Lactobacillus and Bifidobacterium species in the colon.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Asparagus, Japanese Artichoke, Onions, garlic, Cocoa"}'::jsonb),
('mc:prebiotics can stimulate the growth or activity of resident bacteria within the large intestine. prebiotics can be found in the following foods except:::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'prebiotics can stimulate the growth or activity of resident bacteria within the large intestine. prebiotics can be found in the following foods except', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Miso is a traditional Japanese seasoning produced by fermenting soybeans with salt and koji. Because it is a fermented food, miso naturally contains live, active bacterial cultures, which means it is classified as a Probiotic rather than a prebiotic.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Miso\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Miso is a traditional Japanese seasoning produced by fermenting soybeans with salt and koji. Because it is a fermented food, miso naturally contains live, active bacterial cultures, which means it is classified as a Probiotic rather than a prebiotic.\n\nPrebiotics are non-digestible, fiber-rich plant carbohydrates or polyphenols that act as structural \"food\" to nourish your resident gut bacteria.\n\t• Onions and garlic, along with Asparagus and Japanese artichoke, are exceptionally rich in inulin and fructooligosaccharides (FOS), which are the most clinically recognized prebiotic fibers.\n\t• Cocoa contains a high concentration of non-absorbable polyphenols (flavonoids), which are officially classified as non-carbohydrate prebiotic candidates because they stimulate the selective growth of beneficial Lactobacillus and Bifidobacterium species in the colon.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Asparagus, Japanese Artichoke, Onions, garlic, Cocoa"}'::jsonb),
('mc:the international dysphagia diet standardisation initiative (iddsi) framework provides a common terminology to describe food textures and drink thickness has how many levels of standards:::7 levels', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'the international dysphagia diet standardisation initiative (iddsi) framework provides a common terminology to describe food textures and drink thickness has how many levels of standards', '7 levels', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"The correct answer is not explicitly listed among the given options, but mathematically the framework consists of 8 levels (numbered 0 to 7).","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"7 levels\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"The correct answer is not explicitly listed among the given options, but mathematically the framework consists of 8 levels (numbered 0 to 7).\n\nThe International Dysphagia Diet Standardisation Initiative (IDDSI) framework is structurally designed as a continuous scale spanning 8 distinct levels, using the numbers 0 through 7 to standardize care:\n• Levels 0 to 4 are used to classify drinks (Thin, Slightly Thick, Mildly Thick, Moderately Thick, and Extremely Thick).\n• Levels 3 to 7 are used to classify foods (Liquidised, Pureed, Minced & Moist, Soft & Bite-Sized, and Regular).","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: 4 Levels, 5 Levels, 6 Levels"}'::jsonb),
('mc:the international dysphagia diet standardisation initiative (iddsi) framework provides a common terminology to describe food textures and drink thickness has how many levels of standards:::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'the international dysphagia diet standardisation initiative (iddsi) framework provides a common terminology to describe food textures and drink thickness has how many levels of standards', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"The correct answer is not explicitly listed among the given options, but mathematically the framework consists of 8 levels (numbered 0 to 7).","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"7 levels\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"The correct answer is not explicitly listed among the given options, but mathematically the framework consists of 8 levels (numbered 0 to 7).\n\nThe International Dysphagia Diet Standardisation Initiative (IDDSI) framework is structurally designed as a continuous scale spanning 8 distinct levels, using the numbers 0 through 7 to standardize care:\n• Levels 0 to 4 are used to classify drinks (Thin, Slightly Thick, Mildly Thick, Moderately Thick, and Extremely Thick).\n• Levels 3 to 7 are used to classify foods (Liquidised, Pureed, Minced & Moist, Soft & Bite-Sized, and Regular).","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: 4 Levels, 5 Levels, 6 Levels"}'::jsonb),
('mc:the iddsi framework describes level 5 as:::minced and moist', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'the iddsi framework describes level 5 as', 'Minced and moist', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"The International Dysphagia Diet Standardisation Initiative (IDDSI) framework uses a standardized 8-level system (Levels 0–7) with specific names and color codes to manage texture-modified foods and thickened liquids for individuals with dysphagia:","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Minced and moist\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"The International Dysphagia Diet Standardisation Initiative (IDDSI) framework uses a standardized 8-level system (Levels 0–7) with specific names and color codes to manage texture-modified foods and thickened liquids for individuals with dysphagia:\n\n• Level 5 – Minced & Moist (Orange): This level describes foods that are soft, moist, and finely minced into small pieces no larger than 4mm for adults (the width of a standard fork prong gap). It requires minimal chewing and no biting.\n\n\t• Liquidized: Represents Level 3.\n\t• Pureed: Represents Level 4.\n\t• Soft and Bite size: Represents Level 6 (pieces no larger than 15mm).","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Liquidized, Soft and Bite size, Pureed"}'::jsonb),
('mc:one gallon of ice cream is equivalent to how many pints?:::8', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'one gallon of ice cream is equivalent to how many pints?', '8', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"In standard liquid volume measurements used in food service and kitchen management, one gallon is equivalent to 8 pints.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"8\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"In standard liquid volume measurements used in food service and kitchen management, one gallon is equivalent to 8 pints.\n\nQuick Volume Conversion Chart:\n\n• 1 Gallon = 4 Quarts\n• 1 Gallon = 8 Pints\n• 1 Gallon = 16 Cups\n• 1 Gallon = 128 Fluid Ounces","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: 16, 4, None of the above"}'::jsonb),
('mc:the iddsi framework describes level 5 as:::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'the iddsi framework describes level 5 as', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"The International Dysphagia Diet Standardisation Initiative (IDDSI) framework uses a standardized 8-level system (Levels 0–7) with specific names and color codes to manage texture-modified foods and thickened liquids for individuals with dysphagia:","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Minced and moist\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"The International Dysphagia Diet Standardisation Initiative (IDDSI) framework uses a standardized 8-level system (Levels 0–7) with specific names and color codes to manage texture-modified foods and thickened liquids for individuals with dysphagia:\n\n• Level 5 – Minced & Moist (Orange): This level describes foods that are soft, moist, and finely minced into small pieces no larger than 4mm for adults (the width of a standard fork prong gap). It requires minimal chewing and no biting.\n\n\t• Liquidized: Represents Level 3.\n\t• Pureed: Represents Level 4.\n\t• Soft and Bite size: Represents Level 6 (pieces no larger than 15mm).","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Liquidized, Soft and Bite size, Pureed"}'::jsonb),
('mc:foods that may increase intestinal gases includes the following but not:::peanuts', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'foods that may increase intestinal gases includes the following but not', 'Peanuts', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"While legumes like beans and lentils are notorious gas producers, peanuts (despite being botanically classified as legumes) are primarily composed of fats and proteins rather than high amounts of gas-producing fermentable carbohydrates (FODMAPs). Protein- and fat-heavy foods are digested without significant colonic fermentation, making peanuts much less likely to increase intestinal gas.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Peanuts\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"While legumes like beans and lentils are notorious gas producers, peanuts (despite being botanically classified as legumes) are primarily composed of fats and proteins rather than high amounts of gas-producing fermentable carbohydrates (FODMAPs). Protein- and fat-heavy foods are digested without significant colonic fermentation, making peanuts much less likely to increase intestinal gas.\n\n• Beer: Beer is carbonated, releasing trapped carbon dioxide gas into the digestive tract, and it contains fermentable carbohydrates (from grains like barley/wheat) that gut bacteria break down, producing significant intestinal gas.\n• Broccoli and Turnips: Both are cruciferous vegetables. Cruciferous vegetables are famously high in complex fibers and a sugar called raffinose, which humans lack the enzymes to break down. Instead, they ferment heavily in the large intestine, causing a great deal of gas.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Broccoli, Turnips, Beer"}'::jsonb),
('mc:foods that may increase intestinal gases includes the following but not:::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'foods that may increase intestinal gases includes the following but not', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"While legumes like beans and lentils are notorious gas producers, peanuts (despite being botanically classified as legumes) are primarily composed of fats and proteins rather than high amounts of gas-producing fermentable carbohydrates (FODMAPs). Protein- and fat-heavy foods are digested without significant colonic fermentation, making peanuts much less likely to increase intestinal gas.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Peanuts\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"While legumes like beans and lentils are notorious gas producers, peanuts (despite being botanically classified as legumes) are primarily composed of fats and proteins rather than high amounts of gas-producing fermentable carbohydrates (FODMAPs). Protein- and fat-heavy foods are digested without significant colonic fermentation, making peanuts much less likely to increase intestinal gas.\n\n• Beer: Beer is carbonated, releasing trapped carbon dioxide gas into the digestive tract, and it contains fermentable carbohydrates (from grains like barley/wheat) that gut bacteria break down, producing significant intestinal gas.\n• Broccoli and Turnips: Both are cruciferous vegetables. Cruciferous vegetables are famously high in complex fibers and a sugar called raffinose, which humans lack the enzymes to break down. Instead, they ferment heavily in the large intestine, causing a great deal of gas.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Broccoli, Turnips, Beer"}'::jsonb),
('mc:the following foods may worsen diarrhea but not:::banana', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'the following foods may worsen diarrhea but not', 'Banana', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Bananas are a staple of the classic therapeutic BRAT diet (Bananas, Rice, Applesauce, Toast) used to treat diarrhea. Ripe bananas are exceptionally rich in soluble fiber (pectin), which acts like a sponge in the digestive tract by absorbing excess water and firming up loose stools. They are also highly digestible and replenish crucial potassium lost during fluid evacuation.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Banana\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Bananas are a staple of the classic therapeutic BRAT diet (Bananas, Rice, Applesauce, Toast) used to treat diarrhea. Ripe bananas are exceptionally rich in soluble fiber (pectin), which acts like a sponge in the digestive tract by absorbing excess water and firming up loose stools. They are also highly digestible and replenish crucial potassium lost during fluid evacuation.\n\n\t• Asparagus and cabbage: These are high-fiber, gas-producing foods (high-FODMAP) that increase intestinal gas, bloating, and painful contractions in an already irritated bowel.\n\t• Caffeine-containing beverages: Caffeine is a powerful gastrointestinal stimulant that accelerates intestinal motility and triggers excess fluid secretion, which directly worsens loose stools.\n\t• Dried fruits and sugar-free gums: Sugar-free gums contain sugar alcohols (such as sorbitol, xylitol, or mannitol) that exert a strong osmotic laxative effect, drawing water straight into the colon and worsening diarrhea. Dried fruits are highly concentrated in fructose and fiber, which also exacerbate gastrointestinal distress.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: caffeine containing beverages, dried fruits, sugar free gums, asparagus, cabbage"}'::jsonb),
('mc:one gallon of ice cream is equivalent to how many pints?:::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'one gallon of ice cream is equivalent to how many pints?', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"In standard liquid volume measurements used in food service and kitchen management, one gallon is equivalent to 8 pints.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"8\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"In standard liquid volume measurements used in food service and kitchen management, one gallon is equivalent to 8 pints.\n\nQuick Volume Conversion Chart:\n\n• 1 Gallon = 4 Quarts\n• 1 Gallon = 8 Pints\n• 1 Gallon = 16 Cups\n• 1 Gallon = 128 Fluid Ounces","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: 16, 4, None of the above"}'::jsonb),
('mc:the following foods may worsen diarrhea but not:::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'the following foods may worsen diarrhea but not', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Bananas are a staple of the classic therapeutic BRAT diet (Bananas, Rice, Applesauce, Toast) used to treat diarrhea. Ripe bananas are exceptionally rich in soluble fiber (pectin), which acts like a sponge in the digestive tract by absorbing excess water and firming up loose stools. They are also highly digestible and replenish crucial potassium lost during fluid evacuation.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Banana\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Bananas are a staple of the classic therapeutic BRAT diet (Bananas, Rice, Applesauce, Toast) used to treat diarrhea. Ripe bananas are exceptionally rich in soluble fiber (pectin), which acts like a sponge in the digestive tract by absorbing excess water and firming up loose stools. They are also highly digestible and replenish crucial potassium lost during fluid evacuation.\n\n\t• Asparagus and cabbage: These are high-fiber, gas-producing foods (high-FODMAP) that increase intestinal gas, bloating, and painful contractions in an already irritated bowel.\n\t• Caffeine-containing beverages: Caffeine is a powerful gastrointestinal stimulant that accelerates intestinal motility and triggers excess fluid secretion, which directly worsens loose stools.\n\t• Dried fruits and sugar-free gums: Sugar-free gums contain sugar alcohols (such as sorbitol, xylitol, or mannitol) that exert a strong osmotic laxative effect, drawing water straight into the colon and worsening diarrhea. Dried fruits are highly concentrated in fructose and fiber, which also exacerbate gastrointestinal distress.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: caffeine containing beverages, dried fruits, sugar free gums, asparagus, cabbage"}'::jsonb),
('mc:people at risk of dumping syndrome should generally avoid:::sweets and sugars', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'people at risk of dumping syndrome should generally avoid', 'Sweets and sugars', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Dumping syndrome occurs when large amounts of undigested food—especially highly concentrated carbohydrates—move too rapidly from the stomach into the small intestine, often after a gastrectomy or gastric bypass surgery.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Sweets and sugars\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Dumping syndrome occurs when large amounts of undigested food—especially highly concentrated carbohydrates—move too rapidly from the stomach into the small intestine, often after a gastrectomy or gastric bypass surgery.\n\nSimple sugars and sweets (like candy, table sugar, juices, and sodas) are highly hypertonic (concentrated). When they dump into the small intestine, they pull an immense amount of fluid out of the bloodstream and into the bowel. This causes sudden, distressing symptoms like severe abdominal cramps, explosive diarrhea, nausea, dizziness, rapid heart rate, and a dangerous drop in blood pressure.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: High fiber foods, Beverages, Bread and Potatoes"}'::jsonb),
('mc:people at risk of dumping syndrome should generally avoid:::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'people at risk of dumping syndrome should generally avoid', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Dumping syndrome occurs when large amounts of undigested food—especially highly concentrated carbohydrates—move too rapidly from the stomach into the small intestine, often after a gastrectomy or gastric bypass surgery.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Sweets and sugars\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Dumping syndrome occurs when large amounts of undigested food—especially highly concentrated carbohydrates—move too rapidly from the stomach into the small intestine, often after a gastrectomy or gastric bypass surgery.\n\nSimple sugars and sweets (like candy, table sugar, juices, and sodas) are highly hypertonic (concentrated). When they dump into the small intestine, they pull an immense amount of fluid out of the bloodstream and into the bowel. This causes sudden, distressing symptoms like severe abdominal cramps, explosive diarrhea, nausea, dizziness, rapid heart rate, and a dangerous drop in blood pressure.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: High fiber foods, Beverages, Bread and Potatoes"}'::jsonb),
('mc:the dietitian assessing a patient who had undergone gastrectomy several years ago should be alert to signs of ::::anemia', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'the dietitian assessing a patient who had undergone gastrectomy several years ago should be alert to signs of :', 'Anemia', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"A patient who underwent a gastrectomy (removal of all or part of the stomach) several years ago is at high risk for post-gastrectomy anemia due to two main nutritional deficiencies:","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Anemia\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"A patient who underwent a gastrectomy (removal of all or part of the stomach) several years ago is at high risk for post-gastrectomy anemia due to two main nutritional deficiencies:\n\n1. Vitamin B12 Deficiency (Pernicious Anemia): The stomach''s parietal cells produce intrinsic factor, which is essential for vitamin B12 absorption in the small intestine. Without a stomach, intrinsic factor is missing, leading to severe B12 deficiency and megaloblastic anemia over time (as B12 stores in the liver take a few years to deplete).\n\n2. Iron Deficiency Anemia: Stomach acid (gastric acid) is required to convert dietary iron into an absorbable form. Without a stomach, iron absorption drops significantly, causing iron deficiency anemia.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Dysphagia, GERD, Gastritis"}'::jsonb),
('mc:the dietitian assessing a patient who had undergone gastrectomy several years ago should be alert to signs of ::::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'the dietitian assessing a patient who had undergone gastrectomy several years ago should be alert to signs of :', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"A patient who underwent a gastrectomy (removal of all or part of the stomach) several years ago is at high risk for post-gastrectomy anemia due to two main nutritional deficiencies:","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Anemia\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"A patient who underwent a gastrectomy (removal of all or part of the stomach) several years ago is at high risk for post-gastrectomy anemia due to two main nutritional deficiencies:\n\n1. Vitamin B12 Deficiency (Pernicious Anemia): The stomach''s parietal cells produce intrinsic factor, which is essential for vitamin B12 absorption in the small intestine. Without a stomach, intrinsic factor is missing, leading to severe B12 deficiency and megaloblastic anemia over time (as B12 stores in the liver take a few years to deplete).\n\n2. Iron Deficiency Anemia: Stomach acid (gastric acid) is required to convert dietary iron into an absorbable form. Without a stomach, iron absorption drops significantly, causing iron deficiency anemia.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Dysphagia, GERD, Gastritis"}'::jsonb),
('mc:what is the temperature danger zone?:::41 to -140ºf', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'what is the temperature danger zone?', '41 to -140ºF', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"The Temperature Danger Zone represents the environmental range where foodborne pathogenic bacteria (such as Salmonella, E. coli, and Staphylococcus aureus) reproduce most rapidly. Under optimal conditions inside this window, bacterial populations can double in number in as little as 10 to 20 minutes.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"41 to -140ºF\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"The Temperature Danger Zone represents the environmental range where foodborne pathogenic bacteria (such as Salmonella, E. coli, and Staphylococcus aureus) reproduce most rapidly. Under optimal conditions inside this window, bacterial populations can double in number in as little as 10 to 20 minutes.\n\nTo protect public health, regulatory standards mandate keeping cold foods strictly below 41ºF and hot foods above 140ºF (or 135ºF depending on the specific municipal framework) to prevent bacteria from reaching dangerous, illness-causing thresholds.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: 0 to -10ºF, 161 – 212ºF, 32 – 40ºF"}'::jsonb),
('mc:what is the temperature danger zone?:::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'what is the temperature danger zone?', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"The Temperature Danger Zone represents the environmental range where foodborne pathogenic bacteria (such as Salmonella, E. coli, and Staphylococcus aureus) reproduce most rapidly. Under optimal conditions inside this window, bacterial populations can double in number in as little as 10 to 20 minutes.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"41 to -140ºF\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"The Temperature Danger Zone represents the environmental range where foodborne pathogenic bacteria (such as Salmonella, E. coli, and Staphylococcus aureus) reproduce most rapidly. Under optimal conditions inside this window, bacterial populations can double in number in as little as 10 to 20 minutes.\n\nTo protect public health, regulatory standards mandate keeping cold foods strictly below 41ºF and hot foods above 140ºF (or 135ºF depending on the specific municipal framework) to prevent bacteria from reaching dangerous, illness-causing thresholds.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: 0 to -10ºF, 161 – 212ºF, 32 – 40ºF"}'::jsonb),
('mc:what is considered the catalyst that drives all food service operational functions?:::menu', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'what is considered the catalyst that drives all food service operational functions?', 'Menu', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"The menu is considered the primary driver, catalyst, and focal point of the entire foodservice operation. It dictates every other function within the system, including:","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Menu\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"The menu is considered the primary driver, catalyst, and focal point of the entire foodservice operation. It dictates every other function within the system, including:\n\n• Purchasing and Procurement: What raw materials and ingredients need to be ordered.\n• Equipment and Space Requirements: The types of ovens, prep stations, or storage needed to prepare the items.\n• Manpower and Labor: The skill level and number of staff required for preparation and service.\n• Budget and Food Cost: The pricing strategy, financial targets, and cost control measures.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Food cost, Manpower, Materials"}'::jsonb),
