('mc:all are types of nonprobability sampling except::::cluster sampling', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'all are types of nonprobability sampling except:', 'cluster sampling', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Convenience, purposive, and quota sampling are nonprobability methods, meaning population units do not have a known or equal chance of selection, and selection is instead guided by accessibility, judgment, or predetermined quotas. ","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"cluster sampling\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Convenience, purposive, and quota sampling are nonprobability methods, meaning population units do not have a known or equal chance of selection, and selection is instead guided by accessibility, judgment, or predetermined quotas. \n\nCluster sampling, in contrast, is a probability sampling technique in which naturally occurring groups (clusters) are randomly selected, giving every unit in the population a calculable chance of inclusion.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: convenience sampling, purposive sampling, quota sampling"}'::jsonb),
