('mc:this is the human resource strategy of the philippine plan of action for nutrition.:::barangay nutrition scholar program', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'this is the human resource strategy of the philippine plan of action for nutrition.', 'Barangay Nutrition Scholar Program', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"The Barangay Nutrition Scholar (BNS) Program is the human resource development strategy of the PPAN, involving the recruitment, training, deployment, and supervision of volunteer community nutrition workers in every barangay, originally mandated under Presidential Decree No. 1569.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Barangay Nutrition Scholar Program\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"The Barangay Nutrition Scholar (BNS) Program is the human resource development strategy of the PPAN, involving the recruitment, training, deployment, and supervision of volunteer community nutrition workers in every barangay, originally mandated under Presidential Decree No. 1569.\n\nOperation Timbang Plus, Nutriskwela, and the Promote Good Nutrition Program are service-delivery or advocacy programs.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Nutriskwela Community Radio Network Program, Promote Good Nutrition Program, Operation Timbang Plus Program"}'::jsonb),
('mc:this is the human resource strategy of the philippine plan of action for nutrition.:::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'this is the human resource strategy of the philippine plan of action for nutrition.', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"The Barangay Nutrition Scholar (BNS) Program is the human resource development strategy of the PPAN, involving the recruitment, training, deployment, and supervision of volunteer community nutrition workers in every barangay, originally mandated under Presidential Decree No. 1569.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Barangay Nutrition Scholar Program\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"The Barangay Nutrition Scholar (BNS) Program is the human resource development strategy of the PPAN, involving the recruitment, training, deployment, and supervision of volunteer community nutrition workers in every barangay, originally mandated under Presidential Decree No. 1569.\n\nOperation Timbang Plus, Nutriskwela, and the Promote Good Nutrition Program are service-delivery or advocacy programs.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Nutriskwela Community Radio Network Program, Promote Good Nutrition Program, Operation Timbang Plus Program"}'::jsonb),
('mc:which agency is not a member of the nnc governing board?:::dar', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'which agency is not a member of the nnc governing board?', 'DAR', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"According to the official National Nutrition Council (NNC) Guidelines, the Governing Board is the highest policy-making body on nutrition in the Philippines. It is composed of 10 national government agencies and three representatives from the private sector:","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"DAR\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"According to the official National Nutrition Council (NNC) Guidelines, the Governing Board is the highest policy-making body on nutrition in the Philippines. It is composed of 10 national government agencies and three representatives from the private sector:\n\n1. Department of Health (DOH) – Chairperson\n2. Department of Agriculture (DA) – Vice-Chairperson\n3. Department of the Interior and Local Government (DILG) – Vice-Chairperson\n4. Department of Budget and Management (DBM)\n5. Department of Education (DepEd)\n6. Department of Labor and Employment (DOLE)\n7. Department of Science and Technology (DOST)\n8. Department of Social Welfare and Development (DSWD)\n9. Department of Trade and Industry (DTI)\n10. National Economic and Development Authority (NEDA)","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: DOST, DOLE, DBM"}'::jsonb),
('mc:which agency is not a member of the nnc governing board?:::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'which agency is not a member of the nnc governing board?', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"According to the official National Nutrition Council (NNC) Guidelines, the Governing Board is the highest policy-making body on nutrition in the Philippines. It is composed of 10 national government agencies and three representatives from the private sector:","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"DAR\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"According to the official National Nutrition Council (NNC) Guidelines, the Governing Board is the highest policy-making body on nutrition in the Philippines. It is composed of 10 national government agencies and three representatives from the private sector:\n\n1. Department of Health (DOH) – Chairperson\n2. Department of Agriculture (DA) – Vice-Chairperson\n3. Department of the Interior and Local Government (DILG) – Vice-Chairperson\n4. Department of Budget and Management (DBM)\n5. Department of Education (DepEd)\n6. Department of Labor and Employment (DOLE)\n7. Department of Science and Technology (DOST)\n8. Department of Social Welfare and Development (DSWD)\n9. Department of Trade and Industry (DTI)\n10. National Economic and Development Authority (NEDA)","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: DOST, DOLE, DBM"}'::jsonb),
('mc:who is the first director of the national nutrition council?:::dr. florentino solon', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'who is the first director of the national nutrition council?', 'Dr. Florentino Solon', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Dr. Florentino S. Solon, widely regarded as the \"Father of Nutrition\" in the Philippines, served as the first Executive Director of the National Nutrition Council beginning in 1974, and concurrently founded the Nutrition Center of the Philippines the same year (NNC, 2025).","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Dr. Florentino Solon\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Dr. Florentino S. Solon, widely regarded as the \"Father of Nutrition\" in the Philippines, served as the first Executive Director of the National Nutrition Council beginning in 1974, and concurrently founded the Nutrition Center of the Philippines the same year (NNC, 2025).","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Dr. Juan Salcedo, Jr., Dr. Coronado Pascual, Dr. Francisco Santos"}'::jsonb),
('mc:who is the first director of the national nutrition council?:::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'who is the first director of the national nutrition council?', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Dr. Florentino S. Solon, widely regarded as the \"Father of Nutrition\" in the Philippines, served as the first Executive Director of the National Nutrition Council beginning in 1974, and concurrently founded the Nutrition Center of the Philippines the same year (NNC, 2025).","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Dr. Florentino Solon\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Dr. Florentino S. Solon, widely regarded as the \"Father of Nutrition\" in the Philippines, served as the first Executive Director of the National Nutrition Council beginning in 1974, and concurrently founded the Nutrition Center of the Philippines the same year (NNC, 2025).","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Dr. Juan Salcedo, Jr., Dr. Coronado Pascual, Dr. Francisco Santos"}'::jsonb),
('mc:the nutrient that is considered most difficult to meet during pregnancy::::iron', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'the nutrient that is considered most difficult to meet during pregnancy:', 'iron', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Iron requirements roughly double during pregnancy to support the expansion of maternal blood volume, fetal and placental growth, and stores for the newborn, and this increased demand is very difficult to meet through diet alone, which is why iron-folic acid supplementation is routinely recommended for pregnant women (WHO, 2001). ","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"iron\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Iron requirements roughly double during pregnancy to support the expansion of maternal blood volume, fetal and placental growth, and stores for the newborn, and this increased demand is very difficult to meet through diet alone, which is why iron-folic acid supplementation is routinely recommended for pregnant women (WHO, 2001). \n\nAmong the macro- and micronutrients needed in pregnancy, iron is consistently identified as the nutrient hardest to obtain in adequate amounts from food sources alone.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: calcium, protein, vitamin D"}'::jsonb)
