('mc:what is the name of this campaign character of the 10 kumainments introduced by the nnc?:::mang moi', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'what is the name of this campaign character of the 10 kumainments introduced by the nnc?', 'Mang Moi', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"The National Nutrition Council (NNC) introduced Mang Moi (also known as Moises \"Mang Moi\" Dalisay) as the official campaign character and mascot patterned after the biblical Moses. He serves as the friendly messenger who carries the \"10 Kumainments,\" which are simplified, easy-to-remember nutritional guidelines aimed at helping Filipinos practice healthier eating habits and live active lifestyles.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Mang Moi\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"The National Nutrition Council (NNC) introduced Mang Moi (also known as Moises \"Mang Moi\" Dalisay) as the official campaign character and mascot patterned after the biblical Moses. He serves as the friendly messenger who carries the \"10 Kumainments,\" which are simplified, easy-to-remember nutritional guidelines aimed at helping Filipinos practice healthier eating habits and live active lifestyles.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Mang Jose, Mang Tomas, Mang Ted"}'::jsonb),
('mc:what is the name of this campaign character of the 10 kumainments introduced by the nnc?:::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'what is the name of this campaign character of the 10 kumainments introduced by the nnc?', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"The National Nutrition Council (NNC) introduced Mang Moi (also known as Moises \"Mang Moi\" Dalisay) as the official campaign character and mascot patterned after the biblical Moses. He serves as the friendly messenger who carries the \"10 Kumainments,\" which are simplified, easy-to-remember nutritional guidelines aimed at helping Filipinos practice healthier eating habits and live active lifestyles.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Mang Moi\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"The National Nutrition Council (NNC) introduced Mang Moi (also known as Moises \"Mang Moi\" Dalisay) as the official campaign character and mascot patterned after the biblical Moses. He serves as the friendly messenger who carries the \"10 Kumainments,\" which are simplified, easy-to-remember nutritional guidelines aimed at helping Filipinos practice healthier eating habits and live active lifestyles.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Mang Jose, Mang Tomas, Mang Ted"}'::jsonb),
('mc:computed as the annual number of deaths between 28 days and 1 year divided by the number of live births per year:::post-neonatal mortality rate', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'computed as the annual number of deaths between 28 days and 1 year divided by the number of live births per year', 'post-neonatal mortality rate', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"The post-neonatal mortality rate specifically measures deaths occurring between 28 days and 1 year of age per number of live births in a year.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"post-neonatal mortality rate\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"The post-neonatal mortality rate specifically measures deaths occurring between 28 days and 1 year of age per number of live births in a year.\n\n• Infant mortality rate: Annual number of deaths in the first year (0–12 months) \n• Neonatal mortality rate: Annual number of deaths within the first 28 days \n• Perinatal mortality rate: Annual number of stillbirths and deaths in the first 7 days","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: infant mortality rate, neonatal mortality rate, perinatal mortality rate"}'::jsonb),
('mc:this award is given to a province, city, or municipality that has shown outstanding nutrition implementation for three consecutive years.:::crown award', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'this award is given to a province, city, or municipality that has shown outstanding nutrition implementation for three consecutive years.', 'CROWN Award', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"The Green Banner Award (or Green Banner Seal of Compliance) is given by the NNC to a city, municipality, or province that demonstrates very satisfactory performance in implementing its local nutrition action plan for a given year. ","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"CROWN Award\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"The Green Banner Award (or Green Banner Seal of Compliance) is given by the NNC to a city, municipality, or province that demonstrates very satisfactory performance in implementing its local nutrition action plan for a given year. \n\nAn LGU that wins the Green Banner for three consecutive years becomes eligible for the higher Consistent Regional Outstanding Winner in Nutrition (CROWN) award, and sustained performance beyond that can lead to the Nutrition Honor Award, the highest distinction the NNC gives to an LGU.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: BNS Award, Nutrition Honor Award, Green Banner Award"}'::jsonb),
('mc:this award is given to a province, city, or municipality that has shown outstanding nutrition implementation for three consecutive years.:::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'this award is given to a province, city, or municipality that has shown outstanding nutrition implementation for three consecutive years.', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"The Green Banner Award (or Green Banner Seal of Compliance) is given by the NNC to a city, municipality, or province that demonstrates very satisfactory performance in implementing its local nutrition action plan for a given year. ","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"CROWN Award\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"The Green Banner Award (or Green Banner Seal of Compliance) is given by the NNC to a city, municipality, or province that demonstrates very satisfactory performance in implementing its local nutrition action plan for a given year. \n\nAn LGU that wins the Green Banner for three consecutive years becomes eligible for the higher Consistent Regional Outstanding Winner in Nutrition (CROWN) award, and sustained performance beyond that can lead to the Nutrition Honor Award, the highest distinction the NNC gives to an LGU.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: BNS Award, Nutrition Honor Award, Green Banner Award"}'::jsonb),
('mc:what is the law that stipulates the provision of allowances and incentives to barangay health workers (bhw)?:::ra 7883', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'what is the law that stipulates the provision of allowances and incentives to barangay health workers (bhw)?', 'RA 7883', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Republic Act No. 7883, the \"Barangay Health Workers'' Benefits and Incentives Act of 1995,\" grants accredited barangay health workers benefits such as hazard allowance, subsistence allowance, and other incentives in recognition of their voluntary community health services (Republic Act No. 7883, 1995). ","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"RA 7883\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Republic Act No. 7883, the \"Barangay Health Workers'' Benefits and Incentives Act of 1995,\" grants accredited barangay health workers benefits such as hazard allowance, subsistence allowance, and other incentives in recognition of their voluntary community health services (Republic Act No. 7883, 1995). \n\n• RA 7875: National Health Insurance Act \n• RA 7305: Magna Carta of Public Health Workers \n• RA 7160 is the Local Government Code","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: RA 7160, RA 7875, RA 7305"}'::jsonb),
('mc:what is the law that stipulates the provision of allowances and incentives to barangay health workers (bhw)?:::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'what is the law that stipulates the provision of allowances and incentives to barangay health workers (bhw)?', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Republic Act No. 7883, the \"Barangay Health Workers'' Benefits and Incentives Act of 1995,\" grants accredited barangay health workers benefits such as hazard allowance, subsistence allowance, and other incentives in recognition of their voluntary community health services (Republic Act No. 7883, 1995). ","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"RA 7883\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Republic Act No. 7883, the \"Barangay Health Workers'' Benefits and Incentives Act of 1995,\" grants accredited barangay health workers benefits such as hazard allowance, subsistence allowance, and other incentives in recognition of their voluntary community health services (Republic Act No. 7883, 1995). \n\n• RA 7875: National Health Insurance Act \n• RA 7305: Magna Carta of Public Health Workers \n• RA 7160 is the Local Government Code","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: RA 7160, RA 7875, RA 7305"}'::jsonb),
('mc:computed as the annual number of deaths between 28 days and 1 year divided by the number of live births per year:::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'computed as the annual number of deaths between 28 days and 1 year divided by the number of live births per year', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"The post-neonatal mortality rate specifically measures deaths occurring between 28 days and 1 year of age per number of live births in a year.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"post-neonatal mortality rate\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"The post-neonatal mortality rate specifically measures deaths occurring between 28 days and 1 year of age per number of live births in a year.\n\n• Infant mortality rate: Annual number of deaths in the first year (0–12 months) \n• Neonatal mortality rate: Annual number of deaths within the first 28 days \n• Perinatal mortality rate: Annual number of stillbirths and deaths in the first 7 days","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: infant mortality rate, neonatal mortality rate, perinatal mortality rate"}'::jsonb),
('mc:an innovation made by former president fe marcos with the proclamation of martial law, serving to arrogate unto the chief executive the lawmaking powers of congress. in the freedom constitution of 1986, former president corazon aquino recognized its validity unless otherwise repealed.:::presidential decree', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'an innovation made by former president fe marcos with the proclamation of martial law, serving to arrogate unto the chief executive the lawmaking powers of congress. in the freedom constitution of 1986, former president corazon aquino recognized its validity unless otherwise repealed.', 'Presidential Decree', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Presidential Decrees (PDs) were issuances with the force of law used by former President Ferdinand E. Marcos to exercise the legislative powers of Congress during Martial Law, when the regular legislature was effectively supplanted. ","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Presidential Decree\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Presidential Decrees (PDs) were issuances with the force of law used by former President Ferdinand E. Marcos to exercise the legislative powers of Congress during Martial Law, when the regular legislature was effectively supplanted. \n\nUnder the 1986 Freedom Constitution, President Corazon Aquino recognized existing Presidential Decrees, such as PD No. 1569 establishing the Barangay Nutrition Scholar Program, as remaining valid unless subsequently amended or repealed.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Republic Act, Administrative Order, Letter of Instruction"}'::jsonb),
('mc:an innovation made by former president fe marcos with the proclamation of martial law, serving to arrogate unto the chief executive the lawmaking powers of congress. in the freedom constitution of 1986, former president corazon aquino recognized its validity unless otherwise repealed.:::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'an innovation made by former president fe marcos with the proclamation of martial law, serving to arrogate unto the chief executive the lawmaking powers of congress. in the freedom constitution of 1986, former president corazon aquino recognized its validity unless otherwise repealed.', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Presidential Decrees (PDs) were issuances with the force of law used by former President Ferdinand E. Marcos to exercise the legislative powers of Congress during Martial Law, when the regular legislature was effectively supplanted. ","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Presidential Decree\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Presidential Decrees (PDs) were issuances with the force of law used by former President Ferdinand E. Marcos to exercise the legislative powers of Congress during Martial Law, when the regular legislature was effectively supplanted. \n\nUnder the 1986 Freedom Constitution, President Corazon Aquino recognized existing Presidential Decrees, such as PD No. 1569 establishing the Barangay Nutrition Scholar Program, as remaining valid unless subsequently amended or repealed.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Republic Act, Administrative Order, Letter of Instruction"}'::jsonb),
('mc:what is also known as the "sickness of the older child when the next child is born"?:::kwashiorkor', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'what is also known as the "sickness of the older child when the next child is born"?', 'kwashiorkor', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"The term \"kwashiorkor\" originates from the Ga language of Ghana and translates roughly to \"the sickness of the older child when the next child is born,\" describing the protein-energy malnutrition that develops in a toddler abruptly weaned from breastmilk after the birth of a younger sibling and shifted to a low-protein, starchy diet.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"kwashiorkor\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"The term \"kwashiorkor\" originates from the Ga language of Ghana and translates roughly to \"the sickness of the older child when the next child is born,\" describing the protein-energy malnutrition that develops in a toddler abruptly weaned from breastmilk after the birth of a younger sibling and shifted to a low-protein, starchy diet.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: stunting, vitamin A deficiency disorder, marasmus"}'::jsonb),
('mc:what is also known as the "sickness of the older child when the next child is born"?:::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'what is also known as the "sickness of the older child when the next child is born"?', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"The term \"kwashiorkor\" originates from the Ga language of Ghana and translates roughly to \"the sickness of the older child when the next child is born,\" describing the protein-energy malnutrition that develops in a toddler abruptly weaned from breastmilk after the birth of a younger sibling and shifted to a low-protein, starchy diet.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"kwashiorkor\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"The term \"kwashiorkor\" originates from the Ga language of Ghana and translates roughly to \"the sickness of the older child when the next child is born,\" describing the protein-energy malnutrition that develops in a toddler abruptly weaned from breastmilk after the birth of a younger sibling and shifted to a low-protein, starchy diet.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: stunting, vitamin A deficiency disorder, marasmus"}'::jsonb),
('mc:sentence a. addition of acid to sucrose produces invert sugars. sentence b. saccharin is an invert sugar:::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'sentence a. addition of acid to sucrose produces invert sugars. sentence b. saccharin is an invert sugar', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Sentence A is correct because acid hydrolyzes sucrose into glucose and fructose, producing invert sugar. ","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Sentence A is correct\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Sentence A is correct because acid hydrolyzes sucrose into glucose and fructose, producing invert sugar. \n\nSentence B is incorrect because saccharine is an artificial sweetener and is not an invert sugar.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Sentence B is correct, Bothsentences are correct, Bothsentences are incorrect"}'::jsonb),
('mc:having a sense of truthfulness and reliability with regard to the performance of roles and functions:::integrity', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'having a sense of truthfulness and reliability with regard to the performance of roles and functions', 'integrity', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Integrity is the value that describes a person''s consistent truthfulness, honesty, and reliability in carrying out their duties and responsibilities. It is distinguished from accountability (being answerable for outcomes and decisions), transparency (openness of processes to scrutiny), and efficiency (achieving results with minimal waste of resources). These are recognized as related but distinct standards of good governance and public service conduct.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"integrity\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Integrity is the value that describes a person''s consistent truthfulness, honesty, and reliability in carrying out their duties and responsibilities. It is distinguished from accountability (being answerable for outcomes and decisions), transparency (openness of processes to scrutiny), and efficiency (achieving results with minimal waste of resources). These are recognized as related but distinct standards of good governance and public service conduct.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: efficiency, accountability, transparency"}'::jsonb),
('mc:having a sense of truthfulness and reliability with regard to the performance of roles and functions:::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'having a sense of truthfulness and reliability with regard to the performance of roles and functions', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Integrity is the value that describes a person''s consistent truthfulness, honesty, and reliability in carrying out their duties and responsibilities. It is distinguished from accountability (being answerable for outcomes and decisions), transparency (openness of processes to scrutiny), and efficiency (achieving results with minimal waste of resources). These are recognized as related but distinct standards of good governance and public service conduct.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"integrity\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Integrity is the value that describes a person''s consistent truthfulness, honesty, and reliability in carrying out their duties and responsibilities. It is distinguished from accountability (being answerable for outcomes and decisions), transparency (openness of processes to scrutiny), and efficiency (achieving results with minimal waste of resources). These are recognized as related but distinct standards of good governance and public service conduct.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: efficiency, accountability, transparency"}'::jsonb),
('mc:it is a comprehensive and integrated package of services and communication on health, nutrition, and environment for children available everyday in various settings (home, school, health facilities, and communities) by government, ngos, private sectors, and civic organizations.:::garantisadong pambata program', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'it is a comprehensive and integrated package of services and communication on health, nutrition, and environment for children available everyday in various settings (home, school, health facilities, and communities) by government, ngos, private sectors, and civic organizations.', 'Garantisadong Pambata Program', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Under Department of Health (DOH) Administrative Order No. 36 s. 2010, the Expanded Garantisadong Pambata (GP) Program was redesigned from a biannual institutional campaign into a comprehensive and integrated package of services and communication on health, nutrition, and environment for children available every day in various settings (such as the home, school, health facilities, and communities).","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Garantisadong Pambata Program\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Under Department of Health (DOH) Administrative Order No. 36 s. 2010, the Expanded Garantisadong Pambata (GP) Program was redesigned from a biannual institutional campaign into a comprehensive and integrated package of services and communication on health, nutrition, and environment for children available every day in various settings (such as the home, school, health facilities, and communities).\n\nWhile it is led by the DOH and National Nutrition Council (NNC), it relies heavily on inter-agency and intersectoral cooperation with other government bodies, NGOs, private sectors, and civic organizations to systematically manage child survival interventions.\n\n• Applied Nutrition Program: A historic nutrition initiative in the Philippines launched in the 1960s and 1970s primarily focused on school food production and feeding, which later evolved into broader national nutrition strategies.\n• Healthy Settings Approach: A broader WHO health promotion framework that targets structural environments (e.g., Healthy Cities, Healthy Workplaces, Healthy Schools) but is not a package specifically called out by this child-survival definition.\n• Promote Good Nutrition Program: A component under the Philippine Plan of Action for Nutrition (PPAN) primarily focused on social marketing, behavioral change communication (BCC), and nutrition education regarding infant and young child feeding (IYCF).","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Applied Nutrition Program, Healthy Settings Approach, Promote Good Nutrition Program"}'::jsonb),
('mc:it is a comprehensive and integrated package of services and communication on health, nutrition, and environment for children available everyday in various settings (home, school, health facilities, and communities) by government, ngos, private sectors, and civic organizations.:::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'it is a comprehensive and integrated package of services and communication on health, nutrition, and environment for children available everyday in various settings (home, school, health facilities, and communities) by government, ngos, private sectors, and civic organizations.', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Under Department of Health (DOH) Administrative Order No. 36 s. 2010, the Expanded Garantisadong Pambata (GP) Program was redesigned from a biannual institutional campaign into a comprehensive and integrated package of services and communication on health, nutrition, and environment for children available every day in various settings (such as the home, school, health facilities, and communities).","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Garantisadong Pambata Program\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Under Department of Health (DOH) Administrative Order No. 36 s. 2010, the Expanded Garantisadong Pambata (GP) Program was redesigned from a biannual institutional campaign into a comprehensive and integrated package of services and communication on health, nutrition, and environment for children available every day in various settings (such as the home, school, health facilities, and communities).\n\nWhile it is led by the DOH and National Nutrition Council (NNC), it relies heavily on inter-agency and intersectoral cooperation with other government bodies, NGOs, private sectors, and civic organizations to systematically manage child survival interventions.\n\n• Applied Nutrition Program: A historic nutrition initiative in the Philippines launched in the 1960s and 1970s primarily focused on school food production and feeding, which later evolved into broader national nutrition strategies.\n• Healthy Settings Approach: A broader WHO health promotion framework that targets structural environments (e.g., Healthy Cities, Healthy Workplaces, Healthy Schools) but is not a package specifically called out by this child-survival definition.\n• Promote Good Nutrition Program: A component under the Philippine Plan of Action for Nutrition (PPAN) primarily focused on social marketing, behavioral change communication (BCC), and nutrition education regarding infant and young child feeding (IYCF).","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Applied Nutrition Program, Healthy Settings Approach, Promote Good Nutrition Program"}'::jsonb),
('mc:a process whereby an individual influences a group of individuals to achieve a common goal and relating with followers and making them change.:::leadership', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'a process whereby an individual influences a group of individuals to achieve a common goal and relating with followers and making them change.', 'leadership', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Leadership is defined as the process by which one individual influences and guides a group toward the achievement of a shared goal, engaging and motivating followers to adopt new behaviors or directions. ","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"leadership\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Leadership is defined as the process by which one individual influences and guides a group toward the achievement of a shared goal, engaging and motivating followers to adopt new behaviors or directions. \n\nThis is broader than advocacy (influencing policy or decision-makers), mobilization (organizing resources or people for a specific action), or nutrition education (imparting knowledge), though leadership often makes use of all three.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: advocacy, mobilization, nutrition education"}'::jsonb),
('mc:a process whereby an individual influences a group of individuals to achieve a common goal and relating with followers and making them change.:::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'a process whereby an individual influences a group of individuals to achieve a common goal and relating with followers and making them change.', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Leadership is defined as the process by which one individual influences and guides a group toward the achievement of a shared goal, engaging and motivating followers to adopt new behaviors or directions. ","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"leadership\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Leadership is defined as the process by which one individual influences and guides a group toward the achievement of a shared goal, engaging and motivating followers to adopt new behaviors or directions. \n\nThis is broader than advocacy (influencing policy or decision-makers), mobilization (organizing resources or people for a specific action), or nutrition education (imparting knowledge), though leadership often makes use of all three.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: advocacy, mobilization, nutrition education"}'::jsonb),
('mc:the policy also known as the universal health care (uhc) act, which aims to provide a comprehensive set of quality and cost-effective, promotive, preventive, curative, rehabilitative, and palliative health services by fostering a whole-of-system, whole-of-government approach, and whole-of-society approach in development, implementation, monitoring, and evaluation of health policies, programs, and plans.:::ra 11223', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'the policy also known as the universal health care (uhc) act, which aims to provide a comprehensive set of quality and cost-effective, promotive, preventive, curative, rehabilitative, and palliative health services by fostering a whole-of-system, whole-of-government approach, and whole-of-society approach in development, implementation, monitoring, and evaluation of health policies, programs, and plans.', 'RA 11223', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Republic Act No. 11223, the Universal Health Care Act of 2019, institutionalizes automatic health coverage for all Filipinos and mandates a whole-of-system, whole-of-government, and whole-of-society approach to delivering promotive, preventive, curative, rehabilitative, and palliative health services.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"RA 11223\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Republic Act No. 11223, the Universal Health Care Act of 2019, institutionalizes automatic health coverage for all Filipinos and mandates a whole-of-system, whole-of-government, and whole-of-society approach to delivering promotive, preventive, curative, rehabilitative, and palliative health services.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: RA 11123, RA 11233, RA 12233"}'::jsonb),
('mc:the policy also known as the universal health care (uhc) act, which aims to provide a comprehensive set of quality and cost-effective, promotive, preventive, curative, rehabilitative, and palliative health services by fostering a whole-of-system, whole-of-government approach, and whole-of-society approach in development, implementation, monitoring, and evaluation of health policies, programs, and plans.:::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'the policy also known as the universal health care (uhc) act, which aims to provide a comprehensive set of quality and cost-effective, promotive, preventive, curative, rehabilitative, and palliative health services by fostering a whole-of-system, whole-of-government approach, and whole-of-society approach in development, implementation, monitoring, and evaluation of health policies, programs, and plans.', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Republic Act No. 11223, the Universal Health Care Act of 2019, institutionalizes automatic health coverage for all Filipinos and mandates a whole-of-system, whole-of-government, and whole-of-society approach to delivering promotive, preventive, curative, rehabilitative, and palliative health services.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"RA 11223\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Republic Act No. 11223, the Universal Health Care Act of 2019, institutionalizes automatic health coverage for all Filipinos and mandates a whole-of-system, whole-of-government, and whole-of-society approach to delivering promotive, preventive, curative, rehabilitative, and palliative health services.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: RA 11123, RA 11233, RA 12233"}'::jsonb),
('mc:a community health program seeks to understand why mothers are not practicing recommended complementary feeding. the assessment examines mothers'' knowledge, attitudes, beliefs, available resources, social support, and encouragement from family members and health workers. which phase of the precede-proceed model is being conducted?:::educational and ecological assessment', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'a community health program seeks to understand why mothers are not practicing recommended complementary feeding. the assessment examines mothers'' knowledge, attitudes, beliefs, available resources, social support, and encouragement from family members and health workers. which phase of the precede-proceed model is being conducted?', 'Educational and ecological assessment', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"In the PRECEDE-PROCEED model, Phase 3: Educational and Ecological Assessment categorizes the factors that influence a specific health behavior into three distinct groups:","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Educational and ecological assessment\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"In the PRECEDE-PROCEED model, Phase 3: Educational and Ecological Assessment categorizes the factors that influence a specific health behavior into three distinct groups:\n• Predisposing factors: A person''s internal knowledge, attitudes, beliefs, values, and perceptions (e.g., mothers'' knowledge, attitudes, and beliefs about complementary feeding).\n• Enabling factors: The availability, accessibility, and affordability of resources or skills needed to perform the behavior (e.g., available resources).\n• Reinforcing factors: The feedback, social support, and encouragement from others that either reward or discourage the behavior (e.g., social support and encouragement from family members and health workers).\n\n• Epidemiological assessment (Phase 2): This phase focuses on identifying the specific health problems, distribution of diseases, and health trends within a population (e.g., statistics on malnutrition or stunting rates).\n• Social assessment (Phase 1): This phase evaluates the community''s quality of life, subjective concerns, and social problems (e.g., poverty, unemployment, or overall well-being).\n• Behavioral and environmental assessment (Phase 2/3 transition): This phase identifies the specific behaviors and external environmental conditions that contribute directly to the health problem (e.g., the actual act of not feeding the child complementary food, or a lack of clean water supply).","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Social assessment, Behavioral and environmental assessment, Epidemiological assessment"}'::jsonb),
('mc:a community health program seeks to understand why mothers are not practicing recommended complementary feeding. the assessment examines mothers'' knowledge, attitudes, beliefs, available resources, social support, and encouragement from family members and health workers. which phase of the precede-proceed model is being conducted?:::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'a community health program seeks to understand why mothers are not practicing recommended complementary feeding. the assessment examines mothers'' knowledge, attitudes, beliefs, available resources, social support, and encouragement from family members and health workers. which phase of the precede-proceed model is being conducted?', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"In the PRECEDE-PROCEED model, Phase 3: Educational and Ecological Assessment categorizes the factors that influence a specific health behavior into three distinct groups:","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Educational and ecological assessment\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"In the PRECEDE-PROCEED model, Phase 3: Educational and Ecological Assessment categorizes the factors that influence a specific health behavior into three distinct groups:\n• Predisposing factors: A person''s internal knowledge, attitudes, beliefs, values, and perceptions (e.g., mothers'' knowledge, attitudes, and beliefs about complementary feeding).\n• Enabling factors: The availability, accessibility, and affordability of resources or skills needed to perform the behavior (e.g., available resources).\n• Reinforcing factors: The feedback, social support, and encouragement from others that either reward or discourage the behavior (e.g., social support and encouragement from family members and health workers).\n\n• Epidemiological assessment (Phase 2): This phase focuses on identifying the specific health problems, distribution of diseases, and health trends within a population (e.g., statistics on malnutrition or stunting rates).\n• Social assessment (Phase 1): This phase evaluates the community''s quality of life, subjective concerns, and social problems (e.g., poverty, unemployment, or overall well-being).\n• Behavioral and environmental assessment (Phase 2/3 transition): This phase identifies the specific behaviors and external environmental conditions that contribute directly to the health problem (e.g., the actual act of not feeding the child complementary food, or a lack of clean water supply).","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Social assessment, Behavioral and environmental assessment, Epidemiological assessment"}'::jsonb),
('mc:this law institutionalized the national poverty reduction strategy of the philippine government, providing conditional cash transfers to poor and near-poor households to invest in health, nutrition, and education to break the intergenerational cycle of poverty.:::ra 11310', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'this law institutionalized the national poverty reduction strategy of the philippine government, providing conditional cash transfers to poor and near-poor households to invest in health, nutrition, and education to break the intergenerational cycle of poverty.', 'RA 11310', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Republic Act No. 11310, signed on April 17, 2019, is the 4Ps Act. It legally institutionalized the Pantawid Pamilyang Pilipino Program (4Ps).","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"RA 11310\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Republic Act No. 11310, signed on April 17, 2019, is the 4Ps Act. It legally institutionalized the Pantawid Pamilyang Pilipino Program (4Ps).\n\nIt legally institutionalized the 4Ps as the national poverty reduction strategy and human capital investment program of the Philippines, ensuring its long-term continuity and funding through the Department of Social Welfare and Development (DSWD).\n\nThe program provides monthly human development grants (conditional cash transfers) focusing explicitly on health, nutrition, and education for qualified poor and near-poor households with children aged 0–18 or pregnant members.\n\n• RA 11130: This is the Charter of the City of Carcar (converted into a component city).\n• RA 11210: This is the 105-Day Expanded Maternity Leave Law, which is also highly relevant to public health but covers maternal leave benefits.\n• RA 11120: This is the Declaration of a National Shrine or specific regional landmark declarations, unrelated to national poverty interventions.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: RA 11120, RA 11130, RA 11210"}'::jsonb),
('mc:this law institutionalized the national poverty reduction strategy of the philippine government, providing conditional cash transfers to poor and near-poor households to invest in health, nutrition, and education to break the intergenerational cycle of poverty.:::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'this law institutionalized the national poverty reduction strategy of the philippine government, providing conditional cash transfers to poor and near-poor households to invest in health, nutrition, and education to break the intergenerational cycle of poverty.', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Republic Act No. 11310, signed on April 17, 2019, is the 4Ps Act. It legally institutionalized the Pantawid Pamilyang Pilipino Program (4Ps).","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"RA 11310\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Republic Act No. 11310, signed on April 17, 2019, is the 4Ps Act. It legally institutionalized the Pantawid Pamilyang Pilipino Program (4Ps).\n\nIt legally institutionalized the 4Ps as the national poverty reduction strategy and human capital investment program of the Philippines, ensuring its long-term continuity and funding through the Department of Social Welfare and Development (DSWD).\n\nThe program provides monthly human development grants (conditional cash transfers) focusing explicitly on health, nutrition, and education for qualified poor and near-poor households with children aged 0–18 or pregnant members.\n\n• RA 11130: This is the Charter of the City of Carcar (converted into a component city).\n• RA 11210: This is the 105-Day Expanded Maternity Leave Law, which is also highly relevant to public health but covers maternal leave benefits.\n• RA 11120: This is the Declaration of a National Shrine or specific regional landmark declarations, unrelated to national poverty interventions.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: RA 11120, RA 11130, RA 11210"}'::jsonb),
('mc:sentence a. addition of acid to sucrose produces invert sugars. sentence b. saccharin is an invert sugar:::sentence a is correct', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'sentence a. addition of acid to sucrose produces invert sugars. sentence b. saccharin is an invert sugar', 'Sentence A is correct', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Sentence A is correct because acid hydrolyzes sucrose into glucose and fructose, producing invert sugar. ","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Sentence A is correct\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Sentence A is correct because acid hydrolyzes sucrose into glucose and fructose, producing invert sugar. \n\nSentence B is incorrect because saccharine is an artificial sweetener and is not an invert sugar.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Sentence B is correct, Bothsentences are correct, Bothsentences are incorrect"}'::jsonb),
('mc:the doh package list for emergencies and disasters refer to the following except::::clothing', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'the doh package list for emergencies and disasters refer to the following except:', 'clothing', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"The DOH standard nutrition in emergencies (NiE) package for disaster response focuses on nutrition-specific items (micronutrient supplements, weighing and measuring equipment, and processed or ready-to-use foodstuff for supplementary feeding) rather than clothing, which falls under general relief or social welfare provisions handled by other response agencies (DOH, n.d.).","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"clothing\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"The DOH standard nutrition in emergencies (NiE) package for disaster response focuses on nutrition-specific items (micronutrient supplements, weighing and measuring equipment, and processed or ready-to-use foodstuff for supplementary feeding) rather than clothing, which falls under general relief or social welfare provisions handled by other response agencies (DOH, n.d.).","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: micronutrient supplements, equipment, ready-to-use food"}'::jsonb),
