('mc:lechon kawali is best made from this pork cut:::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'lechon kawali is best made from this pork cut', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Lechon kawali is best made from liempo or pork belly because its layers of meat and fat produce the characteristic rich, crisp, and succulent result.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Liempo\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Lechon kawali is best made from liempo or pork belly because its layers of meat and fat produce the characteristic rich, crisp, and succulent result.\n\nLomo and pigue are leaner cuts, while ribs are less suitable for the usual preparation.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Lomo, Pigue, Tadyang"}'::jsonb),
('mc:the minimum monthly income that enables a family of 5 to buy the needed food to meet their nutritional requirements and other non-basic food needs is called::::poverty threshold', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'the minimum monthly income that enables a family of 5 to buy the needed food to meet their nutritional requirements and other non-basic food needs is called:', 'poverty threshold', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"The poverty threshold (or poverty line) is officially defined as the minimum income required by a family or individual to meet their basic food and non-food needs, including the cost of a nutritionally adequate food basket (PSA, n.d.). Families whose income falls below this threshold are classified as poor, making the poverty threshold a key benchmark used in nutrition and socioeconomic planning.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"poverty threshold\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"The poverty threshold (or poverty line) is officially defined as the minimum income required by a family or individual to meet their basic food and non-food needs, including the cost of a nutritionally adequate food basket (PSA, n.d.). Families whose income falls below this threshold are classified as poor, making the poverty threshold a key benchmark used in nutrition and socioeconomic planning.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: per capita food cost, income elasticity, gross net income"}'::jsonb),
('mc:the minimum monthly income that enables a family of 5 to buy the needed food to meet their nutritional requirements and other non-basic food needs is called::::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'the minimum monthly income that enables a family of 5 to buy the needed food to meet their nutritional requirements and other non-basic food needs is called:', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"The poverty threshold (or poverty line) is officially defined as the minimum income required by a family or individual to meet their basic food and non-food needs, including the cost of a nutritionally adequate food basket (PSA, n.d.). Families whose income falls below this threshold are classified as poor, making the poverty threshold a key benchmark used in nutrition and socioeconomic planning.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"poverty threshold\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"The poverty threshold (or poverty line) is officially defined as the minimum income required by a family or individual to meet their basic food and non-food needs, including the cost of a nutritionally adequate food basket (PSA, n.d.). Families whose income falls below this threshold are classified as poor, making the poverty threshold a key benchmark used in nutrition and socioeconomic planning.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: per capita food cost, income elasticity, gross net income"}'::jsonb),
('mc:what are the four components of pimam? i. community mobilization and outreach ii. outpatient department iii. counseling iv. outpatient therapeutic care v. home visit vi. inpatient therapeutic care vii. supplementary feeding program viii. referral:::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'what are the four components of pimam? i. community mobilization and outreach ii. outpatient department iii. counseling iv. outpatient therapeutic care v. home visit vi. inpatient therapeutic care vii. supplementary feeding program viii. referral', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"The four components of the Philippine Integrated Management of Acute Malnutrition (PIMAM) are:","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"I-IV-VI-VII\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"The four components of the Philippine Integrated Management of Acute Malnutrition (PIMAM) are:\n\n1. I. Community mobilization and outreach\n2. IV. Outpatient Therapeutic care (OTC)\n3. VI. Inpatient Therapeutic care (ITC)\n4. VII. Supplementary Feeding Program (SFP) (also known as Targeted Supplementary Feeding Program / Management of MAM)","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: I-II-III-IV, V-VI-VII-VIII, I-III-V-VIII"}'::jsonb),
