('mc:sentence a. rice starch has smaller granules than potato starch. sentence b. rice starch granules gelatinize earlier than potato starch.:::sentence a is correct', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'sentence a. rice starch has smaller granules than potato starch. sentence b. rice starch granules gelatinize earlier than potato starch.', 'Sentence A is correct', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Rice starch generally has smaller granules than potato starch.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Sentence A is correct\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Rice starch generally has smaller granules than potato starch.\n\nRice starch gelatinizes at a higher temperature range, usually between 65°C to 85°C (depending on the variety), while potato starch gelatinizes at a lower temperature range, usually starting around 58°C to 66°C. \n\nEven though smaller granules can sometimes melt faster under specific conditions, potato starch''s molecular structure (such as higher paste viscosity and lower thermal stability requirements for its crystalline arrangement) allows it to start the swelling and gelatinization process earlier during heating than rice starch.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Sentence B is correct, Both sentences are correct, Both sentences are incorrect"}'::jsonb),
('mc:sentence a. rice starch has smaller granules than potato starch. sentence b. rice starch granules gelatinize earlier than potato starch.:::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'sentence a. rice starch has smaller granules than potato starch. sentence b. rice starch granules gelatinize earlier than potato starch.', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Rice starch generally has smaller granules than potato starch.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Sentence A is correct\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Rice starch generally has smaller granules than potato starch.\n\nRice starch gelatinizes at a higher temperature range, usually between 65°C to 85°C (depending on the variety), while potato starch gelatinizes at a lower temperature range, usually starting around 58°C to 66°C. \n\nEven though smaller granules can sometimes melt faster under specific conditions, potato starch''s molecular structure (such as higher paste viscosity and lower thermal stability requirements for its crystalline arrangement) allows it to start the swelling and gelatinization process earlier during heating than rice starch.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Sentence B is correct, Both sentences are correct, Both sentences are incorrect"}'::jsonb),
('mc:sentence a. ice cream is agitated to form tiny crystals. sentence b. large crystals may be formed with slow freezing.:::both sentences are correct', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'sentence a. ice cream is agitated to form tiny crystals. sentence b. large crystals may be formed with slow freezing.', 'Both sentences are correct', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Agitation during ice-cream freezing helps distribute air and prevents the formation of excessively large crystals. ","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Both sentences are correct\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Agitation during ice-cream freezing helps distribute air and prevents the formation of excessively large crystals. \n\nSlow freezing favors the growth of larger ice crystals and produces a coarser texture.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Sentence A is correct, Sentence B is correct, Both sentences are incorrect"}'::jsonb),
