('mc:sentence a. oven spring is the rapid rise of breads during baking. sentence b. oven spring is due to the action of gluten.:::sentence a is correct', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'sentence a. oven spring is the rapid rise of breads during baking. sentence b. oven spring is due to the action of gluten.', 'Sentence A is correct', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Sentence A is correct because oven spring is the rapid increase in bread volume during the early stage of baking. ","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Sentence A is correct\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Sentence A is correct because oven spring is the rapid increase in bread volume during the early stage of baking. \n\nSentence B is incorrect because oven spring results mainly from expansion of gases and continued yeast activity before yeast is inactivated, while gluten provides structure that helps retain the expanding gases.\n\nOven Spring happens during baking, while gluten formation happens during kneading.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Sentence B is correct, Both sentences are correct, Both sentences are incorrect"}'::jsonb),
('mc:sentence a. oven spring is the rapid rise of breads during baking. sentence b. oven spring is due to the action of gluten.:::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'sentence a. oven spring is the rapid rise of breads during baking. sentence b. oven spring is due to the action of gluten.', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Sentence A is correct because oven spring is the rapid increase in bread volume during the early stage of baking. ","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Sentence A is correct\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Sentence A is correct because oven spring is the rapid increase in bread volume during the early stage of baking. \n\nSentence B is incorrect because oven spring results mainly from expansion of gases and continued yeast activity before yeast is inactivated, while gluten provides structure that helps retain the expanding gases.\n\nOven Spring happens during baking, while gluten formation happens during kneading.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Sentence B is correct, Both sentences are correct, Both sentences are incorrect"}'::jsonb),
('mc:sentence a. margarine is a mixture of skim milk and vegetable fat. sentence b. margarine contains hydrogenated fat.:::both sentences are correct', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'sentence a. margarine is a mixture of skim milk and vegetable fat. sentence b. margarine contains hydrogenated fat.', 'Both sentences are correct', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Margarine – a butter substitute made from vegetable oils and may contain butter, salt, artificial coloring, emulsifiers, and preservatives; made by emulsifying fat other than milk fat with cultured milk; contains eighty percent (80%) fat and sixteen percent (16%) water; usually fortified","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Both sentences are correct\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Margarine – a butter substitute made from vegetable oils and may contain butter, salt, artificial coloring, emulsifiers, and preservatives; made by emulsifying fat other than milk fat with cultured milk; contains eighty percent (80%) fat and sixteen percent (16%) water; usually fortified\n\nMargarine is roughly 80% fat (from vegetable oils or animal fats) and about 17% to 18.5% liquid. Pasteurized skimmed milk, water, or soybean protein fluid makes up this liquid portion.\n\nTraditional margarines also commonly contain hydrogenated or otherwise modified vegetable fats to obtain suitable texture and plasticity.\n\n[Note: \"made by emulsifying fat other than milk fat with cultured milk\"  is a specific traditional or legal standard (resembling statutory definitions found in some food codes like the FDA/Codex Alimentarius for standard margarine blends), though today''s products can use various water-in-oil formulations without strictly requiring cultured milk.]","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Sentence A is correct, Sentence B is correct, Both sentences are incorrect"}'::jsonb),
('mc:sentence a. margarine is a mixture of skim milk and vegetable fat. sentence b. margarine contains hydrogenated fat.:::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'sentence a. margarine is a mixture of skim milk and vegetable fat. sentence b. margarine contains hydrogenated fat.', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Margarine – a butter substitute made from vegetable oils and may contain butter, salt, artificial coloring, emulsifiers, and preservatives; made by emulsifying fat other than milk fat with cultured milk; contains eighty percent (80%) fat and sixteen percent (16%) water; usually fortified","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Both sentences are correct\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Margarine – a butter substitute made from vegetable oils and may contain butter, salt, artificial coloring, emulsifiers, and preservatives; made by emulsifying fat other than milk fat with cultured milk; contains eighty percent (80%) fat and sixteen percent (16%) water; usually fortified\n\nMargarine is roughly 80% fat (from vegetable oils or animal fats) and about 17% to 18.5% liquid. Pasteurized skimmed milk, water, or soybean protein fluid makes up this liquid portion.\n\nTraditional margarines also commonly contain hydrogenated or otherwise modified vegetable fats to obtain suitable texture and plasticity.\n\n[Note: \"made by emulsifying fat other than milk fat with cultured milk\"  is a specific traditional or legal standard (resembling statutory definitions found in some food codes like the FDA/Codex Alimentarius for standard margarine blends), though today''s products can use various water-in-oil formulations without strictly requiring cultured milk.]","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Sentence A is correct, Sentence B is correct, Both sentences are incorrect"}'::jsonb),
('mc:sentence a. chicken fat contains soft fats. sentence b. chicken fat is more plastic than pork fat.:::sentence a is correct', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'sentence a. chicken fat contains soft fats. sentence b. chicken fat is more plastic than pork fat.', 'Sentence A is correct', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"• Sentence A is CORRECT: Chicken fat contains a very high concentration of monounsaturated and polyunsaturated fatty acids. These are chemically classified as \"soft fats\" because they have low melting points, making chicken fat soft and oily at room temperature.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Sentence A is correct\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"• Sentence A is CORRECT: Chicken fat contains a very high concentration of monounsaturated and polyunsaturated fatty acids. These are chemically classified as \"soft fats\" because they have low melting points, making chicken fat soft and oily at room temperature.\n\n• Sentence B is INCORRECT: Because chicken fat turns into liquid oil so easily, it cannot retain its shape under manual pressure. Pork fat (lard) is actually more plastic than chicken fat. Lard forms a stable crystalline matrix at room temperature that allows it to be pliable, spreadable, and hold structural shape—which is the textbook definition of a plastic fat in food science.\n\nPork Fat (Lard): Higher saturated fat content (around 39–45%) gives it a firmer, more stable crystalline structure at room temperature, allowing it to hold a shape and perform well as a plastic shortening in baking and meat processing. \n\nChicken Fat: Higher unsaturated fat content (around 65–68%) gives it a much lower melting point (melting around 23–27°C or lower) and causes it to liquefy rapidly. \n\nBecause it turns to liquid oil so quickly, it has a narrower solid-to-liquid range and lower structural plasticity than pork fat.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Sentence B is correct, Both sentences are correct, Both sentences are incorrect"}'::jsonb),
('mc:sentence a. chicken fat contains soft fats. sentence b. chicken fat is more plastic than pork fat.:::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'sentence a. chicken fat contains soft fats. sentence b. chicken fat is more plastic than pork fat.', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"• Sentence A is CORRECT: Chicken fat contains a very high concentration of monounsaturated and polyunsaturated fatty acids. These are chemically classified as \"soft fats\" because they have low melting points, making chicken fat soft and oily at room temperature.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Sentence A is correct\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"• Sentence A is CORRECT: Chicken fat contains a very high concentration of monounsaturated and polyunsaturated fatty acids. These are chemically classified as \"soft fats\" because they have low melting points, making chicken fat soft and oily at room temperature.\n\n• Sentence B is INCORRECT: Because chicken fat turns into liquid oil so easily, it cannot retain its shape under manual pressure. Pork fat (lard) is actually more plastic than chicken fat. Lard forms a stable crystalline matrix at room temperature that allows it to be pliable, spreadable, and hold structural shape—which is the textbook definition of a plastic fat in food science.\n\nPork Fat (Lard): Higher saturated fat content (around 39–45%) gives it a firmer, more stable crystalline structure at room temperature, allowing it to hold a shape and perform well as a plastic shortening in baking and meat processing. \n\nChicken Fat: Higher unsaturated fat content (around 65–68%) gives it a much lower melting point (melting around 23–27°C or lower) and causes it to liquefy rapidly. \n\nBecause it turns to liquid oil so quickly, it has a narrower solid-to-liquid range and lower structural plasticity than pork fat.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Sentence B is correct, Both sentences are correct, Both sentences are incorrect"}'::jsonb),
('mc:sentence a. moist heat cooking is used in tough or less tender meat. sentence b. water solubilizes collagen making meat tender.:::sentence a is correct', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'sentence a. moist heat cooking is used in tough or less tender meat. sentence b. water solubilizes collagen making meat tender.', 'Sentence A is correct', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Moist-heat cooking is suitable for less tender cuts because prolonged heating in the presence of moisture converts collagen into gelatin, thereby improving tenderness.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Sentence A is correct\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Moist-heat cooking is suitable for less tender cuts because prolonged heating in the presence of moisture converts collagen into gelatin, thereby improving tenderness.\n\nWater alone is not enough to dissolve collagen. When meat reaches temperatures between 160°F to 205°F (71°C to 96°C) range, the tightly wound collagen proteins begin to uncoil and denature.\n\nThe presence of water is chemically required to break the molecular bonds of the uncoiled collagen. Through a process called partial hydrolysis, the water solubilizes the tough collagen and converts it into gelatin.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Sentence B is correct, Both sentences are correct, Both sentences are incorrect"}'::jsonb),
('mc:sentence a. moist heat cooking is used in tough or less tender meat. sentence b. water solubilizes collagen making meat tender.:::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'sentence a. moist heat cooking is used in tough or less tender meat. sentence b. water solubilizes collagen making meat tender.', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Moist-heat cooking is suitable for less tender cuts because prolonged heating in the presence of moisture converts collagen into gelatin, thereby improving tenderness.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Sentence A is correct\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Moist-heat cooking is suitable for less tender cuts because prolonged heating in the presence of moisture converts collagen into gelatin, thereby improving tenderness.\n\nWater alone is not enough to dissolve collagen. When meat reaches temperatures between 160°F to 205°F (71°C to 96°C) range, the tightly wound collagen proteins begin to uncoil and denature.\n\nThe presence of water is chemically required to break the molecular bonds of the uncoiled collagen. Through a process called partial hydrolysis, the water solubilizes the tough collagen and converts it into gelatin.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Sentence B is correct, Both sentences are correct, Both sentences are incorrect"}'::jsonb),
