('mc:sentence a. freezing meat is considered a method of tenderizing meat. sentence b. freezing breaks the connective tissues of meat.:::both sentences are correct', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'sentence a. freezing meat is considered a method of tenderizing meat. sentence b. freezing breaks the connective tissues of meat.', 'Both sentences are correct', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Freezing and thawing can cause ice crystals to damage muscle structure and some connective tissues, which may contribute slightly to tenderization, although freezing is primarily a preservation method rather than a standard tenderizing method.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Both sentences are correct\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Freezing and thawing can cause ice crystals to damage muscle structure and some connective tissues, which may contribute slightly to tenderization, although freezing is primarily a preservation method rather than a standard tenderizing method.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Sentence A is correct, Sentence B is correct, Both sentences are incorrect"}'::jsonb),
('mc:sentence a. freezing meat is considered a method of tenderizing meat. sentence b. freezing breaks the connective tissues of meat.:::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'sentence a. freezing meat is considered a method of tenderizing meat. sentence b. freezing breaks the connective tissues of meat.', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Freezing and thawing can cause ice crystals to damage muscle structure and some connective tissues, which may contribute slightly to tenderization, although freezing is primarily a preservation method rather than a standard tenderizing method.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Both sentences are correct\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Freezing and thawing can cause ice crystals to damage muscle structure and some connective tissues, which may contribute slightly to tenderization, although freezing is primarily a preservation method rather than a standard tenderizing method.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Sentence A is correct, Sentence B is correct, Both sentences are incorrect"}'::jsonb),
('mc:sentence a. liquid milk is scalded before adding to the flour. sentence b. scalding is done to inactivate the enzyme lipase which may soften the dough too much.:::both sentences are correct', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'sentence a. liquid milk is scalded before adding to the flour. sentence b. scalding is done to inactivate the enzyme lipase which may soften the dough too much.', 'Both sentences are correct', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Heating milk to about 180°F (82°C) breaks down whey proteins and destroys glutathione (a tripeptide), preventing them from weakening the gluten structure and making the dough too soft or sticky. ","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Both sentences are correct\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Heating milk to about 180°F (82°C) breaks down whey proteins and destroys glutathione (a tripeptide), preventing them from weakening the gluten structure and making the dough too soft or sticky. \n\nScald – (1) to heat milk to just below the boiling point. (2) To dip certain foods in boiling water; (3) to subject to the action of boiling water as for tomatoes or other fruits to kill germs","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Sentence A is correct, Sentence B is correct, Both sentences are incorrect"}'::jsonb),
