('mc:sentence a. scorching in milk is mainly due to accumulation of lactalbumin at the bottom and side of pan. sentence b. use of double boiler may prevent scorching.:::both sentences are correct', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'sentence a. scorching in milk is mainly due to accumulation of lactalbumin at the bottom and side of pan. sentence b. use of double boiler may prevent scorching.', 'Both sentences are correct', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Scorching – caused by overheating the serum proteins (lactalbumin) that precipitate; stirring once in a double boiler or low heat during cooking minimizes scorching.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Both sentences are correct\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Scorching – caused by overheating the serum proteins (lactalbumin) that precipitate; stirring once in a double boiler or low heat during cooking minimizes scorching.\n\nA double boiler provides gentler, indirect heating and reduces the risk of scorching.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Sentence A is correct, Sentence B is correct, Both sentences are incorrect"}'::jsonb),
('mc:sentence a. scorching in milk is mainly due to accumulation of lactalbumin at the bottom and side of pan. sentence b. use of double boiler may prevent scorching.:::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'sentence a. scorching in milk is mainly due to accumulation of lactalbumin at the bottom and side of pan. sentence b. use of double boiler may prevent scorching.', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Scorching – caused by overheating the serum proteins (lactalbumin) that precipitate; stirring once in a double boiler or low heat during cooking minimizes scorching.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Both sentences are correct\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Scorching – caused by overheating the serum proteins (lactalbumin) that precipitate; stirring once in a double boiler or low heat during cooking minimizes scorching.\n\nA double boiler provides gentler, indirect heating and reduces the risk of scorching.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Sentence A is correct, Sentence B is correct, Both sentences are incorrect"}'::jsonb),
('mc:sentence a. lactase is the most common enzyme used in cheese production. sentence b. casein is first to coagulate upon application of heat.:::both sentences are incorrect', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'sentence a. lactase is the most common enzyme used in cheese production. sentence b. casein is first to coagulate upon application of heat.', 'Both sentences are incorrect', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Rennet, whose active enzyme is chymosin, is the traditional coagulant used in many cheeses, not lactase. ","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Both sentences are incorrect\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Rennet, whose active enzyme is chymosin, is the traditional coagulant used in many cheeses, not lactase. \n\nCasein is not simply the first component to coagulate from ordinary heating alone under all conditions.\n\nProtein coagulation starts at sixty-five (65)C with lactalbumin as the first to be denatured; casein needs higher temperature to coagulate.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Sentence A is correct, Sentence B is correct, Both sentences are correct"}'::jsonb),
('mc:sentence a. lactase is the most common enzyme used in cheese production. sentence b. casein is first to coagulate upon application of heat.:::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'sentence a. lactase is the most common enzyme used in cheese production. sentence b. casein is first to coagulate upon application of heat.', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Rennet, whose active enzyme is chymosin, is the traditional coagulant used in many cheeses, not lactase. ","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Both sentences are incorrect\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Rennet, whose active enzyme is chymosin, is the traditional coagulant used in many cheeses, not lactase. \n\nCasein is not simply the first component to coagulate from ordinary heating alone under all conditions.\n\nProtein coagulation starts at sixty-five (65)C with lactalbumin as the first to be denatured; casein needs higher temperature to coagulate.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Sentence A is correct, Sentence B is correct, Both sentences are correct"}'::jsonb),
('mc:used to limit the migration of moisture into the product.:::packaging', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'used to limit the migration of moisture into the product.', 'Packaging', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Packaging can act as a moisture barrier and limit the migration of moisture into or out of a food product. ","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Packaging\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Packaging can act as a moisture barrier and limit the migration of moisture into or out of a food product. \n\nHeating, freezing, and drying change the product but do not primarily provide a barrier.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Heating, Drying, Freezing"}'::jsonb),
('mc:sentence a. pasteurized milk can be stored at room temperature. sentence b. in pasteurization, pathogenic microorganisms are destroyed.:::sentence b is correct', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'sentence a. pasteurized milk can be stored at room temperature. sentence b. in pasteurization, pathogenic microorganisms are destroyed.', 'Sentence B is correct', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Sentence A is incorrect because pasteurized milk still requires refrigeration. ","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Sentence B is correct\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Sentence A is incorrect because pasteurized milk still requires refrigeration. \n\nSentence B is correct because pasteurization is designed to destroy pathogenic microorganisms and reduce spoilage organisms, although it does not sterilize the product.\n\nPasteurization – the process of heating food to a certain temperature and holding it at that temperature until most of the possible pathogens present in food are destroyed or killed. Used for products that are heat-sensitive like milk and fruit juices.\n\nSterilization – the process by which food is rendered completely free of microorganisms, which could be injurious to the health of man. Use temperatures higher than that in pasteurization. A “commercially sterile” product is not completely free of microorganisms but has killed all but the more resistant bacterial (thermophilic) spores and conditions in the container are not conducive for the growth of the bacterial spores.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Sentence A is correct, Both sentences are correct, Both sentences are incorrect"}'::jsonb),
('mc:sentence a. pasteurized milk can be stored at room temperature. sentence b. in pasteurization, pathogenic microorganisms are destroyed.:::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'sentence a. pasteurized milk can be stored at room temperature. sentence b. in pasteurization, pathogenic microorganisms are destroyed.', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Sentence A is incorrect because pasteurized milk still requires refrigeration. ","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Sentence B is correct\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Sentence A is incorrect because pasteurized milk still requires refrigeration. \n\nSentence B is correct because pasteurization is designed to destroy pathogenic microorganisms and reduce spoilage organisms, although it does not sterilize the product.\n\nPasteurization – the process of heating food to a certain temperature and holding it at that temperature until most of the possible pathogens present in food are destroyed or killed. Used for products that are heat-sensitive like milk and fruit juices.\n\nSterilization – the process by which food is rendered completely free of microorganisms, which could be injurious to the health of man. Use temperatures higher than that in pasteurization. A “commercially sterile” product is not completely free of microorganisms but has killed all but the more resistant bacterial (thermophilic) spores and conditions in the container are not conducive for the growth of the bacterial spores.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Sentence A is correct, Both sentences are correct, Both sentences are incorrect"}'::jsonb),
('mc:sentence a. agglomerated dried milk is soluble in cold water. sentence b. this is due to the hygroscopic property of dried milk.:::sentence a is correct', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'sentence a. agglomerated dried milk is soluble in cold water. sentence b. this is due to the hygroscopic property of dried milk.', 'Sentence A is correct', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Agglomerated milk powder is an \"instant\" milk powder made by clustering fine spray-dried milk particles into larger, porous granules. ","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Sentence A is correct\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Agglomerated milk powder is an \"instant\" milk powder made by clustering fine spray-dried milk particles into larger, porous granules. \n\nWater penetrates the porous clusters rapidly, allowing the powder to dissolve instantly without clumping.\n\nSentence A is correct because agglomerated dried milk is designed to disperse and dissolve more readily in water, including cold water. \n\nSentence B is incorrect because this improved reconstitution is mainly due to agglomeration (spray drying followed by other processes) and increased particle structure, not simply to hygroscopicity.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Sentence B is correct, Both sentences are correct, Both sentences are incorrect"}'::jsonb),
('mc:sentence a. agglomerated dried milk is soluble in cold water. sentence b. this is due to the hygroscopic property of dried milk.:::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'sentence a. agglomerated dried milk is soluble in cold water. sentence b. this is due to the hygroscopic property of dried milk.', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Agglomerated milk powder is an \"instant\" milk powder made by clustering fine spray-dried milk particles into larger, porous granules. ","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Sentence A is correct\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Agglomerated milk powder is an \"instant\" milk powder made by clustering fine spray-dried milk particles into larger, porous granules. \n\nWater penetrates the porous clusters rapidly, allowing the powder to dissolve instantly without clumping.\n\nSentence A is correct because agglomerated dried milk is designed to disperse and dissolve more readily in water, including cold water. \n\nSentence B is incorrect because this improved reconstitution is mainly due to agglomeration (spray drying followed by other processes) and increased particle structure, not simply to hygroscopicity.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Sentence B is correct, Both sentences are correct, Both sentences are incorrect"}'::jsonb),
('mc:iloilo’s version of espasol:::baye-baye', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'iloilo’s version of espasol', 'Baye-baye', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Baye-baye is a popular native rice cake delicacy from Western Visayas, especially in Santa Barbara and Miagao, Iloilo. Much like the Tagalog espasol (which is traditionally rolled in toasted rice flour), baye-baye shares a very similar chewy structural profile. It is prepared by mixing finely ground, newly harvested toasted rice (pinipig) or corn with freshly grated young coconut and sugar, then pounding and shaping the mixture into dense patties or cylinders.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Baye-baye\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Baye-baye is a popular native rice cake delicacy from Western Visayas, especially in Santa Barbara and Miagao, Iloilo. Much like the Tagalog espasol (which is traditionally rolled in toasted rice flour), baye-baye shares a very similar chewy structural profile. It is prepared by mixing finely ground, newly harvested toasted rice (pinipig) or corn with freshly grated young coconut and sugar, then pounding and shaping the mixture into dense patties or cylinders.\n\n• Budbud kabog: A famous native delicacy from Catmon, Cebu. It is a sweet sticky rice cake roll wrapped securely in banana leaves, but it is made using millet seeds (kabog) instead of rice flour.\n• Moron: A distinct, two-toned chocolate rice cake specialty from Leyte and Samar. It features a twisted strip of sweet milk-infused glutinous rice intertwined with a rich chocolate-cacao rice paste wrapped in banana leaves.\n• Binagol: A famous sweet delicacy from Leyte. It is made from mashed taro root (talyan), condensed milk, and sugar, which is packed and steamed inside a halved coconut shell (bagol) and topped with a rich egg yolk-sweet center.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Binagol, Moron, Budbud kabog"}'::jsonb),
('mc:iloilo’s version of espasol:::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'iloilo’s version of espasol', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Baye-baye is a popular native rice cake delicacy from Western Visayas, especially in Santa Barbara and Miagao, Iloilo. Much like the Tagalog espasol (which is traditionally rolled in toasted rice flour), baye-baye shares a very similar chewy structural profile. It is prepared by mixing finely ground, newly harvested toasted rice (pinipig) or corn with freshly grated young coconut and sugar, then pounding and shaping the mixture into dense patties or cylinders.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Baye-baye\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Baye-baye is a popular native rice cake delicacy from Western Visayas, especially in Santa Barbara and Miagao, Iloilo. Much like the Tagalog espasol (which is traditionally rolled in toasted rice flour), baye-baye shares a very similar chewy structural profile. It is prepared by mixing finely ground, newly harvested toasted rice (pinipig) or corn with freshly grated young coconut and sugar, then pounding and shaping the mixture into dense patties or cylinders.\n\n• Budbud kabog: A famous native delicacy from Catmon, Cebu. It is a sweet sticky rice cake roll wrapped securely in banana leaves, but it is made using millet seeds (kabog) instead of rice flour.\n• Moron: A distinct, two-toned chocolate rice cake specialty from Leyte and Samar. It features a twisted strip of sweet milk-infused glutinous rice intertwined with a rich chocolate-cacao rice paste wrapped in banana leaves.\n• Binagol: A famous sweet delicacy from Leyte. It is made from mashed taro root (talyan), condensed milk, and sugar, which is packed and steamed inside a halved coconut shell (bagol) and topped with a rich egg yolk-sweet center.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Binagol, Moron, Budbud kabog"}'::jsonb),
('mc:sentence a. bran is prone to rancidity. sentence b. polished and whole corn kernels store longer than unpolished ones.:::both sentences are correct', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'sentence a. bran is prone to rancidity. sentence b. polished and whole corn kernels store longer than unpolished ones.', 'Both sentences are correct', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Whole grain and undermilled cereals deteriorate more rapidly because of the presence of the bran and germ than refined or undermilled; oxidative rancidity attacks the unsaturated fats in the germ and bran; they should not be kept in storage too long unless refrigerated.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Both sentences are correct\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Whole grain and undermilled cereals deteriorate more rapidly because of the presence of the bran and germ than refined or undermilled; oxidative rancidity attacks the unsaturated fats in the germ and bran; they should not be kept in storage too long unless refrigerated.\n\nRemoving or protecting outer layers and reducing exposure to damaging conditions can improve storage stability of cereal grains.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Sentence A is correct, Sentence B is correct, Both sentences are incorrect"}'::jsonb),
('mc:sentence a. bran is prone to rancidity. sentence b. polished and whole corn kernels store longer than unpolished ones.:::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'sentence a. bran is prone to rancidity. sentence b. polished and whole corn kernels store longer than unpolished ones.', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Whole grain and undermilled cereals deteriorate more rapidly because of the presence of the bran and germ than refined or undermilled; oxidative rancidity attacks the unsaturated fats in the germ and bran; they should not be kept in storage too long unless refrigerated.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Both sentences are correct\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Whole grain and undermilled cereals deteriorate more rapidly because of the presence of the bran and germ than refined or undermilled; oxidative rancidity attacks the unsaturated fats in the germ and bran; they should not be kept in storage too long unless refrigerated.\n\nRemoving or protecting outer layers and reducing exposure to damaging conditions can improve storage stability of cereal grains.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Sentence A is correct, Sentence B is correct, Both sentences are incorrect"}'::jsonb),
('mc:sentence a. hydrogenation prevents oxidative rancidity. sentence b. oxidative rancidity is more prone in oils with higher saturated fatty acids.:::sentence a is correct', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'sentence a. hydrogenation prevents oxidative rancidity. sentence b. oxidative rancidity is more prone in oils with higher saturated fatty acids.', 'Sentence A is correct', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Sentence A is correct because hydrogenation reduces the number of double bonds and can improve resistance to oxidative rancidity. ","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Sentence A is correct\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Sentence A is correct because hydrogenation reduces the number of double bonds and can improve resistance to oxidative rancidity. \n\nSentence B is incorrect because highly unsaturated fats are generally more prone to oxidation than saturated fats.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Sentence B is correct, Both sentences are correct, Both sentences are incorrect"}'::jsonb),
