('mc:sentence a. polished rice cooks longer than unpolished rice. sentence b. this is due to the fact that polished rice contains more starch.:::both sentences are incorrect', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'sentence a. polished rice cooks longer than unpolished rice. sentence b. this is due to the fact that polished rice contains more starch.', 'Both sentences are incorrect', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Unpolished rice generally requires longer cooking because the bran layer and intact structure slow water penetration. ","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Both sentences are incorrect\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Unpolished rice generally requires longer cooking because the bran layer and intact structure slow water penetration. \n\n• Sentence A is incorrect: Polished rice (white rice) actually cooks much faster than unpolished rice (brown or red rice). Polished rice usually takes about 15 to 20 minutes to cook, whereas unpolished rice takes 40 to 45 minutes. This is because unpolished rice retains its tough, fibrous outer bran layer, which slows down water absorption and requires a longer time to soften.\n• Sentence B is incorrect: Since the premise of Sentence A is completely backward, the explanation in Sentence B is also fundamentally incorrect. While milling and polishing do increase the percentage of starch by weight (by stripping away the fiber-rich bran and nutrient-dense germ), it makes the grain cook faster, not longer, because the water can instantly penetrate the exposed starch.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Sentence A is correct, Sentence B is correct, Both sentences are correct"}'::jsonb),
('mc:sentence a. polished rice cooks longer than unpolished rice. sentence b. this is due to the fact that polished rice contains more starch.:::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'sentence a. polished rice cooks longer than unpolished rice. sentence b. this is due to the fact that polished rice contains more starch.', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Unpolished rice generally requires longer cooking because the bran layer and intact structure slow water penetration. ","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Both sentences are incorrect\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Unpolished rice generally requires longer cooking because the bran layer and intact structure slow water penetration. \n\n• Sentence A is incorrect: Polished rice (white rice) actually cooks much faster than unpolished rice (brown or red rice). Polished rice usually takes about 15 to 20 minutes to cook, whereas unpolished rice takes 40 to 45 minutes. This is because unpolished rice retains its tough, fibrous outer bran layer, which slows down water absorption and requires a longer time to soften.\n• Sentence B is incorrect: Since the premise of Sentence A is completely backward, the explanation in Sentence B is also fundamentally incorrect. While milling and polishing do increase the percentage of starch by weight (by stripping away the fiber-rich bran and nutrient-dense germ), it makes the grain cook faster, not longer, because the water can instantly penetrate the exposed starch.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Sentence A is correct, Sentence B is correct, Both sentences are correct"}'::jsonb),
('mc:sentence a. breads from sweet potato, corn or cassava are crumbly. sentence b. sweet potato, corn or cassava flours do not contain gluten:::both sentences are correct', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'sentence a. breads from sweet potato, corn or cassava are crumbly. sentence b. sweet potato, corn or cassava flours do not contain gluten', 'Both sentences are correct', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Flours from sweet potato, corn, and cassava lack gluten-forming proteins, so breads containing large amounts of these flours tend to have a more crumbly structure.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Both sentences are correct\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Flours from sweet potato, corn, and cassava lack gluten-forming proteins, so breads containing large amounts of these flours tend to have a more crumbly structure.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Sentence A is correct, Sentence B is correct, Both sentences are incorrect"}'::jsonb),
