('mc:which of the following is arranged according to the prescribed menu pattern for lunch?:::chicken pastel, steamed broccoli, saffron rice, jasmine tea', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'which of the following is arranged according to the prescribed menu pattern for lunch?', 'Chicken Pastel, Steamed Broccoli, Saffron Rice, Jasmine Tea', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"The conventional menu sequence for lunch presents the food items in a specific sequential order:","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Chicken Pastel, Steamed Broccoli, Saffron Rice, Jasmine Tea\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"The conventional menu sequence for lunch presents the food items in a specific sequential order:\n\n1. Soup or Appetizer (if included)\n2. Main Course / Protein (Chicken Pastel)\n3. Vegetables or Salad (Steamed Broccoli)\n4. Cereal or Rice (Saffron Rice)\n5. Dessert (if included)\n6. Beverage (Jasmine Tea)","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Jasmine Tea, Chicken Pastel, Steamed Broccoli, Saffron Rice, Chicken Pastel, Saffron Rice, Steamed Broccoli, Jasmine Tea, None of the above"}'::jsonb),
('mc:which of the following is arranged according to the prescribed menu pattern for lunch?:::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'which of the following is arranged according to the prescribed menu pattern for lunch?', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"The conventional menu sequence for lunch presents the food items in a specific sequential order:","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Chicken Pastel, Steamed Broccoli, Saffron Rice, Jasmine Tea\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"The conventional menu sequence for lunch presents the food items in a specific sequential order:\n\n1. Soup or Appetizer (if included)\n2. Main Course / Protein (Chicken Pastel)\n3. Vegetables or Salad (Steamed Broccoli)\n4. Cereal or Rice (Saffron Rice)\n5. Dessert (if included)\n6. Beverage (Jasmine Tea)","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Jasmine Tea, Chicken Pastel, Steamed Broccoli, Saffron Rice, Chicken Pastel, Saffron Rice, Steamed Broccoli, Jasmine Tea, None of the above"}'::jsonb),
('mc:the last recommended day of retail sale is referred to as::::pull date', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'the last recommended day of retail sale is referred to as:', 'pull date', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"A pull date is the last recommended date for retail display or sale before the product should be removed from shelves.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"pull date\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"A pull date is the last recommended date for retail display or sale before the product should be removed from shelves.\n\n• Expiration: This is the last date on which the product should be used or consumed. It is often used for products where safety is a concern, indicating that after this date, the product may no longer be safe to consume.\n• Best before and Freshness Date: both indicate the last date recommended for the use of the product at its peak quality. It does not refer to safety but rather optimal quality.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: expiration date, freshness date, best before date"}'::jsonb),
('mc:the last recommended day of retail sale is referred to as::::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'the last recommended day of retail sale is referred to as:', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"A pull date is the last recommended date for retail display or sale before the product should be removed from shelves.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"pull date\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"A pull date is the last recommended date for retail display or sale before the product should be removed from shelves.\n\n• Expiration: This is the last date on which the product should be used or consumed. It is often used for products where safety is a concern, indicating that after this date, the product may no longer be safe to consume.\n• Best before and Freshness Date: both indicate the last date recommended for the use of the product at its peak quality. It does not refer to safety but rather optimal quality.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: expiration date, freshness date, best before date"}'::jsonb),
('mc:a recipe of buffalo wings makes 30 pcs (3 servings). what would you do to lower the food cost of the menu item?:::make the portion size 6 pcs/serving', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'a recipe of buffalo wings makes 30 pcs (3 servings). what would you do to lower the food cost of the menu item?', 'Make the portion size 6 pcs/serving', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Reducing the number of pieces per plate from 10 pieces down to 6 pieces uses fewer raw ingredients per order. Using fewer chicken wings directly decreases the cost to make each individual plate.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Make the portion size 6 pcs/serving\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Reducing the number of pieces per plate from 10 pieces down to 6 pieces uses fewer raw ingredients per order. Using fewer chicken wings directly decreases the cost to make each individual plate.\n\n• 15 pcs/serving: Increasing the portion size uses more ingredients, which raises the food cost per plate.\n• Prefabricated products: Pre-made or pre-cut items usually cost more because you pay extra for the supplier''s labor.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Make the portion size 15 pcs/serving, Use prefabricated products, None of the above"}'::jsonb),
('mc:a recipe of buffalo wings makes 30 pcs (3 servings). what would you do to lower the food cost of the menu item?:::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'a recipe of buffalo wings makes 30 pcs (3 servings). what would you do to lower the food cost of the menu item?', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Reducing the number of pieces per plate from 10 pieces down to 6 pieces uses fewer raw ingredients per order. Using fewer chicken wings directly decreases the cost to make each individual plate.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Make the portion size 6 pcs/serving\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Reducing the number of pieces per plate from 10 pieces down to 6 pieces uses fewer raw ingredients per order. Using fewer chicken wings directly decreases the cost to make each individual plate.\n\n• 15 pcs/serving: Increasing the portion size uses more ingredients, which raises the food cost per plate.\n• Prefabricated products: Pre-made or pre-cut items usually cost more because you pay extra for the supplier''s labor.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Make the portion size 15 pcs/serving, Use prefabricated products, None of the above"}'::jsonb),
('mc:a menu item that sells for p70.00 has a food cost percent of 20%. what would the food cost percent be if the selling price was increased to p150.00?:::9.3%', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'a menu item that sells for p70.00 has a food cost percent of 20%. what would the food cost percent be if the selling price was increased to p150.00?', '9.3%', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Original Food Cost Percentage (Php70.00x 0.2 = 14.00 PHP)","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"9.3%\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Original Food Cost Percentage (Php70.00x 0.2 = 14.00 PHP)\n\nNew FCP= (Food Cost/New SP) x100\n\nNew FCP = (14.00/150.00) x 100 = 9.3%","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: 10.3%, 11.3%, 8.3%"}'::jsonb),
('mc:a menu item that sells for p70.00 has a food cost percent of 20%. what would the food cost percent be if the selling price was increased to p150.00?:::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'a menu item that sells for p70.00 has a food cost percent of 20%. what would the food cost percent be if the selling price was increased to p150.00?', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Original Food Cost Percentage (Php70.00x 0.2 = 14.00 PHP)","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"9.3%\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Original Food Cost Percentage (Php70.00x 0.2 = 14.00 PHP)\n\nNew FCP= (Food Cost/New SP) x100\n\nNew FCP = (14.00/150.00) x 100 = 9.3%","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: 10.3%, 11.3%, 8.3%"}'::jsonb),
('mc:your recipe calls for 3 kg diced carrots. if a vendor sells a 10 kg case of carrots for p499.00, how much will the carrots purchased for this recipe cost? yield percent of carrots is 81.3%:::p184.13', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'your recipe calls for 3 kg diced carrots. if a vendor sells a 10 kg case of carrots for p499.00, how much will the carrots purchased for this recipe cost? yield percent of carrots is 81.3%', 'P184.13', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"The edible yield required is 3 kg. ","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"P184.13\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"The edible yield required is 3 kg. \n\nWith an 81.3% yield, the as-purchased quantity needed is 3 ÷ 0.813 ≈ 3.69 kg. \n\nAt ₱499 per 10 kg, the cost is approximately ₱184.13.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: P149.70, P121.70, None of the above"}'::jsonb),
('mc:your recipe calls for 3 kg diced carrots. if a vendor sells a 10 kg case of carrots for p499.00, how much will the carrots purchased for this recipe cost? yield percent of carrots is 81.3%:::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'your recipe calls for 3 kg diced carrots. if a vendor sells a 10 kg case of carrots for p499.00, how much will the carrots purchased for this recipe cost? yield percent of carrots is 81.3%', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"The edible yield required is 3 kg. ","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"P184.13\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"The edible yield required is 3 kg. \n\nWith an 81.3% yield, the as-purchased quantity needed is 3 ÷ 0.813 ≈ 3.69 kg. \n\nAt ₱499 per 10 kg, the cost is approximately ₱184.13.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: P149.70, P121.70, None of the above"}'::jsonb),
('mc:you have a béarnaise recipe that makes seven 14-gram portions but you need to make enough to have twenty 8-gram portions. what is the recipe conversion factor?:::1.63', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'you have a béarnaise recipe that makes seven 14-gram portions but you need to make enough to have twenty 8-gram portions. what is the recipe conversion factor?', '1.63', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Original yield is 7 × 14 g = 98 g. ","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"1.63\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Original yield is 7 × 14 g = 98 g. \n\nRequired yield is 20 × 8 g = 160 g. \n\nThe conversion factor is 160 ÷ 98 ≈ 1.63.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: 0.61, 0.57, None of the above"}'::jsonb),
('mc:a salsa recipe calls for 3 oz chopped fresh cilantro to serve 48 guests. if chopped cilantro gives a 48% yield, how many servings of salsa can you make using 1 ½ lbs of cilantro you have purchased?:::184 servings', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'a salsa recipe calls for 3 oz chopped fresh cilantro to serve 48 guests. if chopped cilantro gives a 48% yield, how many servings of salsa can you make using 1 ½ lbs of cilantro you have purchased?', '184 servings', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"1 ½ lb equals 24 oz AP. At 48% yield, usable cilantro is 11.52 oz. ","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"184 servings\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"1 ½ lb equals 24 oz AP. At 48% yield, usable cilantro is 11.52 oz. \n\nAt 3 oz per 48 guests, this is about 184 servings.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: 11 servings, 12 servings, 194 servings"}'::jsonb),
('mc:a salsa recipe calls for 3 oz chopped fresh cilantro to serve 48 guests. if chopped cilantro gives a 48% yield, how many servings of salsa can you make using 1 ½ lbs of cilantro you have purchased?:::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'a salsa recipe calls for 3 oz chopped fresh cilantro to serve 48 guests. if chopped cilantro gives a 48% yield, how many servings of salsa can you make using 1 ½ lbs of cilantro you have purchased?', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"1 ½ lb equals 24 oz AP. At 48% yield, usable cilantro is 11.52 oz. ","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"184 servings\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"1 ½ lb equals 24 oz AP. At 48% yield, usable cilantro is 11.52 oz. \n\nAt 3 oz per 48 guests, this is about 184 servings.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: 11 servings, 12 servings, 194 servings"}'::jsonb),
('mc:tea-like beverage using herbs/plants other than camella sinensis:::tisanes', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'tea-like beverage using herbs/plants other than camella sinensis', 'Tisanes', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Tisanes, commonly known as herbal teas, are beverages made from the infusion or decoction of herbs, spices, fruits, or other plant material in hot water. Unlike true teas (such as Oolong and Sencha), they do not contain leaves from the Camellia sinensis plant.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Tisanes\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Tisanes, commonly known as herbal teas, are beverages made from the infusion or decoction of herbs, spices, fruits, or other plant material in hot water. Unlike true teas (such as Oolong and Sencha), they do not contain leaves from the Camellia sinensis plant.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Oolong, Tea imitations, Sencha"}'::jsonb),
