('mc:when fish fillet is on the menu, you need fish that will be::::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'when fish fillet is on the menu, you need fish that will be:', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Fish fillets should be boneless and cut into appropriate portions or slices because a fillet is a boneless section of fish flesh.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"deboned and sliced\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Fish fillets should be boneless and cut into appropriate portions or slices because a fillet is a boneless section of fish flesh.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: served whole, flaked, stuffed"}'::jsonb),
('mc:paella negra gets its black color from::::squid ink', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'paella negra gets its black color from:', 'Squid ink', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Paella Negra (also known as Arroz Negro in Spain) gets its signature deep black color and distinct savory, umami flavor from squid ink (or cuttlefish ink), which is incorporated directly into the cooking liquid while the rice simmers.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Squid ink\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Paella Negra (also known as Arroz Negro in Spain) gets its signature deep black color and distinct savory, umami flavor from squid ink (or cuttlefish ink), which is incorporated directly into the cooking liquid while the rice simmers.\n\n• Bile: This bitter fluid produced by the liver is used in specific traditional dishes (like the Filipino beef soup papaitan) to add a bitter flavor profile and a greenish tint, not a rich black color.\n• Pirurutong: A native heirloom variety of sticky, deep purple-to-black rice commonly used in the Philippines to make traditional desserts like puto bumbong. It is not used in traditional paella.\n• Black rice: While natural black rice grains exist (like forbidden rice), classic Paella Negra is made using standard white medium-grain rice (like Bomba or Arborio) that is stained black during the cooking process using the ink.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Bile, Black rice, Pirurutong"}'::jsonb),
('mc:lenten observance among roman catholics prescribes abstinence from::::meat', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'lenten observance among roman catholics prescribes abstinence from:', 'Meat', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Roman Catholic Lenten abstinence traditionally prescribes abstaining from meat on designated days. Fish and other non-meat foods may be consumed.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Meat\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Roman Catholic Lenten abstinence traditionally prescribes abstaining from meat on designated days. Fish and other non-meat foods may be consumed.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Fish and shellfish, Vegetables, Fruits"}'::jsonb),
('mc:paella negra gets its black color from::::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'paella negra gets its black color from:', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Paella Negra (also known as Arroz Negro in Spain) gets its signature deep black color and distinct savory, umami flavor from squid ink (or cuttlefish ink), which is incorporated directly into the cooking liquid while the rice simmers.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Squid ink\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Paella Negra (also known as Arroz Negro in Spain) gets its signature deep black color and distinct savory, umami flavor from squid ink (or cuttlefish ink), which is incorporated directly into the cooking liquid while the rice simmers.\n\n• Bile: This bitter fluid produced by the liver is used in specific traditional dishes (like the Filipino beef soup papaitan) to add a bitter flavor profile and a greenish tint, not a rich black color.\n• Pirurutong: A native heirloom variety of sticky, deep purple-to-black rice commonly used in the Philippines to make traditional desserts like puto bumbong. It is not used in traditional paella.\n• Black rice: While natural black rice grains exist (like forbidden rice), classic Paella Negra is made using standard white medium-grain rice (like Bomba or Arborio) that is stained black during the cooking process using the ink.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Bile, Black rice, Pirurutong"}'::jsonb),
('mc:a savory pie with a predominant egg filling.:::quiche', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'a savory pie with a predominant egg filling.', 'Quiche', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"A quiche is a French savory pie consisting of a pastry crust filled with savory custard, cheese, meats, seafood, or vegetables.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Quiche\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"A quiche is a French savory pie consisting of a pastry crust filled with savory custard, cheese, meats, seafood, or vegetables.\n\nThe defining characteristic of a quiche is its predominant egg and cream filling, which bakes into a rich, custard-like texture.\n\n• Omelet: A dish made from beaten eggs fried with butter or oil in a frying pan, usually folded around a filling. It does not have a pie crust.\n• Egg tart: A sweet pastry crust filled with a sugary egg custard, popular in Portuguese, English, and Hong Kong cuisines. It is a dessert, not a savory pie.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Egg tart, Omelet, None of the above"}'::jsonb),
('mc:a savory pie with a predominant egg filling.:::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'a savory pie with a predominant egg filling.', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"A quiche is a French savory pie consisting of a pastry crust filled with savory custard, cheese, meats, seafood, or vegetables.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Quiche\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"A quiche is a French savory pie consisting of a pastry crust filled with savory custard, cheese, meats, seafood, or vegetables.\n\nThe defining characteristic of a quiche is its predominant egg and cream filling, which bakes into a rich, custard-like texture.\n\n• Omelet: A dish made from beaten eggs fried with butter or oil in a frying pan, usually folded around a filling. It does not have a pie crust.\n• Egg tart: A sweet pastry crust filled with a sugary egg custard, popular in Portuguese, English, and Hong Kong cuisines. It is a dessert, not a savory pie.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Egg tart, Omelet, None of the above"}'::jsonb),
('mc:spore-forming bacteria that grows without oxygen and can produce a deadly toxin when food is time-temperature abused.:::clostridium botulinum', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'spore-forming bacteria that grows without oxygen and can produce a deadly toxin when food is time-temperature abused.', 'Clostridium botulinum', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Clostridium botulinum is a notorious spore-forming bacterium. Its spores can survive extreme environmental conditions, including normal cooking temperatures.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Clostridium botulinum\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Clostridium botulinum is a notorious spore-forming bacterium. Its spores can survive extreme environmental conditions, including normal cooking temperatures.\n\nIt is strictly anaerobic, meaning it thrives and multiplies only in environments devoid of oxygen—such as improperly home-canned foods, vacuum-packed foods, garlic-in-oil mixtures, or tightly wrapped baked potatoes.\n\nWhen contaminated food is time-temperature abused, the spores germinate into active bacteria and produce a highly lethal neurotoxin. Ingesting even a microscopic amount of this toxin causes botulism, a severe foodborne illness that leads to muscle paralysis and can be fatal if respiratory muscles fail.\n\n• Salmonella spp: A non-spore-forming, facultative anaerobic bacterium commonly found in poultry and eggs. It causes an infection rather than a pre-formed deadly neurotoxin.\n• Listeria monocytogenes: A non-spore-forming bacterium that can grow in cold environments (like refrigerators). It is an aerobe/facultative anaerobe commonly linked to unpasteurized dairy and deli meats.\n• Bacillus cereus: While this is a spore-forming bacterium linked to time-temperature abuse (classically found in fried rice), it is a facultative anaerobe (grows with or without oxygen) and its toxins cause vomiting or diarrhea, but are rarely lethal compared to botulinum neurotoxins.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Salmonella spp, Listeria monocytogenes, Bacillus cereus"}'::jsonb),
('mc:lenten observance among roman catholics prescribes abstinence from::::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'lenten observance among roman catholics prescribes abstinence from:', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Roman Catholic Lenten abstinence traditionally prescribes abstaining from meat on designated days. Fish and other non-meat foods may be consumed.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Meat\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Roman Catholic Lenten abstinence traditionally prescribes abstaining from meat on designated days. Fish and other non-meat foods may be consumed.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Fish and shellfish, Vegetables, Fruits"}'::jsonb),
('mc:spore-forming bacteria that grows without oxygen and can produce a deadly toxin when food is time-temperature abused.:::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'spore-forming bacteria that grows without oxygen and can produce a deadly toxin when food is time-temperature abused.', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Clostridium botulinum is a notorious spore-forming bacterium. Its spores can survive extreme environmental conditions, including normal cooking temperatures.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Clostridium botulinum\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Clostridium botulinum is a notorious spore-forming bacterium. Its spores can survive extreme environmental conditions, including normal cooking temperatures.\n\nIt is strictly anaerobic, meaning it thrives and multiplies only in environments devoid of oxygen—such as improperly home-canned foods, vacuum-packed foods, garlic-in-oil mixtures, or tightly wrapped baked potatoes.\n\nWhen contaminated food is time-temperature abused, the spores germinate into active bacteria and produce a highly lethal neurotoxin. Ingesting even a microscopic amount of this toxin causes botulism, a severe foodborne illness that leads to muscle paralysis and can be fatal if respiratory muscles fail.\n\n• Salmonella spp: A non-spore-forming, facultative anaerobic bacterium commonly found in poultry and eggs. It causes an infection rather than a pre-formed deadly neurotoxin.\n• Listeria monocytogenes: A non-spore-forming bacterium that can grow in cold environments (like refrigerators). It is an aerobe/facultative anaerobe commonly linked to unpasteurized dairy and deli meats.\n• Bacillus cereus: While this is a spore-forming bacterium linked to time-temperature abuse (classically found in fried rice), it is a facultative anaerobe (grows with or without oxygen) and its toxins cause vomiting or diarrhea, but are rarely lethal compared to botulinum neurotoxins.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Salmonella spp, Listeria monocytogenes, Bacillus cereus"}'::jsonb),
('mc:the following are yogurt variations or dishes with yogurt except::::hummus', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'the following are yogurt variations or dishes with yogurt except:', 'Hummus', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Hummus is a popular Middle Eastern dip or spread. It is fundamentally made from cooked, mashed chickpeas (garbanzo beans) blended with tahini (toasted ground sesame paste), olive oil, lemon juice, salt, and garlic. It does not traditionally contain any yogurt or dairy products.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Hummus\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Hummus is a popular Middle Eastern dip or spread. It is fundamentally made from cooked, mashed chickpeas (garbanzo beans) blended with tahini (toasted ground sesame paste), olive oil, lemon juice, salt, and garlic. It does not traditionally contain any yogurt or dairy products.\n\n• Kefir: A fermented milk drink very similar to a thin, pourable yogurt. It is made by inoculating cow, goat, or sheep milk with symbiotic colonies of bacteria and yeast known as \"kefir grains.\"\n• Tzatziki: A classic Greek yogurt sauce or dip. It is prepared by mixing salted strained yogurt (Greek yogurt) with grated cucumber, garlic, olive oil, and fresh herbs like dill or mint.\n• Raita: A traditional South Asian (Indian) condiment. It consists of dahi (native yogurt) mixed with raw or cooked vegetables (like cucumber, onions, or tomatoes), fruits, and toasted spices like cumin and coriander.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Raita, Kefir, Tsatziki"}'::jsonb),
('mc:the following are yogurt variations or dishes with yogurt except::::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'the following are yogurt variations or dishes with yogurt except:', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Hummus is a popular Middle Eastern dip or spread. It is fundamentally made from cooked, mashed chickpeas (garbanzo beans) blended with tahini (toasted ground sesame paste), olive oil, lemon juice, salt, and garlic. It does not traditionally contain any yogurt or dairy products.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Hummus\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Hummus is a popular Middle Eastern dip or spread. It is fundamentally made from cooked, mashed chickpeas (garbanzo beans) blended with tahini (toasted ground sesame paste), olive oil, lemon juice, salt, and garlic. It does not traditionally contain any yogurt or dairy products.\n\n• Kefir: A fermented milk drink very similar to a thin, pourable yogurt. It is made by inoculating cow, goat, or sheep milk with symbiotic colonies of bacteria and yeast known as \"kefir grains.\"\n• Tzatziki: A classic Greek yogurt sauce or dip. It is prepared by mixing salted strained yogurt (Greek yogurt) with grated cucumber, garlic, olive oil, and fresh herbs like dill or mint.\n• Raita: A traditional South Asian (Indian) condiment. It consists of dahi (native yogurt) mixed with raw or cooked vegetables (like cucumber, onions, or tomatoes), fruits, and toasted spices like cumin and coriander.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Raita, Kefir, Tsatziki"}'::jsonb),
('mc:when cost is not a problem, the best material for sinks is::::stainless steel', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'when cost is not a problem, the best material for sinks is:', 'Stainless steel', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Stainless steel is the preferred sink material when cost is not limiting because it is durable, corrosion-resistant, smooth, and easy to clean and sanitize.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Stainless steel\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Stainless steel is the preferred sink material when cost is not limiting because it is durable, corrosion-resistant, smooth, and easy to clean and sanitize.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Japanese steel, Galvanized iron, Aluminum"}'::jsonb),
