('mc:being non-flammable, asbestos is good for::::roofing material', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'being non-flammable, asbestos is good for:', 'Roofing material', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Asbestos was historically valued for non-flammability and heat resistance and was used in construction materials, but it is now recognized as a serious health hazard and should not be recommended for new applications.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Roofing material\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Asbestos was historically valued for non-flammability and heat resistance and was used in construction materials, but it is now recognized as a serious health hazard and should not be recommended for new applications.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Office furniture, Kitchen table tops, All of the above"}'::jsonb),
('mc:being non-flammable, asbestos is good for::::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'being non-flammable, asbestos is good for:', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Asbestos was historically valued for non-flammability and heat resistance and was used in construction materials, but it is now recognized as a serious health hazard and should not be recommended for new applications.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Roofing material\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Asbestos was historically valued for non-flammability and heat resistance and was used in construction materials, but it is now recognized as a serious health hazard and should not be recommended for new applications.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Office furniture, Kitchen table tops, All of the above"}'::jsonb),
('mc:bacteria found in the intestines of cattle that causes hemorrhagic colitis.:::shiga-toxin producing e. coli', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'bacteria found in the intestines of cattle that causes hemorrhagic colitis.', 'Shiga-toxin producing E. coli', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Shiga toxin-producing Escherichia coli (STEC), most notably the E. coli O157:H7 strain, naturally and asymptomatically colonizes the intestines of healthy cattle and other ruminants. When humans consume food or water contaminated with bovine feces (such as undercooked ground beef or unpasteurized raw milk), the bacteria colonize the human large intestine and release potent Shiga toxins. These toxins cause severe vascular damage to the intestinal lining, resulting in acute hemorrhagic colitis (profuse, bloody diarrhea).","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Shiga-toxin producing E. coli\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Shiga toxin-producing Escherichia coli (STEC), most notably the E. coli O157:H7 strain, naturally and asymptomatically colonizes the intestines of healthy cattle and other ruminants. When humans consume food or water contaminated with bovine feces (such as undercooked ground beef or unpasteurized raw milk), the bacteria colonize the human large intestine and release potent Shiga toxins. These toxins cause severe vascular damage to the intestinal lining, resulting in acute hemorrhagic colitis (profuse, bloody diarrhea).\n\n• Staphylococcus aureus: A major cause of food intoxication, but the bacteria reside primarily on human skin and nasal passages. It produces heat-stable enterotoxins that trigger rapid onset vomiting and abdominal cramps, not hemorrhagic colitis.\n• Clostridium perfringens: While it can be found in animal intestines and causes common foodborne diarrhea, it is associated with meat products held at improper serving temperatures, resulting in watery diarrhea rather than hemorrhagic colitis. (Note: In veterinary medicine, it is linked to Hemorrhagic Bowel Syndrome in cattle itself, but not as the zoonotic agent causing human hemorrhagic colitis).\n• Vibrio vulnificus: A marine bacterium found in warm coastal waters. It is contracted by eating raw shellfish (like oysters) or through open wound exposure to seawater, causing severe septicemia or necrotizing fasciitis, not cattle-borne colitis.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Clostridium perfringens, Staphylococcus aureus, Vibrio vulnificus"}'::jsonb),
('mc:bacteria found in the intestines of cattle that causes hemorrhagic colitis.:::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'bacteria found in the intestines of cattle that causes hemorrhagic colitis.', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Shiga toxin-producing Escherichia coli (STEC), most notably the E. coli O157:H7 strain, naturally and asymptomatically colonizes the intestines of healthy cattle and other ruminants. When humans consume food or water contaminated with bovine feces (such as undercooked ground beef or unpasteurized raw milk), the bacteria colonize the human large intestine and release potent Shiga toxins. These toxins cause severe vascular damage to the intestinal lining, resulting in acute hemorrhagic colitis (profuse, bloody diarrhea).","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Shiga-toxin producing E. coli\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Shiga toxin-producing Escherichia coli (STEC), most notably the E. coli O157:H7 strain, naturally and asymptomatically colonizes the intestines of healthy cattle and other ruminants. When humans consume food or water contaminated with bovine feces (such as undercooked ground beef or unpasteurized raw milk), the bacteria colonize the human large intestine and release potent Shiga toxins. These toxins cause severe vascular damage to the intestinal lining, resulting in acute hemorrhagic colitis (profuse, bloody diarrhea).\n\n• Staphylococcus aureus: A major cause of food intoxication, but the bacteria reside primarily on human skin and nasal passages. It produces heat-stable enterotoxins that trigger rapid onset vomiting and abdominal cramps, not hemorrhagic colitis.\n• Clostridium perfringens: While it can be found in animal intestines and causes common foodborne diarrhea, it is associated with meat products held at improper serving temperatures, resulting in watery diarrhea rather than hemorrhagic colitis. (Note: In veterinary medicine, it is linked to Hemorrhagic Bowel Syndrome in cattle itself, but not as the zoonotic agent causing human hemorrhagic colitis).\n• Vibrio vulnificus: A marine bacterium found in warm coastal waters. It is contracted by eating raw shellfish (like oysters) or through open wound exposure to seawater, causing severe septicemia or necrotizing fasciitis, not cattle-borne colitis.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Clostridium perfringens, Staphylococcus aureus, Vibrio vulnificus"}'::jsonb),
