('mc:the optimum working tº is between::::65-73ºf', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'the optimum working tº is between:', '65-73ºF', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"The optimum working temperature for productivity and human comfort in an office environment is typically between 65–73ºF (18–23ºC).","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"65-73ºF\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"The optimum working temperature for productivity and human comfort in an office environment is typically between 65–73ºF (18–23ºC).","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: 60-71ºF, 3-72ºF, 67-74ºF"}'::jsonb),
('mc:type of oven in which heat is generated from within the food:::microwave', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'type of oven in which heat is generated from within the food', 'Microwave', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Unlike traditional ovens that heat the air around the food, a microwave oven uses an electronic tube called a magnetron to produce electromagnetic waves (microwaves).","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Microwave\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Unlike traditional ovens that heat the air around the food, a microwave oven uses an electronic tube called a magnetron to produce electromagnetic waves (microwaves).\n\nThese waves pass directly into the food and are absorbed by water, fat, and sugar molecules. The microwaves cause these polar molecules to rotate rapidly billions of times per second.\n\nThis intense friction generates heat from within the food itself, which then spreads outward to cook the rest of the item. This is why microwaving is incredibly fast compared to surface-heating methods.\n\n• Convection: An oven equipped with a fan and exhaust system that actively circulates hot air around the cavity, transferring heat to the food''s surface via currents.\n• Quartz: An oven that uses quartz heating elements to emit infrared radiant heat, cooking the food from the outside in using light energy.\n• Conventional: A standard radiant oven where stationary hot air rises from heating elements at the bottom or top of the appliance, slowly warming the exterior of the food.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Conventional, Convection, Quartz"}'::jsonb),
('mc:type of oven in which heat is generated from within the food:::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'type of oven in which heat is generated from within the food', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Unlike traditional ovens that heat the air around the food, a microwave oven uses an electronic tube called a magnetron to produce electromagnetic waves (microwaves).","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Microwave\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Unlike traditional ovens that heat the air around the food, a microwave oven uses an electronic tube called a magnetron to produce electromagnetic waves (microwaves).\n\nThese waves pass directly into the food and are absorbed by water, fat, and sugar molecules. The microwaves cause these polar molecules to rotate rapidly billions of times per second.\n\nThis intense friction generates heat from within the food itself, which then spreads outward to cook the rest of the item. This is why microwaving is incredibly fast compared to surface-heating methods.\n\n• Convection: An oven equipped with a fan and exhaust system that actively circulates hot air around the cavity, transferring heat to the food''s surface via currents.\n• Quartz: An oven that uses quartz heating elements to emit infrared radiant heat, cooking the food from the outside in using light energy.\n• Conventional: A standard radiant oven where stationary hot air rises from heating elements at the bottom or top of the appliance, slowly warming the exterior of the food.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Conventional, Convection, Quartz"}'::jsonb),
('mc:the following are under the danger zone except::::80ºc', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'the following are under the danger zone except:', '80ºC', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"The food temperature danger zone is the range between 40°F and 140°F (4°C and 60°C) where harmful bacteria multiply rapidly, doubling in number in as little as 20 minutes.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"80ºC\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"The food temperature danger zone is the range between 40°F and 140°F (4°C and 60°C) where harmful bacteria multiply rapidly, doubling in number in as little as 20 minutes.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: 41ºF, 120ºF, None of the above"}'::jsonb),
