('mc:intrinsic factors that have the capacity to either stimulate or retard the growth of microorganisms in food:::ph, redox potential, water activity and antimicrobial substances', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'intrinsic factors that have the capacity to either stimulate or retard the growth of microorganisms in food', 'pH, redox potential, water activity and antimicrobial substances', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Intrinsic factors are properties inherent in the food itself.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"pH, redox potential, water activity and antimicrobial substances\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Intrinsic factors are properties inherent in the food itself.\n\nThese are:\n• pH\n• Moisture\n• Redox potential\n• Nutrient Content\n• Antimicrobial constituents\n• Biological structures","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Temperature, relative humidity, pH and water activity, Redox potential, atmosphere, relative humidity and antimicrobial substances, Relative humidity, atmosphere and temperature"}'::jsonb),
('mc:bse or mad cow disease involves::::prions', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'bse or mad cow disease involves:', 'Prions', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Bovine Spongiform Encephalopathy (BSE), commonly known as mad cow disease, is caused by an abnormal, infectious type of protein called a prion.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Prions\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Bovine Spongiform Encephalopathy (BSE), commonly known as mad cow disease, is caused by an abnormal, infectious type of protein called a prion.\n\nPrions are not alive like bacteria, viruses, or parasites; they are misfolded proteins. When they enter the body, they induce normal proteins in the brain and central nervous system to also misfold.\n\nThis causes a progressive, fatal neurological degeneration that leaves the brain looking like a sponge (spongiform). In humans, consuming meat contaminated with BSE prions can lead to Variant Creutzfeldt-Jakob Disease (vCJD), which is also fatal. Prions are extremely resilient and cannot be destroyed by normal cooking, freezing, or standard chemical disinfection.\n\n• Virus: Infectious agents made of genetic material (DNA or RNA) inside a protein coat (e.g., Rabies, Foot-and-Mouth disease). BSE does not contain any genetic material.\n• Bacteria: Single-celled living microorganisms (e.g., Salmonella, Anthrax). They can be killed with antibiotics or heat, whereas prions cannot.\n• Parasites: Organisms that live on or inside a host and feed off it (e.g., Tapeworms, Trichinella in pork).","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Virus, Bacteria, Parasites"}'::jsonb),
('mc:bse or mad cow disease involves::::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'bse or mad cow disease involves:', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Bovine Spongiform Encephalopathy (BSE), commonly known as mad cow disease, is caused by an abnormal, infectious type of protein called a prion.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Prions\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Bovine Spongiform Encephalopathy (BSE), commonly known as mad cow disease, is caused by an abnormal, infectious type of protein called a prion.\n\nPrions are not alive like bacteria, viruses, or parasites; they are misfolded proteins. When they enter the body, they induce normal proteins in the brain and central nervous system to also misfold.\n\nThis causes a progressive, fatal neurological degeneration that leaves the brain looking like a sponge (spongiform). In humans, consuming meat contaminated with BSE prions can lead to Variant Creutzfeldt-Jakob Disease (vCJD), which is also fatal. Prions are extremely resilient and cannot be destroyed by normal cooking, freezing, or standard chemical disinfection.\n\n• Virus: Infectious agents made of genetic material (DNA or RNA) inside a protein coat (e.g., Rabies, Foot-and-Mouth disease). BSE does not contain any genetic material.\n• Bacteria: Single-celled living microorganisms (e.g., Salmonella, Anthrax). They can be killed with antibiotics or heat, whereas prions cannot.\n• Parasites: Organisms that live on or inside a host and feed off it (e.g., Tapeworms, Trichinella in pork).","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Virus, Bacteria, Parasites"}'::jsonb),
('mc:sentence a. monosodium glutamate is found in seaweeds. sentence b. msg is a food seasoning that enhances the flavor of dishes.:::both sentences are correct', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'sentence a. monosodium glutamate is found in seaweeds. sentence b. msg is a food seasoning that enhances the flavor of dishes.', 'Both sentences are correct', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"MSG was discovered in 1908 by Japanese chemist Kikunae Ikeda who studied a traditional Japanese broth (dashi) made from kombu, a type of edible kelp/seaweed.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Both sentences are correct\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"MSG was discovered in 1908 by Japanese chemist Kikunae Ikeda who studied a traditional Japanese broth (dashi) made from kombu, a type of edible kelp/seaweed.\n\nHe extracted glutamic acid crystals from the seaweed broth and realized they created a savory, delicious taste he named umami.\n\nHe combined this glutamate with sodium to create monosodium glutamate (MSG) because it dissolved easily and tasted best.\n\nIt is used as a seasoning because it enhances umami and intensifies savory flavor.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Sentence A is correct, Sentence B is correct, Both sentences are incorrect"}'::jsonb),
('mc:sentence a. monosodium glutamate is found in seaweeds. sentence b. msg is a food seasoning that enhances the flavor of dishes.:::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'sentence a. monosodium glutamate is found in seaweeds. sentence b. msg is a food seasoning that enhances the flavor of dishes.', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"MSG was discovered in 1908 by Japanese chemist Kikunae Ikeda who studied a traditional Japanese broth (dashi) made from kombu, a type of edible kelp/seaweed.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Both sentences are correct\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"MSG was discovered in 1908 by Japanese chemist Kikunae Ikeda who studied a traditional Japanese broth (dashi) made from kombu, a type of edible kelp/seaweed.\n\nHe extracted glutamic acid crystals from the seaweed broth and realized they created a savory, delicious taste he named umami.\n\nHe combined this glutamate with sodium to create monosodium glutamate (MSG) because it dissolved easily and tasted best.\n\nIt is used as a seasoning because it enhances umami and intensifies savory flavor.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Sentence A is correct, Sentence B is correct, Both sentences are incorrect"}'::jsonb),
('mc:minimum internal cooking temperature of eggs:::160ºf', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'minimum internal cooking temperature of eggs', '160ºF', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Eggs: 160°F (71°C) for combined dishes like casseroles or quiches; for whole, fried, or scrambled eggs, cook until both the white and the yolk are firm.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"160ºF\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Eggs: 160°F (71°C) for combined dishes like casseroles or quiches; for whole, fried, or scrambled eggs, cook until both the white and the yolk are firm.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: 140ºF, 150ºF, 170ºF"}'::jsonb),
('mc:sentence a. spices come from different parts of the plant. sentence b. herbs that are dried are called spices.:::sentence a is correct', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'sentence a. spices come from different parts of the plant. sentence b. herbs that are dried are called spices.', 'Sentence A is correct', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Sentence A is correct because spices may come from seeds, bark, roots, fruits, flowers, or other plant parts. ","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Sentence A is correct\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Sentence A is correct because spices may come from seeds, bark, roots, fruits, flowers, or other plant parts. \n\nSentence B is incorrect because herbs and spices are classified primarily by the plant part used rather than by whether the material is dried.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Sentence B is correct, Both sentences are correct, Both sentences are incorrect"}'::jsonb),
