('mc:processed cheese is predominantly made with:::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'processed cheese is predominantly made with', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Processed cheese is predominantly made with natural cheese, typically accounting for about 50–60% of its composition).","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"None of the above\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Processed cheese is predominantly made with natural cheese, typically accounting for about 50–60% of its composition).\n\nWater, dairy solids, emulsifying salts, and sometimes vegetable oils or fats make up the remaining portion.\n\nMilk: While derived from milk originally via the natural cheese base, processed cheese is blended from finished cheese rather than fluid milk.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Milk, Vegetable oil, Water"}'::jsonb),
('mc:deterioration that is formed when unwrapped or uncovered food items are stored in low temperature.:::freezer burn', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'deterioration that is formed when unwrapped or uncovered food items are stored in low temperature.', 'freezer burn', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"When food items are left unwrapped or uncovered in a low-temperature environment (specifically a freezer), the cold, dry air continuously pulls moisture out from the surface of the food. This leads to sublimation (where ice crystals turn directly into water vapor), leaving behind dehydrated, dry, and spongy pockets on the food''s surface.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"freezer burn\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"When food items are left unwrapped or uncovered in a low-temperature environment (specifically a freezer), the cold, dry air continuously pulls moisture out from the surface of the food. This leads to sublimation (where ice crystals turn directly into water vapor), leaving behind dehydrated, dry, and spongy pockets on the food''s surface.\n\n• Case hardening: A structural defect that occurs during high-temperature drying or dehydration. If the heat is turned up too high too quickly, the outer surface of the food dries out and forms a hard, impermeable crust, trapping moisture inside and preventing proper preservation.\n• Oxidative rancidity: The chemical breakdown of fats and oils caused by exposure to oxygen, resulting in an off-odor and unpleasant flavor. While open freezing accelerates oxygen exposure, freezer burn is the direct, visible physical deterioration that forms on uncovered foods.\n• Putrefaction: The biological decomposition of proteins by anaerobic bacteria, resulting in foul odors. This happens in warm or improper storage conditions, as freezing temperatures typically halt or drastically slow down putrefactive bacterial growth.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: oxidative rancidity, case hardening, Putrefaction"}'::jsonb),
('mc:deterioration that is formed when unwrapped or uncovered food items are stored in low temperature.:::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'deterioration that is formed when unwrapped or uncovered food items are stored in low temperature.', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"When food items are left unwrapped or uncovered in a low-temperature environment (specifically a freezer), the cold, dry air continuously pulls moisture out from the surface of the food. This leads to sublimation (where ice crystals turn directly into water vapor), leaving behind dehydrated, dry, and spongy pockets on the food''s surface.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"freezer burn\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"When food items are left unwrapped or uncovered in a low-temperature environment (specifically a freezer), the cold, dry air continuously pulls moisture out from the surface of the food. This leads to sublimation (where ice crystals turn directly into water vapor), leaving behind dehydrated, dry, and spongy pockets on the food''s surface.\n\n• Case hardening: A structural defect that occurs during high-temperature drying or dehydration. If the heat is turned up too high too quickly, the outer surface of the food dries out and forms a hard, impermeable crust, trapping moisture inside and preventing proper preservation.\n• Oxidative rancidity: The chemical breakdown of fats and oils caused by exposure to oxygen, resulting in an off-odor and unpleasant flavor. While open freezing accelerates oxygen exposure, freezer burn is the direct, visible physical deterioration that forms on uncovered foods.\n• Putrefaction: The biological decomposition of proteins by anaerobic bacteria, resulting in foul odors. This happens in warm or improper storage conditions, as freezing temperatures typically halt or drastically slow down putrefactive bacterial growth.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: oxidative rancidity, case hardening, Putrefaction"}'::jsonb),
('mc:certification that a food safety management system is in place:::iso 22000', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'certification that a food safety management system is in place', 'ISO 22000', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"ISO 22000 is the correct answer because it specifically defines the international requirements for a Food Safety Management System (FSMS). ","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"ISO 22000\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"ISO 22000 is the correct answer because it specifically defines the international requirements for a Food Safety Management System (FSMS). \n\n• Good Manufacturing Practices (GMP): Operational guidelines and baseline conditions for safe food processing facilities, rather than an overarching management system certification.\n• Good Agricultural Practices (GAP): Principles for safe and sustainable farming practices at the primary production level.\n• CODEX Alimentarius: A collection of international standards, guidelines, and codes of practice adopted by the FAO/WHO, rather than a certifiable management system itself.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Good Manufacturing practices, Good Agricultural practices, CODEX Alimentarius"}'::jsonb),
('mc:certification that a food safety management system is in place:::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'certification that a food safety management system is in place', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"ISO 22000 is the correct answer because it specifically defines the international requirements for a Food Safety Management System (FSMS). ","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"ISO 22000\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"ISO 22000 is the correct answer because it specifically defines the international requirements for a Food Safety Management System (FSMS). \n\n• Good Manufacturing Practices (GMP): Operational guidelines and baseline conditions for safe food processing facilities, rather than an overarching management system certification.\n• Good Agricultural Practices (GAP): Principles for safe and sustainable farming practices at the primary production level.\n• CODEX Alimentarius: A collection of international standards, guidelines, and codes of practice adopted by the FAO/WHO, rather than a certifiable management system itself.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Good Manufacturing practices, Good Agricultural practices, CODEX Alimentarius"}'::jsonb),
('mc:processed cheese is predominantly made with:::none of the above', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'processed cheese is predominantly made with', 'None of the above', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Processed cheese is predominantly made with natural cheese, typically accounting for about 50–60% of its composition).","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"None of the above\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Processed cheese is predominantly made with natural cheese, typically accounting for about 50–60% of its composition).\n\nWater, dairy solids, emulsifying salts, and sometimes vegetable oils or fats make up the remaining portion.\n\nMilk: While derived from milk originally via the natural cheese base, processed cheese is blended from finished cheese rather than fluid milk.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Milk, Vegetable oil, Water"}'::jsonb),
('mc:precipitating agent of pectin causing it to form network of the jelly:::sugar', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'precipitating agent of pectin causing it to form network of the jelly', 'Sugar', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Sugar acts as the precipitating agent that draws water away from the pectin molecules, allowing them to bond and form the network of the gel.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Sugar\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Sugar acts as the precipitating agent that draws water away from the pectin molecules, allowing them to bond and form the network of the gel.\n\nIt attracts and holds water, breaking the pectin-water equilibrium and forcing the pectin chains to link together.\n\n• Acid: Neutralizes the negative charges on pectin molecules so they do not repel each other, assisting the set.\n• High heat activates the gelling properties of pectin so it can form a molecular web that traps the remaining liquid and thickens the jam. The ideal setting temperature for homemade jam is  around 220°F (105°C) at sea level, but may vary depending on type of fruit used.\n• Water naturally exists inside fruit cells and acts as the liquid medium where sugar and natural pectin dissolve.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Acid, Heat, water"}'::jsonb),
('mc:precipitating agent of pectin causing it to form network of the jelly:::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'precipitating agent of pectin causing it to form network of the jelly', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Sugar acts as the precipitating agent that draws water away from the pectin molecules, allowing them to bond and form the network of the gel.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Sugar\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Sugar acts as the precipitating agent that draws water away from the pectin molecules, allowing them to bond and form the network of the gel.\n\nIt attracts and holds water, breaking the pectin-water equilibrium and forcing the pectin chains to link together.\n\n• Acid: Neutralizes the negative charges on pectin molecules so they do not repel each other, assisting the set.\n• High heat activates the gelling properties of pectin so it can form a molecular web that traps the remaining liquid and thickens the jam. The ideal setting temperature for homemade jam is  around 220°F (105°C) at sea level, but may vary depending on type of fruit used.\n• Water naturally exists inside fruit cells and acts as the liquid medium where sugar and natural pectin dissolve.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Acid, Heat, water"}'::jsonb),
('mc:the process of jelly making involves boiling fruit to::::extract pectin, obtain maximum juice and extract flavor', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'the process of jelly making involves boiling fruit to:', 'extract pectin, obtain maximum juice and extract flavor', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Boiling fruit in jelly making helps extract pectin and juice and develop the characteristic flavor of the finished jelly.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"extract pectin, obtain maximum juice and extract flavor\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Boiling fruit in jelly making helps extract pectin and juice and develop the characteristic flavor of the finished jelly.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: hydrolyze pectic acid, dissolve the fruit pulp and extract color, dissolve the pectic acid, extract the sugar and flavor, volatize the aroma, extract pulp and hydrolyze the protopectin"}'::jsonb),
