('mc:minimum internal cooking temperature of eggs:::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'minimum internal cooking temperature of eggs', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Eggs: 160°F (71°C) for combined dishes like casseroles or quiches; for whole, fried, or scrambled eggs, cook until both the white and the yolk are firm.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"160ºF\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Eggs: 160°F (71°C) for combined dishes like casseroles or quiches; for whole, fried, or scrambled eggs, cook until both the white and the yolk are firm.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: 140ºF, 150ºF, 170ºF"}'::jsonb),
('mc:the following are all vegetable dishes except::::none of the above', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'the following are all vegetable dishes except:', 'None of the above', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Laswa, bulanglang, and utan are vegetable dishes.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"None of the above\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Laswa, bulanglang, and utan are vegetable dishes.\n\n• Laswa: A famous, clear-broth vegetable soup native to the Western Visayas (Iloilo). It is packed with local vegetables like okra, saluyot, squash, and eggplant, and seasoned simply with salt or fish sauce.\n• Utan: The native Visayan and Mindanao term for a clear, healthy vegetable broth (most commonly Utan Bisaya or Utan Kamunggay), featuring leafy greens, squash, and root vegetables.\n• Bulanglang: A traditional Southern Tagalog (specifically Batangas) vegetable stew made by boiling a medley of local crops like calabaza squash, okra, sitaw, and moringa leaves in rice-washing water seasoned with bagoong.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Laswa, Bulanglang, Utan"}'::jsonb),
('mc:the following are all vegetable dishes except::::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'the following are all vegetable dishes except:', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Laswa, bulanglang, and utan are vegetable dishes.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"None of the above\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Laswa, bulanglang, and utan are vegetable dishes.\n\n• Laswa: A famous, clear-broth vegetable soup native to the Western Visayas (Iloilo). It is packed with local vegetables like okra, saluyot, squash, and eggplant, and seasoned simply with salt or fish sauce.\n• Utan: The native Visayan and Mindanao term for a clear, healthy vegetable broth (most commonly Utan Bisaya or Utan Kamunggay), featuring leafy greens, squash, and root vegetables.\n• Bulanglang: A traditional Southern Tagalog (specifically Batangas) vegetable stew made by boiling a medley of local crops like calabaza squash, okra, sitaw, and moringa leaves in rice-washing water seasoned with bagoong.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Laswa, Bulanglang, Utan"}'::jsonb),
('mc:the following are all noodle dishes, except::::picadillo', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'the following are all noodle dishes, except:', 'Picadillo', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Picadillo is a savory, traditional ground meat dish found in Spain and Latin America. In the Philippines, Picadillo is known locally as giniling, it is often cooked as a hearty soup or dry sauté with diced potatoes, carrots, and green peas and served over steamed white rice or as arroz a la cubana alongside a fried egg.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Picadillo\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Picadillo is a savory, traditional ground meat dish found in Spain and Latin America. In the Philippines, Picadillo is known locally as giniling, it is often cooked as a hearty soup or dry sauté with diced potatoes, carrots, and green peas and served over steamed white rice or as arroz a la cubana alongside a fried egg.\n\n• La Paz Batchoy: A legendary Filipino noodle soup that originated in the district of La Paz, Iloilo City. It is made with fresh egg noodles (miki), a rich pork and shrimp paste (ginamos) broth, sliced pork innards, and a heavy topping of crushed pork cracklings (chicharon).\n• Bami: Also known as Pancit Bami, this is a classic Visayan (particularly Cebuano) stir-fried noodle dish. It features a unique combination of two types of noodles tossed together: thick egg noodles (miki) and translucent mung bean thread noodles (sotanghon).\n• Laksa: A widely popular Southeast Asian spicy noodle soup predominant in Peranakan cuisine (Malaysia, Singapore, and Indonesia). It features thick rice noodles submerged in either a rich, fiery coconut curry broth or a sour, tamarind-based fish broth.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Bami, La Paz Batchoy, Laksa"}'::jsonb),
('mc:the following are all noodle dishes, except::::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'the following are all noodle dishes, except:', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Picadillo is a savory, traditional ground meat dish found in Spain and Latin America. In the Philippines, Picadillo is known locally as giniling, it is often cooked as a hearty soup or dry sauté with diced potatoes, carrots, and green peas and served over steamed white rice or as arroz a la cubana alongside a fried egg.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Picadillo\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Picadillo is a savory, traditional ground meat dish found in Spain and Latin America. In the Philippines, Picadillo is known locally as giniling, it is often cooked as a hearty soup or dry sauté with diced potatoes, carrots, and green peas and served over steamed white rice or as arroz a la cubana alongside a fried egg.\n\n• La Paz Batchoy: A legendary Filipino noodle soup that originated in the district of La Paz, Iloilo City. It is made with fresh egg noodles (miki), a rich pork and shrimp paste (ginamos) broth, sliced pork innards, and a heavy topping of crushed pork cracklings (chicharon).\n• Bami: Also known as Pancit Bami, this is a classic Visayan (particularly Cebuano) stir-fried noodle dish. It features a unique combination of two types of noodles tossed together: thick egg noodles (miki) and translucent mung bean thread noodles (sotanghon).\n• Laksa: A widely popular Southeast Asian spicy noodle soup predominant in Peranakan cuisine (Malaysia, Singapore, and Indonesia). It features thick rice noodles submerged in either a rich, fiery coconut curry broth or a sour, tamarind-based fish broth.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Bami, La Paz Batchoy, Laksa"}'::jsonb),
('mc:a dish of rice, ground pork and bananas.:::arroz a la cubana', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'a dish of rice, ground pork and bananas.', 'Arroz a la Cubana', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Arroz a la Cubana (literally \"Cuban-style rice\") is a classic comfort dish that is highly popular in Filipino cuisine. It traditionally consists of a visual layout featuring a mound of white rice, a savory ground meat hash (picadillo/giniling usually made with ground pork or beef sautéed with raisins and peas), fried saba bananas (plantains), and a fried egg.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Arroz a la Cubana\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Arroz a la Cubana (literally \"Cuban-style rice\") is a classic comfort dish that is highly popular in Filipino cuisine. It traditionally consists of a visual layout featuring a mound of white rice, a savory ground meat hash (picadillo/giniling usually made with ground pork or beef sautéed with raisins and peas), fried saba bananas (plantains), and a fried egg.\n\n• Paella: A famous Spanish rice dish cooked in a wide, shallow pan, typically flavored with saffron and loaded with seafood, chicken, and vegetables.\n• Torta: In Filipino cuisine, a torta refers to an omelet or pan-fried patty (such as tortang talong made with eggplant, or tortang giniling made with ground meat mixed into beaten eggs). It is not served as a specific configuration of rice and bananas.\n• Arroz Valenciana: A traditional Filipino festive rice dish heavily influenced by Spanish paella. It features glutinous (sticky) rice cooked with coconut milk, turmeric (or annatto oil), chicken, chorizo, and boiled eggs, but it does not feature ground meat or fried bananas.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Arroz Valenciana, Torta, Paella"}'::jsonb),
('mc:a dish of rice, ground pork and bananas.:::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'a dish of rice, ground pork and bananas.', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Arroz a la Cubana (literally \"Cuban-style rice\") is a classic comfort dish that is highly popular in Filipino cuisine. It traditionally consists of a visual layout featuring a mound of white rice, a savory ground meat hash (picadillo/giniling usually made with ground pork or beef sautéed with raisins and peas), fried saba bananas (plantains), and a fried egg.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Arroz a la Cubana\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Arroz a la Cubana (literally \"Cuban-style rice\") is a classic comfort dish that is highly popular in Filipino cuisine. It traditionally consists of a visual layout featuring a mound of white rice, a savory ground meat hash (picadillo/giniling usually made with ground pork or beef sautéed with raisins and peas), fried saba bananas (plantains), and a fried egg.\n\n• Paella: A famous Spanish rice dish cooked in a wide, shallow pan, typically flavored with saffron and loaded with seafood, chicken, and vegetables.\n• Torta: In Filipino cuisine, a torta refers to an omelet or pan-fried patty (such as tortang talong made with eggplant, or tortang giniling made with ground meat mixed into beaten eggs). It is not served as a specific configuration of rice and bananas.\n• Arroz Valenciana: A traditional Filipino festive rice dish heavily influenced by Spanish paella. It features glutinous (sticky) rice cooked with coconut milk, turmeric (or annatto oil), chicken, chorizo, and boiled eggs, but it does not feature ground meat or fried bananas.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Arroz Valenciana, Torta, Paella"}'::jsonb),
('mc:filipino dish traditionally made of chicken, rice washing, green papaya, pepper leaves, and ginger.:::tinola', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'filipino dish traditionally made of chicken, rice washing, green papaya, pepper leaves, and ginger.', 'Tinola', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Tinola (specifically Tinolang Manok) is a traditional Filipino soup that serves as the quintessential comfort food. It features chicken pieces simmered in rice washing (the water saved from rinsing raw rice, which acts as a starchy, flavorful broth base) heavily flavored with crushed ginger, garlic, and onions. It is traditionally garnished with wedged green papaya (or chayote) and fresh chili pepper leaves (siling labuyo leaves).","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Tinola\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Tinola (specifically Tinolang Manok) is a traditional Filipino soup that serves as the quintessential comfort food. It features chicken pieces simmered in rice washing (the water saved from rinsing raw rice, which acts as a starchy, flavorful broth base) heavily flavored with crushed ginger, garlic, and onions. It is traditionally garnished with wedged green papaya (or chayote) and fresh chili pepper leaves (siling labuyo leaves).\n\n• Suam: A traditional Kapampangan soup (like Suam na Mais) made with shredded sweet corn, meat, and spinach or chili leaves. While it uses ginger and rice washing, its main structural component is corn rather than a clear chicken broth with papaya.\n• Pesa: A ginger-based fish or chicken stew (like Pesang Dalag) that is cooked with cabbage, potatoes, pechay, and whole black peppercorns. It is traditionally served alongside a side dipping sauce made of fermented soybean cake (miso).\n• Nilaga: A broad term for a boiled Filipino meat soup (usually beef or pork). While it features a clear broth, it is seasoned predominantly with whole black peppercorns and onions rather than a heavy ginger base, and it uses vegetables like cabbage, pechay, potatoes, and saba bananas.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Suam, Pesa, Nilaga"}'::jsonb),
('mc:filipino dish traditionally made of chicken, rice washing, green papaya, pepper leaves, and ginger.:::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'filipino dish traditionally made of chicken, rice washing, green papaya, pepper leaves, and ginger.', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Tinola (specifically Tinolang Manok) is a traditional Filipino soup that serves as the quintessential comfort food. It features chicken pieces simmered in rice washing (the water saved from rinsing raw rice, which acts as a starchy, flavorful broth base) heavily flavored with crushed ginger, garlic, and onions. It is traditionally garnished with wedged green papaya (or chayote) and fresh chili pepper leaves (siling labuyo leaves).","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Tinola\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Tinola (specifically Tinolang Manok) is a traditional Filipino soup that serves as the quintessential comfort food. It features chicken pieces simmered in rice washing (the water saved from rinsing raw rice, which acts as a starchy, flavorful broth base) heavily flavored with crushed ginger, garlic, and onions. It is traditionally garnished with wedged green papaya (or chayote) and fresh chili pepper leaves (siling labuyo leaves).\n\n• Suam: A traditional Kapampangan soup (like Suam na Mais) made with shredded sweet corn, meat, and spinach or chili leaves. While it uses ginger and rice washing, its main structural component is corn rather than a clear chicken broth with papaya.\n• Pesa: A ginger-based fish or chicken stew (like Pesang Dalag) that is cooked with cabbage, potatoes, pechay, and whole black peppercorns. It is traditionally served alongside a side dipping sauce made of fermented soybean cake (miso).\n• Nilaga: A broad term for a boiled Filipino meat soup (usually beef or pork). While it features a clear broth, it is seasoned predominantly with whole black peppercorns and onions rather than a heavy ginger base, and it uses vegetables like cabbage, pechay, potatoes, and saba bananas.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Suam, Pesa, Nilaga"}'::jsonb),
('mc:management by objective implies the need to have:::clearly defined key result areas and time', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'management by objective implies the need to have', 'Clearly defined key result areas and time', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Management by Objectives (MBO) is a strategic management model that aims to improve organizational performance by aligning employee goals with the overall objectives of the company. It fundamentally relies on setting specific, measurable targets (Key Result Areas) within a designated timeframe so that progress can be tracked objectively.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Clearly defined key result areas and time\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Management by Objectives (MBO) is a strategic management model that aims to improve organizational performance by aligning employee goals with the overall objectives of the company. It fundamentally relies on setting specific, measurable targets (Key Result Areas) within a designated timeframe so that progress can be tracked objectively.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: A good manager, Clear organizational chart, Adequate staff"}'::jsonb),
('mc:management by objective implies the need to have:::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'management by objective implies the need to have', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Management by Objectives (MBO) is a strategic management model that aims to improve organizational performance by aligning employee goals with the overall objectives of the company. It fundamentally relies on setting specific, measurable targets (Key Result Areas) within a designated timeframe so that progress can be tracked objectively.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Clearly defined key result areas and time\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Management by Objectives (MBO) is a strategic management model that aims to improve organizational performance by aligning employee goals with the overall objectives of the company. It fundamentally relies on setting specific, measurable targets (Key Result Areas) within a designated timeframe so that progress can be tracked objectively.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: A good manager, Clear organizational chart, Adequate staff"}'::jsonb),
('mc:tsokolate espeso refers to::::thick chocolate drink', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'tsokolate espeso refers to:', 'thick chocolate drink', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"In Filipino culinary tradition (heavily influenced by Spanish terminology), the word espeso translates to \"thick\" or \"dense.\" Therefore, Tsokolate Espeso refers to a rich, velvety, and thick hot chocolate beverage traditionally prepared by melting pure cacao tablets (tabliya) in a special copper pitcher called a tsokolatera and frothing it vigorously with a wooden whisk called a batidor.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"thick chocolate drink\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"In Filipino culinary tradition (heavily influenced by Spanish terminology), the word espeso translates to \"thick\" or \"dense.\" Therefore, Tsokolate Espeso refers to a rich, velvety, and thick hot chocolate beverage traditionally prepared by melting pure cacao tablets (tabliya) in a special copper pitcher called a tsokolatera and frothing it vigorously with a wooden whisk called a batidor.\n\n• Café Mocha / Mochaccino: These are espresso-based coffee drinks that combine milk and chocolate syrup or cocoa powder. They are not purely cacao-based.\n• Thin chocolate drink: A thin, lighter version of traditional hot chocolate is instead called Tsokolate Ahon (or tsokolate aguado, meaning watery).","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Café Mocha, Mochaccino, thin chocolate drink"}'::jsonb),
('mc:porto is a::::wine', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'porto is a:', 'Wine', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Vinho do Porto (commonly known simply as Porto or Port wine) is a Portuguese fortified wine produced exclusively in the Douro Valley in the northern provinces of Portugal.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Wine\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Vinho do Porto (commonly known simply as Porto or Port wine) is a Portuguese fortified wine produced exclusively in the Douro Valley in the northern provinces of Portugal.\n\nIt is called a fortified wine because a neutral grape spirit (known as aguardente, similar to brandy) is added during the fermentation process. This stops fermentation early, leaving residual sugar in the wine and boosting its alcohol content—usually to around 19% to 22% ABV.\n\nIt is typically a sweet, rich red wine served as a digestif or dessert wine, though it also comes in dry, semi-dry, and white varieties. It gets its name from the coastal city of Porto, where the wine is aged and shipped globally.\n\n• Whiskey: A distilled alcoholic beverage made from fermented grain mash (such as barley, corn, rye, or wheat).\n• Beer: An alcoholic beverage produced by brewing and fermenting starches, mainly derived from cereal grains—most commonly malted barley, though wheat, maize (corn), rice, and oats are also used.\n• Brandy: A spirit produced by distilling wine or fermented fruit mash. While brandy (specifically a clear grape spirit) is added to Porto to stop its fermentation, Porto itself remains classified as a fortified wine rather than a distilled spirit like brandy.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Beer, Whiskey, Brandy"}'::jsonb),
('mc:porto is a::::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'porto is a:', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Vinho do Porto (commonly known simply as Porto or Port wine) is a Portuguese fortified wine produced exclusively in the Douro Valley in the northern provinces of Portugal.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Wine\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Vinho do Porto (commonly known simply as Porto or Port wine) is a Portuguese fortified wine produced exclusively in the Douro Valley in the northern provinces of Portugal.\n\nIt is called a fortified wine because a neutral grape spirit (known as aguardente, similar to brandy) is added during the fermentation process. This stops fermentation early, leaving residual sugar in the wine and boosting its alcohol content—usually to around 19% to 22% ABV.\n\nIt is typically a sweet, rich red wine served as a digestif or dessert wine, though it also comes in dry, semi-dry, and white varieties. It gets its name from the coastal city of Porto, where the wine is aged and shipped globally.\n\n• Whiskey: A distilled alcoholic beverage made from fermented grain mash (such as barley, corn, rye, or wheat).\n• Beer: An alcoholic beverage produced by brewing and fermenting starches, mainly derived from cereal grains—most commonly malted barley, though wheat, maize (corn), rice, and oats are also used.\n• Brandy: A spirit produced by distilling wine or fermented fruit mash. While brandy (specifically a clear grape spirit) is added to Porto to stop its fermentation, Porto itself remains classified as a fortified wine rather than a distilled spirit like brandy.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Beer, Whiskey, Brandy"}'::jsonb),
('mc:the following are liqueurs except::::grenadine', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'the following are liqueurs except:', 'Grenadine', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Kahlúa, crème de menthe, and Baileys are liqueurs. ","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Grenadine\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Kahlúa, crème de menthe, and Baileys are liqueurs. \n\nGrenadine is a sweet syrup used as a cocktail ingredient and is not itself a liqueur.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Kahlua, Crème de Menthe, Baileys"}'::jsonb),
('mc:the following are liqueurs except::::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'the following are liqueurs except:', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Kahlúa, crème de menthe, and Baileys are liqueurs. ","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Grenadine\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Kahlúa, crème de menthe, and Baileys are liqueurs. \n\nGrenadine is a sweet syrup used as a cocktail ingredient and is not itself a liqueur.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Kahlua, Crème de Menthe, Baileys"}'::jsonb),
('mc:a flapjack refers to::::pancake', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'a flapjack refers to:', 'Pancake', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"\"Flapjack\" is a traditional, centuries-old colloquial term used primarily in North America to refer to a pancake or griddle cake.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Pancake\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"\"Flapjack\" is a traditional, centuries-old colloquial term used primarily in North America to refer to a pancake or griddle cake.\n\n• Hash brown: Shredded or finely chopped potatoes that are pan-fried until golden brown.\n• Shoe string potatoes: Extremely thin, finely cut strips of potato that are deep-fried until very crisp (similar to thin French fries).\n• Popcorn: Puffed, heated corn kernels.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: shoe string potatoes, popcorn, hashbrown"}'::jsonb),
('mc:a flapjack refers to::::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'a flapjack refers to:', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"\"Flapjack\" is a traditional, centuries-old colloquial term used primarily in North America to refer to a pancake or griddle cake.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Pancake\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"\"Flapjack\" is a traditional, centuries-old colloquial term used primarily in North America to refer to a pancake or griddle cake.\n\n• Hash brown: Shredded or finely chopped potatoes that are pan-fried until golden brown.\n• Shoe string potatoes: Extremely thin, finely cut strips of potato that are deep-fried until very crisp (similar to thin French fries).\n• Popcorn: Puffed, heated corn kernels.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: shoe string potatoes, popcorn, hashbrown"}'::jsonb),
('mc:tsokolate espeso refers to::::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'tsokolate espeso refers to:', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"In Filipino culinary tradition (heavily influenced by Spanish terminology), the word espeso translates to \"thick\" or \"dense.\" Therefore, Tsokolate Espeso refers to a rich, velvety, and thick hot chocolate beverage traditionally prepared by melting pure cacao tablets (tabliya) in a special copper pitcher called a tsokolatera and frothing it vigorously with a wooden whisk called a batidor.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"thick chocolate drink\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"In Filipino culinary tradition (heavily influenced by Spanish terminology), the word espeso translates to \"thick\" or \"dense.\" Therefore, Tsokolate Espeso refers to a rich, velvety, and thick hot chocolate beverage traditionally prepared by melting pure cacao tablets (tabliya) in a special copper pitcher called a tsokolatera and frothing it vigorously with a wooden whisk called a batidor.\n\n• Café Mocha / Mochaccino: These are espresso-based coffee drinks that combine milk and chocolate syrup or cocoa powder. They are not purely cacao-based.\n• Thin chocolate drink: A thin, lighter version of traditional hot chocolate is instead called Tsokolate Ahon (or tsokolate aguado, meaning watery).","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Café Mocha, Mochaccino, thin chocolate drink"}'::jsonb),
('mc:churros is more like which filipino fritter:::bitso-bitso', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'churros is more like which filipino fritter', 'Bitso-bitso', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Churros are closest in form to bitso-bitso, a fried Filipino dough snack, rather than vegetable fritters or toasted bread products.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Bitso-bitso\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Churros are closest in form to bitso-bitso, a fried Filipino dough snack, rather than vegetable fritters or toasted bread products.\n\n Classic Filipino bitso-bitso is made by twisting or shaping yeast- or flour-based dough into sticks, deep-frying them until golden and crisp, and then dusting them with white sugar or coating them in a caramel glaze. It mirrors the exact preparation mechanism and eating experience of a Spanish churro.\n\n• Biscocho: A twice-baked, crunchy Filipino bread topper (similar to biscotti or melba toast) that is brushed with butter and sugar. It is baked dry until crisp, not deep-fried.\n• Gurgurya: A small, hard, shell-like Filipino pastry or cookie that is deep-fried and then coated in a sugary glaze. While fried, it has a dense, hard cookie crunch rather than the soft-on-the-inside, crisp-on-the-outside structural profile of a churro.\n• Okoy: A distinctly savory Filipino street food fritter made of shredded vegetables, bean sprouts, and small whole shrimp tossed in an egg-and-flour batter and fried flat.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Okoy, Gurgurya, Biscocho"}'::jsonb),
('mc:churros is more like which filipino fritter:::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'churros is more like which filipino fritter', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Churros are closest in form to bitso-bitso, a fried Filipino dough snack, rather than vegetable fritters or toasted bread products.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Bitso-bitso\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Churros are closest in form to bitso-bitso, a fried Filipino dough snack, rather than vegetable fritters or toasted bread products.\n\n Classic Filipino bitso-bitso is made by twisting or shaping yeast- or flour-based dough into sticks, deep-frying them until golden and crisp, and then dusting them with white sugar or coating them in a caramel glaze. It mirrors the exact preparation mechanism and eating experience of a Spanish churro.\n\n• Biscocho: A twice-baked, crunchy Filipino bread topper (similar to biscotti or melba toast) that is brushed with butter and sugar. It is baked dry until crisp, not deep-fried.\n• Gurgurya: A small, hard, shell-like Filipino pastry or cookie that is deep-fried and then coated in a sugary glaze. While fried, it has a dense, hard cookie crunch rather than the soft-on-the-inside, crisp-on-the-outside structural profile of a churro.\n• Okoy: A distinctly savory Filipino street food fritter made of shredded vegetables, bean sprouts, and small whole shrimp tossed in an egg-and-flour batter and fried flat.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Okoy, Gurgurya, Biscocho"}'::jsonb),
('mc:open market buying means:::after a canvass, purchases are made from the vendor who offers the best terms', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'open market buying means', 'After a canvass, purchases are made from the vendor who offers the best terms', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Open market buying is a flexible procurement method commonly used in food service and small businesses. ","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"After a canvass, purchases are made from the vendor who offers the best terms\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Open market buying is a flexible procurement method commonly used in food service and small businesses. \n\nInstead of locking into a long-term contract with a single supplier, the buyer \"canvasses\" (requests price quotes from) multiple vendors and places the order with whoever provides the best price, quality, and delivery terms for that specific purchase.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: The buyer has to go to the market, Purchasing through a middle man, Purchasing from one source only"}'::jsonb)
ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO public.ai_explanation_cache (id, question_hash, front_text, back_text, options, explanation, breakdown, created_at, question_text, user_answer, is_correct, ai_explanation) VALUES
('mc:open market buying means:::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'open market buying means', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Open market buying is a flexible procurement method commonly used in food service and small businesses. ","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"After a canvass, purchases are made from the vendor who offers the best terms\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Open market buying is a flexible procurement method commonly used in food service and small businesses. \n\nInstead of locking into a long-term contract with a single supplier, the buyer \"canvasses\" (requests price quotes from) multiple vendors and places the order with whoever provides the best price, quality, and delivery terms for that specific purchase.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: The buyer has to go to the market, Purchasing through a middle man, Purchasing from one source only"}'::jsonb),
('mc:pertains to a disciplined, data-driven approach and methodology for eliminating defects in any process from manufacturing to transactional and from product to service.:::six sigma', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'pertains to a disciplined, data-driven approach and methodology for eliminating defects in any process from manufacturing to transactional and from product to service.', 'Six sigma', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Six Sigma is a disciplined, data-driven method for eliminating defects and reducing variation in manufacturing, service, and transactional processes.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Six sigma\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Six Sigma is a disciplined, data-driven method for eliminating defects and reducing variation in manufacturing, service, and transactional processes.\n\nHACCP (Hazard Analysis Critical Control Point) focuses specifically on food safety risk management.\n\nISO 22000 is an international standard for food safety management systems.\n\nTQM (Total Quality Management) is a broader organization-wide management approach to long-term success through customer satisfaction, rather than a specific statistical defect-reduction methodology like Six Sigma.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: HACCP, ISO 22000, TQM"}'::jsonb),
('mc:pertains to a disciplined, data-driven approach and methodology for eliminating defects in any process from manufacturing to transactional and from product to service.:::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'pertains to a disciplined, data-driven approach and methodology for eliminating defects in any process from manufacturing to transactional and from product to service.', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Six Sigma is a disciplined, data-driven method for eliminating defects and reducing variation in manufacturing, service, and transactional processes.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Six sigma\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Six Sigma is a disciplined, data-driven method for eliminating defects and reducing variation in manufacturing, service, and transactional processes.\n\nHACCP (Hazard Analysis Critical Control Point) focuses specifically on food safety risk management.\n\nISO 22000 is an international standard for food safety management systems.\n\nTQM (Total Quality Management) is a broader organization-wide management approach to long-term success through customer satisfaction, rather than a specific statistical defect-reduction methodology like Six Sigma.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: HACCP, ISO 22000, TQM"}'::jsonb),
('mc:function of management involved in the development of formal structure through which work is defined, divided and coordinated to achieve goals.:::organizing', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'function of management involved in the development of formal structure through which work is defined, divided and coordinated to achieve goals.', 'Organizing', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"In management science (including Henri Fayol''s and Luther Gulick''s classic management frameworks), organizing is the process of building the formal structural framework of an organization.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Organizing\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"In management science (including Henri Fayol''s and Luther Gulick''s classic management frameworks), organizing is the process of building the formal structural framework of an organization.\n\nIt specifically involves arranging resources, defining roles, dividing tasks or labor into manageable departments, and establishing a hierarchy or chain of command so that workflows can be successfully coordinated to achieve organizational objectives.\n\n• Planning: The foundational function that involves setting goals, projecting future scenarios, and mapping out the specific courses of action required to achieve them. It happens before structure is built.\n• Coordinating: The function of harmonizing and unifying separate team efforts and workflows to ensure they run smoothly together. While related to structure, it is a component of directing or the output of a well-organized system rather than the function that develops the formal structural framework.\n• Staffing: The process of recruiting, selecting, hiring, training, and maintaining the human resources needed to fill the positions established by the organizational structure.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Coordinating, Planning, Staffing"}'::jsonb),
('mc:function of management involved in the development of formal structure through which work is defined, divided and coordinated to achieve goals.:::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'function of management involved in the development of formal structure through which work is defined, divided and coordinated to achieve goals.', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"In management science (including Henri Fayol''s and Luther Gulick''s classic management frameworks), organizing is the process of building the formal structural framework of an organization.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Organizing\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"In management science (including Henri Fayol''s and Luther Gulick''s classic management frameworks), organizing is the process of building the formal structural framework of an organization.\n\nIt specifically involves arranging resources, defining roles, dividing tasks or labor into manageable departments, and establishing a hierarchy or chain of command so that workflows can be successfully coordinated to achieve organizational objectives.\n\n• Planning: The foundational function that involves setting goals, projecting future scenarios, and mapping out the specific courses of action required to achieve them. It happens before structure is built.\n• Coordinating: The function of harmonizing and unifying separate team efforts and workflows to ensure they run smoothly together. While related to structure, it is a component of directing or the output of a well-organized system rather than the function that develops the formal structural framework.\n• Staffing: The process of recruiting, selecting, hiring, training, and maintaining the human resources needed to fill the positions established by the organizational structure.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Coordinating, Planning, Staffing"}'::jsonb),
('mc:the aim of work design is to::::increase productivity and employee satisfaction', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'the aim of work design is to:', 'increase productivity and employee satisfaction', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Work design aims to develop jobs and methods that improve productivity and employee satisfaction while supporting efficient, safe work.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"increase productivity and employee satisfaction\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Work design aims to develop jobs and methods that improve productivity and employee satisfaction while supporting efficient, safe work.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: provide efficient and effective work methods, provide safe and healthy work environment, design a staff of fit people"}'::jsonb),
('mc:sentence a. crystals are formed from concentrated sugar solutions. sentence b. crystallization is inversely related to the ease of solubility.:::both sentences are correct', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'sentence a. crystals are formed from concentrated sugar solutions. sentence b. crystallization is inversely related to the ease of solubility.', 'Both sentences are correct', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Sentence A is correct because crystallization occurs when a supersaturated or concentrated sugar solution allows dissolved sugar molecules to arrange into crystals. ","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Both sentences are correct\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Sentence A is correct because crystallization occurs when a supersaturated or concentrated sugar solution allows dissolved sugar molecules to arrange into crystals. \n\nSentence B is also correct: substances that are less soluble crystallize more readily as the solution becomes supersaturated.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Sentence A is correct, Sentence B is correct, Bothsentences are incorrect"}'::jsonb),
('mc:sentence a. crystals are formed from concentrated sugar solutions. sentence b. crystallization is inversely related to the ease of solubility.:::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'sentence a. crystals are formed from concentrated sugar solutions. sentence b. crystallization is inversely related to the ease of solubility.', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Sentence A is correct because crystallization occurs when a supersaturated or concentrated sugar solution allows dissolved sugar molecules to arrange into crystals. ","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Both sentences are correct\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Sentence A is correct because crystallization occurs when a supersaturated or concentrated sugar solution allows dissolved sugar molecules to arrange into crystals. \n\nSentence B is also correct: substances that are less soluble crystallize more readily as the solution becomes supersaturated.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Sentence A is correct, Sentence B is correct, Bothsentences are incorrect"}'::jsonb),
('mc:sentence a. shortening impedes gluten development by hydrolysis. sentence b. impeding gluten development would result in flaky products.:::sentence b is correct', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'sentence a. shortening impedes gluten development by hydrolysis. sentence b. impeding gluten development would result in flaky products.', 'Sentence B is correct', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Sentence A is incorrect because shortening impedes gluten development mainly by coating flour proteins and physically limiting hydration and gluten formation, not by hydrolysis. ","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Sentence B is correct\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Sentence A is incorrect because shortening impedes gluten development mainly by coating flour proteins and physically limiting hydration and gluten formation, not by hydrolysis. \n\nSentence B is correct because limiting gluten development produces a tender or flaky texture, as desired in products such as pastry.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Sentence A is correct, Bothsentences are correct, Bothsentences are incorrect"}'::jsonb),
('mc:sentence a. vinaigrette is an example of a temporary emulsion. sentence b. temporary emulsions are separated by heat.:::sentence a is correct', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'sentence a. vinaigrette is an example of a temporary emulsion. sentence b. temporary emulsions are separated by heat.', 'Sentence A is correct', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Sentence A is correct because vinaigrette is a temporary emulsion of oil and vinegar that separates on standing. ","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Sentence A is correct\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Sentence A is correct because vinaigrette is a temporary emulsion of oil and vinegar that separates on standing. \n\nSentence B is incorrect because temporary emulsions separate primarily because the phases are immiscible and the emulsion is unstable, not simply because of heat.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Sentence B is correct, Both sentences are correct, Both sentences are incorrect"}'::jsonb),
('mc:sentence a. vinaigrette is an example of a temporary emulsion. sentence b. temporary emulsions are separated by heat.:::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'sentence a. vinaigrette is an example of a temporary emulsion. sentence b. temporary emulsions are separated by heat.', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Sentence A is correct because vinaigrette is a temporary emulsion of oil and vinegar that separates on standing. ","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Sentence A is correct\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Sentence A is correct because vinaigrette is a temporary emulsion of oil and vinegar that separates on standing. \n\nSentence B is incorrect because temporary emulsions separate primarily because the phases are immiscible and the emulsion is unstable, not simply because of heat.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Sentence B is correct, Both sentences are correct, Both sentences are incorrect"}'::jsonb),
('mc:compute for the kcal of 850 ml 5% amino acid:::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'compute for the kcal of 850 ml 5% amino acid', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Step-by-Step Calculation","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"170 kcal\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Step-by-Step Calculation\n\nIn clinical nutrition and parenteral nutrition calculations, a 5% amino acid solution means there are 5 grams of pure amino acid in every 100 mL of fluid. Amino acids provide 4 kcal per gram.\n\n1. Calculate the total grams of amino acid:\n\t• Total grams = 850 mL x 0.05 (5%)\n\t• Total grams = 42.5 grams\n\n2. Calculate the total calories (kcal):\n\t• Total kcal = 42.5 grams x 4 kcal/gram","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: 200 kcal, 300 kcal, 250 kcal"}'::jsonb),
('mc:best temperature for drying.:::140-145ºf', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'best temperature for drying.', '140-145ºF', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"In food dehydration, 140°F or 60°C is widely recognized as the optimum standard temperature. If the temperature is too high, the outside of the food cooks and hardens (“case hardening”), which traps moisture inside and leads to molding. ","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"140-145ºF\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"In food dehydration, 140°F or 60°C is widely recognized as the optimum standard temperature. If the temperature is too high, the outside of the food cooks and hardens (“case hardening”), which traps moisture inside and leads to molding. \n\nThis temperature range is also high enough to safely drive out moisture quickly before spoilage micro-organisms can replicate, but low enough to avoid cooking the food. \n\nConsider also:\n• Air Flow\n• Time\n• Nature of food","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: 100-115ºF, 120-125ºF, 130-135ºF"}'::jsonb),
('mc:best temperature for drying.:::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'best temperature for drying.', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"In food dehydration, 140°F or 60°C is widely recognized as the optimum standard temperature. If the temperature is too high, the outside of the food cooks and hardens (“case hardening”), which traps moisture inside and leads to molding. ","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"140-145ºF\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"In food dehydration, 140°F or 60°C is widely recognized as the optimum standard temperature. If the temperature is too high, the outside of the food cooks and hardens (“case hardening”), which traps moisture inside and leads to molding. \n\nThis temperature range is also high enough to safely drive out moisture quickly before spoilage micro-organisms can replicate, but low enough to avoid cooking the food. \n\nConsider also:\n• Air Flow\n• Time\n• Nature of food","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: 100-115ºF, 120-125ºF, 130-135ºF"}'::jsonb),
('mc:all of the following are nuts except::::munggo', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'all of the following are nuts except:', 'Munggo', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Munggo is a legume, not a culinary nut. ","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Munggo\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Munggo is a legume, not a culinary nut. \n\nKastanyas, pili, and coconut are commonly classified or used as nuts.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Kastanyas, Pili, Coconut"}'::jsonb),
