('mc:how can the stomach protect itself from the strong acidity of hcl?:::mucus secretion forms a protective coat over the mucosa', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'how can the stomach protect itself from the strong acidity of hcl?', 'Mucus secretion forms a protective coat over the mucosa', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"The cells lining the inner wall of the stomach (surface mucous cells and mucous neck cells) continuously secrete a thick, gel-like layer of mucus.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Mucus secretion forms a protective coat over the mucosa\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"The cells lining the inner wall of the stomach (surface mucous cells and mucous neck cells) continuously secrete a thick, gel-like layer of mucus.\n\nThis mucus layer is rich in bicarbonate ions (HCO₃⁻). The alkaline bicarbonate is trapped within the sticky mucus gel, creating a highly effective physical and chemical buffer system. While the liquid inside the stomach cavity is highly acidic with a pH of 1.5 to 2.0, this specialized protective coat maintains a near-neutral pH of around 7.0 right at the surface of the stomach''s epithelial cells, preventing hydrochloric acid (HCl) and pepsin from digesting the stomach wall.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Secretin stimulates the release of sodium bicarbonate to neutralize acid, Stomach is covered by thick microvilli, Pancreatic enzymes protect the stomach"}'::jsonb),
('mc:how can the stomach protect itself from the strong acidity of hcl?:::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'how can the stomach protect itself from the strong acidity of hcl?', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"The cells lining the inner wall of the stomach (surface mucous cells and mucous neck cells) continuously secrete a thick, gel-like layer of mucus.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Mucus secretion forms a protective coat over the mucosa\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"The cells lining the inner wall of the stomach (surface mucous cells and mucous neck cells) continuously secrete a thick, gel-like layer of mucus.\n\nThis mucus layer is rich in bicarbonate ions (HCO₃⁻). The alkaline bicarbonate is trapped within the sticky mucus gel, creating a highly effective physical and chemical buffer system. While the liquid inside the stomach cavity is highly acidic with a pH of 1.5 to 2.0, this specialized protective coat maintains a near-neutral pH of around 7.0 right at the surface of the stomach''s epithelial cells, preventing hydrochloric acid (HCl) and pepsin from digesting the stomach wall.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Secretin stimulates the release of sodium bicarbonate to neutralize acid, Stomach is covered by thick microvilli, Pancreatic enzymes protect the stomach"}'::jsonb),
('mc:an essential fatty acid derived from plant oils such as corn oil:::linoleic acid', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'an essential fatty acid derived from plant oils such as corn oil', 'Linoleic acid', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Linoleic acid (LA) is an essential omega-6 polyunsaturated fatty acid (PUFA) that must be obtained from the diet because the human body cannot synthesize it. It is abundant in plant oils, particularly corn oil, soybean oil, sunflower oil, safflower oil, and cottonseed oil. ","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Linoleic acid\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Linoleic acid (LA) is an essential omega-6 polyunsaturated fatty acid (PUFA) that must be obtained from the diet because the human body cannot synthesize it. It is abundant in plant oils, particularly corn oil, soybean oil, sunflower oil, safflower oil, and cottonseed oil. \n\nLinoleic acid plays important roles in maintaining cell membrane integrity, supporting growth and development, promoting skin health, and serving as the precursor of arachidonic acid, which is involved in the production of eicosanoids (prostaglandins, thromboxanes, and leukotrienes). \n\nAnother essential fatty acid is α-linolenic acid (ALA), an omega-3 fatty acid, which is commonly found in flaxseed, chia seeds, walnuts, canola oil, and soybean oil. Both linoleic acid and α-linolenic acid are considered essential because they cannot be synthesized by humans.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: α-Linolenic acid, Arachidonic acid, Palmitic acid"}'::jsonb),
('mc:an essential fatty acid derived from plant oils such as corn oil:::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'an essential fatty acid derived from plant oils such as corn oil', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Linoleic acid (LA) is an essential omega-6 polyunsaturated fatty acid (PUFA) that must be obtained from the diet because the human body cannot synthesize it. It is abundant in plant oils, particularly corn oil, soybean oil, sunflower oil, safflower oil, and cottonseed oil. ","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Linoleic acid\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Linoleic acid (LA) is an essential omega-6 polyunsaturated fatty acid (PUFA) that must be obtained from the diet because the human body cannot synthesize it. It is abundant in plant oils, particularly corn oil, soybean oil, sunflower oil, safflower oil, and cottonseed oil. \n\nLinoleic acid plays important roles in maintaining cell membrane integrity, supporting growth and development, promoting skin health, and serving as the precursor of arachidonic acid, which is involved in the production of eicosanoids (prostaglandins, thromboxanes, and leukotrienes). \n\nAnother essential fatty acid is α-linolenic acid (ALA), an omega-3 fatty acid, which is commonly found in flaxseed, chia seeds, walnuts, canola oil, and soybean oil. Both linoleic acid and α-linolenic acid are considered essential because they cannot be synthesized by humans.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: α-Linolenic acid, Arachidonic acid, Palmitic acid"}'::jsonb),
('mc:compute the percent shrinkage of pork kasim if the raw weight is 850 g and the cooked weight is 600 g.:::29%', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'compute the percent shrinkage of pork kasim if the raw weight is 850 g and the cooked weight is 600 g.', '29%', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Formula: (raw weight - cooked weight) / raw weight * 100","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"29%\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Formula: (raw weight - cooked weight) / raw weight * 100\n= (850–600 ÷ 850) × 100 = 29%","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: 25%, 31%, 33%"}'::jsonb),
('mc:what is the immediate substrate that feeds into the tca cycle?:::acetyl coa', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'what is the immediate substrate that feeds into the tca cycle?', 'Acetyl CoA', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"The Tricarboxylic Acid (TCA) cycle, also known as the Citric Acid Cycle or Krebs Cycle, is the central metabolic pathway responsible for the oxidation of carbohydrates, fats, and proteins to produce energy. The immediate substrate that enters the TCA cycle is acetyl-CoA. ","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Acetyl CoA\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"The Tricarboxylic Acid (TCA) cycle, also known as the Citric Acid Cycle or Krebs Cycle, is the central metabolic pathway responsible for the oxidation of carbohydrates, fats, and proteins to produce energy. The immediate substrate that enters the TCA cycle is acetyl-CoA. \n\nAcetyl-CoA is produced from several metabolic pathways: \n• Carbohydrates: Glucose → Glycolysis → Pyruvate → Acetyl-CoA \n• Fats: β-oxidation of fatty acids → Acetyl-CoA \n• Proteins: Ketogenic amino acids → Acetyl-CoA \n\nOnce formed, acetyl-CoA combines with oxaloacetate, catalyzed by citrate synthase, to produce citrate, the first compound of the TCA cycle. The cycle then proceeds through a series of reactions that generate NADH, FADH₂, and GTP (or ATP), which are subsequently used in oxidative phosphorylation to produce ATP. \n\nFor the NDLE, remember that acetyl-CoA is the metabolic crossroads because it links carbohydrate, lipid, and protein metabolism.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Pyruvate, Citrate, Oxaloacetate"}'::jsonb),
('mc:recommended high protein regimen for wound healing::::a and c', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'recommended high protein regimen for wound healing:', 'A and C', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Adequate protein is essential for collagen synthesis, immune function, and tissue repair. Patients with severe wounds or extensive burns may require protein intakes as high as 2.0–3.0 g/kg/day, while protein may also account for approximately 20–25% of total energy intake depending on the clinical condition.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"A and C\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Adequate protein is essential for collagen synthesis, immune function, and tissue repair. Patients with severe wounds or extensive burns may require protein intakes as high as 2.0–3.0 g/kg/day, while protein may also account for approximately 20–25% of total energy intake depending on the clinical condition.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: 20-25% of TER, 15-20% of TER, 2.5-3 g/kg"}'::jsonb),
('mc:what is the immediate substrate that feeds into the tca cycle?:::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'what is the immediate substrate that feeds into the tca cycle?', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"The Tricarboxylic Acid (TCA) cycle, also known as the Citric Acid Cycle or Krebs Cycle, is the central metabolic pathway responsible for the oxidation of carbohydrates, fats, and proteins to produce energy. The immediate substrate that enters the TCA cycle is acetyl-CoA. ","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Acetyl CoA\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"The Tricarboxylic Acid (TCA) cycle, also known as the Citric Acid Cycle or Krebs Cycle, is the central metabolic pathway responsible for the oxidation of carbohydrates, fats, and proteins to produce energy. The immediate substrate that enters the TCA cycle is acetyl-CoA. \n\nAcetyl-CoA is produced from several metabolic pathways: \n• Carbohydrates: Glucose → Glycolysis → Pyruvate → Acetyl-CoA \n• Fats: β-oxidation of fatty acids → Acetyl-CoA \n• Proteins: Ketogenic amino acids → Acetyl-CoA \n\nOnce formed, acetyl-CoA combines with oxaloacetate, catalyzed by citrate synthase, to produce citrate, the first compound of the TCA cycle. The cycle then proceeds through a series of reactions that generate NADH, FADH₂, and GTP (or ATP), which are subsequently used in oxidative phosphorylation to produce ATP. \n\nFor the NDLE, remember that acetyl-CoA is the metabolic crossroads because it links carbohydrate, lipid, and protein metabolism.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Pyruvate, Citrate, Oxaloacetate"}'::jsonb),
('mc:in the electron transport system (ets), electrons from hydrogen carriers pass through a series of electron carriers, ultimately driving the conversion of adp to atp. this process is called::::oxidative phosphorylation', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'in the electron transport system (ets), electrons from hydrogen carriers pass through a series of electron carriers, ultimately driving the conversion of adp to atp. this process is called:', 'Oxidative phosphorylation', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Oxidative phosphorylation is the process by which energy released from the transfer of electrons through the electron transport chain (ETC) is used to generate ATP. During the oxidation of carbohydrates, fats, and proteins, hydrogen/electron carriers such as NADH and FADH₂ deliver high-energy electrons to the ETC located in the inner mitochondrial membrane. As electrons pass through the electron carriers, the released energy is used to pump protons (H⁺) across the membrane, creating a proton gradient. The return of H⁺ through ATP synthase provides the energy needed to synthesize ATP from ADP + inorganic phosphate (Pi). Thus, the process described in the question is oxidative phosphorylation.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Oxidative phosphorylation\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Oxidative phosphorylation is the process by which energy released from the transfer of electrons through the electron transport chain (ETC) is used to generate ATP. During the oxidation of carbohydrates, fats, and proteins, hydrogen/electron carriers such as NADH and FADH₂ deliver high-energy electrons to the ETC located in the inner mitochondrial membrane. As electrons pass through the electron carriers, the released energy is used to pump protons (H⁺) across the membrane, creating a proton gradient. The return of H⁺ through ATP synthase provides the energy needed to synthesize ATP from ADP + inorganic phosphate (Pi). Thus, the process described in the question is oxidative phosphorylation.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Oxidative decarboxylation, Gluconeogenesis, Glycogenesis"}'::jsonb),
('mc:in the electron transport system (ets), electrons from hydrogen carriers pass through a series of electron carriers, ultimately driving the conversion of adp to atp. this process is called::::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'in the electron transport system (ets), electrons from hydrogen carriers pass through a series of electron carriers, ultimately driving the conversion of adp to atp. this process is called:', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Oxidative phosphorylation is the process by which energy released from the transfer of electrons through the electron transport chain (ETC) is used to generate ATP. During the oxidation of carbohydrates, fats, and proteins, hydrogen/electron carriers such as NADH and FADH₂ deliver high-energy electrons to the ETC located in the inner mitochondrial membrane. As electrons pass through the electron carriers, the released energy is used to pump protons (H⁺) across the membrane, creating a proton gradient. The return of H⁺ through ATP synthase provides the energy needed to synthesize ATP from ADP + inorganic phosphate (Pi). Thus, the process described in the question is oxidative phosphorylation.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Oxidative phosphorylation\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Oxidative phosphorylation is the process by which energy released from the transfer of electrons through the electron transport chain (ETC) is used to generate ATP. During the oxidation of carbohydrates, fats, and proteins, hydrogen/electron carriers such as NADH and FADH₂ deliver high-energy electrons to the ETC located in the inner mitochondrial membrane. As electrons pass through the electron carriers, the released energy is used to pump protons (H⁺) across the membrane, creating a proton gradient. The return of H⁺ through ATP synthase provides the energy needed to synthesize ATP from ADP + inorganic phosphate (Pi). Thus, the process described in the question is oxidative phosphorylation.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Oxidative decarboxylation, Gluconeogenesis, Glycogenesis"}'::jsonb),
('mc:non-digestible food ingredients that encourage the growth of beneficial bacteria::::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'non-digestible food ingredients that encourage the growth of beneficial bacteria:', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Prebiotics are substrates that are selectively utilized by host microorganisms and provide a health benefit. Many are non-digestible carbohydrates, such as certain oligosaccharides and inulin-type fructans, that reach the colon and are fermented by beneficial microorganisms. This fermentation can produce short-chain fatty acids (SCFAs), which contribute to intestinal and metabolic health. Thus, the description in the question corresponds to a prebiotic, not a probiotic. ","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Prebiotic\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Prebiotics are substrates that are selectively utilized by host microorganisms and provide a health benefit. Many are non-digestible carbohydrates, such as certain oligosaccharides and inulin-type fructans, that reach the colon and are fermented by beneficial microorganisms. This fermentation can produce short-chain fatty acids (SCFAs), which contribute to intestinal and metabolic health. Thus, the description in the question corresponds to a prebiotic, not a probiotic. \n\nFor NDLE purposes, distinguish the three terms clearly: \n\nPrebiotics are substrates that beneficial microorganisms use; \nProbiotics are live microorganisms that provide a health benefit when administered in adequate amounts; and \nMicrobiota refers to the community of microorganisms living in a particular environment, such as the gastrointestinal tract.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Probiotic, Microbiota, None of the above"}'::jsonb),
('mc:the following are functions of sodium, except::::participation in detoxification reactions', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'the following are functions of sodium, except:', 'Participation in detoxification reactions', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Sodium is the major extracellular cation and plays an important role in maintaining extracellular fluid volume and osmotic balance. It is also essential for nerve impulse transmission and muscle contraction through its role in generating membrane potentials. Sodium-dependent transporters in the intestinal epithelium also facilitate the absorption of glucose and other nutrients, such as certain amino acids. ","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Participation in detoxification reactions\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Sodium is the major extracellular cation and plays an important role in maintaining extracellular fluid volume and osmotic balance. It is also essential for nerve impulse transmission and muscle contraction through its role in generating membrane potentials. Sodium-dependent transporters in the intestinal epithelium also facilitate the absorption of glucose and other nutrients, such as certain amino acids. \n\nHowever, detoxification reactions are not a primary function of sodium. Detoxification involves processes primarily carried out by the liver, including enzymatic reactions such as oxidation, reduction, hydrolysis, and conjugation.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Maintenance of normal extracellular fluid balance, Nerve impulse transmission and muscular contraction, Absorption of glucose and transport of other nutrients"}'::jsonb),
('mc:the primary risk factor for copd is::::cigarette smoking', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'the primary risk factor for copd is:', 'cigarette smoking', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Cigarette smoking is the most important and common risk factor for COPD. Chronic exposure to tobacco smoke causes airway inflammation, destruction of lung tissue, and progressive, largely irreversible airflow limitation. Other inhaled pollutants can also contribute, particularly occupational exposure to dusts, fumes, and chemicals, but cigarette smoking remains the major preventable risk factor. ","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"cigarette smoking\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Cigarette smoking is the most important and common risk factor for COPD. Chronic exposure to tobacco smoke causes airway inflammation, destruction of lung tissue, and progressive, largely irreversible airflow limitation. Other inhaled pollutants can also contribute, particularly occupational exposure to dusts, fumes, and chemicals, but cigarette smoking remains the major preventable risk factor. \n\nAlpha-1 antitrypsin deficiency is an important genetic risk factor for COPD, particularly emphysema at a younger age, but it is relatively uncommon. Frequent respiratory infections may worsen respiratory health but are not the primary risk factor for developing COPD.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: alpha-I antitrypsin deficiency, occupational exposure to dusts or chemicals, frequent respiratory infections"}'::jsonb),
('mc:the primary risk factor for copd is::::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'the primary risk factor for copd is:', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Cigarette smoking is the most important and common risk factor for COPD. Chronic exposure to tobacco smoke causes airway inflammation, destruction of lung tissue, and progressive, largely irreversible airflow limitation. Other inhaled pollutants can also contribute, particularly occupational exposure to dusts, fumes, and chemicals, but cigarette smoking remains the major preventable risk factor. ","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"cigarette smoking\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Cigarette smoking is the most important and common risk factor for COPD. Chronic exposure to tobacco smoke causes airway inflammation, destruction of lung tissue, and progressive, largely irreversible airflow limitation. Other inhaled pollutants can also contribute, particularly occupational exposure to dusts, fumes, and chemicals, but cigarette smoking remains the major preventable risk factor. \n\nAlpha-1 antitrypsin deficiency is an important genetic risk factor for COPD, particularly emphysema at a younger age, but it is relatively uncommon. Frequent respiratory infections may worsen respiratory health but are not the primary risk factor for developing COPD.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: alpha-I antitrypsin deficiency, occupational exposure to dusts or chemicals, frequent respiratory infections"}'::jsonb),
('mc:the weight loss and wasting that often occur in copd can be caused by::::all of the above', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'the weight loss and wasting that often occur in copd can be caused by:', 'all of the above', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Weight loss and muscle wasting are common in advanced COPD and may result from several interacting factors. ","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"all of the above\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Weight loss and muscle wasting are common in advanced COPD and may result from several interacting factors. \n• Reduced food intake may occur because of dyspnea, fatigue, early satiety, or difficulty eating while breathing. \n• Increased metabolic rate can occur because the work of breathing is increased, raising energy expenditure. \n• Reduced exercise tolerance decreases physical activity and contributes to loss of muscle mass and strength. \n\nThese factors can create a cycle of poor intake → weight loss → muscle loss → reduced respiratory muscle strength → greater difficulty breathing. Therefore, nutrition management aims to provide adequate energy and protein while avoiding overfeeding.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: reduced food intake, increased metabolic rate, reduced exercise tolerance"}'::jsonb),
('mc:compute the percent shrinkage of pork kasim if the raw weight is 850 g and the cooked weight is 600 g.:::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'compute the percent shrinkage of pork kasim if the raw weight is 850 g and the cooked weight is 600 g.', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Formula: (raw weight - cooked weight) / raw weight * 100","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"29%\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Formula: (raw weight - cooked weight) / raw weight * 100\n= (850–600 ÷ 850) × 100 = 29%","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: 25%, 31%, 33%"}'::jsonb),
('mc:the weight loss and wasting that often occur in copd can be caused by::::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'the weight loss and wasting that often occur in copd can be caused by:', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Weight loss and muscle wasting are common in advanced COPD and may result from several interacting factors. ","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"all of the above\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Weight loss and muscle wasting are common in advanced COPD and may result from several interacting factors. \n• Reduced food intake may occur because of dyspnea, fatigue, early satiety, or difficulty eating while breathing. \n• Increased metabolic rate can occur because the work of breathing is increased, raising energy expenditure. \n• Reduced exercise tolerance decreases physical activity and contributes to loss of muscle mass and strength. \n\nThese factors can create a cycle of poor intake → weight loss → muscle loss → reduced respiratory muscle strength → greater difficulty breathing. Therefore, nutrition management aims to provide adequate energy and protein while avoiding overfeeding.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: reduced food intake, increased metabolic rate, reduced exercise tolerance"}'::jsonb),
('mc:nutrition therapy for a person with respiratory failure includes::::careful attention to providing enough, but not too much energy', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'nutrition therapy for a person with respiratory failure includes:', 'careful attention to providing enough, but not too much energy', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"In patients with acute or chronic respiratory failure, providing too much total energy (overfeeding) is dangerous. Excess calories—especially from carbohydrates but also from fats and proteins—accelerate lipogenesis, which drives up carbon dioxide (CO₂) production. Since the patient''s compromised respiratory system cannot handle the increased respiratory workload needed to blow off this extra CO₂, overfeeding can trigger hypercapnia and make it extremely difficult to wean them off a mechanical ventilator.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"careful attention to providing enough, but not too much energy\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"In patients with acute or chronic respiratory failure, providing too much total energy (overfeeding) is dangerous. Excess calories—especially from carbohydrates but also from fats and proteins—accelerate lipogenesis, which drives up carbon dioxide (CO₂) production. Since the patient''s compromised respiratory system cannot handle the increased respiratory workload needed to blow off this extra CO₂, overfeeding can trigger hypercapnia and make it extremely difficult to wean them off a mechanical ventilator.\n\nAt the same time, underfeeding must be strictly avoided. If a patient does not receive enough calories, their body will enter a catabolic state, breaking down skeletal muscle—including the diaphragm and intercostal respiratory muscles—which worsens muscle weakness and leads to respiratory muscle failure. Therefore, nutrition therapy must be balanced to provide enough energy to meet metabolic needs without exceeding them.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: a generous fluid intake to facilitate mucus clearance, a high fat intake to prevent weight loss and wasting, a high carbohydrate intake to increase carbon dioxide production"}'::jsonb),
('mc:nutrition therapy for a person with respiratory failure includes::::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'nutrition therapy for a person with respiratory failure includes:', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"In patients with acute or chronic respiratory failure, providing too much total energy (overfeeding) is dangerous. Excess calories—especially from carbohydrates but also from fats and proteins—accelerate lipogenesis, which drives up carbon dioxide (CO₂) production. Since the patient''s compromised respiratory system cannot handle the increased respiratory workload needed to blow off this extra CO₂, overfeeding can trigger hypercapnia and make it extremely difficult to wean them off a mechanical ventilator.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"careful attention to providing enough, but not too much energy\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"In patients with acute or chronic respiratory failure, providing too much total energy (overfeeding) is dangerous. Excess calories—especially from carbohydrates but also from fats and proteins—accelerate lipogenesis, which drives up carbon dioxide (CO₂) production. Since the patient''s compromised respiratory system cannot handle the increased respiratory workload needed to blow off this extra CO₂, overfeeding can trigger hypercapnia and make it extremely difficult to wean them off a mechanical ventilator.\n\nAt the same time, underfeeding must be strictly avoided. If a patient does not receive enough calories, their body will enter a catabolic state, breaking down skeletal muscle—including the diaphragm and intercostal respiratory muscles—which worsens muscle weakness and leads to respiratory muscle failure. Therefore, nutrition therapy must be balanced to provide enough energy to meet metabolic needs without exceeding them.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: a generous fluid intake to facilitate mucus clearance, a high fat intake to prevent weight loss and wasting, a high carbohydrate intake to increase carbon dioxide production"}'::jsonb),
('mc:consider the following statements about indoles: statement 1: indoles are phytochemicals found in broccoli and other cruciferous vegetables that induce the production of detoxification enzymes, helping protect cells against carcinogens. statement 2: indoles may influence estrogen metabolism and modulate estrogen activity.:::both statements are correct.', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'consider the following statements about indoles: statement 1: indoles are phytochemicals found in broccoli and other cruciferous vegetables that induce the production of detoxification enzymes, helping protect cells against carcinogens. statement 2: indoles may influence estrogen metabolism and modulate estrogen activity.', 'Both statements are correct.', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"• Statement 1 is CORRECT: Indoles, such as indole-3-carbinol (I3C) and its metabolic derivative 3,3''-diindolylmethane (DIM), are potent sulfur-containing phytochemicals derived from the breakdown of glucosinolates found heavily in cruciferous vegetables like broccoli, cabbage, cauliflower, and Brussels sprouts. Biochemically, they stimulate and upregulate Phase I and Phase II biotransformation (detoxification) enzymes in the liver and gastrointestinal tract. This metabolic acceleration helps the body break down, neutralize, and safely eliminate carcinogens, xenobiotics, and cellular toxins before they can cause DNA damage.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Both statements are correct.\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"• Statement 1 is CORRECT: Indoles, such as indole-3-carbinol (I3C) and its metabolic derivative 3,3''-diindolylmethane (DIM), are potent sulfur-containing phytochemicals derived from the breakdown of glucosinolates found heavily in cruciferous vegetables like broccoli, cabbage, cauliflower, and Brussels sprouts. Biochemically, they stimulate and upregulate Phase I and Phase II biotransformation (detoxification) enzymes in the liver and gastrointestinal tract. This metabolic acceleration helps the body break down, neutralize, and safely eliminate carcinogens, xenobiotics, and cellular toxins before they can cause DNA damage.\n\n• Statement 2 is CORRECT: Indoles are heavily researched for their distinct capacity to influence estrogen metabolism and modulate overall estrogen activity. They alter the pathways of hormone breakdown in the liver, effectively driving the conversion of strong, highly active estrogens into less potent, protective metabolites (favoring 2-hydroxyestrone over the more harmful 16-alpha-hydroxyestrone). By acting as natural estrogen modulators or blockers, dietary indoles provide systemic chemopreventive traits against hormone-dependent conditions, including breast and endometrial cancers.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Only statement 1 is correct., Only statement 2 is correct., Both statements are incorrect."}'::jsonb),
('mc:consider the following statements about indoles: statement 1: indoles are phytochemicals found in broccoli and other cruciferous vegetables that induce the production of detoxification enzymes, helping protect cells against carcinogens. statement 2: indoles may influence estrogen metabolism and modulate estrogen activity.:::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'consider the following statements about indoles: statement 1: indoles are phytochemicals found in broccoli and other cruciferous vegetables that induce the production of detoxification enzymes, helping protect cells against carcinogens. statement 2: indoles may influence estrogen metabolism and modulate estrogen activity.', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"• Statement 1 is CORRECT: Indoles, such as indole-3-carbinol (I3C) and its metabolic derivative 3,3''-diindolylmethane (DIM), are potent sulfur-containing phytochemicals derived from the breakdown of glucosinolates found heavily in cruciferous vegetables like broccoli, cabbage, cauliflower, and Brussels sprouts. Biochemically, they stimulate and upregulate Phase I and Phase II biotransformation (detoxification) enzymes in the liver and gastrointestinal tract. This metabolic acceleration helps the body break down, neutralize, and safely eliminate carcinogens, xenobiotics, and cellular toxins before they can cause DNA damage.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Both statements are correct.\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"• Statement 1 is CORRECT: Indoles, such as indole-3-carbinol (I3C) and its metabolic derivative 3,3''-diindolylmethane (DIM), are potent sulfur-containing phytochemicals derived from the breakdown of glucosinolates found heavily in cruciferous vegetables like broccoli, cabbage, cauliflower, and Brussels sprouts. Biochemically, they stimulate and upregulate Phase I and Phase II biotransformation (detoxification) enzymes in the liver and gastrointestinal tract. This metabolic acceleration helps the body break down, neutralize, and safely eliminate carcinogens, xenobiotics, and cellular toxins before they can cause DNA damage.\n\n• Statement 2 is CORRECT: Indoles are heavily researched for their distinct capacity to influence estrogen metabolism and modulate overall estrogen activity. They alter the pathways of hormone breakdown in the liver, effectively driving the conversion of strong, highly active estrogens into less potent, protective metabolites (favoring 2-hydroxyestrone over the more harmful 16-alpha-hydroxyestrone). By acting as natural estrogen modulators or blockers, dietary indoles provide systemic chemopreventive traits against hormone-dependent conditions, including breast and endometrial cancers.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Only statement 1 is correct., Only statement 2 is correct., Both statements are incorrect."}'::jsonb),
('mc:phytochemicals found in whole grains that minds to minerals and prevents free radical formation.:::phytic acid', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'phytochemicals found in whole grains that minds to minerals and prevents free radical formation.', 'Phytic acid', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Phytic acid (also known as phytate) is a unique compound found in the bran and germ layer of whole grains, nuts, and legumes.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Phytic acid\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Phytic acid (also known as phytate) is a unique compound found in the bran and germ layer of whole grains, nuts, and legumes.\n\nIt is well-known for its ability to bind (chelate) to minerals like iron, zinc, calcium, and magnesium.\n\nWhile this mineral-binding property often labels it as an \"antinutrient\" because it reduces mineral absorption, it also gives phytic acid a potent antioxidant function. By specifically binding to free iron, it suppresses iron-catalyzed oxidative reactions, thereby preventing the formation of harmful free radicals.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Phenols, Flavonoids, Phytosterols"}'::jsonb),
('mc:phytochemicals found in whole grains that minds to minerals and prevents free radical formation.:::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'phytochemicals found in whole grains that minds to minerals and prevents free radical formation.', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Phytic acid (also known as phytate) is a unique compound found in the bran and germ layer of whole grains, nuts, and legumes.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Phytic acid\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Phytic acid (also known as phytate) is a unique compound found in the bran and germ layer of whole grains, nuts, and legumes.\n\nIt is well-known for its ability to bind (chelate) to minerals like iron, zinc, calcium, and magnesium.\n\nWhile this mineral-binding property often labels it as an \"antinutrient\" because it reduces mineral absorption, it also gives phytic acid a potent antioxidant function. By specifically binding to free iron, it suppresses iron-catalyzed oxidative reactions, thereby preventing the formation of harmful free radicals.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Phenols, Flavonoids, Phytosterols"}'::jsonb),
('mc:recommended high protein regimen for wound healing::::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'recommended high protein regimen for wound healing:', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Adequate protein is essential for collagen synthesis, immune function, and tissue repair. Patients with severe wounds or extensive burns may require protein intakes as high as 2.0–3.0 g/kg/day, while protein may also account for approximately 20–25% of total energy intake depending on the clinical condition.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"A and C\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Adequate protein is essential for collagen synthesis, immune function, and tissue repair. Patients with severe wounds or extensive burns may require protein intakes as high as 2.0–3.0 g/kg/day, while protein may also account for approximately 20–25% of total energy intake depending on the clinical condition.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: 20-25% of TER, 15-20% of TER, 2.5-3 g/kg"}'::jsonb),
('mc:which of the following are included in the nutrition management of peptic ulcer?:::all of the above', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'which of the following are included in the nutrition management of peptic ulcer?', 'All of the above', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"The nutritional management of Peptic Ulcer Disease (PUD) heavily focuses on reducing foods and beverages that stimulate the overproduction of stomach acid, cause direct irritation to the gastric mucosa, or delay the healing process:","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"All of the above\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"The nutritional management of Peptic Ulcer Disease (PUD) heavily focuses on reducing foods and beverages that stimulate the overproduction of stomach acid, cause direct irritation to the gastric mucosa, or delay the healing process:\n\n• Decrease consumption of coffee and caffeine: Both caffeinated and decaffeinated coffee trigger gastric acid secretion and can worsen upper gastrointestinal discomfort.\n• Decrease consumption of spices: Irritating spices—especially black pepper, chili powder, and red pepper—can induce severe dyspemia (indigestion) and irritate the open lining of an existing gastric or duodenal ulcer.\n• Decrease consumption of alcohol: Alcohol can directly damage the protective mucosal barrier of the stomach, exacerbate inflammation, and increase susceptibility to acid damage.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Decrease consumption of alcohol, Decrease consumption of spices, Decrease consumption of coffee and caffeine"}'::jsonb),
('mc:which of the following are included in the nutrition management of peptic ulcer?:::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'which of the following are included in the nutrition management of peptic ulcer?', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"The nutritional management of Peptic Ulcer Disease (PUD) heavily focuses on reducing foods and beverages that stimulate the overproduction of stomach acid, cause direct irritation to the gastric mucosa, or delay the healing process:","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"All of the above\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"The nutritional management of Peptic Ulcer Disease (PUD) heavily focuses on reducing foods and beverages that stimulate the overproduction of stomach acid, cause direct irritation to the gastric mucosa, or delay the healing process:\n\n• Decrease consumption of coffee and caffeine: Both caffeinated and decaffeinated coffee trigger gastric acid secretion and can worsen upper gastrointestinal discomfort.\n• Decrease consumption of spices: Irritating spices—especially black pepper, chili powder, and red pepper—can induce severe dyspemia (indigestion) and irritate the open lining of an existing gastric or duodenal ulcer.\n• Decrease consumption of alcohol: Alcohol can directly damage the protective mucosal barrier of the stomach, exacerbate inflammation, and increase susceptibility to acid damage.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Decrease consumption of alcohol, Decrease consumption of spices, Decrease consumption of coffee and caffeine"}'::jsonb),
('mc:a patient is receiving treatment for crohn''s disease. which food found on the patient''s food tray should the patient avoid?:::fresh salad', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'a patient is receiving treatment for crohn''s disease. which food found on the patient''s food tray should the patient avoid?', 'Fresh salad', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Crohn''s disease is a chronic inflammatory bowel disease (IBD) that can affect any part of the gastrointestinal tract. During an acute flare, patients often experience abdominal pain, diarrhea, weight loss, fatigue, and malabsorption. Nutrition therapy focuses on reducing gastrointestinal irritation, maintaining nutritional status, and preventing deficiencies. ","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Fresh salad\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Crohn''s disease is a chronic inflammatory bowel disease (IBD) that can affect any part of the gastrointestinal tract. During an acute flare, patients often experience abdominal pain, diarrhea, weight loss, fatigue, and malabsorption. Nutrition therapy focuses on reducing gastrointestinal irritation, maintaining nutritional status, and preventing deficiencies. \n\nFresh salad contains raw vegetables that are generally high in insoluble fiber, which can increase stool volume and intestinal motility. In patients with active Crohn''s disease—especially those with intestinal inflammation, strictures, or obstruction risk—raw vegetables may worsen abdominal pain, bloating, diarrhea, or cramping.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: White rice, Baked chicken, Cooked skinless apples"}'::jsonb),
('mc:a patient is receiving treatment for crohn''s disease. which food found on the patient''s food tray should the patient avoid?:::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'a patient is receiving treatment for crohn''s disease. which food found on the patient''s food tray should the patient avoid?', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Crohn''s disease is a chronic inflammatory bowel disease (IBD) that can affect any part of the gastrointestinal tract. During an acute flare, patients often experience abdominal pain, diarrhea, weight loss, fatigue, and malabsorption. Nutrition therapy focuses on reducing gastrointestinal irritation, maintaining nutritional status, and preventing deficiencies. ","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Fresh salad\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Crohn''s disease is a chronic inflammatory bowel disease (IBD) that can affect any part of the gastrointestinal tract. During an acute flare, patients often experience abdominal pain, diarrhea, weight loss, fatigue, and malabsorption. Nutrition therapy focuses on reducing gastrointestinal irritation, maintaining nutritional status, and preventing deficiencies. \n\nFresh salad contains raw vegetables that are generally high in insoluble fiber, which can increase stool volume and intestinal motility. In patients with active Crohn''s disease—especially those with intestinal inflammation, strictures, or obstruction risk—raw vegetables may worsen abdominal pain, bloating, diarrhea, or cramping.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: White rice, Baked chicken, Cooked skinless apples"}'::jsonb),
('mc:it refers to the accumulation of fluids in the abdominal cavity::::ascites', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'it refers to the accumulation of fluids in the abdominal cavity:', 'Ascites', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Ascites specifically refers to the abnormal accumulation of excess fluid within the peritoneal (abdominal) cavity. It is most commonly driven by severe liver diseases like cirrhosis, where high blood pressure in the liver''s veins (portal hypertension) forces fluid to leak into the abdomen.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Ascites\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Ascites specifically refers to the abnormal accumulation of excess fluid within the peritoneal (abdominal) cavity. It is most commonly driven by severe liver diseases like cirrhosis, where high blood pressure in the liver''s veins (portal hypertension) forces fluid to leak into the abdomen.\n\n\t• Esophageal varices: These are enlarged, abnormal veins in the lower part of the esophagus that develop due to obstructed blood flow through the liver. While they share a common cause with ascites (portal hypertension), they are blood vessels, not fluid accumulation.\n\t• Collateral formation: This is the biological process where the body grows alternative blood pathways (collateral vessels) to detour around a blocked or high-pressure vein.\n\t• Edema: This is a broad, generic medical term for swelling caused by fluid trapped in any body tissue. While ascites is technically a localized type of edema, the term edema on its own usually refers to generalized swelling in the limbs (like ankle or leg edema) rather than the abdominal space.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Esophageal varices, Edema, Collateral formation"}'::jsonb),
('mc:it refers to the accumulation of fluids in the abdominal cavity::::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'it refers to the accumulation of fluids in the abdominal cavity:', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Ascites specifically refers to the abnormal accumulation of excess fluid within the peritoneal (abdominal) cavity. It is most commonly driven by severe liver diseases like cirrhosis, where high blood pressure in the liver''s veins (portal hypertension) forces fluid to leak into the abdomen.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Ascites\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Ascites specifically refers to the abnormal accumulation of excess fluid within the peritoneal (abdominal) cavity. It is most commonly driven by severe liver diseases like cirrhosis, where high blood pressure in the liver''s veins (portal hypertension) forces fluid to leak into the abdomen.\n\n\t• Esophageal varices: These are enlarged, abnormal veins in the lower part of the esophagus that develop due to obstructed blood flow through the liver. While they share a common cause with ascites (portal hypertension), they are blood vessels, not fluid accumulation.\n\t• Collateral formation: This is the biological process where the body grows alternative blood pathways (collateral vessels) to detour around a blocked or high-pressure vein.\n\t• Edema: This is a broad, generic medical term for swelling caused by fluid trapped in any body tissue. While ascites is technically a localized type of edema, the term edema on its own usually refers to generalized swelling in the limbs (like ankle or leg edema) rather than the abdominal space.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Esophageal varices, Edema, Collateral formation"}'::jsonb),
('mc:non-digestible food ingredients that encourage the growth of beneficial bacteria::::prebiotic', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'non-digestible food ingredients that encourage the growth of beneficial bacteria:', 'Prebiotic', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Prebiotics are substrates that are selectively utilized by host microorganisms and provide a health benefit. Many are non-digestible carbohydrates, such as certain oligosaccharides and inulin-type fructans, that reach the colon and are fermented by beneficial microorganisms. This fermentation can produce short-chain fatty acids (SCFAs), which contribute to intestinal and metabolic health. Thus, the description in the question corresponds to a prebiotic, not a probiotic. ","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Prebiotic\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Prebiotics are substrates that are selectively utilized by host microorganisms and provide a health benefit. Many are non-digestible carbohydrates, such as certain oligosaccharides and inulin-type fructans, that reach the colon and are fermented by beneficial microorganisms. This fermentation can produce short-chain fatty acids (SCFAs), which contribute to intestinal and metabolic health. Thus, the description in the question corresponds to a prebiotic, not a probiotic. \n\nFor NDLE purposes, distinguish the three terms clearly: \n\nPrebiotics are substrates that beneficial microorganisms use; \nProbiotics are live microorganisms that provide a health benefit when administered in adequate amounts; and \nMicrobiota refers to the community of microorganisms living in a particular environment, such as the gastrointestinal tract.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Probiotic, Microbiota, None of the above"}'::jsonb),
('mc:drug that may cause nausea and vomiting:::digitalis', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'drug that may cause nausea and vomiting', 'Digitalis', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Nausea and vomiting are the classic, earliest gastrointestinal hallmarks of digitalis toxicity (e.g., from digoxin). Digitalis works by inhibiting the Na⁺/K⁺ ATPase pump to treat heart failure and arrhythmias, but it has a very narrow therapeutic window.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Digitalis\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Nausea and vomiting are the classic, earliest gastrointestinal hallmarks of digitalis toxicity (e.g., from digoxin). Digitalis works by inhibiting the Na⁺/K⁺ ATPase pump to treat heart failure and arrhythmias, but it has a very narrow therapeutic window.\n\nWhen digitalis levels become elevated in the blood, the drug directly stimulates the chemoreceptor trigger zone (CTZ) in the medulla oblongata of the brainstem, which triggers a powerful urge to vomit. It is also frequently accompanied by neurological symptoms like anorexia, confusion, and classic visual disturbances (such as a yellow-green halo or blurred vision).\n\n\t• Antacids: Are primarily used to neutralize gastric acid and help alleviate nausea associated with indigestion or GERD rather than causing it.\n\t• Laxatives: Typically cause abdominal cramping, bloating, and diarrhea rather than upper GI symptoms like vomiting.\n\t• Amphetamines: While stimulants can occasionally cause nausea due to sympathetic nervous system activation, it is not a hallmark warning sign of toxicity or a key diagnostic indicator in the way it is uniquely tied to digitalis management in medical examinations.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Antacid, Laxative, Amphetamines"}'::jsonb),
('mc:drug that may cause nausea and vomiting:::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'drug that may cause nausea and vomiting', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Nausea and vomiting are the classic, earliest gastrointestinal hallmarks of digitalis toxicity (e.g., from digoxin). Digitalis works by inhibiting the Na⁺/K⁺ ATPase pump to treat heart failure and arrhythmias, but it has a very narrow therapeutic window.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Digitalis\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Nausea and vomiting are the classic, earliest gastrointestinal hallmarks of digitalis toxicity (e.g., from digoxin). Digitalis works by inhibiting the Na⁺/K⁺ ATPase pump to treat heart failure and arrhythmias, but it has a very narrow therapeutic window.\n\nWhen digitalis levels become elevated in the blood, the drug directly stimulates the chemoreceptor trigger zone (CTZ) in the medulla oblongata of the brainstem, which triggers a powerful urge to vomit. It is also frequently accompanied by neurological symptoms like anorexia, confusion, and classic visual disturbances (such as a yellow-green halo or blurred vision).\n\n\t• Antacids: Are primarily used to neutralize gastric acid and help alleviate nausea associated with indigestion or GERD rather than causing it.\n\t• Laxatives: Typically cause abdominal cramping, bloating, and diarrhea rather than upper GI symptoms like vomiting.\n\t• Amphetamines: While stimulants can occasionally cause nausea due to sympathetic nervous system activation, it is not a hallmark warning sign of toxicity or a key diagnostic indicator in the way it is uniquely tied to digitalis management in medical examinations.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Antacid, Laxative, Amphetamines"}'::jsonb),
('mc:for cancer patients in chemotherapy treatment, the following medical nutrition therapy helps in the management of dysgeusia, except::::avoid drinking fluids with meals', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'for cancer patients in chemotherapy treatment, the following medical nutrition therapy helps in the management of dysgeusia, except:', 'Avoid drinking fluids with meals', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Patients with dysgeusia benefit from good oral hygiene, mouth rinses, and sugar-free gum to stimulate saliva. Avoiding fluids during meals is generally recommended for early satiety or dumping syndrome, not for taste alterations.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Avoid drinking fluids with meals\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Patients with dysgeusia benefit from good oral hygiene, mouth rinses, and sugar-free gum to stimulate saliva. Avoiding fluids during meals is generally recommended for early satiety or dumping syndrome, not for taste alterations.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Use of mouth rinse, Chewing sugar-free gums and mints, SFF"}'::jsonb),
('mc:for cancer patients in chemotherapy treatment, the following medical nutrition therapy helps in the management of dysgeusia, except::::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'for cancer patients in chemotherapy treatment, the following medical nutrition therapy helps in the management of dysgeusia, except:', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Patients with dysgeusia benefit from good oral hygiene, mouth rinses, and sugar-free gum to stimulate saliva. Avoiding fluids during meals is generally recommended for early satiety or dumping syndrome, not for taste alterations.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Avoid drinking fluids with meals\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Patients with dysgeusia benefit from good oral hygiene, mouth rinses, and sugar-free gum to stimulate saliva. Avoiding fluids during meals is generally recommended for early satiety or dumping syndrome, not for taste alterations.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Use of mouth rinse, Chewing sugar-free gums and mints, SFF"}'::jsonb),
('mc:common side effect of gastric resection::::dumping syndrome', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'common side effect of gastric resection:', 'Dumping syndrome', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Dumping syndrome is one of the most common complications following partial or total gastric resection, particularly after procedures such as Billroth I, Billroth II, Roux-en-Y reconstruction, and sleeve gastrectomy. It occurs because the stomach loses part of its reservoir function, allowing hyperosmolar food—especially those rich in simple carbohydrates—to empty rapidly into the small intestine. There are two recognized forms: ","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Dumping syndrome\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Dumping syndrome is one of the most common complications following partial or total gastric resection, particularly after procedures such as Billroth I, Billroth II, Roux-en-Y reconstruction, and sleeve gastrectomy. It occurs because the stomach loses part of its reservoir function, allowing hyperosmolar food—especially those rich in simple carbohydrates—to empty rapidly into the small intestine. There are two recognized forms: \n\n• Early dumping syndrome (10–30 minutes after eating): Characterized by abdominal cramps, nausea, diarrhea, bloating, flushing, tachycardia, dizziness, and hypotension due to rapid fluid shifts from the bloodstream into the intestinal lumen. \n• Late dumping syndrome (1–3 hours after eating): Results from an exaggerated insulin response following rapid glucose absorption, leading to reactive hypoglycemia with symptoms such as sweating, weakness, tremors, hunger, and confusion. \n\nMedical nutrition therapy (MNT) is the primary treatment and includes: \n• Eating small, frequent meals \n• Limiting foods high in simple sugars \n• Increasing protein and moderate fat intake \n• Consuming soluble fiber when appropriate \n• Separating liquids from meals by about 30 minutes \n• Resting in a semi-recumbent position after meals in some patients \n\nPersistent symptoms that do not respond to dietary modification may require pharmacologic therapy (e.g., acarbose for late dumping syndrome or octreotide for severe cases).","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Blind loop syndrome, Dysphagia, Fistula formation"}'::jsonb),
('mc:common side effect of gastric resection::::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'common side effect of gastric resection:', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Dumping syndrome is one of the most common complications following partial or total gastric resection, particularly after procedures such as Billroth I, Billroth II, Roux-en-Y reconstruction, and sleeve gastrectomy. It occurs because the stomach loses part of its reservoir function, allowing hyperosmolar food—especially those rich in simple carbohydrates—to empty rapidly into the small intestine. There are two recognized forms: ","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Dumping syndrome\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Dumping syndrome is one of the most common complications following partial or total gastric resection, particularly after procedures such as Billroth I, Billroth II, Roux-en-Y reconstruction, and sleeve gastrectomy. It occurs because the stomach loses part of its reservoir function, allowing hyperosmolar food—especially those rich in simple carbohydrates—to empty rapidly into the small intestine. There are two recognized forms: \n\n• Early dumping syndrome (10–30 minutes after eating): Characterized by abdominal cramps, nausea, diarrhea, bloating, flushing, tachycardia, dizziness, and hypotension due to rapid fluid shifts from the bloodstream into the intestinal lumen. \n• Late dumping syndrome (1–3 hours after eating): Results from an exaggerated insulin response following rapid glucose absorption, leading to reactive hypoglycemia with symptoms such as sweating, weakness, tremors, hunger, and confusion. \n\nMedical nutrition therapy (MNT) is the primary treatment and includes: \n• Eating small, frequent meals \n• Limiting foods high in simple sugars \n• Increasing protein and moderate fat intake \n• Consuming soluble fiber when appropriate \n• Separating liquids from meals by about 30 minutes \n• Resting in a semi-recumbent position after meals in some patients \n\nPersistent symptoms that do not respond to dietary modification may require pharmacologic therapy (e.g., acarbose for late dumping syndrome or octreotide for severe cases).","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Blind loop syndrome, Dysphagia, Fistula formation"}'::jsonb),
('mc:dietary management of gastric secretory disturbances includes the following except::::high fat diet', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'dietary management of gastric secretory disturbances includes the following except:', 'High fat diet', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Gastric secretory disturbances refer to conditions involving abnormal gastric acid secretion, such as gastritis, peptic ulcer disease (PUD), hyperchlorhydria, and gastroesophageal reflux disease (GERD). The primary goals of nutrition therapy are to minimize gastric irritation, reduce symptoms, promote healing, and maintain adequate nutritional status. ","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"High fat diet\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Gastric secretory disturbances refer to conditions involving abnormal gastric acid secretion, such as gastritis, peptic ulcer disease (PUD), hyperchlorhydria, and gastroesophageal reflux disease (GERD). The primary goals of nutrition therapy are to minimize gastric irritation, reduce symptoms, promote healing, and maintain adequate nutritional status. \n\nA high-fat diet is generally not recommended because fat delays gastric emptying and may stimulate gastric acid secretion, prolong gastric distention, and worsen symptoms such as epigastric discomfort, reflux, and indigestion. Therefore, meals are usually moderate to low in fat, depending on the patient''s tolerance.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Complex carbohydrate, Low fat diet, Low protein"}'::jsonb),
('mc:dietary management of gastric secretory disturbances includes the following except::::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'dietary management of gastric secretory disturbances includes the following except:', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Gastric secretory disturbances refer to conditions involving abnormal gastric acid secretion, such as gastritis, peptic ulcer disease (PUD), hyperchlorhydria, and gastroesophageal reflux disease (GERD). The primary goals of nutrition therapy are to minimize gastric irritation, reduce symptoms, promote healing, and maintain adequate nutritional status. ","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"High fat diet\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Gastric secretory disturbances refer to conditions involving abnormal gastric acid secretion, such as gastritis, peptic ulcer disease (PUD), hyperchlorhydria, and gastroesophageal reflux disease (GERD). The primary goals of nutrition therapy are to minimize gastric irritation, reduce symptoms, promote healing, and maintain adequate nutritional status. \n\nA high-fat diet is generally not recommended because fat delays gastric emptying and may stimulate gastric acid secretion, prolong gastric distention, and worsen symptoms such as epigastric discomfort, reflux, and indigestion. Therefore, meals are usually moderate to low in fat, depending on the patient''s tolerance.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Complex carbohydrate, Low fat diet, Low protein"}'::jsonb),
('mc:what should be done when a known diabetic patient is undergoing hypoglycemia?:::give 1 tbsp sugar, then wait for 15 minutes', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'what should be done when a known diabetic patient is undergoing hypoglycemia?', 'Give 1 tbsp sugar, then wait for 15 minutes', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"For a conscious patient with hypoglycemia who can safely swallow, the recommended immediate treatment is the 15-15 rule: provide approximately 15 g of fast-acting carbohydrate, then recheck blood glucose after 15 minutes. If blood glucose remains low, repeat the treatment. One tablespoon of table sugar provides approximately 12–15 g of carbohydrate, making it the best answer among the choices.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Give 1 tbsp sugar, then wait for 15 minutes\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"For a conscious patient with hypoglycemia who can safely swallow, the recommended immediate treatment is the 15-15 rule: provide approximately 15 g of fast-acting carbohydrate, then recheck blood glucose after 15 minutes. If blood glucose remains low, repeat the treatment. One tablespoon of table sugar provides approximately 12–15 g of carbohydrate, making it the best answer among the choices.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Give a cup of water to rehydrate, Inject an insulin short, Provide 1 pc candy"}'::jsonb),
('mc:what should be done when a known diabetic patient is undergoing hypoglycemia?:::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'what should be done when a known diabetic patient is undergoing hypoglycemia?', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"For a conscious patient with hypoglycemia who can safely swallow, the recommended immediate treatment is the 15-15 rule: provide approximately 15 g of fast-acting carbohydrate, then recheck blood glucose after 15 minutes. If blood glucose remains low, repeat the treatment. One tablespoon of table sugar provides approximately 12–15 g of carbohydrate, making it the best answer among the choices.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Give 1 tbsp sugar, then wait for 15 minutes\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"For a conscious patient with hypoglycemia who can safely swallow, the recommended immediate treatment is the 15-15 rule: provide approximately 15 g of fast-acting carbohydrate, then recheck blood glucose after 15 minutes. If blood glucose remains low, repeat the treatment. One tablespoon of table sugar provides approximately 12–15 g of carbohydrate, making it the best answer among the choices.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Give a cup of water to rehydrate, Inject an insulin short, Provide 1 pc candy"}'::jsonb),
('mc:how many mg of potassium is contained in 40 meq?:::1560 mg k', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'how many mg of potassium is contained in 40 meq?', '1560 mg K', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"For potassium (K⁺), the conversion between mEq and mg is: mg = mEq * (atomic weight ÷ valence) ","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"1560 mg K\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"For potassium (K⁺), the conversion between mEq and mg is: mg = mEq * (atomic weight ÷ valence) \n\nPotassium has an atomic weight of approximately 39 mg/mmol and a valence of +1. \n\nTherefore: 40 mEq * (39mg ÷ 1) = 1560 mg K \n\nThus, 40 mEq of potassium = 1,560 mg K. This conversion is clinically important because electrolytes may be prescribed or reported in mEq, while nutrition labels, laboratory references, and calculations may use mg.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: 1500 mg K, 2000 mg K, 1864 mg K"}'::jsonb),
('mc:how many mg of potassium is contained in 40 meq?:::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'how many mg of potassium is contained in 40 meq?', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"For potassium (K⁺), the conversion between mEq and mg is: mg = mEq * (atomic weight ÷ valence) ","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"1560 mg K\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"For potassium (K⁺), the conversion between mEq and mg is: mg = mEq * (atomic weight ÷ valence) \n\nPotassium has an atomic weight of approximately 39 mg/mmol and a valence of +1. \n\nTherefore: 40 mEq * (39mg ÷ 1) = 1560 mg K \n\nThus, 40 mEq of potassium = 1,560 mg K. This conversion is clinically important because electrolytes may be prescribed or reported in mEq, while nutrition labels, laboratory references, and calculations may use mg.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: 1500 mg K, 2000 mg K, 1864 mg K"}'::jsonb),
('mc:inherent na in the diet is 950 mg. the diet rx states that your patient is allowed 90 meq/day. how much is the salt is to be added to meet the diet rx?:::2.8 grams', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'inherent na in the diet is 950 mg. the diet rx states that your patient is allowed 90 meq/day. how much is the salt is to be added to meet the diet rx?', '2.8 grams', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Step 1: Convert the Diet Rx from mEq to milligrams (mg) of Sodium","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"2.8 grams\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Step 1: Convert the Diet Rx from mEq to milligrams (mg) of Sodium\n\t• Rule: 1 mEq of Sodium (Na) = 23 mg\n\t• Calculation: 90 mEq x 23 mg/mEq = 2,070 mg of total allowed Sodium\n\nStep 2: Calculate the additional Sodium needed\n\t• Subtract the inherent sodium already in the food from the total allowed sodium.\n\t• Calculation: 2,070 mg (Total Allowed) - 950 mg (Inherent) = 1,120 mg of additional Sodium needed\n\nStep 3: Convert the additional Sodium to grams of Table Salt (NaCl)\n\t• Rule: Table salt is 40% sodium (0.40). To find the amount of salt, divide the sodium milligrams by 0.40.\n\t• Calculation: 1,120 mg / 0.40 = 2,800 mg of salt\n\t• Convert to grams: 2,800 mg / 1,000 = 2.8 grams of salt","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: 2.0 grams, 4.3 grams, 5.0 grams"}'::jsonb),
('mc:inherent na in the diet is 950 mg. the diet rx states that your patient is allowed 90 meq/day. how much is the salt is to be added to meet the diet rx?:::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'inherent na in the diet is 950 mg. the diet rx states that your patient is allowed 90 meq/day. how much is the salt is to be added to meet the diet rx?', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Step 1: Convert the Diet Rx from mEq to milligrams (mg) of Sodium","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"2.8 grams\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Step 1: Convert the Diet Rx from mEq to milligrams (mg) of Sodium\n\t• Rule: 1 mEq of Sodium (Na) = 23 mg\n\t• Calculation: 90 mEq x 23 mg/mEq = 2,070 mg of total allowed Sodium\n\nStep 2: Calculate the additional Sodium needed\n\t• Subtract the inherent sodium already in the food from the total allowed sodium.\n\t• Calculation: 2,070 mg (Total Allowed) - 950 mg (Inherent) = 1,120 mg of additional Sodium needed\n\nStep 3: Convert the additional Sodium to grams of Table Salt (NaCl)\n\t• Rule: Table salt is 40% sodium (0.40). To find the amount of salt, divide the sodium milligrams by 0.40.\n\t• Calculation: 1,120 mg / 0.40 = 2,800 mg of salt\n\t• Convert to grams: 2,800 mg / 1,000 = 2.8 grams of salt","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: 2.0 grams, 4.3 grams, 5.0 grams"}'::jsonb),
('mc:which of the following is a free food?:::tea', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'which of the following is a free food?', 'Tea', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"In clinical dietetics and food exchange systems, a free food is defined as any food or beverage that contains negligible amounts of macronutrients—specifically less than 20 calories and 5 grams or less of carbohydrates per serving.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Tea\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"In clinical dietetics and food exchange systems, a free food is defined as any food or beverage that contains negligible amounts of macronutrients—specifically less than 20 calories and 5 grams or less of carbohydrates per serving.\n\nUnsweetened black, green, or herbal tea is virtually calorie-free and carbohydrate-free. Because it does not cause any significant spike in blood glucose or daily caloric intake, it can be consumed freely by patients (such as those managing diabetes or weight) without needing to be calculated or swapped out in a meal plan.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Peanut brittle, Soft drinks, All of the above"}'::jsonb),
('mc:which of the following is a free food?:::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'which of the following is a free food?', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"In clinical dietetics and food exchange systems, a free food is defined as any food or beverage that contains negligible amounts of macronutrients—specifically less than 20 calories and 5 grams or less of carbohydrates per serving.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Tea\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"In clinical dietetics and food exchange systems, a free food is defined as any food or beverage that contains negligible amounts of macronutrients—specifically less than 20 calories and 5 grams or less of carbohydrates per serving.\n\nUnsweetened black, green, or herbal tea is virtually calorie-free and carbohydrate-free. Because it does not cause any significant spike in blood glucose or daily caloric intake, it can be consumed freely by patients (such as those managing diabetes or weight) without needing to be calculated or swapped out in a meal plan.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Peanut brittle, Soft drinks, All of the above"}'::jsonb),
('mc:what is the bmi of a 5''4" individual with an actual weight of 60 kg?:::22.7 kg/m²', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'what is the bmi of a 5''4" individual with an actual weight of 60 kg?', '22.7  kg/m²', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"BMI is calculated using: Weight in kg ÷ Height in m² ","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"22.7  kg/m²\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"BMI is calculated using: Weight in kg ÷ Height in m² \n\nFirst, convert 5''4\" to meters: 5’4” = 64 inches = 1.6256 meters \n\nThen calculate BMI: 60 kg ÷ (1.6256)² = 60 ÷ 2.6426 = ~22.7 kg/m²","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: 22  kg/m², 23  kg/m², 25  kg/m²"}'::jsonb),
('mc:what is the bmi of a 5''4" individual with an actual weight of 60 kg?:::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'what is the bmi of a 5''4" individual with an actual weight of 60 kg?', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"BMI is calculated using: Weight in kg ÷ Height in m² ","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"22.7  kg/m²\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"BMI is calculated using: Weight in kg ÷ Height in m² \n\nFirst, convert 5''4\" to meters: 5’4” = 64 inches = 1.6256 meters \n\nThen calculate BMI: 60 kg ÷ (1.6256)² = 60 ÷ 2.6426 = ~22.7 kg/m²","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: 22  kg/m², 23  kg/m², 25  kg/m²"}'::jsonb),
('mc:which of the following statements is false?:::none of the above', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'which of the following statements is false?', 'None of the above', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"All three statements are considered true based on commonly used Philippine food-composition references for purine content. Purines are naturally occurring compounds found in many foods. They are metabolized to uric acid, making dietary purine intake particularly relevant in the nutritional management of gout and hyperuricemia. ","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"None of the above\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"All three statements are considered true based on commonly used Philippine food-composition references for purine content. Purines are naturally occurring compounds found in many foods. They are metabolized to uric acid, making dietary purine intake particularly relevant in the nutritional management of gout and hyperuricemia. \n\n• Tamban (sardines) is a fish with relatively high purine content. \n• Dried beans also contain purines, but their purine content may be lower than that of certain high-purine fish such as sardines. \n• Chicken organ meats, including gizzard and intestine, contain substantial amounts of purines. \n• Pork liver is a high-purine organ meat and has a higher purine content than pork blood in the referenced food data.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Tamban has higher purine content as compared to dried beans., Chicken gizzard has about the same purine content of that of the chicken intestine., Pork liver has higher purine content as compared to pork blood"}'::jsonb)
ON CONFLICT (id) DO NOTHING;
INSERT INTO public.ai_explanation_cache (id, question_hash, front_text, back_text, options, explanation, breakdown, created_at, question_text, user_answer, is_correct, ai_explanation) VALUES
('mc:a patient is prescribed a diet containing 1,500 mg of sodium. how many meq of sodium are contained in the diet?:::65 meq na', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'a patient is prescribed a diet containing 1,500 mg of sodium. how many meq of sodium are contained in the diet?', '65 mEq Na', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"To convert milligrams of sodium to milliequivalents (mEq), use: mEq = (mg * valence) ÷ atomic weight ","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"65 mEq Na\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"To convert milligrams of sodium to milliequivalents (mEq), use: mEq = (mg * valence) ÷ atomic weight \n\nSodium (Na⁺) has: \n• Atomic weight ≈ 23 \n• Valence = 1 \n\nTherefore: mEq Na = (1500 * 1) ÷ 23 = 65.2 mEq \n\nThus, the calculated value is approximately 65 mEq.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: 55 mEq Na, 60 mEq Na, 70 mEq Na"}'::jsonb),
('mc:a low calcium diet contains no more than mg calcium:::600 mg', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'a low calcium diet contains no more than mg calcium', '600 mg', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"In traditional therapeutic diet planning, a low-calcium diet is generally prescribed to provide no more than 600 mg calcium per day. This is a therapeutic restriction, not the recommended daily calcium intake for healthy individuals. ","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"600 mg\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"In traditional therapeutic diet planning, a low-calcium diet is generally prescribed to provide no more than 600 mg calcium per day. This is a therapeutic restriction, not the recommended daily calcium intake for healthy individuals. \n\nCalcium is essential for bone and tooth mineralization, muscle contraction, nerve transmission, and blood clotting, so restricting calcium should be done only when clinically indicated. For NDLE purposes, when the question asks for the traditional definition of a low-calcium diet, remember ≤600 mg/day.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: 500 mg, 700 mg, 800 mg"}'::jsonb),
('mc:which of the following statements is false?:::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'which of the following statements is false?', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"All three statements are considered true based on commonly used Philippine food-composition references for purine content. Purines are naturally occurring compounds found in many foods. They are metabolized to uric acid, making dietary purine intake particularly relevant in the nutritional management of gout and hyperuricemia. ","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"None of the above\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"All three statements are considered true based on commonly used Philippine food-composition references for purine content. Purines are naturally occurring compounds found in many foods. They are metabolized to uric acid, making dietary purine intake particularly relevant in the nutritional management of gout and hyperuricemia. \n\n• Tamban (sardines) is a fish with relatively high purine content. \n• Dried beans also contain purines, but their purine content may be lower than that of certain high-purine fish such as sardines. \n• Chicken organ meats, including gizzard and intestine, contain substantial amounts of purines. \n• Pork liver is a high-purine organ meat and has a higher purine content than pork blood in the referenced food data.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Tamban has higher purine content as compared to dried beans., Chicken gizzard has about the same purine content of that of the chicken intestine., Pork liver has higher purine content as compared to pork blood"}'::jsonb),
('mc:which of the following statements is true?:::evaporated milk has higher cholesterol content than lean fish', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'which of the following statements is true?', 'Evaporated milk has higher cholesterol content than lean fish', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Evaporated milk is an animal-derived food and therefore contains cholesterol. Based on the food-composition values used in the original question, its cholesterol content is higher than that of lean meat when compared using the specified serving/reference amounts. The question is testing the ability to compare cholesterol content among commonly consumed foods, rather than simply identifying whether a food contains cholesterol. ","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Evaporated milk has higher cholesterol content than lean fish\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Evaporated milk is an animal-derived food and therefore contains cholesterol. Based on the food-composition values used in the original question, its cholesterol content is higher than that of lean meat when compared using the specified serving/reference amounts. The question is testing the ability to compare cholesterol content among commonly consumed foods, rather than simply identifying whether a food contains cholesterol. \n\n\"Vegetables have a higher cholesterol content than fruits\" is false because plant foods do not contain cholesterol. Vegetables and fruits naturally contain zero dietary cholesterol. \n\n\"Shrimp has a higher cholesterol content than butter\" should also not be selected based simply on the fact that shrimp is recognized as relatively high in cholesterol. Cholesterol comparisons must be made using the same reference/serving basis in the food-composition table.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Vegetable has higher cholesterol content as compared to fruits, Shrimp has higher cholesterol content than butter, None of the above"}'::jsonb),
('mc:which of the following statements is true?:::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'which of the following statements is true?', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Evaporated milk is an animal-derived food and therefore contains cholesterol. Based on the food-composition values used in the original question, its cholesterol content is higher than that of lean meat when compared using the specified serving/reference amounts. The question is testing the ability to compare cholesterol content among commonly consumed foods, rather than simply identifying whether a food contains cholesterol. ","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Evaporated milk has higher cholesterol content than lean fish\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Evaporated milk is an animal-derived food and therefore contains cholesterol. Based on the food-composition values used in the original question, its cholesterol content is higher than that of lean meat when compared using the specified serving/reference amounts. The question is testing the ability to compare cholesterol content among commonly consumed foods, rather than simply identifying whether a food contains cholesterol. \n\n\"Vegetables have a higher cholesterol content than fruits\" is false because plant foods do not contain cholesterol. Vegetables and fruits naturally contain zero dietary cholesterol. \n\n\"Shrimp has a higher cholesterol content than butter\" should also not be selected based simply on the fact that shrimp is recognized as relatively high in cholesterol. Cholesterol comparisons must be made using the same reference/serving basis in the food-composition table.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Vegetable has higher cholesterol content as compared to fruits, Shrimp has higher cholesterol content than butter, None of the above"}'::jsonb),
('mc:diet prescribed for patients with calcium oxalate stones:::acid-ash diet', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'diet prescribed for patients with calcium oxalate stones', 'Acid-ash diet', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Historically, an acid-ash diet was prescribed for calcium oxalate kidney stones. The traditional rationale was that acidification of the urine was thought to help maintain the solubility of calcium salts and reduce the formation of calcium-based stones. In contrast, an alkaline-ash diet was traditionally used for uric acid and cystine stones, for which increasing urinary pH promotes greater solubility.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Acid-ash diet\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Historically, an acid-ash diet was prescribed for calcium oxalate kidney stones. The traditional rationale was that acidification of the urine was thought to help maintain the solubility of calcium salts and reduce the formation of calcium-based stones. In contrast, an alkaline-ash diet was traditionally used for uric acid and cystine stones, for which increasing urinary pH promotes greater solubility.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Alkaline-ash diet, High fiber diet, Mediterranean Diet"}'::jsonb),
('mc:diet prescribed for patients with calcium oxalate stones:::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'diet prescribed for patients with calcium oxalate stones', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Historically, an acid-ash diet was prescribed for calcium oxalate kidney stones. The traditional rationale was that acidification of the urine was thought to help maintain the solubility of calcium salts and reduce the formation of calcium-based stones. In contrast, an alkaline-ash diet was traditionally used for uric acid and cystine stones, for which increasing urinary pH promotes greater solubility.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Acid-ash diet\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Historically, an acid-ash diet was prescribed for calcium oxalate kidney stones. The traditional rationale was that acidification of the urine was thought to help maintain the solubility of calcium salts and reduce the formation of calcium-based stones. In contrast, an alkaline-ash diet was traditionally used for uric acid and cystine stones, for which increasing urinary pH promotes greater solubility.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Alkaline-ash diet, High fiber diet, Mediterranean Diet"}'::jsonb),
('mc:a low calcium diet contains no more than mg calcium:::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'a low calcium diet contains no more than mg calcium', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"In traditional therapeutic diet planning, a low-calcium diet is generally prescribed to provide no more than 600 mg calcium per day. This is a therapeutic restriction, not the recommended daily calcium intake for healthy individuals. ","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"600 mg\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"In traditional therapeutic diet planning, a low-calcium diet is generally prescribed to provide no more than 600 mg calcium per day. This is a therapeutic restriction, not the recommended daily calcium intake for healthy individuals. \n\nCalcium is essential for bone and tooth mineralization, muscle contraction, nerve transmission, and blood clotting, so restricting calcium should be done only when clinically indicated. For NDLE purposes, when the question asks for the traditional definition of a low-calcium diet, remember ≤600 mg/day.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: 500 mg, 700 mg, 800 mg"}'::jsonb),
('mc:diet prescribed for patients with cystine stone:::alkaline-ash diet', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'diet prescribed for patients with cystine stone', 'Alkaline-ash diet', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"An alkaline-ash diet is the traditional dietary prescription for patients with cystine stones. Cystinuria is an inherited disorder in which excessive amounts of cystine are excreted in the urine. Because cystine has poor solubility in acidic urine, it is more likely to precipitate and form stones. Increasing urinary pH makes cystine more soluble and therefore helps reduce cystine crystallization. This principle remains clinically relevant. ","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Alkaline-ash diet\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"An alkaline-ash diet is the traditional dietary prescription for patients with cystine stones. Cystinuria is an inherited disorder in which excessive amounts of cystine are excreted in the urine. Because cystine has poor solubility in acidic urine, it is more likely to precipitate and form stones. Increasing urinary pH makes cystine more soluble and therefore helps reduce cystine crystallization. This principle remains clinically relevant. \n\nCurrent management of cystinuria emphasizes high fluid intake to dilute urinary cystine and urinary alkalinization, commonly with potassium citrate, to maintain an appropriately alkaline urine. Dietary sodium restriction is also important because high sodium intake can increase urinary cystine excretion.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Acid-ash diet, High fiber diet, Mediterranean Diet"}'::jsonb),
('mc:diet prescribed for patients with cystine stone:::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'diet prescribed for patients with cystine stone', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"An alkaline-ash diet is the traditional dietary prescription for patients with cystine stones. Cystinuria is an inherited disorder in which excessive amounts of cystine are excreted in the urine. Because cystine has poor solubility in acidic urine, it is more likely to precipitate and form stones. Increasing urinary pH makes cystine more soluble and therefore helps reduce cystine crystallization. This principle remains clinically relevant. ","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Alkaline-ash diet\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"An alkaline-ash diet is the traditional dietary prescription for patients with cystine stones. Cystinuria is an inherited disorder in which excessive amounts of cystine are excreted in the urine. Because cystine has poor solubility in acidic urine, it is more likely to precipitate and form stones. Increasing urinary pH makes cystine more soluble and therefore helps reduce cystine crystallization. This principle remains clinically relevant. \n\nCurrent management of cystinuria emphasizes high fluid intake to dilute urinary cystine and urinary alkalinization, commonly with potassium citrate, to maintain an appropriately alkaline urine. Dietary sodium restriction is also important because high sodium intake can increase urinary cystine excretion.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Acid-ash diet, High fiber diet, Mediterranean Diet"}'::jsonb),
('mc:which among the following has the highest sodium content?:::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'which among the following has the highest sodium content?', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Among the choices, carrots have the highest sodium content based on the food-composition data underlying this question. Although all four are fresh vegetables and are generally considered low-sodium foods, their naturally occurring sodium concentrations are not identical. This is an important distinction in nutrition assessment and diet therapy: foods can be categorized as generally low in sodium while still having measurable differences in their sodium content. ","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Carrots\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Among the choices, carrots have the highest sodium content based on the food-composition data underlying this question. Although all four are fresh vegetables and are generally considered low-sodium foods, their naturally occurring sodium concentrations are not identical. This is an important distinction in nutrition assessment and diet therapy: foods can be categorized as generally low in sodium while still having measurable differences in their sodium content. \n\nFor NDLE purposes, this question is primarily testing knowledge of the relative sodium content of common vegetables, rather than asking which food is considered “high sodium” clinically. In a sodium-restricted diet, carrots, cabbage, sayote, and pake may all be used, with the major dietary sodium reduction usually coming from limiting salt, processed foods, canned products, seasoning mixes, sauces, and other sodium-containing condiments.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Cabbage, Sayote, Pake"}'::jsonb),
('mc:statements describing the management of celiac disease::::the management of celiac disease is a life-long gluten-free diet.', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'statements describing the management of celiac disease:', 'The management of celiac disease is a life-long gluten-free diet.', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"The cornerstone of celiac disease management is a strict, lifelong gluten-free diet. Gluten exposure triggers an immune-mediated response that can cause intestinal mucosal injury even when the patient does not experience obvious gastrointestinal symptoms. Therefore, the absence of symptoms does not mean that celiac disease has disappeared or that gluten can safely be reintroduced. Strict adherence to a gluten-free diet promotes intestinal healing, improves nutritional status, and reduces the risk of complications associated with ongoing gluten exposure.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"The management of celiac disease is a life-long gluten-free diet.\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"The cornerstone of celiac disease management is a strict, lifelong gluten-free diet. Gluten exposure triggers an immune-mediated response that can cause intestinal mucosal injury even when the patient does not experience obvious gastrointestinal symptoms. Therefore, the absence of symptoms does not mean that celiac disease has disappeared or that gluten can safely be reintroduced. Strict adherence to a gluten-free diet promotes intestinal healing, improves nutritional status, and reduces the risk of complications associated with ongoing gluten exposure.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Small quantities of gluten may be allowed when celiac disease no longer manifests., Enough scientific evidence exists for what constitutes a safe amount of gluten intake., All of the above"}'::jsonb),
('mc:which of the following statements is true?:::creatinine is directly affected by dietary protein intake', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'which of the following statements is true?', 'Creatinine is directly affected by dietary protein intake', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"While serum creatinine is primarily a reflection of endogenous muscle mass, it can be significantly and directly affected by dietary protein intake, specifically through the consumption of cooked meat.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Creatinine is directly affected by dietary protein intake\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"While serum creatinine is primarily a reflection of endogenous muscle mass, it can be significantly and directly affected by dietary protein intake, specifically through the consumption of cooked meat.\n\nDuring the cooking process, the intense heat converts a portion of the creatine naturally present in animal muscle tissue into creatinine. When a patient consumes a cooked-meat meal, this pre-formed creatinine is rapidly absorbed across the gastrointestinal tract and enters the bloodstream.\n\nThis causes a temporary, artificial spike in serum creatinine levels independently of the patient''s actual kidney function. This dietary variable is clinically critical to recognize during nutrition and renal assessments, as a recent high-protein meat meal can skew laboratory results and lead to a falsely lower estimated Glomerular Filtration Rate (eGFR).","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: BUN can be used as the sole indicator of nutritional status, Cereals are acid-forming foods, None of the above"}'::jsonb),
('mc:which of the following statements is true?:::all of the above', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'which of the following statements is true?', 'All of the above', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"All three statements describe principles used in therapeutic diet planning for renal conditions. ","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"All of the above\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"All three statements describe principles used in therapeutic diet planning for renal conditions. \n\nCreatinine clearance is an indicator of renal filtration, and protein prescription in renal disease is adjusted according to the degree of renal impairment. As renal function declines, protein intake may need to be controlled to reduce nitrogenous waste production. Therefore, within the context of the diet-therapy principle tested here, better/higher creatinine clearance permits a less restricted protein intake, whereas greater renal impairment generally requires closer protein management. \n\nFor urinary stone management, oatmeal can be included in an oxalate-controlled diet because it is not among the foods classically considered very high in oxalate. \n\nThe statement regarding fats reflects the older diet-therapy teaching that high fat intake may promote urinary urate retention, particularly in the context of uric acid metabolism and stone formation.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: The higher the creatinine clearance, the greater the level of protein allowed., Oatmeal is allowed in oxalate-controlled diet, Fats favor the retention of urates"}'::jsonb),
('mc:this diet is known as the cholesterol- and saturated fat restricted diet:::tlc diet', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'this diet is known as the cholesterol- and saturated fat restricted diet', 'TLC diet', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"TLC stands for Therapeutic Lifestyle Changes. It is a clinically designed medical nutrition therapy created by the National Institutes of Health (NIH) specifically to lower high blood cholesterol and reduce the risk of heart disease. Its primary guidelines mandate restricting saturated fat to less than 7% of total daily calories and limiting dietary cholesterol to less than 200 mg per day.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"TLC diet\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"TLC stands for Therapeutic Lifestyle Changes. It is a clinically designed medical nutrition therapy created by the National Institutes of Health (NIH) specifically to lower high blood cholesterol and reduce the risk of heart disease. Its primary guidelines mandate restricting saturated fat to less than 7% of total daily calories and limiting dietary cholesterol to less than 200 mg per day.\n\n\t• Atkin''s diet & Ketogenic Diet: These are low-carbohydrate, high-fat diets. They encourage high consumption of fats (including saturated fats) and proteins, making them the opposite of a fat-restricted eating plan.\n\t• DASH diet: While DASH (Dietary Approaches to Stop Hypertension) is also a heart-healthy diet low in saturated fat, its definitive, hallmark feature is sodium restriction aimed at lowering blood pressure. The TLC diet is the classic textbook answer explicitly labeled as the cholesterol- and saturated fat-restricted diet.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Ketogenic Diet, DASH diet, Atkin''s diet"}'::jsonb),
('mc:this diet is known as the cholesterol- and saturated fat restricted diet:::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'this diet is known as the cholesterol- and saturated fat restricted diet', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"TLC stands for Therapeutic Lifestyle Changes. It is a clinically designed medical nutrition therapy created by the National Institutes of Health (NIH) specifically to lower high blood cholesterol and reduce the risk of heart disease. Its primary guidelines mandate restricting saturated fat to less than 7% of total daily calories and limiting dietary cholesterol to less than 200 mg per day.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"TLC diet\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"TLC stands for Therapeutic Lifestyle Changes. It is a clinically designed medical nutrition therapy created by the National Institutes of Health (NIH) specifically to lower high blood cholesterol and reduce the risk of heart disease. Its primary guidelines mandate restricting saturated fat to less than 7% of total daily calories and limiting dietary cholesterol to less than 200 mg per day.\n\n\t• Atkin''s diet & Ketogenic Diet: These are low-carbohydrate, high-fat diets. They encourage high consumption of fats (including saturated fats) and proteins, making them the opposite of a fat-restricted eating plan.\n\t• DASH diet: While DASH (Dietary Approaches to Stop Hypertension) is also a heart-healthy diet low in saturated fat, its definitive, hallmark feature is sodium restriction aimed at lowering blood pressure. The TLC diet is the classic textbook answer explicitly labeled as the cholesterol- and saturated fat-restricted diet.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Ketogenic Diet, DASH diet, Atkin''s diet"}'::jsonb),
('mc:which among the following has the highest sodium content?:::carrots', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'which among the following has the highest sodium content?', 'Carrots', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Among the choices, carrots have the highest sodium content based on the food-composition data underlying this question. Although all four are fresh vegetables and are generally considered low-sodium foods, their naturally occurring sodium concentrations are not identical. This is an important distinction in nutrition assessment and diet therapy: foods can be categorized as generally low in sodium while still having measurable differences in their sodium content. ","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Carrots\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Among the choices, carrots have the highest sodium content based on the food-composition data underlying this question. Although all four are fresh vegetables and are generally considered low-sodium foods, their naturally occurring sodium concentrations are not identical. This is an important distinction in nutrition assessment and diet therapy: foods can be categorized as generally low in sodium while still having measurable differences in their sodium content. \n\nFor NDLE purposes, this question is primarily testing knowledge of the relative sodium content of common vegetables, rather than asking which food is considered “high sodium” clinically. In a sodium-restricted diet, carrots, cabbage, sayote, and pake may all be used, with the major dietary sodium reduction usually coming from limiting salt, processed foods, canned products, seasoning mixes, sauces, and other sodium-containing condiments.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Cabbage, Sayote, Pake"}'::jsonb),
('mc:all the foods in the list are allowed among patients with celiac disease::::sour cream, dried beans, millet, plain gelatin', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'all the foods in the list are allowed among patients with celiac disease:', 'Sour cream, dried beans, millet, plain gelatin', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Patients diagnosed with celiac disease must strictly follow a lifelong gluten-free diet to prevent an autoimmune reaction that damages the villi of the small intestine. This means avoiding the proteins found in wheat, barley, and rye.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Sour cream, dried beans, millet, plain gelatin\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Patients diagnosed with celiac disease must strictly follow a lifelong gluten-free diet to prevent an autoimmune reaction that damages the villi of the small intestine. This means avoiding the proteins found in wheat, barley, and rye.\n\n• Sour cream: A plain dairy product that is naturally gluten-free.\n• Dried beans: Legumes that contain no gluten proteins.\n• Millet: A naturally gluten-free cereal grain that serves as a safe alternative to wheat.\n• Plain gelatin: A pure animal collagen product containing no grain fillers or gluten.\n\nWhy the Other Options contain forbidden foods:\n• Option A is incorrect because: Graham crackers are made from wheat flour, and Ovaltine contains malt extract derived from barley. Both will trigger a celiac flare-up. Vienna sausage also frequently uses wheat flour as a binder.\n• Option B is incorrect because: Breaded fish is coated in wheat-based breadcrumbs. Additionally, textured vegetable protein (TVP) can sometimes be cross-contaminated or derived from wheat gluten.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Textured vegetable protein, plain yogurt, breaded fish, popcorn, Tapioca starch, ovaltine, graham, Vienna sausage, None of the above"}'::jsonb),
('mc:all the foods in the list are allowed among patients with celiac disease::::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'all the foods in the list are allowed among patients with celiac disease:', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Patients diagnosed with celiac disease must strictly follow a lifelong gluten-free diet to prevent an autoimmune reaction that damages the villi of the small intestine. This means avoiding the proteins found in wheat, barley, and rye.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Sour cream, dried beans, millet, plain gelatin\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Patients diagnosed with celiac disease must strictly follow a lifelong gluten-free diet to prevent an autoimmune reaction that damages the villi of the small intestine. This means avoiding the proteins found in wheat, barley, and rye.\n\n• Sour cream: A plain dairy product that is naturally gluten-free.\n• Dried beans: Legumes that contain no gluten proteins.\n• Millet: A naturally gluten-free cereal grain that serves as a safe alternative to wheat.\n• Plain gelatin: A pure animal collagen product containing no grain fillers or gluten.\n\nWhy the Other Options contain forbidden foods:\n• Option A is incorrect because: Graham crackers are made from wheat flour, and Ovaltine contains malt extract derived from barley. Both will trigger a celiac flare-up. Vienna sausage also frequently uses wheat flour as a binder.\n• Option B is incorrect because: Breaded fish is coated in wheat-based breadcrumbs. Additionally, textured vegetable protein (TVP) can sometimes be cross-contaminated or derived from wheat gluten.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Textured vegetable protein, plain yogurt, breaded fish, popcorn, Tapioca starch, ovaltine, graham, Vienna sausage, None of the above"}'::jsonb),
('mc:all the foods in the list are not allowed among patients with celiac disease::::distilled white wine, vodka, vanilla ice cream in cone, crema de fruta', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'all the foods in the list are not allowed among patients with celiac disease:', 'Distilled white wine, vodka, vanilla ice cream in cone, crema de fruta', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"• Vanilla ice cream in a cone: Regular ice cream cones are made from wheat flour, which contains gluten and is highly toxic to individuals with celiac disease.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Distilled white wine, vodka, vanilla ice cream in cone, crema de fruta\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"• Vanilla ice cream in a cone: Regular ice cream cones are made from wheat flour, which contains gluten and is highly toxic to individuals with celiac disease.\n• Crema de fruta: This traditional Filipino dessert is natively constructed using layers of sponge cake (made with standard wheat flour) or graham crackers, both of which contain gluten and are strictly forbidden.\n\nBecause a person with celiac disease cannot consume wheat-based cones or traditional sponge cake/graham desserts, they cannot safely eat everything presented in this selection.\n\n• Other choices: These lists contain naturally gluten-free foods (like corn, maple syrup, wine, fresh vegetables, tofu, and lactose-free milk) which are generally allowed on a celiac diet. Therefore, they do not fulfill the criteria of a \"not allowed\" list.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Fresh vegetables washed in water with baking soda, margarine, tofu, Lactaid, Corn and maple syrup, fruit ices, sorbet, wine, All of the above"}'::jsonb),
('mc:all the foods in the list are not allowed among patients with celiac disease::::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'all the foods in the list are not allowed among patients with celiac disease:', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"• Vanilla ice cream in a cone: Regular ice cream cones are made from wheat flour, which contains gluten and is highly toxic to individuals with celiac disease.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Distilled white wine, vodka, vanilla ice cream in cone, crema de fruta\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"• Vanilla ice cream in a cone: Regular ice cream cones are made from wheat flour, which contains gluten and is highly toxic to individuals with celiac disease.\n• Crema de fruta: This traditional Filipino dessert is natively constructed using layers of sponge cake (made with standard wheat flour) or graham crackers, both of which contain gluten and are strictly forbidden.\n\nBecause a person with celiac disease cannot consume wheat-based cones or traditional sponge cake/graham desserts, they cannot safely eat everything presented in this selection.\n\n• Other choices: These lists contain naturally gluten-free foods (like corn, maple syrup, wine, fresh vegetables, tofu, and lactose-free milk) which are generally allowed on a celiac diet. Therefore, they do not fulfill the criteria of a \"not allowed\" list.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Fresh vegetables washed in water with baking soda, margarine, tofu, Lactaid, Corn and maple syrup, fruit ices, sorbet, wine, All of the above"}'::jsonb),
('mc:statements describing the management of celiac disease::::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'statements describing the management of celiac disease:', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"The cornerstone of celiac disease management is a strict, lifelong gluten-free diet. Gluten exposure triggers an immune-mediated response that can cause intestinal mucosal injury even when the patient does not experience obvious gastrointestinal symptoms. Therefore, the absence of symptoms does not mean that celiac disease has disappeared or that gluten can safely be reintroduced. Strict adherence to a gluten-free diet promotes intestinal healing, improves nutritional status, and reduces the risk of complications associated with ongoing gluten exposure.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"The management of celiac disease is a life-long gluten-free diet.\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"The cornerstone of celiac disease management is a strict, lifelong gluten-free diet. Gluten exposure triggers an immune-mediated response that can cause intestinal mucosal injury even when the patient does not experience obvious gastrointestinal symptoms. Therefore, the absence of symptoms does not mean that celiac disease has disappeared or that gluten can safely be reintroduced. Strict adherence to a gluten-free diet promotes intestinal healing, improves nutritional status, and reduces the risk of complications associated with ongoing gluten exposure.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Small quantities of gluten may be allowed when celiac disease no longer manifests., Enough scientific evidence exists for what constitutes a safe amount of gluten intake., All of the above"}'::jsonb),
('mc:pregnancy-induced hypertension is not::::a complication of diabetes', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'pregnancy-induced hypertension is not:', 'a complication of diabetes', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Diabetes mellitus (both preexisting type 1/type 2 diabetes and gestational diabetes) is a major risk factor that predisposes a woman to developing pregnancy-induced hypertension (PIH). However, PIH itself is a specific vascular disorder that arises directly from placental dysfunction and endothelial stress, not a medical complication caused by diabetes.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"a complication of diabetes\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Diabetes mellitus (both preexisting type 1/type 2 diabetes and gestational diabetes) is a major risk factor that predisposes a woman to developing pregnancy-induced hypertension (PIH). However, PIH itself is a specific vascular disorder that arises directly from placental dysfunction and endothelial stress, not a medical complication caused by diabetes.\n\n\t• Characterized by edema: PIH is classically identified in board exam frameworks by a hallmark triad of symptoms: hypertension, generalized or pitting edema, and proteinuria.\n\t• A complication of pregnancy: PIH is, by definition, a major medical complication that specifically occurs during pregnancy (typically developing after the 20th week of gestation).\n\t• Characterized by hypertension: Elevated blood pressure (systolic BP equal to or greater than 140 mmHg or diastolic BP equal to or greater than 90 mmHg) is the primary diagnostic feature of the condition.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: a complication of pregnancy, characterized by hypertension, characterized by edema"}'::jsonb),
('mc:pregnancy-induced hypertension is not::::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'pregnancy-induced hypertension is not:', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Diabetes mellitus (both preexisting type 1/type 2 diabetes and gestational diabetes) is a major risk factor that predisposes a woman to developing pregnancy-induced hypertension (PIH). However, PIH itself is a specific vascular disorder that arises directly from placental dysfunction and endothelial stress, not a medical complication caused by diabetes.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"a complication of diabetes\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Diabetes mellitus (both preexisting type 1/type 2 diabetes and gestational diabetes) is a major risk factor that predisposes a woman to developing pregnancy-induced hypertension (PIH). However, PIH itself is a specific vascular disorder that arises directly from placental dysfunction and endothelial stress, not a medical complication caused by diabetes.\n\n\t• Characterized by edema: PIH is classically identified in board exam frameworks by a hallmark triad of symptoms: hypertension, generalized or pitting edema, and proteinuria.\n\t• A complication of pregnancy: PIH is, by definition, a major medical complication that specifically occurs during pregnancy (typically developing after the 20th week of gestation).\n\t• Characterized by hypertension: Elevated blood pressure (systolic BP equal to or greater than 140 mmHg or diastolic BP equal to or greater than 90 mmHg) is the primary diagnostic feature of the condition.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: a complication of pregnancy, characterized by hypertension, characterized by edema"}'::jsonb),
('mc:the addition of 15 grams salt in the diet increases sodium content by::::6 grams', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'the addition of 15 grams salt in the diet increases sodium content by:', '6 grams', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Table salt is chemically known as sodium chloride (NaCl). By molecular weight, pure table salt is composed of approximately 40% sodium and 60% chloride.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"6 grams\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Table salt is chemically known as sodium chloride (NaCl). By molecular weight, pure table salt is composed of approximately 40% sodium and 60% chloride.\n\nTo determine how much sodium is present in a given mass of table salt, you multiply the total mass by 40% (0.40):\n15 grams of salt x 0.40 = 6 grams of sodium","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: 3,000 mg, 2 grams, 8 grams"}'::jsonb),
('mc:the main goal of the krebs cycle is to produce h⁺ and electrons. these by-products are then transferred to the::::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'the main goal of the krebs cycle is to produce h⁺ and electrons. these by-products are then transferred to the:', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"The Krebs cycle (citric acid cycle/TCA cycle) does not directly produce large amounts of ATP. Its major role is to oxidize acetyl-CoA and capture energy in the form of the reduced electron carriers NADH and FADH₂. These carriers transport high-energy electrons to the electron transport chain (ETC) located in the inner mitochondrial membrane. As electrons move through the ETC, energy is used to pump H⁺ across the inner mitochondrial membrane, creating a proton gradient that drives ATP synthesis through oxidative phosphorylation. ","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Electron Transport Chain\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"The Krebs cycle (citric acid cycle/TCA cycle) does not directly produce large amounts of ATP. Its major role is to oxidize acetyl-CoA and capture energy in the form of the reduced electron carriers NADH and FADH₂. These carriers transport high-energy electrons to the electron transport chain (ETC) located in the inner mitochondrial membrane. As electrons move through the ETC, energy is used to pump H⁺ across the inner mitochondrial membrane, creating a proton gradient that drives ATP synthesis through oxidative phosphorylation. \n\nFor NDLE purposes, the wording “H⁺ and electrons” is a little imprecise. The Krebs cycle primarily produces NADH and FADH₂, which carry the high-energy electrons to the ETC. The Krebs cycle also contributes indirectly to proton generation through subsequent oxidative phosphorylation, but free H⁺ is not its principal product.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Embden-Meyerhoff Pathway, Citric Acid Cycle, Cori Cycle"}'::jsonb),
('mc:vitamin/s known to be at low levels in patients following gluten-free diets:::all of the above', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'vitamin/s known to be at low levels in patients following gluten-free diets', 'All of the above', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Traditional gluten-containing wheat flour products are heavily fortified and enriched by law with crucial micronutrients, including thiamine, riboflavin, niacin, iron, and folic acid (folate). In contrast, commercially available gluten-free substitute products are frequently manufactured using refined starches (such as tapioca, corn, or rice starch) that are not routinely fortified, leading to chronic dietary deficits.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"All of the above\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Traditional gluten-containing wheat flour products are heavily fortified and enriched by law with crucial micronutrients, including thiamine, riboflavin, niacin, iron, and folic acid (folate). In contrast, commercially available gluten-free substitute products are frequently manufactured using refined starches (such as tapioca, corn, or rice starch) that are not routinely fortified, leading to chronic dietary deficits.\n\n• Folate (Vitamin B9): Folate deficiency is exceptionally common among individuals adhering to a gluten-free diet (GFD) due to the absence of enriched wheat products, coupled with a typically low intake of alternative whole grains.\n• Vitamin B6 and Vitamin B12: Multiple nutritional monitoring studies and clinical reviews indicate that patients on a strict GFD for extended periods show suboptimal plasma concentrations of Vitamin B6 and Vitamin B12. In patients with celiac disease, initial B12 deficiency stems from mucosal malabsorption in the ileum, which can persist if the diet is structurally restrictive or lacking in variety.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Folate, Vit B6, Vit B12, Vit B12, folate"}'::jsonb),
('mc:vitamin/s known to be at low levels in patients following gluten-free diets:::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'vitamin/s known to be at low levels in patients following gluten-free diets', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Traditional gluten-containing wheat flour products are heavily fortified and enriched by law with crucial micronutrients, including thiamine, riboflavin, niacin, iron, and folic acid (folate). In contrast, commercially available gluten-free substitute products are frequently manufactured using refined starches (such as tapioca, corn, or rice starch) that are not routinely fortified, leading to chronic dietary deficits.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"All of the above\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Traditional gluten-containing wheat flour products are heavily fortified and enriched by law with crucial micronutrients, including thiamine, riboflavin, niacin, iron, and folic acid (folate). In contrast, commercially available gluten-free substitute products are frequently manufactured using refined starches (such as tapioca, corn, or rice starch) that are not routinely fortified, leading to chronic dietary deficits.\n\n• Folate (Vitamin B9): Folate deficiency is exceptionally common among individuals adhering to a gluten-free diet (GFD) due to the absence of enriched wheat products, coupled with a typically low intake of alternative whole grains.\n• Vitamin B6 and Vitamin B12: Multiple nutritional monitoring studies and clinical reviews indicate that patients on a strict GFD for extended periods show suboptimal plasma concentrations of Vitamin B6 and Vitamin B12. In patients with celiac disease, initial B12 deficiency stems from mucosal malabsorption in the ileum, which can persist if the diet is structurally restrictive or lacking in variety.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Folate, Vit B6, Vit B12, Vit B12, folate"}'::jsonb),
('mc:it is formed when two amino acids are bonded together::::dipeptide', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'it is formed when two amino acids are bonded together:', 'Dipeptide', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"A dipeptide is formed when two amino acids are joined by a peptide bond. This occurs through a condensation (dehydration) reaction in which the carboxyl group of one amino acid reacts with the amino group of another, releasing one molecule of water. The resulting molecule contains two amino acid residues linked by one peptide bond. ","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Dipeptide\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"A dipeptide is formed when two amino acids are joined by a peptide bond. This occurs through a condensation (dehydration) reaction in which the carboxyl group of one amino acid reacts with the amino group of another, releasing one molecule of water. The resulting molecule contains two amino acid residues linked by one peptide bond. \n\nFor NDLE purposes, remember the progression: \n2 amino acids = dipeptide; 3 amino acids = tripeptide; several amino acids = oligopeptide; long chains = polypeptide/protein. \n\nThe number of peptide bonds is also important: two amino acids form one peptide bond, three amino acids form two peptide bonds, and so forth.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Alanine, Dissacharide, Protein"}'::jsonb),
('mc:it is formed when two amino acids are bonded together::::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'it is formed when two amino acids are bonded together:', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"A dipeptide is formed when two amino acids are joined by a peptide bond. This occurs through a condensation (dehydration) reaction in which the carboxyl group of one amino acid reacts with the amino group of another, releasing one molecule of water. The resulting molecule contains two amino acid residues linked by one peptide bond. ","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Dipeptide\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"A dipeptide is formed when two amino acids are joined by a peptide bond. This occurs through a condensation (dehydration) reaction in which the carboxyl group of one amino acid reacts with the amino group of another, releasing one molecule of water. The resulting molecule contains two amino acid residues linked by one peptide bond. \n\nFor NDLE purposes, remember the progression: \n2 amino acids = dipeptide; 3 amino acids = tripeptide; several amino acids = oligopeptide; long chains = polypeptide/protein. \n\nThe number of peptide bonds is also important: two amino acids form one peptide bond, three amino acids form two peptide bonds, and so forth.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Alanine, Dissacharide, Protein"}'::jsonb),
('mc:the main goal of the krebs cycle is to produce h⁺ and electrons. these by-products are then transferred to the::::electron transport chain', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'the main goal of the krebs cycle is to produce h⁺ and electrons. these by-products are then transferred to the:', 'Electron Transport Chain', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"The Krebs cycle (citric acid cycle/TCA cycle) does not directly produce large amounts of ATP. Its major role is to oxidize acetyl-CoA and capture energy in the form of the reduced electron carriers NADH and FADH₂. These carriers transport high-energy electrons to the electron transport chain (ETC) located in the inner mitochondrial membrane. As electrons move through the ETC, energy is used to pump H⁺ across the inner mitochondrial membrane, creating a proton gradient that drives ATP synthesis through oxidative phosphorylation. ","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Electron Transport Chain\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"The Krebs cycle (citric acid cycle/TCA cycle) does not directly produce large amounts of ATP. Its major role is to oxidize acetyl-CoA and capture energy in the form of the reduced electron carriers NADH and FADH₂. These carriers transport high-energy electrons to the electron transport chain (ETC) located in the inner mitochondrial membrane. As electrons move through the ETC, energy is used to pump H⁺ across the inner mitochondrial membrane, creating a proton gradient that drives ATP synthesis through oxidative phosphorylation. \n\nFor NDLE purposes, the wording “H⁺ and electrons” is a little imprecise. The Krebs cycle primarily produces NADH and FADH₂, which carry the high-energy electrons to the ETC. The Krebs cycle also contributes indirectly to proton generation through subsequent oxidative phosphorylation, but free H⁺ is not its principal product.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Embden-Meyerhoff Pathway, Citric Acid Cycle, Cori Cycle"}'::jsonb),
('mc:factors that improve ldl to hdl ratio except::::higher intakes of saturated fatty acids', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'factors that improve ldl to hdl ratio except:', 'Higher intakes of saturated fatty acids', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Consuming higher amounts of saturated fatty acids raises both low-density lipoprotein (LDL) and high-density lipoprotein (HDL) cholesterol. However, it drives a much larger increase in LDL (\"bad\") cholesterol compared to HDL. This selectively worsens the overall LDL-to-HDL ratio, increasing cardiovascular risk.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Higher intakes of saturated fatty acids\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Consuming higher amounts of saturated fatty acids raises both low-density lipoprotein (LDL) and high-density lipoprotein (HDL) cholesterol. However, it drives a much larger increase in LDL (\"bad\") cholesterol compared to HDL. This selectively worsens the overall LDL-to-HDL ratio, increasing cardiovascular risk.\n\n• Moderate R-OH (Alcohol) Consumption: In medical terminology, \"R-OH\" stands for alcohol. Scientific literature consistently shows that light-to-moderate alcohol intake (such as one drink a day for women or two for men) can moderately elevate HDL cholesterol levels, which helps optimize the blood lipid ratio.\n• Antioxidants: Dietary antioxidants (found in abundance in colorful fruits, vegetables, and whole grains) shield circulating LDL particles from undergoing oxidative modification. Because oxidized LDL is what triggers plaque accumulation in artery walls, antioxidants maintain healthy vascular transport and protect the functional efficiency of lipid handling.\n• Weight Control: Dropping excess body weight and avoiding abdominal obesity are highly effective ways to manage dyslipidemia. Achieving a healthy weight directly reduces total triglycerides and lowers LDL cholesterol levels while boosting cardioprotective HDL.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Weight control, Antioxidants, Moderate R-OH consumption"}'::jsonb),
('mc:factors that improve ldl to hdl ratio except::::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'factors that improve ldl to hdl ratio except:', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Consuming higher amounts of saturated fatty acids raises both low-density lipoprotein (LDL) and high-density lipoprotein (HDL) cholesterol. However, it drives a much larger increase in LDL (\"bad\") cholesterol compared to HDL. This selectively worsens the overall LDL-to-HDL ratio, increasing cardiovascular risk.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Higher intakes of saturated fatty acids\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Consuming higher amounts of saturated fatty acids raises both low-density lipoprotein (LDL) and high-density lipoprotein (HDL) cholesterol. However, it drives a much larger increase in LDL (\"bad\") cholesterol compared to HDL. This selectively worsens the overall LDL-to-HDL ratio, increasing cardiovascular risk.\n\n• Moderate R-OH (Alcohol) Consumption: In medical terminology, \"R-OH\" stands for alcohol. Scientific literature consistently shows that light-to-moderate alcohol intake (such as one drink a day for women or two for men) can moderately elevate HDL cholesterol levels, which helps optimize the blood lipid ratio.\n• Antioxidants: Dietary antioxidants (found in abundance in colorful fruits, vegetables, and whole grains) shield circulating LDL particles from undergoing oxidative modification. Because oxidized LDL is what triggers plaque accumulation in artery walls, antioxidants maintain healthy vascular transport and protect the functional efficiency of lipid handling.\n• Weight Control: Dropping excess body weight and avoiding abdominal obesity are highly effective ways to manage dyslipidemia. Achieving a healthy weight directly reduces total triglycerides and lowers LDL cholesterol levels while boosting cardioprotective HDL.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Weight control, Antioxidants, Moderate R-OH consumption"}'::jsonb),
('mc:sources of trans fatty acid except::::sardines', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'sources of trans fatty acid except:', 'Sardines', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Trans fatty acids can occur naturally in small amounts in milk and butter because ruminant animals produce naturally occurring trans fats through microbial fermentation in the rumen. Trans fats can also be present in commercial baked products such as cookies, particularly when partially hydrogenated oils are used, although their use has been substantially reduced or eliminated in many countries.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Sardines\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Trans fatty acids can occur naturally in small amounts in milk and butter because ruminant animals produce naturally occurring trans fats through microbial fermentation in the rumen. Trans fats can also be present in commercial baked products such as cookies, particularly when partially hydrogenated oils are used, although their use has been substantially reduced or eliminated in many countries.\n\nSardines, on the other hand, are oily fish and are primarily valued as sources of omega-3 polyunsaturated fatty acids, particularly EPA and DHA. Although naturally occurring trans fatty acids can be detected in various foods in very small amounts, sardines are not considered a meaningful dietary source of trans fat.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Cookies, Milk, Butter"}'::jsonb),
('mc:sources of trans fatty acid except::::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'sources of trans fatty acid except:', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Trans fatty acids can occur naturally in small amounts in milk and butter because ruminant animals produce naturally occurring trans fats through microbial fermentation in the rumen. Trans fats can also be present in commercial baked products such as cookies, particularly when partially hydrogenated oils are used, although their use has been substantially reduced or eliminated in many countries.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Sardines\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Trans fatty acids can occur naturally in small amounts in milk and butter because ruminant animals produce naturally occurring trans fats through microbial fermentation in the rumen. Trans fats can also be present in commercial baked products such as cookies, particularly when partially hydrogenated oils are used, although their use has been substantially reduced or eliminated in many countries.\n\nSardines, on the other hand, are oily fish and are primarily valued as sources of omega-3 polyunsaturated fatty acids, particularly EPA and DHA. Although naturally occurring trans fatty acids can be detected in various foods in very small amounts, sardines are not considered a meaningful dietary source of trans fat.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Cookies, Milk, Butter"}'::jsonb),
('mc:the soul of every foodservice operation.:::menu', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'the soul of every foodservice operation.', 'Menu', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"In foodservice management, the menu is universally referred to as the \"soul,\" focal point, or primary control of the entire operation.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Menu\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"In foodservice management, the menu is universally referred to as the \"soul,\" focal point, or primary control of the entire operation.\n\nIt dictates everything that happens in the facility—including purchasing, budget, required equipment, staff skill levels, kitchen layout, and guest satisfaction.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Dietitian, Meal Plan, Income generation"}'::jsonb),
('mc:allowed amount of purine in a purine-restricted diet::::150 mg', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'allowed amount of purine in a purine-restricted diet:', '150 mg', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"A purine-restricted diet is designed to reduce dietary purine exposure in patients with gout, hyperuricemia, or uric acid–related conditions. Traditional diet manuals commonly identify foods containing 150 mg or more of purine per serving as foods to be restricted or avoided. Thus, 150 mg is an important threshold associated with purine-restricted diet teaching.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"150 mg\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"A purine-restricted diet is designed to reduce dietary purine exposure in patients with gout, hyperuricemia, or uric acid–related conditions. Traditional diet manuals commonly identify foods containing 150 mg or more of purine per serving as foods to be restricted or avoided. Thus, 150 mg is an important threshold associated with purine-restricted diet teaching.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: 250 mg, 300 mg, 0 mg"}'::jsonb),
('mc:allowed amount of purine in a purine-restricted diet::::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'allowed amount of purine in a purine-restricted diet:', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"A purine-restricted diet is designed to reduce dietary purine exposure in patients with gout, hyperuricemia, or uric acid–related conditions. Traditional diet manuals commonly identify foods containing 150 mg or more of purine per serving as foods to be restricted or avoided. Thus, 150 mg is an important threshold associated with purine-restricted diet teaching.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"150 mg\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"A purine-restricted diet is designed to reduce dietary purine exposure in patients with gout, hyperuricemia, or uric acid–related conditions. Traditional diet manuals commonly identify foods containing 150 mg or more of purine per serving as foods to be restricted or avoided. Thus, 150 mg is an important threshold associated with purine-restricted diet teaching.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: 250 mg, 300 mg, 0 mg"}'::jsonb),
('mc:vitamin e maintains prothrombin level in the blood plasma. vitamin k is an anticoagulant.:::both statements are incorrect.', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'vitamin e maintains prothrombin level in the blood plasma. vitamin k is an anticoagulant.', 'Both statements are incorrect.', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Statement 1 is incorrect. Vitamin K, not vitamin E, is essential for the synthesis of several blood-clotting proteins, including prothrombin (factor II), in the liver. Vitamin K functions as a cofactor for the γ-carboxylation of specific glutamate residues on vitamin K–dependent clotting factors, allowing them to participate effectively in coagulation. Vitamin E, in contrast, is primarily recognized for its antioxidant function and does not maintain plasma prothrombin levels as its physiological role. ","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Both statements are incorrect.\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Statement 1 is incorrect. Vitamin K, not vitamin E, is essential for the synthesis of several blood-clotting proteins, including prothrombin (factor II), in the liver. Vitamin K functions as a cofactor for the γ-carboxylation of specific glutamate residues on vitamin K–dependent clotting factors, allowing them to participate effectively in coagulation. Vitamin E, in contrast, is primarily recognized for its antioxidant function and does not maintain plasma prothrombin levels as its physiological role. \n\nStatement 2 is also incorrect. Vitamin K is not an anticoagulant; it supports normal blood coagulation. Vitamin K deficiency can impair clotting-factor activation and increase bleeding tendency. In fact, warfarin, an anticoagulant medication, works partly by inhibiting vitamin K recycling, thereby reducing the activity of vitamin K–dependent clotting factors.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Only statement 1 is correct., Only statement 2 is correct., Both statements are correct."}'::jsonb),
('mc:vitamin e maintains prothrombin level in the blood plasma. vitamin k is an anticoagulant.:::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'vitamin e maintains prothrombin level in the blood plasma. vitamin k is an anticoagulant.', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Statement 1 is incorrect. Vitamin K, not vitamin E, is essential for the synthesis of several blood-clotting proteins, including prothrombin (factor II), in the liver. Vitamin K functions as a cofactor for the γ-carboxylation of specific glutamate residues on vitamin K–dependent clotting factors, allowing them to participate effectively in coagulation. Vitamin E, in contrast, is primarily recognized for its antioxidant function and does not maintain plasma prothrombin levels as its physiological role. ","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Both statements are incorrect.\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Statement 1 is incorrect. Vitamin K, not vitamin E, is essential for the synthesis of several blood-clotting proteins, including prothrombin (factor II), in the liver. Vitamin K functions as a cofactor for the γ-carboxylation of specific glutamate residues on vitamin K–dependent clotting factors, allowing them to participate effectively in coagulation. Vitamin E, in contrast, is primarily recognized for its antioxidant function and does not maintain plasma prothrombin levels as its physiological role. \n\nStatement 2 is also incorrect. Vitamin K is not an anticoagulant; it supports normal blood coagulation. Vitamin K deficiency can impair clotting-factor activation and increase bleeding tendency. In fact, warfarin, an anticoagulant medication, works partly by inhibiting vitamin K recycling, thereby reducing the activity of vitamin K–dependent clotting factors.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Only statement 1 is correct., Only statement 2 is correct., Both statements are correct."}'::jsonb),
('mc:the soul of every foodservice operation.:::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'the soul of every foodservice operation.', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"In foodservice management, the menu is universally referred to as the \"soul,\" focal point, or primary control of the entire operation.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Menu\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"In foodservice management, the menu is universally referred to as the \"soul,\" focal point, or primary control of the entire operation.\n\nIt dictates everything that happens in the facility—including purchasing, budget, required equipment, staff skill levels, kitchen layout, and guest satisfaction.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Dietitian, Meal Plan, Income generation"}'::jsonb),
('mc:a recommended dietary allowance (rda) represents the::::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'a recommended dietary allowance (rda) represents the:', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"The Recommended Dietary Allowance (RDA) is the average daily nutrient intake level that is sufficient to meet the nutrient requirements of nearly all (97–98%) healthy individuals in a particular age, sex, and life-stage group. It is primarily used as a goal for planning nutritionally adequate diets for individuals. Importantly, the RDA is not literally enough for 100% of people, because individual nutrient requirements vary. ","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Average amount of a nutrient considered adequate to meet the nutrient needs of all healthy people\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"The Recommended Dietary Allowance (RDA) is the average daily nutrient intake level that is sufficient to meet the nutrient requirements of nearly all (97–98%) healthy individuals in a particular age, sex, and life-stage group. It is primarily used as a goal for planning nutritionally adequate diets for individuals. Importantly, the RDA is not literally enough for 100% of people, because individual nutrient requirements vary. \n\nThe RDA is derived from the Estimated Average Requirement (EAR). When the requirement distribution is approximately normal, the RDA is generally set at the EAR plus two standard deviations, thereby covering the requirements of approximately 97–98% of healthy individuals. In clinical and community nutrition practice, RDAs are useful for planning diets, while the EAR is generally more appropriate for assessing the adequacy of nutrient intakes in groups.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Highest amount of a nutrient that appears safe for most healthy people, Average amount of a nutrient that will maintain a specific biochemical or physiological function in half of the population, Lowest amount of a nutrient considered adequate to meet the nutrient needs of all healthy people"}'::jsonb),
('mc:the enzyme sucrase acts on sucrose to produce::::glucose and fructose', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'the enzyme sucrase acts on sucrose to produce:', 'Glucose and fructose', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Sucrose is a disaccharide composed of one molecule of glucose and one molecule of fructose. The brush-border enzyme sucrase hydrolyzes the glycosidic bond in sucrose, releasing glucose and fructose as the final monosaccharide products. These monosaccharides can then be absorbed by the small intestine through specific transport mechanisms. ","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Glucose and fructose\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Sucrose is a disaccharide composed of one molecule of glucose and one molecule of fructose. The brush-border enzyme sucrase hydrolyzes the glycosidic bond in sucrose, releasing glucose and fructose as the final monosaccharide products. These monosaccharides can then be absorbed by the small intestine through specific transport mechanisms. \n\nSucrase is part of the intestinal sucrase-isomaltase complex located on the brush border of enterocytes. The resulting glucose and fructose are absorbed primarily in the small intestine and enter the portal circulation, with glucose and fructose using different transport proteins. This illustrates the importance of brush-border enzymes in completing carbohydrate digestion before absorption.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Glucose and galactose, Glucose, Maltose"}'::jsonb),
('mc:the enzyme sucrase acts on sucrose to produce::::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'the enzyme sucrase acts on sucrose to produce:', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Sucrose is a disaccharide composed of one molecule of glucose and one molecule of fructose. The brush-border enzyme sucrase hydrolyzes the glycosidic bond in sucrose, releasing glucose and fructose as the final monosaccharide products. These monosaccharides can then be absorbed by the small intestine through specific transport mechanisms. ","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Glucose and fructose\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Sucrose is a disaccharide composed of one molecule of glucose and one molecule of fructose. The brush-border enzyme sucrase hydrolyzes the glycosidic bond in sucrose, releasing glucose and fructose as the final monosaccharide products. These monosaccharides can then be absorbed by the small intestine through specific transport mechanisms. \n\nSucrase is part of the intestinal sucrase-isomaltase complex located on the brush border of enterocytes. The resulting glucose and fructose are absorbed primarily in the small intestine and enter the portal circulation, with glucose and fructose using different transport proteins. This illustrates the importance of brush-border enzymes in completing carbohydrate digestion before absorption.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Glucose and galactose, Glucose, Maltose"}'::jsonb),
('mc:the enzyme pepsin is not denatured in the stomach despite the very acidic environment due to hydrochloric acid (hcl). how can this be?:::pepsin is secreted in its precursor form, pepsinogen', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'the enzyme pepsin is not denatured in the stomach despite the very acidic environment due to hydrochloric acid (hcl). how can this be?', 'Pepsin is secreted in its precursor form, pepsinogen', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"The stomach avoids auto-digestion and protects its own mucosal lining from premature proteolysis (protein breakdown) by not secreting active pepsin directly. Instead, gastric chief cells synthesize and secrete pepsinogen, which is an inactive precursor or zymogen.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Pepsin is secreted in its precursor form, pepsinogen\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"The stomach avoids auto-digestion and protects its own mucosal lining from premature proteolysis (protein breakdown) by not secreting active pepsin directly. Instead, gastric chief cells synthesize and secrete pepsinogen, which is an inactive precursor or zymogen.\n\nOnce pepsinogen enters the gastric lumen, the strongly acidic environment created by hydrochloric acid (HCl) causes the protein chain to unfold. This allows pepsinogen to cleave itself, initiating its conversion into the active, functional enzyme pepsin precisely where it is needed to digest dietary proteins.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Pepsin is the only protein enzyme that functions best in an acidic environment, Pepsin is able to resist denaturation by increasing the pH within the stomach, making it less acidic, Pepsin is also digested along with other proteins in the stomach"}'::jsonb),
('mc:the enzyme pepsin is not denatured in the stomach despite the very acidic environment due to hydrochloric acid (hcl). how can this be?:::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'the enzyme pepsin is not denatured in the stomach despite the very acidic environment due to hydrochloric acid (hcl). how can this be?', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"The stomach avoids auto-digestion and protects its own mucosal lining from premature proteolysis (protein breakdown) by not secreting active pepsin directly. Instead, gastric chief cells synthesize and secrete pepsinogen, which is an inactive precursor or zymogen.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Pepsin is secreted in its precursor form, pepsinogen\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"The stomach avoids auto-digestion and protects its own mucosal lining from premature proteolysis (protein breakdown) by not secreting active pepsin directly. Instead, gastric chief cells synthesize and secrete pepsinogen, which is an inactive precursor or zymogen.\n\nOnce pepsinogen enters the gastric lumen, the strongly acidic environment created by hydrochloric acid (HCl) causes the protein chain to unfold. This allows pepsinogen to cleave itself, initiating its conversion into the active, functional enzyme pepsin precisely where it is needed to digest dietary proteins.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Pepsin is the only protein enzyme that functions best in an acidic environment, Pepsin is able to resist denaturation by increasing the pH within the stomach, making it less acidic, Pepsin is also digested along with other proteins in the stomach"}'::jsonb),
('mc:nutrients that leave the git via the lymphatic system::::fats and fat-soluble vitamins', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'nutrients that leave the git via the lymphatic system:', 'Fats and fat-soluble vitamins', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"During digestion in the small intestine, long-chain fatty acids and fat-soluble vitamins (Vitamins A, D, E, and K) are packed into spherical lipid clusters called micelles. Once absorbed into the intestinal lining cells (enterocytes), they are re-assembled into even larger lipoprotein spheres called chylomicrogens. Because these structures are too bulky to fit through the tiny pores (fenestrations) of standard blood capillaries, they cannot enter the bloodstream directly.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Fats and fat-soluble vitamins\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"During digestion in the small intestine, long-chain fatty acids and fat-soluble vitamins (Vitamins A, D, E, and K) are packed into spherical lipid clusters called micelles. Once absorbed into the intestinal lining cells (enterocytes), they are re-assembled into even larger lipoprotein spheres called chylomicrogens. Because these structures are too bulky to fit through the tiny pores (fenestrations) of standard blood capillaries, they cannot enter the bloodstream directly.\n\nInstead, these large lipid packages slip into lacteals—specialized, wide-pored lymphatic capillaries located at the center of each intestinal villus. The fluid (called chyle) travels slowly through the lymphatic system, completely bypassing the liver at first, before emptying directly into the main bloodstream via the thoracic duct near the heart.\n\n• Water, Proteins, Minerals, and Water-Soluble Vitamins: These nutrients are water-soluble and split into small, single molecules (like glucose, amino acids, and free ions). Because they are small enough to pass easily through the pores of regular capillaries, they exit the GI tract straight into the portal blood circulation, traveling directly to the liver for immediate processing.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Proteins and minerals, Water and minerals, All vitamins and minerals"}'::jsonb),
('mc:nutrients that leave the git via the lymphatic system::::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'nutrients that leave the git via the lymphatic system:', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"During digestion in the small intestine, long-chain fatty acids and fat-soluble vitamins (Vitamins A, D, E, and K) are packed into spherical lipid clusters called micelles. Once absorbed into the intestinal lining cells (enterocytes), they are re-assembled into even larger lipoprotein spheres called chylomicrogens. Because these structures are too bulky to fit through the tiny pores (fenestrations) of standard blood capillaries, they cannot enter the bloodstream directly.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Fats and fat-soluble vitamins\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"During digestion in the small intestine, long-chain fatty acids and fat-soluble vitamins (Vitamins A, D, E, and K) are packed into spherical lipid clusters called micelles. Once absorbed into the intestinal lining cells (enterocytes), they are re-assembled into even larger lipoprotein spheres called chylomicrogens. Because these structures are too bulky to fit through the tiny pores (fenestrations) of standard blood capillaries, they cannot enter the bloodstream directly.\n\nInstead, these large lipid packages slip into lacteals—specialized, wide-pored lymphatic capillaries located at the center of each intestinal villus. The fluid (called chyle) travels slowly through the lymphatic system, completely bypassing the liver at first, before emptying directly into the main bloodstream via the thoracic duct near the heart.\n\n• Water, Proteins, Minerals, and Water-Soluble Vitamins: These nutrients are water-soluble and split into small, single molecules (like glucose, amino acids, and free ions). Because they are small enough to pass easily through the pores of regular capillaries, they exit the GI tract straight into the portal blood circulation, traveling directly to the liver for immediate processing.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Proteins and minerals, Water and minerals, All vitamins and minerals"}'::jsonb),
('mc:in conditions characterized by hypochlorhydria or achlorhydria, a suitable diet is one that is::::easy to digest or consists of predigested protein like protein hydrolysates', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'in conditions characterized by hypochlorhydria or achlorhydria, a suitable diet is one that is:', 'easy to digest or consists of predigested protein like protein hydrolysates', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Hypochlorhydria refers to reduced gastric acid secretion, while achlorhydria refers to an absence or near-absence of hydrochloric acid (HCl) in the stomach. Gastric acid contributes to the denaturation of dietary proteins and facilitates the conversion of pepsinogen to pepsin, which begins protein digestion in the stomach. When gastric acid is markedly reduced or absent, protein digestion in the stomach may be impaired. ","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"easy to digest or consists of predigested protein like protein hydrolysates\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Hypochlorhydria refers to reduced gastric acid secretion, while achlorhydria refers to an absence or near-absence of hydrochloric acid (HCl) in the stomach. Gastric acid contributes to the denaturation of dietary proteins and facilitates the conversion of pepsinogen to pepsin, which begins protein digestion in the stomach. When gastric acid is markedly reduced or absent, protein digestion in the stomach may be impaired. \n\nTherefore, traditionally, a diet emphasizing easily digested foods and, when clinically indicated, predigested protein such as protein hydrolysates may be recommended. However, the important modern clinical point is that hypochlorhydria or achlorhydria does not automatically require a low-protein diet or routine use of protein hydrolysates. Protein digestion continues in the small intestine through pancreatic and brush-border enzymes. Management should focus on the underlying cause and the patient''s nutritional status.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: low in fiber, low in protein, acid-ash"}'::jsonb),
('mc:in conditions characterized by hypochlorhydria or achlorhydria, a suitable diet is one that is::::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'in conditions characterized by hypochlorhydria or achlorhydria, a suitable diet is one that is:', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Hypochlorhydria refers to reduced gastric acid secretion, while achlorhydria refers to an absence or near-absence of hydrochloric acid (HCl) in the stomach. Gastric acid contributes to the denaturation of dietary proteins and facilitates the conversion of pepsinogen to pepsin, which begins protein digestion in the stomach. When gastric acid is markedly reduced or absent, protein digestion in the stomach may be impaired. ","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"easy to digest or consists of predigested protein like protein hydrolysates\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Hypochlorhydria refers to reduced gastric acid secretion, while achlorhydria refers to an absence or near-absence of hydrochloric acid (HCl) in the stomach. Gastric acid contributes to the denaturation of dietary proteins and facilitates the conversion of pepsinogen to pepsin, which begins protein digestion in the stomach. When gastric acid is markedly reduced or absent, protein digestion in the stomach may be impaired. \n\nTherefore, traditionally, a diet emphasizing easily digested foods and, when clinically indicated, predigested protein such as protein hydrolysates may be recommended. However, the important modern clinical point is that hypochlorhydria or achlorhydria does not automatically require a low-protein diet or routine use of protein hydrolysates. Protein digestion continues in the small intestine through pancreatic and brush-border enzymes. Management should focus on the underlying cause and the patient''s nutritional status.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: low in fiber, low in protein, acid-ash"}'::jsonb)
