('mc:a diagram shows a server approaching a seated guest with a beverage. according to conventional service, from which side should the beverage generally be served?:::guest''s right', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'a diagram shows a server approaching a seated guest with a beverage. according to conventional service, from which side should the beverage generally be served?', 'Guest''s right', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"In conventional table service, beverages are generally served from the guest’s right side. The server should approach carefully without reaching across the guest and place the beverage on the table from the right. ","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Guest''s right\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"In conventional table service, beverages are generally served from the guest’s right side. The server should approach carefully without reaching across the guest and place the beverage on the table from the right. \n\n• Guest’s left: generally associated with serving certain foods, particularly when food is presented from a platter.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Guest''s left, Directly from the back, Directly from the front"}'::jsonb),
('mc:a diagram shows a server approaching a seated guest with a beverage. according to conventional service, from which side should the beverage generally be served?:::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'a diagram shows a server approaching a seated guest with a beverage. according to conventional service, from which side should the beverage generally be served?', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"In conventional table service, beverages are generally served from the guest’s right side. The server should approach carefully without reaching across the guest and place the beverage on the table from the right. ","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Guest''s right\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"In conventional table service, beverages are generally served from the guest’s right side. The server should approach carefully without reaching across the guest and place the beverage on the table from the right. \n\n• Guest’s left: generally associated with serving certain foods, particularly when food is presented from a platter.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Guest''s left, Directly from the back, Directly from the front"}'::jsonb),
('mc:lactose is composed of::::glucose and galactose', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'lactose is composed of:', 'Glucose and galactose', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Lactose is a disaccharide composed of glucose and galactose.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Glucose and galactose\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Lactose is a disaccharide composed of glucose and galactose.\n\n• Glucose and fructose form sucrose, not lactose.\n• Fructose and galactose do not form lactose.\n• Glucose and sucrose are not the two monosaccharides that make up lactose.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Glucose and fructose, Glucose and sucrose, Fructose and galactose"}'::jsonb),
('mc:lactose is composed of::::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'lactose is composed of:', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Lactose is a disaccharide composed of glucose and galactose.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Glucose and galactose\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Lactose is a disaccharide composed of glucose and galactose.\n\n• Glucose and fructose form sucrose, not lactose.\n• Fructose and galactose do not form lactose.\n• Glucose and sucrose are not the two monosaccharides that make up lactose.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Glucose and fructose, Glucose and sucrose, Fructose and galactose"}'::jsonb),
('mc:which type of amino acid must be obtained from the diet because the body cannot synthesize it in sufficient amounts?:::essential amino acid', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'which type of amino acid must be obtained from the diet because the body cannot synthesize it in sufficient amounts?', 'Essential amino acid', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Essential amino acids cannot be synthesized by the body in adequate amounts and therefore must be supplied by the diet.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Essential amino acid\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Essential amino acids cannot be synthesized by the body in adequate amounts and therefore must be supplied by the diet.\n\nRemember the Essential AAs: PVTTIMHALL Phenylalanine Valine Threonine Tryptophan Isoleucine Methionine Histidine Leucine Lysine\n\n• Non-essential amino acids can generally be synthesized by the body.\n• Structural amino acid is not the nutritional classification described in the question.\n• Neutral amino acid describes a chemical property rather than the nutritional requirement being tested.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Neutral amino acid, Non-essential amino acid, Structural amino acid"}'::jsonb),
('mc:which type of amino acid must be obtained from the diet because the body cannot synthesize it in sufficient amounts?:::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'which type of amino acid must be obtained from the diet because the body cannot synthesize it in sufficient amounts?', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Essential amino acids cannot be synthesized by the body in adequate amounts and therefore must be supplied by the diet.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Essential amino acid\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Essential amino acids cannot be synthesized by the body in adequate amounts and therefore must be supplied by the diet.\n\nRemember the Essential AAs: PVTTIMHALL Phenylalanine Valine Threonine Tryptophan Isoleucine Methionine Histidine Leucine Lysine\n\n• Non-essential amino acids can generally be synthesized by the body.\n• Structural amino acid is not the nutritional classification described in the question.\n• Neutral amino acid describes a chemical property rather than the nutritional requirement being tested.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Neutral amino acid, Non-essential amino acid, Structural amino acid"}'::jsonb),
('mc:all are biological contaminants except::::pesticide residue', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'all are biological contaminants except:', 'Pesticide residue', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Biological contaminants are hazards that come from living organisms or their products:","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Pesticide residue\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Biological contaminants are hazards that come from living organisms or their products:\n• Paralytic shellfish poisoning — Biological. It is caused by toxins produced by certain marine microorganisms, particularly dinoflagellates.\n• Hepatitis A virus — Biological. Viruses are biological contaminants and can be transmitted through contaminated food or water.\n• Aflatoxin from molds — Biological. Aflatoxins are toxins produced by certain molds, particularly Aspergillus species.\n• Pesticide residue — Chemical, not biological. Pesticides are chemical substances that can remain on or in food.\n\nRemember:\n• Biological = bacteria, viruses, parasites, molds, and their toxins\n• Chemical = pesticides, cleaning chemicals, heavy metals, etc.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Paralytic shellfish poisoning, Hepatitis A virus, Aflatoxin from molds"}'::jsonb),
('mc:all are biological contaminants except::::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'all are biological contaminants except:', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Biological contaminants are hazards that come from living organisms or their products:","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Pesticide residue\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Biological contaminants are hazards that come from living organisms or their products:\n• Paralytic shellfish poisoning — Biological. It is caused by toxins produced by certain marine microorganisms, particularly dinoflagellates.\n• Hepatitis A virus — Biological. Viruses are biological contaminants and can be transmitted through contaminated food or water.\n• Aflatoxin from molds — Biological. Aflatoxins are toxins produced by certain molds, particularly Aspergillus species.\n• Pesticide residue — Chemical, not biological. Pesticides are chemical substances that can remain on or in food.\n\nRemember:\n• Biological = bacteria, viruses, parasites, molds, and their toxins\n• Chemical = pesticides, cleaning chemicals, heavy metals, etc.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Paralytic shellfish poisoning, Hepatitis A virus, Aflatoxin from molds"}'::jsonb),
('mc:a triglyceride is composed primarily of::::one glycerol molecule and three fatty acids', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'a triglyceride is composed primarily of:', 'One glycerol molecule and three fatty acids', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"One glycerol plus three fatty acids describes the basic structure of a triglyceride.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"One glycerol molecule and three fatty acids\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"One glycerol plus three fatty acids describes the basic structure of a triglyceride.\n\n• A triglyceride has a glycerol backbone esterified with three fatty acids.\n• Triglycerides are lipids, not compounds made from amino acids and glycerol.\n• Glucose is not the backbone of a triglyceride.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Three glycerol molecules and one fatty acid, One glucose molecule and three fatty acids, Three amino acids and one glycerol molecule"}'::jsonb)
