('mc:a central facility prepares meals in large quantities and distributes the prepared meals to several remote service locations. which system best fits this arrangement?:::commissary system', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'a central facility prepares meals in large quantities and distributes the prepared meals to several remote service locations. which system best fits this arrangement?', 'Commissary system', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"The commissary foodservice system (also referred to as a central kitchen or central production facility) concentrates food procurement and production in a single, large-scale facility.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Commissary system\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"The commissary foodservice system (also referred to as a central kitchen or central production facility) concentrates food procurement and production in a single, large-scale facility.\n\nOnce the meals are prepared in bulk, they are packaged and transported to distinct, remote service locations (often called satellite units or receiving kitchens) for final finishing and service.\n\n• À la carte system: A style of menu where items are priced and ordered separately, rather than a production and distribution system.\n• Conventional system: The traditional model where food is purchased, prepared from scratch, and served on the same premises.\n• Individualized system: A specialized approach focused on custom dietary tailoring (often used in clinical healthcare), not a bulk production and remote distribution network.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Conventional system, À la carte system, Individualized system"}'::jsonb),
('mc:a central facility prepares meals in large quantities and distributes the prepared meals to several remote service locations. which system best fits this arrangement?:::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'a central facility prepares meals in large quantities and distributes the prepared meals to several remote service locations. which system best fits this arrangement?', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"The commissary foodservice system (also referred to as a central kitchen or central production facility) concentrates food procurement and production in a single, large-scale facility.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Commissary system\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"The commissary foodservice system (also referred to as a central kitchen or central production facility) concentrates food procurement and production in a single, large-scale facility.\n\nOnce the meals are prepared in bulk, they are packaged and transported to distinct, remote service locations (often called satellite units or receiving kitchens) for final finishing and service.\n\n• À la carte system: A style of menu where items are priced and ordered separately, rather than a production and distribution system.\n• Conventional system: The traditional model where food is purchased, prepared from scratch, and served on the same premises.\n• Individualized system: A specialized approach focused on custom dietary tailoring (often used in clinical healthcare), not a bulk production and remote distribution network.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Conventional system, À la carte system, Individualized system"}'::jsonb),
('mc:under pd 856, the minimum kitchen size for a 40 m² dining area is .:::10 m²', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'under pd 856, the minimum kitchen size for a 40 m² dining area is .', '10 m²', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"According to the Implementing Rules and Regulations (IRR) of Chapter III (Food Establishments) of the Code on Sanitation of the Philippines (Presidential Decree No. 856), the kitchen area must be at least ¼ (25%) of the total dining area, provided it is not less than a base minimum of 9.30 m² (100 sq. ft.).","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"10 m²\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"According to the Implementing Rules and Regulations (IRR) of Chapter III (Food Establishments) of the Code on Sanitation of the Philippines (Presidential Decree No. 856), the kitchen area must be at least ¼ (25%) of the total dining area, provided it is not less than a base minimum of 9.30 m² (100 sq. ft.).\n\nCalculation: Required Kitchen Size = 40 m² x ¼ = 10 m²\n\nSince 10 m² meets the legal ratio and exceeds the minimum threshold of 9.30 m², it serves as the absolute statutory minimum required floor area for that setup.\n\n• 12 m² – Not prescribed.\n• 8 m² – Too small.\n• 15 m² – Above requirement.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: 12 m², 8 m², 15 m²"}'::jsonb),
