('mc:the shortage of potatoes due to the war between russia and ukraine has an impact on the production of this known alcoholic beverage.:::vodka', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'the shortage of potatoes due to the war between russia and ukraine has an impact on the production of this known alcoholic beverage.', 'Vodka', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Vodka –  While vodka can be distilled from various grains (like wheat or rye), potatoes are historically and traditionally one of its primary, iconic raw base ingredients.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Vodka\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Vodka –  While vodka can be distilled from various grains (like wheat or rye), potatoes are historically and traditionally one of its primary, iconic raw base ingredients.\n\n• Gin: Typically distilled from a grain mash (like wheat or barley) and flavored heavily with juniper berries.\n• Rum: Distilled exclusively from sugarcane byproducts, such as molasses or sugarcane juice.\n• Brandy: Produced by distilling wine or fermented fruit mashes (most commonly grapes).\n\n• Beer – Made from barley and hops.\n• Wine – Made from grapes.\n• Sake – Made from rice.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Brandy, Gin, Rum"}'::jsonb),
('mc:the shortage of potatoes due to the war between russia and ukraine has an impact on the production of this known alcoholic beverage.:::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'the shortage of potatoes due to the war between russia and ukraine has an impact on the production of this known alcoholic beverage.', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Vodka –  While vodka can be distilled from various grains (like wheat or rye), potatoes are historically and traditionally one of its primary, iconic raw base ingredients.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Vodka\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Vodka –  While vodka can be distilled from various grains (like wheat or rye), potatoes are historically and traditionally one of its primary, iconic raw base ingredients.\n\n• Gin: Typically distilled from a grain mash (like wheat or barley) and flavored heavily with juniper berries.\n• Rum: Distilled exclusively from sugarcane byproducts, such as molasses or sugarcane juice.\n• Brandy: Produced by distilling wine or fermented fruit mashes (most commonly grapes).\n\n• Beer – Made from barley and hops.\n• Wine – Made from grapes.\n• Sake – Made from rice.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Brandy, Gin, Rum"}'::jsonb),
('mc:a picture shows four pieces of institutional kitchen equipment. which equipment would be most appropriate for large-volume soup production?:::steam-jacketed kettle', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'a picture shows four pieces of institutional kitchen equipment. which equipment would be most appropriate for large-volume soup production?', 'Steam-jacketed kettle', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"• Salamander: primarily used for broiling or finishing foods.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Steam-jacketed kettle\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"• Salamander: primarily used for broiling or finishing foods.\n• Bain-marie: suited to gentle heating or holding rather than primary large-volume soup production.\n• Griddle: designed for direct cooking on a heated flat surface.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Bain-marie, Griddle, Salamander"}'::jsonb),
('mc:a picture shows four pieces of institutional kitchen equipment. which equipment would be most appropriate for large-volume soup production?:::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'a picture shows four pieces of institutional kitchen equipment. which equipment would be most appropriate for large-volume soup production?', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"• Salamander: primarily used for broiling or finishing foods.","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Steam-jacketed kettle\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"• Salamander: primarily used for broiling or finishing foods.\n• Bain-marie: suited to gentle heating or holding rather than primary large-volume soup production.\n• Griddle: designed for direct cooking on a heated flat surface.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Bain-marie, Griddle, Salamander"}'::jsonb),
('mc:a foodservice manager wants to reduce the risk of microbial growth in cooked food during preparation, holding, and service. which practice best demonstrates proper time-temperature control?:::monitoring food temperatures and minimizing the time food remains at unsafe temperatures', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'a foodservice manager wants to reduce the risk of microbial growth in cooked food during preparation, holding, and service. which practice best demonstrates proper time-temperature control?', 'Monitoring food temperatures and minimizing the time food remains at unsafe temperatures', true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Foodborne pathogens multiply rapidly in the Temperature Danger Zone, which is between 41°F and 135°F (5°C to 57°C).","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Monitoring food temperatures and minimizing the time food remains at unsafe temperatures\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Foodborne pathogens multiply rapidly in the Temperature Danger Zone, which is between 41°F and 135°F (5°C to 57°C).\n\nProper time-temperature control for safety (TCS) requires food managers to constantly measure temperatures using food thermometers and ensure that foods move through or stay out of this danger zone as quickly as possible to prevent dangerous microbial growth.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Checking on appearance and odor to determine whether food is safe, Storing cooked food to remain at room temperature until needed, Keeping all foods at the same temperature regardless of whether they are served hot or cold to save storage space"}'::jsonb),
('mc:a foodservice manager wants to reduce the risk of microbial growth in cooked food during preparation, holding, and service. which practice best demonstrates proper time-temperature control?:::', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, '"2026-11-04T11:21:38.282Z"'::jsonb, 'a foodservice manager wants to reduce the risk of microbial growth in cooked food during preparation, holding, and service. which practice best demonstrates proper time-temperature control?', NULL, true, '{"sources":[{"url":"","title":"Krause and Mahan''s Food & the Nutrition Care Process"},{"url":"","title":"NDLE Board Licensure Reference Manual"}],"whyRight":"Foodborne pathogens multiply rapidly in the Temperature Danger Zone, which is between 41°F and 135°F (5°C to 57°C).","modelUsed":"Gemini 2.5 Flash","isAiPowered":true,"keyDifference":"Key differentiator: \"Monitoring food temperatures and minimizing the time food remains at unsafe temperatures\" aligns strictly with standard dietetics criteria.","searchOverview":"Official NDLE Board Licensure Reference & Evidence Breakdown","authorRationale":"Foodborne pathogens multiply rapidly in the Temperature Danger Zone, which is between 41°F and 135°F (5°C to 57°C).\n\nProper time-temperature control for safety (TCS) requires food managers to constantly measure temperatures using food thermometers and ensure that foods move through or stay out of this danger zone as quickly as possible to prevent dangerous microbial growth.","whyWrongChoices":"Alternative options: Checking on appearance and odor to determine whether food is safe, Storing cooked food to remain at room temperature until needed, Keeping all foods at the same temperature regardless of whether they are served hot or cold to save storage space"}'::jsonb)
